<Placemark><name>Thomas Quarry hominins (~773 ka) basal to the Homo sapiens lineage</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published in Nature with full stratigraphic documentation; the morphological case is detailed, but it is one quarry complex and the LCA attribution rests on the current genetic-split window — corroborated, not verified.
Layer: real · Place: Thomas Quarry I, Casablanca, Morocco
Dates: -800000 → -750000 (confidence: medium)

Hominin fossils from Thomas Quarry I (Casablanca, Morocco), dated to approximately 773,000 years ago, are basal to the Homo sapiens lineage and push the African origin of the last common ancestor of humans and Neanderthals to the older end of the genetic split estimates — with northwest Africa now central to the deep-roots story, linked to other African regions by population movement across the Sahara during wetter phases.

Earliest attestation: Thomas Quarry I fossils, excavated over decades of stratigraphic work; announced as a lineage-basal assemblage in the January 2026 Nature paper
Sources: Hublin, Lefèvre, Perini et al. 2026, Nature, doi:10.1038/s41586-025-09914-y — 'Early hominins from Morocco basal to the Homo sapiens lineage'; Genetic split estimates for the sapiens–Neanderthal divergence (~765–550 ka)
Falsifiability: A securely dated, directly comparable stem-lineage fossil assemblage elsewhere in Africa older than Thomas Quarry would break the northwest-Africa centrality claim; retraction of the dating on stratigraphic grounds would collapse the age.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-7.683,33.572,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tromerosaurus solumons — the mosaic tyrannosaur (Oldman Formation, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed species description (JVP, online first 2 Oct 2026) with holotype diagnosis, maxillary morphometrics, and character-taxon analysis; withheld from VERIFIED because the taxon rests on a single partial skeleton and the albertosaurine-vs-tyrannosaurine placement is analysis-dependent.
Layer: real · Place: Oldman Formation, Alberta, Canada (exposures associated with the upper Oldman Formation; exact locality in the describing paper)
Dates: -76000000 → -76000000 (confidence: medium)

A new tyrannosaurid genus and species, Tromerosaurus solumons, described October 2026 from a partial skeleton excavated in 2010–2011 from the Upper Cretaceous Oldman Formation of Alberta (~76 Ma, Campanian), mixes features of both major tyrannosaurid subfamilies — a mosaic of gracile albertosaurine and robust tyrannosaurine characters — and supports a younger divergence of the two lineages with rapid character acquisition during the Late Cretaceous (Santonian–Maastrichtian). The description also reassigns the 'largest adult Gorgosaurus' specimen to Tromerosaurus.

Earliest attestation: The fossil itself — partial skeleton (substantial skull portion, vertebrae, both humeri, hind limb material, parts of pelvis) discovered 2010 in the upper Oldman Formation, recovered over the 2010 and 2011 field seasons by University of Alberta expeditions; skull prepared at the University of Alberta Laboratory for Vertebrate Palaeontology; described in print October 2026
Sources: Powers et al. 2026, Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology (online first 2 October 2026) — description of Tromerosaurus solumons from the Oldman Formation; holotype UALVP 52981; maxillary morphometrics (anteroposteriorly longer maxillary fenestra, narrower pila promaxillaris than Gorgosaurus libratus); anatomical diagnosis permitting referral of the 'largest adult Gorgosaurus' specimen; parsimony vs Bayesian topology disagreement; younger divergence + rapid character acquisition inference (as reported in the Oct 2026 press cycle); New Scientist, 19 October 2026 — 'Discovery of T. rex's cousin upends our understanding of tyrannosaur evolution' (early albertosaurine; 'frightful lizard' + Tolkien 'lonely mountain' etymology; Jared Voris's call for deeper 78–79 Ma sampling); Everything Dinosaur (blog), 5 October 2026 — 'Remarkable New Tyrannosaur Highlights an Evolutionary Puzzle' (author-compiled with images from lead author Mark J. Powers, University of Alberta; ~76 Ma Campanian; JVP venue confirmed)
Falsifiability: A re-examination reassigning the holotype to Gorgosaurus or Daspletosaurus on overlapping material, or a phylogenetic re-analysis collapsing the diagnosis into existing taxa, would sink the species; a second independent tyrannosaurid dataset recovering deep divergence timing would weaken the rapid-radiation reading.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-112.0,50.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-760000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first adult T. rex trackway — Hell Creek, North Dakota, described 2026</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed primary description in the Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology (12 September 2026) with documented measurements, high-resolution 3D scan archive, and a museum deposition plan — withheld from VERIFIED because it is a single study and the trackmaker attribution is explicitly 'likely', not certain.
Layer: real · Place: Hell Creek Formation near Marmarth, southwestern North Dakota (Dakota Prairie Grasslands)
Dates: -66500000 → -66500000 (confidence: medium)

A peer-reviewed study published in September 2026 in the Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology (DOI 10.1080/02724634.2026.2706201) (Falkingham, Hups, Tamez-Galvan, Bercovici &amp; Lyson) formally describes 'a large tridactyl trackway from the Upper Cretaceous Hell Creek Formation of North Dakota, likely attributable to an adult Tyrannosaurus rex' — the first known multi-step adult tyrannosaur trackway: four consecutive three-toed prints (left-right-left-right) across ~7 m (23 ft), each print ~1 m long and 0.6–0.8 m wide, stride ~4 m (13 ft), yielding an estimated walking speed of ~3.5–4.5 mph (5.6–7.2 km/h). The prints were found in July 2025 by Colorado high-school science teacher Kent M. Hups (volunteer research associate, Denver Museum of Nature &amp; Science) on Dakota Prairie Grasslands ~2.5 miles northwest of Marmarth, ND, ~33 m below the top of the Hell Creek Formation (~66.5 Ma). High-resolution 3D surface scans were analyzed by lead author Peter Falkingham in England via computer and VR; three exposed prints were scheduled for helicopter removal to the Denver Museum of Nature &amp; Science in autumn 2026. Trackmaker attribution rests on size, narrow-elongate toe shape (theropod, not the broad-toed Edmontosaurus, the only other Hell Creek animal that big), location, and the local abundance of adult T. rex remains; the authors' own wording is 'likely attributable' and the measurements are presented as approximate because the impressions are strongly weathered.

Earliest attestation: Falkingham, P. L., Hups, K. M., Tamez-Galvan, E. M., Bercovici, A. D. &amp; Lyson, T. R. (2026) 'A large tridactyl trackway from the Upper Cretaceous Hell Creek Formation of North Dakota, likely attributable to an adult Tyrannosaurus rex.' Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology. https://doi.org/10.1080/02724634.2026.2706201 (accepted manuscript, open access via LJMU Research Online; submitted 23 Jan 2026, accepted 13 July 2026; published September 2026).
Sources: Falkingham, P. L. et al., Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology (2026), DOI 10.1080/02724634.2026.2706201 — 'A large tridactyl trackway from the Upper Cretaceous Hell Creek Formation of North Dakota, likely attributable to an adult Tyrannosaurus rex' (accepted manuscript, open access); Denver Museum of Nature &amp; Science press release, via earth.com 2026-10-22 — Kent Hups discovery (July 2025), four prints, ~66.5 Ma, 3.5–4.5 mph walking estimate, three prints helicopter-lifted in early October; Tyler Lyson (DMNS senior curator) on the attribution basis
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed ichnological re-study re-identifying the four impressions as erosion features or non-tyrannosaurian would overturn the attribution; the trackway's existence stands on the peer-reviewed description and the Denver Museum's 3D scan archive.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-103.92,46.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-665000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The K-Pg boundary's microfossil witness: the planktic foraminiferal turnover and the &lt;2,000-year recovery</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent boundary sections worldwide record the foram turnover at the horizon; the recovery-timing sub-claim rests on one 2026 peer-reviewed paper (six sites, independent chronometer) — CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED; the mechanistic selectivity claim is single-model, fenced accordingly.
Layer: real · Place: K-Pg boundary global — stratotype El Kef, Tunisia (plus the six 2026 3He sites spanning Europe, North Africa, the Gulf of Mexico)
Dates: -66043000 → -66043000 (confidence: high)

The planktic foraminiferal turnover at the K-Pg boundary is the extinction's highest-resolution fossil witness. Cretaceous planktic foraminifera vanish at the boundary horizon worldwide, and the first new Paleocene species — Parvularugoglobigerina eugubina, the base of Biochron P0 — appear within thousands of years of the impact: Lowery et al. 2026's 3He recalibration at six boundary sites gives Biochron P0 a duration of 3.5–11.1 kyr (average 6.4 kyr) and puts the first new species under 2,000 years after the Chicxulub impact — replacing the long-accepted ~30 kyr estimate that assumed constant sedimentation across the boundary. The selectivity is real and mechanistic: Rui Ying et al. 2026's EcoGENIE model of the first 100 post-impact years reproduces the fossil pattern — large forams and zooplankton die, small phytoplankton and mixotrophs survive — with impact-driven darkness plus body-size-dependent extinction thresholds doing the work.

Earliest attestation: The boundary clay itself — Cretaceous planktic forams absent above it; Parvularugoglobigerina eugubina first appears centimeters above the horizon
Sources: Lowery, Bralower et al. 2026, Geology 54(3):285–288, DOI 10.1130/G53313.1 — 'New species evolved within a few thousand years of the Chicxulub Impact': 3He sedimentation-rate recalibration at six K-Pg sites; Biochron P0 = 3.5–11.1 kyr (avg 6.4 kyr), first new planktic foram species &lt;2 kyr post-impact; up to 10 new species within Zone P0 (taxonomy varies); the old ~30 kyr estimate assumed constant sedimentation, shown to be invalid across the cataclysm; Rui Ying et al. 2026, Nature, DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-10541-4 — 'Ocean environmental and plankton community responses to abrupt K-Pg climate change within a century (100 years) of the Chicxulub impact': EcoGENIE global ocean ecosystem model; size-dependent extinction rule reproduces the fossil pattern (large forams/zooplankton die, small phytoplankton/mixotrophs survive); darkness + body-size thresholds as the coupled driver. A model, fenced — reproduces, does not attest; The boundary horizon's foram turnover is textbook paleontology: the planktic foram extinction is the primary biostratigraphic marker of the K-Pg boundary (Zone P0 defined by the extinction of Cretaceous species and the first appearance of Parvularugoglobigerina eugubina) — the micropaleontological discipline's standard reference
Falsifiability: If Cretaceous planktic forams were shown to cross the boundary in substantial numbers (not as reworked fossils), or if the 3He cosmic-dust-flux calibration underpinning the 2026 timing is shown to vary with the solar system dust input at the boundary, the timing claim reopens.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>8.58,36.15,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-660430</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The rock that fell: end-Cretaceous extinction</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Independent lines converge: global iridium layer, shocked quartz, impact spherules at 50+ boundary sites, a ~180 km crater drilled and dated to the boundary, and isotope fingerprints of the impactor — all coincident with the extinction horizon.
Layer: real · Place: Chicxulub, Yucatan Peninsula, Mexico
Dates: -66040000 → -66040000 (confidence: high)

66.04 million years ago an asteroid 10–15 km wide struck Chicxulub (Yucatan, 21.40°N 89.52°W), ending the non-avian dinosaurs and ~75% of species. Ruthenium isotopes (Fischer-Gödde et al. 2024) identify a carbonaceous-type impactor from beyond Jupiter; nickel isotopes (Makhatadze et al. 2026) narrow it to a CO (Ornans-class) chondrite. The kill-mechanism evidence has shifted from sulfur aerosols toward fine silicate dust (Senel et al. 2023; Johnson et al. 2026). Deccan Traps volcanism is the main competing cause (Keller camp, 2025 book) — current peer-reviewed consensus holds the impact as the trigger and main driver, Deccan a contributing stressor.

Earliest attestation: Global K-Pg boundary clay; Chicxulub crater (identified Hildebrand et al. 1991); IODP Expedition 364 peak-ring core (2016)
Sources: Alvarez, Alvarez, Asaro &amp; Michel 1980, Science 208:1095–1108 (global iridium anomaly; impactor 10 ± 4 km); Hildebrand et al. 1991, Geology 19:867–871 (Chicxulub crater: ~180 km, ~1 km buried under Yucatán); Morgan et al. 2016, Science 354:878–882 (IODP Expedition 364: peak-ring shocked granite uplifted from 8–11 km; gypsum vaporized); Fischer-Gödde et al. 2024, Science 385:752–756 (Ru isotopes: carbonaceous-type asteroid from beyond Jupiter; comet ruled out); Makhatadze et al. 2026, Science Advances (Ni isotopes: CO chondrite — one of the rarest meteorite classes); Senel et al. 2023, Nature Geoscience (fine silicate dust: cooling up to 15°C, photosynthesis shutdown ~2 yr); Hull et al. 2020, Science 367:266–272 (impact sole driver of the extinction pulse; Deccan caused ~2°C warming, not mass extinction); DePalma et al. 2019, PNAS 116:8190–8199 (Tanis, North Dakota — seismically induced surge deposit at the boundary; sedimentology accepted, season claim contested); Keller 2025, The Last Extinction (Diversion Books) — the credentialed minority's Deccan-as-primary case, stated fairly; Johnson, Johnson, Wakita &amp; Robertson 2026, Journal of Geophysical Research: Biogeosciences 131(7) (fine-dust-enhanced heat and wildfires at the boundary; doi:10.1029/2026jg009837 — the kill-mechanism sequence keeps being revised; the impact itself stays VERIFIED strength); Baker &amp; Casadevall 2026, PNAS (pre-impact fungal spike — carried via secondary coverage of the May 2026 PNAS paper; fenced: the authors' own reading is ecological stress, not an alternative extinction mechanism); Lowery, Bralower et al. 2026, Geology 54(3):285–288, DOI 10.1130/G53313.1 — 3He recalibration at six boundary sites: Biochron P0 3.5–11.1 kyr (avg 6.4 kyr), first new planktic foram species &lt;2 kyr after the impact; the old ~30 kyr estimate assumed constant sedimentation — shown invalid across the cataclysm; Rui Ying et al. 2026, Nature, DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-10541-4 — EcoGENIE first-100-years model: size-dependent extinction (large forams/zooplankton die, small phytoplankton/mixotrophs survive); impact-driven darkness + body-size thresholds as the coupled driver. A model — reproduces the fossil pattern, does not attest it
Falsifiability: If the iridium layer, shocked quartz, and spherules were shown to sit stratigraphically above or below the extinction horizon rather than coincident with it, the impact-extinction link would reopen</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-89.52,21.4,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-660400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Infernogallus jamesi — the 'hell chicken' and its first complete skull (Hell Creek, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed Nature Communications description with the first nearly complete caenagnathid skull (a genuine completeness milestone for a group whose delicate skeletons rarely fossilize); withheld from VERIFIED because the species rests on one specimen and the diet is a mechanics-based inference.
Layer: real · Place: Hell Creek Formation, Montana / South Dakota / North Dakota
Dates: -66000000 → -66000000 (confidence: medium)

A new caenagnathid species, Infernogallus jamesi ('hell chicken'), described in Nature Communications 2026 from the Hell Creek Formation (~66 Ma), preserves the first nearly complete skull ever found for the caenagnathid group of toothless, beaked, birdlike theropods — a 26-pound (12-kg) turkey-sized animal whose jaw mechanics point toward a fruit-eating diet, widening the documented dietary range of late-Cretaceous feathered dinosaurs at the K-Pg boundary.

Earliest attestation: The fossil itself — ROM VP 65050, excavated from the Hell Creek Formation; described in print 2026 (Nature Communications 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41467-026-78343-w)
Sources: Nature Communications 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41467-026-78343-w — Infernogallus jamesi (ROM VP 65050): first nearly complete caenagnathid skull; 12-kg turkey-sized; Hell Creek ~66 Ma; jaw mechanics consistent with frugivory (as reported in the Oct 2026 press cycle); Phys.org, 14 October 2026 — 'Toothless 'hell chicken' dinosaur may have had a taste for fruit' (caenagnathid context; skull completeness as the research unlock; ~66 Ma; toothless beak and slender body)
Falsifiability: A re-examination reassigning the skull to an existing caenagnathid taxon, or biomechanical re-analysis contradicting the jaw-mechanics dietary inference, would downgrade the species or the diet claim; the first-complete-skull finding stands on the specimen's own completeness.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-103.5,46.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-660000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cambrian explosion: the fossil record's rapid animal diversification, ~541–520 Ma</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The diversification pattern in the fossil record is multiply attested: five independent Lagerstätten across three continents plus the small shelly fossil record, the Fortunian GSSP, and the 2026 Huayuan and myllokunmingid additions. Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: the pattern is observed, but the central interpretive claim — biological explosion vs. taphonomic unveiling — is actively disputed by the 2026 evidence (Durbin et al. clocks, Dunn et al. Ediacaran complex animals).
Layer: real · Place: Fortune Head, Newfoundland (Fortunian GSSP); the Lagerstätten span Canada, China, Greenland
Dates: -541000000 → -520000000 (confidence: high)

The fossil record shows a geologically rapid diversification of animal body plans in the early Cambrian, ~541–520 million years ago: most animal phyla first appear in Cambrian strata, preserved in exceptional soft-bodied detail across multiple independent localities worldwide — the Burgess Shale (Canada, 1909, ~508 Ma), Chengjiang (China, 1984, ~518 Ma), Sirius Passet (North Greenland, ~518 Ma), Doushantuo/Weng'an (China), and now the Huayuan biota (China, 2026, ~512 Ma, post-Sinsk extinction). The Cambrian base is fixed at the Fortunian GSSP, Fortune Head, Newfoundland (541 Ma). The small shelly fossils mark the boundary interval. The 'explosion' is a fossil-record pattern; whether it was a biological radiation or a taphonomic unveiling is the live dispute.

Earliest attestation: Charles Doolittle Walcott's 1909 Burgess Shale discoveries, Stephen Jay Gould's 'Wonderful Life' (1989) for the explosion narrative; the Fortunian GSSP ratified 2007.
Sources: Fortunian GSSP, Fortune Head, Newfoundland (ratified 2007) — the Cambrian base at 541 Ma; Walcott 1909 + the Burgess Shale (~508 Ma, British Columbia) — the original soft-bodied Lagerstätte; Hou Xianguang 1984 + the Chengjiang biota (~518 Ma, Yunnan) — the earliest chordate Myllokunmingia; 2026 Nature paper: myllokunmingids carried four camera-type eyes (Xu Xing, Cong Peiyun et al., reported by University of Leicester, Jan 2026); Sirius Passet biota (~518 Ma, North Greenland) — the northernmost Cambrian Lagerstätte; Han Zeng et al., Nature, Jan 2026 — the Huayuan biota (~512 Ma): 90+ new species, 153 species total, deep-water refuge 1.5 Myr after the Sinsk extinction; Helmetia and Surusicaris shared with Burgess Shale; fossil pelagic tunicates date the ocean carbon pump to the Cambrian (reported Quanta Magazine, May 2026); Durbin, Anderson, Briggs et al. 2026, Science Advances (doi:10.1126/sciadv.aeg6289) — the taphonomy challenge: the Kheseen Biota (Mongolia) is as well-preserved as Weng'an but contains no animals despite their known existence elsewhere; the Weng'an absence-inference collapses; Tonian-calibrated clocks push animal origins to 800–700 Ma. MODEL, fenced: a clock inference, not an observation (evt-animal-origins-molecular-clock-2026-001); Dunn et al. 2026, Science (April 2, 2026) — the Jiangchuan biota (~539 Ma): 700+ fossils with mouths, anuses, burrowing, three-dimensional lives in the waning Ediacaran, millions of years before the Cambrian boundary (reported phys.org, April 2026) (evt-jiangchuan-ediacaran-complex-2026-001); First-pass review 2026-10-18 (via #535 Dr. Stian Viken — The Explosion Reader): CORROBORATED assigned — the timeline's first Cambrian-explosion record; the fossil-record pattern graded, the interpretation carried as live dispute; no prior cambrian record existed on disk; He et al., Current Biology (2026), doi:10.1016/j.cub.2026.08.053 — Yujingia glutenotunica (~518 Ma, Maotianshan Shales): tentaculate feeding near the ambulacrarian stem; the observed anatomy enriches the soft-bodied pattern (evt-yujingia-glutenotunica-2026-001); Swiss Journal of Palaeontology (2026, via scienmag Oct 2026) — Kaotaia globosa (~500 Ma, Kaili Biota, Guizhou): 145 specimens across nearly the full post-embryonic sequence; a head-to-tail increasing growth gradient governing trunk segmentation — Cambrian developmental biology as a measured mechanism; Second-pass review 2026-10-18 (via #544 Dr. Sigrid Aas — The Burrow Reader): CORROBORATED held — no supersession; the Oct-2026 Cambrian press cycle (Yujingia, Kaotaia) adds soft-bodied and developmental exhibits to the pattern without resolving the explosion-vs-unveiling interpretation dispute (the II.7 pattern: chain strengthened, grades held); fenced: the bioscience.com.pk 'Youti yuanshi' 520-Ma larva-brain piece — aggregator only, primary Nature reference unverified, not counted as evidence; Chen, Lirong et al. 2026, Proc. R. Soc. B (doi:10.1098/rspb.2026.1709) — Polyostiosphaera operculata gen. et sp. nov. (Kuanchuanpu Biota, early Cambrian, South China): phosphatized multicellular algae with plug-like opercula sealing apertures over an inner reproductive cell cluster (micro-CT); 'Was the Cambrian explosion merely an explosion of animals?' — the primary-producer complexity exhibit; ECOSYSTEM CONTEXT, not an animal-diversification exhibit; grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent); Song, Baopeng et al. 2026, Nature (3 June 2026, doi:10.1038/s41586-026-10590-9) — High-fidelity modular skeletons authenticate a Cambrian origin for Bryozoa: Protomelission gatehousei + Dayingomelission hexaclitia gen. et sp. nov. (Xiannüdong Formation, ~520 Ma) with phosphatized soft tissues (styles, annular muscles, membranous sacs, ring septa) in modular skeletons; crown-group stenolaemates. Bryozoa was the last major animal phylum without a Cambrian representative — the gap is now closed, and the phylum was already diversifying during the radiation (the authors place the origin possibly pre-Cambrian). Grade-neutral: confirms the CORROBORATED pattern more completely; appended 2026-10-23 (I.7 fourteenth pass, ep954, Turin-shroud precedent)
Falsifiability: A well-documented pre-Cambrian assemblage of diverse, clearly animal body-fossils (not traces, not contested microfossils) would collapse the 'explosion' as a biological timing claim; the fossil-record pattern itself would only fall if the Lagerstätten's ages or taxonomic assignments were systematically wrong.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-81.22,42.78,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-541000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yujingia glutenotunica (2026): the tentacled ancestor that killed the burrowing-worm reading</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed primary (Current Biology 2026) with multiple independent press corroborations of the same paper (Cambridge press release, phys.org, scitechdaily, scienmag, geologypage) — the anatomy is photographed and described; the 'last common ancestor' placement is carried as the authors' inference, fenced as interpretation. Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: a new species from a few specimens makes one paper, and the tree position is an inference, not a second observation.
Layer: real · Place: Maotianshan Shales, Yunnan Province, China (Chengjiang biota)
Dates: -518000000 → -518000000 (confidence: high)

The 2026 Current Biology paper by Kaiyue He, Degan Shu, Graham Mussini and colleagues describes Yujingia glutenotunica, a ~518-million-year-old fossil from the Maotianshan Shales (Yunnan, China — the Chengjiang biota), interpreted as close to the last common ancestor of the ambulacrarians (modern starfish, sea urchins, and acorn worms). The 8–22 mm animal carried two sets of appendages: feather-like feeding tentacles that captured suspended particles, and a second set for temporary seabed anchoring — an active particle-feeding lifestyle. The paper's central argument: this contradicts the long-standing hypothesis that the ambulacrarian common ancestor was a burrowing worm that filtered food through its throat and gill openings. The authors (incl. Degan Shu, Northwest University) frame it as evidence that ecology — the chordate branch streamlining for movement, the ambulacrarian branch anchoring and specializing for particle feeding — drove the two deuterostome lineages onto divergent body plans.

Earliest attestation: He et al., Current Biology (2026), doi:10.1016/j.cub.2026.08.053; University of Cambridge press material, Sept 2026.
Sources: He et al., Current Biology (2026), doi:10.1016/j.cub.2026.08.053 — 'Cambrian fossils illuminate the ambulacrarian common ancestor' (Yujingia glutenotunica, ~518 Ma, Maotianshan Shales); University of Cambridge press material (Sept 2026), reprinted via geologypage.com (Oct 7, 2026) — Mussini: ecology drove the lineages onto divergent paths; Shu: Yujingia connects primitive tentacle-bearing animals with modern echinoderms and hemichordates; phys.org (Sep 16, 2026) — the Maotianshan Shales' soft-body preservation window: one of the handful of sites where soft Cambrian anatomy survives; Second-pass review 2026-10-18 (via #544 Dr. Sigrid Aas — The Burrow Reader): CORROBORATED assigned — peer-reviewed primary with photographed anatomy; the ancestor placement fenced as tree inference; note: the reading it kills (the burrowing-worm ancestor) is a body-fossil answer to what was partly a trace-fossil question — the Burrow Reader's lane
Falsifiability: Re-examination showing the 'feeding tentacles' are taphonomic artifacts or misidentified structures, or a phylogenetic re-analysis nesting Yujingia outside the ambulacrarian stem, would collapse the ancestor claim; the observed anatomy (two appendage sets, tentaculate feeding structures, 8–22 mm size range) is the photographic evidence.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>102.94,24.66,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-518000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Herugavialis cairensis (2026) — the crocodile in the Sphinx's stone</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed taxonomic revision in Royal Society Open Science by an international team (UK, Sweden, Germany, Egypt), based on CT re-examination of museum specimens with the diagnostic characters stated — independent secondary attestation via CNN, The Watchers, and others within days. The genus call is the authors' conclusion; the fossil-in-the-formation fact is multiply attested.
Layer: real · Place: Gebel Mokattam, east of Cairo, Egypt (the formation the Sphinx was carved from)
Dates: -45000000 → -45000000 (confidence: high)

A new genus of ancient crocodile, Herugavialis cairensis, was identified in 2026 from fossils in the Mokattam Formation — the same Middle Eocene (~45 million-year-old) limestone from which the Great Sphinx of Giza was carved. The reclassification (Burke et al., Royal Society Open Science, 7 Oct 2026) moves a 1920s skull (Müller 1927, 'Tomistoma cairense') into its own genus via CT re-analysis; the genus name honors Heru (Horus), after the coincidence that the skull — like the Sphinx — is missing the tip of its snout.

Earliest attestation: Burke, P. et al. 2026, Royal Society Open Science (published 7 Oct 2026); specimen collected from Gebel Mokattam in the 1920s, described by Lorenz Müller in 1927.
Sources: Burke, P. et al. 2026, Royal Society Open Science (7 Oct 2026) — the Herugavialis cairensis paper: CT re-analysis of the 1920s Mokattam skull, new genus, named for Heru/Horus after the missing-snout coincidence; CNN 2026-10-06: 'Scientists name ancient crocodile species found in rock that built the Great Sphinx' — Paul Burke (Swedish Museum of Natural History) and Philip Mannion (UCL) as co-leads; 'one of the six species of gharials that we see in the Mediterranean across a 43 million year period'; The Watchers 2026-10: 'Crocodilian fossil from the Sphinx's rock formation reveals a different evolutionary history' — the Müller 1927 Tomistoma classification history and the CT-driven reclassification
Falsifiability: A published phylogenetic re-analysis returning the specimen to Tomistoma, or demonstration that the CT characters used are within Tomistoma's variation, would revise the genus call.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.28,30.03,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-450000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The contested Nuvvuagittuq microfossils</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Published in Nature (2017) and Science Advances (2022) with a follow-up structure no known abiotic process reproduces; but named skeptics dispute both the biogenicity and the age, and the host-belt chronology is actively being revised. Attested, single-source lineage, contested.
Layer: real · Place: Nuvvuagittuq Supracrustal Belt, northern Quebec (Hudson Bay east coast)
Dates: -4280000000 → -3770000000 (confidence: low)

Hematite tubes, filaments, and knobs in jasper of the Nuvvuagittuq Supracrustal Belt (Quebec) are microfossils of iron-metabolizing bacteria that lived 3.77–4.28 billion years ago near seafloor hydrothermal vents.

Earliest attestation: Dodd et al. 2017, Nature (hematite tubes as microfossils); Papineau et al. 2022, Science Advances (tree-like stem, metabolic by-products)
Sources: Dodd et al. 2017, Nature — hematite tubes/filaments/knobs in NSB jasper as iron-oxidizing microfossils, 3.77–4.28 Ga; Bower: 'I am not convinced the structures are biogenic'; Papineau et al. 2022, Science Advances — cm-scale tree-like hematite stem with parallel branches, hundreds of distorted ellipsoids, metabolic by-products consistent with Fe/S/CO2 metabolism; O'Neil et al. 2025, Science (June 26, 2025) — dual Sm–Nd dating of crosscutting Nuvvuagittuq intrusions to 4.16 Ga; NEW record evt-nuvvuagittuq-belt-oldest-rocks-2025-001 CORROBORATED (the belt as Earth's oldest dated rocks); the 2026 coverage explicitly fences the result: 'establishes a geological clock and ordering, not a fossil, metabolic product or inhabited ecosystem' — the clock is settled, the tubes' biogenicity is not.
Falsifiability: If the structures are shown to be metamorphic artifacts, or the host features dated reliably below ~3.5 Ga, the claim as stated falls</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-77.9,58.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-428000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The oldest rocks on Earth — Nuvvuagittuq at 4.16 Ga (the clock, not the fossil)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent isotopic chronometers converging on the same age in a peer-reviewed Science paper; single-team result on extraordinary-age rocks keeps it at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED. The 2026 reporting (ScienceDaily) independently reaffirms the finding and carries the crucial fence: a clock, not a fossil.
Layer: real · Place: Nuvvuagittuq Supracrustal Belt, Hudson Bay east coast near Inukjuak, Quebec
Dates: -4160000000 → -4160000000 (confidence: medium)

The Nuvvuagittuq Supracrustal Belt (northern Quebec) contains the oldest dated rocks on Earth: crosscutting intrusions dated to 4.16 billion years by two independent samarium–neodymium chronometers (O'Neil et al., Science, June 2025), making this the only known intact rock record of the Hadean eon.

Earliest attestation: O'Neil et al. 2025, Science (dual Sm–Nd dating, published June 26, 2025)
Sources: O'Neil et al. 2025, Science — dual samarium–neodymium dating of Nuvvuagittuq belt intrusions to 4.16 Ga; the belt's older host rocks must predate the intrusions; Earth's oldest known rocks, the only Hadean rock record; ScienceDaily coverage, August 27, 2026 — reaffirms the 4.16 Ga result; the belt is on Inuit-owned land (Pituvik Landholding Corporation) with restricted access after specimen damage; explicit fence: the paper 'establishes a geological clock and ordering, not a fossil, metabolic product or inhabited ecosystem'
Falsifiability: An independent chronometer system (e.g. U–Pb on better-constrained phases) returning a significantly younger age for the same intrusions would revise the 4.16 Ga figure; the crosscutting logic itself would need a structural reinterpretation to fall.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-77.9,58.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-416000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The contested 3.7 Ga Isua 'stromatolites'</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single primary paper claims biogenicity; a peer-reviewed Nature reassessment (2018) argues abiotic tectonic deformation is the more plausible explanation; the claimants dispute the sampling; the planned 2019 joint field trip has no published resolution. Contested, not settled.
Layer: real · Place: Isua supracrustal belt, southwestern Greenland
Dates: -3700000000 → -3700000000 (confidence: medium)

The cone-shaped structures in the 3.7-billion-year-old Isua Greenstone Belt (southwest Greenland) are fossilized microbial stromatolites — life on Earth by 3.7 Ga.

Earliest attestation: Nutman et al. 2016, Nature (biogenic claim); Allwood et al. 2018, Nature (abiotic reassessment)
Sources: Nutman et al. 2016, Nature — cone structures interpreted as stromatolites; four reasons: onlapping sediment, low-temperature dolomite, sedimentary (non-hydrothermal) geochemistry, laminated internal fabric; Allwood et al. 2018, Nature — 3D morphological and geochemical reassessment: structures 'most plausibly explained' by deformation of layered rock under tectonic compression/elongation; no biosignatures found; Allwood/Nutman dispute coverage — Nutman: 'classic comparing-apples-and-oranges scenario' (samples ~0.5 m apart); Allwood's planned joint field trip to settle it; van Zuilen commentary on the outcrop's under-studied geology; Wu &amp; Jiang 2022, Biopetrology 1(2):61-69 — agree the Nutman cones are structural folds (with Zawaski et al. 2020); report two other candidate stromatolite structures with domal/conic laminae, furcation, and discontinuity in the same Isua rocks; October 2026 evidence-state check (third pass): literature search found no peer-reviewed resolution of the Nutman (2016) vs Allwood (2018) sampling dispute; the planned joint field trip has no published outcome; Wu &amp; Jiang 2022's two other Isua candidates have no follow-up found. The 2018 reassessment stands unrebutted in print; Phys.org (Jan 2026) review carries Isua as the contested rival to Strelley Pool.
Falsifiability: If the biogenicity is demonstrated for the original outcrop (or falsified wholesale), the grade moves — up on independent corroboration, to DEBUNKED as fossils on a demonstrated abiotic mechanism</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-50.03,65.18,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-370000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Homo sapiens emerged ~300,000 years ago in Africa</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent dating methods (TL on associated flints, ESR/U-series on the Irhoud 3 mandible) plus corroborating fauna and microfauna; the taxon attribution and the emergence model are debated by specialists.
Layer: real · Place: Jebel Irhoud, Morocco (west of Marrakech)
Dates: -350000 → -280000 (confidence: medium)

The earliest fossils attributed to Homo sapiens are the Jebel Irhoud (Morocco) remains, dated 315 ± 34 thousand years ago by thermoluminescence — a mosaic of modern facial morphology with an archaic elongated braincase; the evidence now supports a pan-African, gradual emergence rather than a single ~200 ka East African origin.

Earliest attestation: Jebel Irhoud fossils, first excavated 1960s, re-excavated and re-dated 2017 (Hublin et al., Nature 2017, doi 10.1038/nature22336; author correction Nature 2018, doi 10.1038/s41586-018-0166-3)
Sources: Hublin et al. 2017, Nature 546:289-292, doi 10.1038/nature22336 — new fossils, mosaic morphology, pan-African origin; Richter et al. 2017, Nature 546:293-296, doi 10.1038/nature22335 — thermoluminescence dating 315 ± 34 ka; ESR 286 ± 32 ka; Hublin et al. 2018, Nature 558:E6, doi 10.1038/s41586-018-0166-3 — author correction; Vidal et al. 2022 (Omo Kibish re-dating to 233 ± 22 ka) — the previous 'oldest', now second; Florisbad skull, South Africa (~259 ka), now attributed to H. sapiens on the Irhoud basis; Moraes et al. 2026, Heritage 9(9):369, doi 10.3390/heritage9090369 (13 Sept 2026) — digital facial approximation of the Irhoud composite skull; brain-volume estimate ~1,230 cm³; the 'face' is a five-individual composite from 2017 press-video stills with modern-donor soft tissue (provenance caveat carried); Jakobsson, Schlebusch et al. 2026, Nature 650(8100):156–163, doi 10.1038/s41586-025-09811-4 — 28 ancient southern-African genomes: ≥200 kyr long-term isolation, deepest split ~310–240 ka, 490 sapiens-specific variants; the genomic anchor for pan-African structured emergence (PubMed 41339558)
Falsifiability: Securely dated fossils with fully modern globular braincases at 400+ ka would push the emergence back; a retraction of the Irhoud dating on stratigraphic grounds would pull it forward.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-8.8725,31.855,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-350000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bhitardari chert — the oldest directly dated rock with a confirmed biosignature (3.497 Ga)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed PNAS 2026; four independent lines in one study (field relationships, syn-depositional zircon geochronology, carbon isotopes, Raman) — but a single primary publication awaiting independent replication.
Layer: real · Place: Bhitardari, Singhbhum Craton, eastern India
Dates: -3497000000 → -3497000000 (confidence: high)

A carbonaceous chert at Bhitardari (Singhbhum Craton, eastern India) dated 3.497 ± 5 Ma by syn-depositional tuffaceous zircons contains laminated kerogen with biological carbon-isotope depletion (d13C -30.9 per mil) and Raman-confirmed indigenous organics — the tightest radiometric life–age link at ~3.5 Ga.

Earliest attestation: Chaudhuri, Harrison et al. 2026, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences (August 2026)
Sources: Chaudhuri, Harrison et al. 2026, PNAS — Bhitardari banded chert, Singhbhum Craton: eight zircons, four concordant (3497 ± 5 Ma, MSWD 1.2), tuffaceous interpretation; micron-scale alternating carbonaceous/siliceous layers; d13C -30.9 per mil; Raman poorly ordered kerogen; 'to our knowledge, the oldest directly dated rock with a confirmed biosignature'; ScienceAlert 2026-08-20 — lead author Trisrota Chaudhuri (Geological Survey of India): needle-like vs rounded detrital zircon morphology; the embedded-clock distinction; 'life thrived in a chemically extreme world 3.5 billion years ago' (coverage, not a second source); Phys.org 2026-08 — coverage: stratigraphic relationships + petrographic corroboration of the isotopic value; explicit caveat that independent studies must test whether the zircons truly date deposition and confirm the biological interpretation
Falsifiability: Independent dating showing the zircons are detrital, or demonstration that the carbon is post-depositional (e.g., migrated kerogen with no stratigraphic control)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>86.0,22.45,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-349700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The most widely accepted fossil evidence of life</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines (morphology across tens of km, seven morphologies, growth geometry, cooked organics tied to lamination) published in peer-reviewed literature and independently reported; initial skepticism (Lowe's own doubts, Grotzinger &amp; Rothman 1996) addressed by subsequent work.
Layer: real · Place: Strelley Pool Chert, East Pilbara, Western Australia
Dates: -3430000000 → -3430000000 (confidence: medium)

The oldest widely accepted fossil evidence of life on Earth is the stromatolite reef of the Strelley Pool Chert, Pilbara, Western Australia — layered structures built by microbial communities ~3.43 billion years ago.

Earliest attestation: Lowe 1980 (first description); Allwood et al. 2006, Science (biogenicity case)
Sources: Allwood et al. 2006, Science 312:1457 — stromatolite reef facies at Strelley Pool; seven distinct morphologies, growth geometry inconsistent with abiotic processes; Allwood, Grotzinger et al. 2009, Caltech/JPL — organic mat layers contouring the stromatolites edge to edge, Raman-confirmed burial-temperature carbon, mat fragments in storm deposits; New Scientist (2018) — 'the oldest accepted fossils are those from Strelley Pool in the Pilbara region of western Australia ... 3.4 billion years old'; Phys.org, 'How microbial fossils illuminate life's origins' (Jan 2026) — contemporary review: Strelley Pool stromatolites' spatial organization, vertical growth, and internal textures 'consistent with the activity of microbial mats' — 'especially strong candidates for biological origin'; treats Isua 3.7 Ga structures as the contested rival. The 2026 review-consensus state.; Alcheringa review (Oct 2026) — 50 years of microbialite research in Western Australia: Strelley Pool (~3.5 Ga Pilbara) held as the oldest accepted fossil examples; living microbialites at Shark Bay/Lake Clifton under pressure; fossil sites vulnerable to over-collection/degradation (the May 2024 Lake Clifton thrombolite trampling). Review-level synthesis, grade-neutral for the Strelley Pool CORROBORATED standing.; Nerearchaeum marumarumayae (Apr 2026) — Current Biology 10.1016/j.cub.2026.03.041 (Burns, Duggin, Ghosal et al.): first visual evidence of an Asgard archaeon physically interacting with a bacterium via nanotubes, isolated from the modern Shark Bay stromatolite microbial mats — the living analogue of the ancient mats. A model for how the archaeon–bacterium partnership at the root of eukaryogenesis may have looked; organism named with Malgana Traditional Owner consultation. Secondary coverage: phys.org 2026-04-09. Grade-neutral for the Strelley Pool claim (Turin-shroud precedent — strengthens the chain, no supersession); Wong, Prabhu, Hazen et al. 2025, PNAS 10.1073/pnas.2514534122 (17 Nov 2025) — pyrolysis-GC-MS + supervised machine learning on 406 samples; molecular biosignatures to ~3.3 Ga (Josefsdal Chert, Barberton; Singhbhum Craton 3.51 Ga biogenicity), earliest molecular evidence of photosynthesis ~2.5 Ga (Gamohaan Fm); &gt;90% accuracy biogenic-vs-abiogenic discrimination. Independent detection line at the same horizon as the Strelley Pool morphological record. Grade-neutral for the ~3.43 Ga CLAIMED-free accepted-rung standing (Turin-shroud precedent — strengthens the chain, no supersession); Alleon et al. (MIT/Sorbonne, reported Sept 2026) — intact Strelley Pool microstructures preserve nitrogen- and oxygen-rich organic molecules only slightly degraded despite exposure to temperatures above 300°C: chemical preservation at Strelley Pool is real, not powdered-sample artifact. Grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent) — strengthens the biogenicity case, does not move the grade.; Journal of Microscopy (2026) — near-infrared confocal imaging of far-red photosynthesis (chlorophyll d/f, Halomicronema sp. 'Clifton') in intact Shark Bay microbial mats; modern-analogue photobiology, special issue honoring Guy C. Cox (authors not surfaced in sweep — cited by journal + year, not invented).
Falsifiability: A demonstrated abiotic mechanism reproducing the full assemblage (seven morphologies, onlap geometry, burial-cooked organics) would collapse the biogenicity case</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>119.43,-21.07,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-343000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Musango matusadonaensis — Zimbabwe's fifth dinosaur, and the end of the homogeneous southern fauna</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed species description in the Journal of Systematic Palaeontology with a stated autapomorphy set, osteohistology, and phylogenetic analysis; withheld from VERIFIED because the taxon rests on one partial specimen without a skull, the sister-taxon support is weak by the authors' own analysis, and no independent replication exists yet.
Layer: real · Place: Musango Archosaur Site, southern margin of Lake Kariba, Zimbabwe — Pebbly Arkose Formation (approximate regional pin, southern shore)
Dates: -210000000 → -210000000 (confidence: medium)

A new early sauropodomorph dinosaur species, Musango matusadonaensis, described in 2026 from a partial associated skeleton found on the southern margin of Lake Kariba, Zimbabwe, shows that Late Triassic southern Africa held distinct regional dinosaur communities rather than one homogeneous fauna — a fifth dinosaur named from Zimbabwe, living ~210 million years ago while dinosaurs were diversifying toward Jurassic dominance.

Earliest attestation: The fossil itself — partial associated skeleton collected from the Musango Archosaur Site, bank of the Nyamasango River near Musango Island, Lake Kariba, Zimbabwe; described in print 2026 (received November 2025, accepted April 2026).
Sources: Barrett et al. 2026, Journal of Systematic Palaeontology — 'A new sauropodomorph dinosaur from the Pebbly Arkose Formation (Upper Triassic: Norian) of Kariba, Zimbabwe' (received 28 Nov 2025, accepted 17 Apr 2026; partial associated skeleton: vertebrae, pectoral and pelvic girdle elements, partial fore- and hind limbs; autapomorphies distinct from sympatric Musankwa sanyatiensis; sister-taxon relationship within Unaysauridae with weak support; holotype a small-bodied sub-adult or adult, at least eight years old, growth decelerating); Natural History Museum, London, Science News, 29 July 2026, James Ashworth — 'Musango: The new Zimbabwean dinosaur discovered from a boat' (fifth dinosaur named from Zimbabwe; ~4.5 m long; same rocks as Musankwa sanyatiensis; ~210 Ma Late Triassic; Barrett: southern Africa was a series of smaller ecosystems, not one fauna; one further undescribed species already in hand); Phys.org, 10 October 2026, Paul Barrett — 'Zimbabwe's dinosaurs were different from South Africa's: New species offers clues' (bipedal, ~4.5 m, ~220 kg, sub-adult at death; skull not preserved so diet unknown; discovery history: first thought a non-dinosaurian archosaur in the ground, recognized as a dinosaur only after preparation)
Falsifiability: A re-examination placing the specimen in Musankwa sanyatiensis or another described taxon, or a revised formation date moving the Pebbly Arkose out of the Norian, would sink the species hypothesis; the 'distinct ecosystems' reading dies if wider sampling shows the same fauna across southern Africa.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>28.8,-16.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-210000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Drimolen Homo erectus DNH 127 (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed publication of physical specimens with stratigraphic context, reported independently by multiple science outlets; the specimens are the exhibit.
Layer: real · Place: Drimolen Main Quarry, Cradle of Humankind, Gauteng, South Africa
Dates: -2000000 → -2000000 (confidence: high)

In September 2026, a La Trobe-led team published 18 new hominin fossils from Drimolen Main Quarry, South Africa — including DNH 127, the first adult Homo erectus cranial fossil from the site — confirming the species' first appearance in southern Africa at 2.0 million years ago, ~200,000 years earlier than the Dmanisi adults; the same paper documents microevolutionary change in co-occurring Paranthropus robustus (larger teeth and chewing muscles between 2.0 and 1.8 Ma), supporting the subspecies P. robustus ukusa.

Earliest attestation: Martin, Leece, Baker, Herries &amp; Strait 2026, Annals of Human Biology (peer-reviewed, 25 Sept 2026)
Sources: Martin et al. 2026, 'New hominin cranial and mandibular remains from Drimolen Main Quarry, South Africa', Annals of Human Biology, doi:10.1080/03014460.2026.2725046; La Trobe University media release, 30 Sept 2026 (18 new fossils; DNH 127 first adult H. erectus at Drimolen); phys.org, 30 Sept 2026 — 'Adult fossil strengthens evidence for Homo erectus in southern Africa 2 million years ago'; SciTechDaily, Oct 2026 — 'Homo erectus Was Outnumbered 10 to One by Another Human Relative' (P. robustus 10:1 ratio at Drimolen)
Falsifiability: Re-dating of the Drimolen deposits outside ~2.0 Ma, or published re-attribution of DNH 127 to Paranthropus on morphological grounds</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.75,-26.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-200000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Brontosaurus excelsus — the thunder lizard</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The animal was never in doubt: the YPM 1980 skeleton exists at the Yale Peabody Museum and every specialist from Riggs 1903 on agreed excelsus is a distinct species; the only question was which genus name to file it under, and the 2015 reinstatement is peer-reviewed, quantitative, and adopted by the Paleobiology Database and modern museum mounts.
Layer: real · Place: Como Bluff, Wyoming — type locality (Morrison Formation)
Dates: -154000000 → -150000000 (confidence: medium)

Brontosaurus excelsus — the 'thunder lizard' — was a real Late Jurassic sauropod. Othniel Charles Marsh named the species in 1879 from a partial skeleton (type specimen YPM 1980) found at Como Bluff, Wyoming, during the Bone Wars. In 1903 Elmer Riggs sank Brontosaurus into Apatosaurus on the principle of taxonomic priority (Marsh had named Apatosaurus ajax first, in 1877), so the name sat unused for 112 years. In 2015, a ~300-page peer-reviewed revision of Diplodocidae (Tschopp, Mateus &amp; Benson, PeerJ 3:e857, 7 April 2015) — 477 morphological characters scored across 81 specimens — found Brontosaurus distinct enough to stand as its own genus again, with three species: B. excelsus, B. parvus, and B. yahnahpin.

Earliest attestation: Othniel Charles Marsh, 'Principal characters of American Jurassic dinosaurs' (1879) — Brontosaurus excelsus named from the Como Bluff skeleton
Sources: Marsh, O.C. (1879) — Brontosaurus excelsus named from Como Bluff skeleton; type specimen YPM 1980 (Yale Peabody Museum); Riggs, E.S. (1903) 'Structure and Relationships of Opisthocoelian Dinosaurs, Part I: Apatosaurus Marsh', Field Columbian Museum: 'As the term Apatosaurus has priority, Brontosaurus will be regarded as a synonym'; Tschopp, E., Mateus, O. &amp; Benson, R.B.J. (2015) 'A specimen-level phylogenetic analysis and taxonomic revision of Diplodocidae', PeerJ 3:e857, doi:10.7717/peerj.857 — 477 characters, 81 specimens; Brontosaurus reinstated with B. excelsus, B. parvus, B. yahnahpin; McIntosh, J.S. &amp; Berman, D.S. (1975) — sorted the skull problem: Marsh's mount wore a chimeric Camarasaurus skull; Earl Douglass's 1909 skull (CM 3018) was the real one, mislabeled Diplodocus for decades until McIntosh recognized it
Falsifiability: Proof that the YPM 1980 holotype is a chimera of unrelated taxa in a way that invalidates the species (Marsh's mounted skull was a chimera — Camarasaurus bones from three quarries — but the postcranial skeleton is a real individual, sorted by McIntosh &amp; Berman 1975), or a peer-reviewed specimen-level analysis overturning the 2015 reinstatement</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-107.55,41.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-154000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dragon Man identified as Denisovan (2025 molecular work)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent molecular lines (mtDNA from calculus, proteomics from petrous bone) converge on the same identification, published in Cell and Science; the variant-panel caution from Xijun Ni is stated and keeps this below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Harbin, Heilongjiang, China (cranium found 1933, described 2021)
Dates: -150000 → -140000 (confidence: medium)

The Harbin ('Dragon Man') cranium — a Middle Pleistocene skull with thick brow ridges, a roomy braincase, and cheeks between Neanderthal ruggedness and modern delicacy — has been identified as a Denisovan individual: mitochondrial DNA recovered from its dental calculus (maternal line) matched Denisovans from Denisova Cave, and 95 ancient proteins from the petrous bone (19,299 peptides) placed it with Denisovans at 98–100% statistical support, with three Denisovan-associated amino-acid variants (one high-confidence). After 15 years as a genome without a face, the Denisovans have a skull.

Earliest attestation: Fu et al., Cell, June 2025 (dental calculus mtDNA); Fu et al., Science, June 2025 (petrous-bone palaeoproteomics)
Sources: Fu et al. 2025, Cell (June) — mitochondrial DNA from the Harbin cranium's dental calculus identifies it as Denisovan; Fu et al. 2025, Science (June) — petrous-bone proteomics: 95 ancient proteins, 19,299 peptides, Denisovan-specific variants; Science News 2025; CNN 2025-12-26 ('How 2025 tipped the scales'); Earth.com 2025 — coverage; Bence Viola: 'pretty clear this is a Denisovan individual'; Chris Stringer on H. longi as the likely official designation; Xijun Ni (IVPP) dissent: larger ancient-protein-variant sample needed; Bianfu Cave (Yunnan, China), two Nature papers 9 Sept 2026 (doi:10.1038/s41586-026-10997-4) — five new Denisovan specimens incl. the first Denisovan forearm bone, ~167-134 ka, proteomic ID (Fu Qiaomei team); appended 2026-10-18 as grade-neutral chain-strengthening (Turin-shroud precedent): enriches the Denisovan fossil picture, does not independently confirm the Harbin ID; see evt-bianfu-cave-denisovans-2026-001
Falsifiability: Nuclear DNA recovery from the Harbin cranium assigning it to a different lineage would overturn the identification; a larger protein-variant panel failing to group Harbin with Denisovans would weaken it to CLAIMED.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>126.535,45.8038,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Birds are living theropod dinosaurs</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines (feathered fossils, skeletal synapomorphies, genome-scale phylogenetics) converge; the named historical skeptic (Feduccia) holds a tiny, effectively rejected minority position.
Layer: real · Place: Liaoning Province, China (feathered theropod beds)
Dates: -150000000 → 0 (confidence: high)

Birds are not merely descended from dinosaurs — they are theropod dinosaurs, the one dinosaur lineage to survive the K-Pg extinction. Feathered non-avian theropods (Sinosauropteryx 1996 onward, scores of Liaoning taxa, Norellraptor barsboldi 2026) plus skeletal synapomorphies (furcula, three-fingered manus, hollow thin-walled bones, semi-lunate carpal, brooding posture, air-sac pneumaticity) make the dinosaur–bird link a scientific consensus.

Earliest attestation: Archaeopteryx (1861); first non-avian feathered dinosaur Sinosauropteryx (1996)
Sources: Archaeopteryx specimens (1861 onward) — the original dinosaur–bird bridge; Sinosauropteryx (1996) and the Liaoning feathered theropods — scores of plumage-bearing specimens; Cau, Ji, Wang et al. 2026, Nature Communications (Norellraptor barsboldi — newest feathered microraptorine, mosaic flight-feature evolution); Feduccia, Lingham-Soliar &amp; Hinchliffe 2005, Journal of Morphology — the prominent historical minority (BAND view); effectively rejected by subsequent evidence, stated fairly; Cau, Ji, Wang et al. 2026, Nature Communications, DOI 10.1038/s41467-026-77804-6 — Norellraptor barsboldi (Jiufotang Fm, Liaoning): complete 57-cm feathered microraptorine; ~30% of the anatomical changes across microraptorine evolution also evolved in the bird lineage, but in a different order — flight-adjacent features converging independently rather than once; Xu, L., Wang, M., Lin, G. et al. 2026, Nature 658, 690-697 — Yuezhuornis yangyuanensis (Zhenghe Fauna, Fujian, ~148-150 Ma): the earliest known flightless bird — crow-sized, terrestrial, placed just above Archaeopteryx within Avialae; early bird evolution explored non-flight niches from the start — grade-neutral enrichment of the mosaic-evolution case, Turin-shroud precedent (see evt-yuezhuornis-flightless-2026-001); Nature Communications 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41467-026-78343-w — Infernogallus jamesi, Hell Creek ~66 Ma: first nearly complete caenagnathid skull; jaw mechanics consistent with frugivory — widens the documented dietary range of feathered non-avian dinosaurs at the boundary (recorded in evt-infernogallus-jamesi-2026-001)
Falsifiability: A crown-group bird fossil demonstrably predating all theropod dinosaurs, or phylogenetic evidence placing birds outside Dinosauria with support exceeding the current fossil+genomic stack</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>121.0,40.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yuezhuornis yangyuanensis (2026) — the runner-bird</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed description of a near-complete fossil in Nature, reported by Reuters; the specimen is the exhibit, the 'runner' reading is the authors' fenced interpretation.
Layer: real · Place: Fujian Province, southeastern China
Dates: -150000000 → -150000000 (confidence: medium)

In October 2026, paleontologists described Yuezhuornis yangyuanensis, a crow-sized Jurassic bird (~150 Ma) from Fujian, China, with undersized wings suggesting it was more runner than flier — complicating the paradigm that the early avian body plan evolved almost exclusively under selection for flight (Wang et al., Nature, Oct 2026).

Earliest attestation: Wang et al. 2026, Nature (published 14 Oct 2026)
Sources: Wang et al. 2026, Nature (14 Oct 2026) — Yuezhuornis yangyuanensis description; Reuters (Will Dunham), 14 Oct 2026 — 'Chinese fossil of bird with undersized wings scrambles avian evolution'
Falsifiability: Biomechanical analysis demonstrating powered-flight capability in Yuezhuornis, or re-dating outside the Late Jurassic</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>118.0,26.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yuezhuornis yangyuanensis — the Jurassic flightless bird</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Single peer-reviewed primary description in Nature (Oct 2026); independent corroboration pending. Consistent with the graded dinosaur-bird transition family; no competing published refutation.
Layer: real · Place: Zhenghe County, Fujian Province, China — Zhenghe Fauna (approximate county coordinates)
Dates: -150000000 → -148000000 (confidence: medium)

A crow-sized (~800 g) avialan from the Zhenghe Fauna of Fujian, China (~148-150 Ma, Late Jurassic), described October 2026 (Xu, Wang, Lin et al., Nature 658:690-697, DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-10972-z) as the earliest known bird to have lost (or diminished) the ability to fly. Forelimbs ~one-third shorter than hindlimbs; limb proportions match modern ground-dwelling birds (377 living species compared); retains dinosaurian traits — serrated teeth, hyperextensible deinonychosaur-style second toe. Placed just above Archaeopteryx within Avialae, closest relative Zhengheornis. The authors' reading: early bird evolution explored non-flight niches from the start, not a linear march toward better flight.

Earliest attestation: Xu, L., Wang, M., Lin, G. et al., Nature 658, 690-697, published 14 Oct 2026
Sources: Xu, L., Wang, M., Lin, G. et al. 2026, Nature 658, 690-697 — 'Jurassic flightless bird reveals early ecological diversity of avialans' (nearly complete skeleton; forelimb/hindlimb ratio ~2/3; 377-living-species limb-proportion comparison; serrated teeth + hyperextensible second toe; phylogenetic placement just above Archaeopteryx within Avialae, closest relative Zhengheornis); Reuters (Dunham), 14 Oct 2026 — Wang: 'Yuezhuornis challenges the traditional paradigm that the avian body plan evolved almost exclusively under the selective pressures of flight'; roadrunner-style runner inference stated by the authors; Xinhua, 15 Oct 2026 (via People's Daily) — IVPP + Fujian Institute of Geological Sciences provenance; earliest known example of a bird losing flight, per the describing team; Fenced nuance (diversionedge, Oct 2026, summarizing the paper): short wings do not by themselves prove flightlessness — the claim rests on limb measurements + ecological/evolutionary analysis combined; the fossil alone 'does not reveal exactly how this species' flightlessness evolved'. The ecological-diversity reading is the authors'; the terrestrial-flightless lifestyle is an inference from proportions, not preserved behavior.
Falsifiability: Overlapping new specimens showing different limb proportions, or a phylogenetic re-analysis placing Yuezhuornis outside Avialae, would sink the earliest-flightless-bird reading; additional Zhenghe avialans would test the 'ecological diversity from the start' claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>119.28,27.32,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Adult Sinovenator — the troodontid grows up (Ningcheng Basin, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed description of a diagnostic adult specimen with postcranial anatomy, histological ontogeny, and range data; withheld from VERIFIED because the adult series is one data point and ontogenetic staging is method-dependent.
Layer: real · Place: Ningcheng Basin, Inner Mongolia, China (Jehol Biota)
Dates: -130000000 → -120000000 (confidence: medium)

A new adult postcranial skeleton of Sinovenator changii from the Jehol Biota of the Ningcheng Basin, Inner Mongolia, described October 2026, provides the first adult ontogenetic data for this early troodontid and expands its known geographic range beyond Liaoning's Yixian Formation — refining the dinosaur–bird transition record with growth data, not just feathers.

Earliest attestation: Described October 2026 (Yu, Zhang, Zhou &amp; Pei, Swiss Journal of Palaeontology 145)
Sources: Yu, Zhang, Zhou &amp; Pei 2026, Swiss Journal of Palaeontology 145:901–926 — 'A new postcranial skeleton of Sinovenator changii (Theropoda, Troodontidae) from the Jehol Biota of the Ningcheng Basin, Inner Mongolia, China' (postcranial anatomy, ontogeny via osteohistology, paleogeographic range); ScienMag, 15 October 2026 — reporting the adult specimen and the expanded range of early troodontids (Jehol Biota, Ningcheng Basin, Inner Mongolia)
Falsifiability: Re-examination reassigning the skeleton to a different troodontid taxon, or histology re-read as subadult, would downgrade the ontogeny claim; the locality itself is the range evidence and stands unless the provenance is challenged.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>119.3,41.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-130000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>First-star imprints in a metal-poor galaxy overdensity (Li/Kakiichi et al. 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed Nature Astronomy detection: JWST-mapped galaxy context (17 members, ~3% solar) plus an absorber chemistry matching Pop III faint-supernova yields; the individual first stars are still not seen — this is their imprint, fenced as such.
Layer: real · Place: Sightline to quasar SDSS J0100+2802 (RA ~01h00m, Dec +28°02') — the absorber at z=5.945
Dates: -12900000000 → -12850000000 (confidence: medium)

A metal absorber at redshift z=5.945 along the sightline to quasar SDSS J0100+2802 shows a carbon-and-silicon over-abundance pattern consistent with Population III supernova yields; the absorber sits inside a protocluster-like overdensity of 17 spectroscopically confirmed galaxies with a mean gas-phase metallicity of ~3% solar — the strongest environmental evidence yet that first-star formation and its chemical imprints lingered into the end of reionization.

Earliest attestation: Li, Kakiichi, Christensen, Cai, D'Odorico, Matthee, Kashino, Bordoloi, Mackenzie, Berg, Vanni, Salvadori, Venditti, Zhang, Bosman, Bañados, Davies, Fan, Jun, Zhu et al., 'First-star imprints in a metal-poor galaxy overdensity near the end of reionization', Nature Astronomy (published 30 Sep 2026, doi:10.1038/s41550-026-02993-5)
Sources: Li et al. 2026, Nature Astronomy, doi:10.1038/s41550-026-02993-5 (peer-reviewed; JWST/EIGER+ASPIRE [O III] galaxy sample, quasar absorption spectroscopy, MCMC halo-occupation fit, log(M_h,min/M_sun)=10.68); EIGER survey + ASPIRE companion program (JWST NIRCam [O III]-emitter surveys around bright z&gt;6 quasars); Prior z=5.945 absorber identification (pre-JWST quasar spectroscopy; the absorber's Pop III-like C/Si pattern predates the galaxy-context work); Theoretical Pop III supernova yields (faint low-energy explosions: C-rich, Fe-poor — matching the absorber pattern and the Milky Way halo CEMP-star population); THESAN-ZOOM simulations (prediction that Pop III star formation persists to the very end of reionization in low-density pockets)
Falsifiability: Independent JWST follow-up of the J0100+2802 field failing to reproduce the absorber's abundance pattern, or detailed modeling showing Population II yields reproduce the C/Si ratio without Pop III input, would collapse the imprint reading</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-15.0,28.03,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-129000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Guantou small-theropod footprints — mid-Cretaceous Jialingpus trackways, Dongyang, Zhejiang, published 2026</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed published find (MDPI Fossils, 20 Oct 2026), 18+ natural molds on one slab, measured morphometrics, tentative ichnotaxonomic assignment openly fenced by the authors; withheld from VERIFIED because the assignment is tentative and the material is collapsed-block, not in-situ.
Layer: real · Place: Shangantian tracksite, Dongyang City, Zhejiang Province, China
Dates: -120000000 → -100000000 (confidence: medium)

On 20 October 2026, a peer-reviewed paper in MDPI Fossils (2026, 4(4), 30) described newly discovered small theropod footprints from collapsed rock blocks of the Lower Cretaceous Guantou Formation at the Shangantian tracksite, Dongyang City, Zhejiang Province, eastern China: at least 18 natural molds on a single siltstone slab, minute tridactyl tracks 6.8–8.8 cm long, tentatively assigned to Jialingpus isp., with three single-step trackways indicating slow walking speeds of 0.4–0.8 m/s (1.4–2.9 km/h) — enriching mid-Cretaceous dinosaur track diversity in Zhejiang and informing Jialingpus morphometric variation across the Jurassic–Cretaceous boundary.

Earliest attestation: MDPI Fossils 2026, 4(4), 30 — 'Small Theropod Footprints from the Lower Cretaceous Guantou Formation of Dongyang City, Zhejiang Province, China' — published 20 October 2026 (Feature Paper; authors at China University of Geosciences, Dinosaur Odyssey Science Museum, Zhejiang Geological Museum).
Sources: MDPI Fossils 2026, 4(4), 30 — 'Small Theropod Footprints from the Lower Cretaceous Guantou Formation of Dongyang City, Zhejiang Province, China' — published 20 October 2026; 13 pages; ≥18 natural molds, 6.8–8.8 cm tridactyl tracks, tentatively Jialingpus isp., 0.4–0.8 m/s walking speeds
Falsifiability: A re-description re-identifying the molds as non-dinosaurian trace features or a different ichnotaxon would revise the Jialingpus reading; the described assemblage itself stands on the peer-reviewed print.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>120.24,29.27,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-120000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Invictibellatrix amberina — Europe's first megaraptoran</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed species description with CT endocast evidence, a stated unique character combination, and consistent phylogenetic placement in Megaraptora; withheld from VERIFIED because the taxon rests on one partial specimen and awaits independent replication.
Layer: real · Place: Isle of Wight, UK — southwest coast, Wessex Formation (found at the foot of a cliff by R. W. Hooley)
Dates: -120000000 → -120000000 (confidence: medium)

A new megaraptoran theropod species, described October 2026 from a partial braincase collected on the Isle of Wight and held unstudied at the Natural History Museum, London since 1924, is the first confirmed European member of the Megaraptora — a group of large-armed, long-clawed theropods previously known from Asia and the southern continents — and its placement supports the Asia→Europe→Gondwana dispersal route over southern island-hopping.

Earliest attestation: The fossil itself — collected c. 1920s at the foot of a southwest-coast cliff by amateur paleontologist Reginald Walter Hooley; acquired by the Natural History Museum, London, 1924; described in print October 2026
Sources: Morrison, Scherer, Barrett et al. 2026, Royal Society Open Science 13(10):260937 — 'A new megaraptoran dinosaur from the Lower Cretaceous Wessex Formation, UK, and its implications for theropod evolution and biogeography' (CT scan, 3D endocast, phylogenetic algorithms; the oval spinal-cord foramen + hourglass-shaped crest diagnosis); Phys.org, 13 October 2026 — 'Isle of Wight fossil Europe's first known long-clawed megaraptoran dinosaur' (UCL/NHM reporting: 3–4 m long, ~1.5 m tall, claws 10–20 cm, hyena-like ecological role; Europe→Gondwana route inference; northern Laurasian extinction modeled, cause unexplained); New Scientist, 14 October 2026 — 'Dinosaur named new species 102 years after fossil found on Isle of Wight' (nearest comparative match: Murusraptor, ~80 Ma Argentina; no overlapping material with the similar-sized Wessex theropod Eotyrannus)
Falsifiability: A re-examination placing the braincase in Eotyrannus or another described taxon, or overlapping new specimens contradicting the megaraptoran placement, would sink the species hypothesis; the 'first European megaraptoran' claim dies the moment an older confirmed European specimen is published.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.3,50.65,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-120000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Udon Thani trackway — Thailand's first Udon Thani dinosaur footprints, announced 2026</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced and measured by Thailand's national geological agency (a primary institutional witness), not a content farm: six impressions, single trackway, documented dimensions; withheld from VERIFIED because no peer-reviewed description exists yet and the trackmaker/age readings are preliminary.
Layer: real · Place: Phu Hin Chom That–Phu Phrabat National Park, Kut Chap District, Udon Thani Province, Thailand
Dates: -120000000 → -120000000 (confidence: medium)

On 20 October 2026, Thailand's Department of Mineral Resources announced that six tridactyl impressions exposed in sandstone of the Phu Phan Formation inside Phu Hin Chom That–Phu Phrabat National Park, Udon Thani province, are genuine dinosaur footprints — a single trackway, each print ~29 cm long and ~26 cm wide, attributed by the agency to a carnivorous theropod (~120 Ma, Early Cretaceous), estimated ~1.42 m at the hip and ~6.3 m long, comparable in build to Siamotyrannus isanensis. The site was first noticed by a tourist's photograph posted to Facebook on 18 October 2026; park rangers measured the impressions on 19 October.

Earliest attestation: Thailand's Department of Mineral Resources announcement, 20 October 2026 (via the department's official Facebook page); the discoverer is tourist Pichai Prathetsena, whose 18 October 2026 photograph in the 'What Animal Is This?' Facebook group prompted the ranger inspection of 19 October.
Sources: Department of Mineral Resources (Thailand) announcement, 20 October 2026 — six tridactyl footprints, single trackway, Phu Phan Formation sandstone; 29 × 26 cm; ~1.42 m hip height, ~6.3 m length; Early Cretaceous theropod (as reported in the Oct-21 press cycle); Wochenblitz English, 2026-10-21 — tourist Pichai Prathetsena's 18 October 2026 photograph, ranger inspection 19 October, agency confirmation 20 October; Udon Thani's first dinosaur tracks
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed ichnological description re-identifying the impressions as non-dinosaurian (erosion features, pseudo-fossils) would overturn the agency's reading; the trackway's existence stands on the agency's measured field report.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>102.5,17.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-120000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Denny — the first-generation Neanderthal/Denisovan hybrid (~90 ka)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed ancient-DNA analysis; first-generation hybrid status follows from the 50/50 split and maternal mtDNA
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: -90000 → -90000 (confidence: high)

Denisova 11 ('Denny'), ~90,000 years old from Denisova Cave: nuclear genome ~38.6% Neanderthal / 42.3% Denisovan; maternally Neanderthal (mtDNA). A first-generation hybrid of a Neanderthal mother and a Denisovan father — the father himself carried Neanderthal ancestry 300-600 generations earlier, implying interbreeding was common, not rare.

Earliest attestation: Slon et al., Nature 2018
Sources: Slon et al. 2018, Nature
Falsifiability: Additional archaic genomes could show hybrids are rare after all</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>84.68,51.4,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-90000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Youngest Toba Tuff (~74 ka) — the dated catastrophe</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary lines: the caldera itself, the chemically-fingerprinted distal ash blanket, and polar ice-core sulfate/cryptotephra — the best-dated supervolcanic eruption of the Quaternary.
Layer: real · Place: Lake Toba caldera, northern Sumatra, Indonesia
Dates: -73900 → -73800 (confidence: high)

The Youngest Toba Tuff — a super-eruption in northern Sumatra, Indonesia, dated 73.88 ± 0.32 ka (Storey et al. 2012, 40Ar/39Ar) — the largest explosive volcanic eruption of the Quaternary (VEI 8; thousands of cubic kilometres of magma). It left a caldera lake ~100 km long, laid a dated ash isochron from the Indian Ocean to the South China Sea and across South Asia, and left sulfate and cryptotephra fingerprints in both Greenland (GISP2) and Antarctic ice cores. It happened inside the Homo sapiens dispersal window — a catastrophe with a date, not a cause of one.

Earliest attestation: The eruption itself — Lake Toba caldera (lat 2.6845, lon 98.8756); the ash isochron recorded in marine, terrestrial, and ice archives worldwide
Sources: Storey et al. 2012, 40Ar/39Ar geochronology: the Youngest Toba Tuff at 73.88 ± 0.32 ka — the standing reference age; Lake Toba caldera, northern Sumatra — the eruption's own scar: ~100 km × 30 km collapse structure; GISP2 ice core, Greenland: the ~74 ka sulfate spike attributed to Toba (Zielinski &amp; Mershon 1997; Williams et al. 2009); Smith et al. 2018 (and Svensson et al. 2013): cryptotephra ties between polar ice and the Toba system — the fingerprint in both hemispheres; The Youngest Toba Tuff isochron: distal ash beds across South Asia (Jwalapuram locality C, India) to the South China Sea — one layer, one date, everywhere at once
Falsifiability: An independent redating that moves the Youngest Toba Tuff outside ~74 ± 2 ka; a better-candidate eruption for the global isochron; geochemical re-assignment of the distal ashes</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>98.8756,2.6845,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-73900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The main out-of-Africa dispersal, ~50-70 ka</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent lines converge: coalescent genetics (the deepest non-African splits sit inside the 70-50 ka window, no surviving earlier contribution), archaeology (Sahul sealed ~65-60 ka with two mtDNA routes, Richards team), and the fossil exits (Misliya/Apidima/Zhirendong pre-date the window with no genetic continuity). The failed-exit catalogue is fossil-solid; the ancestry claim rests on the genetic null.
Layer: real · Place: Bab al Mandab strait, Red Sea (the southern exit); exit routes also northern (Sinai/Levant)
Dates: -70000 → -50000 (confidence: medium)

All living non-African human populations descend from a single main dispersal of Homo sapiens out of Africa ~50-70 thousand years ago; earlier exits (Misliya ~180 ka, Apidima ~210 ka, Zhirendong, Tam Pa Ling) left fossils and tools but no surviving genetic legacy. The Sahul landmass (Australia/New Guinea) was settled ~60-65 ka via two routes (a southern corridor through Indonesia and a northern corridor toward the Philippines/New Guinea), crossing open water by boat.

Earliest attestation: N/A (paleogenomic/archaeological reconstruction, not a text)
Sources: Mallick et al. 2016, Nature 538:201-206 (Simons Genome Diversity Project) — divergence dating of the out-of-Africa bottleneck; Hershkovitz et al. 2018, Science 359:456-459 — Misliya maxilla, ~177-194 ka, earliest Levantine sapiens; Harvati et al. 2019, Nature 571:500-504 — Apidima 1, ~210 ka, earliest known sapiens outside Africa; Clarkson et al. 2017, Nature 547:306-310 — Madjedbebe, Sahul occupation by ~65 ka; Richards team mtDNA/Y-chromosome/genome-wide Sahul study (archaeologymag Dec 2025 secondary coverage) — two-route arrival, no earlier than ~60 ka, oldest unbroken non-African lineages; wsws.io Sept 11, 2026 — TRACE program: two intra-African ghost lineages inferred from genomes (fenced: secondary coverage only, primary paper unverified); IFLScience Feb 2026 — Dhofar Oman ~109-95 ka Nubian Levallois (fenced: unreviewed preprint, held in competing hypotheses); Zhang, Biddanda, Moorjani et al. 2026, Science (doi:10.1126/science.aef8874) — the TRACE twin-ghost paper; appended 2026-10-18 (grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent): the ~800 ka ghost is intra-African introgression into the dispersing population, not a competing exit
Falsifiability: A pre-50 ka fossil with surviving DNA clustering inside a living non-African haplogroup, or ancient DNA from Misliya/Apidima-grade material showing continuity to present populations, would break the single-main-wave reading.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.4,12.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-70000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Madjedbebe — Australia's 65,000-Year Story</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed primary publication with optical dating and stratigraphic refits; the 50 ka floor is long-accepted. Held at CORROBORATED (not VERIFIED) because the 65 ka re-dating carries named expert skepticism and the OSL debate is unresolved.
Layer: real · Place: Madjedbebe rock shelter, Kakadu, Northern Territory, Australia
Dates: -65000 → -50000 (confidence: medium)

Humans occupied the Madjedbebe rock shelter in northern Australia (Kakadu) by about 65,000 years ago — the earliest known human presence in Australia — arriving by sea voyage from Sunda, alongside megafauna and within sight of Homo floresiensis on Flores.

Earliest attestation: Clarkson, Jacobs, Marwick et al., 'Human occupation of northern Australia by 65,000 years ago', Nature 547:306-310 (2017) — optical dating, artefact refits, three dense occupation bands.
Sources: Clarkson et al., Nature 547:306-310 (2017), doi:10.1038/nature22968 — 10,000+ artefacts in the lowest dense layer: world's oldest ground-edge axe technology, Australia's oldest seed-grinding tools, ochre processing, thylacine jaw with pigment; Science News 2017 — 'When people think about our ancient ancestors, they either tend to have a view that our ancestors must have been primitive... This indicates the latter view' (Peter Hiscock, Univ. of Sydney); Madjedbebe site record — the 65 ka dating 'questioned by some experts' as of 2017; the 50 ka figure 'widely accepted since the 1990s'; excavated with custodianship of the Gundjeihmi Aboriginal Corporation
Falsifiability: Demonstration of sediment disturbance invalidating the OSL dates, or a replicate excavation showing the artefacts are younger, would downgrade the record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>132.87,-12.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-65000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peopling of the Americas: three lines converge on 14–20 ka, reviewed 2026-10-17 (VIII.c third pass)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed 2026 synthesis explicitly testing three migration models against three independent evidence lines; the convergence pattern (not the terminal date) is the graded claim.
Layer: real · Place: North America (continental)
Dates: -20000 → -14000 (confidence: medium)

Three independent lines of evidence — ancient-DNA population-split timing, the archaeological site record, and environmental/ice-sheet data — converge on a 14,000–20,000-year-ago window for the arrival of the ancestors of today's Indigenous Americans, per Potter et al. 2026 (Current Opinion in Genetics &amp; Development). The genetic data show a major branching event around 15 ka dividing Northern and Southern Native American lineages; post-14-ka arrival conflicts with the split timing; pre-25-ka arrival finds no genomic population-split support. This is the convergence argument's fifth demonstration case: genetics, archaeology, and paleoenvironment sharing no method, landing in the same window.

Earliest attestation: Bethany Potter et al., 'Genomic insights regarding early migrations into the Americas', Current Opinion in Genetics &amp; Development (2026), DOI: 10.1016/j.gde.2026.102534 — reported 5 Oct 2026
Sources: Potter et al. 2026, 'Genomic insights regarding early migrations into the Americas', Current Opinion in Genetics &amp; Development, DOI: 10.1016/j.gde.2026.102534; phys.org / Science X Network report, 5 Oct 2026 (fact-checked; summarizes the review's three-line test of the three migration models); phys.org / Science X Network report, 4 Oct 2026 on Potter et al. (Current Opinion in Genetics &amp; Development, doi:10.1016/j.gde.2026.102534) — 257 published pre-contact whole genomes + genome-wide data from 1,204 ancient individuals; no DNA evidence of population splits older than ~25 ka; arrival no earlier than ~21 ka, best-fit 14–20 ka; early traces may be dead-end groups or unsampled DNA; Hunemeier et al., Nature (published 22 Apr 2026) — the Ypykuera ghost lineage: an ancient Asian ghost population contributing genes to both Indigenous Americans and early Australasians, present &gt;10 ka, with no fossil record anywhere — convergence caveat: a genetic line that converges on the same populations without any physical witness; Graf, Goebel, Henry, Hockett et al. 2026, 'Highlights from an early base camp in Beringia 13,800 years ago: Shég’ Xdaltth’í’ and the Late Pleistocene peopling of Alaska', Science Advances 12(42), doi:10.1126/sciadv.aeh0802
Falsifiability: Securely dated pre-25-ka human genomes showing deep population splits, or an uncontested pre-LGM site with directly dated human context, would break the convergence</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-100.0,45.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-20000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kuk Swamp — Independent Agriculture in the New Guinea Highlands</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested: phytolith and starch-grain residue evidence, phase-sequence stratigraphy, a former skeptic's published conversion, and a UNESCO inscription naming the independence inference explicitly. Stops short of VERIFIED on the exact domestication-vs-intensive-management reading of the 10 ka phase.
Layer: real · Place: Kuk Swamp, Wahgi Valley, Papua New Guinea Highlands
Dates: -8250 → -7950 (confidence: medium)

People in the Wahgi Valley of the New Guinea highlands were practicing agriculture by about 10,000 years ago — planting taro and banana on the Kuk Swamp wetland margin — independently of any Near Eastern farming tradition.

Earliest attestation: Denham et al., Science 302:189-193 (2003) — banana phytoliths, taro starch grains on stone-tool edges, ditch-margin planting in phase I.
Sources: Denham, Haberle et al., 'Origins of agriculture at Kuk Swamp in the highlands of New Guinea', Science 302:189-193 (2003) — phase I planting 10,220-9,910 cal BP; phases II (6,950-6,440 cal BP mounds) and III (4,350-3,980 cal BP ditch networks); Fullagar, Field, Denham &amp; Lentfer, 'Early and mid Holocene tool-use and processing of taro (Colocasia esculenta), yam (Dioscorea sp.) and other plants at Kuk Swamp' (2006) — starch on tool edges, processing from at least 10,200 cal BP; Matthew Spriggs (ANU), Science News 2003 — 'Denham's finds... have convinced me that there really [was] agriculture at Kuk as early as anywhere in the world' (a former skeptic's conversion); UNESCO World Heritage, Kuk Early Agricultural Site (inscribed 2008) — 'independent technological leap... around 7,000-6,400 years ago', 'genesis of that land-use... from possibly as early as 10,000 BP'; criteria iii &amp; iv
Falsifiability: Re-dating of the phase-I palaeosurface to significantly younger ages, or demonstration that the residues are wild-plant processing unrelated to cultivation, would weaken the independence claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>144.32,-5.78,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-8250</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The oldest mirrors: obsidian discs of Çatalhöyük</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The objects exist in museum collections and their reflectivity has been independently verified; the use-purpose is honestly debated.
Layer: real · Place: Çatalhöyük, Konya Plain, Turkey
Dates: -6000 → -5900 (confidence: medium)

The earliest identifiable mirrors are hand-sized polished obsidian discs from Çatalhöyük, Anatolia, dated ~6000–5900 BCE — highly polished volcanic glass found in burial contexts.

Earliest attestation: James Mellaart's 1960s excavations, published in Earliest Civilizations of the Near East (1965); Naomi Hamilton's later inventory verified 8 of 11 candidate objects as mirrors.
Sources: Mellaart 1965, Earliest Civilizations of the Near East, p.85 — obsidian discs ~9 cm in Neolithic graves, early sixth millennium BCE; Archaeology magazine archive, 'Grinding it Out' — Hamilton's verification of 8 of 11 objects as mirrors; contested uses; Google Arts &amp; Culture, 'Mirror, Mirror On The Wall' — early mirrors date back to 6000 BC, polished obsidian in Anatolia
Falsifiability: Re-examination of the surviving discs showing they cannot produce a specular reflection would end the claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.8283,37.6672,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-6000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Persian Gulf's retreating head — Eridu and Ur on a coast that left</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multi-team, multi-method convergence (sea-level curves, marine-shell radiocarbon, delta-progradation chronology) on the retreating-Gulf picture; the Uruk-reach and exact-6000-BP-position disputes are stated in the record, not hidden.
Layer: real · Place: Head of the Persian Gulf, southern Iraq — the migrating Holocene shoreline
Dates: -5000 → 1000 (confidence: medium)

In the early Holocene the head of the Persian Gulf stood far north of its present position: c. 5000 BP the shoreline lay near Eridu and Ur (Tell el-Muqayyar), on the evidence of Larsen &amp; Evans 1978, Lambeck 1996, and Sanlaville 1989 as synthesized in the geoarchaeological literature. The Gulf then retreated south as the Tigris-Euphrates delta prograded, reaching its current configuration c. AD 1000 via the Shatt al-Arab delta's growth south of Basra (Al-Hamad et al. 2017; Aqrawi 2001; Bogemans et al. 2017). The exact position of the shoreline at 6000 BP is still debated — Jotheri et al. 2018's remote-sensing work in the Uruk region finds the marine transgression never reached the Uruk area (~300 km from the present shoreline).

Earliest attestation: Larsen &amp; Evans 1978 (the modern literature baseline for the northern Gulf-head model).
Sources: Jotheri, Allen, et al. 2018 — Holocene fluvial and anthropogenic processes in the region of Uruk; Iacobucci et al. 2022 (Geomorphology, Rome) — geomorphology of the lower Mesopotamian plain at Tell Zurghul (synthesis of the Gulf-head debate: Aqrawi &amp; Evans 1994; Altaweel 2019; Al-Hamad et al. 2017; Aqrawi 2001; Bogemans et al. 2017); Lagash Archaeological Project (UPenn) — Ubaid settlements in estuarine marshes/along the Gulf coast; sea withdrawal then regularized Tigris-Euphrates courses; Giosan &amp; Goodman, PLOS ONE (2025) — 'Morphodynamic Foundations of Sumer': high-resolution satellite mapping + a 25-meter sediment core from Lagash reconstructing the vanished early-Sumerian coastline; tidal-dynamics model of the head-of-Gulf estuarine world (daily tides irrigating the land) — the substrate moving inside the argument's own demonstration zone (Lagash Archaeological Project, Iraqi/Penn Museum)
Falsifiability: A published, dated marine deposit from the Uruk area c. 4000 BCE would restore the maximalist model; a redated progradation sequence would move the AD 1000 figure.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.2,30.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-5000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Chang'e-6 lunar cratering chronology</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent 2026 peer-reviewed studies (crater-count chronology and argon-isotope dating of far-side melts) converge on the same monotonic-decline curve; single-study lineage awaits further replication, so CORROBORATED, not higher.
Layer: real · Place: Chang'e-6 landing site, Apollo crater, South Pole–Aitken Basin, lunar far side
Dates: -4247 → -2807 (confidence: high)

The lunar cratering chronology function, revised with Chang'e-6 far-side samples (2.807 Ga basalts and 4.247 Ga South Pole–Aitken norite impact melts), shows near-side and far-side impact flux indistinguishable within uncertainty — a single unified global chronology in which impact activity declines smoothly and rapidly from planetary accretion, with no cataclysmic spike.

Earliest attestation: Yue et al., Science Advances (February 2026) — 'Lunar chronology model with the Chang'e-6 farside samples and implications for the early impact history'
Sources: Yue et al., Science Advances (2026), DOI 10.1126/sciadv.ady9265 — far-side-anchored lunar chronology: smooth rapid decline, no 3.9 Ga spike, unified global chronology; Zhang et al., Science Advances (23 July 2026) — independent argon-dating of 28 far-side impact melts confirms the monotonic decline; Institute of Geology and Geophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences research news (9 Feb 2026) — institutional summary
Falsifiability: New far-side samples whose age–crater-density pairs fall outside the 95% confidence interval of the near-side-derived curve</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-153.99,-41.64,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-4247</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The moving substrate — the synthesis argument of master-list VIII.h</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Each exhibit is independently attested by peer-reviewed geoarchaeology or survey: the Gulf-head reconstruction has multi-team convergence with the debate stated; the Pelusiac trace is a published Science survey; the 1855 avulsion is documented history. Chain strengthened 12→14 sources at the tenth pass (ep723): the 2026 Apollonia drowned-harbor study (tectonic ± sea-level, dated slipways) and the 2026 Northern Adriatic paleolandscape reconstruction (dated 8 m + 5 m rises, up to 25 mm/yr) join the shorelines-retreat arm as peer-reviewed exhibits.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia / Nile Delta / North China Plain (three demonstration zones)
Dates: -4000 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The map's own ground moves: river avulsions, delta migrations, and shoreline shifts mean that ancient texts' geographic descriptions ("the port of X," "the mouth of the Y," "on the coast") cannot be pinned at their modern coordinates without a substrate baseline. Pins at excavated sites are stable (the site does not move); textual geography without a dated substrate reconstruction carries a precision grade no better than 'region' and risks tens-of-kilometers error. Demonstration exhibits: the Persian Gulf head (coastline at c. 5000 BCE near Eridu/Ur per Larsen &amp; Evans 1978 / Lambeck 1996 / Sanlaville 1989, never reaching Uruk per Jotheri et al. 2018; Gulf reached its current configuration c. AD 1000 via Shatt al-Arab progradation), the Nile's defunct branches (Canopic branch — Naukratis's river — and Pelusiac branch silted; the Pelusiac's lower-reach death c. AD 25 per the 1973 Science survey; Pi-Ramesse/Qantir abandoned when its river dried), and the Yellow River's 1855 Tongwaxiang avulsion (Yellow Sea to Bohai).

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories 2.17 — the seven mouths of the Nile (5th c. BCE), the oldest textual witness that a river's mouths are plural and movable.
Sources: Jotheri, Allen, et al. 2018 — Holocene fluvial and anthropogenic processes in the region of Uruk (marine transgression never reached the Uruk area, ~300 km from the present shoreline; discussion of the 6000 BP shoreline debate); Larsen &amp; Evans 1978; Lambeck 1996; Sanlaville 1989 — Eridu and Ur on the Persian Gulf coast c. 5000 BP (via the Uruk geoarchaeology synthesis); Al-Hamad et al. 2017; Aqrawi 2001; Bogemans et al. 2017 — the Gulf reached its current configuration c. AD 1000 via Shatt al-Arab delta progradation south of Basra (via Iacobucci et al. 2022 geomorphology study); Bunbury, Pennington &amp; Pryer 2019 (British Museum Studies in Ancient Egypt and Sudan 24) — the evolving Nile Delta: distributary network focused from many branches to few; the Canopic branch (Naukratis's river) now silted; delta head migrated south then north; Science 180(4081):59–61 (1973) — the defunct Pelusiac branch traced east of the Suez Canal; critical silting stage c. AD 25 by prograding beach accretion; Wikipedia summary of the Yellow River course-change chronology (cross-checked against the MDPI 14(9):1826 delta study and Nature s41598-017-03140-x): the 1 Aug 1855 Tongwaxiang breach (Xianfeng 5), ending the river's Yellow Sea course (1128–1855) and returning it to the Bohai; Madof et al., Nature Geoscience (2026) — the Euphrates as the product of deep-time tectonic avulsions (Paleo-Karasu + Paleo-Murat merger; the Fertile Crescent's rivers as tectonic accidents); J. Mar. Sci. Eng. 14(19):1830 (MDPI, Oct 2026) — four-stage morphodynamic model of the abandoned Diaokou lobe, 1976–2026: the 1976 Yellow River avulsion flipped the subaqueous delta from net-prograding to net-erosional over 50 years of observation (maximum erosion at the 7–8 m isobath; the 15 m isobath as the erosion/equilibrium threshold) — the argument's modern analog: a river avulsion visibly renegotiates the map within a human lifetime; Garbe, P. 2026, E&amp;G Quaternary Science Journal 75(2):169-171 (DOI 10.5194/egqsj-75-169-2026, published 2026-10-08) — Holocene landscape evolution and settlement development at Bubastis/Tell Basta (c. 3200 BCE - c. 200 CE): first area-wide geoarchaeological reconstruction (sediment coring, ERT, phytoliths); temples/palaces deliberately sited on flood-safe Pleistocene Gezira sand mounds; 9.5 m anthropogenic deposits in quarter S1 — the counter-exhibit: excavated pins hold when the substrate is read; textual geography is what needs the dated baseline; Brocard, Goiran &amp; Robinson 2024 (chapter, Nov 2024) — 'Fate of two cities built on sinking ground: slow and fast submergence at Thonis-Heracleion and Canopus': both cities grew as fluvial ports 1-2 km inland from the mouth of the Canopic Branch (Frihy 1992); Canopic sediment discharge dwindled after founding, branch vanished after the 5th c. CE (Flaux et al. 2017); Thonis-Heracleion displaced its monumental core to the Central Island 480-350 BCE as the shoreline retreated; North-East Channel silted 5th-4th c. BCE and the port moved with it — the harbor-side witness of a branch's life and death; Pennington et al. 2025, Antiquity 99(408) (doi:10.15184/aqy.2025.10185) — Karnak's island-terrace as the inverse exhibit for the baselines argument: geoarchaeology dates the substrate's choice of site (permanent occupation only after the terrace stabilized, 2520 BCE +/-420); the Eighth argument's rule inverted and still holding — a pin without a dated substrate baseline reads wrong in BOTH directions; Rayne, de Gruchy, Jotheri &amp; Abdan 2026, Iraq (Cambridge University Press) — the first direct radiocarbon dates for Eridu's irrigation canals: the oldest dated canal ~4540 BCE (~6,500 years ago); the five sorted channel systems fed by a now-dried-up Euphrates branch, abandoned or replaced as the Euphrates shifted its course — the dated channel-shift that retired the farm systems that fed the 'first city'; Flemming, Emrage et al. 2026, Libyan Studies — Apollonia, Cyrene's artificial harbor: Greek settlers removed ~170,000 m³ of limestone/sandstone bedrock (shore activity by pottery date 8th c. BCE, Cyrene founded 631 BCE); relative sea-level rise &gt;2.4 m over ~2,500 years driven chiefly by tectonic motion ( ~3.8 m subsidence 8th–9th c. CE, partially offset by earlier uplift episodes) — 1958–59 underwater surveys reassessed against 2025 drone/underwater work; the drowned slipways are a dated measurement of where the sea was; Ongaro, Donda et al. 2026, Quaternary Science Reviews — the drowned Northern Adriatic: sediment cores + OGS high-resolution geophysics reconstruct 5,000 years of coastal-barrier retreat — 8 m of sea-level rise in the 1,200 years to 6600 BCE, a further ~5 m after 6000 BCE, rates up to 25 mm/yr, barriers repeatedly created, subsumed and recreated kilometers inland — the shoreline that retreated within a human lifetime; Rayne, de Gruchy, Jotheri &amp; Abdan 2026, Iraq (Cambridge UP) — satellite mapping + first direct scientific dates for the irrigation-canal systems around Eridu (~6,500 BP): five distinct overlapping channel/canal systems digitized from VISION-1, Google Earth and declassified CORONA imagery; systems abandoned or replaced as the Euphrates shifted its course — the moving substrate caught in the act, dated. Grade-neutral strengthening: corroborates the method claim, moves no grade.
Falsifiability: A dated, peer-reviewed substrate reconstruction showing that any one exhibit's ancient river-mouth or shoreline sat where the modern map puts it would retire that exhibit; the argument stands on the aggregate of independent reconstructions.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.5,31.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-4000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The White Temple on the Anu ziggurat, Uruk</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavated, measured, and repeatedly rebuilt in visible stratigraphy — but the 'Anu' dedication is modern convention, and the labor figures are estimates; the record states both out loud.
Layer: real · Place: Anu ziggurat, Uruk (Warka, Iraq)
Dates: -4000 → -3000 (confidence: medium)

The Anu ziggurat at Uruk — the oldest known ziggurat, begun c. 4000 BCE and raised in at least ten rebuilds to ~12 m — carried the White Temple on its summit (Uruk III, c. 3500–3000 BCE): a 17.5 × 22.3 m tripartite temple, whitewashed inside and out, shining across the flat plain and visible over the city's walls.

Earliest attestation: The terrace and temple themselves (German Archaeological Institute excavations, Uruk, 1928–1939 and later)
Sources: German Archaeological Institute Uruk excavations — Anu district terrace (≥10 phases, ~12 m), White Temple summit building; phys.org synthesis (2026-01): the Anu ziggurat as the oldest known, White Temple 3500–3000 BCE, zaqāru etymology; Labor estimate: ~1,500 laborers × 10 hr/day × ~5 years for the last terrace revetment (modern calculation); No-evidence caveat on the Anu dedication (modern literature's assumption, unattested)
Falsifiability: Re-excavation showing the terrace is natural, or dating moving the phases millennia later</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.6391,31.3234,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-4000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Uruk, one of the world's first great cities</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Extensive stratified archaeology shows urban-scale settlement and monumentality; the superlative 'first' is definitional, not evidential — and the northern evidence (Tell Brak) now names the definitional rival.
Layer: real · Place: Uruk (Warka)
Dates: -3900 → -3200 (confidence: medium)

Uruk in southern Mesopotamia grew into one of the world's first true cities, reaching urban scale (tens of thousands of inhabitants, monumental precincts like the Eanna) during the Uruk period, c. 3800-3200 BCE.

Earliest attestation: Archaeological strata (Uruk excavations, German Archaeological Institute, from 1912); contemporary tablets and cylinder seals
Sources: German Archaeological Institute Uruk excavations (stratigraphy, Eanna precinct); Uruk-period tablets and administrative artifacts (CDLI corpus); Pleiades gazetteer entry for Uruk; University of Cambridge Tell Brak survey (Ur, 2007): proto-urban center c. 4200-3900 BCE, concurrent with or earlier than Uruk
Falsifiability: A securely dated, larger urban center predating Uruk's urban phase, or re-dating showing Uruk's urban phase is much later</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.6389,29.3119,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Uruk, one of the world's first great cities</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Extensive stratified archaeology shows urban-scale settlement and monumentality; the superlative 'first' is definitional, not evidential — and the northern evidence (Tell Brak) now names the definitional rival.
Layer: real · Place: Uruk (Warka)
Dates: -3900 → -3200 (confidence: medium)

Uruk in southern Mesopotamia grew into one of the world's first true cities, reaching urban scale (tens of thousands of inhabitants, monumental precincts like the Eanna) during the Uruk period, c. 3800-3200 BCE.

Earliest attestation: Archaeological strata (Uruk excavations, German Archaeological Institute, from 1912); contemporary tablets and cylinder seals
Sources: German Archaeological Institute Uruk excavations (stratigraphy, Eanna precinct); Uruk-period tablets and administrative artifacts (CDLI corpus); Pleiades gazetteer entry for Uruk (place 912986) — representative point 31.3234, 45.6391; University of Cambridge Tell Brak survey (Ur, 2007): proto-urban center c. 4200-3900 BCE, concurrent with or earlier than Uruk; Fragnoli, Crocco, Sterba &amp; Balossi Restelli, Archaeological and Anthropological Sciences 17:178 (Aug 2025): Tell Brak pottery petrography — paste recipes unaffected by urbanization; the LC5 (3350-3100 BCE) recipe shift marks full establishment of southern-Mesopotamian craftspeople, with few imported Upper/Lower Euphrates-Tigris jars — the Uruk phenomenon as real craft-and-people arrival, not just style diffusion; Grzegorska, Jakob &amp; Soltysiak, Int J Osteoarchaeology 36(3) (2026), doi:10.1002/oa.70123: possible non-accidental infant trauma (rib fractures) at Tell Brak — one of the oldest such cases; the authors treat Brak as an early urban centre under rapid-growth social strain — grade-neutral on the first-city question
Falsifiability: A securely dated, larger urban center predating Uruk's urban phase, or re-dating showing Uruk's urban phase is much later</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.6391,31.3234,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hisarlık is Troy — and the Hittite Wilusa</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Four converging lines: (1) topography — the plain, rivers, and sea at Hisarlık match the Iliad's geography; (2) stratigraphy — a 4,000-year tell with a Late Bronze Age citadel and a burned VIIa; (3) Hittite diplomacy — the Alaksandu treaty (early 13th c. BCE), the Tawagalawa letter, and the Ahhiyawa/Wilusa contention texts, naming people whose names map onto the epic's (Alaksandu/Alexandros); (4) the 1995 Luwian seal — Bronze Age writing found at Troy itself, tying the site to the Hittite sphere. Held below VERIFIED on the named minority dissent (Carpenter; the DOAJ 2024 name-association critique) — honestly carried, not hidden. Convergence, not consensus alone, carries the grade.
Layer: real · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: -3600 → 0500 (confidence: high)

That the tell at Hisarlık in northwest Anatolia is the Troy of the epic tradition — and the Wilusa of 13th-century BCE Hittite diplomatic texts.

Earliest attestation: Schliemann's identification, 1871 — his own claim, refined by every excavator since. The Hittite side: Alaksandu of Wilusa ↔ Alexandros/Paris (equated 1911), Wilusa ↔ (W)Ilios (1924), Taruwisa ↔ Troy (1924), Appaliunas (a Wilusian god) ↔ Apollo (1931).
Sources: Dartmouth Aegean Prehistory, Lesson 27 — 'Blegen, Dörpfeld, Schliemann, and many others' accept Hisarlık as Homeric Troy; Carpenter's arguments against noted; Troy VIIa 'perhaps the most likely candidate for the city of Priam'; the layer table (Troy 0 3600–3000 BCE through Troy IX 85 BCE–500 CE).; Penn Gazette / C. Brian Rose (2004): the 13th-century BCE Hittite tablet describing the alliance between the Hittites and 'Aleksandu (Alexandros), the ruler of Wilusa' — Alexandros being another name for Paris; other tablets frame Troy/Ilion as a bone of contention between the Hittites and the Ahhiyawans (Mycenaean Greeks); the 1995 Luwian-inscribed seal from Troy — 'the only piece of writing that has come down to us from Bronze Age Troy.'; Popular Archaeology interview with Dr. Paul Cartledge — Hittite documents of the 13th century referring to Wilusa ('which sounds like the Greeks' (W)Ilion'), Ahhijawa ↔ Achaea, Aleksandu ↔ Alexandros; excavated at Hattusa (Boğazköy) and deciphered.; DOAJ/Gephyra: 'Is Troy the Hittite Wilusa?' — the dissenting paper: the link 'is based on little other than name associations,' none of the equations 'cogent'; the stronghold 'was neither Troy nor the capital of Wilusa.' Carried as the named minority position.; Wikipedia, 'Hissarlik' / 'Late Bronze Age Troy' — the stratigraphic sequence and the scholarly consensus on the identification.
Falsifiability: A securely provenienced Hittite text fixing Wilusa's location elsewhere (with excavated confirmation) would collapse the identification; a Troy-side inscription naming the city (W)Ilion in Linear B or Luwian would push it to VERIFIED.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The chewing sticks — humanity's first toothbrushes</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across archaeological finds and the continuous living practice (miswak), but the precise 3500 BCE figure is a widely repeated convention rather than a single excavated-and-dated object.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (Babylonian context)
Dates: -3500 → -3000 (confidence: medium)

Chewed twigs with frayed, brush-like ends were used to clean teeth in Mesopotamia by ~3500 BCE and in Egypt by ~3000 BCE; the practice continues today as the miswak (Salvadora persica).

Earliest attestation: Babylonian use, conventionally ~3500 BCE; Egyptian tomb finds containing tooth sticks, c. 3000 BCE.
Sources: Dental-history convention (Central Coast Dental Care; Sure Dental; Scribd 'Ancient Dental Hygiene Practices' citing Journal of Orofacial Research 2012): chew sticks from ~3500 BCE Mesopotamia; Egyptian tomb finds from 3000 BCE; Gurudath et al., Journal of Orofacial Research 2012: the Mesopotamian royal tomb toilet set c. 3000 BCE with golden toothpick; Babylonian chewing-stick origins; Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry (Living on Earth, 2002): the chew stick dates from Babylonian times; still used in parts of Africa and rural America
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the earliest 'chew stick' reports are all 19th/20th-century ethnographic projections with no ancient material or textual leg.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4,32.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The clay envelopes of Uruk, c. 3500-3100 BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The objects exist in museum collections (~150 recovered); their token contents and seal impressions are documented; the accounting function is the scholarly consensus reading of Schmandt-Besserat's catalogue, held below VERIFIED on the inferential link between form and function.
Layer: real · Place: Uruk (Warka), southern Iraq — the type-site region; examples from multiple Mesopotamian sites
Dates: -3500 → -3100 (confidence: medium)

Hollow clay balls (bullae) enclosing accounting tokens, sealed and seal-impressed, were in use in Uruk-period Mesopotamia c. 3500-3100 BCE as tamper-proof containers of recorded information — the envelope's oldest known ancestor.

Earliest attestation: The bullae themselves, Late Uruk-period archaeological deposits, c. 3500-3100 BCE.
Sources: ThoughtCo — 'Clay Tokens: Neolithic Seeds of Mesopotamian Writing' (Late Uruk bullae 3500-3100 BCE, ~150 recovered, stamped exteriors, pinched-shut closures); Schmandt-Besserat, D. — 'An Archaic Recording System and the Origin of Writing' (SMS 1, 1977); Woods, C. (Oriental Institute) — Royal Ontario Museum lecture on the clay balls as data-storage devices; Nuzi bulla with 49 pebbles + sheep-and-goats contract (archaeology.wiki summary); MacGinnis / Ziyaret Tepe project — tokens sealed in bullae as certified records from the late fourth millennium (Live Science summary)
Falsifiability: Stratigraphic or typological re-dating placing all known bullae after the first proto-cuneiform tablets would break the sequence; proof the tokens were votive rather than accounting would break the function reading.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.0917,31.3222,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The invention of cuneiform writing</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tablets physically exist in stratified, dated contexts; 'earliest writing' is a genuine tie with Egypt's Abydos U-j tags (both c. 3400-3200 BCE, both administrative), not a settled Sumerian first.
Layer: real · Place: Uruk
Dates: -3400 → -3200 (confidence: medium)

Cuneiform writing was invented in southern Mesopotamia (Uruk), with the earliest proto-cuneiform tablets dated c. 3400-3200 BCE — the earliest known true writing system.

Earliest attestation: Proto-cuneiform tablets from Uruk, Uruk IV-III strata (c. 3400-3200 BCE)
Sources: Uruk IV/III proto-cuneiform tablets (CDLI corpus); Denise Schmandt-Besserat, token-to-tablet research (secondary analysis); Abydos tomb U-j ivory tags, c. 3400-3200 BCE (Dreyer 1998; contested readings by Kahl 2003 — the inventory-tag function and date range hold); Robert K. Englund, 'Accounting in proto-cuneiform' (CDLI): Uruk archives are ~85% administrative; Uruk IV tablets all from Uruk, discarded as refuse once checked; Karenleigh A. Overmann, 'Beyond Writing', Cambridge Archaeological Journal 26(2) (2016): the complex-token-to-pictograph derivation 'remains the most controversial aspect of this hypothesis'; Silvia Ferrara, Kathryn Kelley, Mattia Cartolano et al., Antiquity (Nov 2024): designs on pre-literate cylinder seals (c. 4400 BCE onward) identified as graphic-and-contextual precursors of proto-cuneiform signs (e.g. ZATU639) for pottery/textile transport — a parallel origin line for sign shapes alongside Schmandt-Besserat's token sequence
Falsifiability: Securely dated writing anywhere predating Uruk IV by centuries, or proof the Uruk tablets are administrative tokens without linguistic content</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.6389,29.3119,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ötzi's 61 tattoos — the world's oldest preserved (3370–3100 BCE)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED across radiocarbon dating, multispectral imaging, and peer-reviewed publication (Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports). Ötzi's 61 tattoos — groups of thin parallel lines and crosses along the lower spine, left calf, right instep, both ankle joints, left wrist, and right torso — were made with campfire-soot pigment. Their placement along degenerating joints and the spine supports the therapeutic reading: ancient cultures believing tattoos could relieve physical ailments. A 2024 experiment (Deter-Wolf et al., European Journal of Archaeology) confirmed the technique was hand-poking with a pigment-tipped pointed tool, not slicing and rubbing ash — overturning 30 years of conventional wisdom. Ötzi is highly unlikely to be the first tattooed human; he is simply the oldest whose skin survived.
Layer: real · Place: Ötztal Alps, Austrian-Italian border (discovery site); body at the South Tyrol Museum of Archaeology, Bolzano
Dates: -3370 → -3100 (confidence: high)

Ötzi the Tyrolean Iceman — the naturally mummified man found in the Ötztal Alps in 1991, dated 3370–3100 BCE — bears 61 tattoos (thin parallel black lines and crosses on the spine, left calf, right instep, ankle joints, left wrist, and torso), making him the oldest tattooed human remains discovered to date.

Earliest attestation: Discovery 1991; full tattoo mapping via multispectral imaging (Samadelli et al.)
Sources: Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports (via HAL): 'This research conclusively determines that the oldest preserved tattoos known to date belong to the Tyrolean Iceman, also known as Ötzi.'; IFLScience: Ötzi's 61 tattoos (3370–3100 BCE); the lines and crosses; the therapeutic/medicinal reading; the campfire-soot pigment.; Science News (2024): the Deter-Wolf hand-poke experiment — 'the past 30 years of conventional wisdom as to how the Iceman was tattooed is incorrect'; the single-pointed pigment-tipped tool.
Falsifiability: Older preserved tattoos on human skin</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>10.8445,46.7787,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3370</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kani Shaie 2025 — the Uruk expansion reaches the Zagros foothills</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The find is documented by the excavation project itself (multi-university team with Iraqi Kurdistan heritage authorities) and reported with project-supplied imagery; the peer-reviewed preliminary publication is pending — documented excavation, awaiting print confirmation. The building's function stays the excavators' provisional interpretation, fenced.
Layer: real · Place: Kani Shaie, Basian Basin, Sulaimania province, Iraqi Kurdistan (approximate — site coordinates not yet published)
Dates: -3300 → -3100 (confidence: medium)

In 2025 the Kani Shaie Archaeological Project (KSAP, University of Coimbra CEAACP — co-directed by André Tomé, Steve Renette, Maria da Conceição Lopes, Michael Lewis) excavated a monumental public-or-ritual building of the Uruk period (c. 3300–3100 BCE) on the summit tell at Kani Shaie, in the Basian Basin at the western edge of the Zagros Mountains, Sulaimania province, Iraqi Kurdistan — with an associated fragment of a gold earring, an Uruk-period cylinder seal, and decorative wall cones of Uruk monumental-architecture type — the strongest newly excavated exhibit in a decade for the Uruk expansion's eastern reach.

Earliest attestation: The excavated structure and associated artifacts themselves (KSAP 2025 campaign); announced October 2025 via the project/University of Coimbra communications, reported by Labrujulaverde with project-supplied imagery
Sources: KSAP/University of Coimbra announcement via Labrujulaverde (Oct 2025): monumental Uruk-period structure, gold earring, Uruk cylinder seal, wall cones — project-supplied imagery; Kani Shaie Archaeological Project (KSAP) — co-directors André Tomé, Steve Renette, Maria da Conceição Lopes, Michael Lewis; Universities of Coimbra, Algarve, Cambridge; Iraqi Kurdistan heritage authorities; campaigns begun 2013; Algaze, G., The Uruk World System (1993; 2nd ed. 2005) — the core-periphery expansion thesis; Stein, G.J. (1999, Rethinking World-Systems) — the distance-parity/trade-diaspora counter-thesis; the frame the new site enters
Falsifiability: Publication of the preliminary results showing the building's levels are not Uruk-period (would move the record); full publication confirming a Uruk-period administrative presence with tablets or sealed archives (would strengthen toward the 'node' reading)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.0,35.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Narmer and the unification of Egypt</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Narmer's existence is multiply attested (palette, maceheads, year-labels, the Den seal list, Levantine pottery at Arad and Tel Erani); the identification of Narmer with the legendary Menes and the single-event unification narrative remain debated — the palette's documentary reading is now graded separately at evt-narmer-palette-001 (CLAIMED).
Layer: real · Place: Hierakonpolis (find spot of the Palette)
Dates: -3150 → -3050 (confidence: medium)

Narmer (c. 3100 BCE) unified Upper and Lower Egypt, founding the First Dynasty; the Narmer Palette depicts him wearing the crowns of both lands.

Earliest attestation: Narmer Palette and Narmer macehead from the Hierakonpolis Main Deposit, contemporary; Dreyer's 1993 Abydos year-label of Narmer depicts the same smiting scene (read as evidence for a real event by Wilkinson and Davies &amp; Friedman; contested by Baines and Wengrow); later king lists name Menes as first king
Sources: Narmer Palette (Egyptian Museum, Cairo); Narmer macehead (Hierakonpolis Main Deposit); Smarthistory — Palette of King Narmer (iconography: both crowns, serpopards, smiting scene, serekh); Bronk Ramsey et al., Proceedings of the Royal Society A (2013), radiocarbon chronology of early Egypt; UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology (peer-reviewed, open access)
Falsifiability: Evidence that Upper and Lower Egypt were already politically unified before Narmer, or that the Palette is a later forgery</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.7794,25.0972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Narmer Palette as a documentary record of the unification</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The palette is a genuine contemporary artifact bearing Narmer's name; the DOCUMENTARY reading — that it records a real event rather than asserting kingship — is the dominant scholarly opinion (Dreyer's year label, Wilkinson, Davies &amp; Friedman) but has named, serious dissent (Kohler, O'Connor, Baines, Wengrow, Millet, Davis, Schulman).
Layer: real · Place: Hierakonpolis (Kom el-Ahmar) — find spot of the Palette, Main Deposit
Dates: -3150 → -3000 (confidence: medium)

The Narmer Palette (siltstone, c. 3100 BCE, found 1897-98 by Quibell and Green in the Main Deposit of the temple of Horus at Hierakonpolis) depicts an actual historical event — Narmer's defeat of a Lower-Egyptian foe and unification of the Two Lands — rather than being a purely symbolic composition.

Earliest attestation: The palette itself (Egyptian Museum, Cairo, Gallery 43; siltstone, 64 x 42 cm); found with the Scorpion Macehead and Narmer Macehead in the Main Deposit
Sources: Egyptian Museum Cairo — Narmer Palette artefact record (Gallery 43, Ground Floor; Late Predynastic, Dynasty 0; discovered by Quibell and Green 1897-1898 in the temple of Horus at Nekhen); Smarthistory — Palette of King Narmer (iconography: smiting scene, serpopards, bull destroying a walled city, decapitated/castrated foes, serekh with catfish-and-chisel name, both crowns on one ruler for the first time); Dreyer 1993 Abydos year-label of Narmer depicting the same smiting scene; Wilkinson and Davies &amp; Friedman read it as evidence the palette records a real event (dominant opinion); Kohler 2002, p. 505 — the 'subjecting the enemy' motif reaches back to Naqada Ic (~400 years before Narmer); the palette as ritual defeat of chaos, not unification; Baines — the events portrayed are 'tokens of royal achievement'; the chief purpose of the piece is not to record an event but to assert that the king dominates the ordered world; Schulman (following Breasted 1931) — the palette commemorates a Dynasty-0 Libyan conquest; the Palermo Stone shows predynastic kings already wearing the double crown
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription explicitly naming the event the palette depicts (Dreyer's year-label is the closest candidate and is itself contested), or proof the palette is a later copy or forgery</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.7794,25.0972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Narmer Palette's making — the 2026 tool-mark study (preliminary)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-team preliminary findings presented in a lecture, not yet peer-reviewed: the imaging and experimental method is documented, but the interpretations (tool marks, scene changes, repair) are unattested elsewhere and await publication.
Layer: real · Place: Hierakonpolis (the palette's find spot, 1897–98)
Dates: -3100 → -3000 (confidence: traditional)

New 2026 imaging of the Narmer Palette shows tool marks, possible changes to the carved scenes, and evidence of damage and repair — meaning the object has a production history beyond its finished iconography. Dr. Kathryn Piquette (with stonecarver Mick Oakey, Essex Egyptology Group, March 2026 lecture): high-resolution computer-enhanced images of the palette compared against experimental stonecarving on a similar stone slab with replica tools, in an attempt to reverse-engineer the production process. Findings are preliminary, presented as a lecture, and not yet peer-reviewed.

Earliest attestation: The palette itself (Quibell &amp; Green, 1897–98, Main Deposit, Hierakonpolis); the 2026 imaging study.
Sources: Piquette, K. &amp; Oakey, M. — 'New Research on the Making of the Narmer Palette', Essex Egyptology Group lecture (March 2026): experimental stonecarving + computer-enhanced imaging; findings preliminary; EBSCO Research Starters: Narmer Palette — summary of the 2026 finding (tool marks, possible changes to carved scenes, damage and repair); EBSCO Research Starters, History: Narmer Palette — bibliography entry citing Piquette &amp; Oakey, 'New Research on the Making of the Narmer Palette,' Essex Egyptology Group, Mar 2026 (indexed as of Sept 2026); citation only, no new data, grade-neutral
Falsifiability: Full peer-reviewed publication with the enhanced images and experimental comparisons; independent re-examination of the tool-mark attributions.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.5311,15.5776,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Retainer sacrifice at Umm el-Qaab — the 2026 forensic re-reading</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent forensic determination (modern method, curated collection) of perimortem trauma on a documented sample — a genuine second line beyond Petrie's suspicion — but single-study, sample-only (46 skulls of hundreds buried), and the interpretation, not just the fractures, is what the record grades; the 39% is a share of surviving skulls, not of the population. The Oct-14 Saleem rebuttal keeps the dispute live on sampling grounds; two specialist voices (Ikram: rigorous methodology, endorses; van Dijk: practice still controversial) sit on opposite sides of the interpretation. Grade HELD at CORROBORATED — no upward move while the study is contested by named specialists; no downward move — the fractures are the fractures.
Layer: real · Place: Umm el-Qaab, Abydos, Upper Egypt
Dates: -3078 → -2900 (confidence: medium)

The hundreds of subsidiary burials around the First-Dynasty royal tombs at Umm el-Qaab (Abydos) include victims of retainer sacrifice — people killed to accompany the king in death. The 2026 forensic re-examination (Roselyn Campbell, Purdue University, Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology): 46 skulls surviving from Flinders Petrie's early-20th-century excavations (Duckworth Collection, University of Cambridge) were re-examined with modern forensic methods; 18 of the 46 (39%) carry major fractures judged perimortem and probably fatal — mostly linear fractures (thin cracks without inward displacement, distinct from depressed fractures a mace would leave), plus sharp-force injuries including a chopping wound to one individual's eye. The study supports Petrie's century-old suspicion that the simultaneous burials around the king were killings, while acknowledging the 28 skulls without such injuries are unexplained, and that the skulls are a surviving sample, not the whole buried population.

Earliest attestation: Petrie's excavations (early 20th c.); the forensic re-analysis published 2026.
Sources: Campbell, R. A. 2026. Frontiers in Environmental Archaeology — forensic re-examination of 46 First-Dynasty skulls from Umm el-Qaab (Duckworth Collection, Cambridge); Petrie, W. M. F. — early-20th-c. excavations of the Abydos royal tombs and subsidiary burials; plans, photographs, and notes (the original suspicion of simultaneous death); labrujulaverde.com report (2026-10): 'Egypt's Earliest Kings Were Buried Among Hundreds of Servants — Forensics Finally Finds Signs of Violent Deaths'; Baadsgaard, A., Monge, J. &amp; Zettler, R. 2011. Antiquity — CT analysis of Great Death Pit (PG 1237) skulls at Ur: blows to the head, not poison (the independent parallel); Saleem, S. 2026 (Times of Lanka, 14 Oct 2026) — expert rebuttal of the Campbell paper: the 46 skulls are a selective 5–15% of the 700–900 subsidiary graves; paraffin-wax coating; macroscopic-only inspection cannot distinguish perimortem trauma from excavation damage; Reisner (1936) + recent DAI excavations show modular phased roofs, not simultaneous burial; calls retainer sacrifice a colonial-era interpretation, appeals for repatriation; The Guardian, 5 Oct 2026 ('Skull fractures suggest servants of Egypt's ancient kings were sacrificed') — Salima Ikram (American University in Cairo, not involved): 'one of the few times that rigorous current methodologies are being used to evaluate these remains'; subsidiary burials declined early Second Dynasty, statuettes replaced people; killing skilled retainers 'not really sustainable' (Ars Technica) — the abandonment mechanism; van Dijk, J. (Egyptologist, not involved, via Live Science Oct 2026) — welcomed the Campbell work but said the practice is still controversial (the live-dispute specialist voice)
Falsifiability: A published forensic re-examination of the same collection showing the fractures are postmortem or consistent with excavation damage; recovery and analysis of the reburied bodies showing a different mortality pattern; demonstration that the Duckworth skulls are not from the subsidiary burials.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.9189,26.1857,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3078</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The measured ancient alignments (archaeoastronomy ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The alignments are multiply and independently measured (surveys, theodolite, excavation context); the interpretation of intent is fenced, not graded
Layer: real · Place: Stonehenge (family anchor; Chankillo -9.56,-78.23; Giza 29.9792,31.1342; Nabta Playa 22.53,30.75)
Dates: -3000 → -200 (confidence: high)

A subset of ancient monuments carry sky alignments that survive measurement and dating: Stonehenge's solstitial axis (the Avenue on the summer-solstice sunrise alignment, Neolithic-Bronze Age phases), the Great Pyramid's near-cardinal orientation (arcminute-level precision, 4th Dynasty), Chankillo's thirteen towers as a working solar calendar (4th c. BCE, Peru) — while other claimed alignments fail on measurement or stay contested (Nabta Playa's proposed solstice alignments remain disputed among specialists). The fringe 'impossible knowledge of the heavens' reading is not needed to explain any measured alignment.

Earliest attestation: Stonehenge solstice alignment: recorded in 18th-c. antiquarian literature (Stukeley 1740); measured in 20th-c. archaeoastronomy surveys
Sources: Darvill et al. — Stonehenge phase and solstice-axis survey literature (Antiquity); Spence 2000 Nature — simultaneous-transit method for the pyramid cardinal orientations; Ghezzi &amp; Ruggles 2007 Science — Chankillo: the 13 towers as a solar calendar; Belmonte — archaeoastronomy surveys of ancient Egypt (the peer-reviewed corpus against which fringe readings are measured); Malville et al. — Nabta Playa megalithic complex (claimed solstice alignments, contested)
Falsifiability: A securely dated monument whose claimed alignment cannot be measured within stated error, or a showing that counted alignments across the full surveyed corpus sit at chance levels</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-1.8262,51.1789,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Meretneith's serekhs — the 2026 digital re-reading (First Dynasty female king)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single new reading of a single photograph of a lost artifact: the digital enhancement (Macquarie University) is methodologically documented, but the evidence base is one black-and-white image and the reading has not yet survived scholarly reception. Attested in one new paper; independent confirmation absent.
Layer: real · Place: Saqqara (unknown tomb excavated in the 1940s)
Dates: -3000 → -2900 (confidence: traditional)

Meretneith of Egypt's First Dynasty (c. 3000–2900 BCE) ruled as a king in her own right — not merely a queen consort — as evidenced by her name written in the royal serekh, the emblem that marked Egyptian rulers. The 2026 re-reading (Susan Kelly et al., Prague Egyptological Studies 2026) digitally enhanced a 1940s photograph of a lost mud stopper from an unknown Saqqara tomb (Walter B. Emery's archive, Egypt Exploration Society, London): the signs in the name portions of the second and third serekhs are consistent with the main sign used to write Meretneith's name, identifying her with the primary mark of rulers. The authors propose a staged political career: the serekh beside Djer's marks co-regency; tomb seals confirm a later regent/king's-mother role; studies estimate she ruled at least 30 years in one capacity or another. The claim is new and awaits scholarly reception.

Earliest attestation: The 1940s photograph of the mud stopper (13 such stoppers excavated from an unknown Saqqara tomb; only one photograph survives, in Emery's EES archive); Emery's original hand-illustration of the signs.
Sources: Kelly, S. et al. 2026. 'New technology illuminating ancient evidence: Re-evaluating Meretneith's serekhs and political position', Prague Egyptological Studies (2026) — the digital re-reading of Emery's photograph; Emery, W. B. — 1940s excavation photograph of the mud stopper, Egypt Exploration Society archive, London (the only surviving image of the 13 stoppers); phys.org / The Conversation republication, 2026-09-15: 'A 5,000-year-old royal mystery: Digital clues point to Meretneith as Egypt's female king'; Meretneith's tomb complex at Abydos (Umm el-Qaab) and associated seals — the material body of evidence for her royal career
Falsifiability: Recovery of the original artifact or its glass-plate negative showing different signs; published scholarly refutation of the enhanced reading; the authors' own framing is a new hypothesis open to the field's response.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.2165,29.8711,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Yamnaya steppe migration (~3000–2500 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent lines converge: archaeology (Yamnaya kurgans, wagons, material culture flowing west ~3300–2500 BCE), linguistics (shared wheel/transport vocabulary across Indo-European, pointing to a wheeled culture), and ancient DNA (Corded Ware ~75% steppe ancestry). The MIGRATION is corroborated by all three; the language-spread inference is the weakest leg (DNA records no words) and stays CLAIMED within the claim.
Layer: real · Place: Pontic-Caspian steppe (Yamnaya homeland) → central/northern Europe (Corded Ware horizon)
Dates: -3000 → -2500 (confidence: medium)

Around 3000–2500 BCE, a large-scale population movement carried people of the Yamnaya (Pit-Grave) culture out of the Pontic-Caspian steppe — the grass sea north of the Black Sea, in what is now Ukraine and Russia — into central and northern Europe. The migrants were cattle-and-sheep pastoralists who had adopted wheels and wagons around 3300 BCE. Ancient DNA shows the Corded Ware population of what is now Germany drew about three-quarters of its ancestry from the steppe newcomers — 'the largest migration into Europe in the last 5000 years' (Anthony). The migration is the leading explanation for the spread of Indo-European languages into Europe: the inference from genes to language rests on the migration's magnitude (if the people came in those numbers, their language came with them).

Earliest attestation: Haak, W. et al. 2015, Nature 522:207–211 ('Massive migration from the steppe was a source for Indo-European languages in Europe'); Allentoft, M. E. et al. 2015, Nature 522:167–172; archaeological case in Anthony 2007, The Horse, the Wheel, and Language.
Sources: Haak, W. et al. 2015, 'Massive migration from the steppe was a source for Indo-European languages in Europe,' Nature 522:207–211 — the core ancient-DNA result: Corded Ware ancestry ~75% steppe/Yamnaya.; Allentoft, M. E. et al. 2015, 'Population genomics of Bronze Age Eurasia,' Nature 522:167–172 — companion ancient-DNA study of the same migration.; Anthony, D. W. 2007, The Horse, the Wheel, and Language: How Bronze-Age Riders from the Eurasian Steppes Shaped the Modern World (Princeton) — archaeology + linguistics case; wagons ~3300 BCE.; Lazaridis, I. et al. 2026, Nature pair of papers (co-authored with David Anthony) — DNA pointing to Eurasian herders ~6400 years ago; homeland refinement toward the Caucasus–Lower Volga / South Caucasus. Science AAAS reporting, 2026.
Falsifiability: Ancient genomes showing Corded Ware populations with no steppe admixture; or securely dated steppe languages (if such were ever found) belonging to a non-Indo-European family; or the discovery that the '75% steppe ancestry' signal is an artifact of unrepresentative reference populations.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>38.0,47.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alulim, first king of Eridu</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested only in the King List tradition; no contemporary inscription or archaeological evidence of Alulim exists.
Layer: real · Place: Eridu (traditional)
Dates: -2900 → -2700 (confidence: low)

The Sumerian King List opens with Alulim of Eridu as the first king, reigning a mythical 28,800 years 'before the flood'.

Earliest attestation: The Sumerian King List itself, copies c. 2100-1800 BCE
Sources: Sumerian King List, opening lines (ETCSL t.2.1.1); Weld-Blundell Prism
Falsifiability: Contemporary third-millennium evidence for or against a ruler of Eridu by this name; absent such evidence the grade cannot rise above CLAIMED</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.9969,30.8158,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Babylonian soap recipes (c. 2800/2200 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The recipe tradition is attested in secondary scholarship (Roth; Conchon &amp; Rasmussen, 'An Ancient Cleaner: Soap Production and Use in Antiquity', Chemical Technology in Antiquity) and the Nabonidus-reign recipe is textually specific (uhulu/cypress/sesame). Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: the early tablets' identity and contents are thinly reported in the literature, and the 'soap-like' label is a modern chemical reading of an ancient material that was used for textiles and medicine, not personal washing.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon, Mesopotamia
Dates: -2800 → -2200 (confidence: medium)

The earliest recorded evidence of soap-like materials dates to around 2800 BCE in ancient Babylon: clay tablets carry written soap recipes — the ~2200 BCE formula of water, alkali, and cassia oil, and a Nabonidus-reign (556-539 BCE) recipe of uhulu (ashes), cypress oil, and sesame seed oil.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian clay tablets (excavated at Babylon; recipe texts c. 2200 BCE and 556-539 BCE)
Sources: Roth, on Sumerian/Babylonian clay tablets (3rd millennium BCE) — cited in the Invention podcast soap episode: 'with water, I bathed myself with soda... I cleansed myself with soda from a shiny basin'; Conchon &amp; Rasmussen — 'An Ancient Cleaner: Soap Production and Use in Antiquity' (Chemical Technology in Antiquity): Nabonidus-reign recipe (uhulu [ashes], cypress oil, sesame seed oil 'for washing the stones for the servant girls'); caveat that 'details concerning the identity and contents of these tablets have not been reported'; soaphistory.net — 'First Soap': first recorded evidence 'Babylonian clay cylinders dating from 2800 B.C.'; the product 'not necessarily used to wash the body; it might have been used to clean textile fibers such as wool and cotton in preparation for weaving into cloth'; Ebers Papyrus (Egypt, c. 1550 BCE) — animal/vegetable oils with alkaline salts for washing and skin ailments: the Egyptian leg of the early record
Falsifiability: Re-examination of the tablets showing the inscriptions describe something else entirely (unrelated workshop accounts, not soap recipes)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4207,32.5419,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gilgamesh, king of Uruk</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Named in the King List and the Tummal building inscription, but no contemporary inscription of a king Gilgamesh survives; the epic is literature, not history.
Layer: real · Place: Uruk
Dates: -2750 → -2600 (confidence: low)

The Sumerian King List names Gilgamesh as king of Uruk with a 126-year reign; he may be the historical kernel behind the Epic of Gilgamesh.

Earliest attestation: Sumerian King List, copies c. 2100-1800 BCE; Tummal inscription names him as a builder of the Ekur at Nippur
Sources: Sumerian King List, Uruk dynasty entry; Tummal inscription; Epic of Gilgamesh (Standard Babylonian version, c. 1200 BCE tablets)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription of a king named Gilgamesh of Uruk would raise this to CORROBORATED; continued absence keeps it CLAIMED</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.6389,29.3119,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Step Pyramid of Djoser</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The monument exists and contemporary inscriptions name Imhotep; the Djoser attribution is strong but partly carried by later king lists.
Layer: real · Place: Saqqara
Dates: -2700 → -2640 (confidence: medium)

Pharaoh Djoser (c. 2670 BCE, Third Dynasty) built the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, designed by his vizier Imhotep — the first monumental stone building in Egypt and a prototype for later pyramids.

Earliest attestation: The pyramid itself at Saqqara; inscriptions naming Imhotep in the pyramid complex; later king lists name Djoser
Sources: Step Pyramid complex at Saqqara (standing monument); Imhotep inscriptions in the Djoser complex; UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology; UCL Digital Egypt chronology
Falsifiability: Inscriptions attributing the complex to a different king, or re-dating placing it in a different dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.2164,29.8711,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Enmebaragesi of Kish</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Named in the King List AND in a contemporary inscription, giving independent attestation beyond the list.
Layer: real · Place: Kish
Dates: -2650 → -2550 (confidence: medium)

Enmebaragesi, king of Kish (c. 2600 BCE), is the earliest Sumerian king attested outside the King List itself, named in a contemporary alabaster vase inscription.

Earliest attestation: Alabaster vase inscription from Nippur, Early Dynastic period; also named in the Tummal inscription (later copy)
Sources: Vase inscription naming Enmebaragesi (Nippur, Early Dynastic); Tummal inscription (later copy preserving the king's building works); Sumerian King List, Kish dynasty entry
Falsifiability: Re-examination showing the vase inscription names a different king, or proof the King List Kish entries are entirely invented</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.6044,32.5403,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2650</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Harappan (Indus Valley) Civilization</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Hundreds of excavated sites across 1,000,000+ km² with consistent urban planning signatures: grid streets, covered drains, standard cubical weights, the Great Bath, the Lothal dockyard.
Layer: real · Place: Mohenjo-daro, Sindh (representative pin; the civilization spans the Indus-Ghaggar system)
Dates: -2600 → -1900 (confidence: high)

A planned urban civilization covering the Indus river system and beyond — with Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Ganweriwala, and Rakhigarhi among its great cities — at its mature height from c. 2600 to 1900 BCE, spread over a larger area than contemporary Mesopotamia or Egypt.

Earliest attestation: Seals published 1875 (Cunningham); cities identified by Marshall's ASI excavations, 1920s
Sources: Kenoyer, J.M. — Ancient Cities of the Indus Valley Civilization (1998); Possehl, G. — The Indus Civilization: A Contemporary Perspective (2002); Harappa.com archaeological database (archaeological survey record); Dholavira excavations, Archaeological Survey of India
Falsifiability: A single secure context showing the 'Harappan' signature to be a modern misattribution of unrelated settlements, or radiocarbon showing the mature phase to lie entirely outside 2600–1900 BCE</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>68.14,27.33,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The tomb of Sekhentiu-Ptah, 'keeper of the king's secrets' — 2026 Saqqara discovery</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Ministry/SCA announcement with specific in-situ finds (three sealed sarcophagi, intact false door); independent press reporting; continued study expected — title readings and reign may refine.
Layer: real · Place: Bubasteum necropolis, Saqqara, Egypt
Dates: -2600 → -2200 (confidence: low)

Excavations at Saqqara's Bubasteum necropolis uncovered an Old Kingdom tomb of the senior official Sekhentiu-Ptah (keeper of the king's secrets, royal chamberlain, priest of Maat, supervisor of the royal library) with three sealed stone sarcophagi found intact in their original positions, an intact false door, painted funerary cartonnage, alabaster ritual tools, a wooden falcon statue, and over 100 ushabti figurines; parts of the complex were looted in antiquity.

Earliest attestation: Excavated and announced September 2026 (official SCA report 29 Aug 2026); Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities statement via SCA Secretary-General.
Sources: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities / SCA statement, Sept 2026 (Bubasteum, Saqqara; three sealed sarcophagi in situ; inscriptions naming Sekhentiu-Ptah and his titles); Daily Mail reporting of the ministry announcement, Sept 2026: 'keeper of the king's secrets' tomb; crbcnews.com report, 6 Sept 2026 (updated 14 Oct 2026): sealed sarcophagi and painted cartonnage in original conditions; CRBC News, updated 14 Oct 2026: Bubasteum Sekhentiu-Ptah tomb re-report — three sealed sarcophagi, painted cartonnage, intact false door; venue-echo of the 4 Sept 2026 ministry announcement, grade-neutral
Falsifiability: Re-attribution of the tomb to a different owner, or demonstration that the sarcophagi were sealed in modern times</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.22,29.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The monument stands, Vyse's 1837 relieving-chamber graffiti names Khufu's crews (the 'Friends of Khufu' gang), and the Wadi al-Jarf papyri independently document construction logistics in his reign — multiple independent contemporary sources. The Djedi 2013 hieratic numerals ('121') corroborate construction-phase recording; the Big Void is not evidence for or against the attribution.
Layer: real · Place: Giza
Dates: -2589 → -2566 (confidence: medium)

The Great Pyramid at Giza was built for Pharaoh Khufu (c. 2560 BCE), the largest of the three Giza pyramids; workers' graffiti inside names Khufu's crews.

Earliest attestation: The pyramid itself; quarry marks and workers' graffiti naming 'the gang, Friends of Khufu' in the relieving chambers above the King's Chamber, discovered 1837 by Howard Vyse's team (the Khufu cartouche claim that grounds the attribution). Djedi Project robot (2010-11; published Journal of Field Robotics, May 2013) filmed red hieratic numerals '121' (the length of the Queen's Chamber southern shaft in cubits, per Miatello) in that shaft's hidden space — numerical mason's marks, NOT a crew-name cartouche, and NOT in a relieving chamber (the 2013 'cartouche in a relieving chamber' shorthand is a conflation; the cartouches are Vyse's 1837 find).
Sources: Workers' graffiti in the Great Pyramid relieving chambers (Howard Vyse, 1837: Khufu cartouches, 'Friends of Khufu' gang name); Wadi al-Jarf papyri (Diary of Merer, contemporary logbook of pyramid construction logistics); Richardson et al., 'The Djedi Robot Exploration of the Southern Shaft of the Queen's Chamber', Journal of Field Robotics 30(3), May 2013; Procureur et al., 'Precise characterization of a corridor-shaped structure in Khufu's Pyramid by observation of cosmic-ray muons', Nature Communications, 2 March 2023 (North Face Corridor); UCLA Encyclopedia of Egyptology; ScanPyramids collaboration 2017, Nature: 'Discovery of a big void in Khufu's Pyramid by observation of cosmic-ray muons' — the ≥30 m Big Void above the Grand Gallery; ScanPyramids 2023, Nature Communications: the ~9 m North Face Corridor behind the chevron blocks, confirmed by endoscope camera; Zahi Hawass, Sharjah International Book Fair, Nov 2025: a new Great Pyramid discovery to be revealed in 2026 ('write a new chapter in the history of the pharaohs'); as of Oct 2026 no published evidence of the reveal — viral 'sealed door opened' stories carry no published evidence; theegyptfiles.com (Sept 2026 review): Hawass's announced ~30 m passage ending at a door still has no published data or announcement as of September 2026 — kept separate from the Italian team's Khafre SAR work and from the ScanPyramids Big Void / North Face Corridor; ScIDEP collaboration 2025 paper + June 2026 conference abstract: muon-telescope prototypes for Khafre still under construction in 2026 — no pyramid data from that instrument yet; FORFOXS Evidence File, Oct 2026: 'The Great Pyramid's Hidden Spaces — What ScanPyramids Actually Found' — Big Void (2017, ~30m, above Grand Gallery) confirmed; North Face Corridor (2023, 9.06m); Aug-2026 SAR retraction does not affect muon findings; status summary, grade-neutral
Falsifiability: Contemporary inscriptions attributing the pyramid to another king, or secure re-dating of the Wadi al-Jarf papyri away from Khufu's reign</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.1342,29.9792,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2589</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Lagash omitted archive — the royal inscription control on the King List's Early Dynastic sequence</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple contemporary (Early Dynastic) royal inscriptions with excavated findspots, independently catalogued in the CDLI/ETCSL corpora; the dynasty's internal sequence is cross-witnessed by year-ordered inscriptions (Ur-Nanshe through Urukagina); the omission from the King List is a fact of the list's content, not an inference. CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: precise absolute dates of individual reigns remain middle-chronology estimates.
Layer: real · Place: Telloh (Girsu) and Tell al-Hiba (Lagash), Dhi Qar, Iraq
Dates: -2550 → -2350 (confidence: high)

The Lagash royal inscription archive — contemporary Early Dynastic royal records that the Sumerian King List never names — is an independent control on the King List's dynastic scheme: the Treaty of Mesilim (c. 2500 BCE, the king of Kish arbitrating Lagash and Umma's Gu'edena border, the oldest known boundary treaty), the Vulture Stele of Eannatum (c. 2450 BCE, the earliest identified victory monument, with its oath-bound border settlement), the Cone of Enmetena, and the reform cones of Urukagina (c. 2350 BCE). Lagash's inscribed royal line (Ur-Nanshe through Urukagina, ED III) runs parallel in time to the King List's named dynasties of Kish I, Uruk I, Ur I and Kish III — the archive's existence and sequence are established from contemporary inscriptions, not from any later copy.

Earliest attestation: Vulture Stele of Eannatum, c. 2450 BCE (contemporary).
Sources: Archaeology Magazine (May/June 2016), 'The World's Oldest Writing — Warfare': the Vulture Stele, c. 2450 BCE, earliest identified victory monument; long inscription recounts Lagash-Umma history ending in oath-bound border settlement; Umma's later plunder of Lagash; The Sumerian World (Routledge, via summary): Bänder 1995/Nigro 1998 — Eanatum of Lagash's Early Dynastic stela as the earliest identified victory monument; Ningirsu's battle-net with POWs; the oath formula as a legal instrument on the defeated ruler; Cuneiform law reference corpus: the Urukagina reforms c. 2350 BCE — referenced in other sources, the code text itself never found by archaeologists; the Code of Ur-Nammu (c. 2060 BCE) remains the earliest code with surviving fragments; CDLI/ETCSL corpora (general): the Lagash ED III royal inscriptions (Ur-Nanshe, Eannatum, Enannatum I, Enmetena, Urukagina) as a catalogued contemporary corpus; the Treaty of Mesilim tradition transmitted via the Eannatum/Enmetena inscriptions; Marta Santos Pérez, 'A Historical Synthesis on Lagaš and the archaeological excavations at Tell al-Hiba/Lagaš (Iraq)', Astarté (2024): Tell al-Hiba as a major Sumerian urban centre; dynastic sequence (Gudea, Urukagina); the Nin-Girsu temple tablets and inscriptions documenting Lagash's political organisation
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed demonstration that the key Lagash inscriptions are Old Babylonian or later forgeries, or a contemporary Lagash record placing the Lagash dynasty in the King List's sequence as a named dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.4072,31.4114,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2550</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Karnak on a river island — the temple the substrate chose</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed geoarchaeological survey (cores, chronostratigraphy, paleolandscape reconstruction) independently published in Antiquity; the myth-link reading is the authors' stated interpretation, fenced as such.
Layer: real · Place: Karnak, Luxor, Egypt
Dates: -2520 → 0300 (confidence: medium)

The temple of Amun-Ra at Karnak (Luxor, Egypt) was built on a fluvial terrace island formed between Nile channels — the only known such high-ground-in-water configuration in the area, recalling the primeval-mound of Egyptian creation myth — with permanent occupation becoming possible only after 2520 BCE +/-420 (Old Kingdom), and subsequent occupants engineering the channels themselves (desert-sand dumping to make land).

Earliest attestation: Pennington et al. 2025 (Antiquity) — first comprehensive geoarchaeological survey of the Karnak area; builds on the 2024 Nature Geoscience Nile Valley landscape paper (doi:10.1038/s41561-024-01451-z).
Sources: Pennington et al. 2025, Antiquity 99(408):1535-1549, doi:10.15184/aqy.2025.10185 — core-drilling + sediment analysis; island-terrace configuration; 2520 BCE +/-420 occupation threshold; opportunistic/proactive channel engineering; Pennington/Graham et al. 2024, Nature Geoscience (doi:10.1038/s41561-024-01451-z) — climatic/environmental shaping of the Nile Valley over 11,500 years; the substrate-context the Karnak survey builds on
Falsifiability: A re-dated chronostratigraphy showing permanent occupation well before the terrace stabilized, or a peer-reviewed rebuttal overturning the island configuration</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.6573,25.7188,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2520</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kingdom of Kush</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in Egyptian, Assyrian, and Kushite sources plus extensive excavation (Reisner 1908–1932, ongoing Sudanese missions); the exact Kerma-to-Napata continuity has some scholarly debate, so it stops short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Napata / Jebel Barkal (representative capital; Kerma and Meroë also pinned in media)
Dates: -2500 → 350 (confidence: medium)

The Kingdom of Kush — the Nubian kingdom along the Nile from the First Cataract south, with capitals successively at Kerma, Napata (near Jebel Barkal), and Meroë — was a major independent civilization of the ancient Nile Valley, with state-level organization, temple building, iron working, and its own writing, spanning roughly two thousand years.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian execration texts and Kerma archaeological horizon (c. 2500–1500 BCE); the kingdom's own record begins in the Napatan period (8th c. BCE).
Sources: Kerma excavations: Reisner 1913–1916; Bonnet (Swiss mission) — tumuli, Deffufa, the cemetery-city of 30,000+; Piye's Victory Stela (Jebel Barkal, 1862; Cairo JE 48862) — the Kushite conquest of Egypt in its own words; Assyrian royal annals: Esarhaddon and Ashurbanipal prisms — the Kushite pharaohs as named adversaries (external witness); UNESCO World Heritage: Archaeological Sites of the Island of Meroe (inscribed 2011) — Meroë, Musawwarat es-Sufra, Naqa
Falsifiability: Proof that the Napatan/Meroitic material culture is an Egyptian colonial outpost rather than a self-governing kingdom — contradicted by Kushite kings ruling Egypt itself in the 25th Dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.83,18.53,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ku-Baba, the tavern-keeper who became king</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the King List tradition and the Weidner Chronicle — two witnesses inside the same scribal-literary tradition — with no contemporary inscription or seal naming her; the person cannot rise above CLAIMED.
Layer: real · Place: Kish (Tell al-Uhaymir, traditional)
Dates: -2500 → -2400 (confidence: low)

The Sumerian King List's 3rd dynasty of Kish names Ku-Baba — 'the woman tavern-keeper' (munus-kas-de-a), the only female ruler in the entire list — as reigning 100 years and 'establishing the foundation of Kish'; she is also named in the Weidner Chronicle's literary history of kingship.

Earliest attestation: Old Babylonian copies of the Sumerian King List (WB 444 and duplicates); the Weidner Chronicle (early 2nd millennium literary letter) as second witness within the scribal tradition
Sources: ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1 (the Ku-Baba passage, 3rd dynasty of Kish); Weld-Blundell Prism, Ashmolean AN1923.444 (carrier of the passage); Weidner Chronicle (ABC 19; literary letter naming Ku-Baba among the kings)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Early Dynastic inscription or seal impression naming Ku-Baba of Kish (would raise the grade); demonstration that the passage is absent from all pre-Old-Babylonian witnesses and a late interpolation (would lower it)</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.1,33.13,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sixteen tablets against the evil demons</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The series' existence, sixteen-tablet canonical form, bilingual format, and multi-millennium transmission are established by the manuscript record and the modern critical editions; graded as artifact + belief-phenomenon.
Layer: real · Place: Nineveh / Assur, Assyria
Dates: -2500 → 100 (confidence: medium)

The Utukkū Lemnūtu ('evil demons') is a canonical sixteen-tablet series of bilingual (Sumerian-Akkadian) exorcistic incantations directed against evil demons, ghosts, and other malefactors; some incantations derive from 3rd-millennium BCE compositions, and the series was transmitted for over two millennia as the professional lore of the āšipu (exorcist). The Metropolitan Museum holds tablets 3, 12, and 16; tablet 12 (Seleucid) treats demonic disruption of nature and agriculture with a scapegoat ritual.

Earliest attestation: 3rd-millennium BCE forerunner incantations; canonical series known to Assyrian and Babylonian scholars of the first millennium BCE
Sources: Geller, Evil Demons: The Canonical Utukkū Lemnūtu Incantations (SAACT 5, Helsinki 2007); Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (de Gruyter, BAM 8, 2016); Metropolitan Museum of Art — Utukku lemnutu tablet 12 (Seleucid; scapegoat ritual) and tablets 3, 16; SBL review: 'a sixteen-tablet series' — sickness/health polarity as the series' organizing presumption; Haus des Beschwörungspriesters (House of the Exorcism Priest), Assur — generations of exorcists' working archive
Falsifiability: A first-millennium library catalogue listing the series' supposed companions while omitting Utukkū Lemnūtu would weaken the canonicity claim; the grade covers the artifacts and the attested belief, never the demons.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.15,36.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Yunmin and Iti mastaba — 2026 Saqqara discovery</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced by the Egyptian antiquities ministry with named mission, named owners, and readable titles; reported independently by international press; excavation still in analysis — final publication pending.
Layer: real · Place: Mariette Cemetery, north Saqqara, Egypt
Dates: -2430 → -2370 (confidence: medium)

A joint Egyptian-Spanish mission excavated a late Fifth Dynasty mastaba at the Mariette Cemetery north of Saqqara (reign of Djedkare Isesi, c. 24th century BCE) with two decorated funerary chapels — the main chapel of the senior official Yunmin (judge, court director, chief of the petition-handling scribes) and the southern chapel of his father Iti (scribal official of agricultural lands and offerings) — with reliefs retaining vivid original color.

Earliest attestation: Excavated and announced September 2026; the mastaba site had been known since Auguste Mariette's 19th-century excavations but never excavated or documented with modern scientific methods.
Sources: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, Sept 2026 (Egyptian-Spanish mission, Autonomous University of Barcelona + Supreme Council of Antiquities, 2026 excavation season, Mariette Cemetery); CBS News, Sept 2026: 'Exceptionally well-preserved' tomb of judge and his father; ImNews Africa, 19 Sept 2026: Saqqara excavation reveals Fifth-Dynasty chapels of Egyptian judge and father; Archaeology Magazine (archaeologymag.com), Sept 2026: 'Senior judge's tomb with colorful funerary scenes uncovered at Saqqara' — coverage of the Yunmin/Iti mastaba (Mariette Cemetery, 2026 season), venue-echo of the recorded ministry announcement, grade-neutral; Medium (Adam Jones), Oct 2026: 'Fifth Dynasty Tomb Reveals Colorful Life at Saqqara' — popular restatement of the same 2026-season find, venue-not-evidence
Falsifiability: Re-reading of the inscriptions attributing the chapels to different owners, or re-dating of the mastaba outside the late Fifth Dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.22,29.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2430</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Osiris: the murdered, reassembled, enthroned god of the dead</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief-phenomenon is multiply attested across ~2,300 years of independent textual layers — Old Kingdom funerary texts, Middle Kingdom Coffin Texts, New Kingdom Book of the Dead, Abydos material culture, and the late classical witness. Grade covers the attestation, never the entities.
Layer: real · Place: Abydos (Osiris cult center; Osireion)
Dates: -2350 → -100 (confidence: medium)

The Egyptian tradition attests Osiris as a god who was murdered (by his brother Seth), dismembered, reassembled by Isis, and enthroned as judge and king of the dead — a death-and-return attested from the Old Kingdom onward, forming the theological core of Egyptian funerary religion.

Earliest attestation: Pyramid Texts, Pyramid of Unas, 5th Dynasty, c. 2350 BCE; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead; the Abydos Osireion; Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride, 2nd c. CE
Sources: Pyramid Texts of Unas (5th Dynasty, c. 2350 BCE) — Osiris mythos in the oldest religious literature; Coffin Texts; Book of the Dead (Book of Coming Forth by Day); Abydos Osireion — material cult center of Osiris; Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride (2nd c. CE) — fullest narrative retelling; late outsider witness; Mettinger, Tryggve N.D., The Riddle of Resurrection: 'Dying and Rising Gods' in the Ancient Near East (Coniectanea Biblica OT Series; Eisenbrauns, 2001) — Osiris among the pre-Christian cases
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Pyramid Texts are modern forgeries, or that no pre-Hellenistic Egyptian source describes Osiris's death and enthronement as king of the dead, would overturn the attestation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.9189,26.1842,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2350</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The destruction argument: deletion and destruction, read properly, are evidence</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Method claim demonstrated across independent cases: Ebla (fire preserves), Herculaneum (catastrophe preserves the only intact ancient library, read 2026), Alexandria (the destruction telling itself debunked as the artifact), Baghdad 1258 (sack attested, legendary telling fenced), damnatio memoriae (erasure as evidence of the eraser).
Layer: real · Place: Multi-site method claim (Ebla, Herculaneum, Alexandria, Baghdad)
Dates: -2300 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Deletion and destruction, read properly, are historical evidence: what a regime erases proves the hand that feared it (the chisel's mark grades the belief that commissioned the hammer — evt-attestation-rule-001); what catastrophe preserves proves the archive that was there (fire bakes clay to permanence — evt-fire-baked-archives-001; Vesuvius carbonized the only intact ancient library — evt-herculaneum-pherc1667-2026-001); and the measured pattern of what is missing, checked against what should have survived, is itself a record. Destruction is deposition.

Earliest attestation: The method is demonstrated in the excavation and manuscript records cited below
Sources: Ebla Palace G: fire-baked tablet archives preserved in shelving order (evt-fire-baked-archives-001); PHerc.1667 read end-to-end without unrolling, June 2026, Vesuvius Challenge — ESRF Grenoble scans + AI ink detection, 22 columns of Greek Stoic text (evt-herculaneum-pherc1667-2026-001); The single-catastrophic-fire account of Alexandria's library is DEBUNKED — Strabo's library outlives Caesar's 48 BCE fire; no library in five contemporary 391 CE accounts; the 642 story is 13th-century fabrication (evt-library-alexandria-001); Mongol sack of Baghdad 1258 — siege, sack, and caliph's death multiply attested (evt-baghdad-1258-sack-001); the 'Tigris ran black with ink' telling is later/popular only (evt-baghdad-ink-river-legend-001); Syria 2015–2025: the measured-destruction exhibit — Casana et al., Heritage, July 2026: 199 sites, 59% looted or destroyed 2015–2025 vs 13% in 2011–2016; mechanical (bulldozer) looting associated with military activity; Ain Dara nearly leveled by 2022 (evt-syria-heritage-destruction-2026-001). The destruction pattern is the record.; Crimea, 16 Oct 2026: Center for Spatial Technologies + CISS interactive satellite map — Russian fortifications (trenches, firing positions) cutting through ~3,000 years of settlement layers along the western coast; a live exhibit of erasure-as-deposition; Hagia Sophia, Oct 16–17, 2026: the third church on the site; the first burned in the 5th c., the second burned in the Nika riots (532) — foundations 2.3 m below the current floor; destruction is deposition, measured in stratigraphy (Diker presentation, Istanbul); Damnatio memoriae: chiselled-out cartouches and gouged imperial names as evidence of the erasing regime (evt-attestation-rule-001 demonstration chain); PHerc. 139 virtually unwrapped Oct 2026 — 'Philodemus, On Gods, Book 8': the method repeats (second scroll read without unrolling); 70+ columns of Philodemus' On Vices deciphered; sharper scan of PHerc. Paris 4 confirmed the 2023 grand-prize reading (discover-science.com, Oct 2026); Vesuvius Challenge $1M prize for the next full scroll read, deadline 25 June 2027 (discover-science.com, Oct 2026); Lead-in-ink screening (Seiler et al., PLOS One, Oct 2026): Berkeley lab-built carbonized scroll stand-ins tested for X-ray lead discrimination — proof of method on stand-ins only, not yet applied to real scrolls; fenced as method R&amp;D, not evidence; Turkish Ministry of Culture and Tourism (8 Oct 2026): Kayalipinar/Samuha royal Hittite archive announced — 184 cuneiform tablets, 'most comprehensive Hittite archive discovered in this century' per Minister Ersoy; preservation mechanism (fire-baking) unreported in press — new clay archive adjacent to the fire-baked-archive leg, fenced, grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent); dailydispatch.com / FOX 9 (13 Oct 2026): Minneapolis three-alarm fire spread to tribal offices and threatened the Dakota language archive — destruction-as-deposition happening live; contemporary news illustration of the mechanism, not historical evidence (grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent); Biblical Archaeology Society / Bible History Daily, Lauren K. McCormick (16 Oct 2026): 'Archive of Hittite Tablets Discovered' — second-wave re-report of the 8 Oct 2026 ministry announcement already on the chain: 131 new tablets + 53 from the 2025 season = 184 pieces, Tudhaliya II, Hittite and Hurrian languages, content details (city-to-city epidemic, infant princess's illness/death and funeral rites, urgent military reinforcement appeals, bird-divination diary entries) — context, grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent
Falsifiability: A destroyed-and-erased corpus whose pattern of survival bears no recoverable relation to what the eraser feared or what the fire took — i.e., a case where the destruction leaves literally no signature</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.8,35.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dumuzi becomes Tammuz: a documented cross-cultural transmission</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Same figure across three languages with textual continuity, culminating in a Hebrew-prophetic witness to his Jerusalem cult; independent attestations on both ends of the transmission.
Layer: real · Place: Sumer to Babylonia to Jerusalem (the Dumuzi-Tammuz route)
Dates: -2300 → -0500 (confidence: medium)

The Sumerian Dumuzi is the same figure as the Akkadian and Hebrew Tammuz — one god transmitted across languages and empires, his lament cult reaching the Jerusalem Temple court (Ezekiel 8:14).

Earliest attestation: Sumerian Dumuzi literature, 3rd millennium BCE (Inanna's Descent, ETCSL t.1.4.1; Dumuzi's Dream)
Sources: ETCSL, Inanna's Descent to the Nether World (t.1.4.1); Dumuzi's Dream; Ezekiel 8:14 — women weeping for Tammuz at the north gate of the Temple; Tryggve N.D. Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — Dumuzi chapter; Private pre-Christian letter likening survival of uprisings to Tammuz's ability to return from the dead (cited Mettinger 2001, p. 201)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Sumerian Dumuzi and the Akkadian Tammuz are lexically and cultically distinct figures (no name or lament continuity) would downgrade this to CLAIMED.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>40.0,31.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The preservation paradox: palace fire bakes Ebla's archives to permanence</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavation reports multiply attest the fire-baked tablets in their shelving order; the mechanism (sun-dried clay + palace fire = kiln-fired permanence) is demonstrated material science applied to an archaeological fact.
Layer: real · Place: Tell Mardikh (Ebla), Syria — Palace G
Dates: -2300 → -2300 (confidence: medium)

The cuneiform archives of Palace G at Ebla (Syria) survived for ~4,300 years because the palace burned: sun-dried clay tablets stored on wooden shelves were fire-baked by the palace's destruction (c. 2300 BCE) and collapsed in place, preserving the archive's original shelving order — destruction as the mechanism of preservation.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves, excavated in situ by Paolo Matthiae's team 1974–75
Sources: Ebla Palace G archive: ~1,800 complete tablets, 4,700 fragments, found in situ on collapsed shelves; Wikipedia — Ebla tablets: 'Many of the tablets had not previously been baked, but when all were preserved by the fire that destroyed the palace, their storage method served to fire them almost as thoroughly as if in a kiln' (citing the excavation reports); Wikipedia — Ebla: 'palace G was burned, baking the clay tablets of the royal archives and preserving them'
Falsifiability: Unbaked tablets from the same archive surviving unbaked elsewhere, or proof the Palace G fire postdates the tablets' deposition</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.8,35.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The exposed-infant-on-the-river topos: Sargon's basket as the older witness</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Sargon legend's text exists and carries the topos; the chronological priority of the attested witness is a matter of the tablets, not of interpretation.
Layer: real · Place: Akkad / Mesopotamia (legend's setting; copies from Nineveh)
Dates: -2300 → -650 (confidence: medium)

The birth narrative of Sargon of Akkad — exposed as an infant in a reed basket caulked with bitumen, set on the river, recovered and raised by a water-drawer — is attested in cuneiform copies (Neo-Assyrian, preserving older tradition; Brian Lewis, The Sargon Legend, ASOR 1978). The exposed-infant-on-the-river topos therefore predates the Moses birth narrative (Exodus 2) by roughly a millennium in its attested form. Whether the Exodus writer knew Sargon's legend directly, or both drew on a shared older topos, is a literary judgment, not a provable borrowing.

Earliest attestation: The Sargon Birth Legend (cuneiform; surviving copies Neo-Assyrian, 7th c. BCE, preserving older tradition)
Sources: The Sargon Birth Legend (cuneiform text; Neo-Assyrian copies); Brian Lewis, The Sargon Legend (American Schools of Oriental Research, 1978); Exodus 2:1–10 (the Moses birth narrative — the topos's later witness); Michael Heiser, Naked Bible Podcast 257 analysis: the Sargon legend's current form may post-date the Exodus composition; structural differences between the narratives
Falsifiability: A securely dated copy of the Sargon legend older than the earliest Exodus composition would settle the priority; a demonstration that the topos is a modern scholarly construct would collapse the record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4,33.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The King List as political charter (the legitimation thesis)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — A well-supported scholarly thesis resting on the text's structure (the one-monarchy formula, the Isin ending, documented deliberate edits between recensions) — but the composition act itself is inferred, and the Akkad-origin alternative is live.
Layer: real · Place: Isin (whose dynasty the WB 444 recension ends with)
Dates: -2300 → -1800 (confidence: low)

The Sumerian King List was composed and redacted to legitimize contemporary rulers' claims to power over Babylonia — the 'Chronicle of the One Monarchy' in which kingship, a single institution, passes from city to city — rather than to record political history; the Weld-Blundell recension's ending with the Isin dynasty marks it as Isin's turn as rightful inheritor of the Ur III legacy.

Earliest attestation: Inferred from the text's structure and recension history, not from any contemporary statement of purpose; scholarly thesis (Jacobsen 1939; Michalowski 1983)
Sources: Sumerian King List overview (list created/redacted to legitimize claims to power; Steinkeller's Akkad-origin argument; WB 444's Isin ending; antediluvian section as Kish-downplaying insertion); Piotr Michalowski, 'History as Charter: Some Observations on the Sumerian King List', JAOS 103 (1983), pp. 237-248; Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (AS 11, 1939); Piotr Steinkeller, 'An Ur III Manuscript of the Sumerian King List' (Festschrift Wilcke, 2003)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription stating the list's purpose as straight historiography, or a single Ur III-period exemplar identical in ideology to the Isin recension (which would collapse the deliberate-editing evidence)</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2794,32.0706,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The King List's dynasties overlapped — the sequential 'one monarchy' is editorial</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent lines: (1) the Sumerian composition 'Gilgamesh and Aga' shows Aga of Kish as Gilgamesh of Uruk's contemporary, while the List files Kish I before Uruk I; (2) the ETCSL composite text's own manuscript variants disagree about dynasty order, boundaries, and reign numbers, proving the sequence was fluid and editorial; (3) contemporary royal inscriptions (Enmetena, the Lagash ruler line) document courts the List's sequence compresses into succession. CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: some dynastic boundaries rest on thin inscriptional ground and the Jacobsen-preservation minority position is live.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (Kish, Uruk, Ur, Lagash, Mari, Akkad)
Dates: -2300 → -1800 (confidence: medium)

The Sumerian King List's dynasties did not all rule sequentially: several city-state dynasties were contemporary with each other, so the list's 'kingship was taken to [city]' sequence — the one-monarchy formula — is an editorial arrangement, not a political record of Mesopotamia.

Earliest attestation: The finding is modern scholarship's (Jacobsen 1939; collated variants in ETCSL t.2.1.1); the ancient evidence is the 'Gilgamesh and Aga' composition and contemporary royal inscriptions
Sources: ETCSL t.2.1.1 composite text — the manuscript variants: mss. P2+L2 reorder the 10th/11th rulers of Kish ahead of the 8th/9th; P3+BT14, Su1, S, IB add, omit, and reverse dynasties and reigns throughout (re-verified live 2026-10-16); 'Gilgamesh and Aga' (ETCSL t.1.8.1.1): Aga of Kish besieges Uruk while Gilgamesh reigns there — the two kings of the List's supposedly sequential Kish I and Uruk I dynasties as contemporaries; Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (AS 11, 1939) — the compositional and variant analysis; Finkelstein 1963 version comparison (7-8 vs 10 kings); Piotr Steinkeller, 'An Ur III Manuscript of the Sumerian King List' (2003) — the USKL (Ur III copy) differs structurally from the Old Babylonian recensions, proving editorial reshaping across centuries; the Sargon-Rimush-Manishtusu internal sequence itself contested (Steinkeller's reversal proposal vs the traditional order)
Falsifiability: A recension-independent chronology (a king list tradition plus contemporary dating) showing every named dynasty strictly sequential with no overlap — the contemporary evidence would have to stop contradicting the sequence</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2308,32.1264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Udug: the word that didn't pick a side</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The neutral-to-ambivalent usage is multiply attested in primary texts (Gudea, OB incantations); the evil-udug-as-illness-vector is the canonical series' own premise, graded as belief-phenomenon per the attestation rule.
Layer: real · Place: Lagash (Telloh), southern Mesopotamia
Dates: -2144 → 100 (confidence: medium)

The Sumerian udug (Akkadian utukku) was originally a neutral class-term for spirits — the word itself did not connote good or evil. Gudea of Lagash (c. 2144–2124 BCE) asked a goddess to send him a 'good udug' for protection; exorcism texts invoke the good udug against the evil udug (udug-hul / utukkū lemnūtu), the evil ones blamed as vectors of physical and mental illness. The belief-phenomenon is multiply attested across two millennia of cuneiform texts; the entities are not graded.

Earliest attestation: Gudea cylinders (good udug, c. 2144–2124 BCE); Old Babylonian exorcism texts invoking good udug against evil udug
Sources: Gudea cylinders (Lagash, c. 2144–2124 BCE) — 'good udug' for protection; Konstantopoulos 2017 (on the udug's originally neutral connotation); Black &amp; Green 1992, Gods, Demons and Symbols of Ancient Mesopotamia; Cunningham 2007 ('daimon' preferable to 'demon' for the ambivalent udug); Old Babylonian incantation: 'May the evil udug and the evil galla stand aside. May the good udug and good galla be present.'
Falsifiability: Securely dated pre-Gudea attestations using udug as inherently evil would overturn the original-neutrality claim; the grade covers the attestation, never the entities.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.4,31.41,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2144</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dumuzi/Tammuz: the shepherd-god condemned, dragged down, and portioned between worlds</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The death is textually ironclad (the 'look of death' passage, ETCSL c.1.4.1:354-358); the return/partition is attested across variant endings and Dumuzi's Dream; the living lament reaches the Hebrew Bible. The grade covers the belief-phenomenon with the half-year crux fenced in competing hypotheses.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (Nippur — tablet copies; Uruk — Dumuzi's city)
Dates: -2100 → -500 (confidence: medium)

The Sumerian tradition attests Dumuzi (Akkadian Tammuz) as a shepherd-god condemned to death by Inanna's 'look of death' (igi mu-un-ši-in-bar), seized by the galla-demons, and granted a divided fate — six months in the underworld, six months above (shared with his sister Geshtinanna) — with laments for him attested into the Hebrew Bible (Ezekiel 8:14).

Earliest attestation: Inanna's Descent to the Netherworld (ETCSL c.1.4.1); Dumuzi's Dream (ETCSL c.1.4.3); Ezekiel 8:14 (the women weeping for Tammuz at the Temple)
Sources: Inanna's Descent to the Netherworld (ETCSL c.1.4.1:354-358) — the 'look of death' and the galla-demons seizing Dumuzi; Dumuzi's Dream (ETCSL c.1.4.3) — the pursuit, death, and the six-months-below/six-months-above decree; Ezekiel 8:14 — the women of Jerusalem 'weeping for Tammuz' in the Temple court (6th c. BCE); Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — Dumuzi as a genuine pre-Christian dying-and-rising case against the 1990s consensus
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Descent tablets are forgeries, or that Ezekiel 8:14's 'Tammuz' refers to something other than the Mesopotamian god, would overturn the attestation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1,31.32,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kish I reign spans are mathematically constructed, not chronology</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A peer-reviewed mathematical observation about the transmitted text: the reign figures are verifiable in the manuscripts (ETCSL), and Young's two JNES studies argue the pattern is deliberate design, not transmission noise; it remains a scholarly interpretation of the scribal method, not an excavated fact, so the ceiling is CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Kish (Tell al-Uhaymir), Iraq — the manuscripts' findspots vary (Nippur, Larsa, Susa, Sippar)
Dates: -2100 → -1800 (confidence: medium)

The post-flood First Dynasty of Kish reign lengths in the Sumerian King List (Gushur 1,200 through Aga 625 years, ~24,510 years total) are arithmetically designed numbers — most exact multiples of 60 (1,200 = 20×60, 960 = 16×60, 840 = 14×60, 720 = 12×60, 600 = 10×60, 420 = 7×60, 300 = 5×60), with perfect squares among them (900 = 30² for Zuqaqip, Melem-Kish, Ilku, and Enmebaragesi; 324 = 18² for Meshkiangasher of Uruk I) — a different numerical scheme from the antediluvian set (sars of 3,600, ners of 600, sosses of 60).

Earliest attestation: Old Babylonian copies of the Sumerian King List, c. 19th–18th c. BCE
Sources: Young, Dwight W. (1988). 'A Mathematical Approach to Certain Dynastic Spans in the Sumerian King List.' JNES 47(2): 123–129 (doi:10.1086/373263) — the multiples-of-60 and perfect-squares reading (900 = 30², 324 = 18²); Young, Dwight W. (1991). 'The Incredible Regnal Spans of Kish I in the Sumerian King List.' JNES 50(1): 23–35 (doi:10.1086/373462) — dedicated study of the Kish I dynasty spans; ETCSL t.2.1.1 composite text, post-flood Kish I section (opened 2026-10-16) — the reign figures as transmitted: 1,200 / 960 / 670 / 420+3mo3.5d / 300 / 840 / 960 / 840 / 900 / 600 / 840 / 720 / 1,500 / 400 / 660 / 900 / 1,200 / 140 / 305 / 900 / 1,200 / 900 / 625; 23 kings, 24,510 years 3 months 3.5 days; Wikipedia Sumerian King List article, Oct-2026 state — restates Young's reading ('various reigns were multiples of 60 ... while others were squares (e.g. Ilku reigned for 900 years (square of 30) while Meshkiangasher ruled for 324 years (square of 18))'); Rowton, M. B. (1960). 'The Date of the Sumerian King List.' JNES 19(2): 156–162 — the parallel argument for the Gutian dynasty (5/6/7-year reigns as sexagesimal round numbers, i.e. 'about 6' = decimal 'about 10')
Falsifiability: A contemporary Early Dynastic inscription documenting actual Kish I reign lengths in human scale, or demonstration that the multiples-of-60/squares pattern is a cherry-picked coincidence against the full variant corpus</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.66,32.54,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sumerian King List</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact exists and is multiply attested across independent tablet copies; the pre-flood reign lengths are not historical.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (copies found at Nippur, Larsa, Babylon)
Dates: -2100 → -1800 (confidence: medium)

A cuneiform list of Sumerian kings, beginning with kings 'before the flood' whose reigns are given in tens of thousands of years, then continuing with historically plausible dynasties.

Earliest attestation: Cuneiform tablets, earliest copies c. 2100-1800 BCE
Sources: Weld-Blundell Prism (Ashmolean Museum); ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1 (Oxford); Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (AS 11, 1939); Livius.org SKL translation (cross-check only)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription contradicting the dynastic sequence in a systematic way, or proof the tablets are modern forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2308,32.1264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sumerian King List</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact exists and is multiply attested across independent tablet copies (≥16 witnesses, two pre-OB traditions); the pre-flood reign lengths are not historical (see evt-skl-antediluvian-reigns-001, DEBUNKED). Supersedes -001 with the USKL, the recension count, and the WB 444 year-11 dating.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (copies found at Nippur, Larsa, Babylon, Susa, Tell Leilan)
Dates: -2100 → -1800 (confidence: medium)

A cuneiform list of Sumerian kings, beginning with kings 'before the flood' whose reigns are given in tens of thousands of years, then continuing with historically plausible dynasties — not a single text but a literary composition with a version history: at least 16 tablets/fragments, the oldest a Ur III-period copy (USKL, colophon: copied under Shulgi, 2084-2037 BCE) whose pre-Sargonic section is completely different, the rest Old Babylonian, differing by copying errors and deliberate editorial changes.

Earliest attestation: The Ur III Sumerian King List (USKL), copied during the reign of Shulgi (2084-2037 BCE); the main recension tradition is Old Babylonian
Sources: Weld-Blundell Prism, Ashmolean AN1923.444 (the only relatively undamaged complete manuscript; year 11 of Sin-magir); ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1 (Oxford; takes WB 444 as main source); Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (AS 11, 1939); Sumerian King List overview (≥16 witnesses; USKL copied under Shulgi; recension variants from copying errors and deliberate edits; Enmebaragesi first contemporary-attested king); 2026 evidence-state review (III.11 third pass): ETCSL t.2.1.1 re-verified live 2026-10-16 — the composite text's variant apparatus unchanged and resolving; no new 2026 peer-reviewed publication found that moves any SKL-cluster grade (the 2026 numerology pieces — 'divide by 144', sar-division flood chronologies — belong to the numerology family dismissed at the second pass: constructed data, not evidence); the parallel-dynasties finding now carries its own record (evt-skl-parallel-dynasties-001); CORRECTIONS.md: one row (the sequential-reading textbook shorthand); 2026 evidence-state review (III.11 fourth pass): the "Hidden Treasures" project (University of Copenhagen / National Museum of Denmark) identified a previously unrecognized copy of the Sumerian King List regnal-list tradition in the museum's cuneiform collection — a school text naming pre-Flood kings and end-of-3rd-millennium BCE rulers; announced via University of Copenhagen, April 2026 (assyriologist Troels Pank Arboell: "We had no idea we had a copy of that list here in Denmark"); project led by Nicole Brisch (Univ. of Hamburg) and Anne Haslund Hansen (National Museum), supported by the Carlsberg Foundation, Augustinus Foundation, Edubba Foundation; "Cuneiform Texts in the National Museum of Denmark" (Troels Pank Arboell, Nicole Brisch, Christian Halvgaard, Anne Haslund Hansen, Ulla Koch, Seraina Nett, Rasmus Johan Aarslev; Museum Tusculanum Press, Carsten Niebuhr Institute Publications, released 29 August 2026, ISBN 9788763547376) — the first complete catalog of the museum's cuneiform holdings, integrated with CDLI (high-resolution photographs/scans, ATF transliterations, material descriptions)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription contradicting the dynastic sequence in a systematic way, or proof the tablets are modern forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2308,32.1264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Code of Ur-Nammu — the earliest extant law code</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tablets physically exist and are multiply attested; authorship (Ur-Nammu vs Shulgi) and exact date are debated, so it stops one step short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Ur (Tell el-Muqayyar), Iraq
Dates: -2100 → -2050 (confidence: medium)

The Code of Ur-Nammu (c. 2100-2050 BCE), issued in the name of the Ur III king Ur-Nammu (2112-2095 BCE) — or possibly published by his son Shulgi — is the earliest extant written law code: 57 laws in casuistic if-then form, mostly compensation-based (fines rather than lex talionis), preserved on tablets now in the Schøyen Collection and the Istanbul Archaeological Museum.

Earliest attestation: Cuneiform tablets: copies found at Ur and Sippar; the 1965 Ur tablets allowed ~30 of the 57 laws to be reconstructed
Sources: Schøyen Collection tablets of the Code of Ur-Nammu (physical artifacts); Istanbul Archaeological Museum tablets of the Code of Ur-Nammu (physical artifacts); Ur tablets translated 1965; Sippar copy with slight variants (textual witnesses); Martha T. Roth, Law Collections from Mesopotamia and Asia Minor (SBL Writings from the Ancient World, 1997) — standard English edition
Falsifiability: Proof the tablets are modern forgeries (none alleged), or a redating of Ur III that pushes Ur-Nammu after Hammurabi — incompatible with all stratigraphy</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1,30.96,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the biblical tradition only; no contemporary inscription or archaeological confirmation of the person exists.
Layer: real · Place: Ur (traditional: Tell el-Muqayyar, southern Iraq — consensus, contested)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Patriarch of the Abrahamic traditions; said to have journeyed from Ur of the Chaldees to Canaan, and to be the ancestor through whom Judaism, Christianity, and Islam trace covenantal descent.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 11-25 narratives; composed centuries after the events they describe (mainstream scholarly consensus)
Sources: Genesis 11-25 (Masoretic Text); OpenBible.info Bible-Geocoding-Data (Ur of the Chaldees geocoding); Cyrus H. Gordon, 'Abraham and the Merchants of Ura,' JNES 17 (1958), 28-31; Gordon J. Wenham, Genesis 1-15, WBC (1987), 272 ('of the Chaldaeans' as late gloss); Stephen O. Smoot, 'In the Land of the Chaldeans: The Search for Abraham's Homeland Revisited,' BYU Studies Quarterly 56.3 (2017); UrOnline (Penn/British Museum): Woolley's 'AH' = 'Abraham's Housing' excavation label
Falsifiability: No single falsifier is agreed; the grade stays CLAIMED until contemporary evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1031,30.9625,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Apiru/'Habiru in the Bronze Age sources</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent primary corpora (Amarna cuneiform, Egyptian administrative texts, Hittite/Mari/Nuzi archives) attest the term across ~7 centuries; mainstream scholarship reads it as a social category. Not VERIFIED: no single corpus fixes the term's exact semantic range, and the 'Apiru-to-'Ivri equation remains a separate debated claim.
Layer: real · Place: Tell el-Amarna, Egypt (densest attestation cluster; the term's range runs Mesopotamia–Levant–Egypt)
Dates: -2000 → -1100 (confidence: medium)

The 'Apiru (Akkadian ḫa-bi-ru / SA.GAZ) appear in Bronze Age texts from the early 2nd millennium through the 12th century BCE — the Amarna letters (c. 1350 BCE, ~60 occurrences), Egyptian New Kingdom administrative records, and the Mari, Nuzi, Alalakh, and Hittite archives — as a social category of displaced persons, mercenaries, laborers, and outsiders. In those texts the term is a status label, not an ethnic self-designation.

Earliest attestation: Early 2nd-millennium cuneiform archives (Mari); the Amarna letters, c. 1350 BCE, carry roughly sixty occurrences.
Sources: The Amarna letters (EA corpus; e.g. EA 68, 71–74) — Moran, The Amarna Letters (1992); Egyptian New Kingdom administrative texts listing 'Apiru laborers (19th–20th dynasty, incl. Ramesses IV's expedition — the last known mention); Mari, Nuzi, and Alalakh archives; Hittite treaties (early 2nd-millennium SA.GAZ/ḫa-bi-ru attestations); uasvbible.org (2025-10-24): 'Habiru in the Late Bronze Age' — consensus summary: social class, not ethnic group; philology, chronology, and context dissolve the equation (confessional venue, carried for the consensus statement it records, fenced as such); biblicaanalytica.com (2026-07-19): Amarna letters summary — 'They do not narrate the conquest or name Israel... The 'Apiru are not proof of the Hebrews. What the tablets give is context, not corroboration of a specific event.'
Falsifiability: A philological demonstration that SA.GAZ/ḫa-bi-ru never denotes a social category in any corpus, or proof that the Amarna 'Apiru references are modern forgeries, would reopen this record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>30.9,27.64,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mesopotamian flood tradition</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tablets exist, are multiply attested, and the structural parallels are documented by generations of scholars. The MECHANISM (borrowing vs shared tradition vs independent invention) remains disputed, not graded.
Layer: real · Place: Shuruppak (Tell Fara), Ziusudra's city, southern Iraq
Dates: -2000 → -600 (confidence: medium)

Great-flood survivor narratives circulated in Mesopotamian literature centuries before the biblical account — Sumerian Ziusudra (Nippur tablet, pre-2000 BCE), the Old Babylonian Atrahasis epic (ca. 1630 BCE), and the Akkadian Gilgamesh Tablet XI — sharing the core structure: divine warning, boat, animals, mountain, birds, sacrifice. Direct literary borrowing of Genesis from Gilgamesh is NOT established; scholars argue shared tradition rather than word-for-word copying.

Earliest attestation: Sumerian Flood Story (Ziusudra), Nippur, pre-2000 BCE; Atrahasis epic, Old Babylonian, ca. 1630 BCE; Gilgamesh Tablet XI translated by George Smith 1872 (British Museum)
Sources: Lambert &amp; Millard, Atra-Hasis: The Babylonian Story of the Flood (1969); Andrew George, Gilgamesh translation (Ugarit fragment 2007); Heidel, The Gilgamesh Epic and Old Testament Parallels (1946); Gilgamesh Tablet XI (British Museum); Ugarit Gilgamesh fragment (13th c. BCE); Megiddo Gilgamesh fragment; Tell al-Hiba (Lagash) geoarchaeology 2025 — significant Early Dynastic flood c. 2400–2350 BCE (&gt;1 m silt), upstream river avulsion cutting the temple district; warfare-linked canal-breach hypothesis (Lugalzagesi); LATER than the classic literary flood tradition's placement (ResearchSquare rs-4192733, preprint; Clemson coverage). Grade-neutral exhibit: real Mesopotamian flooding, dated after the tradition.; Jodi Magness (UNC Chapel Hill) via HISTORY.com (~Oct 2026): 'What we have in Genesis is just one version of this flood story that had been circulating widely for a long time' — the pre-biblical Mesopotamian flood stories (Utnapishtim, Atrahasis, Ziusudra) as the circulation the biblical account joins; the 2026 Durupınar drilling reported in the same piece (see evt-durupinar-drilling-2026-001).
Falsifiability: Discovery of a Genesis manuscript older than the Mesopotamian texts, or proof the Mesopotamian flood tablets are modern forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.5,31.28,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Isaac, son of Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested only within the biblical and Qur'anic traditions; no contemporary inscription, archaeological trace, or extra-biblical text names Isaac as a historical person.
Layer: real · Place: Hebron / Beersheba (traditional)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Isaac, son of Abraham and Sarah, the heir of the covenant in the biblical tradition (Genesis 17–28); the binding of Isaac (Akedah) is the central event of his narrative.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 17–28; composed centuries after the events they describe (scholarly consensus)
Sources: Genesis 17–28 (narrative of Isaac, the Akedah); Qur'an (Ishaq as a prophet, e.g. Surah 37:112–113)
Falsifiability: A contemporary 2nd-millennium BCE inscription naming an Isaac of this role and lineage would corroborate; the grade is CLAIMED until such evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.1,31.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Isaac, son of Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Richly attested but within a single textual family (Genesis and the Jewish, Christian, and Islamic traditions depending on it). No contemporary inscription, seal, papyrus, or external document names the patriarch; Tel Beersheba has no Bronze Age occupation (Aharoni 1969-1976; Beer-Sheba III); source criticism shows redaction (wife-sister triplet Gen 12/20/26; double naming of Beersheba Gen 21/26; anachronistic Philistine Gerar). Supersedes evt-isaac-001 (grade unchanged; evidence chain enriched).
Layer: real · Place: Hebron / Beersheba (traditional)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Isaac, son of Abraham and Sarah, heir of the covenant in the biblical tradition (Genesis 17-28); the binding of Isaac (Akedah) is the narrative center. Attested in Genesis, Hebrews 11:17-19 (Isaac as type, Abraham 'able even to raise him from the dead'), and Qur'an 37:112-113 (Ishaq 'a prophet from among the righteous', Saheeh International). All attestations belong to one textual family.

Earliest attestation: Genesis narratives composed centuries after the events they describe (Van Seters: Yahwist 6th c. BCE); Qur'an 7th c. CE; Hebrews 1st c. CE
Sources: Genesis 17–28 (narrative of Isaac, the Akedah); Qur'an (Ishaq as a prophet, e.g. Surah 37:112–113); John Van Seters, Abraham in History and Tradition (Yale, 1975); Thomas L. Thompson, The Historicity of the Patriarchal Narratives (de Gruyter, 1974); Pardee review JNES 36:3 (1977):328; Richard Elliott Friedman, The Bible with Sources Revealed (HarperOne, 2003), 27-30 (wife-sister triplet source assignments); Yohanan Aharoni / Ze'ev Herzog, Beer-Sheba III (Tel Aviv, excavations 1969-1976) — no Bronze Age occupation at the tell; Jon D. Levenson, The Death and Resurrection of the Beloved Son (Yale, 1993), ch. 14 — the Akedah became central in later Judaism, not the biblical period
Falsifiability: A contemporary 2nd-millennium BCE inscription naming an Isaac of this role and lineage would corroborate; the grade is CLAIMED until such evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.1,31.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ishmael, son of Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested only within the biblical and Qur'anic traditions; no contemporary inscription or archaeological trace names Ishmael as a historical person.
Layer: real · Place: Paran / Sinai-Negev corridor (traditional)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Ishmael, son of Abraham and Hagar, blessed in Genesis 17:20 to become a great nation of twelve rulers; Islamic tradition traces the Arabs' (and Muhammad's) lineage to him.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 16, 21, 25; composed centuries after the events they describe (scholarly consensus)
Sources: Genesis 16, 21, 25 (Ishmael narrative; Gen 25:18 locates his settlements between Egypt and Assyria); Qur'an (Ismail as prophet, Ka'ba tradition); Hawting, Gerald — the claim that the Arabs descend from Abraham through Ishmael 'is indeed taken by many, non-Muslims as well as Muslims, as a genealogical and historical fact' (survey of the assumption, Middle East Forum review)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription naming an Ishmael of this role and lineage would corroborate; the grade is CLAIMED until such evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>34.5,29.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ishmael, son of Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in Genesis, the NT, the Qur'an, and later Arabic genealogy — all one textual family plus later tradition. No inscription or contemporary document attests the patriarch; the epigraphy attests the personal NAME only (South Arabian ysm'il stela, Wadi Jawf, ca. 6th c. BCE; Judean Desert Hebrew scrap, ca. 7th c. BCE — names, not the person). The Qur'an does not name the sacrificed son; early tafsir was divided (al-Tabari inclining to Isaac; the Ishmael reading became the later majority, 14th c.). The Arab-descent genealogies are medieval claims (Ibn Ishaq's Sira is lost, surviving only in Ibn Hisham's amplified edition 125-200 years after; the 'Adnan-to-Ishmael segment disputed even within the tradition). Supersedes evt-ishmael-001 (grade unchanged; evidence chain enriched).
Layer: real · Place: Paran / Sinai-Negev corridor (traditional)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Ishmael, son of Abraham and Hagar, blessed in Genesis 17:20 to become a great nation of twelve rulers; expelled with Hagar to the wilderness of Paran (Genesis 21); the Ishmaelite tribal genealogy in Genesis 25:12-18 dwells 'from Havilah unto Shur... before Egypt' (east of Egypt, not near Mecca). Paul's allegory (Galatians 4:21-31): Ishmael the child of the flesh, Isaac the child of promise. The Qur'anic Isma'il: prophet alongside Abraham, co-raiser of the House's foundations (Qur'an 2:125-129); the sacrifice narrative (37:102-107) names NO son. Islamic tradition links Arab genealogy (Quraysh via 'Adnan) to Ishmael.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 16/21 (redacted J/P/E per Friedman); Galatians 4 (1st c. CE); Qur'an 7th c. CE; genealogies 8th-9th c. CE
Sources: Genesis 16, 21, 25 (Ishmael narrative; Gen 25:18 locates his settlements between Egypt and Assyria); Qur'an (Ismail as prophet, Ka'ba tradition); Hawting, Gerald — the claim that the Arabs descend from Abraham through Ishmael 'is indeed taken by many, non-Muslims as well as Muslims, as a genealogical and historical fact' (survey of the assumption, Middle East Forum review); J. Louis Martyn, Galatians (Anchor Bible 33A, Doubleday, 1997), 431-466 (Gal 4:21-31 allegory); Al-Tabari, Jami' al-Bayan (c. 883-923) — early exegetes divided on the sacrificed son, Tabari inclining to Isaac; Ta'rikh 1:270 (intra-community dispute); IxTheo: 'Ishmael as Abraham's Sacrifice' (Islam and Christian-Muslim Relations, DOI 10.1080/09596410.2013.786339) — Tabari's philological analysis for Isaac; Ibn Kathir, Tafsir on 37:102; al-Bidaya wa'l-Nihaya 1:157-158 — the 14th-c. Ishmael majority; Ibn Ishaq / Ibn Hisham, Sira (d. 767/833 CE) — lineage via 'Adnan disputed even within the tradition; Patricia Crone, Meccan Trade and the Rise of Islam (1987) — pre-Islamic Mecca reconstruction rests on centuries-later sources; Neal Rappleye, 'An Ishmael Buried Near Nahom,' Interpreter 48 (2021), 33-48 — South Arabian ysm'il stela, Wadi Jawf, ca. 6th c. BCE (a NAME, not the patriarch); Israel Antiquities Authority, Judean Desert papyrus scrap with the Hebrew name Ishmael (announced Sep 7, 2022) — a NAME, not the patriarch
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription naming an Ishmael of this role and lineage would corroborate; the grade is CLAIMED until such evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>34.5,29.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ishmael, son of Abraham</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in Genesis, the NT, the Qur'an, and later Arabic genealogy — all one textual family plus later tradition. No inscription or contemporary document attests the patriarch; the epigraphy attests the personal NAME only (South Arabian ysm'il stela, Wadi Jawf, ca. 6th c. BCE; Judean Desert Hebrew scrap, ca. 7th c. BCE — names, not the person). The Qur'an does not name the sacrificed son; early tafsir was divided (al-Tabari inclining to Isaac; the Ishmael reading became the later majority, 14th c.). The Arab-descent genealogies are medieval claims (Ibn Ishaq's Sira is lost, surviving only in Ibn Hisham's amplified edition 125-200 years after; the 'Adnan-to-Ishmael segment disputed even within the tradition). Supersedes evt-ishmael-001 (grade unchanged; evidence chain enriched). Supersedes evt-ishmael-002 (grade unchanged; chain enriched: the Qedarite ethnonym dossier per the Neo-Assyrian annals via Eph'al 1982, and Eph'al's demolition of the Delitzsch/Thompson/Lewy Šumuʾilu=Ishmaelites identification).
Layer: real · Place: Paran / Sinai-Negev corridor (traditional)
Dates: -2000 → -1800 (confidence: traditional)

Ishmael, son of Abraham and Hagar, blessed in Genesis 17:20 to become a great nation of twelve rulers; expelled with Hagar to the wilderness of Paran (Genesis 21); the Ishmaelite tribal genealogy in Genesis 25:12-18 dwells 'from Havilah unto Shur... before Egypt' (east of Egypt, not near Mecca). Paul's allegory (Galatians 4:21-31): Ishmael the child of the flesh, Isaac the child of promise. The Qur'anic Isma'il: prophet alongside Abraham, co-raiser of the House's foundations (Qur'an 2:125-129); the sacrifice narrative (37:102-107) names NO son. Islamic tradition links Arab genealogy (Quraysh via 'Adnan) to Ishmael.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 16/21 (redacted J/P/E per Friedman); Galatians 4 (1st c. CE); Qur'an 7th c. CE; genealogies 8th-9th c. CE
Sources: Genesis 16, 21, 25 (Ishmael narrative; Gen 25:18 locates his settlements between Egypt and Assyria); Qur'an (Ismail as prophet, Ka'ba tradition); Hawting, Gerald — the claim that the Arabs descend from Abraham through Ishmael 'is indeed taken by many, non-Muslims as well as Muslims, as a genealogical and historical fact' (survey of the assumption, Middle East Forum review); J. Louis Martyn, Galatians (Anchor Bible 33A, Doubleday, 1997), 431-466 (Gal 4:21-31 allegory); Al-Tabari, Jami' al-Bayan (c. 883-923) — early exegetes divided on the sacrificed son, Tabari inclining to Isaac; Ta'rikh 1:270 (intra-community dispute); IxTheo: 'Ishmael as Abraham's Sacrifice' (Islam and Christian-Muslim Relations, DOI 10.1080/09596410.2013.786339) — Tabari's philological analysis for Isaac; Ibn Kathir, Tafsir on 37:102; al-Bidaya wa'l-Nihaya 1:157-158 — the 14th-c. Ishmael majority; Ibn Ishaq / Ibn Hisham, Sira (d. 767/833 CE) — lineage via 'Adnan disputed even within the tradition; Patricia Crone, Meccan Trade and the Rise of Islam (1987) — pre-Islamic Mecca reconstruction rests on centuries-later sources; Neal Rappleye, 'An Ishmael Buried Near Nahom,' Interpreter 48 (2021), 33-48 — South Arabian ysm'il stela, Wadi Jawf, ca. 6th c. BCE (a NAME, not the patriarch); Israel Antiquities Authority, Judean Desert papyrus scrap with the Hebrew name Ishmael (announced Sep 7, 2022) — a NAME, not the patriarch; Eph'al, Israel. The Ancient Arabs (Jerusalem/Leiden, 1982) — the Qedarite dossier; rejection of the Šumuʾilu = Ishmaelites identification (Eph'al, pp. 142-169 per the Wikipedia-sourced summary chain; verify page range against the print); RINAP (Royal Inscriptions of the Neo-Assyrian Period) — Tiglath-Pileser III, Sennacherib, Esarhaddon, Ashurbanipal Arabian attestations; Ashurbanipal 11 IX 36 (Abi-Yate' the Qedarite)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription naming an Ishmael of this role and lineage would corroborate; the grade is CLAIMED until such evidence appears either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>34.5,29.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The ancient parasols: shade-umbrellas before the rain umbrella</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent iconographic and textual traditions — Assyrian royal reliefs, Egyptian tomb paintings, the Roman umbraculum in Latin texts, and the Chinese oil-paper tradition — agree that shade-parasols are ancient; held below VERIFIED because this pass relied on trade-history aggregations (aina.org, umbrella histories) rather than a museum-cataloged primary artifact per claim.
Layer: real · Place: Ancient Near East &amp; China (iconographic record: Assyrian reliefs at Nimrud/Nineveh, Egyptian tomb paintings, Chinese texts)
Dates: -2000 → 200 (confidence: medium)

Parasols — umbrellas used as sun-shades and emblems of rank — existed in the ancient Near East and China millennia before the modern rain umbrella: royal parasols in ancient Assyria and Egypt (conventionally ~4,000 years, carried by servants for kings), waterproof oil-paper and silk umbrellas in China (conventionally 11th c. BCE), early folding forms (conventionally c. 21 CE), and the Roman umbraculum as a sun-shade for women and priests.

Earliest attestation: Iconographic: Assyrian palace reliefs (parasol-bearers over kings), Egyptian tomb paintings; textual: Chinese historical tradition for oil-paper umbrellas
Sources: The King's Parasol (aina.org, 2021): Egypt/Assyria origins c. 4,000 years; palm-leaf parasols as rank emblems; only Assyrian kings' right to the elaborate parasol; Chinese 11th-c.-BCE waterproof umbrellas; 'umbra' from Latin 'shade'; History of Umbrellas Facts (trade history): Mesopotamia/Egypt sun-shade origins; Assyrian ceremonial parasols; China silk-and-bamboo, first collapsible c. 21 AD; Greeks/Romans adopted the parasol for sun; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Umbrella' (public domain): the umbrella 'as at first used, was based on its Eastern prototype'
Falsifiability: Proof that the Assyrian parasol reliefs and Egyptian parasol depictions are modern forgeries, or a dated primary Chinese text denying oil-paper umbrellas before the 19th century</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.33,36.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ur of the Chaldees = Tell el-Muqayyar (identification)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The identification is the scholarly consensus (mainstream Bible atlases follow Woolley) but rests on inference, not on any document or excavation linking the city to Abraham; the 'of the Chaldees' qualifier is near-certainly a late editorial gloss.
Layer: real · Place: Tell el-Muqayyar (Ur), Dhi Qar Governorate, Iraq
Dates: -1862 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The biblical 'Ur of the Chaldees' (Hebrew 'Ur Kasdim, Genesis 11:28, 31) is identified with the excavated Sumerian city of Ur at Tell el-Muqayyar in southern Iraq — the identification proposed by Henry Rawlinson (1862) and popularized by Leonard Woolley's excavations (1922 onward).

Earliest attestation: Genesis 11:28, 31 (the phrase 'Ur Kasdim); the modern identification: Rawlinson 1862, then Woolley's excavation reports 1927-1936
Sources: Leonard Woolley, Abraham: Recent Discoveries and Hebrew Origins (1936) — review in Nature 137 (1936):764, noting the excavation produced 'no concrete evidence of the presence of Abraham at Ur' and that 'of the Chaldees' is an anachronism; Cyrus H. Gordon, 'Abraham and the Merchants of Ura,' JNES 17 (1958), 28-31 — the northern-Ur dissent; Gordon had worked with Woolley at Tell el-Muqayyar (1932); Gordon J. Wenham, Genesis 1-15, WBC (1987), 272 — 'of the Chaldaeans' as anachronistic late gloss (Kaldu not in Babylonia before c. 1000 BCE); C. J. Gadd concurs; Stephen O. Smoot, 'In the Land of the Chaldeans: The Search for Abraham's Homeland Revisited,' BYU Studies Quarterly 56.3 (2017) — surveys Woolley/Millard consensus and Gordon's northern dissent; AVAR 2023 (Rubin) — 'the attribution of Ur to the Chaldeans' is anachronistic; Chaldean tribes settled the southern marshlands only in the early first millennium BCE
Falsifiability: A contemporary (early 2nd millennium BCE) cuneiform text mentioning 'Ur Kasdim' would support the phrase's antiquity; one naming a different northern Ur as Terah's home would sink the southern identification</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1031,30.9625,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1862</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ur of the Chaldees = Tell el-Muqayyar (identification)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The identification is the scholarly consensus (mainstream Bible atlases follow Woolley) but rests on inference, not on any document or excavation linking the city to Abraham; the 'of the Chaldees' qualifier is near-certainly a late editorial gloss; the northern thesis has grown from Gordon's lone dissent into the field's majority-inclination. Grade held at CLAIMED — this pass strengthens the chain, not the grade.
Layer: real · Place: Tell el-Muqayyar (Ur), Dhi Qar Governorate, Iraq
Dates: -1862 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The biblical 'Ur of the Chaldees' (Hebrew 'Ur Kasdim, Genesis 11:28, 31) is identified with the excavated Sumerian city of Ur at Tell el-Muqayyar in southern Iraq — the identification proposed by Henry Rawlinson (1862) and popularized by Leonard Woolley's excavations (1922 onward).

Earliest attestation: Genesis 11:28, 31 (the phrase 'Ur Kasdim); the identification dispute: Eupolemus c. 150 BCE (Camarina/Ouria); LXX chōra; the modern identification: Rawlinson 1862, Woolley's excavation reports 1927-1936; the dissent: Gordon 1958
Sources: Leonard Woolley, Abraham: Recent Discoveries and Hebrew Origins (1936) — review in Nature 137 (1936):764, noting the excavation produced 'no concrete evidence of the presence of Abraham at Ur' and that 'of the Chaldees' is an anachronism; Cyrus H. Gordon, 'Abraham and the Merchants of Ura,' Journal of Near Eastern Studies 17 (Jan. 1958), pp. 28-31 (JSTOR stable 542500) — the northern-Ur dissent; Gordon had worked with Woolley at Tell el-Muqayyar (1932); two northern candidates: Ura (NE of Harran) and Urfa; International Standard Bible Encyclopedia, 'Ur of the Chaldees' — the two-millennium history of the dispute: LXX chōra ('land'); Eupolemus (c. 150 BCE) Camarina/Ouria; Talmud Erech; Ammianus Marcellinus desert castle between Hatra and Nisibis (Persian-era, ruled out); Urfa (Seleucus-founded, ruled out); Uru (Mar-tu) near Sippar; 'the land of Uri or Ura (Akkad)' region-reading; consensus Mugheir; Exploring Genesis: The Bible's Ancient Traditions in Context — Peter Machinist revises his HarperCollins Bible Dictionary 'largely rejected' framing of Gordon; Claus Westermann: 'many [scholars] took over Gordon's thesis; the majority incline to a northern Mesopotamian origin'; 1901 Jewish Encyclopedia, 'Chaldea' — the biblical Kasdim restricted to the Persian-Gulf head region until the 8th c. BCE; 'the only doubtful passages' are those speaking of 'Ur of the Chaldees'; VU Research Portal (Greek–West Semitic onomastics study, ch. 10) — Akkadian kaldu never denotes Babylon as a whole, only its southernmost parts; Greek Chaldaioi = 'Babylonia' betrays West-Semitic influence no earlier than the mid-5th century — the gloss-theory linguistics; Biblical Archaeology Review, January 2000, p. 16 (via secondary summary) — tablets at Ugarit, Nuzi, and Ebla mention a northern Ur/URA/Urau; Harper's Bible Dictionary, p. 373 (via secondary summary) — names of Abraham's kin (Peleg, Serug, Nahor, Terah) appear as city names in the Harran region; Stephen O. Smoot, 'In the Land of the Chaldeans: The Search for Abraham's Homeland Revisited,' BYU Studies Quarterly 56.3 (2017) — surveys Woolley/Millard consensus and Gordon's northern dissent; Gordon J. Wenham, Genesis 1-15, WBC (1987), 272 — 'of the Chaldaeans' as anachronistic late gloss (Kaldu not in Babylonia before c. 1000 BCE); C. J. Gadd concurs; AVAR 2023 (Rubin) — 'the attribution of Ur to the Chaldeans' is anachronistic; Chaldean tribes settled the southern marshlands only in the early first millennium BCE; Bülent Ortakçı, 'Exploring the Land of Abraham's Birth,' Biblical Archaeology Review Fall 2026 — Urfa excursion piece; notes 'many scholars consider Urfa to be a better candidate' for Ur Kasdim, citing Hershel Shanks, 'Abraham's Ur — Is the Pope Going to the Wrong Place?' BAR March/April 2000 (the northern thesis persisting in the 2026 literature); beliefnet.com, 'Ancient Mosaic Uncovered Near the Birthplace of Abraham' (Kozbe, Şanlıurfa castle excavation, 2025/26) — four medallion mosaics at Edessa/Urfa; 'some archaeologists believe the city of Ur in Iraq is a more likely candidate for the site of Abraham's origin' — the Urfa local tradition and the southern consensus stated side by side in one newsroom (grade-neutral news, carried as exhibit); uasvbible.org, 'From the Dawn of Civilization to Abraham' (Oct 2025) — the modern apologetic maximalist telling carried for correction: 'Archaeology identifies Ur with Tell el-Muqayyar beyond dispute' + a 2091 BCE covenant anchor; evidence-state record holds CLAIMED, not beyond dispute (see CORRECTIONS.md, 2026-10-16); FAIR Latter-day Saints wiki, 'Assessment of Claimed Anachronisms in the Book of Abraham' (revised 2026-03-12) — moved from 'the location of Ur of the Chaldees is not known' to 'Scholars have long debated… The issue ultimately remains unresolved. While many modern scholars place it in southern Iraq, Latter-day Saint scholars have given careful attention to a northern location in Syria or southern Turkey' — confessional/apologetic venue, not peer-reviewed; carried as a 2026 exhibit of the stalemate's continuing visibility, grade-neutral; Albert Dietz, 'Ceramic Traditions and Cultural Territories' (2026; in the 66th Rencontre Assyriologique Internationale volume, 2025 — open access via iraqarchaeology.org) — peer-reviewed study of Ur III/Old Babylonian pottery from Area 5 of the renewed Ur excavations (Stony Brook/LMU joint program, SBAH); documents continued scholarly production from the digs, nothing bearing on the Abraham identification — grade-neutral
Falsifiability: A contemporary (early 2nd millennium BCE) cuneiform text mentioning 'Ur Kasdim' would support the phrase's antiquity; one naming a northern Ur as Terah's home would sink the southern identification; a pre-Exilic Hebrew witness reading 'Ur Kasdim' as a northern place would settle the gloss question</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1031,30.9625,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1862</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Proto-Sinaitic — the first alphabet (c. 1800 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The inscriptions are real, multiply published, and dated by context; Gardiner's 1916 reading of lbʿlt ('to the Lady' = Hathor) is practically universally accepted; but the exact century, the language identification, and WHO held the chisel are all still argued between the leading scholars.
Layer: real · Place: Serabit el-Khadim, Sinai Peninsula (turquoise-mining region; Wadi el-Hol on the Theban desert road is the second anchor)
Dates: -1850 → -1550 (confidence: low)

The earliest alphabetic writing is the Proto-Sinaitic script, invented c. 19th-16th century BCE — roughly seven centuries before the Phoenician abjad — by Semitic-speaking workers in Egyptian service, working from hieroglyphic signs by the acrophonic principle (the 'Egyptian origin of the Semitic alphabet').

Earliest attestation: Serabit el-Khadim inscriptions, Sinai — discovered winter 1904-1905 by Flinders Petrie (published 1906); decipherment breakthrough by Alan Gardiner 1916 ('The Egyptian Origin of the Semitic Alphabet,' JEA 3); Wadi el-Hol inscriptions discovered 1993, published 1999 (Darnell et al.)
Sources: Gardiner, A.H. (1916), 'The Egyptian Origin of the Semitic Alphabet,' Journal of Egyptian Archaeology 3; Darnell, J.C., Dobbs-Allsopp, F.W., Lundberg, M.J., McCarter, P.K., Zuckerman, B. (2005), 'Two Early Alphabetic Inscriptions from the Wadi el-Hôl,' BASOR 340 — the 1999/2005 publication pushing the alphabet's origin to c. 1800-1900 BCE; Goldwasser, O. (2010), 'How the Alphabet Was Born from Hieroglyphs,' Biblical Archaeology Review 36(1) — the Semitic-miners inventor thesis; Rollston, C. — rollstonepigraphy.com: the Serabit corpus, Wadi el-Hol, and the honest-dating work (18th c. BCE argued); BBC News (15 Nov 1999), 'Oldest alphabet found in Egypt' — contemporary report of the Darnell publication
Falsifiability: A securely dated alphabetic inscription in an independent tradition that clearly predates the Wadi el-Hol finds, or positive proof that the Wadi el-Hol marks are non-phonetic decoration rather than writing</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.4667,29.0333,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1850</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Pythagorean' triples of Old Babylon — Plimpton 322 &amp; YBC 7289, c. 1800 BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifacts are multiply documented and their mathematical content (the triples, the √2 approximation) is uncontested; only the tablet's PURPOSE is debated (trig table vs teacher's problem list) — the triples themselves are not in dispute.
Layer: real · Place: Larsa (Tell as-Senkereh), southern Mesopotamia — attributed provenance
Dates: -1822 → -1762 (confidence: high)

Old Babylonian scribes (c. 1822-1762 BCE, Larsa, the age of Hammurabi) knew and used the relationship between the sides of right triangles: the Plimpton 322 tablet lists 15 rows of Pythagorean triples (integer sets satisfying a² + b² = c²), and the YBC 7289 school tablet computes the diagonal of a square as 1;24,51,10 in sexagesimal (~1.41421296, i.e. √2 to six sexagesimal places) — the 'diagonal rule' in working use roughly twelve centuries before Pythagoras of Samos was born.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves — contemporary Old Babylonian artifacts, c. 19th-18th century BCE
Sources: Plimpton 322 (Columbia University Rare Book &amp; Manuscript Library) — the artifact; YBC 7289 (Yale Babylonian Collection) — the √2 school tablet; Neugebauer &amp; Sachs, Mathematical Cuneiform Texts (1945) — first publication of Plimpton 322; Robson, 'Neither Sherlock Holmes nor Babylon: a reassessment of Plimpton 322', Historia Mathematica 28(3):167-206 (2001), doi:10.1006/hmat.2001.2317 — the pedagogy reading; the trig-table view 'cannot be correct' given the Old Babylonian concept of angle; Britton, Proust &amp; Shnider, 'Plimpton 322: a review and a different perspective', Archive for History of Exact Sciences 65(5):519-566 (2011)
Falsifiability: Proof the tablet is a modern forgery, or a redating outside the Old Babylonian period; or a demonstration that the number columns do not in fact satisfy the triple relationship</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1,31.28,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1822</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Weld-Blundell Prism (WB 444)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The object exists in the Ashmolean, its inscription is published and multiply photographed, and its internal dating (year 11 of Sin-magir) is read directly from the clay.
Layer: real · Place: Larsa (findspot; prism now in the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford)
Dates: -1820 → -1810 (confidence: medium)

The Weld-Blundell Prism is the only relatively undamaged, complete surviving witness of the Sumerian King List: a four-sided baked-clay prism (~20 x 9 cm), inscribed in Sumerian cuneiform in two columns per face, found at Larsa in 1922 and dated to year 11 of Sin-magir of Isin (c. 1800 BCE).

Earliest attestation: The prism itself, Old Babylonian period; published by Stephen Langdon (Oxford Editions of Cuneiform Texts, vol. II, 1923)
Sources: Ashmolean Museum AN1923.444 (object page); Weld-Blundell Prism summary (dimensions, Larsa 1922 find, AN1923.444); Sumerian King List overview (WB 444 as the only relatively undamaged complete manuscript; dated to year 11 of Sin-magir); ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1 (takes the WB 444 text as its main source)
Falsifiability: Epigraphic re-examination showing the year formula names a different king, or proof the prism is a modern forgery (its 1922 excavation context and 1923 publication argue against this)</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1131,31.2858,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1820</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The ukkinu: the Babylonian assembly that voted Marduk king</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The epic exists in multiple ancient copies; the assembly/decree vocabulary is documented Akkadian philology.
Layer: real · Place: Nineveh (copies); Babylon (composition)
Dates: -1800 → -1100 (confidence: medium)

In Enūma Eliš (the Babylonian Epic of Creation, Tablet II–III), the divine assembly is the mechanism of cosmic government: the gods are summoned by ministerial herald (Gaga/Kakka), 'make ready for a feast, at a banquet let them sit, let them eat bread, let them mix wine, that for Marduk, their avenger they may decree the fate.' Drunk at the banquet, the assembled gods invest Marduk — 'they granted him the right to exercise kingship over the gods' (Tablet IV) — and in Tablet VI the same assembly presides as a court: 'Let the great gods be here in Assembly,' Kingu is tried and executed for the uprising, and the fifty great gods take their seats to divide the stations of heaven and netherworld. The council decrees fates (šīmatu), names, and destinies; it is where divine kingship is voted, not born.

Earliest attestation: Enūma Eliš Tablets II–IV, VI; copies from Ashurbanipal's library at Nineveh (excavated 1849–1851; George Smith's 1876 publication)
Sources: Enūma Eliš, Tablets II–III (the feast), IV (the investiture), VI (the assembly court); W.G. Lambert's edition and translation; Copies from Ashurbanipal's library, Nineveh (7th c. BCE)
Falsifiability: Proof the Nineveh copies are modern forgeries, or that the 'assembly' lines are mistranslated ritual instructions</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.15,36.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lamashtu: four thousand years of fear</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The BELIEF and its material culture are multiply attested across two millennia. The grade covers the attestation, not the entity.
Layer: real · Place: Mesopotamia
Dates: -1800 → -600 (confidence: medium)

Mesopotamians believed in, performed rituals against, and made amulets warding off the demoness Lamashtu (Sumerian DIMME) — attested from Old Babylonian incantations through the Neo-Assyrian period, roughly 4,000 years of continuous fear.

Earliest attestation: Old Babylonian incantations (DIMME); canonical Lamashtu series; Louvre AO 22205 (Plaque des Enfers)
Sources: Farber, Lamaštu: An Edition of the Canonical Series (2014); Wiggermann, 'Lamaštu, Daughter of Anu' (2000); Amulets and plaques (British Museum, Louvre AO 22205)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the incantations and amulets are modern forgeries would overturn it</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4,33.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bar-Ron Proto-Sinaitic 'Moses' reading (2026)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — A real proposal by a named researcher about real Middle Bronze Age inscriptions — but Proto-Sinaitic is undeciphered (letter values, reading direction, word divisions insecure), the reading is rejected by the relevant specialist, and it has not survived peer review. The artifacts grade high; the reading grades CLAIMED. A vote is not evidence — and neither is a reading.
Layer: real · Place: Serabit el-Khadim, Sinai Peninsula, Egypt (turquoise mines)
Dates: -1800 → -1600 (confidence: inferred)

In 2026, researcher Michael S. Bar-Ron (Ariel University) proposed that two Proto-Sinaitic inscriptions (Sinai 357 and 361) from the turquoise mines at Serabit el-Khadim, Egypt, c. 1800-1600 BCE, read 'zot m'Moshe' ('This is from Moses') and 'ne'um Moshe' ('A saying of Moses') — presented as a possible extra-biblical attestation of the name Moses centuries before the Exodus narratives. The inscriptions are real Middle Bronze Age carvings; the Moses reading is one researcher's proposal, contested by specialists and not peer-reviewed. It does not constitute a verified contemporary attestation of the figure and does not overturn evt-moses-figure-attestation-001.

Earliest attestation: Bar-Ron's proposed readings, reported July 2026 (Archaeology Magazine) and October 2026 (Fox News Digital); the inscriptions themselves discovered in the early 1900s (Petrie)
Sources: Archaeology Magazine, July 2026 — report of Bar-Ron's proposed 'zot m'Moshe' reading via high-resolution photography and 3D scanning of 20+ proto-Sinaitic inscriptions (as quoted in cogwriter.com, Aug 2025); attractions.net.au, Oct 2026 — Bar-Ron's Fox News Digital interview: 'zot m'Moshe' / 'ne'um Moshe', inscriptions reference El and curse the Ba'alat cult ('BŠ' — 'for shame'; 'nimosh' — 'let us leave'); gallery.wellbeingwhisper.net, April 2026 — balanced report: van der Veen's reported support ('You're absolutely correct, I read this too') vs Schneider's rejection ('completely unproven and misleading'); the reading not yet peer-reviewed; Thomas Schneider (UBC), Egyptologist — 'The claims are entirely unproven and misleading'; 'arbitrary identifications of letters can distort ancient history' (as quoted in the April 2026 coverage); exploringbible.com, 13 Sept 2026 — analysis of the earlier Sinai 361 'Moses' claim (Petrovich 2016): the methodological caution against reading a proper name into an undeciphered script, applicable to the 2026 proposal as well
Falsifiability: Independent peer-reviewed confirmation of the specific sign identifications, or any future decipherment of Proto-Sinaitic that reproduces the reading, would raise the grade; a demonstrated misreading by an independent epigrapher would lower it</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.45,29.03,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hammurabi of Babylon</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources — year names, inscriptions, and diplomatic correspondence — confirm his reign and deeds.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon
Dates: -1792 → -1750 (confidence: high)

Hammurabi, sixth king of Babylon's First Dynasty, reigned c. 1792-1750 BCE (middle chronology), unifying southern Mesopotamia under Babylonian rule; his reign is documented by year names, royal inscriptions, and an extensive archive of letters.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary year names, royal inscriptions, and the Mari archive letters (early 18th century BCE)
Sources: Hammurabi year-name lists (contemporary administrative records); Hammurabi's letters (e.g., the Mari archive correspondence); Royal inscriptions of Hammurabi; Manning et al. 2016, 'Integrated Tree-Ring-Radiocarbon High-Resolution Timeframe to Resolve Earlier Second Millennium BCE Mesopotamian Chronology' (PLOS ONE 11(7):e0157144) — independent dendro-radiocarbon support for the middle chronology
Falsifiability: A contemporary source placing a different king on Babylon's throne for those years, or proof the Mari letters are misattributed</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1792</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The middle chronology for Hammurabi and Old Babylon</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent physical evidence (dendro-14C, Manning et al. 2016) converges on the same chronology the textual relative-chronology had long made the mainstream default; the rival high/low/ultra-low schemes are excluded by that anchor — though the ultra-low camp's eclipse-based case remains a stated minority position.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon, Iraq
Dates: -1792 → -1750 (confidence: medium)

The middle chronology is the correct absolute chronology for the Old Babylonian period: Hammurabi of Babylon reigned 1792–1750 BCE, the fall of Babylon to Mursili I dates to 1595 BCE, and the fall of Ur to 2004 BCE — against the rival high (Hammurabi 1848–1806), low (1728–1685), and ultra-low/new (1696–1654) chronologies.

Earliest attestation: Relative chronology from Old Babylonian year-name lists and king lists; absolute anchor via dendro-14C in Manning et al. 2016 (PLOS ONE)
Sources: Manning, Griggs, Lorentzen, Barjamovic, Bronk Ramsey, Kromer &amp; Wild 2016, 'Integrated Tree-Ring-Radiocarbon High-Resolution Timeframe to Resolve Earlier Second Millennium BCE Mesopotamian Chronology', PLOS ONE — tree-ring sequences integrated with radiocarbon from Kültepe (Waršama Palace) and Acemhöyük (Sarıkaya Palace), tied to Old Assyrian tablets naming Shamshi-Adad I; found 'compatible only with the so-called Middle Chronology'; Old Babylonian year-name lists and king lists (internal relative chronology; Hammurabi year 18 = death of Shamshi-Adad I — the tie between Anatolian timber contexts and Babylonian reigns); Venus Tablet of Ammisaduqa (Enuma Anu Enlil Tablet 63) — genuine transmitted observational record whose 8/56/64-year cycle degeneracy leaves multiple chronologies selectable; see evt-venus-tablet-002; Gurzadyan 1998, 'Dating the Fall of Babylon' (Univ. of Ghent / Oriental Institute Chicago) — the ultra-low case (fall of Babylon 1499 BCE): pottery evidence, rejection of the extrapolated 56/64-year Venus cycles (8-year cycle only), lunar-eclipse identifications; Gurzadyan 2020, 'The Double Eclipse at the Downfall of Old Babylon' — lunar-eclipse record analysis of the Old Babylonian downfall; Wikipedia, 'Chronology of the ancient Near East' (page updated October 2026 — cross-checked 2026-10-17): middle chronology still 'the most commonly seen solution' with 'far from a consensus' stated; the October 2026 text is consistent with the held record state — no 2026 development on any chronology leg; The Old Assyrian merchant archive at Kaneš (Kültepe): ~23,000 tablets (letters, debt notes, caravan accounts, marriage contracts) plus the Revised Eponym List (Veenhof) — the 125-year limmu (year-eponym) sequence from Ērišum I. The documentary half of the Manning 2016 anchor: the Waršama Palace timbers grew where the merchants lived, and tablets naming Assyrian officials tie the tree-ring sequence to the year-name lists that tie to Shamshi-Adad I (died Hammurabi year 18). The REL's sequence is independently reconstructible across the Karum levels (Kt 92/k 94–96 eponym groups) and converges with the Kültepe building levels — the year-names merchants wrote to keep loans due are the dates the chronology stands on. (Grade-neutral source, appended 2026-10-18 via #512 — Turin-shroud precedent, no grade change.); Eclipses, Volcanoes, and Eponyms: New Perspectives on the Chronology of the Early Second Millennium BC (Orbis Biblicus et Orientalis, Series Archaeologica 43, 2025, open access) — conference-volume position favoring the Lower Middle Chronology (Hammurabi accession 1784 BCE), an 8-year position inside the record's stated ~8-year residual window; grade-neutral (venue = proceedings, not a peer-reviewed journal anchor), Turin-shroud precedent
Falsifiability: A securely dated 14C/dendro series from a context tied by sealed year-names to the Old Babylonian kings that lands &gt;30 years off the middle-chronology anchor, or a verified total solar/lunar eclipse matching a low-chronology year-name formula, would falsify the middle chronology.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4211,32.5464,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1792</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Code of Hammurabi</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The inscribed stele physically exists and is contemporary with Hammurabi; its text is complete and legible.
Layer: real · Place: Susa (find spot; originally erected at Babylon or Sippar)
Dates: -1754 → -1750 (confidence: medium)

Hammurabi promulgated a law code of 282 laws, inscribed on a 2.25-meter dark-igneous stele (traditionally called 'diorite' since Scheil 1902; the Louvre identifies the material as basalt — the lithology is an unresolved scientific question per the peer-reviewed 2026 Comment, Liu &amp; Xu, npj Heritage Science 14:141), covering commerce, family, labor, and criminal justice — one of the oldest and most complete legal codes known.

Earliest attestation: The diorite stele itself (Louvre, Sb 8), inscribed in Hammurabi's reign
Sources: Code of Hammurabi stele (Louvre Sb 8, excavated at Susa 1901-1902); Laws of Ur-Nammu and Lipit-Ishtar (earlier codes, comparison); Liu &amp; Xu 2026, npj Heritage Science 14:141 (doi:10.1038/s40494-026-02411-z) — Comment reframing the century-old diorite-vs-basalt controversy over the Louvre stele's material as a testable hypothesis; proposes a noninvasive pXRF/SWIR/Raman protocol; leans basalt on philological-historical review but leaves the question explicitly unresolved pending direct analysis (received 16 Dec 2025, accepted 26 Feb 2026). Grade-neutral: the material question does not touch the code's authenticity or date.
Falsifiability: Proof the stele is a later copy or forgery, or that its legal provisions postdate Hammurabi's reign</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>48.5164,32.1892,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1754</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tell el-Koua Second Intermediate Period settlement (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced by the Egyptian ministry and reported by the press; the facts are the site and its dating, both documented. Graded on the discovery, with the Exodus framing explicitly fenced as press context, not evidence — the site proves a town existed in the corridor, never that anyone left through it.
Layer: real · Place: Tell el-Koua, Wadi Tumilat, eastern Delta, Egypt
Dates: -1650 → -1550 (confidence: inferred)

In June 2026, Egypt's Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announced the discovery of a Second Intermediate Period settlement at the Tell el-Koua site in the Wadi Tumilat region — the ancient corridor linking the eastern Nile Delta with Egypt's eastern frontier. The site includes 15th Dynasty (Hyksos-period) tombs and evidence of occupation continuing into the 18th Dynasty, spanning the transition from Hyksos rule to the New Kingdom — the era that has long figured in scholarly debates over the historical setting of the Exodus. This is context for the Hyksos-expulsion hypothesis, never evidence of the Exodus itself.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, late June 2026
Sources: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, late June 2026 — Tell el-Koua settlement: 15th Dynasty tombs, occupation into the 18th Dynasty; 10 mudbrick tombs, homes, ovens, grain silos, walled neighborhood; scarab amulets, bronze tools, pottery, alabaster cosmetic containers, human and animal remains; 98-by-197-ft residential complex with halls, kilns, silos; first crouched burials outside the mudbrick tombs at the site; NY Post, 27 July 2026 — 'Ancient Egyptian settlement discovered from era that fueled Exodus debate' (the press framing fenced as context: the Hyksos period 'may provide historical context' for the biblical account, per scholars)
Falsifiability: Full excavation report altering the dating or character of the site would revise the record; the discovery itself is ministry-announced and press-reported</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.95,30.65,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1650</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Theran (Santorini) eruption</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The eruption is multiply attested (volcanic deposits, buried town, ice cores, tsunamites); the exact year is still contested, so VERIFIED is parked.
Layer: real · Place: Santorini (Thera), Aegean Sea
Dates: -1650 → -1500 (confidence: medium)

That the island of Thera in the Aegean erupted catastrophically in the Late Bronze Age — an eruption ~60x Mount St. Helens that buried the town of Akrotiri, collapsed the volcanic center into a caldera, and sent tsunamis toward Minoan Crete ~110 km south.

Earliest attestation: Tephra and pumice layers across the eastern Mediterranean; Akrotiri buried in life position; Greenland ice-core sulfate spike; tsunamites on Cretan coasts
Sources: Friedrich et al. 2006, Science 312:548 — olive branch buried alive in pumice, wiggle-matched to 1627–1600 BCE (95.4%); Pearson et al. 2018, Science Advances — IntCal recalibration widening the window to 1600–1525 BCE; Marinatos 1939 — hypothesis linking the eruption's memory to the Atlantis legend (hypothesis, not verification)
Falsifiability: Proof that the tephra layers are from another volcano, or that the buried Akrotiri dates to a different millennium.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>25.459,36.405,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1650</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Venus Tablet of Ammisaduqa</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact is multiply attested — 20 copies in 6 groups — but the astronomical content is a heavily copied tradition: copying corruptions are probable and the entries for years 13 and 21 are not physically possible, so the observations are CLAIMED as transmitted, the tablet's existence is CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (copies); principal copy recovered at Nineveh
Dates: -1646 → -1626 (confidence: medium)

Twenty cuneiform copies in six groups (oldest copied at Babylon c. 720-704 BCE under Sargon II; main copy K.160 recovered from Ashurbanipal's library at Nineveh) preserve 21 years of Venus's heliacal risings and settings — first and last visibility before sunrise and after sunset — originally observed during the reign of the Babylonian king Ammisaduqa, fourth king after Hammurabi. It is the principal relative anchor for Old Babylonian absolute chronology.

Earliest attestation: Observations attributed to the reign of Ammisaduqa (mid-17th century BCE, middle chronology); surviving copies are 7th-century BCE. Earliest published: Rawlinson &amp; Smith, 1870, as Enuma Anu Enlil Tablet 63.
Sources: British Museum tablet K.160 (the 'Venus tablet K.160'), recovered from the library at Nineveh; Reiner, E. &amp; Pingree, D. (1975), Babylonian Planetary Omens, Part 1: The Venus Tablet of Ammisaduqa (Bibliotheca Mesopotamica) — the fundamental modern edition; Langdon, S., Fotheringham, J.K. &amp; Schoch, C. (1928), The Venus Tablets of Ammizaduga (Oxford); Gurzadyan, V.G. (2003), 'The Venus Tablet and refraction' (Akkadica 124) — atmospheric refraction corrections; Gurzadyan (2000) argues the 56/64-year Venus cycle cannot be traced in the tablet; de Jong, T. &amp; Foertmeyer, V., 'The Venus observations of Ammisaduqa reanalysed' (JEOL) — physical-model reanalysis; ~2 years of enhanced atmospheric extinction, possibly the Thera eruption signature (unconfirmed); Weir, J.D. (1982), 'The Venus Tablets — a Fresh Approach' (Journal for the History of Astronomy 13); Weir (1998), 'The Pattern of Venus Tablet Solutions' — tabular overview of the candidate solutions
Falsifiability: Proof the tablets are modern forgeries (no such evidence exists), or demonstration that all 20 copies descend from one corrupt late source with no genuine observational original behind them</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1646</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Venus Tablet of Ammisaduqa</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact is multiply attested — 20 copies in 6 groups — but the astronomical content is a heavily copied tradition: copying corruptions are probable and the entries for years 13 and 21 are not physically possible, so the observations are CLAIMED as transmitted, the tablet's existence is CORROBORATED. The transmission claim itself (Rank VII.1) is a vote, not evidence: the panel's earlier 2/2 TRUE is recorded in MASTER-LIST.md as a ranking, not as attestation. Grade held at CLAIMED for the transmitted observations; the artifact stays CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (copies); principal copy recovered at Nineveh
Dates: -1646 → -1626 (confidence: medium)

Twenty cuneiform copies in six groups (oldest copied at Babylon c. 720-704 BCE under Sargon II; main copy K.160 recovered from Ashurbanipal's library at Nineveh) preserve 21 years of Venus's heliacal risings and settings — first and last visibility before sunrise and after sunset — originally observed during the reign of the Babylonian king Ammisaduqa, fourth king after Hammurabi. It is the principal relative anchor for Old Babylonian absolute chronology.

Earliest attestation: Observations attributed to the reign of Ammisaduqa (mid-17th century BCE, middle chronology); surviving copies are 7th-century BCE. Earliest published: Rawlinson &amp; Smith, 1870, as Enuma Anu Enlil Tablet 63.
Sources: British Museum tablet K.160 (the 'Venus tablet K.160'), recovered from the library at Nineveh; Reiner, E. &amp; Pingree, D. (1975), Babylonian Planetary Omens, Part 1: The Venus Tablet of Ammisaduqa (Bibliotheca Mesopotamica) — the fundamental modern edition; Langdon, S., Fotheringham, J.K. &amp; Schoch, C. (1928), The Venus Tablets of Ammizaduga (Oxford); Gurzadyan, V.G. (2003), 'The Venus Tablet and refraction' (Akkadica 124) — atmospheric refraction corrections; Gurzadyan (2000) argues the 56/64-year Venus cycle cannot be traced in the tablet; de Jong, T. &amp; Foertmeyer, V., 'The Venus observations of Ammisaduqa reanalysed' (JEOL) — physical-model reanalysis; ~2 years of enhanced atmospheric extinction, possibly the Thera eruption signature (unconfirmed); Weir, J.D. (1982), 'The Venus Tablets — a Fresh Approach' (Journal for the History of Astronomy 13); Weir (1998), 'The Pattern of Venus Tablet Solutions' — tabular overview of the candidate solutions; Manning, S.W. et al. (2016), 'Integrated tree-ring-radiocarbon high-resolution timeframe to resolve earlier second millennium BCE Mesopotamian chronology' (PLoS ONE) — dendro-14C anchoring compatible with the middle chronology derived from the Venus Tablet; independent of the Venus data; Manning, S.W. et al. (2017), 'A new integrated archaeological science-based chronology for the Second Millennium BCE' — narrows the Middle Bronze Age chronology; converges with the tablet-derived middle chronology
Falsifiability: Proof the tablets are modern forgeries (no such evidence exists), or demonstration that all 20 copies descend from one corrupt late source with no genuine observational original behind them</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1646</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Fall of Babylon to Mursili I (1595 BCE, middle chronology)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The raid is independently attested in two traditions (Babylonian chronicles and Hittite royal inscriptions) and the aftermath — a real settlement hiatus in Babylon's own archives — is archaeologically visible. The absolute year is an inference from the chronology scheme, not a contemporary statement, so the event sits below VERIFIED: the raid is attested, the 1595 date is our reckoning.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon, Iraq
Dates: -1595 → -1595 (confidence: medium)

The First Dynasty of Babylon ended when the Hittite king Mursili I marched down the Euphrates, sacked Babylon, and carried off the cult statue of Marduk from the Esagila; the last Amorite king, Samsu-Ditana, fell. In the middle chronology the sack dates to 1595 BCE; the date is one of the absolute anchors of Old Babylonian chronology (1651 / 1595 / 1531 / 1499 BCE across the four schemes).

Earliest attestation: Babylonian chronicle tradition (the Chronicle of Early Kings: Mursili's raid and the removal of the Marduk statue); the Hittite Edict of Telepinu (Mursili I's reign and its end); the Merkes/Babylon text gap marking the settlement hiatus after the fall
Sources: Chronicle of Early Kings (Babylonian chronicle): Mursili's raid on Babylon, the taking of the Marduk statue, the Kassite succession under Agum II; Edict of Telepinu (Hittite): Mursili I's reign, the campaign against Babylon, his assassination on his return; Babylonian King List A: the First Dynasty ends with Samsu-Ditana; the Kassite dynasty begins (Gandaš); Academia.edu / 'Babylon in the Second Millennium BCE: New Insights on the Transitions from Old Babylonian to Kassite and Isin II Periods' (~2025): the Merkes-area text gap marking the transition — a settlement hiatus coinciding with the suggested Fall of Babylon (1595 MC), followed by resettlement under the early Kassite kings (Pedersén 2005 Archive und Bibliotheken von Babylon text census); Manning, Griggs, Lorentzen, Barjamovic, Bronk Ramsey, Kromer &amp; Wild 2016, PLOS ONE — the dendro-14C anchor that selects the middle-chronology 1595 over 1531/1499
Falsifiability: A contemporary dated source placing Samsu-Ditana or a successor on Babylon's throne in the years immediately after 1595 BCE; or a securely dated cuneiform record of the Marduk statue's return that predates Agum II's reign</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1595</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kassite Dynasty of Babylon (c. 1595–1155 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge: the Kassite king list (Babylonian King List A), ~hundreds of surviving kudurru stones with royal names, the Amarna correspondence (external witness), Chronicle P, and the terminal Elamite conquest attested on both sides. The 'dark age' telling is the only casualty — the dynasty is one of the best-documented long reigns in the ancient Near East, yet it has no dedicated scholarly synthesis in English. Below VERIFIED: the early-dynasty decades (Gandaš–Agum II, the Sealand overlap) are thinly documented.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon, Iraq
Dates: -1595 → -1155 (confidence: medium)

The Kassite dynasty ruled Babylon from c. 1595 to 1155 BCE — roughly 440 years, the longest dynasty in Mesopotamian history: a stable, literate great-power era (the Amarna age: Kadashman-Enlil I and Burna-Buriaš II corresponding with Egypt's Amenhotep III), not a dark age. Kassite kings kept Akkadian as the language of administration and cuneiform as its script, copied Hammurabi's Code for six centuries, and left the kudurru boundary stones as their own documentary record.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian King List A; the kudurru boundary stones (oldest major witness: the Louvre Meli-Shipak kudurru granting land to Marduk-apla-iddina I); the Amarna letters (EA 1–11: Kadashman-Enlil I, Burna-Buriaš II); Chronicle P (Kassite–Assyrian conflicts)
Sources: Babylonian King List A: the Kassite dynasty — 36 kings, 576 traditional years; Kudurru boundary stones (e.g., the Meli-Shipak kudurru, Louvre AO 6684; the corpus catalogued in Brinkman 1976, 'Materials and Studies for Kassite History') — the dynasty's own legal-visual documentary record; Amarna letters EA 1–11: Kassite kings Kadashman-Enlil I and Burna-Buriaš II in correspondence with Amenhotep III/Akhenaten — external attestation of the Kassite great-power status; Chronicle P: the Kassite–Middle Assyrian conflicts; the Assyrian interregnum inside the Kassite period; The 1155 BCE Elamite overthrow: Shutruk-Nahhunte's own inscriptions on the trophies carried to Susa (including Hammurabi's Code stele, Louvre Sb 8) — the end of the dynasty attested by its conqueror; Wikipedia 'Kassite dynasty' consensus page (status Oct 2026): 1595–1155 BCE brackets, Zagros origin, Sealand parallel, assimilation of the later kings
Falsifiability: A contemporary source showing Babylon under non-Kassite rule for a substantial part of the 15th–12th centuries BCE (the Assyrian interregnum is already accounted for); or demonstration that the kudurru corpus is a late antiquarian fabrication (no such evidence exists)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1595</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Temple menorah burned olive oil</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested within the Hebrew textual tradition only — Exodus 27:20 ('pure oil olive beaten for the light'), Leviticus 24:2, and Josephus's eyewitness-adjacent account — with the Arch of Titus relief confirming the seven-branched form externally; no surviving Temple lampstand exists to inspect, and no contemporary inscription names the fuel.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (the Temple)
Dates: -1500 → 70 (confidence: traditional)

The seven-branched menorah of the Temple in Jerusalem burned pure beaten olive oil — seven oil lamps on a gold lampstand — not wax candles.

Earliest attestation: Exodus 25:31-37 (seven lamps on the gold lampstand) and Exodus 27:20 (pure beaten olive oil for the light); Leviticus 24:2; Josephus, Antiquities 3.7.7 (three of the seven lights burned by day)
Sources: Exodus 25:31-37; 27:20-21 — the gold lampstand with seven lamps; 'pure oil of pressed olives for the light, so that the lamps may be kept burning'; Leviticus 24:2 — the command to bring pure beaten olive oil for the lamps; Josephus, Antiquities 3.7.7 — three of the seven lights burned in the daytime (reported via Bible commentaries citing the text); The Arch of Titus, Rome (c. 81 CE) — the triumphal relief showing the seven-branched menorah carried as spoil from the 70 CE sack; contemporary external depiction of the form
Falsifiability: A surviving Temple lampstand with wax-candle fixtures, or a contemporary inscription describing wax as the Temple fuel, would overturn the reading</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Crossing of the Yam Suf (Exodus 14)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested across multiple tradition-internal witnesses, but with no independent contemporary attestation and no archaeological footprint; even the water's identity (a sea vs reed marshes) is disputed within scholarship.
Layer: real · Place: Eastern Nile Delta candidate zone — Ballah Lakes / Lake Timsah / Lake of Tanis (disputed; no agreed pin)
Dates: -1450 → -1200 (confidence: traditional)

In Exodus 14, Moses stretches out his hand over the yam suf and the sea parts — the waters standing as walls on the right hand and on the left while Israel crosses on dry ground, and the returning water drowns Pharaoh's army.

Earliest attestation: Exodus 14 (composed centuries after the events it describes, per scholarly consensus). Tradition-internal echoes: Psalms 106:9, 114:3; Wisdom 19:7-8; Josephus, Antiquities 2.16.
Sources: Exodus 14 (the narrative; yam suf rendered 'Red Sea' in the Septuagint's Greek thalassa erythra); Psalms 106:9, 114:3; Wisdom 19:7-8 (tradition-internal echoes); Josephus, Antiquities 2.16 (1st-c. CE tradition-internal account); Merneptah Stele c. 1207 BCE (attests 'Israel' in Canaan; says nothing about a crossing — the silence is on the record); Hoffmeier, 'The Location of the Re(e)d Sea' (Ancient Israel in Sinai, 2005): yam suf = eastern-Delta reed lakes (Ballah Lakes / pa-tjufy), not the Gulf of Suez; Drews &amp; Han (2010), PLoS ONE 5(8): e12481 — models the mechanism only, never the event
Falsifiability: A contemporary Egyptian or Canaanite record of the crossing — or of an army lost in the water — would raise the grade. The grade stays CLAIMED until any independent contemporary evidence appears either way.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.35,30.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1450</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Exodus</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — A central tradition of the Hebrew Bible, but with no contemporary extra-biblical attestation and no archaeological trace of a mass migration as described. The Merneptah Stele (c. 1207 BCE) proves 'Israel' existed as a people in Canaan by then — a terminus ante quem, not a confirmation.
Layer: real · Place: Sinai / Nile Delta (route traditionally placed here; unattested)
Dates: -1446 → -1200 (confidence: traditional)

The biblical tradition holds that the Israelites, enslaved in Egypt, departed under Moses, crossed the sea, and wandered the wilderness before entering Canaan.

Earliest attestation: The Exodus narratives (Exodus-Deuteronomy); no contemporary Egyptian source mentions the event
Sources: Exodus-Deuteronomy narratives; Merneptah stele (terminus ante quem for 'Israel' in Canaan, c. 1207 BCE; people-determinative); Santa Clara University timeline: Exodus c. 1280 BCE paired with composition dates; Finkelstein &amp; Silberman, The Bible Unearthed (2001): narrative reached final form in the 26th Dynasty (late 7th-early 6th c. BCE); Hoffmeier (maximalist): concedes no direct archaeological evidence for the Exodus as described; Egyptian royal-propaganda caveat: monuments routinely omit defeats (e.g., Kadesh spun as triumph)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Egyptian inscription describing the departure would raise the grade; the current silence is ambiguous (Egyptian monuments omit defeats), so silence alone cannot lower it further</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.0,30.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1446</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baal's return: death at Mot's hands, the rains coming back</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tablets are securely dated pre-Christian by centuries; the death-and-return narrative is read from the texts themselves, with the storm-god/rain logic intrinsic to the narrative. Mettinger's rehabilitation counts Baal among the genuine pre-Christian cases.
Layer: real · Place: Ugarit (Ras Shamra, Syria)
Dates: -1400 → -1200 (confidence: high)

The Ugaritic Baal Cycle (KTU 1.1–1.6, c. 1400–1200 BCE) attests the storm-god Baal's death at the hands of Mot (Death) and his return — with Anat's slaughter of Mot and the restoration of the rains — a pre-Christian dying-and-rising narrative in which the god's death is the dry season and his return the weather.

Earliest attestation: The Baal Cycle, KTU 1.1–1.6 (Ugarit, Ras Shamra)
Sources: The Baal Cycle (KTU 1.1–1.6), Ugarit, c. 1400–1200 BCE — Baal's death at Mot's hands, Anat's vengeance, the return of the rains; Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — Baal as a genuine pre-Christian dying-and-rising case; Day, John — on Baal's death-and-resurrection behind Hosea 5:12–6:3 (the imagery's afterlife in the Hebrew Bible)
Falsifiability: A redating of the Ugarit tablets to post-Christian times, or a demonstration that no tablet describes Baal's death and return, would overturn the attestation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.7822,35.6028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>El's assembly at Ugarit: the council of the sons of El</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tablets exist, are multiply attested, and the assembly vocabulary (mphrt bn il) is documented philology.
Layer: real · Place: Ugarit (Ras Shamra), northern Levant coast
Dates: -1400 → -1100 (confidence: medium)

At Late Bronze Age Ugarit (14th–12th c. BCE), the divine assembly is a documented institution: El presides over the mphrt bn il ('the assembly of the sons of El') — the seventy sons of El (cf. Deut 32:8's 'seventy'), deliberating kingship, war, and fate. The Baal Cycle's council scenes (KTU 1.2 i, iii–iv) show the assembly in session: El presiding from his tent at the source of the cosmic rivers, Yam's messengers demanding Baal's surrender before the seated gods, Athirat interceding with El for Baal's palace, the vote-style deliberation by which divine authority moves. The assembly is not metaphor; it is the pantheon's government.

Earliest attestation: KTU (Die keilalphabetischen Texte aus Ugarit) 1.2 i–iv; 1.40 (list of the seventy); excavated 1929 onwards
Sources: KTU³ (Dietrich/Loretz/Sanmartín), esp. KTU 1.2 i–iv; KTU 1.40; Charles Virolleaud, critical edition of the Baal Cycle (1938); Lowell K. Handy, Among the Host of Heaven (1994); Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford, 2001); E. Theodore Mullen Jr., The Divine Council in Canaanite and Early Hebrew Literature
Falsifiability: Proof the tablets are modern forgeries or the 'assembly' passages are mistranslations of mundane ritual language</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.78,35.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The name 'Moses' as Egyptian (contested)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The Egyptian derivation is the long-standing majority position (Lepsius 19th c. to Hoffmeier 2016's 'widespread agreement'), but it is contested within peer-reviewed scholarship: Kitchen (2003) and Hoffmeier (2016) themselves expressed doubts; Redford (2009) argues the Hebrew shin-for-Egyptian-s rendering cannot be 2nd-millennium phoneticism (Ex 1:11 renders the s in 'Ramses' as Samek); Schneider's reassessment concludes the name is 'not likely of Egyptian origin' — most probably a NW Semitic name of uncertain etymology, with Mosheh consistent with an Iron Age I-or-later form. The folk-etymology status of Exod 2:10's explanation is broadly shared; the origin question itself is not settled.
Layer: real · Place: Egypt/Levant onomastics (the name, not the person)
Dates: -1400 → 2026 (confidence: low)

The personal name 'Moses' (Hebrew Mosheh) is of Egyptian origin — the standard scholarly reading deriving it from Egyptian msi/ms ('child, born of, son'), the common element of New Kingdom theophoric names (Thutmose, Ramose, Ahmose, Ptahmose, Ramesses). The Hebrew etymology given in Exodus 2:10 ('because I drew him out of the water', from mashah 'drew out') is a folk etymology — Hebrew wordplay on an Egyptian-sounding name, not the name's origin.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian theophoric -mose names throughout the New Kingdom (c. 1550-1070 BCE); the Hebrew form in the biblical text; the etymological question enters scholarship with Lepsius (19th c.)
Sources: James Hoffmeier (2016), cited for 'widespread agreement that at the root of the name of the great Hebrew leader is the Egyptian word msi, which was a very common element in theophoric names throughout the New Kingdom (e.g., Amenmose, Thutmose, Ahmose, Ptahmose, Ramose, Ramesses)' and derives from the Egyptian verb 'to give birth'; Louis Feldman (2004): the name 'is almost certainly Egyptian, from the root msy, meaning "to be born", as in Tuthmosis ("Tuth is born") and Rameses ("Re is born")'; Ernest Klein, A Comprehensive Etymological Dictionary of the English Language (via etymonline): 'Most scholars see in it the Hebraization of Egyptian mes, mesu "child, son," which is often used in theophorous names'; Ex. 2:10 read as Hebrew sound-wordplay on mashah 'he drew out'; Thomas Schneider, 'Moses the Egyptian? A Reassessment of the Etymology of the Name "Moses"' (peer-reviewed reassessment): concludes the name is 'not likely of Egyptian origin' — most probably a NW Semitic personal name of uncertain etymology; Mosheh consistent with an Iron Age I (or later) form via the Canaanite a&gt;o shift; 'Moses the Egyptian would indeed be a later construct'; Kitchen (2003) and Hoffmeier (2016) cited for expressed doubts; Redford (2009: 175) cited for the sibilant objection; Exodus 2:10 (the folk etymology: 'she called his name Moses, and said, Because I drew him out of the water'); National Geographic 2025-08-07 (Serabit el-Khadim coverage): Joshua Huddlestun cited — 'Mose' appears as a personal name in New Kingdom letters and legal documents (a land-inheritance plaintiff named Mose); Liane Feldman (Princeton): the name may well be Egyptian and is less distinctive than it appears. Grade-neutral: supports the Egyptian-name plausibility already in the record while weakening any distinctive-identification reading of the name.
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-Exodus Egyptian attestation of 'Mose' standing alone as a complete personal name in a non-theophoric context would strengthen the Egyptian reading; a NW Semitic onomastic parallel with the same consonantal form and matching sibilant would strengthen Schneider's.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.0,31.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Samuha (Kayalıpınar) royal archive announcement</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced by a state ministry and reported by multiple independent outlets with consistent figures; fenced as an announced find — the objects' contents and full dating await the peer-reviewed edition, so this is the announcement, not the archive's content.
Layer: real · Place: Kayalıpınar (ancient Samuha), Türkiye
Dates: -1400 → -1300 (confidence: medium)

Türkiye's Ministry of Culture and Tourism announced on 8 October 2026 that archaeologists recovered 131 cuneiform tablets and fragments at Kayalıpınar (ancient Samuha) in the 2026 season, bringing the reported royal archive total to 184 tablets and fragments with 53 found in 2025; the texts are attributed to King Tudhaliya II's reign and include Hittite and Hurrian, covering military, administrative, ritual, and royal-household affairs; excavators call it the most extensive Hittite royal archive found in the 21st century and one of the largest concentrations of Hittite documents outside Hattusa; translation and excavation are ongoing.

Earliest attestation: Türkiye Ministry of Culture and Tourism announcement, 8 October 2026 (press; peer-reviewed edition pending)
Sources: Heritage Daily, 8 Oct 2026: '184 cuneiform tablets discovered in Hittite royal archive'; The Debrief, 13 Oct 2026: 'Archaeologists Uncover a Hittite Royal Archive of 184 Cuneiform Tablets'
Falsifiability: A retraction or correction by the excavation team or the ministry revising the tablet count or attribution; the peer-reviewed edition contradicting the announcement</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.53,39.58,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Shasu of Yhw in the Egyptian lists</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent Egyptian temple lists, ~150 years apart, both carry the yhw entry among the Shasu lists; the reading is long-established in the scholarly literature (Leclant 1960, Goedicke 1965). Graded as belief-phenomenon attestation per the attestation rule: it proves Egypt recorded the name in the southern wilderness, never that a deity did anything. It says nothing about the Exodus itself.
Layer: real · Place: Soleb, Nubia (Sudan) — temple of Amenhotep III; also Amarah West, Nubia — temple of Ramesses II
Dates: -1400 → -1250 (confidence: inferred)

Two New Kingdom Egyptian temple topographical lists contain 't3 sh3sw yhw3' — 'the land of the Shasu of Yhw' — the oldest extra-biblical occurrence of the divine name YHWH in human records. The Soleb list (temple of Amenhotep III, Nubia, c. 1400 BCE) carries the name among the Shasu lists; the Amarah West list (Ramesses II, c. 1300 BCE) repeats the 'Shasu of Yhw' entry and separately names 'the land of the Shasu of Seir' (t3 sh3sw s3rr). Egypt knew a group called the Shasu attached to the name Yhw, located in the Seir/Edom/Midian region — the biblical tradition's own homeland of YHWH (cf. Deut 33:2, Judg 5:4, Hab 3:3, 1 Kgs 19). This grades the NAME's antiquity and geography, never the Exodus.

Earliest attestation: The Soleb topographical column (Amenhotep III, c. 1400 BCE); the reading published by Leclant 1960 and refined by Goedicke 1965
Sources: Soleb topographical list (Amenhotep III, c. 1400 BCE) — column naming 't3 sh3sw yhw3' ('the land of the Shasu of Yhw'); published by Jean Leclant 1960; Amarah West topographical list (Ramesses II, c. 1300 BCE) — 't3 sh3sw yhw3' and 't3 sh3sw s3rr' ('the land of the Shasu of Seir'); Goedicke 1965 — the yhw reading confirmed and discussed; AncientBibleHistory (groups.io), Sept 2026 — secondary discussion summarizing the Soleb/Amarah West evidence: Shasu as West Semitic pastoral nomads, Yhw placed in the southern wilderness of northwestern Arabia/Edom, coupled with Seir at Amarah West
Falsifiability: A definitive re-reading of both lists removing the yhw entry, or proof the lists are later restorations, would lower the grade; the name is in the stone, not in dispute since Goedicke — the interpretation is where the fight is</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>30.33,20.43,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Adonis: the mourned youth, the withering gardens, the witnessed return</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The festival laments and the mourning are multiply attested in the classical record; the pre-Hellenistic pedigree is argued with named evidence (Amarna, Byblian inscriptions) but stays fenced as argued-per-link. Grade covers the belief-phenomenon, never the entities.
Layer: real · Place: Byblos (Gebal, Lebanon) — the Adonis river, the Lady of Byblos
Dates: -1350 → 400 (confidence: low)

The tradition attests Adonis as a youth-god mourned in the annual Adonia festival (rooftop laments, the withering 'gardens of Adonis') and celebrated in return — with a pre-Hellenistic Phoenician pedigree: the Damu/Adon of Byblos, the male deity beside the Lady of Byblos in the Amarna letters, and early-Christian witnesses (Origen, Jerome) who describe the festival without claiming it as theirs.

Earliest attestation: Amarna letters EA 84 ('my DA.MU') and EA 129 ('my living god'), 14th c. BCE; the Adonia festivals in Greek and Roman sources; Origen and Jerome (3rd–4th c. CE)
Sources: The Adonia festivals — Greek and Roman sources on the rooftop laments and the 'gardens of Adonis'; Origen; Jerome (3rd–4th c. CE) — early-Christian witnesses to the Adonis festival, connecting it with the Tammuz of Ezekiel 8:14; Amarna letters EA 84, EA 129 (14th c. BCE) — the male deity of Byblos ('my DA.MU', 'my living god'); 10th-c. Byblian inscriptions — the pedigree chain; Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — Adonis 'may well have been' a pre-Christian dying-and-rising god; the Damu/Adon reconstruction
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Adonia festival never included a return/celebration phase, or that the Byblos pedigree is broken (the Amarna 'DA.MU' referring to a different deity), would weaken the attestation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.65,34.12,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1350</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The divine council: a shared West Semitic concept</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Both the Ugaritic tablets and the biblical texts exist and are independently attested; the shared vocabulary (council of El) is documented by philology.
Layer: real · Place: Ugarit (Ras Shamra), northern Levant coast
Dates: -1300 → -200 (confidence: medium)

The idea of a heavenly council of gods is attested in Late Bronze Age Ugaritic texts (the assembly of El, KTU, 13th–12th c. BCE) and in the Hebrew Bible (Psalm 82's 'council of El', Job, 1 Kings 22, Daniel 7) — same cultural world, with Israelite writers reshaping the vocabulary around YHWH's sovereignty. Deuteronomy 32:8's Qumran/Septuagint reading ('sons of God' / 'angels of God') against the Masoretic 'sons of Israel' shows the concept was live and edited.

Earliest attestation: Ugaritic tablets from Ras Shamra (KTU³, Dietrich/Loretz/Sanmartín); excavated 1929 onwards
Sources: KTU³ (Die keilalphabetischen Texte aus Ugarit), ed. Dietrich, Loretz, Sanmartín; Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford, 2001); The Early History of God (2002); E. Theodore Mullen Jr., The Divine Council in Canaanite and Early Hebrew Literature; Deuteronomy 32:8 — 4QDeutj (Qumran), Septuagint, Masoretic Text
Falsifiability: Proof the Ugaritic tablets are misdated or forged, or a Psalm 82 manuscript tradition with no council reading at all periods</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.78,35.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Shiloh favissa: ritual repository of sacrificial remains</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The deposit is real and excavated; the bone counts and cultic vessels are published excavation data; the 2026 reassessment is a scholarly re-analysis confirming ritual decommissioning practice.
Layer: real · Place: Tel Shiloh (Khirbet Seilun), West Bank
Dates: -1300 → -1150 (confidence: medium)

The subterranean ritual repository (favissa) at Tel Shiloh contains roughly 10,000 animal bones interspersed with cultic chalices, votive bowls, and ceremonial vessels — systematically buried sacred paraphernalia from Late Bronze Age sacrificial activity, re-analyzed in an exhaustive September 2026 reassessment published via the Enoch Seminar &amp; Biblical Archaeology surveys.

Earliest attestation: The favissa exposed in the Finkelstein-era excavations (1981–84); faunal and excavation records re-analyzed and published September 23, 2026
Sources: Enoch Seminar &amp; Biblical Archaeology surveys, September 2026 reassessment (favissa, ~10,000 bones, ritual decommissioning); Fox News Digital / jubileecast: three MBA storage jars with charred olives, wheat, lentils; ritual deposit above (10,000 bones, 8 gold/silver objects)
Falsifiability: Published independent faunal re-analysis contradicting the ritual-decommissioning interpretation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2897,32.0556,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The denominator rule: where VIII.d's silence ends and VIII.f's destruction begins</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Method claim demonstrated across independent cases sharing one evidentiary logic: the Oxyrhynchus/Rome climate contrast, the Ebla medium contrast, the Kadesh regime-baseline crossover, and the Herculaneum denominator bound — each an independent line, each requiring the same denominator work.
Layer: real · Place: Oxyrhynchus (climate exhibit); Kadesh/Qadesh (regime exhibit); Herculaneum (denominator exhibit)
Dates: -1300 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The timeline's two negative-evidence rules — VIII.d (silence is ambiguous: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, and not evidence either) and VIII.f (deletion and destruction, read properly, are evidence) — are not rivals. They are one rule applied with and without a denominator. The baseline is the denominator: the expected survival rate for a given medium in a given climate under a given regime's archiving and destroying habits. Where the baseline is a GUESS, VIII.d retains jurisdiction — silence stays ambiguous (Herculaneum: ~1,800 scrolls found, library size unknown, so 'what else the villa held' is not evidence). Where the baseline is a MEASUREMENT, VIII.f operates — the pattern of what is missing, checked against what should have survived, is itself a record. The Kadesh case is the crossover exhibit: Egyptian royal inscriptions never record defeats (the regime baseline — Ramses II's battle reliefs claim victory), so the monuments' silence about the stalemate is VIII.d silence; but once the regime baseline is stated, that systematic silence becomes an VIII.f-pattern — the equal-terms treaty DOES survive on temple walls (Karnak, Ramesseum) in the Hittite and Egyptian versions alike, so the boasting is measurable policy, not news. The Oxyrhynchus case is the climate exhibit: ~500,000 papyrus fragments from the rubbish mounds vs almost none from Rome's damp soil — taphonomy, not literacy; Rome's papyrus 'silence' is evidence of groundwater, not of lesser bureaucracy. The honesty fence that keeps this from becoming a universal solvent: the baseline itself must be evidenced, not assumed. 'Of course nothing survived — it all burned' is unfalsifiable when the regime's archiving rate is unknown; the Alexandria single-fire telling died precisely on this (the expected survival was asserted, not measured — evt-library-alexandria-001 DEBUNKED). When the baseline is a guess, VIII.d governs; when it is a measurement, VIII.f governs; and the work of the historian is moving cases across that line by measuring denominators.

Earliest attestation: The rule is synthesized 2026-10-16 from the timeline's own records: evt-silence-argument-002 (VIII.d) and evt-destruction-argument-001 (VIII.f). The taphonomic components are standard archaeological method (medium survival rates); the denominator formulation is this pass's synthesis.
Sources: evt-silence-argument-002 — the VIII.d rule (Kadesh two-sided record; Egyptian royal monuments never record defeats; McGrew 2013 independent formulation; the expected-evidence refinement); evt-destruction-argument-001 — the VIII.f rule (the chisel's mark; fire-baked clay; the measured pattern of what is missing checked against what should have survived); Oxyrhynchus papyri: ~500,000+ fragments from the town's rubbish mounds (Grenfell &amp; Hunt excavations 1896–1907 onward); dry desert conditions as the preservation mechanism — the standard taphonomic contrast with damp-soil sites; Ebla Palace G: fire-baked tablet archives preserved in shelving order (evt-fire-baked-archives-001) — the medium-baseline demonstration: sun-dried clay survives the fire that should destroy it; PHerc.1667 read end-to-end without unrolling, June 2026, Vesuvius Challenge — the denominator exhibit: ~1,800 scrolls recovered, total villa library size unknown; Kadesh treaty: equal-terms Egyptian version on temple walls (Karnak, Ramesseum) alongside the Hittite Akkadian version — the regime-baseline crossover (battle reliefs claim victory per royal-inscription policy; the treaty records parity); evt-library-alexandria-001 DEBUNKED — the asserted-but-unmeasured baseline that failed: the single-fire telling; Oxyrhynchus April 2026: University of Barcelona Oxyrhynchus Archaeological Mission (Nov 2025–Feb 2026 campaign, Al-Bahnasa necropolis) recovered a papyrus of Homer's Iliad Book II (Catalogue of Ships) from inside a Roman-era mummy's wrappings — the first Greek literary (non-magical) papyrus found in a mummy-wrapping context (phys.org, 20 Apr 2026) — the climate-baseline exhibit keeps being measured: new deposition contexts arrive in 2026, same dry-soil mechanism; PHerc.1667 read end-to-end, announced 25 June 2026 at the Biblioteca Nazionale di Napoli (Vesuvius Challenge; reported across the October 2026 press cycle, e.g. Gizmodo Science Fair 2026 writeup) — exhibit detail: first Herculaneum scroll ever virtually unwrapped and read end to end without physical opening; 22 columns of Greek over ~4.6 feet (1.4 m) of papyrus, a Stoic treatise on ethics; scans by high-resolution phase-contrast X-ray microtomography at ESRF Grenoble; machine-learning ink detection trained on carbon-ink-vs-carbonized-papyrus; transcriptions examined by papyrologists; data, code and transcriptions released openly under CC licence. The denominator exhibit, measured: the scroll carried a pre-read 'readability score of zero' and survived every 19th/20th-century opening attempt by being given up on.; Herculaneum denominator bound, re-confirmed October 2026: ~1,800 carbonized scrolls the measured population, of which ~300 intact scrolls remain sealed and unread (some outlets report ~600 unopened — press-level variance across the October 2026 coverage, fenced as unadjudicated). The total size of the Villa of the Papyri's original collection is still unknown in print — so 'what else the villa held' remains VIII.d territory: the baseline is a guess, and VIII.d retains jurisdiction. No 2026 publication changes the measured/guessed line.
Falsifiability: A case where a measured, evidenced baseline (medium survival rate + regime archiving rate + climate) exists and the silence still yields no readable pattern — or a case where the denominator is a pure guess and the pattern reads cleanly anyway, breaking the jurisdiction claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>30.66,28.54,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Battle of Kadesh (c. 1274 BCE): the battle both kings claimed to win</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Occurrence is VERIFIED: two hostile traditions independently agree a major battle happened at Kadesh in Ramesses II's fifth year. The Egyptian 'decisive victory' is CLAIMED: it is attested only in Ramesses's own inscriptions, contradicted by the strategic outcome (Kadesh/Amurru stayed Hittite) and by the Hittite tradition. The stalemate is the consensus position but sits inside competing_hypotheses rather than the grade — grades belong to claims, not to scholarly consensus.
Layer: real · Place: Kadesh on the Orontes (Tell Nebi Mend) — pinned at Homs, Syria
Dates: -1274 → -1274 (confidence: high)

In year 5 of Ramesses II (c. 1274 BCE), Egyptian and Hittite armies fought at Kadesh on the Orontes (Tell Nebi Mend, south of Lake Homs, modern Syria) — one of the largest chariot battles of the Late Bronze Age. Both empires' records agree a battle happened; they do not agree on who won. The Egyptian version (the Poem and the Bulletin, inscribed at Karnak, Luxor, Abydos, Abu Simbel, the Ramesseum) tells it as a personal triumph of the pharaoh, who was deceived by Hittite spies, ambushed, and rallied his forces. The Hittite tradition tells it as a checked Egyptian invasion: Kadesh was never captured, and Ramesses withdrew south to Egypt. The modern scholarly reading is a stalemate: Ramesses survived near-disaster and drove the Hittites from the field on the second day, but failed to take the city — and Kadesh and Amurru, the disputed territory the battle was fought over, stayed Hittite. Egypt's 'victory' is a single-tradition claim.

Earliest attestation: The Poem and the Bulletin of the Battle of Kadesh (Egyptian, inscribed c. 1270s BCE on multiple temple walls — contemporary royal account); the standard edition: Gardiner, 'The Kadesh Inscriptions of Ramesses II' (1960). Hittite-side tradition via Muwatalli II's administration and later Hattusili III's Apology (which mentions the campaign).
Sources: The Kadesh inscriptions (Poem and Bulletin) — temple walls: Karnak, Luxor, Abydos, Abu Simbel, Ramesseum; Gardiner ed. 1960 (standard edition); Hattusili III's Apology (Hittite cuneiform; references the Kadesh campaign from the Hittite side); The Treaty of Kadesh (c. 1258 BCE) — the diplomatic aftermath that constrains the outcome: a treaty between equals, not victor and vanquished; Bryce, 'The Kingdom of the Hittites' — both sides claimed victory; the modern stalemate reading
Falsifiability: Discovery of a Hittite contemporary account describing a Hittite rout and flight from the field would collapse the stalemate reading toward an Egyptian victory; a Hittite inscription describing an Egyptian rout would collapse it the other way. What would NOT count: another Egyptian temple wall repeating the Poem — that's one tradition echoing itself.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.7177,34.7322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1274</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The silence argument (VIII.d): absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — but it is not evidence either</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED as a method claim: the principle is not a single event but a demonstrated reasoning rule — the Kadesh two-sided record exhibits it, the Exodus-silence correction applies it, and the expected-evidence refinement bounds it. Like the convergence record (ep22), method claims are graded CORROBORATED when the principle is multiply exhibited across independent cases.
Layer: real · Place: Kadesh on the Orontes (the exhibit's geography)
Dates: -1274 → -1258 (confidence: high)

The synthesis-docket argument d ('Silence is ambiguous — Kadesh. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, but it is not evidence either'), stated as the timeline's method rule with its Kadesh exhibit and its honest refinement. The rule: (1) Silence is not evidence. Egyptian royal monuments never recorded defeats — Ramses II's Kadesh walls are proof of the mechanism — so the absence of an Egyptian record for an event (e.g., the Exodus, evt-exodus-001) is expected silence and moves no grade either way. (2) Silence IS informative where evidence would be expected: the expected-evidence refinement. The Babylonian chronicle series records Nebuchadnezzar's campaigns year by year through his 11th year (594 BCE); its silence on the 586/587 BCE destruction of Jerusalem is silence at the tablets' edge, not silence inside the coverage — an honest, narrow distinction the record carries. And the Kadesh correction: if we had only Ramesses's wall, 'Egypt won' would be the record. The Hittite account exists, and the 1258 BCE treaty between equals exposes the stalemate. The rule's discipline is the schema's falsifiability line: a claim cannot be rescued by 'absence of evidence' unless it states what evidence WOULD exist if it were true — the standard the timeline's contact-proof-bar record (evt-contact-proof-bar-001) states in advance. (3) The failure mode, named: 'absence of evidence' used as an apologetic blanket that excuses every failed prediction. That is the rule misused, and the misuse is itself a pattern the map can mark.

Earliest attestation: Kadesh exhibit: the Poem and Bulletin (Egyptian, c. 1270s BCE) vs. the Hittite tradition + the Treaty of Kadesh (c. 1258 BCE, Hattusa clay copy + Egyptian versions). Sagan's slogan: 'The Demon-Haunted World' (1995). The refinement: the timeline's own synthesis-docket argument d, built on the Exodus-silence correction (evt-exodus-001) and the Babylonian chronicle edge (evt-babylonian-exile-001 date_note).
Sources: The Kadesh record pair (Poem/Bulletin vs Hittite tradition + 1258 BCE treaty) — the exhibit; evt-exodus-001's silence correction (Merneptah/Exodus pass, CORRECTIONS.md) — the application; The Babylonian chronicle series ending 594 BCE (evt-babylonian-exile-001 date_note) — the edge distinction; The doctrine's falsifiability line (HISTORIAN-DOCTRINE.md) — the discipline
Falsifiability: The rule fails if a case is found where silence inside a complete, hostile-to-the-claim record series leaves the claim's grade correctly unchanged — i.e., where expected evidence is absent and the claim still holds at its grade. Each application is audited per-record, so the rule can lose case by case.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.7177,34.7322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1274</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The silence argument (VIII.d): absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — but it is not evidence either</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED as a method claim: the principle is not a single event but a demonstrated reasoning rule — the Kadesh two-sided record exhibits it, the Exodus-silence correction applies it, the expected-evidence refinement bounds it, and McGrew's 2013 formal treatment reaches the same refinement from an independent discipline (philosophy of history), which is the rule exhibiting itself. Grade held, chain strengthened.
Layer: real · Place: Kadesh on the Orontes (the exhibit's geography)
Dates: -1274 → -1258 (confidence: high)

The synthesis-docket argument d ('Silence is ambiguous — Kadesh. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence, but it is not evidence either'), stated as the timeline's method rule with its Kadesh exhibit and its honest refinement, strengthened 2026-10-16 with an independent philosophical formulation and a public-domain literary form. The rule: (1) Silence is not evidence. Egyptian royal monuments never recorded defeats — Ramses II's Kadesh walls are proof of the mechanism — so the absence of an Egyptian record for an event (e.g., the Exodus, evt-exodus-001) is expected silence and moves no grade either way. (2) Silence IS informative where evidence would be expected: the expected-evidence refinement. The Babylonian chronicle series records Nebuchadnezzar's campaigns year by year through his 11th year (594 BCE); its silence on the 586/587 BCE destruction of Jerusalem is silence at the tablets' edge, not silence inside the coverage — an honest, narrow distinction the record carries. (3) The independent formulation: Timothy McGrew, 'The Argument from Silence' (Acta Analytica, 2013) — arguments from silence are distinctive precisely because 'there is typically a source (or set of sources) from which the evidence is (rightly or wrongly) expected'; it is the absence of evidence from THAT source that drives the argument. McGrew's treatment is a philosopher's version of the timeline's expected-evidence refinement, arrived at from logic rather than from the Kadesh walls — convergence on the rule from an independent discipline. (4) The literary form: Doyle's 'Silver Blaze' (1892, public domain) — the dog that did not bark in the night tells Holmes the intruder was no stranger. Silence is informative only where a bark was expected. (5) The Kadesh correction stands: if we had only Ramesses's wall, 'Egypt won' would be the record. The Hittite account exists, and the 1258 BCE treaty between equals exposes the stalemate — re-verified 2026-10-16 (Bryce: both sides claimed victory; modern reading a stalemate; treaty of Kadesh, year 21 of Ramesses II, 1258 BCE, earliest known international peace treaty). The rule's discipline is the schema's falsifiability line: a claim cannot be rescued by 'absence of evidence' unless it states what evidence WOULD exist if it were true — the standard the timeline's contact-proof-bar record (evt-contact-proof-bar-001) states in advance. (6) The failure mode, named: 'absence of evidence' used as an apologetic blanket that excuses every failed prediction. That is the rule misused, and the misuse is itself a pattern the map can mark.

Earliest attestation: Kadesh exhibit: the Poem and Bulletin (Egyptian, c. 1270s BCE) vs. the Hittite tradition + the Treaty of Kadesh (c. 1258 BCE, Hattusa clay copy + Egyptian versions). Sagan's slogan: 'The Demon-Haunted World' (1995). The refinement: the timeline's own synthesis-docket argument d, built on the Exodus-silence correction (evt-exodus-001) and the Babylonian chronicle edge (evt-babylonian-exile-001 date_note). The independent formulation: McGrew, Acta Analytica (2013).
Sources: The Kadesh record pair (Poem/Bulletin vs Hittite tradition + 1258 BCE treaty) — the exhibit; re-verified 2026-10-16 (Bryce: both sides claimed victory, stalemate reading; treaty year 21 of Ramesses II = 1258 BCE, earliest known international peace treaty); evt-exodus-001's silence correction (Merneptah/Exodus pass, CORRECTIONS.md) — the application; The Babylonian chronicle series ending 594 BCE (evt-babylonian-exile-001 date_note) — the edge distinction; The doctrine's falsifiability line (HISTORIAN-DOCTRINE.md) — the discipline; Timothy McGrew, 'The Argument from Silence,' Acta Analytica (2013) — the independent formulation: arguments from silence are distinctive because 'there is typically a source (or set of sources) from which the evidence is (rightly or wrongly) expected'; Doyle, 'Silver Blaze' (1892, public domain) — the literary form of the expected-evidence principle: the dog that did not bark; The Kadesh exhibit strengthened 2026-10-17: evt-kadesh-treaty-fragment-2026-001 (the September 2026 Hattusa fragment, Articles 12-20) — the very treaty text the rule's two-sided-record argument rests on just grew a new witness; grade-neutral on the rule (CORROBORATED held), the exhibit's documentary base widened; Wikipedia 'Argument from silence' article (accessed 2026-10-18) — tertiary reference, grade-neutral: its three-condition structure (Lange — the document D, the exhaustive intent, the unmissable event) and Howell &amp; Prevenier's position-to-know requirement converge on the record's expected-evidence refinement from an independent survey of the literature; no new evidence, no grade effect (the McGrew 2013 independent formulation already on the record stands as the peer-reviewed anchor).
Falsifiability: The rule fails if a case is found where silence inside a complete, hostile-to-the-claim record series leaves the claim's grade correctly unchanged — i.e., where expected evidence is absent and the claim still holds at its grade. Each application is audited per-record, so the rule can lose case by case.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.7177,34.7322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1274</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Treaty of Kadesh (c. 1258 BCE): the earliest surviving international peace treaty</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED: the treaty survives in two independent primary textual traditions (Hittite clay copy from Hattusa, Egyptian papyrus + temple inscriptions) preserving the same agreement, dated internally to year 21 of Ramesses II. The 'earliest treaty' superlative is the standard description, honestly fenced with 'known/surviving'.
Layer: real · Place: Treaty concerns Kadesh region on the Orontes (Homs, Syria); texts found at Hattusa (Turkey) and Karnak (Egypt)
Dates: -1258 → -1258 (confidence: high)

In year 21 of Ramesses II (c. 1258 BCE), Egypt and Hatti signed the Treaty of Kadesh between Ramesses II and the Hittite king Hattusili III (Muwatalli II's brother and successor) — sixteen years after the Battle of Kadesh, and ending the borderland conflicts in Syria. The treaty was written on a silver tablet; a Hittite cuneiform clay copy was excavated at Hattusa (now in the Istanbul Archaeology Museum), and an Egyptian version survives on papyrus and temple walls (Karnak, Ramesseum). Its terms: mutual non-aggression, mutual defense against external enemies, and extradition of fugitives. It is widely described as the earliest known surviving international peace treaty; a replica hangs at the United Nations headquarters. The treaty's existence is evidence that the Kadesh-era stalemate held: it is a treaty between equals, not a victor imposing terms.

Earliest attestation: Hittite cuneiform clay copy of the silver tablet, excavated at Hattusa (Boğazköy); Egyptian versions on papyrus and carved at Karnak and the Ramesseum — two independent textual traditions of the same agreement.
Sources: Hittite cuneiform clay copy from Hattusa (Istanbul Archaeology Museum); Egyptian versions on papyrus and temple walls (Karnak, Ramesseum); The silver tablet is lost; the texts are copies of it — carried in competing_hypotheses; evt-kadesh-treaty-fragment-2026-001 (Sept 2026): newly identified Hattusa (Büyükkale) fragment preserving Articles 12-20 — the fugitives provisions; a new securely-located witness to the Hittite copy; full transcription pending — grade-neutral on the treaty's VERIFIED existence, chain strengthened
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription from either court describing resumed hostilities in the treaty year with no mention of the agreement would undermine it; the agreement's existence would be falsified if either version's text proved to be a later fabrication — but the Hittite archive's tablet is a 13th-c.-BCE original in the royal archive context.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>36.7177,34.7322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1258</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Admonitions of Ipuwer (Papyrus Leiden I 344 recto)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript is a catalogued museum artifact with a standard modern edition (Enmarch 2009/2011, OUP/British Academy); the copy date and genre classification are the Egyptological consensus
Layer: real · Place: Rijksmuseum van Oudheden, Leiden (findspot unknown, probably Memphis-Saqqara area)
Dates: -1250 → -1250 (confidence: medium)

The Admonitions of Ipuwer survive in a single damaged hieratic manuscript, Papyrus Leiden I 344 recto (Rijksmuseum van Oudheden, Leiden), a 19th-Dynasty copy (c. 1250 BCE) of a Middle Kingdom composition (late 12th Dynasty, c. 1850-1700 BCE) in the Egyptian 'national distress' lament genre; a sage named Ipuwer laments a world turned upside down ('the river is blood', rank reversals, Asiatics in the Delta).

Earliest attestation: Papyrus Leiden I 344 recto itself, 19th-Dynasty copy; acquired 1828 via Giovanni Anastasi, first edited by Gardiner 1909
Sources: Gardiner, A.H. (1909). The Admonitions of an Egyptian Sage; Enmarch, R. (2009/2011). A World Upturned: Commentary on and Analysis of The Dialogue of Ipuwer and the Lord of All. OUP/British Academy; Lichtheim, M. (1973). Ancient Egyptian Literature, Vol. I. UC Press, pp. 135, 149-163; Parkinson, R.B. (1998). The Dialogue of Ipuur and the Lord of All. The Tale of Sinuhe and Other Ancient Egyptian Poems. OUP, pp. 166-202; Wikipedia 'Ipuwer Papyrus' (citing Quirke 2014 p.167; Willems 2010 p.83; Van Seters 1964 JEA 50:13-23) — tertiary consensus summary, accessed 2026-10-20; hebrewexplorer.com 'Ipuwer Papyrus (Leiden I 344)' — acquisition history (Anastasi 1828), Leiden custody
Falsifiability: Radiocarbon/palaeographic re-dating of the papyrus outside the New Kingdom, or discovery of a Middle Kingdom copy predating the 12th Dynasty, would move the composition window; the genre classification would require overturning Lichtheim/Enmarch/Willems</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>4.4848,52.1653,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1250</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Merneptah Stele: earliest mention of 'Israel'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The stele exists and the reading 'Israel' (with the people-determinative) is the mainstream scholarly consensus.
Layer: real · Place: Thebes (find spot)
Dates: -1209 → -1206 (confidence: medium)

Pharaoh Merneptah's victory stele (c. 1207 BCE) names 'Israel' as a people in Canaan — the earliest known extra-biblical attestation of the name.

Earliest attestation: The stele itself, from Merneptah's mortuary temple at Thebes (discovered 1896 by Flinders Petrie)
Sources: Merneptah Stele (Egyptian Museum, Cairo, JE 31408); Petrie's 1896 excavation report (Thebes)
Falsifiability: A demonstrated misreading of the hieroglyphs in the relevant line would overturn the claim</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.6103,25.7208,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1209</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The cowrie as currency</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent documentary lines (customs/import quantities, European merchant accounts, the 1986 Hogendorn &amp; Johnson monograph, Johnson's 1970 JAH pair, colonial price schedules) converge on the same system; the inflation-collapse mechanism is documented, not inferred.
Layer: real · Place: Maldives (harvest) → Bengal → Bight of Benin/West Africa (circulation)
Dates: -1200 → 1930 (confidence: medium)

The money cowrie (Monetaria moneta, syn. Cypraea moneta), harvested chiefly in the Maldives, circulated as a genuine currency across the Indian Ocean world and West Africa for centuries: a Bengal standard (3,840 shells to the rupee in the 19th century), a regular West African market currency from the 16th to the 19th century, and the 'sine qua non' of the European slave trade's purchasing price — before collapsing under 19th-century inflation when European traders flooded West Africa with cheaper Zanzibar cowries.

Earliest attestation: Chinese bronze cowrie imitations, Shang dynasty (archaeological, 2nd millennium BCE); Maldivian cowrie trade described by medieval Arab geographers; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica 'Shell-money' article summarizes the 19th-century state
Sources: Hogendorn, Jan &amp; Johnson, Marion — The Shell Money of the Slave Trade (Cambridge University Press, 1986); Johnson, Marion — 'The Cowrie Currencies of West Africa', Journal of African History 11(1):17-49 &amp; 11(3):331-353 (1970); 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Shell-money' — Bengal 3,840 cowries to the rupee, £30,000 annual importation; West Africa 'the usual tender' until past mid-19th c.; Maldives/Ceylon/Malabar/Borneo/Ras Hafun–Mozambique harvest grounds; Wiley — 'Atlantic trade and the West African market economy: New evidence from cowry imports, 1650–1905' (Economic History Review, 2026) — 400 cowries/lb standard, 200/lb adopted middle ground, cowries as fiduciary money; The Collector — 'How Underwater Shells Became the First Global Money' — Portuguese first European Maldives→Bight of Benin shipment 1515; late-17th-c. purchase price 10–30,000 shells (20–50% of a person's price), 160–176,000 by 1770; 'sine qua non' (Hogendorn p.32); Encyclopaedia Romana, 'money' (U. Chicago penelope) — 18th-c. imports ~150 tons/year (~800,000 shells/ton), one-third of slave purchase price; 2,000 shells = silver dollar; inflation + European currency ended the system; Citéco (Cité de l'Économie) — cowrie as currency 'more than three millennia', 13th-c.-BC Chinese bronze cowrie motifs, Maldives origin, Ghana/Mali/Songhai unit of account
Falsifiability: Port/customs records showing no large-scale Maldives→West Africa cowrie shipments in the 16th–19th centuries, or evidence the shells circulated only as ornament with no price function</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>73.2,3.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The yazatas of the Avesta</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief-phenomenon is multiply attested: the Avestan texts exist in a manuscript tradition, name the yazatas individually, and are studied by an independent scholarly discipline. Per the attestation rule this grades the belief, never the beings.
Layer: real · Place: Eastern Iran / southern Central Asia (Bactria–Margiana; the Avestan composition zone — disputed)
Dates: -1200 → -400 (confidence: low)

The Avesta — the Zoroastrian scripture — attests a developed angelology: named divine beings called yazatas ('worthy of worship'), who serve as the good powers under Ahura Mazda, each with a domain (Mithra of contract, Sraosha of conscience and the soul's journey, Rashnu of judgment, Anahita of waters, Tishtrya of rain, Verethragna of victory). The Yashts (hymns 5, 8, 10, 13–19) are dedicated to individual yazatas; the Yasna liturgy invokes them collectively.

Earliest attestation: The Gathas (Yasna 28–34, 43–51, Old Avestan) for the tradition's core; the individual Yashts (Young Avestan) for the developed yazata hymns
Sources: The Avesta: Yasna, Visperad, Vendidad — the Yashts (esp. Yashts 5, 8, 10, 13–19), Avestan text with Darmesteter/Mills translations (Sacred Books of the East); Mary Boyce, 'Fravaši' and 'Frawardīn Yašt', Encyclopaedia Iranica, vol. 10 (2001), pp. 195–201 — the scholarly reference apparatus on the fravashi corpus; The multi-witness Avestan manuscript tradition (the Yasna manuscripts, the Yasht manuscripts) — the texts survive in independent manuscript families
Falsifiability: A demonstrated forgery of the Avestan manuscript tradition, or proof that the Yashts are modern compositions rather than Young Avestan, would collapse the attestation. (The grade concerns the belief's attestation; the beings are not graded.)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>62.5,36.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The face that launched a thousand ships' — Helen's abduction as the war's cause</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — CLAIMED: attested only in the epic tradition; no independent evidence that Helen of Sparta existed as a historical person, let alone that her abduction caused a war. The 'thousand ships' is Marlowe 1604, not Bronze Age evidence. The competing trade-route reading is honestly carried as SPECULATION — reasoned but unattested — not smuggled in as fact. The grade is on the cause, not on the poems: the Iliad's premise is real and ancient; its historicity is not.
Layer: real · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: -1180 → -1180 (confidence: low)

That the Trojan War was caused by Paris's abduction (or elopement) of Helen of Sparta — 'the face that launched a thousand ships' as a historical casus belli.

Earliest attestation: The epic tradition — the Iliad's premise (composed c. 8th c. BCE). 'The face that launched a thousand ships' is NOT Homer: it is Christopher Marlowe, Doctor Faustus (1604) — 'Was this the face that launch'd a thousand ships?'
Sources: Homer, Iliad (public domain) — the war's premise: the abduction/elopement as casus belli; the epic tradition's own account.; Christopher Marlowe, Doctor Faustus (1604) — 'Was this the face that launch'd a thousand ships, / And burnt the topless towers of Ilium?' The line is Marlowe's invention, not Homer's.; Penn Gazette / C. Brian Rose — the Alaksandu treaty naming 'Aleksandu (Alexandros), the ruler of Wilusa' (Alexandros = Paris in the epic); 'proving there is real history behind the myth' — the treaty attests a ruler, never an abduction.
Falsifiability: A Bronze Age record naming Helen — a Hittite or Linear B attestation of a Spartan queen — would move the person to CORROBORATED; proof the abduction premise is a later poetic invention would drop the cause to SPECULATION.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1180</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Trojan Horse — the Greeks took Troy in a giant wooden horse</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single literary tradition (Odyssey 8's brief song, four centuries later; Aeneid 2's full account, a millennium later) with zero archaeological corroboration — Troy VIIa shows a burned, fought-over city (~1180 BCE), not a mechanism. Four live mechanism readings: (1) a battering ram or siege engine draped in damp horse-hides (D'Angour); (2) a ship (the 'hippos'-as-ship reading); (3) an earthquake metaphor (Poseidon = god of horses and earthquakes; Troy VI's seismic damage is real); (4) poetic invention (the majority reading). None proved; none has falsified the others. The attestation is real and ancient; the mechanism is CLAIMED — held below DEBUNKED per the silence argument: the tell proves a fire, never a horse, and silence about the horse is not evidence against every possible mechanism.
Layer: real · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: -1180 → -1180 (confidence: low)

That the Greeks captured Troy by concealing soldiers inside a giant wooden horse, which the Trojans dragged through their own gates, after which the soldiers emerged at night and opened the gates — the horse mechanism as history, as literally described.

Earliest attestation: Homer's Odyssey, Book 8 (Demodocus's song — fewer than sixty lines), composed c. 8th c. BCE, some four to five centuries after the claimed event. The Iliad never mentions the horse; Laocoön and Sinon are not in Homer. The full account is Virgil's Aeneid, Book 2 (~19 BCE) — a millennium after the event. 'Timeo Danaos et dona ferentes' (beware Greeks bearing gifts) is Virgil's line, not Homer's.
Sources: Homer, Odyssey Book 8 — Demodocus's song (public domain): the earliest literary attestation of the horse, brief and embedded in a song-within-a-song.; Virgil, Aeneid Book 2 (~19 BCE) — the first full account of the episode, including Laocoön, Sinon, and 'timeo Danaos et dona ferentes'; composed roughly a millennium after the claimed event.; IFLScience, 'Did The Famous Trojan Horse Really Exist?' — Dr Armand D'Angour (Oxford): Troy was indeed burned down, 'but the wooden horse is an imaginative fable, perhaps inspired by the way ancient siege-engines were clothed with damp horse-hides to stop them being set alight'; Homer mentions the horse only in passing in the Odyssey, the first substantial description is the Aeneid.; The Tab / History Today (Julia Kindt): the live mechanism theories — siege tower or battering ram, an earthquake (horses = Poseidon, god of horses and earthquakes), or a ship carrying Greek soldiers.; Dartmouth Aegean Prehistory, Lesson 27 — Troy VIIa: 'perhaps the most likely candidate for the city of Priam'; destruction in a general conflagration ~1180 BCE; no mechanism attested in the stratigraphy.
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous Bronze Age record of the ruse — a Hittite tablet, a Linear B entry, an inscription — naming the horse stratagem at Wilusa would move this to CORROBORATED. Conversely, textual proof that the Odyssey 8 horse passage is a post-Homeric interpolation would drop the earliest attestation and push this toward SPECULATION.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1180</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Troy VIIa burned ~1180 BCE — a city destroyed, an attacker unnamed</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED on the destruction: a general conflagration destroying both citadel and extramural buildings (Dartmouth Lesson 27), with attack signatures — Mycenaean arrowheads embedded in the walls, unburied skeletons, buried food rations, Korfmann's subdivided lower-city homes packed for siege. Held below VERIFIED: the date rests on relative ceramic dating (no absolute anchor), and the agent is genuinely contested — siege, earthquake, or both; the 'Greeks did it' reading is SPECULATION on the current evidence. The ~1180 date landing near Eratosthenes's 1184 is convergence, not proof — stated honestly.
Layer: real · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: -1180 → -1180 (confidence: medium)

That Troy VIIa — the Late Bronze Age city at Hisarlık — suffered violent destruction by fire around 1180 BCE, with evidence of attack (arrowheads, unburied dead, emergency food stores, a packed lower town). The agent of destruction (Greek attackers, earthquake, or both) is contested.

Earliest attestation: The stratigraphy itself (Blegen 1932–1938; Korfmann 1988–2005) — the burn layer is the primary document. The epic tradition's war-date reckonings are later Greek chronography, not attestation of this layer.
Sources: Dartmouth Aegean Prehistory, Lesson 27 — Troy VIIa: occupied from ~1300/1280 BCE to ~1180 BCE (LH IIIB imports in, LH IIIC sherds out); Blegen's dating debate (1240 → 1270; Nylander 1200–1190); 'Troy VIIa perished in a general conflagration which destroyed both the buildings within the citadel and those outside'; the Greek war-date reckonings (Eratosthenes 1184, Parian Marble 1209/8, Herodotus ~1250, Douris 1334/3); the VIh minority and Wood 1985.; Wikipedia, 'Late Bronze Age Troy' — Troy VIIa 1300–1180 BCE; the layer table (Troy VI 1750–1300, VIIa 1300–1180, VIIb 1180–950).; Korfmann excavations 1988–2005 (via secondary summaries): the lower city packed with subdivided homes, buried food rations, unburied skeletons, Mycenaean arrowheads in the walls — the siege signatures.
Falsifiability: Dendrochronological or radiocarbon re-dating shifting VIIa's destruction outside ~1250–1150 BCE would force re-evaluation; a contemporaneous inscription naming the attackers would settle the agent; proof the destruction horizon is seismic-only would move the 'attack' reading down.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1180</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The monumental Iron I structure at Tel Shiloh</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The building is real, measured, and excavated over multiple seasons; its dimensions and orientation are the excavation team's published claims, reported consistently across outlets. The identification question is a separate record.
Layer: real · Place: Tel Shiloh (Khirbet Seilun), West Bank
Dates: -1100 → -1000 (confidence: medium)

The Associates for Biblical Research (ABR) excavations at Tel Shiloh (Khirbet Seilun), in their 2025 and 2026 seasons, exposed a monumental Iron I building; the June 2026 season located its southern wall, allowing the full footprint to be reconstructed — east-west orientation, proportions reported to match the biblical Tabernacle's dimensions (Exodus 26–27). The season also recovered a bent-axis city gate (1 Samuel 4's Eli's gate) and three Middle Bronze Age storage jars with charred grain beneath the later deposits.

Earliest attestation: The walls themselves (Iron I), exposed in the ABR's 2025–2026 seasons; reported via the Bible Archaeology Report and Fox News Digital interviews, June–July 2026
Sources: ABR Bible Archaeology Report, 2026 season summary (Dr. Scott Stripling); kingdomkutzmedia summary (carries both the find and Maeir's pushback); answeredfaith 2026 Shiloh evidence summary; kingdomkutzmedia, 'Shiloh Dig Confirms the Gate Where Eli the Priest Died' (Sept 2026) — gate walls with bent-axis design matching the 1 Samuel 4 gate; carries Maeir's public pushback on the tabernacle framing; Tim Lopez, 'Monumental Building at Shiloh &amp; Israelite Tabernacle' — EHJ 2026 conference presentation abstract (2026 season area supervisor): the southern wall completes the footprint; explicitly frames the architectural similarities as warranting investigation, not proof
Falsifiability: A published independent re-dating of the structure's walls to a period incompatible with Iron I, or the ABR's own publication retracting the reported measurements</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2897,32.0556,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Shiloh structure as the biblical Tabernacle</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested by the excavation team only; ABR is a confessional organization whose conclusions favor the biblical text, and mainstream archaeologist Aren Maeir disputes the identification publicly. No independent scholarly consensus.
Layer: real · Place: Tel Shiloh (Khirbet Seilun), West Bank
Dates: -1100 → -1000 (confidence: medium)

The monumental Iron I structure excavated at Tel Shiloh (2025–2026 ABR seasons) is the biblical Tabernacle — the portable sanctuary that housed the Ark of the Covenant at Shiloh.

Earliest attestation: The ABR team's interpretation, published in the Bible Archaeology Report (2025–2026 seasons)
Sources: ABR Bible Archaeology Report, 2026 season summary (Dr. Scott Stripling); Aren Maeir's public pushback (cultic activity across periods, not a confirmed tabernacle)
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed independent publication endorsing the identification would raise the grade toward CORROBORATED; the grade holds until then</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2897,32.0556,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Yellow River's first prehistoric diversion (~3000–2600 BP): the Taihang-foothill course</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multi-proxy sedimentary analyses published in a peer-reviewed journal document the displaced channel and its effects; the archaic-period pathway was previously unknown, and the finding is consistent with the river's documented avulsion-prone behavior.
Layer: real · Place: Eastern Taihang foothills foreland / North China Plain (diversion pathway into the Bohai Sea)
Dates: -1050 → -650 (confidence: medium)

Around 3000–2600 years before present, the lower Yellow River underwent a dramatic channel displacement — its first prehistoric diversion documented in the sediment record — flowing northward into the Bohai Sea through the foreland depression along the eastern foothill of the Taihang Mountains. Frequent floods after ~3000 BP infilled the lower-reach depressions, accelerating the formation of the North China Plain and driving human migration from the Taihang piedmont into the central plain.

Earliest attestation: The sedimentary record itself — multi-proxy analyses of the lower-reach deposits (the first sedimentary evidence for this archaic-period pathway).
Sources: Sedimentary Geology (ScienceDirect S0031018223005278) — 'Sedimentary evidence for the diversion of the Yellow River onto the North China Plain 3000–2600 years ago': multi-proxy analyses showing the first prehistoric diversion of the lower Yellow River ~3000 yr BP, northward flow into the Bohai Sea via the Taihang Mountains' foreland basin; post-3-ka-BP floods infilled depressions, accelerating North China Plain formation and piedmont-to-hinterland migration
Falsifiability: A dated, peer-reviewed stratigraphic reconstruction showing no such northward channel in the 3000–2600 BP window along the Taihang foothills would retire this exhibit; the argument's other exhibits stand independently.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>115.5,37.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1050</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>David, king of Israel</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 'House of David' inscription gives independent 9th-century attestation of the dynasty; the biblical biography of David himself is single-tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem
Dates: -1040 → -970 (confidence: traditional)

David (traditional dates c. 1040-970 BCE) founded a dynasty centered on Jerusalem; the 9th-century Tel Dan stele's reference to the 'House of David' provides extra-biblical attestation of his dynasty.

Earliest attestation: The books of Samuel; the Tel Dan stele (c. 840 BCE) names the 'House of David' two centuries after his traditional dates
Sources: Books of Samuel, 1 Kings 1-2; Tel Dan stele (c. 840 BCE), 'House of David' inscription; Biran, A. &amp; Naveh, J. 1993. 'An Aramaic Stele Fragment from Tel Dan', Israel Exploration Journal 43/2-3, 81-98; Biran &amp; Naveh 1995, 'The Tel Dan Inscription: A New Fragment', IEJ 45/1 (fragment A found July 1993 by Gila Cook, B1/B2 June 1994; stele KAI 310, Israel Museum); Mesha Stele line 31 'House of David' reading - CONTESTED, not a source for this claim. Lemaire (1994) restored bt[d]wd after the Tel Dan find; Finkelstein, Na'aman &amp; Roemer (2019, Tel Aviv 46/3) challenged it on new imaging, proposing 'Balak'; RTI/Louvre imaging (2022, Lemaire &amp; Langlois) reaffirmed the dalet. Status: genuinely unresolved; the timeline does not rest on it.; Khirbet Qeiyafa (Garfinkel et al., excavations 2007-2013): fortified Iron-Age site, 1050-915 BCE radiocarbon span (Finkelstein &amp; Piasetzky 2010); claimed Judahite/Davidic, contested (pottery re-attribution: Singer-Avitz 2010) - context for 10th-century Judah, not attestation of David; Low Chronology debate: Finkelstein (1996, 2005) - 10th-century Jerusalem a small unfortified settlement; Iron I/IIA transition ~900 BCE per Sharon et al. 2007 radiocarbon; Mazar's modified conventional chronology counters. David/Solomon dates carry 'traditional' confidence until the chronology is settled.; OpenBible.info geocoding (Jerusalem, City of David)
Falsifiability: Proof the Tel Dan reading is wrong, or contemporary 10th-century inscriptions naming a different Jerusalem dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1040</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>David, king of Israel</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 'House of David' inscription gives independent 9th-century attestation of the dynasty; the biblical biography of David himself is single-tradition. Post-2026 scholarly position: the argument over whether the dynasty existed ended at Tel Dan (1993-94); the live argument moved to the kingdom's scale — tribal-chieftain highlands vs. high/modified-conventional chronology urban monarchy (Finkelstein 1996 low chronology vs. Mazar's modified conventional; Khirbet Qeiyafa radiocarbon 1051-969 BCE confirms 10th-c. fortified Judah, names no king).
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem
Dates: -1040 → -970 (confidence: traditional)

David (traditional dates c. 1040-970 BCE) founded a dynasty centered on Jerusalem; the 9th-century Tel Dan stele's reference to the 'House of David' provides extra-biblical attestation of his dynasty.

Earliest attestation: The books of Samuel; the Tel Dan stele (c. 840 BCE) names the 'House of David' two centuries after his traditional dates
Sources: Books of Samuel, 1 Kings 1-2; Tel Dan stele (c. 840 BCE), 'House of David' inscription; Biran, A. &amp; Naveh, J. 1993. 'An Aramaic Stele Fragment from Tel Dan', Israel Exploration Journal 43/2-3, 81-98; Biran &amp; Naveh 1995, 'The Tel Dan Inscription: A New Fragment', IEJ 45/1 (fragment A found July 1993 by Gila Cook, B1/B2 June 1994; stele KAI 310, Israel Museum); Mesha Stele line 31 'House of David' reading - CONTESTED, not a source for this claim. Lemaire (1994) restored bt[d]wd after the Tel Dan find; Finkelstein, Na'aman &amp; Roemer (2019, Tel Aviv 46/3) challenged it on new imaging, proposing 'Balak'; RTI/Louvre imaging (2022, Lemaire &amp; Langlois) reaffirmed the dalet. Status: genuinely unresolved; the timeline does not rest on it.; Khirbet Qeiyafa (Garfinkel et al., excavations 2007-2013): fortified Iron-Age site, 1050-915 BCE radiocarbon span (Finkelstein &amp; Piasetzky 2010); claimed Judahite/Davidic, contested (pottery re-attribution: Singer-Avitz 2010) - context for 10th-century Judah, not attestation of David; Low Chronology debate: Finkelstein (1996, 2005) - 10th-century Jerusalem a small unfortified settlement; Iron I/IIA transition ~900 BCE per Sharon et al. 2007 radiocarbon; Mazar's modified conventional chronology counters. David/Solomon dates carry 'traditional' confidence until the chronology is settled.; OpenBible.info geocoding (Jerusalem, City of David); Grabbe, L.L. 2007. Ahab Agonistes. T&amp;T Clark — Tel Dan inscription now widely regarded (a) as genuine and (b) as referring to the Davidic dynasty; Cline, E.H. 2009. Biblical Archaeology: A Very Short Introduction. OUP — "accepted by most archaeologists that the inscription is not only genuine but that the reference is indeed to the House of David"
Falsifiability: Proof the Tel Dan reading is wrong, or contemporary 10th-century inscriptions naming a different Jerusalem dynasty</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1040</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Witch of Endor (1 Samuel 28)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the biblical tradition only; no contemporary extra-biblical attestation of the event — the narrative is the exhibit, the summoning is not independently verified.
Layer: real · Place: Endor (traditional, Jezreel Valley)
Dates: -1010 → -1000 (confidence: traditional)

The biblical narrative in which King Saul, on the eve of battle, consults a medium at Endor who summons the spirit of the prophet Samuel, who prophesies Saul's defeat and death.

Earliest attestation: 1 Samuel 28 (Hebrew Bible); composed in the exilic/post-exilic Deuteronomistic History, centuries after its narrative setting
Sources: 1 Samuel 28:3-25 (Masoretic Text); Septuagint 1 Kingdoms 28 (the 'ventriloquist' / engastrimythos rendering); Tertullian, De anima 57 (the apparition read as a demonic deception); Augustine, Letter 143 to Evodius (the uncertainty: Samuel, a demon, or a phantom — honestly fenced)
Falsifiability: As a literary-tradition claim the grade stays CLAIMED absent independent attestation; a contemporary inscription naming the Endor medium or contradicting the Samuel summoning would move it</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.37,32.63,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1010</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Ark of the Covenant in Axum</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The ATTENTATION is multiply documented (the 14th-c. text, the continuous guardian institution, Hancock's 1992 investigation, Raffaele's 2007 reportage) — and the OBJECT is by design unobservable: the single person who claims to see it permits no verification, and the one person who claimed to have forced entry (Ullendorff, 1941) reported a medieval model and never published. Per the attestation rule we grade the attestation, not the metaphysics: CLAIMED is exactly where a solemn, unfalsifiable, institutionally-guarded claim sits.
Layer: real · Place: Axum (Aksum), Tigray Region, Ethiopia — Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion
Dates: -1000 → 2026 (confidence: low)

That the Ark of the Covenant — the gilded acacia-wood chest of the Hebrew Bible holding the tablets of the Ten Commandments, last attested in Jerusalem before the Babylonian sack of 586 BCE — is currently kept in the Chapel of the Tablet at the Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion in Axum, Ethiopia, seen only by a single guardian monk who may not leave the compound.

Earliest attestation: The Kebra Nagast (14th c. CE compilation) — the Ark's migration to Ethiopia with Menelik I; the living guardian tradition (30 robes of previous guardians displayed in the church); Graham Hancock's The Sign and the Seal (1992) — guardian testimony collected but the chapel never entered
Sources: The Kebra Nagast (14th c. Ge'ez compilation) — Menelik I and the Ark migration narrative; Graham Hancock, The Sign and the Seal (1992) — the documentary trail to Axum; guardian never entered by the author; the inconsistency he found himself (Axum did not exist in Menelik's time); Paul Raffaele, Smithsonian (2007) — the Tana Kirkos monastery tradition and the Axum guardian account ('We don't need proof because it's a fact. The monks here have passed this down for centuries.'); Live Science reporting (Tudor Parfitt on Edward Ullendorff) — Ullendorff entered Mary of Zion in 1941 as a British officer and saw a wooden model ark 'middle-to late-medieval construction'; he never published, not wanting to hurt Ethiopians' feelings; The Christian Forums / BASE Institute field logbook — 30 guardian robes displayed; guardian: 'no other man besides himself could lay eyes on the Ark'
Falsifiability: By design, none available to a third party: only the guardian sees the object, and his testimony is not independently checkable. The claim is unfalsifiable, which is why it cannot grade above CLAIMED no matter how sincerely held.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>38.7229,14.1379,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Genealogies as Political Documents</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The editorial pattern is visible in the documents themselves — schematic numbering, divergent totals across traditions, selective inclusion — and is the scholarly consensus reading (Wilson 1977; Johnson).
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (compositional setting of the compiled lists)
Dates: -1000 → 0100 (confidence: traditional)

Biblical genealogical lists functioned as political and theological documents: their editors telescoped generations, selected or reordered names, and used schematic numbering to make arguments, so the lists cannot be read as complete literal family trees.

Earliest attestation: The genealogical lists in Genesis, Chronicles, Matthew 1, Luke 3; the divergent Genesis 5 number-sets visible across the Masoretic Text, Septuagint, and Samaritan Pentateuch
Sources: Robert R. Wilson, Genealogy and History in the Biblical World (Yale, 1977); Marshall D. Johnson, The Purpose of the Biblical Genealogies (Cambridge, 1969; 2nd ed. 1988); The divergent Genesis 5 number-sets across Masoretic (1656 yrs), Samaritan (1307), Septuagint (2242) — see evt-genesis5-genealogy-divergence-001 (VERIFIED); Matthew 1:17 — 'fourteen generations' stated thrice as the list's own organizing principle
Falsifiability: A genealogical list corroborated name-for-name by contemporary extra-biblical inscriptions with no telescoping or schematic patterning</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The burning: one night against witchcraft</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The series' eight-plus-one tablet structure, the ~100 incantations, the single-night Abu ceremony, and the figurine-burning are established by the critical edition; performance (not just scribal exercise) is anchored by the contemporary Esarhaddon correspondence.
Layer: real · Place: Assyria (Nineveh court)
Dates: -1000 → -600 (confidence: medium)

The Maqlû ('burning') series — eight tablets of nearly a hundred anti-witchcraft (kišpū) incantations plus a ritual tablet of incipits and directions — records a single complex ceremony performed over one night at the end of the month Abu (July/August), when spirits moved between the netherworld and the living world; figurines of the witch were burned, and the ritual deliberately does not name the witch, trusting the gods to know the target. Mature form early first millennium BCE; performance attested by the exorcist Nabû-nādin-šumi's correspondence with Esarhaddon.

Earliest attestation: Early-first-millennium BCE mature form; Nabû-nādin-šumi's letter to Esarhaddon on the Maqlû ritual
Sources: Abusch, The Witchcraft Series Maqlû (SBL Writings from the Ancient World 37, 2015) — transcription, translation, introduction; Letter of the exorcist Nabû-nādin-šumi to Esarhaddon concerning the Maqlû ritual; Maqlû ritual tablet (incipits + ceremony directions for the single-night Abu performance)
Falsifiability: Evidence the series was a scribal exercise never performed (no ritual tablet in working archives, no contemporary references to performance) would downgrade the practice claim; the Esarhaddon correspondence currently anchors it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.15,36.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Solomon and the First Temple</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — No contemporary extra-biblical attestation of Solomon exists; the united-monarchy extent described is not confirmed by 10th-century archaeology.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (traditional Temple location)
Dates: -970 → -930 (confidence: traditional)

Solomon (traditional dates c. 970-930 BCE), son of David, built the First Temple in Jerusalem and ruled a united monarchy of Israel and Judah at its greatest extent.

Earliest attestation: 1 Kings 1-11; composed centuries after the events (Deuteronomistic history)
Sources: 1 Kings 1-11 (Solomon narratives, First Temple account); Shishak invasion records (Egyptian, c. 925 BCE) — corroborates the period's geopolitics, not Solomon
Falsifiability: A contemporary 10th-century inscription naming Solomon of Jerusalem, or stratified evidence of a large 10th-century Jerusalem administration</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-970</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Solomon and the First Temple</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — No contemporary extra-biblical attestation of Solomon exists; the united-monarchy extent described is not confirmed by 10th-century archaeology. Counterweight, honestly stated: Khirbet Qeiyafa's radiocarbon places a fortified Judahite city in the early 10th c. BCE (1051-969 BCE) — the 'no 10th-century Judah' minimalist position has conceded the city; what it has not produced is a single stone naming Solomon. The six-chambered gates at Hazor, Megiddo and Gezer are real Iron Age IIA architecture; their Solomonic attribution (Yadin 1958) is the claim under dispute (low chronology: 9th c., Omrides).
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (traditional Temple location)
Dates: -970 → -930 (confidence: traditional)

Solomon (traditional dates c. 970-930 BCE), son of David, built the First Temple in Jerusalem and ruled a united monarchy of Israel and Judah at its greatest extent.

Earliest attestation: 1 Kings 1-11; composed centuries after the events (Deuteronomistic history)
Sources: 1 Kings 1-11 (Solomon narratives, First Temple account); Shishak invasion records (Egyptian, c. 925 BCE) — corroborates the period's geopolitics, not Solomon; Garfinkel, Y., Ganor, S. &amp; Hasel, M. 2010. Khirbet Qeiyafa radiocarbon (OxCal IntCal04): 1051-969 BCE — a fortified city in Judah in the early 10th c. BCE; Finkelstein, I. &amp; Piasetzky, E. 2010 — accept fortified cities in Judah during the 10th c. BCE; dispute Qeiyafa interpretation; Yadin, Y. 1958 (six-chambered gates as Solomonic) vs Finkelstein, I. 1996 (low chronology; gates reassigned to 9th-c. Omrides) — the attribution debate; Hardin, J., Rollston, C. &amp; Blakely, J. — Khirbet Summeily (Tell el-Hesi region) Iron IIA bullae: six official clay seals from 10th-c. BCE strata, administrative activity at the periphery — context for 10th-century administration; names no king, proves no reign (contextual, grade-neutral); 2026 evidence-state note (Oct 2026): no 2026 excavation has produced a contemporary 10th-century inscription naming Solomon; the Ophel monumental ritual baths (2026, Hebrew University — 1st c. CE/BCE), the Givati Parking Lot 586 BCE burnt beams (2026), and the missing City of David wall section all document periods after David/Solomon — grade-neutral by the attestation rule
Falsifiability: A contemporary 10th-century inscription naming Solomon of Jerusalem, or stratified evidence of a large 10th-century Jerusalem administration</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-970</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Genesis flood narrative</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The TEXT is multiply attested (MT, LXX, DSS, Samaritan Pentateuch) — attestation of the tradition is solid. It is single-source as a claim about an event: no contemporary extra-biblical witness to the Genesis flood as described. The literary-dependence sub-claim is CORROBORATED by the documented parallels and chronological priority.
Layer: real · Place: Judah/Yehud (composition context)
Dates: -900 → -450 (confidence: medium)

The Genesis flood narrative (Genesis 6-9) is a composite text — the Priestly and Yahwist sources interwoven — with a final form from the Persian period (5th c. BCE). Its structure (divine warning, ark, pitch caulking, birds released, sacrifice, divine promise) parallels the earlier Mesopotamian flood tradition so closely — down to specific plot sequences (the three-bird release: dove, swallow, raven in Gilgamesh XI vs raven, dove in Gen 8; the post-flood sacrifice and the deity's 'sweet savor' response) — that the scholarly majority holds the Genesis account was composed in literary dependence on the Mesopotamian tradition (Atrahasis/Gilgamesh), circulating via shared scribal culture.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 6-9 (Hebrew Bible; the oldest surviving witnesses are the Dead Sea Scrolls fragments, 2nd-1st c. BCE)
Sources: Tikva Frymer-Kensky, 'What the Babylonian Flood Stories Can and Cannot Teach Us About the Genesis Flood,' Biblical Archaeology Review 4:4 (1978); Jeffrey H. Tigay, The Evolution of the Gilgamesh Epic (1982); Genesis 6-9 (MT; LXX; DSS fragments)
Falsifiability: A Genesis flood manuscript older than the Mesopotamian tablets, or demonstration that the plot parallels are post-hoc pattern-matching</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.0,31.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The fravashis: Zoroastrian guardian spirits</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in the Avestan corpus (a full dedicated Yasht plus the calendar dedications), with an independent scholarly reference tradition from Bailey (1943) to Boyce (2001). Per the attestation rule this grades the belief, never the beings.
Layer: real · Place: Eastern Iran / southern Central Asia (Bactria–Margiana; the Avestan composition zone — disputed)
Dates: -900 → -400 (confidence: low)

The Avesta attests the fravashis (fravartis): guardian spirits — the pre-existing, higher doubles of every living being (humans, animals, even Ahura Mazda and the yazatas themselves) — who protect the soul, warn and admonish it through life, and return to the celestial realm at death. Yasht 13 (the Fravardin Yasht) is the dedicated hymn; the fravashis preside over the 19th day of the Zoroastrian month and the first month (Farvardin), whose feast (farvardin jashan) is kept especially by those who lost kin in the preceding year.

Earliest attestation: Yasht 13 (Fravardin Yasht), Young Avestan; Boyce's Encyclopaedia Iranica treatment (2001) fixes the scholarly baseline
Sources: Yasht 13 (Fravardin Yasht), the Avestan hymn to the fravashis — Avestan text with translation; Mary Boyce, 'Fravaši', Encyclopaedia Iranica, vol. 10 (2001), pp. 195–199; and 'Frawardīn Yašt', pp. 199–201; Harold W. Bailey, Zoroastrian Problems in the Ninth-Century Books (Ratanbai Katrak Lectures, Oxford: Clarendon, 1943) — the fravashi theological exegesis
Falsifiability: Proof that the Fravardin Yasht is a modern composition, or collapse of the Avestan manuscript tradition's antiquity, would collapse the attestation. (The grade concerns the belief's attestation; the beings are not graded.)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>62.5,36.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pentateuch as a composite work</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The composite character is visible in the documents themselves — doublet narratives, divine-name variation, divergent legal traditions, linguistic strata — and is the mainstream scholarly consensus; the exact source map (JEDP vs supplementary vs fragmentary) is contested, so the record claims compositeness, not the four-source inventory.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem / Yehud (compositional and redactional setting)
Dates: -0900 → -0400 (confidence: low)

The Pentateuch (Genesis-Deuteronomy) is a composite work assembled from multiple literary strata and redactional layers over several centuries, not a single composition by Moses in the Late Bronze Age; the classical four-source map (JEDP) is one model of those strata, not a settled document inventory.

Earliest attestation: The Pentateuchal texts themselves (doublets, divine-name variation, divergent legal traditions); Ketef Hinnom silver amulets c. 600 BCE (Priestly blessing = oldest biblical text); Dead Sea Scrolls Pentateuch manuscripts 3rd c. BCE–1st c. CE (pluriform textual traditions)
Sources: Julius Wellhausen, Prolegomena zur Geschichte Israels (1878; English 1885) — the classical documentary statement; Martin Noth, Überlieferungsgeschichte des Pentateuch (1948) — tradition-history extension of the model; John Van Seters, Abraham in History and Tradition (1975) and Prologue to History (1992) — the supplementary challenge; Rolf Rendtorff, The Problem of the Process of Transmission in the Pentateuch (1977; English 1990) — the fragmentary challenge; Erhard Blum, Studien zur Komposition des Pentateuch (1990) — the compositional/fragmentary synthesis; The Ketef Hinnom silver amulets (excavated 1979, published Barkay 1986; oldest biblical text, c. 600 BCE) — the latest date by which the Priestly blessing already existed; The Dead Sea Scrolls Pentateuch manuscripts (3rd c. BCE–1st c. CE) — the text still pluriform in the Second Temple period, consistent with a long editorial process; The Samaritan Pentateuch — an independent textual tradition witnessing the Pentateuch's pluriform transmission
Falsifiability: A single ancient manuscript of the full Pentateuch predating the supposed sources and matching the final text verbatim, or contemporary (Late Bronze Age) extra-biblical attestation of the complete five-book composition</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mesha Stele, Line 31 — the contested 'House of David' reading</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single contested attestation on a damaged line: two serious epigraphic camps (Lemaire/Delorme/Langlois for bt[d]wd via 2015-2022 imaging vs Finkelstein/Na'aman/Römer for 'Balak' via 2019 squeeze photos), with Richelle &amp; Burlingame holding the evidence inconclusive. The stele as artifact is CORROBORATED; this line's reading is CLAIMED.
Layer: real · Place: Dhiban (biblical Dibon), Jordan
Dates: -870 → -830 (confidence: medium)

Line 31 of the 9th-century BCE Mesha Stele (the Moabite Stone, found 1868 at Dhiban, now in the Louvre) reads 'bt[d]wd' — 'House of [Da]vid' — which would be a second extra-biblical attestation of the Davidic dynasty, older than the Tel Dan stele. The reading is genuinely contested and unresolved.

Earliest attestation: The stele itself (9th c. BCE); the 'House of David' reading proposed by André Lemaire 1994 on the basis of the squeeze and surviving letter traces
Sources: Mesha Stele (Louvre AO 5066), line 31 — the stone and the 1868 Caravacca squeeze; Lemaire, A. 1994. The 'House of David' reading of line 31 (bt[d]wd hypothesis); Finkelstein, I., Na'aman, N. &amp; Römer, T. 2019. 'Restoring Line 31 in the Mesha Stele: The "House of David" or Biblical Balak?', Tel Aviv 46/1 — the 'Balak' counter-reading from high-resolution squeeze photographs; Langlois, M. — independent RTI/Polynomial Texture Mapping study supporting bt[d]wd; Lemaire, A. &amp; Delorme, J.-P. 2022. RTI (USC West Semitic Research Project, 2015) + Louvre 2018 backlit squeeze photographs support the 'House of David' reading; Hendel, R. — the chronology objection: Balak lived ~200 years before David; 'nothing more than a guess'; Richelle, M. — the alleged dividing stroke sits on later plaster reconstruction, not the stone; and with Burlingame, A.: the evidence is inconclusive, the reading 'remains uncertain'
Falsifiability: A legible new image of the line, or a scholarly consensus accepting one reading and retiring the other, would move the grade up; demonstration that neither reading is epigraphically sustainable would move it to SPECULATION</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.7822,31.5006,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-870</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tel Dan Stele</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact is genuine and the 'House of David' reading is the mainstream consensus, though the text is fragmentary and a minority disputes the reading.
Layer: real · Place: Tel Dan
Dates: -870 → -835 (confidence: medium)

A 9th-century BCE Aramaic victory stele from Tel Dan, erected by a king of Damascus (likely Hazael), contains the phrase 'House of David' (bytdwd) — independent evidence for David's dynasty.

Earliest attestation: The stele fragments themselves, excavated at Tel Dan 1993-1994 (Avraham Biran)
Sources: Tel Dan stele fragments (Israel Museum); Biran and Naveh excavation reports (1993-1995); Arkeonews retrospective (26 July 2026): 'The Tel Dan Stele: the broken monument that changed the historical question' — states the consensus honestly (the stone did not prove every story told about David; it showed that by the 9th century BCE Judah's ruling line was known beyond its borders as the House of David; cites Biran &amp; Naveh 1993/1995 and the Israel Museum). Grade-neutral source, appended 2026-10-17 (Turin-shroud precedent — no supersession, grade held).; Langlois, 'The Tel Dan Inscription After 30 Years: A Fresh Look', Israel Exploration Journal 74:2 (RTI/3D study, reported Armstrong Institute Sept-Oct 2026): fragment A and fragments B1/B2 in different scribal hands (letter-form synoptic chart — beth, kaph, lamedh, resh differ systematically); B does not continue A's lines — the Biran/Naveh placement abandoned; the bytdwd ('House of David') reading in fragment A UNCHANGED, attestation grade-neutral
Falsifiability: A demonstrated alternative reading of the disputed line that the scholarly majority accepts</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.6522,33.2489,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-870</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Carthage Tophet: urns, stelae, and the very young</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply excavated and documented: thousands of burial urns with cremated infant remains under inscribed stelae, in a bounded precinct; the archaeology of the deposition is not in dispute — only its meaning is.
Layer: real · Place: Salammbo, Carthage (Tunisia) — the Tophet precinct
Dates: -0800 → -0146 (confidence: high)

At Carthage and other Phoenician-Punic settlements, walled precincts called tophets held thousands of urns with cremated infant remains beneath votive stelae dedicated to Tanit and Baal Hammon — a ritual deposition of the very young, c. 800-146 BCE.

Earliest attestation: The precinct itself: excavated at Salammbo, Carthage (Pricot de Sainte-Marie 1870s; full excavation 1921-; Delattre; Cintas); Punic votive stelae with Tanit/Baal Hammon dedications and 'mlk' terminology
Sources: Carthage Tophet excavations, Salammbo (Delattre, Cintas) — the precinct, the urn fields, the votive stelae to Tanit and Baal Hammon; ~thousands of urns; Punic votive inscriptions — 'mlk' (molk) offering terminology; cf. Heider 1985, Moscati 1987; 2 Kings 23:10 — Topheth in the Valley of Hinnom; Jeremiah 7:31-32, 19:5-6; Leviticus 18:21 (Molech) — the biblical Tophet tradition; Diodorus Siculus 20.14 — the late, hostile Greek account of Carthaginian sacrifice (the brazen bull of Kronos); treated as secondary and hostile, not as neutral evidence
Falsifiability: Proof that the Carthage urn field is a modern forgery or misidentified would collapse the record; the record grades the precinct and the urns, not the motive — motive is carried separately at evt-tophet-systematic-sacrifice-001.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>10.3242,36.8417,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>YHWH's courtroom: the Hebrew Bible's own divine council</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The texts exist and are independently multiply attested; the council vocabulary and the Deut 32:8 variant are documented philology, not interpretation.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem / Judea (textual origin)
Dates: -800 → -100 (confidence: low)

The Hebrew Bible preserves a working divine council, not a discarded metaphor: Psalm 82 opens 'God stands in the council of El (baʿădat-ʾēl)' and verse 6 declares 'I said, you are gods, sons of the Most High'; 1 Kings 22:19–23 stages a courtroom — 'I saw YHWH sitting on his throne, and all the host of heaven standing beside him on his right and on his left,' debating strategy and commissioning a 'lying spirit'; Job 1:6 and 2:1 show 'the sons of God came to present themselves before YHWH'; Daniel 7:10 has 'a thousand thousands served him, and ten thousand times ten thousand stood before him.' And the Deut 32:8–9 textual variant proves the council concept was live and being edited: 4QDeutj (Dead Sea Scrolls, ~2nd c. BCE) reads 'according to the number of the SONS OF GOD' (bĕnê ʾĕlōhîm), the Septuagint reads 'angels of God,' while the later Masoretic Text reads 'sons of Israel' — two oldest witnesses against the standardized one, with the lectio difficilior ('sons of God') original (Heiser 2001; Skehan 1954; NRSV renders 'according to the number of the gods'). The older reading makes Elyon divide the nations among his divine sons, with YHWH receiving Jacob as his allotted portion.

Earliest attestation: Psalm 82; 1 Kings 22:19–23; Job 1–2; Deuteronomy 32:8–9; manuscript witnesses: 4QDeutj (Qumran, 2nd c. BCE), Septuagint (3rd c. BCE), Masoretic Text (standardized ~1st c. CE)
Sources: Psalm 82; 1 Kings 22:19–23; Job 1:6, 2:1; Daniel 7:10; Deuteronomy 32:8–9; 4QDeutj (Dead Sea Scrolls), Septuagint, Masoretic Text — Deut 32:8 variants; Michael S. Heiser, 'Deuteronomy 32:8 and the Sons of God' (2001); Patrick W. Skehan (1954); Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford, 2001); The Early History of God (2002)
Falsifiability: A manuscript tradition of Psalm 82 with no council reading at all periods, or a Deuteronomy 32 manuscript older than 4QDeutj reading 'sons of Israel'</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Manu's flood</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Textually attested in a datable Sanskrit prose tradition. No contemporary corroboration as an event; graded as a CLAIMED tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Kuru-Pancala region (Brahmana composition context)
Dates: -800 → -500 (confidence: medium)

Manu's flood: the Shatapatha Brahmana (1.8.1) tells of Manu warned by a fish (the Matsya avatar of Vishnu) of a great flood; he builds a ship, the fish tows it to the northern mountain, and Manu survives to become the progenitor of mankind. One of the earliest non-Mesopotamian flood narratives with the warned-survivor-ship structure (c. 8th-6th c. BCE). It shares the skeleton — warning, vessel, surviving seed of humanity — with the Mesopotamian and Genesis floods, graded as a literary attestation, not a dated event.

Earliest attestation: Shatapatha Brahmana 1.8.1.1-6 (Vajasaneyi recension)
Sources: Shatapatha Brahmana 1.8.1 (trans. J. Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East)
Falsifiability: N/A as event claim — this record grades the attestation of the tradition only</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>80.0,26.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Greek vowel innovation (8th c. BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The letter-names are the confession: the Greeks kept the Phoenician names (aleph→alpha) and the Phoenician order, and the repurposed signs map exactly onto Greek's missing vowels. The innovation is real and multiply attested; the single-event 8th-century framing is scholarly inference, so it stops below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Greece (Dipylon, Athens — the oinochoe's findspot; the adaptation's exact location is unknown)
Dates: -800 → -700 (confidence: medium)

In the 8th century BCE the Greeks adapted the Phoenician consonantal abjad by repurposing unused consonant signs as vowels — aleph became alpha, he became epsilon, yod became iota, ayin became omicron — creating the first true alphabet: a writing system that records vowels as well as consonants, and the ancestor of the Latin and Cyrillic scripts.

Earliest attestation: 8th-century BCE Greek inscriptions (Dipylon oinochoe, c. 740 BCE — among the earliest); the letter-names themselves are the evidence — alpha, beta, gamma preserve the Semitic names (aleph, bet, gimel) and their order
Sources: Powell, B.B. (1991), Homer and the Origin of the Greek Alphabet — the standard account of the adaptation; The letter-name evidence: alpha/beta/gamma from aleph/bet/gimel, order preserved — the transmission's own fingerprint
Falsifiability: An earlier Greek vowel-inscription securely predating Phoenician contact, or positive demonstration that the Greek letter-names derive from a non-Semitic source</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.73,37.98,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The messengers: mal'akhim, cherubim, seraphim, and the divine council</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The BELIEF-phenomenon is multiply attested across independent textual layers spanning ~800 years, with iconographic parallels for the cherubim. The grade covers the attestation, never the entities.
Layer: real · Place: Ancient Judah (textual origin)
Dates: -800 → -165 (confidence: medium)

The Hebrew Bible attests, across textual layers spanning roughly eight centuries, to a populated heaven: mal'akhim ('messengers' — over 200 occurrences, about half human and half heavenly), the mal'akh YHWH who speaks as God himself, cherubim (throne-guardians), seraphim (Isaiah 6 only), the bene elohim and the divine council, named angels only in the latest texts (Gabriel, Michael — Daniel), and ha-satan as a title ('the accuser') becoming a proper name only in the latest text (1 Chronicles 21:1).

Earliest attestation: Hebrew Bible: Genesis 18-19 (the visitors), Exodus 3 (the burning bush), Isaiah 6 (seraphim), Ezekiel 1 and 10 (cherubim), Job 1-2 (ha-satan, bene elohim), Psalm 82 and 1 Kings 22:19-23 (the divine council), Zechariah 3 (the satan as accuser), Daniel 8-12 (Gabriel, Michael — the only named angels in the Hebrew Bible), 1 Chronicles 21:1 (satan as proper name)
Sources: Hebrew Bible — Genesis 18-19; Exodus 3; Isaiah 6; Ezekiel 1, 10; Job 1-2, 38:7; Psalm 82; 1 Kings 22:19-23; Zechariah 3; Daniel 8-12; 1 Chronicles 21:1; Genesis 3:24; Exodus 25:18-22; New Interpreter's Dictionary of the Bible, s.v. 'Angel' / 'Cherub, Cherubim' / 'Seraph, Seraphs': mal'akh cognate with Semitic l'k ('to send'), 'a messenger' — of 200+ OT occurrences about half designate humans and half heavenly beings; 'the winged seraphim and cherubim are classes of heavenly beings distinguishable from angels in general'; Hamori, Esther J., God's Monsters: Vengeful Spirits, Deadly Angels, Hungry Giants, and Other Divine Creatures of the Bible (Broadleaf Books, 2023); Cherubim iconographic parallels: winged sphinx/lion throne-guardians in Syro-Hittite and Phoenician art (e.g., the Ahiram sarcophagus; the Megiddo ivories) — the visual world the texts describe
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the cited texts are modern forgeries would overturn the attestation; a securely dated pre-exilic Hebrew text describing a winged mal'akh would overturn the wings distinction carried in evt-winged-angels-001</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The terra-cotta oil lamp: the Mediterranean's light</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines: hundreds of thousands of excavated lamps in museum catalogues (the Getty alone catalogues ~630 lamps, 800 BCE-800 CE), the Loeschcke/Dressel typologies, and the Mutina factory-town excavation (Modena) with hundreds of lamps bearing maker's stamps (FORTIS, Strobili, Communis, Phoetaspi, Eucarpi) — mass production with brand names and pirated copies, documented by the excavator Donato Labate.
Layer: real · Place: The Mediterranean basin; factory cluster at Mutina (Modena), Italy
Dates: -800 → 1850 (confidence: high)

The terra-cotta oil lamp was the dominant source of artificial light across the Mediterranean world for millennia — a palm-sized clay vessel of oil and a wick, mass-produced in Roman times by named workshops whose stamps survive on the clay.

Earliest attestation: Excavated lamps, thousands of years of stratigraphy; Loeschcke's 1919 Vindonissa typology is the modern reference system
Sources: Discovery News / ANI via The Free Library, 7 Dec 2008 — the Modena factory-town excavation: 'hundreds of oil lamps, each bearing their maker's name'; FORTIS 'one of the earliest examples of pirated brands'; Neagu &amp; Barbu, 'Roman Lucernae Discovered West of the Roman Fort' (Cercetari Arheologice XXXII, 2025) — FORTIS officina at Savignano sul Panaro near Mutina (Modena); Loeschcke IX type, early 1st c. AD; stamp variants across the empire; Hyperallergic, 'Ancient Mediterranean Oil Lamps Capture the Pop Culture of Their Time' — the Getty's free digital catalogue 'Ancient Lamps in the J. Paul Getty Museum' (Bussiere &amp; Lindros Wohl), ~630 lamps produced 800 BCE-800 CE, 'the basic engineering of oil lamps remained the same for thousands of years'
Falsifiability: A Mediterranean settlement of the Roman period with no oil-lamp finds and a different documented primary light source would weaken the 'dominant' claim; proof the Firmalampen stamps are modern forgeries would kill the mass-production claim</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>10.92,44.65,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sulba diagonal rule — Baudhayana's general statement, c. 800-500 BCE</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The statement is real and directly quoted in the text, but the composition date is contested and no transmission chain to or from Babylon or Greece is attested — independent formulation is the honest default, not a proven fact.
Layer: real · Place: Northern India (Vedic cultural zone — no findspot; textual tradition)
Dates: -800 → -500 (confidence: low)

The Baudhayana Sulba Sutra — the earliest of the Indian Sulba Sutras, appendices to the Vedas giving rules for constructing sacrificial altars — states the diagonal rule in general form: the rope stretched along the diagonal of a rectangle produces the area which the vertical and horizontal sides produce together. An explicit general statement of the Pythagorean relationship, conventionally dated centuries before Pythagoras.

Earliest attestation: The Sulba Sutra corpus itself (Sanskrit); Baudhayana the earliest of the four major Sulba authors
Sources: Baudhayana Sulba Sutra 1.12 (the diagonal-verse: dīrghasyākṣaṇayā rajjuḥ pārśvamānī, tiryaṅmānī, cha yatpṛthagbhūte kurutastadubhayāṅ karoti); Apastamba Sulba Sutra (the companion statement and numerical demonstrations)
Falsifiability: A firm scholarly redating of the Baudhayana Sulba Sutra to after c. 500 BCE would collapse the priority claim; a documented transmission route (Babylon↔India↔Greece) would change the independence fence</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>78.0,27.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'They burned their own children' — the systematic-sacrifice interpretation of the Tophet</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Genuinely contested between two peer-reviewed camps: the dental-estimation camp (Smith et al. 2013, Antiquity 87(338)) reads a skewed age profile as selection; the cranial-estimation camp (Schwartz et al. 2010, PLoS ONE 5(2): e9177) reads natural perinatal mortality. Both studied the same urns. The question is unresolved.
Layer: real · Place: Salammbo, Carthage (Tunisia) — the Tophet precinct
Dates: -0800 → -0146 (confidence: medium)

The Carthaginians systematically sacrificed their own live, healthy children — burning firstborns to Baal Hammon and Tanit as a regular rite, not an emergency measure.

Earliest attestation: The hostile classical tradition (Diodorus Siculus 20.14, 3rd-century-BCE-rooted Greek accounts) plus the biblical Molech/Tophet passages; the modern archaeological debate begins with Stager &amp; Wolff 1984
Sources: Stager &amp; Wolff 1984, 'Child Sacrifice at Carthage: Religious Rite or Population Control?', Biblical Archaeology Review (Jan/Feb 1984) — the founding modern argument for sacrifice from the urn evidence; Schwartz, Houghton, Macchiarelli &amp; Bondioli 2010, 'Skeletal Remains from Punic Carthage Do Not Support Systematic Sacrifice of Infants', PLoS ONE 5(2): e9177 — 'the age distribution is consistent with modern-day data on perinatal mortality'; tophets as 'cemeteries for the remains of human prenates and infants who died from a variety of causes'; Smith, Stager, Greene &amp; Houck 2013, 'Age estimations attest to infant sacrifice at the Carthage Tophet', Antiquity 87(338) — rebuttal: teeth (not heat-shrunk cranial bones) are the reliable clock; the majority died between one and one-and-a-half months, a profile 'markedly different from that expected in the case of death from natural causes'; University of Pittsburgh press release (Feb 22, 2010), quoting Jeffrey H. Schwartz: 'Our results show that some children were sacrificed, but they contradict the conclusion that Carthaginians were a brutal bunch who regularly sacrificed their own children.'
Falsifiability: A securely-dated Punic text explicitly describing routine live-child sacrifice as policy would promote the grade; a complete, agreed osteological profile matching natural mortality across all excavated urns would push the systematic reading toward DEBUNKED.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>10.3242,36.8417,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bur-Sagale Eclipse — the 15 June 763 BCE anchor</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A near-contemporary administrative record (a year-name, not omen literature), independently cross-checked by Ptolemy's Canon and by retrocalculated eclipse visibility — but the reign-year assignment wobbles across sources (Ashur-dan II vs III; 9th/10th/11th year), so it falls one step short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Assur, Assyria (observation point inferred; computed greatest-eclipse point lies east of the Caspian at 38.9N 54.3E — the identification depends on ΔT-corrected visibility at Assur/Nineveh)
Dates: -763 → -763 (confidence: high)

The Neo-Assyrian eponym (limmu) list entry for the year of the official Bur-Sagale records a solar eclipse in the month Simanu; modern retrocalculation identifies it as the total solar eclipse of 15 June 763 BCE (proleptic Julian calendar). This identification is the keystone anchoring the Neo-Assyrian absolute chronology (912–609 BCE).

Earliest attestation: Neo-Assyrian limmu (eponym) lists; the surviving lists run 911–631 BCE and record the year of 'Bur-Sagale of Guzana' with the note that in month Simanu 'an eclipse of the sun took place.'
Sources: Assyrian limmu/eponym lists (multiple surviving tablets); Canon of Ptolemy (747–631 BCE overlap with the eponym lists); Five Millennium Canon of Solar Eclipses, NASA/GSFC (Saros 44; greatest eclipse 38.9N 54.3E); F.R. Stephenson, Historical Eclipses and Earth's Rotation (ΔT modeling underlying the visibility claim); Wikipedia, 'Assyrian eclipse' and 'Chronology of the ancient Near East' (cross-checks; cites refs 25–30 on ΔT uncertainty); arXiv:2007.07141, 'The Double Eclipse at the Downfall of Old Babylon' (the contrast case: EAE #20's eclipse pair cannot anchor chronology cleanly under any of three interpretations); G. van Driel, 'The Assyrian Eponym Chronicle and the Eclipse' literature; Parpola 2007 eponym canon (912–648 BCE) and Novotny &amp; Jeffers 2018 prosopography (648–609 BCE, ranking contested)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Bur-Sagale entry's 'eclipse' refers to a different phenomenon or is a scribal error; or a securely-dated alternative eclipse candidate visible at Assur within the eponymy window</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.26,35.46,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-763</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bur-Sagale Eclipse — the 15 June 763 BCE anchor (second pass, 2026-10-16)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A near-contemporary administrative record (a year-name, not omen literature), independently cross-checked by Ptolemy's Canon and by retrocalculated eclipse visibility — but the tablet's own text is vague, the totality and observation-point readings are modern inference (Hunger 2006), and the reign-year assignment wobbles across sources (Ashur-dan III 10th year mainstream, 9th/11th variants live), so it falls one step short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Assur, Assyria (observation point inferred; computed greatest-eclipse point lies east of the Caspian at 38.9N 54.3E — the identification depends on ΔT-corrected visibility at Assur/Nineveh)
Dates: -763 → -763 (confidence: high)

The Neo-Assyrian eponym (limmu) list entry for the year of the official Bur-Sagale records a solar eclipse in the month Simanu; modern retrocalculation identifies it as the total solar eclipse of 15 June 763 BCE (proleptic Julian calendar). This identification is the keystone anchoring the Neo-Assyrian absolute chronology (912–609 BCE).

Earliest attestation: Neo-Assyrian limmu (eponym) lists; the surviving lists run 911–631 BCE and record the year of 'Bur-Sagale of Guzana' with the note that in month Simanu 'an eclipse of the sun took place.'
Sources: Assyrian limmu/eponym lists (multiple surviving tablets); Rawlinson 1866 publication; Millard 1994 translation of the Bur-Sagale entry; Hermann Hunger, 'How uncertain is Mesopotamian chronology?' (tidsskrift.dk, MoDIA) — the &gt;95%-totality/Assur-visibility inference stated as modern reasoning; Canon of Ptolemy (747–631 BCE overlap with the eponym lists); Five Millennium Canon of Solar Eclipses, NASA/GSFC (Saros 44; total; gamma 0.27139; magnitude 1.05960; greatest eclipse 38.9N 54.3E); F.R. Stephenson, Historical Eclipses and Earth's Rotation (ΔT modeling underlying the visibility claim); The 800–750 BCE uniqueness finding (the 763 BCE eclipse is the only solar eclipse visible over Assyria in that window); Wikipedia, 'Assyrian eclipse' and 'Chronology of the ancient Near East' (cross-checks; 9th/11th-year variants; cites refs 25–30 on ΔT uncertainty); arXiv:2007.07141, 'The Double Eclipse at the Downfall of Old Babylon' (the contrast case: EAE #20's eclipse pair cannot anchor chronology cleanly under any of three interpretations); S. Parpola 2007 eponym canon (912–648 BCE) and Novotny &amp; Jeffers 2018 prosopography (648–609 BCE, ranking contested); Wikipedia, 'Eponym dating system' (limmu sortition → fixed rotation; Karum-Kanesh origins; Revised Eponym List 1972–1718 BCE; the REL-127 eclipse of 5 Aug 1845 BCE — the parallel eclipse-anchor case); Cornell / Manning et al. PLOS ONE (2016): dendro+14C middle-chronology convergence via Shamshi-Adad I bullae — the Old Assyrian anchor the REL eclipse cross-checks; Wikipedia, 'Chronology of the ancient Near East' (page updated October 2026 — cross-checked 2026-10-17): middle chronology still 'the most commonly seen solution' with 'far from a consensus' stated; the October 2026 text is consistent with the held record state — no 2026 development on any chronology leg
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Bur-Sagale entry's 'eclipse' refers to a different phenomenon or is a scribal error; or a securely-dated alternative eclipse candidate visible at Assur within the eponymy window</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.26,35.46,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-763</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The pyramids of Nubia</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Directly countable extant structures, aerially photographed for a century, inventoried in the standard archaeological literature; the ~255 figure is stable across reference works.
Layer: real · Place: Meroë / Begrawiya (representative necropolis; El-Kurru, Nuri, Jebel Barkal also pinned)
Dates: -751 → 350 (confidence: high)

The Kingdom of Kush built roughly 255 pyramids in Sudan — at El-Kurru, Nuri, Jebel Barkal, and Meroë (Begrawiya) — from 751 BCE through the Meroitic period (c. 350 CE): steeper (~70°), smaller-footprint royal tombs with attached chapels, numbering more than twice Egypt's 118–138.

Earliest attestation: The structures themselves (extant); first systematically documented by Reisner's Harvard-Boston expeditions 1908–1932; UNESCO listing 2011
Sources: Reisner, Harvard-Boston excavations at Kurru, Nuri, Barkal, Meroë (1908–1932) — the standing corpus; UNESCO: Archaeological Sites of the Island of Meroe (2011) — the Begrawiya necropolises; National Geographic reporting on the Nubian pyramids: construction c. 900 BCE–400 CE, ~255 structures; Lepsius, Denkmäler (1849–1859) — the pre-Ferlini survey; Ferlini's 1830s destruction recorded in his own account and Lepsius's check
Falsifiability: A full modern inventory showing fewer Nubian than Egyptian pyramids — contradicted by the catalogued standing structures; the Ferlini-destroyed 40 are counted from his and Lepsius's records</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.75,16.94,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-751</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Olympian assembly: Zeus's council in Homer</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The poems exist in an ancient manuscript tradition; the council scenes are multiple and internally consistent across both epics.
Layer: real · Place: Ionia (composition); Mount Olympus (imagined setting)
Dates: -750 → -650 (confidence: medium)

Homer's Olympus runs on council procedure: the gods are formally summoned to assembly (Iliad 20.4–30: Zeus bids Themis 'call the gods to council in the starry hall' — the senate of the skies convenes, every god seated on marble thrones, Zeus presiding and announcing his policy), deliberate, and vote by acclaim; the Odyssey opens in council (1.26–95: the gods assembled at Zeus's house, Athena opening the case for Odysseus while Poseidon is absent at the Ethiopians' feast — absence counted, debate recorded); and Iliad 16.433–461 shows the council's limit: Zeus holds the golden scales for Hector but does not overrule fate. The Olympian assembly is the Greek world's picture of how power is exercised among unequals — a constitutional monarchy of gods with Zeus as chairman, not absolute sovereign.

Earliest attestation: Homer, Iliad 20.4–30; Iliad 1.544–559; Iliad 16.433–461; Odyssey 1.26–95
Sources: Homer, Iliad 20.4–30; 1.544–559; 16.433–461; Odyssey 1.26–95; Perseus Project / Tufts editions of the Homeric texts
Falsifiability: Proof the poems are late fabrications rather than archaic compositions, or that the assembly passages are interpolations</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.5,37.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pazuzu: the demon you hire</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — First attestation dated by curatorial scholarship; apotropaic function confirmed by incantation texts on the artifacts.
Layer: real · Place: Assyria/Babylonia
Dates: -750 → -600 (confidence: medium)

The demon Pazuzu — first attested in the late 8th century BCE — was invoked in amulets and healing rituals as protection AGAINST the demoness Lamashtu: Mesopotamians used one feared spirit to ward off another.

Earliest attestation: Neo-Assyrian amulets and plaques; Heeßel 2002
Sources: Harvard Art Museums Pazuzu plaque (first attestation late 8th c. BCE); Heeßel, Pazuzu (Brill/Styx 2002); Pazuzu amulets, seals, fibulae (British Museum, Met); Niederreiter, Z., 'Two Pazuzu-head amulets inscribed with the Standard B incantation' (Revue d'assyriologie et d'archéologie orientale 111, 2017, pp. 109–132) — the unpublished bronze H.894 Pazuzu-head pendant (BnF, de l'Écluse bequest) with an abbreviated Standard B incantation + the re-examined AO 1197 steatite head (Louvre); six known inscribed heads total; Toledo Museum of Art, 'Cursed! The Power of Magic in the Ancient World' (2026, shown at the Getty Villa; guest curator Jeffrey Spier; catalogue with Foy Scalf and Gina Konstantopoulos; loans from the British Museum, Louvre, and Met, Pazuzu head amulet as a key piece) — serious citable exhibition venue corroborating the apotropaic-Pazuzu reading in public display; grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent, appended 2026-10-18 (IV.25 sixth pass)
Falsifiability: Securely dated earlier attestations would push the date back</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.0,36.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kushite pharaohs — Egypt's 25th Dynasty</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested: the Kushite royal inscriptions, the Egyptian temple record (Karnak), and the Assyrian royal annals all name the same kings in the same sequence, with building programs and burials to match.
Layer: real · Place: Thebes (representative seat of Kushite rule in Egypt; Napata in Nubia)
Dates: -747 → -656 (confidence: high)

The 25th Dynasty of Egypt (c. 747–656 BCE) was a line of Kushite (Nubian) kings — Piye, Shabaka, Shebitku, Taharqa, Tantamani — who conquered and ruled all of Egypt for ninety-one years, restoring temples, reviving Old and Middle Kingdom art, and being expelled only by the Assyrian Empire.

Earliest attestation: Piye's Victory Stela (commissioned in his regnal year 21, c. 727 BCE; dark-gray granite, found at Jebel Barkal 1862, Cairo JE 48862) — the conquest in the conqueror's own words
Sources: Piye's Victory Stela (Jebel Barkal; Cairo JE 48862) — year-21 account of the conquest; Assyrian royal annals: Esarhaddon's and Ashurbanipal's prisms — Taharqa and Tantamani as named adversaries (hostile external witness); Karnak building inscriptions and Taharqa's constructions (Kawa, Jebel Barkal, Karnak) — the temple-restoration program; The Nuri and El-Kurru pyramids themselves (Kurru 15–18, Nuri 1) — the royal burials in Nubian form; Smarthistory: 'King Piye and the Kushite control of Egypt' (with British Museum object references)
Falsifiability: Proof that Piye's stela is a fabrication or that the Assyrian annals' named Kushite adversaries (Taharqa, Tantamani) are invented — no serious scholar holds this</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.61,25.72,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-747</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Qedarites — the Arab tribal confederacy of the Neo-Assyrian annals, and the biblical Kedar ethnonym</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Qedarite confederacy is multiply attested in independent primary documents: five Neo-Assyrian reigns' royal inscriptions (enemy witnesses — the empire's own tax and war ledgers, not the tribes' self-description) plus the Hebrew prophetic corpus (a separate literary tradition). The Assyrian annals and the biblical texts are genuinely independent witnesses to the same ethnonym and the same region.
Layer: real · Place: North Arabian desert oases, south of Damascus toward the northern Hejaz (traditional)
Dates: -738 → -626 (confidence: high)

A North Arabian tribal confederation called Qedar (KUR qi-id-ri / qa-da-ri) is attested across Neo-Assyrian royal inscriptions of the 8th–7th centuries BCE: queen Zabibe's tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III (738 BCE, the first explicit mention), Tiglath-Pileser's 733 BCE campaign against queen Samsi south of Damascus, and further attestations under Sennacherib, Esarhaddon, and Ashurbanipal (including the war against Abi-Yate' the Qedarite, RINAP 5 Ashurbanipal 11 IX 36). Assyrian sources name seven Arab queens (Zabibe, Samsi, Yati'e, Apkallatu, Te'elhunu, Tabu'a, Adiya) ruling the Arabs — documented female leadership of a confederacy whose trade routes (Damascus, Tyre, incense aromatics) the empire bargained with. The biblical Kedar (qēdār, 'dark') — second son of Ishmael in Genesis 25:13 — recurs as an ethnonym across the prophetic corpus: the glory of Kedar (Isaiah 21:16–17), the archers and mighty men of Kedar, the tents and flocks of Kedar (Jeremiah 49:28–32), the princes of Kedar trading with Tyre (Ezekiel 27:21), 'I dwell among the tents of Kedar' (Psalm 120:5), 'black as the tents of Kedar' (Song 1:5). The ethnonym Qedar/Kedar is real and independently multiply attested. The kinship frame — 'Kedar, son of Ishmael' — is the biblical tradition's claim about that ethnonym, not the Assyrian record's.

Earliest attestation: Tiglath-Pileser III's annals: Zabibe of Qedar tribute, 738 BCE (first explicit mention by name).
Sources: Tiglath-Pileser III annals — tribute of Zabibe of Qedar, 738 BCE; 733 BCE campaign against queen Samsi; RINAP 5, Ashurbanipal 11 IX 36 — war against Abi-Yate' the Qedarite; RINAP 5, Ashurbanipal 11 IX 65 (Arab land calamity passage); Sennacherib and Esarhaddon annals — further Qedarite attestations; under Esarhaddon/Ashurbanipal 'Qedarite' approaches synonymy with 'Arabs' in the annals; Genesis 25:13 (Kedar as second son of Ishmael); Isaiah 21:16–17, 42:11, 60:7; Jeremiah 49:28–32, 2:10; Ezekiel 27:21; Psalm 120:5; Song 1:5; Eph'al, Israel. The Ancient Arabs: Nomads on the Borders of the Fertile Crescent, 9th–5th Centuries B.C. (Jerusalem/Leiden, 1982) — the Qedarite dossier; demolition of the Delitzsch/Thompson/Lewy Šumuʾilu = Ishmaelites identification (historical + phonetic grounds); Knauf, Ernst Axel. Ismael: Untersuchungen zur Geschichte Palästinas und Nordarabiens im 1. Jahrtausend v. Chr. (Wiesbaden, 1985)
Falsifiability: A securely dated Qedarite inscription attesting descent from an eponym Ishmael would corroborate the kinship frame (it does not exist). A secure redaction date for Genesis 25:12–18 earlier than the 738 BCE Assyrian attestations would change the direction of the ethnonym's documentation; later than the 6th c. would favor the literary-composition hypothesis.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>38.5,29.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-738</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Isaiah 7:14: almah to parthenos</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Both readings are multiply attested in the manuscript traditions; the fork's location at the 3rd-2nd c. BCE translation is documented, not inferred.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (oracle); Alexandria (translation)
Dates: -0730 → 0090 (confidence: medium)

Isaiah 7:14's Hebrew reads ha-almah ('the young woman'); the Septuagint's 3rd-2nd-century-BCE Jewish translators rendered it parthenos ('virgin'); the Gospel of Matthew (1:23) quotes the Greek, not the Hebrew — so the 'virgin birth prophecy' reading enters the record at the translation, centuries after the oracle.

Earliest attestation: Hebrew: Masoretic Text and 1QIsaa (2nd c. BCE, same reading ha-almah); Greek: Septuagint (parthenos); the fork is visible in the witnesses themselves
Sources: HALOT (Koehler-Baumgartner): almah from root '-l-m III; the GOARCH survey counts nine biblical occurrences, all 'young woman' outside liturgical uses; The three 2nd-c. CE Greek revisions (Aquila, Symmachus, Theodotion) render neanis ('young woman') — the parthenos reading is the LXX's choice, not the Greek tradition's consensus; 1QIsaa (Great Isaiah Scroll, 2nd c. BCE): reads 'lmh, agreeing with the Masoretic Text against parthenos; Matthew 1:22-23 quotes Isaiah 7:14 as parthenos — the citation follows the LXX
Falsifiability: A pre-Septuagint Hebrew witness reading betulah at Isaiah 7:14, or evidence the LXX translators worked from a Hebrew Vorlage reading 'virgin' explicitly</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-730</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hesiod's golden-race daimones: the word's original meaning</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The poem exists in an ancient manuscript tradition; the daimon-guardian passage is multiply cited and undisputed in the text.
Layer: real · Place: Boeotia (composition); pan-Hellenic circulation
Dates: -725 → -675 (confidence: medium)

In Works and Days (lines 122-126), Hesiod says the men of the golden race, once the earth covered them, became 'pure daimones' dwelling on the earth — kindly guardians of mortal men who roam everywhere 'clothed in mist,' keep watch on judgments and cruel deeds, and give wealth. This is the earliest surviving attestation of daimōn as a class of guardian spirit-beings, and it fixes the word's original semantic range: benevolent, intermediary, never worshipped, never evil.

Earliest attestation: Hesiod, Works and Days 122-126
Sources: Hesiod, Works and Days 122-126 (Evelyn-White trans.; Perseus Project / Tufts); E.R. Dodds, The Greeks and the Irrational (1951), ch. 2 on daimones; Walter Burkert, Greek Religion (1985), on daimones as intermediary powers
Falsifiability: A pre-Hesiodic text using daimōn in the guardian sense (would push the attestation earlier), or demonstration that WD 122-126 is a later interpolation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.5,38.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-725</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Hanging Gardens at Nineveh (Dalley's relocation)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Dalley's reading is influential and textually grounded, but it remains one scholar's identification of the Wonder — not a scholarly consensus, and the Greek 'Babylon' tradition is not disproven.
Layer: real · Place: Nineveh (near Mosul, Iraq)
Dates: -0704 → -0681 (confidence: medium)

The 'Hanging Gardens' were built at Nineveh by the Assyrian king Sennacherib (r. 704-681 BCE), a century before Nebuchadnezzar: his prism inscriptions describe an 'unrivalled palace' and a high garden 'a wonder for all peoples' imitating the Amanus mountains, watered by a bronze water-raising screw and the Jerwan aqueduct system.

Earliest attestation: Sennacherib's own prism inscriptions (contemporary, early 7th c. BCE); Nineveh palace reliefs (Sennacherib's palace and Ashurbanipal's North Palace, 7th c. BCE); Stephanie Dalley, The Mystery of the Hanging Garden of Babylon (Oxford University Press, 2013)
Sources: Sennacherib's prisms: 'I raised the height of the surroundings of the palace to be a Wonder for all peoples... A high garden imitating the Amanus mountains I laid out next to it with all kinds of aromatic plants' — plus the bronze water-raising screw passage (Dalley's retranslation of a text mistranslated in the 1920s); The Jerwan aqueduct and Nineveh canal system (Sennacherib's waterworks; inscription: 'Sennacherib king of the world... Over a great distance, I had a watercourse directed to the environs of Nineveh'); The Nineveh garden relief (British Museum; Ashurbanipal's North Palace): terraced trees, arched aqueduct, pavilion — the most complete Assyrian royal-garden image; excavators' drawings of the lost Sennacherib-palace tiered-garden relief; Dalley 2013 (the relocation thesis); Archaeology Magazine Nov/Dec 2025 'Secrets of the Seven Wonders' (recent survey of the argument)
Falsifiability: Demonstration that Sennacherib's garden passages describe an unexceptional palace park with no 'hanging' mechanism; or a contemporary Babylonian attestation settling the Babylon location</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.1528,36.359,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-704</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Wall of China</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The walls exist, are UNESCO-listed (1987), and were measured in the first comprehensive survey (2012); multi-dynasty construction is attested archaeologically and in texts.
Layer: real · Place: Badaling — representative Ming-era section; the system spans 15 provinces, Shanhaiguan to Jiayuguan
Dates: -0700 → 1644 (confidence: high)

The Great Wall of China is a system of fortification walls, trenches, passes, and watchtowers built across northern China by multiple states and dynasties from the 7th century BCE through the Ming dynasty (1368-1644). The first comprehensive archaeological survey (State Administration of Cultural Heritage, 2007-2012) measured 21,196.18 km of wall-related remains across 15 provinces; Ming-era structures alone were preliminarily measured at 8,850 km.

Earliest attestation: The walls themselves (archaeology); textual attestation of early walls in Warring States texts and Sima Qian's Shiji on Qin joining
Sources: State Administration of Cultural Heritage survey 2007-2012: 21,196.18 km, 43,721 sites (announced 5-8 Jun 2012, Xinhua/China Daily); UNESCO World Heritage listing of the Great Wall, 1987; SACH preliminary Ming-only figure: 8,850 km (2008/2009); Only 8.2% of Ming-era structures remain intact (SACH 2012 report); First walls: Warring States 475-221 BCE; Qin joined stretches c. 220 BCE (Sima Qian's Shiji)
Falsifiability: A full independent resurvey substantially contradicting the SACH 2012 figures, or proof the dated sections are misattributed</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>116.57,40.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lilith: the name's fifteen-century paper trail</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The WORD is multiply attested across independent textual layers spanning ~1,500 years, from biblical Hebrew through Qumran, the Talmud, and buried magic texts. The grade covers the name's attestation, never the being — and never the first-wife story, which is separately graded.
Layer: real · Place: Ancient Judah (textual origin); Qumran; Sasanian Babylonia
Dates: -700 → 900 (confidence: medium)

The name 'Lilith' has a continuous written trail across ~1,500 years: a single biblical occurrence (Isaiah 34:14, 'night creature' — rendered 'screech owl' in the KJV), the first clear supernatural usage in the Songs of the Sage (4Q510/11, late 1st c. BCE), Talmudic mentions (b. Shabbat 151b; b. Eruvin 18b; b. Niddah 24b), the incantation bowls (Lilith bound by name; Ktar-Lilith iconography), and the medieval Alphabet of Ben Sira — the name's attestation CORROBORATED as belief-phenomenon; the first-wife story is a separate, later claim (see evt-lilith-first-wife-genesis-001).

Earliest attestation: Isaiah 34:14 — the sole biblical occurrence of the word (a hapax in context, in a list of desert creatures)
Sources: Hebrew Bible — Isaiah 34:14 (sole occurrence); 4Q510–511, Songs of the Sage (late 1st c. BCE) — first clear usage of 'lilith' as a supernatural creature, alongside demons, howlers, desert-dwellers; Babylonian Talmud — b. Shabbat 151b; b. Eruvin 18b; b. Niddah 24b; Aramaic incantation bowls — Lilith bound by name; Ktar-Lilith iconography (Hunter); Mesopotamian background: lilitu / Lamashtu class (Fauth; cited comparatively)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Isaiah occurrence is a scribal error, or that the 4Q510/11 'lilith' reading is a misrestoration of the fragments, would weaken the trail.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The shedim: the Jewish tradition's attested demons</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The BELIEF-phenomenon is multiply attested across independent textual layers spanning ~1,300 years, from biblical poetry to Talmudic law to buried magic texts. The grade covers the attestation, never the entities.
Layer: real · Place: Ancient Judah / Babylonia (textual origin; bowls from the Sasanian east)
Dates: -700 → 700 (confidence: medium)

The Jewish textual tradition attests across ~1,300 years to a population of harmful spirits called shedim: from the Hebrew Bible (Deuteronomy 32:17; Psalm 106:37 — sacrifices 'to shedim'), through the Testament of Solomon's demon register (1st–3rd c. CE), the Babylonian Talmud's demonology (Pesachim 109b–112a; Gittin 68a–b on Ashmedai; Eruvin 18b on Adam's 130 years), to the late-antique Aramaic incantation bowls that bind named demons — the belief-phenomenon, not the entities.

Earliest attestation: Hebrew Bible: Deuteronomy 32:17 ('they sacrificed to shedim, to gods they did not know'); Psalm 106:37 ('they sacrificed their sons and daughters to shedim')
Sources: Hebrew Bible — Deuteronomy 32:17; Psalm 106:37; Testament of Solomon (1st–3rd c. CE) — the demon register: names, domains, binding angels; Babylonian Talmud — Pesachim 109b–112a (demonology); Gittin 68a–b (Ashmedai); Eruvin 18b (Adam's 130 years); Aramaic incantation bowls — named demons bound in the texts (e.g. the Ktar-Lilith iconography; see evt-incantation-bowls-001); Hamori, Esther J., God's Monsters (Broadleaf Books, 2023) — the biblical spirit-world in context
Falsifiability: A demonstration that Deuteronomy 32:17's 'shedim' is a mistranslation disproven by cognate evidence, or that the cited texts are modern forgeries, would overturn the attestation.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Nile's lost branches — Canopic and Pelusiac, and the capital the river abandoned</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published surveys (1973 Science; 2019 BMSAES synthesis; the cited geoelectric studies) independently trace the defunct branches; the Pi-Ramesse/Qantir case ties the geomorphology to a named city with an archaeological record.
Layer: real · Place: Nile Delta — the defunct Canopic and Pelusiac branches
Dates: -685 → 25 (confidence: high)

The Nile Delta once carried a branching distributary network (Herodotus 2.17 counts seven mouths) that has collapsed to essentially two. The Canopic branch — the river of Naukratis, founded in Dynasty 26 (c. 685–525 BCE) at the strategic point within the delta — is now silted up (Pennington &amp; Thomas 2016, via Bunbury et al. 2019), as are others of the former branches (Stanley &amp; Warne 1993). The Pelusiac branch, the easternmost, was traced on the deltaic plain east of the Suez Canal in the 1973 Science survey; its lower reaches died by silting c. AD 25 under prograding beach accretion. Pi-Ramesse (Qantir, built over Avaris), Ramesses II's Delta capital with its chariot workshops and stables, was sited on the Pelusiac branch; when the branch dried, the city was abandoned and its monuments moved to Tanis (21st Dynasty). The delta head itself migrated, first south then north, across the pyramid age (Bunbury et al. 2019).

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories 2.17 — the seven mouths (5th c. BCE); the branches themselves are older.
Sources: Bunbury, Pennington &amp; Pryer 2019, BMSAES 24 — the evolving Nile Delta 6000 BP to present (Canopic silt-up, distributary focus, delta-head migration); Science 180(4081):59–61 (1973) — 'The Defunct Pelusiac Branch Identified': traced east of the Suez Canal; critical silting stage c. AD 25; Herodotus, Histories 2.17–19 — the seven mouths and the Canopic branch; 2.178–79 — Naukratis; Garbe, P. 2026, E&amp;G Quaternary Science Journal 75(2):169-171 (DOI 10.5194/egqsj-75-169-2026, published 2026-10-08) — Holocene landscape evolution and settlement development at Bubastis/Tell Basta (c. 3200 BCE - c. 200 CE): first area-wide geoarchaeological reconstruction (sediment coring, ERT, phytoliths); temples/palaces deliberately sited on flood-safe Pleistocene Gezira sand mounds; 9.5 m anthropogenic deposits in quarter S1 — the counter-exhibit: excavated pins hold when the substrate is read; textual geography is what needs the dated baseline; Brocard, Goiran &amp; Robinson 2024 (chapter, Nov 2024) — 'Fate of two cities built on sinking ground: slow and fast submergence at Thonis-Heracleion and Canopus': both cities grew as fluvial ports 1-2 km inland from the mouth of the Canopic Branch (Frihy 1992); Canopic sediment discharge dwindled after founding, branch vanished after the 5th c. CE (Flaux et al. 2017); Thonis-Heracleion displaced its monumental core to the Central Island 480-350 BCE as the shoreline retreated; North-East Channel silted 5th-4th c. BCE and the port moved with it — the harbor-side witness of a branch's life and death; Garbe, Behl, Es-Senussi, LangeAthinodorou &amp; Meister 2026, Front. Environ. Archaeol. 5:1778152 (doi:10.3389/fearc.2026.1778152) — first Holocene paleovegetation reconstruction at Bubastis (Tell Basta): 17 sediment cores + phytolith analysis within established lithostratigraphic/paleotopographic frameworks; city founded c. 3200 BCE and continuously inhabited until ~200 CE — 3,000+ years on one of the most dynamic delta landscapes on Earth — the moving substrate as the thing the city learned to live on; Early Old Kingdom cereal-type phytoliths already in floodplain sediments; El-Behairy et al. 2026, Remote Sensing 18(8):1184 — multi-temporal Sentinel-2 imagery + the new MGEVI index mapped buried Nile paleochannels and fluvial landforms near Qena, Egypt; revealed a late anabranching Nile phase validated by DEM data, historical maps, and field evidence — the river's former courses as measurable evidence that the substrate keeps moving; Pennington, Graham, Masson-Berghoff, Millet, Peeters, Toonen, Winkels, Sollars, Emery, Strutt &amp; Barker 2025, Antiquity 99(408):1535-1549 (doi:10.15184/aqy.2025.10185) — first comprehensive geoarchaeological survey of Karnak: the temple of Amun-Ra built atop a fluvial terrace island between river channels (primeval-mound configuration); permanent occupation after 2520 BCE +/-420 (Old Kingdom); occupants responded opportunistically and proactively (desert-sand dumping into channels to make land) — the inverse exhibit: the substrate did not just move under the pin, it picked the pin
Falsifiability: A published coring sequence showing the Pelusiac branch active past AD 100 would revise the c. AD 25 date; evidence that Pi-Ramesse's water came from another branch would re-site the case.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.5,30.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-685</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh (7th c. BCE)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines: the physical tablets (~30,000+ in the British Museum), the colophons recording acquisition and shelf order, the excavated palace rooms, and the fall-of-Nineveh 612 BCE firing — the collection exists, was organized, and dates exactly as claimed.
Layer: real · Place: Nineveh (Kuyunjik mound, Mosul, Iraq) — the South-West and North Palaces
Dates: -669 → -612 (confidence: high)

The Library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh (7th c. BCE) was the ancient world's oldest organized state library: ~30,000+ cuneiform tablets and fragments gathered by royal acquisition, catalogued with colophons, organized by subject in the South-West and North Palaces, and preserved because the fire that destroyed Nineveh in 612 BCE baked the sun-dried tablets into ceramic.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves, c. 669-612 BCE, with royal colophons (the king's acquisition inscriptions — the 'Ashurbanipal Colophons'); modern excavation records from 1849 (Layard) onward.
Sources: British Museum collection — the Kuyunjik tablets (~30,000+ tablets and fragments); the Epic of Gilgamesh tablets (e.g. Tablet XI flood narrative) among the holdings; The Ashurbanipal Colophons — the royal acquisition inscriptions on the tablets themselves, recording series name, tablet number, and shelving order; A. H. Layard, excavations 1849-1851 at Kuyunjik — the South-West Palace discovery; H. Rassam's later Nineveh work; palace-room tablet finds recorded in situ
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the tablets are modern fabrications, or stratigraphic proof the finds belong to a later period — the tablets' physical presence in dated palace strata closes both doors.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>43.1524,36.3605,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-669</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thales of Miletus</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — No writings survive and all accounts are centuries later; the prediction story is single-source (Herodotus 1.74), absent from the doxographical tradition that read Thales, and denied by Neugebauer and Martin as beyond 6th-century Greek capability — the eclipse itself is firm (see evt-thales-eclipse-001), the prediction is the legend.
Layer: real · Place: Miletus
Dates: -624 → -546 (confidence: low)

Thales of Miletus (c. 624-546 BCE) is traditionally regarded as the first Greek philosopher, proposing water as the fundamental principle of nature and reportedly predicting a solar eclipse.

Earliest attestation: Aristotle, Metaphysics (4th century BCE) — two centuries after Thales; Herodotus on the eclipse (c. 585 BCE)
Sources: Aristotle, Metaphysics 983b (account of Thales' water thesis); Herodotus, Histories 1.74 (eclipse prediction during the Battle of Halys); Wikidata entity for Thales (dates/places, CC0, derivative)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 6th-century evidence naming Thales as a thinker would raise the grade; the eclipse story is the weakest link and is independently testable by retrocalculation</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.2783,37.5311,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-624</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Etemenanki, the ziggurat of Babylon</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavation confirms the footprint, the stele depicts the form, and the tablet dimensions match the dug base — but the 91 m height rests on the tablet's idealized geometry (the ikû square), not surviving fabric; the tower was brick-robbed in 1886 and is now largely a pit.
Layer: real · Place: Etemenanki, Babylon (Al Hillah, Iraq)
Dates: -605 → -562 (confidence: medium)

Etemenanki — 'house of the foundation platform of heaven and earth' — was a seven-stage mudbrick ziggurat at Babylon dedicated to Marduk, rebuilt by Nebuchadnezzar II (604–562 BCE). The Esagila tablet gives its plan as seven stages, 91 meters high on a 91-meter square base; German excavations confirmed a 91.5-meter square base with a triple staircase on the south side.

Earliest attestation: Neo-Babylonian royal inscriptions (Nabopolassar cylinder, RIBo Babylon 7 Q005365); the Esagila tablet (Louvre AO 6555), translated by George Smith 1876, republished by Andrew R. George 1992
Sources: Esagila tablet (Louvre AO 6555) — 229 BCE Uruk copy; seven stages, 91 m height on 91 m square base; Tower of Babel Stele (Schøyen MS 2063, now Iraq Museum, Baghdad) — identified 2003, published George 2011; Nebuchadnezzar II with stylus and surveyor's staff beside the seven-stage tower, ground plan, labeled 'great ziggurat of Babylon'; Robert Koldewey's German excavations (after 1913) — 91.5 m square base, triple southern staircase, traces of dismantling; Herodotus, Histories 1.181–183 (mid-5th c. BCE) — the 'tower of Zeus Belus', eight towers; second-hand, contains inaccuracies; O. Pedersén, 'The Tower of Babylon Stele Found in Babylon', Iraq 85 (2023; publ. 2024)
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription giving different dimensions, or re-excavation showing the 91.5 m base belongs to a different structure</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4208,32.5364,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-605</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Hanging Gardens of Babylon (traditional attribution)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the Greek tradition only, centuries late, with no Babylonian textual or archaeological corroboration at Babylon.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (traditional location — unattested archaeologically)
Dates: -0605 → -0562 (confidence: traditional)

The Hanging Gardens — one of the canonical Seven Wonders of the ancient world — stood in the city of Babylon, built by Nebuchadnezzar II (r. 605-562 BCE), traditionally for his Median wife Amytis, as a terraced garden imitating her mountainous homeland.

Earliest attestation: Greek sources centuries after the fact: Berossus (Babyloniaca, c. 290 BCE, via Josephus Against Apion 1.19 and Jewish Antiquities 10.11); Diodorus Siculus 2.10 (1st c. BCE); Strabo Geography 16.1.5 (1st c. BCE-1st c. CE); Quintus Curtius Rufus 5.1.25-35 (1st c. CE); Philo of Byzantium's On the Seven Wonders
Sources: Berossus via Josephus, Against Apion 1.19 (the Amytis story: terraced gardens built for the Median queen); Diodorus Siculus 2.10; Strabo 16.1.5 (the Greek descriptions: square terraces, stone arches, the screw mechanism); Negative evidence: no Babylonian cuneiform text names the gardens; German excavations at Babylon (Koldewey 1899-1917) found no structure matching the descriptions (the vaulted 'hanging garden' building in the Southern Palace is a cellar complex, not a garden)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Babylonian inscription naming terraced gardens at Babylon, or excavated garden structures at Babylon matching the Greek descriptions</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-605</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Book of Daniel — the exile's file, in two stages</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The c. 165 BCE final-form dating of the visions rests on three independent lines: the language seam, the prediction-accuracy cutoff at Antiochus IV, and Qumran copies already circulating by the mid-2nd century BCE. The court tales' historicity is graded separately inside this record, not folded into the composition grade.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (the book's claimed setting)
Dates: -600 → -160 (confidence: medium)

The book of Daniel is a two-stage composition: Aramaic court tales (chapters 2-6, plus 7) rooted in the exile/Persian-era storytelling tradition, and visions (chapters 7-12) in their final form from the Maccabean crisis, c. 165 BCE. The dating rests on the ex eventu signature (the 'predictions' are accurate down to Antiochus IV Epiphanes and then fail), the Hebrew/Aramaic language division (Hebrew 1:1-2:4a and 8-12; Aramaic 2:4b-7:28), and Qumran's eight Daniel manuscripts, which put the book in circulation by the mid-2nd century BCE at the latest.

Earliest attestation: Qumran Daniel manuscripts (4QDan-a through 4QDan-e and others), copies from the mid-2nd century BCE; the book itself claims a 6th-century Babylonian setting
Sources: Qumran Daniel manuscripts (8 copies — among the most represented biblical books in the caves); Masoretic Text of Daniel (Hebrew/Aramaic division a textual fact); Old Greek (Septuagint) and Theodotion Daniel, with the additions: Susanna, Bel and the Dragon, the Prayer of Azariah; Porphyry, Against the Christians (3rd c. CE), preserved via Jerome's Commentary on Daniel — the ex eventu observation; John J. Collins, Daniel (Hermeneia, 1993) — the mainstream compositional analysis; Verse Account of Nabonidus + Harran stelae: Belshazzar as mar sarri (crown prince, never king); Nabonidus Chronicle: October 539 BCE fall; Thaddeus Nduka Oparah, The Doxologies in Daniel 2-6 (Brill, hardback 23 Oct 2026; e-book 29 Sep 2026) + KU Leuven Theology Research News, 8 June 2026 — doxologies in Dan 4-6 added first (late Persian/early Hellenistic), Dan 2 doxologies later, linked to Dan 7 redactors: a staged-redaction model consistent with the record's two-stage composition, grade-neutral (redaction detail, no historicity finding)
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-3rd-century-BCE manuscript of Daniel 7-12, or a Persian/Babylonian record naming a king 'Darius the Mede', would overturn the dating. Fenced inside the record: Daniel's 'Belshazzar son of Nebuchadnezzar' (5:2, 5:22) is wrong on the father (the cylinders name Nabonidus) and on the title (mar sarri, never king); 'Darius the Mede' (5:31) appears in no Persian or Babylonian source — the candidates (Ugbaru/Gubaru the governor, Cyrus himself, Cyaxares II) are identifications, none of them an attested king.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.42,32.54,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Decalogue in two versions: Exodus 20 vs Deuteronomy 5</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The difference is verifiable by reading: any two printed Bibles show it. The Nash Papyrus (evt-nash-papyrus-001) independently witnesses the Second-Temple plurality by harmonizing both. No scholar of any camp disputes the variants; the dispute is only what they mean.
Layer: real · Place: Mount Sinai (the tradition's own geography for the giving; the comparison is documentary, not excavated)
Dates: -0600 → -0400 (confidence: high)

The Ten Commandments exist on the documentary record in at least two different versions inside the canonical Hebrew Bible itself: Exodus 20:2-17 and Deuteronomy 5:6-21. The versions differ materially: the Sabbath commandment's rationale (Ex 20:11 — God rested after six days of creation; Deut 5:15 — 'remember that you were a slave in the land of Egypt'), the order of the coveting commandment's objects (Ex 20:17: 'your neighbor's house... your neighbor's wife'; Deut 5:21: 'your neighbor's wife... your neighbor's house'), and the wording of the preamble's closing. The 'word-for-word' telling of the Decalogue — one fixed dictation, transmitted unchanged — fails on the documentary record before any archaeology is consulted. The words on the stone, as written, come in two drafts.

Earliest attestation: The biblical texts themselves (Exodus 20:2-17; Deuteronomy 5:6-21) — the variants are visible in every printed edition.
Sources: Exodus 20:2-17 and Deuteronomy 5:6-21 in any printed edition (e.g., BHS) — the Sabbath rationale and the coveting order differ on the page; Cambridge University Library, 'The ten commandments' (Lines of Thought) — the Nash Papyrus 'contains the Ten Commandments... The text of the Ten Commandments preserved on the papyrus is different from that found in the Bible, suggesting that even this fundamental religious text has changed'
Falsifiability: A single manuscript tradition in which Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 read identically would falsify this record; none exists. Held below VERIFIED: the record grades a textual fact, and textual facts carry the edition-history caveat.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.75,29.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tower of Babel was Etemenanki</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The mainstream identification, converging on place (Shinar/Babylon), name (Babel), and the tower's fame — but no ancient text makes the equation explicit; it is scholarship's inference, not a document's statement.
Layer: real · Place: Etemenanki, Babylon (Al Hillah, Iraq)
Dates: -600 → -500 (confidence: traditional)

The tower of Genesis 11:1–9 — built in 'the land of Shinar' at 'Babel' — is the ziggurat Etemenanki at Babylon. This is the mainstream scholarly identification.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 11:1–9 (the narrative); the Etemenanki identification from 1876 (Smith's Esagila translation) through George 1992/2011
Sources: Genesis 11:1–9; Akkadian Bāb-ili / Babilu 'gate of god(s)' (Sumerian Ka.dingir.ra) — Genesis 11:9's balal 'confuse' is the text's own Hebrew folk-etymology; Esagila tablet (Louvre AO 6555) + Koldewey excavations — the building the identification points at; Tower of Babel Stele (Schøyen MS 2063, now Iraq Museum) — George 2011; the labeled seven-stage 'great ziggurat of Babylon'
Falsifiability: Evidence the Genesis narrative predates any ziggurat, or a contemporary text identifying a different tower as the story's referent</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4208,32.5364,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Babylonian Exile</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Babylonian administrative and chronicle texts independently confirm the capture, the deportations, and the exiled king's presence in Babylon; the 586/587 destruction rests on the biblical record plus Jerusalem's destruction layers.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (destination of the deportees)
Dates: -597 → -516 (confidence: high)

Nebuchadnezzar II of Babylon captured Jerusalem (597 BCE; Babylonian Chronicle) and destroyed the First Temple (586/587 BCE; biblical chronology), deporting Judah's elite to Babylon; returns began under Cyrus (538 BCE decree tradition), but Judahite communities persisted in Babylonia and the Temple was not rebuilt until Darius I (516/515 BCE).

Earliest attestation: The Babylonian Chronicle (contemporary cuneiform); the Lachish letters (c. 588 BCE); Jehoiachin's ration tablets from Babylon (c. 592 BCE)
Sources: Babylonian Chronicle BM 21946 (Wiseman 1956): 7th year of Nebuchadnezzar, 2 Adar — capture of the 'city of Judah', seizure of Jehoiachin, Zedekiah installed; 2 Kings 24:8-17 gives the same event from inside; Lachish ostraca (letters from the Babylonian invasion, c. 588 BCE) — 'Azekah we do not see'; Jehoiachin ration tablets (Weidner 1939, Babylon administrative texts): 'Yaukin, king of the land of Judah' on Babylonian rations; 2 Kings 25; Jeremiah 52 (destruction narrative — the only detailed account; no cuneiform coverage); Nabonidus Chronicle: Babylon falls to Cyrus, October 539 BCE; Ezra 1 / Ezra 6 (return tradition; Second Temple completed under Darius I, 516/515 BCE, Ezra 6); Pearce &amp; Wunsch (2014), Documents of Judean Exiles and West Semites in Babylonia (Al-Yahudu, 'town of Judah', ~100 tablets, 572-477 BCE) + Murashu archive (5th c. BCE Nippur) — Judahite life in Babylonia long after 538
Falsifiability: Babylonian records showing no deportation or no Jehoiachin in Babylon would overturn the corroboration</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-597</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Jehoiachin ration tablets (Babylon, 592 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavated Babylonian administrative tablets, multiply represented within a ~300-tablet palace archive; a single archive, so the grade holds at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon, southern palace area near the Ishtar Gate (findspot)
Dates: -592 → -592 (confidence: high)

Babylonian palace ration tablets from Nebuchadnezzar II's 13th year (592 BCE) record oil rations to 'Ya'ukin, king of the land of Judah' (Yaukin = Jehoiachin), his five sons, and other Judeans — the Babylonian administration's own record of the exiled king, five years after the 597 deportation.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves: palace-storehouse ration receipts, Babylon, c. 595-570 BCE
Sources: Ernst Weidner, 'Jojachin, Koenig von Juda, in babylonischen Keilschrifttexten,' Melanges syriens II (1939): 923-935 — editio princeps; The ration tablets (primary): ~300 palace-store receipts, Babylon; entry of Nebuchadnezzar's 13th year: 10 sila of oil to 'Ya'ukin, king of the land of Yahudu' (Akkadian Ya-`u-kinu, sar mat Ya-hudu), plus 2.5 sila for his five sons; other Judeans, Ashkelonite princes, Egyptian and Ionian craftsmen on the same lists; Excavation context: Robert Koldewey's Babylon expedition (1899-1917), palace-storehouse fill near the Ishtar Gate; tablets now in the Vorderasiatisches Museum, Berlin; Babylonian Chronicle BM 21946: the 597 BCE capture that put Jehoiachin in Babylon — the chronicle context for the ration lists; Ezekiel's dating formula ('in the Nth year of the exile of King Jehoiachin') — a second, independent tradition counting time from the same king's deportation
Falsifiability: A re-reading of the name Ya'ukin as someone else, or proof the tablets are modern forgeries — unlikely, given the excavated context</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-592</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Yahweh' — the scholarly reconstruction of the divine-name pronunciation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines (theophoric name forms, Lachish Letters, Greek transcriptions, Masoretic vowel-pattern analysis) converge on the Yahweh reading; held below VERIFIED since no audio of the ancient pronunciation exists.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem — locus of the divine-name tradition
Dates: -590 → -586 (confidence: medium)

The scholarly reconstruction of the Tetragrammaton's pronunciation is 'Yahweh' — the best current reading of how ancient Hebrew speakers said YHWH.

Earliest attestation: Modern scholarly consensus — reconstructed from theophoric names (Yeho-/Yahu- prefixes), the Lachish Letters (pre-586 BCE), and Greek transcriptions (e.g., Iao/Iabe) in ancient sources.
Sources: Encyclopedia Judaica, vol. 7: the name was pronounced 'Yahweh' regularly at least until 586 B.C., 'as is clear from the Lachish Letters written shortly before this date'; The Jewish Encyclopedia (1901), 'Tetragrammaton': distinguishes the unpronounced written form from the 'Yahweh' scholarly reading of the ancient pronunciation
Falsifiability: A contemporary pre-exilic Hebrew transcription voweled differently would demote the reconstruction; the consensus rests on converging independent lines, not a single attestation.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-590</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Judaism's formation: from Judahite kingdom to portable religion</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The people and their practice are multiply attested in contemporary administrative records (Elephantine papyri, Yehud coins, ration tablets) independent of the later literary tradition; the belief-phenomenon is graded, not the metaphysics.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (with Elephantine at 24.08 N, 32.89 E and Babylon at 32.54 N, 44.43 E as diaspora nodes)
Dates: -586 → -100 (confidence: high)

Between the 6th century BCE (exile, First Temple destruction) and the 2nd century BCE (Maccabees), Judahite religion transformed into Judaism — a Torah-centered, synagogue-bearing identity carried in exile and diaspora; the word 'Judaism' (Ioudaismos) first appears in print in 2 Maccabees (2:21; 8:1; 14:38), late 2nd century BCE.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian-era cuneiform (Jehoiachin ration tablets, Al-Yahudu texts); the Elephantine Aramaic papyri (Passover Papyrus 419 BCE; Petition to Bagoas 407 BCE); Yehud coinage inscribed YHD (c. 350–333 BCE); 2 Maccabees for the word 'Judaism' (late 2nd c. BCE)
Sources: Elephantine papyri: the Jewish temple at Yeb, Passover Papyrus (419 BCE), Petition to Bagoas (407 BCE) — Porten 1968; Kraeling 1953; Yehud coinage: small silver coins inscribed YHD/YHWD in Paleo-Hebrew and Aramaic, minted c. 350 BCE onward; Gitler et al. typological corpus (2023); MDPI archaeometallurgy (2023); 2 Maccabees 2:21; 8:1; 14:38 — earliest printed use of Ioudaismos (Skarsaune: hellenismos → Ioudaismos, the first 'isms' as identity responses); Cohen, Shaye J. D. — the meaning of 'Ioudaios' evolves through the Second Temple period; 2 Maccabees marks the cultural-religious emphasis; Theophrastus (4th c. BCE, via Josephus C. Apion 1.166): earliest Greek mention of the Ioudaioi as 'a nation of philosophers'
Falsifiability: Documentary evidence that Torah-centered practice, synagogue worship, or the Ioudaios identity predates the exile in the form claimed by tradition would sharpen or shift the bracket</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-586</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Eclipse of Thales — the battle interrupted, 28 May 585 BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Herodotus's battle narrative converges with Pliny's independent Olympiad date and modern retrocalculation; the eclipse itself is the firmest event in the Thales tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Battle on the Halys (Kızılırmak) frontier, Anatolia
Dates: -585 → -585 (confidence: high)

A solar eclipse interrupted the battle between the Lydians and the Medes near the Halys river, prompting the armies to break off fighting and make peace (Herodotus 1.74). Modern retrocalculation fixes it to 28 May 585 BCE (proleptic Julian).

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories 1.74 (c. 440-430 BCE) — roughly 150 years after the event
Sources: Herodotus, Histories 1.74 (battle interrupted by the eclipse); Pliny the Elder, Naturalis Historia 2.53 (4th year of the 48th Olympiad); Stephenson &amp; Fatoohi, retrocalculation of the 585 BCE eclipse (totality path over Asia Minor); Querejeta, 'On the Eclipse of Thales' (Culture and Cosmos 15.1) — survey of the date debate
Falsifiability: A retrocalculation showing the 585 BCE totality band missed the Halys battlefield entirely, or a contemporary source contradicting the Lydian-Mede peace</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.5,39.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-585</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Eclipse of Thales — the battle interrupted, 28 May 585 BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Herodotus's battle narrative converges with Pliny's independent Olympiad date and modern retrocalculation; the eclipse itself is the firmest event in the Thales tradition. The prediction story is fenced OUT of this record: Worthen's finding stands — Herodotus is the sole source for the prediction, while the doxographical tradition that actually read Thales claims only causal understanding, never prediction. Neugebauer denied a 6th-century prediction was possible; the Saros cycle cannot fix a solar eclipse's locality; no eclipse prediction is attested anywhere before the Seleucid era. Grade CORROBORATED held from -001; the evidence chain, not the grade, is what this supersession refines.
Layer: real · Place: Battle on the Halys (Kızılırmak) frontier, Anatolia
Dates: -585 → -585 (confidence: high)

A solar eclipse interrupted the battle between the Lydians and the Medes near the Halys river, prompting the armies to break off fighting and make peace (Herodotus 1.74). Modern retrocalculation fixes it to 28 May 585 BCE (proleptic Julian). The eclipse itself is firm; everything about Thales's foreknowledge is the legend (see evt-thales-001).

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories 1.74 (c. 440-430 BCE) — roughly 150 years after the event
Sources: Herodotus, Histories 1.74 (battle interrupted by the eclipse; ὁρίσας ἐνιαυτόν — he marked out the YEAR); Pliny the Elder, Naturalis Historia 2.53 (4th year of the 48th Olympiad); Stephenson &amp; Fatoohi, retrocalculation of the 585 BCE eclipse (totality path over Asia Minor); Querejeta, 'On the Eclipse of Thales' (Culture and Cosmos 15.1) — survey of the date debate; the translation controversy (year vs day); Worthen, 'Herodotus's Report on Thales' Eclipse' (Electronic Antiquity 3.7) — Herodotus likely the sole source of the prediction; the doxographical tradition claims only causal understanding; 'How Ancient Greeks Explained and Predicted Eclipses' (brewminate.com, 2026) — the 'Thales Problem': astronomy confirms an appropriate eclipse occurred, but cannot place armies under the shadow or identify a prediction; Panchenko, 'Thales's Prediction of a Solar Eclipse' (JHA 25, 1994) — the Saros-based defense of a year-prediction, heard and fenced as the live counter-argument
Falsifiability: A retrocalculation showing the 585 BCE totality band missed the Halys battlefield entirely (beyond ΔT margins), or a contemporary source contradicting the Lydian-Mede peace</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.5,39.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-585</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The al-Yahudu archive (Judean exiles in Babylonia, 572-477 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A substantial internally-dated archive with Judean theophoric names (Gedalyahu, Hanan, Shaltiel, Netanyahu) across promissory notes, leases, tax receipts, a lawsuit, and a marriage agreement; capped at CORROBORATED because the tablets are unprovenanced (antiquities market), which bars VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: al-Yahudu ('Judah-town'), Babylonia — actual village location unknown; the tablets are unprovenanced
Dates: -572 → -477 (confidence: high)

The al-Yahudu archive — roughly 200 cuneiform tablets (572-477 BCE), 103 published by Pearce &amp; Wunsch (2014) — documents Judean exiles as tenant farmers, taxpayers, litigants, and merchants in Babylonian villages including al-Yahudu ('Judah-town'): an exiled community living ordinary economic lives, not languishing in chains.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves (Neo-Babylonian and Persian-period cuneiform business documents)
Sources: Laurie E. Pearce &amp; Cornelia Wunsch, Documents of Judean Exiles and West Semites in Babylonia in the Collection of David Sofer (CUSAS 28, CDL Press, 2014) — editio princeps: 103 cuneiform texts, 'insights into the social and economic life of the Judeans (along with other groups forcibly settled in Mesopotamia by Nebuchadnezzar II) in their community of Al Yahudu (Jewtown)'; The tablets (document): ~200 known total; Judean theophoric names; the Samak-Yama family traceable over five generations; land-for-service tenure as susanu Yahudaya (Judean state dependents); PROVENANCE CAVEAT: unprovenanced — entered the antiquities market by unknown means (possibly looting); held in private collections, primarily the David and Cindy Sofer Collection; some later acquired by the British Museum. Stated on the record.; 'By the Rivers of Babylon' exhibit, Bible Lands Museum Jerusalem (opened Oct 2026, ~100 al-Yahudu tablets from the private David Sofer collection, first public display; curator Dr. Filip Vukosavovic) — the archive's first mass public exhibition; PROVENANCE FENCE CARRIED: antiquities-market tablets, findspot unknown — the exhibit publicizes the evidence, it does not add excavated context; Pre-opening coverage (Times of Israel / Evangelical Focus, 16-17 Oct 2026) — GRADE-NEUTRAL detail append, no supersession (Turin-shroud precedent): exhibit opens Sunday 18 Oct 2026, scheduled to run at least one year; animated Mesopotamian-village centerpiece; the Samak-Yama family's four-generation paper trail (inheritance to five great-grandchildren); Judean theophoric names incl. Natanyahu; handful of Aramaic epigraphs; press head-counts ("80,000 chose to stay", "1,500 to Lebanon/Syria in 587 BCE") carried as press figures, not evidence; Wikipedia's Al-Yahudu article updated Oct 2026 to cite the exhibit opening
Falsifiability: Excavation of a provenanced al-Yahudu village archive confirming the readings would raise the grade; demonstration of forgery would collapse it. The grade is capped by provenance, not by content.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4,32.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-572</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pythagoras of Samos</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — No contemporary or near-contemporary writings; the detailed life is built from sources many centuries later, and the theorem was known in Babylon a millennium earlier.
Layer: real · Place: Samos (birthplace, traditional)
Dates: -570 → -495 (confidence: low)

Pythagoras (c. 570-495 BCE) founded a religious-philosophical community at Croton; the Pythagorean theorem tradition is attached to his name, though no writings of his survive.

Earliest attestation: Heraclitus and Herodotus mention him (5th century BCE); the detailed biographies (Diogenes Laertius, Porphyry, Iamblichus) are 700+ years later
Sources: Herodotus 2.81, 4.95 (earliest literary mentions); Diogenes Laertius, Lives of the Philosophers (3rd c. CE, much later); Wikidata entity for Pythagoras (CC0, derivative)
Falsifiability: A contemporary 6th-century inscription or text naming Pythagoras of Samos as a teacher would raise the grade</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.9469,37.6944,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-570</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pythagoras of Samos — the name on the theorem (attribution autopsy)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The figure's historicity is thinly attested but real (Heraclitus, Herodotus); every mathematical attribution is late doxography, and the theorem's priority now sits on the Old Babylonian tablets — the name on the theorem is a 2,000-year-old misattribution that the record keeps fenced, not repeated.
Layer: real · Place: Samos (birthplace, traditional)
Dates: -570 → -495 (confidence: low)

Pythagoras of Samos (c. 570-495 BCE) founded a religious-philosophical community at Croton; the Pythagorean theorem tradition is attached to his name. The attachment is late doxography: no source within five centuries of Pythagoras connects him to the theorem — the attribution rides Proclus's 5th-century CE commentary on Euclid I.47, citing the lost History of Geometry of Eudemus (4th c. BCE), roughly a thousand years after Pythagoras; the ox-sacrifice-on-discovery legend comes through Apollodorus via Diogenes Laertius 8.12. Plato and Aristotle never connect Pythagoras to the theorem. Burkert's Lore and Science in Ancient Pythagoreanism (1972): the earliest brotherhood was a religious community, and the 'mathematical Pythagoras' is largely later construction. The relationship itself was in working use in Old Babylon ~1,200 years before his birth (evt-pythagorean-triples-babylon-001); Euclid's Elements I.47 (c. 300 BCE) is the earliest fully verifiable proof and does not name Pythagoras.

Earliest attestation: Heraclitus and Herodotus mention Pythagoras (5th century BCE) — neither connects him to the theorem; the detailed biographies (Diogenes Laertius, Porphyry, Iamblichus) are 700+ years later
Sources: Herodotus 2.81, 4.95 (earliest literary mentions — no theorem connection); Proclus, Commentary on Euclid I.47 (5th c. CE), citing Eudemus's History of Geometry — the attribution chain; Diogenes Laertius 8.12 (via Apollodorus) — the ox-sacrifice-on-discovery legend; Euclid, Elements I.47 (c. 300 BCE) — the earliest fully verifiable proof; names no discoverer; Burkert, Lore and Science in Ancient Pythagoreanism (1972) — the religious-brotherhood-first reading; the mathematical Pythagoras as later construction
Falsifiability: A contemporary 6th-century inscription or text naming Pythagoras of Samos as a teacher would raise the grade; a pre-Euclidean Greek proof text naming him would change the attribution grade</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>26.9469,37.6944,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-570</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Ashoka's pillar gives 3rd-century BCE state-level attestation of the Buddha and his birthplace; the life details come from much later texts.
Layer: real · Place: Lumbini (traditional birthplace)
Dates: -563 → -400 (confidence: traditional)

Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, taught the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path in northern India; tradition dates him 563-483 BCE, though modern scholarship favors c. 480-400 BCE.

Earliest attestation: Ashoka's Lumbini pillar inscription (c. 249 BCE) marking the birthplace; Pali canon texts compiled centuries after his death
Sources: Ashoka's Lumbini (Rummindei) pillar edict (c. 249 BCE); Pali Canon (compiled 1st c. BCE and later, Sri Lanka); Bechert (ed.), The Dating of the Historical Buddha (1988 symposium volume)
Falsifiability: The dating dispute is live: new epigraphic or radiocarbon evidence fixing 6th-5th century BCE northern Indian chronology would narrow the range</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>83.2767,27.4833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-563</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cyrus the Great</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources — the Cylinder, the Babylonian chronicle tradition, and Greek accounts — confirm his conquests and reign; the return-decree tradition rests on Ezra, not the Cylinder.
Layer: real · Place: Pasargadae (Cyrus's capital)
Dates: -559 → -530 (confidence: high)

Cyrus II of Persia (reigned c. 559-530 BCE) conquered Media, Lydia, and Babylon, founding the Achaemenid Empire. The Jewish return tradition credits Cyrus with a decree permitting the exiles to return (Ezra 1; Aramaic version in Ezra 6); the Cyrus Cylinder itself does not mention Judah or the Jews.

Earliest attestation: The Cyrus Cylinder (539/538 BCE); the Nabonidus Chronicle (contemporary Babylonian); Herodotus Histories Book 1
Sources: Cyrus Cylinder, BM 90920 (British Museum) — genuine artifact; ll. 30-32 restore only Mesopotamian sanctuaries east of the Tigris; names no Jews, no Judah; Nabonidus Chronicle (Babylonian, contemporary) — fall of Babylon, October 539 BCE; Herodotus, Histories 1.46-216 (independent Greek account); Ezra 1 (Hebrew decree tradition) vs Ezra 6:3-5 (Aramaic version) — the two versions differ substantially; wording-authenticity debated, but the tradition is held to reflect real Persian practice; Kuhrt 1983; Finkel 2013; Grabbe — the cylinder as Babylonian royal formula, not a general repatriation policy (see evt-cyrus-cylinder-charter-001)
Falsifiability: Contemporary sources placing a different king over Persia in those years, or proof the Cylinder is not Cyrus's</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>53.1756,30.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-559</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Confucius</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in the Analects tradition and in Sima Qian's biography with Lu-state context; no contemporary inscription, and the Analects has debated layers.
Layer: real · Place: Qufu, Lu state (traditional)
Dates: -551 → -479 (confidence: medium)

Confucius (Kong Qiu, 551-479 BCE) of Lu taught ethics centered on ren (humaneness) and li (ritual propriety); his disciples compiled his sayings in the Analects, which became foundational to East Asian thought.

Earliest attestation: The Analects (compiled by disciples, 5th-3rd c. BCE); Sima Qian's Records of the Grand Historian (c. 109-91 BCE) biography
Sources: The Analects (Lunyu); Sima Qian, Records of the Grand Historian, 'Biography of Confucius' (c. 100 BCE); Zuo Zhuan (contemporary-ish Spring and Autumn chronicle of Lu)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 6th-5th century BCE texts from Lu naming Kong Qiu as a teacher would strengthen historicity; proof the Analects is a Han forgery would collapse it</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>116.9869,35.5967,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-551</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Achaemenid Empire — Persia, 550–330 BCE</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary and contemporary sources: the Behistun trilingual inscription, the Persepolis Fortification tablets (administrative clay archive), Babylonian and Egyptian administrative documents, and Greek historiography — the state's reality is uncontested in scholarship.
Layer: real · Place: Persepolis, Fars, Iran (empire-wide record; pinned at the ceremonial capital)
Dates: -550 → -330 (confidence: high)

The Achaemenid Empire (c. 550–330 BCE), founded by Cyrus the Great, was the largest empire the ancient world had seen — about 5.5 million km² at greatest extent under Darius I and Xerxes I, spanning three continents, administered through satrapies, linked by the Royal Road and a state postal system, and documented in its own trilingual royal inscriptions and tens of thousands of administrative clay tablets.

Earliest attestation: Behistun inscription of Darius I, c. 522–486 BCE — trilingual (Old Persian, Elamite, Babylonian) royal account carved on Mount Behistun
Sources: Behistun inscription of Darius I (c. 522–486 BCE) — trilingual Old Persian/Elamite/Babylonian royal account; the king's own list of the lands he ruled; Persepolis Fortification tablets — thousands of Elamite administrative clay tablets recording rations, wages, travel permits, and personnel; the empire's own paperwork; Babylonian and Egyptian administrative documents naming Persian satraps and taxation — independent provincial attestation; Herodotus, Histories (5th c. BCE) — Greek-side account of the empire's scale, satrapies, and the Royal Road; Multiple secondary aggregators converge on ~5.5M km² at greatest extent under Darius I/Xerxes I (e.g., empire-size surveys citing the satrapy lists)
Falsifiability: Proof the Behistun inscription and the Persepolis Fortification tablets are modern forgeries, or a contemporary document showing the satrapy administration never existed</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>52.8916,29.9355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-550</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cyrus edict of return (Ezra's decree tradition)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the biblical tradition only; no contemporary Persian copy of the decree survives, and the two versions differ substantially — though the Aramaic memorandum carries genuine Persian chancellery form.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (where the decree tradition originates)
Dates: -538 → -538 (confidence: medium)

Cyrus the Great issued a decree in his first year (538 BCE) permitting the Jewish exiles in Babylonia to return to Jerusalem and rebuild the Temple, with royal support.

Earliest attestation: Ezra 1:2-4 (Hebrew) and Ezra 6:3-5 (Aramaic); the biblical book of Ezra, composed centuries after the events it describes
Sources: Ezra 1:2-4 (Hebrew proclamation): universal scope, Yahweh-theology, voluntary local funding for returnees; Ezra 6:3-5 (Aramaic archive memorandum): temple dimensions (60 x 60 cubits), construction method, costs from the royal treasury, restitution of the gold and silver vessels Nebuchadnezzar had taken — widely accepted as genuine Persian chancellery form; Ezra 6:6-12 (Darius's decree): proceeds on the premise that a Cyrus decree existed — the search of the Ecbatana archive is narrated as finding it; Elias Bickerman, 'The Edict of Cyrus in Ezra 1,' JBL 65 (1946): 'Ezra 1 preserves a genuine edict of Cyrus, which had the same formula and the same modes of promulgation, by herald and through poster, as the Roman edictum'; Kuhrt 1983; Grabbe: the cylinder confirms a genuine imperial restoration policy, so the Ezra wording is a localized, theological adaptation of real Persian practice — not dismissible as fiction; Philip R. Davies 1992 (minimalist position): the proclamation as largely post-exilic invention to legitimize Judean land and temple claims
Falsifiability: A contemporary Persian cuneiform copy of the decree naming Judah would raise the grade to CORROBORATED or VERIFIED; proof the Aramaic memorandum's chancellery form is a later forgery would collapse the middle position</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-538</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Abrahamic offshoots tree: a shared patriarch, claimed by three traditions</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The claim that all three traditions ground themselves in Abraham is multiply attested in each tradition's foundational documents — a belief-phenomenon graded on attestation, not metaphysics. The trunk (Abraham the person) stays CLAIMED; this grade covers the tree as tradition-map only.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem / the shared sacred geography
Dates: -0500 → 0700 (confidence: medium)

Judaism, Christianity, and Islam each ground their origins in the figure of Abraham — the shared 'Abrahamic' lineage is a genuine feature of each tradition's own foundational texts (Hebrew Bible, New Testament, Qur'an), not a modern scholarly invention. The tree is the map of that shared tradition-claim; it is not a verified genealogy of a verified person.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 17 (covenant with Abraham) in the biblical tradition; Romans 4 / Galatians 3 (Paul's Abraham) 1st c. CE; Qur'an 2:135, 3:67, 2:124-129 (Ibrahim) 7th c. CE
Sources: Genesis 17; 25 (covenant, the sons); Paul's epistles: Romans 4, Galatians 3 (1st c. CE) — Abraham as father of Gentile believers; Qur'an 2:135, 3:67 ('Abraham was not a Jew, nor a Christian'), 2:124-129 (Ka'ba foundations), 14:35-41; F.E. Peters, The Monotheists (Princeton, 2003) — the Abrahamic family as analytic category; Seed records: evt-abraham-001 (trunk, CLAIMED), evt-judaism-001, evt-christianity-emergence-001, evt-islam-origin-001, evt-quran-manuscripts-001 (branches, CORROBORATED)
Falsifiability: Any one tradition's foundational texts lacking an Abraham claim would downgrade the tree; evidence that the 'shared Abraham' framing originated only in modern scholarship would debunk the 'not a modern invention' clause</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Adon to Adonis: the Semitic root behind the Greek god</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The linguistic root and the Byblos-to-Greece transmission are documented; the dying-and-rising reading of Adonis is the debated part, honestly dated late.
Layer: real · Place: Byblos (origin) to the Greek world (transmission)
Dates: -0500 → 0300 (confidence: medium)

The Greek Adonis derives from the Semitic title adon ('lord') — the Byblos cult's god, carried into the Greek world; his explicit resurrection references are largely of the Christian era.

Earliest attestation: Byblos cult of the Baal of Gebal/Adonis, 1st millennium BCE; Greek Adonia attested by the 3rd c. BCE
Sources: Theocritus, Idyll 15 (Adonia festival, 3rd c. BCE); Roland de Vaux (1933) — critique of Frazer's dying-and-rising category, late-dating of Adonis resurrection; Marcel Detienne, The Gardens of Adonis (1972/1977) — the Adonia festival in its Greek context; Tryggve N.D. Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — Adonis chapter ('may well have been' a dying-and-rising god)
Falsifiability: A pre-Christian Greek text narrating Adonis's resurrection as cult doctrine would move the dying-and-rising reading from debated to attested.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.65,34.12,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Attis: the Phrygian lament and the argued return</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-tradition, late, and Roman-overlayed attestation: the lament is attested, the 'rising' half is argued rather than witnessed. The grade stays CLAIMED — the team's honesty about the weakest column.
Layer: real · Place: Phrygia (central Anatolia)
Dates: -500 → 400 (confidence: low)

The Phrygian/Gallo-Roman tradition attests Attis as a dying-and-rising figure of the Cybele cult, with the Hilaria festival of March 25 celebrated as his return/rebirth — but the 'resurrection' half of the story is the weakest of the five columns, largely a later Roman reading of an older Phrygian lament tradition.

Earliest attestation: Phrygian cult material; Roman-period sources on the Cybele-Attis rites and the Hilaria (March 25)
Sources: Phrygian cult material — Attis in the Cybele cult; the self-castration and pine traditions; Roman-period sources on the March rites: the arbor intrat (March 22) and the Hilaria (March 25)
Falsifiability: A pre-Roman Phrygian source describing Attis's return/rebirth would raise the grade; a demonstration that the Hilaria never concerned Attis would drop it to SPECULATION</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dead Sea Scrolls</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — PROVES multiple variant textual traditions of the Hebrew Bible coexisted in the Second Temple period (Emanuel Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible, 1992/2001) and organized Jewish sectarianism (Community Rule, Damascus Document, pesharim). DOES NOT PROVE eyewitness records of patriarchs, the Exodus, or any First Temple event. The Qumran-Essene link is CORROBORATED, not settled (Norman Golb's Jerusalem-origin hypothesis named fairly). Via Geza Vermes, The Dead Sea Scrolls in English (1962).
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

~900 manuscripts / tens of thousands of fragments from eleven Qumran caves (Bedouin discovery of Cave 1's seven scrolls incl. the Great Isaiah Scroll, 1947); c. 250 BCE-68 CE (paleography, stratified archaeology, radiocarbon converge).

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Geza Vermes, The Dead Sea Scrolls in English (1962; rev. 1975); Emanuel Tov, Textual Criticism of the Hebrew Bible (1992; 2nd ed. 2001); ERC 'Tracing Scribes and Scrolls' project (2026–2031, €2.5M) — Mladen Popović (Univ. of Groningen) + Israel Antiquities Authority + European labs: chemical-signature database (parchment, collagen, inks) + palaeography/codicology/AI for scroll provenance. Grade-neutral: documents the 2026 research program, moves no manuscript grade (the Turin-shroud precedent).; Marcello Fidanzio (Universita della Svizzera Italiana) — material analysis of the Great Isaiah Scroll reported Jan 2026 (Times of Israel via United with Israel, 4 Jan 2026): first 8 parchment sheets vs final 9 differ in manufacture, folding patterns, layout, and repairs — the 7.34m manuscript began life as two independent scrolls later joined into one. Manufacturing history, not authenticity; the scroll's 2nd-c.-BCE date unchallenged.; Eshbal Ratzon (Tel Aviv Univ.), 'The Qumran 364-day calendar,' Tarbiz (published Sept 2026, reported newsy-today): the sect practiced the rigid solar calendar — 52 exact weeks, festivals fixed to weekday — then abandoned it as the ~1.25-day/yr drift made the agricultural festivals seasonally untenable. Resolves the practiced-vs-ideal debate.; CSNTM multispectral-imaging project (Sept 2026, csntm.org): state-of-the-art imaging of dozens of purported DSS fragments in the Martin Schoyen collection (31 Dead Sea Scroll artifacts) with the Oslo Cathedral School Rare Book &amp; Manuscript Library — most fragments show modern-forgery characteristics; some show physical characteristics of genuine 1st-c.-CE fragments; MS 4612-1 possibly the first known pre-2002 forgery (Justnes). Strengthens the forgery-detection side of the DSS manuscript story.; University of Manchester / John Rylands Library (announced Jan 2021; Joan Taylor et al.): four Dead Sea Scroll fragments in the JRL collection, photographed with multispectral imaging; one fragment preserves 4 lines with 'Shabbat' legible, possibly Ezekiel 46:1-3 — grade-neutral context (Turin-shroud precedent): the finding predates the record; the Oct-2026 press cycle recycles it as news.; Dead Sea Discoveries 33(3) (2026) thematic issue 'Fifty Years After Milik's Enoch' (Goff &amp; Uusimaki intro online Oct 1, 2026; Dugan on fragment-joining; Ratzon &amp; Hadad on 4Q319 calendar)
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Elephantine Papyri</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — PROVES a functioning Yahw temple with sacrificial cult outside Jerusalem in the 5th c. BCE and lived Judean religion under Achaemenid rule (Bezalel Porten, Archives from Elephantine, 1968; Porten &amp; Yardeni, TAD, 1986-1999). DOES NOT PROVE normative monotheism (the colony honored other deities) or anything about patriarchs or the Exodus. Three independent archives, internally dated, cross-consistent.
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The Judean military colony at Yeb/Elephantine (Upper Egypt) left ~100 Aramaic papyri and ostraca (495-399 BCE), including the Passover letter of 419 BCE (TAD A4.1) and the 407 BCE petitions to rebuild the Yahw temple destroyed in 410 BCE (TAD A4.7/A4.8).

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Bezalel Porten, Archives from Elephantine (1968); Porten &amp; Ada Yardeni, Textbook of Aramaic Documents from Ancient Egypt, 4 vols. (1986-1999)
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Deucalion flood</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The tradition is textually attested across centuries and authors. No contemporary corroboration as an event; graded as a CLAIMED tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Mount Parnassus, Phocis (mythic landfall)
Dates: -500 → 100 (confidence: low)

The Greek Deucalion flood: Deucalion and Pyrrha survive a Zeus-sent flood in a chest/ark (larnax), land on Parnassus, and repopulate the world by casting stones ('the bones of the mother'). Attested in Pindar (Olympian 9), Apollodorus (Library 1.7.2), and Ovid (Metamorphoses 1.313-415) — a literary tradition, not a dated event. It shares the ark/bird/landfall skeleton of the Mesopotamian story at a structural level, via the common ancient Near Eastern/Mediterranean flood-memory pool, not direct copying.

Earliest attestation: Pindar, Olympian 9.41-56 (476 BCE); Apollodorus, Library 1.7.2; Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.313-415
Sources: Pindar, Olympian 9.41-56; Apollodorus, Library 1.7.2-3; Ovid, Metamorphoses 1.313-415
Falsifiability: N/A as event claim — this record grades the attestation of the tradition only</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.6,38.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ioudaismos First Use</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — PROVES a named, self-aware 'Judaism' consciously opposed to 'Hellenism' by the Hasmonean era. DOES NOT PROVE the coinage date (an earlier unattested use can't be ruled out) or that it meant 'religion' in the modern sense (Steve Mason, 'Jews, Judaeans, Judaizing, Judaism,' JSJ 38 (2007):457-512, reads it dynamically as 'Judaizing'; John J. Collins counters it denotes distinct cultural content — honestly underdetermined: only four pre-Christian attestations). Rests on a single text tradition; no independent contemporary source attests the word.
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The Greek word Ioudaismos first occurs in surviving literature in 2 Maccabees — 2:21 and 8:1 (the epitomist's preface; admitting to Judas Maccabeus's forces only those 'who had remained in Judaism'), set against Hellenismos; recurs 2 Macc 14:38, 4 Macc 4:26, Galatians 1:13-14. 2 Maccabees dates to the late 2nd c. BCE (c. 124 BCE).

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Steve Mason, 'Jews, Judaeans, Judaizing, Judaism,' JSJ 38 (2007):457-512
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ketef Hinnom Amulets</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — PROVES the blessing's text circulated as a liturgical amulet in Jerusalem by ~600 BCE on the conventional dating (paleography + stratigraphy; Barkay, Vaughn, Lundberg &amp; Zuckerman, BASOR 334, 2004, 41-71). DOES NOT PROVE the Pentateuch's composition date or Moses. The dating is contested (Renz &amp; Roellig, Handbuch der althebraeischen Epigraphik I, 1995; later-origin minority unexcluded); the object's existence and text are not.
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Two tiny rolled silver amulets (KH1, KH2) with paleo-Hebrew variants of the priestly blessing (Numbers 6:24-26), excavated 1979 from burial-cave repository 24 at Ketef Hinnom, Jerusalem (Gabriel Barkay's team; first published Biblical Archaeology Review 9.4, 1983).

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Barkay et al., 'The Amulets from Ketef Hinnom: A New Edition and Evaluation,' BASOR 334 (2004); Johannes Renz &amp; Wolfgang Roellig, Handbuch der althebraeischen Epigraphik I (1995); 2026 imaging refinements of the Ketef Hinnom silver scrolls — reflectance transformation imaging (RTI) and 3D scanning of the tarnished silver surfaces, rendering letters previously marked illegible readable and refining earlier transcriptions (reported 2026 via truthstodiefor.com — an advocacy site; the underlying imaging techniques and the refined readings are the technical fact, the site's apologetic framing fenced). The 1979 Barkay controlled-excavation provenance (sealed Chamber 25 repository) is what defeats the forgery question; the imaging defeats only the legibility question.
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mishnah</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — PROVES the shape of 1st-2nd c. CE rabbinic practice and law (Sabbath, purity, tithes, festivals, civil damages) — the documentary baseline of rabbinic Judaism. DOES NOT PROVE anything contemporary about the biblical period (the Sinai-chain claim in Avot 1 is theological, not historical). The Judah ha-Nasi attribution rests on later tradition — CORROBORATED. Via Jacob Neusner, Judaism: The Evidence of the Mishnah (1981); Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Mishnah'.
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The Mishnah — 63 tractates in six Sedarim, the earliest systematic rabbinic legal work, redacting Tannaitic traditions; rabbinic tradition attributes redaction to Judah ha-Nasi, c. 200 CE (scholarly consensus ~190-220 CE).

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Jacob Neusner, Judaism: The Evidence of the Mishnah (1981); Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Mishnah'
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Moses's horns: qaran to cornuta</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Both readings are multiply attested; the marble statue is a documented material witness to which reading the Latin West received.
Layer: real · Place: San Pietro in Vincoli, Rome (the statue); the Hebrew belongs to the Exodus tradition
Dates: -0500 → 1515 (confidence: medium)

Exodus 34:29's Hebrew says Moses's face qaran ('was radiant,' 'shone'); Jerome's Vulgate (late 4th c. CE) rendered it cornuta est ('was horned'); Michelangelo's Moses (1513-1515, San Pietro in Vincoli, Rome) wears the Latin's horns — a fifteen-hundred-year-old translation choice, in marble.

Earliest attestation: Hebrew qaran in the Masoretic Text; Latin cornuta est in Jerome's Vulgate; the horns in marble from 1513-1515
Sources: Exodus 34:29-35 — Hebrew qaran ('shone/was radiant'), vv. 30, 35 fixing the sense by repetition; Jerome, Vulgate: 'cornuta esset facies sua' — the Latin reading the medieval West inherited; Michelangelo, Moses (1513-1515), tomb of Julius II, San Pietro in Vincoli — the horns as sculpted; Lee M. Jefferson, 'The Horns of Moses,' Biblical Archaeology Society, Bible History Daily (first published May 2023; updated 4 Feb 2026): Jerome's cornuta read as the best Latin available, not malice; the LXX's Ex 34:29 reading as dedoxastai ('glorified') — a third witness at the crossing; Moses depicted hornless for centuries after the Vulgate, earliest horned depiction the 11th-c. Aelfric Paraphrase; the anti-Jewish weaponization of the horns from the 13th c. onward (Mellinkoff 1970, The Horned Moses in Medieval Art and Thought).
Falsifiability: A pre-Vulgate Latin or Greek witness reading 'horned' at Exodus 34:29, or a Hebrew manuscript tradition reading qeren ('horn') at this verse</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.4932,41.8939,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Samaritan temple on Mount Gerizim (5th c. BCE - 110 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The excavated sanctuary (precinct, inscriptions, thousands of finds) + Josephus's destruction account + the Delos dedications converge; the Persian start-date is the single contested element.
Layer: real · Place: Mount Gerizim summit, Samaria
Dates: -500 → -110 (confidence: medium)

A Yahwistic sanctuary stood on Mount Gerizim's summit from the Persian period (5th century BCE), massively expanded under the Hellenistic kings into a city and sacred precinct, and was destroyed by John Hyrcanus I c. 110 BCE.

Earliest attestation: Josephus, Jewish Antiquities 13.254-256 (c. 93-94 CE) records Hyrcanus's destruction; the precinct's Persian-phase stratigraphy from excavation
Sources: Magen, Y., Mount Gerizim Excavations vols. I-II (Judea and Samaria Publications) — the sacred precinct, finds, and inscriptions; Josephus, Jewish Antiquities 13.254-256 (Hyrcanus destroys the Gerizim temple, c. 110 BCE); Sauter, M., 'The Temple on Mount Gerizim—In the Bible and Archaeology', BAR (8 Sept 2026) — explainer of Gudme 2019 + Magen; venue only; The two Samaritan dedicatory inscriptions on Delos (2nd c. BCE) — 'Israelites who make offerings on Mount Gerizim'
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed evidence that the summit complex was not a temple (no altar, no votive inscriptions), or a re-dating of the precinct entirely into the Hellenistic period</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2725,32.1998,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Yehud Coinage</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — PROVES a Persian-administered province named Yehud with a functioning local mint — Judah was a real, governed Achaemenid unit, not a literary memory. DOES NOT PROVE the Davidic kingdom or a united monarchy (imperial provincial issues; early types likely struck in Philistia on commission). Leo Mildenberg, 'Yehud' (1979); Gitler, Lorber &amp; Fontanille, The Yehud Coinage (2023) — ~2,000 specimens, 44 types, 198 obverse/282 reverse dies. Die-linked sequences, hoard-distributed — the timeline's own receipts, fenced for Dr. Rosa Delgado's numismatic beat.
Layer: real · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Small silver fractional coins inscribed YHD (the Achaemenid province 'Judah/Yehud'), many imitating Athenian owl types; Persian-period series mid-4th c. BCE (c. 350-333 BCE), die typology extending to ~30 BCE.

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Leo Mildenberg, 'Yehud: A Preliminary Study of the Provincial Coinage of Judaea' (1979); Gitler, Lorber &amp; Fontanille, The Yehud Coinage (Israel Numismatic Society, 2023); Economou, Michael (2026), 'The Paleo-Hebrew Script in Achaemenid, Hellenistic and Early Roman Jewish Coinage', European Hebrew Journal 1(2):314-332 (paleography of paleo-Hebrew on Yehud coinage)
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Battle of Thermopylae, 480 BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in Herodotus's near-contemporary account built from veteran interviews, corroborated across the later Greek tradition (Diodorus, etc.); no contemporary Persian-side record names the battle, so it stops at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Thermopylae (the Hot Gates), Phthiotis, Greece
Dates: -480 → -480 (confidence: traditional)

In late August/early September 480 BCE, a Greek allied force under the Spartan king Leonidas I held the pass of Thermopylae against the Persian invasion army of Xerxes I for two full days of battle; after the mountain path was betrayed, the rear guard — 300 Spartans, 700 Thespians, and others — was annihilated on the third day and the pass was forced.

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories Book VII (written c. 450s–420s BCE, ~50 years after the battle; Herodotus interviewed Greek veterans of the Persian Wars)
Sources: Herodotus, Histories VII.201–239 — the battle narrative: the pass, the two days, the betrayal by Ephialtes, the last stand; Herodotus VII.202–203 — the Greek contingent list (300 Spartans, 500 Tegeans, 500 Mantineans, 120 Orchomenians, 1,000 other Arcadians, 400 Corinthians, 200 Phliasians, 80 Mycenaeans, 700 Thespians, 400 Thebans, 1,000 Phocians, plus Locrians and Malians); Herodotus VII.222 — the Thespians 'very willingly stayed' with Leonidas on the last day; Diodorus Siculus, Bibliotheca historica XI — later Greek corroboration of the battle tradition
Falsifiability: Contemporary Persian administrative or royal evidence that Xerxes's 480 invasion never reached central Greece, or proof the battle is a later invention with no 5th-c. root</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.5364,38.7961,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-480</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Murasu archive (Judean descendants in Babylonia, 455-403 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavated, massive (~800 texts), internally dated across 28 Nippur-area settlements; the Judean marker is onomastic (names), which caps the community-identity reading at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Nippur (excavation site)
Dates: -455 → -403 (confidence: high)

The Murasu archive — roughly 800 legal texts from Nippur (455-403 BCE, excavated 1893) — records the business of a single family across four generations, with some 80 distinctively Judean personal names among its clients: the exiles' descendants, still identifiably Judean a century-plus after the fall, as tenants and entrepreneurs in Persian-period Babylonia. A large Judean community stayed and thrived rather than returning.

Earliest attestation: The tablets themselves: excavated legal archive, Nippur, 5th century BCE
Sources: The Murasu archive (document): ~800 legal texts and fragments, Nippur; records of a single business family over four generations, 455-403 BCE; excavated 1893 under John Henry Haynes (University of Pennsylvania expedition); held at the Penn Museum, Istanbul, and elsewhere; ~80 distinctively Judean personal names (Shabbatai, Minyamin, Haggai; yah/yaw theophorics) in contracts and land-for-service deals across ~28 Nippur-area settlements; M. W. Stolper's editions and studies of the archive (the standard publication of the corpus)
Falsifiability: The archive is too large, too excavated, and too internally consistent to be a fabrication; the falsifiable part is the identity reading — a prosopographic study showing the Judean-named clients were not a community would weaken it</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2309,32.1264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-455</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kingdom of Aksum</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested from independent sides: the king's own trilingual inscriptions, a Roman emperor's letter, the coin series with the conversion signal in the metal, and the stelae. Stops short of VERIFIED only on the conventional termini (no dated foundation charter, no dated end); the core (the 4th-century Christian Aksumite state) is as secure as ancient African history gets.
Layer: real · Place: Axum (Aksum), Tigray Region, Ethiopia
Dates: -0400 → 0960 (confidence: medium)

That the Kingdom of Aksum — centered on Axum in the Ethiopian highlands, c. 400 BCE–c. 960 CE, at its height spanning the Red Sea into Himyar (Yemen) — was a literate, minting, trading empire: trilingual royal inscriptions (Ge'ez, Sabaean, Greek), giant granite stelae, the first sub-Saharan African minted coinage (gold/silver/bronze), the Red Sea trade hub at Adulis, and the 4th-century CE conversion to Christianity under King Ezana.

Earliest attestation: Ezana Stone (trilingual: Ge'ez, Sabaean, Greek), standing at Axum, UNESCO 1980; Ezana's Ge'ez inscriptions at Meroë; Aksumite coinage from c. 270 CE (sun/moon symbols, replaced by the cross post-conversion); the giant stelae field; Constantius II's letter to Ezana and Saizana requesting Frumentius be sent to Alexandria
Sources: The Ezana Stone — trilingual stele at Axum documenting Ezana's conversion and campaigns incl. Meroë (4th c. CE); Rufinus, Ecclesiastical History — Frumentius converts the child-king Ezana; Constantius II's letter to Ezana and Saizana (c. 356 CE) — requests Frumentius be sent to Alexandria for doctrinal examination; Munro-Hay, Stuart — Aksum: An African Civilisation of Late Antiquity (1991); 'most famous of the Aksumite kings before Kaleb'; Stephanie L. Black — study of fourth-century inscriptions identifying Ezana as Ethiopia's first Christian king
Falsifiability: Proof that the Ezana Stone inscriptions are later fabrications, or that the Aksumite coin series is misattributed.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>38.7229,14.1379,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Socrates's daimonion: the sign that only ever said no</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent near-contemporary witnesses (Plato and Xenophon, who disagreed with each other freely) report the same phenomenon with the same prohibitive-only character; the indictment's 'new daimonia' charge corroborates the public notoriety of the claim.
Layer: real · Place: Athens
Dates: -399 → -350 (confidence: medium)

Plato's Apology (31c-d, 40a-b) and Xenophon's Memorabilia (1.1.2-4) attest that Socrates experienced a daimonion — 'a divine thing,' a voice or sign that came to him since childhood and always held him back from actions he was about to take, never urging him forward. The same charge appears in the indictment at Apology 24b-c ('introducing new daimonia'). The attestation is graded as belief-phenomenon: the texts attest what Socrates reported and what Athens heard, not the sign's metaphysical status.

Earliest attestation: Plato, Apology 31c-d, 40a-b; Xenophon, Memorabilia 1.1.2-4, Apology 12-13
Sources: Plato, Apology 31c-d, 40a-b (Perseus Project / Tufts); Xenophon, Memorabilia 1.1.2-4; Xenophon, Apology 12-13 (Perseus Project / Tufts); Plutarch, De genio Socratis — the ancient reception and debate over what the sign was
Falsifiability: Proof that the Apology passages are later insertions (no such evidence exists in the manuscript tradition)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.73,37.98,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-399</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Eros the great daimon: the middle term of the cosmos</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The text is securely transmitted; the daimon-intermediary doctrine is stated explicitly and consistently, and its function-language echoes Hesiod's guardians.
Layer: real · Place: Athens (composition); dramatic setting: Agathon's house, Athens
Dates: -385 → -370 (confidence: medium)

In the Symposium (202d-203a), Diotima teaches Socrates that Eros is neither god nor mortal but a 'great daimon' (daimon megas) — an intermediary power whose work is to interpret and convey between gods and men: carrying prayers and sacrifices upward and commands and rewards downward, 'filling the space between' so that the whole is bound together. This is the most explicit classical statement of the daimon as the middle term of the cosmos.

Earliest attestation: Plato, Symposium 202d-203a (R.G. Bury commentary, Perseus / Tufts)
Sources: Plato, Symposium 202d-203a (Perseus Project / Tufts; R.G. Bury commentary); F.E. Brenk, 'In Mist Apparelled' (1977) — the daimon as intermediary in classical religion
Falsifiability: A manuscript tradition showing the 202d-203a passage is interpolated (no such variant exists)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>23.73,37.98,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-385</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alexander the Great</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Contemporary Babylonian records, coinage, and inscriptions confirm his existence, dates, and conquests; multiple independent traditions agree on the campaign outline.
Layer: real · Place: Pella (birthplace and capital of Macedon)
Dates: -356 → -323 (confidence: high)

Alexander III of Macedon (356-323 BCE) conquered the Persian Empire from Greece to India in twelve years, founding cities including Alexandria and spreading Hellenistic culture across three continents.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian astronomical diaries (contemporary, record his death 11 June 323 BCE); coins and inscriptions in his name; the primary histories (Callisthenes, Ptolemy) are lost and survive via Arrian, Plutarch, Diodorus
Sources: Babylonian astronomical diaries (death of Alexander, 323 BCE); Contemporary coinage and inscriptions in Alexander's name; Arrian, Anabasis (2nd c. CE, based on Ptolemy and Aristobulus); The 20/21 September 331 BCE lunar eclipse (Plutarch, Life of Alexander 31.8, 'eleven days before Gaugamela') — an independent astronomical anchor on the campaign chronology; The lost primary histories: Callisthenes (court historian), Ptolemy I and Aristobulus (participants); surviving only through Arrian/Plutarch (2nd c. CE) and the 'Vulgate' line Cleitarchus -&gt; Diodorus/Curtius/Justin — the narrative texture is ~400 years removed
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence contradicting the campaign's basic geography or chronology; none exists</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.5258,40.7625,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-356</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mieza Royal Gymnasium identified as Aristotle's school where Alexander was tutored (Sept 2026 announcement)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The announcement itself and the excavation are documented by the excavating authority (Hellenic Ministry of Culture, Sept 2026) and multiple independent news reports with on-the-record archaeologists; the building remains are archaeologically real. The IDENTIFICATION is the ministry/excavator's claim — it awaits peer-reviewed publication (Hatzopoulos's caveat) — so the announcement event is CORROBORATED while the identification stays a claim pending the paper. VERIFIED is not reached. Wrightson's 'one stylus belonged to Alexander' remark is reported as a dream, not evidence.
Layer: real · Place: Mieza (ancient town; Royal Gymnasium / Didaskaleion complex), near Naoussa, northern Greece — ~43 km from Pella
Dates: -343 → -340 (confidence: medium)

The excavated royal complex at ancient Mieza, northern Greece — the Didaskaleion (teaching area) with lecture rooms and stone benches, a three-part banquet complex with mosaic floors, palaestra, stadium and 200-meter running track — is the royal school where Aristotle tutored the young Alexander (c. 343–340 BCE). The Hellenic Ministry of Culture announced the identification in early September 2026 from the ongoing excavation led by Angeliki Kottaridi (work began 2024 under a Ministry–Municipality of Naoussa agreement). The complex dates to Philip II's reign; its architecture closely resembles Philip's palace at Aigai. Independent scholars (Graham Wrightson, Jeanne Reames) publicly endorsed the identification; Miltiades Hatzopoulos cautioned it is probably right but awaits a peer-reviewed publication of the evidence.

Earliest attestation: Ancient literary tradition that Aristotle taught Alexander at Mieza (Plutarch, Alexander 7–8); excavation announcement September 2026
Sources: Hellenic Ministry of Culture — announcement of the Mieza Royal Gymnasium / Didaskaleion findings, early September 2026 (excavation led by honorary curator of antiquities Angeliki Kottaridi, since 2024); PEOPLE (Sept 29, 2026): newly uncovered mosaics and ruins strengthening the identification; on-the-record comment from Jeanne Reames (Univ. of Nebraska) — 'all the evidence suggests' this is the school; Graham Wrightson (South Dakota State Univ.) on the four styluses — 'allows us to dream that one belonged to Alexander' (reported as aspiration, not evidence); Miltiades Hatzopoulos — probably the right place, but no peer-reviewed paper yet (the caution); Daily Galaxy (Sept 30, 2026) summary of the ministry's release: lecture hall, banquet spaces, athletic facilities, architectural decorations
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed publication of the excavation evidence confirming or rejecting the identification; an alternative identification of the complex's function from the epigraphy or stratigraphy</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.133,40.633,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-343</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alexander's consultation of the oracle of Zeus-Ammon at Siwa (331 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Four independent surviving traditions (Arrian, Plutarch, Diodorus, Curtius) agree on the essentials — the desert march, the consultation, the public greeting as son of the god — plus a near-contemporary fragment (Callisthenes via Strabo) and corroborating material evidence: a genuine oracle temple at Aghurmi whose fame demonstrably grew after 331, and Alexander's subsequent son-of-Ammon titulary and posthumous ram's-horns iconography. No contemporary attestation survives, so VERIFIED is not reached.
Layer: real · Place: Oracle Temple of Zeus-Ammon (Ammoneion), Aghurmi, Siwa Oasis, Matrouh Governorate, Egypt
Dates: -331 → -331 (confidence: medium)

In early 331 BCE, Alexander the Great marched across the Libyan desert from the Mediterranean coast to the Siwa oasis and consulted the oracle of Zeus-Ammon at the Ammoneion (Oracle Temple, Aghurmi), where the priests publicly greeted him as the son of the god.

Earliest attestation: Callisthenes of Olynthus (contemporary court historian, lost), preserved via Strabo 17.1.43 on the son-of-Zeus claim. Earliest surviving full accounts: Diodorus 17.49-51, Curtius 4.7, Plutarch Alex. 26-27, Arrian Anab. 3.3-4 (all 1st-2nd c. CE, built on the lost Ptolemy/Aristobulus/Cleitarchus memoirs).
Sources: Arrian, Anabasis of Alexander 3.3-4 (based on Ptolemy and Aristobulus; Arrian explicitly flags which embellishments came from which source, 3.3.5-6); Plutarch, Life of Alexander 26-27 (public greeting 'as a father greeting his son'; private consultation; alleged letter to Olympias); Diodorus Siculus 17.49-51 (the journey; oracular answers: vengeance on Philip's murderers, dominion of the world); Curtius Rufus 4.7 (the consultation; the priest's 'son of Ammon' greeting); Justin 11.11; Strabo 17.1.43, preserving Callisthenes on the son-of-Zeus claim; Robin Lane Fox, Alexander the Great (1973): the visit as 'the strangest strand in Alexander's life and legend'; The Oracle Temple (Ammoneion) at Aghurmi, Siwa Oasis — 26th-Dynasty fabric, Amasis reliefs, Graeco-Roman annexes (archaeological)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Egyptian or Persian administrative document contradicting the visit, or proof the Ammoneion had no oracle function in the 4th century BCE — no such evidence exists.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>25.5195,29.2032,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-331</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alexandria-on-the-Tigris (Charax Spasinou) — location confirmed at Jebel Khayyaber</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 324 BCE foundation is attested in the ancient campaign tradition; the location is now independently confirmed by multi-method archaeological survey (drone/aerial, caesium magnetometer, 500+ km² surface survey) reporting a fully planned metropolis consistent with the literary city. No contemporary inscription naming Alexander at the site has yet been published, so VERIFIED is not reached.
Layer: real · Place: Jebel Khayabir (spelled 'Khayyaber' in some 2026 reporting), Al-Qurna District, Basra Governorate, southern Iraq — the Alexandria-on-the-Tigris / Charax Spasinou site
Dates: -324 → -324 (confidence: medium)

Alexander the Great founded the port city of Alexandria-on-the-Tigris in 324 BCE on his return from the Indus Valley; the city (later renamed Charax Spasinou) stood at Jebel Khayyaber in southern Iraq, and its location — first proposed by John Hansman in 1965 — was confirmed in early 2026 by the Charax Spasinou Project (Stefan Hauser, University of Konstanz) using aerial/drone photography, caesium-magnetometer geophysics, and over 500 km² of surface survey, revealing a planned grid, harbor/canal works, and residential blocks among the largest in the ancient world.

Earliest attestation: Ancient written sources on the 324 BCE foundation (Arrian; the campaign tradition); site identification proposed by John Hansman, 1965
Sources: Stefan R. Hauser / Charax Spasinou Project, University of Konstanz — press release, January 2026; synthesis in Hauser 2025, Economies of the Edge (Heidelberg UP); Archaeology Magazine, 'New Research Confirms Location of Lost City Founded by Alexander the Great' (Feb 2026, print July/August 2026): aerial photo of the walls at Jebel Khayyaber; grid, canals, insulae; temple complexes and industrial districts; Hansman, John (1965): first modern identification of Charax Spasinou at Jebel Khayyaber; Fassbinder/Lambers 2019 magnetometer study (prior geophysical basis)
Falsifiability: Excavation producing a foundation inscription or stratified sequence inconsistent with the 324 BCE foundation / Hansman identification</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>47.582,30.893,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-324</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The death of Alexander at Babylon and the lost tomb</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The death at Babylon in June 323 BCE is anchored by a contemporary Babylonian diary and agreed by all independent traditions — VERIFIED by convergence. The cause is unattested: the ancient symptom list is second-hand and ~400 years removed, and every modern diagnosis is a retro-diagnosis competing with five others on the same thin evidence — SPECULATION by doctrine ('reasoned but unattested'). The tomb's location is simply unknown, and the record says so instead of grading a location.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (death place); Alexandria, Egypt (tomb — location lost)
Dates: -323 → -323 (confidence: high)

Alexander III died at Babylon on 11 June 323 BCE (29 Ajaru), after about ten days of fever, at age 32. The medical cause is unattested: poisoning, malaria, typhoid, West Nile virus, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and pancreatitis are all modern retro-diagnoses from symptom lists written ~400 years later. His body was carried to Memphis then Alexandria by Ptolemy I; the tomb survived into the Roman period (Augustus visited it) and is now lost — the famous 'Alexander Sarcophagus' from Sidon is not his (it belonged to Abdalonymus, king of Sidon).

Earliest attestation: Babylonian astronomical diary -323 B (contemporary, rev.: 'The king died'); Arrian 7.25-27 (2nd c. CE, from the Ephemerides journal + Ptolemy/Aristobulus); Plutarch Alex. 76-77 (1st/2nd c. CE)
Sources: Babylonian astronomical diary No. -323 B (Sachs &amp; Hunger, Astronomical Diaries and Related Texts from Babylonia, vol. III): contemporary record of the king's death, 29 Ajaru; Arrian, Anabasis 7.25-27: the ten-day fever narrative, drawn from the Ephemerides (royal journal) and the lost memoirs of Ptolemy and Aristobulus; Plutarch, Life of Alexander 76-77: fever account and explicit dismissal of the poisoning story; Diodorus 17.117-118; Curtius 10.5 (the Vulgate tradition via Cleitarchus): alternative, more sensational narratives of the death; Suetonius, Augustus 18; Dio 51.16: Augustus visits Alexander's tomb in Alexandria — the tomb still existed in the Roman period; Hall, Katherine (2018), Ancient History Bulletin: Guillain-Barre ascending-paralysis retro-diagnosis (accounts for the 'body showed no decay for six days' claim — he may not have been dead yet); Marr &amp; Calisher (2003), Emerging Infectious Diseases: West Nile virus hypothesis; Engels (1978): malaria hypothesis — both as modern retro-diagnoses
Falsifiability: A contemporary document contradicting the Babylonian diary's death notice; or a verified, excavated tomb with inscriptions naming Alexander III. None exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-323</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The death of Alexander at Babylon and the lost tomb</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The death at Babylon in June 323 BCE is anchored by a contemporary Babylonian diary and agreed by all independent traditions — VERIFIED by convergence. The cause is unattested: the ancient symptom list is second-hand and ~400 years removed, and every modern diagnosis is a retro-diagnosis competing with five others on the same thin evidence — SPECULATION by doctrine ('reasoned but unattested'). The 2025 paper does not change this: it is an AI-assisted re-reading of the same symptom lists, not new evidence. The tomb's location is simply unknown, and the record says so instead of grading a location.
Layer: real · Place: Babylon (death place); Alexandria, Egypt (tomb — location lost)
Dates: -323 → -323 (confidence: high)

Alexander III died at Babylon on 11 June 323 BCE (29 Ajaru), after about ten days of fever, at age 32. The medical cause is unattested: poisoning, malaria, typhoid, West Nile virus, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and pancreatitis are all modern retro-diagnoses from symptom lists written ~400 years later. His body was carried to Memphis then Alexandria by Ptolemy I; the tomb survived into the Roman period (Augustus visited it) and is now lost — the famous 'Alexander Sarcophagus' from Sidon is not his (it belonged to Abdalonymus, king of Sidon).

Earliest attestation: Babylonian astronomical diary -323 B (contemporary, rev.: 'The king died'); Arrian 7.25-27 (2nd c. CE, from the Ephemerides journal + Ptolemy/Aristobulus); Plutarch Alex. 76-77 (1st/2nd c. CE)
Sources: Babylonian astronomical diary No. -323 B (Sachs &amp; Hunger, Astronomical Diaries and Related Texts from Babylonia, vol. III): contemporary record of the king's death, 29 Ajaru; Arrian, Anabasis 7.25-27: the ten-day fever narrative, drawn from the Ephemerides (royal journal) and the lost memoirs of Ptolemy and Aristobulus; Plutarch, Life of Alexander 76-77: fever account and explicit dismissal of the poisoning story; Diodorus 17.117-118; Curtius 10.5 (the Vulgate tradition via Cleitarchus): alternative, more sensational narratives of the death; Suetonius, Augustus 18; Dio 51.16: Augustus visits Alexander's tomb in Alexandria — the tomb still existed in the Roman period; Hall, Katherine (2018), Ancient History Bulletin: Guillain-Barre ascending-paralysis retro-diagnosis (accounts for the 'body showed no decay for six days' claim — he may not have been dead yet); Marr &amp; Calisher (2003), Emerging Infectious Diseases: West Nile virus hypothesis; Engels (1978): malaria hypothesis — both as modern retro-diagnoses; Zhou AL et al. (2025), 'Decoding Alexander the Great's gastrointestinal cause of death using artificial wisdom: An artificial intelligence-human inquiry into a medical mystery,' World J Gastroenterol 31(46):111669. ChatGPT-4 Pro's differential (typhoid, malaria, WNV encephalitis, pancreatitis, poison) cross-checked by two human clinicians — the AI hallucinated citations and was corrected by the humans; final human-reviewed conclusion: C. jejuni-induced Guillain-Barre syndrome most credible. First scholarly engagement with Hall's line since 2019; reinforces it.; 2026 evidence-state survey: no 2025–2026 publication challenges the Gaugamela diary/eclipse anchor (331 BCE) or locates the tomb; Kasta tumulus (Amphipolis) peribolos fully exposed May 2026 (Greek Ministry of Culture) with no occupant identified — no Alexander attribution; Mieza 'School of Aristotle' excavation announcements (Sept 2026) carry no inscription and no attribution finding.; Greek Ministry of Culture statement (May 2026, reported via vema.com.au): Kasta tumulus (Amphipolis) to open to the public in early 2028 — conservation and partial restoration ongoing, visitor facilities and a small museum under construction; minister Lina Mendoni: aiming 'to deliver the monument, safe and in its entirety, at the beginning of 2028.' The opening is an administrative event, not an attribution finding: the five sets of scattered remains (woman aged 60+, two men 35-40, a newborn, one cremated adult) remain unidentified; the excavator's Hephaestion suggestion and press 'mother/wife/friend' readings carry no inscription.; Hellenic Ministry of Culture announcement (Sept 2026, via Smithsonian Magazine 28 Sept 2026): Mieza Royal Gymnasium (near Naoussa) excavation — mosaic-decorated Didaskaleion (teaching area), palaestra, stadium, 200m xystus; four writing styluses, coins, pottery; excavator Angeliki Kottaridi's attribution (Aristotle taught the teenage Alexander here) vs Miltiades Hatzopoulos's caution that no scientific paper has yet been published. The site's identity is the excavator's stated hypothesis, not a published finding; no inscription names Aristotle or Alexander. Looser press tellings ('school discovered', myteachnology) carry the same announced facts with weaker hedging — venue-not-evidence, fenced.
Falsifiability: A contemporary document contradicting the Babylonian diary's death notice; or a verified, excavated tomb with inscriptions naming Alexander III. None exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-323</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 300-year source gap behind Alexander's life</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The dates of composition of the five continuous accounts are uncontested manuscript-chronology facts, and the fragment-only survival of the eyewitness memoirs is the standing position of the field (Nawotka et al. 2019; standard handbooks). VERIFIED by convergence of independent bibliographical evidence — not by any single excavation.
Layer: real · Place: Alexandria, Egypt (composition center of the lost traditions and the Romance; the Library as the symbol of what survived and what burned)
Dates: -323 → 2026 (confidence: high)

No contemporary continuous account of Alexander's life survives. The five surviving full-length ancient narratives — Diodorus (1st c. BCE), Curtius Rufus (1st c. CE), Plutarch (c. 46–c. 120 CE), Arrian (c. 86–160 CE), and Justin's epitome of Pompeius Trogus (2nd/3rd c. CE) — were all written centuries after Alexander's death in 323 BCE, each drawing on lost eyewitness and near-eyewitness works: Callisthenes (court historian), Ptolemy, Aristobulus, Onesicritus, and Nearchus, which survive only as fragments quoted in later authors. The historical Alexander is therefore reconstructed through intermediaries, and every statement about him must be weighted by the distance of its chain.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian astronomical diary -323 B (contemporary death notice); the eyewitness memoirs survive only as fragments (FGrH/Fragmente der griechischen Historiker)
Sources: Nawotka, Rollinger, Wiesehöfer, Wojciechowska (eds.), The Historiography of Alexander the Great (Harrassowitz, Classica et Orientalia 20, 2019): 'the most serious problem facing modern Alexander the Great studies: that of inadequate sources'; surviving accounts 'at least three hundred years younger than Alexander'; the search for Callisthenes, Onesicritus, Aristobulus, Ptolemy fragments and papyri; Arrian, Anabasis, preface: stated reliance on Ptolemy and Aristobulus as the most trustworthy because they wrote while Alexander lived (the one ancient source-consciousness on record); Plutarch, Life of Alexander 1.1-3: 'I am writing biography, not history' — the ancient disclaimer on his own genre
Falsifiability: The discovery of a complete contemporary narrative (e.g., a surviving copy of Callisthenes or Ptolemy) would close the gap; a verified, complete eyewitness history would supersede this record.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>29.92,31.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-323</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Library of Alexandria — the institution</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The institution is attested across independent literary lines (Strabo's eyewitness description, Athenaeus, Galen's acquisition accounts, the scholarly canon of librarians) plus the Serapeum daughter library; held below VERIFIED because the scroll-count figures are literary inflation and no excavated building is securely the Library.
Layer: real · Place: Alexandria, Egypt (Brucheion royal quarter, traditional; no excavated building securely identified)
Dates: -300 → -048 (confidence: medium)

The Great Library of Alexandria was a real institution: part of the Mouseion (Place of the Muses) founded under Ptolemy I Soter and brought to fruition under Ptolemy II Philadelphus, advised by Demetrius of Phaleron, that aggressively acquired scrolls (Galens ship-search account), produced the Pinakes of Callimachus (a 120-book catalogue), and employed Zenodotus, Apollonius of Rhodes, Eratosthenes, Aristarchus of Samothrace, and Hero — with a daughter library established at the Serapeum under Ptolemy III.

Earliest attestation: The Letter of Aristeas (2nd c. BCE — itself a Jewish forgery pushing the Septuagint story, but an early witness to the library's fame, claiming 200,000+ scrolls); Strabo, Geography 17.1.8 (c. 25 BCE — an eyewitness description of the Mouseion and library still functioning).
Sources: Strabo, Geography 17.1.8 (c. 25 BCE) — describes the Mouseion and its library still functioning ~23 years after Caesar's fire; The Letter of Aristeas (2nd c. BCE) — earliest description of the library's ambition; claims 200,000+ scrolls; itself judged a forgery (Septuagint propaganda), so its numbers are not evidence of the numbers; Athenaeus, Deipnosophistae 5.203e — the 490,000 + 42,800 scroll figures (literary, untested); Galen on the Ptolemaic ship-search — every ship entering Alexandria searched for books, copied, originals kept; The head-librarian succession: Zenodotus, Callimachus (Pinakes), Apollonius of Rhodes, Eratosthenes, Aristarchus of Samothrace — multiply attested across ancient sources
Falsifiability: Excavation of the Brucheion quarter definitively proving no such institution existed (impossible now — the royal quarter is under modern Alexandria and the sea), or contemporary Ptolemaic documents contradicting the scholarly payroll.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>29.9187,31.2001,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Manetho's Osarseph account (via Josephus)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The account is multiply attested through Josephus's quotation; the quoted content's inverting relationship to the Exodus narrative is recognized by mainstream scholarship.
Layer: real · Place: Avaris (Tell el-Dab'a, per Manetho's setting)
Dates: -300 → -250 (confidence: medium)

The 3rd-century BCE Egyptian priest-historian Manetho wrote an account, preserved in Josephus's Contra Apion (1.26-31), in which a renegade Egyptian priest named Osarseph (Osarsiph) led a group of polluted/lepers who seized Egypt for a time, were expelled, and — at the end of the story — Osarseph took the name Moses.

Earliest attestation: Josephus, Contra Apion 1.26-31, quoting Manetho's Aegyptiaca (early 1st c. CE composition, quoting a 3rd-c. BCE text)
Sources: Josephus, Contra Apion 1.26-31 (the quotation itself; Josephus cites Manetho to refute him); Wikipedia, 'Osarseph' — three proposed interpretations (Amarna memory, Hyksos memory, anti-Jewish propaganda); Assmann's composite-folk-memory proposition; Jan Assmann, Moses the Egyptian: The Memory of Egypt in Western Monotheism (Harvard UP, 1997); Israel Knohl's identification of Osarseph with Irsu (the Shasu who seized power in Egypt after Queen Twosret, per Papyrus Harris I and the Elephantine Stele; defeated and expelled by Setnakhte) — reported via TheTorah.com (2021) and the Osarseph summary page; the competing Chancellor Bay identification is 'usually rejected by scholars' (Albertz &amp; Becking); the hypothesis that the Osarseph tale is a 1st-c. BCE anti-Jewish interpolation into Manetho (Cook) is noted as unresolved; Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, 29 Jun 2026: Tell el-Ku'a (Ismailia, southern edge of the Wadi Tumilat) — complete walled Second Intermediate Period settlement: residential compound ~30x60m behind a mudbrick wall, kilns and grain silos, ten mudbrick tombs attributed to the Fifteenth (Hyksos) Dynasty, Tell el-Yahudiyeh pottery; occupation continuing into the mid-Eighteenth Dynasty. August 2026: SIP residential area at Kom el-Khilgan (Dakahlia).; vlakov.substack.com, "The 'Priest of Set' Charge Against Moses," ~2026-09-20 — essay recap of Manetho's Osarseph account (via Josephus, Contra Apion 1.26-31) and Josephus's refutation; venue recap, not new research
Falsifiability: A complete pre-Josephan manuscript of Manetho contradicting Josephus's quotation; or demonstration that Josephus invented the passage (unlikely — he cites it to attack it)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.27,30.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Maya hieroglyphic script — deciphered</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Convergent independent readings: thousands of texts read by independent scholars agree; predictive readings confirmed by new finds (e.g., San Bartolo murals, 2001); phonetic complements make the readings testable, not interpretive.
Layer: real · Place: Maya lowlands (southern Mexico, Guatemala, Belize, western Honduras, El Salvador)
Dates: -300 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

The Maya hieroglyphic script is a fully functional logosyllabic writing system that records spoken Mayan languages, and it has been substantially deciphered — the great majority of its signs can now be read.

Earliest attestation: Long Count inscriptions, Late Preclassic onward; the codex corpus (Dresden, Madrid, Paris) copied in the Postclassic
Sources: Knorozov, Yuri V. — 'Ancient Writing of Central America' (Sovetskaya Etnografiya, 1952): the phonetic break — Landa's 'alphabet' read as syllabic signs; Kelley, David H. — 'Phoneticism in the Maya Script' (1962): first Western corroboration of the phonetic reading; Proskouriakoff, Tatiana — Piedras Negras date pattern (1960): monuments record dynastic history, not astronomy alone; Berlin, Heinrich — emblem glyphs (1958): polity name-signs, enabling political geography; Coe, Michael D. — Breaking the Maya Code (1992): the decipherment history; The Dresden, Madrid, and Paris codices (bark-paper books; Dresden Codex, Sächsische Landesbibliothek)
Falsifiability: A newly found Maya text that consistently resists the established phonetic readings, or a demonstration that the phonetic complements are coincidental rather than systematic</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-90.0,17.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Samaritan tenth commandment: Mount Gerizim</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Samaritan tenth commandment is documented in the Samaritan Pentateuch's Decalogue recensions and in the community's practice; no scholar of any camp disputes that the Samaritan version differs. The dispute is only direction (insertion vs preservation), which the record fences.
Layer: real · Place: Mount Gerizim (the Samaritan Decalogue's tenth word; the comparison is documentary across living traditions)
Dates: -0300 → -0100 (confidence: medium)

The Samaritan Pentateuch's Decalogue carries a tenth commandment absent from the Jewish and Christian versions: a commandment to build an altar on Mount Gerizim (commanded, the Samaritan text says, as Moses received it), constructed from the Deuteronomy 27:4 altar-command with 'Gerizim' in place of 'Ebal.' The Samaritan tradition thus holds that the Ten Commandments number ten differently — the same covenant, a different tenth word. The 'word-for-word' telling of the Decalogue fails a second time: the commandments were counted differently by living communities, not only copied differently by scribes.

Earliest attestation: The Samaritan Pentateuch manuscripts (Exodus 20 / Deuteronomy 5 in the Samaritan recension); the living Samaritan liturgy on Mount Gerizim.
Sources: The Samaritan Pentateuch, Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 recensions — the Gerizim altar commandment (Deut 27:4 'Gerizim' reading) interpolated as the tenth word; Encyclopaedia Judaica / standard Samaritan-studies reference — the Samaritan Decalogue's tenth commandment concerning Mount Gerizim, documented across Samaritan manuscripts
Falsifiability: A Samaritan manuscript tradition without the Gerizim commandment would falsify this record; none is known. Held below VERIFIED: the record grades a version-plurality fact across traditions, not a single excavated artifact.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2705,32.2016,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Translation Rule (VIII.i synthesis)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent, multiply-attested crossings (almah/parthenos, qaran/cornuta, kamelos/kamilos) each show the grade moving at the translation, not the source.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (oracle) / Alexandria (Septuagint) / Bethlehem (Vulgate) — the rule spans the crossings
Dates: -0300 → 1611 (confidence: high)

A claim's evidence grade can turn on a translation choice: the earliest surviving witness to a text is often a translation, and the translation is a new, separately dated claim — so the timeline grades the crossing, not just the source. Where the Hebrew says one thing and the Greek, Latin, or English says another, the grade follows the word.

Earliest attestation: Demonstrated in the 2026 VIII.i synthesis pass; the exhibits are ancient
Sources: Isaiah 7:14 — Hebrew ha-almah vs LXX parthenos; HALOT; the GOARCH almah etymology survey; Exodus 34:29 — Hebrew qaran vs Vulgate cornuta; Michelangelo's Moses (1513-1515, San Pietro in Vincoli, Rome) as the material witness; Matthew 19:24 // Mark 10:25 // Luke 18:25 — kamelos vs kamilos variant; Metzger, Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament ('an attempt to soften the rigor of the statement')
Falsifiability: A demonstrated case where a major doctrine's textual basis reads identically in the earliest Hebrew/Aramaic and in every ancient translation, with no variant at any crossing, would bound the rule's scope</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pharos of Alexandria (early 3rd c. BCE)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested across independent lines: classical literary tradition (Strabo, Pliny), a millennium of Arabic and European eyewitness descriptions, and the underwater archaeological record (Empereur 1994-present, 2025 block raisings). Exact height and summit details stay estimates.
Layer: real · Place: Island of Pharos, Alexandria, Egypt (site of the Citadel of Qaitbay)
Dates: -280 → -247 (confidence: medium)

The Pharos of Alexandria — the great lighthouse on the island of Pharos off Alexandria, Egypt — was a purpose-built monumental navigational tower constructed in the early 3rd century BCE (commissioned under Ptolemy I Soter, traditionally completed under Ptolemy II Philadelphus), attributed to the architect Sostratus of Cnidus; it stood in three tiers (square base, octagonal middle, cylindrical top), burned a furnace-fed beacon by night (with a polished-bronze mirror reported for day), endured earthquakes from 956 CE onward with the 1303 Crete quake decisive, and its stones were reused for the Citadel of Qaitbay built on the site in 1477.

Earliest attestation: Classical: Strabo (Geography 17.1.6, 1st c. BCE/CE) and Pliny the Elder (Natural History 36.83) on Sostratus; Arabic travelers' accounts (e.g. al-Idrisi, al-Balawi) on the medieval tower; archaeology: Jean-Yves Empereur's CEAlex underwater survey from 1994 (&gt;3,300 objects documented, including tower blocks), 2025 block-raising program.
Sources: Strabo, Geography 17.1.6 — the Pharos tower on the island, its beacon fires (classical literary attestation); Pliny the Elder, Natural History 36.83 — Sostratus of Cnidus as builder, the dedicatory-inscription anecdote (classical literary attestation); archaeologymag.com (2025) — CEAlex/Empereur: Sostratus of Cnidus attribution, &gt;100 m height, earthquake of 1303 rendering it inoperable, Qaitbay fortress 1477 on the same site, systematic underwater work since 1994; dailygalaxy.com (2026) — 80-ton stones raised; commissioned by Ptolemy I, designed by Sostratus; 1303 Crete quake; remains quarried, Qaitbay Citadel 1477 on the site; airial.travel Pharos of Alexandria — height estimates 100-140 m; three tapering tiers; bronze mirror/fire; earthquakes 956-1303 CE; 'pharos' as the root of 'lighthouse' in several languages
Falsifiability: Empereur's underwater block survey proving the tower blocks are later medieval spolia with no Ptolemaic origin, or a pre-Ptolemaic monumental tower elsewhere predating the Pharos as the type, would overturn the archetype claim; the exact height can never be 'verified' and stays an estimate.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>29.8856,31.2135,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-280</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The three Genesis 5 number traditions</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The three number-sets are read directly from the three manuscript traditions; every witness inspected (Ellicott, Barnes, Lange, Peake, ICDST) reports the same divergence, and the divergence sits in the begetting ages while total lifespans mostly agree.
Layer: real · Place: Textual tradition: Jerusalem (MT), Samaria (Samaritan Pentateuch), Alexandria (LXX)
Dates: -0250 → 1000 (confidence: medium)

The Adam-to-Noah genealogy's begetting ages differ systematically across the Masoretic Text, the Samaritan Pentateuch, and the Septuagint, yielding three different Adam-to-Flood totals — 1656, 1307, and 2242 years — while the patriarchs' total lifespans mostly agree.

Earliest attestation: The divergent number-sets are visible in the manuscript traditions themselves (LXX, Samaritan Pentateuch, Masoretic Text), and are tabulated in classical commentaries: Ellicott, Barnes, Lange, Peake.
Sources: Ellicott's Commentary for English Readers, Genesis 5 — the three-authority table (Hebrew 1656, Samaritan 1307, LXX 2262); Barnes Notes on Genesis 5 — Hebrew/Samaritan/Septuagint/Josephus comparison table (deluge totals 1656/1307/2262/2256); Lange's Commentary, Genesis 5 — Creation-to-Flood 1656 (Hebrew) / 1307 (Samaritan) / 2242 (Septuagint); post-Flood-to-Abraham 365 / 1015 / 1245; ICDST tabulation — begetting ages and flood dates in the MT, Jubilees, and Samaritan Pentateuch (dl.icdst.org PDF)
Falsifiability: A pre-divergence manuscript witness showing uniform begetting ages across all three traditions, or a single tradition internally reconciling the three totals</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-250</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Eratosthenes measures the Earth (c. 240 BCE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree on the method (solstice shadows, 1/50 of a circle, 5,000 stadia by bematists, 250,000/252,000 stadia total), the ~240 BCE date, the Cleomedes/Strabo transmission, and the two cancelling false assumptions (Syene not exactly on the Tropic, not exactly due south). The accuracy is fenced: 'historians believe that Eratosthenes' conclusion was between .5% and 17% off the mark' (Gizmodo) — the Egyptian-stade reading gives ~1%, the Attic-stade reading ~16%. The method is sound; the ruler is uncertain.
Layer: real · Place: Alexandria (with Syene/Aswan)
Dates: -0245 → -0235 (confidence: medium)

Around 240 BCE, Eratosthenes of Cyrene — librarian at Alexandria — measured the Earth's circumference using the summer-solstice shadows at Syene and Alexandria: the 7.2° shadow angle (1/50 of a circle) times the 5,000-stadia intercity distance gave ~250,000 stadia.

Earliest attestation: Cleomedes' Meteora (1st c. CE); Strabo's Geography (reports 252,000 stadia)
Sources: IFLScience: 'Eratosthenes measured the angle and worked out it was about 1/50th of a circumference, so knowing that the distance between Alexandria and Syene was 5,000 stadia, the full circumference of the Earth must be 50 times that... 250,000 stadia.' Caveats: 'We do not know the exact length of a stadia... that puts the circumference between about 38,624 kilometers and 46,671 kilometers' vs the true 40,075 km. The method relied on the simplified account of Cleomedes (1st c. CE).; Wikipedia, 'Eratosthenes': 'The distance between Alexandria and Syene was reported to be about 5,000 stadia, as measured by professional bematists. Eratosthenes multiplied this number by 50 and arrived at a total of roughly 250,000 stadia.' His two false assumptions 'fortuitously cancelled each other out' — Syene 1° north of the Tropic, 3° east of Alexandria.; Gizmodo: 'Due to the uncertain distance that stadia represents (and particularly which stadia he was using), historians believe that Eratosthenes' conclusion was between .5% and 17% off the mark... many scholars think it likely that he was using the Egyptian stadia (157.5 m), being in Egypt at the time. This would make his estimate only about 1% too small.'; Scribd, 'Eratosthenes: Measuring Earth's Circumference': 'Seventeen hundred years after Eratosthenes's death, while Christopher Columbus studied what Eratosthenes had written about the size of the Earth, he chose to believe, based on a map by Toscanelli, that the Earth's circumference was one-third smaller.'
Falsifiability: Evidence that the Cleomedes/Strabo accounts are fabrications, or that the measurement belongs to another scholar</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>29.9187,31.2001,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-245</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jewish reception: almah was the young woman next door</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across independent Jewish witnesses spanning a millennium; converges with modern lexicons (BDB/HALOT) and the NABRE/JPS notes.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (oracle); Alexandria (LXX)
Dates: -0200 → 1105 (confidence: medium)

Pre-Christian Jewish reading of Isaiah 7:14 was non-messianic and contemporary: Targum Jonathan renders 'the young woman is pregnant' (Aramaic 'ulaymta, no virginity term) with no messianic language at 7:14 (though it reads 9:5-6 and 11:1 messianically); Rashi reads the almah as Isaiah's own wife ('My wife will conceive this year. This was the fourth year of Ahaz') and rejects the Hezekiah identification on chronology; Ibn Ezra agrees. The 2nd-c. CE Jewish Greek revisers (Aquila, Symmachus, Theodotion) all render neanis ('young woman'), correcting the LXX's parthenos. The 'virgin' reading enters via the Septuagint, which Matthew 1:23 quotes.

Earliest attestation: Targum Jonathan on Isaiah 7:14; Aquila/Symmachus/Theodotion Greek renderings; Rashi's commentary
Sources: Targum Jonathan Isa 7:14 ('ulaymta); the Three's neanis; Rashi ('My wife will conceive this year'); Soncino Isaiah p.35 summary — surveyed 2026-10-18; NABRE footnote: 'Hebrew almah designates a young woman of marriageable age without specific reference to virginity... this translation underlies Mt 1:23'
Falsifiability: A pre-Christian Jewish source reading 7:14 messianically with a virginity term</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Nazca Geoglyphs</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines — a century of aerial and ground survey, excavation, pottery-motif links to the Nazca culture, and the 2024 AI survey — agree the geoglyphs are indigenous human-made works; no credible source disputes authorship.
Layer: real · Place: Nazca pampa, Ica region, Peru
Dates: -200 → 700 (confidence: high)

The Nazca lines and geoglyphs — ~800 straight lines, 300 geometric figures, and dozens of biomorphic figures (hummingbird, spider, monkey, condor) up to hundreds of meters long on the coastal pampa of southern Peru — are human-made works created by the pre-Incan Nazca culture (c. 200 BCE–700 CE) by clearing dark, sun-oxidized surface stones to expose the pale gypsiferous subsoil beneath; shallow grooves only a few centimeters deep, preserved by the hyper-arid climate.

Earliest attestation: Peruvian archaeologist Toribio Mejía Xesspe noted the figures in 1927; scientific study began with Paul Kosok's 1939 aerial observations (Long Island University).
Sources: UNESCO World Heritage Centre — 'Lines and Geoglyphs of Nasca and Palpa' (inscribed 1994); Paul Kosok (1939) aerial observations — launched scientific study of the pampa; Maria Reiche (1941–1998) — four decades of ground survey, mapping, preservation; Reiche's astronomical-calendar thesis for the lines; Gerald Hawkins (1968) — archaeoastronomical computer analysis found no statistically significant celestial correlation; Reiche's calendar thesis not sustained; Johan Reinhard — water-cult/fertility ritual hypothesis (mountain-deity ethnographic-archaeological work); Sakai, M. et al. (2024) 'AI-accelerated Nazca survey nearly doubles the number of known figurative geoglyphs and sheds light on their purpose', PNAS 121(40), e2407652121 — 303 new figurative geoglyphs in six months (Yamagata University + IBM Research); relief-type geoglyphs as trail-side 'billboards', line-type for community ceremony; Anthony Aveni — Between the Lines (statistical rebuttal of celestial alignments; processional/ritual walkway readings)
Falsifiability: Proof that the geoglyphs are modern forgeries on a mass scale (e.g., 20th-century construction) would overturn authorship; absent that, the indigenous-human origin stands</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-75.13,-14.739,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The oldest extant paper: Fangmatan, early 2nd c. BCE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A surviving artifact in excavated context (Tomb 5, early Western Han), identified as paper by fiber analysis, with the map drawn on it; the early-Han Xuanquan and Zhongyan paper fragments corroborate repeated utilitarian papermaking before 105 CE. Held below VERIFIED on the absolute-date line (tomb-context, not radiocarbon dating) and because the 'oldest extant' superlative is hostage to future finds.
Layer: real · Place: Fangmatan, near Tianshui, Gansu
Dates: -0199 → -0100 (confidence: medium)

Paper was already being made in China by the early 2nd century BCE — the Fangmatan Tomb 5 map fragment is the oldest extant sheet of paper on Earth.

Earliest attestation: The fragment itself, excavated 1986, Fangmatan Tomb 5, near Tianshui, Gansu — the artifact is the attestation
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Fangmatan' — Tomb 5 excavated 1986, early Western Han (early 2nd c. BCE); occupant buried with a paper map on his chest; water damage left a single 5.6 x 2.6 cm fragment; the map is drawn on the oldest extant piece of paper (citing Yi &amp; Liu 2010, p.64; Behr 2007, p.113); China Development Gateway (Xinhua), Nov 2016 — Li Xiaocen (Nanjing University of Information Science and Technology) on the fragment: uneven fiber distribution under the microscope; yellow, rough surface, randomly yet densely interlaced fibers; traits of an ancient paper-making technology very different from Cai Lun's, still used by ethnic minorities in Tibet, Yunnan and Xinjiang; hal.science hal-03221031 — 'A Study of Ancient Paper Fragments from an Eastern Han Dynasty Tomb in Minfeng County, Xinjiang': the Niya paper (flaxlike fibers, no screen pattern, watering method) consistent with the techniques of early Han paper such as the Xuanquan paper from Gansu and the Zhongyan paper from Shaanxi — repeated, distinctively primitive Han papermaking
Falsifiability: Re-dating of Tomb 5 to post-105 CE, or a laboratory finding that the fragment is not felted plant-fiber paper, would demote the grade; a pre-Western-Han paper find would retire the 'oldest extant' superlative, not the grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>105.72,34.58,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-199</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pergamum Library — Alexandria's Hellenistic rival</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The institution converges across Strabo, the excavated building with its roll-niches, and the Roman literary tradition; held below VERIFIED because the 200,000 figure and the Antony gift are single-line literary reports, not independently tested.
Layer: real · Place: Pergamum (Bergama, Turkey) — the acropolis library building near the sanctuary of Athena
Dates: -197 → -041 (confidence: medium)

Pergamum under Eumenes II (197-158 BCE) built the ancient world's great rival to the Alexandrian library — ~200,000 scrolls by literary report, in a library building excavated in the 1880s (Carl Conze's team) with wall niches for the rolls — and the Pergamene workshop refined parchment (pergamena) into a durable, smooth writing surface; the 'Antony gave Cleopatra the 200,000 Pergamene scrolls' episode (Plutarch, Antony 58.9) is single-source and untested.

Earliest attestation: Strabo on Pergamum's library; Plutarch, Antony 58.9 (c. 100 CE) for the Cleopatra gift; Pliny NH 13.21 for the parchment-invention story (DEBUNKED as invention — separate record).
Sources: Strabo, Geography — Pergamum's library as the Hellenistic rival to Alexandria; Plutarch, Antony 58.9 — Antony gave Cleopatra 200,000 rolls from the Pergamene library (single-source; carried at CLAIMED); Carl Conze's 1880s excavations — the Pergamene acropolis library building with wall niches for scroll rolls, near the sanctuary of Athena; Reed (1975) — the Pergamene refinement of parchment manufacture: the simplified liquor bath and stretched-state drying that produced durable, smooth, uniform sheets
Falsifiability: Proof the excavated 'library' building was something else, or a contemporary source listing Pergamum's collection as small — neither exists; the building's niches and Strabo's testimony converge.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.1836,39.1322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-197</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Jewish-internal resurrection lineage (Daniel, 2 Maccabees, Josephus)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent witnesses in a documentary chain — apocalypse, martyrology, and a historian's report of a living school's doctrine — all pre- or contemporary with Christian origins.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem / Judea
Dates: -0165 → 0100 (confidence: medium)

Bodily resurrection belief was already documented inside Second Temple Judaism before Christianity: Daniel 12:2 (c. 165 BCE), 2 Maccabees 7 (c. 124 BCE), and Josephus's report of Pharisaic doctrine (Jewish War 2.163) — the proximate, textually chained source of the early Christian resurrection concept.

Earliest attestation: Daniel 12:2 — 'multitudes who sleep in the dust of the earth will awake'
Sources: Daniel 12:2 (Maccabean-era apocalyptic; also Isaiah 24-27, the 'Isaianic Apocalypse', earlier mythic substrate); 2 Maccabees 7 (the martyred brothers' resurrection hope, c. 124 BCE); Josephus, Jewish War 2.163 (the Pharisees' doctrine of resurrection)
Falsifiability: Evidence that Daniel 12:2 and 2 Maccabees 7 postdate Christian origins, or that Pharisaic resurrection doctrine is a Josephan invention, would collapse the chain.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-165</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The cruse of oil: Hanukkah's eight-day miracle telling</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested only in a sixth-century CE aggadah; absent from 1 Maccabees, 2 Maccabees, Josephus, the Jerusalem Talmud, and the Al HaNissim prayer — every source closer to the event.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem — the Temple
Dates: -0164 → -0164 (confidence: low)

When the Temple in Jerusalem was rededicated in 164 BCE, a single cruse of pure oil bearing the High Priest's seal burned for eight days instead of one — the oil miracle is the original account of Hanukkah.

Earliest attestation: Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 21b — a sixth-century CE aggadah, roughly five to six centuries after the event
Sources: Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 21b — 'When the Greeks entered the Temple, they desecrated all the oils... they could only find one jar of oil with the High Priest's seal, with enough to last for one day. A miracle happened, and it lasted for eight' (sixth-century CE aggadah); Wikipedia 'Miracle of the cruse of oil' — 'Contemporary accounts of the revolt, including 1 Maccabees, 2 Maccabees, and writings by Josephus, do not mention the miracle of the oil, attributing the eight-day holiday instead to the military victory and the rededication of the Temple. The story is also absent in the Jerusalem Talmud.'; The Algemeiner, 'The Myths of Hanukkah' (2020) — the miracle 'is first described in the Talmud (Shabbat 21b), completed about 600 years after the events described in the books of the Maccabees'; Al HaNissim 'talks about Matisyahu and his sons defeating the Greeks... But no miracle'; 1 Maccabees 4:36-59 — the rededication account, no oil (negative attestation); Josephus, Antiquities 12.316-325 — the rededication account, no oil (negative attestation)
Falsifiability: A pre-Bavli source (e.g. a securely dated Dead Sea or early rabbinic text) narrating the eight-day cruse would promote the grade; proof that Shabbat 21b's story is a later editorial insertion with no earlier tradition would push it toward DEBUNKED.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2354,31.7781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-164</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Temple rededication of 164 BCE — the festival before the miracle story</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent ancient witnesses (1 Maccabees, 2 Maccabees, Josephus) agree on the rededication and the eight-day festival; none mentions an oil miracle.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem — the Temple
Dates: -0164 → -0164 (confidence: high)

In 164 BCE, on the 25th of Kislev, the Maccabees rededicated the Jerusalem Temple after defeating the Seleucids and celebrated for eight days — the festival that became Hanukkah.

Earliest attestation: 1 Maccabees 4:36-59 (written ~100 BCE)
Sources: 1 Maccabees 4:36-59 — the rededication, the 25th of Kislev, the eight-day celebration; no oil miracle; 2 Maccabees 10:1-8 — the eight-day celebration 'in the manner of the feast of booths' (Sukkot); no oil miracle; Josephus, Antiquities 12.316-325 — the rededication, the eight-day 'Festival of Lights'; no oil miracle; Al HaNissim prayer — prays of Matisyahu and his sons defeating the Greeks, God's deliverance, purifying the Temple, lighting lights — but no miracle
Falsifiability: A contemporary Seleucid record showing the Temple un-rededicated through 163 BCE, or proof 1 Maccabees is a wholly fictional composition, would collapse the grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2354,31.7781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-164</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Nash Papyrus: the oldest Decalogue, and it is not the Masoretic one</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Artifact held and published by Cambridge; dating and text-description converge across independent encyclopedia entries (Jewish Virtual Library / Encyclopaedia Judaica, encyclopedia.com, Cambridge's own Lines of Thought exhibition, historyofinformation.com) and the original Cooke publication. The variant facts (harmonization, LXX order, 'house of bondage' omission) are reported identically in all of them. CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: provenance unknown.
Layer: real · Place: Cambridge University Library, Cambridge, UK (MS Or.233; alleged Fayyum, Egypt provenance fenced)
Dates: -0150 → -0100 (confidence: high)

The Nash Papyrus (Cambridge University Library, MS Or.233) is a second-century BCE (c. 150-100 BCE) Hebrew manuscript containing the Decalogue followed by the start of the Shema (Deut 6:4-5). Four fragments, 24 lines, acquired from an Egyptian antiquities dealer by W. L. Nash (1898/1902, published by S. A. Cooke 1903); before the Dead Sea Scrolls it was the oldest Hebrew biblical text known. Its Decalogue harmonizes Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5, accords closely with the Septuagint's Exodus 20, orders the later commandments Adultery-Murder-Steal (the LXX order, not the Masoretic Murder-Adultery-Steal), and omits the phrase 'house of bondage' (used in both Masoretic versions) — plausibly a reflection of its Egyptian composition setting. The manuscript is not a scroll but a single sheet, likely a phylactery/tefillin extract or a liturgical copy matching the Second-Temple daily recitation (m. Tamid 5:1). The oldest Decalogue we own does not match the Masoretic text in any hymnal.

Earliest attestation: The papyrus itself (2nd c. BCE); S. A. Cooke, 'A Pre-Masoretic Biblical Papyrus,' Proceedings of the Society of Biblical Archaeology 25 (1903): 34-56.
Sources: Cambridge University Library, 'The ten commandments' (Lines of Thought exhibition) — Nash Papyrus, Egypt, 2nd c. BCE; the text 'is different from that found in the Bible, suggesting that even this fundamental religious text has changed'; MS Or.233; Jewish Virtual Library / Encyclopaedia Judaica, 'Nash Papyrus' — 2nd-c. BCE, 24 lines, Decalogue + Shema, published Cooke 1903; text accords with the Septuagint of Exodus 20; daily-liturgy reading per m. Tamid 5:1; historyofinformation.com, 'Nash Papyrus' — acquired 1898 by W. L. Nash, presented to Cambridge; Cook 1903 first description; re-dated to c. 150-100 BCE; harmonizes Ex 20 + Deut 5; Adultery-Murder-Steal order = LXX order; omits 'house of bondage'
Falsifiability: Redating the papyrus securely outside the 2nd c. BCE, or a demonstrated forgery finding, would supersede this record. Held below VERIFIED: provenance is unknown and the reading-context function is inferred, not proven.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>0.1149,52.2043,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Silk Road trade network (c. 130 BCE – c. 1500 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across independent traditions (Han documents, Sogdian merchant letters, Persian/Arabic geographies, Italian merchant handbooks) and physically excavated at the oasis cities; the exact structure and volume remain debated.
Layer: real · Place: Samarkand (the road-city that taxed it)
Dates: -0130 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

A long-lived, braided network of overland and maritime trade routes connected China, Central Asia, Persia, India, and the Mediterranean world for roughly sixteen centuries — many routes, not one road.

Earliest attestation: Han sources on Zhang Qian's missions to the Western Regions (Shiji 123, c. 100 BCE) for the opening; the Sogdian Ancient Letters (c. 313 CE) for the merchant system in operation
Sources: Sima Qian, Shiji 123 (c. 100 BCE) — Zhang Qian's missions 138–115 BCE, the Han opening of the western routes; The Sogdian Ancient Letters (c. 313 CE), Stein's 1907 Dunhuang watchtower find — merchant houses operating the network in their own words; Francesco Pegolotti, Pratica della mercatura (c. 1340) — the 14th-century route to China with station-by-station costs; UNESCO Silk Roads Programme (1988–); the Chang'an–Tianshan corridor World Heritage inscription (2014, 33 sites); Valerie Hansen, The Silk Road: A New History (2012) — the 'many routes' thesis; Beckwith, Empires of the Silk Road — the regional-exchange caution
Falsifiability: Excavation of the full oasis-city sequence yielding no long-distance trade goods at all, or documentary proof that all east-west movement in the period was coastal-maritime, would falsify the overland network as a system</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>66.96,39.65,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-130</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Seven years' bad luck for a broken mirror — the Roman attribution</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The belief's existence as folklore is undoubted; the Roman origin story is widely asserted but unsourced in every source consulted — grade carries the attribution, not the folklore itself.
Layer: real · Place: Rome (claimed origin; unproven)
Dates: -100 → 200 (confidence: traditional)

The folklore that breaking a mirror brings seven years of bad luck is said to be Roman in origin — the Romans supposedly believed the gods watched mortal souls through mirrors, and that the body renewed itself every seven years, so the bad luck would expire at the next renewal. The Roman attribution is asserted by modern pop outlets; no primary Latin source is cited by any of them, so it sits one grade below folklore-with-a-trail.

Earliest attestation: Modern pop treatments (Discover Magazine 2024, Google Arts &amp; Culture '18 Superstitions', sc.edu 2021, Brewminate) — all asserting a Roman origin without citing a Roman text.
Sources: Discover Magazine, 'Black Cats and Broken Mirrors' (2024) — Roman origin, seven-year body renewal; Google Arts &amp; Culture, '18 Superstitions from Around the World' — Roman contribution of the seven-years notion; sc.edu, 'How did the superstition... start' (2021) — Greek soul-reflection + Roman manufacture, seven-year renewal
Falsifiability: A primary Latin text describing seven years of bad luck for a broken mirror would raise the Roman attribution to CORROBORATED; a documented 19th-century coinage would drop it to SPECULATION.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The gougu rule — China's own right-triangle tradition</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The tradition and the diagram are real and documented, but the earliest layer's date is disputed and no transmission chain connects it to the Babylonian, Indian, or Greek traditions.
Layer: real · Place: China (textual tradition; no findspot)
Dates: -100 → 100 (confidence: low)

The Chinese gougu (勾股) tradition carries the right-triangle relationship under its own name: the Zhou Bi Suan Jing preserves the 3-4-5 triangle and the gou-gu-xian (勾股弦) rule, and Zhao Shuang's 3rd-century CE commentary gives a dissection diagram — right triangles arranged inside a square, areas compared — a visual demonstration of the theorem in a non-Euclidean style.

Earliest attestation: The received Zhou Bi Suan Jing (Han); Zhao Shuang commentary (c. 250 CE) for the dissection diagram
Sources: Zhou Bi Suan Jing (received text, Han) — the gougu passages; Zhao Shuang commentary (c. 3rd c. CE) — the dissection diagram; Cullen, Astronomy and Mathematics in Ancient China: The Zhou Bi Suan Jing (Cambridge, 1996)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the gougu passages are late interpolations, or a documented transmission route, would re-grade the record</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>113.6,34.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jinn in pre-Islamic Arabia: the Palmyrene ginnayê and the poets</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Epigraphic attestation (the carved dedications) plus an independent poetic-tradition strand — graded as the belief-phenomenon, never the beings.
Layer: real · Place: Palmyra region (inscriptions); the Arabian Peninsula (poetic tradition)
Dates: -100 → 600 (confidence: medium)

Beings named as jinn (or the Aramaic ginnayê) were attested in Arabia before Islam: Palmyrene inscriptions dedicate offerings to the 'good and rewarding gods' ginnayê, and pre-Islamic poetic tradition ascribes poetic inspiration and soothsaying to jinn companions.

Earliest attestation: The Beth Fasi'el inscription near Palmyra dedicating tribute to the Ginnaye ('the good and rewarding gods'); pre-Islamic Arabic poetry (the jinn-inspired poet / kahin tradition); the Arabic root j-n-n ('to conceal') with Semitic cognates.
Sources: Beth Fasi'el inscription near Palmyra — dedication to the Ginnaye; Pre-Islamic Arabic poetry as transmitted in Islamic-era compilations (jinn-inspiration topos); Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Jinni' — pre-Islamic belief in poet/soothsayer inspiration
Falsifiability: A re-reading of the Beth Fasi'el inscription disproving the ginnayê reading, or proof the inspiration topos appears only in post-Islamic fabrications with no authentic pre-Islamic kernel</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>38.27,34.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Magdala — the archaeology of Mary's hometown</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Excavated, published, and independently visitable; the 2026 second-synagogue report is news-grade but the excavation it reports is a real dig.
Layer: real · Place: Magdala/Migdal, Sea of Galilee
Dates: -100 → 67 (confidence: high)

The town of Magdala (Migdal; Josephus's Tarichaea) on the Sea of Galilee's western shore has yielded a first-century Jewish town destroyed in 67 CE (Vespasian's campaign): a synagogue discovered in 2009 with the carved Magdala Stone (its seven-branched menorah, at the time of the IAA excavation report, the earliest known Second Temple-period menorah depiction outside Jerusalem), mosaic floors, mikvaot, and harbor works — and in September-October 2026 the excavation of a second first-century synagogue at the site (University of Haifa), reported as making Magdala the only known site with two Second Temple-period synagogues (the superlative is press framing, fenced; the excavation itself is the graded claim).

Earliest attestation: The 2009 IAA excavation report (Avshalom-Gorni &amp; Najar)
Sources: IAA excavation report, Avshalom-Gorni &amp; Najar (2009+) — the synagogue, the Magdala Stone; Josephus, Jewish War 3.462 (Tarichaea); arkeonews, Sept 2026 — the second-synagogue excavation (University of Haifa); TBN Watchman Newscast, Oct 2026 — the second-synagogue report (Christian-media framing, fenced)
Falsifiability: Stratigraphic re-dating of either synagogue to post-70 CE, or identification of the 'second synagogue' as a non-synagogue building</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.5097,32.8276,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Roman lead-pipe water system (the foil)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested: Frontinus's firsthand treatise, surviving stamped lead fistulae across the Empire (e.g. Portus, Pompeii), and Delile et al. 2014's sediment-core Pb-isotope record matching the pipe lead to mines in Spain, France, England, and Germany. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the remaining interpretive margin around exact ingested doses.
Layer: real · Place: Rome, Italy
Dates: -0100 → 0400 (confidence: high)

Roman cities distributed water through lead pipes (fistulae); lead levels in Roman tap water were measurably elevated (about 100 times natural background), though modern sediment-core analysis finds the contamination was unlikely to have been truly harmful.

Earliest attestation: Sextus Julius Frontinus, De aquaeductu (De aquis urbis Romae), c. 97 CE — the water commissioner's own treatise documenting the nine aqueducts; Vitruvius, De architectura 8.6, on lead vs earthen pipes.
Sources: Delile, Hugo et al., 'Lead in ancient Rome's city waters,' PNAS 111(18):6594-6599, 2014 — Pb-isotope fingerprinting ties water lead to foreign mines (Spain, France, England, Germany); calcium-carbonate crust insulation; 'unlikely to have been truly harmful'; Frontinus, De aquaeductu, c. 97 CE — documents the nine aqueducts, their maintenance, and water quality administration; Vitruvius, De architectura 8.6.10-11 — warns that water through lead pipes 'must be injurious' (white lead forms) and that earthen pipes are more wholesome; notes lead workers' pallor
Falsifiability: Excavation showing Roman urban distribution used no lead pipes, or sediment analysis showing no anthropogenic lead in Imperial-era water sediment — the 2014 study confirmed both the lead and its measurable-but-unlikely-harmful level.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.5,41.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Teotihuacan, the Classic-period metropolis of central Mexico</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The city stands: 21–30 km² of excavated urban fabric, a monumental core dated by stratigraphy and radiocarbon across a century of independent campaigns (Millon's mapping project, INAH excavations, UNESCO 1987) — multiple independent lines, not one excavation.
Layer: real · Place: Teotihuacan, Valley of Mexico
Dates: -100 → 750 (confidence: medium)

Teotihuacan was a vast planned city in the Valley of Mexico, founded c. 100 BCE: at its zenith (c. 150–450 CE) it held 100,000–200,000 people, making it one of the largest cities in the world and the largest in the pre-Columbian Americas; its monumental core — the Pyramid of the Sun, the Pyramid of the Moon, the Avenue of the Dead, the Feathered Serpent Temple — was sacked and systematically burned c. 550 CE, and the city was abandoned by c. 750 CE.

Earliest attestation: The archaeology itself is the earliest witness — 21–30 km² of excavated urban fabric. Earliest surviving external written mention: Maya hieroglyphic texts of the Classic period render the city as 'puh', 'Place of Reeds'.
Sources: Rene Millon, Urbanization at Teotihuacan (University of Texas Press, 1973) — the Teotihuacan Mapping Project; George L. Cowgill, Ancient Teotihuacan: Early Urbanism in Central Mexico (Cambridge University Press, 2015); Saburo Sugiyama, Human Sacrifice, Militarism, and Rulership (Cambridge University Press, 2005) — Feathered Serpent dedicatory sacrifices (~200 victims); Sergio Gomez Chavez, Proyecto Tlalocan field reports, INAH 2003–2018 — the Feathered Serpent tunnel and its offerings; UNESCO World Heritage inscription 1987 — 'Pre-Hispanic City of Teotihuacan'
Falsifiability: Sealed-context radiocarbon or stratigraphic dates placing the monumental architecture outside c. 100 BCE–750 CE, or demonstration that the excavated fabric is modern reconstruction</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-99.8628,19.6925,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The gladiator's missio — mercy in the arena</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent Roman sources describe the mechanism (Martial's ad digitum formula, the shout vocabulary Mitte!/Iugula!, the editor's decision, the missio term, the sine missione exception); held below VERIFIED because the crowd-vs-editor decision mechanics rest on literary and later accounts rather than a single procedural document.
Layer: real · Place: Amphitheatres of the Roman world (Colosseum as the type site)
Dates: -80 → 300 (confidence: medium)

A defeated gladiator could appeal for mercy by raising an index finger (ad digitum); the munus's editor then granted or refused 'missio' (release), following the crowd's expressed wishes.

Earliest attestation: Martial, De Spectaculis 29 (1st c. CE): the law 'when the prize was set up, to fight until the finger was raised (ad digitum)'
Sources: Martial, De Spectaculis 29 — 'when the prize was set up, to fight until the finger was raised (ad digitum)'; Caesar 'obeyed his own law'; The crowd vocabulary: 'Mitte!' (let him go) / 'Iugula!' (kill him); 'Habet, hoc habet!' (he's had it) — the attested shout lexicon; The editor muneris (games' sponsor) as the decider; the emperor as the most conspicuous editor at Rome; Munus sine missione — bouts without release, the documented exception (Augustus-era restrictions tradition); Cavillargues medallion — 'STANTES MISSI' (released standing): the mercy granted, commemorated
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that defeated gladiators had no formal mercy procedure, or that 'missio' meant something other than release from the bout</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.4922,41.8902,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-80</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sepulchre road toward Calvary (Stasolla's hypothesis, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested by the excavator herself as an explicit hypothesis — single-source, unreviewed, with the excavator's own fence that it is no more than a possibility.
Layer: real · Place: Church of the Holy Sepulchre, Jerusalem (traditional)
Dates: -63 → 135 (confidence: low)

A broad stepped road uncovered in the Holy Sepulchre excavations, from the early-Roman phase before Hadrian's 2nd-century rebuilding, may have ascended toward the area of Calvary — providing access to a place of public executions. Stasolla's own words: 'One possibility – at present no more than a hypothesis.'

Earliest attestation: Excavator's hypothesis reported via press, c. 16 October 2026 (Stasolla, Sapienza)
Sources: catholiconline.news, ~16 Oct 2026: Stasolla 'suggested that the road may have provided access to an area used for public executions'; quoting her directly: 'One possibility – at present no more than a hypothesis – is that this was a suburban road ascending toward the area of the Calvary, assuming that the site functioned as a place of public executions.' Researchers also considered, and downweighted, the Hadrian-era construction-material-transport explanation (the broad step complicates cart movement).; Stasolla's context: 'What is certain is that the place of execution must have been public and accessible' — consistent with Roman crucifixion practice as deliberately public spectacle.
Falsifiability: Absolute dating of the road's construction/uses to the post-Hadrianic period, or excavation evidence that the area was not used for executions in the early-Roman phase, would close the hypothesis</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2297,31.7784,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-63</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The invention of glassblowing (c. 50–25 BCE, Levant)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: the invention event is archaeologically attested — the earliest glassblowing debris comes from a sealed Herodian-period context in Jerusalem, with Judean glass production sites also found in Galilee — but the inventor is anonymous, so it stays below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (Jewish Quarter excavations)
Dates: -50 → -25 (confidence: medium)

Glassblowing was invented in the coastal Levant in the final quarter of the 1st century BCE.

Earliest attestation: Workshop debris in the Jewish Quarter, Jerusalem — a fill layer inside a ritual bath sealed by Herodian street paving (Herod the Great / Herod Archelaus era, under Augustus)
Sources: Phys.org / Corning Museum of Glass reporting: glassblowing invented toward the end of the 1st century BCE in the coastal Levant (Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Palestine, Jordan); earliest evidence Jerusalem, final quarter of 1st c. BCE, Herod the Great / Herod Archelaus under Augustus; the tube-reworking recycling experiment; Wikipedia 'History of glass': glass blowing discovered on the Syro-Judean coast during the 1st century BCE; first evidence in the Jewish Quarter of Jerusalem, in fill inside a ritual bath overlain by Herodian street paving; Judean glass sites in Galilee; Penn Museum, 'Roman Glass': no Latin word for glass until ~65 BCE; scarcely a century after blown glass was invented it was in virtually every Roman house; Syrian and Judaean craftsmen shipped to Italy as spoils of war ~10 BCE; David Whitehouse, 'Looking Through Roman Glass', Archaeology Magazine (1997): glassblowing discovered in the Syro-Palestinian region during the first century B.C.; vessels commonplace empire-wide by the first century A.D.
Falsifiability: A blown glass vessel securely dated earlier than the Jerusalem floor, from outside the Levant</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-50</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jesus of Nazareth</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations within a century - Pauline letters (20-30 years out), the Gospels, Josephus's two passages (one near-universally authentic, one majority-partial), Tacitus's hostile Roman notice, Suetonius, and Pliny - agree on a historical figure crucified under Pilate; the prefect's own name is in stone at Caesarea. No contemporary inscription or Roman record of Jesus survives, so the ceiling is CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Nazareth (traditional)
Dates: -4 → 30 (confidence: traditional)

Jesus of Nazareth, a Jewish teacher in Roman Galilee and Judea, was crucified under Pontius Pilate (c. 30 CE); his followers proclaimed his resurrection, founding the movement that became Christianity.

Earliest attestation: Paul's letters (c. 50s CE, earliest); the Gospel of Mark (c. 70 CE); Josephus, Antiquities 18 and 20 (c. 93 CE, partially interpolated); Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (c. 116 CE). NB: these are composition dates — the earliest surviving physical witnesses are centuries later: P52 (2nd c., disputed exact date), P46/P66/P75 (c. 200–250 CE). See evt-p52-fragments-001 for the transmission gap.
Sources: Paul's letters (1 Thessalonians, Galatians, 1 Corinthians — c. 50s CE); Gospel of Mark (c. 70 CE); Josephus, Antiquities 18.63-64 and 20.200 (c. 93 CE; Testimonium partly interpolated); Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (c. 116 CE, independent Roman attestation); OpenBible.info geocoding (Nazareth, Capernaum, Jerusalem); The transmission gap: no autograph survives; earliest NT copies are 2nd-3rd c. papyri (see evt-p52-fragments-001); Josephus, Antiquities 20.200 - 'the brother of Jesus, who was called Christ, whose name was James' (authenticity accepted by the overwhelming majority of scholars); The Testimonium Flavianum (Antiquities 18.63-64): authentic core + Christian interpolations - assessed as its own record, evt-testimonium-flavianum-001; Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (c. 116 CE): 'Christus ... suffered the extreme penalty during the reign of Tiberius at the hands of one of our procurators, Pontius Pilatus' - a hostile Roman witness (calls Christianity a 'destructive superstition'); Suetonius, Life of Claudius 25.4 ('Chrestus' - Jews expelled from Rome, c. 49 CE); Pliny the Younger, Letters 10.96-97 to Trajan (Christians in Bithynia, c. 112 CE); Pilate Stone (Caesarea Maritima, 1961, Frova excavations): '[Pon]tius Pilatus [praef]ectus Iuda[ea]e' - the only non-literary confirmation of Pilate's existence; settles the title as prefect, not procurator (Tacitus's word is the anachronism). Israel Museum, Jerusalem; Gregor et al. (2025) — re-analysis of Carrier's Bayesian historicity argument reported in the secondary literature (cited via the 'On the Historicity of Jesus' survey, accessed Oct 2026): Carrier's reference class of pre-10th-century-BCE figures was challenged as producing false negatives; with post-10th-century-BCE samples (closer to Jesus' period) the computed historicity probability moved from Carrier's 33% to 99% on the corrected class. Carried secondhand — the primary paper was not read in this pass; the point recorded is the peer-reviewed direction of the 2025 exchange, not the headline number. Added 2026-10-16 (III.21 second pass).; Gathercole, S., 'The undisputed Pauline letters presuppose a historical and human Jesus', Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus (2025, per the secondary survey) — a direct peer-reviewed counter to the mythicist reading of Paul as describing a purely celestial figure. Carried secondhand; primary not read in this pass. Added 2026-10-16 (III.21 second pass).; McGrath, J., 'The Obsolete Paradigm Of A Purely Mythical Jesus' (Patheos, Aug 2026) summarizing the JSNT (Journal for the Study of the New Testament) review of a 2026 volume engaging Carrier's mythicist case — carried secondhand, the primary review not read in this pass: the review's verdict (the historicity paradigm 'alive and well', mythicism 'obsolete in 2026 as it was prior') recorded as the peer-reviewed direction of the 2026 exchange, not evidence. Added 2026-10-18 (III.21 fifth pass).
Falsifiability: A contemporary Roman or Jewish source naming Jesus of Nazareth would raise this to VERIFIED; the grade honestly reflects the gap between attestation and contemporaneity</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.3035,32.7011,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-4</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cova Dones Roman sanctuary — the Domina inscriptions (announced 2026-10-20)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — House standard for named-institution excavation announcements (cf. Saudi Heritage Commission 2026 records): named project (DONARQ), named universities, named epigraphers, specific counts and named comparanda, artifacts documented in situ — the corpus's existence and scale are CORROBORATED. Two sub-claims are graded lower and fenced: (1) the 'well-organized, structured cult' reading is the team's interpretation of the inscriptions' concentration — CLAIMED as interpretation; (2) the suggestion that the site's recorded name Cova de les Dones ('Cave of the Women') preserves a fossilized memory of the cult is explicitly offered by the team as a possibility — SPECULATION. The Domina epithet is unread as of the announcement; no peer-reviewed edition exists yet, so no part of this record is VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Cova Dones, Millares, Valencia, Spain
Dates: 1 → 200 (confidence: team-dated (epigraphic/palaeographic, preliminary))

At Cova Dones (Millares, Valencia, Spain), the DONARQ project has documented at least 120 Latin inscriptions in an underground Roman sanctuary chamber more than 200 m below the surface — the largest Latin cave-inscription corpus known in the Roman Empire, surpassing La Griega (107). Most are dated by the team to the 1st–2nd centuries CE. Five votive inscriptions are dedicated to a goddess reverentially called Domina ('the Lady'), whose specific epithet (possibly water-connected) is still being read; preliminary readings indicate some women inscribed their own vows, and in-situ offerings include spindle whorls, rings, a bracelet, chalice-shaped vessels and a Roman coin of Claudius found in the 2024 campaign.

Earliest attestation: DONARQ project announcement via University of Southampton / University of Zaragoza / University of Alicante, 20 October 2026 (phys.org syndication of the university release). The Roman sanctuary itself was first revealed in 2024; only ~15 inscriptions were previously known.
Sources: University of Southampton news release, syndicated as 'Largest collection of Roman cave inscriptions discovered in Spain', phys.org, 2026-10-20 — DONARQ project led by Dr. Aitor Ruiz-Redondo (Zaragoza/Southampton) and Dr. Virginia Barciela (Alicante); epigraphy by Dr. Silvia Alfayé (Zaragoza) and Dr. Sergio García-Dils (UNED-Seville); quotes, counts (&gt;=120 inscriptions; 5 Domina votives), La Griega comparison (107, one female name), offerings in situ, Claudius coin (2024 campaign); Ancient Pages, 'Archaeologists Uncover Largest Collection Of Roman Cave Inscriptions, Revealing Ancient Women's Cult', 2026-10-20/22 — secondary restatement of the same university release (NOT an independent source; counted as venue echo, not corroboration)
Falsifiability: Publication of the full epigraphic corpus: if the Domina dedications or the female dedicants dissolve on full reading (misread names, joins, or re-dating), the cult claim falls; if the corpus confirms &gt;=120 inscriptions with the Domina cluster intact, the grade can rise toward VERIFIED.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0001-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.773,39.186,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The folding fan was invented in Japan (~9th c.) and traveled Japan→China</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED — independent lines agree: Kyoto Women's University (Google Arts &amp; Culture) dates the invention to ~9th c. and the China export to the 10th (wasen); USC Pacific Asia Museum gives 9th c.; the haes-producties survey documents 10th-c. literary attestation and Song-court records. Held below VERIFIED — the 9th-c. date rests on court tradition and scholarship, not a surviving 9th-c. artifact.
Layer: real · Place: Heian-kyo (Kyoto), Japan
Dates: 9th century CE → 10th century CE (confidence: medium)

The folding fan (sensu/ōgi) was invented in Japan around the 9th century, in the hi-ogi form of thin cypress slats riveted at one end — and from Japan the folding fan traveled to China, not the reverse.

Earliest attestation: Japanese court use, Heian period; Japanese dictionary c. 935 listing Uchiwa and Ogi; Song-dynasty Chinese court records of folding-fan gifts
Sources: Kyoto Women's University, Lifestyle Design Laboratory (Google Arts &amp; Culture, 'Kyō-sensu — Folding Fans from Kyoto'): 'The folding fan was invented in Japan around the 9th century... Japanese folding fans were exported to mainland China in the 10th century and were known as wasen in China'; USC Pacific Asia Museum (Google Arts &amp; Culture, 'Fans of East Asia'): 'In the 9th century, the first folding fans, known as Sensu, emerged in Japan'; 'History — Japanese Fans' (haes-producties.nl): earliest literary references to the folding fan in both Japan and China date from the 10th century; Japanese dictionary c. 935 lists Uchiwa and Ogi; Song-dynasty records of folding fans as gifts at the Chinese court; Choho-era (999–1003) sumptuary laws on fans; anart4life.com ('The mystical appeal of Japanese fans'): oldest known surviving fans are Chinese woven bamboo hand screens (tomb c. 2nd c. BCE); Japan's fixed uchiwa arrived from China in the Nara period; the folding sensu emerged 6th–9th c. in Japan and was introduced back to China
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-9th-century folding fan from outside Japan, or a Chinese textual attestation of a folding fan predating Japanese use</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0009-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>135.7681,35.0116,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>9</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mary Magdalene — the canonical figure</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across the earliest narrative sources, including the embarrassment-grade detail that a woman is the first commissioned witness.
Layer: real · Place: Magdala/Migdal, Sea of Galilee
Dates: 28 → 33 (confidence: traditional)

Mary of Magdala is attested in the earliest Christian writings as a follower of Jesus: named among the women who 'provided for them out of their resources' (Luke 8:1-3, with the note that seven demons had gone out of her), named at the crucifixion (Mark 15:40; John 19:25), named at the burial (Mark 15:47), named first at the tomb in all four gospels (Mark 16:1; Matt 28:1; Luke 24:10; John 20:1), and in John 20:11-18 the first person commissioned to carry the resurrection claim ('go to my brothers'). 'Magdalene' is a place-name: Mary of Magdala/Migdal, the fishing town on the Sea of Galilee (Josephus, Jewish War 3.462, Tarichaea).

Earliest attestation: The Gospel of Mark (c. 70 CE); Paul's letters (c. 50s CE) do not name her
Sources: Luke 8:1-3 (the women who provided; the seven demons note); Mark 15:40, 15:47, 16:1 (cross, burial, tomb); John 19:25, 20:1-18 (cross; first witness, the commission); Josephus, Jewish War 3.462 (Tarichaea/Magdala)
Falsifiability: A pre-Markan textual witness naming a different 'Mary of Magdala' tradition, or proof the Markan tomb narratives are second-century compositions</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0028-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.5097,32.8276,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>28</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The emergence of Christianity</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary 1st-century letters document the communities, and independent Roman officials (Pliny, Tacitus) confirm Christianity's spread and persecution by the early 2nd century.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem (traditionally the movement's origin)
Dates: 30 → 112 (confidence: high)

In the mid-1st century CE, the Jesus movement spread from Judea/Galilee through the eastern Roman Empire as distinct Christian communities, documented by Paul's letters and noted by Roman officials by the early 2nd century.

Earliest attestation: Paul's letters (c. 50s CE); Acts of the Apostles; Pliny the Younger, Epistles 10.96 (c. 112 CE); Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (c. 116 CE)
Sources: Paul's letters (c. 50s CE); Pliny the Younger, Epistles 10.96 to Trajan (c. 112 CE); Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (Neronian persecution, c. 116 CE); Dura-Europos house church (archaeology of 3rd-c. Christian worship, later but material)
Falsifiability: 1st-century evidence showing no Christian communities outside Judea by 100 CE would overturn the spread narrative</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0030-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>30</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Red sky at night, shepherd's delight' (verified folk meteorology)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The mechanism is documented across independent sources: high-pressure air traps dust and aerosols, which scatter blue wavelengths (Rayleigh scattering) leaving red; with mid-latitude systems moving west to east, a red sunset means high pressure (fair weather) approaching from the west, while a red sunrise means the high pressure has passed eastward and low pressure follows. Wikipedia's weather-lore survey, BBC Science Focus, and the Met Office (via the Gazette) all confirm the physical basis with the mid-latitude scope fence.
Layer: real · Place: England (mid-latitude saying; biblical and Shakespearean attestations)
Dates: 0030 → 2026 (confidence: low)

'Red sky at night, shepherd's delight; red sky in the morning, shepherd's warning' — a red sunset presages fair weather the next day, and a red sunrise presages foul weather, because mid-latitude weather systems move from west to east.

Earliest attestation: Matthew 16:2-3 — 'When it is evening, ye say, It will be fair weather: for the sky is red. And in the morning, It will be foul weather: for the sky is red and threatening.'
Sources: Wikipedia 'Weather lore' — 'Red sky at night': 'When weather systems predominantly move from west to east, a red sky at night indicates that the high pressure air (and better weather) is westwards. In the morning the light is eastwards, and so a red sky then indicates the high pressure (and better weather) has already passed, and an area of low pressure is following behind.' Notes the Rayleigh scattering mechanism and the Australian reversal.; BBC Science Focus Magazine, 'Is there any truth behind the saying red sky at night, shepherd's delight?': 'a red sunset does typically presage clear skies the following day. However, this only holds true in regions where weather systems mostly travel from west to east, which includes most mid-latitude areas such as the UK.' Explains the pressure–particle–scattering mechanism for both the evening and morning cases.; Matthew 16:2-3 (authorized 1903 translation, via Farm Collector): 'When it is evening, ye say, It will be fair weather: for the sky is red. And in the morning, It will be foul weather: for the sky is red and threatening.' — the saying's oldest attestation, ~2,000 years of currency; Shakespeare, Venus and Adonis, lines 453-456 (1593): 'Like a red morn that ever yet betoken'd, Wreck to the seaman, tempest to the field...' — the saying in Elizabethan English
Falsifiability: A meteorological analysis showing no correlation between sunset/sunrise redness and next-day weather in mid-latitudes, or a physical account contradicting the pressure-scattering mechanism</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0030-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.17,52.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>30</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'This is my body': the institution narrative, earliest written form (~55 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations with the earliest in fixed liturgical form within ~25 years of the crucifixion
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 55 → 55 (confidence: high)

The words of institution ('this is my body... this cup is the new covenant in my blood') are quadruply attested: 1 Cor 11:23-25 (~55 CE, the earliest written account, already a fixed liturgical formula predating the gospels), Mark 14:22-25, Matt 26:26-29, Luke 22:19-20. Whether ipsissima verba or early-church formulation is debated; an early meal tradition is not in doubt.

Earliest attestation: 1 Cor 11:23-25
Sources: 1 Cor 11:23-25; Mark 14:22-25; Matt 26:26-29; Luke 22:19-20
Falsifiability: A pre-55 CE manuscript lacking the formula would revise the dating</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0055-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>22.96,37.94,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>55</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Fire of Rome, July 64 CE</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The fire as an event is independently attested by three ancient historians writing in three different centuries' removes (Tacitus ~116, Suetonius ~121, Dio ~229), with Nero's relief measures (Antium return, opened gardens/palaces, grain-price reduction) and the subsequent Christian persecution (Tacitus 15.44) as the independently witnessed political facts. The cause — not the occurrence — is what the sources contest.
Layer: real · Place: Rome, Italy — the 14 Augustan regions
Dates: 64-07-18 → 64-07-27 (confidence: high)

The Great Fire of Rome broke out in the night of 18–19 July 64 CE (reign of Nero) and burned for six days and seven nights (Tacitus; Dio says nine days), devastating the city.

Earliest attestation: Tacitus, Annals 15.38–44 (written c. 116–120 CE, ~50 years after); Suetonius, Nero 38; Dio Cassius, Roman History 62.16–18. No contemporary surviving account.
Sources: Tacitus, Annals 15.38–44 — the fire's outbreak (shops at the Circus Maximus), spread, Nero at Antium and his return, relief measures (opened gardens and buildings, grain-price cut), the rumor of the private-stage performance (15.39), the Christian scapegoating (15.44); the cause hedged as uncertain (incertum an dolo principis); Suetonius, Nero 38 — the fire watched from the tower of Maecenas, 'the Sack of Ilium' in stage costume; the arson asserted more flatly (men with tow and torches on Nero's orders); Dio Cassius, Roman History 62.16–18 — the fire's course, the arson asserted (soldiers setting fires by order), Nero singing the 'Capture of Troy' from the palace roof in lyre-player's costume; duration 9 days; HISTORY.com, 'Did Nero Really Fiddle While Rome Burned?' — fire began July 64, 10 of 14 districts burned, Nero at Antium 35 miles away, relief measures, Christians blamed; Live Science / Prof. Anthony Drake — Rome 64: 10 of 14 districts burned, thousands displaced in a city of 500,000–1M; rumor mechanics and the cithara correction
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription or dated document fixing a different year, or dendrochronological/archaeological evidence from Rome contradicting a city-wide conflagration in 64 CE, would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0064-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>64</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Roman persecution of Christians</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent primary sources across three centuries agree on the phenomenon; the exact death counts and Eusebius's 'ten persecutions' schema are later historiographical construction, so the ceiling stays CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Rome (Neronian persecution); the phenomenon spans the empire, 64–313 CE
Dates: 64 → 313 (confidence: high)

The Roman state persecuted Christians across three centuries — episodic and regional, never continuous — documented by contemporary and near-contemporary sources: Tacitus on the Neronian scapegoating of 64 CE, the Pliny–Trajan exchange of c. 112 CE, the Decian universal-sacrifice edict and its 45 surviving libelli certificates of 250 CE, and the Diocletianic Great Persecution of 303–311 CE, closed by Galerius's 311 Edict of Toleration and the Milan letters of 313.

Earliest attestation: Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (c. 116 CE, on the events of 64 CE); Pliny, Epistles 10.96–97 (c. 112 CE, the in-office exchange)
Sources: Tacitus, Annals 15.44 (Neronian scapegoating, 64 CE); Pliny the Younger, Epistles 10.96–97 (the Bithynian inquiry and Trajan's rescript, c. 112 CE); 45 Decian libelli (sacrifice certificates, 250 CE; archaeology); Letter of the Churches of Vienne and Lyons (177 CE; the Blandina account); Lactantius, De Mortibus Persecutorum (Great Persecution; Galerius's deathbed plea, c. 311–315 CE — partisan author, carried as such); Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History (partisan contemporary; the 'ten persecutions' schema is his construction); Edict of Serdica / Galerius's Edict of Toleration (311 CE); Edict of Milan letters (313 CE; via Lactantius 48 / Eusebius — toleration, not establishment); Ehrman blog, 'When Emperors Became More Involved in Christian Persecutions' (secondary; carries J. Rives's Decius revisionism)
Falsifiability: A contemporary imperial rescript forbidding anti-Christian action across these reigns, or proof that the Tacitus notice or the Pliny letters are later insertions</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0064-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>64</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jairus's daughter: 'the child is not dead but sleeping' (triple tradition)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Triple attestation of the pericope as an early tradition; the event itself and the unnamed family are beyond verification
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 65 → 90 (confidence: medium)

The raising of Jairus's daughter is triple-tradition (Mark 5:21-43; Matt 9:18-26; Luke 8:40-56): Jairus named (Mark/Luke), the daughter twelve years old, Peter/James/John taken inside, the mockers expelled, 'not dead but sleeping' (Mark 5:39), the Aramaic 'talitha koum' (Mark 5:41). The girl herself is NEVER named in any gospel, nor is her mother — the family cannot be traced further. Meier grants the raising stories a claim to historicity on multiple attestation; mainstream form-criticism treats the miracle element with reserve.

Earliest attestation: Mark 5:21-43
Sources: Mark 5:21-43; Matt 9:18-26; Luke 8:40-56; Meier, A Marginal Jew
Falsifiability: No independent attestation exists to test against</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0065-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.57,32.88,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>65</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Joseph of Arimathea: the council member who buried Jesus</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Fourfold gospel attestation plus Gospel of Peter, but genuine scholarly split on historicity; Grail accretions fenced off as later
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 65 → 100 (confidence: medium)

Joseph of Arimathea — 'a respected member of the council' (Mark 15:43) — buries Jesus in all four gospels (Mark 15:43-46; Matt 27:57-60; Luke 23:50-53; John 19:38-42) plus the Gospel of Peter. For historicity: Brown/Meier argue via the criterion of embarrassment (an awkward Sanhedrin burial for the early church, a named checkable Jerusalem figure). Against: Crossan/Ehrman argue Mark created him to solve the body-disposal problem, since Roman practice left crucified corpses unburied. A 2004 JSHJ study splits the difference: Mark likely found the name in earlier tradition. Verdict: plausible, not proven. The Grail legends around him (Glastonbury, the blood-cup) are later accretions with no 1st-century root.

Earliest attestation: Mark 15:43-46
Sources: Mark 15:43-46; Matt 27:57-60; Luke 23:50-53; John 19:38-42; JSHJ 2004, doi:10.1177/147686900400200102
Falsifiability: Contemporary non-Christian attestation would raise the grade; none exists</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0065-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>65</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The women at the tomb: the least convenient, most consistent witnesses</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Fourfold attestation of the least convenient possible witnesses; granted even by skeptical scholars
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 65 → 110 (confidence: high)

All four gospels agree the first witnesses of the empty tomb were women (Mary Magdalene named in all four; Mark 16:1-8; Matt 28:1-10; Luke 24:1-12; John 20:1-18) — witnesses whose testimony did not count in a first-century court. By the criterion of embarrassment, this is the strongest internal evidence that a memory rather than pure invention sits beneath the narratives: no inventor optimizing for ancient credibility chooses female witnesses.

Earliest attestation: Mark 16:1-8
Sources: Mark 16:1-8; Matt 28:1-10; Luke 24:1-12; John 20:1-18
Falsifiability: A gospel tradition with male first-witnesses would weaken the embarrassment argument; none exists</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0065-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>65</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Murabba'at deeds — the revolt's legal paper trail</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The deeds exist in excavation contexts, their internal year-counts converge with the revolt's externally attested dates, and the legal formulas match known Second Temple–period Jewish documentary practice.
Layer: real · Place: Wadi Murabba'at, ~11 km north of En-Gedi (Judean Desert)
Dates: 72 → 135 (confidence: high)

The Wadi Murabba'at caves yielded legal documents of the Bar Kokhba administration's own calendar and bureaucracy: land leases dated 'year one / year two of the Redemption of Israel' — e.g. Mur 24E, a five-year tenancy at Herodium dated Shevat 20 of year two (~Jan/Feb 134 CE), 'of the Redemption of Israel by Shimeon ben Kosba, the nasi of Israel' — alongside the revolt-era divorce corpus: the Aramaic 'Masada get' (Joseph son of Naqsan to Miriam daughter of Jonathan, 'year six' = 72 CE, presented at Masada) and the Shelamzion document from Nahal Se'elim ('year three of the freedom of Israel' = 135 CE, naming Bar Kokhba 'nasi of Israel'). A Jewish legal system was operating under the revolt, in Aramaic, in its own year-count.

Earliest attestation: The documents themselves (72 CE, 134 CE, 135 CE).
Sources: Mur 24E (Wadi Murabba'at) — land lease, Shevat 20, year two of the Redemption of Israel (~Jan/Feb 134 CE), 'Shimeon ben Kosba, the nasi of Israel'; The Masada get (72 CE) and the Shelamzion divorce/Nahal Se'elim document (135 CE) — Posen Library, 'Early Jewish Divorce Documents'; Benoit, Milik &amp; de Vaux, Les grottes de Murabba'at (DJD II, 1961)
Falsifiability: Proof the Murabba'at documents are modern forgeries; or that their year-counts postdate the revolt (antedating by later scribes).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0072-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.36,31.59,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>72</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pliny's sapo: soap's first Latin word (77-79 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A directly surviving primary text, precisely book-chapter locatable, reporting both the manufacture (tallow + ashes) and the Celtic origin — held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because it rests on one author, and Pliny is reporting foreign manufacture he did not observe himself. The hair-pomade-only use is the documented Roman-era reality: the word arrived in Latin from Celtic/Germanic, not from any Roman hill.
Layer: real · Place: Gaul (the manufacture Pliny describes)
Dates: 77 → 79 (confidence: high)

The Latin word sapo (soap) appears first in a European language in Pliny the Elder's Natural History (77-79 CE): he describes soap manufactured from tallow and ashes as an invention of the Gauls ('Galliarum hoc inventum'), used principally as a hair pomade — and notes, disapprovingly, that among the Gauls and Germans men use it more than women.

Earliest attestation: Pliny the Elder, Naturalis Historia 28.51.191
Sources: Pliny the Elder, Naturalis Historia 28.51.191 — 'Prodest et sapo, Galliarum hoc inventum rutilandis capillis' (soap, an invention of the Gauls, for reddening hair); discussed as hair pomade, men likelier users among Gauls and Germans; Soap history survey (Wikipedia 'History of soap'): the word 'soap' (Latin sapo) 'appears first in a European language in Pliny the Elder's Historia Naturalis, which discusses the manufacture of soap from tallow and ashes, but the only use he mentions for it is as a pomade for hair'
Falsifiability: An earlier surviving Latin text using sapo</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0077-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>6.5,45.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>77</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The magnetic compass was invented in China — the Han spoon tradition and the Song nautical needle, ~150 years before Europe's first mention (c. 80 CE - 1088 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The navigational-priority claim rests on a five-century textual chain of dated, surviving texts (Lunheng c. 80 CE; Wujing Zongyao 1040-1044; Shen Kuo ~1088; Zhu Yu 1111-1117), Needham's analysis (Science and Civilisation in China, vol. IV pt 1) treating Shen Kuo as the fixed point 'a century before the earliest European mention,' and Kreutz's scholarly-consensus margin of ~150 years over Neckam (~1180-1190). Held below VERIFIED because the Han spoon element — the linchpin of the 'invented in Han China' wording — is itself only CLAIMED: the Lunheng passage never says 'magnet,' no physical spoon survives (the 1951 Wang reconstruction model was never based on a recovered artifact and has been challenged by modern Chinese historians of science, e.g. Jiang Xiaoyuan), and modern skeptics (Meynard &amp; Zhang 2026) accept the Han lodestone spoon and Song compass while rejecting only the exaggerated antiquity. The grade applies to Chinese invention and navigational priority; it does not assert the spoon was a working compass, nor a transmission path.
Layer: real · Place: Kaifeng / Song China (compass textual tradition)
Dates: 0080-01-01 → 1088-12-31 (confidence: high)

The magnetic compass was invented in China, centuries before any European mention: a lodestone 'south-pointing spoon' (sinan) tradition in the Han era, a magnetized-needle compass for navigation by the Song dynasty, while Europe's first surviving description of the mariner's compass is Alexander Neckam's De naturis rerum, c. 1180-1190 — roughly a century and a half later.

Earliest attestation: Wang Chong's Lunheng (c. 70-80 CE) 'south-pointing spoon' passage — though the passage does not explicitly mention magnetism; the lodestone reading is scholarly inference (Chen-Cheng Yih: 'widely considered' the earliest form of the magnetic compass).
Sources: Wikipedia 'History of the compass': Han lodestone devices (divination/geomancy); Wang Chong's Lunheng (70-80 CE) spoon passage — 'does not explicitly mention magnetism,' 'speculated to be a lodestone'; Chen-Cheng Yih — 'widely considered to be the earliest form of the magnetic compass'; Wujing Zongyao (1040-1044, south-pointing fish); Shen Kuo Dream Pool Essays (1088, first incontestable magnetized-needle reference); Zhu Yu Pingzhou Table Talks (written 1111-1117, first maritime use); Kreutz — Chinese navigational invention pre-dates first European mention by 150 years; Meynard &amp; Zhang, 'The Chinese Compass: How the Jesuits Created and Spread the Myth,' Journal of Jesuit Studies 13 (2026), 96-116: accepts Han lodestone spoon + Song nautical compass as real; Needham cited — Chinese pilots used the compass by ~1090, possibly ~850; 'we hardly know enough' on the China-Arab transmission question; Needham, Science and Civilisation in China, vol. IV pt 1, pp. 249-250 (quoted via Wikipedia Talk:Shen Kuo): Shen Kuo's Meng Chhi Pi Than (~1088) is 'our fixed point,' 'a century before the earliest European mention of the magnetic compass'; OpenEdition/Artefact journal: skeptical scholarship arguing Needham's 'compass-based navigation' was misleading — the Song water-bowl pointer 'could not have acted as a useable and reliable sea-going navigational aid'; traditional Chinese navigation not obviously compass-based; Sixth Tone: historian Jiang Xiaoyuan (Shanghai Jiao Tong University) — 'a physical model was never recovered' of the spoon compass; recommends removing it from history textbooks; modern Chinese scholarly skepticism of the sinan model
Falsifiability: A securely dated European or other non-Chinese compass description that predates Shen Kuo (1088) would overturn the Chinese navigational-priority claim; a recovered Han-dynasty magnetic spoon would raise the spoon element from CLAIMED to CORROBORATED or better.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0080-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>113.62,34.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>80</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gamaliel: the historical teacher behind a composed speech (Acts 5)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Person independently attested; the speech's Theudas/Judas inversion is a documented anachronism
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 80 → 90 (confidence: medium)

Gamaliel I is a real historical figure (Mishnah; Josephus Ant. 20; Acts 22:3 as Paul's teacher). But his Acts 5:34-40 speech — the one that freed the apostles from the Sanhedrin — is widely judged Lukan composition: set ~30s CE, it cites the revolt of Theudas BEFORE Judas of Galilee, yet Josephus dates Theudas to 44-46 CE and Judas to 6 CE. The person is historical; the freeing speech is a literary set-piece, not a transcript.

Earliest attestation: Acts 5:34-40
Sources: Acts 5:34-40; Josephus, Ant. 20; Mishnah
Falsifiability: None available; the anachronism is the evidence</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0080-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>80</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Blood and water from the side (John 19:34-35): attested, read as theology</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Secure attestation with explicit eyewitness framing; the theological reading is the scholarly consensus
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 90 → 110 (confidence: high)

John 19:34 (the soldier's spear; blood and water flow) with 19:35's explicit eyewitness claim is securely attested. Mainstream scholarship reads it theologically, not forensically: the water as the Holy Spirit given now that Jesus is glorified (cf. John 7:39), the blood as the Passover-lamb sacrificial motif (cf. m. Pesahim 5:3-5 on free-flowing sacrificial blood). Medical readings (pericardial fluid) exist but are secondary; the evangelist's interest is christological.

Earliest attestation: John 19:34-35
Sources: John 19:34-35; John 7:39; 1 John 5:6-8
Falsifiability: Textual; the attestation is not in doubt, only the reading</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0090-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>90</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Testimonium Flavianum (Josephus on Jesus)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The passage cannot be fully authentic as it stands: a non-Christian Jew would not write 'He was the Messiah,' the narrative flow breaks around the passage, and the 1935-style scholarly tally favors a partially interpolated text. But the authentic core (a Jesus, condemned by Pilate, followers surviving him) fits Josephus's style and subject matter, and the independently authentic James passage (Ant 20.200) proves Josephus referred to Jesus anyway - so the whole-passage forgery thesis has to explain away two passages, not one.
Layer: real · Place: Rome (where Josephus wrote the Antiquities; the claim's subject is Roman Judea and Galilee)
Dates: 93 → 94 (confidence: high)

Josephus's Antiquities of the Jews, Book 18 (written c. 93-94 CE), contains an authentic first-century core mentioning Jesus — his execution under Pontius Pilate and the survival of his followers — with later Christian interpolations overlaid on that core.

Earliest attestation: Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews 18.63-64, written c. 93-94 CE in Rome; earliest surviving quotation: Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History (c. 324 CE)
Sources: Josephus, Antiquities 18.63-64 (Testimonium Flavianum); Feldman survey of the literature (1937-1980): 87 articles; 39 of 52 surveyed scholars support partial authenticity (4 entirely genuine, 6 mostly genuine, 20 with some interpolations, 9 with several; 13 total interpolation); John P. Meier, A Marginal Jew (1991): three Christian interpolations identified - 'He was the Messiah,' 'if indeed one ought to call him a man,' and the resurrection/prophecies passage; Josephus, Antiquities 20.200 - 'the brother of Jesus, who was called Christ, whose name was James': authenticity accepted by the overwhelming majority of modern scholars; the highest-authenticity Josephus reference to Christianity; Minority positions on record: Ken Olson's Eusebian-authorship thesis (1999/2013); Louis Feldman's 2012 move to wholesale interpolation (Brill, persuaded by Olson + Origen's silence)
Falsifiability: A pre-4th-century manuscript of Antiquities 18 lacking the passage would kill the partial-authenticity consensus; a pre-Eusebian quotation of the full Christian phrasing would kill the interpolation thesis. Either discovery is possible in principle.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0093-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>93</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Babatha archive — a Jewish woman's legal papers, 94–132 CE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The archive exists, was excavated in controlled archaeological context (1961), is internally dated across four decades, and is multiply documented; the documents' legal formulas match known 2nd-century Roman-provincial practice.
Layer: real · Place: Cave of Letters, Nahal Hever (Judean Desert); Babatha's Mahoza on the southeastern Dead Sea shore (modern Jordan)
Dates: 94 → 132 (confidence: high)

A leather pouch holding 35 legal documents — wedding contracts (ketubot), property transfers, land registration, loans, guardianship petitions and summonses — written in Nabatean Aramaic, Judean Aramaic, and Greek and dated c. 94–132 CE, belonged to Babatha bat Shimon, a Jewish woman of Mahoza (Maoza) on the southeastern shore of the Dead Sea (the Nabatean kingdom until 106 CE, then the Roman province of Arabia). She carried the pouch with her when she fled to the Cave of Letters at Nahal Hever with other refugees at the end of the Bar Kokhba revolt (132–135 CE), and hid it in a crevice; she and the others died in the cave. Yigael Yadin's expedition found the pouch in 1961. The documents show a woman litigating, lending, inheriting and registering property under Roman bureaucracy.

Earliest attestation: The documents themselves, dated 94–132 CE; excavated in controlled context 1961.
Sources: The Babatha archive (P. Yadin 1–35), Cave of Letters, Nahal Hever — Israel Museum / Israel Antiquities Authority; discovered 1961, Yadin expedition; Lewis, N. (ed.), The Documents from the Bar Kokhba Period in the Cave of Letters (Greek Papyri), 1989; Jewish Review of Books, 'Businesswomen Before Bar Kokhba' (Babatha archive overview: 35 documents, 94–132 CE, Nabatean Aramaic/Judean Aramaic/Greek); OUPblog, 'Ancient legal papyri bring lost world to life' (2017) — the Judean Desert legal papyri, 100 BCE–200 CE; Esler, Philip F. (2026), 'Babatha, En-Gedi and the Bar Kokhba revolt: A new approach using evidence from the Cave of Letters', Biblical Theology Bulletin 56(1):18-32 (P. Yadin 27 as draft receipt; revolt outbreak re-dating)
Falsifiability: Linguistic or material anachronism in the documents proving modern forgery; or evidence the 1961 pouch context was planted rather than excavated.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0094-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.37,31.38,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>94</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The earliest New Testament manuscript fragments</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The fragments exist in major libraries and are multiply attested; radiocarbon work (e.g., on the Isaiah Scroll parallel) corroborates paleographic ranges. But the precise ~125 CE date of P52 is contested and rests on paleography alone, never confirmed by physical dating.
Layer: real · Place: John Rylands Library, Manchester (current holding; findspot in Egypt unknown)
Dates: 100 → 225 (confidence: medium)

The oldest surviving physical copies of New Testament texts are 2nd–3rd century Greek papyrus fragments from Egypt, headed by P52 (Rylands Gr. P. 457, a fragment of John 18). No autograph — no authorial original — of any New Testament text survives.

Earliest attestation: The fragments themselves: P52 (John 18:31–33, 37–38; editio princeps Roberts 1935); P90, P104 (2nd c.); P46 (Pauline epistles, c. 200 CE); P66, P75 (John; Luke–John; c. 175–225 CE)
Sources: P52 — Rylands Greek Papyrus 457, John Rylands Library, Manchester (editio princeps: C. H. Roberts, 1935); Brent Nongbri, 'The Use and Abuse of P52' (HTR 98:1, 2005) — the paleographic date cannot be fixed precisely; the range extends into the 3rd century; Orsini &amp; Clarysse — propose 125–175 CE for P52 on comparator documents (P. Flor. 1.1, 153 CE; PSI V 446, 132–137 CE); John Rylands Library's own statement: 'Recent research points to a date nearer to 200 AD'; carbon-dating has not been used (destructive); Chester Beatty papyri P45/P46/P47 and Bodmer papyri P66/P75 (2nd–3rd c.; &gt;140 NT papyri known as of 2021); Codex Sinaiticus (c. 330–350 CE) — the earliest surviving copy of the complete New Testament; 1QIsaa, the Great Isaiah Scroll (c. 125 BCE; radiocarbon + paleography agree) — the Hebrew-Bible parallel: &gt;2,600 small differences from the Masoretic text, mostly spelling/grammar, a few sense-changing
Falsifiability: An autograph (authorial original) of any New Testament text would falsify the 'no originals survive' half of the claim; a securely dated 1st-century NT fragment would move the frontier earlier and supersede P52's lead.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.24,53.48,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The real history of the padded horse collar: Chinese origin, gradual European adoption</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across independent lines: Chinese depictions and texts (Needham), European iconography, and Langdon's economic analysis of the ox-to-horse mix; held below VERIFIED on the exact diffusion route and the precise arrival timing in Europe.
Layer: real · Place: China (Han-era collar origin) → medieval Europe (diffusion, gradual adoption)
Dates: 0100 → 1300 (confidence: medium)

The padded horse collar originated in China (Han-era depictions, documented in Joseph Needham's Science and Civilisation in China research), diffused westward, and was in European use by the High Middle Ages; the European shift from ox to horse draught was then gradual and governed by economics — relative animal prices, fodder (oat) costs, and land regimes (John Langdon, Horses, Oxen and Technological Innovation, 1986) — not a sudden collar-triggered breakthrough.

Earliest attestation: Han-dynasty Chinese depictions and texts (per Needham's SCC documentation); European iconographic evidence from the early second millennium CE
Sources: Joseph Needham et al., Science and Civilisation in China — padded horse collar attested in Han-era China; European arrival c. 9th–10th century; John Langdon, Horses, Oxen and Technological Innovation (1986) — gradual, price-driven English adoption; Thomas E. Lambert, 'The Horse Versus the Ox during Medieval Times' (2023, Univ. of Louisville) — horse/ox cost comparison
Falsifiability: A securely dated European padded collar pre-dating any Chinese attestation, or English manorial data showing a sudden, price-independent ox-to-horse flip</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>113.62,34.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cai Lun's 105 CE presentation (the real event)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The event is attested in the standard dynastic history with a named reign year, named materials, and the named aftermath ('Cai Hou paper'); held below VERIFIED because the Hou Hanshu is a 5th-century compilation writing three centuries after the event.
Layer: real · Place: Luoyang (Eastern Han capital, the imperial court)
Dates: 105 → 105 (confidence: high)

In 105 CE (the first year of Yuanxing), the Han court official Cai Lun presented an improved papermaking process — using tree bark (likely mulberry), hemp waste, old rags, and fishing nets — to Emperor He, and the court adopted it; the paper was thereafter called 'Cai Hou paper' (Caihou zhi).

Earliest attestation: Hou Hanshu 78/68:2513-14 (Fan Ye, 5th c.) — the standard history's biography of Cai Lun; the 'Cai Hou paper' (Caihou zhi) naming tradition
Sources: Hou Hanshu 78/68:2513-14 (Fan Ye, Liu-Song dynasty) — the biography passage: writings of old were bamboo and silk (silk costly, bamboo heavy); Cai Lun devises paper from tree bark, hemp tops, rags, and fishing nets; presents it in Yuanxing 1 (105); the emperor praises his ability; paper used everywhere thereafter, called 'Cai Hou paper' (text via Wikipedia, 'Cai Lun'); acclaimedfigures.com, 'Cai Lun Biography' (Dec 2025) — synthesizes Britannica, the Met Museum, and the Silk Road Foundation: eunuch official, director of imperial workshops under Emperor He, presents improved paper in 105; the method spreads across China by the 3rd c., Korea/Japan by the 7th, the Islamic world via Samarkand (Talas 751), Europe by the 12th
Falsifiability: A contemporary Han document contradicting the 105 presentation, or proof the biography's papermaking passage is a later interpolation, would demote the grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0105-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>112.45,34.66,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>105</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bar Kokhba letters — the revolt's own correspondence, 132–135 CE</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The letters exist as physical documents in controlled excavation contexts (Murabba'at 1950s, Nahal Hever 1960–61), their language mix matches the period's documentary practice, and they converge with the revolt's independently attested dates — though they are undated internally, which fences the grade below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Cave of Letters, Nahal Hever (main group); Wadi Murabba'at group ~11 km north of En-Gedi
Dates: 132 → 135 (confidence: high)

Roughly fifteen letters attributed to Shimon bar Kosiba (Bar Kokhba) survive in two groups: a Wadi Murabba'at group (early 1950s, via the antiquities market; published by the de Vaux expedition) and a Nahal Hever Cave of Letters group (1960–61, Yadin expedition, found in a tied bundle in a waterskin). They are addressed to subordinates — Yehonathan ben Be'ayan and Masabala, sitting at En-Gedi — and deal with military discipline, agricultural supplies, taxation, and the movement of people; most are in Aramaic, five in Hebrew, two in Greek; they are not internally dated. They sign the leader 'Shimeon ben Kosba, nasi (prince) of Israel' — the only contemporary voice of the revolt's leadership.

Earliest attestation: The letters themselves, 132–135 CE (contemporary with the events).
Sources: The Bar Kokhba letters (5/6Hev, Murabba'at) — Israel Antiquities Authority / Shrine of the Book; excavated contexts: Wadi Murabba'at (early 1950s), Cave of Letters (1960–61); Biblical Archaeology Society, 'What Is the Bar-Kokhba Revolt?' — the revolt reconstructed from contemporary documents, coins, caves and tunnels (2026); Archeobiblion, 'Bar Kokhba: the correspondence' — the two archive groups, ~15 letters, Aramaic/Hebrew/Greek, the 'nasi of Israel' signature; Yadin, Y. Bar-Kokhba: The Rediscovery of the Legendary Hero of the Second Jewish Revolt against Rome (1971); Esler, Philip F. (2026), 'Babatha, En-Gedi and the Bar Kokhba revolt', Biblical Theology Bulletin 56(1):18-32 (Bar Kokhba opened with the capture of En-Gedi)
Falsifiability: Material or linguistic proof that the letters are modern forgeries; or a contemporary Roman document showing the 'nasi of Israel' administration did not exist.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0132-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.37,31.38,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>132</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The great inversion: when daimōn became 'demon'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Christian texts exist and are explicit: the identification of daimones with the fallen angels is stated, and the contrast with Hesiod's and Plato's usage is a matter of lexical record, not interpretation.
Layer: real · Place: Rome, Alexandria, Caesarea (the writers' cities); the Greek texts they redefine are pan-Hellenic
Dates: 150 → 250 (confidence: medium)

In the 2nd-3rd centuries CE, Christian writers redefined daimones — previously neutral or benevolent intermediary beings in Greek usage — as wholly evil supernatural beings hostile to God and man: Justin Martyr (2 Apology 5) identifies the pagan daimones as the fallen angels of Genesis 6 who enslave mankind; Tatian and Origen (Contra Celsum) carry the identification forward. This is a semantic inversion inside the Christian tradition, not a discovery about the Greek texts: the English word 'demon' descends from the redefined Christian usage, while Hesiod, Plato, and Xenophon attest the earlier, non-evil range. Graded as belief-phenomenon: the redefinition is multiply attested; the beings' status is never adjudicated.

Earliest attestation: Justin Martyr, 2 Apology 5; Tatian, Oration to the Greeks 7-18; Origen, Contra Celsum 4.92-99, 7.64-70
Sources: Justin Martyr, 2 Apology 5 (the fallen-angel identification of the daimones); Origen, Contra Celsum (the polemic that fixes the redefinition); The semantic history of daimōn → 'demon' in the lexical record (LSJ; Lampe's Patristic Lexicon)
Falsifiability: A 2nd-century Christian text using daimones in the older benevolent sense without polemic (would show the inversion was slower and less programmatic)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0150-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>29.0,41.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Gospel of Mary — a 2nd-century text about her, not by her</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript witnesses are dated and published; a 2nd-century text cannot be the 1st-century woman's own writing.
Layer: real · Place: Berlin (Papyrus Berolinensis 8502 repository)
Dates: 150 → 200 (confidence: medium)

The Gospel of Mary survives in a 5th-century Coptic codex (Papyrus Berolinensis 8502, bought in Cairo 1896, published 1955) plus two 3rd-century Greek fragments (P. Oxyrhynchus 3525; P. Rylands 463): a 2nd-century revelation dialogue in which the risen Savior gives Mary (understood as the Magdalene) private teaching, Peter challenges her authority ('Did he really speak with a woman privately, without our knowledge? Should we turn about and all listen to her?'), and Levi defends her. It was not written by Mary Magdalene and dates a century-plus after the events it describes.

Earliest attestation: P. Rylands 463 / P. Oxyrhynchus 3525 (3rd-century Greek fragments)
Sources: Papyrus Berolinensis 8502 (5th-c. Coptic codex); P. Oxy. 3525 and P. Ryl. 463 (3rd-c. Greek fragments); Gospel of Mary 17-18 (Peter's challenge; Levi's defense)
Falsifiability: A 1st-century manuscript of the text, or a contemporary attestation of Magdalene literacy and authorship</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0150-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Martyrdom of Polycarp</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The letter's epistolary form, early circulation, and Eusebius's 4th-century quotation anchor it in the second century; mainstream scholarship accepts substantial authenticity.
Layer: real · Place: Smyrna (Izmir), stadium — the execution site per the letter
Dates: 155 → 167 (confidence: medium)

The letter of the church of Smyrna recounting the arrest, trial, and burning of bishop Polycarp in the stadium is a substantially authentic second-century document, not a later forgery. The core narrative is genuine; the dove emerging from the wound appears only in some manuscripts as a later insertion; the exact date (155/156 CE under Antoninus Pius vs. 167 CE under Marcus Aurelius) remains debated among scholars.

Earliest attestation: The letter itself (Martyrdom of Polycarp), circulated with instructions for annual liturgical reading; quoted at length by Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History 4.15 (early 4th c.)
Sources: Martyrdom of Polycarp (the Smyrna letter); Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History 4.15; Barnes 1968 (date under Antoninus Pius)
Falsifiability: Discovery of the letter's dependence on a later source (e.g., verbatim dependence on Eusebius rather than the reverse), or a manuscript tradition showing the whole text composed after 300 CE</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0155-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>27.14,38.42,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>155</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Scillitan Martyrs (180 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Consular dating fixes the document to 180 CE; the spare bureaucratic style matches Roman court procedure and shows no legendary overlay.
Layer: real · Place: Carthage — the trial before the proconsul; Scilli the martyrs' home town
Dates: 180 → 180 (confidence: high)

The Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs — the record of the trial and execution of Speratus and six companions at Carthage on 17 July 180 CE — are substantially authentic proconsular court records, the earliest surviving Christian text written in Latin.

Earliest attestation: The acta themselves, surviving in Latin (and a later Greek version); the earliest Latin Christian document
Sources: Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs (Latin acta); Tertullian's contemporary North African context (secondary witness to the milieu)
Falsifiability: Manuscript evidence showing the Latin text composed centuries later, or proof the trial records were fabricated as propaganda</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0180-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.18,36.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>180</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Alexander Romance: the legend's literary afterlife</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript tradition is multiply attested (recension alpha onward; the Armenian witnesses preserving passages lost in Greek). Byzantine use as history is documented in the chronicles themselves. The DEBUNKED half rests on the absence of any corroboration for the Romance's exclusive episodes in the five ancient accounts or the fragmentary eyewitness tradition — a fiction the field recognizes as fiction.
Layer: real · Place: Alexandria, Egypt (composition); recensions across Europe, the Near East, and the Indian Ocean world
Dates: 200 → 300 (confidence: medium)

The Alexander Romance (Pseudo-Callisthenes), composed at Alexandria c. 3rd century CE, is a work of fiction about Alexander — opening with the claim that his true father was the Egyptian sorcerer-king Nectanebo, not Philip II — which became the most influential Alexander narrative in the East. It survives in dozens of recensions across some eighty versions (Greek, Latin, Syriac, Arabic, Persian, Armenian, Ethiopic, medieval vernaculars). Byzantine chroniclers (e.g., John Malalas; the Excerpta latina Barbari, 8th c.) treated it as a historical source. As a literary tradition its existence and influence are multiply documented; as a source for the historical Alexander it is graded DEBUNKED.

Earliest attestation: Pseudo-Callisthenes recension alpha (3rd c. CE); Armenian translation preserving lost passages; Wolohojian (1969) standard English translation
Sources: Pseudo-Callisthenes, The Romance of Alexander the Great, trans. Albert Mugrdich Wolohojian (Columbia UP, 1969): standard English translation; the Armenian witnesses preserve passages lost in the surviving Greek; 'Alexander Romance and Byzantine world chronicles: history cross-fertilized by fiction and the reverse' (2017): comparative textual analysis — Byzantine chroniclers (Malalas, Excerpta latina Barbari) incorporated Pseudo-Callisthenian motifs (Nectanebo's transformation, divine heritage, fantastic geography) as factual history; Ionescu, Dan-Tudor, 'Alexander the Great in the Persian legends: from the Pseudo-Callisthenes's Greek Romance to the Sikandar of Firdousi's Shah-Nameh': the Greek Romance (Alexandria, 3rd c. CE) → the Persian Sikandar; Ferdowsi (completed 1010) as the first complete exemplar of the Pseudo-Callisthenes tradition in Persian
Falsifiability: A contemporary document confirming any Romance-exclusive episode (the Nectanebo paternity, the glass-bell dive, the Kandake embassy) as historical fact; none exists — the Romance's own earliest manuscripts postdate Alexander by five centuries.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0200-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>29.92,31.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Babylonian Talmud — layered corpus, thin manuscript record</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The dated manuscripts (Florence 1175, Munich 95) are catalogued, photographed, and independently studied artifacts — the documentary facts are VERIFIED-as-documents. The layered composition (Mishnah → Amoraic Gemara → Savoraic redaction → Geonic closure) is the scholarly consensus reconstruction of a text with no contemporary external attestation of its editors — so the corpus-level claim stays CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED. The Talmud proves the shape of its own centuries of argument; it does not prove anything contemporary about the Second Temple period it remembers (the Mishnah record's honesty rule, extended).
Layer: real · Place: Babylonia (Sura / Pumbedita academies; manuscripts in Florence, Munich, Cairo)
Dates: 200 → 700 (confidence: medium)

The Babylonian Talmud is a layered rabbinic corpus — the Mishnah (redacted c. 200 CE) wrapped in the Babylonian Gemara, whose Amoraic arguments span c. 200–500 CE with Savoraic redaction and Geonic closure (c. 500–650 CE). Its surviving manuscript record is late and thin: the oldest surviving dated copy is Florence, Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, Plut. II.1.7–9 (dated 1175 CE), and Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, Cod.hebr. 95 (1342) is the only complete manuscript of the Babylonian Talmud; earlier witnesses survive only as Cairo Genizah fragments and citations in later works. The printed standard (Vilna/Romm 1880–1886) carries the censor's erasures — the 1553 Roman burnings under Julius III and the Russian expurgations are part of the record's own history.

Earliest attestation: Florence Plut. II.1.7–9 (1175 CE) as a dated artifact; the Genizah fragments as earlier undated witnesses; the corpus's own traditions date its layers centuries earlier (theological-traditional, not historical evidence)
Sources: Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, Cod.hebr. 95 (dated 1342) — the only complete manuscript of the Babylonian Talmud; Florence, Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, Plut. II.1.7–9 (dated 1175) — oldest surviving dated manuscript of the Babylonian Talmud; Cairo Genizah Talmud fragments (Cambridge Taylor-Schechter collection et al.) — earlier partial witnesses; Günter Stemberger, Introduction to the Talmud and Midrash (2nd ed. 1996) — the layering: Tannaim / Amoraim / Savoraim / Geonim; The 1553 Roman burnings (Campo de' Fiori, 9 Sept 1553, under Julius III) and the Vilna/Romm edition's censor-approved expurgations — the censorship archaeology of the printed text
Falsifiability: A securely dated complete manuscript earlier than Florence 1175; or codicological proof that the Munich 95 colophon date (1342) is false; or a securely dated Gemara citation predating the Amoraic attribution it carries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0200-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.4,32.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Passion of Perpetua and Felicity (203 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Internal evidence (the diary's distinct first-person voice, the frame's separate editorial voice, early liturgical use) supports the mainstream two-layer reading.
Layer: real · Place: Carthage — prison and amphitheater per the Passio
Dates: 203 → 203 (confidence: high)

The Passion of Perpetua and Felicity records the imprisonment and execution (7 March 203 CE, Carthage) of the noblewoman Perpetua — whose own prison diary (chapters 3–10) is one of the earliest surviving writings by a Christian woman — alongside her slave Felicity; the framing introduction/conclusion are by a later editor, and the authenticity of the diary portion is the mainstream position.

Earliest attestation: The Passio itself (Latin, with an early Greek version); quoted and honored in North African liturgy within a generation
Sources: Passio Sanctarum Perpetuae et Felicitatis (Latin); The early Greek version (secondary witness to the text's circulation)
Falsifiability: Evidence the Latin style is uniformly late, or proof the diary was composed by the same hand as the frame narrative</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0203-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.18,36.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>203</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Origen's Hexapla (six-column comparative edition)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The edition's existence, column structure, critical signs, and Caesarean origin are independently attested (Eusebius, the two palimpsest witnesses, Field) and the Hexaplaric revision is documented in living Septuagint research; the ~240 CE date is traditional-inference, not inscribed.
Layer: real · Place: Caesarea Maritima (compilation); surviving witnesses at Cairo and Milan
Dates: 0230 → 0245 (confidence: high)

Origen of Alexandria compiled at Caesarea Maritima, before c. 240 CE, a six-column comparative edition of the Hebrew Bible: (1) the Hebrew in Hebrew characters, (2) the Hebrew transliterated into Greek (the Secunda), (3) Aquila, (4) Symmachus, (5) the Septuagint with asterisked additions (mainly from Theodotion) marking where the Greek had nothing for the Hebrew and obeloi marking where the Greek had no Hebrew beneath it, (6) Theodotion. The asterisked and obelized differences are openly marked, not silently rewritten.

Earliest attestation: Eusebius of Caesarea, Ecclesiastical History 6.16 (early 4th c.), describing Origen's collection of the Hebrew and the editions of 'the seventy' and Aquila, Symmachus, and Theodotion 'arranged opposite one another in parallel columns, together with the Hebrew texts'; the Hexapla itself survives in fragments
Sources: Eusebius, Ecclesiastical History 6.16 (the six-column arrangement, the Nicopolis and Jericho 'fifth, sixth, seventh' editions); Cairo Genizah palimpsest T-S 12.182 — surviving Hexapla fragments; Milan Ambrosian palimpsest (O.39 sup.), recovered by Giovanni Mercati 1895; Frederick Field, Origenis Hexaplorum quae supersunt (1875); University of Helsinki, 'The Septuagint and Its Ancient Versions' — the B-text's later 'Hexaplaric' revision: Hebraizing additions done on the basis of Origen's Hexapla
Falsifiability: A contemporary Caesarean witness describing a wholly different edition by Origen, or demonstration that the palimpsest fragments belong to a later compilation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0230-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>34.9,32.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>230</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Camel or rope: the kamelos/kamilos variant</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The variant's existence, its late attestation, and the direction (camel to rope as softening) all follow the documented manuscript record.
Layer: real · Place: Jerusalem/Judea (the saying); Alexandria (Cyril's variant)
Dates: 0300 → 0900 (confidence: high)

In the 'eye of the needle' saying (Matthew 19:24 // Mark 10:25 // Luke 18:25), the mainstream Greek witnesses read kamelos ('camel'); a kamilos ('rope, ship's cable') variant appears in later witnesses, traced to Cyril of Alexandria (5th c. CE); Metzger characterizes it as 'an attempt to soften the rigor of the statement.' The variant is documented transmission-drift; the 'rope was the original' telling is rejected by the manuscript evidence.

Earliest attestation: The kamelos reading in the 4th-c. uncials; the kamilos variant first in 9th-c.-and-later witnesses and the fathers' speculations (Cyril, and Origen before him)
Sources: Codex Vaticanus, Sinaiticus (4th c.), Alexandrinus (5th c.): kamelos — the mainstream ancient reading; Metzger, Textual Commentary on the Greek New Testament: the kamilos evidence 'an attempt to soften the rigor of the statement'; Cyril of Alexandria (5th c.) as the variant's patristic source; Origen's earlier speculation noted; The Babylonian Talmud's 'elephant through the eye of a needle' as the Semitic impossibility-hyperbole parallel — the largest animal, not the nearest thread; kiwihellenist (Nov 2023), 'The camel, the rope, and the needle's eye': philological survey — kamilos 'rope' first appears in the 400s CE, vanishingly rare, never used in a sentence by any ancient/medieval writer, always in lexicographical contexts defined via Cyril of Alexandria's phrasing; the sole possible epigraphic use (the Anarbazos inscription) is speculative and proves nothing about the 1st c.; conclusion: kamilos was 'coined specifically to contrive a variant interpretation of the gospels,' exactly as Liddell &amp; Scott suggested ('Perh(aps) coined as an emendation of the phrase' at Matt 19.24). Supports the record's Metzger-direction reading.; Pirtea, Adrian (2021), 'To Pass a Rope Through the Eye of a Needle: The Influence of Byzantine Catenae and Homiliaries on the Greek, Church Slavonic, and Old Romanian Readings of Matthew 19,24' (Festschrift for Christoph Koch): the reception history of the rejected reading — earliest manuscript witnesses of kamilon: GA 1424 (s. IX), Vatican gr. 354 (a. 949), Family 13 (10th-15th c.); the Old Armenian (malowx 'rope, cable') and Old Georgian (sabeli; Khanmeti Gospels 'rope of a ship's mechanism') daughter versions attest 'rope' in all three synoptic verses; Byzantine catenae/homiliaries as the dissemination mechanism into Church Slavonic and Old Romanian
Falsifiability: A pre-9th-century Greek witness reading kamilos at Matthew 19:24, or a demonstrated Aramaic Vorlage with 'rope'</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'great horse' study (Ameen et al. 2021) — the bones against the image</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed large-scale sample with published methods; held below VERIFIED because the study's own authors fence their inference — size alone cannot identify a war horse.
Layer: real · Place: England (171 sites; study led by University of Exeter, AHRC-funded)
Dates: 300 → 1650 (confidence: high)

The 'great horse' zooarchaeological study (Ameen et al. 2021, published/announced January 2022): 1,965 horse remains from 171 English sites, AD 300–1650 — the first large-scale osteoarchaeological assessment of medieval English horse size, showing medieval horses were markedly smaller than modern riding and draft horses, with the largest animals of the high medieval period reaching about 15–16 hands.

Earliest attestation: Ameen et al., International Journal of Osteoarchaeology (2021), DOI 10.1002/oa.3038
Sources: Ameen, C. et al., 'In search of the 'great horse': A zooarchaeological assessment of horses from England (AD 300–1650)', International Journal of Osteoarchaeology (2021), DOI 10.1002/oa.3038 — lead author Carly Ameen, PI Oliver H. Creighton, Alan Outram, University of Exeter; AHRC-funded; 1,965 remains, 171 sites; Science Daily release, 10 Jan 2022: 'Medieval warhorses were surprisingly small in stature'; Gizmodo coverage: 'none in the almost 2,000 we studied would be able to be modern police horses' (Outram); shire horses (18 hands) cast in film roles but much larger than any medieval specimen
Falsifiability: Re-analysis of the same sample with different height-estimation methods overturning the size distributions, or a comparable multi-site sample showing substantially larger medieval horses</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.5,52.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The philosophers' stone as a historical goal</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across ~1,600 years and three civilizations' manuscript traditions — Greek, Arabic, Latin — with surviving texts, annotated copies in Newton's and Lavoisier's libraries, and documented court demonstrations. Per the doctrine's attestation rule (master-list item 25): we grade the BELIEF, not the entity; the phenomenon is verified, the metaphysics untouched.
Layer: real · Place: Panopolis (Akhmim), Roman Egypt — Zosimos's ground
Dates: 0300 → 1750 (confidence: medium)

For roughly sixteen centuries, a continuous tradition of practitioners — from Zosimos of Panopolis (c. 300 CE) through the Arabic Jabirian corpus, the Latin Pseudo-Geber Summa perfectionis (c. 1280-1310), the Rosarium philosophorum (1550) to Sendivogius's Novum lumen chymicum (1604) — sought a transmuting agent, the philosophers' stone, that would perfect base metals into gold. The GOAL and the PRACTICE are historically real; only the claimed RESULT is debunked.

Earliest attestation: Zosimos of Panopolis, Chemeu, c. 300 CE — the earliest surviving alchemical author, writing in Greek in Roman Egypt.
Sources: Zosimos of Panopolis, Chemeu (c. 300 CE) — the earliest surviving alchemical corpus, in Greek; Pseudo-Geber, Summa perfectionis (c. 1280-1310, attributed to Jabir ibn Hayyan; likely Paul of Taranto) — the Latin corpus that carried the stone's laboratory program into the Latin West; Sendivogius, Novum lumen chymicum (1604) — 56+ editions over two centuries in Latin, German, French, English, Russian and Dutch; Sir Isaac Newton's annotated copy (British Library); Antoine Lavoisier's bookplated copy (Cornell); Wikipedia, Michael Sendivogius — the Polish court's transmutation demonstrations (e.g. before Sigismund III Vasa) and the TV-afterlife of the legend
Falsifiability: Absence of the stone-goal from any one of the three manuscript streams (Greek, Arabic, Latin) would collapse the continuity claim. All three carry it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.8,26.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sogdian Ancient Letters (c. 313 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The physical letters exist, were found archaeologically in context, and are read in the original script; only the precise dating and some passage readings are debated.
Layer: real · Place: Dunhuang, Gansu — the Han watchtower where Stein found them
Dates: 310 → 315 (confidence: medium)

A cache of personal letters written in Sogdian (an Iranian language of the Samarkand region) by merchants stranded in China, found in a ruined Han watchtower near Dunhuang — the voice of the merchant houses that ran the network: debts, partners, camels, and a lost loan, 3,000 km from home.

Earliest attestation: The letters themselves, c. 310–313 CE; found 1907 by Aurel Stein in a Han-era watchtower at Dunhuang
Sources: The Ancient Letters (British Library): Sogdian text in Aramaic-derived script; one letter in Brahmi, one fragment in Bactrian — found in situ in the watchtower, 1907; W. B. Henning's decipherment and translations (the foundational edition); subsequent re-editions; The 'Lady 1' letter describing the famine and death in Luoyang after 311 CE — the crisis the merchants lived through
Falsifiability: Proof that the letters are modern forgeries (the find context, script, and linguistic strata rule this out) or a re-dating that breaks the Luoyang-311 anchor would force revision</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0310-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>94.66,40.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>310</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The stirrup's Asian origin and westward transmission</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifacts exist and multiply attest the arc — Chinese tombs to Avar graves to Byzantine manuals — but the exact route, the inventor, and the Strategikon's date stay unproven.
Layer: real · Place: Nanjing, China (earliest known representation; the transmission runs west across the steppe to the Carpathian Basin)
Dates: 322 → 900 (confidence: medium)

The stirrup was invented in East Asia no later than the 4th century CE and reached Europe by the 6th–7th centuries via the steppe peoples (above all the Avars), with Frankish adoption following in the late 8th–early 9th centuries.

Earliest attestation: Ceramic horse figurine with paired triangular stirrups from a First Jin Dynasty tomb near Nanjing, 322 CE (representation); actual stirrups: gold-plated bronze stirrup from an Anyang grave, same era
Sources: 322 CE Jin-dynasty ceramic horse figurine, Nanjing (paired triangular stirrups); Tomb of Feng Sufu (d. 415 CE), Northern Yan — first directly-dated stirrups (mulberry wood, gilded bronze, iron plates, lacquer); 111 early Avar-age apple-shaped cast-iron stirrups from 55 burial sites in Hungary and environs (excavated by 2005); Strategikon attributed to Emperor Maurice (575–628) — 'attached to the saddles should be two iron steps [skala]' (dating of the manual disputed, 8th–9th c. alternative); KPU Pressbooks — MEDIEVAL TECHNOLOGIES: The Stirrup (Hemming): East Asia no later than 300 CE; Europe mid-6th c.; Byzantine army by end of 6th c.; Franks from the neighboring Avars late 8th–early 9th c.
Falsifiability: A securely-dated European stirrup predating the 6th century, or proof the Jin-dynasty figurines are misdated or the Avar grave associations are intrusive</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0322-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>118.8,32.06,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>322</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Eastern Roman Empire (the 'Byzantine' state), 330–1453</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Uncontested in scholarship; the state's continuous existence across the span is attested by an unbroken chain of contemporary administrative, literary, numismatic, and diplomatic sources, plus the conquest accounts of its end.
Layer: real · Place: Constantinople (modern Istanbul)
Dates: 330 → 1453 (confidence: high)

A continuous Roman state centered on Constantinople existed from the city's dedication (11 May 330 CE) to the Ottoman conquest of the city (29 May 1453 CE) — an Eastern Roman Empire of eleven centuries, with continuous imperial administration, law, coinage, and historical writing across the span.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the Theodosian Code (438), Procopius's Wars (c. 550), Theophanes's Chronicle (9th c.), Anna Komnene's Alexiad (12th c.), imperial coinage throughout, Ottoman conquest accounts (1453)
Sources: Procopius, Wars (c. 550); Theophanes the Confessor, Chronicle (9th c.); Anna Komnene, Alexiad (c. 1148); Imperial coinage, 330–1453 (continuous minting record); Treadgold, Warren (1997), A History of the Byzantine State and Society, Stanford UP; Herrin, Judith (2007), Byzantium: The Surprising Life of a Medieval Empire, Princeton UP
Falsifiability: A documented break in the imperial succession with no institutional continuity, or proof the administrative and legal tradition was founded anew after 330</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0330-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>330</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Rhomaioi: the empire called itself Roman</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The self-identification is attested in the empire's own documents across the span and independently by external sources (the Qur'anic usage, the Sultanate of Rûm's name, the Ottoman Kayser-i Rum title); stops short of VERIFIED on the weaker sub-claims of Kaldellis's ethnicity thesis, which the reviewers flag as partly conjectural.
Layer: real · Place: Constantinople (modern Istanbul)
Dates: 330 → 1453 (confidence: high)

The people of the Eastern Roman Empire self-identified as Romans (Romaioi/Rhomaioi) and called their state Romania — Romanland — the Empire of the Romans (hê Rhômaíôn Basileía), from Late Antiquity to 1453.

Earliest attestation: Imperial titulature and chancery documents throughout; Anna Komnene's Alexiad; the Qur'an's Sura 30 (Ar-Rum, c. 615–616 CE) refers to the contemporary Eastern Empire as 'the Romans'
Sources: Browning, Robert (1992), The Byzantine Empire (revised ed.) — 'The Byzantines did not call themselves Byzantines, but Romaioi — Romans'; Kaldellis, Anthony (2019), Romanland: Ethnicity and Empire in Byzantium, Harvard UP — the empire as an ethnically Roman state; barrage of primary sources for Roman self-identification; Qur'an, Sura 30 (Ar-Rum), c. 615–616 CE — external contemporary attestation: the 7th-century Eastern Empire is 'the Romans'; Kazhdan &amp; Constable (1982), People and Power in Byzantium
Falsifiability: A contemporary self-document in which the imperial chancery or the population-at-large calls the state 'Byzantium' or its people 'Byzantines' as an endonym before 1557</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0330-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>330</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first celebration of Christmas on December 25 (Rome, by 336 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The liturgical fact is documented: the Roman depositio lists the nativity at December 25 by c. 336, and the list's structure treats the nativity as the year's opening entry.
Layer: real · Place: Rome
Dates: 0336 → 0336 (confidence: high)

By 336 CE, the church at Rome was celebrating Christ's nativity on December 25 — the first undisputed attestation of Christmas on that date.

Earliest attestation: Chronograph of 354 (Philocalian Calendar), Depositio Martirum, headed: 'VIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae' (December 25: Christ born in Bethlehem of Judea); the depositio is arranged December 25 to December 25, marking the nativity as the start of the ecclesiastical year.
Sources: Chronograph of 354 (Philocalian Calendar), Depositio Martirum: 'VIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae'; Depositio Episcoporum (bishop list ending before 336); Nothaft, C. P. E., 'The Origins of the Christmas Date: Some Recent Trends in Historical Research' — the depositio as terminus ad quem 336; Simmons, E., 'The Origins of Christmas and the Date of Christ's Birth,' JETS 58.2 (2015): 'the earliest undisputed evidence we possess for celebration of Christ's nativity on December 25th'
Falsifiability: Evidence that the depositio martirum's December-25 nativity entry is a later interpolation would drop the grade; an earlier securely dated attestation would raise it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0336-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9029,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>336</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Aramaic incantation bowls: two thousand buried pleas</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifacts exist by the thousands, the texts are published and translated by multiple independent scholars, and the practice (burial under thresholds, face-down) is archaeologically consistent. The grade covers the artifacts and the lived belief they attest; never the demons they bind.
Layer: real · Place: Nippur and Sasanian Babylonia (Iraq)
Dates: 350 → 700 (confidence: medium)

In late-antique Babylonia (4th–7th c. CE), Jewish (and Mandaic, and Syriac) households buried earthenware bowls face-down under thresholds, inscribed with Aramaic incantations binding demons — including Lilith and the lilith-classes — quoting the Hebrew Bible, the Targum, and the liturgy. ~2,000 such bowls are known; the published corpora (Montgomery 1913, Nippur; Naveh &amp; Shaked 1985; Naveh &amp; Shaked 1993; Shaked et al. 2013) establish the practice as the material record of lived protective belief.

Earliest attestation: The bowls themselves: earthenware, inscribed in spiral text, buried under domestic thresholds; excavated (Nippur) and unprovenanced market finds alike
Sources: Montgomery, James A., Aramaic Incantation Texts from Nippur (1913) — 40 bowls, the founding corpus; Naveh, Joseph &amp; Shaked, Shaul, Amulets and Magic Bowls (1985) — the legible Aramaic amulets plus 13 unpublished bowls; Naveh, Joseph &amp; Shaked, Shaul, Magic Spells and Formulae: Aramaic Incantations of Late Antiquity (1993); Shaked, Shaul et al., Aramaic Bowl Spells (Brill, 2013); Hunter, Erica C.D., iconography studies — the Ktar-Lilith female-demon figure drawings on the bowls; National Library of Israel bowl research (Gross &amp; Vilozny) — the bowls as products of competing scribal circles, quoting biblical, Targumic, and liturgical texts; Atamni, Madi, Amar, Saabni &amp; El-Sana (2026), 'Beyond Labels: Visual Invariance in Self-supervised Learning for Aramaic Bowls Document Analysis and Recognition' — ICDAR 2026 (doi:10.1007/978-3-032-36023-6_27, online 26-Aug-2026) — self-supervised visual learning for Aramaic bowl script recognition; machine reading of the bowls, grade-neutral (the Turin-shroud precedent), grade held; Fein, Sari, 'Amulets and Ancestors: Jewish Women's Reproductive Agency in Late Antiquity', AABNER 5, no. 2 (Autumn 2025): 129-163, doi:10.35068/aabner.v5i2.1127 — the Museu da Farmácia Lisbon Jewish Aramaic bowl (Inv. 10895, 5th-8th c. CE): a fertility incantation for Mihranahid daughter of Ahat deploying Gen 30:22 ('And God remembered Rachel...') as imitative magic — a published 5th-8th-c. bowl using the biblical barren-matriarch tradition as a client-side technology; the bowls' users commissioned scripture as a tool, not just a text.
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the dated corpora are modern forgeries would overturn the record; the looted provenance of many market bowls affects context, not the paleographic dating, which rests on excavated parallels.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0350-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>45.23,32.13,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>350</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Indian origin of chess: chaturanga → chatrang → shatranj</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Four independent witness lines — Sanskrit literary, Persian literary, Arabic treatise, and the continuous European adoption trail — converge on the India → Persia → Arab → Europe route; no single contemporary 6th-century Indian artifact survives, so it stops short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Gupta Empire, northern India (traditional)
Dates: 0400 → 0650 (confidence: medium)

Modern chess descends from the Indian game chaturanga (6th century CE), transmitted westward through Persian chatrang and Arab shatranj into medieval Europe.

Earliest attestation: Subandhu's Sanskrit romance Vasavadatta (dated 400–650 CE), referencing chaturanga; Persian Chatrangnamak tradition (earliest surviving ms 1323, claiming a 6th-c. Sasanian transmission under Khosrow I); the Arabic chess treatise of al-Adli in Baghdad, c. 840 CE, which treats the Indian game as the source the Persians altered.
Sources: Subandhu, Vasavadatta (400–650 CE), earliest literary reference to chaturanga (Murray 1913, p.51); Chatrangnamak (Middle Persian tradition; earliest surviving ms 1323; Meissenburg narrows composition to c. 700–800, with claimed 6th-c. oral transmission); Firdausi, Shahnama — the Buzurjmihr/Buzurjmihr chess legend (Sasanian-era transmission story; e.g. Met folio ca. 1330–40); al-Adli, chess treatise, Baghdad c. 840 CE — quoted as stating the Persians altered some rules of Indian chess when they took it over; H.J.R. Murray, A History of Chess (Oxford, 1913) — the founding synthesis of the Indian-origin thesis
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-400 CE chess-family board or piece-set from a non-Indian context with chess-like armies and movement, or decisive philology showing the Vasavadatta chaturanga reference is a later interpolation.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0400-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>82.99,25.31,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nalanda Mahavihara — the ancient university</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The institution is multiply attested across independent traditions — contemporary Chinese eyewitness accounts (Xuanzang, Yijing), excavated architecture and inscriptions (ASI), and the Tibetan transmission record — but foundation dates, population figures, and the library buildings' form rest on later tradition, so the grade stops short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Nalanda Mahavihara, Bihar, India (near Rajgir)
Dates: 427 → 1193 (confidence: medium)

From c. 427 CE (Gupta foundation under Kumaragupta I) into the late 12th century CE, Nalanda in Magadha (Bihar) was one of the ancient world's great residential universities: a mahavihara complex with multiple monasteries, temples, lecture halls, and a major manuscript library tradition (Dharmaganja), drawing students from across Asia — attested by the Chinese pilgrims Xuanzang (at Nalanda 630–643) and Yijing (675–685), by excavated architecture (ASI campaigns from 1915), and by UNESCO's 2016 World Heritage inscription.

Earliest attestation: Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang), Great Tang Records on the Western Regions (Da Tang Xiyu Ji), 7th c. CE — eyewitness account of 630–643; Yijing (I-tsing), 675–685; Kumaragupta I seals (archaeological, 5th c.)
Sources: Xuanzang, Da Tang Xiyu Ji (Great Tang Records on the Western Regions), 7th c. — eyewitness 630–643 (8 compounds, 10 temples, meditation halls; admissions examination); Yijing (I-tsing), account of Nalanda 675–685 — &gt;3,000 monks resident; curriculum (Vinaya, Sanskrit grammar, logic, medicine); Kumaragupta I seals from the Nalanda excavations — 5th-c. Gupta foundation attestation; Archaeological Survey of India excavations (1915–1937, 1974–1982) — 9+ viharas, stupas, temples, drainage systems; destruction layers; Taranatha, History of Buddhism in India (1608) — Tibetan tradition: the three-building Dharmaganja library (Ratnasagara, Ratnodadhi, Ratnaganj), the nine-storey building; UNESCO World Heritage inscription, 'Archaeological Site of Nalanda Mahavihara' (2016)
Falsifiability: A dated inscriptional or stratigraphic sequence showing no large monastic complex at the site before the 10th century, or proof that the Chinese pilgrim accounts are later fabrications</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0427-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>85.4439,25.1368,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>427</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Birmingham Mingana folios' radiocarbon date (568–645 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Oxford radiocarbon measurement is a rigorous primary lab result on the parchment itself; the folios' text matches the standard Qur'an; the skin-vs-hand caveat is carried inside the record.
Layer: real · Place: Cadbury Research Library, University of Birmingham, UK (Mingana Collection, acquired from the Middle East)
Dates: 568 → 645 (confidence: high)

The two Birmingham (Mingana Collection) Qur'anic folios — Suras 18–20 in Hijazi script on sheep/goat parchment — were radiocarbon-dated by the Oxford Radiocarbon Accelerator Unit to 568–645 CE at 95.4% probability, making the parchment contemporary with or pre-dating the traditional compilation; the written text matches the standard Qur'anic text with no significant variations.

Earliest attestation: The folios themselves (undated); identified by Alba Fedeli (PhD researcher, University of Birmingham), announced July 2015
Sources: Oxford University Radiocarbon Accelerator Unit measurement: 568–645 CE at 95.4% probability (sheep/goat parchment), announced July 2015; University of Birmingham institutional announcement and Susan Worrall / David Thomas statements (the folios' discovery, the ink-dating limitation); Alba Fedeli's paleographic identification of the folios and their Hijazi script (the discovery pathway)
Falsifiability: A demonstrated contamination source in the Oxford measurement, or a successful ink-dating method showing the writing is centuries later than the skin</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0568-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.93,52.45,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>568</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The early Qur'anic manuscript record</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent physical witnesses (radiocarbon-dated parchment + Hijazi paleography + monumental inscriptions + external chronicles) converge on a substantially fixed Qur'anic text within decades of 632 CE — while the Sanaa lower text keeps the grade honest: one non-Uthmanic tradition demonstrably existed before standardization.
Layer: real · Place: Sanaa, Yemen (Great Mosque, 1972 find) / Birmingham, UK (Mingana Collection)
Dates: 568 → 800 (confidence: medium)

A substantial Qur'anic text existed in written form within decades of Muhammad's death: the Birmingham Mingana 1572a folios (parchment radiocarbon-dated 568-645 CE, containing Surahs 18-20 in present-day sequence), the Sanaa palimpsest DAM 01-27.1 (parchment before 671 CE at 99% probability; upper text Uthmanic, lower erased text a non-Uthmanic variant tradition), and the Codex Parisino-petropolitanus (BnF Arabe 328ab, ~45% of the Qur'an, 7th-8th c.) all substantially match the canonical text — while the Sanaa lower text proves textual plurality existed before effective standardization.

Earliest attestation: Parchment radiocarbon ranges: Birmingham Mingana 1572a (Oxford Radiocarbon Accelerator Unit, 1456/1461 ± 21 BP, calibrated 568-645 CE, 95.4%); Sanaa palimpsest DAM 01-27.1 (before 671 CE at 99%, before 646 at 75% — Sadeghi &amp; Goudarzi 2012); Tübingen fragment Ms M a VI 165 (649-675 CE, 95%)
Sources: Birmingham Mingana 1572a: radiocarbon OxA-29418 (Oxford, 2015), Hijazi script, Surahs 18-20; Sanaa palimpsest DAM 01-27.1: upper text Uthmanic; lower text (scriptio inferior) edition Sadeghi &amp; Goudarzi 2012; multispectral recovery at Stanford; Codex Parisino-petropolitanus, BnF Arabe 328(ab): ~70 folios Paris + 46 St. Petersburg + Vatican + Khalili; ~45% of the Qur'an; Dome of the Rock inscriptions (691 CE): first monumental Qur'anic text in public
Falsifiability: A dated 7th-century Qur'an manuscript showing a substantially different arrangement of surahs or doctrine, or proof the radiocarbon samples were contaminated or the lower-text edition is a misreading</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0568-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.21,15.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>568</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The early Qur'anic text substantially fixed before 700 CE (rasm closure)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Four independent lines converge: (1) radiocarbon-dated parchment (Birmingham 568-645, Sanaa before-671, Tübingen 649-675), (2) Cook 2004's stemma — the regional codices' variants form a tree, i.e. one archetype, (3) van Putten 2019's shared-orthography proof of a written archetype, (4) Sinai 2014's two-part assessment finding no evidence incompatible with conventional dating. The grade stays CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: the rasm is what closed — the vocalization/readings layer did not (the Seven canonical readings were fixed by Ibn Mujahid in 324/936 and are only rarely evident in first-two-century manuscripts; regional and dotting variants lived on for centuries). The 'fixed by Uthman in 650, crisp and final' telling is the tradition's version; the manuscripts support 'the skeleton closed by ~700, the readings froze in the 10th century.'
Layer: real · Place: Sanaa, Yemen (Great Mosque, 1972 find) / Birmingham, UK (Mingana Collection) / Paris (BnF)
Dates: 568 → 700 (confidence: medium-high)

The early Qur'anic text was substantially fixed before 700 CE: the consonantal skeleton (rasm) reached closure by the end of the 7th century — multiple independent lines converge on a single written archetype within decades of the traditional Uthmanic date, while the vocalized/readings layer froze much later.

Earliest attestation: Radiocarbon ranges: Birmingham Mingana 1572a (Oxford Radiocarbon Accelerator Unit, OxA-29418, 1456/1461 ± 21 BP, calibrated 568-645 CE, 95.4%); Sanaa palimpsest DAM 01-27.1 (before 671 CE at 99%, before 646 at 75% — Sadeghi &amp; Goudarzi 2012); Tübingen Ms M a VI 165 (649-675 CE, 95%); stemmatic argument: Cook 2004, Graeco-Arabica 9-10 (regional variants of the Uthmanic codices form a tree → single archetype); van Putten 2019, BSOAS 82(2):271-288 (shared orthographic idiosyncrasies — e.g. the 'grace of God' spelling — across Uthmanic-type manuscripts prove a written archetype); Sinai 2014 Part I, BSOAS 77(2):273-292 + Part II, BSOAS 77(3):509-521 (systematic assessment of the late-7th-c. closure hypothesis: neither epigraphic nor literary evidence is incompatible with the conventional dating; the emergent-canon counter-position heard and answered); Sidky 2021 (four regional exemplar codices; the Syrian codex traced to Hims, not Damascus)
Sources: Cook, M. (2004) 'The Stemma of the Regional Codices of the Koran,' Graeco-Arabica 9-10 — regional variants form a tree, single archetype (generally Uthman); van Putten, M. (2019) "The 'Grace of God' as evidence for a written Uthmanic archetype," BSOAS 82(2):271-288 — shared orthographic idiosyncrasies prove a written archetype; Sinai, N. (2014) 'When did the consonantal skeleton of the Quran reach closure? Part I,' BSOAS 77(2):273-292; Part II, BSOAS 77(3):509-521 — systematic assessment of the c.700-closure hypothesis; Sadeghi, B. &amp; Goudarzi, M. (2012) 'San'a' 1 and the Origins of the Qur'an,' Der Islam 87(1-2):1-129 — the Sanaa lower text as early companion tradition; Birmingham Mingana 1572a: radiocarbon OxA-29418 (Oxford, 2015), Hijazi script, Surahs 18-20; Codex Parisino-petropolitanus, BnF Arabe 328(ab), ~45% of the Qur'an, 7th-8th c.; Dome of the Rock inscriptions (691 CE): first monumental Qur'anic text in public; BiSaME project (DFG, 2026–2029, Hamburg + Staatsbibliothek Berlin, Fedeli &amp; Eich): the first systematic ensemble biography of the Great Mosque of Sanaa manuscript collection — computer vision/AI/paleography/philology; 2026 evidence-state context, no new dating yet; Al-Fanar: Jurnal Ilmu Al-Qur'an dan Tafsir, Vol. 9 No. 1 (2026), pp. 13–25: interdisciplinary review of radiocarbon dating in early-Qur'an historiography (Birmingham, Sanaa, Tübingen) — secondary synthesis, confirms the parchment-vs-text fence
Falsifiability: A dated 7th-century Qur'an manuscript showing a substantially different arrangement of surahs or doctrine; proof the radiocarbon samples were contaminated; or a published refutation of the Cook/van Putten tree showing the regional codices do NOT descend from one archetype</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0568-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.21,15.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>568</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Muhammad</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Near-contemporary non-Muslim sources (634, 640 CE) confirm an Arab prophet in Arabia; the detailed biography comes from Islamic sources written a century-plus later.
Layer: real · Place: Mecca (traditional)
Dates: 570 → 632 (confidence: traditional)

Muhammad (c. 570-632 CE) of Mecca proclaimed the Qur'anic revelation, united the Arabian tribes, and founded the community (umma) at Medina in 622 CE — the event from which the Islamic calendar counts.

Earliest attestation: Doctrina Iacobi (634 CE, earliest external reference to an Arab prophet); Thomas the Presbyter chronicle (640 CE); Ibn Ishaq's Sira (d. 767 CE, surviving in Ibn Hisham's recension)
Sources: Doctrina Iacobi (634 CE, earliest external mention — 'a prophet has appeared, coming with the Saracens'; Anthony 2014, Der Islam 91.2:243-265 — the 'keys of paradise' motif studied in its late-antique context); Thomas the Presbyter, Chronicle (640 CE — 'the Arabs of Muhammad', tayyaye d-Mhmt); PERF 558 (643 CE — bilingual Greek-Arabic receipt, the earliest Arabic-language papyrus of the conquest era; documentary witness, not tradition); Sebeos, Armenian History (c. 660s — external account naming 'Mahmet', sparse details); Hoyland, R.G., Seeing Islam As Others Saw It (1997; new Gorgias edition with 2020s foreword — the non-Muslim documentary survey; method: external witnesses first); Ibn Ishaq / Ibn Hisham, Sira (8th c., traditional biography); Constitution of Medina (preserved in the Sira tradition; debated authenticity — Anthony/Hobson line treats it as early documentary residue; Shoemaker 2022 line doubts it)
Falsifiability: Earlier, contradictory contemporary accounts of 7th-century Arabia, or proof the Doctrina Iacobi passage is interpolated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0570-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.8262,21.4225,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>570</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 591 conflation: three women fused into one Magdalene</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The homily is a dated, quoted primary source; the identification it asserts contradicts the texts it claims to read and was officially undone in 1969.
Layer: real · Place: Rome (Homily 33 preached in Rome)
Dates: 591 → 591 (confidence: high)

On 21 September 591, Pope Gregory the Great (Homily 33 on the Gospels) identified Mary Magdalene with the 'sinful woman' of Luke 7:36-50 and with Mary of Bethany (John 12): 'she whom Luke calls the sinful woman, whom John calls Mary, we believe to be the Mary from whom seven devils were ejected according to Mark.' The fusion created the penitent-prostitute Magdalene of fourteen centuries of Western art and devotion. No canonical text makes the identification; the Eastern churches never adopted it; the 1969 General Roman Calendar reform (Mysterii Paschalis) separated the celebrations again (July 22, St Mary Magdalene; the Bethany commemoration removed), and in 2016 the July 22 memorial was elevated to a feast, the apostles' own liturgical rank.

Earliest attestation: Gregory the Great, Homiliarum in Evangelia 33 (591)
Sources: Gregory the Great, Homily 33 on the Gospels (591) — the fusion sentence; Luke 7:36-50 vs Luke 8:1-3 — the texts name different women; General Roman Calendar reform, Mysterii Paschalis (14 Feb 1969) — the feasts separated; Congregation for Divine Worship decree (3 June 2016) — July 22 elevated to a feast
Falsifiability: A pre-591 Latin text making the same three-way identification, or a canonical manuscript variant naming Magdalene in Luke 7</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0591-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>591</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The khipu as the Inca empire's information technology</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Hundreds of surviving artifacts converge with colonial eyewitness testimony, independent decimal-system decoding (Locke, the Aschers), and a living Andean tradition still observed in the 1990s — three independent lines on the same claim.
Layer: real · Place: Cuzco, Peru — imperial capital; khipu use was empire-wide across Tawantinsuyu (~2 million sq km)
Dates: 0600 → 1800 (confidence: medium)

The khipu — cords of cotton or camelid wool, hung from a primary cord and tied with systematic knots encoding decimal numbers, with information also carried in cord color, fiber type, ply direction, knot type, and attachment position — was the Inca empire's (Tawantinsuyu's) standard system for recording census data, tribute, storehouse accounts, and other administrative information, read and maintained by trained specialists, the quipucamayocs.

Earliest attestation: Colonial Spanish chronicles (mid-16th c.: Cieza de Leon, Polo de Ondegardo, Guaman Poma) describing the quipucamayoc system; surviving khipus from Inca-period contexts.
Sources: Marcia and Robert Ascher, 'Code of the Quipu' (1981): analysis of several hundred khipus — most information numeric, decimal knot clusters (simple, long, figure-of-eight knots), powers of ten by position; Leland Locke (1912): demonstration that khipu knots record a full base-10 numerical system; Gary Urton, 'Signs of the Inka Khipu' (2003): ~600 extant khipus estimated; the Open Khipu Repository holds 702 with public data (Jan 2026); a 2021 survey places known pre-Columbian khipus just under 1,400; Colonial chronicles describing quipucamayocs maintaining census, tribute, and storehouse records across Tawantinsuyu; Frank Salomon (1994, Tupicocha): khipus still used for local government record-keeping — a living tradition under observation
Falsifiability: Systematic failure of the decimal reading across newly excavated imperial-period khipus with clear archaeological context, or demonstration that knot placement is random/decorative rather than positional-numeric</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0600-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.96746,-13.53195,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The origin of Islam</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — 7th-8th century manuscripts, monumental inscriptions, and independent external chronicles converge on Islam's 7th-century Arabian origin and rapid expansion.
Layer: real · Place: Mecca (traditional origin)
Dates: 610 → 750 (confidence: medium)

In 7th-century Arabia, the Qur'anic revelation proclaimed through Muhammad founded Islam; within a century of his death the caliphate stretched from Spain to Central Asia, and the Qur'anic text was standardized.

Earliest attestation: Early Qur'anic manuscripts (Birmingham Mingana, Sanaa palimpsest — 7th-8th c.); the Dome of the Rock inscriptions (691 CE); non-Muslim chronicles of the conquests
Sources: Birmingham Qur'an manuscript (Mingana 1572a, radiocarbon 568-645 CE); Sanaa palimpsest (Dar al-Makhtutat, 7th-8th c.); Dome of the Rock inscriptions (691 CE); Sebeos, Armenian History (7th c. external account of the conquests); PERF 558 (643 CE — bilingual Greek-Arabic receipt; the conquest era's own paperwork, documentary not traditional); Arab-Sasanian coins naming 'Muhammad rasul Allah' (691 CE); Abd al-Malik's reformed coinage (696 CE) — the epigraphic-numismatic line (Rosa Delgado #26's beat, cited comparatively)
Falsifiability: Manuscript evidence of a substantially different early Qur'anic text, or proof the 7th-century conquest chronicles describe a non-Islamic movement</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0610-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.8262,21.4225,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>610</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Qur'anic attestation of the jinn</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief-phenomenon is multiply attested: the 7th-century text, its manuscript record, and the hadith layer all agree on fire-created, pre-Adamic, morally-agented jinn distinct from angels — graded as attestation, never as entities.
Layer: real · Place: Mecca/Hijaz (traditional revelation setting)
Dates: 610 → 632 (confidence: traditional)

The Qur'an attests jinn as created beings distinct from angels and humans: made of smokeless fire, created before Adam, addressed as moral agents (believers and unbelievers among them) alongside humans.

Earliest attestation: The Qur'anic text itself: Surah 72 (al-Jinn, 28 verses — a company of jinn hearing the Qur'an and converting, 72:1–14); Surah 55:15 (created from a smokeless flame of fire); Surah 15:26–27 (jinn created before Adam); Surah 51:56 (jinn and men created for service); Surah 18:50 (Iblis was 'of the jinn'); Surah 6:100, 6:128, 34:41 (the distinction from angels).
Sources: Qur'an 72:1–14; 55:15; 15:26–27; 51:56; 18:50; 6:100,128; 34:41; Sahih Muslim (on A'ishah's authority): angels created from light — the light/fire distinction
Falsifiability: A pre-Uthmanic Qur'anic manuscript tradition without the jinn passages, or proof the Surah 72 complex is a later interpolation — neither has appeared in the Birmingham or Sana'a evidence</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0610-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.83,21.42,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>610</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Al-Ma'mala mosque: a first-century-Hijri mosque with a Muhammad-name qibla inscription</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced by the national authority through official channels and covered by the Biblical Archaeology Review (2 Oct 2026): the mosque, its qibla-wall Muhammad-name inscription, and the mining context are reported as finds, not tradition — epigraphic confirmation still pending.
Layer: real · Place: Al-Ma'mala, Al-Aqiq Governorate, Al-Baha region, southwestern Saudi Arabia
Dates: 622 → 700 (confidence: medium)

The Saudi Heritage Commission's fourth excavation season at Al-Ma'mala (Al-Aqiq Governorate, Al-Baha region, announced 13 September 2026 via the Commission's official channels) uncovered a mosque dated to the first century AH (7th century CE) — dressed-granite construction with mihrab, entrances, and minarets — bearing wall inscriptions including blessings upon 'Muhammad, son of Abdullah' on the qibla wall, plus religious supplications and Quranic passages. The mosque stands in a mining settlement where laboratory analyses date gold, silver, and copper extraction from the late 6th through the 9th century CE.

Earliest attestation: Saudi Heritage Commission (@MOCHeritage) announcement, 13 September 2026; Biblical Archaeology Review 'Mining and Merchants in Ancient Arabia', 2 October 2026
Sources: Saudi Heritage Commission — fourth excavation season announcement at Al-Ma'mala, 13 September 2026; Biblical Archaeology Review — 'Mining and Merchants in Ancient Arabia', 2 October 2026 (seventh-century mosque, qibla-wall inscriptions, mining 6th–9th c. CE); Curly Tales — first-century-Hijri mosque report with qibla-wall inscription text, September 2026
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed publication of the fourth season contradicting the mosque's dating or the reported inscriptions</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0622-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>41.6,20.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>622</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Darb Zubayda inscription: a first-Hijri-century pilgrim graffito quoting Al-Baqarah</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Announced by the national authority and reported with inscription text and exact location: the graffito is reported as a find, not tradition — verse identification and dating still announcement-grade.
Layer: real · Place: Dhali al Nis, Darb Zubayda, Hail region (south of Samira)
Dates: 622 → 700 (confidence: medium)

The Saudi Heritage Commission documented a first-Hijri-century inscription at Dhali al Nis (south of Samira, Hail region) on the Darb Zubayda, carved on a flat granite rock at a waystation summit: a supplication for forgiveness for an individual named Ibrahim ibn Ziyad, including a quotation from the end of a verse of Surah Al-Baqarah — a documented Kufan Hajj-route waypoint.

Earliest attestation: Saudi Heritage Commission announcement, 7 May 2026; cairoscene.com report
Sources: Saudi Heritage Commission — Dhali al Nis inscription announcement, 7 May 2026
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed epigraphic publication dating the inscription outside the 1st c. AH or reading the text differently</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0622-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>42.0,27.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>622</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Wadi al-Usaylah record: 60+ early Islamic inscriptions on the Iraq Hajj corridor</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Documented by the Royal Commission for Makkah City and Holy Sites via SPA: 60+ inscriptions and two Abbasid-era wells reported as finds, not tradition; the paleographic weight of individual faces is fenced pending publication.
Layer: real · Place: Wadi al-Usaylah, northeast of Makkah
Dates: 622 → 800 (confidence: medium)

Wadi al-Usaylah (northeast of Makkah, ~6 km by 2 km), documented by the Royal Commission for Makkah City and Holy Sites in a Saudi Press Agency release of 12 September 2026, preserves more than 60 early Islamic rock inscriptions dated to the 7th–8th centuries CE (1st–2nd c. AH) — evidence for the orthographic and aesthetic development of early Arabic script — plus two surviving historical wells from water infrastructure commissioned by successive Islamic rulers (some Abbasid-era) along the Iraq Hajj corridor; a comprehensive preservation program is now underway.

Earliest attestation: Saudi Press Agency release, 12 September 2026; OANA/Oananews report
Sources: Saudi Press Agency — Wadi al-Usaylah documentation release, 12 September 2026
Falsifiability: Publication of the inscriptions showing dates outside the claimed range, or proof the SPA release fabricated the assemblage</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0622-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>40.1,21.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>622</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Al Mahd survey: Umar-name inscriptions in the Madinah desert</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A documented national-authority survey (announced via SPA) with enumerated finds: the Umar-name inscriptions are reported as finds, not tradition — but the personal link to Umar himself is fenced, per the report's own caution.
Layer: real · Place: Al Mahd Governorate, Madinah region (Al Suwayriqiyah, Al Muwayhiyah, Hadhah)
Dates: 634 → 750 (confidence: medium)

The Saudi Heritage Commission's second survey season in Al Mahd Governorate (Madinah region, announced 10 June 2026) documented 1,774 finds across 156 new sites — 461 Islamic inscriptions, 34 Thamudic inscriptions, 1,259 rock art panels, stone structures, palaces, two caravan routes, and four wells — including rock inscriptions in Hijazi script bearing the name of Umar ibn al-Khattab, the second Rashidun caliph (r. 634–644 CE).

Earliest attestation: Saudi Heritage Commission survey announcement via Saudi Press Agency, 10 June 2026; arkeonews.net report
Sources: Saudi Heritage Commission — Al Mahd second-season survey announcement, 10 June 2026 (via SPA); The Islamic Information — rock inscription text report, 11 June 2026 ("May Allah be the guardian of Umar ibn al-Khattab in this world and the Hereafter")
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed epigraphic publication showing the reported Umar-name inscriptions do not exist or read differently</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0634-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>40.85,24.08,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>634</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Doctrina Iacobi (July 634 CE) — earliest non-Muslim writing on an Arabian prophet</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple manuscript copies preserve the passage; the internal indiction date is explicit and broadly accepted by scholars — but the passage names no name: it says only 'the prophet who has appeared with the Saracens.' The identification with Muhammad is the mainstream reading, not the text's own claim.
Layer: real · Place: Carthage (composition); the prophet passage is set at Caesarea Maritima
Dates: 634 → 634 (confidence: high)

The Doctrina Iacobi nuper baptizati, a Byzantine Christian-Jewish polemic completed in Carthage before 13 July 634 CE, contains — in a letter from a certain Abraham in Caesarea — the earliest known non-Muslim writing to describe a prophet who 'has appeared among the Saracens': an armed figure proclaiming the advent of the anointed one and claiming the keys of paradise.

Earliest attestation: The tract itself — completed July 634 CE, i.e., about two years after Muhammad's traditional death date of 632 CE
Sources: Doctrina Iacobi nuper baptizati, V.16, 209 (ed. Bonwetsch; passage quoting the letter of Abraham in Caesarea); Sean W. Anthony, 'Muhammad, the Keys to Paradise, and the Doctrina Iacobi: A Late Antique Puzzle' (Der Islam 91.2, 2014: 243-265)
Falsifiability: Manuscript evidence showing the prophet passage is a later interpolation, or a scholarly re-dating of the tract's composition decades after 634 CE</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0634-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.32,36.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>634</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>PERF 558 (643 CE) — the oldest dated Arabic document</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The document survives, is self-dated in both languages, and has been multiply published since 1894 — the Arabic text is 7th-century administration, not later recollection.
Layer: real · Place: Egypt (exact findspot unknown; held Vienna Papyrus Collection)
Dates: 643 → 643 (confidence: high)

PERF 558 (P.Vindob. inv. G 39726, Vienna): a bilingual Greek-Arabic tax protocol dated 643 CE (22 AH), issued under 'Abd Allah b. Jabr — the oldest dated Arabic documentary text in the world.

Earliest attestation: The papyrus itself, 643 CE — a primary bureaucratic document, a decade after the conquest of Egypt
Sources: PERF 558 (P.Vindob. inv. G 39726), Greek-Arabic tax protocol, 22 AH / 643 CE, issued under 'Abd Allah b. Jabr (primary, Vienna Papyrus Collection); J. von Karabacek, Papyrus Erzherzog Rainer Führer (PERF), 1894 — the editio princeps
Falsifiability: Proof the document's date is a later scribal addition, or the discovery of an Arabic documentary text demonstrably and securely earlier</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0643-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.0,29.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>643</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Zuhayr inscription (24 AH / 644–645 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact survives and dates itself; its reading and its 24 AH date are uncontested in the Arabic epigraphic literature.
Layer: real · Place: al-Ula region, Hijaz, Saudi Arabia
Dates: 644 → 645 (confidence: high)

The Zuhayr graffito from the al-Ula region of the Hijaz — self-dated 'in the year twenty-four' (24 AH / 644–645 CE) and reporting the death of the caliph 'Umar — is the oldest securely dated Arabic inscription of the Islamic era.

Earliest attestation: The graffito itself, 644–645 CE — a primary document of the first caliphate year
Sources: The Zuhayr graffito, self-dated 24 AH, al-Ula region (primary); The Arabic epigraphic literature on early Islamic graffiti (the graffito is the field's standard earliest securely dated Islamic-era example)
Falsifiability: Proof that the date line was cut later than the rest of the text, or the discovery of an Arabic inscription demonstrably and securely earlier in the Islamic era</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0644-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.92,26.61,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>644</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dome of the Rock inscriptions (72 AH / 691–92 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The monument survives in situ, the date is carried inside the inscription itself, and the text has been multiply published and studied — the strongest dating a Qur'anic text can carry.
Layer: real · Place: Temple Mount / Haram al-Sharif, Jerusalem
Dates: 691 → 692 (confidence: high)

The Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem, dedicated 72 AH / 691–92 CE, carries about 240 meters of Arabic inscription — including Qur'anic verses and paraphrases — making it the oldest securely dated Qur'anic text in the world: the date belongs to the writing itself, not to the material it is written on.

Earliest attestation: The building itself, 691–92 CE — a monumental primary source, still in situ
Sources: Inner ambulatory inscription naming 'Abd al-Malik, 72 AH (in situ, Jerusalem); Estelle Whelan, 'Inscriptions in the Dome of the Rock and their reinterpretation' — the anti-Trinitarian polemic reading (Q 4:171, 19:33–35, 112) and the verse-selection argument; Oleg Grabar, The Dome of the Rock — the monument's Umayyad context and inscription program
Falsifiability: An epigraphic demonstration that the 72 AH inscription is a later addition to the building, or a Qur'anic inscription demonstrably and securely earlier</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0691-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2354,31.7781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>691</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dhul-Qarnayn identification question</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The attestation half is CORROBORATED by the Syriac text itself, Tabari's commentary, and Ferdowsi's Iskandar passages — independent witnesses to the same identification over centuries. The identity half stays SPECULATION because the identification is interpretive, not documentary: nothing in the Qur'anic text names Alexander, and the horns motif exists in multiple Near Eastern traditions.
Layer: real · Place: The tradition is textual, not geographic: the Syriac Legend (northern Mesopotamia milieu), al-Tabari (Baghdad), Ferdowsi (Tus, Khorasan)
Dates: 700 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The Qur'an's 'Dhu al-Qarnayn' ('the Two-Horned One,' Surah 18:83–101) — a builder of an iron wall against Gog and Magog — has been traditionally identified with Alexander since the 8th century (al-Tabari and later commentators), drawing on the Syriac Alexander Legend's Christianized Alexander and Pseudo-Callisthenian material; Ferdowsi's Shahnameh (completed 1010) carries the Persian 'Iskandar' further. The identification-tradition is attested across a millennium of texts. Whether the Qur'anic figure IS Alexander is disputed (minority proposals include Cyrus the Great); the identity itself is unresolved and graded SPECULATION.

Earliest attestation: The Syriac Alexander Legend (Neṣḥānā d-Aleksandrōs, c. 629–630 CE, dated by its Heraclius-era allusions); al-Tabari's Tafsir (9th c.); Ferdowsi's Shahnameh (completed 1010)
Sources: The Syriac Alexander Legend (Neṣḥānā d-Aleksandrōs), c. 629–630 CE: the Christianized Alexander building the gate against Gog and Magog — the bridge text between the Greek Romance tradition and the Islamic Dhu al-Qarnayn; al-Tabari, Jami' al-Bayan (9th c.): the classical tafsir carrying the Alexander identification; Ionescu, Dan-Tudor, 'Alexander the Great in the Persian legends': early Islamic scholarship from the 8th century 'began to conflate Dhū'l-Qarnayn with Alexander, notably in al-Tabari's works, reflecting cross-cultural influences between Persian and Arabic literature'; Iranian Zoroastrian texts portray Alexander negatively — the same king, opposite legend; Qur'an 18:83–101 (the Dhu al-Qarnayn pericope): the primary text — the figure is unnamed by the Qur'an itself
Falsifiability: No single document can settle the identity; the tradition-grade would fall only if the 8th-century identification were shown to be a modern fabrication — it is not, the attestations are genuine.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.42,32.53,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Persian panemone — the first practical windmills (700–900 AD)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED: the Persian panemone is the earliest recorded working windmill design. Vertical lightweight wooden sails attached by horizontal struts to a central vertical shaft; first built to pump water, subsequently modified to grind grain. The 9th-century Sistan mills are documented by Muslim geographers; the design spread across the Islamic world to India and China. At Nashtifan in northeastern Iran, about 30 such mills — 50 to 60 feet high, wooden blades rotating on a vertical axis between high walls of red clay and straw — have stood for over a thousand years and still grind grain in the 'storm's sting' wind that gave the village its name. Earlier wind-powered devices (Heron's organ, Tibetan prayer wheels) are toys or design concepts, not practical windmills; the panemone is the first machine that did real work.
Layer: real · Place: Sistan, eastern Iran / western Afghanistan; Nashtifan, Razavi Khorasan Province, Iran
Dates: 700 → 900 (confidence: medium)

The first practical windmills were the Persian panemone — vertical-axis machines with lightweight wooden sails on a central vertical shaft, built in the Sistan region (modern Iran/Afghanistan) around 700–900 AD, first for pumping water and later for grinding grain.

Earliest attestation: 9th-century Muslim geographers; the Nashtifan tradition
Sources: Wikipedia 'Panemone windmill': the earliest recorded windmill design, Persian, 700–900 AD; vertical sails on a central shaft; water-pumping first, grain-grinding after; the spread across the Islamic world.; Green Prophet (Nashtifan): ~30 mills, 1,000+ years, still grinding; the 'storm's sting' wind; the vertical-axis design predating European mills.
Falsifiability: Earlier documented practical windmills</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>61.0,34.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tariq's 711 crossing and the Battle of Guadalete — CORROBORATED</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The crossing and the Guadalete victory are attested on both sides of the religious divide — the Latin Chronicle of 754 and the 9th-century Arabic tradition agree on the event; held below VERIFIED because force size (~7,000), the Julian ferry contract, and the exact battlefield rest on the later Arabic tradition alone.
Layer: real · Place: Gibraltar (Jabal Tariq) — the crossing's landing point, named for the commander
Dates: 711 → 711 (confidence: medium)

In 711, the Umayyad commander Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed the Strait with about 7,000 men — mostly Berbers — aboard vessels associated with Julian of Ceuta, landed at Gibraltar (Jabal Tariq, 'Mount Tariq'), and defeated the Visigothic king Roderic at Guadalete, opening the Muslim conquest of Iberia.

Earliest attestation: Latin Chronicle of 754 — the earliest near-contemporary account of the conquest; Ibn Abd al-Hakam, Futuh Misr (9th c.) — the earliest substantial Arabic account of the crossing's logistics.
Sources: Chronicle of 754 (Latin, Mozarabic) — the earliest near-contemporary account of the 711 conquest; Ibn Abd al-Hakam, Futuh Misr wa'l-Maghrib (9th c.) — reports the ~7,000-man force and vessels 'crossing and recrossing'; the ferry logic that refutes the burning; Ibn al-Qutiyya (10th c.) — the Julian tradition's second anchor; Wikipedia, 'Muslim conquest of the Iberian Peninsula' — crossing, force size, Guadalete in July 711, the 712 reinforcement under Musa ibn Nusayr
Falsifiability: A contemporary 8th-century inscription or coinage contradicting the crossing year, or a 711-dated document placing Roderic elsewhere alive after July, would force a re-grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0711-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.35,36.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>711</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Charles Martel's cavalry funding and the Church lands</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Martel's military reforms and church-land precaria are independently documented; the stirrup link is absent from the record, so this foil survives White's kill intact.
Layer: real · Place: Tours/Poitiers, Francia (732 — the battle Brunner's thesis keys to)
Dates: 715 → 741 (confidence: medium)

Charles Martel (mayor of the palace 715–741) built Frankish military power in part on mounted warriors and funded it in part through church-land grants (precaria); the documented record shows the reforms and the seizures, but no contemporary source ties either to the stirrup.

Earliest attestation: Heinrich Brunner's 19th-century military analysis (White's source for the cavalry argument); Pepin the Short's 758 demand of horses as tribute from the Saxons (evidence of a cavalry-dependent army one generation on)
Sources: Heinrich Brunner (19th c.) — the shift to mounted warfare after Tours 732; Pepin's 758 Saxon horse-tribute; the Muslim-incursion prompting of church-land confiscation for cavalry support (as summarized in the Great Stirrup Controversy article); Rebus Community — History of Applied Science &amp; Technology, ch. 5: Charlemagne (not Martel) placed increased emphasis on cavalry for rapid-response missions; feudal dominance of mounted aristocrats follows the post-Charlemagne royal collapse
Falsifiability: Contemporary 8th-century Frankish records explicitly linking Martel's cavalry funding to stirrup adoption, or proving the precaria system predated the military need</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0715-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.69,47.39,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>715</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 al-Juhfah miqat excavation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A documented excavation season by the national authority with a university partner: the artifacts, kilns, channel, and dated tombstones are reported as finds, not tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Miqat al-Juhfah, 187 km NW of Mecca
Dates: 719 → 816 (confidence: medium)

The Saudi Heritage Commission's first scientific excavation season at Miqat al-Juhfah (2026, with the University of Exeter) documented the Hijrah-era pilgrimage station: 1,700+ artifacts, six pottery kilns, a water channel, and 13 tombstones (some Umayyad/Abbasid), with imported goods from the Levant, Egypt, and Abyssinia — the pilgrimage route's infrastructure in the material record.

Earliest attestation: Saudi Heritage Commission announcement, 2026; archaeologymag.com report, 11 June 2026
Sources: Saudi Heritage Commission / University of Exeter — first excavation season report, 2026; The Gulf Gazette — Heritage Commission dispatch, 2026
Falsifiability: Peer-reviewed publication contradicting the excavation's finds, or proof the 2026 report fabricated the assemblage</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0719-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.58,22.25,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>719</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The sira transmission gap</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The dated recension chain is multiply attested: the lost Ibn Ishaq original (d. 767/768), the Ibn Hisham recension (d. 833/834) with its recognizable interpolations (Guillaume 1955), and the al-Tabari quotations supplying omitted passages. The gap is a dated-text fact, not an inference; the Qur'an's biographical reticence is textual fact.
Layer: real · Place: Fustat (Old Cairo), Egypt — where Ibn Hisham produced the recension
Dates: 767 → 834 (confidence: medium)

The earliest surviving biography of Muhammad — Ibn Hisham's recension (d. 833/834 CE) of Ibn Ishaq's Sirat Rasul Allah (d. 767/768 CE, original lost) — postdates the events it describes by roughly 120–200 years; no biography of Muhammad from the 600s survives, and the Qur'an itself contains no narrative biography (no birth story, no childhood, no year-by-year life).

Earliest attestation: Ibn Hisham's recension of the Sirat Rasul Allah (d. 833/834 CE) — the earliest extant full biography; the Ibn Ishaq original (d. 767/768) survives only through it and through quotations in al-Tabari.
Sources: A. Guillaume, The Life of Muhammad: A Translation of Ibn Ishaq's Sirat Rasul Allah (Oxford University Press, 1955), preface pp. xvii, xxxi — the original text of the Sirat Rasul Allah did not survive; Ibn Hisham 'abbreviated, annotated, and sometimes altered' it, his interpolations recognizable; al-Tabari passages citing Ibn Ishaq supply what Ibn Hisham omitted.; Wikipedia, 'Al-Sirah al-Nabawiyyah (Ibn Ishaq)' — summary of the transmission: Ibn Ishaq d. 767/768, original lost, Ibn Hisham (Basra; Fustat, d. 833) recension the earliest substantial surviving biography.; Tarif Khalidi, Arabic Historical Thought in the Classical Period (1994), p. 17 — the early sira as 'a Sira of primitive devotion... The guiding principle is inclusion rather than exclusion' (quoted via the Center for Islamic Pluralism's survey of the historical-Muhammad literature, which also records: 'The oldest Sira is that of Ibn Ishaq (c. 704–767), and it has been the source of all subsequent biographical writings'; the original is lost and survives through Ibn Hisham's redaction, d. 833).
Falsifiability: A securely dated 7th-century biography manuscript, or proof that Ibn Hisham's recension was composed before 700 CE.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0767-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.231,30.008,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>767</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cahokia: the earthen city of the Mississippi floodplain</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The city is multiply attested archaeologically (earthworks, burials, trade goods, chronology) and by an international heritage register; VERIFIED is parked only on the exact peak population, which ranges 10,000-50,000 across the literature.
Layer: real · Place: Cahokia Mounds, Collinsville, Illinois (Monks Mound)
Dates: 800 → 1400 (confidence: medium)

At its peak around 1050-1150 CE, Cahokia — the pre-eminent center of the Mississippian culture (800-1350 CE) — was a city of 10,000-20,000+ people covering ~1,600 hectares with ~120 earthen mounds; Monks Mound, its central pyramid, is the largest prehistoric earthen structure in the New World, and the city ran a solar observatory (Woodhenge) and a continent-spanning trade network. This is the timeline's first pre-Columbian North American record.

Earliest attestation: The earthworks themselves; Monks Mound's construction sequence and radiocarbon dates from the excavation record (14 construction stages)
Sources: UNESCO World Heritage Centre, Cahokia Mounds State Historic Site listing (1982): largest pre-Columbian settlement north of Mexico; 10,000-20,000 people at peak 1050-1150; Monks Mound the largest prehistoric earthen structure in the New World, 30 m high on a ~6-hectare base, built in 14 stages; Woodhenge astronomical observatory; Monks Mound engineering record: ~22 million cubic feet of earth, dug by hand and carried in baskets, base over 14 acres (316 x 241 m), 100 ft tall; summit building of the paramount chief; Timothy R. Pauketat, Cahokia: Ancient America's Great City on the Mississippi (2009): the city's rise, the Grand Plaza, the palisade, the copper workshop at Mound 34 — the material basis for the higher population estimates (up to 40,000) that UNESCO's conservative figure sits beneath; Benson et al. 2009, American Antiquity 74(3): Cahokia's boom and bust — the environmental/archaeological data on the city's decline by c. 1400
Falsifiability: Stratigraphic proof that Monks Mound was built by post-contact Europeans, or a published radiocarbon series placing the mounds outside the pre-Columbian window</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0800-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-90.061,38.658,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Theology of Aristotle: Plotinus in Arabic dress, shelved under Aristotle for a millennium</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript tradition and the preface colophon multiply attest the text, its date, and its makers; the philosophical scholarship (Zimmermann 1986, Adamson 2002, SEP) unanimously identifies the content as Plotinus's Enneads IV-VI, not Aristotle - the attribution fails on content, not on doubt.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad, Abbasid Iraq (production; al-Kindi's circle)
Dates: 830 → 850 (confidence: high)

The Arabic philosophical text known as the Theology of Aristotle (Uthulujiya Aristu), which circulated for roughly a millennium as a work of Aristotle and shaped Islamic, Jewish, and Christian philosophy, is a creative Arabic paraphrase of Plotinus's Enneads (books IV-VI, on Soul, Intellect, and the One), produced in 9th-century Baghdad in the circle of al-Kindi. Its own preface names the layers: 'the book of Aristotle the philosopher, called in Greek Theologia... The exposition of Porphyry of Syria, translated into Arabic by Ibn Na'ima al-Himsi, and corrected for Ahmad ibn al-Mu'tasim by al-Kindi' - Ahmad being the son of the Abbasid caliph who reigned 833-842, which dates the production to the 830s-840s. The work was the single most important conduit by which Neoplatonism reached the Islamic world, read by al-Kindi, al-Farabi, Avicenna (who wrote comments on it and may have doubted its authenticity), and later by Latin scholastics and Safavid thinkers.

Earliest attestation: The Arabic manuscripts themselves (vulgate version); the preface colophon naming Ibn Na'ima al-Himsi and al-Kindi
Sources: Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, 'Theology of Aristotle' (Summer 2019 ed.): the Arabic version produced in the circle of al-Kindi; Zimmermann 1986 the major provenance study, conclusions accepted in subsequent work; the Greek exemplar earlier than our oldest Greek Enneads manuscripts (contains Enn. IV.7.8 via Eusebius, absent from all Greek mss); preface colophon quoted verbatim; Peter Adamson, 'Aristotelianism and the Soul in the Arabic Plotinus': the Theology is a paraphrase of parts of Plotinus's Enneads, authored by a member of al-Kindi's circle in 9th-century Baghdad; Avicenna's notes on the text survive and he may have doubted its authenticity; Peter Adamson, The Arabic Plotinus: A Philosophical Study of the 'Theology of Aristotle' (Duckworth, 2002): the first book-length study; the translation 'the single most important conduit by which Neoplatonism reached the Islamic world'; the paraphrase's creative liberties (new paragraphs, Plotinus harmonized with Aristotle, adapted for Islamic/Christian theological context)
Falsifiability: For the CORROBORATED half: a manuscript trail showing pre-9th-century Arabic circulation or a Greek exemplar contradicting the paraphrase relationship. For the DEBUNKED half: the attribution is closed - no serious scholar defends Aristotelian authorship; only new evidence of an actual lost Aristotelian Theologia could reopen it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0830-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.36,33.31,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>830</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Varangian (Scandinavian) contribution to the formation of the Rus' polity</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge on a Scandinavian founding core: a contemporary 839 Frankish identification of the Rhos as Swedes, the 944/945 treaty's overwhelmingly Norse name-list (primary text preserved in the Chronicle), a 10th-century Byzantine emperor recording Norse river-names, and 850s–860s Scandinavian strata at Gorodishche/Ladoga.
Layer: real · Place: Staraya Ladoga and the Volkhov–Dnieper river routes (region: NW Russia / Ukraine)
Dates: 0839 → 0971 (confidence: medium)

The Rus' — the people whose name Russia, Ukraine and Belarus all trace their statehood to — began as a polity whose founding elite and early identity were Scandinavian (Norse/Varangian), arriving via the Baltic–Volga–Dnieper river arteries in the 9th century, before assimilating into the East Slavic majority.

Earliest attestation: Annales Bertiniani, s.a. 839 — the Frankish court at Ingelheim identifies envoys of the Rhos' king (chacanus) as Sueones (Swedes).
Sources: Annales Bertiniani, s.a. 839 (Latin text, e.g. Waitz MGH edition) — the Rhos identified as Sueones; Rus'-Byzantine Treaty 944/945 as preserved in the Primary Chronicle — 76 plenipotentiaries, overwhelming majority Old Norse names (Igor = Ingvar, Olga = Helga, Akun = Hákon, Uleb = Óleifr; only 3 Slavic names in the ruling family: Svjatoslav, Volodislav, Predslava) — via Melnikova 2003 and Feldbrugge 2008; Constantine VII Porphyrogennetos, De Administrando Imperio, ch. 9 — the Dnieper rapids named in both Slavic (Slavonic) and Rhos' (Norse) languages; Rurikovo Gorodishche (Novgorod) / Staraya Ladoga archaeology — Scandinavian settlement strata of the 850s–860s (cited in the CORE historiography review via Noonan); V. Thomsen, The Relations between Ancient Russia and Scandinavia (1877) — Finnic Ruotsi etymology for the name Rus'; J. Shepard &amp; S. Franklin, The Emergence of Rus 750–1200 (1996) — modern synthesis
Falsifiability: A securely dated 9th-century East Slavic (not Norse) documentary source showing a Slavic-led Rus' polity before the Scandinavian strata — e.g. a Slavic name-list replacing the 944 Norse majority — would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0839-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.2987,59.9997,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>839</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Al-Kindi's frequency analysis (c. 850 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript survives, is multiply edited and translated, and contains al-Kindi's own statistical study of Arabic letter frequencies; the 'first documented' framing is explicitly scoped — a lost earlier text would move the grade, not destroy it.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad, Abbasid Caliphate
Dates: 840 → 860 (confidence: high)

Abu Yusuf al-Kindi's treatise Risalah fi Istikhraj al-Mu'amma ('On Deciphering Cryptographic Messages'), written c. 850 CE in Baghdad, is the oldest surviving work describing frequency analysis — the statistical method of breaking substitution ciphers by counting symbol frequencies and matching them against known letter frequencies of the language. It is the first documented systematic method of cryptanalysis: the first time anyone wrote down that a cipher dissolves into arithmetic.

Earliest attestation: The treatise itself (manuscript tradition; edited and published in Arabic by Mrayati et al., 1987, in nine volumes; English translations circulate). Al-Kindi (801-873) is multiply attested as author of ~290 works.
Sources: Mrayati et al. (eds.), Arabic Origins of Cryptology vol. 1 — analytical study of the edited al-Kindi manuscript: statistical study of Arabic letter frequencies, originality of contributions; IJCSIS Vol. 15 No. 1 (2017): survey of medieval Arabic cryptologists — al-Kindi's Risalah fi Istikhraj al-Mu'amma, ca 850 AD, 'the oldest existent book on cryptology'; frequency analysis; al-Khalil's lost Kitab al-Mu'amma noted
Falsifiability: The discovery and authentication of an older surviving cryptanalysis text describing frequency analysis; or proof that the attributed manuscript is a later fabrication.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.3661,33.3152,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The False Decretals — the forgery's transmission machine</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The collection's existence, dating, and contents are multiply attested; its role as the Donation's first surviving witness and transmission path into the Decretum is stated by the 1911 Britannica and the Catholic Encyclopedia 1913; held at CORROBORATED on the motive questions (Roman import vs local composition).
Layer: real · Place: Reims, Francia (Pseudo-Isidorian collection's working milieu)
Dates: 847 → 852 (confidence: medium)

The Pseudo-Isidorian Decretals (the False Decretals), a 9th-century Frankish collection (c. 847-852) of some 100 forged decretals and synodal acts, contains the first surviving witness of the Donation of Constantine; two centuries later the Donation entered Gratian's Decretum (12th century, via a pupil) and thus became part of canon law — the transmission machine that carried the forgery into law.

Earliest attestation: The Pseudo-Isidorian collection itself, c. 847-852: first surviving textual witness of the Donation.
Sources: 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Donation of Constantine': included in the 9th-century False Decretals; two centuries later incorporated in the Decretum by a pupil of Gratian; 'enrolled among the decrees of the canon law' in Gibbon's day; Catholic Encyclopedia (1913), 'Donation of Constantine': the Donation's introduction and conclusion imitate authentic imperial-period writings, while other formulæ belong to other periods — the source-analysis work (Grauert and others) on the forger's materials
Falsifiability: An earlier surviving manuscript witness of the Donation (pre-847) would push the transmission date back; none has been found.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.0317,49.2583,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Al-Qarawiyyin of Fez: the 859 foundation and the 'oldest university' dispute</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 859 foundation, the centuries of continuous operation as mosque-madrasa, and the 1963 university-status reorganization are independently documented; UNESCO and Guinness World Records cite it as the oldest university or oldest continually operating higher learning institution. Held below VERIFIED on the live definitional dispute over 'university.'
Layer: real · Place: Fez (Fès), Morocco — the al-Qarawiyyin complex
Dates: 857 → 1965 (confidence: medium)

The University of al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco — founded as a mosque by Fatima al-Fihri in 857-859, later a madrasa, incorporated into Morocco's modern state university system in 1963 — is the oldest existing, continually operating degree-awarding higher learning institution in the world.

Earliest attestation: The foundation by Fatima al-Fihri in 857-859 (traditional); the 1963-1965 Moroccan state reorganization as a modern university (documented).
Sources: Guinness World Records — citation of al-Qarawiyyin as the oldest higher learning institution / oldest university; UNESCO recognition of the al-Qarawiyyin complex; Moroccan state university system incorporation 1963; official renaming 1965
Falsifiability: A documented break in the institution's continuous operation, or evidence that the 1963 reorganization created an entirely new institution with no continuity to the mosque-madrasa</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0857-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.2637,34.0615,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>857</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'invitation of the Varangians' in 862 (the Chronicle's Rurik narrative)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-source, 12th-century narrative written ~250 years after the claimed event, shaped as an invitation legend with worldwide folklore parallels; the ethnographic detail (named tribes, the tribute-expulsion prelude) is plausible but unattested anywhere outside the Chronicle.
Layer: real · Place: Novgorod (the Chronicle's settlement of Rurik)
Dates: 0862 → 0862 (confidence: low)

In the year 862, the East Slavic and Finnic tribes of the northwest (Slovenes, Krivichi, Chud, Ves', Merya) — after expelling their Varangian tribute-takers and falling into internecine strife — sent envoys across the sea inviting the Varangians to rule over them; three brothers came, Rurik, Sineus and Truvor, and Rurik settled at Novgorod, Sineus at Beloozero and Truvor at Izborsk.

Earliest attestation: The Russian Primary Chronicle (Povest' vremennykh let), compiled at the Kiev Caves Monastery c. 1113; earliest surviving manuscript copy (Laurentian Codex) 1377.
Sources: Russian Primary Chronicle, Laurentian Text, s.a. 859–862 (Cross &amp; Sherbowitz-Wetzor trans.) — the tribute, the expulsion, the invitation, the three brothers; Boris Rybakov — the invitation as late folkloric insertion; Cross &amp; Sherbowitz-Wetzor on the controversy volume; John H. Lind (2006) — the PVL compiler's ethnographic problem; N1L preserving an older and sometimes contradicting text; The Normanist-theory historiography review (CORE) — 'the whole issue of the ethnic origin of Rus' is not essential'; Pritsak and Noonan on the multi-ethnic trade union
Falsifiability: A 9th–10th-century source (Byzantine, Arabic, or Latin) describing a Slavic-initiated invitation to Varangian princes would raise the grade; the absence of any such attestation keeps it here.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0862-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.2754,58.5219,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>862</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Diamond Sutra of 868 CE — the oldest dated printed book</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The object exists, is dated by its own printed colophon to a precise day, was produced by a mature woodblock technique, and is multiply documented from discovery (1900) to purchase (1907) to the present British Library holding.
Layer: real · Place: Mogao Caves, Dunhuang (Cave 17, the Library Cave)
Dates: 868 → 868 (confidence: high)

A woodblock-printed scroll of the Diamond Sutra, printed in China in 868 CE, is the oldest complete printed book in the world that carries a date — printed nearly six centuries before Gutenberg's Bible.

Earliest attestation: The colophon printed on the scroll itself (May 868 CE)
Sources: The Diamond Sutra, woodblock print scroll, Or.8210/P.2, British Library (Stein collection) — colophon: 'Reverently made for universal free distribution by Wang Jie on behalf of his two parents'; Smithsonian Magazine, 'Five Things to Know About the Diamond Sutra' (May 11, 2016) — 'acknowledged as the oldest dated printed book in existence'; UNESCO World Heritage documentation, Mogao Caves — cave 17 (the Library Cave) discovered 1900 by the caretaker Wang Yuanlu, sealed c. 1000 CE; Stein's 1907 acquisition of the scroll for the British Museum/Library
Falsifiability: An independently authenticated printed book bearing an earlier printed date would take the title; radiocarbon/ink analysis showing the scroll is a modern forgery would collapse the record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>94.81,40.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bimaristan Hospitals (872-1284 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent witness-types converge: surviving architecture (al-Nuri), legal endowment deeds with the open-access clauses, and contemporary eyewitness accounts (Ibn Jubayr 1184); the Mansuri's million-dirham endowment and ~15th-century operation are documented.
Layer: real · Place: Damascus (al-Nuri bimaristan, survives); al-Mansuri in Cairo, city precision
Dates: 872 → 1284 (confidence: high)

The bimaristan: the medieval Islamic hospital as an institution — endowed by waqf (charitable trust), free to rich and poor, male and female, Muslim and non-Muslim, with separate wards (surgery, medicine, eye, mental), pharmacies, lecture halls, running water, and paid discharge stipends. Founded first at al-Fustat (c. 872, Ibn Tulun), institutionalized at the Nuri hospital of Damascus (c. 1154, Nur al-Din Zanki), and monumentalized at the Mansuri hospital of Cairo (completed 1284, al-Mansur Qalawun).

Earliest attestation: Waqf endowment deeds (notably Qalawun's 1284 dedication: 'for the soldier and the prince, the large and the small, the free and the slave, for men and women'); Ibn Jubayr's 1184 eyewitness account of the Nuri hospital's registers and physician rounds
Sources: Waqf deed of the al-Mansuri hospital (1284): open-access dedication clause; four entrances with fountains; separate wards; dispensary; teaching room; Ibn Jubayr (1184): eyewitness account — the Nuri hospital's patient registers, morning physician rounds, ~15 dinar daily budget; al-Nuri bimaristan, Damascus (c. 1154): surviving building; major hospital into the 15th century
Falsifiability: The institution stands on surviving buildings (the Nuri bimaristan in Damascus), endowment law, and independent travelers' accounts; it would collapse only if the waqf deeds proved forgeries — they have not been challenged</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0872-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>36.2765,33.5138,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>872</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alfred's candle clock</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-source attestation (Asser, c. 893, panegyric) with no independent corroboration of the horn lantern or the invention claim; the 'invented the candle clock' reading goes beyond what Asser describes.
Layer: real · Place: Wessex (Alfred's court)
Dates: 893 → 893 (confidence: medium)

Alfred the Great measured his devotional hours with a candle marked in divisions, burning inside an ox-horn lantern — the origin of the 'Alfred invented the candle clock' telling.

Earliest attestation: Asser, Life of King Alfred, c. 893 — sole source; no independent corroboration.
Sources: Asser, Life of King Alfred (J.A. Giles translation, via orthodoxengland.org.uk) — Alfred resolved to devote half his service to God, 'measured accurately by a candle, which burned inside an ox-horn lantern'; answers.com compilation — 'King Alfred the Great. Source: Bishop Asser, Life of King Alfred' as the attribution for the candle-clock telling
Falsifiability: A second contemporary source describing Alfred's horn lantern, or textual evidence the passage is an interpolation.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.31,51.06,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The medieval Inquisition's actual targets — and its own law on witches</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested from independent lines: the Canon Episcopi's text is a primary canon-law source; the medieval inquisition's heretic-targeting is the consensus of specialist historiography; the 1484/1486 turn is dated by primary documents (the bull, the Malleus). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the full targets list (regional variation) — the skepticism doctrine itself is VERIFIED-grade.
Layer: real · Place: Languedoc, southern France (Cathar inquisition's main theater)
Dates: 906 → 1484 (confidence: high)

The medieval Inquisition (established 1231 under Gregory IX) was a court directed against heretics — principally Cathars (Albigensians) and Waldensians — and its own law books treated witchcraft skeptically: Canon Episcopi (c. 906, incorporated into Gratian's Decretum c. 1140) declared that belief in night-flight witchcraft was a demonic delusion, not a crime to prosecute.

Earliest attestation: Canon Episcopi (c. 906, in Regino of Prum's De ecclesiasticis disciplinis), carried into Gratian's Decretum, C.26 q.5: those who believe in the night flight with Diana are infidels misled by the devil.
Sources: Canon Episcopi (c. 906), in Gratian's Decretum C.26 q.5 — belief in night flight with Diana declared a demonic delusion; Gregory IX, Ille humani generis (1231): the papal inquisition's founding commission against heresy (Cathars, Waldensians); Brian P. Levack, The Witch-Hunt in Early Modern Europe: the witch-hunt era 1450-1750, peak 1560-1630, largely secular/Protestant courts
Falsifiability: A 13th- or 14th-century inquisitorial manual with witchcraft as a standard prosecution category, or court registers showing systematic medieval witch trials, would force revision; none exist.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0906-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>1.4442,43.6047,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>906</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Kilwa Kisiwani — the Swahili coast's gold-port city-state</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — An eyewitness account by a demanding traveler, the standing coral-stone architecture, the mint's own coin evidence, and systematic excavations converge — held at CORROBORATED on the foundation date and exact sultanate extents.
Layer: real · Place: Kilwa Kisiwani, Lindi Region, Tanzania
Dates: 0950 → 1505 (confidence: medium)

Kilwa Kisiwani, on an island off the Tanzanian coast, was a major Swahili city-state from the 11th–15th centuries, built on customs revenue from the gold trade running from the Zimbabwe plateau via Sofala — minting its own gold coins, building in coral stone, and ranked by Ibn Battuta in 1331 as one of the most beautiful cities he had seen.

Earliest attestation: The Kilwa Chronicle (16th-c. histories) and the coins themselves; contemporary eyewitness: Ibn Battuta's Rihla, 1331 visit; excavations by Kirkman and Chittick (British Institute in Eastern Africa, 1950s–60s)
Sources: Ibn Battuta, Rihla (1331 visit): 'one of the most beautiful and well-constructed towns in the world' — the dressed stone, the mosque already ~400 years old, the palace, the piety of the people; The Great Mosque: earliest section 1131–1170 (coral limestone blocks, nine hexagonal tree-trunk columns); the 14th-c. domed extension under al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman; Kilwa-minted gold coins from the 11th c. onward — some found as far as Great Zimbabwe and the Maldives: the customs economy's own paperwork; Chittick's excavations: Husuni Kubwa (the palace, unrivaled in East African architectural sophistication) and Husuni Ndogo (the market) as the 14th-c. building program; Portuguese accounts: Cabral 1500 (the 100-room palace of coral stone); the 1502–1505 sack that broke the coast's independent trade era
Falsifiability: Excavation showing no pre-Portuguese stone architecture, or coin hoards contradicting the gold-trade chronology, would collapse the commercial reading</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0950-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.5117,-8.9578,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>950</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Al-Zahrawi's Kitab al-Tasrif (c. 1000 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested: 50+ surviving Arabic manuscripts, five Latin translations, 500 years as the surgical manual of Salerno and Montpellier, and Guy de Chauliac's Chirurgia Magna (1363) cites al-Zahrawi over 200 times — an independent contemporary witness to his authority.
Layer: real · Place: Cordoba, Umayyad al-Andalus
Dates: 1000 → 1000 (confidence: high)

Abu al-Qasim al-Zahrawi (936-c.1013, Cordoba) compiled Kitab al-Tasrif (c. 1000 CE), a 30-treatise medical encyclopedia whose surgical treatise was the first work in Arabic to describe and illustrate surgical instruments — about 200 of them, many of his own design: cauterization irons, lithotrites for bladder stones, obstetrical forceps, catgut absorbable sutures, arterial ligation instruments.

Earliest attestation: Kitab al-Tasrif manuscripts, 11th century onward; the surgical treatise translated into Latin by Gerard of Cremona (d. 1187)
Sources: Campbell, D. (1974) + European Scientific Journal special issue: the Tasrif's surgical treatise, ~200 illustrated instruments, earliest known teaching illustrations; Gerard of Cremona's 12th-century Latin translation; used five centuries at Salerno, Montpellier, and other European schools; Guy de Chauliac, Chirurgia Magna (1363): cites al-Zahrawi 200+ times — the 'Restorer of European Surgery' leaning on the Cordoban's instruments
Falsifiability: Would be weakened if the instruments proved to be copyist additions (they appear consistently across the manuscript tradition); the 200-instrument figure would shrink if counts are shown to double-count variants</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1000-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-4.7794,37.8882,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Norse settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows, c. 1000 CE</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Independent lines converge: Norse-style turf buildings and artifacts (iron rivets, jasper fire-strikers, bronze ringed pin, soapstone spindle whorl, iron-working slag) excavated from sealed contexts; metal-cut wood dated to the exact year 1021 CE via the global 992-993 CE solar-storm 14C marker; butternuts (Juglans cinerea, not native north of New Brunswick) proving travel farther south; the saga tradition independently describes the same voyages.
Layer: real · Place: L'Anse aux Meadows National Historic Site, Great Northern Peninsula, Newfoundland
Dates: 1000 → 1021 (confidence: high)

Norse seafarers established a short-lived settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows, on Newfoundland's Great Northern Peninsula, around the year 1000 CE — a European presence in North America roughly five centuries before Columbus.

Earliest attestation: Eiríks saga rauða and Grænlendinga saga (13th-14th c. manuscripts preserving older oral tradition); archaeological confirmation from 1960.
Sources: Anne Stine Ingstad, excavations 1961-1968 (site identified with Helge Ingstad, 1960); Kuitems, M. et al. (2021), 'Evidence for European presence in the Americas in AD 1021', Nature 601; Eiríks saga rauða; Grænlendinga saga (Vinland sagas); UNESCO World Heritage inscription, 1978
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the excavated Norse-style artifacts and metal-cut wood are misattributed (e.g., a non-Norse iron-working tradition in pre-contact Newfoundland) would collapse the identification; no such evidence exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1000-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-55.53095,51.5986,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Maria Argyropoulina's golden fork at Venice (1004) and Peter Damian's condemnation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED as the earliest recorded table-fork controversy: the episode is attested by Damian's letters and carried across independent secondary sources. The divine-punishment reading is Damian's moralizing and is graded as attestation of belief, not as metaphysics (the attestation rule).
Layer: real · Place: Venice, Republic of Venice (the Orseolo marriage)
Dates: 1004 → 1007 (confidence: medium)

In 1004, the Byzantine noblewoman Maria Argyropoulina, marrying into Venice's ruling Orseolo family, refused to touch her food with her fingers and instead ate with a small golden two-pronged fork, scandalizing Venice. The theologian St. Peter Damian (1007-1072) recorded the episode in his letters and condemned her 'artificial metal forks' as decadent luxury, interpreting her early death from plague as divine punishment for that vanity.

Earliest attestation: St. Peter Damian's collected letters, late 11th century (secondary account, decades after the event)
Sources: St. Peter Damian, letters (collected); cited in National Geographic History Jan/Feb 2022, p. 17; PopSci: 'The thousand-year story of how the fork crossed Europe, and onto your plate today' (2025); Wikipedia: 'Fork' (Peter Damian as 'excessive delicacy'; Maria Argyropoulina 985-1007, sister of Emperor Romanos III Argyros); 925-1000.com forum: forks &amp; spoons history (Maria Agyropoulaina 1004/5, small gold fork at Venice)
Falsifiability: A Venetian record contradicting the Orseolo-Argyropoulina marriage, or proof that Damian's letters are misdated or mistranscribed on this point</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1004-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.3355,45.4408,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1004</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tale of Genji</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent near-contemporary attestations converge: the author's own diary (c. 1008, internal-evidence dating) and a second diarist's acquisition record (1021). The work survives complete; the superlative rests on the dated artifact, not on a claim.
Layer: real · Place: Ishiyamadera temple, Otsu (traditional composition site per legend) / Heian-kyo (Kyoto), Japan — the legend puts the first inspiration at Ishiyamadera; the court life it describes is Heian-kyo
Dates: 1008 → 1021 (confidence: high)

The Tale of Genji (Genji Monogatari) — 54 chapters of sustained prose fiction with interior characterization, court intrigue, and psychological realism — was composed by the Heian court lady Murasaki Shikibu (sobriquet; c. 973 – c. 1014/1031) c. 1008–1021, making it the earliest surviving work of extended prose fiction meeting the criteria most literary historians use to define the novel: it predates Cervantes's Don Quixote (1605) by roughly six centuries.

Earliest attestation: Murasaki Shikibu Nikki (diary, entries dated c. 1008–1010 by internal evidence): the author refers to the tale and to her 'Murasaki' nickname, drawn from the heroine, being already in use — the tale was circulating at court by 1008. Second independent witness: the Sarashina Nikki (c. 1021): the author records her joy at acquiring a complete copy.
Sources: Murasaki Shikibu Nikki (c. 1008–1010): diary entries — the tale referenced, the 'Murasaki' nickname in use, the Emperor's read-aloud of the opening chapter; Sarashina Nikki (c. 1021): the author's diary entry recording acquisition of a complete copy ('over 50 chapters,' a character from the work's end); Arthur Waley (trans.), The Tale of Genji, Project Gutenberg ed. (via sites.google.com World Literature I textbook: author c. 973–1014, date c. 11th century, court context); HISTORY.com, 'What Is the World's Oldest Novel?' — Genji 'widely regarded as the world's first novel' (54 chapters, Heian setting, psychological realism as the defining criterion)
Falsifiability: A securely dated earlier work with sustained interior characterization in prose; or proof the 1008 diary entries are misdated wholesale. The authorship-share dispute could move the 'single author' detail; it cannot move the work's date or existence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1008-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>135.86,34.98,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1008</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bi Sheng's movable type (1041–1048 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The account is detailed, technical, and internally consistent, written by a reliable near-contemporary who knew the inventor's milieu — but it is single-tradition literary attestation: no Bi Sheng type piece survives, and no independent witness describes the method.
Layer: real · Place: Yingshan County, Hubei (Bi Sheng's home; invention in the Song capital region)
Dates: 1041 → 1048 (confidence: medium)

Bi Sheng, a commoner of the Northern Song dynasty, invented movable type printing using fired-clay characters, more than four centuries before Gutenberg — described in detail by his contemporary Shen Kuo in the Dream Pool Essays (~1088).

Earliest attestation: Shen Kuo, Mengxi Bitan (Dream Pool Essays), c. 1088 — the only surviving account of Bi Sheng's method
Sources: Shen Kuo, Dream Pool Essays (Mengxi Bitan), c. 1088 — full technical description: clay characters 'as thin as the edge of a coin', baked hard, set in an iron frame with pine-resin/wax/paper-ash paste, two alternating formes; Bi Sheng tomb, Yingshan County, Hubei (discovered 1990) — tombstone records death 1051, four sons, three grandsons; Thomas Carter, The Invention of Printing in China and Its Spread Westward (1955) — English transmission of the Shen Kuo passage
Falsifiability: A dated earlier movable-type artifact would revise priority; evidence that Shen Kuo fabricated the account would collapse the record to SPECULATION</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1041-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>113.8,31.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1041</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The East–West Schism of 1054: the excommunications that did not, by themselves, split Christianity</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The July 1054 excommunications are CORROBORATED as documented events (surviving texts), but the claim under review — that Christianity 'split in 1054' as a single event — is the conventional simplification: the historiographical consensus (Ware and the subsequent literature) holds the schism was a centuries-long process, ordinary Christians largely unaware, contemporary chroniclers unimpressed, and 1204 (the sack of Constantinople) the likelier point of no return. The event is documented; the single-day-division reading stays CLAIMED.
Layer: real · Place: Hagia Sophia, Constantinople (Istanbul)
Dates: 1054 → 1054 (confidence: high)

The Christian Church divided into two churches — Roman Catholic (West) and Eastern Orthodox (East) — in a single event in 1054, when Cardinal Humbert laid a bull of excommunication on the altar of Hagia Sophia and Patriarch Michael Cerularius was excommunicated in return.

Earliest attestation: The bull of excommunication of 16 July 1054 (text survives); the synodal act of 20 July 1054 excommunicating the legates (text survives)
Sources: The bull of excommunication, 16 July 1054 (text preserved; laid on the altar of Hagia Sophia by Cardinal Humbert and two legates); Synodal act of Constantinople, 20 July 1054 (counter-excommunication of the legates); Timothy Ware, 'The Great Schism' (Orthodox Church of the Mother of God histories) — 'It is this incident which has conventionally been taken to mark the beginning of the great schism... But the schism, as historians now generally recognize, is not really an event whose beginning can be exactly dated. It was something that came about gradually'; Wikipedia, East-West Schism (survey of the modern literature): 'There was no single event that marked the breakdown. Rather, the two churches slid into and out of schism over a period of several centuries, punctuated with temporary reconciliations'; 'Even after 1054 friendly relations between East and West continued' (quoting Ware); contemporary chroniclers did not consider the event significant; Father Bill's Orthodox Blog, '55. Orthodoxy and Other Faiths: Roman Catholicism, Part Two — The Great Schism' (2018): Humbert did not excommunicate the other Eastern patriarchs; the Pope was dead at the time, so technically the anathema did not count; the Photian Schism precedent; communion continued in parts of the Middle East and Aegean after 1054; Seed records: evt-abrahamic-tree-001 (the tree as attestation map, CORROBORATED — internal splits explicitly not yet mapped, now mapped in part here)
Falsifiability: The record would be upgraded toward CORROBORATED if evidence showed communion between Rome and Constantinople's churches ceased abruptly and permanently in the years immediately after 1054; it would move toward DEBUNKED if the July 1054 bull itself proved to be a later forgery</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1054-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1054</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Maine penny (Norse coin in a Maine midden)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The coin is authenticated Norse (CORROBORATED as object); the 1957 find-context is contested (ANS: 'should probably be considered a hoax'; Carpenter: 'Not proven') — the contact reading rests on the context, not the coin.
Layer: real · Place: Goddard site, Naskeag Point, Penobscot Bay, Brooklin, Maine
Dates: 1065 → 1080 (confidence: high)

The Maine penny — a Norwegian silver coin of Olaf Kyrre's reign (1067–1093) — was found in 1957 in the pre-Columbian Goddard-site midden at Naskeag Point, Maine: genuine Norse object in a New England context.

Earliest attestation: Kolbjørn Skaare (University of Oslo) 1978 identification: minted 1065–1080, in circulation through the 12th–13th centuries — the Goddard site dates 1180–1235, inside the circulation window
Sources: Kolbjørn Skaare (University of Oslo), 1978: the coin is a Norwegian silver penny minted 1065–1080, reign of Olaf Kyrre (1067–1093); in circulation through the 12th–13th centuries; Maine State Museum: the coin donated 1974 with ~30,000 Goddard-site artifacts (Guy Mellgren's 18 August 1957 collection); described as 'the only pre-Columbian Norse artifact generally regarded as genuine found within the United States'; American Numismatic Society: 'There is no reliable confirmation on the documentation of the Goddard coin, and much circumstantial evidence suggests that someone was deliberately trying to manipulate or obfuscate the situation ... should probably be considered a hoax'; Edmund Carpenter / Rock Foundation extensive analysis: conclusion 'Not proven'
Falsifiability: A second Norse artifact in secure context at the Goddard site — or documented proof of the 1957 find</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1065-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-68.57,44.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1065</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bayeux comet — the earliest known European depiction of Halley's Comet</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent witnesses converge: the stitched panel itself (the comet above the marveling men), the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's 1066 comet over England, and the Chinese annals' two-month observation. NASA: the Bayeux panel 'depicts a comet that has been identified as the first illustration of Halley's Comet.' DLR calls it 'likely the oldest depiction of Halley's Comet.' Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: 'earliest known' is survival-dependent (a destroyed earlier image would move the grade), and the Zuqnin Chronicle (760) already anchors the earliest known depiction anywhere — non-European. The European record opens with this stitching.
Layer: real · Place: Bayeux Museum, Normandy, France (the panel); the apparition observed over England, April 1066
Dates: 1066 → 1066 (confidence: high)

The Bayeux embroidery's comet panel (scenes 32, under the Latin caption ISTI MIRANT STELLA — 'these men marvel at the star') depicts the April 1066 apparition of what is now called Halley's Comet — and is the earliest known European pictorial depiction of the comet.

Earliest attestation: Bayeux embroidery panel, scenes 32 (stitched c. 1070) — the stitched comet above the marveling men
Sources: NASA — '955 Years Ago: Halley's Comet and the Battle of Hastings' ('depicts a comet that has been identified as the first illustration of Halley's Comet'; April 1066 over England; Chinese two-month observation); DLR (German Aerospace Center) — 'The Bayeux Tapestry — oldest depiction of Halley's Comet' ('likely the oldest depiction of Halley's Comet'); The Art Newspaper (2026) — 'The tapestry features the first European depiction of Halley's Comet'; earlier non-European depiction in the Chronicle of Zuqnin (760); next image Giotto 1305; Gizmodo (Public Domain Review, 'Flowers of the Sky') — 'A scene from the Bayeux Tapestry shows men staring at Halley's Comet (c1066), and is the first known picture of the comet'; Heritage Daily (2026, arXiv Portegies Zwart &amp; Lewis) — 'appears on the Bayeux Tapestry, the oldest known visual representation of the comet'
Falsifiability: An earlier verified European depiction of the 1066 (or any) Halley apparition — none is known; or a documentary proof that the stitched panel depicts a different celestial event (a meteor, a supernova) — no rival identification has the orbit math</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1066-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.7009,49.2749,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1066</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bayeux Tapestry — the artifact</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The object exists, is measurable, and is stylistically anchored: ~70 m of linen, wool-embroidered in stem stitch and laid-and-couched work, depicting the 1066 conquest in ~58–75 episodes with Latin tituli. The 1476 cathedral inventory anchors its presence; the English manufacture is corroborated by the manuscript tradition, the tituli spellings, and the opus-anglicanum technique (NYRB/Mallinson 2008: 'designed in England and embroidered in England by English craftswomen').
Layer: real · Place: Musée de la Tapisserie de Bayeux, Normandy, France (long-term home; the work is named for the city it resides in, not where it was made)
Dates: 1066 → 1082 (confidence: high)

The Bayeux 'Tapestry' (c. 1070) is a ~70-meter embroidered linen cloth depicting the events of 1066 — the Norman Conquest of England — made in England to a Norman brief (traditionally attributed to Bishop Odo's commission, Canterbury workshop) as a piece of Norman propaganda.

Earliest attestation: The embroidery itself (Bayeux Museum / Musée de la Tapisserie de Bayeux); first documentary mention 1476 (Bayeux Cathedral inventory)
Sources: Smarthistory — The Bayeux Tapestry ('c. 1070, embroidered wool on linen'; 'Although it is called the Bayeux Tapestry... the imagery and inscriptions are embroidered using wool yarn sewed onto linen cloth'); History.com — 'What Makes the Bayeux Tapestry a Medieval Wonder?' (embroidered, not woven on a loom; Canterbury origin; Old English lettering; Norman-propaganda reading); Columbia Encyclopedia (FactMonster) — 'This so-called tapestry is in fact an embroidery'; 230 ft × 20 in (70 m × 51 cm); 'embroidered in worsteds of eight colors in couching and stem stitch'; NYRB letters (Mallinson, 2008) — 'It was, of course, made to a Norman brief, but was designed in England and embroidered in England by English craftswomen'; Smithsonian Magazine (AP/Bayeux Museum, 2021) — 'Though the work is widely known as a tapestry, it technically counts as a work of embroidery'; Bayeux Museum on the propaganda function
Falsifiability: A documented provenance placing the commission elsewhere than the Norman-English 1066–1082 window (e.g., a verifiable later workshop signature in the stitching) — none exists; the object, the 1476 inventory, and the manuscript-stylistic evidence all converge</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1066-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.7009,49.2749,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1066</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Domesday Book (1086) — the first great count</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscripts exist, are physically dated to 1086, and their contents — ~13,000 settlements, landholders, ploughs, livestock, and population-derived values — are internally consistent with the fiscal purpose recorded in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle's 1085 entry ('he sent his men all over England... not one ox, nor one cow, nor one pig was left out'). The oldest and largest census-like record of the Middle Ages.
Layer: real · Place: The National Archives, Kew, England (holding institution); survey coverage: England
Dates: 1085 → 1086 (confidence: high)

The Domesday Book (1086) is a real, surviving, comprehensive survey of landholding in England — the largest single record of its kind from the Middle Ages, and the foundation of medieval English demography.

Earliest attestation: The two manuscript volumes themselves (Great Domesday and Little Domesday), held by The National Archives, Kew.
Sources: Domesday Book, Great and Little Domesday volumes (1086), The National Archives, Kew — the manuscripts themselves; Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, entry for 1085 (Gloucester): the Christmas court order for the survey — 'so very narrowly, that there was no single hide nor yard of land, nor indeed one ox nor one cow nor one pig which was left out'; The National Archives, Domesday Book research guides: ~13,000 places recorded; the survey's fiscal-administrative purpose and coverage limits
Falsifiability: Manuscript evidence that the volumes were compiled later as forgeries, or systematic internal inconsistency in the survey's fiscal logic, would downgrade this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1085-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.284,51.4747,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1085</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shen Kuo's Dream Pool Essays (~1088) — the first clearly-dated, named-author account of magnetic declination, four centuries before Europe wrote it down</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The Mengxi Bitan is an extant, securely dated text by the named author Shen Kuo (1031-1095), and the passage is unambiguous: needles rubbed with lodestone 'are always displaced slightly east rather than pointing due south' (Needham's translation, Science and Civilisation in China vol. IV pt 1, pp. 249-250; the Education About Asia translation renders it 'the needle often inclines to the southeast direction, not pointing to due south'). Shen Kuo adds the silk-fibre suspension method and notes some needles point north — 'No one can explain the principles of these things.' The core claim (Shen Kuo wrote declination down in 1088) is textually proven. Fenced: 'first in the world' is NOT asserted absolutely — the ~880 anonymous geomantic text is a candidate earlier account, so Shen Kuo is the first clearly-dated named author, not the proven first documenter. Europe's lag is measured: Columbus's 18 September 1492 journal note and Hartmann's 1510 determination (reported 1544) are documented, ~4-4.5 centuries later.
Layer: real · Place: Dream Torrent Garden, Runzhou (modern Zhenjiang), Song China — where Shen Kuo compiled the essays
Dates: 1086-01-01 → 1088-12-31 (confidence: high)

Shen Kuo (1031-1095), in his Mengxi Bitan (Dream Pool Essays, compiled c. 1086-1088), wrote the first clearly-dated, named-author account of magnetic declination: the magnetized needle 'is always displaced slightly east rather than pointing due south.' Europe did not document the phenomenon until roughly four centuries later.

Earliest attestation: Shen Kuo's own text, Mengxi Bitan (Dream Pool Essays), extant and securely attributed.
Sources: Wikipedia 'Shen Kuo': Dream Pool Essays (Mengxi Bitan, compiled ~1086-1088) — 'made the first known explicit reference to the magnetic compass-needle and the concept of true north'; needles magnetized by rubbing with lodestone, best hung by a single silk fibre; 'always displaced slightly east rather than pointing due south' — the first known explicit reference to magnetic declination; Needham, Science and Civilisation in China, vol. IV pt 1, pp. 249-250 (quoted via Wikipedia Talk:Shen Kuo): full translation of the declination passage; Shen Kuo's Meng Chhi Pi Than (~1088) is 'our fixed point'; Education About Asia: Shen Kuo (1088) in translation — 'Diviners can make a needle point to the south by rubbing it with a magnetic stone. However, the needle often inclines to the southeast direction, not pointing to due south'; Wikipedia 'History of the compass': declination texts Kuan Shih Ti Li Chih Meng (~880) predating Shen Kuo — the anonymous-earlier fence; Wikipedia 'History of geomagnetism': Georg Hartmann's March 4, 1544 letter to Duke Albrecht of Prussia (~1510 determination); Scientific American (Nov 20, 1869) — Columbus noticed September 18, 1492 that the compass 'pointed due north' where in Europe it 'pointed northeast'
Falsifiability: A clearly-dated, named-author text predating 1086 that documents declination explicitly would demote the 'first' element; the phenomenon-description element is textually secure and not falsifiable short of textual corruption evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1086-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>113.62,34.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1086</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Great Zimbabwe — the Shona stone city</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The standing architecture is multiply attested by independent excavations (1905, 1929–31, later surveys), stratigraphy, and radiocarbon; authorship is uncontested in archaeology — no credible rival attestation exists.
Layer: real · Place: Great Zimbabwe, Masvingo, Zimbabwe
Dates: 1100 → 1450 (confidence: medium)

Great Zimbabwe, on the Zimbabwe plateau, was a stone-built African city and capital of a gold-trading state, constructed in the 11th–15th centuries CE by the ancestors of the Shona/Karanga people — the largest ancient stone construction south of the Sahara.

Earliest attestation: Portuguese traders' accounts, 16th century (earlier European notice: Carl Mauch 1871, searching for the biblical Ophir); indigenous occupation of the site long before
Sources: David Randall-MacIver, Medieval Rhodesia (1905): excavated artifacts matched Shona/Karanga material culture in use around the site; 'unquestionably African in every detail'; Gertrude Caton-Thompson, The Zimbabwe Culture: Ruins and Reactions (1931): stratigraphic excavation; the city a medieval Shona-culture capital; UNESCO World Heritage listing 1986 — the standing dry-stone architecture (Great Enclosure walls up to ~11 m, mortarless) as the evidence of indigenous construction; Imported Persian, Arab, and Chinese ceramics and beads in sealed contexts — the Sofala/Kilwa gold road to the Indian Ocean (the trade the city taxed and lived on); Shona oral tradition and the Bantu etymology: 'zimbabwe' = 'houses of stone' / sacred houses
Falsifiability: Sealed-context artifacts or texts showing non-African construction technology, foreign-language inscriptions predating the Shona occupation, or radiocarbon placing the walls outside the medieval window</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.9331,-20.2731,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Medieval European public bathhouses</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary documentary sources (monastic rule, royal ordinances, guild statutes, chronicles, medical and household literature) across four regions, plus surviving bathhouse archaeology — the documentary lines converge.
Layer: real · Place: Paris (the 1268 étuviers guild statutes)
Dates: 1100 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

Medieval European cities had public bathhouses — licensed, guilded establishments operating across the 12th-16th centuries.

Earliest attestation: Rule of St. Benedict, Ch. 36 (c. 530) presumes a working monastic bath system; the first secular documentary line is Henry II's 1161 Southwark stew ordinances.
Sources: Rule of St. Benedict, Ch. 36 (c. 530) — bath use offered to the sick, rarely to the healthy; Henry II's 39 Ordinances Touching the Government of the Stewholders in Southwark (1161) — licensed bathhouses under the Bishop of Winchester; London 1417 ordinance — banned stews within the city walls, expressly allowing private bathhouses; Livre des métiers (Étienne Boileau, Paris, 1268) — guild statutes of the étuviers (professional bathhouse-keepers); Primary Chronicle / Tale of Bygone Years (c. 1113) — Apostle Andrew passage: Slavic wooden bathhouses heated 'to extreme heat' as routine practice; Tacuinum Sanitatis (Latin translation c. 1250) — bath practice in the medical literature; Le Ménagier de Paris (1393) — soap/herb-bath recipes for the household; Caliphal Baths of Córdoba (10th c., al-Hakam II) and El Bañuelo, Granada (11th c., used as a bathhouse into the 16th c.) — surviving Islamic hammams; Janna Coomans (Utrecht University) + Germanisches Nationalmuseum — German/Netherlands urban Badstuben with licensed 'baders' practicing basic medicine; Ellen Wurtzel (2023) — 13th-16th c. resurgence of urban bathhouses in Paris and northern France from medical treatises, trial records, archives
Falsifiability: Showing the documentary references are fabrications, or that the surviving structures (Córdoba, El Bañuelo) were not bathhouses</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Spices as medieval status: the pepper that meant money</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The elite recipe corpus documents heavy spicing as feast display on fresh food, and the food historians' economic analysis places imported spices firmly in the luxury register — multiple independent lines, same conclusion; held below VERIFIED pending a dedicated price-roll citation this run.
Layer: real · Place: Medieval Europe (the elite table)
Dates: 1100 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

In medieval Europe, imported spices functioned primarily as luxury goods consumed for social display — elite dining used expensive spices the way a modern household uses a luxury car: as a public statement of wealth.

Earliest attestation: The medieval cookbooks themselves (Forme of Cury c. 1390, Le Viandier, Le Ménagier de Paris 1393) — elite display cooking on the page; Paul Freedman, Out of the East (2008) as the modern synthesis.
Sources: Paul Freedman, Out of the East: Spices and the Medieval Imagination (Yale University Press, 2008) — spices as symbols of beauty, affluence, taste, and grace; the elite demand that drove commerce and conquest; Jack Turner, Spice: The History of a Temptation (Knopf, 2004) — 'for a handful of peppercorns, you could have someone killed': the wealth-power nexus of the spice economy; Forme of Cury (c. 1390, British Library Add MS 5016) — the royal cookbook's display sauces; spicing as court fashion; Le Ménagier de Paris (1393) — bourgeois household manual: spices as the mark of a well-appointed table
Falsifiability: Medieval price and recipe evidence showing imported spices priced within reach of common households and consumed as staples rather than as feast-day display</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bologna studium: Europe's first university corporation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge: the glossator tradition around Irnerius and the Four Doctors, the 1158 Authentica Habita as a dated imperial document addressed to scholars at Bologna, and the late-12th-century student universitates. Held below VERIFIED because the emergence was gradual, not a dated act.
Layer: real · Place: Bologna, Italy (the law schools; the Authentica issued at the Diet of Roncaglia)
Dates: 1110 → 1200 (confidence: medium)

In the 12th century Bologna developed the first European studium in the corporate sense: Irnerius's law school and the glossators, imperial protection of wandering scholars, and the student guilds (universitates/nations) that became the university.

Earliest attestation: The imperial constitution Authentica Habita, issued by Frederick I Barbarossa at the Diet of Roncaglia in November 1158, addressed to scholars — documented privilege of safe residence and judicial jurisdiction for wandering students and masters.
Sources: Authentica Habita (1158) — Pearl Kibre, Scholarly Privileges in the Middle Ages (Mediaeval Academy of America, 1962): the Privilegium scholasticum, 'the most famous and influential of its kind'; The glossator tradition: Irnerius and the Four Doctors of Bologna; The student universitates/nations of late-12th-century Bologna (the Natio Germanica Bononiae and others)
Falsifiability: Evidence that the 12th-century law school was a single later fabrication, or a pre-1158 document showing the studium had already vanished</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1110-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.3426,44.4949,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1110</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Angkor Wat — the temple and its continuous life</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The monument exists (measured 162.6 ha, largest religious complex in the world); contemporary inscriptions fix Suryavarman II's reign; an independent 13th-century Chinese eyewitness (Zhou Daguan, 1296–97) describes the functioning capital within living memory of construction; and its continuous religious use is observable into the present — Angkor Wat has remained an active Buddhist center into the modern era.
Layer: real · Place: Angkor Wat, Siem Reap, Cambodia
Dates: 1113 → 1150 (confidence: medium)

Angkor Wat — the world's largest religious complex (162.6 hectares, ~401.8 acres) — was built between c. 1113 and c. 1150 CE during the reign of the Khmer king Suryavarman II as a Hindu state temple dedicated to Vishnu (likely also his mausoleum), and has remained in continuous religious use ever since, converting to Theravada Buddhist use from the late 13th century onward into the present day.

Earliest attestation: Khmer inscriptions of the Angkorian period (Sanskrit and Old Khmer); the earliest surviving full description of the complex is Zhou Daguan's 'The Customs of Cambodia' (1296–97), a Chinese envoy's eyewitness account written within a century and a half of construction.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat; nationalgeographic.com — 'Angkor Wat, the world's biggest religious complex, is sacred to two faiths'; Zhou Daguan, 'The Customs of Cambodia' (Zhenla fengtu ji), 1296–97
Falsifiability: A foundation inscription or documented stratigraphic phase predating or postdating the 12th century, contradicting Suryavarman II's commission; proof the standing fabric is modern.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1113-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>103.867,13.4125,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1113</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gratian's Decretum: the medieval law books and what they do not contain</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The corpus exists and is multiply attested — hundreds of surviving manuscripts, Friedberg's edition, the 1582 printed standard — and its silence on the wedding-night right is complete across the full canon, the glosses, and the diocesan statutes downstream of it.
Layer: real · Place: Bologna, Italy (Gratian's school; the manuscripts live in the Biblioteca dell'Archiginnasio and across Europe)
Dates: 1140 → 1582 (confidence: medium)

Gratian's Decretum (Concordia discordantium canonum, compiled c. 1140 at Bologna) founded medieval canon law; the glossators' Corpus Juris Canonici (completed 1582) regulated marriage, clergy, heresy, and procedure for four centuries — and this entire corpus, thousands of canons and glosses, contains no droit du seigneur, no jus primae noctis, and no seigneurial wedding-night right of any kind.

Earliest attestation: Manuscript copies of the Decretum from the mid-12th century survive in the hundreds; the text is edited in Friedberg's Corpus Iuris Canonici (1879–1881).
Sources: Gratian, Concordia discordantium canonum (c. 1140) — edited in Emil Friedberg, Corpus Iuris Canonici, vol. I (Leipzig, 1879); Anders Winroth, The Making of Gratian's Decretum (Cambridge UP, 2000) — the two-recensions thesis; the founding text of medieval canon law; Corpus Juris Canonici, standard printed form 1582 (Gregory XIII) — the five-part corpus: Decretum, Liber Extra (1234), Liber Sextus (1298), Clementinae (1317), Extravagantes
Falsifiability: A canon in the Decretum, the Decretals, or the Sext granting or recognizing a lord's wedding-night right would falsify the claim — and would have falsified the DEBUNKED grade on evt-droit-du-seigneur-001 instead.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1140-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.3426,44.4949,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1140</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Angkor Wat was never abandoned — the continuous-use record</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Angkor Wat's Buddhist conversion from the late 13th century is documented; the temple is observable as an active religious center in every period since — including through the colonial era — and 'has remained a Buddhist monastery and an active center of Buddhist worship into the present day.' Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the inscriptional coverage between the 14th and 19th centuries is thin — the continuity rests on the living tradition plus the temple's documented modern status, not unbroken year-by-year attestation. The broader capital's decline is fenced: this record is the temple, not the city.
Layer: real · Place: Angkor Wat, Siem Reap, Cambodia
Dates: 1150 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Angkor Wat was never abandoned: it converted to Theravada Buddhist use from the late 13th century onward and has remained a continuously active religious center into the present day — the Khmers never 'lost' it.

Earliest attestation: Zhou Daguan's 'The Customs of Cambodia' (1296–97) describes a functioning imperial capital at the moment of the Buddhist transition; the temple's continuous monastic status is documented into the colonial period and the present.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angkor_Wat; Zhou Daguan, 'The Customs of Cambodia' (Zhenla fengtu ji), 1296–97; wondersofcambodia.com — 'Henri Mouhot and the Rediscovery of Angkor'
Falsifiability: Inscriptional or documentary evidence of a centuries-long dormancy in Angkor Wat's religious use (desecration, abandonment as a working temple) between the 14th century and the colonial period.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1150-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>103.867,13.4125,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Trabia itriyya industry — pasta in Sicily before Polo</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The geographer's 1154 text exists and is the standard scholarly citation, and 13th-century Genoese inventories corroborate pasta's Italian presence before Polo — but al-Idrisi never explicitly writes 'dried'; the drying is historians' inference from long-distance export (fresh dough could not survive the voyage), and the Genoese inventory dates vary across retellings (1244/1279/1284).
Layer: real · Place: Trabia, Sicily
Dates: 1154 → 1154 (confidence: high)

By 1154, the town of Trabia near Palermo (Sicily) produced thread-like dried pasta (itriyya) in industrial quantities and exported it by ship to Calabria and to Muslim and Christian lands — 141 years before Marco Polo's return from China.

Earliest attestation: Muhammad al-Idrisi, Nuzhat al-mushtaq fi ikhtiraq al-afaq (the Book of Roger), 1154, composed at Roger II of Sicily's court in Palermo.
Sources: al-Idrisi, Nuzhat al-mushtaq (1154) — the Trabia itriyya passage; Genoese notarial documents (1240, 1279, 1284 per the Spaghetti Museum, Pontedassio; the commonly cited 1279 estate inventory listing macaroni — a few retellings vary the year); HowStuffWorks, 'Did Marco Polo bring pasta from China?' — durum-wheat and Idrisi summary; Culinary Lore via Cucina Toscana, 'The International Origins of Pasta' — the pre-Polo documentary chain
Falsifiability: Proof that the Trabia passage is a later interpolation into al-Idrisi's text, or a contemporary 12th-century source contradicting a Sicilian pasta trade — either would downgrade the record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1154-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.6547,37.9944,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1154</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Henry II's Assize of Clarendon (1166) — the jury of presentment</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The assize's own Latin text survives and describes the twelve-man sworn inquiry; the Northampton assize (1176) and subsequent plea-roll practice confirm the system operated. Held below VERIFIED on the debate over how much Henry formalized vs invented (the pre-Conquest hundred-jury precedent is real).
Layer: real · Place: Clarendon, Wiltshire, England (traditional site of the council)
Dates: 1166 → 1176 (confidence: high)

Henry II's Assize of Clarendon (1166) established the jury of presentment: twelve lawful men of each hundred and four of each vill, sworn to report under oath those accused or notoriously suspect of robbery, murder, or theft. This jury of twelve — plus the civil-assize recognitions (novel disseisin, mort d'ancestor, darrein presentment) — is the documented seed of the English jury system, forty-nine years before Magna Carta.

Earliest attestation: The Assize's own text (Latin; Stubbs, Select Charters, via Wikisource): clause 1 — inquiry 'through the several counties and through the several hundreds by twelve more lawful men of the hundred and by four more lawful men of each vill, upon oath'; clause 2 — those found by the oath to be arrested 'and go to the judgment of water'.
Sources: Assize of Clarendon, 1166 — Latin text (Stubbs, Select Charters, 9th ed., pp. 170-173; translation via Wikisource): clause 1, twelve lawful men of the hundred + four of the vill; clause 2, the accused go to the judgment of water; Assize of Northampton, 1176 — extending Clarendon; added arson, forgery; six itinerant justice circuits; R.D. Groot, 'The jury of presentment before 1215', American Journal of Legal History 26 (1982), pp. 1-24 — the scholarly account of the pre-charter jury
Falsifiability: Evidence that the Assize of Clarendon is a later fabrication (the Latin text survives in Stubbs's Select Charters tradition) or that its 'twelve lawful men' were never sworn in practice (the Northampton assize and the itinerant justices' plea rolls show the system operating)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1166-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.7672,51.0703,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1166</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alexander Neckam's De naturis rerum (c. 1180-1190) — the earliest surviving European description of the mariner's compass, recorded as existing seamen's practice</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Two Neckam texts survive and are securely attributed: De naturis rerum (the north-pointing needle passage, confirmed in Thomas Wright's 1863 Rolls Series edition) and De utensilibus (~1190). The 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica's 'Compass' article confirms this is 'the earliest definite mention as yet known of the use of the mariner's compass in the middle ages' and notes that Neckam 'merely records what had apparently become the regular practice of at least many seamen of the Catholic world' — describing existing practice, not claiming invention. Guiot de Provins's poem (~1190s-1204) corroborates the period's seamen using the lodestone. Fenced honestly: the *pivoted* needle detail is not asserted categorically (d'Avezac's emendation is contested); De utensilibus's 'placed upon a dart' passage may even contain an inadvertent observation of variation ('looks northeast' for north).
Layer: real · Place: Oxford / St Albans, England — Neckam's teaching world; the sailors he describes are the Channel seamen
Dates: 1180-01-01 → 1202-12-31 (confidence: medium)

Alexander Neckam (1157-1217), in De naturis rerum (book ii, ch. 89/98 'De vi attractiva,' written c. 1180-1190), gives the earliest surviving European literary description of the mariner's compass: sailors in cloudy weather 'touch the magnet with a needle,' which 'whirls round in a circle until, when its motion ceases, the point looks direct to the north.' Neckam describes it as existing seamen's practice, not his own invention.

Earliest attestation: Neckam's own surviving manuscripts; Thomas Wright's 1863 Rolls Series edition confirms the text.
Sources: IEEE REACH Early Maritime Navigation background (teacher resource): Neckam's De Naturis Rerum passage translated — 'The sailors, moreover, as they sail over the sea, when in cloudy weather they can no longer profit by the light of the sun... they touch the magnet with a needle. This then whirls round in a circle until, when its motion ceases, the point looks direct to the north'; De Utensilibus — needle 'placed upon a dart' that 'looks northeast' (possible inadvertent variation observation); 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Compass': De utensilibus (Neckam, 12th c.) is the 'earliest definite mention as yet known of the use of the mariner's compass in the middle ages' — needle on a pivot; Neckam 'merely records what had apparently become the regular practice of at least many seamen of the Catholic world'; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Alexander Neckam': the earliest European notices of the magnet as a guide to seamen — outside China, the earliest surviving notices in any civilization; arXiv 2209.02371 (Martín Asín et al.): dates Neckam's De utensilibus 'around 1190'; Latin/translation of the pivot passage; quotes Guiot de Provins's Bible (~1204) and later European witnesses; Cambridge scholarly note (core.cambridge.org): d'Avezac's reading of De utensilibus as describing a pivoted needle depends on emending copyist errors — caution against categorical pivot claims
Falsifiability: A surviving, securely dated European compass description predating ~1180 would demote the 'earliest' element; none is known. The text itself is multiply transmitted.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1180-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.25,51.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1180</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The blood-catching Grail is a ~1200 literary invention (Robert de Boron)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Established literary history; the invention point is documented in the texts themselves
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 1190 → 1210 (confidence: high)

The Holy Grail as a vessel of Christ's blood originates in literature, not 1st-century tradition: Chrétien de Troyes' Perceval (~1190) has a graal as a serving dish — no blood, not holy. Robert de Boron's Joseph d'Arimathie (~1190-1200) is FIRST to make it the Last Supper cup used to catch Christ's blood. Wolfram von Eschenbach's Parzival (~1210) makes the grail a stone (lapsit exillis), not a cup at all. The blood-catching grail is twelve centuries younger than the Last Supper.

Earliest attestation: Robert de Boron, Joseph d'Arimathie, ~1200
Sources: Chrétien de Troyes, Perceval ~1190; Robert de Boron, Joseph d'Arimathie ~1200; Wolfram von Eschenbach, Parzival ~1210
Falsifiability: A pre-1190 blood-grail text would revise; none exists</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1190-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.0,48.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1190</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pray Codex's shroud image (c. 1192–1195)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manuscript is securely dated to 1192–1195, the illumination exists, and its particulars (L-pattern marks, hidden thumbs, crossed hands) are described in the published literature; what is CORROBORATED is the image-tradition's existence and date, not its identification with the Turin Shroud.
Layer: real · Place: Budapest (National Széchényi Library)
Dates: 1192 → 1195 (confidence: high)

The Hungarian Pray Codex (c. 1192–1195, the first book in Hungarian, National Széchényi Library, Budapest) contains an Entombment illumination showing a burial cloth with distinctive marks — including an L-pattern resembling the Shroud of Turin's burn holes, a long narrow cloth, hands crossed over the body without visible thumbs — attesting a Shroud-like image-tradition roughly 160 years before the Shroud's first undisputed documentation at Lirey (c. 1355).

Earliest attestation: The codex itself (National Széchényi Library, Budapest); the illumination's particulars described in the literature since the 20th century
Sources: The Hungarian Pray Codex (c. 1192–1195), Entombment illumination; Lejeune, J. — first-hand study of the codex in Budapest (presented at the 1993 CIELT Rome symposium): the correspondences with the Shroud's particulars; Markwardt, J., The Hidden History of the Shroud of Turin (2021) — the 1192–1195 dating and the illumination's particulars (carried as the sindonology reading, not as neutral scholarship); The Shroud's first undisputed documentation: Lirey, France, c. 1355 (the terminus the illumination predates)
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the L-pattern marks are a standard medieval artistic convention for burial cloths would collapse the identification reading; the depiction itself (dated codex, L-pattern marks) stands regardless.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1192-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>19.0402,47.4979,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1192</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1190s–1200s sack of the Bihar monasteries (Khilji)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Bihar monastic sackings are attested in the near-contemporary Tabaqat-i Nasiri and converge with the Tibetan witnesses of Nalanda's damaged-but-functioning remnant (Chag Lotsawa 1235, Dharmasvamin 1234–36); but the passage's exact target identification (Nalanda vs Odantapuri), the single-year date, and casualty figures stay contested, so the grade stops at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Nalanda / Bihar monastic belt, India
Dates: 1193 → 1200 (confidence: medium)

In the 1190s–1200s CE, the Turkic general Bakhtiyar Khilji's campaigns in Bihar sacked the great Buddhist monastic universities — Nalanda, Vikramashila, and Odantapuri — killing many monks and damaging the institutions; the near-contemporary Persian chronicle Tabaqat-i Nasiri (Minhaj-i Siraj, c. 1260) describes the Bihar campaign's assault, the killing of the shaven-headed inhabitants, and the discovery of the great book collections.

Earliest attestation: Minhaj-i Siraj, Tabaqat-i Nasiri (c. 1260) — near-contemporary Persian chronicle, Raverty's English translation of the Bihar campaign passage
Sources: Minhaj-i Siraj, Tabaqat-i Nasiri (c. 1260) — Raverty translation: the Bihar campaign passage (assault on the 'fortress of Bihar', shaven-headed inhabitants slain, the great book collections discovered); Dharmasvamin's account (1234–1236, via Tibetan transmission) — Nalanda c. 1236: 80 small viharas mostly abandoned/damaged by the Turushkas, 2 functional, &lt;100 monks, 90-year abbot Rahula Shribhadra; Chag Lotsawa's pilgrimage account (c. 1235) — found the 90-year-old teacher Rahula Shribhadra instructing ~70 students at damaged Nalanda; Taranatha, History of Buddhism in India (1608) — Tibetan tradition on the invasions and the flight of monks to Tibet/Nepal; Arun Shourie, 'How history was made up at Nalanda' (The Indian Express, 28 June 2014) — on the ransacking's description in the contemporary Tabaqat-i Nasiri and Taranatha's 'mine of learning'
Falsifiability: A contemporary 13th-century account of the Bihar monasteries flourishing untouched after 1200, or proof that the Tabaqat passage is a later interpolation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1193-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>85.4439,25.1368,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1193</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The torture was the procedure: the law-of-proof thesis</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The specialist consensus (Langbein 1977/2006, the definitive account): the two-witness-or-confession rule is documented in the glossators; the half-proof threshold and safeguards are documented in the Carolina's articles and the 1670 ordonnance; the 1808 code's termination of the classical procedure is dated. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because it is a historiographical thesis about causation, not a single dated event.
Layer: real · Place: Regensburg (the Carolina, Diet of 1532); the procedure's theater is the Empire and France
Dates: 1200 → 1808 (confidence: medium)

Continental Europe's judicial torture was not lawless cruelty but a formal, rule-bound evidentiary stage of the Romano-canonical inquisitorial procedure: the law of proof required two eyewitnesses or the accused's confession for conviction, so torture existed to produce the confession — permitted only on 'half proof' (one eyewitness or sufficient circumstantial indicia), governed by codified safeguards (the Carolina 1532, the ordonnance criminelle 1670), and it died when the law of proof was reformed rather than when a book was read.

Earliest attestation: The 13th-century Italian glossators' law of proof (two unimpeachable eyewitnesses, 'clear as the noonday sun', or the accused's confession); codified safeguards in the Constitutio Criminalis Carolina (Diet of Regensburg, 1532).
Sources: Constitutio Criminalis Carolina (1532), proclaimed at the Diet of Regensburg — 219 articles; the interrogation-under-torture rules with the indicia threshold, the surgeon's duty, ratification, and appeal; Ordonnance criminelle de 1670 (Saint-Germain-en-Laye; registered 26 Aug 1670, in force 1 Jan 1671) — the question préparatoire / question préalable as formal evidentiary stages; John H. Langbein, Torture and the Law of Proof: Europe and England in the Ancien Régime (Univ. of Chicago Press, 1977; 2nd ed. 2006) — the definitive account: torture as the institutional consequence of the two-witness-or-confession rule; French Code d'instruction criminelle (16 November 1808) — terminates the classical inquisitorial procedure in German territories
Falsifiability: A body of pre-13th-century systematic judicial-torture regulation, or evidence that the Carolina's torture articles were dead letter everywhere (routine judicial violation of the half-proof threshold), would weaken the thesis; Langbein's account is the specialist consensus, not a court transcript.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1200-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.1016,49.0134,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1200</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Al-Jazari's Book of Ingenious Devices (1206 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in the manuscript tradition (the Topkapi 1206 copy plus later illustrated copies) and independently verified by reconstruction: the diagrams encode buildable geometry — the strongest possible attestation for a machine treatise.
Layer: real · Place: Amid (Diyarbakir), Artuqid Diyar Bakr
Dates: 1206 → 1206 (confidence: high)

Ismail al-Jazari (d. 1206), chief engineer at the Artuqid court of Diyar Bakr (Amid), compiled al-Jami' bayn al-'ilm wa al-'amal al-nafi' fi sina'at al-hiyal ('The Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices', presented 1206): 50+ illustrated machines including the elephant clock, castle clock, musical automata with programmable peg-and-cam mechanisms, the double-action suction pump, the camshaft, and the crankshaft with crank-slider mechanism — the rotary-to-reciprocating conversion at the heart of every modern engine.

Earliest attestation: Multiple illustrated manuscript copies (Topkapi Palace Ms. Ahmet III 3472, 1206-dated); Donald R. Hill's 1974 English translation; working reconstructions by modern engineers from the diagrams
Sources: Topkapi Palace Ms. Ahmet III 3472 (1206): the presentation copy, illustrated; Hill, D.R. (1974): English translation of the Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices; Modern working reconstructions of the elephant clock, pumps, and automata from the diagrams (museums worldwide)
Falsifiability: The devices are reconstructible: modern engineers have built working replicas from the 800-year-old diagrams; a reconstruction failing the geometry would force a re-grade — none has</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1206-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>40.21,37.91,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1206</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mongol Empire at its greatest extent (c. 1279–1294) — the largest contiguous land empire in history</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent atlases and scholarly syntheses converge on the ~23.5–24 million km² maximal extent and the largest-contiguous-empire superlative; the claim is held at CORROBORATED (not VERIFIED) because pre-modern borders are approximate and the population figure is a modeled estimate, not a count.
Layer: real · Place: Karakorum (capital 1235–1271); the empire at height stretched from the Sea of Japan to Eastern Europe and the Levant
Dates: 1206 → 1294 (confidence: high)

At its greatest extent, around 1279–1294, the Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous land empire in history: roughly 23.5–24 million square kilometers (~9.1 million square miles), about 17.8% of Earth's land surface, with on the order of 110 million subjects — about a quarter of humanity under one administration.

Earliest attestation: The Secret History of the Mongols (c. 1228–1240s, Mongolian) for the founding; Persian (Juvayni, Rashid al-Din) and Chinese (Yuan administrative) records for the empire at its height.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Mongol Empire' — 'The Mongol Empire was the largest contiguous empire in history'; infobox area series: 1206: 4,000,000 km²; 1227: 13,500,000 km²; 1294: 23,500,000 km² (9,100,000 sq mi); historyandmystery.org, 'History's Largest Contiguous Land Empire Was the Mongol Empire' — 24 million km² (9.27 million sq mi), ~17.81% of Earth's total land surface at peak; Wikipedia, 'Outline of the Mongol Empire' — 'The Mongol Empire at its greatest extent in 1279'; Timothy May, The Mongol Empire (Edinburgh University Press, 2018) — modern scholarly synthesis of the empire's extent and administration, carried as the current reference; The Secret History of the Mongols — the Mongol-side founding narrative (the horde's own book)
Falsifiability: A rigorously surveyed pre-modern border reconstruction showing a larger contiguous land empire (e.g., a corrected Russian Empire figure above ~24 million km² of contiguous territory) would take the superlative.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1206-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>106.92,47.92,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1206</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Magna Carta, Clause 39 (the 'peers' clause)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The text survives in four contemporary engrossments; the clause's baronial, non-jury meaning is the scholarly consensus across the charter's 800th-anniversary literature.
Layer: real · Place: Runnymede, Surrey, England (traditional site of the sealing)
Dates: 1215 → 1215 (confidence: high)

Clause 39 of the 1215 Magna Carta, sealed at Runnymede (15 June 1215), provides that no free man shall be seized, imprisoned, stripped of rights or possessions, outlawed, exiled, or destroyed except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land. The clause's actual historical content is a baronial guarantee against arbitrary royal action — judgment by fellow barons in the feudal court — not a grant of trial by jury to the population.

Earliest attestation: Four surviving engrossments of the 1215 charter (British Library (2), Lincoln Cathedral, Salisbury Cathedral); the operative clause in Latin: 'nullus liber homo capiatur, vel imprisonetur, aut disseisiatur, aut utlagetur, aut exuletur, aut aliquo modo destruatur, nec super eum ibimus, nec super eum mittemus, nisi per legale judicium parium suorum vel per legem terre.'
Sources: The four 1215 engrossments — British Library (Cotton MS Augustus II.106; Add MS 48386), Lincoln Cathedral, Salisbury Cathedral; Latin text of clause 39; Library of Congress, 'Trial by Jury — Magna Carta: Muse and Mentor' exhibit: 'The origins of the jury trial precede the creation of Magna Carta... Magna Carta did not institute the jury system in the modern sense'; Daniel Klerman (USC Gould, via Metropolitan News): clause 39's 'judgment of his peers' meant the old feudal court — barons judged by fellow barons — 'this is not trial by jury'; jury trials in criminal cases were unknown in 1215 (cases were resolved by ordeal or battle); Scholarship summarized for the charter's 700th anniversary: Coke's interpretation of chapter 29 was anachronistic — a 17th-century reading of a 13th-century text; the Latin wording is ambiguous
Falsifiability: An engrossment carrying a clause 39 that promises jury trial in recognizably modern terms; or 13th-century evidence that 'pares sui' was read by contemporaries as a body of commoners sworn to find facts rather than baronial co-tenants</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1215-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.5336,51.4433,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1215</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The end of the ordeal in England (1215–1219) — how the jury got its courtroom</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Canon 18's text is primary and explicit; the English plea-roll record shows no ordeal after 1219; Henry III's 1219 instructions are documented. Held below VERIFIED on the causal weight of the Council vs pre-existing decline, and on the exact mechanics of the 1219 instructions.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (seat of the 1219 instructions; the ordeal-to-jury turn)
Dates: 1215 → 1219 (confidence: high)

In 1215, the Fourth Lateran Council (Canon 18) forbade clergy to take part in trials by ordeal — priests could no longer bless the water or the iron. With the rite's divine machinery removed, England abandoned the ordeal: the plea rolls contain no trial by ordeal after 1219, when Henry III's regency issued instructions to the itinerant justices ordering a new method of proof — the jury. The ordeal-to-jury turn was papal-and-crown work, not the charter's.

Earliest attestation: Lateran IV, Canon 18 (1215): clerics may neither pronounce nor execute death sentences, nor take part in judicial tests and trials by ordeal; English royal writs of 1219 instructing the eyre justices; the plea rolls' absence of ordeal cases after 1219.
Sources: Fourth Lateran Council, 1215, Canon 18 — De indicio sanguinis et duelli clericis interdicto: clerics 'neither pronounce nor execute a sentence of death... nor take part in matters connected with judicial tests and trials by ordeal'; Trial-by-ordeal scholarship (Wikipedia synthesis): 'In 1215, clergy were forbidden to participate in ordeals by the Fourth Lateran Council. The English plea rolls contain no cases of trial by ordeal after 1219, when Henry III recognized its abolition.'; Legal-history account (Mercatus working paper): ordeals abandoned where their religious trappings evaporated — Denmark 1216, England 1219, Scotland 1230, Italy 1231; LawLessons.ca / JusticeEducation.ca: Henry III issued an edict in 1219 encouraging trial by judge and jury — twelve worthy local citizens using common sense and knowledge of the people and events
Falsifiability: Plea-roll ordeal cases in England after 1219; or evidence the ordeal persisted in England with priests' blessings after Canon 18</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1215-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1215</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wandering Jew (1228) is not Cain</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in the chronicle record; the Cain-conflation is DEBUNKED by the legend's own dramatis personae.
Layer: real · Place: St Albans (recording); Jerusalem (narrative)
Dates: 1228 → 1602 (confidence: high)

The 'wandering man who cannot die' legend first recorded in 1228 (Roger of Wendover's Flores Historiarum, via an Armenian archbishop at St Albans; incorporated into Matthew Paris's Chronica Majora) concerns Cartaphilus, Pilate's doorkeeper — not Cain. Christ's sentence, 'I will go, but thou shalt remain waiting until I come,' launched 800 years of folklore (renamed Ahasuerus the cobbler in the 1602 German chapbook). Conflating this figure with Cain is a popular error; the legend says nothing about Cain at all.

Earliest attestation: Roger of Wendover, Flores Historiarum (1228); Matthew Paris, Chronica Majora
Sources: Catholic Encyclopedia, 'Wandering Jew' — 1228, Cartaphilus, 1602 Ahasuerus; Britannica 9th ed. entry detail (1902encyclopedia transcription)
Falsifiability: A pre-1228 witness to the Cartaphilus story, or a version naming Cain</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1228-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.3036,51.7527,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1228</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ibn al-Nafis Describes Pulmonary Circulation (1242 CE)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — One manuscript but a century of scholarly absorption: Meyerhof's 1924-1935 editions, Spink &amp; Lewis 1973 English translation, and mainstream acceptance in medical-history literature (textbook-level). VERIFIED is withheld because the attestation chain rests on a single surviving copy.
Layer: real · Place: Damascus (Ibn al-Nafis's city; he later headed the al-Mansuri hospital in Cairo)
Dates: 1242 → 1242 (confidence: high)

In his Sharh Tashrih al-Qanun ('Commentary on the Anatomy of the Canon of Avicenna', c. 1242 CE), the Damascus physician Ibn al-Nafis (1213-1288) described the pulmonary circulation: blood passes from the right ventricle through the lungs to the left ventricle, explicitly denying Galen's 'invisible pores' in the interventricular septum; he also described the coronary arteries and predicted small passages (manafidh) between pulmonary artery and vein — about 300 years before Servetus and Harvey.

Earliest attestation: Sharh Tashrih al-Qanun manuscript, discovered 1924 by Egyptian physician Muhyo al-Deen Tatawi (Altawi) in the Prussian State Library, Berlin; translated by orientalist Max Meyerhof into German, French, and English
Sources: Meyerhof, M. (1935): edition and translation of Sharh Tashrih al-Qanun; first modern publication of the pulmonary-circulation passages; Spink, M.S. &amp; Lewis, G.L. (1973): Arabic Surgical Treatises translation series; English rendering of the anatomical commentary; JBIMA review: 'Ibn al-Nafis — The First Who Described the Pulmonary Blood Circulation' — the coronary-vessel and manafidh (capillary) passages translated
Falsifiability: A forgery finding on the manuscript, or proof the pulmonary-circulation passages were interpolated by a later hand, would collapse the grade; corroboration grows if further copies surface</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1242-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>36.2765,33.5138,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1242</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tusi Couple's Road to Copernicus</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent witnesses converge: Hartner 1973 diagram-notation match; Swerdlow &amp; Neugebauer 1984 on Ibn al-Shatir's models inside De revolutionibus; surviving Byzantine Greek Tusi-couple manuscripts (Vat. gr. 211); the Hebrew Meyasher 'Aqov. But the exact transmission chain is unidentified (Wikipedia 'Tusi couple': 'an exact chain of transmission has not yet been identified'), so VERIFIED is withheld.
Layer: real · Place: Maragha, East Azerbaijan, Iran (observatory site); the devices travel to Copernicus via routes still debated (Byzantine, Hebrew, Iberian)
Dates: 1247 → 1543 (confidence: high)

Late Islamic astronomical devices — above all Nasir al-Din al-Tusi's 'Tusi couple' (Tadhkira fi 'ilm al-hay'a) and the planetary models of the 14th-century Damascene astronomer Ibn al-Shatir — appear inside Nicolaus Copernicus's De revolutionibus orbium coelestium (1543), used by Copernicus in his reformulation of mathematical astronomy.

Earliest attestation: Hartner, W. (1973) — 'Copernicus, the Man, the Work, and its History': the Tusi-couple diagram in Copernicus matches Tadhkira; Latin notation follows Arabic (alif-&gt;A)
Sources: Hartner, W. (1973): Tusi-couple diagram in De revolutionibus III.4 matches Tadhkira; Latin notation follows Arabic alif-&gt;A, ba-&gt;B (JSTOR 986460); Swerdlow, N. &amp; Neugebauer, O. (1984) — Mathematical Astronomy in Copernicus's De Revolutionibus: Ibn al-Shatir's lunar/mercury models nearly identical to Copernicus's; Saliba, G. (2007): Maragha models were equant-elimination devices for geocentrism; Copernicus's step was heliocentric redeployment; Neugebauer, O. (1975: 11, 1035, 1456) — Vaticanus Graecus MS 211 (pre-1308, Chioniades tradition) contains the Tusi couple in Greek: the device reached Byzantium within decades; Wikipedia 'Tusi couple' (crawled 2026-10-13): coined by Kennedy 1966; 'growing consensus' Copernicus became aware 'in some way'; exact chain unidentified — the honest gap named, not hidden; phys.org 2025-04 (al-Mansouri study): Ibn al-Shatir's solar model maximum equation 2;2,6 deg parallels Copernicus's solar calculations
Falsifiability: Would be weakened if a complete Latin translation stream were ruled out and the diagram matches were shown to be generic geometry; would be strengthened toward VERIFIED by a documented copy-chain. The exact chain remains unidentified, so the grade stays at CORROBORATED.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1247-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>46.24,37.39,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1247</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mongol Sack of Baghdad (1258)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The siege, sack, and caliph's death are multiply attested across hostile and friendly traditions (Mongol-commissioned Persian, Arabic chronicles) within decades of the event; casualty figures and the caliph's death-method stay contested and are not smuggled into the event grade.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad, Iraq (Abbasid capital)
Dates: 1258 → 1258 (confidence: high)

The Mongol army under Hülegü besieged Baghdad in January 1258, took the city in February, massacred its population over roughly a week, and killed the Abbasid caliph al-Musta'sim — ending the Abbasid Caliphate's five centuries as a territorial power.

Earliest attestation: 'Ata-Malik Juvayni, Tarikh-i Jahan-gusha (c. 1260) — near-contemporary Persian account written under Mongol patronage; Rashid al-Din, Jami' al-Tawarikh (c. 1300-1310); Arabic chronicles incl. Ibn Kathir (14th c.)
Sources: Juvayni, Tarikh-i Jahan-gusha (c. 1260) — near-contemporary Persian account under Mongol patronage; Rashid al-Din, Jami' al-Tawarikh (c. 1300-1310) — Ilkhanid court chronicle; Ibn Kathir, al-Bidaya wa al-Nihaya (14th c.) — Arabic chronicle tradition; Morgan, David — The Mongols (modern scholarly synthesis; death-toll discussion incl. Sicker 2000 p. 111, Wassaf)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1258 documentation of an intact Baghdad, or proof that the Juvayni/Rashid al-Din accounts are later fabrications</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1258-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.3661,33.3152,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1258</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The ink-black Tigris: Baghdad 1258's legendary river</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The telling is attested in later and popular accounts (which agree on the image) but no contemporary 13th-century witness was documented in this pass; the sack itself is CORROBORATED separately, so this record grades the telling, not the sack.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad, Tigris River
Dates: 1258 → 2026 (confidence: low)

After the Mongol sack of Baghdad in 1258, so many books from the House of Wisdom were thrown into the Tigris that the river's water ran black with ink (and, in some tellings, red with blood).

Earliest attestation: Later and popular accounts only — no contemporary 13th-century witness documented in this pass; current write-ups frame it explicitly as folklore/legend
Sources: The Golden Age of Islam: When Baghdad Lit the World (Aug 2026): 'according to popular accounts, so many manuscripts were thrown into the Tigris that its waters ran black with ink'; Bayt al-Hikmah: The Lost Library That Once Lit the World: 'According to folklore, so many burned manuscripts were thrown into the Tigris that the river's water turned black from the ink and soot. Whether this actually happened, however, is a matter of debate.'
Falsifiability: A contemporary 13th-century account describing the books' disposal would raise this to CORROBORATED; a documented source-study tracing the telling to a specific late invention would hold it here or lower it</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1258-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.36,33.31,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1258</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The House of Wisdom Destroyed (1258)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The institution existed (Ibn al-Nadim); the specific claim that the Mongols destroyed it in 1258 is late-tradition only, contested by the documented 9th-century decline and the modern source-critical challenge to the grand-university image — the building's fate, not the institution's existence, is what stays CLAIMED.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad, Iraq (Abbasid capital)
Dates: 1258 → 1258 (confidence: low)

The Bayt al-Hikma (House of Wisdom) — Baghdad's grand Abbasid institution of translation and learning — was destroyed by the Mongols in the 1258 sack, its library burned and its books thrown into the Tigris.

Earliest attestation: The institution's existence is attested by Ibn al-Nadim, al-Fihrist (d. 995); its 1258 destruction is attested only in the later popular tradition
Sources: Ibn al-Nadim, al-Fihrist (d. 995) — the primary source for the Bayt al-Hikma's existence and holdings; Current House of Wisdom historiography — the 19th/20th-c. Orientalist-construction challenge to the grand-university image (translation errors, retrospective projection); al-Wasiti's 1237 illustrations to the Maqamat al-Hariri — Baghdad manuscript culture alive a generation before the sack
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1258 source naming the Bayt al-Hikma's destruction would raise the grade; so would archaeological identification of the building</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1258-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.3661,33.3152,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1258</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tigris Ran Black with Ink (1258)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested, but late: the ink-black river is a 14th-century-and-after tale, absent from the near-contemporary accounts; it is a detail about the sack, not evidence of it, and stays CLAIMED until a contemporary attestation appears.
Layer: real · Place: Baghdad — the Tigris at the city
Dates: 1258 → 1400 (confidence: low)

During the 1258 Mongol sack of Baghdad, so many books and manuscripts were thrown into the Tigris River that its waters ran black with ink (and, in the paired version, red with the blood of the slain).

Earliest attestation: No contemporary (1258) source records the ink-black river; the tale belongs to the 14th-century-and-later chronicle tradition
Sources: 14th-century-and-later chronicle tradition (tales of book destruction in the Tigris; origin of the ink-black detail per the Siege of Baghdad historiography); Juvayni, Tarikh-i Jahan-gusha (c. 1260) — the near-contemporary account that does NOT carry the ink detail (the silence, honestly recorded); Nasir al-Din al-Tusi's rescue of books to Maragheh (tradition: thousands of volumes saved in advance — the 'total loss' framing fails on the record)
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1258 source (Juvayni or equivalent) explicitly describing the ink-black river would raise the grade; physical evidence is unrecoverable either way</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1258-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.3661,33.3152,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1258</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The invention of eyeglasses in Italy, c. 1268–1289 (anonymous)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: no single contemporary source names the inventor, but independent near-contemporary witnesses converge on the same decade — Rivalto's 1306 sermon, Redi's 1299 manuscript, the Pisa Dominican chronicle's account of Alessandro della Spina (d. 1313) re-making the art 'first made by someone else' and sharing it freely, Bernard Gordon's 1305 medical reference (Montpellier), and Venetian guild regulations governing spectacle sales by 1301. The earliest pictorial evidence is Tommaso da Modena's 1352 portrait of Cardinal Hugh de Provence.
Layer: real · Place: Pisa / Venice, Italy (the two documented early centers)
Dates: 1268 → 1289 (confidence: medium)

Eyeglasses were invented in Italy, between about 1268 and 1289, by a maker whose name the record does not preserve; the art spread by the early 14th century.

Earliest attestation: Fra Giordano da Rivalto, sermon 23 February 1306 (Pisa): the art of making glasses invented within the last twenty years; the inventor unnamed. Francesco Redi (1676) cites a 1299 manuscript calling spectacles 'lately invented.'
Sources: Francesco Redi's 1676 letters (via mrcophth.com): the 1299 manuscript ('glasses... lately invented for the comfort of the old souls who have become weak-sighted') and Fra Giordano da Rivalto's 1305/1306 sermon ('not yet twenty years since the art of making glasses was invented'); Alessandro della Spina's obituary (d. 1313) — 'he made glasses and freely taught the art to others; glasses had previously been made by someone else'; FixMyGlasses history of glasses: Venice guild regulations governing the sale of eyeglasses by 1301; Tommaso da Modena's 1352 portrait of Cardinal Hugh de Provence as the earliest pictorial evidence
Falsifiability: A securely dated eyeglass artifact or text from before 1268, or proof the 1299 manuscript and the 1306 sermon are forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1268-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.4017,43.7228,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1268</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The fall-blade's two-century head start: Halifax and the Scottish Maiden</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independent machines, two independent paper trails. Halifax: the wooden structure (two uprights, horizontal beam, 4.5 m; 3.5 kg axe head on a sliding wooden block), the execution register 1541–1650, the 1532 Weiditz woodcut and 1577 Holinshed illustration as independent pictorial corroboration. Edinburgh: the Maiden — built 1564 on the order of the Provost and Magistrates, in use April 1565 to 1710 (last execution 30 June 1716, John Hamilton), 150+ executed including the Marquess of Argyll (1661) and his son (1685), now in the National Museum of Scotland; the treasurer's accounts record moving and assembling the machine. The Morton-commission story is itself debunked inside this record: the machine was commissioned in 1564, seven years before Morton became Regent — Hume's 1644 telling is the legend's birth certificate, not its evidence. Held below VERIFIED on two honest uncertainties: the exact origin date of the Gibbet (the 1280 execution's method is unknown), and the open historical question of whether the French designers knew of these machines (one secondary source notes it is uncertain whether Guillotin was familiar with the Gibbet).
Layer: real · Place: Halifax, West Yorkshire (Gibbet); Edinburgh Mercat Cross (Maiden) — pins carried jointly, region precision for the pair
Dates: 1280-01-01 → 1716-06-30 (confidence: medium)

Fall-blade beheading machines predated the 1792 French guillotine by centuries: the Halifax Gibbet (England) and the Scottish Maiden (Edinburgh, 1564) are documented precedents.

Earliest attestation: The Halifax execution register (names from 1541 to 1650; the plaque lists 52 executed by the device); a Hans Weiditz 1532 woodcut showing a Gibbet-like device; Holinshed's Chronicles (1577) showing a similar machine at an Irish execution in 1307; Edinburgh City Treasurer accounts recording the Maiden's construction, transport, assembly, and dismantling.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Guillotine' — Halifax Gibbet description (4.5 m wooden structure, 3.5 kg axe head, sliding block); first recorded Halifax execution 1280 (method uncertain); Maiden constructed 1564, in use April 1565–1710; Morton executed 1581; Morton-commission legend from Hume of Godscroft 1644; Wikipedia, 'Halifax Gibbet' — register 1541–1650; dismantled after 1650; 1974 full-size replica on original stone base; plaque with 52 names; Weiditz 1532 woodcut; Holinshed 1577 illustration; Wikipedia, 'Maiden (guillotine)' — Edinburgh treasurer accounts (transport/assembly); dismantlable oak construction; 150+ executed 1564–1716; Argyll executions 1661/1685; last execution 30 June 1716 (John Hamilton); now National Museum of Scotland; visitnesm.org.uk, 'The Gibbet and the Maiden' — Halifax wool-trade context; 53 executed by the gibbet; blade axe-shaped, never sharpened, relied on weight and drop
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Halifax register, the Weiditz woodcut, and the Edinburgh treasurer accounts all postdate 1792 or are forgeries — the accounts are mundane municipal paperwork, the least romantic evidence class.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1280-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.8575,53.727,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1280</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Venice confines glassmaking to Murano (8 November 1291)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED: the law is an archival record with a precise date, and the industry's centuries-long concentration on Murano is independently attested by the guild records, the 1295 no-leaving law, and Murano's documented dominance of European glassmaking through the Renaissance.
Layer: real · Place: Murano, Venetian Lagoon
Dates: 1291 → 1291 (confidence: high)

On 8 November 1291, Venice confined most of its glassmaking industry to the island of Murano.

Earliest attestation: The Venetian law of 8 November 1291
Sources: Wikipedia 'Murano glass': 'A law dated November 8, 1291 confined most of Venice's glassmaking industry to the island of Murano'; furnaces as fire hazard in a city of wooden structures; the move also kept the technology and glassmakers confined and monitored; The Italian Tribune, 'The History of Venetian Glass in Italy': 1291 order by La Serenissima; historians' dual reading — fire hazard vs isolating craftsmen to protect trade secrets
Falsifiability: A contradictory archival record, or evidence the law was never enforced (instead the industry demonstrably concentrated on Murano for centuries)</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1291-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.3547,45.4581,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1291</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Ottoman Empire (c. 1299–1922)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The state is multiply attested in its own vast chancery archive and in every contemporary neighboring power's records for six centuries; its beginning and legal end are both documented to the file.
Layer: real · Place: Istanbul (seat of the Sublime Porte; founded as Ottoman state in Bithynia, northwestern Anatolia)
Dates: 1299 → 1922 (confidence: high)

The Ottoman Empire existed as a continuous state from c. 1299 (Osman I) until 1 November 1922 (the Grand National Assembly's abolition of the sultanate) — roughly six hundred and twenty years, one of the longest-lived states in recorded history.

Earliest attestation: Earliest surviving Ottoman chancery documents (14th c.); the empire's own Tahrir registers, firmans, and the Başbakanlık (Prime Ministry) Ottoman Archives — estimated ~100 million documents; multiply attested by Byzantine, Venetian, Genoese, Papal, and European diplomatic records from the 14th century onward.
Sources: Başbakanlık Osmanlı Arşivleri (Prime Ministry Ottoman Archives), Istanbul — ~100 million documents; Grand National Assembly resolution of 1 November 1922 — abolition of the sultanate; İnalcık, Halil — 'The Ottoman Empire: The Classical Age, 1300-1600' (Weidenfeld, 1973) — the documentary-state argument; Contemporary external attestations: Byzantine chronicles, Venetian relazioni, Genoese and Papal records from the 14th century onward
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous 14th-century documentation showing no polity under Osman I's successors, or proof that the 1922-abolition documents are forgeries.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1299-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1299</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The fibula (14th c. BCE) — the safety pin's ancestor</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independently attested in archaeological typology (Peschiera, Unter-Radl types), museum scholarship (Metropolitan Museum Journal), and art-historical survey (Smarthistory) — three independent lines on the same construction principle.
Layer: real · Place: Greek Peloponnese (Mycenaean heartland; violin-bow fibula introduction)
Dates: 1300BCE → 1300BCE (confidence: high)

The ancient fibula is the safety pin's ancestor: the Mycenaean violin-bow fibula (14th c. BCE) combined a single wire, a spring, a sharp point, and a hooked catch — the same four elements as Hunt's 1849 Dress-Pin — and the type ran for millennia through the Roman bow fibulae.

Earliest attestation: Mycenaean violin-bow fibulae (Drahtbügel), 14th c. BCE
Sources: Wikipedia "Fibula (brooch)" — Historical development: violin bow fibulae (Drahtbügel) appear in the late Bronze Age; introduced 14th c. BC by the Mycenaeans on the Greek Peloponnese; Metropolitan Museum of Art Journal v.35 (2000) — Dandridge, on the crossbow fibula's construction: basic form is a safety pin from a single wire; Smarthistory — "Fibulae" (Herbert Kuhn typology; thousands of specimens in museum collections)
Falsifiability: Dated stratigraphic re-analysis pushing the first fibulae after 1849 — impossible; held at CORROBORATED only because exact earliest-dated specimens carry typological dating ranges</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>21.68,37.25,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The predecessor's Atlantic expedition</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in one near-contemporary tradition — Musa's own account as recorded by al-Umari — with no independent corroboration and no returns; the popular name 'Abu Bakr II' is a modern misattribution, not a listed mansa.
Layer: real · Place: West African Atlantic coast (departure point uncertain)
Dates: 1311 → 1312 (confidence: low)

Mansa Musa's predecessor equipped a first fleet of 200 ships, then 2,000 ships (1,000 for men, 1,000 for water and provisions) to cross the Atlantic Ocean, and never returned; Musa became king after the fleet vanished.

Earliest attestation: al-Umari, Masalik al-absar, from Ibn Amir Hajib's conversations with Musa in Cairo, 1324
Sources: al-Umari, Masalik al-absar, from Ibn Amir Hajib (Levition &amp; Hopkins, Corpus of Early Arabic Sources for West African History, 1981); Ibn Khaldun on the Mali succession; Tarikh al-Sudan (17th c.) — the mansa sequence (no 'Abu Bakr II')
Falsifiability: A contemporary West African or Arabic source describing the predecessor's reign and succession that contradicts the expedition story, or evidence that al-Umari invented the conversation</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1311-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-17.44,14.72,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1311</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kebra Nagast as 14th-century compilation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 14th-century compilation date is multiply convergent: the colophon testimony, the dynastic-legitimation reading (Marcus: 'the Kebra Negest's prime goal was to legitimize the ascendancy of Emperor Yekuno Amlak'), Ullendorff's 1967 Schweich Lecture reading ('the repository of Ethiopian national and religious feelings'), and Munro-Hay's observation that no Ethiopian monarch claimed Solomonic descent before the 13th century. The book's power is real; its antiquity is not.
Layer: real · Place: Axum (Aksum), Tigray Region, Ethiopia — composition circle
Dates: 1314 → 1322 (confidence: high)

That the Kebra Nagast (The Glory of the Kings) — the Ge'ez national epic narrating the Queen of Sheba (Makeda), Solomon, their son Menelik I, and the migration of the Ark of the Covenant to Ethiopia — is a 14th-century CE compilation written to legitimize the restored Solomonic dynasty, not an eyewitness account from the 10th century BCE.

Earliest attestation: The colophon itself (Arabic Year of Mercy 409 = 1225 CE translation claim); the Solomonic dynasty's 1270 restoration it serves; the 1955 Ethiopian constitution, which wrote its genealogy in as law
Sources: Colophon testimony + Conti Rossini / Littmann / Cerulli — composition period 1314–1321/22 (via Wikipedia, citing the scholarship); Harold Marcus, A History of Ethiopia, p. 17 — 'a pastiche of legends conflated early in the fourteenth century by six Tigrayan scribes' to legitimize Yekuno Amlak; Edward Ullendorff, 1967 Schweich Lectures — 'The Kebra Nagast is not merely a literary work, but it is the repository of Ethiopian national and religious feelings'; Stuart Munro-Hay — no record of Ethiopian monarchs claiming descent from Solomon before the 13th century; Columbia World Epics project / Sites.Google course unit — date c. 14th c. CE; written into the 1955 Ethiopian constitution as historical truth
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-14th-century manuscript of the full narrative would reopen the question; none exists.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1314-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>38.7229,14.1379,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1314</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mansa Musa's Hajj of 1324</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent traditions attest the visit and the gold spending: al-Umari's near-contemporary account, Ibn Khaldun, the Sudanese chronicles, and a European map drawn within 50 years depicting the king from the story.
Layer: real · Place: Cairo, Egypt
Dates: 1324 → 1325 (confidence: high)

In 1324, Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire (r. c. 1312–1337), traveled from his capital to Mecca with a vast caravan, distributing such quantities of gold in Cairo that the gold price there fell.

Earliest attestation: Shihab al-Umari, Masalik al-absar, written from the account of Ibn Amir Hajib (the Cairo district governor who met Musa), within decades of the visit
Sources: al-Umari, Masalik al-absar (via Ibn Amir Hajib; Levition &amp; Hopkins, Corpus of Early Arabic Sources for West African History, 1981, pp. 269-273); Ibn Khaldun, Kitab al-Ibar; al-Maqrizi on the gold-price depression in Cairo; Tarikh al-Sudan (17th c., from earlier Timbuktu scholarship); Catalan Atlas, 1375 (Bibliothèque nationale de France) — 'Rex Melli' enthroned with gold orb and sceptre
Falsifiability: A contemporary Mamluk administrative or market record contradicting the visit, or proof that al-Umari's account fabricates the meeting with Ibn Amir Hajib</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1324-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.2357,30.0444,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1324</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Black Death (1347–1352): Yersinia pestis and the 30–50% toll</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Convergent independent lines: dozens of contemporary chronicles across independent traditions (Latin, Byzantine, Arabic, Russian) agree on timing, symptoms (buboes), and catastrophic mortality; demography corroborates the 30–50% range; ancient DNA from plague-pit victims (East Smithfield; Issyk-Kul) confirms Y. pestis as the pathogen; Spyrou et al. 2022 (Nature) placed the source strain's diversification node at Issyk-Kul 1338. Single chroniclers' absolute numbers stay CLAIMED; the cause and the range are CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Lake Issyk-Kul, Chu Valley, Kyrgyzstan (origin of the source strain, Spyrou et al. 2022) — spread to all of Europe 1347–1352
Dates: 1347 → 1352 (confidence: high)

The Black Death pandemic of 1347–1352 — the first and most lethal wave of the second plague pandemic — was caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis (the bubonic-plague pathogen), entered the Mediterranean in 1347 on trade ships from the Black Sea, and killed an estimated 30–50% of Europe's population (~25–30 million people; the literature ranges 25–60% depending on region).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary chronicles across Europe and the Near East (1347+); Boccaccio's Decameron preface; London burial ledgers; East Smithfield cemetery (consecrated 1348–50). Pathogen attestation: Haensch et al. 2010 (PLOS Pathogens); Bos et al. 2011 (Nature).
Sources: Haensch, S. et al. (2010) 'Distinct clones of Yersinia pestis caused the Black Death', PLoS Pathogens 6, e1001134 — Y. pestis DNA from Black Death victims, including the East Smithfield cemetery, London; the study that ended the not-plague debate; Bos, K. et al. (2011) 'A draft genome of Yersinia pestis from victims of the Black Death', Nature 480, 506–509 — complete medieval Y. pestis genome; confirmed the ancient strain as direct ancestor of modern plague lineages; Spyrou, M. et al. (2022) 'The source of the Black Death in fourteenth-century central Eurasia', Nature 606, 718–724 — ancient Y. pestis DNA from teeth of 1338–39 Issyk-Kul cemetery victims (Chu Valley, Kyrgyzstan); the strain sits at the node of the Black Death diversification event — the pandemic's origin placed in Central Asia; Schuenemann, V. J. et al. (2011) 'Targeted enrichment of ancient pathogens yielding the pPCP1 plasmid of Yersinia pestis from victims of the Black Death', PNAS 108, E746–E752; Bramanti, B. &amp; Hänsch, S. (2013) — Aschheim, Bavaria cemetery aDNA confirmed Y. pestis as the agent of the first (Justinianic) pandemic, PLOS Pathogens; Benedictow, O. J. (2004) The Black Death 1346–1353: The Complete History (Boydell) — demographic synthesis; Cowal, L. et al. (2008) The Black Death Cemetery, East Smithfield, London (Museum of London Archaeology Service)
Falsifiability: Recovery of pre-1347 European plague-pit remains bearing a non-Yersinia pathogen with complete genomes would overturn the cause; a coherent demographic record showing &lt;10% European mortality would overturn the toll range.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1347-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>77.25,42.45,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1347</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The forty days that gave the world 'quarantine'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The etymology is solidly attested across independent secondary sources: Italian quarantena = forty days; the Venice sequence (1348 thirty-day hold → 1403 forty-day extension → 1423 Lazzaretto Vecchio → 1468 institutional decree at the Lazzaretto Nuovo) is carried consistently (Global Campus of Human Rights repository paper; Proto Magazine's plague timeline; italyheaven.co.uk's Lazzaretto Nuovo history). Held below VERIFIED for two honest reasons: (1) secondary sources disagree on the first-hold year (1348 vs 1370), and (2) the 'Venice invented quarantine' superlative fails — Ragusa legislated first in 1377. What Venice verifiably gave the world is the word, the forty-day standard, and the lazaretto as an institution — the teze cargo-sanitization sheds, the fumigation with juniper and rosemary, the health passes. The Ragusan priority is carried as a competing hypothesis, not hidden.
Layer: real · Place: Lazzaretto Nuovo, Venetian Lagoon
Dates: 1348 → 1468 (confidence: medium)

The word 'quarantine' comes from the Venetian Republic's forty-day (quaranta giorni) isolation of suspect ships.

Earliest attestation: Venetian Senate decrees: the 1348 hold, the 1403 extension to forty days, the 1423 creation of the Lazzaretto Vecchio on Santa Maria di Nazareth ('Nazaretum' — the name that became 'Lazzaretto'), the 1468 decree for the Lazzaretto Nuovo; Benedetto Bordone's 1528 Isolario showing both lazzaretti
Sources: Global Campus of Human Rights repository, 'Pandemic Management During the Republic of Venice' — 1423 Senate creation of the hospital on Santa Maria di Nazareth ('Nazaretum' → 'Lazzaretto'); Lazzaretto Vecchio vs Lazzaretto Nuovo; the 1468 decree institutionalizing quarantine ('from quarantena, meaning 40 days'); suspect ships held at the Lazzaretto Nuovo pending clearance; teze cargo-sanitization sheds; fumigation with juniper and rosemary; Proto Magazine, 'From the Black Death to SARS' — 1348: Venice 'introduces one of the earliest formal systems of quarantine, requiring ships, goods and people to wait 40 (quaranta, in Italian) days'; 1423: 'Rise of Lazarettos'; notes the miasma theory behind the practice and the flea-die-off side effect; italyheaven.co.uk, 'Lazzarettto Nuovo: Venice Island Excursions' — 'the word quarantine actually comes from the Italian for forty (days, at one time the proscribed duration of isolation)'; suspect ships and crews held for a disease-check period; the ill removed to Lazzaretto Vecchio; Public Radio East / NPR, 'A History Of Quarantines, From Bubonic Plague To Typhoid Mary' — Venice's 40-day waiting period as quarrantinario 'from the Italian word for 40'; notes the isolation later shrank to trentinario (30 days) while the original name stuck
Falsifiability: Venetian archival research showing the forty-day period was never legislated before 1468, or that the word derives from a different root, would move the grade. A Ragusan document using 'quarantena' before 1403 would hand the word's origin to Dubrovnik.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1348-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4119,45.4536,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1348</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jikji (1377) — the oldest extant book printed with movable metal type</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The object exists, is dated by its own printed colophon to 1377, is confirmed as movable-metal-type printing, and its status is certified by UNESCO's Memory of the World listing.
Layer: real · Place: Cheongju, Korea (Heungdeoksa temple site); volume held in Paris
Dates: 1377 → 1377 (confidence: high)

The Korean Buddhist anthology Jikji, printed in 1377 at Heungdeoksa temple in Cheongju, is the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type — 78 years before Gutenberg's Bible.

Earliest attestation: The printed colophon of the metal-type edition (July 1377)
Sources: Jikji (Baegun hwasang chorok buljo jikji simche yojeol), metal-type edition, 1377 — 38 of 41 volumes extant, held by the Bibliothèque nationale de France (Richelieu Oriental); UNESCO Memory of the World Register, 2001 — inscription of Jikji as the oldest extant book printed with movable metal type; Korean movable metal type tradition: Goryeo-era cast bronze type of the 13th century, documented in Korean printing histories (the Wang Zhen 1313 account of wooden type; Hua Sui's bronze type 1490 for the Chinese continuation); Heritage (2023), vol. 3, pp. 2559-2581 — ink-tone analysis identifying the Gongin version of the 'Song of Enlightenment' (September 1239 imprint) as metal-type printing, 138 years before the Jikji
Falsifiability: An authenticated earlier movable-metal-type printed book would take the title; proof the colophon was added later would reopen the dating</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1377-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>127.49,36.64,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1377</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mamluk 52-Card Pack — Europe's Deck Ancestor</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Topkapi fragments exist and match the European 52-card structure point for point — four suits, ten pips, three courts with na'ib nomenclature — and the 1377 European references describe the same template; held below VERIFIED because fragment dating varies across cataloguers and the transmission route is reconstructed, not documented.
Layer: real · Place: Istanbul, Türkiye (Topkapi Palace)
Dates: 1377 → 1400 (confidence: medium)

The European 52-card pack descends from the Islamic world's Mamluk cards: a 52-card deck in four suits (cups, coins, swords, polo sticks) with ten pip cards and three court cards — malik (king), na'ib malik (viceroy), thani na'ib (second viceroy) — the 'na'ib' being the probable origin of the early Italian word naibi for playing cards.

Earliest attestation: John of Rheinfelden, 'De moribus et disciplina humanae conversationis id est ludus cartularum' (Basel, 1377) — the oldest surviving detailed European description; Signoria of Florence, 23 March 1377
Sources: Topkapi Palace Museum, Mamluk 'muluk wa-nuwwab' card fragments (c. 1400, catalogued Depaulis); John of Rheinfelden, De moribus et disciplina humanae conversationis (Basel, 1377); L. A. Smith, 'The Playing Cards of the Mamluks' (1939); Thierry Depaulis, Mamluk cards research (2013); Simon Wintle, Early History of Playing Cards &amp; Timeline (1996) — na'ib→naibi note
Falsifiability: A complete European 52-card pack predating the Mamluk fragments, or proof the Topkapi cards are modern forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1377-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.98,41.01,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1377</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1378 iconoclast: al-Maqrizi's account of the Sphinx's nose</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-source attestation: one medieval historian, writing decades after the event, is the entire documentary base. That is the textbook definition of CLAIMED — attested but single-source. Lehner's competing material reading keeps the grade honest and low.
Layer: real · Place: Great Sphinx of Giza, Giza Plateau, Egypt
Dates: 1378 → 1378 (confidence: low)

The Great Sphinx's nose was chiseled off in 1378 CE by Muhammad Sa'im al-Dahr, a Sufi iconoclast who was angered by local villagers making offerings to the Sphinx; he was seized and killed by the same villagers after the act.

Earliest attestation: al-Maqrizi (Taqi al-Din Ahmad ibn Ali al-Maqrizi, d. 1442), 15th-century Egyptian historian — the only near-source for the 1378 episode, writing roughly 50 years after the alleged event.
Sources: al-Maqrizi (d. 1442), 'al-Khitat' / historical writings — the 1378 account of Muhammad Sa'im al-Dahr defacing the Sphinx and being killed by villagers (as summarized in the modern historiography); Smithsonian Journeys, Sarah Erdman (Sept 2022) — 'The medieval Egyptian historian al-Maqrizi wrote that the nose was actually destroyed by a 14th-century Sufi dervish named Muhammad Sa'im al-Dahr... While scholars debate the theory...'; Mark Lehner's Sphinx geological survey — chisel-mark analysis suggesting the nose may have been removed between the 3rd and 10th century (competing material reading, via the modern summaries)
Falsifiability: An earlier dated image of the Sphinx without its nose (pre-1378) would falsify the 1378 attribution; so would any 14th-century document contradicting al-Maqrizi. A second independent textual witness would move the grade up; none is known.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1378-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.1376,29.9753,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1378</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Chinese bristle toothbrush (15th century, conventionally)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The design tradition is multiply described and its westward transmission documented; the precise century wanders across sources, so VERIFIED stays parked on the dating.
Layer: real · Place: China (Ming context, conventionally)
Dates: 1400 → 1600 (confidence: medium)

A toothbrush with hog bristles set into a bone or bamboo handle was in use in China by the 15th century; European travelers carried the design west in the 17th century.

Earliest attestation: Chinese writings mentioning bristle brushes, conventionally 13th–15th century; European adoption of hog/horse-hair brushes in the 17th century.
Sources: Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry, via HowStuffWorks: 'recorded in China circa 1500 — straight bamboo handles pierced with trimmed hog bristles set at a right angle to the brush'; Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry (Living on Earth, 2002): 'beginning about 1400 years ago, people in China made toothbrush bristles by plucking the neck hairs of the Siberian wild boar'; Dental-history convention: 15th-century China, bone/bamboo handles, hog's-neck bristles; European adaptation with horsehair/feathers (CK Dental; Sure Dental)
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-1400 Chinese bristle brush would move the date earlier; proof that no bristle brush predates the European 17th-century specimens would collapse the Chinese priority.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1400-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>105.0,35.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1400</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The treasure ships: '44 zhang' (400-foot) claim</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single late literary source (a 1597 novel) for the exact figure; no excavated hull, no contemporary shipyard dimension sheet; the archaeological evidence supports 'very large' but not the specific 44-zhang number. Attested once, by fiction, for a number everyone quotes as fact.
Layer: real · Place: Nanjing, China (Longjiang treasure-shipyard site; 1962 rudder-post find)
Dates: 1405 → 1433 (confidence: low)

Zheng He's largest 'treasure ships' (baochuan) were 44.4 zhang long and 18 zhang wide — roughly 400+ feet, with nine masts — the largest wooden ships ever built.

Earliest attestation: Luo Maodeng, Xiyang ji (Romance of the Western Ocean), 1597 — a historical novel; historicity of the ship measurements disputed
Sources: Luo Maodeng, Xiyang ji (1597) — the 44 × 18 zhang figure; disputed historicity; Ma Huan, Yingya shenglan (c. 1451) — records the large treasure ships of the fourth voyage; the earliest documentation of the type; 1962 Nanjing rudder-post find: 36-foot timber steering post, Ming-era wood, sized for a ship of 540–600 feet — suggestive, not decisive; 2005 broken stone tablet: 2,000-liao and 1,500-liao sea ships recorded for the early voyages (via web.archive.org travel-silkroad summary); Xu Gongsheng's construal: large-size treasure ships as imperial ceremonial vessels; main ship type the 2,000-liao sea ship
Falsifiability: An excavated baochuan hull with measurable dimensions, or a shipyard record contemporaneous with the voyages, would settle it either way; the 1962 Nanjing rudder-post timber (36-ft steering post, Ming-era wood) is suggestive of very large ships but does not verify the 44-zhang figure</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1405-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>118.8,32.06,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1405</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Zheng He's seven treasure voyages (1405–1433)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested in contemporary primary sources: the official Ming Veritable Records, three first-person crew accounts (Ma Huan sailed on four voyages, Fei Xin on three), and contemporary stone inscriptions in China and Sri Lanka.
Layer: real · Place: Nanjing, China (Longjiang shipyard, departure point)
Dates: 1405 → 1433 (confidence: high)

Between 1405 and 1433 the Ming court dispatched seven large maritime expeditions under the eunuch admiral Zheng He, sailing from Nanjing through Southeast Asia and the Indian Ocean to Calicut (India), Hormuz and the Persian Gulf, the Red Sea, and the east coast of Africa (Malindi) — the largest wooden fleets assembled before the industrial age, on tribute-diplomacy and trade missions.

Earliest attestation: Ming Veritable Records (Ming shilu) entries for the reigns of Yongle and Xuande; Liuhe (Longjiang) inscription 1407; Galle (Sri Lanka) trilingual inscription 1411
Sources: Ming shilu (Veritable Records of the Ming Dynasty) — official court record of the expeditions; Ma Huan, Yingya shenglan (The Overall Survey of the Ocean's Shores), c. 1451 — first-person account of voyages 4–7; first text to record the treasure ships and their dimensions; Fei Xin, Xingcha shenglan (c. 1436) — crew member's account; Liuhe/Longjiang inscription, Nanjing, 1407; Changle inscription 1431; Galle trilingual inscription (Chinese, Tamil, Persian), Sri Lanka, 1411; Columbia University, Asia for Educators: The Ming Voyages (http://afe.easia.columbia.edu/special/china_1000ce_mingvoyages.htm)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Ming document or inscription denying the expeditions occurred, or proof that the Liuhe, Changle, and Galle inscriptions are modern forgeries — none exists</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1405-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>118.8,32.06,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1405</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Machu Picchu</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The site is an extensively documented archaeological fact: standing masonry, surveyed terraces, excavated burials with radiocarbon dates, and a century of continuous scholarship.
Layer: real · Place: Machu Picchu ridge, Urubamba Province, Peru
Dates: 1420 → 1532 (confidence: medium)

An Inca settlement on a mountain ridge at ~2,430 m above the Urubamba River, built c. 1420-1450 and occupied until the 1530s; the majority scholarly reading (Rowe; Burger) is that it was a royal estate of the Inca ruler Pachacuti (reigned c. 1438-1471), housing some 500-750 people including Pachacuti's lineage, religious specialists, and skilled yanacona artisans. The Spanish never found or plundered it.

Earliest attestation: The site itself (standing Inca masonry); no pre-1911 Inca reference to it survives, and the conquistadors' documents note a place called 'Piccho' without record of a visit.
Sources: Burger et al. 2021, AMS radiocarbon study of 26 individuals from the site's cemeteries (Antiquity); John Howland Rowe — the Pachacuti-estate attribution; Richard L. Burger &amp; Lucy C. Salazar — the estate reading's principal modern defenders; UNESCO World Heritage inscription, 1983; Gonzales &amp; Bauer 2021, 'The Ancient Inca Town Named Huayna Picchu' (Ñawpa Pacha, Journal of the Institute of Andean Studies) — on the site's pre-Bingham name
Falsifiability: A dated Inca or colonial document naming the site and contradicting the estate reading, or AMS dates placing occupation outside the imperial period</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1420-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.545,-13.163,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1420</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gutenberg's integrated printing system</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The system is demonstrated by its surviving products and the near-contemporary paper trail: the 42-line Bible (c. 1454-1455; ~180 copies, over 40 substantially complete surviving in BnF, British Library, Library of Congress), the hand-mold method of casting uniform interchangeable type, the oil-based ink that adhered to metal, and the screw-press adaptation of wine/olive presses (Lehmann-Haupt via Britannica; Clemson STS synthesis; LoC). Gutenberg's goldsmith training explains the alloy and mold; the epochal act was the combination into a practical manufacturing process — method 'virtually unchanged for six centuries' until computer typesetting. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because Gutenberg's own workshop records are lost and the 1439 experiments are known only through later legal documents.
Layer: real · Place: Mainz, Electorate of Mainz
Dates: 1439 → 1455 (confidence: medium-high)

In Mainz in the 1440s-1450s, Johannes Gutenberg built the first integrated European printing system: reusable metal type cast in a hand mold, a suitable metal alloy, oil-based ink, and an adapted screw press — and with it produced the 42-line Bible (completed late 1455).

Earliest attestation: The products themselves — the 42-line Bible and the surviving Mainz fragments — plus the 1455 legal/financial record (the Fust dispute) and Piccolomini's March 1455 letter describing printed Bible quires at Frankfurt.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'The Gutenberg Bible' — printing completed late 1455 at Mainz; Gutenberg 'generally credited for inventing the process of making uniform and interchangeable metal types and for solving the many problems of finding the right materials and methods for printing'; ~180 copies; Clemson Open Textbook (STS), 'The First Moveable Type Printing Press' — hand mold for mass casting, metal alloy, oil-based ink adhering to metal, screw press adapted from wine presses; 42-line Bible by 1455 as the proof of the manufacturing process; Lehmann-Haupt, H.E. (1995), 'Johannes Gutenberg', Encyclopaedia Britannica 15th ed. (via Maine lecture notes) — the mould for casting movable type in large numbers, the new metal alloy, the press adapted from wine presses, the oil-based ink
Falsifiability: A contemporary document showing the 42-line Bible printed by another hand, or Gutenberg's type shown to be wood-carved rather than cast, would downgrade the system claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1439-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.2473,49.9929,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1439</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Valla's 1440 proof: the philologist who broke the Donation</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The treatise survives in manuscript and print; its method and conclusion are independently confirmed by Cusa's prior doubt, Pecock's independent confirmation, the Catholic Encyclopedia 1913, the 1911 Britannica.
Layer: real · Place: Naples (Valla wrote in the service of Alfonso V of Aragon)
Dates: 1439 → 1440 (confidence: high)

In 1439-1440 Lorenzo Valla, in his Declamatio 'De falso credita et ementita Constantini donatione' (dedicated to Alfonso V of Naples), proved by philological analysis that the Constitutum Constantini could not have been written in the 4th century: the Latin carries vocabulary impossible for a 4th-century imperial chancery, and the document's own internal dating is self-contradictory.

Earliest attestation: The treatise itself, surviving in manuscript with its dedication to Alfonso V of Naples; first print 1518 (Mainz).
Sources: Lorenzo Valla, De falso credita et ementita Constantini donatione declamatio (1439-1440; first printed Mainz, 1518) — the declamatio itself; Fiveable AP Euro, 'Lorenzo Valla': 'proved the Donation of Constantine was a medieval forgery, not a 4th-century document'; philology as the dating-and-authenticating method; Valla's work challenged the Church's institutional power; written while employed by King Alfonso of Naples; The AP-exam standard examples of Valla's linguistic arguments: the medieval word *banna* where a Roman would write *vexillum*; the diadem described as gold and jewels when a 4th-century diadem was made of cloth (per the QuestionAI analysis of Valla's arguments)
Falsifiability: A demonstration that Valla's linguistic data is wrong — i.e., that the cited anachronistic usages were genuinely current in 4th-century imperial Latin — would weaken it; 586 years of scholarship have produced none, and the consensus has only hardened.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1439-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.2681,40.8518,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1439</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tarot as a 15th-Century Milanese Game</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple surviving hand-painted decks from the Milanese court (Morgan, Carrara, Colleoni), documented attributions (Bembo/Zavattari workshop), and Dummett's (1980) structural history converge; held below VERIFIED because the single inventor is unproven.
Layer: real · Place: Milan, Italy
Dates: 1440 → 1452 (confidence: high)

The tarot deck began as a 15th-century northern-Italian aristocratic card game (tarocchi) — 78 cards: 56 suit cards plus 22 trump cards — documented by surviving hand-painted luxury decks made for the Milanese court.

Earliest attestation: Visconti-Sforza tarocchi cards — the Pierpont Morgan Library set (MS M.630.1-35, 35 cards, Milan c. 1450–1480) plus Accademia Carrara and Casa Colleoni survivors
Sources: Morgan Library &amp; Museum, Visconti-Sforza Tarot Cards (MS M.630.1-35), Milan c. 1450–1480; Michael Dummett, The Game of Tarot (1980); The Visconti-Sforza Tarot Cards (1986); Kit Stone, 'Tarot in Late Enlightenment France' (Oct 2024) — tarot's game history before the occult era
Falsifiability: Dated tarot artifacts or documents older than the 1430s, or proof the Visconti-Sforza decks are later forgeries</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1440-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>9.19,45.46,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1440</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The First Documented Coffee Drinking — Yemeni Sufi monasteries, 15th century</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations of 15th-c. Yemeni Sufi coffee drinking; held below VERIFIED because the earliest narrative compiler (al-Jaziri, 1587) writes a century after the fact.
Layer: real · Place: Aden, Yemen
Dates: 1450 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

Coffee as a brewed drink is first documented in the Sufi monasteries of Yemen in the mid-15th century; the earliest named adopter is Sheikh Jamal al-Din al-Dhabhani, mufti of Aden (d. 1470), reported as the first to take up the drink c. 1454.

Earliest attestation: Abd al-Qadir al-Jaziri, Umdat al-safwa fi hill al-qahwa (1587) — the earliest surviving compilation tracing coffee's history and legal controversies, reporting the al-Dhabhani adoption c. 1454 and the spread from Yemen northward to Mecca, Medina, Cairo, Damascus, Baghdad, and Constantinople.
Sources: Abd al-Qadir al-Jaziri, Umdat al-safwa fi hill al-qahwa (1587) — reports al-Dhabhani, mufti of Aden (d. 1470), as the first adopter of coffee c. 1454, noting its properties (drove away fatigue and lethargy); Sufi monastery use in 15th-c. Yemen — well established by the late 15th century (general scholarly consensus; no confirmed evidence of coffee as a drink before the 15th century); 2020 Arabica genetic-diversity study — confirms the Yemeni cultivation story: the vast majority of the world's Arabica originates from early Yemeni cultivated varieties
Falsifiability: A dated, authenticated pre-1450 document describing brewed coffee would push the date back and refine this record; genetic/archaeological evidence of pre-Yemeni brewing would do the same</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1450-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>45.03,12.78,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1450</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Orban's great bombard, 1452-1453</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The casting, the transport by ox-teams, and the bombard's wall-breaking role are attested across independent traditions (Doukas, Kritovoulos, Tursun Beg, Barbaro); exact dimensions and the founder's biography vary by source and stay fenced.
Layer: real · Place: Adrianople (Edirne) — the casting; the gun was dragged to Constantinople
Dates: 1452 → 1453-05-29 (confidence: high)

The Hungarian iron-founder Orban (d. 1453), after offering his services to Constantine XI in 1452, cast a giant bronze bombard for Mehmed II at Adrianople — an approximately 8-meter gun firing stone shot of around half a ton to over a ton — which was dragged some 150 miles to Constantinople by teams of oxen and pounded the Theodosian walls through the 53-day siege.

Earliest attestation: Doukas (c. 1462) for the founder and the Byzantine-offer story; Michael Kritovoulos and Tursun Beg for the artillery's role in the siege; the Dardanelles Gun (cast 1464, Royal Armouries) as the surviving design descendant
Sources: Doukas, Historia Turco-Byzantina (c. 1462) — the founder and the Byzantine-offer story; Michael Kritovoulos, History of Mehmed the Conqueror — the artillery's siege role; Tursun Beg, Târîh-i Ebü'l-Feth — Ottoman account of the siege artillery; Nicolò Barbaro, siege diary — the bombardment from the walls; Dardanelles Gun, cast 1464 (Royal Armouries) — surviving design descendant of Orban's bombard
Falsifiability: Ottoman foundry or fiscal records contradicting the Adrianople commission, or proof that Doukas invented the Orban episode wholesale — the artillery's siege role is independently attested beyond Doukas, so the founder could fall while the bombard stands</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1452-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>26.5559,41.6769,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1452</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Fall of Constantinople, 29 May 1453</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by independent contemporary sources from every side of the walls — Venetian, Genoese, Byzantine, and Ottoman — plus an unbroken modern scholarly consensus (Runciman 1965; Philippides &amp; Hanak 2011; Crowley 2005; Kaldellis 2026).
Layer: real · Place: Constantinople (modern Istanbul)
Dates: 1453-04-06 → 1453-05-29 (confidence: high)

On 29 May 1453, after a 53-day siege (6 April - 29 May), the Ottoman army of Sultan Mehmed II stormed Constantinople, ending the Eastern Roman Empire's eleven-century continuous existence and making the city the Ottoman capital.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary and near-contemporary, from all sides: Nicolò Barbaro's siege diary (Venetian eyewitness, 1453); Leonard of Chios's letter to Pope Nicholas V (1453); George Sphrantzes's Chronicon Minus (Byzantine court eyewitness); Tursun Beg's Târîh-i Ebü'l-Feth (Ottoman official historian)
Sources: Nicolò Barbaro, Giornale dell'Assedio di Costantinopoli (1453) — Venetian eyewitness diary; Leonard of Chios, letter to Pope Nicholas V (1453); George Sphrantzes, Chronicon Minus (c. 1470s) — Byzantine court eyewitness; Tursun Beg, Târîh-i Ebü'l-Feth — Ottoman official history; Doukas, Historia Turco-Byzantina (c. 1462); Michael Kritovoulos (Kritoboulos), History of Mehmed the Conqueror (c. 1453-1467); Runciman, Steven (1965), The Fall of Constantinople 1453, Cambridge UP; Philippides, M. &amp; Hanak, W. (2011), The Siege and the Fall of Constantinople in 1453, Ashgate
Falsifiability: A contemporary account from any of the four source traditions (Venetian, Genoese, Byzantine, Ottoman) contradicting the fall — none exists; all agree the city was stormed on 29 May 1453</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1453-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1453</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Columbus's real error: the size of the Earth, not its shape</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The mechanism is documented end to end: al-Farghani's correct 56.67 Arabic-miles-per-degree (~111.1 km/degree, circumference ~39,986 km, within 90 km of modern value) was mangled by d'Ailly's Roman-mile revision into a 25% smaller planet, and Columbus sailed on the mangled number. Carl Sagan's summary stands: Columbus used the smallest possible Earth and the greatest eastward extension of Asia available, 'and then exaggerated even those.' The experts' objection — the ocean is too wide — was the true reading of the planet, and the Americas saved him from it. Grade fenced: covers only the shape-vs-size reading of the debate, not Columbus's conduct or the voyage's consequences.
Layer: real · Place: Salamanca, Spain (the examining faculty)
Dates: 1486 → 1492 (confidence: high)

The learned opposition to Columbus's 1492 plan was not about the Earth's shape — which everyone at the table knew was round — but about its size: Columbus argued the Ocean Sea was crossable because he had shrunk the planet by roughly a quarter; the experts were right and he was wrong.

Earliest attestation: Cardinal Pierre d'Ailly, Imago Mundi (early 15th c.), citing the 9th-century Persian astronomer al-Farghani (Alfraganus): one degree of longitude at the equator = 56.67 miles. Columbus adopted d'Ailly's figures — but d'Ailly had converted al-Farghani's long Arabic miles (~1,960 m) into short Roman miles, producing the 'dwarf Earth' of ~30,194 km circumference vs the true ~40,008 km. Columbus further used the smallest available Earth and the largest available Asia (Esdras: six-sevenths of the world dry land).
Sources: Scientific analysis of d'Ailly's mile conversion: al-Farghani's 56.67 miles/degree in Arabic miles (~1,960 m) = 111.1 km per degree, circumference ~39,986 km; d'Ailly's Roman-mile revision dropped it to 83.872 km/degree and a 30,194 km 'dwarf Earth' — a 25% gap Columbus adopted and built his case on; Columbus's arithmetic: one degree of longitude treated as 56.66 miles → Earth circumference ~20,400 miles (a ~25% underestimate vs 24,901 miles); his Canaries-to-China estimate: ~3,000 miles. He sailed to where he thought the Orient was; the Americas were in the way; Carl Sagan, Cosmos (1980): Columbus 'used the smallest possible circumference of the Earth and the greatest eastward extension of Asia he could find in all the books available to him, and then exaggerated even those. Had the Americas not been in the way, Columbus' expeditions would have failed utterly'; Constant Contact / Columbus miscalculation overview: Columbus read d'Ailly's Imago Mundi; the 56.7 miles-per-degree figure was in longer Arabic miles, not shorter Roman miles; hence ~19,000 vs ~24,901 miles
Falsifiability: Falsifiable by showing the Salamanca faculty's surviving objections concerned sphericity rather than distance — they do not; they concerned the voyage's feasibility. Corroborated by the arithmetic itself: the Arabic-mile vs Roman-mile confusion is a checkable conversion error, and Columbus's 3,000-mile Canaries-to-China estimate vs the true ~10,000+ miles is a matter of record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1486-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.6699,40.9633,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1486</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The real shinobi of Iga and Kōka</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations of the operatives' existence (1487 shogunal records, the 1579/1581 campaigns, Tokugawa employment including Hattori Hanzō, the 1676 compendium); the exact content of the craft rests on later compendia, stopping the grade at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Iga (Mie Prefecture) — the Iga sōkoku ikki
Dates: 1487 → 1676 (confidence: medium)

Professional shinobi existed: Iga and Kōka families produced trained intelligence and infiltration operatives hired by daimyō in 1485–1581; the Tenshō Iga War (1579/1581) destroyed the Iga sōkoku ikki as an independent power; survivors served Tokugawa Ieyasu; the craft was codified in the Bansenshukai (1676).

Earliest attestation: Go Kagami Furoku and the Nochi Kagami supplement: Iga men serving as shinobi in Ashikaga Yoshihisa's camp at Magari, 1487
Sources: Iga ikki (Wikipedia, compiled with cited sources) — the 1487 Nochi Kagami supplement ('shinobi whose names were famous throughout the land'); the failed 1579 Nobukatsu invasion; Nobunaga's 1581 five/six-direction assault; Turnbull quoted on the 1579 defeat as 'one of the greatest triumphs of unconventional warfare over traditional samurai tactics in the whole of Japanese history'; Iga-ryū (Wikipedia, compiled with cited sources) — Iga-mono first appear in the historical record in 1487; the 1581 conquest; Hattori Hanzō's guidance of Tokugawa Ieyasu's 1582 escape through Iga; 200 Iga men settled in Edo's Yotsuya as Tokugawa guards; Bansenshukai (1676), Fujibayashi Yasutake — the Edo-period compendium of ninjutsu: climbing equipment, disguise, espionage craft, concealable armor (primary source, post-dates the operational heyday)
Falsifiability: Proof that the 1487 Iga-shinobi references in the shogunal records, or the Tenshō Iga War accounts, are Edo-period fabrications; or that the Bansenshukai is a modern forgery</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1487-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>136.1296,34.7698,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1487</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The surrender of Granada (2 January 1492)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources — the signed treaty text, a private eyewitness letter six days after the event, and Columbus's contemporary journal entry — converge on the date, the ceremony, and the terms.
Layer: real · Place: The Alhambra, Granada
Dates: 1492-01-02 → 1492-01-02 (confidence: high)

Muhammad XII of Granada (Boabdil) surrendered the Nasrid kingdom — the last Muslim polity of Iberia — to Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castile on 2 January 1492, under the Capitulations of Santa Fe signed 25 November 1491. He received a fief at Laujar de Andarax in the Alpujarras, which he sold in 1493 before crossing to Fez.

Earliest attestation: The Capitulations of Santa Fe, 25 November 1491 (Archivo General de Simancas); eyewitness letter to the bishop of Leon, 8 January 1492; Columbus's journal prologue, 1492.
Sources: Capitulations of Santa Fe, 25 November 1491 (Archivo General de Simancas) — the surrender treaty: religious tolerance and fair treatment for Granada's Muslims in return for capitulation; Eyewitness letter to the bishop of Leon, 8 January 1492 — private contemporary account of the handover ceremony at the Genil (Boabdil with 80-100 horsemen); also records that Ferdinand and Isabella declined Boabdil's offer to kiss their hands, at his mother's insistence; Christopher Columbus, journal prologue, 1492 ("on the 2nd January I saw the royal banners of Your Highnesses planted by force of arms on the towers of the Alhambra"); Granada War historiography (Prescott 1837; Harvey, Islamic Spain 1250 to 1500) — the eight-month siege of April 1491-January 1492, the secret negotiations, the Alpujarras fief and 1493 crossing to Fez
Falsifiability: A contemporary source placing the surrender on a materially different date or denying the Capitulations' terms</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.5881,37.176,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The post-1492 depopulation of the Americas (the Great Dying)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The collapse is multiply documented (tribute records, missionary accounts, pollen/charcoal archaeology), but the record's headline numbers — 60.5M, 90%, 56M ha — all come from one 2019 study's modeling chain, and the climate-link attribution is a hypothesis, not a measured fact.
Layer: real · Place: The Americas (hemisphere-wide claim)
Dates: 1492 → 1600 (confidence: medium)

In the century after 1492, European colonization and introduced disease caused a massive demographic collapse in the Americas — roughly 90% of the pre-contact population died by ~1600 — on a scale that left ~56 million hectares of farmland to revert to forest.

Earliest attestation: Colonial tribute rolls, missionary accounts, and the archaeological/palynological record; the modern quantitative synthesis is Koch et al. 2019
Sources: Koch, Brierley, Maslin &amp; Lewis, 2019, Quaternary Science Reviews: pre-1492 population estimated ~60.5M; ~90% died by 1600; ~56M ha of farmland reverted to forest; atmospheric CO2 fell 7-10 ppm, contributing to the coldest phase of the Little Ice Age (~0.15 C cooling); Denevan (ed.), The Native Population of the Americas in 1492 (1976, rev. 1992): 43-65M — the edited-volume consensus range the field argued over for decades; Antarctic ice-core CO2 record: the 16th-century CO2 minimum coincides with the depopulation window; Koch et al. attribute ~half of the observed dip to New World reforestation
Falsifiability: A published population reconstruction showing no post-1492 discontinuity in the ice-core-linked land-use record, or demographic evidence that the pre-contact population was under 10 million with no 16th-century collapse</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-60.0,0.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Letterlocking — the letter as its own envelope</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Library of Congress's own exhibit states the technique as institutional knowledge, grounded in surviving artifacts; it is the second of the three independent pre-1840 envelope lines. Held below VERIFIED — the grade rests on one institutional summary plus the physical archive rather than the peer-reviewed letterlocking literature.
Layer: real · Place: Early modern Europe (exemplars in European archives)
Dates: 1500 → 1845 (confidence: medium)

Before cheap pre-made envelopes, letters were routinely folded, secured, and sent without any envelope at all — the technique of 'letterlocking' — used alongside handmade envelopes for centuries.

Earliest attestation: Surviving folded-and-sealed letters in European archives, early modern period onward.
Sources: Library of Congress, Inside Adams — 'Opening the Envelope' (letterlocking: 'a technique in which a letter is folded, secured, and sent without the use of an envelope,' used alongside handmade envelopes; mass production ended it); Baylor University, Texas Collection — 'envelope history' (pre-1840s cut-and-fold-your-own world)
Falsifiability: If the entire surviving corpus of folded letters proved to be modern constructions, the technique's documentary basis would fail — it rests on the physical archive itself.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The pointed personal knives that came before the table knife</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple descriptive accounts agree: in the pre-fork era each man had his own knife, seized the joint with his hand, cut off what he wished, and the dish passed on. Knives were kept sharp for their dual role as weapon and eating implement; tooth-picking with the point was a documented bad habit (the habit Richelieu is supposed to have corrected). CORROBORATED on descriptive attestation; the exact ubiquity by country is not dated per-household.
Layer: real · Place: Europe (dining practice)
Dates: 1500 → 1670 (confidence: high)

Before the table knife, the European dining knife was a pointed personal implement — each diner carried his own sharp knife, used it to cut what he had seized with his hand, and the same point served as toothpick, lance for the mouth, and occasional weapon.

Earliest attestation: 17th-century table-practice accounts; the WSU 'Knives and Forks' description of pre-fork practice
Sources: Washington State University Clark collection, 'Knives and Forks': 'each man had his own knife, and at dinner seized the joint with his hand, and cut off what he wished'; Tegan Jones / TodayIFoundOut via Gizmodo (2013): knives as weapon-and-implement; wine-and-ale punctured mouths; the fork's spread reduced the need for a point
Falsifiability: A dated household inventory or table-setting record showing blunt dedicated table knives as the norm before 1600</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Venetian mirror monopoly</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The technique, the Murano confinement, and the export dominance are multiply attested in trade and guild history; the death-penalty enforcement detail rides on period sources rather than modern documentation.
Layer: real · Place: Murano, Venice, Italy
Dates: 1500 → 1700 (confidence: medium)

In the 16th century, Venetian glassmakers on Murano mastered glass backed with a tin-mercury amalgam, giving Europe's only crystal-clear mirrors — and a Venetian export monopoly lasting ~150 years, guarded by laws forbidding glassmakers from leaving and by trade-secrets oaths enforced on penalty of death.

Earliest attestation: Venetian guild records; the 1295 law forbidding glassmakers from leaving the city; 15th–16th-century Venetian mirror production.
Sources: Furniture Library, 'The History of Mirror: Through A Glass, Darkly' — tin-mercury amalgam, ~150 years of Venetian supremacy, trade secrets on penalty of death; IPM, 'Mirror, Mirror' (2019) — 16th-century Venice tin-and-mercury backing, much clearer reflection; Martino Interiors / MURANO GLASS doc — 1295 law forbidding glassmakers from leaving; monopoly lasting centuries; Italian Tribune, history of Venetian glass — Angelo Barovier's cristallo, Murano as Europe's only mirror producer
Falsifiability: Venetian guild records showing no restriction on glassmaker travel, or pre-16th-century Venetian glass mirrors equal in clarity, would break the claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.3567,45.4579,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The scale of the transatlantic slave trade: 12.5 million embarked, 10.7 million disembarked</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Built on ~35,000 documented voyages (~80% of the trade by the compilers' own accounting, with published imputation for the rest), converging across ship logs, financial records, and customs/court archives from all trading nations (Portugal ~5.8M, Britain ~3.3M, France ~1.4M, Spain ~1.1M, Netherlands ~0.55M, US ~0.31M, Denmark ~0.11M per the database's country breakouts). The 12.5M/10.7M/1.8M triad (Eltis &amp; Richardson 2010) is the field's consensus estimate, not a single-author figure. Stops short of VERIFIED because ~20% of voyages are imputed, not documented.
Layer: real · Place: the Atlantic crossing corridor (Africa to the Americas)
Dates: 1501-01-01 → 1867-12-31 (confidence: medium)

Between 1501 and 1867, slaving vessels embarked about 12.5 million African captives on the transatlantic crossing; about 10.7 million disembarked alive in the Americas. About 1.8 million died in the Middle Passage. Just 4% of the embarkations were destined for what became the United States.

Earliest attestation: Ship logs, manifests, merchant ledgers, admiralty and customs records, and post-1808 court prosecution records of illegal slave trading — the documentary base assembled since the 1970s.
Sources: David Eltis &amp; David Richardson, Atlas of the Transatlantic Slave Trade (Yale University Press, 2010) — pp. 2-5: 12.5 million embarked, 10.7 million disembarked; Voyages: The Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database (Rice University / Emory), ~35,000 documented voyages 1514-1866, ~80% coverage with published imputation; Philip Misevich, Daniel Domingues, David Eltis, Nafees M. Khan &amp; Nicholas Radburn, 'A Digital Archive of Slave Voyages' (The Conversation via Smithsonian Magazine, May 2017); Sowande' M. Mustakeem, cartographic analysis drawing on Eltis &amp; Richardson 2010 — '1.8 million, more than 14 percent, of the 12.5 million who embarked never disembarked' (OpenEdition, Slaveries journal)
Falsifiability: Discovery of a comparable independent documentary corpus yielding an embarkation total materially different from 12.5 million; demonstration that the imputation model for missing voyages is systematically biased in one direction.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1501-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0,8.7832,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1501</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Lenox Globe — the apple with dragons</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The object exists, is publicly held and photographed, and its inscription is multiply transcribed by independent scholars; held below VERIFIED because its precise date (1508 vs 1510) varies between institutions and its maker is unknown.
Layer: real · Place: New York Public Library, Rare Book Division (Stephen A. Schwarzman Building)
Dates: 1508 → 1510 (confidence: medium)

The Hunt–Lenox Globe (c. 1508–1510) is the second-oldest known terrestrial globe after Martin Behaim's Erdapfel (1492); a hollow red-copper engraved globe, ca. 112 mm in diameter, housed in the NYPL Rare Book Division; and the only accepted historical object bearing the inscription 'HC SVNT DRACONES' ('here be dragons'), placed just below the equator on the eastern coast of Asia.

Earliest attestation: The globe itself, c. 1508–1510 (copper engraving by an unknown creator; 'the earliest such surviving engraving from the period right after the discovery of the New World' per NYPL); purchased in Paris in 1855 by architect Richard Morris Hunt 'for a song', recognized by bookdealer Henry Stevens, donated to the Lenox Library
Sources: NYPL — The Hunt-Lenox Globe ('one of the earliest surviving terrestrial globes,' 'one of the oldest known cartographic depictions of the Americas,' 'one of only two medieval or Renaissance-era maps or globes known to bear the famous motto'); Wikipedia — Hunt–Lenox Globe (second-oldest terrestrial globe after the Erdapfel; hollow red copper, ca. 112 mm; 'the only known example of an historical map actually using the phrase HC SVNT DRACONES'); Henry Stevens's 1870 recollections via the Wikipedia synthesis (Hunt dining-room provenance; Julius Erasmus Hilgard dating assistance)
Falsifiability: A second accepted pre-1510 globe bearing the phrase would downgrade the 'only accepted object' superlative; a peer-reviewed dating study moving the globe before 1504 would promote the date side of this record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1508-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9822,40.7532,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1508</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Luther Nailed the 95 Theses to the Church Door</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested but single-source and late: the only witness is Melanchthon (1546), who was not in Wittenberg in 1517, and Luther never corroborates his own legend; but routine posting of disputation theses on the Schlosskirche door (the university's notice board) was standard practice, so a routine posting may well have happened — hence CLAIMED, not DEBUNKED.
Layer: real · Place: All Saints' Church (Schlosskirche), Wittenberg, Germany
Dates: 1517 → 1517 (confidence: traditional)

On 31 October 1517 (the eve of All Saints' Day), Martin Luther nailed his 95 Theses on indulgences to the door of All Saints' Church (the Schlosskirche) in Wittenberg — the dramatic hammer scene that popular tradition holds to be the first act of the Reformation.

Earliest attestation: Philipp Melanchthon's preface to the second volume of Luther's Latin works (1546), 29 years after the alleged event — the ONLY source for the posting.
Sources: Melanchthon, preface to Tomus secundus of Luther's Latin works (1546): 'he affixed them publicly to the door of the church which is next to the castle of Wittenberg, the eve of the Feast of All Saints, in the year 1517' — sole attestation; Erwin Iserloh, 'Luthers Thesenanschlag — Tatsache oder Legende?', Trierer Theologische Zeitschrift 70 (1961), 303–312 — the legend thesis, reaffirmed in his later work (Luther zwischen Reform und Reformation, 1962); Luther's letter to Archbishop Albert of Mainz, 31 Oct 1517, enclosing the theses — proves mailing, not nailing; the contemporary paper trail carries the letter, never the door; Luther's own complete silence on the posting across letters, Table Talk, and reminiscences — the act is never mentioned by the alleged actor himself (the negative evidence, honestly recorded); La Civilta Cattolica survey (History or Legend) — notes Melanchthon was 17 and a student at Tubingen in 1517, arriving in Wittenberg only in 1518; not an eyewitness
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1517 source (letter, diary, printer's note, university record) describing the posting would raise the grade to CORROBORATED; a 1517-dated refutation of it would sink it to DEBUNKED</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1517-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.6439,51.8667,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1517</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Theses Went Viral on the Press</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested: the dated letter survives, and multiple independent printed editions with colophons survive in libraries; held below VERIFIED only on exact print-run figures, which are estimates.
Layer: real · Place: Wittenberg to Nuremberg, Leipzig, Basel — the early print corridor
Dates: 1517 → 1518 (confidence: high)

Luther's 95 Theses were printed and spread across Germany and beyond within weeks and months of October 1517 — Nuremberg (December 1517), Leipzig and Basel (January 1518), with a German translation in January 1518 — largely without Luther's involvement: the printing press, not the hammer, made the theses famous.

Earliest attestation: Surviving printed editions: Nuremberg (Dec 1517), Leipzig and Basel (Jan 1518); Luther's letter to Albert of Mainz, 31 Oct 1517
Sources: Surviving printed editions of the 95 Theses: Nuremberg (December 1517), Leipzig (January 1518), Basel (January 1518) — multiple independent surviving witnesses to the rapid print circulation; Luther's letter to Archbishop Albert of Mainz, 31 October 1517, enclosing the theses (also sent the same day to Bishop Jerome Schulz of Brandenburg) — the documented act of publication; German translation of the theses (January 1518) — the move from Latin disputation text to vernacular reading material, the true transmission event
Falsifiability: Provenance-examination showing the December 1517 Nuremberg edition to be a later forgery, or discovery of evidence that Luther himself organized the print run</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1517-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4,51.4,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1517</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The siege of Tenochtitlan, 1521</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Spanish letters, the Nahuatl accounts, and the epidemic record converge on the siege dates, the coalition's composition, and smallpox's decisive role; indigenous casualty figures vary widely by source and stay honest.
Layer: real · Place: Tenochtitlan (present-day Mexico City)
Dates: 1521-05 → 1521-08-13 (confidence: high)

From late May to 13 August 1521, a Spanish–indigenous coalition besieged and took Tenochtitlan, the Triple Alliance capital, after a smallpox epidemic had devastated the city; Cuauhtémoc was captured on 13 August 1521 and the Mexica state fell.

Earliest attestation: Cortés's third carta de relación (1522); Bernal Díaz del Castillo's Historia verdadera; the Florentine Codex, Book 12 (Nahuatl account, c. 1555–1585).
Sources: Florentine Codex, Book 12 (Fray Bernardino de Sahagún — the Nahuatl-side account of the conquest); Wikipedia, 'Siege of Tenochtitlan' — siege dates, the 13 brigantines, the Chapultepec aqueduct cut, 900–1,300 Spaniards plus ~200,000 native allies; Wikipedia, 'Cuitláhuac' — the 10th Huey Tlatoani's 80-day reign (1520) and death from smallpox; MedicalXpress / AP (2020), '500 years ago, another epidemic swept Mexico: smallpox' — the huey zahuatl epidemic: introduced via Narváez's men in 1520, Cuitláhuac's death, the disease's decisive role; globalsecurity.org, 'The Spanish Conquest' — Cortés exploited the hostile tribes' divisions, using tribe against tribe
Falsifiability: Contemporary Mexica-dated records placing the city's fall in a different year, or under a different attacker, would reopen the whole account.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1521-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-99.131,19.435,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1521</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The deathbed copy: the legend and the one letter</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-source attestation through a long transmission chain, with the witness himself describing days of lost memory and vigour; no independent corroboration of what Copernicus perceived. The legend's facts are fenced exactly where the one letter draws them.
Layer: real · Place: Frauenburg (Frombork), Poland — Copernicus's death
Dates: 1543-05-24 → 1543-05-24 (confidence: low)

A finished printed copy of De revolutionibus reached Nicolaus Copernicus on the day of his death (24 May 1543) — the legend of the dying astronomer presented with his book as he lay on his deathbed. The ENTIRE claim rests on one primary source: a letter from Canon Tiedemann Giese to Rheticus, written weeks after the death (recorded via Koestler 1963, citing Prowe 1883-84, vol. 1, pt. 2, p. 554): 'For many days he had been deprived of his memory and mental vigour; he only saw his completed book at the last moment, on the day he died.' Christie's catalog states 'a finished copy of the De revolutionibus only reached Copernicus on the eve of his death, his reaction to this addition is unknown.' Jagiellonian classics scholar Jacek Partyka (2023) calls it 'legend' and wryly doubts Copernicus could have approved anything. Copernicus was apoplectic and possibly unconscious; what he actually perceived is unknown.

Earliest attestation: Single source: Tiedemann Giese's letter to Rheticus, weeks after 24 May 1543. Recorded via Koestler (1963) citing Prowe (1883-84). No independent witness.
Sources: Tiedemann Giese to Rheticus, weeks after 24 May 1543 (via Koestler 1963, citing Prowe, Nicolaus Copernicus, 1883-84, vol. 1, pt. 2, p. 554): 'he only saw his completed book at the last moment, on the day he died'; Christie's lot notes: 'a finished copy of the De revolutionibus only reached Copernicus on the eve of his death, his reaction to this addition is unknown'; Astronomy.com (2023): Jagiellonian scholar Jacek Partyka — 'According to legend, Copernicus was presented with a copy of the published book as he lay on his deathbed'; Edward Rosen (1995) scathing on Koestler's account
Falsifiability: No contemporary evidence could falsify what a dying man perceived; the grade stays CLAIMED unless a second independent witness emerges or the Giese letter is shown to be inauthentic.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1543-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>19.6822,54.3566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1543</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>De revolutionibus (1543): the book as printed</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The 1543 imprint, the surviving manuscript, the Rheticus/Giese/Schreiber/Praetorius contemporary evidence of Osiander's unauthorized preface, Kepler's 1609 exposure, and Gingerich's 269-copy census with the 400-500 print-run estimate — multiple independent documentary and bibliographic lines.
Layer: real · Place: Nuremberg (Johannes Petreius's press, 1543)
Dates: 1543 → 1543 (confidence: high)

Nicolaus Copernicus's De revolutionibus orbium coelestium was printed at Nuremberg by Johannes Petreius in 1543. Rheticus delivered the manuscript in 1542 and supervised the printing, then left for a Leipzig mathematics professorship before completion; Andreas Osiander took over. Osiander added the unsigned instrumentalist preface Ad lectorem ('To the Reader Concerning the Hypotheses of this Work' — 'these hypotheses need not be true nor even probable; if they provide a calculus consistent with the observations, that alone is enough') without Copernicus's authorization: the standard scholarly position is that it was added unsigned and long mistaken for Copernicus's own (evidence: Rheticus crossed it out in his copy and would 'rough up the fellow' if he knew; Giese protested to the Nuremberg city council; Schreiber's note; Praetorius learned of Osiander's authorship from Rheticus and wrote his name in the margin; Kepler methodically exposed Osiander as the author in the 1609 introduction to Astronomia nova). Owen Gingerich's census located 269 surviving copies and estimates the print run at 400-500 copies — usual for the period. The book was 'suspended until corrected' by the Index Decree of 5 March 1616.

Earliest attestation: The 1543 Nuremberg printing itself (Johannes Petreius); Copernicus's manuscript survives at the Jagiellonian Library, Krakow. Osiander's authorship of the preface exposed by Kepler in Astronomia nova (1609); Rheticus's and Praetorius's marginal evidence contemporaneous.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'De revolutionibus orbium coelestium' — Nuremberg 1543, Petreius; Rheticus's delivery and Leipzig departure; Osiander's takeover; the Rheticus/Giese/Schreiber/Praetorius evidence; Kepler's 1609 exposure; Gingerich's 269-copy census and 400-500 estimate; Christie's lot notes (via quoted Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy / Gingerich): 269 surviving copies, print run 'quite usual for the period' at 400-500; 'orbium coelestium' as probable late Osiander addition; Kepler, Astronomia nova (1609), introduction — methodical exposure of Osiander as the preface's author
Falsifiability: A pre-1543 printed edition, or evidence Copernicus authorized Osiander's preface, would revise the record; the manuscript, the 1543 imprint, the crossed-out prefaces in contemporary copies, and Kepler's 1609 exposure form a closed documentary loop.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1543-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.0767,49.4521,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1543</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shakespeare of Stratford wrote the canon — the documentary case</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The historical record is unequivocal in ascribing the canon to a William Shakespeare: 23+ lifetime references name him specifically as a writer, his name stands on the poems' title pages and at least 16 plays published in his lifetime, fellow actors Heminges and Condell assembled the 1623 First Folio, Jonson's Folio eulogy names the 'Sweet Swan of Avon,' and the 1612 Belott-Mountjoy deposition carries his signed testimony. No autograph play manuscript survives, which holds the grade below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Stratford-upon-Avon &amp; London (the Globe), England
Dates: 1564 → 1623 (confidence: high)

William Shakespeare of Stratford-upon-Avon (1564-1616) wrote the plays and poems attributed to him.

Earliest attestation: 1592 — Robert Greene's Groatsworth of Wit attacks Shakespeare as an actor turned playwright ('Shake-scene'); 1593-94 title pages of the poems name William Shakespeare; 1598 title pages of Richard II and Richard III name 'William Shake-speare'
Sources: Robert Greene, Groatsworth of Wit (1592) — 'Shake-scene' attack, contemporary; Title pages: Venus and Adonis (1593), Lucrece (1594), Richard II &amp; Richard III (1598) — 'William Shakespeare' as author; Francis Meres, Palladis Tamia (1598) — names Shakespeare as playwright and poet, lists 12 plays; Heminges &amp; Condell, Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, &amp; Tragedies (First Folio, 1623); Ben Jonson, eulogy in the First Folio (1623) — 'Sweet Swan of Avon'; Belott v. Mountjoy deposition (1612) — Shakespeare's signed legal testimony as witness; Wikipedia, 'Shakespeare authorship question' — survey of the documentary case and scholarly consensus
Falsifiability: A contemporary document showing someone else contracted or paid for the plays under Shakespeare's name, or a contemporary expression of doubt — none exists in 170 years of searching.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1564-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.71,52.19,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1564</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gesner's 1565 lead-holder: the first pencil drawing</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independently attested by the digitized-book record (Biodiversity Heritage Library) and multiple histories; held below VERIFIED because this pass consulted the attestations secondhand, not the 1565 edition itself.
Layer: real · Place: Zurich, Switzerland
Dates: 1565 → 1565 (confidence: high)

In 1565, Conrad Gesner (Conrad von Gesner) published, in his book on fossils, a drawing of a strip of graphite held inside a tube of wood — the first depiction of a lead-holder / wood pencil.

Earliest attestation: Gesner's 1565 publication, held digitized by the Biodiversity Heritage Library.
Sources: NPR / KHSU 'Trace The Remarkable History Of The Humble Pencil' (2016): 'In 1565, Swiss naturalist Conrad Gessner published a drawing of a strip of graphite inside a tube of wood — the first depiction of a wood pencil.'; MRS Bulletin (Cambridge, 1994), 'Leading Up To The Pencil': 'In 1565 the German-Swiss naturalist Konrad von Gesner first wrote about a new writing instrument composed of a chip of black lead inserted into a wooden holder.'
Falsifiability: An earlier published depiction of a lead-holder, or proof the 1565 drawing is a later insertion, would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1565-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.5417,47.3769,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1565</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Delft tower experiment, 1586 — the documented drop Galileo never did</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary self-published account naming a witness and the place, consistent with Stevin's statics program; held below VERIFIED because the only source is Stevin's own book and the measurement was human ears hearing one bang.
Layer: real · Place: Nieuwe Kerk, Delft, Netherlands
Dates: 1586 → 1586 (confidence: medium)

In 1586, Simon Stevin and Jan Cornets de Groot (the father of Hugo de Groot) carried two lead balls - one ten times heavier than the other - up the Nieuwe Kerk tower in Delft, dropped them 30 feet onto a wooden platform, and heard a single bang: the balls fell at the same rate, disproving Aristotle's weight-proportional fall theory.

Earliest attestation: Simon Stevin, De Beghinselen der Weeghconst ('The Principles of the Art of Weighing'), 1586 - Stevin's own contemporary published report of the experiment.
Sources: Simon Stevin, De Beghinselen der Weeghconst (1586): 'Let us take ... two balls of lead, the one ten times bigger and heavier than the other, and let them drop together from 30 feet high ... This proves that Aristotle is wrong.'; St Andrews MacTutor History of Mathematics biography of Simon Stevin - experiment dated 1586, report preceded Galileo's first gravity treatise by 3 years; E. J. Dijksterhuis, The Principal Works of Simon Stevin (1955-1966) - the scholarly edition of Stevin's Dutch text
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence (de Groot family records, Delft civic records) contradicting the experiment, or proof that the Weeghconst passage was a later interpolation, would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1586-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.3571,52.0116,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1586</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The tine evolution: two tines to three to four</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: multiply attested by museum artifacts (the V&amp;A's 1632 hallmarked fork), cutlery scholarship, and period sources (Coryate 1611). Held below VERIFIED only on the exact transition dates, which vary by source and region.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (the V&amp;A's 1632 hallmarked fork)
Dates: 1600 → 1900 (confidence: high)

The European table fork evolved from two tines to three to four. The two-prong fork dominated through the 17th century (the earliest two-prong fork bearing an English hallmark, engraved with the Earl of Rutland's coat of arms, dates to 1632 and is held by the V&amp;A). Between the 17th and 18th centuries the tines increased from two to three and then to four — the widely spaced two-prong fork could not spear peas and grains, and from the late 17th to mid-18th century the fork's profile changed from flat to slightly curved to accommodate scooping. The four-tine design became current in the early 19th century; two-tine forks nevertheless lasted in British common usage into the 20th century.

Earliest attestation: Period cutlery and museum artifacts; the 1632 Earl of Rutland hallmarked two-prong fork (Victoria and Albert Museum, London)
Sources: Scribd: 'The Evolution of the Fork' — 1632 Earl of Rutland hallmarked two-prong fork (V&amp;A); tines increased 2→3→4 between the 17th and 18th c.; flat-to-curved profile, late 17th to mid-18th c.; Thomas Coryate, 'Coryat's Crudities' (1611); Encyclopedia.com: 'Cutlery' — early European forks bronze/silver, two tines; tines varied 2→4 in late 16th/early 17th c.; two-tined fork lasted in British common usage until the 20th c.; Wikipedia: 'Fork' — four-tine design became current in the early 19th century; Fast Company: 'A tool of the devil' — 17th c. addition of third then fourth tine; curving the tines; Scandinavia the last European holdout, end of 17th c.; Charles I's 1633 'It is decent to use a fork'; Queen Elizabeth I preferring fingers
Falsifiability: A securely dated pre-1600 four-tine European table fork would overturn the progression; none is attested</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1600-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cawdrey's Table Alphabeticall, 1604 — the first English dictionary</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The artifact survives: the sole surviving copy of the 1604 first edition is held at the Bodleian Library, Oxford, and was republished by the Bodleian in 2007 with an introduction by John Simpson (chief editor of the OED's second edition). The OED's own historical introduction treats Cawdrey's 1604 volume as the acorn of the tradition ('like placing the original acorn beside the oak that has grown out of it').
Layer: real · Place: London, England (published 1604; Cawdrey was living in Coventry)
Dates: 1604 → 1604 (confidence: high)

In 1604 the schoolmaster Robert Cawdrey published A Table Alphabeticall in London: the first monolingual English dictionary — 120 pages, 2,543 headwords with brief (often single-word) definitions, written 'for the benefit and help of ladies, gentlewomen, or any other unskillful persons.'

Earliest attestation: The 1604 title page itself: 'A table alphabeticall conteyning and teaching the true writing, and vnderstanding of hard vsuall English wordes... gathered for the benefit &amp; helpe of ladies, gentlewomen, or any other vnskilfull persons.'
Sources: Wikipedia — 'A Table Alphabeticall': first monolingual English dictionary, London 1604; 120 pages, 2,543 words with brief definitions; claimed purpose 'for the benefit and help of unskillful persons'; sole surviving copy at the Bodleian Library, Oxford.; Wikipedia — 'Robert Cawdrey': Cawdrey, assisted by his son Thomas, created the first monolingual English dictionary while living in Coventry; first six entries quoted (abandon, abash, abba...).; Bodleian Library Publishing / readings.com.au (2015): the 1604 Table Alphabeticall republished from the sole surviving Bodleian copy with John Simpson's introduction; 'for the benefit of Ladies, Gentlewomen or any other unskilfull persons.'
Falsifiability: The existence of an earlier monolingual English dictionary (there is none in the record; Cawdrey is the consensus origin) or proof the 1604 imprint is misdated would overturn it.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1604-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1604</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first documented telescopes: Lipperhey's 1608 States-General patent bid</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent contemporaneous documentary lines — the States-General Acts, the committee test, the florin payments, the rival Metius application, the Hague newsletter that carried the invention to Venice by November 1608 — but 'first ever inventor' cannot be closed against untestable priority claims, so the grade stops at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Middelburg, Zeeland, Netherlands (Lipperhey's shop; States-General proceedings at The Hague)
Dates: 1608 → 1608 (confidence: high)

The first documented telescopes were made in the Netherlands in 1608: Hans Lipperhey, a spectacle-maker of Middelburg, petitioned the States General for a patent on an instrument 'for seeing things far away as if they were nearby' on 2 October 1608; the assembly tested it (4 Oct), paid him 900 florins (6 Oct), and ordered binocular versions (15 Dec) — denying the patent because Jacob Metius filed a competing application weeks later and the knowledge was already widespread. Lipperhey is the first DOCUMENTED claimant; the original Dutch design (convex objective + concave eyepiece, ~3x) spread across Europe within months. This is the timeline's first early-modern-optics record.

Earliest attestation: Contemporaneous government record: the Acts of the States General of the Netherlands, 2 Oct 1608 — Lipperhey's petition; committee test report 4 Oct; 900-florin payment 6 Oct; binocular-order 15 Dec 1608. Reported by Van Swinden's research from the Hague government archives (summarized in Encyclopaedia Britannica, 9th ed., Telescope article).
Sources: Acts of the States General, 2 Oct 1608: Lipperhey's petition (via Van Swinden's research in the Hague government archives; summarized in Encyclopaedia Britannica, 9th ed., 'Telescope'); Wikipedia, 'History of the telescope' — first record of a telescope: Lipperhey's 2 Oct 1608 patent filing; Metius's competing application weeks later; the patent denied because the knowledge seemed ubiquitous; Britannica (print article, updated 22 March 2024): Lipperhey 'traditionally credited with inventing the telescope (1608)'; 900 florins paid, patent denied, instruments supplied to Henry IV of France before end of 1608; Nick Pelling, 'Who invented the telescope?' (The Free Library, Astronomy Now): the 2 Oct patent submission, 4 Oct committee, 900-florin commission, 14 Oct Zeeland 'young man,' 17 Oct Metius application; the Hague peace-conference diplomats as the transmission vector; Sarpi receiving the newsletter in Venice in November 1608
Falsifiability: A dated 1607-or-earlier document showing a working lens-tube telescope built by a named maker would move the priority — none exists; the 1590 Janssen claim fails on its own timeline (born late 1585, 'age two at invention').</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1608-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.6136,51.4988,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1608</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The compound microscope before Leeuwenhoek (documented by 1619)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two independently documented compound microscopes predate Leeuwenhoek's publications by half a century: Galileo's occhiolino (built 1609–10, demonstrated to Cesi in 1624, testified by Viviani in 1654) and Cornelis Drebbel's two-convex compound microscope presented in London in 1619 (and shown in Rome c. 1622). Giovanni Faber of the Linceans coined the word 'microscope' in 1625 — a word cannot be coined for an instrument that does not exist. Held below VERIFIED on the identity of the absolute first maker: the c. 1590 Janssen attribution (Boreel 1655 / Pierre Borel 1656) is sworn testimony, not contemporary documentation, and Zacharias Janssen's age at the alleged date (2–5) keeps the name contested — likely his father Hans Janssen or grandfather Hans Martens, or no single inventor at all. The instrument's existence by 1619 is the graded claim; who first built it is honestly held open.
Layer: real · Place: London (Drebbel's 1619 demonstration); Rome/Florence (Galileo's occhiolino)
Dates: 1609 → 1619 (confidence: high)

Compound microscopes — two-lens tubes — existed and were publicly demonstrated by 1619 at the latest, well before Antonie van Leeuwenhoek's first publications in the 1670s.

Earliest attestation: Documented exemplars: Galileo's occhiolino 1609–10 (presented to Cesi 1624); Cornelis Drebbel's two-convex compound microscope presented in London 1619.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Timeline of microscope technology' — Janssen attribution (disputed), Galileo occhiolino 1609–10, Drebbel London 1619, Faber coins 'microscope' 1625; IMSS (Florence), 'History of the microscope' (estoria04.pdf) — Viviani's 1654 testimony crediting Galileo with the compound microscope; Fontana's 1646 claim; Drebbel's instruments; Wikipedia, 'Zacharias Janssen' — Boreel 1655 investigation; Johannes Zachariassen's sworn 1590 testimony; Pierre Borel 1656 adoption; the age problem and the Hans Martens alternative
Falsifiability: Proof that the 1619 Drebbel instrument and the 1609–10 Galileo instrument are later fabrications with no contemporary documentation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1609-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1609</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The expulsion of the Moriscos (1609-1614)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The decrees and the multi-kingdom embarkation sequence are documented across independent records; the number rests on contemporary estimates (honestly ranged 270,000-300,000), which holds the grade at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Valencia (first embarkations, 1609)
Dates: 1609 → 1614 (confidence: high)

Philip III of Spain, at the instigation of the Duke of Lerma and Archbishop Juan de Ribera of Valencia, ordered the expulsion of the Moriscos (baptized descendants of Iberian Muslims) between 1609 and 1614. Contemporary accounts put the number expelled at roughly 270,000-300,000 people.

Earliest attestation: The 1609 expulsion decrees and the Council of State decision of April 1609; contemporary embarkation accounts (e.g. Pere Oromig's 'Embarkation of Moriscos in Valencia').
Sources: Expulsion decrees 1609-1614 ("under the pain of death and confiscation ... to take with them no money, bullion, jewels or bills of exchange ... just what they could carry"); Contemporary accounts setting the number expelled at 270,000-300,000 (about 4% of Spain's population); Treaty of Granada, November 1491 — the broken promise (religious tolerance and fair treatment); Cisneros's forced conversions 1499-1501 and the Alpujarras revolts as the breach's record; Wright 2017 ("Ethnic Cleansing in Habsburg Spain: Philip III's Motivations"; annotations Smith 2023) — the Council decision, Lerma's role, the 12,000 killed before embarkation; Dominguez Ortiz on the Valencian economic collapse
Falsifiability: Archival embarkation rolls showing a materially different scale, or proof the decrees were substantially unexecuted</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1609-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.3763,39.4699,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1609</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>January 1610 — the moons of Jupiter (what Galileo really proved with a real instrument)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Galileo's dated notebooks + the published Sidereus Nuncius + multiple independent 1610 confirmations with their own instruments; no serious dispute in the literature.
Layer: real · Place: Padua, Italy (observations); Venice (publication)
Dates: 1610-01-07 → 1610-03-10 (confidence: high)

In January 1610, Galileo Galilei observed four points of light near Jupiter with his ~20x telescope, tracking them night after night (7-15 January) as they changed position relative to the planet; they were moons orbiting Jupiter. Published March 1610 in Sidereus Nuncius ('The Starry Messenger').

Earliest attestation: Galileo's observing notebooks, 7-15 January 1610, then Sidereus Nuncius (Venice, March 1610) - the first scientific work built on telescopic observation.
Sources: Galileo Galilei, Sidereus Nuncius (Venice, March 1610) - the daily observation record with diagrams; Galileo's observing notebooks, 7-15 January 1610 - first entry shows Jupiter with three asterisks east and west; Johannes Kepler, Dissertatio cum Nuncio Sidereo (August 1610) - independent confirmation, delayed only by telescope scarcity; Simon Marius, Mundus Iovialis (1614) - the priority challenge; Galileo's calendar rebuttal (Gregorian Jan 7, 1610 = Julian Dec 28, 1609 vs Marius's claimed Dec 29, 1609)
Falsifiability: None now possible on the discovery: independent confirmations within months (Kepler's Dissertatio, August 1610; Harriot, Gaultier de la Vatelle, Fabri de Peiresc, Marius) and continuous observation ever since make the attested event multiply established.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1610-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.8859,45.4078,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1610</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1621 Plymouth Harvest Feast</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two contemporary or near-contemporary primary sources (Winslow 1622, Bradford's manuscript) attest the feast. Fowl and the five deer are documented; turkey as a centerpiece is not documented, and the wider menu is reconstructed, not attested.
Layer: real · Place: Plymouth, Massachusetts
Dates: 1621-09-21 → 1621-11-11 (confidence: medium)

In autumn 1621, after the first successful harvest at Plymouth, Governor William Bradford's colonists held a three-day feast attended by Massasoit and about ninety Wampanoag men; wildfowl was served, and the Wampanoag guests contributed five deer they hunted themselves.

Earliest attestation: Edward Winslow's letter in Mourt's Relation (London, 1622) — written months after the event, the only detailed eyewitness account; corroborated briefly by William Bradford's Of Plymouth Plantation manuscript (written 1630-1651).
Sources: Edward Winslow's letter, Mourt's Relation (London: John Bellamie, 1622), pp. 60-61; William Bradford, Of Plymouth Plantation (manuscript, 1630-1651) — brief mention of the feast; Plimoth Patuxet Museums research: exactly two primary sources exist for autumn 1621 in Plymouth
Falsifiability: A contemporary document showing the Winslow letter fabricated, or archaeological/documentary evidence that the 1621 harvest failed and no surplus existed to feast on</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1621-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-70.6673,41.9584,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1621</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wampum as colonial legal tender (1637–1661)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Statutes, rates, and account-book payments are quoted directly from colonial records across multiple independent secondary compilations; the inflation mechanism (European manufacturing) is the documented cause of the 1661 end.
Layer: real · Place: Massachusetts Bay / New Netherland (Boston, New Amsterdam)
Dates: 1627 → 1710 (confidence: high)

After contact, European colonists made wampum a currency by law: Massachusetts declared it legal tender in 1637 (6 white or 3 black beads per penny, sums under 1 shilling; extended 1643 to sums up to 40 shillings), the Dutch used it as the colony's circulating medium for half a century, and it remained legal tender in New England until 1661 (New York to 1673, North Carolina briefly 1710). European mass-manufacturing then inflated its value roughly a hundredfold and killed it as money — a documented commodity-currency collapse.

Earliest attestation: Massachusetts Bay legal-tender statutes 1637/1643; Dutch West India Company accounts; Ashbel Woodward's 19th-c. numismatic paper compiling church, school, and ferry payments in wampum
Sources: JSTOR Daily — 'Wampum was Massachusetts' First Legal Currency': 1637 legal tender (6 white / 3 black per penny, &lt;1s), 1643 extension (≤40s); used into the 19th century; Ashbel Woodward — 'Wampum' (Numismatic and Antiquarian Society of Philadelphia paper, via p-books): church contributions, 1683 Flatbush schoolmaster paid in wampum, 1693 NY–Brooklyn ferry 8 stivers in wampum; 'for about a quarter of a century a legal tender in New England... among the Dutch during half a century often the only circulating medium'; 'Origins of Money: Wampum and Trade' — legal tender New England 1637–1661, NY to 1673, NC 1710; value 'inflated one hundred fold by Western harvesting and manufacturing techniques'; Rogue Art Historian — New England colonial governments 'briefly accepted wampum strings as legal tender, responding to a scarcity of metal coinage'; the Haudenosaunee Guide for Educators frames this as European appropriation
Falsifiability: Colonial statutes and account books showing wampum never denominated obligations — the statutes exist and are quoted verbatim in the sources</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1627-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.06,42.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1627</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Harvey describes the circulation of the blood (1628)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Published experimental demonstration, independently replicated, with the missing capillaries observed by Malpighi (1661) completing the circuit.
Layer: real · Place: London (experiments); Frankfurt (publication)
Dates: 1628 → 1628 (confidence: high)

William Harvey published the experimental demonstration that blood circulates — pumped by the heart through arteries, returning through veins — in Exercitatio Anatomica de Motu Cordis et Sanguinis in Animalibus (Frankfurt, 1628).

Earliest attestation: De Motu Cordis itself (1628); Harvey's Lumleian lectures (1616–1618) survive in manuscript
Sources: William Harvey, Exercitatio Anatomica de Motu Cordis et Sanguinis in Animalibus (Frankfurt: Fitzer, 1628); Harvey's Lumleian lecture notes (1616), British Library manuscript; Independent replication of the circulation experiments by Harvey's successors (e.g., Malpighi's 1661 capillary observation in frog lungs, completing the circuit)
Falsifiability: Proof the 1628 text is a forgery, or a dated earlier publication containing the full circulatory circuit</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1628-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1628</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Loudun possessions, 1632–1634: the trial that burned a priest</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The documentary event is multiply attested: the trial transcript, the dated sentence, contemporary accounts, and the long scholarly consensus on the persecution reading. The grade covers the events and the paper trail. The entity — what, if anything, possessed the nuns — is ungraded, per the attestation rule.
Layer: real · Place: Loudun, Poitou, Kingdom of France
Dates: 1632 → 1634 (confidence: high)

In 1632–1634 in Loudun, Kingdom of France, the nuns of the Ursuline convent reported demonic possession. The parish priest Urbain Grandier was accused of causing the possessions by sorcery, tried by a royal commission (Cardinal Richelieu's), convicted — on the evidence of the nuns' own demonic identifications (including the 'Astaroth' testimony) and a presented pact with the Devil — tortured without confessing, and burned at the stake on 18 August 1634. Public exorcisms of the nuns continued for years after his death (to c. 1638), drawing crowds. Most modern commentators (Jules Michelet, Aldous Huxley, historians of European witchcraft) read the case as a politically motivated persecution led by Richelieu.

Earliest attestation: The authorized transcript of the 1634 trial (sentence pronounced 18 August 1634); contemporary pamphlet and chronicle accounts; the Ursuline convent's own records
Sources: The authorized transcript of Grandier's 1634 trial — sentence of 18 August 1634 (amende honorable, torture, burning at the stake at the place Sainte-Croix); Wikipedia, 'Loudun possessions' — trial sequence, Richelieu's commission, the continued post-1634 exorcisms; Encyclopedia.com, 'Grandier, Urbain (d. 1634)' — the 1632 onset, the nuns' accusations, the libel-on-Richelieu motive, the trial's 'evidence of Astaroth'; Aldous Huxley, The Devils of Loudun (1952) — literature built from the documentary record, fenced as interpretation not evidence
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the 1634 trial transcript is inauthentic; contemporary documentation that Grandier was never executed or died otherwise. This record claims the documentary events (trial, burning, continued exorcisms), never the cause of the nuns' behavior.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1632-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.08,47.01,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1632</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Taj Mahal: Shah Jahan's 1632-1653 marble mausoleum at Agra</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The artifact stands, immovable, on the Yamuna: the monument's commission, patron, dates, and chief architect are multiply attested — contemporary Mughal court chronicles, European travelers' accounts of the period, the ASI's survey record, and UNESCO's 1983 World Heritage inscription as the 'jewel of Muslim art in India.' Independent contemporary + modern attestation, plus the artifact itself.
Layer: real · Place: Taj Mahal, Agra, Uttar Pradesh, India
Dates: 1632 → 1653 (confidence: medium)

The Taj Mahal is the marble mausoleum on the Yamuna at Agra commissioned by the fifth Mughal emperor Shah Jahan (r. 1628-1658) for his wife Mumtaz Mahal (died 1631 giving birth to their fourteenth child); construction ran c. 1632-1648 with the surrounding complex finished c. 1653, under the principal direction of the court architect Ustad Ahmad Lahauri, using Makrana marble and pietra-dura inlay.

Earliest attestation: The reign's own court chronicles (the Badshah Nama of Abdul Hamid Lahori, completed c. 1648, covering the first two decades of Shah Jahan's reign including the Taj's construction); contemporary European travelers' accounts; the Archaeological Survey of India survey record; UNESCO World Heritage inscription 1983.
Sources: UNESCO World Heritage Centre — 'Taj Mahal' inscribed 1983: 'the jewel of Muslim art in India and one of the universally admired masterpieces of the world's heritage.'; Badshah Nama of Abdul Hamid Lahori (completed c. 1648) — the reign's court chronicle covering Shah Jahan's first two decades, including the Taj Mahal's construction and the atelier.; Archaeological Survey of India — the monument's survey record and the official guardian's position on the construction history; ASI classifies the artisan-mutilation story as a folk tale.; Contemporary European travelers' accounts of Agra during and after construction — independent foreign attestation of the building campaign, noted in ASI and secondary literature.
Falsifiability: Demolition or scientific re-dating of the monument's core fabric contradicting the 1632-1653 window, or the production of a contemporary document assigning the commission to another emperor or another subject.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1632-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>78.042,27.175,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1632</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1633 sentence: what the Inquisition actually wrote</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The sentence is preserved in the full trial dossier (multiple overlapping records: minutes, depositions, injunction, sentence text), the canon-law categories are documented, and the commutation and house-arrest residences are multiply attested — independent lines from the dossier, the Bellarmine file, and the secondary scholarly apparatus (Finocchiaro, Heilbron, Fantoli).
Layer: real · Place: Convent of Santa Maria sopra Minerva, Rome (site of the 22 June 1633 abjuration and sentencing)
Dates: 1633-06-22 → 1633-06-22 (confidence: high)

The Roman Inquisition's sentence of 22 June 1633 found Galileo Galilei guilty not of formal heresy but of 'vehement suspicion of heresy' — a technical canon-law category short of the most serious crime (Finocchiaro, The Galileo Affair). He abjured, cursed, and detested heliocentrism kneeling before the Congregation; the Dialogue was banned; he was ordered to recite the seven penitential psalms weekly for three years. The formal sentence condemned him 'to prison at the pleasure of the Sacred Congregation' — but this was commuted the next day to house arrest, which he served the rest of his life: first the Tuscan Embassy and Villa Medici in Rome, then Archbishop Piccolomini's residence in Siena (five months), then his own Villa Il Gioiello at Arcetri. He was never tortured (the 21 June 1633 deposition records only a verbal threat — 'otherwise one would have recourse to torture' — which Von Gebler describes as never leaving the ordinary judgment hall) and never entered a dungeon (during proceedings he occupied a five-room suite in the Holy Office palace with his own valet). The 16 June 1633 minutes ordered interrogation 'even with the threat of torture'; the threat was the instrument, not the act.

Earliest attestation: The Inquisition trial dossier: sentence of 22 June 1633; 16 June 1633 minutes (interrogation order); 21 June 1633 fourth deposition (verbal threat, Galileo's 'I am here to obey' reply); the filed special injunction of 26 February 1616. Published in full in Favaro's National Edition (1890-1909); translated in Finocchiaro's The Galileo Affair: A Documentary History (1989) and Retrying Galileo (2005).
Sources: Maurice Finocchiaro, The Galileo Affair: A Documentary History (1989) and Retrying Galileo (2005) — 'vehement suspicion of heresy' as intermediate category; 16 June 1633 minutes (p. 247); Laemmel 1926-1929 X-ray/UV tests refuting the forgery thesis (p. 262); The 1633 trial dossier: sentence of 22 June 1633; fourth deposition 21 June 1633 (verbal threat, 'I am here to obey'); filed injunction 26 Feb 1616 — full text in Favaro's National Edition (1890-1909); Wikipedia, 'Galileo affair' — Fantoli (2005) and Heilbron (2005) on 'vehemently suspect of heresy' as a technical term not implying Copernicanism itself was judged heretical; formal sentence and next-day commutation; Bellarmine's certificate of 26 May 1616 (text via famous-trials.com, from Koestler's Sleepwalkers) — Galileo 'has not abjured... only the declaration made by the Holy Father... has been notified to him'
Falsifiability: A record of formal-heresy sentencing or of physical torture would break the grade; the complete dossier contains neither, and the 19th-century forgery suspicion against the 1616 injunction note was refuted by Rudolf Laemmel's 1926-1929 X-ray/ultraviolet tests (no erased-and-overwritten text anywhere in the problematic documents).</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1633-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4777,41.8972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1633</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tulip mania, winter 1636–1637 — the futures trade and its February collapse</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The futures trade, the high prices for rare bulbs, and the February 1637 collapse are multiply documented in 1630s notarial records, guild disputes, and pamphlets — the event itself is not in doubt.
Layer: real · Place: Amsterdam / Haarlem, Dutch Republic
Dates: 1636-10 → 1637-02 (confidence: high)

In the winter of 1636–1637, Dutch merchants and skilled craftsmen traded tulip-bulb futures contracts at rapidly rising prices; in February 1637 buyers defaulted on their promises and the market for those contracts collapsed.

Earliest attestation: 1630s notarial contracts, florists' guild dispute records, and the 1637 pamphlet literature
Sources: Anne Goldgar, Tulipmania: Money, Honor, and Knowledge in the Dutch Golden Age (University of Chicago Press, 2007): archival audit of contracts, inventories, letters, court records — the trade was 'a fairly small group', almost exclusively merchants and skilled craftsmen, wealthy but not nobility; Peter M. Garber, 'Tulipmania,' Journal of Political Economy 97(3) (1989): rare-bulb prices reflected genuine scarcity (the tulip breaking virus); the common-bulb futures were 'no more than a meaningless winter drinking game'; The 1637 pamphlet literature and notarial records (as surveyed in Goldgar 2007 and the Wikipedia Tulip mania article): the February 1637 price collapse and the contract defaults
Falsifiability: Proof that the 1630s contract and notarial records are fabrications, or archival evidence that no price collapse occurred in February 1637</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1636-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.9,52.37,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1636</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Torricelli invents the barometer — and the vacuum (1643–44)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree: the pump-makers' 33-foot limit and Galileo's suggestion; Torricelli's insight (air pressure pushes, the vacuum doesn't pull); the 1643 mercury experiment — 6-foot tube, 30 inches retained, the Torricellian vacuum above; the day-to-day variation noticed and correctly attributed to atmospheric pressure (the barometer's birth); the 1644 Viviani mercury run; Berti's ~1641 water tube as precedent; Boyle's 1665 coinage of 'barometer' ('weight measure'); Pascal's 1648 Puy de Dôme confirmation; the Torr (1 mmHg). The 'lone genius' version is fenced — Berti, Viviani, Galileo, Boyle, and Pascal all have documented shares.
Layer: real · Place: Florence
Dates: 1643 → 1644 (confidence: high)

In 1643, Evangelista Torricelli — Galileo's former assistant — invented the mercury barometer: a 6-foot glass tube filled with mercury, inverted in a dish, retaining 30 inches (760 mm) of column against air pressure — and in the space above the column created the first artificial vacuum (the Torricellian vacuum).

Earliest attestation: Torricelli's correspondence and the reported experiment, 1643–1644
Sources: The Free Library (HarperCollins): 'In 1643, to check this theory, Torricelli made use of mercury... Torricelli filled a 6-foot length of glass tubing with mercury, stoppered the open end, upended it in a dish of mercury, unstoppered it, and found the mercury pouring out of the tube, but not altogether: 30 inches of mercury remained in the tube, as expected. Above the mercury in the upended tube was a vacuum... It was the first one ever artificially created — a Torricellian vacuum. Torricelli noticed that the height of the mercury column varied slightly from day to day and surmised correctly that the atmosphere possessed a slightly different pressure at different times. He had invented the first barometer.'; CERN, 'History of Vacuum Devices' (Redhead): 'The first recorded experiment to produce a vacuum appears to have taken place in about 1641 when Gasparo Berti experimented with a water barometer... In 1644 Vincenzio Viviani repeated Berti's experiment using a mercury-filled glass tube... The experiment was probably planned by Evangelista Torricelli who was credited by earlier historians with performing the famous experiment in 1643.'; Handy Weather Answer Book (via Papertrell): 'Invented in 1644 by Evangelista Torricelli (1608–1647)... He tested his theory by filling a dish with mercury... 30 inches (760 millimeters) remained... Not only did this experiment prove Torricelli's theory that air had pressure, but he was also the first to create a vacuum (now called a Torricellian vacuum). The word "barometer," which means "weight measure," was not coined until 1665 by Irish scientist and theologian Robert Boyle.'
Falsifiability: Evidence that the mercury experiment predates 1643 or belongs to another investigator</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1643-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.2558,43.7696,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1643</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Old Deluder Satan Act of 1647</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The statute text survives as a primary document with multiple independent lines of confirmation (the General Court record tradition, HeinOnline text, encyclopedia treatment); the 1642 predecessor and 1648 extension converge on the colony's compulsory-schooling project.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts (seat of the Massachusetts General Court)
Dates: 1647 → 1647 (confidence: high)

In 1647 the Massachusetts General Court passed the 'Old Deluder Satan' school law: every township with 50 or more households had to hire and maintain a teacher for reading and writing; towns of 100 or more families had to establish a grammar school preparing students for the university; towns neglecting the order for over a year paid 5 pounds per annum to the next school. The act's first line frames the project as thwarting 'that old deluder, Satan' in keeping men from the knowledge of the Scriptures.

Earliest attestation: The statute text itself (surviving primary document); 1642/1647/1648 Massachusetts School Laws
Sources: Massachusetts School Laws — Wikipedia (1647 act text, 50-household/100-family provisions, 5-pound fine, John Cotton influence); Louis Pressbooks — full statute text of the 1647 law (HeinOnline blog; the 'fifty Housholders' / 'one hundred Families' clauses); First Amendment Encyclopedia (MTSU) — the act as laying 'the basis for public schools in America'; 1642 parent-duty predecessor; Hazlett 2011, 'American Education's Beginnings' (ERIC EJ944210) — compulsory education 'arguably originated' with the 1642/1647/1648 acts; laws not strictly enforced until Mann's era
Falsifiability: The General Court's records showing no 1647 act, or a forgery proof for the statute text</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1647-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1647</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Huygens builds the first pendulum clock (25 December 1656)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree: the 25 December 1656 completion, the Coster construction, the 1657 patent, the Horologium (1658) description; the accuracy leap (15 min/day → 15 sec/day, later &lt;10 sec/day); Galileo's isochronism discovery (~1582–1602) and his unbuilt 1637/1641 design (partly constructed by his son Vincenzo in 1649, never finished — 'neither lived to build it'); Huygens' 1673 cycloid (Horologium Oscillatorium) making the pendulum truly isochronous; the seconds pendulum (0.994 m); the 300-year reign until quartz. The 'Galileo invented the pendulum clock' telling is fenced — he found the principle and sketched the design; Huygens built the first working clock fourteen years after Galileo's death.
Layer: real · Place: The Hague (Huygens; built by Coster)
Dates: 1656-12-25 → 1656-12-25 (confidence: high)

On 25 December 1656, Christiaan Huygens completed the first working pendulum clock — built by Salomon Coster, patented 1657, described in Horologium (1658) — cutting daily error from ~15 minutes to ~15 seconds and making the pendulum clock the world's most accurate timekeeper for 300 years.

Earliest attestation: Huygens' records; the Coster-built clock; Horologium (1658)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Pendulum clock': 'The pendulum clock was invented on 25 December 1656 by Dutch scientist and inventor Christiaan Huygens. The invention was patented the following year. Huygens contracted the construction of his clock to Salomon Coster.' Galileo 'had discovered the key property... they behave isochronically.' 'In 1637, Galileo described a mechanism to his son, Vincenzo that could keep a pendulum swinging. The mechanism was partly constructed by his son in 1649.' Accuracy: 'about 15 minutes per day' before, '15 seconds per day' after.; Things Have History, 'Galileo's pendulum, or the clock he never lived to build': 'It wasn't until 1641 — when he was 77, blind, and under house arrest at his villa at Arcetri — that he finally conceived the design. He described a pinwheel escapement mechanism to his son Vincenzio from memory; Vincenzio drew it up. Neither of them lived to build it. Galileo died in January 1642; Vincenzio in 1649... Christiaan Huygens, working independently in The Hague, built the first functioning pendulum clock in 1656... His clock reduced daily error from fifteen minutes to under a minute. Pendulum clocks would remain the world's most accurate timekeepers for three hundred years, until quartz. The man who found time's steady beat inside a swinging lamp never heard it counted in a clock.'; GitHub, 'The Pendulum Story': 'Christiaan Huygens was twenty-seven... when, on Christmas Day 1656, he completed the first working pendulum clock... drift of perhaps 10 seconds per day, against the previous state of the art's 15 minutes. Huygens filed for patent on 16 June 1657.' The 1673 Horologium Oscillatorium: the cycloid — 'a simple circular pendulum is only approximately isochronous... That curve is the cycloid.' Also the centripetal force law, vis viva, and T = 2π√(ℓ/g).
Falsifiability: Evidence of a working pendulum clock predating December 1656</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1656-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.3007,52.0705,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1656</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Puritan ban on Christmas (Massachusetts Bay, 1659-1681)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The statute text survives and is quoted verbatim across independent reports; the five-shilling fine, the 1659 enactment, and the 1681 repeal are multiply attested.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts Bay Colony
Dates: 1659-05-11 → 1681 (confidence: high)

In 1659 the Massachusetts Bay Colony made it a crime to observe Christmas — five shillings fine per offense — and the ban stood for 22 years until its repeal in 1681.

Earliest attestation: The Massachusetts General Court statute, 11 May 1659: 'For preventing disorders arising in several places within this jurisdiction, by reason of some still observing such festivals as were superstitiously kept in other countries... whosoever shall be found observing any such day as Christmas or the like, either by for-bearing of labor, feasting, or any other way... shall pay for every such offense five shillings, as a fine to the county.'
Sources: Massachusetts General Court statute, 11 May 1659 — statute text quoted verbatim (five-shilling fine; 'superstitiously kept in other countries'); Nissenbaum, S., The Battle for Christmas (Knopf, 1996): the Puritans' scriptural objection — nowhere in the Bible is the Nativity's celebration mentioned; Reason, 'Years Without a Santa Claus' (2012): the 1659 ban, five-shilling fine, repeal 1681 under Crown pressure; Increase Mather's 1687 denunciation of 'Christmas-keepers'; Christian Post, 'This week in Christian history: Massachusetts bans Christmas' (2023): Dana P. Marriott (American Heritage) on the 1681 repeal as a Crown-imposed conformity, not a Christmas victory; William Bradford, Of Plymouth Plantation: the 1621 Christmas entry — the newcomers' day off ended when found 'playing stool-ball and such like sports' in the street
Falsifiability: The statute text is documentary; a repeal record dated before 1681 would refine the end date. The ban's existence is not seriously contestable.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1659-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1659</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The rounded-tip table knife's French standardization</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent attestation lines converge on late-17th-century France as the period of standardization: the fork's documented adoption removed the functional need for a pointed knife, and surviving tableware chronologies place the blunt form in the Louis-XIV era. The Richelieu-1637 origin is killed (see evt-richelieu-invented-table-knife-myth-001), which is itself evidence the real change came later. Held below VERIFIED on the exact edict text, which this pass did not verify from primary sources.
Layer: real · Place: France (court tradition)
Dates: 1660 → 1700 (confidence: medium)

The blunt/rounded-tip table knife became the European standard through a gradual 17th-century French development — the fork's spread removed the need for a pointed table knife, and French court etiquette standardized the blunt form (commonly associated with Louis XIV's reign, with 1669 and 1699 royal-edict variants in the telling).

Earliest attestation: Late-17th-century French court practice; rounded-tip table knives as material culture of the period
Sources: The Retrospectors, 'Cardinal Richelieu and the Table Knife' (2024): the 1699 royal-edict popularization across France; Tegan Jones / TodayIFoundOut via Gizmodo, 'The History of Knives, Forks and Spoons' (2013): the 1669 Louis XIV telling — sharp knives made illegal at table, blunter/wider ones substituted; Washington State University Clark collection, 'Knives and Forks': pre-fork table practice — each man his own pointed knife, joint seized by hand
Falsifiability: A dated, provenanced rounded-tip table knife from before 1600, or evidence that the blunt form was standard at European tables before the French court took it up</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1660-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1660</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Christopher Merret's 1662 Royal Society paper: the documented sparkling-wine method</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The paper exists in the Royal Society archives; its contents are quoted verbatim by multiple independent sources (National Geographic, Smithsonian, Tim Atkin MW, Laithwaites, Tom Stevenson). It documents the deliberate method — sugar/molasses added to finished wine in a sealed bottle to produce sparkle — six years before Dom Pérignon arrived at Hautvillers (1668).
Layer: real · Place: London, England (Royal Society)
Dates: 1662-12 → 1662-12 (confidence: high)

In December 1662, the English scientist and physician Christopher Merret presented a paper to the Royal Society documenting the deliberate addition of sugar and molasses to finished wine to make it 'brisk and sparkling' — the technique that became the basis of champagne production.

Earliest attestation: Christopher Merret's paper to the Royal Society, December 1662: 'Our wine-coopers of recent times use vast quantities of Sugar Molasses to all sorts of wines to make them brisk and sparkling.' The eight-page report detailed experiments by cider makers who added sugar to dry cider before bottling to produce sparkling cider through a second fermentation.
Sources: National Geographic, 'Who put the bubbles in Champagne?' — Merret's December 1662 Royal Society paper: 'Our wine-coopers of recent times use vast quantities of Sugar Molasses to all sorts of wines to make them brisk and sparkling'; 'This description is the first documented use of deliberately adding sugars to wine in a sealed bottle, the technique that became the basis of Champagne production'; Smithsonian Magazine, Sarah Zielinski (2009) — 'It was an English scientist and physician, Christopher Merrett, who discovered the process of double fermentation; he presented his paper on the subject to the Royal Society in 1662'; Tim Atkin MW, 'A beginner's guide to Champagne' — 'Christopher Merret presented a paper to the Royal Society about sparkling wine in December 1662, six years before Dom Perignon arrived in Champagne'; thisdayinwinehistory.com, 'The Discovery of Sparkling Wine' — the eight-page 1662 report detailed cider makers' experiments adding sugar to dry cider before bottling to produce sparkling cider through a second fermentation; Laithwaites (Richard Bull), 'Beginner's Guide to English Sparkling Wine' — Royal Society archives show the December 1662 Merret paper documenting the traditional method 'a full 30 years before Champagne officially embraced it'
Falsifiability: Proof that the Royal Society paper is misdated or misattributed, or that an earlier documented description of the deliberate sealed-bottle second-fermentation method exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1662-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1662</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lower's dog-to-dog transfusions (1665)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary published report of witnessed experiments; the famous bled-and-revived dog is multiply cited in Royal Society correspondence.
Layer: real · Place: Oxford / London (Royal Society)
Dates: 1665 → 1666 (confidence: medium)

Richard Lower performed the first successful documented blood transfusions between animals: in 1665 he bled a dog nearly to death and revived it by transfusing the blood of a second dog through a tied artery, demonstrating the technique on circulation's new map.

Earliest attestation: Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society (1666), reporting Lower's method of 'Transfusing the Bloud out of one Animal into another'
Sources: Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society (1666) — 'The Method observed in Transfusing the Bloud out of one Animal into another' (Lower's method, reported by Oldenburg); Irish Blood Transfusion Service history — '1665: Dr. Richard Lower successfully transfused blood from one dog to another'; News-Medical history of blood transfusion — 'The first successful blood transfusion recorded was performed by British physician Richard Lower in 1665 when he bled a dog almost to death and then revived the animal by transfusing blood from another dog via a tied artery.'
Falsifiability: Proof the 1665/66 Royal Society reports are fabricated or that no such experiments occurred</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1665-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1665</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Newton's fluxions: the unprinted calculus of 1665–1666</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent manuscripts, dated correspondence, and the testimony of contemporaries (Barrow, Collins, Oldenburg) all attest the 1665–1676 development; publication priority rests with Leibniz, so VERIFIED stays parked.
Layer: real · Place: Cambridge (Trinity College), England
Dates: 1665 → 1676 (confidence: high)

Isaac Newton developed his own method of infinitesimals (the method of fluxions) independently in 1665–1666 during the plague years, circulated it in manuscript (De analysi, 1669, via Barrow and Collins; De methodis, 1671), and communicated results to Leibniz through the 1676 epistolae (via Henry Oldenburg), but did not publish his calculus until decades later — well after Leibniz's 1684 publication.

Earliest attestation: Newton's 1665–1666 fluxion manuscripts; De analysi per aequationes numero terminorum infinitas (1669), circulated in manuscript by Isaac Barrow
Sources: Newton's De analysi (1669) manuscript, circulated via Isaac Barrow and John Collins; Newton's epistola prior (13 June 1676) and epistola posterior (24 October 1676), via Henry Oldenburg — published in the Commercium Epistolicum (1712/13) itself; Westfall, R.S., Never at Rest: A Biography of Isaac Newton (1980); Hall, A.R., Philosophers at War: The Quarrel between Newton and Leibniz (1980)
Falsifiability: Dated manuscript evidence showing Newton began work on fluxions only after receiving Leibniz's 1676–1677 communications</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1665-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.1218,52.2053,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1665</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Fire of London, 2–6 September 1666, and the law written in its ashes</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED by convergence: two independent eyewitness diarists (Pepys, Evelyn), the first newspaper account (London Gazette), the statutory afterlife (Rebuilding Act 1667 — the brick-and-stone code, the jetties ban, the builder registration), and the commercial afterlife (Barbon's Fire Office 1680–81, 'the world's first fire insurance company' per the London Fire Brigade's own history; by the 1680s over 4,000 subscribers; firemarks and the insurance brigades). The event, its scale, and its consequences are all independently documented.
Layer: real · Place: Pudding Lane, City of London (the street no longer exists — built over after the fire)
Dates: 1666-09-02 → 1666-09-06 (confidence: high)

The Great Fire of London burned 2–6 September 1666, destroying ~13,200 houses, 89 parish churches, and St Paul's Cathedral, leaving ~80,000 Londoners homeless — and wrote the law in its ashes: the Rebuilding Act 1667 (the first modern building code) and Barbon's Fire Office 1680 (the world's first fire-insurance company).

Earliest attestation: Samuel Pepys's diary (2–7 September 1666 entries — burying his wine and papers; watching St Paul's burn from a boat); John Evelyn's diary; the London Gazette (first issue carried the fire account); the Bills of Mortality (the death-toll-6 record and its undercounting). The Rebuilding Act 1667 (the statute itself); Barbon's Fire Office 1680–81 (London Fire Brigade's own history; Barbon's own pamphlets; Gresham College lecture).
Sources: Samuel Pepys, diary, 2–7 September 1666 (public domain) — the fire's course as witnessed: Pudding Lane, the mayor's inaction, the pigeons of St Paul's, the buried wine; burying his wine and papers in the garden; London Gazette, first issue (September 1666) — the first newspaper account of the fire; Bills of Mortality, September 1666 — the recorded death toll of 6 (the undercount fenced: parish lists lost with the churches; the poor unrecorded); The Rebuilding Act 1667 (the statute) — brick/stone exteriors, no overhanging jetties, four permitted house types, builder registration at the Guildhall (6s 8d fee); London Fire Brigade, 'Early insurance brigades' — 'The first insurance company, called the Fire Office was formed in 1680 by the economist and financial speculator Nicolas Barbon'; firemarks, the watermen brigades, the Phoenix name from 1705; Insurance Times, 'Firemarks: insurers on their marks' — 13,200 homes, 89 churches, St Paul's destroyed; Barbon's 1667 beginnings, the Fire Office 1680, the Friendly Society 1683, Hand in Hand 1696; Gresham College, 'Protecting London — The Phoenix' — Barbon's Fire Office at the back of the Royal Exchange; 2.5% brick / 5% timber premiums; over 4,000 subscribers by the 1680s; Wikipedia, 'Nicholas Barbon' — the 1680–81 Insurance Office for Houses (up to 5,000 households, 31-year terms); the 1690 National Land Bank noted as the banking-history fence line (Honoria Blythe's beat)
Falsifiability: None plausibly reachable: the documentary density (diaries, the Gazette, the Bills, the statute, the insurance records) fixes the event beyond the reach of ordinary revision.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1666-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0859,51.5123,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1666</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Denys's 1667 transfusions and the 150-year ban</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Denys's own contemporary publications, the murder trial's public record, and the documented 1668–70 condemnations form multiple independent lines; exact transfused volumes vary by retelling and are not part of the graded claim.
Layer: real · Place: Paris
Dates: 1667 → 1670 (confidence: high)

Jean-Baptiste Denys (physician to Louis XIV) performed the first documented animal-to-human blood transfusions: 15 June 1667, lamb's blood into a 15-year-old boy; then a laborer; in December 1667 into Antoine Mauroy, who died. Denys was tried for murder in 1668 and acquitted; the Paris Faculty of Medicine condemned the practice (1668) and the French Parliament banned it (1670), opening the ~150-year hiatus in transfusion.

Earliest attestation: Denys's own published accounts (1667–68); Philosophical Transactions correspondence; court records of the 1668 murder trial (acquittal)
Sources: Jean-Baptiste Denys, published case accounts (1667–68) — the June 15 lamb-to-boy transfusion, the laborer, the December Mauroy case; Philosophical Transactions (1667–68) — Denys's correspondence reporting the transfusions; News-Medical / Irish Blood Transfusion Service histories — '1667: Jean-Baptiste Denis, physician to Louis XIV, transfused the blood of a lamb into a human'; the ban following Mauroy's death; Transfusion-medicine histories (e.g., Scribd 'Highlights of Transfusion Medicine History') — 'Within 10 years, transfusing the blood of animals to humans becomes prohibited by law because of reactions'
Falsifiability: Proof Denys's published case reports are fabrications, or a dated earlier human transfusion record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1667-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1667</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dom Pérignon's real craft: blending and white wine from black grapes</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Pérignon's position and tenure are documented; his technical contributions (blending, white-from-black-grape technique) are carried by the specialist historiography rather than by contemporary abbey paperwork, so the record sits one rung below VERIFIED. This is the foil record: it is what the DEBUNKED invention legend killed, and it must survive it — a real cellar master with real craft.
Layer: real · Place: Abbey of Saint-Pierre d'Hautvillers, Champagne, France
Dates: 1668 → 1715 (confidence: medium)

Dom Pérignon (1638–1715), cellar master of the Abbey of Saint-Pierre d'Hautvillers from 1668, made genuine contributions to winemaking: he mastered the technique of producing white wine from black/red grapes (gentle pressing of clear juice, blending across vineyards), contributing substantially to the development of champagne as it is known today.

Earliest attestation: Pérignon's role as cellar master at Hautvillers is documented from 1668. His technical contributions (blending, gentle pressing to make white wine from black grapes) are carried by the champagne historiography (National Geographic: 'he mastered the technique that allows winemakers to produce white wines from red grapes — something that is an important part of the champagne process').
Sources: National Geographic, 'Who put the bubbles in Champagne?' — 'However, Pérignon did make a significant contribution to the development of champagne as we know it today. In particular, he mastered the technique that allows winemakers to produce white wines from red grapes — something that is an important part of the champagne process'; thisdayinwinehistory.com, 'The Discovery of Sparkling Wine' — 'Dom Pérignon is said to have tasted wine that had accidentally re-fermented in 1697 and was ecstatic at the delightfully sparkling result. He began to experiment until he was finally able to ferment wine in a bottle without it exploding' — carried as the historiography's account of his experimental work, not the invention
Falsifiability: Documentary proof that the attributed techniques were entirely unknown at Hautvillers during his tenure, or that the contributions credited to him belong demonstrably to a different cellar master.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1668-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.9453,49.0778,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1668</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The rushlight as the poor's light</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent witnesses across centuries (Aubrey, Cobbett, White, 19th-c. rural survival, WWII revival) describe the same cheap technology, explicitly contrasted with costly candles.
Layer: real · Place: British Isles
Dates: 1673 → 1945 (confidence: medium)

For centuries, the rushlight — dried rush pith soaked in fat or grease — was the common artificial light of poor people in the British Isles, costing almost nothing to make.

Earliest attestation: John Aubrey, 1673 — earliest printed description.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Rushlight' — Cobbett on near-zero cost and better light than poorly dipped candles; Gilbert White's 1789 rushlight-making description (Letter XXVI); rural survival to the end of the 19th c.; WWII revival; Welsh use into the mid-20th c.; Country Life, 'From fish oil to chandeliers' — rushlights the most common early domestic lighting; in the 18th c. 'the poor man's candle', cheap, untaxed under the 1709 tallow candle tax; 'burning the candle at both ends' originally a rushlight practice
Falsifiability: Contemporary household accounts showing the poor buying tallow candles in preference to rushlights at equal or lower cost.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1673-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.0,54.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1673</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Leeuwenhoek's animalcules (1674–1723)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The discovery is one of the best-documented observations in 17th-century science: ~375 letters to the Royal Society (from his first 1673 publication on mold and bee stings, via de Graaf's introduction), the September 1674 Berkelse Mere letter describing the first animalcules, the 1676 letter to Oldenburg, publication in Philosophical Transactions (1677) after the Royal Society sent observers to Delft to verify (they returned amazed — even Hooke, who had suspected delusion, was convinced), election as Fellow in 1680 (he never visited London), and 27 further letters to the Paris Academy. Modern replication with surviving bead microscopes (Ford; Lane 2015 Phil. Trans. B) confirms he saw what he described. Held below VERIFIED on priority fine points: the role of Johan Ham in the 1677 spermatozoa observation is contested, and some species identifications are retrospective rather than certain.
Layer: real · Place: Delft, Dutch Republic (Leeuwenhoek's workshop; Berkelse Mere nearby)
Dates: 1674 → 1723 (confidence: high)

Antonie van Leeuwenhoek discovered and documented microorganisms ('animalcules') in 1674–1723, the first human observation of microbial life — reported in letters to the Royal Society, published in Philosophical Transactions (1677), and independently verified at the time.

Earliest attestation: Leeuwenhoek's letters to the Royal Society: 7 September 1674 (first animalcule description), the 1676 letter to Henry Oldenburg, published in Philosophical Transactions vol. 12, 821–831 (1677).
Sources: Discover Magazine, 'Leeuwenhoek's Lucky Break' — the 1674 letter, the Royal Society's skepticism, Hooke's verification delegation to Delft, the 1677 publication, election 1680; Royal Society Publishing, Phil. Trans. B 370:20140344 (Lane 2015) — the 1676 letter to Oldenburg in copyist's hand; Oldenburg's translation and the 1677 publication (Philos. Trans. R. Soc. Lond. 12, 821–831; DOI 10.1098/rstl.1677.0003); New Scientist, 'Leeuwenhoek's animalcules, just as he saw them' (2015) — the tenth surviving authentic microscope recovered in Delft; replica images confirming the bead-lens quality; the 1677 protozoa report ('100 of these very wee animals ... could not reach the length of a grain of coarse sand'); MMEGIAS histology atlas (University of Vigo) — the single-lens design avoiding compound chromatic aberration; ~375 letters to the Royal Society from 1673; Paris Academy letters; fellowship 1680/1699
Falsifiability: Proof that the 1674/1676 letters are fabrications, or that the Royal Society's verification never occurred — the archive of ~500 letters and the published Transactions make this effectively closed</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1674-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.3571,52.0116,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1674</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Leibniz's calculus: independent invention, first publication (1684)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1684 publication is a dated, printed, multiply-attested fact; the 1674–1675 independent development rests on Leibniz's own dated manuscripts plus the scholarly consensus of independent invention, which keeps VERIFIED parked.
Layer: real · Place: Leipzig, Saxony (Acta Eruditorum printing)
Dates: 1674 → 1684 (confidence: high)

Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz developed the differential and integral calculus independently in Paris in 1674–1675 (his October 1675 manuscripts contain the differential notation dy/dx and the integral sign, extended form), and published first: the Nova Methodus pro Maximis et Minimis in the Acta Eruditorum of Leipzig, October 1684. His notation — dy/dx, the long-s integral sign, the differentiation rules — is the notation mathematics still uses; Newton's dot notation for fluxions is obsolete.

Earliest attestation: Leibniz's Paris manuscripts of October 1675 (differential notation); Nova Methodus, Acta Eruditorum, October 1684
Sources: Leibniz, G.W., 'Nova methodus pro maximis et minimis', Acta Eruditorum, October 1684; Leibniz manuscripts of October 1675 (Leibniz-Archiv, Hanover); Hall, A.R., Philosophers at War (1980), p.1: independent invention by both men; Hofmann, J.E., Leibniz in Paris 1672–1676 (1974)
Falsifiability: A dated document proving Leibniz saw Newton's fluxion papers before his own October 1675 notation manuscripts</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1674-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.3731,51.3397,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1674</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Denner invents the clarinet (c. 1700, Nuremberg)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree: Denner (1655–1707), Nuremberg workshop from 1678, is 'universally recognized today as the clarinet inventor'; the innovation was the register/speaker key on the chalumeau, enabling overblow at the twelfth into the clarion register; surviving Denner-family instruments in Nuremberg, Berlin, and Vienna show the small-bore two- or three-key design; the chalumeau disappeared as the clarinet flourished. The precise year is fenced — Doppelmayr 1730 ('a little after 1700') is preferred over Murr 1778 ('1690') — so the record claims 'around the turn of the 18th century,' not a year.
Layer: real · Place: Nuremberg (Denner's workshop; born Leipzig, 1655)
Dates: 1690 → 1705 (confidence: medium)

Johann Christoph Denner of Nuremberg invented the clarinet around the turn of the 18th century by modifying the chalumeau — adding a register (speaker) key that forced the instrument to overblow at the twelfth, creating the upper 'clarion' register (named for a trumpet style) that gave the instrument its name.

Earliest attestation: J.G. Doppelmayr's Historische Nachricht von den Nürnbergischen Mathematicis und Künstlern (1730)
Sources: Discogs (Denner biography): 'Johann Christoph Denner (13 Aug 1655—20 Apr 1707) was a German maker of woodwind instruments, universally recognized today as the clarinet inventor... In the early 1700s, while experimenting with chalumeau... Denner developed a novelty, original design. He added a "speaker," or "registry key," forcing chalumeau to "overblow," akin flutes and oboes, into the higher clarion register.'; Flute Almanac: 'Johann Christoph Denner is best known for his significant contribution to the development of the clarinet, which he invented around the turn of the 18th century, between 1690 and 1700. His innovation involved modifying the chalumeau... by increasing its range and adding a register key.'; Scribd, 'History of the Clarinet Evolution': 'It is generally agreed, based on a 1730 statement by J. G. Doppelmayr... that Johann Christoph Denner (1655-1707) invented the clarinet sometime after 1698 by modifying the chalumeau... Before this historical text was discovered, historians and scholars only had a piece written in 1778 by C.G. Murr... wherein Murr wrote that Denner created the clarinet in 1690.' The clarion register 'is thought to be the origin of the name of the instrument.'; Martin Frères: 'Johann Christoph Denner was a Nuremberg instrument maker active around 1655-1707, widely credited with transforming the chalumeau into the clarinet. By adding a register key and refining bore and fingering designs, he created an instrument with an extended range.' Surviving instruments in Nuremberg, Berlin, Vienna.
Falsifiability: A dated instrument or document proving a clarinet predating the 1690s, or evidence the register-key innovation belongs to another maker</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1690-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.0767,49.4521,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1690</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The idiom-origin audit (method)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Demonstrated across six independently documented exhibits (nine-yards, rule-of-thumb, posh, crap, mad-as-a-hatter, let-them-eat-cake), each with published scholarship showing the story failing at the dates.
Layer: real · Place: English-language print culture (London anchor)
Dates: 1692 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

An idiom's history is graded on two separate claims: the earliest printed attestation (the anchor, graded on its documentation) and the origin story that explains the phrase (a separate claim, graded on its own evidence). The most confidently told origin stories are the ones that fail at the dates.

Earliest attestation: Demonstrated in the 2026 VIII.i idiom pass; the exhibits are centuries of English print
Sources: Kelly, Henry Ansgar (1994). 'Rule of Thumb and the Folklaw of the Husband's Stick', Journal of Legal Education 44(3):341-365 — the model demolition; Fred R. Shapiro / Ben Zimmer (Yale Alumni Magazine; Visual Thesaurus 'Word Routes') — the antedating method on 'the whole nine yards': 1962, then 1956/57 Kentucky Happy Hunting Ground, then 1946-51 Lexington KY, plus the 1912 'whole six yards'; Ben Yagoda, Gobsmacked! — the OED on 'posh': 'no evidence has been found for the existence of such tickets' (P.O.S.H.); OED (2d ed., 1989) via Men and Masculinities 5(1), 2002 — earliest known 'rule of thumb': Sir William Hope, The Compleat Fencing-Master (1692); TheCollector (Oct 10, 2026) — OED: first waste-sense 'crap' recorded 1846, when Thomas Crapper (b. 1836) was ten
Falsifiability: A case where a famous, confidently-told origin story survives a full antedating check with its earliest prints — demonstrating that stories are reliable evidence of origins</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1692-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1692</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The real Cornish wrecking economy (1700-1860)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Converging independent source families — manor courts, company letter books, case papers, salvage accounts, assize records, Trinity House correspondence — all document the same three-activity salvage economy; held below VERIFIED as a historiographical synthesis (2010) rather than a single dated event.
Layer: real · Place: Cornwall, England
Dates: 1700 → 1860 (confidence: high)

Cornish wrecking in 1700-1860 was a real, multi-faceted coastal salvage economy: (1) rare direct attack and plunder of vessels, (2) the frequent 'harvesting' of wrecked goods from beaches and shorelines, and (3) salvage of goods washed ashore from wrecks that might lie many miles over the horizon — governed by a developing wreck law (the right to salvage goods washed on shore) and carried out by poor and gentry alike, with lifesaving activity far more common in the record than violence.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary sources compiled by Pearce 2010: manor wreck presentments, East India Company letter books, wreck case papers, salvage accounts, family papers, broadsheets, Trinity House correspondence, assize records, diaries, newspapers, government reports; statute record 26 Geo. II c. 19 (1753).
Sources: Cathryn J. Pearce, 'Cornish Wrecking 1700-1860: Reality and Popular Myth' (Boydell Press, 2010) — three activities of wrecking; development of wreck law; poor and gentry alike involved; 155 Mounts Bay wrecks 1738-1860: 11 attack reports (7%), 77% lifesaving; 26 Geo. II c. 19 — Stealing Shipwrecked Goods Act 1753 ('An Act for enforcing the Laws against Persons who shall steal or detain shipwrecked Goods'), royal assent 15 May 1753; repealed 1854; Wreck and Salvage Act 1846 — repeal schedule listing the 1753 Act among 13 repealed wreck statutes
Falsifiability: A contemporary coastal-community source showing that beach harvesting and salvage were not customary practiced economies but inventions of the 19th-century record would overturn this; Pearce's multi-source archive makes that unlikely.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.5,50.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cristofori invents the piano (documented 1700; named 1711)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree: the 1700 Medici inventory entry ('newly invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori,' hammers and dampers, two keyboards, four octaves); Meccoli's marginal note ('invented by Master Bartolomeo Christofani of Padua in the year 1700'); Maffei's 1711 Giornale de' letterati d'Italia article giving the name 'gravicembalo col piano, e forte' with a diagram of the action; the three surviving instruments (1720 Metropolitan Museum New York — the oldest surviving piano; 1722 Rome; 1726 Leipzig); the documented action innovations (escapement, check, dampers, isolated soundboard, thicker tenser strings, paired strings, soft pedal). The 1709 variant is fenced as a statement about completed instruments, not the invention date.
Layer: real · Place: Florence (the Medici court)
Dates: 1700 → 1700 (confidence: medium)

Bartolomeo Cristofori invented the piano in Florence around 1700 — first documented in the 1700 Medici instrument inventory ('arpicimbalo... newly invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori,' a harpsichord-like instrument with hammers and dampers), and first named 'pianoforte' in Scipione Maffei's 1711 article ('gravicembalo col piano, e forte').

Earliest attestation: The 1700 Medici instrument inventory; Federigo Meccoli's marginal note dating the invention to 1700
Sources: Italy On This Day: 'An inventory of Medici instruments from 1700 described an "arpicimbalo", which resembled a harpsichord but which created sounds through hammers and dampers rather than the plucking mechanism... It was said to be "newly invented by Bartolomeo Cristofori". In 1711, Scipione Maffei... referred to Cristofori's "gravicembalo col piano, e forte" (harpsichord with soft and loud), the first time it was called by its eventual name, pianoforte. A Florentine court musician, Federigo Meccoli, noted that the "arpi cimbalo del piano e forte" was first made by Cristofori in 1700, which is regarded as the birth date for the piano.' Three survivors: 1720 Met New York, 1722 Rome, 1726 Leipzig. Bach dismissed an early model; Silbermann built on Cristofori's blueprint from 1730.; Timenote: Meccoli's marginal note — 'These are the ways in which it is possible to play the Arpicimbalo del piano e forte, invented by Master Bartolomeo Christofani [sic] of Padua in the year 1700' (transl. Stewart Pollens). By 1711 Cristofori had built three pianos.; Wikipedia, 'Fortepiano': 'The first reliable record of his invention appears in the inventory of the Medici family (who were Cristofori's patrons), dated 1700.' The 1720 Met instrument 'is the oldest surviving piano.' Maffei's 1711 article included a diagram of the action; the 1725 German translation 'was perhaps the triggering event in the spread of the fortepiano to German-speaking countries.'; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Pianoforte': Maffei's description appeared in the Giornale dei letterati d'Italia, 1711; 'in 1709 Christofori had completed four "gravecembali col piano e forte"' — the source of the 1709 dating variant.
Falsifiability: A dated instrument or document proving a piano predating 1700, or evidence the 1700 inventory entry refers to a different instrument</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.2558,43.7696,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Newcomen's atmospheric engine (1712): the first successful steam engine</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent institutional lines converge on the 1712 Conygree installation (ASME landmark documentation, Newcomen Society, Black Country Living Museum's 1986 working replica erected a mile or two from the original site); held below VERIFIED on the thinness of surviving contemporary documentation and the exact-site ambiguity.
Layer: real · Place: Conygree Coalworks, Tipton, West Midlands (near Dudley Castle)
Dates: 1712 → 1712 (confidence: medium)

Thomas Newcomen's first commercially successful atmospheric steam engine was erected in 1712 at the Conygree Coalworks near Dudley (Tipton, in the West Midlands) to pump water from a flooded coal mine — the practical steam engine that pumped the mines of the early 18th century.

Earliest attestation: The 1712 Conygree (Tipton, near Dudley Castle) engine — reported in engineering-history literature from the Newcomen Society tradition and J. T. Desaguliers's 1717 course of experimental philosophy
Sources: ASME Engineering History Landmark documentation: Newcomen atmospheric engine — first success 1712 near Dudley Castle, Tipton; ~600 engines built; operated under Savery patent; Newcomen Memorial Engine / Newcomen atmospheric engine research tradition — first successful engine considered the Dudley Castle engine of 1712; 104 engines by 1733; Conygree Coalworks, Tipton; Black Country Living Museum — full-scale 1986 working replica of the 1712 Newcomen engine, erected near the original site; Chris Upton (Birmingham Post, via Free Library) — 1712: Newcomen and partner John Calley erected the engine to pump Lord Dudley's coal mines; the 'atmospheric engine' naming
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1712 document showing the Conygree installation never operated, or an earlier commercially successful steam engine with a verifiable installation record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1712-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.08,52.53,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1712</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The world's first lightship: the Nore, 1731-1732</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent secondary sources plus a 1929 abstract of the actual 1730 patent grant converge on Hamblin/Avery/Nore; the 'world's first' phrasing is Trinity House's own tradition and is carried with the 1629 unanswered proposal as a competing earlier that was never built. Held below VERIFIED because the loop has not inspected the primary patent records themselves, and the placement year spreads 1731-1732 (1734 in one outlier source).
Layer: real · Place: The Nore sandbank, Thames Estuary, England (approx station area)
Dates: 1731 → 1732 (confidence: medium)

The world's first lightship — a floating lighthouse — was moored at the Nore sandbank at the mouth of the Thames Estuary in 1731-1732: the private venture of Captain Robert Hamblin (shipowner of King's Lynn), the vessel built by David Avery, operating on a 1730 patent from George II; Trinity House revoked the original patent as an infringement of its powers, and the Admiralty allowed tolls to be levied while the vessel became Trinity House property.

Earliest attestation: The patent grant itself, 14 July 1730, as abstracted in Nature 124:76 (13 July 1929), 'Calendar of Patent Records'; George II's 1730 patent for 'the first English lightship' (Grupp 1956:30, via US lightship history)
Sources: Nature 124, 76 (13 July 1929), 'Calendar of Patent Records' — patent granted to Captain Robert Hamblin of Lynn, 14 July 1730, for 'a new method for distinguishing of lights'; 'Hamblin also financed the first light-ship, which was established at the Nore in 1732 by David Avery'; Elder Brethren of Trinity House (history section) — 'The Nore lightship was established as the world's first floating light in 1732'; Lightship (history) — 'In 1731, David Avery and Robert Hamblin placed the earliest British lightship at The Nore near the mouth of the River Thames. This was a private venture that operated profitably'; History of lighthouses — 'The Nore lightship was established as the world's first floating light in 1732'; US lightship history (media.defense.gov; Grupp 1956:30) — 1629 unanswered petition to Charles I for a Goodwin Sands lightship; 'In 1730, upon receiving a formal request from Robert Hamblin, George II granted a patent for the first English lightship'; Docklands History Group, 'Trinity House' talk (1999) — 'The first effective lightship was built by David Avery on the Thames at the Nore in 1732 under licence from Trinity House'; FamilySearch Wiki, 'Trinity House' — 'its first lightship was placed at the Nore in 1732'
Falsifiability: Discovery of a built, stationed, functioning lightship with documentary attestation predating 1731, or proof that the Nore vessel was never stationed (e.g. Trinity House records showing no vessel, no tolls)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1731-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>1.0,51.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1731</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Norden's 1737 plates: the Sphinx already noseless</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The plates exist, are multiply held in libraries, carry a 1755 imprint, and are independently corroborated: Pococke's 1743-45 drawings likewise show the nose gone, and the stone's physical state matches. This is the engraving-as-testimony doctrine made concrete — a dated hand, no retouching possible, falsifying a claim 61 years younger than the drawing.
Layer: real · Place: Great Sphinx of Giza, Giza Plateau, Egypt (the plate's subject)
Dates: 1737 → 1755 (confidence: high)

Frederic Louis Norden's Egypt plates — drawn in Egypt in 1737, engraved and published posthumously in 'Voyage d'Egypte et de Nubie' (Copenhagen, 1755) — constitute a dated eyewitness image record of the Sphinx that predates both the Napoleonic myth (1798) and photography (1840s), and functions as documentary evidence: the 1737 plate shows the Sphinx already noseless.

Earliest attestation: Norden's own travel account and plates, 'Voyage d'Egypte et de Nubie', Copenhagen 1755 (French edition; English translation London 1757).
Sources: Frederic Louis Norden, 'Voyage d'Egypte et de Nubie', Copenhagen 1755 (posthumous; Royal Danish Academy of Sciences) — the plates of the Sphinx, noseless; historytools.org, 'The Enigma of the Great Sphinx's Missing Nose' — Norden's 'extraordinarily detailed drawing clearly depicts the Sphinx without a nose'; Richard Pococke, 'A Description of the East' (1743-45) — independent near-contemporary drawn views showing the Sphinx without its nose
Falsifiability: Show that the 1755 plates do not derive from 1737 on-site observation (e.g., that they are studio inventions), or produce a dated pre-1737 image showing the Sphinx WITH a nose — which would narrow, not widen, the myth's kill.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1737-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.1376,29.9753,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1737</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Prussia 1754: the first abolition of judicial torture</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — A documented act of state with a stable scholarly dating: the 1740 restriction order and the 1754 abolition order are the standard account (Langbein); the sequence predates Beccaria's 1764 book by a decade, which is the chronological fact that kills the single-cause myth.
Layer: real · Place: Berlin, Prussia (the cabinet orders)
Dates: 1740 → 1754 (confidence: high)

Frederick II of Prussia abolished judicial torture by cabinet order in 1754 — the first European abolition of the practice, a decade before Beccaria's book.

Earliest attestation: Frederick II's cabinet orders: the 1740 restriction of torture and the 1754 abolition order — documented acts of state.
Sources: Frederick II of Prussia: cabinet order of 3 June 1740 restricting judicial torture; cabinet order of 1754 abolishing it — documented acts of state; standard dating per Langbein, Torture and the Law of Proof; Scholarly account of Frederick II's judicial and legal reforms (abolition of torture and corporal punishment, judicial centralization) — original German sources
Falsifiability: A pre-1754 Prussian cabinet order fully reinstating torture, or evidence the 1754 order was never issued, would force revision; the 1740/1754 sequence is the standard scholarly account.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1740-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1740</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Celsius's 1742 paper: the reversed scale and the birth of pressure-calibrated thermometry</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1742 paper is a documented publication, and its content is corroborated across the standard histories: (1) Celsius demonstrated that the melting point of ice is essentially unaffected by air pressure — a genuine fixed point; (2) he determined with remarkable precision how the boiling point of water varies with atmospheric pressure and proposed calibrating his scale's zero point (the boiling point on his reversed scale) at the mean barometric pressure at mean sea level — the conceptual seed of what the BIPM's 10th CGPM defined in 1954 as one standard atmosphere (1,013,250 dynes/cm² = 101.325 kPa). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the paper is known through the standard historiography and encyclopedic transmission rather than multiple independent primary sources consulted directly in this pass. The grade covers the paper and its science, never the schoolbook claim about the scale's orientation — that claim is dead in the companion record.
Layer: real · Place: Uppsala, Sweden (Celsius's observatory; the 1742 paper)
Dates: 1742 → 1742 (confidence: high)

In 1742, Anders Celsius's paper 'Observations of two persistent degrees on a thermometer' established a mercury thermometer calibrated on two fixed points — with the melting point of ice shown to be essentially pressure-independent and the boiling point of water calibrated at the mean barometric pressure at mean sea level.

Earliest attestation: Celsius's paper 'Observations of two persistent degrees on a thermometer' (1742), reporting the experiments
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Degree Celsius' — History section: the 1742 paper's content — melting-point pressure independence, boiling-point pressure dependence, calibration at mean sea-level pressure; the 1954 CGPM one-atmosphere definition
Falsifiability: Showing the 1742 paper does not exist, is misdated, or describes a different scale than the boiling-zero / freezing-100 instrument</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1742-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>17.6389,59.8586,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1742</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Jonas Hanway popularizes the umbrella in London (c. 1750-1786)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations converge: the 1911 Britannica's 'first Englishman who habitually carried an umbrella'; Sangster's trade history (30 years of carrying, the mockery, the hackney coachmen's hostility, Wolfe's 1752 letter); Portsmouth Cathedral's biography (first man to carry his own umbrella around London, with the umbrella's physical description). Held below VERIFIED on the single-biography provenance of some details (the 30-year figure, the ebony-handle description).
Layer: real · Place: London
Dates: 1750 → 1786 (confidence: medium)

Jonas Hanway (1712-1786) was the first Englishman who habitually carried an umbrella in London rain — beginning c. 1750 and persisting for about thirty years despite public mockery, notably from hackney coachmen who saw umbrellas as a threat to their fares; by his death umbrellas were commonplace in London, and men even called their umbrellas a 'Hanway'.

Earliest attestation: Pugh's life of Hanway (via Sangster 1868); 1911 Britannica 'Umbrella'; contemporary context in Wolfe's 1752 Paris letter
Sources: 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Umbrella' (public domain): 'The traveller Jonas Hanway, who died in 1786, is credited with having been the first Englishman who habitually carried an umbrella'; William Sangster, Umbrellas and Their History (1868): ~30 years of carrying (hence c. 1750); derision from hackney coachmen; Wolfe's 1752 Paris letter; the small parapluie defending his face and wig; Portsmouth Cathedral, Life of Jonas Hanway: 'the first man to carry his own umbrella around London'; umbrella described — ebony handle with small fruits and flowers, pale green silk outside, straw-coloured satin lining, folding to the length of a man's hand; acrosswalls.org Hanway notes: Female Tatler 12 Dec 1709 coffee-house umbrella (pre-Hanway London umbrellas); 'men even called their umbrellas a Hanway'
Falsifiability: A dated source showing habitual male umbrella-carrying in London before 1750, or proof that Pugh's account of Hanway's umbrella use was invented</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1750-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Encyclopédie (1751–1772): the Enlightenment's printing war</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary attestation of the strongest kind: the 28 published volumes themselves, the contemporary subscriber and police records, the 1759 suppression decree, and the surviving correspondence of the editors. The book is its own evidence.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (Le Breton's print shop; the volumes were printed in Paris and Neuchâtel)
Dates: 1751 → 1772 (confidence: high)

Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d'Alembert's Encyclopédie, ou dictionnaire raisonné des sciences, des arts et des métiers (1751–1772) — 28 folio volumes (17 of text, 11 of plates), some 72,000 articles — systematically organized all human knowledge with the mechanical arts given the same dignity as the liberal ones, was suppressed in 1759, and was completed anyway: the Enlightenment's masterwork and its printing war.

Earliest attestation: The published volumes themselves (1751 onward) — thousands of surviving copies in libraries worldwide; contemporary press coverage, subscriber lists, and the 1759 Conseil du Roi suppression decree.
Sources: The 28 published volumes (1751–1772) — the artifact itself, thousands of surviving copies; The 1759 Conseil du Roi suppression decree — the state's own record of the book it tried to kill; Diderot's correspondence and Malesherbes's protection (the censor who warned the editors before raids) — the contemporary paper trail of the clandestine completion
Falsifiability: The claim is the publication record; it is falsified by the non-existence of the volumes — they exist, on shelves, in thousands. Individual article attributions (which contributor wrote what) are a separate, finer question and not covered by this grade.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1751-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1751</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Liberty Bell (State House Bell)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by the commissioning documents, the bell's own surviving inscription naming Pass &amp; Stow and 1753, the Whitechapel order record, and continuous institutional custody (State House to Liberty Bell Center).
Layer: real · Place: Liberty Bell Center, Independence National Historical Park, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1751 → 1753 (confidence: high)

The Liberty Bell — originally the Pennsylvania State House bell — was ordered in 1751 by Assembly Speaker Isaac Norris from the London firm of Lester and Pack (later the Whitechapel Bell Foundry), cast with the inscription 'Proclaim LIBERTY Throughout all the Land unto all the Inhabitants Thereof' (Leviticus 25:10). It arrived in Philadelphia in September 1752, cracked on its first test strike, and was twice recast in 1753 by Philadelphia workmen John Pass and John Stow, whose surnames appear on the bell. It hung in the State House (now Independence Hall) steeple, was used to summon lawmakers and mark proclamations, weighs about 2,080 pounds, was dubbed the 'Liberty Bell' by abolitionist societies in the 1830s, and today stands in the Liberty Bell Center, Independence National Historical Park.

Earliest attestation: Pennsylvania Assembly minutes (order, 1751); the bell's own inscription (Pass &amp; Stow, 1753); Whitechapel/Assembly correspondence
Sources: National Park Service — 'The Liberty Bell Story' (Independence National Historical Park film/description): Whitechapel casting 1751, first crack on arrival 1752, Pass &amp; Stow 1753 recasting, inscription text, ~2,000 lbs, elm yoke; Wikipedia 'Liberty Bell' — Lester and Pack commission, first crack on ringing after arrival, two Pass &amp; Stow recastings, Leviticus 25:10 inscription, abolitionist naming 1830s, 1847 short story origin of the July 4, 1776 association; American Anti-Slavery Society newspaper (1839) — first 'Liberty Bell' naming via the abolitionist image
Falsifiability: Assembly records showing no such order, or metallurgical proof the bell is not the Pass &amp; Stow recasting — no such evidence exists; the bell's inscription and the foundry paperwork agree</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1751-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1501,39.9489,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1751</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Schönbrunn 1752 — the zoo that predates London</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Three independent confirmations converge: the zoo's own official history, UNESCO's World Heritage designation of the palace-and-gardens ensemble, and standard encyclopedic sources — all agree on 1752 (public 1779) and continuous operation to the present. The foil to the London-first myth: the documented older institution.
Layer: real · Place: Tiergarten Schönbrunn, Vienna (coordinates via map.geocode, FULL_ADDRESS match)
Dates: 1752 → 1752 (confidence: high)

Tiergarten Schönbrunn (Vienna), founded 1752, is the oldest zoo in the world still in operation.

Earliest attestation: 1752 imperial menagerie (Francis I); 1779 general public opening; UNESCO WHS listing for the palace gardens including the zoo.
Sources: Schönbrunn Zoo official history — founded 1752, public 1779, 'oldest zoo in the world still in operation'; UNESCO World Heritage Centre — Palace and Gardens of Schönbrunn; tourism-review.com — 'world's oldest zoo is in Austria' (article1326)
Falsifiability: Documented closure of the institution between 1752 and present, or an earlier continuously operating zoo with public access.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1752-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3056,48.18271,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1752</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Johnson's Dictionary, 1755 — the nine-year monument that wasn't the first</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The book's existence, date, scale, and method are multiply attested: the 1755 first edition survives in numerous copies; the nine-year composition and quotation method (books marked by Johnson and six scribes, over 100,000 quotations) are documented in the Preface and contemporary accounts; the famous definitions (lexicographer as 'a harmless drudge,' 'to make dictionaries is dull work,' the oats definition) are printed in the work itself.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (published 15 April 1755)
Dates: 1755 → 1755 (confidence: high)

On 15 April 1755 Samuel Johnson published A Dictionary of the English Language: the product of nine years' work — about 2,300 pages, close to 43,000 entries, and the first English dictionary built on extensive literary quotations (over 100,000) from Shakespeare, Milton, Swift, Pope and others. The standard English dictionary until the OED, 150 years later.

Earliest attestation: The 1755 two-volume first edition itself; Johnson's Preface ('It is the fate of those who toil at the lower employments of life...'); the famous definitions on the page: 'Lexicographer. n.s. A writer of dictionaries; a harmless drudge, that busies himself in tracing the original, and detailing the signification of words.'
Sources: MoneyWeek — '15 April 1755: Samuel Johnson publishes his Dictionary': nine years of work, 2,300 pages, 'close to 43,000 entries, or around 80% of the English words in use'; the 'harmless drudge' and oats definitions quoted.; Karen Swallow Prior (Substack, Oct 2026): Johnson's process — reading and marking books with a team of about six scribes, 'more than 100,000 quotations to support the definitions of over 40,000 words'; nine years; the 'harmless drudge' lexicographer definition quoted in full.; Wikipedia — 'Dictionary of the English Language': Johnson's humor and prejudice in definitions documented (excise, lexicographer, oats, monsieur, patron); the meticulousness 'heretofore unseen' in completeness of definitions.
Falsifiability: Proof the 1755 edition did not exist or was misdated would overturn it — the book is one of the most reprinted works in English bibliography.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1755-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1755</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The lunar-distance rival method</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in the Board's own financial records and the published Almanac tradition; the foil record — it explains why the Board treated Harrison's clock as one method among two, not as an obvious winner. Carried below VERIFIED because 'workable rival' is a historiographical judgment, not a measurement.
Layer: real · Place: Greenwich Observatory (Royal Observatory, home of the Nautical Almanac)
Dates: 1755 → 1767 (confidence: high)

The lunar-distance method — measuring the Moon's position against known stars and comparing with tabulated predictions to recover Greenwich time — was a genuine, workable rival solution to the longitude problem, championed by Astronomer Royal Nevil Maskelyne, with Tobias Mayer's lunar tables at its core; the Board of Longitude paid Mayer's widow £3,000 and Leonhard Euler £300 for the lunar-table work, and Maskelyne's Nautical Almanac (from 1767) put the method in sailors' hands.

Earliest attestation: Board of Longitude reward records: £3,000 to Mayer's widow, £300 to Euler for contributions to the lunar-distance method (Howse appendix).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Longitude rewards' — Mayer's widow £3,000, Euler £300 (Howse appendix); Wikipedia, 'John Harrison' — Maskelyne's lunar-distance advocacy, the Nautical Almanac 1767, Maskelyne's 1761 St. Helena expedition
Falsifiability: Board minutes showing the lunar-table rewards were fictitious, or evidence the Nautical Almanac's tables were unusable at sea, would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1755-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0005,51.4769,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1755</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Linnaeus's paper slips (mid-1760s) — the card before the catalog</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The objects exist and were studied at first hand by historians: standard size (~7.5x13 cm), uniform format (genus name at top), never bound in a fixed order, shufflable. Held below VERIFIED on the authors' own honesty fences — no independent evidence Linnaeus filed them alphabetically, and the 'invention' was inadvertent, never a system he designed or taught.
Layer: real · Place: Uppsala, Sweden — Linnaeus's home; the slips survive in Stockholm collections
Dates: 1760 → 1778 (confidence: medium)

Carl Linnaeus used standardized, unbound paper slips (~7.5x13 cm) in the mid-1760s for organizing taxonomic data — a paper tool functionally identical to modern index cards.

Earliest attestation: The surviving slips themselves, held by the Natural History Museum, Stockholm; studied by Charmantier &amp; Muller-Wille (2012).
Sources: Isabelle Charmantier &amp; Staffan Muller-Wille, 'Carl Linnaeus's Botanical Paper Slips (1767-1773)' — slip dimensions, uniform format, unbound storage, and the mid-1760s dating while preparing Systema naturae 12th edition; Staffan Muller-Wille (University of Exeter), BSHS 2009 presentation, reported by Phys.org — 'Towards the end of his career, in the mid-1760s, Linnaeus took this further, inventing a paper tool that has since become very common: index cards'; LSE Research Online, 'Indexing nature' (Muller-Wille) — the 1764 Museum Ludovicae Ulricae cards kept by the Natural History Museum, Stockholm, exhibited 1995
Falsifiability: A re-dating of the slips to the post-Linnaean period, or evidence they were bound/ordered by the archivists rather than produced as loose slips, would weaken the claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1760-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>17.6389,59.8586,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1760</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The H4 Jamaica trial, 1761–1762</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The measurement comes from the Board's own commissioned trial — the Board never disputed the 5.1-second figure, only what to infer from it; held below VERIFIED because the rate-correction method (fixed daily rate carried over 81 days) was exactly what the following decade's dispute was about (Maskelyne's 'cancellation of errors' objection, the Greenwich re-test).
Layer: real · Place: Kingston, Jamaica (arrival; the voyage ran Portsmouth → Kingston)
Dates: 1761-11 → 1762-01 (confidence: high)

On the Board of Longitude's official sea trial from Portsmouth to Kingston, Jamaica (HMS Deptford, 18 November 1761 – 19 January 1762, 81 days 5 hours), John Harrison's H4 lost approximately 5.1 seconds after correction for its known daily rate — a longitude error of 1.25 minutes (about one nautical mile), far inside the half-degree (30 nautical miles) required by the 1714 Longitude Act for the £20,000 reward.

Earliest attestation: The Board of Longitude's own trial records and the Deptford's ship logs, 1761–1762 (Robertson, Master of the Academy at Portsmouth, fixed the watch's daily rate at 24/9 seconds per day before departure).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'John Harrison' — trial account citing R.T. Gould, The Marine Chronometer (1923) and Jonathan Betts, Time Restored (2006); thingshavehistory.com, 'John Harrison's H4 and the Longitude Prize' (H4 = 13 cm silver watch, 5 beats/sec, citing Royal Museums Greenwich); Board of Longitude minutes / the Deptford trial documentation (rate-fixing by Robertson, Kingston measurement)
Falsifiability: A contemporary ship's log or Board minute recording a materially different rate at Kingston, or proof the known-longitude reference used at Kingston was wrong by more than the margin.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1761-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.8,18.02,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1761</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rousseau's 'great princess' and the brioche (1765)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The text exists, is securely attributed, and says what it says — the anecdote is in Book 6 in every edition. Multiple independent histories of the 'let them eat cake' myth (Wikipedia's sourced article, Britannica, Fraser) converge on this manuscript as the anecdote's origin point.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (where Rousseau set the anecdote and wrote the manuscript)
Dates: 1765 → 1765 (confidence: medium)

In his Confessions, Book 6 (first six books written c. 1765, published posthumously 1782), Jean-Jacques Rousseau recounts that an unnamed 'great princess,' told that the peasants had no bread, replied 'Qu'ils mangent de la brioche' ('Then let them eat brioches') — the true paper trail of the 'let them eat cake' anecdote.

Earliest attestation: Rousseau's Confessions manuscript, Book 6 — the anecdote's first recorded appearance in any form; published 1782.
Sources: Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Confessions, Book 6 — the 'great princess' / 'Qu'ils mangent de la brioche' passage; Publication history: first six books written c. 1765, published posthumously 1782; The 16th-century German folklore variants of the same anecdote pattern (noblewoman proposes sweetened bread) — the story predates the princess, whoever she was
Falsifiability: The record is a text record — it is superseded only by a better manuscript history (e.g., a documented earlier appearance of the anecdote with the named princess). The grade covers the TEXT's attestation, never the truth of the princess.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1765-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1765</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Watt's separate condenser, Patent No. 913 (5 January 1769)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent itself is a surviving primary legal document with the inventor's name, the date, and a title that states exactly what was invented — a method of lessening consumption in fire engines, not the engine.
Layer: real · Place: Great Britain (patent enrolled at the Patent Office, London; developed at Glasgow)
Dates: 1769 → 1769 (confidence: high)

On 5 January 1769 James Watt received UK Letters Patent No. 913 for the separate condenser — 'A New Invented Method of Lessening the Consumption of Steam and Fuel in Fire Engines' — the improvement that kept the working cylinder hot while condensing steam in a separate chamber, cutting fuel consumption by roughly two-thirds and making the steam engine a general-purpose industrial power source.

Earliest attestation: UK Letters Patent No. 913 (5 January 1769) — primary legal document with the title confessing the improvement reading
Sources: UK Letters Patent No. 913, 5 January 1769 — 'A New Invented Method of Lessening the Consumption of Steam and Fuel in Fire Engines' (primary document); Age of Revolution: Boulton &amp; Watt engine — Watt's Glasgow repair commission, the 1769 patent, the 1775 Boulton partnership, Murdoch's installations, engines for mines, mills, distilleries, waterworks; History of technology literature: separate condenser cut fuel consumption by approximately two-thirds vs Newcomen; Boulton &amp; Watt's engine counter and sealed-box coal-savings billing; ~449 engines manufactured during the patent period
Falsifiability: A surviving Patent Office record contradicting the 1769 enrollment, or a contemporary document showing the separate condenser predating Watt</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1769-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.9,52.48,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1769</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nairne's widely marketed rubber eraser, 1770</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Priestley's dated 1770 footnote is contemporary attestation; Nairne's commercial sale at a documented price is independently carried across reference sources. The 'accidental pickup' anecdote is carried as tradition, not evidence.
Layer: real · Place: Royal Exchange, London (Nairne's shop)
Dates: 1770-04-15 → 1770 (confidence: high)

In 1770 the English optician and scientific instrument maker Edward Nairne developed the first widely marketed rubber eraser — natural-rubber cubes sold at three shillings per half-inch cube from his shop opposite the Royal Exchange. The event is documented in Joseph Priestley's footnote of 15 April 1770: 'I have seen a substance excellently adapted to the purpose of wiping from paper the mark of black-lead-pencil. ... It is sold by Mr. Nairne, Mathematical Instrument-Maker, opposite the Royal-Exchange.' Until then the material was known as gum elastic or caoutchouc; the word 'rubber' was in general use in 1770 for anything used to rub and attached to the new material sometime between 1770 and 1778.

Earliest attestation: Joseph Priestley, footnote in 'A Familiar Introduction to the Theory and Practice of Perspective' (London, April 15, 1770)
Sources: Joseph Priestley, 'A Familiar Introduction to the Theory and Practice of Perspective' (1770), footnote: 'I have seen a substance excellently adapted to the purpose of wiping from paper the mark of black-lead-pencil. ... It is sold by Mr. Nairne, Mathematical Instrument-Maker, opposite the Royal-Exchange.'; Wikipedia, 'Eraser' — History section: Nairne, three shillings per half-inch cube; 'rubber' 1770–1778 word history; bread-crumb and wax/pumice predecessors; NPR Skunk Bear (wamc.org), 'Trace The Remarkable History Of The Humble Pencil': Priestley's 1770 observation and the naming of 'rubber'
Falsifiability: A dated 1770-or-earlier reference to a commercially marketed rubber eraser predating Nairne's — none has surfaced.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1770-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0872,51.5133,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1770</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Benjamin Jesty's 1774 cowpox inoculation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1774 inoculation is multiply attested (the 1805 institutional examination with the son re-challenged for smallpox on the spot, the gravestone, the local-history tradition); held below VERIFIED because the documentation is retrospective and second-generation.
Layer: real · Place: Chetnole / Worth Matravers, Dorset, England
Dates: 1774 → 1774 (confidence: medium)

In 1774, Dorset farmer Benjamin Jesty inoculated his wife Elizabeth and his two eldest sons, Robert (3) and Benjamin (2), with cowpox lymph taken from a cow at Chetnole — twenty-two years before Jenner's 1796 experiment.

Earliest attestation: Jesty's sworn-style examination before 12 medical officers of the Original Vaccine Pock Institute, Soho, 1805 (published in the Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Journal); Worth Matravers gravestone inscription
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Benjamin Jesty' — 1805 examination, the 1774 inoculation, the gold lancets, the gravestone; Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Journal (c. 1805–1806) — published verbal evidence of the 1805 examination; Artsreach, 'Tales From the Village: Yetminster' — the Yetminster local-history account (Chetnole cow, darning needle, wife's inflamed arm)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1774 records (parish, surgeon's, or family) explicitly denying the inoculation, or proof the 1805 examination was fabricated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1774-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.98,50.61,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1774</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The deleted anti-slavery paragraph of the Declaration</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The Library of Congress exhibits the evidence directly: the first printed version's transcription shows the full slavery paragraph deleted, with the statement that "the entire paragraph on slavery and the slave trade was deleted by Congress sitting in the Committee of the Whole on July 3-4, 1776." Jefferson's own contemporary account names the objectors. The deletion is one of the best-documented edits in the American founding archive.
Layer: real · Place: Independence Hall, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1776-06 → 1776-07-04 (confidence: high)

Thomas Jefferson's original draft of the Declaration of Independence contained a full paragraph condemning the slave trade — arraigning George III for forcing "that execrable commerce" on the colonies — and Congress deleted the entire paragraph in the Committee of the Whole on July 3-4, 1776, at the objection of South Carolina and Georgia (with the northern states, Jefferson noted, also "pretty considerable carriers" of enslaved people).

Earliest attestation: Jefferson's rough draft of the Declaration (preserved) and his "Notes of Proceedings in Congress" recording the deletion.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Creating the United States' exhibition: 'The entire paragraph on slavery and the slave trade was deleted by Congress sitting in the Committee of the Whole on July 3-4, 1776'; Jefferson reported in his Notes of Proceedings that the clause 'reprobating the enslaving the inhabitants of Africa, was struck out in complaisance to South Carolina &amp; Georgia, who had never attempted to restrain the importation of slaves'; HISTORY.com, 'Why Thomas Jefferson's Anti-Slavery Passage Was Removed from the Declaration of Independence': Jefferson's autobiography (1821) blames South Carolina and Georgia while noting 'our Northern brethren also I believe felt a little tender under these censures; for tho' their people have very few slaves themselves, yet they had been pretty considerable carriers of them to others'; Hypatia Bradlaugh, 'The Slave Struggle in America,' Lecture II (public domain): 'In Jefferson's draft for the Declaration of Independence he arraigned George III. for forcing upon the American colonies that "execrable commerce." This clause was struck out by Congress, "in complaisance," Mr. Jefferson declared, "to South Carolina and Georgia"'
Falsifiability: Would require the draft's text without the passage or a Congress record showing no deletion — both the draft text and the LoC transcription of the printed original show the passage and its absence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1776-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.15,39.9496,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1776</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ned Ludd — real person or figurehead?</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The figurehead use is beyond dispute (letters signed 'Ned Lud' / 'General Lud'; the TNA proclamation), but no independent evidence of the 1779 incident or the man exists — the story's earliest tellings are 1811 newspaper and local-history items, three decades after the fact, and the details shift between tellings (whipped for idleness vs taunted; Ludd vs Ludlam; Anstey vs Sherwood Forest).
Layer: real · Place: Anstey, Leicestershire, England
Dates: 1779 → 1811 (confidence: low)

Ned Ludd — Captain/General/King Ludd — was a real historical person: an Anstey, Leicestershire weaver's apprentice who, in 1779, smashed two stocking frames in a fit of rage after being whipped for idleness (or taunted by youths), and whose name the 1811–1813 frame-breakers adopted as their leader.

Earliest attestation: The Nottingham Review, 20 December 1811 (the earliest traceable telling); John Blackner, 'History of Nottingham' (1811) gives the 'Ludlam' variant
Sources: Wikipedia — Ned Ludd ('Ned Ludd is the legendary, fictitious person to whom the Luddites in England attributed the name of their movement'; story traced to The Nottingham Review, 20 December 1811); HISTORY — 'Who Were the Luddites?' ('There's no evidence Ludd actually existed — like Robin Hood, he was said to reside in Sherwood Forest'); Smithsonian — 'What the Luddites Really Fought Against' ('In fact, no such person existed. Ludd was a fiction concocted from an incident that supposedly had taken place 22 years earlier'); The National Archives — 'The proclamation of Ned Ludd' (HO 42/118, 23 December 1811) — 'Ned Lud' as the signature of an organized correspondence, whatever its author
Falsifiability: A parish register, apprenticeship indenture, or independent contemporary account tying the 1779 Anstey frame-smashing to a real named person</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1779-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1849,52.6722,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1779</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>William Addis and the mass-produced toothbrush (1780)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The manufacture and the firm's long continuity are documented trade history; the prison-prototype details rest on company tradition, so VERIFIED stays parked.
Layer: real · Place: London, England
Dates: 1780 → 1780 (confidence: medium)

In 1780, William Addis of England made a bone-handled toothbrush with swine bristles (the prison-prototype story) and, after release, began mass-producing toothbrushes in England; the family firm (Wisdom Toothbrush) continued into the 1990s.

Earliest attestation: The Addis/Wisdom company tradition; 19th-century trade history of English brush-making.
Sources: Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry, via HowStuffWorks: Addis in 1780, jailed for starting a riot, fashioned a brush from a bone and hog's bristles from a jailer, then began mass-producing them in England; Dental-history convention: the first mass-produced toothbrush, designed by William Addis of England in 1780; Wisdom Toothbrush family-owned until the 1990s (Central Coast Dental Care; Sure Dental); The 1844 first three-row bristle brush (Dr. Meyer L. Rhein) and the 1857 first US toothbrush patent (H. N. Wadsworth) — the post-Addis paper trail (dental-history timelines)
Falsifiability: Business records showing the Addis firm was founded by a successor, not William, or that mass-produced English bristle brushes predate 1780 in the trade record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1780-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.12,51.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1780</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Washington dentures: the materials record</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The objects exist, are multiply held, and are consistent: Mount Vernon's FAQ (primary institutional record), the Greenwood 1789 inscribed set at the New York Academy of Medicine, and the curator's published statements all converge on the same material list. By his 1789 inauguration Washington had one real tooth left (Mount Vernon); his last tooth was kept by dentist John Greenwood with the inscription 'the last one which grew in his head.'
Layer: real · Place: Mount Vernon, Virginia (the surviving complete set); New York Academy of Medicine (Greenwood 1789 set)
Dates: 1781 → 1798 (confidence: high)

George Washington's surviving dentures were constructed from hippopotamus, walrus, and probably elephant ivory; cow, horse, and human teeth; and lead, brass, silver, and gold alloys — with no wood as a tooth material.

Earliest attestation: Four dentures belonging to Washington survive in museum collections (Mount Vernon holds the only complete set; the New York Academy of Medicine holds a Greenwood 1789 lower set; the National Museum of Dentistry holds one; a fifth set is believed entombed with Washington). Each is constructed differently, 'as though the dentist(s) who made them were continually experimenting' (Mount Vernon FAQ).
Sources: George Washington's Mount Vernon — Washington's Dentures FAQ: four dentures, the only surviving complete set on display, full materials list; STAT (2017): New York Academy of Medicine — Greenwood 1789 'Under jaw' set with inscription 'First one made by J. Greenwood 1789'; the 'last one which grew in his head' tooth case; DVIDS (2022): by age 57 a full set despite hygiene (tooth powder, tongue scraping, mouthwash); Washington kept pulled teeth in a locked desk drawer; payments to dentists documented across letters and diaries
Falsifiability: Materials analysis (XRF, dental forensics) of the surviving dentures contradicting the documented material list, or a contemporary invoice/letter describing wooden teeth for Washington.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1781-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0869,38.7087,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1781</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first free flight — Pilatre de Rozier and d'Arlandes, 21 November 1783</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Universally attested contemporary event: the Versailles animal flight (19 September 1783) as the rehearsal, the tethered tests in October, and the 21 November free flight witnessed by the King and Franklin. The champagne toast afterward began the balloonists' tradition that survives to this day.
Layer: real · Place: Chateau de la Muette, Bois de Boulogne → Butte-aux-Cailles, Paris
Dates: 1783-11-21 → 1783-11-21 (confidence: high)

On 21 November 1783, Jean-Francois Pilatre de Rozier and the Marquis d'Arlandes made the first untethered crewed balloon flight — the first confirmed human flight of any kind — in a Montgolfier hot-air balloon, taking off from the garden of the Chateau de la Muette in the Bois de Boulogne (Paris) and flying ~25 minutes, ~9 km, to the Butte-aux-Cailles, before a crowd that included Louis XVI and Benjamin Franklin.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary Parisian accounts, 1783; the Montgolfier demonstration program (June–November 1783)
Sources: Wikipedia 'Francois Laurent d'Arlandes': de Rozier and d'Arlandes made the first crewed free balloon flight on 21 November 1783 in a Montgolfier balloon; took off 1:54 pm from the Chateau de la Muette; 25 minutes, ~5.5 miles (9 km), 3,000 feet, landing at the Butte-aux-Cailles; champagne tradition; HISTORY.com, 'Men fly over Paris in hot air balloon' (21 Nov 1783): first untethered hot-air balloon flight, 5.5 miles over Paris in ~25 minutes; Montgolfier brothers as inventors
Falsifiability: A documented untethered crewed flight before 21 November 1783</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1783-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.25,48.86,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1783</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bramah Challenge Lock stood unpicked for 67 years (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources: the 2 September 1851 arbitration verdict ('Mr. Hobbs has fairly picked or opened the lock'), the Morning Chronicle report of 10 September 1851 recording the 200-guinea payment with Bramah's detailed protest, and the artifact itself — the Challenge Lock (Science Museum Group object 1972-290) — whose rebuild after Hobbs's picking is visible in the mechanism.
Layer: real · Place: 124 Piccadilly, London (shop window); opened at the Great Exhibition, Crystal Palace, Hyde Park
Dates: 1784 → 1851 (confidence: high)

Joseph Bramah's Challenge Lock stood unpicked for 67 years — from the 1784 patent to Alfred Charles Hobbs's opening of it at the Great Exhibition of 1851.

Earliest attestation: The challenge board itself (1790): 'The artist who can make an instrument that will pick or open this lock shall receive 200 guineas the moment it is produced'; the 1851 arbitration verdict and the Morning Chronicle report of 10 September 1851.
Sources: Wikipedia — Bramah lock (patent 1784; 124 Piccadilly; challenge board from 1790; 200-guinea inscription; Hobbs opened the lock at the Great Exhibition 1851, ~51 hours over 16 days; arbitration dispute; lock in the Science Museum); Science Museum Group Collection — Bramah padlock and key (object 1972-290): 'Bramah padlock and key (1801) — the challenge lock of Lock Controversy of 1851'; Science Museum Group Collection — Alfred Charles Hobbs (1812–1891), American locksmith: 'Hobbs was the first pick Bramah's lock'; Daniel Lucian, 'Bramah's Challenge Lock' — committee verdict 2 September 1851 ('Mr. Hobbs has fairly picked or opened the lock'); Bramah's objections (unwitnessed stages, multiple instruments vs 'an instrument', fixed apparatus); payment of 200 guineas announced in the Morning Chronicle, 10 September 1851, with Bramah's detailed protest; IFSEC Global — 'Unlocking 100 years': 67 years unpicked; Hobbs opened it in 1851
Falsifiability: A documented picking attempt before 1851, or proof the 2 September 1851 arbitration verdict and the Morning Chronicle report are fabrications — neither has been produced in 175 years.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1784-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1402,51.5085,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1784</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Franklin's bifocals, 1784</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED: the inventor's own dated letter describes the invention and the motive (presbyopia plus myopia, constant changing of glasses) in his own hand; the letter survives in the documentary record and is multiply reproduced in Franklin's papers.
Layer: real · Place: Passy (Paris), France — where Franklin wrote the 1784 letter
Dates: 1784 → 1784 (confidence: high)

Benjamin Franklin invented bifocals — lenses with two optical powers in one frame — describing the invention in a 1784 letter from Passy to George Whatley.

Earliest attestation: Benjamin Franklin, letter to George Whatley, 23 May 1784 (Passy, France): describes his 'double spectacles' — the upper half for distance, the lower for reading — invented because he grew tired of changing glasses
Sources: Benjamin Franklin, letter to George Whatley, 23 May 1784 (Passy) — 'I have only to move my eyes up or down, as I want to see distinctly far or near'; Indiana Public Media 'A Moment of Science' (2019): Franklin invented bifocals in the 1780s — had his reading glasses cut in half and fused with his distance glasses
Falsifiability: Proof the 1784 letter is forged, or a securely dated double-power lens predating 1784</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1784-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1784</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1784 ledger: nine teeth, 122 shillings</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The ledger entry is a documented primary record, multiply cited in independent secondary treatments (Wikipedia with citations, Poynter 2026, Popular Science 2019, STAT 2017). Held below VERIFIED on the single-document base; the LINKAGE of the nine teeth to Washington's own mouth is the fenced unknown — stated as CLAIMED, never assumed.
Layer: real · Place: Mount Vernon plantation, Virginia (the ledger)
Dates: 1784 → 1784 (confidence: high)

In 1784, Washington's plantation ledger records a cash payment of 122 shillings (£6.10) to several enslaved people for nine teeth, on account of the French dentist Jean-Pierre Le Mayeur.

Earliest attestation: The Mount Vernon plantation ledger notation: 'By cash pd Negroes for 9 Teeth on Acct of Dr. Lemoin' (Le Mayeur). Reported in the Wikipedia synthesis of the ledger, Poynter's 2026 fact-check, and Popular Science's 2019 treatment. It is unknown whether the sellers were coerced, and unknown whether they ever received the money (enslaved people were routinely cheated by overseers).
Sources: Mount Vernon plantation ledger, 1784: 'By cash pd Negroes for 9 Teeth on Acct of Dr. Lemoin' — via Wikipedia synthesis with citations; Poynter fact-check (2026): surviving financial records show the 1784 payment; 'it is possible that the teeth came from enslaved people... but the evidence is unclear'; none of Washington's dentures from this period include human teeth; Popular Science (2019): the ledger notation and its reading; Le Mayeur's known practice of paying individuals for healthy teeth
Falsifiability: Examination of the original ledger page contradicting the transcription, or documentary proof of where the nine teeth went (Le Mayeur's lost records).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1784-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0869,38.7087,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1784</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The storming of the Bastille, 14 July 1789</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The occurrence and the main sequence are multiply attested by contemporary records; exact casualty counts and the surrender chronology vary across eyewitness accounts, so it is held below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Place de la Bastille, Paris (fortress demolished 1789–1790; site marked by the Colonne de Juillet)
Dates: 1789-07-14 → 1789-07-14 (confidence: high)

On 14 July 1789 a Parisian crowd stormed the Bastille fortress; the garrison surrendered, governor Bernard-René de Launay was killed by the mob, roughly 100 people died in the fighting, and the fortress's seven prisoners were freed.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary July 1789 revolutionary records, pamphlets, and eyewitness accounts
Sources: Eyewitness painting of the siege by Claude Cholat (1789); Wikipedia 'Storming of the Bastille' — compiled secondary with citations to Godechot, Lüsebrink &amp; Reichardt; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Bastille' (public domain); Colorado dissertation on the storming (citing Godechot, The Taking of the Bastille; Lüsebrink &amp; Reichardt, The Bastille)
Falsifiability: A contemporary official record showing the fortress was not attacked on 14 July 1789, or showing no casualties</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3692,48.8533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bligh's open-boat voyage to Timor, April–June 1789 (the foil)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Bligh's daily log plus 18 surviving witnesses plus the Dutch factors' independent arrival record converge on the dates, the ~3,600 nm distance, and the Coupang landing.
Layer: real · Place: Coupang (Kupang), Timor — the landing; voyage began near Tofua, Tonga
Dates: 1789-04-28 → 1789-06-14 (confidence: high)

Cast adrift on 28 April 1789, Bligh navigated the Bounty's 23-foot launch some 3,600 nautical miles over 47 days to Coupang, Timor, arriving 14 June 1789 — without charts, by dead reckoning and sextant — one of the great feats of open-boat navigation. One man, John Norton, was killed by islanders at Tofua days into the voyage; the other 18 survived.

Earliest attestation: Bligh's launch log, kept daily through the 47-day voyage.
Sources: Bligh's launch log, 28 April – 14 June 1789 (Mitchell Library) — daily positions, rations, and discipline entries; Accounts of the 18 surviving loyalists — independent corroboration of the route, the rationing, and Norton's death at Tofua; Dutch East India Company records at Coupang, Timor — the arrival of 14 June 1789 entered by a disinterested third party
Falsifiability: A contemporary Dutch record contradicting the 14 June arrival, or a survivor's account placing the launch elsewhere — none exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>123.58,-10.17,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The mutiny on HMS Bounty, 28 April 1789 (the foil)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary attestations from both sides — the victim's same-day log, the mutineers' journals, the Admiralty's court-martial record, and the hostile family's advocacy publication — converge on the seizure, the date, and the launch.
Layer: real · Place: Near Tofua, Tonga
Dates: 1789-04-28 → 1789-04-28 (confidence: high)

On 28 April 1789, near Tofua in the Tonga group, Fletcher Christian and disaffected crewmen seized HMS Bounty — 23 days out from Tahiti — and set William Bligh and 18 loyal men adrift in the ship's 23-foot launch.

Earliest attestation: Bligh's log entry for 28 April 1789, written in the launch in the hours after the seizure.
Sources: Bligh's Bounty log, 28 April 1789 entry (Mitchell Library) — the seizure described by its victim, written the same day; Portsmouth court-martial transcripts of the captured mutineers, September 1792 — the Admiralty's proceedings against the men brought home by HMS Pandora; James Morrison's journal — a mutineer's own account, written on Tahiti and in captivity; Edward Christian's Appendix (1794) — adversarial corroboration: even the hostile Christian-family publication agrees the seizure happened, disputing only its causes
Falsifiability: Proof that the same-day log entry was fabricated — effectively impossible given the 18 corroborating witnesses in the launch and the Admiralty proceedings that followed.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-175.07,-19.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The makers: Louis's design, Schmidt's build, Pelletier's morning — 25 April 1792</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines converge. Antoine Louis, secretary of the Paris Academy of Surgery, is credited with the design across secondary scholarship (History.com via Smithsonian; Wikipedia; TopWar's account). Tobias Schmidt, the German harpsichord maker working in Paris, is credited with the build. The 25 April 1792 first use on Nicolas-Jacques Pelletier (a common-law convict) is consistently dated and placed at the Place de Grève. The machine's continued service to 1977 (public executions ended 1939, after the Weidmann execution at Versailles) is the long institutional tail of the same device.
Layer: real · Place: Place de Grève (now Place de l'Hôtel-de-Ville), Paris — first use, 25 April 1792
Dates: 1789-12-01 → 1792-04-25 (confidence: high)

Antoine Louis designed and Tobias Schmidt built the French guillotine; its first public use was the execution of Nicolas-Jacques Pelletier at the Place de Grève, Paris, on 25 April 1792.

Earliest attestation: The machine's first nickname — the 'Louisette'/'Louison' — is the contemporary counter-attestation to the Guillotin attribution: the era's own vocabulary named the designer, not the advocate.
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine, Kat Eschner (28 Mar 2017) — Louis designed, Schmidt built; first use context; 3 June 1791 decree; service history per Cavendish; Wikipedia, 'Guillotine' — 25 April 1792 first use (Nicolas-Jacques Pelletier, Place de Grève); service to 1977; public executions ended 1939 (Eugen Weidmann, Versailles); en.topwar.ru guillotine history — design by Antoine Louis 'in collaboration with' builder Tobias Schmidt; first public execution 1792; use until 1977
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1791–1792 records naming a different designer or builder — the design memoranda and build credit consistently name Louis and Schmidt across French, English, and German accounts.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3488,48.8534,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chappe's optical telegraph (1791–1794)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent attestations converge: the 1791 demonstration (dated message, municipal officials at both ends, recorded in the standard biographical record), the 1792 Legislative Assembly adoption (1911 Britannica), the ingénieur-télégraphiste appointment and the built Paris–Lille line, and the 1794 Condé-sur-l'Escaut dispatch (graded separately). The machine's French name — the Télégraphe Chappe — and Chappe's own coinage of 'semaphore' (1801) and 'telegraph' attach the invention to its maker.
Layer: real · Place: Brûlon, Sarthe, France (the 1791 demonstration site; Paris–Lille line terminals)
Dates: 1791-03-02 → 1794-12-31 (confidence: high)

Claude Chappe (1763–1805), with his brothers, invented the optical semaphore telegraph; the first public demonstration ran on 2 March 1791 (Brûlon to Parcé, 16 km); the Legislative Assembly adopted the machine in 1792; Chappe was appointed ingénieur-télégraphiste and built the Paris–Lille line (230 km), which carried official military dispatches in 1794.

Earliest attestation: The March 1791 Brûlon–Parcé demonstration and the 1792–1793 Legislative Assembly/National Convention records; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica's Chappe entry (public domain) as the standard biographical account
Sources: 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Chappe, Claude' (public domain): 'He was the inventor of an optical telegraph which was widely used in France until it was superseded by the electric telegraph... The machine was adopted by the Legislative Assembly in 1792, and in the following year Chappe was appointed ingénieur-télégraphe'; Wikipedia, 'Optical telegraph': the 2 March 1791 Brûlon–Parcé demonstration ('If you succeed, you will soon bask in glory'); summer 1792 appointment as Ingénieur-Télégraphiste and the Paris–Lille line; 'semaphore' coined by Chappe in 1801; Shannon Selin, 'Napoleonic Telecommunications: The Chappe Semaphore Telegraph' (2020): 'The Napoleonic telegraph system was invented by Claude Chappe'; the 1791 demonstration, the 24 March 1792 Assembly address, Ignace Chappe's election; thearticle.com, 'How the mechanical telegraph shaped the history of France': the Paris–Lille line inaugurated mid-August 1794; first news the recapture of Condé-sur-l'Escaut; by 1846, 534 stations over ~5,000 km
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1790–1792 record crediting another inventor with the working system — e.g., an Assembly grant or a prior-art patent predating the 1791 demonstration that the Chappes' system demonstrably copied</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.234,47.97,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The revolutionary playing-card catalog (1791) — a million cards to Paris</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple documentary lines: the 1791 instructions, the described card practice (title transcribed, filing word underlined, collation, cards strung through the lower-left corner), and the LoC's million-card account. Held below VERIFIED — the million-card figure and the exact instruction year ride secondary aggregators, and the 'first attempt at a national library card catalog' phrasing is the LoC blog's, not a scholarly consensus.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France — the Bureau de Bibliographie, destination of the cards
Dates: 1791 → 1794 (confidence: medium)

During the French Revolution, officials cataloged libraries confiscated from aristocrats and clergy on blank-backed playing cards — over a million cards sent to Paris within three years — a national card catalog before the term existed.

Earliest attestation: The 1791 instructions to local officials (reported by the Library of Congress blog, 2017); the surviving practice described by Jackson (1974) and Hopkins (1992).
Sources: Library of Congress blog, 'Inquiring Minds: The Unheralded Story of the Card Catalog' (2017) — the 1791 confiscation-cataloging instructions; playing cards 'sturdy and roughly the same size'; 'within three years, over a million cards were sent to overwhelmed offices in Paris'; liswiki.org, 'History of the card catalog' — citing Jackson (1974): constituent assembly confiscations recorded on cards sent to the Paris Bureau de Bibliographie; Hopkins (1992): the transcription/collation rules and the needle-and-thread ordering; Austrian National Library (ONB), '1780: The Oldest Card Catalogue' — Gottfried van Swieten's card catalogue at the Viennese court library; carried as the named competing position on the 'first' superlative
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the playing-card cards were never interfiled as a catalog (e.g., that they were only inventory lists) would drop the claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pandora pencil: 1791, already mechanical</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Single-source chain this pass (Wikipedia's mechanical-pencil history, citing the wreck literature); the object itself not examined and the mechanism claim not independently verified — honestly CLAIMED, and the strongest single-artifact argument the 1822 patent has a prehistory.
Layer: real · Place: Great Barrier Reef, Australia (wreck region)
Dates: 1791 → 1791 (confidence: low)

The earliest extant datable example of a mechanical pencil was recovered from the wreckage of HMS Pandora, which sank in 1791 — a working propelling pencil predating the 1822 patent by 31 years.

Earliest attestation: The wreck assemblage; published in secondary histories citing the find.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Mechanical pencil': 'The earliest extant datable example of a mechanical pencil was found aboard the wreckage of HMS Pandora, which sank in 1791.'
Falsifiability: Re-examination showing the object is a simple leadholder, or a post-wreck intrusion into the assemblage, would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>145.5,-14.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The death of Mozart, 5 December 1791</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The death, date, and registered cause are multiply attested by the parish register and witnesses; the exact disease mechanism is unattested (no autopsy), so the record stops at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Vienna, Austria
Dates: 1791-12-05 → 1791-12-05 (confidence: high)

Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart died in Vienna on 5 December 1791, at age 35, after a short final illness marked by fever, rash, and severe swelling (edema).

Earliest attestation: The St. Stephen's parish death register, Vienna, December 1791 ("hitziges Frieselfieber"); contemporary witness accounts of the final illness
Sources: Vienna St. Stephen's parish death register, 1791 — "hitziges Frieselfieber"; Sophie Haibel (sister-in-law), later recollections: severe swelling, lucidity to the end; Zegers, Weigl &amp; Steptoe, Annals of Internal Medicine 151 (2009): 274–278 — retrospective analysis of 5,011 Vienna death records; spike in edema-related deaths among younger men that winter; streptococcal infection leading to acute nephritic syndrome proposed as the leading retrospective diagnosis; PBS/STAT News summary of the differential: 136+ post-mortem diagnoses in the literature; the 2009 study described as the most rigorous epidemiologic attempt
Falsifiability: A contemporary medical report or parish entry contradicting the date, place, or symptom cluster</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3738,48.2082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peter Plett's 1791 Holstein cowpox vaccinations</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1791 vaccinations and the un-acted-upon Kiel reports are documented in the 1802 interview record and the 2006 Sudhoffs Archiv paper; held below VERIFIED on the retrospective documentation chain.
Layer: real · Place: Hasselburg (Gut Wittenberg), East Holstein
Dates: 1791 → 1791 (confidence: medium)

In 1791, Holstein teacher Peter Plett vaccinated the three children of his employer Martini at Hasselburg with cowpox lymph — five years before Jenner's experiment — and reported the success to the Kiel medical faculty.

Earliest attestation: Plett's reports to the University of Kiel medical faculty, 1790 and 1791/92; von Heinze interview report, 1802, published by Pfaff
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Peter Plett' — the 1791 vaccinations, the Kiel reports, the 1802 von Heinze interview; Plett, P.C., Plett, P., &amp; Jenner, E. (2006). 'Peter Plett and other discoverers of cowpox vaccination before Edward Jenner.' Sudhoffs Archiv 90(2): 219–232
Falsifiability: Kiel faculty records showing no 1790–1792 reports from Plett, or contemporary evidence the 1802 interview was coached</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.95,54.31,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Salieri and Mozart: the documented courtesy</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in Mozart's own correspondence and in the documented teaching record; the exact warmth of the relationship is a matter of interpretation, which holds the record below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Vienna, Austria
Dates: 1791 → 1825 (confidence: high)

Whatever their professional rivalry, Salieri and Mozart behaved as collegial Viennese court composers: Mozart rode with Salieri to The Magic Flute in October 1791 and Salieri praised it number by number, and after Mozart's death Salieri taught composition to Mozart's son.

Earliest attestation: Mozart's correspondence (the 1791 invitation and Salieri's attendance); documented teacher–pupil record of Franz Xaver Wolfgang Mozart
Sources: Mozart to Constanze, 13 October 1791 (last three letters): rode with Salieri to The Magic Flute; Salieri "heard and saw with all his attention, and from the overture to the last choir there was not a piece that didn't elicit a 'Bravo!' or 'Bello!' out of him"; the two had even collaborated on a piece in 1786; Documented record: Salieri as composition teacher to Franz Xaver Wolfgang Mozart (Mozart's younger son)
Falsifiability: Evidence of documented sabotage, threats, or hostile acts by Salieri against Mozart in the primary record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3738,48.2082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Conté's 1795 recipe: clay, graphite, and the hardness dial</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Consistently attested across independent histories (NPR, ThoughtCo, MRS Bulletin) with the wartime-commission mechanism documented; held below VERIFIED on the commission-to-patent chain and on this pass's secondhand sourcing.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France
Dates: 1794 → 1795 (confidence: medium)

In 1795, Nicolas-Jacques Conté patented the process of making pencil lead from powdered graphite mixed with wet clay, shaped into rods and fired — the mixture ratio controlling the lead's hardness — freeing continental pencil-makers from dependence on British graphite.

Earliest attestation: French patent record, 1795; the process still in use in modified form.
Sources: NPR / KHSU (2016): 'In 1794, Revolutionary France was at war with Britain and completely cut off from high-quality English graphite. The minister of war asked engineer Nicolas-Jacques Conté to find a solution. Conté ground up impure, low-quality graphite, mixed it with wet clay, shaped the mixture into rods and then baked them.'; ThoughtCo, 'Writing Instrument History': 'The breakthrough in pencil technology came when French chemist Nicolas Conte developed and patented the process used to make pencils in 1795. He used a mixture of clay and graphite that was fired before it was put in a wooden case.'
Falsifiability: A pre-1794 fired clay-graphite lead process, or proof Conté's patent was never practiced, would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1794-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1794</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Paris 1794 — the first public scientific zoo</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle's own official history confirms the 1794 decree, the Versailles royal-ménagerie stock, and the free-public scientific mission; independent secondary sources (aboutzoos.info) agree ('France's oldest public zoo', 'second oldest zoo according to modern zoological tradition'). Held below VERIFIED on the single institutional-history base for the founding detail.
Layer: real · Place: Ménagerie du Jardin des Plantes, 75005 Paris (coordinates via map.geocode, FULL_ADDRESS match)
Dates: 1794 → 1794 (confidence: high)

The Ménagerie du Jardin des Plantes in Paris, officially inaugurated in 1794, was the first modern public scientific zoo.

Earliest attestation: 1794 revolutionary decree; the Muséum's own official history.
Sources: Muséum national d'Histoire naturelle — History of the Menagerie, the zoo of the Jardin des Plantes (1794 decree, 58 animals, free to all); aboutzoos.info — Paris, La Ménagerie du Jardin des Plantes (France's oldest public zoo)
Falsifiability: The Muséum's own history being contradicted by an earlier public-scientific zoo charter (Schönbrunn's is an imperial menagerie opened to the public, a different institutional model — the distinction is carried honestly).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1794-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.36083,48.845,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1794</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Paris–Lille line's first dispatch (1794)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple secondary sources converge on the core event — a 1794 French victory at Condé-sur-l'Escaut flashed to Paris within the hour over the new line — but they diverge on the exact day and which capture was 'first', so the record holds at CORROBORATED until a dated primary dispatch or operator's logbook pins it.
Layer: real · Place: The Paris–Lille semaphore line (230 km); news terminus Condé-sur-l'Escaut (~50.45N, 3.59E)
Dates: 1794-08-15 → 1794-08-30 (confidence: medium)

The first military dispatches over the Paris–Lille semaphore line carried news of the French recapture of Condé-sur-l'Escaut from the Austrians in 1794 — the victory known in Paris less than an hour after it occurred.

Earliest attestation: The line's own operation, summer 1794; the standard modern accounts (Wikipedia's Optical telegraph and Chappe telegraph articles; thearticle.com's French telegraph history)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Optical telegraph': 'In 1794, it brought news of a French capture of Condé-sur-l'Escaut from the Austrians less than an hour after it occurred'; Wikipedia, 'Chappe telegraph': 'The first military use of the system was made to report the recapture from Austrian forces of the cities of Le Quesnoy on 15 August 1794 and the town of Condé-sur-l'Escaut on 30 August 1794. The news of these victories was known by the National Convention in Paris within a few minutes'; thearticle.com, 'How the mechanical telegraph shaped the history of France': the first line inaugurated mid-August 1794; 'The first news carried by the line was the recapture of Conde-sur-Lescaut'
Falsifiability: A Convention or War Ministry record showing the Paris–Lille line carried no dispatches before 1795, or a dated operator's logbook contradicting the Condé carriage</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1794-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.07,50.63,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1794</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edward Jenner's 1796 experiment and the 1798 Inquiry (the foil)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The experiment, the challenge, the naming, and the publication are all in the primary record (the Inquiry itself) with the Royal Society refusal and Parliament rewards independently documented; held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the thinness of the single-case experiment and the retrospective telling around it.
Layer: real · Place: Berkeley, Gloucestershire, England
Dates: 1796-05-14 → 1798 (confidence: high)

On 14 May 1796, Edward Jenner inoculated 8-year-old James Phipps with cowpox matter from the hand of the dairymaid Sarah Nelmes; on 1 July he challenged Phipps with smallpox matter and no disease followed. He self-published An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae in 1798 after the Royal Society declined the paper, named the method 'vaccination' (from vacca, cow), and drove its global adoption — earning £10,000 (1802) and £20,000 (1807) from Parliament.

Earliest attestation: Jenner, E. (1798). An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae — the primary record of the experiment
Sources: Jenner, E. (1798). An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae (self-published, after Royal Society declined); Wikipedia, 'Benjamin Jesty' — Parliament awards: Jenner £10,000 (1802), £20,000 (1807); Pearson's testimony on Jesty's priority; digpodcast.org, 'The OG Vaccine: Smallpox, Cowpox, and the Procedure that Changed the World' — the Phipps/Nelmes experiment narrative
Falsifiability: Proof the 1796 Phipps experiment records were fabricated, or contemporary evidence the method's spread predated and bypassed Jenner's publication entirely</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1796-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.46,51.69,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1796</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The rival 'first skyscraper' candidates: the 93-year candidate map</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Each candidate is attested by institutional, documentary sources that converge: Historic England (Ditherington), RIBA (Oriel Chambers), the elevator historical record (Equitable Life), and the structural-historical literature (Rand McNally). The record makes no claim about who 'deserves' the title — it is an attestation map, and attestation maps are CORROBORATED by definition.
Layer: real · Place: Shrewsbury 52.7195,-2.7432 → Liverpool 53.4065,-2.9932 → Manhattan 40.7,-74.0 → Chicago 41.8796,-87.632
Dates: 1797 → 1890 (confidence: high)

The 'first skyscraper' title has at least four documented earlier or rival candidates, each holding a different 'first' under a different definition: the Ditherington Flax Mill (1797), Oriel Chambers (1864), the Equitable Life Building (1870), and the Rand McNally Building (1890).

Earliest attestation: The buildings themselves and their institutional records: Historic England on the Ditherington Flax Mill (world's first iron-framed building, 'grandfather of skyscrapers', Grade I listed); RIBA on Oriel Chambers (1864, Peter Ellis — earliest cast-iron-framed glass curtain walling in England, Grade I listed); the Equitable Life Building's passenger-elevator record (1870, George B. Post); the Rand McNally Building (1890) as the first entirely self-supporting steel-framed skyscraper.
Sources: Historic England / Wikipedia 'Shrewsbury Flaxmill Maltings' — 'the first iron-framed building in the world', 'the grandfather of skyscrapers'; built 1797, architect Charles Bage; Grade I listed; RIBA (ribapix) — Oriel Chambers, Water Street, Liverpool (1864, Peter Ellis): 'the earliest use of curtain walling with a cast-iron frame in England'; Grade I listed; Wikipedia 'Oriel Chambers' — 'the world's first building featuring a metal-framed glass curtain wall'; 1864; Grade I listed; Wikipedia 'List of firsts in architecture' — Equitable Life Building (Manhattan), first office building to use passenger elevators (1870); Home Insurance Building 'widely considered to be the first true skyscraper' (1884); Wikipedia 'Early skyscrapers' — the 1890 Rand McNally Building, 'the first entirely self-supporting, steel-framed skyscraper'
Falsifiability: Evidence that any one of these buildings did not exist, did not have its attested feature (iron frame, curtain wall, elevator, self-supporting steel frame), or postdates the Home Insurance Building would remove it from the candidate map — but the map records DOCUMENTED candidates, not a winner.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1797-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.632,41.8796,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1797</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Parthenon sculptures' removal and 1816 purchase</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The removal (1801-1812) and the 1816 parliamentary purchase are multiply attested in contemporary primary sources — Elgin's letters, the Select Committee proceedings, the accession records.
Layer: real · Place: The Parthenon, Athenian Acropolis (sculptures now in the British Museum, London)
Dates: 1801 → 1816 (confidence: high)

Sculptures from the Parthenon on the Athenian Acropolis (carved c. 447-432 BCE) were removed between 1801 and 1812 by agents of Thomas Bruce, 7th Earl of Elgin; the collection was sold to the British Parliament in 1816 for 35,000 pounds and has been held in the British Museum since.

Earliest attestation: Lord Elgin's correspondence, 1801-1812; the 1816 Parliamentary Select Committee proceedings on the purchase; the British Museum's accession registers.
Sources: 1816 Parliamentary Select Committee report on the Elgin purchase; Elgin's correspondence, 1801-1812; British Museum accession records, Duveen Gallery collection; Policy Exchange, 'The Elgin Marbles' (2023) — the British Museum's legal-title position
Falsifiability: Parliamentary records showing no 1816 purchase, or contemporary evidence that the sculptures reached London by a different route — neither exists; the removal-and-purchase chain is multiply attested in Elgin's own letters, committee testimony, and museum records.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1801-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>23.7267,37.9715,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1801</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Luke Howard names the clouds (December 1802)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree on the December 1802 lecture, the three principal forms (cirrus 'wisp of hair,' cumulus 'heap,' stratus 'layer,' plus nimbus 'rain cloud' and the compounds), the 1803 Essay publication, the Latin-as-shared-language mechanism, and the system's continuity into the modern International Cloud Atlas. The Royal Society: 'in 1802 he established the terminology for different cloud formations that is still in use today.' The Lamarck rival system (1802, French) is documented and fenced — Howard won the nomenclature, not the attempt.
Layer: real · Place: London (the Askesian Society)
Dates: 1802-12 → 1802-12 (confidence: high)

In December 1802, the London pharmacist and amateur meteorologist Luke Howard presented 'On the Modification of Clouds' to the Askesian Society, establishing the Latin cloud nomenclature — cirrus, cumulus, stratus (and nimbus) — that remains the basis of cloud classification today.

Earliest attestation: Howard's December 1802 Askesian Society lecture; the 1803 Essay with his own cloud drawings
Sources: Weather.com, 'The man who named the clouds' (2026): 'On a December evening in 1802, a reserved London pharmacist by the name of Luke Howard stood before a small group of amateur thinkers and proposed... Presenting his paper, "On the Modification of Clouds," to London's Askesian Society, he introduced a surprisingly practical Latin naming system' — cirrus ('wisp of hair'), cumulus ('heap'), stratus ('layer'), plus nimbus ('rain cloud').; Royal Society, 'Capital climate': 'Most famously, in 1802 he established the terminology for different cloud formations that is still in use today... the use of Latin terms was Howard's way of ensuring non-English-speaking scientists could adopt the new nomenclature.' Also his urban heat island discovery (The Climate of London, 1818).; Wikipedia, 'Luke Howard (meteorologist)': 'In his late twenties, Luke Howard wrote the Essay on the Modification of Clouds, which was published in 1803... Howard named the three principal categories of clouds – cumulus, stratus, and cirrus, as well as a series of intermediate and compound modifications.' Called 'the father of meteorology.'; The Handy Weather Answer Book (via Papertrell): 'The French naturalist Jean Lamarck (1744–1829) was the first to propose a system for classifying clouds in 1802. His work, however, did not receive wide recognition. A year later, the Englishman Luke Howard (1772–1864) developed a cloud classification system that has been generally accepted and is still in use today.'
Falsifiability: Archival evidence that Howard's lecture did not occur in December 1802, or that the cirrus/cumulus/stratus terms predate him in another author's system</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1802-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1802</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Beaufort devises the wind force scale (1805)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree on the 1805 devising (HMS Woolwich), the January 1806 journal entry ('nothing gives a more indefinite representation of the wind and the weather than the previously used expressions like moderate wind'), the 13-class sail-based design with no wind-speed numbers, the refinement through the 1830s, the first use on the Beagle's 1831–1836 voyage under FitzRoy, the Admiralty's late-1830s mandate (1838), and the 1853 Brussels international adoption. The Defoe-and-others lineage is documented and fenced — Beaufort won the standardization, not the first attempt.
Layer: real · Place: Aboard HMS Woolwich (at sea)
Dates: 1805 → 1806-01 (confidence: high)

In 1805, Francis Beaufort — then a Royal Navy officer serving aboard HMS Woolwich — devised the Beaufort wind force scale: thirteen classes (0–12) describing wind strength by its effects on a frigate's sails, from 'just sufficient to give steerage' to 'that which no canvas sails could withstand.'

Earliest attestation: Beaufort's private journal, January 1806
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Beaufort scale': 'The scale was devised in 1805 by Francis Beaufort (later Rear Admiral), a hydrographer and a Royal Navy officer, while serving on HMS Woolwich, and refined until he was Hydrographer of the Navy in the 1830s, when it was adopted officially. It was first used during the 1831–1836 "Darwin voyage" of HMS Beagle under Captain Robert FitzRoy... The initial scale of 13 classes (zero to 12) did not reference wind speed numbers, but related qualitative wind conditions to effects on the sails of a frigate... from "just sufficient to give steerage" to "that which no canvas sails could withstand". The scale was made a standard for ship's log entries on Royal Navy vessels in the late 1830s and, in 1853, the Beaufort scale was accepted as generally applicable at the First International Meteorological Conference in Brussels.'; Heidorn, 'The Weather Legacy of Admiral Sir Francis Beaufort': 'In his journal for January of 1806, then-Commander Beaufort wrote, "From now on I shall evaluate the force of the wind in accordance with the following scale, since nothing gives a more indefinite representation of the wind and the weather than the previously used expressions like moderate wind or cloudy weather."' The scale 'he conceived in 1805 and the British Navy adopted in 1838.'
Falsifiability: Archival evidence that Beaufort's scale predates or postdates 1805, or that the 13-class sail-based system originated with another author</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1805-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.3,50.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1805</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Chancel's chemical matches (1805) — the dipping predecessor</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Widely and consistently attested in match historiography as the chemical-match predecessor Walker's work varied; held at CORROBORATED because the 1805 date is carried by secondary sources rather than a dated primary document.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (Chancel's workshop)
Dates: 1805 → 1805 (confidence: medium)

In 1805, Paris, Jean-Louis Chancel produced the first chemical match in commercial form: a splint tipped with potassium chlorate, sulfur, sugar, and rubber, ignited by dipping it into a bottle of concentrated sulfuric acid (the oxy-muriatic or 'dipping' chemical match). Walker was already making variations on Chancel's matches for local hunters before his friction-match accident.

Earliest attestation: Jean-Louis Chancel, Paris, 1805 (secondary literature; Walker Wikipedia article notes Walker's pre-1826 work on Chancel variations)
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Walker_(inventor) — 'He had already been creating variations on Jean Chancel's dipping chemical matches'; Stockton local history: Walker's pre-1826 experiments with chemical lighting mixtures (Cleveland &amp; Teesside Local History Society)
Falsifiability: A dated publication or patent record showing Chancel's chemical match in a different year, or primary evidence that the 1805 attribution is a retrospective confusion</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1805-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1805</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Frederic Tudor's ice trade (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: Frederic Tudor (1783–1864), the 'Ice King,' started the trade in 1806 by shipping New England pond ice to Martinique — widely ridiculed at first, with early failures and imprisonment for debt; he persisted, devising sawdust insulation (a waste product of the New England lumber industry) and building ice houses; by 1820 he was steadily shipping to the Caribbean; in 1833 he began shipping to Calcutta, India — 16,000 miles, a four-month voyage — packing 180 tons, losing 80 to melt, and still profiting; India became his most lucrative market for two decades; the trade widened to England (Wenham Ice famous in London), South America, China, and Australia; ice houses stood in southern US cities, Caribbean islands, and Indian ports (Chennai's Ice House, now Vivekananda House, survives); the ice harvesters Thoreau saw at Walden Pond in 1846–47 worked for Tudor; the trade enabled refrigerator cars and the Chicago/Cincinnati meat-packing industry; Tudor's family continued the business until artificial ice production surpassed natural harvesting. The market-creation angle is documented: Tudor taught tropical customers to want ice, bar to bar, iced drinks and ice cream — selling to people who had never seen it.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts (origin); Martinique; Calcutta, India
Dates: 1806-01-01 → 1833-12-31 (confidence: high)

Frederic Tudor of Boston created the global natural-ice trade, shipping New England ice to the Caribbean from 1806 and to India from 1833.

Earliest attestation: Tudor's first Martinique shipment, 1806
Sources: Wikipedia: 'The trade was started by the New England businessman Frederic Tudor in 1806. Tudor shipped ice to the Caribbean island of Martinique... During the 1830s and 1840s the ice trade expanded further, with shipments reaching England, India, South America, China and Australia. Tudor made a fortune from the India trade, while brand names such as Wenham Ice became famous in London.'; ThoughtCo: Tudor's 1806 ridicule, debtor's prison, sawdust insulation, the late-1830s India expansion, the Walden Pond harvesters, the family business continuing until artificial ice won.; FEE: the 1833 Calcutta run — 16,000 miles, four months, 180 tons packed, 80 melted, still profitable; two decades as his most lucrative market; Weightman's Frozen Water Trade (2002).; StoryTrails: Chennai's Ice House (now Vivekananda House) on Marina Beach; the bar-to-bar market creation; sawdust insulation; the transport network.
Falsifiability: Tudor's shipping records proven forged</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1806-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1806</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The British Orders in Council (1807–1812)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Orders and their suspension/repeal dates are documented parliamentary acts, convergently reported by the secondary scholarship (Hickey 1989; Hitsman 1999; Mahon 1991 via the Orders in Council article). Held below VERIFIED — this record rests on secondary scholarship's account of the primary acts, not on the primary texts themselves.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (Parliament — the acts and their suspension)
Dates: 1807 → 1812-08-12 (confidence: high)

Britain's 1807 Orders in Council — retaliatory trade restrictions requiring neutral shipping to clear British ports — strangled American commerce with Europe; they were suspended 16 June 1812, two days before the United States declared war on 18 June 1812, and provisionally repealed 23 June 1812, the news reaching Madison only on 12 August 1812.

Earliest attestation: The Orders in Council themselves, 1807 (British acts of state); Castlereagh's 16 June 1812 suspension announcement in Parliament
Sources: Orders in Council (1807), Wikipedia — Repeal section: suspension 16 June 1812 (Castlereagh, Parliament, two days before the US declaration); provisional repeal 23 June 1812; news reached London of the declaration 29 July; repeal news reached Madison 12 August 1812 (citing Hickey 1989; Hitsman 1999; Mahon 1991); UPenn course materials, 'The War of 1812' — timeline: Orders suspended June 16, war declared June 18, repeal news to Madison August 12, 'better communications would have avoided war'; peacehistory-usfp.org, 'The War of 1812' — the spring-1812 conciliation sequence: Chesapeake affair settled, British navy ordered to keep clear of the American coast, Orders repealed in June; Madison's June 1 war message claiming no 'conciliatory dispositions'
Falsifiability: Would require the parliamentary record to show a different suspension date, or the repeal-date chain to break. The secondary scholarship (Hickey 1989; Hitsman 1999; Mahon 1991) converges on the dates.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1807-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1807</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The tin can (Durand, 1810)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent document itself is the primary source; the 1813 cannery is multiply attested in business history.
Layer: real · Place: London (patent enrolled; first cannery at Bermondsey, opened 1813)
Dates: 1810 → 1813 (confidence: high)

English merchant Peter Durand patented the tinplate food can in 1810 (British Patent 3,372, 25 August 1810), adapting Nicolas Appert's heat-preservation process to iron canisters coated in tin; Donkin, Hall &amp; Gamble bought the rights and opened the world's first commercial cannery in London in 1813.

Earliest attestation: British Patent 3,372 (1810); Donkin, Hall &amp; Gamble business records, 1813
Sources: British Patent 3,372, granted to Peter Durand, 25 August 1810 — tinplate canisters for preserving animal and vegetable food; Researchpod, "Lifting the lid on the tin can opener" — Durand's 1810 patent; rights purchased by Donkin, Hall &amp; Gamble; world's first canning factory in London, 1813; wrought-iron cans, hammer-and-chisel opening, the 1820s label advising 'cut round the top near the outer edge with a chisel and hammer'; Smithsonian Magazine (Kat Eschner, 24 Aug 2017) — Durand's 1810 patent building on Appert; early cans six per hour rising to sixty by the 1840s; wrought-iron cans up to 3/16 in thick; hammer and chisel as the manufacturer's suggested method
Falsifiability: A dated tinplate can manufactured before 1810, or proof the 1810 patent document is spurious</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1810-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1810</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Luddite movement, 1811–1817</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The movement's existence, its regional structure, its targets, and the state's reply are established by primary documents: the Luddites' own letters and proclamations (Binfield corpus; HO 42/118), the 1812 Frame-Breaking Act (capital offense), the York Special Commission records (over 60 charged, 17 hanged January 1813, ~30 acquitted), and Byron's 27 February 1812 Lords speech against the bill.
Layer: real · Place: Arnold, Nottinghamshire, England
Dates: 1811 → 1817 (confidence: high)

The Luddite movement (1811–1817) was a protest movement of English textile workers — framework knitters in Nottinghamshire/Leicestershire/Derbyshire, croppers in the West Riding of Yorkshire, handloom weavers — who conducted organized, often nocturnal raids to destroy specific textile machinery they held responsible for cutting wages, de-skilling work, and breaking standard labor practices; the movement began at Arnold, Nottinghamshire, on 11 March 1811, spread to Yorkshire and Lancashire in 1812, and was suppressed by legal and military force culminating in the York Special Commission of January 1813.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary Luddite writings from 1811 onward, collected in Kevin Binfield's 'Writings of the Luddites' (2004); the TNA HO 42/118 'Ned Lud's Proclamation' of 23 December 1811; the York Special Commission trial records, January 1813
Sources: Kevin Binfield (ed.), 'Writings of the Luddites' (Johns Hopkins UP, 2004) — the corpus of Luddite letters, songs, and proclamations; The National Archives — 'The proclamation of Ned Ludd' (HO 42/118, 23 December 1811); HISTORY — 'The Original Luddites Raged Against the Machine of the Industrial Revolution' (on the York Special Commission: over 60 charged, 17 hanged January 1813); Wikipedia — Luddite (Destruction of Stocking Frames, etc. Act 1812; Byron's 27 February 1812 maiden speech in the Lords); University of Huddersfield news archive — 'The Luddites go online' (West Riding croppers, Rawfolds Mill, York Gaol January 1813); E. P. Thompson, 'The Making of the English Working Class' (1963) — the moral-economy reading of Luddism; Eric Hobsbawm, 'The Machine Breakers' (1952); 'Labouring Men' — 'collective bargaining by riot'; Malcolm I. Thomis, 'The Luddites: Machine-Breaking in Regency England' (1970) — machine-breaking as worker tactic, not anti-machinery hostility
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the 1811–1813 disturbances were unrelated coordinated events with no shared name, aims, or correspondence</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1811-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1271,52.9793,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1811</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The destruction of the Grande Armée, 1812</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The annihilation itself is attested across French, Russian, and neutral sources and visualized in Minard's famous 1869 chart (422,000 crossed, ~100,000 reached Moscow, ~10,000 recrossed in fighting condition); held below VERIFIED because the exact force size (~500,000-650,000) and death toll (~380,000) vary between historians.
Layer: real · Place: Western Russia (Niemen to Moscow to Berezina)
Dates: 1812-06 → 1812-12 (confidence: high)

The 1812 Russian campaign destroyed the Grande Armée as a fighting force: of the several hundred thousand men who crossed the Niemen in June 1812, only tens of thousands recrossed in fighting condition by December — the largest army Europe had yet seen, annihilated.

Earliest attestation: French army returns and muster rolls, 1812-13; Minard's 1869 chart from the official returns
Sources: Minard, Charles Joseph. 'Carte figurative des pertes successives en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne de Russie 1812-1813' (1869); Zamoyski, Adam. Moscow 1812: Napoleon's Fatal March (HarperCollins, 2004)
Falsifiability: A contemporary complete muster showing the army largely intact in December 1812 would falsify the scale; no such record exists.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.0,54.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Burning of Moscow, September 1812</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested as an event: French military accounts, Russian civilian and official accounts, and the physical destruction of the city itself.
Layer: real · Place: Moscow
Dates: 1812-09-14 → 1812-09-18 (confidence: high)

Moscow burned 14-18 September 1812 (Old Style 2-6 September), beginning the day French troops entered the evacuated city; roughly three-quarters of the mostly wooden city was destroyed over five days.

Earliest attestation: French officers' letters and journals from Moscow, September 1812; Rostopchin's own correspondence; Russian civilian accounts
Sources: Zamoyski, Adam. Moscow 1812: Napoleon's Fatal March (HarperCollins, 2004); Mikaberidze, Alexander. The Burning of Moscow: Napoleon's Trial by Fire 1812 (Pen &amp; Sword, 2014); Rostopchin, Fyodor — 'La vérité sur l'incendie de Moscou', Paris pamphlet, 1823, and subsequent admission
Falsifiability: Already falsified alternative 'no fire': the burned city, French orders responding to it, and Rostopchin's own papers all attest the event.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The War of 1812</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary sources converge: the 18 June 1812 declaration, the signed Ghent treaty text (24 December 1814), the 16 February 1815 Senate ratification, and the New Orleans battle returns (8 January 1815).
Layer: real · Place: United States / British North America / the Atlantic (war theater); Ghent, Belgium (treaty)
Dates: 1812-06-18 → 1815-02-17 (confidence: high)

The United States declared war on Great Britain on 18 June 1812; the war ended with the Treaty of Ghent (signed 24 December 1814, ratified 16 February 1815), with the Battle of New Orleans fought 8 January 1815 — after the treaty was signed.

Earliest attestation: The 18 June 1812 declaration of war (Congressional vote); the Treaty of Ghent's signed text, 24 December 1814
Sources: US Naval History &amp; Heritage Command, 'Stalemate: Treaty of Ghent Ends War of 1812 in a Draw' — treaty timeline, New Orleans 8 January 1815, ratification 16 February 1815, proclaimed 18 February 1815; americanhistorycentral.com, 'Treaty of Ghent' — negotiations, the treaty's terms (no impressment, no maritime rights addressed), New Orleans after the signature
Falsifiability: Would require the declaration, treaty, and ratification records to be other than dated — multiple independent primary sources converge on the same dates.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.03,38.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Key writes 'Defence of Fort M'Henry' (The Star-Spangled Banner), 13–16 September 1814</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested primary evidence: Key's surviving manuscript, the next-day broadside with the tune notation, same-week newspaper printings, the 1814 Carrs sheet-music first edition, and the documented British bombardment record (Armistead's report, ship logs).
Layer: real · Place: Fort McHenry, Baltimore, Maryland (written aboard a detained cartel vessel in the Patapsco during the bombardment)
Dates: 1814-09-13 → 1814-09-16 (confidence: high)

On the morning of 14 September 1814, after watching the British bombardment of Fort McHenry from detention aboard a British vessel, Francis Scott Key wrote the poem 'Defence of Fort M'Henry' — the words that became 'The Star-Spangled Banner' — setting them to the tune 'To Anacreon in Heaven' (commonly attributed to John Stafford Smith, c. 1770).

Earliest attestation: Key's own draft (untitled, unsigned, written on the back of a letter) — surviving manuscript held by the Maryland Center for History and Culture; the next-day broadside 'Defence of Fort M'Henry' (notation 'Tune – Anacreon in Heaven'); Baltimore newspapers within days; the first printed edition (Baltimore: Carrs Music Store, [1814]) held by the Morgan Library &amp; Museum, James Fuld Music Collection
Sources: Veteran's Today (citing the record) — Key watched the 25-hour bombardment 13–14 Sept 1814; finished manuscript untitled/unsigned at the Indian Queen Hotel 16 Sept; printed as broadside 'Defence of Fort M'Henry' the next day with 'Tune – Anacreon in Heaven'; Key had previously used the tune for his 1805 'When the Warrior Returns'; The Morgan Library &amp; Museum, 2014 press release — rare first edition: 'The Star Spangled Banner [Music by John Stafford; words by Francis Scott Key]. Baltimore: Carrs Music Store, [1814]'; tune composed for the Anacreontic Society, Crown and Anchor Tavern, Strand; not declared official anthem until 1931; ChoralWiki / CPDL — anthem recognized for official use by the Navy 1889, by President Wilson 1916; made national anthem by congressional resolution 3 March 1931 (46 Stat. 1508, codified at 36 U.S.C. § 301), signed by Hoover; HISTORY.com — Key detained aboard the ship negotiating Dr. William Beanes's release; bombardment watched from ~8 miles; poem penned at dawn; set to Stafford Smith's 'To Anacreon in Heaven'; Wilson 1916 designation; adopted 3 March 1931
Falsifiability: A dated contemporary document placing Key elsewhere during 13–16 September 1814, or a pre-September 1814 printing of the poem, would overturn the record; none exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1814-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.5803,39.2633,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1814</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cambronne's one-word reply — 'le mot de Cambronne'</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested only through a literary tradition 47 years after the fact, and explicitly denied by the only man who could have said it — so it cannot rise above CLAIMED, and it cannot be killed by anything short of a better-attested alternative.
Layer: real · Place: Waterloo battlefield, Walloon Brabant, Belgium
Dates: 1815-06-18 → 1815-06-18 (confidence: traditional)

When the British insisted on the surrender of the last Old Guard square at Waterloo, General Cambronne answered with a single scatological word: 'Merde!'

Earliest attestation: The version is reported by secondary sources as a battlefield tradition; its immortalization is Victor Hugo's in Les Misérables (1862), 47 years after the event
Sources: Victor Hugo, Les Misérables (1862) — 'Brave Frenchmen, will you not surrender?' Cambronne answered, 'Merde!'; Edmond Rostand, L'Aiglon (1900) — repeats the word; Mark Adkin's assessment (via napoleonistyka): Cambronne was 'very much the rough spoken, hard as nails ex-ranker — a soldier's soldier. For this reason perhaps Merde! is the more likely'; Cambronne's own denials (he denied both the noble sentence and the one-word response); The French euphemism industry: 'le mot de Cambronne', the verb 'cambronniser'
Falsifiability: A contemporary battlefield dispatch or a British officer's letter of June 1815 reporting the word would promote it; a battlefield witness explicitly denying any reply was given would demote it toward SPECULATION.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1815-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.4125,50.6794,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1815</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tambora-year motive for the Laufmaschine</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The crisis is verified history (Tambora April 1815; 1816 crop failures and horse slaughter attested in the ADFC biography); but the inventor's motive is unattested in any Drais document, and his 1813/14 four-wheeled human-powered vehicles predate Tambora — so the link stays CLAIMED, not CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Mannheim / the Baden crisis of 1816
Dates: 1815 → 1817 (confidence: medium)

Karl Drais invented the Laufmaschine in response to the horse-feed crisis of the Year Without a Summer (1816), when the April 1815 eruption of Mount Tambora caused crop failures that starved horses across Europe.

Earliest attestation: The causal narrative is asserted in the modern Drais biographical tradition (notably the ADFC/Lessing biography); no statement of motive by Drais himself is on record.
Sources: ADFC Mannheim biography of Karl Drais: sequence of poor harvests from 1812, Tambora April 1815, summer-1816 snow, crops failed, 'horses were slaughtered for lack of food even for the people'; the velocipede as 'a high potential alternative to horse-based traffic'; Drais's own earlier work: two four-wheeled human-powered vehicles (1813/1814), presented in Vienna — predating Tambora, the honest counterpoint to a pure crisis-motive reading
Falsifiability: A Drais letter, memorandum, or contemporary interview stating his motive — for or against the crisis explanation.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1815-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.466,49.4875,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1815</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Battle of Waterloo (18 June 1815)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The event is multiply and independently attested by hostile traditions (British dispatch, Prussian reports, French accounts) written within days, and corroborated by the battlefield archaeology.
Layer: real · Place: Waterloo battlefield, Walloon Brabant, Belgium
Dates: 1815-06-18 → 1815-06-18 (confidence: high)

On 18 June 1815, near Waterloo in present-day Belgium, Napoleon Bonaparte's Armée du Nord was defeated by the Anglo-Allied army under the Duke of Wellington and the Prussian army under Gebhard von Blücher; it was Napoleon's last battle, and he abdicated four days later.

Earliest attestation: Wellington's dispatch to Lord Bathurst, dated 19 June 1815, written the morning after the battle
Sources: Wellington's Waterloo dispatch, 19 June 1815 (The National Archives); Gneisenau's reports and Prussian staff accounts, June 1815; Napoleon's own bulletins and later St Helena accounts; Andrew Roberts, Waterloo: June 18, 1815 — The Battle for Modern Europe (2015); The battlefield archaeology (Mont-Saint-Jean, Hougoumont, Plancenoit)
Falsifiability: No plausible falsifier survives; the grade would collapse only if the converging contemporary dispatch record were shown to be a fabrication.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1815-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.4125,50.6794,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1815</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Laufmaschine's first ride (12 June 1817)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The ride is multiply attested in contemporary and near-contemporary sources (Drais's reports, the Baden decree, the French patent, Johnson's licensed-style copies); held below VERIFIED on small variance in reported distance and time across retellings.
Layer: real · Place: Mannheim, Grand Duchy of Baden
Dates: 1817-06-12 → 1817-06-12 (confidence: high)

Karl Drais rode the first documented two-wheeled steerable vehicle — the Laufmaschine — from Mannheim to the Schwetzinger Relaishaus (Rheinau) and back on 12 June 1817, about 7 km each way, the round trip in a little more than an hour, establishing the two-wheeler principle basic to the bicycle and the motorcycle.

Earliest attestation: Drais's own reports and contemporary notices of the 1817 demonstration; the 1818 Baden protection decree and January 1818 French patent; Denis Johnson's improved London model, late 1818.
Sources: ADFC (Allgemeiner Deutscher Fahrrad-Club), Kreisverband Mannheim: 'Karl Drais — the new biography' (2006/2010/2014) — 12 June 1817 first ride, Mannheim to Schwetzinger Relaishaus, 'a little more than an hour' round trip; Baden 1818 decree; Johnson 1818; Wikipedia, 'Karl Drais' — Laufmaschine, first ride 12 June 1817, ~7 km, French patent January 1818, the 1819 craze and municipal bans
Falsifiability: A dated two-wheeled steerable vehicle predating June 1817 (the 'Comte de Sivrac' 1792 célérifère claim fails this test — the two-wheeled célérifère never existed).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1817-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.466,49.4875,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1817</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Blundell's human-to-human transfusions (1818)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary published case reports with instruments, indications, and outcomes over a decade; the 5-of-10 figure is his own accounting, honestly partial.
Layer: real · Place: London (Guy's Hospital)
Dates: 1818 → 1830 (confidence: high)

James Blundell (British obstetrician) performed the first documented successful human-to-human blood transfusions: 1818, for postpartum hemorrhage — the patient's husband as donor, about four ounces, delivered by syringe. Between 1825 and 1830 he performed ten transfusions, five of which he reported as beneficial, publishing the results and devising transfusion instruments.

Earliest attestation: Blundell's own published reports (1818 onward); the 1825–1830 case series
Sources: James Blundell, published case reports 1818–1830 (Medico-Chirurgical Transactions; the Lancet) — the 1818 postpartum-hemorrhage transfusion, the ten-case series; Transfusion-medicine histories — '1818: James Blundell performs the first successful transfusion of human blood to a patient for the treatment of postpartum hemorrhage... Between 1825 and 1830, he performs 10 transfusions, five of which prove beneficial'; Irish Blood Transfusion Service history — '1818: The first human to human blood transfusion took place in London'
Falsifiability: Proof Blundell's published case series is fabricated, or a dated earlier published human-to-human transfusion</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1818-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1818</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fresnel's lighthouse lens: invented 1819-1822, first lit at Cordouan 25 July 1823</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent secondary sources (the Fresnel biography record, the translated Arago obituary, Smithsonian, lighthouse-history surveys) converge on the 1819-1823 sequence and the Cordouan first lighting; the Buffon/Condorcet/Rogers/Brewster prehistory is independently documented. Held below VERIFIED because the loop has not inspected the primary commission records or Fresnel's 1822 memoir themselves.
Layer: real · Place: Cordouan lighthouse, Gironde estuary, France
Dates: 1819 → 1823 (confidence: medium)

Augustin-Jean Fresnel invented the stepped annular lighthouse lens for the French Commission des Phares (1819-1822): his lentilles a echelons replaced the parabolic reflectors then in use; the revolving eight-panel first-order apparatus passed its official public test atop the unfinished Arc de Triomphe on 20 August 1822 (witnessed by Louis XVIII and his entourage from 32 km away); the world's first lighthouse Fresnel lens was lit at the Cordouan lighthouse on 25 July 1823. The invention carried a documented 74-year prehistory: Buffon had proposed a stepped lens in 1748 (for burning glasses, single piece), Condorcet had suggested assembling annular sections separately on a frame in 1790, Rogers had focused a lighthouse beam with a conventional lens at Portland Bill in 1789, and Brewster had advocated a Condorcet-style lens for British lighthouses by 1820.

Earliest attestation: Fresnel's 1822 memoir on the lighthouse lens system (memoire sur un nouveau systeme d'eclairage des phares); the Commission des Phares records, 1819-1822
Sources: Augustin-Jean Fresnel biography — 21 June 1819 secondment to the Commission des Phares; end-August 1819 lentilles a echelons recommendation; 13 April 1821 public demonstration; 20 August 1822 Arc de Triomphe test witnessed by Louis XVIII from 32 km; 25 July 1823 first lighting at Cordouan; Arago obituary of Fresnel (translated, arXiv) — Buffon imagined dividing a convex lens surface into annular zones displaced parallel to the axis; Fresnel 'found — which no one before him had found — the means of making it feasible, in having the rings of the same stepped lens executed separately,' correcting spherical aberration by ring curvature; Fresnel lens history — Buffon 1748 first to replace a convex lens with concentric annular prisms (single piece); Condorcet 1790 suggested separate sections assembled on a frame (intended for burning glasses, not lighthouses); Rogers 1788 first lighthouse lens (Portland Bill 1789); Brewster 1811/1820 advocacy; Smithsonian (via taggedwiki) — 'the first Fresnel lens was used in 1823 in the Cordouan lighthouse at the mouth of the Gironde estuary; its light could be seen from more than 20 miles (32 km) out'; The Pursuit of History — first Fresnel lens operational at Tour de Cordouan 25 July 1823; Fresnel's fixed lens 1824; Navesink Twin Lights 1841 first in the United States; Cordouan's original lens replaced 1854; ~10,000 Fresnel lenses in service within a century
Falsifiability: Discovery of a working stepped lighthouse lens predating 1822, or primary commission records showing the Cordouan installation was not the first</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1819-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1731,45.5858,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1819</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Starke's Travels on the Continent (1820) — the first modern travel guide</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1820 first edition is a documented artifact (Cambridge UP reprint from the original text); a 2024 scholarly chapter explicitly describes it as 'may accurately be described as the first modern travel guide,' citing its practical apparatus — ratings, itineraries, maps, updated editions.
Layer: real · Place: London (John Murray, publisher)
Dates: 1820 → 1820 (confidence: high)

Mariana Starke's Travels on the Continent, written for the use and particular information of travellers, published by John Murray in 1820, was the first modern travel guide — practical itineraries, journey times, inn and restaurant assessments (the 'daggers of admiration' ratings), maps and archaeological plans, updated across editions.

Earliest attestation: The 1820 first edition itself (John Murray, London)
Sources: Springer Nature chapter, 'Inventing the Travel Guide: Mariana Starke's Advice for the Independent Traveller' (2024): Starke's Travels on the Continent (1820) 'may accurately be described as the first modern travel guide,' written for the new class of affluent British middle-class travellers; notes John Murray II 'unscrupulously plagiarised her work in Murray's Handbooks,' and that Karl Baedeker's guides copied; Cambridge University Press reprint listing (Cambridge Library Collection): first published 1820; updated information from late-1810s research; covers most of mainland Europe with itineraries, journey times, sightseeing and inn recommendations; health-travel advice; Mimesis journal article on Starke and the Grand Tour: the 1820 Murray collaboration — Travels on the Continent, 300 pages in a pocketable 19cm format, with an Advertisement reassuring readers about post-1814 road safety
Falsifiability: An earlier-published guidebook (pre-1820) with the same modern apparatus — star/dagger-style ratings, practical itineraries, maps, and updated editions</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1820-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1820</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Napoleon's actual height</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A single primary post-mortem measurement in French units converts to ~168-169 cm; contemporary descriptions and period clothing evidence are consistent — held at CORROBORATED (single autopsy, variable conversions 168-170 cm) rather than VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Longwood, Saint Helena
Dates: 1821 → 1821 (confidence: medium)

Napoleon Bonaparte's height was approximately 169 cm (about 5 feet 6 to 5 feet 7 inches in English measure), average to slightly above average for French men of his era.

Earliest attestation: Autopsy report, 6 May 1821: 5 pieds 2 pouces (French units). Contemporary descriptions during his life generally describe him as average in stature.
Sources: Autopsy report, Saint Helena, 6 May 1821 (Antommarchi et al.) — 5 pieds 2 pouces; National Geographic, 'Was Napoleon even short? Inside the history of discrimination against short men' (unit conversion + autopsy context + Gillray analysis); YouGov Eurotrack, 'How does Europe remember Napoleon?' (Sept 2023, fieldwork 7-22 Sept 2023) — notes 5'6.5" (168 cm) as average height for the time; Uniform and clothing evidence (standard sizing of the period, not specially tailored for an unusually short man)
Falsifiability: A contemporary French-language record measuring him below ~165 cm in a verified pied-du-roi unit, or discovery that the 1821 autopsy record is fabricated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1821-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.73,-15.95,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1821</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1822 patent: the mechanical pencil's birthday</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across independent secondary sources (Wikipedia, dealer/manufacturer histories, Grace's Guide) with surviving marked examples in the antiques trade; held below VERIFIED because the patent specification itself was not primary-inspected this pass.
Layer: real · Place: London, England
Dates: 1822 → 1822 (confidence: high)

In 1822, Sampson Mordan and John Isaac Hawkins filed in Great Britain the first patent for a refillable metal pencil with an internal mechanism for propelling the graphite lead forward during use (the 'ever-pointed pencil'); Mordan bought out Hawkins and manufactured the pencils with Gabriel Riddle (1823–1837, SMGR hallmark), then as S. Mordan &amp; Co. into the 20th century.

Earliest attestation: British patent record, 1822; 1826 advertisement 'BY HIS MAJESTY'S ROYAL LETTERS PATENT. MORDAN &amp; CO.'S PATENT EVER-POINTED PENCILS.'
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Sampson Mordan': 'In 1822, Mordan and his co-inventor John Isaac Hawkins filed the first patent in Great Britain for a metal pencil with an internal mechanism for propelling the graphite lead shaft forward during use.'; Wikipedia, 'Mechanical pencil': 'The first patent for a refillable pencil with lead-propelling mechanism was issued to Sampson Mordan and John Isaac Hawkins in Britain in 1822.'; also notes the 1791 Pandora example and 160+ patents 1822–1874.; Yore Write!, 'S. Mordan &amp; Co.': Mordan 'filed the first patent (#4742) in England, in 1822, jointly with John Isaac Hawkins'; Mordan bought out Hawkins, partnered with Gabriel Riddle; earliest pencils hallmarked SMGR.; Grace's Guide to British Industrial History: '1822 Mordan patented his first ever pointed or propelling pencil on which the fortunes of his firm were based' (with the Hawkins/Riddle priority note).
Falsifiability: An earlier dated propelling-pencil patent, or proof the 1822 grant was never manufactured (refuted by surviving hallmarked examples), would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1822-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1822</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Walker's friction match (1826/1827) — the first practical friction match</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1827 sale is a primary ledger record; the 1826 invention is the consistent local and museum tradition; the no-patent fact and the Jones 1829 copy are consistently attested. Held at CORROBORATED on the inferential 1826 date — the sale itself is primary.
Layer: real · Place: Stockton-on-Tees, County Durham, England (59 High Street chemist's shop)
Dates: 1826 → 1827 (confidence: medium)

John Walker of Stockton-on-Tees invented the first practical, commercially successful friction match in 1826 (accidental ignition: a dipped stick scraped against his hearth), and sold the first recorded box on 7 April 1827 — recorded in his sales ledger as 'Sulphurata Hyper-Oxygenata Frict', batch 'No. 30', to a Mr. Hixon, a solicitor. The splints were sulfur-dipped, tipped with antimony trisulfide and potassium chlorate in gumwater, sold 1s 2d per 100 with a tin and a folded piece of sandpaper. Walker never patented the invention ('I doubt not it will be a benefit to the public, so let them have it'), despite encouragement from Michael Faraday; Samuel Jones of London copied the design in 1829 as 'Lucifers'. Walker died 1 May 1859 in Stockton, uncredited in his lifetime.

Earliest attestation: Walker's sales ledger, 7 April 1827 ('Sulphurata Hyper-Oxygenata Frict', batch No. 30)
Sources: Tees Valley Museums — 'John Walker and the friction match' (invention 1826, first sale 7 April 1827, Faraday's urging, death 1 May 1859 uncredited); stocktonteesside.co.uk — 'John Walker of Stockton-on-Tees — Inventor of the Friction Match' (first sale 7 April 1827 to Mr. Hixon; no patent despite Faraday; Jones 'Lucifers' 1829); Cleveland &amp; Teesside Local History Society — 'John Walker Sells Friction Matches – 1827' (59 High Street; 'Sulphurata Hyper-Oxygenata Frict'; 'friction lights' from Sept 1827); en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Walker_(inventor) — invention 1826 or 1827; 'I doubt not it will be a benefit to the public, so let them have it'; Walker never used 'Congreves'/'Lucifers' as names
Falsifiability: A dated friction-match record from another maker predating April 1827, or Walker's own notes (none survive — the formulation was recovered by later chemical analysis of his products)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1826-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.3154,54.5686,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1826</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Poenaru's 1827 fountain-pen patent</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply reported from the brevet itself (Wikipedia cites the 25 May 1827 brevet PDF, code 3208, with the French description) and corroborated by trade histories naming it the first patent for a pen holding its own ink supply; held below VERIFIED on the thin transmission of a single surviving document plus the non-survival of the pen.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France — where Poenaru studied at the École Polytechnique and registered the invention
Dates: 1827 → 1827 (confidence: high)

Petrache Poenaru registered a French patent on 25 May 1827 (brevet no. 3208) for a 'plume portable sans fin, qui s'alimente elle-meme avec de l'ancre' — a portable pen that recharges itself with ink, built from a large swan's quill used as an ink reservoir.

Earliest attestation: French Ministry of the Interior Manufacture Department registration, 25 May 1827 (Brevets d'Invention Nr. 3208)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Petrache Poenaru' — registration 25 May 1827, code 3208, 'plume portable sans fin, qui s'alimente elle-meme avec de l'ancre'; swan's quill reservoir; École Polytechnique context; Antique Trader, 'Putting pen to paper with distinction' — 'The first patent for a pen holding its own ink supply was issued in 1827 to Romanian Petrache Poenaru... made from a large swan quill... still messy, like its traditional dipping pen predecessors, and it still needed frequent refilling'
Falsifiability: A demonstration that brevet 3208 does not exist or describes a different device; held below VERIFIED because the pen itself does not survive and the record rides multiply-reported secondary transmission of the brevet</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1827-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1827</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Joseph Henry's wire: the science before the patent (1828–1835)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent secondary attestation of the experiments (Wikipedia's synthesis of Henry's papers; Union College's 1997 exhibit assessment; the Smithsonian's Henry records) plus Morse's own correspondence crediting Henry as his teacher in electromagnetism (HISTORY.com; Library of Congress). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED: Henry's deliberate non-patenting means there is no patent-office primary record of the kind the Morse and Cooke–Wheatstone systems carry — the science is documented in his notes and the Smithsonian, not in a granted patent.
Layer: real · Place: Albany, New York (the Albany Academy)
Dates: 1828-01-01 → 1835-12-31 (confidence: high)

Joseph Henry — at the Albany Academy, 1826–1832 — built the scientific core the telegraph ran on: the high-intensity multi-turn insulated electromagnet (1828), the 1831 'magnetic telegraph' demonstration ringing a bell through a mile of wire, and the 1835 mercury-dipping electrical relay. He refused to patent, choosing open science.

Earliest attestation: Henry's Albany Academy lecture notes and published descriptions, 1828–31; the Smithsonian's Henry collection
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Electric telegraph': Henry 1828 multi-turn electromagnet; 1831 Albany mile-of-wire 'magnetic telegraph'; 1835 mercury-dipping relay (with Davy); Union College exhibit: 'the first person to send a message over an electric wire (nearly 20 years before Samuel F. B. Morse)'; mentored Morse on the telegraph and Bell on the telephone; the henry unit; HISTORY.com: Morse 'learned more about the topic from American physicist Joseph Henry'
Falsifiability: Henry's own published descriptions and the Smithsonian's holdings being shown as misattributed or fabricated; Morse's correspondence explicitly credits learning electromagnetism from Henry</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1828-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.7562,42.6526,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1828</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Thimonnier's sewing machine: the first functional machine and the tailor riot</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1830 patent and the functional machine are multiply attested; the riot's exact year and machine count vary between sources.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (Thimonnier's workshop)
Dates: 1830 → 1841 (confidence: medium)

French tailor Barthélemy Thimonnier patented a working sewing machine in 1830; his chain-stitch machine (barbed needle, foot treadle, spring return) was put into production in a Paris workshop making army uniforms, and the machines were destroyed by a mob of tailors fearing for their livelihoods.

Earliest attestation: French patent 1830; Smithsonian Magazine history of the sewing machine; Sewing Machine News 1880 illustration of Thimonnier's machine
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine — 'The Many, Many Designs of the Sewing Machine'; Getty Images/SSPL — Thimonnier's chain-stitch machine, 1830 (photo caption record); France: A Reference Guide from Renaissance to the Present (Thimonnier entry, 1884)
Falsifiability: French patent records showing no 1830 Thimonnier filing, or proof the preserved machines postdate the patent.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1830-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1830</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>White phosphorus, phossy jaw, and the 1906 Berne Convention</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Lorinser's 1839/1844 work, the 1888 strike, the 1891 Salvation Army factory, and the 1906 convention (concluded 26 September 1906, in force 1 January 1912) are all consistently attested; held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED as the match-worker disease record is medical-historical rather than multi-primary.
Layer: real · Place: Bern, Switzerland (Berne Convention, 1906)
Dates: 1831 → 1912 (confidence: high)

White phosphorus — the active ingredient of most strike-anywhere matches from the 1840s to the 1910s (Charles Sauria's 1831 French substitution of white phosphorus for antimony sulfide in the Walker formula started the industry) — poisoned its workforce. The first case of phossy jaw (phosphorus necrosis of the jaw) was diagnosed in 1839 by Vienna physician Friedrich Wilhelm Lorinser in a Viennese matchstick maker; in 1844 he reported 22 cases and established white phosphorus's toxicity in matches. The disease fed the London matchgirls' strike of 1888 (Bryant &amp; May); William Booth and the Salvation Army opened a red-phosphorus match factory in 1891. The International Convention respecting the Prohibition of the Use of White (Yellow) Phosphorus in the Manufacture of Matches — the Berne Convention — was concluded 26 September 1906 and entered into force 1 January 1912, banning manufacture, import, and sale; it remains in force for 48 states (Switzerland depositary). Edward J. Phelan later said the ILO 'may in one sense be traced to the Berne Convention of 1906'.

Earliest attestation: Lorinser's 1839 diagnosis, Vienna; 1844 report of 22 cases
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phossy_jaw — Lorinser 1839 first case (Vienna matchstick maker, 5-year exposure); 1844 report of 22 cases; white phosphorus active ingredient 1840s–1910s; 1888 matchgirls' strike; Salvation Army 1891 red-phosphorus factory; Berne 1906; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berne_Convention_(1906) — concluded 26 September 1906; in force 1 January 1912; 48 states; Switzerland depositary; Phelan 1925 ILO remark; Scribd 'Evolution of Ethereal Matches' — Sauria's 1831 white-phosphorus substitution; 'from 1845 match-makers exposed to its fumes succumbed to necrosis'
Falsifiability: A dated medical or industrial record showing white phosphorus in matches was understood and regulated before Lorinser 1839, or evidence the Berne Convention did not drive the phase-out</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1831-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.4474,46.948,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1831</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Walter Hunt's 1834 lockstitch machine: built, never patented</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested that Hunt built a working lockstitch machine before Howe and declined to patent it; the 'unemployment fear' motive is the traditional telling, honestly flagged as such.
Layer: real · Place: New York City, USA (Hunt's workshop)
Dates: 1833 → 1834 (confidence: medium)

New York inventor Walter Hunt built a working lockstitch sewing machine in 1834 — the first American lockstitch machine, with an eye-pointed needle and shuttle interlocking two threads — but never patented it, reportedly fearing it would put seamstresses out of work.

Earliest attestation: Hunt's machine attested through later patent-war testimony and sewing-machine histories (19th c.); the same Walter Hunt patented the safety pin (U.S. 6,281, 1849) — see evt-hunt-safety-pin-patent-1849-001 (Sylvie Barlow #271 beat)
Sources: garmenco.org — History of the Sewing Machine (documents Hunt 1834 unpatented lockstitch); Scribd — History of Sewing Machine Inventions (Hunt 1834, never patented, unemployment fear); hooptalent.com — Who Invented the Sewing Machine (Hunt's eye-pointed needle and shuttle as the core mechanism)
Falsifiability: A genuine 1834-era Hunt patent filing, or proof the surviving Hunt machines are misdated post-1846 copies.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1833-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0,40.71,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1833</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Liebig's 1835 chemical silvering</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1835 date and the chemistry are consistently attested across technical and historical sources; the primary 1835 paper was not inspected this pass, so VERIFIED is withheld.
Layer: real · Place: Giessen, Germany (Liebig's university)
Dates: 1835 → 1835 (confidence: high)

In 1835, the German chemist Justus von Liebig developed a process for depositing metallic silver on glass by reducing ammoniated silver nitrate with an aldehyde/sugar — the chemical silvering that replaced poisonous tin-mercury amalgam mirror-making and made the modern looking glass cheap.

Earliest attestation: Liebig's 1835 publication on depositing metallic silver on glass.
Sources: NZIC, 'Silver Mirroring' — silver produced chemically by reducing a silver salt solution; Liebig-era process lineage; BenchChem, 'A Historical Perspective on the Elusive Silver Hydroxide' — 'In 1835, Justus von Liebig developed a method for depositing a thin layer of metallic silver onto glass'; Gizmodo (2017) — Liebig's 1835 breakthrough: 'Add some sugar to ammoniated silver nitrate, pour it onto glass, and blammo: you've got yourself a mirror'
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1835-dated publication by Liebig that contains no silvering work, or an earlier published silvering process, would revise the attribution.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.6727,50.5839,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Moon Hoax of 1835 — six days of lunar life in the New York Sun</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The six articles exist and are multiply documented in the Sun's own columns and the European reprints; the Locke authorship is the mainstream scholarly attribution but rests on an 1840 private letter, never a public admission.
Layer: real · Place: New York City — the Sun's office (claim); Cape of Good Hope (the fictional observatory)
Dates: 1835 → 1835 (confidence: high)

The New York Sun published six articles, 25–31 August 1835, reporting lunar life — bison, blue single-horned goats, miniature zebras, unicorns, bipedal tail-less beavers, and the winged Vespertilio-homo — discovered by Sir John Herschel at the Cape of Good Hope with a 24-foot, 7-ton lens, ostensibly written by his fictitious companion Dr. Andrew Grant and reprinted from the Edinburgh Journal of Science.

Earliest attestation: The Sun, New York, August–September 1835 — the articles themselves, six installments
Sources: Wikipedia 'Great Moon Hoax' (citing the Sun's own columns): headline 'GREAT ASTRONOMICAL DISCOVERIES, LATELY MADE BY SIR JOHN HERSCHEL... At the Cape of Good Hope. [From Supplement to the Edinburgh Journal of Science]'; Dr. Andrew Grant as ostensible author-companion; creatures catalogued (bison, single-horned goats, mini zebras, unicorns, bipedal tail-less beavers, Vespertilio-homo who built temples); lens '24 feet in diameter and 7 tons in weight'; observations terminated by the Sun making the lens a burning glass; Smithsonian Magazine ('The Great Moon Hoax Was Simply a Sign of Its Time'): the 24-foot lens claim, the lunar fauna progression across installments, the man-bats 'scientifically denominated Vespertilio-homo'; Herschel's real Cape expedition as the news hook the hoax rode; EarthSky (Aug 25, 2020): six articles from 25 August 1835; Herschel attribution; Richard Adams Locke the attributed author; the Edinburgh Journal of Science 'had been out of commission for some years'; papers across Europe reprinted the story; Smithsonian Libraries lithographs (Leopoldo Galluzzo, Naples 1836, 'Altre scoverte fatte nella luna dal Sigr. Herschel') — the European reprint industry as physical provenance of the story's travel
Falsifiability: A pre-August-1835 Edinburgh Journal of Science issue carrying the Herschel lunar series, or Herschel's Cape notebooks recording a 24-foot lens</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Poe's own moon hoax — 'Hans Pfaall,' June 1835</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The June 1835 Messenger publication and Poe's repeated plagiarism charge are both documented; held below VERIFIED on the influence question — whether Locke actually used Poe's story — which the scholarship leaves open.
Layer: real · Place: New York (the Sun) and Richmond (the Southern Literary Messenger)
Dates: 1835 → 1846 (confidence: high)

Edgar Allan Poe published 'The Unparalleled Adventure of One Hans Pfaall' — the first installment of his own moon hoax — in the June 1835 Southern Literary Messenger, a month before Locke's Sun series, and afterward charged that the Sun's hoax plagiarized his story.

Earliest attestation: Southern Literary Messenger, June 1835 — 'The Unparalleled Adventure of One Hans Pfaall'
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine ('The Great Moon Hoax Was Simply a Sign of Its Time'): Poe's 'The Unparalleled Adventure of One Hans Pfaall' published in the June 1835 Southern Literary Messenger, shortly before Locke's Sun series; 'Poe later accused Locke of stealing his idea. That isn't certain'; Wikipedia 'Great Moon Hoax': Poe claimed the story was a plagiarism of his earlier work; his 1846 'Literati of New York City' biographical sketch of Locke in Godey's Lady's Book; Wikipedia 'The Unparalleled Adventure of One Hans Pfaall': the original story appeared in the June 1835 Southern Literary Messenger, a month before the Sun's 'Great Moon Hoax' in August; the story was reprinted Sept 2–5, 1835 in the New York Transcript as 'Lunar Discoveries, Extraordinary Aerial Voyage'; Saturday Evening Post ('The Great Moon Hoax of 1835', Aug 2020): 'the editor of the piece was... Richard Adams Locke' — Poe's Moon story was edited by the man he would later accuse; Wikipedia 'The Balloon-Hoax': Poe's 1844 Sun balloon hoax and his decade-long complaint that the Moon Hoax had plagiarized Hans Pfaall and made 'tremendous profits' from his story (chronicled in Goodman's The Sun and the Moon, 2008)
Falsifiability: A June-1835 or earlier Messenger issue without the story, or evidence the Sun series predates Poe's publication</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Marsh test for arsenic (1836)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The paper, the procedure, and the forensic adoption are multiply documented in primary and secondary sources; the chemistry is not in dispute. The famous failure modes (antimony, impure zinc) are themselves documented and graded as limitations.
Layer: real · Place: Royal Arsenal, Woolwich, London (Marsh's workplace; paper published in Edinburgh)
Dates: 1836 → 1836 (confidence: high)

In 1836 the Scottish chemist James Marsh published a sensitive test for arsenic: the sample is treated with arsenic-free zinc and sulphuric acid, any arsenic present forming arsine gas, which is led through a heated tube where it decomposes, leaving a mirror-like deposit of metallic arsenic on a cold porcelain or glass surface. The test could detect minute traces in exhumed matter and became forensic toxicology's first decisive instrument.

Earliest attestation: Marsh (1836) paper; occasioned by an 1833 poisoning trial in which his decomposed sample failed to convince a jury
Sources: Marsh, J. (1836), 'Account of a method of separating small quantities of arsenic from substances with which it may be mixed', Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal 21; Science History Institute, 'Prefiguring the Arsenic Wars' (the Marsh test's gradual acceptance; early Marsh-test nulls at Tulle vs Orfila's positive)
Falsifiability: The method is documented chemistry with a published procedure and a century of use; the grade covers the method's existence and forensic adoption, not the infallibility of any single application.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1836-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.056,51.493,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1836</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cooke &amp; Wheatstone: the first commercial telegraph (1837–1839)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary attestation: the patent record (No. 8345, 10 June 1837), the GWR contract and Cooke's cost accounts, King's College London's Wheatstone exhibition archive, J. J. Fahie's 'Heroes of the Telegraph' contemporary account of the 25 July 1837 trial (Wheatstone at Euston, Cooke at Camden, Robert Stephenson watching), and Wikipedia's synthesis of the same primary sources. The 1839 line's daily operation and the 1840 Blackwall deployment are independently documented.
Layer: real · Place: Paddington Station, London (the 1839 line's London terminus)
Dates: 1837-06-10 → 1840-07-06 (confidence: high)

Cooke &amp; Wheatstone's electric telegraph — patented 10 June 1837, trialed Euston–Camden 25 July 1837 — produced the world's first commercial telegraph line: Paddington to West Drayton (13 miles), opened July 1839 on the Great Western Railway.

Earliest attestation: English patent 10 June 1837 (No. 8345); the GWR agreement finalized April–May 1838; contemporary press and company records
Sources: King's College London, Wheatstone exhibition: patent June 1837; 25 July 1837 trial; 9 July 1839 Paddington–West Drayton line; 'first successful commercial application of the electric telegraph in the world' (Blackwall, 1840); Morse's 1838 English patent application opposed by Cooke &amp; Wheatstone; J. J. Fahie, 'A History of Electric Telegraphy' (via public-library.uk): 25 July 1837 trial account — Wheatstone at Euston, Cooke at Camden, Stephenson observing; the five-needle mechanism; Wikipedia, 'Cooke and Wheatstone telegraph': 1838 Paddington–West Drayton as 'the first commercial telegraph in the world'; five-needle six-wire system; the 1843 Slough extension and first public telegraph service; Cooke's cost accounts (via electrictelegraph.co.uk / Kieve): £2,817 10s. for laying the Paddington line; GWR agreement finalized April–May 1838
Falsifiability: Evidence that no such patent existed, that the 1837 trial did not occur, or that the 1839 line did not operate — the patent office record, the GWR contract (Cooke's £2,817 10s. costs), and King's College's Wheatstone archive would all have to be fabricated</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1837-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1758,51.5154,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1837</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Horace Mann's common-school reforms (1837-1848)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The reform record is multiply attested in Mann's own contemporary reports and the Board's paper trail; held below VERIFIED because this run's pass used secondary aggregators rather than the primary report texts.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1837 → 1848 (confidence: high)

Horace Mann, as first Secretary of the Massachusetts Board of Education (1837-1848), campaigned for free, tax-funded, non-sectarian common schools open to all children: he established the first public normal school (Lexington, 1839) to train teachers, pushed standardized curricula, age-graded classrooms, written examinations and report cards, founded The Common School Journal (1838), and wrote 12 annual reports that carried the common-school model nationwide; most U.S. states adopted a version of the Massachusetts system.

Earliest attestation: Mann's 12 annual reports to the Massachusetts Board of Education, 1837-1848 (contemporary primary documents)
Sources: Wikipedia — Horace Mann (1st Secretary of the Massachusetts Board of Education 1837-1848; 12 annual reports; normal schools adopted by most states); resourcesforhistoryteachers (pbworks) — 1837 secretaryship, Common School Journal 1838, normal schools, school-year lengthening, funding campaigns; ushistoryscene — Mann's 1844 European tour and the Prussian 'common schools' model; 1852 Massachusetts compulsory-attendance law; nodaway.org — Mann 'Father of the Common School Movement' dedication text (Lexington normal school 1839; standardized curricula; tax-supported funding)
Falsifiability: Board minutes contradicting the secretaryship, or proof the 12 reports and the 1839 normal school are misattributed</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1837-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1837</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Morse system: the single wire that won the world (1837–1844)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestation: the 1840 patents (US Patent Office), the 1843 congressional appropriation, the 24 May 1844 demonstration and its press coverage, Vail's own 1845 history, the Library of Congress Morse Papers, and the commercial record (Magnetic Telegraph Company licensing; Western Union's 1851 formation; 4,000+ offices by 1866). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the scientific-originality question: the system's physics rest on Henry's prior, unpatented 1828–31 work, and the code itself is Vail's (Jan 1838) — the 'Morse' system is a genuine American engineering achievement with multiple authors.
Layer: real · Place: Baltimore, Maryland (the 24 May 1844 line: Washington, D.C. → Baltimore)
Dates: 1837-09-01 → 1844-05-24 (confidence: high)

The Morse/Gale/Vail single-wire telegraph system — dots-and-dashes code, practical hand key, embossing receiver — was developed 1837–1844 and connected Washington to Baltimore on 24 May 1844 ('What hath God wrought'), becoming the system that won the world.

Earliest attestation: Morse's 1837 caveat; US patents 20 June 1840 ('Recording Electric Telegraph' and 'Telegraph Symbols'), based on the 1837 caveat; the 24 May 1844 demonstration, widely reported
Sources: Library of Congress, Morse Papers: 1837 caveat, 1840 patents, $30,000 appropriation 23 Feb 1843, 1844 demonstration details; HISTORY.com: single-circuit system of key, battery, wire, receiver; Morse/Gale/Vail collaboration; Vail (1845): code originated Jan 1838 by Alfred Vail; Vail's hand key; partnership terms (Vail half of foreign proceeds)
Falsifiability: Evidence that the 1844 line did not operate or that the system attributed to Morse/Gale/Vail was someone else's — the patents, the congressional record, and the Magnetic Telegraph Company's licensing (50+ companies by the early 1850s; Western Union 1851) would all have to be fabricated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1837-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.6122,39.2904,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1837</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Vulcanization: Goodyear's process, Hancock's patent, the band's enabling science</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent secondary attestation of the patents and the filing order (ACS, Rubber News, encyclopedia entries), but the 'reverse-engineered the samples' mechanism and the exact stove-incident details rest on secondary reporting — held below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Connecticut — the Lower Naugatuck Valley rubber works (Goodyear's manufacturing ground); the 1839 stove incident is traditionally placed at Woburn, Massachusetts
Dates: 1839 → 1844 (confidence: medium)

Vulcanization — heating natural rubber with sulfur to make it durable, elastic, and temperature-stable — was discovered by Charles Goodyear (1839) and patented by him in the US (patent 3,633, 15 June 1844); Thomas Hancock independently developed the process and patented it in Britain about eight weeks before Goodyear filed there, and is credited today as an independent co-inventor. The Goodyear Tire &amp; Rubber Company (founded 1898 by Frank Seiberling in Akron, Ohio) was named for Goodyear, never founded by him; he died 1 July 1860 about $200,000 in debt.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 3,633 (Goodyear, 1844); Hancock's British vulcanization patent (1843); the ACS centenary account of the sample shipment and the British filing race.
Sources: American Chemical Society (C&amp;EN), 'Lessons learned from Charles Goodyear and the Committee on Patents and Related Matters' — 1839 stove discovery; samples sent to England; 'an English competitor was able to reverse engineer Goodyear's discovery and beat him to the British patent office'; Goodyear died 1860 deeply in debt; Rubber News column (2014), 'Happy anniversary to scientist's revolutionary discovery' — Goodyear 'filed his patent in England eight weeks after Thomas Hancock did and lost out'; Hancock credited in some sources as independent co-inventor; ThoughtCo (Mary Bellis, 2018), 'Vulcanized Rubber' — Goodyear US patent 3,633 for vulcanized rubber; 'On March 17, 1845, the first rubber band was patented by Stephen Perry of London, made from vulcanized rubber'
Falsifiability: A showing that Hancock's 1843 British patent does not exist or covers a different process, or that Goodyear filed in Britain first — the filing order is on the patent record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1839-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.0,41.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1839</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1840 Lafarge arsenic trial at Tulle</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Split by doctrine. The CONVICTION is VERIFIED: a court-record event of 19 September 1840, multiply reported (sentence of life with hard labor, commuted by Louis-Philippe). The 'first trial decided by forensic chemistry' reading is CORROBORATED: independently stated in modern secondary literature (Bertomeu-Sánchez 2006; Science History Institute; the trial record's own evidence chain), though an absolute 'first' is hard to prove — fenced, not asserted. The GUILT (that Marie actually poisoned Charles) is CLAIMED: arsenic was found in the body by Orfila's controlled tests, and a witness (Anna Brun) reported seeing white powder stirred into the victim's food; but the evidence is a single forensic chain with real contemporary contamination debate (Raspail's objections were never adjudicated — he arrived four hours too late for the jury), and the accused maintained innocence to her death.
Layer: real · Place: Tulle, Corrèze, France (assize court; crime at Le Glandier)
Dates: 1840-09-03 → 1840-09-19 (confidence: high)

Marie Lafarge was convicted of murdering her husband Charles by arsenic poisoning at the Tulle assizes on 19 September 1840, in a trial dominated by forensic chemistry: the Marsh test for arsenic, applied by Mathieu Orfila as the deciding expert. The trial is widely described as the first murder trial decided by forensic toxicological evidence.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary daily newspaper coverage of the Tulle assizes (summer-autumn 1840); trial record of the Corrèze assize court
Sources: Assize court record, Tulle, 3-19 September 1840 (conviction and sentence; commutation by King Louis-Philippe); Contemporary daily press coverage of the Lafarge affair (cause célèbre, pan-European press interest); Bertomeu-Sánchez, J.R. (2006), 'Sense and sensitivity: Mateu Orfila, the Marsh test and the Lafarge affair', in Bertomeu-Sánchez &amp; Nieto-Galan (eds.), M. Orfila and his times, Science History Publications; Bertomeu-Sánchez, J.R. (2015), La verdad sobre el caso Lafarge, El Serbal; Science History Institute, 'Prefiguring the Arsenic Wars' (Orfila's Tulle tests, the soil/reagent controls, Raspail's critique of Orfila's methods); Adler, L. (1986), L'Amour à l'arsenic: histoire de Marie Lafarge, Denoël; Lafarge, M. (1841), Mémoires de madame Lafarge (self-serving primary source; innocence maintained)
Falsifiability: A documented earlier murder conviction resting principally on chemical evidence would demote the 'first' reading; proof that the tested samples were tampered with would move the poisoning claim to DEBUNKED.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>1.767,45.267,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Penny Black (1 May 1840)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested: Hill's 1837 reform pamphlet and parliamentary record, the 1840 issue and Perkins Bacon print run, and the surviving stamps themselves (about 1.3 million still exist) — the artifact and its paper trail converge.
Layer: real · Place: London, United Kingdom (issue; Perkins Bacon printing)
Dates: 1840-05-01 → 1840-05-06 (confidence: high)

The Penny Black was the world's first adhesive postage stamp used in a public postal system, issued in the United Kingdom on 1 May 1840 (valid for use from 6 May). It bore the profile of the young Queen Victoria, was printed by Perkins Bacon (68,808,000 printed), and was the instrument of Rowland Hill's 1837 postal reform: a flat rate of one penny per half-ounce regardless of distance, prepaid by the sender — replacing the old system under which the recipient paid, charged by the sheet and by distance travelled.

Earliest attestation: Rowland Hill's 1837 pamphlet 'Post Office Reform: Its Importance and Practicability'; parliamentary enquiry record (February 1837); Perkins Bacon print records
Sources: Hill, R. (1837), 'Post Office Reform: Its Importance and Practicability' (the reform pamphlet); Parliamentary enquiry into the Post Office, February 1837 (Hill's testimony on the pre-paid stamp); Perkins Bacon print records, London 1840 (68,808,000 printed); Surviving Penny Black stamps, c. 1.3 million extant (the artifact as its own evidence); Penny Black — Wikipedia (issue dates, print figures, Hill reform summary; secondary aggregator)
Falsifiability: An earlier adhesive stamp verifiably used in a public postal system — a suggestion or a private issue would not suffice.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.12719,51.50643,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The recipient-pays root: before 1840, the addressee paid</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Smithsonian National Postal Museum's own account states the pre-1840 recipient-pays norm and the May 1840 flip; Rowland Hill / Penny Black mechanics are multiply attested; held below VERIFIED on the secondary transmission in this pass.
Layer: real · Place: London, United Kingdom — 1840 postal reform
Dates: 1840 → 1840 (confidence: high)

Before Rowland Hill's 1840 uniform penny postage, recipients — not senders — usually paid for the post. Britain's prepaid-postage revolution (the Penny Black, issued 1 May 1840, valid from 6 May) flipped the default so the sender paid. Business reply mail and the international reply coupon are therefore the formalized return of the older recipient-pays idea: a prepaid-reply instrument lets the party expecting the reply bear the cost, inside a system where sender-pays is now the default.

Earliest attestation: Smithsonian National Postal Museum (via Smithsonian Magazine): 'Before Great Britain introduced prepaid postage in May 1840... Recipients — not senders — paid for postage'
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine (citing the Smithsonian National Postal Museum), 'First Known Piece of Mail Sent Using a Stamp Goes to Auction' — 'Before Great Britain introduced prepaid postage in May 1840, sending mail was cumbersome... Recipients — not senders — paid for postage'; the Penny Black issued 1 May 1840, valid from 6 May; Mark Smith, 'An invention that put its stamp on communication' (Free Online Library) — the Penny Black launched May 1840; 'Before then, postal charges were based on distance and number of sheets in the letter, and the recipient usually paid for the post, not the sender'
Falsifiability: Contemporary rate tables showing senders as the default payers before 1840 in Britain</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>De Moleyns's 1841 patent — the lamp's birth certificate</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The patent grant is multiply attested in the institutional chronologies (Bright 1949 via the UK National Archives; the same chronology the Edison solo-inventor debunk rests on); held below VERIFIED because the primary patent document was accessed here through secondary aggregators, and the surviving record carries the device only to the patent stage — no working surviving de Moleyns lamp is documented.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (British patent register)
Dates: 1841 → 1841 (confidence: medium)

In 1841, Frederick de Moleyns of England was granted the first patent for an incandescent lamp: a design using powdered charcoal heated between two coils of platinum wire in vacuo.

Earliest attestation: The 1841 British patent grant itself; carried in Bright 1949's 'Chronology of the incandescent lamp and thermionic valve' (p39), preserved via the UK National Archives web archive.
Sources: UK National Archives web archive — 'Appendix 2: Chronology of the incandescent lamp and thermionic valve' (citing Bright 1949, p39: '1841 — de Moleyns made lamp by dropping powdered charcoal between two coils of platinum wire in vacuo'); historydraft.com — 'The First patent for an Incandescent Lamp' (1841, England; de Moleyns granted the first incandescent-lamp patent, platinum wires in a vacuum bulb, carbon used); Encyclopedia (Wikipedia-derived) — 'Incandescent lamp': 'In 1841 Frederick de Moleyns of England was granted the first patent for an incandescent lamp, with a design using powdered charcoal heated between two platinum wires'
Falsifiability: A dated pre-1841 incandescent-lamp patent in the registers, or proof the 1841 grant was never issued to de Moleyns</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mackay 1841 — the popularizer of the tulip-mania ruin narrative</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The book's existence, its 1841 date, its claims, its continuous print run, and its dominance of the popular narrative are all independently verifiable — it is the documented source of the ruin version.
Layer: real · Place: London (publication)
Dates: 1841 → 1841 (confidence: high)

Charles Mackay's 1841 book Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds created the popular form of the tulip-mania story — the version every subsequent popular account descends from: all levels of society swept in, fortunes ruined, Dutch commerce shocked.

Earliest attestation: Mackay 1841 itself — the book exists, the tulip chapter exists, and its claims ('the rage among the Dutch to possess them was so great that the ordinary industry of the country was neglected'; merchants 'reduced almost to beggary') are on the page
Sources: Charles Mackay, Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds (1841), tulip-mania chapter: the chimney-sweeps-to-nobility narrative, the ruined fortunes, the 'severe shock' to Dutch commerce; The citation trail: Mackay's account 'unchallenged, and mostly unexamined, until the 1980s' — cited by Galbraith (A Short History of Financial Euphoria), invoked in dot-com and 2008 commentary, quoted by the Dutch central bank president on Bitcoin (2013); Anne Goldgar (2007) on Mackay's sources: most period accounts 'are based on one or two contemporary pieces of propaganda and a prodigious amount of plagiarism' — Mackay treated 17th-c. satirical pamphlets as fact (Smithsonian interview: 'I couldn't find anybody that went bankrupt')
Falsifiability: A pre-1841 popular account carrying the full ruin narrative (nation-wide participation, mass bankruptcies, lasting economic shock) in the same form — the pamphlets carry the moral, Mackay carried the economics</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.13,51.51,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>1841: Poe's 'Rue Morgue' founds detective fiction</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The 1841 magazine publication is a dated, archived primary document — the claim needs no reconstruction. Multiple independent lines (the trilogy itself, the genre historians' consensus from Chesterton to P.D. James, and Doyle's own in-canon acknowledgment of the Dupin lineage) converge on the same first document.
Layer: real · Place: Philadelphia (Graham's Magazine) / Paris (the story's fictional setting)
Dates: 1841 → 1841 (confidence: high)

Edgar Allan Poe's "The Murders in the Rue Morgue" (Graham's Magazine, April 1841) is the first detective story — the founding document of detective fiction, with the locked-room murder, the eccentric reasoning detective (C. Auguste Dupin), and the admiring narrator as the genre's DNA.

Earliest attestation: The magazine publication itself, April 1841 — the document is the claim's own evidence. Lineage attestation: Doyle's own canon cites the descent — in 'A Study in Scarlet' (1887) Holmes dismisses Dupin ('In my opinion, Dupin was a very inferior fellow...'), the genre's founder acknowledged by the genre's most famous inheritor; Doyle repeatedly named Poe as a literary influence.
Sources: Graham's Magazine, April 1841 — the first publication of 'The Murders in the Rue Morgue'; 'The Mystery of Marie Rogêt' (1842) and 'The Purloined Letter' (1844) — the complete Dupin trilogy, the genre's founding corpus; Arthur Conan Doyle, 'A Study in Scarlet' (1887) — Holmes's Dupin comparison, the in-canon lineage acknowledgment
Falsifiability: An earlier published work containing the detective-story form — a crime investigated by a reasoning detective figure delivering a deductive solution — dated and documented before April 1841.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.17,39.95,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cook's first railway excursion, 5 July 1841</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent secondary sources agree on the date, route, fare, party, and purpose; the 'first' is qualified to 'first publicly advertised special railway excursion' because ERIH documents similar trips on the Liverpool &amp; Manchester Railway after 1830.
Layer: real · Place: Leicester, England (Campbell Street station)
Dates: 1841-07-05 → 1841-07-05 (confidence: high)

On 5 July 1841, Thomas Cook chartered a special train from the Midland Counties Railway and carried some 485–500 members of the Leicester Temperance Society from Leicester Campbell Street station to Loughborough and back for one shilling each — the first publicly advertised special railway excursion.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1841 temperance-press reporting of the excursion, via the standard secondary accounts; Cook's own retrospective account of the June 1841 Market Harborough–Leicester walk that sparked the idea
Sources: Wikipedia 'Thomas Cook': the June 1841 walk from Market Harborough to Leicester, the 'thought flashed through my brain' quotation, the 5 July 1841 excursion — 485 members of the Leicester Temperance Society, Leicester Campbell Street to Loughborough, one shilling each, train hired from the Midland Counties Railway; later commercial milestones (1845 Liverpool/Caernarfon/Snowdon with a 60-page guidebook, 1846 Scotland, 165,000 to the Great Exhibition 1851, Cook's Excursionist, the continental coupon system from 1855); Story of Leicester, 'Thomas Cook's Leicester': Leicester as 'the birthplace of popular tourism'; the 5 July 1841 first rail excursion — 485 passengers including Cook's seven-year-old son John Mason Cook, open tub-style carriages, a band, return at 10:30pm; statue outside London Road station; ERIH biography of Thomas Cook: 5 July 1841 excursion for 500 temperance society members at one shilling for the 20 km journey; notes it 'has often been called the first railway excursion, although similar trips were organised on the Liverpool &amp; Manchester Railway soon after its opening in 1830'; 1845 first commercial trip with a 60-page guidebook; Alliance House Foundation, 'Thomas Cook — The Temperance Traveller': the excursion as 'the first publically advertised excursion train in England'; the 175th-anniversary framing; 150,000+ to the Great Exhibition in 1851
Falsifiability: Midland Counties Railway charter records or contemporary July 1841 press showing the excursion never ran, ran on a different date, or was not Cook's organizing</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1333,52.6333,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The showman, minus the sentence — Barnum's documented career</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in Barnum's own published autobiography, contemporary press, and the court record — the Cardiff Giant lawsuit's ruling is a matter of record, not memory. Held below VERIFIED because Barnum was his own principal biographer and shaped the legend (the 'I was born a showman' persona) — the autobiography is a primary source with a motive, so the grade stays at CORROBORATED for the self-authored layers.
Layer: real · Place: New York City — Barnum's American Museum (1842–1865); the Cardiff Giant replica exhibited here 1869–1870
Dates: 1842-01-01 → 1891-04-07 (confidence: high)

Phineas Taylor Barnum (1810–1891) was the American showman of the American Museum in New York (1842–1865 fire), the admitted humbug-promoter (the Feejee Mermaid, Joice Heth, Tom Thumb, Jumbo), and the man who built and exhibited his own replica of the Cardiff Giant in 1869–1870 after David Hannum refused to sell.

Earliest attestation: Barnum's own 1855 autobiography 'The Life of P. T. Barnum' — the showman as his own documentarian, admitting humbugs in print; contemporary newspapers and playbills; the Cardiff Giant court record (Hannum v. Barnum, 1870), in which the judge ruled Barnum's 'original' claim was true because both giants were fakes.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'There's a sucker born every minute' — Barnum (1810–1891) background; the American Museum; the Cardiff Giant affair as the attribution's vehicle; The Independent (Lafayette), 'Too Good To Be True' — Barnum's American Museum, the refused offers to Hannum, the replica, the 1870 ruling
Falsifiability: Evidence that the American Museum, the named attractions, or the Cardiff Giant replica episode did not occur as documented — against Barnum's autobiography, the newspapers, and the court record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1842-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1842</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Crawford Long's ether surgeries, 1842 (published 1849)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Long's own published account with named certificates, the surviving 1849 original in the US National Library of Medicine, and the long institutional afterlife (Doctors' Day, the 1926 Statuary Hall statue, the 1940 stamp, the Crawford W. Long Museum) — held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the single-source structure of the evidence and the 1840s historiographical dispute.
Layer: real · Place: Jefferson, Georgia (Long's office)
Dates: 1842-03-30 → 1849-12-31 (confidence: high)

On 30 March 1842, in Jefferson, Georgia, surgeon Crawford W. Long removed a tumor from the neck of James M. Venable under inhaled sulfuric ether — with earlier 'ether frolic' observation (pain-free bruises while inhaling) as the insight, and further ether operations through 1842 (a second tumor operation 6 June, a toe amputation 3 July). He published the account in the Southern Medical and Surgical Journal in December 1849.

Earliest attestation: Long's 1849 article itself, with certificates from Venable, two witnesses present at the operation, and Venable's family — plus Long's 1854 appeal to Congress via Senator Dawson.
Sources: Long's 1849 account (original, via NIH/Archive.org): the 30 March 1842 operation ('the patient gave no evidence of suffering'), the 6 June second operation, the 3 July toe amputation, the certificates of patient and witnesses, the seven-year-delay explanation ('through negligence... my friends think I would be doing myself injustice').; The Conversation/MedicalXpress (2019): the ether-frolic origin, the six witnesses, the 1990s historiographical diminishing (Osler 1912's 'but it was not until October 16, 1846' framing), Friedman's and Friedland's chapter on Long.; Alamy photo-archive record (scholarly summary): 'recognized as the first physician to have administered ether anesthesia for surgery' (March 30, 1842, James M. Venable); the 1849 publication held at the US National Library of Medicine; the 1854 Dawson Congressional presentation.
Falsifiability: Exposure of the 1849 account as fabricated (e.g. the certificates shown to be forged, or Venable's own denial on the record).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1842-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.5935,34.1179,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1842</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rynd's hollow needle: the first known hypodermic injection (1844)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Rynd's own contemporary-published account gives patient, date, dosage, instrument and outcome ('In the space of a minute all pain ... had ceased'); the secondary literature agrees it is 'the first known hypodermic injection' (Wikipedia, MDDI). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the single-primary-source structure and on Rynd's 1861-delayed elaboration of the instrument.
Layer: real · Place: Meath Hospital, Dublin, Ireland
Dates: 1844-06-03 → 1845-03-12 (confidence: high)

On 3 June 1844, Dublin surgeon Francis Rynd used a thin hollow needle to inject a solution of morphine (fifteen grains of acetate of morphia in one drachm of creosote) under the skin of Margaret Cox, a 59-year-old neuralgia patient at the Meath Hospital, to the supra-orbital, temporal, malar and buccal nerves — the first known hypodermic injection.

Earliest attestation: Rynd, F. (1845). Dublin Medical Press, 12 March 1845 — Rynd's own account of the 3 June 1844 injection (quoted at length in Wikipedia, 'Francis Rynd')
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Francis Rynd' — the 12 March 1845 Dublin Medical Press report quoted at length: Margaret Cox, aetat. 59, the 3 June 1844 injection, 'the first known hypodermic injection'; Medical Independent, 'Through the eye of the needle: A remarkable medical life' — Rynd's training under Sir Philip Crampton at the Meath Hospital, the 1844 invention, 'the greatest boon to medicine since the discovery of chloroform'; MDDI Online, 'The Hypodermic Syringe: Greatest Medical Device of All Time?' — 'The hollow metal needle was invented in 1844 by Irish physician Francis Rynd'
Falsifiability: Proof the Dublin Medical Press report was fabricated, or a documented earlier hypodermic injection</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1844-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-6.2603,53.3498,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1844</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pasch's safety match (1844) — the separated design</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1844 patent and the Bagge &amp; Co. failure are consistently attested; held at CORROBORATED on patent-record basis rather than multi-source primary documentation.
Layer: real · Place: Stockholm, Sweden (J.S. Bagge &amp; Co.'s chemical factory)
Dates: 1844 → 1844 (confidence: high)

Swedish chemist Gustaf Erik Pasch (1788–1862), professor at the Karolinska Institute and student of Berzelius, was granted the first patent on the safety match in 1844. His design moved the phosphorus from the match head to a specially prepared striking surface: the head contained only potassium chlorate (no phosphorus), and ignition required striking on the red-phosphorus surface. Manufacturing at J.S. Bagge &amp; Co.'s chemical factory in Stockholm failed — the striking surface was poor quality and red phosphorus was prohibitively expensive — and production ceased.

Earliest attestation: Swedish patent grant to Gustaf Erik Pasch, 1844
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_Erik_Pasch — 1844 patent; Berzelius red-phosphorus idea; Bagge &amp; Co. failure on cost and surface quality
Falsifiability: A dated safety-match patent or reliably dated commercial separated match predating 1844</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1844-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.0686,59.3293,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1844</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Horace Wells: nitrous oxide 1844, the failed Boston demo, and the 1848 death</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent attestations: the 1847 pamphlet's four witness statements, the Paris letter's account of the Boston trip, Nature's 1948 summary, and the later institutional recognitions — held at CORROBORATED on the retrospective documentary structure (the 1844 events reach us through 1847 publications).
Layer: real · Place: Hartford, Connecticut (Wells's practice); Boston demonstration at MGH
Dates: 1844-12-11 → 1848-01-24 (confidence: high)

On 11 December 1844 (the day after witnessing Samuel Cooley's painless bruised leg at a Gardner Quincy Colton laughing-gas exhibition), Hartford dentist Horace Wells had his own wisdom tooth painlessly extracted under nitrous oxide, used the gas on 12–15 dental patients, and in January 1845 demonstrated it publicly in Boston before John Collins Warren and medical students — the demonstration failed when the gas was removed too soon and the patient groaned, earning cries of 'humbug.' Wells died by suicide under chloroform in New York's Tombs Prison on 24 January 1848; the ADA (1864) and AMA (1870) later declared him the real discoverer of anesthesia.

Earliest attestation: Wells's 1847 pamphlet 'A History of the Discovery of the Application of Nitrous Oxide Gas, Ether, and Other Vapors, to Surgical Operations' with four witness statements (three medical students present at Boston, plus Daniel T. Curtis); his February 1847 Paris letter; Nature's 1948 centenary abstract.
Sources: Nature (1948 centenary): the Colton exhibition of 10 December 1844, Cooley's bruised leg, Wells's own tooth extraction the next day, the January 1845 Boston demonstration ending in 'charlatan' and 'humbug' cries, the 24 January 1848 Tombs Prison suicide under chloroform, the 1864 ADA and 1870 AMA discoverer declarations.; Scribd document summary (archival citations): the gas bag removed too soon, the patient's groan, 'a humbug affair'; Wells's Paris stay and 1847 pamphlet; Morton's unscrupulous handling of the credit dispute; the chloroform addiction and Tombs Prison suicide at 33.; ResearchGate summary of the 1847 sources: three physician signatories (Kennedy, Mignault, Taft) present at the Boston demonstration; Wells's letter in Galignani's Messenger and the Boston Atlas (2 April 1847); his 'experimented with both nitrous oxide and ether prior to 1845.'
Falsifiability: Contemporary proof that the 1844 extractions did not happen (e.g. Colton's or Riggs's denial on the record) — the 1847 witness statements would then be perjury, which no source alleges.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1844-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.6734,41.7658,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1844</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bigaglia's first glass paperweight (1845, credited)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The credit is conventional in the collector literature but rests on trade tradition rather than a dated document or patent, so it stays below VERIFIED; the Venice→France chronology is carried as the cited telling, not absorbed as fact.
Layer: real · Place: Venice, Italy — Pietro Bigaglia's manufactory
Dates: 1845 → 1845 (confidence: medium)

Pietro Bigaglia, a member of an old Venetian family of glass artists, manufactured the first glass paperweight in 1845.

Earliest attestation: Collectors' and trade literature (Christopher Proudlove's account of the French/Venetian paperweight trade).
Sources: Christopher Proudlove (Free Online Library) — Pietro Bigaglia, 'a member of an old Venetian family of glass artists,' is 'generally credited with the manufacture of the first glass paperweight in 1845'; the Venetian millefiori technique traces to the 15th century; Scottish Antiques, 'Paperweights' — the inspiration is generally accepted as the Venetian glassmakers of Murano, working with composite fine glass canes (the millefiori basis) as early as the 16th century; interest mushroomed in the 1840s
Falsifiability: A datable glass paperweight securely earlier than 1845, or documentation that Bigaglia's family had no 1845 paperweight output — neither has surfaced.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.3355,45.4408,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Hill &amp; de la Rue envelope-folding machine, 1845</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two institutional sources (Smithsonian National Postal Museum, Library of Congress) agree on the patentees, the year, and the machine's function; the 1851 exhibition is documented in both. Held below VERIFIED on the 1840/1845 date-fog in secondary sources.
Layer: real · Place: London, England — patent registered; improved machine exhibited at the Crystal Palace, 1851
Dates: 1845 → 1845 (confidence: high)

Edwin Hill (brother of postal reformer Rowland Hill) and Warren de la Rue received the first British patent for an envelope-folding machine in 1845 — a steam-driven machine that creased and folded pre-cut diamond sheets into envelopes — and exhibited an improved version at the London Great Exhibition of 1851.

Earliest attestation: The 1845 British patent registration itself.
Sources: Smithsonian National Postal Museum — 'Envelopes in the Machine Age' (1845 patent; Edwin Hill &amp; Warren de la Rue; 1851 London Exposition; Russell Hawes 1853 US patent); Library of Congress, Inside Adams — 'Opening the Envelope' (1845 steam-powered machine; 1851 improved version at the London Exposition; US patents 6,055 (1849) and 9,812 (1853)); Baylor University, Texas Collection — 'envelope history' (1845 steam machine; Crystal Palace 1851 alongside the Koh-i-Noor and Colt's prototype)
Falsifiability: A documented earlier envelope-machine patent would demote the 'first registered' claim; period accounts denying the 1851 Crystal Palace exhibition would need to surface.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The food that kept leaving (1845–1852)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested on two independent lines: the contemporary export manifests and parliamentary record (the refusal to bar exports 'as had been done on previous occasions'), and the modern quantitative historiography (Ó Gráda 1999:124 — the one-seventh bound; exports reduced vs prior years; imports &gt; exports after 1846). Held below VERIFIED on the single-lineage trade statistics and the honestly approximate arithmetic. This record is the fence that keeps the DEBUNKED blight-only telling honest: the exports were real and damning, and they were not, by the math, the whole story either.
Layer: real · Place: Dublin and the Irish ports — the export manifests; the decision at Westminster
Dates: 1845 → 1852 (confidence: medium)

Throughout the Great Famine, Ireland continued to export food — grain, livestock, dairy, and other produce shipped to Britain — and London refused to bar those exports, as it had done on previous occasions of dearth. The exports are honestly bounded: they were significantly reduced compared to pre-famine years, imports exceeded exports after 1846, and the exported grain would have compensated for only about one-seventh of the potatoes destroyed by the blight (Ó Gráda 1999).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the export manifests and the parliamentary debates over barring exports (the refusal is the documented decision); the quantitative bound is Cormac Ó Gráda's 'Black '47 and Beyond' (1999:124), via the UCL textbook study.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Great Famine (Ireland)' ('Large quantities of food were exported from Ireland during the famine, and the refusal of London to bar such exports, as had been done on previous occasions, was an immediate and continuing source of controversy'); History Education Research Journal (UCL Press) — the Irish/UK textbook study (Ó Gráda 1999:124 — exported grain ≈ one-seventh of potatoes lost; exports reduced; imports exceeded exports after 1846; Mitchel 1854 and the scholarly corrective Nally 2008)
Falsifiability: Port records showing no food exports from Ireland 1845–1852, or an Order in Council barring them, would overturn this; the manifests and the debate record both exist.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-6.26,53.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Franklin expedition, 1845-1848 - the voyage that never came home</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The expedition's own contemporary note documents the beset, the deaths, Franklin's death date, and the abandonment; McClintock's 1859 finds, the Beechey Island graves (1845-46 winter), and the Inuit testimony independently corroborate the sequence.
Layer: real · Place: Off the north-west coast of King William Island, Nunavut (beset position 5 leagues NNW of Point Victory)
Dates: 1845 → 1848 (confidence: high)

In May 1845 Sir John Franklin sailed from England with HMS Erebus and HMS Terror and 129 officers and men to complete the charting of the Northwest Passage. The ships were beset in the ice off King William Island from September 1846; Franklin died on 11 June 1847; the 105 survivors abandoned the ships on 22 April 1848 and attempted a march south toward Back's Fish River (the Great Fish River). None survived.

Earliest attestation: The Victory Point note - the expedition's own record, written 28 May 1847 and 25 April 1848, found in a cairn at Point Victory, King William Island, in May 1859 by Lt. William Hobson (McClintock's Fox expedition).
Sources: The Victory Point note (28 May 1847 / 25 April 1848) - the expedition's own words; the only written record the expedition left behind; McClintock's Fox expedition (1859) - cairn finds, boat, relics, first survey of King William Island's west coast; Admiralty sailing orders, Instructions Addressed to Captain Sir John Franklin (1845); John Rae's 1854 report to the Admiralty - Inuit accounts of white men dying of starvation near the Back River; Franklin relics recovered; David C. Woodman, Unravelling the Franklin Mystery: Inuit Testimony (1991) - synthesis of the Inuit evidence
Falsifiability: Surviving documents from the ships contradicting the beset/abandonment sequence, or Admiralty orders proving a different sailing date, would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.68,69.63,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The French classic period of glass paperweights (1845–1860)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in collector, dealer, and museum literature (Corning, Art Institute of Chicago, Bergstrom-Mahler collections; 1851 exhibition record), but the period boundaries are conventions and most weights are unmarked, so it stays below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Lorraine, France — Baccarat and Saint-Louis glassworks; Paris — Clichy
Dates: 1845 → 1860 (confidence: medium)

From about 1845 to about 1860 — the collectors' 'classic period' — the French glasshouses Baccarat, Saint-Louis, and Clichy (with Monot's Cristallerie de Pantin and lesser lights such as Grenelle and Saint-Mandé) produced the finest glass paperweights in the world: millefiori canes and lampwork flowers under domed crystal, exhibited to acclaim at the 1851 Crystal Palace Great Exhibition, after which the art all but disappeared for some eighty years.

Earliest attestation: The 1851 Great Exhibition at the Crystal Palace (contemporary exhibition record); the factories' own output histories (Baccarat founded 1764; Clichy founded by Louis-Joseph Maës 1842).
Sources: Antique Trader, 'The Zen of Glass Paperweights' — originated in France around 1845 (Baccarat, Saint Louis, Clichy) as affordable objets d'art; 'exhibited to great acclaim in London's Great Exhibition of 1851'; collected by kings, presidents, Colette, Truman Capote; L. H. Selman Fine Glass Paperweights, 'Why Collect' — by 1845 France was 'the undeniable paperweight center of the world'; after about 1860 'the art of paperweight making all but disappeared for eighty years'; Scottish Antiques, 'Paperweights' — French houses made the weights as 'demonstrations of their craftsmanship'; the Clichy manufactory employed some 300 workers to meet demand after the 1851 Crystal Palace success; French houses: Baccarat, Clichy, St Louis, Monot's Cristallerie de Pantin, Grenelle, St Mandé
Falsifiability: Factory records or dated weights showing the 'big three' were not the leading producers of the 1845–1860 boom, or exhibition records showing paperweights absent from the 1851 Crystal Palace displays.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>6.74,48.45,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Irish Famine, 1845–1852</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary lines converge: the decennial census series (1841/1851/1901), contemporary constabulary and relief-commission returns, emigration shipping records, and the parish death registers. The toll's exact integer is honestly rounded; the collapse is not.
Layer: real · Place: Ireland — island-wide; worst in the western counties (Connacht, west Munster)
Dates: 1845 → 1852 (confidence: high)

The Great Irish Famine (An Gorta Mór) ran 1845–1852: potato blight destroyed the staple crop across successive seasons, roughly one million people died of starvation and famine-related disease, and over one million more emigrated; Ireland's population fell from ~8.5 million on the eve of the famine to ~4.4 million by 1901, and has never recovered to its pre-famine level. 1847 — 'Black '47' — was the worst year.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the blighted 1845 harvest (press reports, constabulary returns); the 1841 census (8.175M) as the pre-famine baseline. The demographic collapse is documented in the 1851 and 1901 censuses.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Great Famine (Ireland)' (proximate cause blight; ~1M died, ~1M+ emigrated; 8.5M → 4.4M by 1901; 'Black '47'; exports controversy; quarter-acre clause); Smithsonian Magazine — 'Scientists Finally Pinpoint the Pathogen That Caused the Irish Potato Famine' (1845 splotches; 1845 harvest halved; island population still below pre-famine levels today)
Falsifiability: Census records showing no population collapse 1841–1851, or shipping/relief records contradicting the emigration scale, would overturn this; the census series is the anchor and it is unambiguous.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-9.05,53.27,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Perry &amp; Daft rubber-band patent (17 March 1845)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent document exists, is multiply and consistently cited (patent-office records, trade histories, encyclopedia references), and names both grantees with the sealed date.
Layer: real · Place: London, England — Messrs Perry and Co., Rubber Manufacturers of London
Dates: 1845-03-17 → 1845-03-17 (confidence: high)

British Patent 13880/1845 — sealed 17 March 1845 — granted jointly to Stephen Perry of St John's Wood, Middlesex, and Thomas Barnabas Daft of Birmingham, for 'improvements in springs to be applied to girths, belts, and bandages, and improvements in manufacture of elastic bands.' The elastic bands were cut as narrow rings from vulcanized rubber tube and sold by Perry's firm, Messrs Perry and Co., Rubber Manufacturers of London, advertised as useful for holding 'papers, letters, etc.'

Earliest attestation: The patent specification itself: British Patent 13880/1845, granted to Stephen Perry and Thomas Barnabas Daft, sealed 17 March 1845.
Sources: British Patent 13880/1845 — specification granted to Stephen Perry (St John's Wood, Middlesex, gentleman) and Thomas Barnabas Daft (Birmingham, manufacturer), 'for improvements in springs to be applied to girths, belts, and bandages, and improvements in manufacture of elastic bands'; sealed 17 March 1845 (cited via the rubber-band article's patent reference, todayinsci archive); WCSA 'On This Day — March 17, 2021: Stephen Perry received a patent for the first rubber band, in 1845' — Messrs Perry and Co., bands extruded into tube, cured, sliced widthwise; advertised for 'papers, letters, etc.'; 'Perry opened the UK's first rubber band factory'; WayneDaleNews, 'Stretched Tales of Elastic: The History of Ordinary Things' (2026) — 'In 1845, the modern rubber band was invented by Stephen Perry and Thomas Daft of London. They sliced narrow rings from a vulcanized rubber tube.'
Falsifiability: A showing that the 13880/1845 specification names Perry alone, or that the sealed patent is a misfiled/modern fabrication — neither is the case; the joint grant is on the document.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>HERB-1: the blight strain identified (1845)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Peer-reviewed primary science: Yoshida et al., eLife 2013 (DOI 10.7554/eLife.00731) — 11 historical + 15 modern genomes, HERB-1 a single low-diversity lineage, distinct from US-1, close relative of it, dispersed from a common ancestor outside Mexico in the early 1800s. Corroborated by contemporary 1845–46 field reports and the harvest-collapse figures. The natural half of the famine's causation is verified — which is precisely what makes the blight-only telling a half-truth rather than a truth.
Layer: real · Place: County Cork, Ireland — first Irish reports, summer 1845; the strain's origin: Toluca Valley, Mexico
Dates: 1845 → 1852 (confidence: high)

The potato crop failures of 1845–1852 were caused by potato late blight, the oomycete Phytophthora infestans — and the exact historical strain is now identified: HERB-1, a unique 19th-century genotype distinct from all modern strains (including the long-suspected US-1), which spread from North America to Europe in 1845 from a Mexican Toluca Valley origin. The 1845 harvest was cut roughly in half; by 1846 it had fallen to about a quarter.

Earliest attestation: 1845 field reports of splotched leaves and mushy tubers (e.g., the County Cork accounts); the scientific identification is Yoshida et al. 2013 — shotgun sequencing of 11 herbarium specimens (Kew, Munich, collected from 1845 on) against 15 modern strains.
Sources: Yoshida et al. 2013, eLife 2:e00731 — 'The rise and fall of the Phytophthora infestans lineage that triggered the Irish potato famine' (11 herbarium + 15 modern strains; HERB-1 unique, distinct from US-1; spread North America → Europe 1845); Smithsonian Magazine — 'Scientists Finally Pinpoint the Pathogen That Caused the Irish Potato Famine' (1845 splotches; 1845 harvest halved, 1846 quartered; Kew 1847 specimens); NPR/WEKU — 'How Genomics Solved The Mystery Of Ireland's Great Famine' (22 May 2013; the US-1 theory overturned; Burbano/Kamoun)
Falsifiability: A competing pathogen identified in 1840s herbarium potato tissue, or a demonstration that the herbarium genomes are contaminated, would overturn this; the 11-specimen consensus across two herbaria makes that structurally unlikely.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-8.47,51.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Lundström safety match (1845–1858) — Sweden's commercial match</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1845–47 development, the 1855 Paris award, and the 1858 output figure are consistently attested across sources; the Lundström-to-Swedish-Match lineage is documented. Patent-date details vary, so held at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Jönköping, Sweden (Lundström match factory; original factory now a museum)
Dates: 1845 → 1858 (confidence: medium)

The Lundström brothers of Jönköping, Sweden — Johan Edvard (1815–1888) and Carl Frans (1823–1917) — took Pasch's separated safety-match design and made it commercially successful: experimenting from 1845, a production plant on Lake Vättern by 1847, a prize-winning exhibit at the 1855 Paris World Exhibition, ~12 million matchboxes in 1858, and a path to the Jönköping match monopoly (Jönköpings &amp; Vulcans Tändsticksfabriks AB, 1903; later Swedish Match). Johan Edvard Lundström left the business in 1863 and spent 1875–77 as a government inspector in the match industry, working to prevent and forbid the use of dangerous white phosphorus.

Earliest attestation: Lundström workshop records, Jönköping, 1845 (secondary); Paris World Exhibition award 1855
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_Erik_Pasch — Lundström brothers took Pasch's design; commercial success ~1855–60; 1855 Paris award; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johan_Edvard_Lundström — 1845 workshop start; 1847 Lake Vättern plant; 1855 Paris award; Lagerman's automatic match machine; inspector work 1875–77 against white phosphorus; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safety_matches — 1858 output ~12 million matchboxes; Jönköpings &amp; Vulcans Tändsticksfabriks AB 1903; Coigent patent contest in France; Discover Magazine — 'They Invented It' (Fenella Saunders): 'In 1855 Johan Edvard Lundström of Sweden patented safety matches using less volatile red phosphorus'
Falsifiability: Evidence that commercial safety-match production at scale predates the Lundström brothers, or that the 1855 Paris award went to another maker</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.1618,57.7826,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Adolphe Sax patents the saxophone (28 June 1846) — and the twenty-year patent war</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree: the 28 June 1846 French patent; the design (conical brass + single reed, bridging brass and woodwind); Berlioz's 1842 approval; the French military's mid-1840s embrace; the 20+ years of patent litigation (the original opposition suit unresolved until 1854 — eight years — with Sax's patents ultimately declared valid and the opposition ordered to pay damages); the three bankruptcies (1852/56, 1873, 1877 — sources differ on the first date, fenced); the 1853–1858 lip cancer survived; the 1857 Paris Conservatoire post; the 1894 death in poverty in Paris, buried at Montmartre. The popular 'pure martyr' telling is fenced: Sax was also the plaintiff — he sued rivals for infringement — and his patents were upheld; the litigation costs, not theft, destroyed him.
Layer: real · Place: Paris (Sax's workshop; born Dinant, Belgium, 1814)
Dates: 1846-06-28 → 1846-06-28 (confidence: high)

Adolphe Sax invented the saxophone — a conical brass body with a single-reed mouthpiece, bridging brass power and woodwind agility — patented 28 June 1846 in France for use in orchestras and military bands; he then spent over twenty years in patent litigation that bankrupted him three times, and died in poverty in Paris in 1894 without ever hearing the jazz the instrument would define.

Earliest attestation: The French patent of 28 June 1846; Berlioz's 1842 writings
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Adolphe Sax': 'On 28 June 1846 he patented the saxophone, intended for use in orchestras and military bands... Composer Hector Berlioz wrote approvingly of the new instrument in 1842... Legal troubles involving patents continued for over 20 years, with rival instrument makers attacking the legitimacy of his patents and Sax suing them for patent infringement. He was driven into bankruptcy three times: in 1852, 1873 and 1877... Sax suffered from lip cancer between 1853 and 1858 but made a full recovery. In 1894 he died of pneumonia in Paris, in poverty.'; Scribd, 'Saxophone's Absence in Orchestras': 'The original lawsuit in opposition to his patent application was not resolved until 1854, eight years after it was filed. Although Sax's patents were declared valid and the opposition was ordered to pay damages, the time and money that could have been spent creatively had been expended on endless legal battles.'; AV Club: 'rival instrument makers, several of whom either sued Sax for patent infringement or tried to delegitimize the patents he held. Legal battles over his designs dragged on for 20 years, and he was driven into bankruptcy three times. He died in poverty, never dreaming that when humanity eventually achieved musical perfection, it would come in the form of the saxophone riff from "Baker Street."'; Natalija Ugrina (Dinant): 'In 1841, a young Sax packed his creations and moved to Paris... His solution was the saxophone: a conical brass body like a trumpet, fitted with a single-reed mouthpiece like a clarinet. He patented it in 1846.' Rival makers 'accused him of stealing ideas, sabotaged his business, and filed endless lawsuits.' Lip cancer 1853, recovered; died 1894, Montmartre Cemetery.
Falsifiability: A dated patent or instrument proving a saxophone predating June 1846, or court records contradicting the litigation/bankruptcy account</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ether Day — the 16 October 1846 Ether Dome demonstration</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The event is multiply attested by contemporary witnesses (surgeon, audience of physicians, press) and the preserved site; the 'first ever' superlative is graded separately (see evt-anesthesia-first-ever-001).
Layer: real · Place: Massachusetts General Hospital (Bulfinch Building), Boston
Dates: 1846-10-16 → 1846-10-16 (confidence: high)

On 16 October 1846, in the Bulfinch Building's surgical amphitheater at Massachusetts General Hospital (Boston), dentist William T. G. Morton administered sulfuric ether to patient Edward Gilbert Abbott, after which surgeon John Collins Warren painlessly removed part of a tumor from Abbott's neck before a medical audience — the event known as Ether Day, and the first successful public demonstration of surgical ether anesthesia.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary newspaper accounts and Warren's same-evening journal entry; the amphitheater is preserved as the Ether Dome (National Historic Landmark).
Sources: Linda Hall Library: 'first successful public demonstration of surgical anesthesia' — Warren's journal excerpt (Mass. Historical Society); Abbott's 'neck's been scratched' report; the Wells-failure contrast in the same theater.; Russell Museum (MGH): the amphitheater's history — 8,000+ operations 1821–1867; Warren's 'no humbug' proclamation; the dome as meeting space today.; Edward Gilbert Abbott — Warren's same-evening journal, Abbott's post-operation report; the 'no Humbug' reference and its disputed origin (Warren later said he did not remember Wells's 1845 demo until Wells raised it in 1847).
Falsifiability: A contemporary denial by a present surgeon, or evidence the operation was staged (e.g. Abbott's pain actually severe and documented at the time).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0686,42.3632,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Howe's 1846 lockstitch patent (U.S. 4,750)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent is a primary legal document with a fixed number and grant date, institutionally preserved.
Layer: real · Place: Boston / Spencer, Massachusetts, USA (Howe's shop and patent filing)
Dates: 1846-09-10 → 1846-09-10 (confidence: high)

On 10 September 1846, Elias Howe Jr. of Massachusetts was granted U.S. Patent No. 4,750 for an improvement in sewing machines using a lockstitch design — eye-pointed needle, shuttle beneath the cloth forming the lockstitch, automatic feed — sewing about 250 stitches per minute.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent 4,750, granted 10 September 1846 (primary document); Smithsonian National Museum of American History photograph of the patent record
Sources: U.S. Patent 4,750 — Improvement in Sewing-Machines, Elias Howe Jr., granted 1846 (primary document); Alamy/Smithsonian NMAH — Howe's patent record photograph with institutional caption (gold medal Paris 1867, Légion d'honneur, Hall of Fame 2004); patentplaques-blog.com — Sewing Machine Wars (patent 4,750 issued 10 Sept 1846; lockstitch mechanism description)
Falsifiability: U.S. Patent Office records showing a different number or date for Howe's lockstitch filing.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.06,42.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lipman's eyelet paper-binding method</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by a named institutional source (the Smithsonian's Lipman account); held below VERIFIED — the device and its date were not primary-inspected this pass, and the 'two decades' measure is the source's phrasing.
Layer: real · Place: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania — Lipman's stationery business
Dates: 1846 → 1866 (confidence: medium)

Philadelphia stationer Hymen Lipman — of the 1858 pencil-eraser patent and the first U.S. envelope company — devised an eyelet paper-binding method that, per the Smithsonian's account of his career, 'preceded the stapler by two decades.'

Earliest attestation: The Smithsonian's Lipman biographical account (cited in evt-lipman-first-us-envelope-company-001) — Lipman 'devised an eyelet paper-binding method that preceded the stapler by two decades.'
Sources: Smithsonian Lipman account (via evt-lipman-first-us-envelope-company-001's sources) — Lipman 'put adhesive on the back flap, devised an eyelet paper-binding method that preceded the stapler by two decades'
Falsifiability: A primary document (patent, catalog, advertisement) dating Lipman's eyelet binder would move this to VERIFIED; evidence the Smithsonian account conflated him with another binder-maker would move it down.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1652,39.9526,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Gregory clause (June 1847)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The clause's existence and wording are statute — primary source, VERIFIED. The eviction-driving effect is carried at CORROBORATED inside this record: documented in the constabulary and relief returns, but its exact share of the clearance wave (against landlord grazing conversions) is honestly apportioned as argument. Named for William Gregory, the Galway MP who moved it.
Layer: real · Place: Ireland — the Poor Law unions; worst in Connacht
Dates: 1847-06 → 1847-06 (confidence: high)

The Poor Law Extension Act of June 1847 carried the quarter-acre (Gregory) clause: no occupier of more than a quarter acre of land could receive poor relief. Cottiers had to surrender their holdings — their potato ground, their only subsistence — to qualify for the workhouse, and the clause drove a wave of evictions and 'voluntary' surrenders across the west.

Earliest attestation: The statute itself (10 &amp; 11 Vict. c. 90) — primary; the eviction effect in the constabulary returns and the relief commissioners' reports of 1847–1849.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Great Famine (Ireland)' ('a provision that forbade access to workhouse aid for those possessing more than one-quarter acre of land'; the eviction wave it exacerbated); irisharoundtheworld.com — 'Irish Potato Famine: Ireland's Great Hunger, 1845 to 1852' (the Gregory clause; the quarter-acre rule and the evictions)
Falsifiability: The statute's text is the falsifiability line: any claim that no such clause existed dies on 10 &amp; 11 Vict. c. 90. The eviction-driving effect would be overturned by evidence that evictions fell after June 1847; the returns show the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-9.05,53.27,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Semmelweis's chlorine hand disinfection, Vienna 1847</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The mortality drop is multiply attested — the clinic's own ward books, two contemporary independent reports (Hebra in Vienna, Routh in London), and a modern statistical analysis of the long-run mortality series.
Layer: real · Place: Allgemeines Krankenhaus (Vienna General Hospital), Vienna
Dates: 1847 → 1847 (confidence: high)

Mandatory hand disinfection with chlorinated lime by physicians and students at Vienna General Hospital's First Obstetrical Clinic, ordered by Ignaz Semmelweis in 1847, cut maternal mortality from puerperal (childbed) fever from roughly 18% to roughly 1-2%, a level matching the midwives' Second Clinic.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: Ferdinand von Hebra's accounts in the Journal of the Medical Society of Vienna (December 1847, April 1848); Charles Routh's report to the Royal Medical and Chirurgical Society, London, 21 November 1848.
Sources: Ward books of Vienna General Hospital First Obstetrical Clinic (mortality ~18% pre-intervention, ~1-2% after; thirty deaths/month reduced to seven/month per Routh 1848); Hebra, F. von — reports on Semmelweis's work, Journal of the Medical Society of Vienna, Dec 1847 &amp; Apr 1848; Routh, C. — report to the Royal Medical and Chirurgical Society, 21 Nov 1848; Historical statistical review (Epidemiology &amp; Infection, Cambridge): mean annual mortality analysis 1784-1858 supporting Semmelweis's hypothesis
Falsifiability: Ward-book records showing no mortality change after the intervention began, or documentary proof the chlorine washing was never actually implemented</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3531,48.2177,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Simpson's chloroform, 1847 — and Victoria's 1853 confinement</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Simpson's 1847 presentation and practice, the Queen Street dinner-party account (multiple attendee retellings), Victoria's journal in her own words, and Snow's published anesthesia work — multiply attested; held at CORROBORATED on the retrospective biographical structure rather than a single contemporary document.
Layer: real · Place: Edinburgh (Simpson); London/Buckingham (Snow, Victoria)
Dates: 1847-11-08 → 1853-04-07 (confidence: high)

On 8 November 1847, Edinburgh's Professor of Midwifery James Young Simpson introduced chloroform as an obstetric (and general) anesthetic, after the famous dinner-party self-experiment with assistants Keith and Duncan (waking under the mahogany table). On 7 April 1853, John Snow administered chloroform to Queen Victoria for the birth of Prince Leopold — 'that blessed chloroform... soothing, quieting and delightful beyond measure' — which made obstetric anesthesia respectable across Britain.

Earliest attestation: Simpson's 1847 society presentation; Victoria's journal entry ('Dr. Snow gave that blessed chloroform'); Snow's own anesthesia casebooks.
Sources: H. Laing Gordon (via Google Books): Simpson appointed Chair of Midwifery 1840; ether introduced to UK obstetrics 19 January 1847; the dinner-table trials; chloroform introduced 8 November 1847; the 1853 Victoria confinement making the practice 'generally accepted'; Simpson knighted 1866 — the first physician knighted for medicine.; Historic Geneva: Fanny Longfellow (1847), Emma Darwin (1847, 1848 — 'Get me the chloroform'), Caroline Post Swan (ether, 1851); Victoria's journal quote; the Genesis 3:16 Calvinist objection carried as vocal minority; Simpson's own prophecy that patients would force the profession's hand.; Edinburgh Res Medica (1992): the Queen Street dinner party — guests 'all under the mahogany in a trice'; the 10 November 1847 presentation; Simpson's 'sorrow as toil, not pain' rebuttal of Genesis 3:16; Victoria's 1853 chloroform confinement.
Falsifiability: Simpson's 1847 presentation record or Snow's casebooks shown to be misdated or fabricated; Victoria's journal entry disproven.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.1883,55.9533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Victory Point note - the only words the expedition left behind</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The physical document survives, its text is fully published, the signatures and Admiralty form are undisputed, and its contents are independently consistent with the Beechey Island graves, McClintock's cairn finds, and the Inuit testimony.
Layer: real · Place: Point Victory, north-west coast of King William Island, Nunavut
Dates: 1847 → 1848 (confidence: high)

The Victory Point note is the single written record the Franklin expedition left behind: a pre-printed Admiralty form written in two parts. The first, dated 28 May 1847 and signed by Lt. Graham Gore and Mate Charles Des Voeux, reports the ships wintered in the ice and 'All well.' The second, added in the margins on 25 April 1848 and signed by Capt. James Fitzjames and Capt. Francis Crozier, records that the ships were deserted on 22 April 1848 after being beset since 12 September 1846, that Sir John Franklin died on 11 June 1847, that deaths had reached nine officers and fifteen men, and that the 105 survivors under Crozier would start the next day for Back's Fish River. It was found in May 1859 by Lt. William Hobson of McClintock's Fox expedition in a cairn at Point Victory on the north-west coast of King William Island - originally deposited in James Clark Ross's 1830 Victory Point cairn four miles north.

Earliest attestation: The note itself (1847-48) - the earliest surviving document; the finder was Lt. William Hobson, McClintock's Fox expedition, May 1859.
Sources: The Victory Point note itself - two-part Admiralty form, 28 May 1847 (Gore/Des Voeux) + 25 April 1848 (Fitzjames/Crozier), margins; McClintock's Fox expedition (1857-59) - Hobson's discovery at the Victory Point cairn, May 1859; Royal Museums Greenwich collection MSR/C/9 - the surviving document; James Clark Ross's 1830 cairn - the original deposition site, four miles north (Victory Point)
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous Franklin-expedition document contradicting its dates, or forensic proof the note is a later forgery, would overturn this record; none exists - the handwriting and the Admiralty form are undisputed.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.68,69.63,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The word 'zoo', c. 1847</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Etymonline's dated etymology with its Century Dictionary citation, consistent with the London Zoo timeline (1828 founding, 1847 public opening). Held below VERIFIED on the single lexicographer's base — OED confirmation would raise the grade.
Layer: real · Place: London Zoo, Regent's Park, London (coordinates via map.geocode, FULL_ADDRESS match)
Dates: 1847 → 1847 (confidence: medium)

The word 'zoo' dates to about 1847, as an abbreviation of the London Zoological Society's 'Zoological Gardens'.

Earliest attestation: Etymonline (Douglas Harper), citing the Century Dictionary: c. 1847.
Sources: Etymonline (Douglas Harper) — 'zoo': c. 1847, short for Zoological Gardens of the London Zoological Society
Falsifiability: An antedating of the word 'zoo' in print before 1847, or a documented earlier abbreviation of a different zoological garden.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1847-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.15573,51.53573,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1847</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Seneca Falls Convention, July 1848</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent contemporary lines: the printed 1848 proceedings, Douglass's own newspaper reporting, and the physical chapel held by NPS; attendee counts and the narrow-margin story ride secondary accounts, so the record stops at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Seneca Falls, New York (Wesleyan Chapel)
Dates: 1848 → 1848 (confidence: high)

On 19–20 July 1848, about 300 people gathered at the Wesleyan Chapel in Seneca Falls, New York, at the convention organized by Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott — the first formal women's rights convention in the United States — and adopted the Declaration of Sentiments, modeled on the Declaration of Independence, including the contested ninth resolution demanding women's elective franchise; Frederick Douglass's North Star backed the resolution and Douglass's speech is credited with carrying the vote.

Earliest attestation: Proceedings of the Woman's Rights Convention, printed 1848; the Declaration of Sentiments as adopted
Sources: Proceedings of the Woman's Rights Convention, held at Seneca Falls, N.Y., July 19th and 20th, 1848 (printed 1848); The North Star, 28 July 1848 (Frederick Douglass reporting the convention); National Park Service, Women's Rights National Historical Park (chapel, proceedings, attendee records)
Falsifiability: Absence of the 1848 printed proceedings in any archive, or proof the Wesleyan Chapel did not exist in 1848</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1848-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.8,42.91,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1848</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The $400 sale — Hunt sells the safety pin forever</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The $400 figure is multiply attested across independent secondary sources (Lemelson-MIT, Smithsonian Magazine 2013, retailbrew citing Kevin Baker's America the Ingenious); the buyer-identity variance is carried honestly as a hedge rather than a kill.
Layer: real · Place: New York City (sale of the patent rights)
Dates: 1849 → 1849 (confidence: high)

Walter Hunt sold all rights to the safety-pin patent (U.S. 6,281) in 1849 for $400 — reportedly to settle a $15 debt — and never earned another penny from it, while later rights-holders made millions.

Earliest attestation: Multiple secondary histories converge on the $400 figure (Lemelson-MIT, Smithsonian Magazine, retailbrew, onthisday.com)
Sources: Lemelson-MIT Program — "Walter Hunt": patented April 10, 1849 (U.S. Patent No. 6,281); "willingly sold rights to it for just $400"; died June 8, 1859; Smithsonian Magazine (2013) — "The Inventive Mind of Walter Hunt, Yankee Mechanical Genius": buyer J.R. Chapin, $400; retailbrew — "Tools of the Trade" (2023, citing Kevin Baker, America the Ingenious): sold the patent the same year to manufacturer Jonathan Richardson for $400; todayifoundout.com (2017) — the $15 debt, the three hours, rights ultimately to W.R. Grace and Company
Falsifiability: A documented larger sale price or a royalty agreement in Hunt's papers</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hunt's Dress-Pin patent (1849) — the modern safety pin</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent is a public document with a fixed grant date; the coiled-spring and clasp construction is described in Hunt's own application text.
Layer: real · Place: New York City (Walter Hunt's workshop; patent filed at the U.S. Patent Office)
Dates: 1849 → 1849 (confidence: high)

Walter Hunt's 1849 "Dress-Pin" (U.S. Patent No. 6,281, granted April 10, 1849) is the documented modern safety pin: a single length of brass wire, coiled at the center to act as a spring, with one end sharpened to a point and the other formed into a clasp that shields the point.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent No. 6,281 (April 10, 1849)
Sources: U.S. Patent No. 6,281 (Walter Hunt, "Dress-Pin," granted April 10, 1849); Lemelson-MIT Program — "Walter Hunt" (patent date and sale attested); Smithsonian Magazine — "The Inventive Mind of Walter Hunt, Yankee Mechanical Genius" (2013, buyer named as J.R. Chapin)
Falsifiability: A dated prior U.S. patent for a coiled-spring shielded-point pin predating April 10, 1849</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Meucci's teletrofono — the 1871 caveat, the lapsed renewal, the 2002 recognition</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The caveat filing (1871), the lapsed renewal (1874), and the 2002 House resolution are all documented; the 1860 demonstration is attested in the resolution's preamble and museum record. Held below VERIFIED on the lost prototypes (the Western Union models vanished — the physical evidence is gone) and on the exact 1860 demonstration details.
Layer: real · Place: Clifton, Staten Island, New York
Dates: 1849-01-01 → 2002-06-11 (confidence: medium)

Antonio Meucci developed a 'teletrofono' (speaking telegraph): conceived in Havana c. 1849, demonstrated in New York in 1860, U.S. patent caveat filed December 28, 1871, lapsed after 1874 when he could not pay the $10 renewal; on June 11, 2002 the U.S. House of Representatives passed H. Res. 269 acknowledging his work in the invention of the telephone.

Earliest attestation: Meucci's 1871 patent caveat (filed Dec 28, 1871); the 1860 New York demonstration attested in the H. Res. 269 preamble and the Garibaldi-Meucci Museum record.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — 'Antonio Meucci, an Italian immigrant, began developing the design of a talking telegraph or telephone in 1849. In 1871, he filed a caveat... Due to financial hardships, Meucci could not renew his caveat. His role in the invention of the telephone was overlooked until the United States House of Representatives passed a Resolution on June 11, 2002, honoring Meucci's contributions and work.'; U.S. House of Representatives, H. Res. 269 (107th Congress), passed June 11, 2002 — preamble: 'if Meucci had been able to pay the $10 fee to maintain the caveat after 1874, no patent could have been issued to Bell'; resolved: 'the life and achievements of Antonio Meucci should be recognized, and his work in the invention of the telephone should be acknowledged.' Non-binding.; Garibaldi-Meucci Museum — Meucci's Staten Island record; the Western Union laboratory prototypes 'disappeared and were never recovered.'; Telefónica blog, 'Who was Antonio Meucci?' — the 1871 caveat, the unrenewed 1874 renewal, the 1887 government fraud suit against Bell (remanded, mooted by Meucci's 1889 death and the 1893 patent expiry).
Falsifiability: The 1871 caveat is a filed public document; a showing that no such caveat was filed, or that the 1860 demonstration is wholly unattested in period sources, would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.1496,40.5834,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Rowley's independent safety-pin patent (October 1849)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in multiple secondary histories of the safety pin; held below VERIFIED on the independence question.
Layer: real · Place: Birmingham, England
Dates: 1849 → 1849 (confidence: medium)

Charles Rowley of Birmingham, England independently patented a similar safety pin in October 1849 — the same year as Hunt — which fences the single-inventor version of the safety-pin story.

Earliest attestation: British patent to Charles Rowley, October 1849 (as reported in the safety-pin history)
Sources: Wikipedia-derived safety-pin history (via adam.curry.com archive): "Charles Rowley (Birmingham, England) independently patented a similar safety pin in October 1849"
Falsifiability: Evidence that Rowley's filing copied Hunt's via the patent literature within six months</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.8904,52.4862,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Semmelweis's professional rejection and death in the asylum</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The non-renewal, the late and polemical 1861 book, the asylum commitment, and the death fourteen days later are each attested in contemporary records and modern biographies; the causes are plural and contested, which is why the record stays CORROBORATED, not a martyrdom story.
Layer: real · Place: Vienna (Allgemeines Krankenhaus, then Oberdöbling asylum)
Dates: 1849 → 1865 (confidence: high)

Semmelweis's discovery was not adopted by the medical establishment in his lifetime: his 1849 reappointment as assistant at Vienna's First Obstetrical Clinic was not granted, and he died in the Oberdöbling (Döbling) asylum in Vienna in 1865, fourteen days after commitment.

Earliest attestation: Vienna faculty records (1849); Semmelweis's 1861 book and its open letters; asylum records 1865.
Sources: Semmelweis, I. — Die Ätiologie, der Begriff und die Prophylaxis des Kindbettfiebers (1861), incl. the open letters; Vienna medical faculty records, 1849 (contract expiry and non-renewal); Carter, K. C. &amp; Carter, B. R. — Childbed Fever: A Scientific Biography of Ignaz Semmelweis (2005)
Falsifiability: Records showing his 1849 reappointment was granted, or that he died outside an asylum</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.35,48.22,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sewing Machine War and the 1856 Combination (first U.S. patent pool)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1854 verdict, the $25 royalty, and the 1856 nine-patent pool are multiply attested in legal and institutional sources; held below VERIFIED on the exact litigation date range and the $2M estimate.
Layer: real · Place: Albany, New York, USA (the 1856 Agreement)
Dates: 1849 → 1867 (confidence: high)

Elias Howe's lockstitch patent (4,750, 1846) was so broad that nearly every later machine infringed it; Howe sued Isaac Singer and others (litigation ~1849-1854), won in 1854, collected up to $25 per machine in royalties; then on 24 October 1856 the Albany Agreement formed the Sewing Machine Combination (Howe, Wheeler &amp; Wilson, I.M. Singer &amp; Co., Grover &amp; Baker) — the first patent pool in U.S. history, with nine patents pooled (lockstitch, four-motion feed, vertical needle/horizontal surface), Howe taking $5 per domestic machine and $1 per export until his patent expired in 1867 (roughly $2 million, 1860s dollars).

Earliest attestation: The patent pool's terms are carried in legal-historical sources (Orlando B. Potter's 1856 Albany Agreement); the $25-to-$5 royalty arc is documented by Smithsonian Magazine and Wikipedia's Sewing Machine Combination article
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine — 'How Singer Won the Sewing Machine War' (1854 verdict, $25→$5 royalties, nine patents, three crucial ones, Edward Clark's hire-purchase plan); Wikipedia — Sewing Machine Combination (Albany Agreement 24 Oct 1856; first U.S. patent pool; Howe $5/$1 terms); patentplaques-blog.com — Sewing Machine Wars (1846 patent; 1849-1854 litigation)
Falsifiability: Court records showing Howe lost the 1854 suit, or no 1856 agreement instrument in the patent-pool history.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.76,42.65,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Joseph Swan's carbon-filament lamp — the priority the legend skipped (evidence chain corrected)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by contemporary lecture reports (18 Dec 1878, 17 Jan 1879, 3 Feb 1879 — the last to 700+ with Armstrong presiding), the patent register (No. 4933, 27 Nov 1880), and institutional histories (Swan Electric Light Company 1881; Lit &amp; Phil Library first public room lit by electricity 20 Oct 1880; Savoy Theatre Dec 1881 first public building lit entirely by electricity). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the primary lecture reports were accessed here through secondary aggregators; the chain is strong but one layer removed. Supersedes evt-swan-incandescent-lamp-001, which carried the imprecise '1878 patent' shorthand.
Layer: real · Place: Newcastle upon Tyne / Gateshead, England — the February 1879 lecture demonstration; Swan's Gateshead home, the first home lit by electric light
Dates: 1850 → 1883 (confidence: high)

Joseph Swan built and publicly demonstrated a working carbon-filament incandescent lamp before Edison — public demonstrations at Newcastle on 18 December 1878 (Chemical Society lecture), 17 January 1879 (repeat lecture, lamp shown in operation), and 3 February 1879 (Literary and Philosophical Society theatre, audience 700+, Sir William Armstrong presiding); British Patent No. 4933 granted 27 November 1880; installations followed at his Gateshead home, the Lit &amp; Phil Library (20 October 1880), and the Savoy Theatre, London (December 1881).

Earliest attestation: Newcastle Chemical Society lecture, 18 December 1878; repeat lecture 17 January 1879; Literary and Philosophical Society of Newcastle upon Tyne public demonstration, 3 February 1879 (contemporary lecture reports and institutional histories).
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Swan; W.P.E.H.S. historic supplement No. 65 — Joseph Swan biography (wpehs.org.uk/historicsupplements/Sup065JosephSwanBiog.pdf); gatesheadhistory.com/joseph-swan.html; University of Reading working paper (centaur.reading.ac.uk/79610/1/Fundamental patents.pdf) citing Arapostathis &amp; Gooday; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Swan
Falsifiability: Archival evidence that the December 1878–February 1879 demonstrations did not occur or that Patent No. 4933 was never granted — against the lecture reports and the patent register.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1850-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.6178,54.9783,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1850</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hobbs picked the Chubb detector lock in 25 minutes, 1851 (CORROBORATED)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent secondary attestation across the Exhibition reporting and the lock-trade literature (Traditional Building, Gizmodo/British Library, the detector-lock histories); held below VERIFIED because the exact '25 minutes' timing rests on witness accounts rather than a timed official record.
Layer: real · Place: Crystal Palace, Hyde Park, London (Great Exhibition, 1851)
Dates: 1851 → 1851 (confidence: high)

At the Great Exhibition of 1851, the American locksmith Alfred Charles Hobbs picked the Chubb six-lever detector lock — the lock designed to seize up when picked incorrectly — in about 25 minutes.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1851 press and Exhibition reporting; Chubb's subsequent publications and company history.
Sources: Traditional Building — 'Locks: Centuries of Security': Hobbs challenged the Chubb lock, picking it in 25 minutes, then the Bramah lock; Gizmodo (British Library material) — 'In 1851, A Man Picked Two Unpickable Locks': Hobbs publicly announced he would pick the Chubb detector lock; a witness wrote it took about 25 minutes; en-academic / detector-lock histories: Hobbs was the first person to pick the six-lever Chubb lock, at the Great Exhibition in 1851
Falsifiability: A contemporary denial that the picking happened, or proof the 25-minute account was manufactured — the event is reported across independent accounts including Chubb's own later publications.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1851-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1495,51.5027,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1851</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Howe's 1851 clothing closure — patented, never marketed</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1851 filing is attested across independent histories of the zipper; the 'never marketed' leg is consistently reported and uncontested.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts (Howe's base)
Dates: 1851 → 1851 (confidence: medium)

Elias Howe patented an 'automatic, continuous clothing closure' in 1851 — a device similar to the later zip — but never marketed it; he was occupied with the sewing machine, and the closure never reached the market.

Earliest attestation: US patent record, 1851 (Elias Howe); reported in Thomasnet and Marketplace coverage
Sources: Elias Howe, 'automatic, continuous clothing closure' patent, 1851; Marketplace.org — '10 amazing zipper facts you didn't know you wanted to know' (2014)
Falsifiability: A showing that Howe's 1851 closure reached market, or that the 1851 patent is misattributed</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1851-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1851</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Canterbury Hall opens, 17 May 1852 — Morton's purpose-built hall</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent attestations converge: the licence records, the consistent 17 May 1852 opening date, the documented 1856 rebuild around the old hall, and the institutional record of the Theatres Trust — while the same record corrects the 'first music hall' flattening.
Layer: real · Place: Canterbury Arms / Canterbury Hall, Upper Marsh, Lambeth, London, England
Dates: 1852-05-17 → 1852-05-17 (confidence: high)

On 17 May 1852 Charles Morton and Frederick Stanley opened the 700-seat Canterbury Hall at the Canterbury Arms, Upper Marsh, Lambeth — the purpose-built hall that became the model for the music-hall boom, described by historians as 'the most significant date in all the history of music hall'.

Earliest attestation: Morton's singing-room licences (1849–1851); contemporary press on the 1852 opening; Morton's own publicity, including the 1855–56 advertisements in The Times around the Theatres Act prosecutions
Sources: Theatres Trust theatrical database — Canterbury Hall: 'Charles Morton's Canterbury Hall was a key building in the history of music hall'; opening licences and history; Wikipedia / Charles Morton (impresario): Canterbury Hall 1852 (700 seats), New Canterbury December 1856 (1,500 seats); BBC 'What a Performance! Pioneers of Popular Entertainment' — music hall episode: song-and-supper room roots, the 1843 Theatre Regulations Act division, Morton's 'Father of the Halls' sobriquet
Falsifiability: Licence records or press contradicting the 17 May 1852 opening, the 700-seat capacity, or Morton's proprietorship.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1852-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1136,51.4972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1852</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The electric fire alarm telegraph, Boston 1852 (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: the Boston Fire Department's own records, the IEEE Milestone dedication (1 Oct 2004, Milestone #54: 'On 28 April 1852 the first municipal electric fire alarm system using call boxes with automatic signaling to indicate the location of a fire was placed into operation in Boston'), and the 1857 patent record (Channing &amp; Farmer, 'Electromagnetic Fire Alarm Telegraph for Cities').
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts (original system: three box circuits, three bell circuits, forty boxes, sixteen alarm bells)
Dates: 1852-04-28 → 1852-04-29 (confidence: high)

On 28 April 1852, the first municipal electric fire alarm system — call boxes with automatic signaling indicating the location of a fire — was placed into operation in Boston, Massachusetts, invented by William F. Channing and Moses G. Farmer; the first alarm was received 29 April 1852 at 8:25 p.m.

Earliest attestation: Boston Fire Department records, 1852; contemporary municipal records
Sources: IEEE Milestones: Electric Fire Alarm System, 1852 (Dedication #54, 1 Oct 2004, IEEE Boston Section): 'On 28 April 1852 the first municipal electric fire alarm system using call boxes with automatic signaling to indicate the location of a fire was placed into operation in Boston. Invented by William Channing and Moses Farmer...' — plaque at 59 the Fenway, Boston; Boston Fire Department historical record: first alarm 29 April 1852, 8:25 p.m. (J.H. Goodale's call, Endicott &amp; Cooper streets box, fire at Charleston and Causeway); system of three box circuits, three bell circuits, forty boxes, sixteen alarm bells; US Patent, 19 May 1857 — William F. Channing and Moses G. Farmer, 'Electromagnetic Fire Alarm Telegraph for Cities' (patent applied for two years after service began); FireRescue1 history of fire alarm boxes: John Gamewell acquired marketing rights 1855, full rights thereafter; Gamewell Fire Alarm Telegraph Company formed 1879; 250 cities by 1886, 500 by 1890
Falsifiability: Municipal and engineering records are specific to the date and the inventors; an IEEE Milestone dedication (2004) re-verified the claim against the engineering record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1852-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1852</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Samuel Fox's Paragon rib patent (1852): the modern umbrella frame</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent record itself is the primary evidence, multiply and independently described: the 1911 Britannica names the 1852 Paragon patent and its U-section form; Sangster's contemporary trade history describes the Paragon frame (half the weight of whalebone, equally strong; the Menai-bridge trough inspiration); the Hoyland Fox company history confirms the 1842 Stocksbridge founding and the Paragon name.
Layer: real · Place: Stocksbridge, Sheffield, England
Dates: 1852 → 1852 (confidence: high)

Samuel Fox patented the 'Paragon' umbrella rib in 1852 — a thin steel strip rolled into a U (trough) section, combining lightness with strength, inspired by the tubular construction of the Menai bridge; the Paragon frame became the industry standard for umbrella ribs. Fox founded Fox Umbrella Frames Ltd at Stocksbridge, Sheffield, in 1842, and became the world's largest umbrella-frame manufacturer.

Earliest attestation: The 1852 patent record; 1911 Britannica 'Umbrella' entry
Sources: 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Umbrella' (public domain): 'One of the most notable inventions for combining lightness, strength and elasticity in the ribs of umbrellas was the Paragon rib patented by Samuel Fox in 1852... formed of a thin strip of steel rolled into a U or trough section'; William Sangster, Umbrellas and Their History (1868): the Paragon frame — half the weight of whalebone, equally strong; the trough form from the Menai tubular bridge; Hoyland Fox company history: Samuel Fox founded Fox Umbrella Frames Ltd in 1842 at Stocksbridge; first inventor of the U-shape ribs called 'Paragon'; became the world's largest umbrella-frame manufacturer; Digitized 19th-c. trade book (WSU digital collection): '1851, Fox's paragon frames were introduced' — introduction-vs-patent variance carried honestly
Falsifiability: The 1852 patent record proving to be misattributed, or a dated prior patent for a U-section steel umbrella rib</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1852-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.5369,53.4811,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1852</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pravaz's syringe: the Lyon half of the 1853 twin invention</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The simultaneity is agreed across the specialist literature (MDDI: 'independently invented virtually simultaneously in 1853'; ThoughtCo; the French 'Pravaz syringe' tradition). Held at CORROBORATED on the thinner primary documentation of Pravaz's own 1853 experiments relative to Wood's 1855 publication, and on the genuine French/Anglo split in the credit tradition.
Layer: real · Place: Lyon, France
Dates: 1853 → 1853 (confidence: high)

In 1853, Charles-Gabriel Pravaz of Lyon built a working hypodermic syringe — graduated glass cylinder, screw-driven piston, silver construction — and used it to inject coagulating solutions (perchloride of iron/mercury) via trocar into aneurysms, in sheep and then in practice — independently of Alexander Wood's same-year Edinburgh work, with no channel of transmission between the two.

Earliest attestation: The Pravaz syringe's entry into surgical use, mid-1850s; the 19th-century French surgical literature
Sources: MDDI Online, 'The Hypodermic Syringe: Greatest Medical Device of All Time?' — 'The first devices recognizable as hypodermic syringes were independently invented virtually simultaneously in 1853 by Scottish physician Alexander Wood and French surgeon Charles Gabriel Pravaz'; ThoughtCo, 'The History of Hypodermic Needles and Syringes' — the Pravaz syringe: silver construction, screw piston mechanism, graduated measurements; 'At about the same time, Charles Gabriel Pravaz, of Lyon, was making a similar syringe'; The Free Library, 'The early development of intravenous apparatus' — 'The French physician, Charles Gabriel Pravaz is widely but erroneously credited with the invention of the syringe and subcutaneous medication. His experiments... involved the use of a silver metal syringe and injection of perchloride of mercury via a trochar into arterial aneurysms in sheep'
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence of a documented channel of transmission from Edinburgh to Lyon in 1852–1853, or proof Pravaz's 1853 work postdated and depended on Wood's</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1853-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.8357,45.764,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1853</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wood's real achievement: the all-glass syringe and the 1855 neuralgia paper</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Wood's invention and publication are in the primary record (the 1855 paper and its preface, via Brown 1886) and in the secondary literature (Brunton 2000, 'A Question of Priority: Alexander Wood, Charles Hunter and the Hypodermic Method'); Hunter's 'hypodermic' coinage and the local-vs-general dispute are documented in the same record. Held at CORROBORATED: the surviving achievement is the method and the paper, not the sole-inventor title. The DEBUNKED companion record carries the myth's death; this record carries the man's real work.
Layer: real · Place: 19 Royal Circus, Edinburgh, Scotland
Dates: 1853 → 1855 (confidence: high)

In 1853, Alexander Wood of Edinburgh built an all-glass hypodermic syringe with a hollow needle, and in 1855 published 'A New Method of Treating Neuralgia by the Direct Application of Opiates to the Painful Points' (Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Review) — the first practical method of subcutaneous morphine injection for neuralgia, which Wood argued had 'a far wider application' beyond narcotics.

Earliest attestation: Wood, A. (1855). 'A New Method of Treating Neuralgia by the Direct Application of Opiates to the Painful Points', Edinburgh Medical and Surgical Review; and the separately published preface quoted by his biographer Thomas Brown (1886)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Alexander Wood (physician)' — 'In 1853, he invented the first hypodermic needle that used a true syringe and hollow needle'; Wood's 'subcutaneous' vs Hunter's 'hypodermic'; Wood vs Hunter local-vs-general dispute; Thomas Brown (1886) biography quotes; D. Brunton (2000). 'A Question of Priority: Alexander Wood, Charles Hunter and the Hypodermic Method'. Proceedings of the Royal College of Physicians of Edinburgh 30(4): 349–351
Falsifiability: Proof the 1855 paper's cases were fabricated, or evidence the all-glass syringe predates 1853 in Wood's hands</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1853-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.1883,55.9533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1853</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Inuit testimony - the witnesses the searchers kept ignoring</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The geographic testimony is independently confirmed by two wreck discoveries at Inuit-indicated locations, and the institutional record (Parks Canada) explicitly credits Inuit oral history as decisive - but the testimony is a transmitted oral tradition, so it stays below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: King William Island and the Queen Maud Gulf region, Nunavut
Dates: 1854 → 2016 (confidence: high)

Inuit oral testimony is a primary evidentiary source for the Franklin expedition's fate: Inuit witnesses continuously located the expedition and its ships from the 1850s onward. Charles Francis Hall's 1869 witness In-nook-poo-zhe-jook described a ship sunk off the west coast of King William Island - the account that matches Terror Bay, where HMS Terror was found in 2016. John Rae's 1854 report (the £10,000 Admiralty-reward report) rested on Inuit accounts of starving white men near the Back River. Louie Kamookak of Gjoa Haven spent decades compiling Inuit oral history into a search geography that Parks Canada's teams credit as decisive in locating HMS Erebus in 2014. The testimony was repeatedly discounted by Victorian and modern searchers in favor of their own theories - and was right.

Earliest attestation: John Rae's 1854 report to the Admiralty - Inuit accounts of the expedition's fate; the reward was paid.
Sources: John Rae's 1854 report to the Admiralty - Inuit accounts, Franklin relics; the £10,000 reward report; Charles Francis Hall's 1869 expedition - In-nook-poo-zhe-jook's account of a ship sunk off King William Island's west coast; David C. Woodman, Unravelling the Franklin Mystery: Inuit Testimony (1991) - the synthesis that argued the testimony had been right all along; Parks Canada, Wrecks of HMS Erebus and HMS Terror National Historic Site - published credit to Inuit oral history (Kamookak, Gjoa Haven and Cambridge Bay consultations); 'never gave up on the belief that Inuit oral histories were accurate'; Louie Kamookak's decades of oral-history compilation (Gjoa Haven) - the search geography behind the 2014 find
Falsifiability: Showing that the 2014/2016 wreck positions came from search-technology alone with no Inuit input (contradicted by Parks Canada's own published credit), or that the 1869 Hall account describes a different ship, would weaken this record; it is held at CORROBORATED because individual accounts vary while the geographic consensus holds.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1854-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-96.0,68.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1854</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first can opener (Yeates, 1855)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Patent date and number attested by the specialist research document and encyclopedia summaries; held below VERIFIED because the record rests on secondary reporting of the patent rather than the enrolled patent text itself, and the inventor's surname varies by source.
Layer: real · Place: Trafalgar Place West, Hackney Road, London
Dates: 1855 → 1855 (confidence: high)

The first patented can opener was Robert Yeates's (also rendered Yates in the key specialist source) lever-type opener, British Patent No. 1577, dated 13 July 1855 — a curved-blade lever knife for 'cutting or ripping open preserved provision cases', from Trafalgar Place West, Hackney Road, London.

Earliest attestation: British Patent No. 1577 (13 July 1855)
Sources: Researchpod, "Lifting the lid on the tin can opener" — 'First in Britain was Robert Yates, a cutler and surgical instrument maker of Trafalgar Place West, Hackney Road, in Middlesex. His patent for "lock and lever knives, &amp;c." is dated 13 July 1855 (No.1577), and incorporates a lever knife for "cutting or ripping open preserved provision cases"'; Encyclopedia summaries of the can-opener history — 'The first can opener was actually an English invention, patented by Robert Yeates in 1855'
Falsifiability: A dated can-opener patent (or dated commercial specimen) earlier than July 1855</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1855-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.056,51.534,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1855</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The cannonballs in the road at Crimea</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Both plates are real and both are Fenton's; which came first — and whether cannonballs were deliberately staged onto the road — is an open photo-historical dispute (Morris's reading vs. successor analyses). A claim about staging that cannot be settled from the surviving evidence stays CLAIMED by doctrine.
Layer: real · Place: Valley of the Shadow of Death, near Sevastopol, Crimea
Dates: 1855 → 2011 (confidence: high)

Roger Fenton's 'Valley of the Shadow of Death' (Crimea, April 1855) exists in two plate variants — cannonballs on the road in one, off the road in the other — because Fenton's team moved the cannonballs onto the road for the more dramatic exposure.

Earliest attestation: The two surviving plates, both published from 1855.
Sources: The two surviving 'Valley of the Shadow of Death' plates (April 1855); Errol Morris, Believing Is Seeing (2011) — forensic essay on the plate order and the cannonball question
Falsifiability: A dated field note, letter, or exposure log from the Fenton/Sparling party fixing the sequence would move the grade; absent that, shadow-angle and ball-position analysis keep it contested.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1855-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>33.59,44.62,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1855</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The real 19th-century life of 'lions led by donkeys'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent 19th-century print attestations across three wars and three nations; the phrase's pre-WWI life is documented, even where the original coinage remains untraced.
Layer: real · Place: Western Europe — the phrase's 19th-century print trail (London, Paris, Sevastopol)
Dates: 1855 → 1927 (confidence: high)

The 'lions led by donkeys/asses' image predates the First World War by more than half a century, appearing in the Crimean War, the Franco-Prussian War, the Boxer Rebellion, and in print as early as 1855 — it was a 19th-century commonplace, not a 1914 German verdict.

Earliest attestation: The Daily Journal (American newspaper), August 1855, report of the Siege of Sevastopol: 'an army of lions led by donkeys' — attributed in the piece to the Russians. The Times of London wrote of the British Army in the Crimea, 'The Russians say we are lions led on by asses.'
Sources: The Daily Journal, August 1855 — Sevastopol report, 'an army of lions led by donkeys'; The Times, London — Crimean War ('lions led on by asses'); Franco-Prussian War reuse ('lions led by donkeys' of the French); Francisque Sarcey, 'Paris During The Siege' (1871): 'You are lions led by jackasses' — The Times's utterance 'drummed into' the French troops; Colonel J. M. Grierson, 1901 report on the Russian contingent to the Boxer Rebellion: 'lions led by asses' (via Richard Connaughton); Captain P. A. Thompson, 'Lions Led by Donkeys' (1927) — the phrase as a book title before Clark, subtitled 'Showing how victory in the Great War was achieved by those who made the fewest mistakes'
Falsifiability: Evidence that every pre-1961 instance is misdated or fabricated would overturn this; the 1855, 1870-71, 1901, and 1927 instances are on the print record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1855-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.0,50.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1855</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Yellow River's 1855 avulsion — a river that changed seas</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The event is dated to the day in Qing records and independently corroborated by modern sediment-delta science (delta growth, mouth migrations, marine sediment provenance).
Layer: real · Place: Tongwaxiang, Lankao, Henan — the 1855 breach point
Dates: 1855-08-01 → 1855-08-01 (confidence: high)

On 1 August 1855 (19th day of the 6th month, Xianfeng 5), the Yellow River breached its northern bank at Tongwaxiang in present-day Lankao, Henan, diverting its course from the Yellow Sea (via the Huai system, Jiangsu) to the Bohai Sea via the old Daqing/Ji River course in Shandong. The 1855 event ended the river's 1128–1855 southern course (itself a man-made diversion of 1128, when the river captured the Huai); the 1855 shift was natural. The new delta has since migrated repeatedly (six large natural diversions 1889–1934, four artificial 1953–1996).

Earliest attestation: Qing administrative records of the Xianfeng 5 breach at Tongwaxiang, August 1855.
Sources: Fei et al. 2012, Limnology 13:117 (cited in the 1851–1855 floods chronology) — the breach sequence; MDPI Land 14(9):1826 — cultivated-land dynamics in the Yellow River Delta basin since 1855 (the 1855 Tongwaxiang breach into the Daqing course; the 1128 man-made diversion; the ten post-1855 mouth migrations); Nature s41598-017-03140-x — clay-mineral record confirming the pre-1855 southern (Yellow Sea) course; Frontiers in Marine Science 2026 (doi:10.3389/fmars.2024.1490990) — Yellow River Delta coastline 1984-2024: three stages (rapid transformation, gradual slowdown, stabilization, ~15 yr each); abandoned Diaokou estuary eroding inland while the active Qingshuigou estuary progrades seaward — the 40-year observed modern analog of lobe switching: the abandoned lobe rots, the live one builds, in one generation of observation; MDPI Water 18(5):598 (published 28 Feb 2026) — four OSL-dated sediment cores of the shallow-buried abandoned Yellow River Delta (893–1855 AD): the 893 AD Yellow River avulsion triggered delta abandonment (893–1482 AD, fluvial-to-wave-controlled shift); Little Ice Age storm surges (1482–1855) further reworked the deposits — the delta the 1855 river abandoned had itself been abandoned 962 years earlier; the substrate's confession in two acts. Grade-neutral for the 1855 event (the breach date and geography unchanged) — appended as context for the river's serial abandonment habit
Falsifiability: A published Qing record re-dating the breach would move the date; a sediment record contradicting the Bohai-first arrival would revise the geography.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1855-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>114.7,34.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1855</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Scott's 1860 phonoautogram — the first recorded human voice</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The artifact survives with Scott's own date on it; the 1857 patent documents the machine; and the 2008 virtual-stylus playback — by particle physicist Carl Haber at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, from First Sounds' recovered papers — turned the very tracings into audible sound, independently confirming that the sheet captured a human voice. Multiple independent lines (the paper, the patent, the playback) converge on the same event.
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (phonoautograph patent; phonautogram papers at the French Academy of Sciences / Paris patent office)
Dates: 1857 → 2008 (confidence: high)

On 9 April 1860, a Parisian inventor's machine recorded a human voice on soot-covered paper — the singer singing a fragment of the French folk song 'Au Clair de la Lune' — and that physical trace survives, dated, and was played back audibly in 2008: the earliest recognizable recording of a human voice, made seventeen years before Edison's phonograph.

Earliest attestation: The dated phonautogram sheet itself, 9 April 1860, found among Scott's patent-office and French Academy of Sciences papers
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Phonautograph': French patent 25 March 1857 #17,897/31,470; 9 April 1860 'Au Clair de la Lune' recording; device not designed for playback; First Sounds / Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory 2008 digital conversion; 'prior to this point, the earliest known record of a human voice was thought to be an 1877 phonograph recording by Thomas Edison'; Symmetry Magazine (Dec 2009) — 'Physicist revives oldest recording of the human voice': Scott never intended playback; Carl Haber of Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory pulled the sound from Scott's soot-covered paper in March 2008 — 'the earliest recording of the human voice ever successfully played back'; LiveScience — 'Edison Voice Recording Is Old, but Not Oldest': the December 2007 discovery in the Paris patent office by an American audio historian; the French Academy of Sciences cache; the Lawrence Berkeley digital mapping with a 'virtual stylus'; Scott's first phonautograms dated three years before the 1857 patent, indecipherable without the tuning-fork timing signal; the earliest recognizable recording of any sound is an 1859 tuning-fork recording at 435 Hz; Classical Net / NYT (Jody Rosen, 27 March 2008) — 'Researchers Play Tune Recorded Before Edison': Samuel Brylawski (former head of the Library of Congress recorded-sound division, not affiliated with the research group) — 'This is a historic find, the earliest known recording of sound'
Falsifiability: Proof that the 9 April 1860 sheet is a forgery, is misdated, or was fabricated later (e.g. by Edison-era agents) would collapse the record; independent dating of the paper/ink in the negative would reopen it</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1857-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1857</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lipman's pencil-with-eraser patent, US 19,783 (March 30, 1858)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The patent document is independently verifiable on Google Patents; the 1862 sale to Reckendorfer for $100,000 and the subsequent litigation are carried across reference sources; the embedded-core design detail comes straight from the specification's text.
Layer: real · Place: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1858-03-30 → 1862 (confidence: high)

On March 30, 1858, Philadelphia stationer Hymen L. Lipman (born Kingston, Jamaica, 1817; America's first envelope manufacturer) was granted U.S. patent 19,783 for the 'Combination of Lead-Pencil and Eraser' — the first patent for a pencil with an attached eraser. Lipman sold the patent in 1862 to Joseph Reckendorfer for $100,000. Important design note: Lipman's patent was not the glued-on pink cap of modern memory — the specification embeds a groove of prepared india-rubber inside the pencil wood, exposed by cutting/sharpening the end, so a soiled point could be renewed 'as in the ordinary lead-pencil.'

Earliest attestation: U.S. Letters Patent 19,783 (March 30, 1858); Google Patents scan of the specification
Sources: U.S. Patent 19,783 — 'Combination of Lead-Pencil and Eraser' (March 30, 1858), specification text via Google Patents; Wikipedia, 'Hymen Lipman': patent date, 1862 sale to Reckendorfer for $100,000, 1875 Supreme Court invalidation; cites Petroski (1990); Smithsonian Magazine, 'Happy Birthday to the Modern Pencil' (2017): Lipman's embedded-core design (chunk of rubber exposed by sharpening) vs the modern glued end; Lipman's envelope/postcard career
Falsifiability: The patent document itself is the evidence; a falsifying find would have to show the document is not what it purports to be.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1858-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1652,39.9526,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1858</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Warner's can opener (US 19,063, 1858)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent itself is the primary source; the design details (bayonet + sickle, metal guard) are in the specification and multiply summarized.
Layer: real · Place: Waterbury, Connecticut, USA
Dates: 1858 → 1858 (confidence: high)

Ezra J. Warner of Waterbury, Connecticut received US Patent 19,063 on 5 January 1858 for a can opener — a curved-handle tool combining a piercing 'bayonet' and a sawing 'sickle' blade that cut a jagged line around the can lid, kept from penetrating too deep by a metal guard.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 19,063 (5 January 1858)
Sources: US Patent 19,063 (5 January 1858), Ezra J. Warner — curved handle, long bayonet blade and interchangeable sliding shoe; bayonet pierces the lid, sickle saws around the edge; Researchpod, "Lifting the lid on the tin can opener" — 'Three years later, in 1858, a similar lever opener was patented in America by Ezra J. Warner'; the now-familiar construction: curved blade with a jaw and a projection with shoulders forming an efficient bearing or fulcrum; Google Arts &amp; Culture / Tomato Museum, 'The History of the Can Opener' — Warner's US 19,063: used by the Northern Army during the American Civil War (1861–1865); dangerous for domestic use, production abandoned; Today in Connecticut History — 5 Jan 1858 grant; wrought-iron cans, thinner tin-lined steel by the 1850s; the 'first mass-produced tin cans made in London in 1813 for sale to the Royal Navy' (BBC)
Falsifiability: Proof the patent grant date is misread, or a surviving Warner-manufactured opener dated before 1858</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1858-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.933,41.5582,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1858</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Carrington Event, 1–2 September 1859 (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: on 1 September 1859 at 11:18, Richard Carrington — sketching sunspots at his private observatory near London — saw two brilliant patches of white light erupt in a sunspot group, lasting about five minutes; Richard Hodgson independently observed the same — the first recorded white-light solar flare, published in the Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society; ~17.6 hours later (unusually fast — typical CMEs take 2–4 days, suggesting an earlier CME had cleared the way), the coronal mass ejection struck Earth, producing the most intense geomagnetic storm in recorded history (estimated Dst −850 to −1760 nT, far beyond anything in the space age); auroras were reported globally — as far equatorward as Cuba, Hawaii, Jamaica, northern Australia, and Santiago; telegraph systems worldwide failed, sparked, and caught fire — operators received electric shocks, message paper ignited, and in Boston operators disconnected their batteries and kept sending messages on the storm's own geomagnetically induced current; Rocky Mountain miners woke at midnight and began cooking breakfast, believing dawn had come; compasses swung erratically and magnetometers worldwide registered extreme fluctuations. The modern-vulnerability corollary (a repeat would damage power grids and satellites) is graded separately as expert assessment, not as established fact about 1859.
Layer: real · Place: London, England (observation); global (storm effects)
Dates: 1859-09-01 → 1859-09-02 (confidence: high)

On 1–2 September 1859, the Carrington Event — the most intense geomagnetic storm in recorded history — set telegraph systems sparking and pushed auroras to the tropics.

Earliest attestation: Richard Carrington (and independently Richard Hodgson), 1 September 1859; published in Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society
Sources: Wikipedia: 'The Carrington Event was the most intense geomagnetic storm in recorded history, peaking on 1–2 September 1859 during solar cycle 10. It created strong auroral displays that were reported globally and caused sparking and even fires in telegraph stations.' The 1 September 1859 flare observed independently by Carrington and Hodgson — the first records of a solar flare.; The Watchers: Carrington's 11:18 observation, the five-minute brightening, Hodgson's independent observation, the 17.6-hour CME transit, Dst −850 to −1760 nT, erratic compasses.; Discover Magazine: the September 1, 1859 flare, the ~18-hour particle arrival, global telegraph disruption, equatorial auroras; Carrington's 'singular outbreak of light which lasted about five minutes.'; Energy Daily (July 2026): auroras to Cuba, telegraph paper on fire from Boston to Paris, operators sending messages with batteries disconnected on the storm's own current; Rocky Mountain miners mistaking aurora for dawn.
Falsifiability: Contemporary telegraph/observatory records proven fabricated</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1859-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1859</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Crown's storm-glass distribution to fishing villages</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Wikipedia's FitzRoy biography documents the Crown's distribution of the storm glasses to fishing communities after the 1859 storm; the program is consistent with FitzRoy's documented mission to put instruments in working hands (the warning cones, the telegraphed forecasts). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED pending direct archival citation of the distribution manifests.
Layer: real · Place: British Isles coastal fishing communities
Dates: 1859 → 1865 (confidence: medium)

After the Royal Charter storm of October 1859, the Crown distributed storm glasses — 'FitzRoy's storm barometers' — to many small fishing communities around the British Isles, as part of FitzRoy's program to put forecasting instruments in working hands.

Earliest attestation: Board of Trade / Meteorological Department distribution records, via the secondary accounts
Sources: Wikipedia 'Robert FitzRoy': 'The 1859 storm resulted in the Crown distributing storm glasses, then known as "FitzRoy's storm barometers", to many small fishing communities around the British Isles.'
Falsifiability: Board of Trade archives showing no such distribution program, or evidence the 'storm barometers' were never manufactured or sent</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1859-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.0,54.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1859</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Suez Canal opens (1869)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Opening date, length, builder, and route converge across the company's own archives, contemporary press, and later documentary-heritage inscription.
Layer: real · Place: Isthmus of Suez, Egypt (Port Said → Suez)
Dates: 1859 → 1869 (confidence: high)

On 17 November 1869, a 164 km sea-level waterway opened through the Isthmus of Suez, connecting the Mediterranean at Port Said to the Red Sea at Suez — built 1859–1869 by Ferdinand de Lesseps's Compagnie Universelle du Canal Maritime de Suez.

Earliest attestation: The Compagnie Universelle's own construction records 1859–1869; contemporary press of the 17 November 1869 opening.
Sources: Compagnie Universelle du Canal Maritime de Suez — concession (1854/1856) and construction records 1859–1869; Contemporary press coverage of the 17 November 1869 opening ceremony at Port Said; UNESCO Memory of the World — Suez Canal Company archives (documentary heritage of the build)
Falsifiability: A contemporary survey showing a different route or length, or records showing the canal uncompleted by late 1869</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1859-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.35,30.97,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1859</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pony Express (April 1860 – October 1861)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested: founders, dates, route, relay mechanics, the bankruptcy, and the telegraph terminus are all documented in company records, contemporary 1860-61 press, and modern institutional scholarship (NPS National Historic Trail, National Pony Express Association).
Layer: real · Place: St. Joseph, Missouri (headquarters; eastern terminus of the route)
Dates: 1860-04-03 → 1861-10-26 (confidence: high)

The Pony Express was an American express mail service using relays of horse-mounted riders between St. Joseph, Missouri and Sacramento, California, operated by the Central Overland California and Pikes Peak Express Company (founders Alexander Majors, William B. Waddell, William H. Russell). It ran from 3 April 1860 to 26 October 1861 — about eighteen months. The ~1,900-mile route used relay stations every 10-15 miles (riders changed horses at stations, fresh riders every 75-100 miles), delivering mail in about ten days at an initial rate of $5 per half-ounce. Despite a heavy subsidy it was not a financial success: it went bankrupt within eighteen months, never winning the hoped-for U.S. government postal contract, and was made obsolete by the completion of the first transcontinental telegraph on 24 October 1861.

Earliest attestation: 1860 company advertisements and contemporary newspaper coverage; Patee House / Central Overland California and Pikes Peak Express Company records, St. Joseph
Sources: Pony Express company records and 1860-61 advertisements (Central Overland California and Pikes Peak Express Company); Contemporary 1860-61 newspaper coverage of the service (founding, runs, demise); U.S. National Park Service, Pony Express National Historic Trail — History &amp; Culture; National Pony Express Association — historic trail and founders research; Smithsonian Magazine, 'Murder, Marriage and the Pony Express: Ten Things You Didn't Know About Buffalo Bill' (Lincoln 1860 election news carried Fort Kearney to Placerville in five days); Pony Express — Wikipedia (infobox dates, founders, bankruptcy, telegraph terminus; secondary aggregator)
Falsifiability: Company ledgers showing the firm turned a profit, or documentary evidence of the U.S. government mail contract the founders sought — either would overturn the failure reading.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-94.85607,39.7666,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The secession declarations' own words</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The texts survive as contemporaneous primary documents and say what they say: South Carolina's lists the fugitive-slave clause, 'the right of property in slaves,' northern attacks on slavery as sinful, and a president 'hostile to slavery'; Mississippi's opens 'Our position is thoroughly identified with the institution of slavery — the greatest material interest of the world'; Texas's defends 'negro slavery' as the reason for its revolution from Mexico; none names tariffs or abstract constitutional principle as its cause.
Layer: real · Place: Charleston, South Carolina (the convention that adopted the first declaration)
Dates: 1860-12-24 → 1861-02-01 (confidence: high)

The seceding states of the Lower South stated their reasons for secession in formal written declarations, and those declarations name slavery and hostility to slavery as their cause.

Earliest attestation: The convention-adopted printed declarations themselves, winter 1860-61 (contemporaneous primary documents).
Sources: South Carolina, Declaration of the Immediate Causes (Dec 24, 1860); Mississippi, Declaration of the Immediate Causes (Jan 9, 1861); Georgia, Declaration of Immediate Causes (Jan 29, 1861); Texas, Declaration of the Causes (Feb 1, 1861)
Falsifiability: The declarations, on inspection, not naming slavery among their reasons — inspection shows they do.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.9311,32.7765,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Joseph Swan's carbon-filament lamp — the priority the legend skipped</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the patent registers, contemporary lecture reports, and institutional histories (Smithsonian, Science Museum Group): carbonized paper filaments by 1860, UK patent 1878, public demonstration February 1879, Savoy Theatre 1881 — the first public building in the world lit entirely by electricity. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the primary patent documents and lecture reports were accessed here through secondary aggregators; the chain is strong but one layer removed.
Layer: real · Place: Newcastle upon Tyne / Gateshead, England — the February 1879 lecture demonstration; Swan's Gateshead home, the world's first home lit by electricity
Dates: 1860 → 1883 (confidence: high)

Joseph Swan built and demonstrated a working carbon-filament incandescent lamp before Edison — British patent 1878, public demonstration February 1879, commercial and public-building lighting thereafter.

Earliest attestation: Swan's 1878 UK patent; Smithsonian-recorded February 1879 Newcastle lecture demonstration.
Sources: famousinventors.info — Joseph Swan biography; Smithsonian Institution; Science Museum Group; legaleraonline.com — 'Whose Electric Bulb Is It Anyway?'
Falsifiability: Archival evidence that Swan's 1878 patent did not exist or that the February 1879 demonstration was fabricated — against the patent registers and contemporary Newcastle lecture reports.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.6178,54.9783,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Localization of function (the genuine heir of phrenology's question)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines: lesion cases (Broca 1861, Wernicke 1874), cytoarchitectonic maps (Brodmann 1909, 52 areas), Penfield's intraoperative stimulation mapping (motor/sensory homunculi), split-brain work (Sperry, 1960s), and modern fMRI. Stops short of VERIFIED only because the full functional map remains incomplete (memory, consciousness unmapped).
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (Broca's 1861 presentation)
Dates: 1861 → 1955 (confidence: high)

Cognitive functions are localized in distinct regions of the cerebral cortex: damage to a specific region produces a specific deficit — the lesion logic that built modern neuroscience.

Earliest attestation: Paul Broca, 'Remarques sur le siège de la faculté du langage articulé' (1861) — the Leborgne ('Tan') case: left inferior frontal gyrus lesion, lost articulate speech.
Sources: Paul Broca (1861), 'Remarques sur le siège de la faculté du langage articulé' — the Tan case; Carl Wernicke (1874), Der aphasische Symptomencomplex — sensory aphasia, superior temporal gyrus; Korbinian Brodmann (1909), Vergleichende Lokalisationslehre — 52 cytoarchitectonic areas; Wilder Penfield &amp; Rasmussen (1950), The Cerebral Cortex of Man — stimulation mapping, homunculi
Falsifiability: Consistent failure of focal lesions to produce consistent specific deficits, or whole-brain functional imaging showing no regional specialization for any cognitive function.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1861-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.352,48.856,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1861</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>FitzRoy's storm-warning service, February 1861</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources agree on the trigger (the Royal Charter storm, 26 October 1859, 450+ lives lost off Anglesey), the approval (December 1859), the mechanism (fifteen telegraph stations reporting to London, cones and drums hoisted at harbor towns), and the February 1861 first warnings — and on the service's continuity into the modern shipping forecast.
Layer: real · Place: London (Board of Trade Meteorological Department)
Dates: 1861-02 → 1861-02 (confidence: high)

In February 1861, Vice-Admiral Robert FitzRoy's Meteorological Department of the Board of Trade issued the first telegraphed storm warnings to British harbor towns — cones and drums hoisted on warning masts — the origin of the shipping forecast and the longest-running national forecasting service in the world.

Earliest attestation: The February 1861 telegraphed gale warnings themselves; FitzRoy's synoptic charts and the Board of Trade Meteorological Department records
Sources: phys.org, 'Royal Charter storm of 1859': FitzRoy had lobbied for a storm warning system since summer 1859; after the storm he demonstrated it could have been predicted; 'the new storm warning system was approved' in December 1859 and 'the first warning was issued in February 1861,' delivered by telegraph to harbor towns, which 'hoisted cones and drums on a mast'; the service became the shipping forecast, 'the longest running national forecasting service in the world'; maritime-executive.com, 'The Royal Charter Storm of 1859 and the Roots of the Marine Forecast': same account — December 1859 approval, February 1861 first warning, telegraph-to-harbor-towns with cones and drums; the first public weather forecast printed in The Times in August 1861; Wikipedia 'Robert FitzRoy': the 1859 Royal Charter loss inspired FitzRoy's prediction charts and the coined term 'weather forecast'; fifteen land stations telegraphed daily reports; 1860 storm-warning cones at principal ports; fishing fleet owners objected, the system was abandoned briefly after his 1865 death and reinstated in simplified form in 1874; the Crown distributed 'FitzRoy's storm barometers' to fishing communities; HM Coastguard/Maritime and Coastguard Agency: 'Rewind 101 years and Weather Shipping was broadcast over a telegraphic messaging system first developed in 1861 by Vice-Admiral Robert FitzRoy to issue weather warnings to ships'; the Royal Charter sinking, 26 October 1859, loss of over 450 lives, as the motivation
Falsifiability: Board of Trade or Met Office archives showing no warning system existed before a later date, or that the February 1861 warnings were never sent</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1861-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1861</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Reis's 'Telephon' — the 1861 public demonstrations</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1861 demonstrations are attested in Reis's own published accounts and the Physical Society record, and the LoC carries the device, the term, and the no-patent fact together. Held below VERIFIED on the exact fidelity of what was transmitted (speech vs tones is the contested layer) and on the precise demonstration dates.
Layer: real · Place: Frankfurt am Main, Germany
Dates: 1861-01-01 → 1861-12-31 (confidence: medium)

In 1861, German physics teacher Johann Philipp Reis publicly demonstrated his 'Telephon' — an apparatus that converted sounds to electrical signals and transmitted speech, imperfectly — the first use of the word 'telefon/telephone' for such a device; he demonstrated widely but never sought a patent.

Earliest attestation: Reis's 1861 demonstrations before the Physical Society of Frankfurt and his published accounts; the apparatus and the term 'Telephon' in his own 1860s writings.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — 'Reis was a physics teacher in Germany who developed a device that transmitted speech (albeit badly) in the early 1860s. He called his apparatus a "telefon," the first time the term was used to refer to an apparatus that converts sounds to electrical signals. Reis demonstrated his device widely, but never sought a patent nor did he continue to further develop his idea.'
Falsifiability: Period documentation that Reis's 1861 demonstrations did not occur, or that the device never transmitted voice-like sounds, would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1861-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.6821,50.1109,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1861</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lipman founds America's first envelope company</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent modern retellings (Wikipedia, Smithsonian Magazine, the Davison biography) agree on the 'first envelope company' claim, drawing on the 1893 obituary trail; the adhesive-flap and eyelet details come from the Smithsonian's Petroski-sourced account. Held below VERIFIED — the sources are biographical summaries, not the obituaries themselves.
Layer: real · Place: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1862 → 1873 (confidence: medium)

Philadelphia stationer Hymen Lipman (1817-1893) founded the first envelope company in the United States — three years after he sold his 1858 pencil-with-attached-eraser patent to Joseph Reckendorfer for $100,000 (1862). The Smithsonian account adds that Lipman put adhesive on the back flap, devised an eyelet paper-binding method that preceded the stapler by two decades, and sold the first blank postcards in the US in 1873.

Earliest attestation: Late-19th-century obituary trail (Philadelphia Inquirer obituary Nov 11, 1893; Paper Trade Journal Nov 18, 1893) and modern retellings drawing on them.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Hymen Lipman' ('Three years later, he started the first envelope company in the US' [after the 1862 patent sale]; cites Philadelphia Magazine and Petroski 1990); Smithsonian Magazine — 'Happy Birthday to the Modern Pencil' (Lipman as 'America's first envelope manufacturer'; adhesive back flap; eyelet binding before the stapler; first US blank postcards 1873); George M. Davison — 'Hymen Lipman: Creator of the Attached-Eraser Pencil' (sold patent to Reckendorfer 1862; 'start the first envelope company in the U.S.'); Winterthur Museum, 'Every Trade Card Tells a Story' (early-1840s Lipman trade card; envelopes not yet listed — used to bound the factory's start to post-1843)
Falsifiability: A documented earlier American envelope company would demote the 'first' claim; Philadelphia city-directory evidence showing no envelope concern under Lipman's name in the 1860s would weaken it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1862-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1652,39.9526,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1862</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Emancipation Proclamation as issued</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The signed document survives in the National Archives with the scope and exemptions exactly as issued: it applied to the named states in rebellion (South Carolina, Mississippi, Florida, Alabama, Georgia, Texas, Arkansas, North Carolina), expressly exempted Tennessee, the 48 West Virginia counties, and named counties/parishes of Virginia and Louisiana under Union control, left the border states (Maryland, Delaware, Kentucky, Missouri) untouched, authorized Black enlistment (~200,000 served by war's end), and ended nothing nationally — slavery became illegal only with the Thirteenth Amendment's ratification in December 1865.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (the White House, where Lincoln signed it)
Dates: 1863-01-01 → 1863-01-01 (confidence: high)

President Abraham Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation (preliminary Sept 22, 1862; final Jan 1, 1863) declared free all persons held as slaves in the states then in rebellion, authorized the enlistment of Black men in the Union armed forces, and turned emancipation into a war aim — but did not end slavery nationally (that took the Thirteenth Amendment, ratified December 1865).

Earliest attestation: The signed proclamation itself (National Archives), January 1, 1863.
Sources: Emancipation Proclamation, final version, January 1, 1863 (National Archives, signed original); Equal Justice Initiative, 'History of Racial Injustice: Emancipation Proclamation' — scope summary: rebel states only; exempted Tennessee, portions of Virginia and Louisiana; border states untouched; military-service provision; Thirteenth Amendment ratified Dec 6, 1865; Abraham Lincoln to Horace Greeley, August 22, 1862 — the Union-first war aim, stated by the author of the Proclamation
Falsifiability: A version of the document applying to loyal territory or lacking the military-service provision — the National Archives copy has both the exemptions and the provision.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1863-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1863</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Lincoln portrait that is really Calhoun</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Collection records (Lincoln Financial Foundation Collection, Indiana State Museum) document the image as a composite: Pate's engraving based on Ritchie's 1852 Calhoun, Calhoun's head replaced with Lincoln's, the table papers re-lettered from 'Strict Constitution / Free Trade / the Sovereignty of the States' to 'Constitution / Union / Proclamation of Freedom.' The most famous fake Lincoln portrait ever sold — photoshopped before Photoshop existed.
Layer: real · Place: New York (printmaker William Pate)
Dates: 1863 → 1870 (confidence: high)

The popular 1860s full-length standing portrait of Abraham Lincoln — Lincoln in frock coat and shawl beside a table of documents — is a composite image: Lincoln's head set on John C. Calhoun's body.

Earliest attestation: The engraving itself (mid-1860s), cataloged in the Lincoln Financial Foundation Collection, Indiana State Museum holdings.
Sources: Lincoln Financial Foundation Collection catalog records (items 71.2009.081.0474, .1455, .1466, .2605) — composite attribution, Ritchie 1852 source, document re-lettering details; Alexander H. Ritchie, engraving of John C. Calhoun (1852) — the source plate
Falsifiability: Would require the Ritchie 1852 Calhoun plate and the Pate Lincoln plate to differ in body, background, and drapery — they are the same plate with the head swapped and the table documents re-lettered.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1863-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0,40.71,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1863</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Who wrote the Bixby letter: Lincoln or Hay</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attribution to Lincoln rests on the signature and the contemporary publication; serious scholarship splits between Lincoln and his secretary John Hay, with no holograph or dated draft settling it.
Layer: real · Place: Executive Mansion, Washington, D.C.
Dates: 1864-11-21 → 1864-11-21 (confidence: high)

Abraham Lincoln personally composed the November 21, 1864 condolence letter to Mrs. Bixby.

Earliest attestation: Boston Evening Transcript, November 25, 1864 — the letter printed under Lincoln's name.
Sources: Michael Burlingame, Journal of the Abraham Lincoln Association: Hay-authorship argument — Hay's scrapbooks at Brown hold the letter's clipping among his own writings; 'beguile' recurs in Hay's writings; 'I cannot refrain from tendering' appears in Hay's 1864 letter to Quincy Gillmore; Aston University Centre for Forensic Linguistics (2018): n-gram tracing method devised for short texts identified John Hay as the author; Jean G. Pival (1988, for investigator Joe Nickell): stylistic study of vocabulary and syntax concluded the letter more closely resembled Lincoln's writing than Hay's; Edward Steers and Jason Emerson: Lincoln's authorship — stylistic match to the Gettysburg and Farewell addresses; Robert Todd Lincoln's 1917 letter to Isaac Markens records Hay saying he had 'no special knowledge of the letter'
Falsifiability: A dated holograph in Lincoln's hand would verify Lincoln; a Hay manuscript draft predating November 21, 1864 would verify Hay</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1864-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1864</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bixby letter (Nov 21, 1864)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The letter's issue, its hand-delivery by Schouler, and its contemporary newspaper publication are multiply attested in contemporary records; only the original holograph is missing.
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1864-11-21 → 1864-11-25 (confidence: high)

A condolence letter signed 'A. Lincoln,' dated at the Executive Mansion November 21, 1864, was issued to Mrs. Lydia Bixby of Boston, Massachusetts; Massachusetts Adjutant General William Schouler delivered it to her on November 24, 1864, and its full text appeared in the Boston Evening Transcript's afternoon second edition of November 25, 1864.

Earliest attestation: Boston Evening Transcript, afternoon second edition, November 25, 1864 (the text survives only through this newspaper printing; the original holograph has never been recovered).
Sources: Boston Evening Transcript, Nov 25, 1864 (afternoon second edition); William Schouler papers / Massachusetts Adjutant General's report to the War Department, Oct 12, 1864; Lincoln Financial Foundation Collection, item 71.2009.081.1041 (print of the letter's text)
Falsifiability: A dated holograph differing from the published text, or proof the Transcript never printed it</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1864-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1864</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'McGill invented the brass fastener' — the first-inventor telling</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The first-inventor telling rests on a single patent whose title and specification both say 'Improvement' — McGill claimed an advance, not the device class, and his T-strip construction is not the round-head brad.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. — McGill's residence at filing
Dates: 1866 → 1866 (confidence: medium)

George W. McGill invented the brass paper fastener in 1866 — that the round-head brad with two split legs originated with him and his US 56,587 patent.

Earliest attestation: No documented first-inventor attestation found this pass — the telling rests on the single patent document US 56,587.
Sources: US Patent 56,587 — 'Improvement in metallic paper-fasteners,' specification: 'certain new and useful Improvements in Paper-Fastenings'; claims a single metal strip bent into a T shape with pointed ends; Wikipedia 'Brass fastener' — 'A patent of the fastener was issued in 1866 to George W. McGill,' citing only US 56,587
Falsifiability: Documentation of a pre-1866 round-head brad (patent, advertisement, workshop record) would kill the origin claim; proof that McGill's T-strip is the direct, exclusive ancestor of the modern brad would promote it.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1866-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1866</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>McGill's 1866 paper-fastener patent (US 56,587)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent is a primary document of record, self-cited by number and date in McGill's 1867 follow-on patent; the claim is the patent's existence and content, not 'invented the stapler.'
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. — McGill's residence at filing
Dates: 1866-01-24 → 1866-07-01 (confidence: high)

George W. McGill of Washington, D.C. received U.S. Patent 56,587 in 1866 (antedated January 24, 1866) for 'small, bendable paper fasteners' — brass fasteners inserted one at a time, the documented beginning of the modern American stapler's paper trail.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent 56,587 itself, 1866 — cited in McGill's own 1867 press patent (US 67,665): 'Attaching my Paper-Fastener (patented July, 1866, antedated January 24, 1866)'.
Sources: Google Patents, US67665A (McGill press, Aug 13 1867) — specification cites 'my Paper-Fastener (patented July, 1866, antedated January 24, 1866)'; University at Buffalo — George McGill received U.S. patent #56,587 for 'small, bendable paper fasteners' in 1866
Falsifiability: A patent-office record showing 56,587 as a different invention or date would reopen it — the document itself is the evidence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1866-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1866</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>McGill's 1867 staple press (US 67,665)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1867 specification is a primary document of record describing the press and naming the 1866 fastener it attaches; read here through Google Patents' OCR rather than a certified copy.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. — McGill's residence at filing
Dates: 1867-08-13 → 1867-08-13 (confidence: high)

George W. McGill's follow-on patent US 67,665 (August 13, 1867) describes a spring-plunger press — hammer, punch, and clinching base — built specifically to attach his 1866 brass paper-fastener to papers by punching holes and hammering the shanks down.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent 67,665, August 13, 1867 — the specification text ('a press provided with a spring-plunger, to act as a hammer and punch...').
Sources: Google Patents, US67665A — 'G. W. McGILL... Press for Attaching Paper-Fasteners. Patented Aug. 13, 1867' — spring-plunger, hammer, punch, clinching base described
Falsifiability: The patent document stands; held below VERIFIED because this pass read the Google Patents OCR text, not a certified USPTO copy.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1867-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1867</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The anonymous Ether Monument, 1868</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The monument is a standing, inspectable artifact; its namelessness is verifiable in person and in the Smithsonian/Mass. Historical Society record; the controversy context (Morton vs Jackson, the refused 5,000-franc half-prize, Twain's and Holmes's interventions) is multiply attested.
Layer: real · Place: Boston Public Garden
Dates: 1868 → 1868 (confidence: high)

The Ether Monument (Boston Public Garden, unveiled 1868 — the first statue erected in the Public Garden) is a 40-foot Gothic memorial topped by a Good Samaritan sculpture that names NO discoverer of anesthesia — no Morton, no Wells, no Jackson, no Long — only biblical quotations about relieving pain ('Neither shall there be any more pain'). The anonymity was the settlement of a credit war no committee could decide.

Earliest attestation: The monument itself, still standing in the Boston Public Garden; contemporary press and Oliver Wendell Holmes's 'ether or either' quip.
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine: the 40-foot monument, the nameless inscription ('The sculptor inscribed no names on the structure, only biblical quotations about the blessed work of alleviating pain'), 'the nation's only monument to a drug.'; Mass. Historical Society (Oct 2016 Object of the Month): 'the Ether Monument credited neither Morton nor Jackson, but rather was dedicated to "either"' — Holmes's line; Jackson's asylum years at McLean.; Somerville Times (Oct 2026): the 1868 unveiling as the Public Garden's first statue; 'The memorial doesn't mention the first men who practiced with the mysterious vapor... it simply praises ether and its wondrous contribution to mankind'; Jackson's 1873 commitment to McLean's double domes.
Falsifiability: Discovery of a hidden name on the monument (the sculptor's inscription is physically checkable — it names no one) or a committee record showing a named dedication that was then altered.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0717,42.3536,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Haeckel's embryo drawings were doctored</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The doctoring is multiply attested: a contemporary 1868 exposure, a 1997 photographic re-examination across 39 species, and Gould's scholarly assessment — fraud on the record, not a rumor.
Layer: real · Place: Jena, Germany (Haeckel's chair, 1865–1909)
Dates: 1868 → 1997 (confidence: high)

Ernst Haeckel's embryo drawings (Anthropogenie, 1874) were doctored: he resized embryos to a common scale (erasing up to 10-fold size differences), eliminated limb buds and heart bulges, and omitted species names to exaggerate similarity — documented contemporaneously by Rütimeyer (1868) and forensically by Richardson et al. (1997, Anatomy and Embryology 196:91–106, photographic comparison across 39 species).

Earliest attestation: L. Rütimeyer 1868 (contemporary critique, shown within months of the 1868 publication); Richardson, Hanken et al. 1997, Anatomy and Embryology 196:91–106
Sources: Richardson, Hanken, Gooneratne et al. 1997, Anatomy and Embryology 196:91–106 — 'There is no highly conserved embryonic stage in the vertebrates'; photographic comparison of the species Haeckel drew: resized embryos, erased limb buds and heart bulges, fudged scales, unnamed species; Science, 5 Sept 1997, 'Haeckel's Embryos: Fraud Rediscovered' — Richardson quoted: 'one of the most famous fakes in biology'; Rütimeyer 1868 — contemporary demonstration, within months of the 1868 publication, that Haeckel had altered the embryos to look more alike; Gould's assessment — Haeckel 'committed the academic equivalent of murder' (quoted in the secondary literature; the 'astonished and ashamed' century of textbook recycling)
Falsifiability: A photographic re-comparison showing Haeckel's plates faithful to the embryos at the stated stages; retraction of Richardson et al. 1997. Neither has occurred.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.589,50.928,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Lydia Thompson's British Blondes open in New York, 28 September 1868</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary attestations converge: the 1868 New York press report on the sold-out opening, the documented six-year tour that followed, and Gänzl's 2002 scholarly biography with the full playbill record — 'Ixion' as literary parody, not striptease.
Layer: real · Place: Wood's Museum and Metropolitan Theatre, Broadway and 31st Street, New York City, USA
Dates: 1868-09-28 → 1868-09-28 (confidence: high)

On 28 September 1868 Lydia Thompson's British Blondes opened at Wood's Museum and Metropolitan Theatre, New York, in F. C. Burnand's burlesque 'Ixion' — the arrival that made American burlesque a commercial force.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1868 New York press (New York Times report on the sold-out opening); the company's own playbills and tour schedules.
Sources: Kurt Gänzl, 'Lydia Thompson: A Biography' (Routledge, 2002); Contemporary New York Times report on the 28 September 1868 opening; The Library of Nineteenth-Century Photography — Lydia Thompson as 'Harry Halcyon' in Middy Ashore (April 1860), with biographical record
Falsifiability: Playbills or press records contradicting the date, the venue, or the piece performed.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The QWERTY layout, 1868-1878</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact's paper trail is multiply and independently documented — patents, dated machines, the 1872 engraving, Remington's production records — though the precise rationale for each key's placement remains honestly uncertain.
Layer: real · Place: Milwaukee, Wisconsin (Sholes's workshop; machines shipped to Chicago)
Dates: 1868 → 1878 (confidence: high)

The QWERTY keyboard arrangement emerged between 1868 and 1878 through documented, gradual iteration: Sholes's November 1868 first TypeWriter (28 keys, alphabetical, piano-style) built with Glidden, Soule and Densmore in Milwaukee and shipped to Edward Payson Porter's Telegraph College in Chicago; the April 1870 38-key machine built with Matthias Schwalbach; the August 1872 Scientific American engraving showing a four-row keyboard with a second row beginning QWE.TY; the 1873 sale of manufacturing rights to E. Remington &amp; Sons; the Remington No. 1 (1874, commercially successful); and the Remington No. 2 (1878), which stabilized the modern QWERTY layout. The arrangement was shaped by feedback from early professional users — principally telegraph operators — not by a single design decision.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 79,265 to C. Latham Sholes, C. Glidden and S. W. Soule (23 June 1868); typewritten letters and subsequent Sholes patents (1870-1872)
Sources: Yasuoka, Koichi &amp; Yasuoka, Motoko (2011). 'On the Prehistory of QWERTY.' ZINBUN 42:161-174, Kyoto University; New Scientist, 'The truth about the QWERTY keyboard' (article 2200664) — 1872 Scientific American engraving, Remington No. 1 (1874), 100,000+ machines by 1890; US Patent 79,265 — 'Type-Writing Machine' (Sholes, Glidden &amp; Soule, 23 June 1868)
Falsifiability: Discovery of a Sholes-era document showing the full QWERTY arrangement predating 1868, or proof the 1868-1878 paper trail is misdated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.9065,43.0389,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The transcontinental railroad completed at Promontory Summit, 10 May 1869 — VERIFIED</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED: the joining at Promontory Summit on 10 May 1869 is one of the best-documented single days in 19th-century American history — multi-side contemporary attestation (both railroads' company records, the press, the telegraph log, the surviving ceremony artifacts including the golden spike itself). The timeline's first railroad-era record and the foil to the gauge folklore: the railways' own paperwork is how the real history is known.
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 1869-05-10 → 1869-05-10 (confidence: high)

On 10 May 1869 at Promontory Summit, Utah Territory, the Central Pacific and Union Pacific railroads were joined, completing the first continuous transcontinental railroad across the United States; the ceremony centered on the driving of the 'Last Spike' — a golden spike — with the moment signaled nationwide by telegraph.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary newspaper accounts, Union Pacific and Central Pacific company records, and the telegraph record of the ceremony — the 'DONE' signal sent over the wires at the moment of the driving.
Sources: Golden Spike National Historical Park, National Park Service — the 10 May 1869 joining, the ceremony, the Last Spike; Union Pacific company history — construction 1863–1869, Promontory Summit completion; Contemporary telegraph/press record of the ceremony — the 'DONE' signal
Falsifiability: A Union Pacific or Central Pacific document showing the railheads joined on a different date, or that the ceremony did not occur, would overturn this; the contemporary record is dense and univocal.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1869-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-112.5455,41.6197,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1869</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Chicago Water Tower survived the 1871 fire (CORROBORATED; the 'only survivor' telling corrected)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The tower's survival is multiply attested (municipal records, National Register, National Historic Landmark 1971, the building itself). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the record's real work is the correction: the 'only building to survive' telling dies on the Pumping Station across the street and the St. James bell tower — the careful sources phrase it as the only PUBLIC building in the burned zone, and even that carries the burned-zone boundary as its fence.
Layer: real · Place: 880 North Michigan Avenue, Chicago (Michigan Avenue and East Pearson Street)
Dates: 1869 → 1871-10-10 (confidence: high)

The Chicago Water Tower (designed 1869 by W. W. Boyington, built of Lemont limestone) survived the Great Chicago Fire of 8-10 October 1871 and stands today at 880 North Michigan Avenue — one of the few pre-fire structures still standing.

Earliest attestation: Municipal and architectural records, 1869-1871; National Register listing; National Historic Landmark designation 6 Dec 1971
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Chicago Water Tower': designed 1869 by William W. Boyington with the Chicago Avenue Pumping Station; drew water from Lake Michigan cribs; Lemont limestone; 'the only public building in the burned zone to survive'; the pumping station stopped working during the fire (roof caught fire, pumps stopped); books donated by Queen Victoria stored there after the fire; EBSCO Research Starters, 'Chicago Water Tower': completed 1869; National Historic Landmark 6 Dec 1971; survived the Great Chicago Fire of 1871; 'the only municipal building in Chicago to survive'; Beachwood Reporter, 'Confessions of a Tour Guide' (myth-busting): the 'only building that survived' telling is refuted by the Pumping Station across the street, the St. James Episcopal Cathedral bell tower (predates the tower by 12 years), and the 'haunted bullet hole' in Holy Name Cathedral
Falsifiability: The building stands; the survivor-census is the contested part — a complete, sourced inventory of every surviving pre-fire structure would settle the 'only' question either way</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1869-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6244,41.8971,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1869</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kansas cattle towns' body count</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply documented from independent directions: Dykstra's 1968 newspaper-and-record survey, the Smithsonian's 2018 corroborating account of Dodge's &lt;2/year figure, Trimble's town-by-town maxima, and Roth's independent re-analysis — which disagrees on rates-per-denominator but confirms the absolute counts.
Layer: real · Place: Dodge City, Kansas (with Abilene, Ellsworth, Wichita, Caldwell as comparison set)
Dates: 1870 → 1886 (confidence: high)

The five Kansas railhead cattle towns (Abilene, Ellsworth, Wichita, Caldwell, Dodge City) recorded 45 homicides across 15 town-years of peak cattle-trading seasons; Dodge City during its 1876-1886 cattle-trading period (with the open carry of sidearms banned) averaged fewer than two violent deaths per year; Dodge's most homicidal year had 5 killings, Deadwood's 4, Tombstone's 5.

Earliest attestation: The towns' own coroners' records, newspapers, and court records of the 1870s-1880s, analyzed by Robert R. Dykstra (The Cattle Towns, 1968)
Sources: Robert R. Dykstra, The Cattle Towns (Knopf, 1968; repr. Univ. of Nebraska Press, 1983); Smithsonian Magazine, 'How Dodge City Became a Symbol of Frontier Lawlessness' (2018): Dodge 1876-1886 averaged less than two violent deaths per year; none apparent Aug 1873-1875; open-carry ban from 1876; Harry Gryden's Police Gazette role; Randolph Roth, 'Homicide Rates in the American West' (Criminal Justice Research Center, Ohio State): Dodge City adults ~165 per 100,000/year 1876-1885 — the independent re-analysis; Marshall Trimble, 'Violence in Old West Towns,' True West magazine (Dodge 5, Deadwood 4, Tombstone 5 maxima; Tombstone gun ordinance)
Falsifiability: Discovery of an independent contemporary count (coroner's ledgers, cemetery records, court dockets) for the same towns and years showing substantially higher totals.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1870-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-100.017,37.7528,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1870</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The rotary opener that reached the kitchen (Lyman, 1870)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Patent date and design multiply attested (patent + museum collection history); the 'first widespread domestic opener' reading is the encyclopedia consensus but rests on secondary market history, hence CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Meriden, Connecticut, USA
Dates: 1870 → 1890 (confidence: high)

William W. Lyman of Meriden, Connecticut patented the first practical domestic rotary can opener on 12 July 1870 (US Patent 105,346) — pierce the lid's center, set the butterfly-screw wheel, cut around; the design Baumgarten &amp; Co. put into production from 1890 and that American kitchens actually held.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 105,346 (12 July 1870)
Sources: US Patent 105,346 (12 July 1870), William W. Lyman — circular rotation opener: pierce the lid's center, set the rotating blade with the butterfly screw, cut around the lid; Google Arts &amp; Culture / Tomato Museum, 'The History of the Can Opener' — Lyman's 12 July 1870 patent (US 105,346); put into production by the Baumgarten Company since 1890; J. Osterhoudt's 1866 key-wind system (US 58,554) as the competing opener family
Falsifiability: A dated domestic rotary opener in kitchen circulation before 1870, or proof the Baumgarten 1890 production date is wrong</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1870-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.807,41.5382,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1870</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Chicago Fire (8-10 October 1871)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by contemporary primary sources: the 1871 Board of Police and Fire Commissioners' investigation report, contemporary newspaper coverage, and the city's own records. The commissioner's report estimated 17,000-18,000 buildings destroyed and ~300 dead; drought context documented (no measurable rain since early July, 13 weeks).
Layer: real · Place: Chicago, Illinois (fire began at the O'Leary barn, DeKoven Street, Near West Side)
Dates: 1871-10-08 → 1871-10-10 (confidence: high)

The Great Chicago Fire burned 8-10 October 1871, destroying some 17,000-18,000 buildings across about 3.3 square miles, killing approximately 300 people, and leaving about 100,000 of the city's 300,000 residents homeless — one of the most devastating urban fires of the 19th century.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the Chicago Board of Police and Fire Commissioners' 1871 investigation report; Chicago newspaper coverage October 1871
Sources: Chicago Board of Police and Fire Commissioners, 1871 investigation report (commissioner's report: 17,000-18,000 buildings burned, ~300 dead; blamed poor building codes and insufficient fire-department funds); Encyclopedia of Chicago / Chicago History Museum — Great Chicago Fire entry (dates, toll, drought context: no rain since early July); Scott W. Berg, 'The Burning of the World: The Great Chicago Fire and the War for a City's Soul' (Pantheon) — the fire began in the barn of Patrick and Catherine O'Leary, DeKoven Street, evening of 8 Oct 1871; Chicago Tribune 150th-anniversary retrospective: 'the City Council officially exonerated Mrs. O'Leary and her cow in 1997, and the true cause may never be known'
Falsifiability: Contemporary official documentation, casualty lists, and property records exist in quantity; the event's core facts are not seriously contested</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1871-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6298,41.8781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1871</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Schliemann's excavation of Troy at Hisarlik (1871–1890)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The excavations happened and are multiply documented (his own reports, Ottoman records, the site's later excavation history); Calvert's priority is documented; Hisarlik's identification as Troy is the scholarly consensus — the ancient Greeks themselves placed Ilios there.
Layer: real · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: 1871 → 1890 (confidence: high)

Heinrich Schliemann excavated the mound of Hisarlik in the Troad (1871–1890), identified it as the Troy of Homer's Iliad, and the site is broadly accepted as ancient Troy (Ilios/Ilion).

Earliest attestation: Frank Calvert's identification of Hisarlik (1850s–1860s); Schliemann's published excavation reports from 1874; Ottoman excavation firmans (including the 1870 dig that Ottoman officials penalized with a delayed 1871 permit).
Sources: Smithsonian, 'In Search of Troy' — 'Schliemann converted Hisarlik to Troy' (R. Aslan); Troy II layer dated 2500–2300 BCE; city of Homer's epic thought to correspond to Troy VI–VIIa; National Geographic — Schliemann assumed Homer's Troy lay at the mound's bottom; Homer's Troy c. 1200 BCE corresponds to layer VIIa; 150 workers, rail lines, poor record-keeping; Susan H. Allen, 'Finding the Walls of Troy: Frank Calvert and Heinrich Schliemann at Hisarlik' (UC Press, 1999) — Calvert's priority in identifying Hisarlik; Archaeology.org, Interactive Map of Troy (Troy II, ca. 2550–2300 BCE) — ten+ distinct settlements at the site
Falsifiability: Epigraphic or stratigraphic proof that Hisarlik is not ancient Ilios/Ilion — the Roman-period city's own inscriptions and the 2019 British Museum exhibition's consensus make this very unlikely</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1871-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1871</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Reckendorfer v. Faber (92 U.S. 347, 1875) — the Supreme Court kills the eraser patent</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The Supreme Court opinion is a primary published source; its holding is carried independently across the Wikipedia, Patent Plaques, QuizzClub/NYT lineage, and the static.case.law circuit record. No source disputes the outcome.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (U.S. Supreme Court)
Dates: 1875 → 1875 (confidence: high)

In Reckendorfer v. Faber, 92 U.S. 347 (1875), the U.S. Supreme Court declared Lipman's pencil-with-eraser patent invalid. The ruling: the patent was 'merely the combination of two things, without a new use' — 'A combination, to be patentable, must produce a different force, effect, or result in the combined forces or processes from that given by their separate parts... A combination, therefore, which consists only of the application of a piece of rubber to one end of the same piece of wood which makes a lead pencil is not patentable.' Lipman kept the $100,000 he got from Reckendorfer; Reckendorfer was left with nothing.

Earliest attestation: Reckendorfer v. Faber, 92 U.S. 347 (1875), opinion text; Case No. 11,625 circuit record (May 20, 1874)
Sources: Reckendorfer v. Faber, 92 U.S. 347 (Supreme Court of the United States 1875) — holding: combination of two known things with no new use is not patentable; Case No. 11,625 (20 Fed. Cas. 389) — circuit record: Woodruff, Circuit Judge, May 20, 1874; Patent Plaques blog, 'Pencil Patent Dispute?': quotes the decision's aggregation test; patent 19,783 image; QuizzClub/NYT lineage: Reckendorfer paid $100,000, sued Faber, lost everything; Lipman kept the money
Falsifiability: The published opinion is the evidence; an intact enforcement record after 1875 would contradict the invalidation.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1875-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0364,38.8951,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1875</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Mr. Watson, come here' — the first intelligible telephone transmission, March 10, 1876</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The event is anchored in Bell's own laboratory journal — a contemporary primary record — and the date/place/persons are undisputed. Held below VERIFIED on the exact wording: the LoC itself notes the dispute between the journal's 'Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you!' and the Bell Company's later ceremonial 'Watson, come here, I want you!' (from the 1915 transcontinental call).
Layer: real · Place: 5 Exeter Place, Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1876-03-10 → 1876-03-10 (confidence: high)

On March 10, 1876, at 5 Exeter Place, Boston, Alexander Graham Bell transmitted the first intelligible sentence by telephone to his assistant Thomas Watson in the next room — 'Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you!' — as recorded in Bell's laboratory journal.

Earliest attestation: Bell's laboratory journal entry for March 10, 1876.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — 'it was March 10, 1876 that Bell declared to his assistant, "Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you!" over the lines of his working telephone, as he wrote in his laboratory journal. There is some dispute over the exact words used.'; HISTORY.com, 'First speech transmitted by telephone' (March 10, 1876) — the June 1875 precursor tests ('No intelligible words were transmitted, but sounds resembling human speech were heard').
Falsifiability: Bell's laboratory journal for March 10, 1876 is extant; a showing that the entry is forged or misdated would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>US Patent 174,465 — Bell's telephone patent, granted March 7, 1876</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Surviving primary legal document: the patent grant is a matter of public record with an exact date, number, and title; the application-to-grant interval is documented in the Patent Office daybook.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (U.S. Patent Office)
Dates: 1876-03-07 → 1876-03-07 (confidence: high)

United States Patent No. 174,465, titled 'Improvement in Telegraphy,' was granted to Alexander Graham Bell on March 7, 1876, covering an apparatus for 'transmitting vocal or other sounds telegraphically... causing electrical undulations.'

Earliest attestation: The patent document itself, issued March 7, 1876 (application filed Feb 14, 1876).
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — 'Bell was granted US Patent Number 174,465 for an "Improvement in Telegraphy" on March 7, 1876.'; HISTORY.com, 'First speech transmitted by telephone' — 'on March 7 he was awarded U.S. patent 174,465, which granted him ownership over both his telephone instruments and the concept of a telephone system.'
Falsifiability: The patent document is extant and publicly recorded; only a finding that Patent 174,465 was never issued (or was issued to someone else) would overturn this — not plausible.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bell's telephone offered to Western Union, fall 1876 (and declined)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Two lines: (1) Hubbard's own contemporary letters (16 Oct. and 16 Dec. 1876, to Gertrude Hubbard, Hubbard Papers) record the approach to Western Union — the event is attested in the year it happened; (2) the business history (W. Bernard Carlson, 'Entrepreneurship in the Early Development of the Telephone,' Business and Economic History v23 n2, pp. 161-192) reconstructs the fall-1876 offer and the turndown with Orton's documented business logic. Held below VERIFIED: the famous $100,000 figure does NOT come from the 1876 letters — it comes from Thomas A. Watson's 1926 memoir (p. 107), a fifty-year-late recollection (see also Bruce 1973, p. 229). The amount is fenced at CLAIMED inside this CORROBORATED record.
Layer: real · Place: New York City (Western Union headquarters)
Dates: 1876-10-01 → 1876-12-31 (confidence: high)

In the fall of 1876, Gardiner Hubbard, Alexander Graham Bell's chief backer, brought Bell's inventions to the Western Union Telegraph Company and its president William Orton, and Western Union declined to buy them.

Earliest attestation: Hubbard's own letters to his wife, 16 Oct. and 16 Dec. 1876 (Hubbard Papers), mention taking Bell's 'inventions' to Western Union
Sources: W. Bernard Carlson, 'Entrepreneurship in the Early Development of the Telephone: How Did William Orton and Gardiner Hubbard Conceptualize this New Technology?' Business and Economic History, v23 n2, pp. 161-192 — fall-1876 offer; Orton's turndown; the two Hubbard-to-Gertrude letters (16 Oct., 16 Dec. 1876, Hubbard Papers) as the contemporary anchor; the $100,000 figure traced to Watson 1926, p. 107; Bruce 1973, p. 229; Thomas A. Watson, Exploring Life (1926), p. 107 — the $100,000 figure's earliest known source, a half-century-late recollection; eh.net, 'History of the U.S. Telegraph Industry' — 'Bell offered Western Union the patent for the telephone for $100,000, but the company declined to purchase it' (compiled secondary)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1876 Western Union internal records showing no approach by Hubbard, or showing the approach accepted, would overturn this grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dewey's real achievement: the standardized, commercialized card (1876)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The commercial record converges: the 1876 ALA meeting agreed on standard card stock, Dewey ran the supply department, the Bureau sold cards, cabinets, and the 'approved library hand' system. Held below VERIFIED — the 'standard size agreed at the first ALA meeting' detail rides secondary sources, and the corporate founding date is honestly disputed (1876 vs 1879 vs 1888).
Layer: real · Place: Boston, Massachusetts — the Library Bureau's home
Dates: 1876 → 1905 (confidence: medium)

Melvil Dewey's 1876 work and the Library Bureau made the index card the American library industry's agreed unit — standardization and commercialization, not invention.

Earliest attestation: Proceedings of the 1876 ALA organizing conference (Frederick Leypoldt, Library Journal vol. 1) — the Cooperation Committee on Library Supplies; Dewey's 1876 pamphlet.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Library Bureau' — 1876 ALA Philadelphia meeting: Cooperation Committee on Library Supplies; standard-size card stock agreed; Dewey's supply department; the 1888 separate 'Library Bureau' entity; 1927 Remington Rand absorption; liswiki.org, 'History of the card catalog' — Sharp (1935): the modern card catalog 'began to make its appearance in British and American libraries round about 1876, in which year the well-known firm of Library Bureau was established, with Melvil Dewey at its head'
Falsifiability: A documented 1870s American library standard that predates and contradicts the ALA/Bureau card-stock agreement would change the reading.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Valentine's Day race — Bell's application and Gray's caveat, February 14, 1876</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in the patent-office record and the compiled histories: the same-day filings, the entry numbers (5th vs 39th), and the shared liquid transmitter are documented. Held below VERIFIED on the contested sequencing layer — the popular 'Bell arrived first' story is itself disputed (Evenson) — and on the unproven Wilber bribery allegation.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (U.S. Patent Office)
Dates: 1876-02-14 → 1876-02-14 (confidence: high)

On February 14, 1876, Bell's lawyer filed Bell's telephone patent application and Elisha Gray's lawyer filed Gray's telephone caveat on the same day at the U.S. Patent Office — Bell's the fifth entry of the day, Gray's the thirty-ninth; both documents incorporated a liquid transmitter.

Earliest attestation: The Patent Office cash-receipts blotter and daybook entries of February 14, 1876 (Bell's fee entered before noon, Gray's that afternoon).
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — 'the application for this patent was submitted on February 14, 1876. That same day, but just a few hours later, Elisha Gray applied for a caveat... Bell's was the fifth entry of that day, while Gray's was 39th.'; Wikipedia, 'Elisha Gray and Alexander Bell telephone controversy' — Gardiner Hubbard's lawyer filed Bell's application Feb 14, 1876; Gray's lawyer filed the caveat the same day; the fee-blotter sequencing (Bell before noon, Gray that afternoon; caveat not to the examiner until the next day); Evenson's finding that the popular 'Bell arrived an hour or two earlier' version did not happen.
Falsifiability: The Patent Office daybook for Feb 14, 1876 is the anchor; a showing that Gray's caveat was filed on a different date would overturn the same-day claim (the entries are numbered and dated).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The real doctor behind Holmes: Doyle's 1892 letter to Joseph Bell</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — An author's own dated letter naming his model — a primary document held in an institutional archive, corroborated the same year by Doyle's own published interview describing the ward observations first-hand. Two independent documentary lines; held below VERIFIED because 'modeled on' leaves room for the composite reality (Dupin, Littlejohn, Doyle himself) that the letter's own phrasing admits.
Layer: real · Place: Edinburgh Royal Infirmary / Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh (letter archive)
Dates: 1877 → 1892 (confidence: high)

Sherlock Holmes's analytical method was modeled on the real Edinburgh surgeon Joseph Bell — Arthur Conan Doyle himself named Bell as Holmes's model in writing.

Earliest attestation: The 4 May 1892 letter, one of a set of seven Doyle letters held by the Library of the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh: 'It is most certainly to you that I owe Sherlock Holmes, and though in the stories I have the advantage of being able to place him in all sorts of dramatic positions I do not think that his analytical work is in the least an exaggeration of some effects which I have seen you produce in the outpatient ward.' Corroborating contemporaneous attestation: Doyle's August 1892 Strand Magazine interview (to Harry How): 'I was clerk in Mr. Bell's ward... When everything was ready, I would show them in to Mr. Bell... His intuitive powers were simply marvellous.'
Sources: Arthur Conan Doyle to Joseph Bell, 4 May 1892 — one of seven Doyle letters held by the Library of the Royal College of Surgeons of Edinburgh; The Strand Magazine, August 1892 — Doyle interviewed at his home by Harry How: the ward-clerk account; Arthur Conan Doyle Encyclopedia, 'Joseph Bell' — letter and interview documentation
Falsifiability: Archival evidence (e.g., an earlier Doyle letter or diary) crediting a different sole model, or documentation that the 1892 letter is misdated or inauthentic.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1877-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.19,55.95,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1877</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison's tin-foil phonograph, December 1877 — the first machine that talked back</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The build chain, the contemporary press account, and the patent record converge on the same device: Kruesi's 1–6 December 1877 build from Edison's 29 November sketch; the 22 December 1877 Scientific American demonstration with verbatim quotation; the 24 December 1877 patent filing and the 19 February 1878 grant (U.S. Patent 200,521). The Edison Speaking Phonograph Company was capitalized 24 January 1878. The reproduction half of the invention — playing sound back — is the superlative that survives Scott's 1860 recording-only priority.
Layer: real · Place: Menlo Park, New Jersey (Edison's laboratory; Scientific American demonstration at the magazine's New York offices)
Dates: 1877 → 1878 (confidence: high)

In December 1877 Thomas Edison's shop built the first practical machine capable of BOTH recording and reproducing sound: the tin-foil cylinder phonograph — Edison speaking 'Mary had a little lamb' into the mouthpiece and the machine playing his words back — demonstrated at Scientific American's offices (reported 22 December 1877), patent filed 24 December 1877, granted 19 February 1878 as U.S. Patent 200,521.

Earliest attestation: The Scientific American account of 22 December 1877 ('Mr. Thomas A. Edison recently came into this office, placed a little machine on our desk, turned a crank, and the machine inquired as to our health...')
Sources: Library of Congress — 'History of the Cylinder Phonograph' (Inventing Entertainment): the August-12-vs-December dating argument; Charles Batchelor's diary (constructed 4 December, finished two days later); the 22 December 1877 Scientific American report; patent filed 24 December 1877, issued 19 February 1878; Charles Cros's April 18, 1877 paper as theory-only; Wikipedia — 'Phonautograph': 'Edison's 1877 phonograph was the first machine capable of both recording and replaying sound' — the surviving superlative; Schaeffer's History of Recording: Kruesi built the first practical machine 1–6 December from Edison's 29 November sketch (not 'Aug. 12,' misdated by Edison in 1917); patent 200,521 granted 19 February 1878 for cylinders wrapped with tin foil, 2–3 minute capacity
Falsifiability: Proof that a working record-and-playback machine predated December 1877 (e.g. a Cros working model) would overturn the 'first' superlative — none exists in the documentary record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1877-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.3243,40.5624,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1877</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The telephone war of 1877-78 and the 1879 settlement (the foil)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across secondary histories: Western Union's 1877-78 entry into telephony with Gray and Edison as its inventors, the litigation campaign against Bell, and the 1879 settlement under which Western Union exited the telephone business while Bell acquired the Edison carbon-microphone patents from the Western Union side — the component that finally made the telephone practical over distance (Bell's own voice-powered transmitter required shouting at short range). Held below VERIFIED: this record rests on compiled secondary accounts in this pass; the litigation's primary filings are the path to VERIFIED and are docketed for a future records pass.
Layer: real · Place: New York / the Bell vs Western Union litigation
Dates: 1877-01-01 → 1879-12-31 (confidence: high)

After declining Bell in 1876, Western Union built its own telephone operation — hiring Elisha Gray and Thomas Edison to develop rival telephony — fought Bell for the telephone business in 1877-78, and settled in 1879 by withdrawing from telephony, with Bell acquiring the Edison carbon-microphone technology from Western Union's camp. The rejection had a documented price.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1877-78 telephone litigation and company records (carried via compiled secondary sources for this record)
Sources: Standard telephone-history account of the 1877-78 Western Union telephone operation (Gray, Edison), the Bell litigation, and the 1879 settlement (WU exits telephony; Bell acquires the Edison carbon-microphone patents) — compiled secondary; primary litigation filings docketed for a future pass; rismedia 'You've Come a Long Way Baby' (compiled secondary): 'In 1879 the Bell Company acquired Edison's patents for the carbon microphone from Western Union'
Falsifiability: Evidence that Western Union never entered the telephone business after 1876, or never settled with Bell, would overturn this grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1877-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1877</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison's Menlo Park system — the real achievement the legend mislabels</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multi-source contemporary attestation of both the demonstration and the commercial system: Menlo Park Dec 1879 coverage, the Edison Electric Illuminating Company records, Pearl Street Station's 1882 operation as the first commercial central power station for incandescent lighting. The high-resistance filament plus parallel-circuit distribution is what made power from a central source economically viable — the engineering insight both Swan and Edison histories agree was Edison's decisive contribution. This is the foil that survives the debunk of the solo-inventor myth: the legend is false about priority, true about the system.
Layer: real · Place: Menlo Park, New Jersey (demonstration, Dec 1879); Pearl Street, New York (station, Sep 1882)
Dates: 1879 → 1882 (confidence: high)

Edison's real achievement was the first practical, complete incandescent electric lighting system — long-life lamp plus generators, distribution, and wiring — demonstrated at Menlo Park in December 1879 and commercialized with Pearl Street Station, New York, in September 1882.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary newspaper coverage of the Menlo Park demonstrations, Dec 1879; Edison Electric Illuminating Company of New York records, 1880–82.
Sources: scribd.com document 631252062 — History of the Incandescent Light Bulb; yoo.rs summary (Volta → Davy → Swan → Edison chain); legaleraonline.com — 'Whose Electric Bulb Is It Anyway?'; famousinventors.info — Joseph Swan biography
Falsifiability: Evidence that a city-scale incandescent lighting system with central distribution predated Pearl Street (Sep 1882) — none is known; arc-lighting systems (Brush) and isolated Swan installations are the documented alternatives, and neither is a distributed incandescent system.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1879-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.3318,40.5044,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1879</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The French Panama effort, 1882–1889</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent aggregators converge on the dates, the spending, the excavation fraction, and the death-toll range; exact figures vary between sources so it stops short of VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Isthmus of Panama (Colon → Panama City)
Dates: 1879 → 1889 (confidence: high)

Ferdinand de Lesseps's Compagnie Universelle du Canal Interocéanique de Panama (founded 1879; construction 1882–May 1889) attempted the Panama Canal before the United States: a sea-level design stubbornly kept until the October 1887 conversion to a lock canal; by the 1889 bankruptcy the company had spent ~Fr 1.18 billion (≈ $235 million), excavated ~50–53 million cubic meters — about two-fifths of the final total — and lost roughly 20,000–22,000 workers, mostly to yellow fever and malaria.

Earliest attestation: Compagnie Universelle corporate records 1879–1889; contemporary French and American press coverage of the 1889 bankruptcy and the 1892–93 Panama Scandal trials.
Sources: NASA engineering analysis of the Compagnie Universelle effort (53M m³ of 75M m³ cost-baseline excavated; Fr 1.18B spent; ~16,500 lives); History of the Panama Canal survey (1888 workforce ~20,000, nine-tenths Afro-Caribbean; ~22,000 dead 1881–1889; ~two-fifths complete; $234,795,000 spent); Maritime Executive centenary survey (French effort 1881–1889, ~22,000 dead in the French period); New Civil Engineer archive: the Panama canal (sea-level design kept until October 1887 lock conversion; Culebra landslides; FFr1bn spent)
Falsifiability: Compagnie ledger evidence showing materially different expenditure/excavation, or contemporary records placing the lock-canal conversion at a different date</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1879-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.68,9.15,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1879</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Selden patent monopoly (1903–1911) and Ford's courtroom win</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED: the paper trail is complete and multiply attested (patent record 549,160; ALAM founding 1903; the 1903 suit against Ford; Judge Hough's 1909 decision; the 2d Cir. January 1911 opinion quoting the patent's 'inclosed crank chamber' as the Brayton-type improvement), but key details rest on secondary summaries (BPM Legal, EDN, Wikipedia) rather than the reporter's text read directly — the full 196 F. 615 opinion was cited, not read. Selden never built a commercial car; his own trial expert testified no working automobile used his engine design — the 'monopoly' was a licensing regime over a paper right, and the record grades it as exactly that.
Layer: real · Place: Rochester, New York (Selden's residence and filing origin)
Dates: 1879-05-08 → 1911-01-09 (confidence: high)

The Selden patent (US 549,160, filed 8 May 1879, issued 5 November 1895) gave its holders — the Electric Vehicle Company and the Association of Licensed Automobile Manufacturers (ALAM, founded March 1903) — a working monopoly claim over gasoline automobiles in the United States from 1903 until the 9 January 1911 Second Circuit decision in Ford's favor, which held the patent valid only for Brayton-type two-stroke engines that the industry no longer used.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 549,160, 'Road Engine,' issued to George Selden, 5 November 1895
Sources: US Patent 549,160 — inventor George Selden, Rochester, New York; filed May 8, 1879; issued November 5, 1895 (BPM Legal patent summary); Wikipedia — 'Association of Licensed Automobile Manufacturers': ALAM formed March 1903; campaign threatening buyers of unlicensed cars; Judge Charles Merrill Hough upheld the patent 15 September 1909; court of appeals ruled for Ford 9 January 1911; ALAM declined to contest; EDN (originally posted 5 November 2012, edited 4 November 2019) — Selden sold the patent to the Electric Vehicle Company in 1899; ALAM brass 'Selden Patent' plates on licensed cars; eight-year legal fight; appeal won on the Brayton-vs-Otto engine argument; Jalopnik (14 October 2026) — 'Henry Ford Killed The Patent That Claimed To Own The Automobile': Winton's settlement, the EVC's enforcement, the 1911 verdict (patent valid only for Brayton two-stroke engines), patent expired two years later
Falsifiability: A court record showing the Selden patent enforced against a four-stroke Otto-engine automobile AFTER January 1911 would reopen the monopoly's end date; a Selden-built working road vehicle predating Benz would change the story's meaning but not its American-legal facts</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1879-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.6088,43.1566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1879</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1883 Edison &amp; Swan merger — the lawsuit that became a company</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested: the Manchester Guardian's 3 October 1883 report (via the MIT Press academic history), the 1979 Science centennial reassessment confirming the out-of-court settlement, and the company's own existence. The merger is the documentary proof that Swan's dated prior-research evidence could not be beaten in court — the suit was dropped precisely because it was tried in the record room and lost there.
Layer: real · Place: London, England — the joint company's formation; the British suit was dropped, not decided
Dates: 1883 → 1883 (confidence: high)

After the British Edison Company sued Swan's company for incandescent-lamp patent infringement, the British suit was dropped in favor of a negotiated merger — creating the Edison and Swan United Electric Light Company in 1883 to exploit the two firms' complementary patents.

Earliest attestation: Manchester Guardian, 3 October 1883 (via Arapostathis &amp; Gooday, Patently Contestable, MIT Press 2013).
Sources: Arapostathis &amp; Gooday, Patently Contestable (MIT Press 2013), via University of Reading working paper (centaur.reading.ac.uk/79610/1/Fundamental patents.pdf); Science 1979, vol 204 no 4388 (centennial reassessment, via iarchive.299792458.su)
Falsifiability: Contemporary company records showing no 1883 joint company, or a decisive courtroom verdict against Edison in Britain before October 1883.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1883-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1883</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The day of two noons — US standard time, 18 November 1883</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independently attested in the convention record, contemporary press coverage, and the Library of Congress legal-history account — the event (noon, 18 November 1883, Allen's plan, the telegraph synchronization) is documented from multiple sides; minor details vary between secondary accounts.
Layer: real · Place: Chicago (General Time Convention, Grand Pacific Hotel, 11 Oct 1883)
Dates: 1883-11-18 → 1883-11-18 (confidence: high)

The American railroads adopted standard time at noon on 18 November 1883 — William F. Allen's five-zone plan (Intercolonial, Eastern, Central, Mountain, Pacific) approved by the General Time Convention in Chicago on 11 October 1883 — a full year before the International Meridian Conference met; the day was called 'the day of two noons' because cities reset their clocks at the new standard noon after having already observed local noon.

Earliest attestation: General Time Convention records (11 October 1883, Grand Pacific Hotel, Chicago); the contemporary newspaper instructions for resetting clocks; US Naval Observatory and Allegheny Observatory telegraph signals for synchronization
Sources: Library of Congress, In Custodia Legis: General Time Convention gathered at the Grand Pacific Hotel, Chicago, 11 October 1883; Allen's plan for five zones on the 75th/90th/105th/120th meridians; railroad clocks set to the new standard at noon on 18 November 1883; the US Naval Observatory and Allegheny Observatory telegraph signals; 'The Day of Two Noons' — noon struck twice in several cities; Wikipedia, 'Railway time': standard railway time introduced at noon 18 November 1883; Indianapolis Sentinel protest 17 November 1883; near-unanimous railroad support with Yale/Harvard observatory backing; federal law incorporation only in 1918
Falsifiability: Convention minutes or contemporary press records showing a different date or plan would move it; held at CORROBORATED because minor details (e.g., the exact 85% adoption figure, the 'day of two noons' coinage) rest on secondary synthesis, not primary documents.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1883-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6298,41.8781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1883</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Home Insurance Building's hybrid frame: iron-dominant, steel-incorporating, bolted</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge: the Chicago landmark report (official document) gives the precise 'incorporate structural steel' formulation; the structural-historical scholarship documents the iron-dominant hybrid frame and bolted connections; the construction-halt episode is independently attested (History.com, Wikipedia). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED because the building is gone and the record rests on the scholarship's convergence, not on the artifact itself.
Layer: real · Place: LaSalle &amp; Adams Streets, Chicago, Illinois (demolished 1931)
Dates: 1884 → 1891 (confidence: high)

The Home Insurance Building's frame was a metal skeleton incorporating structural steel alongside wrought and cast iron; the frame carried the floors and helped carry the exterior walls — but masonry walls still carried some of the weight, and the metal frame was bolted rather than riveted.

Earliest attestation: The building's documented design and construction: Jenney's metal skeleton of vertical columns and horizontal beams (1884–1885); city officials halted construction to investigate its safety — the inspection episode corroborates the novelty of the frame.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Early skyscrapers' — 'Jenney's design was unusual in that it incorporated structural steel into the building's internal metal frame alongside the traditional wrought iron... some of the weight was still carried by masonry walls, and the metal frame was bolted, rather than riveted, together'; City of Chicago landmark report — 'the first skyscraper to incorporate structural steel in its skeleton frame'; 'the first in which the exterior became merely a curtain wall supported by the internal fireproofed framing'; History.com — city officials 'halted construction for a period of time so that they could ensure its safety' (1884–1885); Wikipedia, 'Early skyscrapers' — 'The 1890 Rand McNally Building became the first entirely self-supporting, steel-framed skyscraper' (the contrast that bounds this record)
Falsifiability: Structural drawings or demolition records showing a full self-supporting riveted steel skeleton in 1885 (like the Rand McNally's 1890 frame) would overturn the measured version; the documented hybrid frame and bolted connections would have to be disproven.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1884-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.632,41.8796,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1884</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The International Meridian Conference of 1884</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The event and its votes are documented in the conference's own published proceedings with full country-by-country tallies; the recommendation (not law) framing is in the resolution texts themselves.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (Meridian Conference)
Dates: 1884-10 → 1884-10 (confidence: high)

The International Meridian Conference assembled at Washington, D.C. in October 1884 — 41 delegates from 25 nations (26 invited, Denmark absent) — and on 13 October 1884 adopted Resolution II: the meridian through the centre of the transit instrument at the Greenwich Observatory would be the initial meridian for longitude, by 22 votes to 1 (San Domingo against), with Brazil and France abstaining. The conference also recommended a universal day — a mean solar day beginning at mean midnight at Greenwich — expressly for purposes 'for which it may be found convenient,' with no interference to local or standard time, and all resolutions were framed as proposals to the governments represented, not binding acts.

Earliest attestation: The conference's own published proceedings: 'International Conference Held at Washington for the Purpose of Fixing a Prime Meridian and a Universal Day, October, 1884' (including the full vote tallies: ayes 22, noes 1, abstaining 2)
Sources: International Conference Held at Washington for the Purpose of Fixing a Prime Meridian and a Universal Day, October, 1884 — published proceedings (Project Gutenberg eBook 17759): resolutions I–VII with full vote tallies; Resolution II ayes 22 (Austria-Hungary, Mexico, Chili, Netherlands, Colombia, Paraguay, Costa Rica, Russia, Germany, Salvador, Great Britain, Spain, Guatemala, Sweden, Hawaii, Switzerland, Italy, Turkey, Japan, United States, Liberia, Venezuela), noes 1 (San Domingo), abstaining 2 (Brazil, France); Wikipedia, 'International Meridian Conference' — 41 delegates, 26 invited nations (Denmark absent), resolutions adopted 22 October 1884, voting took place 13 October; Sandford Fleming's shipping-tonnage figures (72% of world shipping already using Greenwich vs 8% Paris) as presented to the conference — the practical case for Greenwich
Falsifiability: The published proceedings themselves would have to be forgeries; the vote tallies are multiply cross-attested in the official record and in secondary histories of the conference.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1884-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1884</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Waterman's 1884 three-fissure feed</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 'three fissure feed' scope of the 1884 patent is corroborated by Wikipedia's Lewis Waterman entry and the Holland-takeover narrative; held below VERIFIED because this pass relied on secondary transmission of the patent's claims rather than the specification itself.
Layer: real · Place: New York City, USA — where Waterman assembled and sold the pens (behind a cigar shop, with a five-year guarantee)
Dates: 1884 → 1884 (confidence: high)

Lewis Waterman's first pen-related patent, granted 12 February 1884, was for the 'three fissure feed' — a capillary-action ink feed that let air into the reservoir through narrow channels so ink flowed steadily without flooding; Waterman took over Frank Holland's abandoned 1883 pen company and fitted its pens with this feed, which is what made the Waterman pen the first reliably non-leaking, commercially dominant fountain pen.

Earliest attestation: US patent granted 12 February 1884 (the 'three fissure feed'); Ideal Pen Company founded 1884
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Lewis Waterman' — 'It was for this "three fissure feed" which his first pen-related patent was granted in 1884'; the Frank Holland 1883 takeover ('fitting the pens with a simplified feed of his own design'); National Inventors Hall of Fame 2006; Antique Trader, 'Putting pen to paper with distinction' — 'Waterman was granted a patent on February 12, 1884 for his "Fountain Pen"... Waterman's design limited airflow into the ink reservoir through the use of a small air channel'; Ideal Pen Company 1884, renamed L.E. Waterman 1888; spoon feed 1899 and the 1900 Exposition Universelle gold medal; ksgills.com, 'Lewis Waterman – Inventor of Fountain Pen' — names the lost-contract telling explicitly as 'just that — a legend'; describes the capillary-tube experiments with his brother Elijah
Falsifiability: The patent specification itself (showing a feed, not a pen invention) is the evidence; the grade only falls if the 1884 patent proves to be for a complete new pen rather than the feed mechanism</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1884-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1884</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'first skyscraper' was the Home Insurance Building (1885) — the definition-dependent title</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The building is real and its frame was an advance — but 'first skyscraper' is a title awarded by definition, and the academy has never agreed on the definition. Academic disagreement is documented (Wikipedia 'Early skyscrapers': 'there is academic disagreement over which building should be considered the first skyscraper'); the honor rotates by criterion (iron frame → 1797, curtain wall → 1864, steel → 1885, fully self-supporting steel → 1890). A contested title is CLAIMED, not CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: LaSalle &amp; Adams Streets, Chicago, Illinois (demolished 1931)
Dates: 1885 → 1931 (confidence: high)

The Home Insurance Building in Chicago (designed by William Le Baron Jenney, completed 1885) was the world's first skyscraper — the unqualified textbook form of the claim.

Earliest attestation: The building itself: designed by William Le Baron Jenney 1884, completed 1885, NE corner of LaSalle and Adams Streets, Chicago. The 'first skyscraper' title is a later historiographical attribution, not a contemporary claim.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Early skyscrapers' — 'There is academic disagreement over which building should be considered the first skyscraper... various candidates exist depending on the criteria applied'; Home Insurance Building 'most often labeled the first skyscraper because of its innovative use of structural steel in a metal frame design'; Wikipedia, 'Home Insurance Building' — 10 stories, 138 ft (1885), 180 ft after 1891 addition; designed by William Le Baron Jenney; demolished 1931; construction begun May 1, 1884; City of Chicago landmark report (chicago.gov) — Jenney 'the first architect to use skeleton-frame construction'; Home Insurance 'the first skyscraper to incorporate structural steel in its skeleton frame'; History.com, 'Home Insurance Building' — the steel-frame design, the halted construction, the 138-ft height, and the building as the standard for skyscraper innovations
Falsifiability: A scholarly consensus definition of 'skyscraper' under which exactly one building satisfies all criteria, and under which no earlier candidate (Ditherington, Oriel Chambers, Equitable Life, Produce Exchange) qualifies, would resolve the dispute — but no such definition commands consensus, which is precisely why the grade is CLAIMED.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1885-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.632,41.8796,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1885</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The hyper-radial order: the largest lighthouse lens ever put into use (Makapu'u Point, 1909)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent sources converge: the hyper-radial article (1330 mm; 'largest ever put into use'; ~2 dozen landfall beacons incl. Makapu'u, Cabo de Sao Vicente, Manora Point, Bishop Rock, Cabo de Santa Marta, Cape Race), the Makapu'u Point record ('only U.S. light with such a lens'; 'largest lens in U.S. service' since 1909; 12 ft; 1000+ prisms; 1893 Chicago fair), DVIDS/USCG ('world's largest lighthouse lens'; 19 nm range), and Foghorn Publishing ('largest lens in use in the United States, about 12 feet high'). Held below VERIFIED on the source-dependent production count.
Layer: real · Place: Makapu'u Point Light, Oahu, Hawaii, USA (the flagship hyper-radial)
Dates: 1885 → 1909 (confidence: medium)

The hyper-radial order of Fresnel lens (focal length 1330 mm) is the largest lighthouse-lens size ever put into use: made from 1885 (Barbier test lens for the South Foreland trials; Chance Brothers' first in 1887), installed in about two dozen major landfall beacons worldwide; the Makapu'u Point hyper-radial (lit 1909) is the only such lens in U.S. service and has been the largest lens in U.S. service since 1909 — 12 feet tall, over a thousand prisms, displayed at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair before installation.

Earliest attestation: South Foreland illumination trials, 1885 (Barbier test lens); Chance Brothers production from 1887
Sources: Hyperradiant Fresnel lens — 1330 mm focal length; idea Thomas Stevenson 1869; proposed Wigham 1872 / Stevenson 1885; made 1885 by F. Barbier (Paris) as test lens for the South Foreland trials; Chance Brothers first 1887; 'the largest ever put into use'; installed in about two dozen major landfall beacons (Makapu'u Point, Cabo de Sao Vicente, Manora Point Karachi, Bishop Rock, Cabo de Santa Marta, Cape Race); rendered obsolete by 1920s high-intensity lamps; Makapu'u Point Light — displayed at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair as 'the largest size ever made, off the chart of sixth through first Order'; 'only U.S. light with such a lens'; 'ever since its activation in 1909, it has had the distinction of having the largest lens in U.S. service'; 12 feet tall, over a thousand prisms; DVIDS/U.S. Coast Guard — 'the world's largest lighthouse lens'; 12-foot-tall, 8-foot-wide hyper-radiant; 19 nautical mile beam; 'the largest lens that I have ever seen' (CPO Ernest W. Rucker, ANT Honolulu); Foghorn Publishing Lighthouse Explorer — 'This lighthouse has the largest lens in use in the United States at about 12 feet high'; established 1909; optics 1909 hyperradial
Falsifiability: Discovery of a larger lighthouse-lens order ever put into use, or primary Lighthouse Board records showing Makapu'u's lens is not hyper-radial</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1885-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-157.65,21.3094,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1885</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Smith's conductor's punch (1885)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1885 conductor's punch is reported by a technology-history source citing USPTO material; held below VERIFIED because the patent document itself was not inspected this pass and the transmission is secondary.
Layer: real · Place: Massachusetts, United States (Smith's conductor's punch)
Dates: 1885 → 1885 (confidence: medium)

In 1885, Benjamin Smith of Massachusetts invented the 'conductor's punch' — a single-hole punch for train conductors, using effort-relieving levers, with a collector at the bottom for gathering the cut confetti.

Earliest attestation: The device's 1885 invention is reported in technology histories citing USPTO material; the confetti collector's stated purpose was 'to prevent passengers on railroad-trains and other public conveyances from fraudulently reinserting in their passage-tickets the cuttings removed.'
Sources: Inverse, 'Hole Punch History: Why Google Marked the 131st Anniversary With This Doodle' — Smith's 1885 Massachusetts conductor's punch; single hole; collector for the cuttings (USPTO)
Falsifiability: Inspection of the underlying patent records contradicting the 1885 date or the collector feature</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1885-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1885</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Benz's DRP 37435 (1886) — the automobile's birth certificate</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary sources converge: the patent document itself (held in the Mercedes-Benz Public Archive, text beginning 'The present construction is intended mainly for the operation of light carts and small boats'), the 2011 UNESCO Memory of the World register entry for the Benz patent bundle, contemporary newspaper reports of the July 1886 Mannheim public run, and the surviving Patent-Motorwagen hardware in museum collections (Mercedes-Benz Museum, Deutsches Museum).
Layer: real · Place: Berlin, Germany (Imperial Patent Office, filing)
Dates: 1886-01-29 → 1886-11-02 (confidence: high)

Carl Benz's DRP 37435 — 'vehicle with gas engine operation', filed 29 January 1886 at the Imperial Patent Office in Berlin — is the documentary birth certificate of the automobile: a purpose-built gasoline automobile (the Benz Patent-Motorwagen), distinct from a motorized carriage.

Earliest attestation: Deutsches Reichspatent DRP 37435, filed by Carl Benz, 29 January 1886 (Imperial Patent Office, Berlin)
Sources: Benz &amp; Cie. Patentschrift DRP 37435 — official Mercedes-Benz Public Archive record; patent text and filing record (via InventionsArchive verification); UNESCO Memory of the World register entry — 'Patent DRP 37435 "Vehicle with gas engine operation" submitted by Carl Benz, 1886' (inscribed from 2011); Contemporary press: first public outing of the Benz Patent Motor Car, 3 July 1886, Ringstrasse, Mannheim (via New Atlas 125th-anniversary survey of the record); Deutsches Museum digital catalogue — Benz-Patent-Motorwagen Nr. 1: museum-held object evidence of the machine itself
Falsifiability: Proof the patent application is forged or misdated, or documentary evidence of a purpose-built production gasoline automobile before January 1886</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1886-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1886</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Soennecken's Papierlocher (1886)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The filing and its date are multiply attested in secondary reporting and arithmetic-confirmed by the 2017 Doodle anniversary; held below VERIFIED because the German patent document itself was not inspected this pass.
Layer: real · Place: Bonn, Germany (Soennecken's company and city)
Dates: 1886 → 1886 (confidence: high)

On 14 November 1886, Friedrich Soennecken filed a German patent for the Papierlocher für Sammelmappen — a two-hole paper punch for binders — the device Google celebrated with its 131st-anniversary Doodle on 14 November 2017.

Earliest attestation: The German patent filing, 14 November 1886 ('Papierlocher für Sammelmappen'); Google's 131st-anniversary Doodle, 14 November 2017.
Sources: GKToday, 'What is the history of hole punch?' — two-hole punch introduced by Friedrich Soennecken on 14 November 1886, 'Papierlocher für Sammelmappen'; design little changed since; ANI via thefreelibrary, 'Google celebrates hole puncher on 131st anniversary' (Nov 2017) — 131st anniversary Doodle, 14 November 2017; Soennecken founded his company in 1875; Inverse, 'Hole Punch History: The Brilliantly Simple Mechanism' — Soennecken filed the patent; coupled the punch with the ring binder
Falsifiability: The German patent register showing a different filing date or a different first filer</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1886-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.0982,50.7374,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1886</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Statue of Liberty as a Working Lighthouse (1886–1902)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent documentary lines converge — USCG Historian's Office (established 1886, deactivated 1902, first lit 22 November 1886, the Cleveland order, the 15-arc/14-incandescent lamp inventory, James J. Wood's apparatus, the 'useless' navigation verdict, the 1 March 1902 transfer), Smithsonian (nine arc lights cut into the torch flame), NYPR/Staten Island depot records (keeper Albert E. Littlefield from December 1886, Joseph Funk's Fresnel-lens retrofit attempt, the white-paint debate); held below VERIFIED on the 'first electric lighthouse in the U.S.' superlative, which rides on the NYPR telling rather than a Board record.
Layer: real · Place: Bedloe's (now Liberty) Island, New York Harbor
Dates: 1886 → 1902 (confidence: high)

The Statue of Liberty was a working federal lighthouse from 1886 to 1902: at its dedication President Grover Cleveland placed the statue and pedestal under the U.S. Lighthouse Board, which lit the torch with electric arc lamps (first lit 22 November 1886) and tended it with a dedicated lighthouse keeper, Albert E. Littlefield — making it the first electric lighthouse in the United States. The light was judged ineffective for navigation and was extinguished when care passed to the War Department on 1 March 1902.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Lighthouse Board records and the Coast Guard Historian's Office file: Cleveland's 1886 order, the 22 November 1886 first lighting, the 30 December 1901 Roosevelt-approved transfer.
Sources: U.S. Coast Guard Historian's Office — Statue of Liberty Light file (station established 1886, deactivated 1902; first lit 22 November 1886; Cleveland's order to the Lighthouse Board; lamp inventory and Wood's electrical apparatus; 30 December 1901 Roosevelt approval; 1 March 1902 transfer, light extinguished); Smithsonian Magazine — the 1886 decision to install nine arc lights inside the torch flame with portholes cut in the copper; the underwhelming effect; the 1916 Black Tom explosion that closed the torch; NYPR/WNYC — Staten Island depot history: the statue as the first electric lighthouse in the U.S. (1886); keeper Albert E. Littlefield; Joseph Funk's retrofit cutting holes in the torch base and fitting Fresnel-lens portions; the Board's white-paint debate; transfer to the National Park Service 1933; the 1984–86 torch replacement; Contemporary press and secondary accounts: the light's ineffectiveness as a navigational aid (the 'useless' verdict) — the mechanism of the 1902 surrender
Falsifiability: Lighthouse Board annual reports, the keeper's logs, or the USCG station file showing no light was ever operated would reopen the claim; the documentary record shows the opposite</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1886-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0445,40.6892,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1886</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison's real anti-AC campaign, 1887–1890 — the foil the Topsy myth misdates</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary press, Brown's published papers and the exposed 1889 correspondence, and the Kemmler execution record independently attest the campaign's methods.
Layer: real · Place: New York City (campaign); West Orange, NJ (Edison laboratory); Auburn, NY (Kemmler execution)
Dates: 1887 → 1890 (confidence: high)

In 1887–1890 Edison's interests ran a genuine propaganda campaign against alternating current: Harold P. Brown's public animal electrocutions (including the July 1888 Columbia College demonstration killing a dog with AC), the coinage of 'Westinghoused' as a verb for electrocution, and lobbying that put AC into New York's first electric chair (William Kemmler, Auburn, 6 August 1890).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press (June–July 1888 Brown campaign); Scientific American's summary of the Smithsonian account (Edison's West Orange laboratory demonstrations before the New York State committee: calves and a horse wired to electrodes; Brown commissioned to build the chair; Edison's 'Westinghoused' coinage); the Kemmler execution record (6 August 1890).
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_P._Brown — Brown's June 1888 press campaign; public animal electrocution demonstrations; lobbying to limit AC voltages and to put AC in the electric chair; collusion with the Edison Illuminating Company (New York Sun, August 1889 published correspondence); Discover Magazine, 'Cruel Inventions' (Nov/Dec 2021) — the July 1888 Columbia College demonstration: the 76-pound Newfoundland mix, the AC pulse that killed it, Edison as the sponsor behind Brown's demonstrations; Scientific American, 'The Westinghouse/Edison Rivalry' (Smithsonian blog summary) — Edison's stray-dog experiments at Orange, NJ; the West Orange committee demonstration; Brown commissioned by the state to build the chair; 'Westinghoused' introduced to the public; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Kemmler — 6 August 1890 execution at Auburn; AC supplied by a surreptitiously acquired Westinghouse generator; In re Kemmler appeal failed 9 October 1889
Falsifiability: Evidence that Brown's demonstrations never occurred, or that the Brown–Edison financial link is fabricated, would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1887-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1887</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1888 Figaro obituary mix-up</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The contemporary newspaper record plus the next-day retraction plus an archival letter showing Alfred knew — independent, mutually supporting documentation of the mix-up itself.
Layer: real · Place: Paris (Le Figaro); Cannes (Ludvig's death)
Dates: 1888-04-15 → 1888-04-16 (confidence: high)

After Alfred Nobel's brother Ludvig died in Cannes (12 April 1888), Le Figaro mistakenly published an obituary of Alfred on 15 April 1888 — 'A man who can only with the utmost difficulty be considered a benefactor of mankind has died yesterday at Cannes. It is Mr. Nobel, inventor of dynamite. Mr. Nobel was Swedish' — and retracted it the next day. Alfred knew about the mix-up: a surviving letter from his friend Juliette Adam-Lambert expressing relief the rumor was false is the best archival evidence he did.

Earliest attestation: Le Figaro, first page, 15 April 1888; retraction 16 April 1888.
Sources: Le Figaro, 15 April 1888 (first page; retracted 16 April 1888) — the mistaken obituary's actual text; Juliette Adam-Lambert's letter to Alfred Nobel expressing relief the rumor was false (archival; the best surviving evidence Alfred knew of the mix-up)
Falsifiability: The April 1888 Figaro issues are in the public record; their absence would overturn the claim.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1888-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1888</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'merchant of death' headline (the questioned text)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Repeated by reputable tellers (Britannica, the Nobel Foundation's outreach), but no newspaper copy has ever been produced and historians question whether the 'merchant of death' text ever existed — the known Figaro record carries a different, milder text.
Layer: real · Place: Paris (claimed newspaper)
Dates: 1888-04-15 → 1959 (confidence: low)

The mistaken 1888 obituary of Alfred Nobel ran under the headline 'Le marchand de la mort est mort' ('The merchant of death is dead') and declared that 'Dr. Alfred Nobel, who became rich by finding ways to kill more people faster than ever before, died yesterday.'

Earliest attestation: No contemporary copy has ever been produced; the popular form traces to Nicholas Halasz, 'Nobel: a Biography' (1959), and was repeated by Encyclopaedia Britannica and the Nobel Foundation's own outreach material.
Sources: Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Alfred Nobel' — repeats the 'Le marchand de la mort est mort' version (secondary attestation of the story's currency); Nicholas Halasz, 'Nobel: a Biography' (1959) — the earliest traceable popularization of the 'merchant of death' version; Wikipedia, 'Alfred Nobel' — 'historians have been unable to verify it, and some dismiss the story as a myth'; the questioned existence of the obituary text
Falsifiability: Production of the actual 1888 newspaper page bearing the headline would raise the grade; a comprehensive archival search finding no such page keeps it here.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1888-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1888</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Forth Bridge (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources: the owner-operator's own facts (Network Rail), the UNESCO World Heritage listing (2015), and the 2011 restoration reporting across the engineering press (Network Rail + Balfour Beatty, £130m, 230,000 m², 6.5M rivets).
Layer: real · Place: Firth of Forth, Scotland (South Queensferry ↔ North Queensferry)
Dates: 1890 → 1890 (confidence: high)

The Forth Bridge — the cantilever railway bridge across the Firth of Forth, Scotland, designed by John Fowler and Benjamin Baker, built 1883–1890 and opened in 1890 — is the span behind the idiom 'like painting the Forth Bridge.'

Earliest attestation: Network Rail's bridge facts; the 2011 restoration reporting (newcivilengineer, Rail Technology Magazine) re-stating the 1883–1890 build and 125/120-year age.
Sources: Network Rail / newcivilengineer.com (9 Dec 2011): opened 1890; length 2.47 km; 53,000 t steel; 6.5M rivets; 230,000 m² painting area; 240,000 litres of paint; ~200 trains/day; Rail Technology Magazine (Jan 2012): the £130m refurbishment complete after 10 years (finished 9 Dec 2011, ahead of schedule); bridge free of scaffolding for the first time in a decade; built 1883–1890; building.co.uk (2009/2010): the first bridge in the world built entirely of steel; constant maintenance since completion in 1890; edinburghlive.co.uk: repainting by glass-based epoxy introduced in 2002; last full repaint completed 2011; repaint interval now ~25 years
Falsifiability: The UNESCO World Heritage listing (2015), Network Rail's ownership record, and the 2002–2011 restoration contracts would all have to be wrong together.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1890-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.3886,56.0004,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1890</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Gem paper clip: anonymous invention, commercial by ~1900</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: multiple independent secondary attestations converge — Design News ('in widespread use by 1900', Gem Ltd., never patented); Scientific American/Early Office Museum (Gem-type by ~1892, never patented; Petroski on wire + machinery preconditions); Reason Foundation/Virginia Postrel (Middlebrook's 1899 machine patent illustration 'showed a perfectly proportioned Gem'); James Ward via Profile Books (1883 catalogue evidence, anonymous inventor predating both Schooley and Vaaler). The origin decade stays hedged; the anonymous-inventor and never-patented positions are independently attested.
Layer: real · Place: England (Gem Manufacturing Co.; Middlebrook filed from Waterbury, Connecticut, US)
Dates: 1890 → 1900 (confidence: medium)

The loop-within-a-loop paper clip — the Gem — was already in widespread commercial use by about 1900, produced by the Gem Manufacturing Co. in England (possibly from the 1870s), was never itself patented, and its inventor is anonymous.

Earliest attestation: Middlebrook's 1899 US 636,272 patent illustration; an 1883 Arthur Penn catalogue celebrating the Gem's superiority (cited by Petroski via James Ward's 'Adventures in Stationery')
Sources: Design News, 'Lessons From Paper Clips': Gem known as after late-19th-c. Gem Ltd.; widespread use by 1900; no patent taken out, copied by other manufacturers; Scientific American, 'The Paper Clip': Gem-type appeared until around 1892, never patented; Fay's 1867 bent-wire ticket fastener as earliest patented precursor; Petroski on wire + machinery; Reason Foundation (Virginia Postrel, 2017): Middlebrook's 1899 Waterbury machine patent 'showed a perfectly proportioned Gem'; James Ward, 'Adventures in Stationery' (Profile Books preview): 1883 Arthur Penn catalogue celebrating the Gem; anonymous inventor predated both Schooley and Vaaler; Middlebrook's 1899 illustration clearly a Gem-type; The Zebra, 'Paper Clip History: From Office Tool to Symbol of Resistance' (2026): double-loop Gem 'in widespread use by 1900 though no patent for it was ever filed'; Gem Manufacturing Company Ltd. in England 'likely produced it as early as the 1870s'
Falsifiability: A dated commercial catalogue or invoice showing the Gem design entering trade after 1901, or a patent showing the loop-within-a-loop design claimed by a named inventor before 1899.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1890-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1890</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1890 Chicago Tribune hoax that invented the rope trick</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Lamont's 2004 research identified Wilkie as the author (city editor at the time, later chief of the US Secret Service) and documents the paper's own admission that the story was intentional hokum; the legend's 20th-century spread is traceable to this single Western invention, not an Eastern original.
Layer: real · Place: Chicago, Illinois (the Tribune's newsroom)
Dates: 1890 → 1890 (confidence: high)

In 1890, Chicago Tribune city editor John E. Wilkie wrote a hoax story about the Indian rope trick that launched the modern legend; the newspaper later admitted in print that the story was false.

Earliest attestation: The 1890 Chicago Tribune story itself; the paper's later printed admission that it was false.
Sources: Peter Lamont — 'The Rise of the Indian Rope Trick: How a Spectacular Hoax Became History' (Little, Brown, 2004): identification of John E. Wilkie as the author; the Tribune's printed admission the story was false; the legend's subsequent spread in newspapers and journals worldwide; Peter Lamont — 'History of an illusion' (thefreelibrary.com): Wilkie was city editor of the Chicago Tribune in 1890, later chief of the United States Secret Service; 'the legend's rise to fame began not in the East, but in the West'
Falsifiability: Tribune archives showing the 1890 story was filed as verified foreign correspondence rather than invented copy, or the admission being a later fabrication</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1890-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6298,41.8781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1890</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Judson's Clasp Locker (1893) — the idea, not the teeth</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The patent record is a public document; the Fair debut and the design's failures are attested across independent secondary accounts; 'inventor of the zipper' as popular title is honorary, not priority.
Layer: real · Place: Chicago, Illinois (Columbian Exposition)
Dates: 1891 → 1893 (confidence: high)

Whitcomb L. Judson invented the first metal slide fastener, the 'clasp locker' — a hook-and-eye shoe fastener patented 1891-1893 and debuted at the 1893 Chicago World's Fair; it was cumbersome and popped open unexpectedly, and was never a commercial success.

Earliest attestation: US patents 504,037 and 504,038 (1893); the Columbian Exposition showing
Sources: US Patent 504,037/504,038 (Whitcomb L. Judson, 1893); Pennsylvania Center for the Book / Penn State — 'Zip It Up: Talon Zippers' (2009), citing Robert Friedel, Zipper: An Exploration of Novelty (1994)
Falsifiability: Proof the clasp locker was commercially viable at scale — or that the patent record is misread (it is not)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1891-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.62,41.88,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1891</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1892 Rosenau/Library Bureau vertical-filing prototype</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in Robertson's researched history with archival footnotes; the broad 1890s Library Bureau introduction is independently stated by the Wikipedia summary — held below VERIFIED on the single scholarly chain for the 1892 prototype itself.
Layer: real · Place: Chicago, Illinois (Library Bureau Chicago office)
Dates: 1892 → 1892 (confidence: medium)

In 1892, Dr. Rosenau, secretary of a Buffalo, New York charity organization, took his vertical-filing idea to the Library Bureau's Chicago office, which built a prototype — the earliest documented vertical filing cabinet.

Earliest attestation: Craig Robertson, The Filing Cabinet: A Vertical History of Information (University of Minnesota Press, 2021), with archival footnotes.
Sources: Craig Robertson, The Filing Cabinet: A Vertical History of Information (2021) — Rosenau, Buffalo charity secretary, inspired by index-card cabinets, brought the idea to the Library Bureau's Chicago office in 1892; prototype built; 'business systematizer' prompted business-correspondence cabinets; Wikipedia, 'Library Bureau' — 'In the 1890s, the Library Bureau introduced vertical filing'; Dewey founded the company 1876 but had no day-to-day involvement by the 1890s
Falsifiability: The Library Bureau's Chicago office records showing no 1892 prototype, or a documented earlier vertical file, would overturn the priority.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1892-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.63,41.878,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1892</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ferris's 1893 Chicago wheel (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED across contemporary records, the Chicago History Museum, and modern histories. The 33-year-old bridge engineer George Washington Gale Ferris Jr. answered the exposition's challenge to outdo the Eiffel Tower (1889 Paris) with something that moved rather than another tower. Built in under six months: a 264-foot steel wheel on a 70-ton axle, 36 railroad-car cabins at 60 passengers each, steam-powered 1,000-hp engines, a 20-minute ride. Opened 21 June 1893; 1,453,611 paid admissions (plus dignitary free trips) through the fair's end; gross $726,805. At the time, the tallest object in Chicago. Ferris died three years later at 37. The 'napkin sketch' origin is legend (fenced as CLAIMED); the wheel itself is document.
Layer: real · Place: Chicago, Illinois, USA (World's Columbian Exposition, Midway)
Dates: 1893-06-21 → 1893-11-06 (confidence: high)

George Washington Gale Ferris Jr.'s wheel at the 1893 World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago was 264 feet tall, carried 36 cars of 60 passengers each (2,160 per revolution), opened to the public in June 1893, and carried over a million paying customers during the fair.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1893 press and exposition records
Sources: FactMonster: Ferris unveiled the first Ferris wheel at the 1893 exposition; 250 feet across, 2,160 capacity; over a million paying customers in 19 weeks; Ferris died three years later at 37.; Chicago History Museum via KOZI (Paul Durica): the Eiffel Tower challenge; Ferris's moving alternative; built in under six months; opened June 1893; 264 feet, 36 cars of 60; the tallest object in Chicago at the time.; ICON Park (Wheel Works): the 'Chicago Wheel' proposal, the 'too fragile' rejection, the refined design; the 70-ton axle, 36 cabins, 1,000-hp steam engines; 'the world's first Ferris wheel was rolled out to the public in June of 1893.'
Falsifiability: Exposition records contradicting the dimensions or attendance</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6298,41.8781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mendenhall Order of 1893: metric standards become the fundamental standards of the United States</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED on multiple independent official and secondary records: the U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey Report for 1893 carries the order; NIST Letter Circular 1136 states 'In 1893, metric standards, developed through international cooperation under the auspices of BIPM, were adopted as the fundamental standards for length and mass in the United States. Our customary measurements — the foot, pound, quart, etc. — have been defined in relation to the meter and the kilogram ever since'; the USMA transcribes the original order with the conversion tables (1 yard = 3600/3937 meter; 1 avoirdupois pound = 0.4535924277 kilogram). The reasons are independently documented: the imperial bronze yard No. 11 (an exact copy of the British imperial standard) had shown changes on comparison in 1876 and 1888 that could not be blamed on No. 11 alone, and the two-piece brass mint pound could not be density-tested without leakage risk and oxidized — so the Office of Weights and Measures had already been 'practically forced' to use the metric standards before the order formalized it.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C., United States (the Mendenhall Order was an administrative act of the U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey, not a place-bound event)
Dates: 1893 → 1893 (confidence: high)

In 1893, by the Mendenhall Order issued by T. C. Mendenhall (Superintendent of the U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey), the United States adopted the international meter and kilogram as the fundamental standards for length and mass; the customary units — the yard, the pound, and their relatives — have been defined in relation to the meter and the kilogram ever since.

Earliest attestation: The order itself, published in the U.S. Coast and Geodetic Survey Report for 1893.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Mendenhall Order': the conversions 1 yard = 3600/3937 meter and 1 pound = 0.4535924277 kilogram; 'the Mendenhall order amounted to a formal announcement of a change that had already occurred in practice' — the Office of Weights and Measures had been 'practically forced' to use the metric standards because the imperial bronze yard No. 11 was unstable and the mint pound 'likewise unfit for use.'; NIST Letter Circular 1136 (1997 revision) — 'The U.S.A. and the Metric System': 'In 1893, metric standards, developed through international cooperation under the auspices of BIPM, were adopted as the fundamental standards for length and mass in the United States. Our customary measurements — the foot, pound, quart, etc. — have been defined in relation to the meter and the kilogram ever since.'; US Metric Association — transcription of the Mendenhall Order with the 1893 Report's yard/pound tables: the bronze yard No. 11's instability on 1876/1888 comparisons and the mint pound's two-piece leakage problem; 'the new metres and kilograms represented the most advanced ideas of standards, and it therefore seemed that greater stability in our weights and measures as well as higher accuracy would be secured by accepting the international metre and kilogram as fundamental standards.'
Falsifiability: If the Order had never been issued and U.S. customary units were still legally defined by the bronze yard and the mint pound rather than by metric prototypes, the claim would be false. The order's text survives in the official report and its conversions (1 yard = 3600/3937 meter; 1 pound = 0.4535924277 kilogram) were used by the Office of Weights and Measures thereafter.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dickson Experimental Sound Film (1894/95) - the first known sound film</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The artifact survives and is independently documented: the 17-second restored film (Planquette's 'Song of the Cabin Boy' from Les Cloches de Corneville, 1877), the cylinder's ~2 minutes of sound including 23 seconds of violin music, the Library of Congress selection for preservation in 2003 as 'culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant,' and Musser's assessment (via the EMRO review) as 'the world's first known experiment in producing a motion picture with a recorded synchronized sound track.' Held below VERIFIED: the 'first known' is a claim about absence of predecessors, and the Kinetophone's no-true-sync caveat is fenced, not flattened - there is also no evidence of original-format exhibition. Three decades before Vitaphone, and thirty-two years before The Jazz Singer.
Layer: real · Place: Black Maria studio, West Orange, New Jersey (city precision)
Dates: 1894 → 1895 (confidence: medium)

The Dickson Experimental Sound Film, made by W.K.L. Dickson in late 1894 or early 1895 at Edison's Black Maria studio, is the first known film with live-recorded sound - Dickson playing a violin into a wax-cylinder recording horn while two men dance to the music; selected for the National Film Registry in 2003.

Earliest attestation: The surviving film strip and cylinder; no evidence it was ever exhibited in its original format.
Sources: Wikipedia - 'The Dickson Experimental Sound Film' (late 1894/early 1895; first known film with live-recorded sound; Kinetophone sync caveat; Black Maria; LOC National Film Registry 2003; resynchronized 2000); EMRO - 'Edison: The Invention of the Movies (1891-1918)' (Musser: 'the world's first known experiment in producing a motion picture with a recorded synchronized sound track')
Falsifiability: An earlier film with documented live-recorded sound.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1894-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.2345,40.7838,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1894</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dreyfus Affair: arrest, trials, degradation, exoneration (1894-1906)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by military-justice records across twelve years, the Court of Cassation's 1906 decision with its two stated 'new facts,' contemporary press (Zola's J'Accuse sold 200,000+ copies in an evening), and the physical forgery evidence (the Henry document, exposed 1898).
Layer: real · Place: Paris, France (court-martial, degradation, J'Accuse); Devil's Island, French Guiana (imprisonment); Rennes (1899 retrial)
Dates: 1894-10-15 → 1906-07-12 (confidence: high)

The Dreyfus Affair (1894-1906): French artillery captain Alfred Dreyfus, an Alsatian Jew, was arrested in October 1894 on suspicion of passing military secrets to Germany, convicted of treason by a Paris court-martial on 22 December 1894, publicly degraded (5 January 1895) and deported to Devil's Island; in 1896 the new head of the army's intelligence Statistical Section, Georges Picquart, identified Major Ferdinand Walsin Esterhazy as the real author of the incriminating bordereau; after Zola's 'J'Accuse' (13 January 1898), the exposure of Colonel Henry's forged evidence (August 1898), a second court-martial at Rennes (August-September 1899) that still found Dreyfus guilty 'with extenuating circumstances,' a presidential pardon (19 September 1899), the Court of Cassation annulled the conviction on 12 July 1906, reinstated Dreyfus as a major, and awarded him the Legion of Honour.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary French military-justice records, 1894-1906; the bordereau itself; the Court of Cassation's 1906 decision.
Sources: Court of Cassation, 12 July 1906 decision (annulment of the 1894 conviction; the two 'new facts'); Wikipedia 'Resolution of the Dreyfus affair': Rennes retrial 7 Aug 1899, guilty with extenuating circumstances, 10 years; pardon 19 Sept 1899; annulment 12 July 1906; history.com 'What Was the Dreyfus Affair': bordereau in the waste basket, Picquart's 1896 Esterhazy identification, Henry's forged evidence and suicide; Zola, 'J'Accuse...!', L'Aurore, 13 January 1898 (contemporary press)
Falsifiability: Discovery that the court records are fabrications, or a contradicting authenticated document establishing a different sequence of events.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1894-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1894</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kinetoscope — 1894, the first commercially viable motion-picture system (peep-show, not projection)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The system's essentials are multiply documented: Dickson's 1889–1892 development (Eastman Museum), the perforated 35mm film with sprocketed intermittent movement ('the first practical system of its type'), the April 1894 commercial parlor, and the Fred Ott sneeze as the first copyrighted movie. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED on the internal lab chronology (1888 conception → 1891 tests), which rests on company records and retrospective testimony. The record fences the achievement honestly: peep-show, not projection — Edison initially disdained projection as financially nonviable.
Layer: real · Place: Broadway, New York City — the first Kinetoscope parlor
Dates: 1894-04-14 → 1894-04-14 (confidence: high)

The Edison-lab Kinetograph/Kinetoscope system (1889–1894): William K.L. Dickson's motion-picture camera and peep-show viewer, first commercially exhibited 14 April 1894 at the Holland Brothers' parlor in New York City — the first commercially viable motion-picture system, single-viewer, not projected.

Earliest attestation: The Holland Brothers' 1894 parlor opening; the Edison Manufacturing Co. product labels ('EDISON KINETOSCOPE MANUFACTURED BY EDISON MFG. CO., ORANGE, N.J.'); the Fred Ott sneeze copyright filing (January 1894, Library of Congress).
Sources: 
Falsifiability: Documentation that the April 1894 exhibition was not the first commercial motion-picture exhibition, or that the system did not use perforated 35mm film with intermittent movement.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1894-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1894</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Browne Issue System (1895)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested in library-history sources (Gorman, Queensland CLS manual, Sturges); the 1895 date rides on a single secondary transmission chain — held below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Library Bureau, Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1895 → 1895 (confidence: medium)

Nina E. Browne, librarian at the Library Bureau in Boston, devised the first widely adopted card-and-pocket library charging system by 1895: each borrower's ticket and each book's card were filed together in date trays at checkout, the date was stamped in the book, and on return the borrower's card was handed back and the book card replaced in the book — after which no record of the transaction remained.

Earliest attestation: Attested in Michael Gorman's Broken Pieces (ALA, 2011), Chapter 3 footnote 4; the State Library of Queensland Country Lending Service manual (2006) §3.1 describes the system in operational use; Sturges, Iliffe &amp; Dearnley (2001) discuss its record-keeping privacy aspect.
Sources: Gorman, Michael (2011). Broken Pieces: A Library Life, 1941–1978. ALA. Ch. 3, 4th footnote.; State Library of Queensland, Country Lending Service (CLS) manual (2006), §3.1 'Issue systems'.; Sturges P., Iliffe U. &amp; Dearnley J. 'Privacy in the Digital Library Environment' (2001), §2.3 'Record keeping in twentieth century libraries'.; Prytherch, Ray J. Harrod's Librarians' Glossary, 8th ed. (1995).
Falsifiability: A dated pre-1895 publication describing an identical book-card/borrower-ticket tray system, or archival evidence that Browne's 1895 system postdates the Newark implementation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Lumière first paid public screening — 28 December 1895</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The event is multiply documented: the original program listing the ten films (La Sortie de l'usine Lumière, La Voltige, La Pêche aux poissons rouges, Le Débarquement du congrès de photographie à Lyon, Les Forgerons, Le Jardinier (L'Arroseur arrosé), Repas de bébé, Le Saut à la couverture, Place des Cordeliers à Lyon, La Mer), the paid admission (one franc), and the continuous daily screenings that followed — the commercial cinema was born with a business ledger, not just a camera.
Layer: real · Place: Salon Indien du Grand Café, 14 Boulevard des Capucines, Paris
Dates: 1895-12-28 → 1895-12-28 (confidence: high)

The first paid public film projection in history took place on 28 December 1895 at the Salon Indien du Grand Café, Paris — the Lumière brothers' Cinématographe screening of ten short films before a paying audience.

Earliest attestation: The original printed program of 28 December 1895; the Lumière company business records; the subsequent daily-screening accounts from the Salon Indien (January 1896 press coverage).
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A documented paid public projection before 28 December 1895 (the Skladanowsky Bioskop at Berlin's Wintergarten, November 1895, is the named rival candidate and is carried in the competing hypotheses — it was not charged-admission projection in the Lumière sense and does not overturn the grade on the dated event).</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3303,48.8719,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nobel's 1895 will</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The signed document exists and is multiply attested; its terms are undisputed.
Layer: real · Place: Swedish-Norwegian Club, Paris
Dates: 1895-11-27 → 1895-11-27 (confidence: high)

On 27 November 1895, at the Swedish-Norwegian Club in Paris, Alfred Nobel signed his last will and testament, directing that the bulk of his estate (about 94% after taxes and individual bequests) be converted into a fund whose interest would be distributed annually as prizes to 'those who, during the preceding year, shall have conferred the greatest benefit to mankind' — in physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature, and peace. Nobel died 10 December 1896 in Sanremo, Italy, leaving no public explanation for the bequest.

Earliest attestation: The will itself (27 November 1895), held by the Nobel Foundation; executed after Nobel's death 10 December 1896.
Sources: The will of Alfred Nobel, 27 November 1895 (Nobel Foundation holdings); Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Alfred Nobel' — the will's terms and the five prizes
Falsifiability: The will's text is a public document; a verified contradiction between its terms and this record would overturn it.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hubert's company and the 'Ever Ready' brand</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The commercialization record is multiply attested: the NYPD testimonial distribution, the May 1898 Electrical Show at Madison Square Garden, the 'Ever Ready' trademark, the 1905 company rename, the 1914 National Carbon acquisition and 'Eveready' shortening — all consistently documented.
Layer: real · Place: New York City
Dates: 1896 → 1917 (confidence: high)

Conrad Hubert's American Electrical Novelty and Manufacturing Company commercialized the flashlight, built the 'Ever Ready' brand (1905), and became the Eveready of the battery aisle.

Earliest attestation: The 1899 'Ever Ready' catalogue (ThoughtCo); the January 1899 Ever-Ready flashlight advertisement referencing patent-infringement proceedings against rivals (Wikipedia).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Eveready Battery Company': Hubert renamed the company 'The American Ever Ready Company' in 1905; it joined National Carbon Company in 1914 (trademark shortened to 'Eveready'); Hubert stayed on as president; National Carbon merged with Union Carbide in 1917; AA dry cell 1907, AAA 1911.; ThoughtCo: the 1899 Eveready catalogue advertising the new flashlight ('Let there be light' cover); Misell and Cowen-era background.; patentplaques-blog, 'Let There Be Light! – Invention of the Flashlight': the 1917 National Carbon naming contest for the flashlight — over 530,000 entries, four winners receiving $3,000 each for 'DAYLO'; the name was used 1917-1921 and discontinued after 1922.
Falsifiability: Company-registration records contradicting the 1905 rename or the 1914 National Carbon acquisition would overturn the branding timeline.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1896-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1896</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Why they were called 'flash' lights</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The technical mechanism (zinc-carbon cells that faded fast; carbon filaments that burned briefly) is independently attested by popular-science and collector histories, and the naming follows the behavior: users got flashes, not beams.
Layer: real · Place: New York City
Dates: 1898 → 1911 (confidence: medium)

The word 'flashlight' (American) / 'torch' naming comes from the devices' first decade: weak zinc-carbon batteries and carbon-filament bulbs could only produce brief flashes of light, not sustained beams.

Earliest attestation: 1899 'Ever Ready' catalogue advertising the new flashlight (ThoughtCo).
Sources: Discover Magazine, 'They Invented it': 'Burdened with inefficient carbon-filament bulbs and weak batteries, early flashlights lasted only a few seconds when the user held down a tab or ring.'; ThoughtCo, 'The Invention of the Flashlight': the 1899 Eveready catalogue advertising the new flashlight carried the biblical quote 'let there be light' on the cover — the word 'flashlight' in commercial use within a year of the patent.; Collector account (CandlePowerForums): early users flashed the light briefly and switched off before the bulb blew; 1910 tungsten filaments then made sustained use practical.
Falsifiability: A pre-1898 published use of 'flashlight' for a portable electric lamp, or a contemporary document offering a different naming story, would overturn this.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The coal-bunker fire theory — the Maine's most probable cause</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The US Navy's own 1974–76 engineering investigation ('How the Battleship Maine Was Destroyed') — the most rigorous forensic study the ship ever received — concluded the internal coal-bunker fire was the most probable cause; the 1898 board's dismissal rested on a false claim ('never been a case of spontaneous combustion of coal on board the MAINE') contradicted by other US ships' known incidents. Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: the physical evidence is gone (wreck re-sunk 1912, photos inconclusive per National Geographic), and the Navy itself holds the cause 'unsettled.'
Layer: real · Place: Havana Harbor, Cuba
Dates: 1898-02-15 → 1976 (confidence: high)

The USS Maine was destroyed by an internal explosion: a spontaneous fire in a coal bunker — burning bituminous coal, prone to spontaneous combustion — ignited the adjacent 6-inch reserve magazines, which detonated the forward magazines on the night of 15 February 1898.

Earliest attestation: Dissenting US Navy officers at the time of the 1898 inquiry, who held that the magazines had been ignited by a spontaneous coal-bunker fire — the position the board declined to credit.
Sources: Adm. Hyman G. Rickover, 'How the Battleship Maine Was Destroyed' (1976, reissued 1995): Navy engineering investigation; concluded an internal explosion from a coal-bunker fire adjacent to the 6-inch reserve magazines was the most probable cause; Louis Fisher, 'Destruction of the Maine (1898)' (Law Library of Congress): the 1898 board claimed 'there had never been a case of spontaneous combustion of coal on board the MAINE' — but did not acknowledge that other US ships had experienced spontaneous combustion of coal in bunkers; Newspapers.com blog, 'The Sinking of the USS Maine' (1911 board + Rickover 1976): the 1911 investigation repeated the mine conclusion; the 1976 Rickover-led investigation concluded the explosions were caused by a coal-bunker fire adjacent to one of the ship's magazines
Falsifiability: A forensic re-examination of the wreck proving a mine blast (e.g., explosive residue consistent with a naval mine on the exterior plating) would overturn this; the wreck lies at 1,100 m depth north of Havana and has never been forensically re-examined since 1911.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-82.35,23.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The destruction of the USS Maine</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The explosion, date, place, ship, and death toll are attested by the US Naval Court of Inquiry, the Spanish commission, the US Navy's casualty accounting, and the physical wreck — hostile and domestic traditions agreeing on the event itself.
Layer: real · Place: Havana Harbor, Cuba (moored at buoy 4)
Dates: 1898-02-15 → 1898-02-15 (confidence: high)

On the evening of 15 February 1898, the American battleship USS Maine (ACR-1) exploded and sank in Havana Harbor, Cuba, killing 266 of the 350 men aboard when her forward magazines detonated. She had been sent to Havana three weeks earlier, moored at buoy 4, to protect American interests during the Cuban War of Independence. The wreck was raised within a cofferdam in 1911 and re-sunk with military honors in deep water north of Havana in 1912; her main mast stands as a memorial at Arlington National Cemetery.

Earliest attestation: The 1898 US Naval Court of Inquiry (Sampson Board), convened within weeks; Captain Sigsbee's reports; the Spanish naval commission's contemporaneous findings.
Sources: US Naval History and Heritage Command (DVIDS, 125th anniversary): explosion 15 Feb 1898 sank the ship, killing 266 sailors; the 1898 board ruled a mine detonated under the ship; Louis Fisher, 'Destruction of the Maine (1898)' (Law Library of Congress): 2 officers + 250 enlisted killed outright, 14 of the injured later died — 266 total; the 1898 board concluded a mine under the ship at frame 18; Smithsonian Magazine (Tom Miller, Feb 1998): 266 of 350 aboard killed; the 1976 Rickover investigation found the demise self-inflicted, likely a coal bunker fire
Falsifiability: There is no live falsifier — the explosion is multiply and independently attested by hostile traditions (American board, Spanish commission, the wreck itself).</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-82.35,23.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>David Misell's tubular flashlight, 1898-1899</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Primary patent record corroborated by secondary industrial histories: the design (three D batteries front-to-back in a paper tube, bulb with rough brass reflector at the end), the filing and issue dates, and the assignment to Hubert's company are all consistently attested.
Layer: real · Place: New York City
Dates: 1898 → 1899 (confidence: high)

English inventor David Misell designed and patented the first practical tubular dry-cell flashlight, 1898-1899.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent 617,592, 'electric device', issued to David Misell 10 January 1899 (filed 12 March 1898, with Conrad Hubert as witness).
Sources: U.S. Patent 617,592 ('electric device'), issued to David Misell, 10 January 1899, filed 12 March 1898; tubular design with D batteries in a paper tube, assigned to the American Electrical Novelty and Manufacturing Company; Wikipedia, 'Eveready Battery Company': the flashlight 'designed by Misell was powered by "D" batteries laid front-to-back in a paper tube with the light bulb and a rough brass reflector at the end.'; CandlePowerForums, 'History of the First Tubular Flashlight': Misell applied for the patent 12 March 1898 with Hubert as witness; accepted 3 January 1899, issued 10 January 1899; first flashlights handmade from crude paper and fiber tubes; success at the May 1898 Electrical Show, Madison Square Garden.; ThoughtCo, 'The Invention of the Flashlight': Misell's portable light was designed in the now familiar tube-shape and used three D batteries placed in a line, with a lightbulb at one end of the tube.
Falsifiability: An earlier patented tubular dry-cell design, or proof Misell's patents were fraudulently obtained, would overturn this; neither has surfaced in the patent record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Seibels's 1898 prototype, the 1899 Globe-Wernicke cabinets, and the denied patent</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Robertson's researched account; the surviving 1899 cabinet in the NMAH collection corroborates the Globe-Wernicke production leg — held below VERIFIED on the single-chain transmission of the denial, and the lecture notes' 'one of his original filing cabinets is in the Smithsonian' conflation (the NMAH cabinet is a Globe-Wernicke 1899 cabinet, not one of Seibels's) is corrected here.
Layer: real · Place: National Museum of American History, Washington, DC (holds one of the five 1899 Globe-Wernicke cabinets)
Dates: 1898 → 1899 (confidence: medium)

Edwin Seibels, an insurance salesman, built a vertical-filing prototype in 1898; a year later the Cincinnati company Globe-Wernicke produced five vertical filing cabinets, one of which survives in the Smithsonian Institution's National Museum of American History collection; Seibels's patent application was denied on the grounds that it described a system that was an idea, not a device.

Earliest attestation: Craig Robertson, The Filing Cabinet: A Vertical History of Information (University of Minnesota Press, 2021).
Sources: Craig Robertson, The Filing Cabinet: A Vertical History of Information (2021) — Seibels 1898 prototype; Globe-Wernicke's five 1899 cabinets; one in the Smithsonian NMAH collection; patent denied — 'his application described a system that was an idea, not a device'; University lecture notes, PSYC305A (2008) — earliest advertisement 1901
Falsifiability: A granted Seibels vertical-file patent in the USPTO record would overturn the denial leg; a documented Seibels-made cabinet in the NMAH collection would overturn the Globe-Wernicke attribution.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.03,38.891,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Flannan Isles keepers' disappearance, December 1900</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent contemporary documentary sources (ship log, telegram, NLB investigation, relief-keeper account) attest the disappearance; the lighthouse itself is a documented, dated structure (designed David Alan Stevenson, first lit 7 December 1899). The cause is NOT part of this grade — held separately.
Layer: real · Place: Eilean Mòr, Flannan Isles, Outer Hebrides, Scotland
Dates: 1900-12-15 → 1900-12-15 (confidence: high)

Three lighthouse keepers — James Ducat, Thomas Marshall and Donald McArthur — vanished from the Flannan Isles lighthouse (Eilean Mòr, Outer Hebrides) between the last written log entries of 13 December and the arrival of the relief tender Hesperus on 26 December 1900; the steamer Archtor had already noted the light unlit on 15 December. Their bodies were never found.

Earliest attestation: Archtor's log, 15 December 1900 (light not operational noted in poor weather); Captain Harvie's telegram to the NLB, 26 December 1900
Sources: Northern Lighthouse Board station page, 'Flannan Isles' (nlb.org.uk): discovery by Hesperus 26 Dec 1900; log 13 Dec; slate 14–15 Dec; everything in order; lamp ready to be lit; men disappeared afternoon of Saturday 15 December; Captain Harvie's telegram to the NLB, 26 December 1900 ('A dreadful accident has happened at the Flannans' — public domain); Robert Muirhead's NLB investigation, 29 December 1900: Ducat and Marshall went down to the west landing; McArthur (the 'Occasional') left in shirtsleeves; damage to the west landing 'difficult to believe unless actually seen'; conclusion that an extra-large sea swept the men away while securing the mooring-rope box ~110 ft above sea level; Joseph Moore's relief-keeper account: kitchen utensils very clean (after dinner, contradicting later poetic invention); Wikipedia, 'Flannan Isles Lighthouse' (secondary compilation with citations to Muirhead report, NLB, and period sources)
Falsifiability: Any authenticated contemporary record placing any of the three keepers alive after 15 December 1900, or identified remains contradicting the disappearance.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-7.58806,58.28806,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Landsteiner's ABO blood groups (1900–1901)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The published experiment, the immediate AB extension, the 1907 independent confirmation, and the 1930 Nobel form converging independent lines; the serology is replicable to this day.
Layer: real · Place: Vienna
Dates: 1900 → 1901 (confidence: high)

Karl Landsteiner discovered the human ABO blood groups (A, B, O) — 1900 serum-mixing experiments, published 1901 — establishing the compatibility rules that made transfusion safe; AB followed (Decastello &amp; Sturli, 1902); Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1930.

Earliest attestation: Landsteiner's 1901 paper; his 1900 laboratory experiments mixing staff blood samples
Sources: Karl Landsteiner, 'Zur Kenntnis der antifermentativen, lytischen und agglutinierenden Wirkungen des Blutserums und der Lymphe' (1901) — the A/B/O groups; News-Medical history of blood transfusion — 'In 1901, Karl Landsteiner, an Austrian physician discovered the first human blood groups... Landsteiner discovered blood groups A, B and O and established the basic principals of ABO compatibility'; Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1930, awarded to Karl Landsteiner 'for his discovery of human blood groups'
Falsifiability: Proof the 1900–1901 experiments are misdated or the Nobel-cited discovery was made elsewhere first</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3738,48.2082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Newark Charging System (~1900)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporary operational documentation (Dana/Van de Carr 1908–12), the 1913 ALA adoption survey, and Freedman's 1981 survey converge on the system's existence, form, and dominance; the ~1900 origin date is secondary-attested.
Layer: real · Place: Newark Public Library, Newark, New Jersey
Dates: 1900 → 1900 (confidence: medium)

John Cotton Dana's Newark Public Library introduced its card-based circulation system about 1900: each book carried a book pocket with a book card (4"x2"), each checkout was stamped on a date slip pasted in the book, and the book card was filed in charging trays by date and call number against the borrower's registration — replacing ledger-based circulation and becoming, by the 1913 ALA survey, the most common named charging system in American libraries.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary operational account: Dana &amp; Van de Carr, Modern American library economy as illustrated by the Newark, N.J., Free Public Library, vol. 1, pt. 1 (Elm Tree Press, 1908–12), §2 'Lending and receiving books; the charging system'; 1913 ALA 'Summary of Reports on Charging Systems' lists Newark as the most common named system; Maurice J. Freedman, 'Circulation Systems Past and Present', Journal of Library Automation 14(4), Dec 1981.
Sources: Dana, J.C. &amp; Van de Carr, S.C. Modern American library economy (1908–12), v.1 pt.1 §2.; American Library Association (1913). 'Summary of Reports on Charging Systems'. Papers and Proceedings of the 35th General Meeting.; Freedman, Maurice J. (1981). 'Circulation Systems Past and Present'. Journal of Library Automation 14(4):278–285.; Dean, Gabrielle (2015). 'The Shock of the Familiar: Three Timelines about Gender and Technology in the Library'. Digital Humanities Quarterly 9(2).; Wilson, Martha (1920). School Library Management. H.W. Wilson. (Newark procedure described)
Falsifiability: A Newark library record showing card-based charging before Browne's 1895 system, or the 1908–12 Dana volume describing a different procedure</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0776,40.7282,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Stage versions of the rope trick — the legend's children, not its ancestors</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Lamont's single-scholar audit is the attestation: later stage performances of the trick were inspired by Wilkie's 1890 account. Held at CLAIMED — single-source, late — pending independent attestation of the lineage.
Layer: real · Place: India and the Western stage (the legend's two homes)
Dates: 1900 → 2004 (confidence: medium)

Twentieth-century stage magicians performed rope-rising effects — a rope made to stand or rise apparently without support — inspired by the Indian rope trick legend, not as survivals of an ancient classic feat.

Earliest attestation: 20th-century stage-magic performances and exposure literature; Lamont 2004 traces their lineage to the post-1890 legend rather than to an ancient original.
Sources: Peter Lamont — 'The Rise of the Indian Rope Trick' (2004): 'later stage magic performances of the trick were inspired by Wilkie's account' (via Wikipedia's summary of Lamont's argument)
Falsifiability: A dated pre-1890 stage record of the classic full sequence (rigid rope, vanishing boy, falling limbs, restored boy) performed as described</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>79.0,22.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Conklin's 1901 crescent filler — the first reliable self-filler</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The patent number, mechanism description, company history, Mark Twain endorsement, and 1930 production end are corroborated across the Vintage Pen Doctor, FountainPen.it, and Fountain Pen Network sources; held below VERIFIED on the one-day grant-date variance and the trade/collector transmission.
Layer: real · Place: Toledo, Ohio, USA — Conklin Pen Manufacturing Co.
Dates: 1901 → 1901 (confidence: high)

Roy Conklin's US patent 685,258 (granted October 1901) covered the crescent filler: an internal rubber sac compressed by a pressure bar running through a slot in the barrel, attached to an external crescent-shaped hump; pressing and releasing the hump drew ink in without a separate dropper — the first reliable self-filling fountain pen. Conklin had founded the Self-Filling Fountain Pen Company (1898, with C. B. Gundy), renamed the Conklin Pen Manufacturing Co. (1901); Mark Twain lent his name to the pens; crescent fillers were produced through 1930.

Earliest attestation: US patent 685,258, granted October 1901 (Roy Conklin); extensive marketing began 1904 per Geyer's Stationer
Sources: Vintage Fountain Pen Doctor, 'Conklin Crescent filling fountain pen' — Roy Conklin granted US patent 685258 on October 29, 1901; sac compressed via pressure bar through a barrel slot to an external hump; locking ring; marketing from 1904; Mark Twain convert by 1903; FountainPen.it, 'Conklin/en' — company born as Self-Filling Fountain Pen Co. 1898 (Roy Conklin + C. B. Gundy); Conklin Pen Manufacturing Co. 1901; 'the first working automatic filling system created for a pen'; crescent filler the first truly reliable automatic mechanism; Fountain Pen Network, 'Fountain Pen Chronology' — 1897: Roy Conklin begins working on fountain pens; 1901: Conklin Pen Manufacturing Co founded, patenting the crescent filler; 1915: Waterman introduces the lever filler; 1916: Parker introduces the button filler
Falsifiability: An earlier working self-filling patent predating 1901 (the 1897 Conklin-era claims and earlier 'automatic filling' patents are the audit surface); held below VERIFIED on the collector/trade transmission of this pass</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1901-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.5379,41.6528,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1901</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Marconi's 1901 transatlantic 'S' (the contested reception)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested but not independently corroborated: the only witnesses are Marconi's own expedition party (Kemp, Paget), and Marconi refused a public demonstration at the time; the Antique Wireless Association's two-part technical review (2001) concluded modern propagation physics makes daytime reception at ~350 kHz over ~2,100 miles effectively impossible via the D-layer absorption Marconi could not have known about. Held at CLAIMED rather than DEBUNKED because the 1902 SS Philadelphia voyage, the 1902-03 Glace Bay night receptions, and the 1907 commercial transatlantic service prove the Atlantic was soon spanned by the same system — the anomaly is the December daylight, not the wireless itself.
Layer: real · Place: Signal Hill, St. John's, Newfoundland
Dates: 1901-12-12 → 1901-12-14 (confidence: high)

On 12 December 1901, at Signal Hill, St. John's, Newfoundland, Guglielmo Marconi received three faint clicks — the Morse letter 'S' — transmitted from his station at Poldhu, Cornwall, the first wireless reception across the Atlantic.

Earliest attestation: Marconi's own diary, 12 Dec 1901 (signals logged at 12:30, 1:10, 2:20 Newfoundland time); Kemp and Paget as the on-site witnesses (Marconi's own men)
Sources: Antique Wireless Association, 'Did Marconi Receive Transatlantic Radio Signals in 1901?' Part 1 (pre-history, Poldhu, and the propagation critique: 'could hardly have picked a worse combination of frequency and time of day'; D-layer daytime absorption at AM broadcast frequencies); Antique Wireless Association, Part 2 (conclusion): Marconi's diary — signals at 12:30, 1:10, 2:20 Newfoundland time; 'a rhythmic succession of the 3 dots corresponding to the letter S of the Morse code'; Anglo-American Telegraph Company's monopoly threat ended the Newfoundland experiments; SS Philadelphia follow-up voyage, Feb 1902; IEEE Engineering and Technology History Wiki, 'Reception of Transatlantic Radio Signals, 1901' (IEEE Milestone) — the institutional honor for the event; cited as a named position in competing hypotheses, not as independent evidence
Falsifiability: A second, independent contemporary party's log of the same signals at the same hour — or a verifiable public demonstration in the same conditions — would move the grade up; period evidence that the Poldhu transmitter was not keying at the logged times would move it down to DEBUNKED.</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1901-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-52.6815,47.5753,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1901</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The execution of Topsy the elephant, 4 January 1903</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested contemporary event: period press, the surviving film, and institutional preservation in the Library of Congress.
Layer: real · Place: Luna Park, Coney Island, Brooklyn, New York
Dates: 1903-01-04 → 1903-01-04 (confidence: high)

On 4 January 1903 at Luna Park, Coney Island, New York, the female Asian elephant Topsy was publicly executed by electrocution on the orders of her owners, park proprietors Frederick Thompson and Elmer Dundy; the event was filmed by the Edison Manufacturing Company as 'Electrocuting an Elephant.'

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press reports, January 1903 (NYT: 'a rather inglorious affair'); the Edison Manufacturing film 'Electrocuting an Elephant' (1903), preserved in the U.S. Library of Congress.
Sources: The New York Times, 5 January 1903 ('a rather inglorious affair'); Edison Manufacturing Company, 'Electrocuting an Elephant' (1903), U.S. Library of Congress paper print collection; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrocuting_an_Elephant (event section, citing period press and LoC holdings); onthisday.com/photos/topsy-the-elephant-is-electrocuted (compiled from period sources: owners' hanging plan blocked by the SPCA)
Falsifiability: Evidence that the execution did not occur as reported — e.g., proof the film depicts a different event, or period records contradicting the date, place, or owners' agency.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1903-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9814,40.5755,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1903</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wrights' first powered, controlled flights, December 17, 1903</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Contemporaneous photograph of the event, same-day documentary telegram, five named eyewitnesses, the brothers' own records, and the surviving aircraft — multiple independent primary lines.
Layer: real · Place: Kill Devil Hills, North Carolina (Wright Brothers National Memorial)
Dates: 1903 → 1903 (confidence: high)

At Kill Devil Hills, North Carolina, on December 17, 1903, Orville and Wilbur Wright made four sustained, powered, controlled, man-carrying flights (12 seconds/120 feet to 59 seconds/852 feet), photographed at the moment of liftoff and reported by telegram the same day — the first such flights in history. This is the timeline's first aviation record.

Earliest attestation: Contemporaneous: the December 17, 1903 telegram to Bishop Milton Wright (Western Union via the Kitty Hawk weather bureau); the brothers' diaries; John T. Daniels' 5x7-inch glass-plate photograph of the first liftoff (negative deposited in the Library of Congress, 1949)
Sources: The December 17, 1903 telegram: 'Success four flights Thursday morning all against twenty-one mile wind... longest 57 seconds inform Press home Christmas' — contemporaneous documentary record of the flights the same morning; John T. Daniels photograph of Orville's liftoff, 10:35 a.m.: the famous image annotated by Smithsonian Magazine's photo analysis — the elevator position, the wing-warping cradle, the bench and footprints in the sand all consistent with a genuine, controlled liftoff (the machine had traveled only ~20 feet over the ground when the shutter tripped, and flew on); Five named eyewitnesses from the Kill Devil Hills lifesaving station and town: John T. Daniels, W. S. Dough, A. D. Etheridge, W. C. Brinkley of Manteo, and Johnny Moore of Nags Head; The original 1903 Flyer itself, preserved: loaned to London's Science Museum 1928-1948 after the Smithsonian feud, returned 1948, installed in the Smithsonian's Arts and Industries Building — the artifact survives for inspection; NASA, Space History Photo: The Wright Brothers First Heavier-than-air Flight — confirms the flight parameters (12 s / 120 ft first flight; 59 s / 852 ft final flight; ~10 ft maximum altitude)
Falsifiability: A 1903-dated photograph of the Flyer elsewhere on December 17, or a published diary entry from one of the five eyewitnesses contradicting the flights — neither exists</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1903-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.52,35.21,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1903</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1906 San Francisco Earthquake</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Instrumentally recorded on multiple continents' seismographs, contemporaneously reported, and investigated by the Lawson commission within two years.
Layer: real · Place: San Andreas Fault, northern California (epicenter debated; most recent USGS estimate near Mussel Rock, Daly City)
Dates: 1906-04-18 → 1906-04-18 (confidence: high)

On 18 April 1906 at 05:12 local time, a ~Mw 7.9 strike-slip earthquake ruptured ~296 miles of the San Andreas Fault, shaking northern California from Eureka to the Salinas Valley at up to Mercalli XI (Extreme).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary seismograms (Kobe, Göttingen, Bridston), USGS instrumental record, the 1908 Lawson report of the State Earthquake Investigation Commission
Sources: USGS / ANSS ComCat — Mw 7.9, epicenter 37.75N 122.55W, depth ~8 km; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1906_San_Francisco_earthquake (UTC time 1906-04-18 13:12:27; Mw 7.9; MMI XI; 05:12 local); SEAONC Legacy Project — USGS relocated epicenter near Mussel Rock off Daly City; ~26 miles surface rupture; ~3,000 lives
Falsifiability: Contemporary seismograms are the anchors; a demonstration that the 1906 records were misattributed would move this record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1906-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.55,37.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1906</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Santos-Dumont's 14-bis: the first officially observed powered flights in Europe, 1906</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Officially observed by sporting authorities, publicly performed, contemporaneously reported in the press, prize-awarded — multiple independent contemporary attestations.
Layer: real · Place: Bagatelle field, Bois de Boulogne, Paris
Dates: 1906 → 1906 (confidence: high)

On October 23 and November 12, 1906, Alberto Santos-Dumont flew his 14-bis at Bagatelle field, Paris — officially observed, publicly announced powered flights, taking off under its own power without a launch rail — the first powered, man-carrying flights in Europe and the first to be widely witnessed by the public and sporting authorities of their day.

Earliest attestation: Contemporaneous 1906 French press coverage; the Aéro-Club de France's official observation and prize records; the 14-bis survives in the Musée de l'Air et de l'Espace, Le Bourget
Sources: Aéro-Club de France records: the Archdeacon prize and the Prix d'Aviation awards for the 1906 Bagatelle flights — official observation by sporting authorities; Contemporaneous 1906 French press coverage of the October 23 and November 12 Bagatelle flights; The 14-bis itself, preserved at the Musée de l'Air et de l'Espace, Le Bourget — the artifact survives
Falsifiability: Proof the 1906 Bagatelle hops were towed, launched, or fabricated — the official observers, the prize money, and the press photographs make this essentially closed</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1906-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.26,48.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1906</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1906 San Francisco Firestorms</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Converging independent sources: the Lawson report, contemporary Army reports, Genthe's photographs, the UC Berkeley Seismology Lab, and Hansen &amp; Condon's 1989 document re-examination all place the fires as the dominant destroyer and the true death toll at ~3,000+, against the official 498.
Layer: real · Place: San Francisco, California (burned district: ~490 city blocks)
Dates: 1906-04-18 → 1906-04-21 (confidence: high)

The three-to-four-day firestorms after the 18 April 1906 earthquake — 30+ fires from ruptured gas mains, overturned stoves, and dynamited firebreaks — destroyed roughly 25,000-28,000 buildings on ~490 city blocks, doing at least 80% (some estimates up to ~95%) of the disaster's total destruction, with the death toll now put at ~3,000+ against the official 498.

Earliest attestation: The 1908 Lawson report of the State Earthquake Investigation Commission; contemporary Army Signal Corps reports (Capt. Leonard D. Wildman); Arnold Genthe's photographs
Sources: Wikipedia: fires burned out of control, 'far more destructive' than the quake; ~25,000 buildings on 490 blocks; 80-95% destruction band; fire chief Dennis T. Sullivan died in the quake; Ham and Eggs fire (Hayes &amp; Gough); Scientific American: 'responsible for 90 percent of the 28,000 destroyed buildings'; water mains broken, hydrants useless; Genthe's camera account; Hotaling's Whiskey surviving; Foxweather / UC Berkeley Seismology Lab: &gt;3,000 killed, ~225,000 homeless, $400M (1906) damage; 'most of the damage... was caused by another factor: fire'; Fradkin review: Hansen &amp; Condon, Denial of Disaster (1989) — morgue/coroner re-examination proved ~3,000+ dead vs official ~498; insurers indemnified fire not earthquake, so destruction was systematically blamed on the fires (some owners fired their own damaged buildings)
Falsifiability: Held at CORROBORATED (not VERIFIED) because the destroyed-by-fire share is an estimated band (80-95%), not a witnessed count — though every independent source agrees the fires dominated</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1906-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.4194,37.7749,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1906</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1907 international reply coupon — the world's first prepaid-reply instrument</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1906 Rome resolution, the 1 October 1907 first issue, the Australasian proposal, and the 25-gold-centime exchange value are corroborated across the UPU International Bureau's own centenary account and independent postal-history accounts; held below VERIFIED on the secondary transmission (congress proceedings not inspected this pass).
Layer: real · Place: Bern, Switzerland — UPU headquarters (resolution at the Rome 1906 congress)
Dates: 1907 → 1907 (confidence: high)

The Universal Postal Union's 1906 congress in Rome created the International Reply Coupon: a small paper document (UPU form C22, later CN 01) sold by post offices against cash payment, which the buyer could send to any UPU country so the recipient could exchange it for postage stamps (initially stamps valued at 25 gold centimes) to pay return postage. The proposal came from the British Colonies of Australasia, which wanted a domestic reply-prepayment practice extended worldwide. The first coupons were put on sale on 1 October 1907 (UPU International Bureau). Seven design types have followed; the U.S. discontinued sales on 27 January 2013 and Royal Mail on 31 December 2011, but exchange remains mandatory for UPU members.

Earliest attestation: UPU Congress of Rome proceedings, 1906; UPU International Bureau announcement of first sale, 1 October 1907
Sources: UPU International Bureau, 'The international reply coupon turns 100' (via postandparcel.info) — 1906 Rome congress created the IRC at the request of the British Colonies of Australasia; first introduced 1 October 1907; exchangeable for stamps valued at 25 gold centimes; 121 postal administrations selling at centenary; Wikipedia, 'International reply coupon' — introduced 1906 at the UPU congress in Rome; first on sale 1 October 1907; US discontinued sales 27 January 2013; Royal Mail stopped 31 December 2011; exchange mandatory for UPU members; René Hillesum, 'The international reply coupon' (netpha.nl) — introduced at the Congress of Rome 1906 (27 May), available 1 October 1907; UPU designs; current design period 2017–2021
Falsifiability: UPU congress proceedings showing no 1906 reply-coupon resolution, or a provably dated IRC from before October 1907 with sound provenance</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.45,46.95,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Marie Lloyd's 'Let her through, girls' picket-line quip to Belle Elmore</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Attested in the Lloyd biographical tradition but never in a contemporary 1907 documentary source; the identity Belle Elmore = Cora Crippen is independently firm, the picket-line exchange is anecdote-grade. Hence CLAIMED, not DEBUNKED — the anecdote's provenance is biographical, not invented.
Layer: real · Place: London (the Bedford picket, per the tradition)
Dates: 1907-01-21 → 1907-02-28 (confidence: traditional)

During the 1907 music-hall strike, Marie Lloyd — on picket duty — spotted Belle Elmore crossing the picket line and shouted 'Let her through, girls — she'll close the place faster than we can.' Belle Elmore was Cora Crippen, later murdered by her husband Dr. Hawley Crippen.

Earliest attestation: Lloyd biographical/anecdote tradition (20th-century biographies and music-hall histories); the Elmore=Crippen identification is independently documented in the Crippen case records
Sources: Camden Guides — 'Marie Lloyd – Queen of the Music Hall' (secondary; the quip, the Bedford picket, the Elmore identification); Over the Footlights — 1907 chronology PDF (secondary; the 'Don't be daft! Let her in' variant of the Lockhart's elephants incident)
Falsifiability: A contemporary January–February 1907 newspaper or diary report of the exchange would raise this to CORROBORATED; documentary proof Elmore never crossed a 1907 picket would drop it</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1907 Music Hall Strike (the Music Hall War)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The strike's existence, scale, launch meeting, and Askwith arbitration are attested across independent secondary lineages; held below VERIFIED because the headline numbers (22 theatres, ~2,000 pickets, duration) travel through the union's own reporting and popular encyclopedia lineages, and the 'broadly favourable' reading comes from Lloyd's biographical tradition.
Layer: real · Place: London — Holborn Empire, Scala Theatre, Surrey Theatre, 22 picketed halls
Dates: 1907-01-20 → 1907-02-28 (confidence: medium)

In January–February 1907, London music-hall performers, musicians and stagehands struck across 22 theatres, organized by the Variety Artistes' Federation (founded 18 February 1906), ending in arbitration chaired by George Askwith with a resolution broadly favourable to the performers — national agreements on codes of conduct, contracts and dispute resolution.

Earliest attestation: The Performer (the VAF's weekly, launched 1906) and contemporary press; modern accounts via union/encyclopedia lineages
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Music Hall Strike of 1907' (secondary; union-lineage numbers: 22 theatres, ~2,000 on picket lines); Wikipedia — 'Variety Artistes' Federation' (secondary; founded 18 Feb 1906, ~4,000 members within weeks, Askwith arbitration, national agreements); Trade Union Ancestors — 'Marie Lloyd and the music hall strike of 1907' (secondary; Lloyd's funding and picket role, the Lloyd quote); Over the Footlights — 1907 chronology PDF (secondary; four-week duration claim; the 90 writs, 40 by Walter Gibbons; strikers' demands list); Today in London entertainment history — '1907: striking performers launch the Music Hall War' (secondary; VAF founding memorandum; December 1906 Brixton Hippodrome/Empress lockout; Surrey Theatre mass meeting); Feldner, K. — 'Music Hall and the Age of Resistance' (McMaster M.A. thesis, 2019; secondary academic; strike context; Lloyd 'one of the leaders' framing carried in competing hypotheses)
Falsifiability: VAF minute books, George Askwith's arbitration papers, or the 90 breach-of-contract writs' court records contradicting the scope, dates, or outcome would regrade this record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The twenty-six-year detention of Mary Mallon</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The confinement facts are multiply attested: identified 1907 by Soper; first confinement 1907-1910; release on the no-cooking promise; the 'Mrs. Brown' alias; the 1915 Sloane Hospital outbreak leading to re-confinement; confinement until her death in 1938 (National Geographic: 'a quarter-century of quarantine'; Smithsonian: 'more than two decades'; her 1909 letter quoted in the Smithsonian account); at least 51 cases and 3 deaths attributed through her (National Geographic); nine mourners at St. Luke's in the Bronx (National Geographic). Held below VERIFIED because exact case counts vary across sources ('at least 51,' 'probably much higher') and because the why of the indefinite detention — public-health necessity vs. class/gender discrimination — is interpretation carried as competing hypotheses, not fact. The singularity of the punishment is the record's core: the only carrier confined indefinitely.
Layer: real · Place: North Brother Island, East River, New York City
Dates: 1907 → 1938 (confidence: high)

Mary Mallon was confined on North Brother Island from 1907 to 1910 and again from 1915 until her death in 1938 — the only identified typhoid carrier detained indefinitely.

Earliest attestation: George Soper's 1907 identification work; New York City Health Department records; the 1909 suit (Mallon's letter to her lawyer: 'a peep show for every body,' 'the kidnapped woman'); press coverage 1907 onward
Sources: National Geographic, 'Typhoid Mary's tragic tale exposed the health impacts of super-spreaders' — sent back to North Brother Island permanently 1915; died there of a stroke in 1938 'after a quarter-century of quarantine'; 'at least 51 people caught typhoid through Mallon, and three died'; nine people attended her funeral at St. Luke's in the Bronx; Smithsonian Magazine, 'The Frightening Legacy of Typhoid Mary' — died 1938 'after more than two decades on North Brother Island'; her 1909 letter to a lawyer: 'a peep show for every body,' 'the kidnapped woman'; Colgrove: 'the million-dollar question that nobody can answer'; Wikipedia, 'Mary Mallon' — 1907 identification; first confinement 1907-1910; release on promise; 'Mrs. Brown' alias; 1915 Sloane Hospital outbreak and re-confinement; death 1938; 'the only one confined indefinitely'; Wichita State Fairmount Folio journal article (citing Leavitt, Typhoid Mary, 35) — health department followed five carriers who agreed to surgery and 'not one of them had ceased to shed typhoid bacilli'; U.S. Public Health Service hygienist Milton L. Rosenau: 'it is not necessary to imprison the bacillus carrier. It is sufficient to restrict the activities of such an individual'
Falsifiability: Department records showing Mallon was released and never re-confined, or that another carrier was confined as long or longer, would move the grade. The exact case count attributed to her is the soft edge — sources range from 'at least 51' upward.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.898,40.7967,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Peary's 1909 North Pole claim — the expedition is documented; the exact point stays contested (6 April 1909)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Split grade: the EXPEDITION is VERIFIED — the Roosevelt, the Cape Sheridan departure, the sledge journey, and the return are documented on Peary's own records and the Inughuit party's testimony. The EXACT-POINT claim ('stood at 90 degN on 6 April 1909') is CORROBORATED — not VERIFIED: the National Geographic Society's 1989 reanalysis (ocean-depth soundings + flag-pole shadow geometry) places the party within ~5 miles, which corroborates a near-Pole arrival; but Wally Herbert's 1989 expeditionary arithmetic (34-46 miles/day vs 6.3) and Bryce's 1996 resolution both argue the party fell short — and Peary's refusal to open his records to third-party review keeps the corroboration conditional. CORROBORATED is the honest grade for a contested near-hit: supported by one serious reanalysis, denied by two serious ones.
Layer: real · Place: North Pole (claimed arrival point, vicinity)
Dates: 1908-07-06 → 1909-04-06 (confidence: high)

Robert E. Peary claimed he reached the North Pole on 6 April 1909, traveling by dogsled from the Roosevelt with Matthew Henson and four Inughuit men. The expedition itself is documented to the day; whether his party stood at the exact point 90 degN remains contested by serious scholarship.

Earliest attestation: Peary's wired dispatch to The New York Times, September 1909, from the far north of Canada.
Sources: Wikipedia 'Peary's Expedition (1908-1909)': Herbert (The Noose of Laurels, 1989) — 34 mpd outbound / 46 return vs Cagni 6.3 mpd, conclusion ~50 miles short with falsified data; Bryce (1996) — neither reached, Peary ~160 km short; Crocker Land invention (1906 expedition diary: no land visible) named as Peary's documented fabrication for context; BBC Science Focus, 'Who really discovered the North Pole?': NGS 1989 reanalysis — photographs, ocean-depth soundings, data consistent with within 8 km of the true pole; Herbert 1968-69 crossing, Pole reached 6 April 1969; National Press Club, 'Club served as forum for debate over first to reach North Pole': NGS reanalysis — soundings put Peary within 60 miles at the continental shelf drop-off; flag-pole shadow geometry put the claim within five miles; 'good enough for government work'; HNN, 'North Pole pioneer Ralph Plaisted dies at 80': Cook announced 21 April 1908, Peary wired NYT days later claiming 6 April 1909; Peary declared Cook's claim fraudulent; modern conclusion — 'though both made formidable journeys, they more than likely never got close to the pole'
Falsifiability: A verified independent fix at 90 degN for 6 April 1909; or conclusive demonstration that Peary's sounding and photograph records cannot place him within the claimed radius — the NGS/Herbert dispute in evidence form.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1908-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,90.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1908</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sheaffer's 1908 lever filler</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1908 patent grant, 1912 first production, and 1 January 1913 incorporation are corroborated across Wikipedia, prabook, and Glenn's Pens; the 'single stroke fills the reservoir' mechanism and the 'eliminated the eye-dropper' function are multiply attested; held below VERIFIED on the secondary transmission of the patent text.
Layer: real · Place: Fort Madison, Iowa, USA — the back room of Sheaffer's jewelry store, then the Sheaffer factory
Dates: 1908 → 1913 (confidence: high)

Walter A. Sheaffer patented the lever-filling fountain pen in August 1908: a slim metal lever on the barrel depressed the internal ink sac directly, filling the pen from the ink bottle with a single stroke — eliminating the eye-dropper. The first Sheaffer-marked pens were produced in 1912; the W.A. Sheaffer Pen Company was incorporated 1 January 1913 at Fort Madison, Iowa ($35,000 capital), and the lever became the industry-standard filler, displacing Conklin's crescent.

Earliest attestation: US lever-filler patent granted August 1908 (Walter A. Sheaffer); incorporation 1 January 1913
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Sheaffer' — founded by Walter A. Sheaffer in Fort Madison, Iowa; incorporated 1913 'to exploit his invention of a lever-filling fountain pen'; prabook, 'Walter Sheaffer' — 'In August 1908 Walter Sheaffer received a patent for a lever filling device for a fountain pen. The new device eliminated the need to refill a pen with an eyedropper'; incorporated January 1, 1913 for $35,000; $17,500 first-year profit; the Kraker infringement suit, won after several years; Glenn's Pens, 'Sheaffer — Pen Companies' — 'known for... the first practical lever filling fountain pen. That earned Walter Sheaffer a U.S. patent in August of 1908'; 'A single stroke of a slim metal lever is used to fill the pen reservoir directly from the ink bottle'; pens started 1912; Fountain Pen Network, 'Fountain Pen Chronology' — 1908: Walter A. Sheaffer gets the first patent for his lever filler; 1912: first fountain pens marked Sheaffer produced; 1913: Sheaffer founded as W. A. Sheaffer Pen Company; 1916: Parker introduces the button filler
Falsifiability: The August-1908 patent grant is the load-bearing date; the grade only falls if the patent proves misattributed (e.g. to Kraker, the litigant) rather than to Sheaffer — the lost-then-won Kraker suit is itself evidence the patent was Sheaffer's</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1908-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-91.3145,40.631,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1908</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Amundsen reaches the South Pole, 14 December 1911 — VERIFIED</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Double-attested by the two competitors themselves: Amundsen's logs, three days of sextant observations, and photographs — and the defeated party's own diary, which records finding Amundsen's tent and flag at the Pole. The Norwegian claim is thus corroborated by the British adversary's primary record, the strongest possible attestation structure. The search party's recovery of Scott's diary (12 November 1912) preserves the losing side's contemporaneous account.
Layer: real · Place: South Pole, Antarctica — the 1911 marker site (Polheim); longitude undefined at the pole
Dates: 1911-12-14 → 1912-01-17 (confidence: dated)

Roald Amundsen's Norwegian party reached the geographic South Pole on 14 December 1911 — the first humans to do so — beating Robert Falcon Scott's British Terra Nova party (which arrived c. 17 January 1912 and perished on the return) by roughly five weeks. This is the Race to the Pole, settled by the logs.

Earliest attestation: Amundsen's expedition logs and sextant measurements (14–17 December 1911, three days of observations to fix the pole) + photographs; Scott's diary entry ('The Pole. Yes, but under very different circumstances from those expected... Great God! This is an awful place'), recovered by the search party 12 November 1912
Sources: Roald Amundsen, The South Pole: An Account of the Norwegian Antarctic Expedition in the Fram, 1910–1912 (1912) — logs, sextant observations 14–17 Dec 1911, photographs; Robert Falcon Scott's diary — the Pole entry (17 January 1912) recording the discovery of Amundsen's tent; recovered by the search party 12 November 1912; General survey consensus on the 14 December 1911 / 17 January 1912 dates and the five-week margin
Falsifiability: Sextant logs contradicting Amundsen's fix, or Scott's diary being a later fabrication — both examined repeatedly for a century without contradiction</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1911-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,-90.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1911</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The whole nine yards: attestation (1946-1962)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent dated prints in the documented antedating chain (Shapiro/Zimmer/Taylor-Blake); the six-yard variant independently corroborates the arbitrary-number reading.
Layer: real · Place: Lexington / Kentucky print culture
Dates: 1912 → 1962 (confidence: high)

The phrase 'the whole nine yards' is attested in American print from the mid-20th century: 1946-51 in a Lexington, Kentucky newspaper; July 1956 and January 1957 in Kentucky Happy Hunting Ground (Kentucky Dept. of Fish and Wildlife Resources); 1962 in Michigan's Voices and a Car Life letter. A 1912 Kentucky 'whole six yards' variant shows the number was never fixed — it is a reified-number idiom, not a measurement.

Earliest attestation: Kentucky newspapers, 1912 ('the whole six yards'); the nine-yard form 1946-51 (Lexington, KY)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Nine yards' — post-war usages 1946-51 (Lexington, KY), 1956/57 KHHG, 1962 Michigan's Voices / Car Life; 1912-1927 'six yards' variants; Fred R. Shapiro, Yale Alumni Magazine — the antedating sequence: 1962 (Goranson, Taylor-Blake) → 1956/57 KHHG (Taylor-Blake, verified by the Kentucky Afield editor); Ben Zimmer, Visual Thesaurus 'Word Routes' — 'something of a Holy Grail among word sleuths'; no definitive origin story
Falsifiability: A securely dated, contextually confirmed earlier print of the idiomatic phrase</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1912-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-84.5037,38.0406,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1912</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Titanic's wireless room: the distress calls that brought the Carpathia (1912)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources converge: Bride's sworn Senate testimony, Cottam's Carpathia reception and Rostron's diversion, the inquiries' published records, and the 1912 Radio Act itself as legislative consequence. The CQD-then-SOS sequence (Bride: 'Send S.O.S., it's the new call, and it may be your last chance to send it'), the 58-mile distance, the 705 survivors, and the California's silenced set are all multiply attested.
Layer: real · Place: North Atlantic, Titanic wreck position (distress position transmitted was ~41°46'N 50°14'W, ~13 miles east of the actual wreck)
Dates: 1912-04-14 → 1912-04-15 (confidence: high)

On the night of 14-15 April 1912, RMS Titanic's wireless operators Jack Phillips and Harold Bride transmitted distress calls — first Marconi's CQD, then also the international SOS at Bride's suggestion — that reached RMS Carpathia 58 miles away; the Carpathia altered course and rescued the survivors in the lifeboats (705). The disaster led to the Radio Act of 1912 mandating round-the-clock wireless watches aboard ships.

Earliest attestation: Bride's surviving testimony (U.S. Senate Inquiry, Day 2, Harold Bride); Carpathia operator Harold Cottam's reception log; contemporary press and inquiry records, April-May 1912
Sources: National Geographic, 'Why Titanic's first call for help wasn't an SOS signal' — Phillips/Bride as Marconi employees; CQD as the Marconi standard since 1904; SOS ratified 1908; 'Shut up! I am busy' to the Californian's ice warning; Wikipedia, 'Jack Phillips (wireless operator)' — 23:40 strike; distress sequence shortly after midnight (CQD, then SOS at Bride's suggestion); Carpathia contacted; power gone shortly after 02:00; last seen separating from Bride; body not recovered; U.S. Senate Inquiry, Day 2 — Harold Bride testimony (via Encyclopedia Titanica / secondary summaries) — the primary survivor's sworn account of the wireless room, the calls sent, and the ships contacted
Falsifiability: No single falsifier is available; the record would be overturned by contemporaneous wireless logs showing no distress transmission from Titanic and no Carpathia diversion — the opposite is documented many times over.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1912-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-49.9469,41.7325,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1912</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Death Valley's 134°F (1913) as the reliable world record (disputed)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The WMO lists 134°F as the official world record — but the word 'reliably' in the claim is exactly what the experts dispute. Weather historian Christopher Burt and climatologist William Reid (2016) concluded the reading was 'essentially not possible from a meteorological perspective' — the station's July peak ran 18°F above its monthly average while neighboring stations ran only 7–11°F above theirs — attributing it to observer error. A 2026 Bulletin of the AMS study estimated the true peak near 120°F. The WMO itself now describes Death Valley's 130°F readings of 2020 and 2021 as the highest 'since at least the 1930s.' The record survives by institutional inertia — 'a correction needs an owner, and this one has never found one' — not by surviving scrutiny. Official, but not corroborated as reliable.
Layer: real · Place: Greenland Ranch (Furnace Creek), Death Valley, California
Dates: 1913-07-10 → 1913-07-10 (confidence: high)

The highest air temperature ever reliably measured on Earth is 134°F (56.7°C), recorded at Greenland Ranch, Death Valley, California, on 10 July 1913.

Earliest attestation: The Greenland Ranch station log, 10 July 1913 (U.S. Weather Bureau)
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine, 'How the Record for Hottest Temperature Ever Was Refuted' (on El Azizia): 'The WMO assessment is that the highest recorded surface temperature of 56.7°C (134°F) was measured on 10 July 1913 at Greenland Ranch (Death Valley) CA USA.' Notes Burt's skepticism of the Death Valley measurement as well.; Weather.com (2020): 'a detailed blog published in October 2016 by climatologist William Reid and weather historian Christopher Burt concluded the 134-degree Death Valley high in 1913 "was essentially not possible from a meteorological perspective, using an officially sanctioned USWB shelter and thermometer and following proper procedures observationally." They attributed these extreme temperatures in 1913 to observer error.'; Space Daily (2026): the Burt–Reid case — 'The monthly peak at Greenland Ranch was 18 degrees above its July average, while nearby stations were only 7 to 11 degrees above theirs'; 'A study published this year in the Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society pushed the point further, estimating that the true peak that day was about 120 degrees Fahrenheit'; the WMO 'has described Death Valley's much more recent readings of 130 degrees, reached in both 2020 and 2021, as the highest recorded anywhere in the world since at least the 1930s'; 'A correction needs an owner, and this one has never found one.'; WUOT/NPR (2020): 'Many experts are referring to Sunday's eye-catching high as being the hottest "reliably recorded" temperature, reflecting widespread doubts about the validity of the 1913 record of 134 degrees.' Burt: 'I look back at that record with a little bit of skepticism... he and a colleague "conclude with certainty that the actual hottest temperature ever reliably measured on Earth is 54°C (129.2°F), shared by Death Valley on June 30, 2013 and Mitribah, Kuwait on July 21, 2016."'
Falsifiability: A WMO committee ruling overturning the record (as happened with El Azizia 2012), or the discovery of the original instrument/observer logs resolving the discrepancy</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1913-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-116.8668,36.4623,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1913</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Palace Theatre's slow exit from vaudeville, 1927–1952</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The flagship's slow ending is multiply documented: Wikipedia's Palace Theatre article (specific weeks: last straight vaudeville July 9, 1932, featuring Louis Sobol; last accompaniment November 12, 1932, Nick Lucas and Hal Le Roy; reversion to vaudeville-before-film January 7, 1933), Playbill's theatre archive (1913 opening; Judy Garland's 16 October 1951 triumph and 19-week run), and BroadwayWorld's history (the RKO merger context; 1950s revivals by Sinatra, Kaye, Lewis, Hutton). The five-year gap between October 1927 and November 1932 is the myth's fatal measurement: vaudeville's flagship did not fall overnight.
Layer: real · Place: Palace Theatre, 160 West 47th Street, New York City
Dates: 1913-03-24 → 1952-01-01 (confidence: high)

New York's Palace Theatre (opened 24 March 1913, the flagship of American vaudeville — 'playing the Palace' the industry's pinnacle) played straight vaudeville until July 1932 — five years after The Jazz Singer — then a mixed vaudeville-before-film policy (last vaudeville accompaniment 12 November 1932), converted to a movie palace under RKO, flipped back to vaudeville-before-film on 7 January 1933, alternated formats for two years, showed films almost exclusively 1935–1949, and hosted vaudeville revivals in the 1950s (Judy Garland, 16 October 1951, 19 weeks).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: 1913 opening coverage and Keith-Albee booking records; 1932 trade reporting (Variety); RKO business records; the theater's own later histories (Playbill's 'At This Theatre' archive).
Sources: Wikipedia — Palace Theatre (New York City): 1932 transition detail (last straight vaudeville July 9, 1932; last accompaniment Nov 12, 1932; Jan 7, 1933 reversion; 1935–1949 film-exclusive; two-year alternation); Playbill — Palace Theatre 'At This Theatre' (opened 1913; Martin Beck/Keith/Albee; Judy Garland Oct 16, 1951, 19 weeks; 1950s personal-appearance revivals); BroadwayWorld (2018) — Palace Theatre, the Valhalla of Vaudeville (1929 Kennedy-engineered KAO+RCA+FBO merger into RKO; combination bills tried on and off; 1950s revivals; Citizen Kane 1941 premiere)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that the Palace ceased live vaudeville performance in 1928 (or before 1932), or that it never returned to mixed bills after November 1932.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1913-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9847,40.7614,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1913</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Christmas Truce of 1914</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by independent primary sources on both sides — British and German letters, diaries, and war diaries written in December 1914, plus contemporary press and the subsequent official bans on fraternization, which confirm the event by trying to stop its recurrence.
Layer: real · Place: Frelinghien / Messines / Ploegsteert sector, Western Front, French-Belgian border
Dates: 1914-12-24 → 1914-12-25 (confidence: high)

In late December 1914, on the Western Front in Flanders, soldiers of the British Expeditionary Force and the Imperial German Army initiated a series of localized, unofficial truces: singing carols across the lines on Christmas Eve, meeting in no-man's-land on Christmas Day, exchanging gifts and food, posing for photographs together, and organizing joint burial parties for the dead. The truces were not universal (fighting continued in other sectors), were unofficial (high commands disapproved and issued anti-fraternization orders afterward), and made no difference to the war's course.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary letters and diaries, December 1914 (both sides); war diaries; contemporary press (The Times, Daily Mirror) from late December 1914
Sources: Captain Robert Hamilton (Royal Warwickshire Regiment) diary — basis of 'Meet at Dawn, Unarmed' (co-authored by his grandson); Lt. Kurt Zehmisch (134th Infantry Regiment) diary — among the best German-side accounts: orders that no shot be fired on the holy night and Christmas Day; Imperial War Museums collections — letters, diaries, and Ernie Williams's recorded interview (Messines sector kickabout testimony); Michael Jürgs, Der kleine Frieden im großen Krieg (2003) — collation of German diary fragments; Dan Richards (IWM, head of documents and sound) on the truces as localized 'mini truces'; Smithsonian Magazine, 'The Story of the WWI Christmas Truce' — the 133rd Royal Saxon Regiment's War History on the Saxon-Scots fraternization
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that no fraternization occurred along the front in December 1914 — no such evidence exists; the documentary record on both sides confirms it</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.93,50.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Christmas Truce Kickabouts (December 1914)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent attestation on both sides: Ernie Williams (IWM interview, Messines sector) — 'it wasn't a team game in any sense of the word... a kickabout... no score, no tally at all. It was simply a melee'; a Bedfordshire Regiment report of 'teams of about fifty a side played until this was unfortunately punctured'; Niemann's caps-as-goals account; Kurt Zehmisch's diary context. Multiple witnesses, multiple sectors, same shape of event.
Layer: real · Place: Western Front, French-Belgian border sectors (Frelinghien, Messines, Ploegsteert among the documented areas)
Dates: 1914-12-24 → 1914-12-26 (confidence: high)

During the Christmas truce of December 1914, in multiple sectors of the Western Front, British and German soldiers engaged in informal football kickabouts in no-man's-land — improvised games with real or makeshift balls (bully-beef tins, straw-stuffed sandbags), caps for goalposts, frozen ground, dozens to hundreds of men joining in, no teams, no officials, no kept score.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary letters and diaries, December 1914 (both sides); IWM recorded interviews with veterans (e.g., Ernie Williams)
Sources: Ernie Williams, 19-year-old English soldier near Messines — recorded interview (IWM): 'From somewhere, somehow, this football appeared... it was just a general kickabout... There was no score, no tally at all. It was simply a melee.'; Bedfordshire Regiment report: 'a keen sportsman produced a ball, and teams of about fifty a side played until this was unfortunately punctured'; Lt. Johannes Niemann (133rd Saxon Infantry Regiment), regimental history: 'Suddenly a Tommy came with a football... We marked the goals with our caps... the Fritzes beat the Tommies 3-2'; Dan Richards (IWM, head of documents and sound): 'several smaller, much less formalised kickabouts took place in various parts of No Man's Land'; Prof. Mark Connelly (University of Kent): 'I think it highly likely that someone, somewhere did bring out a ball and a bit of a kick about took place'
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that no football of any kind was played during the truce period — no such evidence exists; independent attestations on both sides confirm kickabouts</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.93,50.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Endurance expedition and the all-28-survived rescue — VERIFIED</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested primary record: the ship's logs, Hurley's glass-plate photographs of the sinking and the ice camps, Shackleton's own published account (South, 1919), Worsley's navigation notes for the James Caird, and multiple survivor memoirs — with external institutional corroboration (the Chilean navy's Yelcho under Captain Luis Pardo, whose government was formally thanked). The wreck's 2022 discovery at the predicted position closes the archaeological loop.
Layer: real · Place: Endurance wreck site, Weddell Sea (found 5 March 2022 by Endurance22 at ~3,000 m; consistent with the drift account)
Dates: 1914-08-01 → 1916-08-30 (confidence: dated)

The Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedition (1914–1917): the Endurance was beset in the Weddell Sea pack ice in January 1915, abandoned 27 October 1915, and sank 21 November 1915; the 28 men of the Weddell Sea party drifted on ice floes, reached Elephant Island 15 April 1916, and were all — every one of the 28 — brought home alive after Shackleton's James Caird open-boat voyage to South Georgia (24 April–10 May 1916) and the Chilean Yelcho's rescue on 30 August 1916.

Earliest attestation: Frank Hurley's photographs (the sinking, 21 November 1915 — taken on glass plates at −30 °C) + the expedition's ship's logs + Shackleton's South (Heinemann, 1919)
Sources: Ernest Shackleton, South: The Story of Shackleton's Last Expedition, 1914–1917 (Heinemann, 1919) — the commander's published account; Frank Hurley's glass-plate photographs — the sinking (21 Nov 1915), Ocean Camp, the ice drift — developed under expedition conditions; National Library of Australia holdings; Endurance ship's logs + Frank Worsley's navigation notes (the James Caird voyage, 24 April–10 May 1916); Wikipedia, 'Endurance (1912 ship)' — consolidated chronology: beset January 1915; abandoned 27 October 1915; sank 21 November 1915; Elephant Island 15 April 1916; James Caird departs 24 April 1916; South Georgia 10 May; Yelcho rescue 30 August 1916, 128 days after Shackleton departed; Australian Geographic, 2022 — Endurance22 expedition (Falklands Maritime Trust): wreck located 5 March 2022, 100 years to the day from Shackleton's 1922 burial; consistent with the 1915 sinking account
Falsifiability: Photographs of the wreck contradicting Hurley's sinking position (the Endurance22 expedition's 2022 wreck discovery sits consistent with the 1915 sinking account)</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-52.3333,-68.5833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gallieni's taxis: the requisition and the run, 6–7 September 1914</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources: Gallieni's order, war-ministry accounts, the preserved taxi, participants' testimony, and the museum record all attest the requisition and the run.
Layer: real · Place: Les Invalides, Paris → Nanteuil-le-Haudouin (49.14033, 2.81303), ~50 km
Dates: 1914-09-06 → 1914-09-07 (confidence: high)

On the night of 6–7 September 1914, General Joseph Gallieni, military governor of Paris, requisitioned approximately 600–630 Paris taxis (chiefly Renault AG1 Landaulets, many from the G7 company) to transport roughly 3,000–6,000 soldiers of the French 7th Division (103rd and 104th Infantry Brigades) from Les Invalides to Nanteuil-le-Haudouin (~50 km). The drivers were paid by the meter; the French war ministry put the total bill at about 70,102 francs.

Earliest attestation: Gallieni's requisition order and French military transport records, September 1914; contemporary press and participants' accounts
Sources: Musée de l'Armée, 'The Marne Taxi' (Google Arts &amp; Culture): ~630 taxis requisitioned from Les Invalides, &gt;3,000 soldiers transported ~60 miles; the preserved G7 Renault taxi; Express (2014), 'Taxi to the front and a place in history': French war ministry total Fr 70,102; first convoy ~250 taxis from La Villette via National Road 2; ~5,000 soldiers; Keegan, J., The First World War (1998) — standard scholarly treatment of the Marne and Gallieni's improvisation; Doughty, R., Pyrrhic Victory (2005) — French strategy and the 1914 campaign, the 7th Division's movement
Falsifiability: A Gallieni order countermanding the requisition, war-ministry accounts showing no taxi payments, or transport records placing the 103rd/104th brigades at the front by rail instead</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.31276,48.85707,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Niemann's 3-2 Game at Frelinghien</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The most detailed single account of a truce football game — but it is ONE man's account, written up after the war and recalled in an interview in the 1960s, not a contemporary diary entry. Niemann honestly describes a real, organized-ish game on frozen mud with caps for goals and a 3-2 result. What the account does NOT support is the legend-form fixture: nothing about Niemann's game makes it an international, eleven-a-side, ninety-minute regulation match. Attested but single-source and late: CLAIMED, held below CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Frelinghien, Western Front (opposite the British/Scottish line)
Dates: 1914-12-25 → 1914-12-25 (confidence: medium)

Lieutenant Johannes Niemann of the 133rd Saxon Infantry Regiment, opposite Scottish troops (Seaforth or Argyll &amp; Sutherland Highlanders) at Frelinghien, described a football game during the Christmas truce: a Tommy arrived with a football, caps were laid down to mark the goals, the teams organized on the frozen ground, and 'the Fritzes beat the Tommies 3-2' — a real game with a claimed score, attested by one man.

Earliest attestation: Niemann's account in the 133rd Royal Saxon Regiment's War History; a later interview in the 1960s. Late relative to the event — the regimental history is a postwar compilation, not a 1914 diary entry.
Sources: Lt. Johannes Niemann (133rd Saxon Infantry Regiment), in the regiment's War History: 'Suddenly a Tommy came with a football, kicking already and making fun, and then began a football match... We marked the goals with our caps. Teams were quickly established for a match on the frozen mud, and the Fritzes beat the Tommies 3-2.'; Niemann's 1960s interview recounting the game (Smithsonian's summary: 'in the one detailed eyewitness account that survives — albeit in an interview not given until the 1960s'); Anonymous letter, The Times, 1 January 1915: a match with the 107th Saxon Infantry Regiment, same 3-2 score — parallel attestation, not independent confirmation
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1914 German or British document describing this exact game would raise it; evidence that Niemann was not at Frelinghien would kill it — neither exists</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.84,50.72,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Assassination at Sarajevo (28 June 1914)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested contemporary event: the victims' deaths, the conspirators' trial records, eyewitness testimony, and the contemporary press all converge on the same morning.
Layer: real · Place: Sarajevo — corner of Appel Quay and Franz Josef Street, in front of Moritz Schiller's delicatessen
Dates: 1914-06-28 → 1914-06-28 (confidence: high)

On the morning of 28 June 1914, in Sarajevo, Gavrilo Princip — a 19-year-old Bosnian Serb nationalist and one of seven conspirators armed by Serbian military intelligence — shot and killed Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, and his wife Sophie, Duchess of Hohenberg, when their motorcar stopped on Franz Josef Street in front of Moritz Schiller's delicatessen.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press, 28 June 1914; the trial of Princip and his co-conspirators (Sarajevo, October 1914; transcripts published by Vojislav Bogicevic, Sarajevski atentat, 1954)
Sources: Trial transcripts: Vojislav Bogicevic (ed.), Sarajevski atentat: stenogram glavne rasprave protiv Gavrila Principa i drugova, odrzane u Sarajevu 1914 (1954); Joachim Remak, Sarajevo: The Story of a Political Murder (1959) — Princip waited outside Schiller's, where he spoke to a friend; David James Smith, One Morning in Sarajevo, June 28 1914 (2008); Christopher Clark, The Sleepwalkers: How Europe Went to War (2012); NPR/WCMU 2014 interview on the day's events
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that the shooting did not occur as documented (e.g., a different shooter, different date) — no such evidence exists in the record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.41,43.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sarajevo Motorcade Route and the Wrong Turn (28 June 1914)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The route, the grenade attack, the drivers' unbriefed continuation, and Princip's position outside Schiller's are independently corroborated by trial testimony, eyewitnesses, and contemporary press; the exact words of the Potiorek–chauffeur exchange vary by account, held honestly at CORROBORATED.
Layer: real · Place: Sarajevo — Appel Quay / Franz Josef Street corner
Dates: 1914-06-28 → 1914-06-28 (confidence: high)

Franz Ferdinand's published motorcade route ran along the Appel Quay, with conspirators stationed along it; after Nedeljko Cabrinovic's grenade bounced off the Archduke's car (10-second fuse; it exploded under the following car), the plan changed — the drivers, not briefed on the change, continued on the original route, and at the corner with Franz Josef Street the chauffeur took the wrong turn, corrected on Governor Potiorek's orders, bringing the car to a halt directly in front of Schiller's delicatessen, where Princip was standing outside (having spoken with his friend Mihajlo Pusara) on the original route — the position he should have held all along.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press, 28 June 1914; trial testimony (Remak 1959; trial transcripts via Trifkovic)
Sources: Roberta Strauss Feuerlicht (via Mike Dash, Smithsonian 2011): Schiller's delicatessen stood on the original route planned for the motorcade; the chauffeur's fatal uncertainty was caused by Oskar Potiorek shouting from the passenger seat that he should have stayed on Appel Quay; Joachim Remak (1959): the assassin waited outside Schiller's, where he spoke to a friend (Mihajlo Pusara) — cited via Dash/Trifkovic; Christopher Clark (NPR/WCMU, 2014): the motorcade route had been published; drivers carried on as before; the miscommunication took the royal couple 'right in front of Gavrilo Princip, one of the assassins who was stationed on the original path'
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that the motorcade route was secret, or that Princip was elsewhere at 10:55 — the published route and his trial statement ('in the vicinity of the Latin bridge') both contradict that</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.41,43.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>What Mata Hari's information was actually worth</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge on the negligible value of what she passed: the French could produce no definite evidence at trial, her own prosecutor later admitted doubts, Ladoux tampered with evidence against her, and the 1970s German releases document payment and codename without any military consequence.
Layer: real · Place: Madrid, Spain (German embassy station, Major Arnold Kalle) and Paris
Dates: 1915 → 1917 (confidence: high)

The intelligence Mata Hari actually transmitted — to Germany, and to France — was of negligible military value: she took German money (autumn 1915, ~20,000 francs, codename H21) and she did work for the French Deuxième Bureau, but no operation, battle, or casualty has ever been documented as a consequence of her information, and the case against her rested substantially on circumstantial and tampered evidence.

Earliest attestation: German Foreign Ministry / III B documents (released 1970s); French military-justice dossier (1917, published 2017); MI5 surveillance files (released 2014).
Sources: MI5 release 2014: Zelle admitted conducting espionage for the German Secret Service, codename H21, payments of ~20,000 francs; invisible-ink vials in her hotel suite; German documents unsealed in the 1970s: autumn 1915 recruitment, Major Roepell / Walter Nicolai / Captain Hoffmann; historians: 'she may have merely accepted money from the Germans without actually carrying out any spy duties'; Wikipedia 'Mata Hari': Ladoux's evidence tampering; prosecutor André Mornet later expressed doubts about her guilt; Clunet denied cross-examination rights; conviction 'a foregone conclusion'; warhistoryonline.com: Ladoux arrested four days after her execution for double espionage (cleared, but the charge raised the frame-up question)
Falsifiability: A documented military consequence of her transmissions — an operation compromised, a unit's loss, an intercepted report with actionable content.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1915-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.7038,40.4168,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1915</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sykes-Picot Agreement, May 1916</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested contemporary diplomatic documents — the Grey-Cambon letters, the signed map, the Russian and Italian assents — published and never denied as a document.
Layer: real · Place: The Ottoman Arab provinces (the Levant and Mesopotamia covered by the 1916 map)
Dates: 1916-05 → 1916-05 (confidence: high)

In May 1916, Britain and France secretly agreed to partition the Ottoman Arab lands into zones of direct control (blue/French, red/British) and spheres of influence (A/French, B/British), with Palestine as an international (brown) zone — the Sykes-Picot Agreement.

Earliest attestation: The letters themselves (1916); public exposure by the Bolsheviks, Moscow, 23 November 1917, reprinted in the Manchester Guardian, 26 November 1917: 'the British were embarrassed, the Arabs dismayed and the Turks delighted.'
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine, 'The Origins of the World War I Agreement That Carved Up the Middle East': terms set out in Grey's letter to Cambon, 16 May 1916; red/blue/brown zones; Bolshevik exposure 1917; affirmed at San Remo 1920, ratified by the League 1922; GlobalSecurity.org, 'Sykes-Picot Agreement - 1916' (negotiation background: Nicolson, Sykes, Picot; zones A/B, the Arab state or confederation formula); Wikipedia, 'Sykes-Picot Agreement' (secondary; used for the assent trail: Russia via Sazonov-Paleologue, Italy 1917; publication dates 23/26 November 1917)
Falsifiability: Proof the letters are forgeries or misattributed would overturn the record; none exists — the text is settled and was never denied as a document.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1916-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.0,34.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1916</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mata Hari: arrest, trial, and execution (1917)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by the French military-justice dossier, contemporary press, the firing-squad record, and the British MI5 surveillance files released in 2014.
Layer: real · Place: Fort de Vincennes, Paris, France
Dates: 1917-02-13 → 1917-10-15 (confidence: high)

Margaretha Geertruida Zelle (born 7 August 1876, Leeuwarden, Netherlands), performing as the exotic dancer Mata Hari, was arrested in Paris on 13 February 1917 on suspicion of spying for Germany, tried by a French military court on 24 July 1917, convicted, and executed by firing squad at Vincennes on 15 October 1917.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press and court records, 1917; the interrogation and trial records in the French military-justice dossier (published online by the French armed forces for the 2017 centenary).
Sources: French military-justice dossier on Mata Hari (interrogation transcripts, trial record, 1917); MI5 release, 2014: surveillance files on Zelle (intelNews.org summary); Wikipedia 'Mata Hari': arrest 13 February 1917 at the Hôtel Élysée Palace; trial 24 July; execution 15 October 1917
Falsifiability: Discovery that the arrest/trial/execution records are forgeries, or a contradicting civil record of her death on another date.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.4379,48.8482,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mae Dix 'accident' at the National Winter Garden, 1917</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED on the memoir + the documented 1917 first-raid pattern + the scholarly consensus (the striptease became burlesque's dominant ingredient by the mid-1920s); held below VERIFIED because the anecdote's sole provenance is the family memoir — the interested party's own origin story.
Layer: real · Place: National Winter Garden theatre, Houston Street at 2nd Avenue, New York City, USA
Dates: 1917 → 1917 (confidence: medium)

In 1917, on a hot night at the Minsky brothers' National Winter Garden on Houston Street, chorus performer Mae Dix walked offstage while still removing her costume — and when the crowd cheered she returned and kept removing it; the Minskys' first raid followed, and the 'accident' became the house's founding striptease legend.

Earliest attestation: Morton Minsky with Milt Machlin, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (Knopf, 1986) — the single interested source.
Sources: Morton Minsky &amp; Milt Machlin, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (Knopf, 1986); Wikipedia 'Minsky's Burlesque' / 'American burlesque' — the 1917 raid and the mid-1920s striptease dominance (secondary aggregators of the scholarly record)
Falsifiability: The Minskys' license records showing no 1917 raid, or house payroll proving Mae Dix was not in the company that year.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.99,40.7235,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Zimmermann's admission: 'I cannot deny it. It is true.' (March 1917)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently attested in contemporary press on both sides of the Atlantic within days; the quote is stable across sources; the pro-German lobby's forgery defense collapsed on the record immediately after.
Layer: real · Place: Berlin (press conference, then the Reichstag)
Dates: 1917-03-03 → 1917-03-29 (confidence: high)

Confronted with the published Zimmermann telegram, Foreign Secretary Arthur Zimmermann publicly admitted it was genuine: at a press conference on 3 March 1917 he told an American journalist, 'I cannot deny it. It is true,' and on 29 March 1917 he admitted it before the Reichstag — ending the Hearst-press and pro-German lobby's forgery defense in a single sentence.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press reports, March 1917: US newspapers, Reuters (Reichstag speech); the Hearst press's reversal documented
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Zimmermann Telegram' (citing press records): 3 March press conference — 'I cannot deny it. It is true'; 29 March Reichstag admission; Friends of the Intelligence Museum, 'The Zimmermann Telegram' (2017): Hale's question, the admission, the Reichstag lie about Carranza, the press reversal; CFR, 'Remembering the Zimmermann Telegram': the front-page publication, the authenticity questions, Zimmermann's 3 March answer settling them
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1917 press records showing Zimmermann denied the telegram instead — no such record exists; the admission is quoted in multiple independent papers</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Zimmermann telegram, 16 January – 1 March 1917</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary lines: the Room 40 decode (Hall's own logs), the State Department cable relay records, the photographed original in the UK National Archives, the published text of 1 March 1917, and Zimmermann's own public admission of authorship (3 March 1917).
Layer: real · Place: Berlin (origin) → intercepted at Room 40, London (51.50, -0.13) → delivered to Eckardt, Mexico City (19.43, -99.13)
Dates: 1917-01-16 → 1917-03-01 (confidence: high)

On 16 January 1917 German Foreign Secretary Arthur Zimmermann sent a coded telegram to Heinrich von Eckardt, the German minister in Mexico City, proposing a German-Mexican military alliance in the event the United States entered WWI: 'make war together, make peace together, generous financial support and understanding on our part that Mexico is to reconquer the lost territory in Texas, New Mexico and Arizona.' It also announced the 1 February resumption of unrestricted submarine warfare and instructed Eckardt to invite Japan to adhere via the Mexican president. The British Admiralty's Room 40 intercepted and decoded it on 17 January 1917 (Nigel de Grey, with William Montgomery; German diplomatic code 13040). After a cover story that it had been 'stolen in deciphered form in Mexico,' the text was published 1 March 1917.

Earliest attestation: The intercepted telegram itself, 16-17 January 1917; photographed German original held at The National Archives (UK); Room 40 decode sheets
Sources: The National Archives (UK): photographed German original of the Zimmermann telegram; HISTORY.com, 'The Secret History of the Zimmermann Telegram': 16 Jan handoff to Gerard; decoded 17 Jan by Nigel de Grey; Hall's cover story; published 1 March; Council on Foreign Relations, 'Remembering the Zimmermann Telegram and the U.S. Entry Into World War I': State Department cable routing; Wilson's release to newspapers; armed-ships filibuster; Tuchman, B., The Zimmermann Telegram (1958, Pulitzer 1959): the standard documentary account of the intercept, decode, and publication
Falsifiability: A proven forgery finding on the National Archives original, Room 40 logs showing no such decode, or State Department cable records showing no such transmission</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sundback's Separable Fastener (1917) — the modern zipper</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The patent is a public document; the 1915 patent model sits in the Smithsonian; the production and sales records of the Hookless Fastener Company are multiply attested.
Layer: real · Place: Meadville, Pennsylvania (Hookless Fastener Company; Talon from 1937)
Dates: 1917 → 1917 (confidence: high)

Gideon Sundback's 1917 'Separable Fastener' (US patent 1,219,881) — the interlocking-tooth slide fastener with slider — is the modern zipper: the first cheap, reliable, mass-producible design.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 1,219,881 (1917), with the 1915 patent model held by the Smithsonian National Museum of American History
Sources: US Patent 1,219,881, 'Separable Fastener' (Gideon Sundback, 1917); Smithsonian National Museum of American History — Sundback's Modern Slide Zipper Patent Model, 1915; Pennsylvania Center for the Book / Penn State — 'Zip It Up: Talon Zippers' (2009), citing Robert Friedel, Zipper: An Exploration in Novelty (1994)
Falsifiability: A dated prior-art patent or commercial product with interlocking teeth and a slider predating Sundback's 1913-1917 work</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-80.1514,41.6404,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cher Ami's flight for the Lost Battalion (4 October 1918)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The flight is VERIFIED across Signal Corps records, contemporary press, and the Smithsonian's holdings. On 4 October 1918, Major Charles W. Whittlesey's ~554 men of the 77th Division were surrounded in the Argonne; two pigeons had already been shot down; Cher Ami — the last bird — carried the message 'We are along the road parallel 276.4. Our own artillery is dropping a barrage directly on us. For heaven's sake stop it.' The bird flew ~25 miles in ~25 minutes despite a breast wound, a blinded eye, and a nearly-severed leg with the capsule dangling from the tendons. The message reached headquarters and the barrage was lifted; 194 men walked out on 7–8 October. Cher Ami received the French Croix de Guerre with Palm and has been displayed at the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History since 1921. The bird's sex is fenced separately (see evt-cher-ami-female-myth-001) — it does not affect this grade.
Layer: real · Place: Argonne Forest, France (Charlevaux ravine to division HQ)
Dates: 1918-10-04 → 1918-10-04 (confidence: high)

On 4 October 1918, the homing pigeon Cher Ami carried Major Whittlesey's message from the surrounded Lost Battalion 25 miles to division headquarters in 25 minutes, despite being shot through the breast, blinded in one eye, and nearly losing the leg carrying the message — and the message halted the friendly-artillery barrage, contributing to the relief of the battalion's survivors.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Army Signal Corps records, October 1918; contemporary press from April 1919
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine (NMAH, July 2021): the 4 October 1918 flight — Whittlesey's message text, the severed leg with the capsule on the tendons, the relief on the night of October 7; the 1919 media sensation and the sex controversy.; Library of Congress research guide (Pigeons in War): the 4 October 1918 date, the 194 survivors, the Croix de Guerre with Palm; the 1870 Paris siege context (800 pigeons, 40,000 messages).; FunFactz: 25 miles in 25 minutes; the breast wound, blinded eye, and severed leg; the capsule on the tendons; the wooden leg; the 1931 Racing Pigeon Hall of Fame induction.
Falsifiability: Signal Corps records contradicting the flight</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1918-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.9,49.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1918</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 19th Amendment ratified, August 1920</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent contemporary sources converge: Congress's own record, the state journal, and the federal certification. The Febb Burn letter story is the best-documented explanation of Burn's switch and is multiply attested in Tennessee sources.
Layer: real · Place: Nashville, Tennessee (state capitol; final ratifying vote)
Dates: 1919-06-04 → 1920-08-26 (confidence: high)

The Nineteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution — 'The right of citizens of the United States to vote shall not be denied or abridged by the United States or by any State on account of sex' — passed the Senate on 4 June 1919 (56–25), was ratified by Tennessee as the 36th state on 18 August 1920 (the House carried it by a single vote, 49–47, after 24-year-old Rep. Harry T. Burn switched at his mother Febb Burn's urging), and was certified in effect on 26 August 1920 by Secretary of State Bainbridge Colby.

Earliest attestation: Congressional Record, 66th Congress (1919); Journal of the Tennessee House of Representatives, 1920; Colby proclamation 26 Aug 1920
Sources: National Archives: the 19th Amendment (text) and the Colby certification of 26 August 1920; Congressional Record, 66th Congress — Senate vote 4 June 1919; Journal of the Tennessee House of Representatives, 1920 (pp. 91–92, the 18 August ratification vote); Tennessee State Museum: 'The War of the Roses in Tennessee' (Senate 13 Aug 1920 vote 25–4; House proceedings)
Falsifiability: A 36th-state ratification from a different state or a different date would break the record — none exists</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-86.78,36.17,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first artificial transmutation — Rutherford 1919</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The 1919 experiment is multiply documented — the papers, the scintillation data, the Manchester laboratory record — and Blackett's 1924-25 cloud-chamber series furnished conclusive photographic proof of the nitrogen-to-oxygen reaction. The gold-making successors (1941, 1980) independently confirm the principle.
Layer: real · Place: University of Manchester, England — the 1917-19 alpha-bombardment experiments
Dates: 1919 → 1919 (confidence: high)

In 1919 Ernest Rutherford achieved the first artificial transmutation of one element into another: bombarding nitrogen-14 with alpha particles produced oxygen-17 and a proton (alpha + 14N -&gt; 17O + p) — the experiment that actually did what the alchemists had promised for sixteen centuries.

Earliest attestation: Rutherford's 1919 Philosophical Magazine papers reporting proton emission under alpha bombardment of nitrogen; conventional credit fixed to 1919 with Blackett's 1924-25 cloud-chamber photographs (Proc. R. Soc.) as the conclusive demonstration.
Sources: Rutherford, 'Collision of alpha Particles with Light Atoms' series, Philosophical Magazine (1919) — proton emission observed under alpha bombardment of nitrogen; Blackett, cloud-chamber photographs 1924-25 (Proc. R. Soc. 1925) — ~400,000 tracks; two-forked tracks confirming nitrogen captures the alpha particle, ejects a proton, and leaves oxygen; Krivit, 'Who Started the Ernest Rutherford Transmutation Myth' (2019) — documents that Rutherford's 1919 papers did not themselves identify the residual atom; the Nobel Prize biography page silently dropped its 'first to deliberately transmute one element into another' phrasing without crediting Blackett; Sherr, Bainbridge &amp; Anderson, Physical Review 60:147 (1941) — mercury + neutrons -&gt; radioactive gold; the first actual gold-making; Aleklett, Morrissey, Loveland, McGaughey &amp; Seaborg, Phys. Rev. Lett. 44:1488 (1980) — bismuth bombarded with heavy ions -&gt; a few thousand gold atoms; Seaborg senior author; Scientific American, 'Fact or Fiction?: Lead Can Be Turned into Gold' — Loveland: ~$5,000/hr Bevalac beam time, ~a day used; Seaborg: 'more than one quadrillion dollars per ounce' vs ~$560/oz gold in 1980; Discover Magazine, 'Turning Lead Into Gold' — McMillan 1937 deuteron-beam artificial gold isotopes (first cyclotron-made gold); decades of accelerator gold before 1980
Falsifiability: The 1919 scintillation data and Blackett's ~400,000 cloud-chamber tracks are the evidence; their retraction or exposure as artifacts would unmake the record. Nothing of the sort is on the horizon.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.23,53.47,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The tribunal dockets as measured denominator</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Method demonstration record: the transcripts are published, multiply held, and built from captured archives — the denominator is auditable, not asserted.
Layer: real · Place: Nuremberg, Palace of Justice (regime-baseline exhibit); Constantinople (scattered-baseline exhibit)
Dates: 1919 → 1948 (confidence: high)

When a regime loses a war intact and its archives are seized whole, the denominator is measured, not guessed. The Nuremberg IMT published 42 volumes of transcript with prosecution document books built from the regime's own captured files; the Tokyo IMTFE left ~48,000 pages of transcript; the victor's archivists catalogued what the defeated had filed while they expected to win. Against that measured baseline, systematic silence reads as VIII.f-pattern: what a regime documented when it thought it would never answer for it is the expectation, and the gaps are the evidence. The Constantinople courts-martial of 1919–20 are the counter-exhibit: the empire that tried the Armenian massacres dissolved, its archives scattered across three continents, and the baseline there stays a guess — VIII.d retains jurisdiction. The tribunal dockets are the VIII.g rule's regime-baseline measured at full strength.

Earliest attestation: The Constantinople courts-martial proceedings 1919–1920 (published proceedings, incomplete survival); the Nuremberg IMT transcript 1945–1946 (42 vols., published contemporaneously)
Sources: Nuremberg IMT transcript, 42 vols. (1945–1946) + prosecution document books built from captured German archives; Tokyo IMTFE transcript (~48,000 pages, 1946–1948); Constantinople courts-martial 1919–1920 (Armenian massacres tried in the empire's own courts; archives scattered with the empire's dissolution)
Falsifiability: A demonstrated case where a tribunal's seized-archive baseline was substantially fabricated — cherry-picking at scale proven against the surviving originals — would demote this exhibit from measurement back to guess.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.0766,49.4542,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Versailles Article 231 — the clause itself</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The signed, published treaty text is a public primary document; the clause's wording, position (Part VIII Section I), and companion Article 232 are all on the page.
Layer: real · Place: Palace of Versailles, Versailles, France
Dates: 1919-06-28 → 1919-06-28 (confidence: high)

Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles (signed 28 June 1919; Part VIII, Section I, 'Reparation — General Provisions') is the opening clause of the treaty's reparations section. Its text: 'The Allied and Associated Governments affirm and Germany accepts the responsibility of Germany and her allies for causing all the loss and damage to which the Allied and Associated Governments and their nationals have been subjected as a consequence of the war imposed upon them by the aggression of Germany and her allies.' The clause contains no word 'guilt'; it names 'Germany and her allies' (not Germany alone); and it sits immediately above Article 232, which recognizes that Germany's resources are inadequate for complete reparation — i.e. Article 231 establishes legal liability while Article 232 limits the financial obligation.

Earliest attestation: The treaty text itself, 28 June 1919 (public document; League of Nations Treaty Series vol. 225).
Sources: Treaty of Versailles, Part VIII, Section I, Articles 231–232 (Great War Primary Documents Archive transcription); League of Nations Treaty Series, vol. 225 (treaty deposit)
Falsifiability: A discovered earlier or official text of the treaty omitting or altering the clause's wording, or treaty drafts showing the final wording was never adopted — the authenticated published text is the record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Treaty of Versailles (1919)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary and contemporary sources converge: the signed originals, Allied and German government records, and the treaty's registration in the League of Nations Treaty Series.
Layer: real · Place: Hall of Mirrors, Palace of Versailles
Dates: 1919-06-28 → 1919-06-28 (confidence: high)

A peace treaty between Germany and the Allied and Associated Powers, signed in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles on 28 June 1919 and entering into force on 10 January 1920, imposing territorial, military, economic, and reparations terms.

Earliest attestation: The signed treaty text itself, 28 June 1919 (440 articles); contemporary Allied and German records.
Sources: Treaty of Versailles, signed text (28 June 1919), Hall of Mirrors; Margaret MacMillan, Paris 1919: Six Months That Changed the World (2001/2002); League of Nations Treaty Series, registration of the treaty
Falsifiability: The signed originals and the League of Nations registration would have to be forgeries — an impossibility given the multi-government documentary trail.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1920-1926 settlement that actually drew the Middle East's borders</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent contemporary instruments — conference resolutions, mandate texts, and treaties — document the border-drawing process of 1920-1926; the 1926 Mosul decision is on the record.
Layer: real · Place: The post-Ottoman Middle East (the Levant, Mesopotamia, and Anatolia's frontier)
Dates: 1920-04 → 1926 (confidence: high)

The Middle East's modern state borders were drawn by the 1920-1926 settlement — the San Remo Conference (April 1920) assigning the League of Nations mandates (British Palestine and Mesopotamia; French Syria and Lebanon), the Treaty of Sevres (August 1920, superseded), the Cairo Conference (1921), the Treaty of Ankara (1921) fixing the Franco-Turkish frontier, the Treaty of Lausanne (July 1923) fixing Turkey's borders, and the 1926 Mosul settlement attaching the vilayet of Mosul to Iraq — not by the 1916 Sykes-Picot map.

Earliest attestation: San Remo Conference resolutions, April 1920; the League of Nations mandate texts (1922-23); the Treaty of Lausanne, 24 July 1923.
Sources: War in Context, 'Don't blame Sykes-Picot for the Middle East's mess' (2016): 'the actual source of the boundaries of the present Middle East can be traced to the San Remo conference... mandates over Palestine and Iraq (1920), French mandate for Syria (1923)... finalized in 1926, when the vilayet of Mosul was attached to Iraq'; Wikipedia, 'Sykes-Picot Agreement' (secondary; used for the mandate assignment at San Remo 'following the Sykes-Picot framework' and the Sevres/Lausanne succession); The Tower, 'The Map that Ruined the Middle East': France's five Levantine states from the old Ottoman vilayets (1920); Lausanne replacing Sevres and setting modern Turkey's borders
Falsifiability: Mandate texts or boundary-commission records showing the 1916 lines directly adopted as state borders would revise the grade toward the framework reading.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.0,34.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1921 Nobel Prize (photoelectric effect, not relativity)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED because the primary document — the Nobel Academy's own published motivation — names the photoelectric effect and does not mention relativity at all. The reservation of the 1921 prize (no nomination meeting the criteria) and its award in 1922 is the Academy's own recorded procedure. The presentation speech by Svante Arrhenius addressed relativity only to say it remained unproven — the committee explicitly chose the safer, experimentally confirmed work (Einstein's 1905 light-quantum paper explaining the photoelectric effect, first noticed by Hertz in 1887).
Layer: real · Place: Stockholm (the award)
Dates: 1921 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Albert Einstein's 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded 'for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect' — not for the theory of relativity.

Earliest attestation: The Nobel Foundation's official record of the Physics Prize 1921: Albert Einstein, sole laureate, motivation as quoted.
Sources: Nobel Foundation, 'The Nobel Prize in Physics 1921: Albert Einstein' — official facts page: prize year 1921, sole laureate, motivation: 'for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect'; prize reserved in 1921 (no nomination met the criteria) and awarded in 1922; Svante Arrhenius, presentation speech for the 1921 Physics prize (Nobel ceremony 1922): notes that relativity theory 'will remain [unproven] for a long time' — the committee deliberately avoided honoring the still-controversial theory directly; ScienceDaily (29 April 2005), 'Photoemission 100 years after Einstein' (New Journal of Physics focus issue): 'In 1921 Einstein won the Nobel Prize not for his work on relativity, but for solving a puzzle that had baffled scientists since 1887 — the photoelectric effect'; spacedaily.com (July 2026), 'Einstein won the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics, but not for relativity': the Nobel Committee avoided honouring the still-controversial theory directly; Einstein received the 1921 prize in 1922; work named was the explanation of how light knocks electrons out of metal
Falsifiability: The Nobel Foundation's published motivation for the 1921 Physics prize mentioning special relativity, general relativity, or E = mc² as the grounds of the award.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1921-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.07,59.33,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1921</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The name 'zipper' (B.F. Goodrich, 1923)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The naming is attested by the company's own product history and independent accounts (Penn State Center for the Book, Entrepreneur, Express); exact year (1921 vs 1923) and exact coiner (president vs staff) vary, so the record stays CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Akron, Ohio (B.F. Goodrich)
Dates: 1921 → 1923 (confidence: medium)

The B.F. Goodrich Company coined the name 'zipper' in 1923, applying Sundback's fastener to rubber galoshes: the 'Mystik Boot' was renamed the 'Zipper Boot,' and the name — from the 'zip' sound of the slider — stuck to the fastener itself.

Earliest attestation: B.F. Goodrich 'Zipper Boot' marketing, early 1920s
Sources: Pennsylvania Center for the Book / Penn State — 'Zip It Up: Talon Zippers' (2009), citing Robert Friedel, Zipper: An Exploration in Novelty (1994); Entrepreneur — 'The Fasten-ating History of the Humble Zipper'; B.F. Goodrich 'Zipper Boot' product line, early 1920s
Falsifiability: Period marketing predating Goodrich's use of 'zipper' for the fastener</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1921-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.519,41.0814,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1921</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Marie Lloyd's final week, 4–7 October 1922</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent attestations converge: the contemporary press on the 4 October Edmonton collapse, the biographical record of the 7 October Alhambra illness, The Times death announcement, and the institutional Leeds Heritage Theatres account — with the exact-venue/day variances carried honestly in the record rather than smoothed over.
Layer: real · Place: Empire Music Hall, Edmonton, North London, England
Dates: 1922-10-04 → 1922-10-07 (confidence: high)

In the first week of October 1922 Marie Lloyd — the 'Queen of the Music Hall' — gave her last performances: on 4 October, against her doctor's advice, she appeared at the Empire Music Hall, Edmonton, North London, sang 'I'm One of the Ruins That Cromwell Knocked About a Bit', was unsteady and fell over on stage while the audience laughed, believing it part of the act; on 7 October she was taken ill on stage at the Alhambra Theatre and found in her dressing room crippled with pain and stomach cramps; she returned home that evening, where she died of heart and kidney failure, aged 52.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press on the Edmonton performance (4 Oct 1922); The Times announcement of her death; biographies (Gänzl; MacQueen-Pope); institutional theatre archive (Leeds Heritage Theatres)
Sources: Wikipedia / Marie Lloyd (citing The Times death announcement, Gänzl biography, MacQueen-Pope): 4 Oct 1922 Empire Edmonton collapse; 7 Oct Alhambra illness; death at home of heart and kidney failure; Leeds Heritage Theatres — 'Marie Lloyd in Leeds': 4 Oct 1922 collapse at Empire Music Hall, Edmonton; three days later at the Alhambra Theatre, too ill to proceed; died at home of heart and kidney failure; The Hoxton Chronicle (steven788.substack.com): 4 Oct 1922 Empire Edmonton collapse; 7 Oct 1922 collapse at the Alhambra Theatre; died at home of heart and kidney failure, aged 52; ~50,000 at the funeral
Falsifiability: Contemporary press contradicting the venue or dates, or a record placing her elsewhere on 4–7 October 1922.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1922-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.001,51.6278,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1922</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alliance Rubber (1923): the rubber band's mass-production era</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The founding, the inner-tube method, and the Beacon Journal pitch are consistently reported across trade press, the company's centennial history, and encyclopedia entries — but the early-years narrative comes substantially from the family/company itself, held below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Alliance, Ohio — the boarding-house garage; later Hot Springs, Arkansas
Dates: 1923-03-07 → 1944 (confidence: medium)

William H. Spencer founded the Alliance Rubber Company on 7 March 1923 in Alliance, Ohio — buying rejected Goodyear inner tubes at auction in Akron, cutting them by hand into rubber bands in a boarding-house garage, and persuading the Akron Beacon Journal to wrap its newspapers with bands so they would not come apart and blow across lawns. Spencer moonlighted with the Pennsylvania Railroad for 14 years; in 1937 the company began extruding its own tubing; a second plant opened in Hot Springs, Arkansas, in 1944. The company remains family-owned (president Bonnie Spencer Swayze, the founder's daughter).

Earliest attestation: Alliance Rubber Company's own centennial history ('A Centennial History of Alliance Rubber'), as reported in the Rubber News centennial piece (2023) quoting president Bonnie Spencer Swayze; Wikipedia's company article (founded 7 March 1923, founder W.H. Spencer).
Sources: Rubber News, 'Alliance Rubber celebrates 100 years of holding your world together' (2023) — Spencer bought rejected inner tubes at auction in Akron 1923, cut them by hand in a boarding-home garage; 14 years moonlighting with the railroad; 1937 extrusion at 629 N. Union Ave.; 1M pounds in 1957; Wikipedia, 'Alliance Rubber Company' — founded 7 March 1923 in Alliance, Ohio by William H. Spencer; 'originally, Alliance rubber bands were made from cutting rejected rubber inner tubes'; the Akron Beacon Journal newspaper-band pitch; Manufacturing.net / IMPO (2012), 'Making It In America' — William Spencer 'began Alliance Rubber in 1923 by cutting Goodyear inner tubes into bands in his basement' (per Bonnie Spencer Swayze)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1920s documents (the Beacon Journal arrangement, the 1923 business registration) contradicting the garage-origin account, or evidence the company predates 1923 under another name.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1923-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.1059,40.9134,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1923</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Phonofilm (1923) - sound-on-film reaches the theaters</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The technology chain is independently attested: Eugene Lauste photographing and reproducing sound on film by 1910 (cinemontage - Variable Area and Variable Density tracks); Theodore Case and Earl Sponable's Aeo Light glow-tube system (1916 on); de Forest exploiting Case's system under the Phonofilm name in 1923; the Phonofilm shorts of 1923-1924 (Weber and Fields, Eddie Cantor, Fleischer cartoons); and the 20-outfit roadshow count by 1924 (cinemontage). Held below VERIFIED: the roadshow counts rest on trade-reportage aggregation, not a surviving outfit manifest. The record also carries the sound-on-film vs sound-on-disc tradeoff honestly - film was easily spliced but grain-noisy; discs were familiar but cracked, wore out after twenty playings, and could be misplayed.
Layer: real · Place: New York City theatrical roadshows (city precision)
Dates: 1923 → 1924 (confidence: high)

Lee de Forest's Phonofilms (1923-1924) were commercial theatrical sound-on-film shorts - vaudeville acts, singers, comedians, and Max Fleischer cartoons - using Theodore Case and Earl Sponable's optical sound system; by 1924, 20 Phonofilm outfits were giving roadshows in theaters.

Earliest attestation: The 1923-1924 roadshow record; the filmreference and cinemontage accounts of the Phonofilm outfit count.
Sources: cinemontage.org - 'When Movies Learned to Talk' (Lauste 1910; Case/Sponable Aeo Light; Phonofilm; 20 outfits giving roadshows by 1924; Eddie Cantor, Abby Mitchell, Weber and Fields); filmreference.com - 'The Coming of Sound' (Case/Sponable improvements; 'Case's system was exploited by audio pioneer Lee de Forest under the name Phonofilm in 1923'; Fox-Case Corporation July 1926); collider.com - 'The First Sound Film Was NOT The Jazz Singer' (Tri-Ergon sound-on-film; de Forest's Phonofilm exhibitions of the early 1920s; Kinetophone lineage)
Falsifiability: Theater contracts or box-office records showing Phonofilm roadshows before 1923 or showing the 20-outfit count to be fabricated.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1923-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1923</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Don Juan (1926) - the first feature with a synchronized soundtrack</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent attestations converge on the same date and the same first: the MoMA archival feature (the premiere program - Hays introduction, six shorts including New York Philharmonic conducted by Hadley, Elman, Martinelli - then the feature with its first-in-history synchronized score), the Wikipedia record (first feature-length film with synchronized Vitaphone score and sound effects), and Gizmodo's anniversary account. Western Electric's Vitaphone - AT&amp;T's Bell Labs process - was sound-on-disc: 16-inch phonograph records played in sync with the projector. It was 'the last and the most successful' sound-on-disc system, and it stimulated modern sound-reinforcement development. The record also fences the scope: Vitaphone was not the first sound process, only the first commercially successful feature-length one.
Layer: real · Place: Warner's Theatre, Times Square, New York City (approximate venue pin)
Dates: 1926-08-06 → 1926-08-06 (confidence: high)

Don Juan (1926), starring John Barrymore, premiered on 6 August 1926 at Warner's Theatre, New York, as the first feature-length film released with a synchronized soundtrack - a Vitaphone sound-on-disc musical score and sound effects, with English intertitles and no spoken dialogue.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1926 trade and newspaper coverage of the Warner's Theatre premiere; MoMA's 1989 archival feature reconstructing the evening.
Sources: Wikipedia - 'Don Juan (1926 film)' (release date 6 August 1926; Vitaphone Corporation; synchronized score and sound effects; no spoken dialogue); MoMA - 'Audience breathless as Vitaphone and talkies make debuts' (archival feature; 'for the first time in history - a synchronized musical score rather than live orchestral accompaniment'; Vitaphone process created by AT&amp;T's Western Electric and Bell Labs); Gizmodo - 'The First Feature-Length Vitaphone-Synchronized Film' (sound-on-disc mechanics: 16-inch records, synced to projector; Vitaphone's fidelity as its success); filmreference.com - 'The Coming of Sound' (Vitaphone agreement June 1925; premiere program 6 August 1926; sound-on-disc vs sound-on-film tradeoffs)
Falsifiability: Theater programs, reviews, or disc records showing a feature-length synchronized soundtrack earlier than 6 August 1926.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1926-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1926</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Einstein–Szilard refrigerator (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: jointly invented 1926 by Einstein and his former student Leó Szilárd; US patent 1,781,541 granted 11 November 1930 (filed 16 December 1927), signed by both inventors; an absorption refrigerator — no moving parts, constant pressure, heat-source operated — using water, ammonia, and butane as working fluids; a modification of the 1922 Platen–Munters three-fluid design; motivated by contemporary newspaper reports of a Berlin family killed when a refrigerator seal failed and leaked toxic fumes — Einstein and Szilárd proposed a device without moving parts to eliminate seal failure; 45 patents granted in six countries for three models; the most promising patents bought by Electrolux for $750 (~$10,000 today); the electromagnetic pump 'howled like a jackal'; the design faded when Thomas Midgley's Freon (non-toxic) arrived. The one live dispute — whether Szilárd did most of the inventing with Einstein consulting on patents, or Einstein contributed design work — is fenced: it does not affect the VERIFIED grade on the invention itself.
Layer: real · Place: Berlin, Germany
Dates: 1926-01-01 → 1930-11-11 (confidence: high)

Albert Einstein co-invented a refrigerator with Leó Szilárd in 1926 — an absorption machine with no moving parts, US patent 1,781,541 granted 11 November 1930.

Earliest attestation: Einstein–Szilárd collaboration, 1926; US patent 1,781,541
Sources: Wikipedia: 'jointly invented in 1926 by Albert Einstein and his former student Leó Szilárd, who patented it in the U.S. on November 11, 1930 (U.S. patent 1,781,541). The three working fluids in this design are water, ammonia, and butane.'; EPFL Graph Search: from 1926–1934 they collaborated on home refrigeration, motivated by reports of a Berlin family killed by a failed refrigerator seal; 45 patents in six countries for three models; inventorship-share dispute noted.; ZME Science: the Berlin family's death from a leaking refrigerator; the no-moving-parts absorption design; the pump that 'howled like a jackal'; Freon's arrival; the pump's later nuclear-reactor applications.
Falsifiability: USPTO records proven forged</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1926-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1926</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Vitaphone–Movietone format war (1926–1931)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The competitive structure and the outcome are multiply attested: the 1926 licensing contract, the Fox-Case formation, the documented disc disadvantages (20-playing wear with label check-boxes, breakage, wrong-disc risk, uneditable discs, separate distribution infrastructure), and the secondary literature's own 'format war' framing (Scribd overview: 'lost the early format war'; shootonline: 'lost out in a format war with Movietone'). Held below VERIFIED on how much was open warfare vs negotiated consolidation and the exact decision points.
Layer: real · Place: Hollywood / New York — the studio-and-laboratory corridor (Warner Bros. Burbank, Western Electric, Fox, RCA)
Dates: 1926 → 1931 (confidence: high)

Sound-on-film (Fox-Case Movietone, then RCA Photophone) defeated sound-on-disc (Western Electric's Vitaphone) in a competitive 'format war' for the talkie market — a contest of corporate licensing systems as much as of engineering, fought 1926–1931.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: Western Electric–Warner Bros. contract (April 1926); Vitaphone Corporation organization; trade-press coverage of the Don Juan premiere (5–6 August 1926); Fox-Case Corporation formation (July 1926); RCA Photophone development 1928.
Sources: filmreference.com — 'The Coming of Sound' encyclopedia entry (June 1925 Warner agreement; Fox-Case July 1926; Western Electric offered the choice; disc advantages/disadvantages); Wikipedia — 'Vitaphone' (mid-1931 towel; fidelity vs sound-on-film; discs pressed by Victor; cheaper theater conversion; Vitaphone brand synonymous with talkies by 1928); shootonline.com — 'Sound Comes to Hollywood' ('lost out in a format war with Movietone... Within a few years, Vitaphone was no longer used for movies'); Scribd — 'Overview of the Vitaphone System' ('lost the early format war'; distribution, 20-playing wear, damage, spare-set costs); Wikipedia — 'Synchronized sound' (Don Juan 1926 as first feature-length synchronized-sound film; the transition record)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that Warner Bros. continued original recording to disc as its primary method past 1931, or that the studios adopted a disc standard after 1931, would overturn the outcome; evidence that no competitive structure existed (a single pre-agreed standard from 1926) would overturn the 'war' framing.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1926-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.32,34.17,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1926</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Farnsworth's first all-electronic TV transmission (7 Sept 1927)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1927 transmission is attested by the lab party and corroborated by independent lines: the January 1927 patent filing (US 1,773,980) predates the demo, the 3 September 1928 press demonstration was witnessed by independent reporters (first image: a dollar sign), the 25 August 1934 Franklin Institute public demo, and Tolman's preserved 1922 sketch that carried the 1935 interference ruling. Held below VERIFIED because the September 1927 moment itself rests on the inventor's own party — the independent witnesses arrive in 1928.
Layer: real · Place: 202 Green Street, San Francisco, California
Dates: 1927-09-07 → 1927-09-07 (confidence: high)

On 7 September 1927, at his laboratory at 202 Green Street, San Francisco, 21-year-old Philo Farnsworth transmitted the first all-electronic television image: his image-dissector camera aimed at a glass slide with a single painted line, the line appearing live on a cathode-ray-tube receiver in the next room — and rotating when the slide was rotated.

Earliest attestation: Farnsworth laboratory record and the lab party's account, 7 September 1927; patent application for the television system filed 7 January 1927 (US 1,773,980); contemporary press coverage of the 3 September 1928 demonstration
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Philo Farnsworth': 'demonstrated the first working version on September 7, 1927, having turned 21 the previous August'; first demonstrated to the press 3 September 1928; public demo at the Franklin Institute, 25 August 1934; image dissector converted image elements into electricity one at a time; The Philo T. Farnsworth Archives / weeklyview.net: 202 Green Street lab; the glass slide backlit by an arc lamp; the 1928 press demo's first image (a dollar sign); 1929 first live human images (wife Pem, eyes closed against the light); US Patent 1,773,980 (filed 7 January 1927) — the television-system patent whose Claim 15 ('electrical image') became the count in Interference No. 64,027
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1927) independent record contradicting the transmission — or period evidence that the 1927 apparatus could not have worked as described — would move the grade down; a 1927 notebook entry from a party independent of Farnsworth's lab witnessing the transmission would move it up.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1927-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.4241,37.8019,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1927</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Jazz Singer premieres — the talkie era's conventional start</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: the AFI catalog, the Library of Congress (National Film Registry essay), the National Museum of American History, and contemporary 1927 trade/newspaper coverage all fix the 6 October 1927 New York premiere, the Vitaphone sound-on-disc system, and the first-feature synchronized-dialogue status (distinguished from Don Juan's 1926 non-dialogue synchronization). The irony fenced into the timeline: the 'killer' of vaudeville's overnight myth starred Al Jolson — vaudeville's own headliner — and grew out of the vaudeville tradition itself.
Layer: real · Place: Warner's Theater, Times Square, New York City (approximate venue pin)
Dates: 1927-10-06 → 1927-10-06 (confidence: high)

The Jazz Singer premiered on 6 October 1927 at Warner's Theater, New York City, as the first feature-length film with sequences of synchronized spoken dialogue (Vitaphone sound-on-disc) — the conventional marker of the talkie era's beginning.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: Warner Bros. trade press and New York newspaper coverage, October 1927; the film print itself (surviving, National Film Registry).
Sources: American Film Institute catalog — The Jazz Singer (1927), New York premiere 6 October 1927; Library of Congress — The Jazz Singer described as the first feature film to include sequences of synchronized spoken dialogue (National Film Registry); National Museum of American History — Jolson's 'Wait a minute...' premiere account (audience reaction noted, not treated as box-office data); Men's Journal (9 Oct 2026, 99th-anniversary piece) — AFI premiere date, Vitaphone system detail; EBSCO — The Jazz Singer Premieres as the First Talkie (research starter; premiere reception and 1928 record run)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that a feature-length film with sequences of synchronized spoken dialogue premiered publicly before 6 October 1927, or that The Jazz Singer's premiere post-dated that day.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1927-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1927</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>US Business Reply Mail introduced in 1928</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1928 introduction is attested in a Smithsonian National Postal Museum-affiliated postal-history symposium paper; the modern mechanics (postage + per-piece fee on returned pieces only) are VERIFIED-level in the USPS Domestic Mail Manual itself; held at CORROBORATED because the 1928 departmental origin document was not inspected this pass.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. — U.S. Post Office Department
Dates: 1928 → 1928 (confidence: medium)

The U.S. Post Office Department introduced Business Reply Mail (together with bulk mail) in 1928: a permit system under which a mailer distributes reply cards, envelopes, self-mailers, cartons, or labels pre-printed with the 'BUSINESS REPLY MAIL' legend, a permit imprint, and a unique ZIP+4; the customer returns them with no postage affixed, and the permit holder pays postage plus a per-piece fee only on the pieces actually returned. The 1928 introduction is attested in the Winton M. Blount Postal History Symposium presentation by Diane DeBlois and Robert Dalton Harris (Smithsonian National Postal Museum, 8 December 2022): 'Business Reply &amp; Bulk Mail introduced 1928.'

Earliest attestation: DeBlois &amp; Harris, 'Big Mail: from Public Good to Private Profit,' 12th Winton M. Blount Postal History Symposium, Smithsonian National Postal Museum, 8 December 2022
Sources: Diane DeBlois &amp; Robert Dalton Harris, 'Big Mail: from Public Good to Private Profit,' Twelfth Winton M. Blount Postal History Symposium, Smithsonian National Postal Museum, 8 December 2022 — slides state 'Business Reply &amp; Bulk Mail introduced 1928'; USPS Domestic Mail Manual 505, Return Services — BRM service enables a permit holder to receive First-Class and Priority Mail back and 'pay postage and a per piece fee only for the pieces returned'; permit holder 'guarantees payment' of postage plus per-piece fee on all returned BRM including blank/empty pieces; The Stamp Forum, 'Business Reply Mail' thread — philatelic evidence of BRM in use: postage-due stamps affixed by the post office to collect postage + BRM fee from the permit holder (e.g. 1931 items at 2 cents postage + 1 cent fee); airmail BRM cards documented
Falsifiability: A Post Office Department order establishing business reply mail before 1928, or evidence that no such service existed until a later year</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1928-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1928</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Italia airship disaster, 25 May – 12 July 1928</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary and contemporary sources converge on the event: the expedition's own logs and Biagi's radio record, the international rescue participants' accounts (Norwegian, Swedish, Finnish, Soviet, French, Italian), contemporary press, and the 1929 commission's report. Only the sub-claims (exact coordinates, crash causes) vary — the event itself does not.
Layer: real · Place: Arctic pack ice ~120 km northeast of Nordaustlandet, Svalbard (crash site; the ice drifted during the 48 days on it)
Dates: 1928-05-23 → 1928-07-12 (confidence: high)

On 25 May 1928, the Italian N-class semi-rigid airship Italia (N-4, designed by Umberto Nobile) crashed on the Arctic pack ice northeast of Svalbard while returning from the North Pole. The control car was smashed open and torn from the keel; nine survivors and one dead crewman were left on the ice, while the envelope rose again with six men aboard and was never found. The survivors dyed their 2.4×2.4 m silk tent red for visibility, radio operator Giuseppe Biagi transmitted SOS from 25 May, the signal was picked up on 3 June by a 21-year-old Soviet radio amateur (Nikolai Schmidt, Vokhma, on a home-made receiver), and after 48 days on the ice the remaining survivors were rescued — Nobile by the Swedish pilot Einar Lundborg on 23 June, the rest by the Soviet icebreaker Krasin on 12 July. Roald Amundsen disappeared on 18 June in the French Latham 47 while flying to join the rescue. Seventeen people died (crew and rescuers); eight survived.

Earliest attestation: The expedition's own records and Biagi's radio log (25 May 1928 onward); the support ship Città di Milano's records; contemporary international press coverage (e.g., TIME, 23 July 1928); the Italian Commission of Inquiry report, Rivista Marittima, January 1930
Sources: Wikipedia 'Italia (airship)': the N-4 design, the three polar flights, the 23–25 May timeline (pole 24 May 00:24, crash 25 May morning), the control car torn from the keel, nine survivors + Pomella dead on the ice, six carried off with the envelope (never found), the red-dyed 2.4×2.4 m silk tent, Biagi's SOS from 25 May, Schmidt's 3 June reception (21-year-old Soviet amateur, Vokhma, home-made lamp-regenerative circuit), radio contact 9 June, Maddalena spotting the tent 20 June, Lundborg ordered to rescue Nobile first (23 June) and crashing on his return, Malmgren's 15–16 June death on the march, Krasin rescues 12 July, Amundsen's 18 June disappearance in the Latham 47, 17 dead / 8 survivors total; Leone &amp; Robotti, 'The Polar Expedition of the Airship Italia (1928): A Chapter in the History of Physics,' Physics in Perspective 27 (2025), 340–402: the Red Tent position 81°14′N 25°25′E; Schmidt's SOS reception 3 June after nine days of failed attempts; Krasin rescue 12 July; Amundsen's 18 June disappearance; the INGV space-weather analysis of the failed radio propagation; naplesldm.com, 'Nobile' page: the one-seat Fokker ('the plane would never take off with the weight of even one more person'), Lundborg's return-flight crash, Nobile's 1930 book defending himself ('he had been ordered off the ice'), the 1929 inquiry finding him largely responsible, his resignation, the USSR years, the 1945 rehabilitation board and rank reinstatement; Wikipedia 'Umberto Nobile': cleared of all charges in 1945, reinstated at major general, promoted to lieutenant general with back pay to 1928
Falsifiability: Contemporary ship, radio, or commission records showing the crash did not occur on 25 May 1928, or that the envelope with six men was recovered — i.e., the primary documentary record contradicting the event</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1928-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.233,81.233,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1928</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Parry-Lord oral-formulaic thesis: the song before the book</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent lines converge. (1) Parry's 1928 analysis showed the noun-epithet formulae ('swift-footed Achilles,' 'cloud-gathering Zeus') are a complete metrical system for the dactylic hexameter — a utilitarian composition technology for composing-in-performance, not decoration. (2) The 1933–35 fieldwork found the living mechanism: the Yugoslav guslars, illiterate bards who recomposed the story each performance; Avdo Mededovic's songs 'Osmanbey and Pavicevic Luka' and 'The Wedding of Smailagic Meho' ran over 12,000 lines each — contemporary analogues to a Homeric bard. (3) The archaic linguistic strata and formulaic density of the Homeric text resist written-composition explanations. Held at CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED: the field is live — analyst vs unitarian, dictation and transitional-text theories, and the exact road from song to the 6th-century Athenian text remain contested.
Layer: real · Place: Yugoslavia (Bosnia) — the 1933–1935 Parry–Lord fieldwork with the guslars
Dates: 1928 → present (confidence: high)

The Homeric epics were composed in the oral tradition using a system of traditional formulae — poems tens of thousands of lines long made without writing.

Earliest attestation: Milman Parry's 1928 Harvard doctoral work (L'epithete traditionnelle dans Homere; Les formules et la metrique d'Homere); the 1933–1935 Yugoslav fieldwork with Albert Lord (then his graduate student); Lord's The Singer of Tales (Harvard, 1960); the Milman Parry Collection of Oral Literature at Harvard Library (the digitized shellacs).
Sources: LA Review of Books, 'The Darwin of the Classics': Parry's theory and fieldwork as 'scientific discovery'-grade research; Lord's Singer of Tales (1960) bringing Parry 'bedrock status'; the guslars, the gusle, the 12,000-line songs of Avdo Mededovic; the Milman Parry Collection shellacs digitized at Harvard; Gale Power Search course document ('Was Homer an Oral Poet?'): Wolf 1795 and the analyst/unitarian split; Parry's mid-20th-century publications as the bridge to the 'monumental, but traditional, composer'; the formula as 'a group of words regularly employed under the same metrical conditions'; 'swiftfooted Achilles' and 'cloudgathering Zeus' as stock phrases; Vectree knowledge map, 'Milman Parry and the theory of oral-formulaic composition': the singer-as-performer (not writer); the guslars; recomposition per performance; formulae as 'utilitarian building blocks' for the dactylic hexameter's temporal constraint
Falsifiability: A written compositional draft tradition for the epics (authorial manuscripts, dated revisions in a single hand from the 8th c. BCE), or a demonstration that the Yugoslav analogues' recomposition-in-performance mechanism cannot produce the observed formulaic density and metrical regularity.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1928-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>19.8,43.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1928</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1929 crash (Black Thursday to Black Tuesday)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent attestations: the exchange's own trading records, same-day newspaper reporting, and a half-century of scholarship built on them.
Layer: real · Place: New York Stock Exchange, 11 Wall Street, New York City
Dates: 1929-10-24 → 1929-10-29 (confidence: high)

In late October 1929 the New York stock market collapsed in a run of record selling days — Black Thursday (24 October) and Black Tuesday (29 October) — driven by forced selling of margin positions, wiping out much of the decade's paper gains and opening the financial chapter of the Great Depression.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary newspaper reporting and NYSE trading records, October 1929
Sources: John Kenneth Galbraith, The Great Crash 1929 (1955) — the classic documented account of the bubble, the crash mechanics, and the aftermath; NYSE trading records, October 1929 (volumes, dates, price data); Wikipedia: The Great Crash, 1929 (survey of the event and historiography); HISTORY.com, '1929 Stock Market Crash: Did Panicked Investors Really Jump From Windows?' (2019, updated 2025) — crash context and Galbraith's statistics
Falsifiability: Exchange records showing the October 1929 selloff did not occur on those dates or at those volumes would overturn the record</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1929-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0113,40.7069,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1929</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Salt March (Dandi March), March–April 1930</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and contemporaneously attested — the march was covered by the world press and newsreels as it happened, and its dates, route, and scale are uncontested in the historiography.
Layer: real · Place: Dandi, Gujarat, India (march terminus; route Sabarmati Ashram, Ahmedabad → Dandi)
Dates: 1930-03-12 → 1930-04-06 (confidence: high)

From 12 March to 6 April 1930, M.K. Gandhi led 78 volunteers on a 240-mile (387 km) march from Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi, Gujarat, where on the morning of 6 April he broke the British salt laws by making salt from seawater — inaugurating the Civil Disobedience Movement; the ensuing satyagraha saw 60,000+ Indians jailed.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1930 press and newsreel coverage (the march was deliberately staged for the cameras); Gandhi's own writings; the Salt March Wikipedia record carries the standard account.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Salt March': 24-day march 12 March–6 April 1930, 78 trusted volunteers, 387 km Sabarmati Ashram to Dandi; salt broken 8:30 am 6 April; 60,000+ jailed; 'the British did not make immediate major concessions'; Drishti IAS — 'Dandi March 1930': 12 March–6 April 1930, 78 followers, 241-mile march, Gandhi arrested 5 May, Dharasana 21 May under Sarojini Naidu, Gandhi released January 1931; Outlook Traveller — Dandi March route: Gandhi picked up salt at dawn on 6 April 1930 as a symbolic act of breaking British law; memorial at Dandi
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1930 records contradicting the dates, the route, or the 78-volunteer starting figure</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1930-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>72.6242,21.3436,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1930</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dord, the ghost word (1934–1947): the dictionary that printed a word that never existed</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The error's entire lifecycle is documented by the institution that made it: the original slip (31 July 1931, Patterson, 'D or d, cont./density.'), the mechanism (headwords typed with spaced letters made 'D or d' read as 'Dord'), the printed entry (1934, p. 771, between Dorcopsis and doré), the 28 February 1939 discovery (an editor noticed the missing etymology), and the 1947 removal — all attested in Gove's letter to American Speech, Merriam-Webster's own retrospective, the Smithsonian account, and Wikipedia. Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED only because the primary-file evidence is carried through Gove's and the company's own retrospective accounts, not an independent external archive.
Layer: real · Place: Springfield, Massachusetts (G. and C. Merriam Company)
Dates: 1931 → 1947 (confidence: high)

For thirteen years (1934–1947), Webster's New International Dictionary, Second Edition, contained the word 'dord' — a ghost word, a non-word accidentally created when a chemistry editor's 3x5 slip reading 'D or d, cont./density.' was misread as the single word 'dord' (headwords were typed with spaces between letters). It appeared on page 771 defined as a noun meaning 'density' in physics and chemistry; an editor caught it on 28 February 1939, and it was excised in 1947.

Earliest attestation: G. and C. Merriam Company's own files: Austin M. Patterson's 31 July 1931 slip ('D or d, cont./density.'); the 1934 printed entry 'dord (dôrd), n. Physics &amp; Chem. Density.'; Philip Babcock Gove's letter to American Speech (c. 1954) reconstructing the whole chain; Merriam-Webster's own retrospective ('Dord: How a Non-Word Made it into the Dictionary').
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Dord': ghost word accidentally created by G. and C. Merriam staff in Webster's New International Dictionary 2nd ed. (1934); defined 'density' in physics and chemistry; Patterson's 31 July 1931 slip 'D or d, cont./density.'; appeared p. 771; caught 28 Feb 1939; removed 1947; Gove's American Speech letter.; Smithsonian Magazine (Kat Eschner, 28 Feb 2017): 'As "Dord" Shows, Being in the Dictionary Doesn't Always Mean Something's a Word' — Merriam-Webster's 'only one'; the 1939 discovery; Gove's 'probably too bad, for why shouldn't dord mean density?'; Merriam-Webster — 'Dord: How a Non-Word Made it into the Dictionary': the company's own account — W2 weighed 17 pounds, 250 editors and consultants; the entry had no etymology, no examples; the Austin M. Patterson slip story retold by the house.
Falsifiability: The Merriam-Webster archive could contradict the slip story — it does the opposite: the company tells the story itself. Proof that 'dord' was a genuine in-use word before 1931 would overturn the ghost-word reading (no such attestation exists; Gove's investigation found no etymology, no examples, no file evidence).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1931-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.5898,42.1015,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1931</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hoover Dam built, 1931-1936</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: the authorizing act, the contract record, the Bureau's construction documentation, the block-pour method, and the 1935 dedication converge on the dates, the builder, the method, and the 96-death official count.
Layer: real · Place: Hoover Dam, Black Canyon, Arizona-Nevada border
Dates: 1931-03-11 → 1936 (confidence: high)

Hoover Dam (authorized as Boulder Dam) was built in Black Canyon on the Arizona-Nevada border by Six Companies, Inc. under a 1931 contract: roughly 3.25 million cubic yards of concrete poured in interlocking blocks, dedicated by President Franklin D. Roosevelt on 30 September 1935, completed 1936; 96 workers died during construction per the official count.

Earliest attestation: Boulder Canyon Project Act (1928); Six Companies contract (March 1931); Bureau of Reclamation construction records; contemporary press coverage.
Sources: National Historic Landmark summary - Hoover Dam (construction 1931, dedication 1935, 96 deaths, $49M, renamed 1947, NHL 1985); Construction Knowledge - 'The Men Who Built Hoover Dam' (96 deaths, 8-inch lifts, block-pour method); Boulder City 2020 Community Guide - Hoover Dam (Boulder Canyon Project Act 1928, Black Canyon site, dedication 30 Sept 1935)
Falsifiability: Bureau of Reclamation construction records showing a different contractor, different dates, or a monolithic (non-block) pour method.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1931-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-114.737,36.016,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1931</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Vitaphone disc's afterlife (1931–mid-1930s)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested in the Vitaphone secondary record: the mid-1931 recording switch, the 'both sound-on-film and sound-on-disc versions' spin as the retirement was announced, and the continuing dubbed-disc supply driven by theaters that could not afford to replace disc-only equipment. Held below VERIFIED on the single-source-lineage (the fandom wiki and Wikipedia carry the same account) and the approximate end date.
Layer: real · Place: United States exhibition circuit — the disc-only small-town and late-converting houses
Dates: 1931 → 1935 (confidence: medium)

After the format war, sound-on-disc lived on as an exhibition medium: Warner Bros. recorded and edited optically from mid-1931 but dubbed the finished soundtracks to discs for theaters with Vitaphone projection equipment, and most Hollywood studios pressed dubbed disc sets for disc-only houses into the mid-1930s.

Earliest attestation: The practice is documented in the Vitaphone secondary record (the mid-1931 Warner announcement and its 'both versions' spin); no single primary contract set has been located by this run — an honest gap, carried in the grade.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Vitaphone' (mid-1931 switch; 'A significant number of theater owners... financially unable or unwilling to replace their sound-on-disc-only equipment... This practice continued, although on an ever-dwindling scale, into the mid-1930s.'); Warner Bros. Entertainment Wiki (fandom) — 'Vitaphone' (same account; the retirement spin as 'doing the entire movie industry a favor'); shootonline.com — 'Sound Comes to Hollywood' (the Vitaphone brand synonymous with talkies by 1928; small-town exhibitor economics)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that no dubbed disc sets were pressed after 1931, or that disc-only theaters converted immediately, would overturn this; the theater-owner economics (recent heavy Vitaphone investment) make the tail structurally expected.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1931-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1931</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Polish break of Enigma (1932) and the Pyry transfer (1939)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiply attested across independent sources: the three principals' accounts, Bertrand's French records, British GCCS documentation of the Pyry handover and its fruits, and Bletchley's own 2002 plaque. The breakthrough date is retrospective testimony, so CORROBORATED is the honest ceiling.
Layer: real · Place: Pyry, south of Warsaw (Biuro Szyfrow facility); Warsaw
Dates: 1932 → 1939 (confidence: high)

In December 1932 (between Christmas and New Year, per Rejewski's account), Marian Rejewski of the Polish Cipher Bureau (Biuro Szyfrow) reconstructed the internal wiring of the German military Enigma machine using permutation theory plus German cipher documents obtained by French intelligence (Captain Gustave Bertrand's source inside the German cipher office, Hans-Thilo Schmidt, 'Asche'). With Jerzy Rozycki and Henryk Zygalski, the Poles then read German military Enigma traffic daily from ~January 1933, building the cyclometer, Zygalski's perforated sheets, and the electromechanical bomba kryptologiczna (Nov 1938). On 25 July 1939 at Pyry, south of Warsaw — five weeks before WWII — Poland handed Britain (Alastair Denniston, Alfred Dillwyn Knox, Humphrey Sandwith) and France (Bertrand, Braquenie) the full documentation, the cryptanalytic machines, and Enigma replicas. Britain, which had concluded Enigma unbreakable and made zero pre-war progress, began reading Enigma within months of the war's outbreak, on Polish foundations.

Earliest attestation: Rejewski's own published accounts (1967, 1976); Gustave Bertrand's intelligence records; British GCCS records of the Pyry meeting and of manufacturing perforated sheets from Polish documentation (mid-Dec 1939); the 2002 Bletchley Park plaque commemorating Rejewski, Rozycki, and Zygalski 'in first breaking the Enigma code.'
Sources: Rejewski's accounts (1967/1976): break between Christmas 1932 and New Year using permutation equations + French-supplied documents; AVA company built the Enigma replicas; Wikipedia (Marian Rejewski): Pyry meeting 25 July 1939 — Denniston, Knox, Sandwith; Bertrand, Braquenie; full documentation + replicas; British manufactured at least two sets of perforated sheets, reading Enigma within months of the war; Bletchley Park 2002 plaque: commemorates Rejewski, Rozycki, Zygalski 'in first breaking the Enigma code' — their work 'greatly assisted the Bletchley Park code breakers'
Falsifiability: Contemporary Polish, French, or British documents showing the 1932 break did not occur, or that Pyry handed over nothing material; or German records showing the 1933 reads did not happen.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1932-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>21.0165,52.1217,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1932</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Akron rubber workers' sit-downs (1934–36) — the tactic Flint inherited</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED — held below VERIFIED because the priority-chain details (the 1934 origin, exact sit-down dates in Jan–Feb 1936, the baseball-game origin story) ride through secondary aggregators and institutional summaries rather than a single converged primary archive; what is multiply attested: the General Tire 1934 origin (Ohio History Connection + labor historian Daniel Nelson), the Firestone 29 January 1936 sit-down over Clayton Dicks (APWU + Brecher), and the Goodyear February 1936 sit-downs and month-long picket ending in the 22 March 1936 settlement (Ohio History Connection + Zinn Education Project + Wikipedia).
Layer: real · Place: Akron, Ohio — the rubber plants
Dates: 1934 → 1936-03-22 (confidence: high)

In early 1936, Akron, Ohio's rubber workers carried out some of the first major sit-down strikes in the United States — the tactic pioneered at General Tire in 1934, used at Firestone in January 1936 and Goodyear in February 1936 — proving the tactic and making the Flint sit-down possible.

Earliest attestation: The actions were contemporaneously documented — local press, the fledgling United Rubber Workers (created September 1935), the Summit County Central Labor Council's 1936 role, and the Ohio History Connection's historical-marker program all converge.
Sources: Ohio History Connection (Remarkable Ohio marker 32-77): 'Five Depression-era strikes against many of Akron's rubber companies culminated in a giant sit-down strike against Goodyear Tire and Rubber Company... in February and March of 1936'; the URW, created September 1935, 'used the tactic of being at work but not working that had been pioneered by rank-and-file workers in a successful 1934 strike against the General Tire and Rubber Company'; the strike was 'one of the earliest successes for the Committee for Industrial Organization (CIO)'; APWU 'Labor History: Akron Rubber Workers Innovate Strike Tactics': 'In January 1936, at the Firestone Tire and Rubber Co.... workers sat down on Jan. 29, 1936, occupying the plant for three days until Dicks was reinstated with back pay. On Feb. 16, 1936, workers at Goodyear also participated in a sit-down strike after the company announced plans to lay off workers, increase work hours, and reduce pay.'; Zinn Education Project, 'Feb. 16, 1936: Akron Rubber Strike': 'On Feb. 16, 1936, rubber workers began a sit-down strike at Goodyear Tire and Rubber Co. in Akron, Ohio' — the sit-down defined as workers quitting work but occupying their places so the factory owners could not send in strikebreakers; Jeremy Brecher, 'Akron rubber workers' struggles, 1933-1936' (libcom.org): the detailed chronology — Goodrich tire department sit-down Feb 8, 1936 ('stopping it completely within six hours, and management rapidly capitulated'); Feb 14, 1936: '137 tire builders in Department 251-A of Goodyear's Plant No.2... shut off the power and sat down'
Falsifiability: A showing that the 1934 General Tire action and the January–February 1936 Akron sit-downs did not involve workers occupying the workplace while refusing to work would reverse the verdict; the contemporary accounts describe exactly that.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1934-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.519,41.0814,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1934</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The RCA–Farnsworth patent interference (1934–1939)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The legal outcome is multiply attested: the February 1935 interference decision for Farnsworth (Tolman testimony, 1922 sketch), RCA's failed 1936 appeal, and the 1939 license — reported by independent secondary sources (Wikipedia, MIT Technology Review, JDSupra, learnbright). Held below VERIFIED because the royalty figure (~$1M) and the 1931 $100,000 buyout offer ride secondary aggregators rather than the agreement's own terms.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (U.S. Patent Office, Interference No. 64,027)
Dates: 1934 → 1939 (confidence: high)

In 1934 the Radio Corporation of America brought patent interference proceedings (Interference No. 64,027) against Philo Farnsworth's television patents, asserting Vladimir Zworykin's priority; the U.S. Patent Office decided the interference for Farnsworth in February 1935, RCA's 1936 appeal failed, and in 1939 RCA took a license to Farnsworth's patents — paying him about $1 million in royalties — before launching television to the public at the New York World's Fair.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent Office Interference No. 64,027 (1934–1935): Examiner's decision February 1935 (Tolman's preserved sketch of Farnsworth's spring-1922 blackboard drawing, teacher's testimony); RCA appeal 1936; 1939 license agreement
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Philo Farnsworth': interference brought by RCA in 1934, decided February 1935; Tolman's sketch of the spring-1922 blackboard drawing; RCA appealed in 1936 and lost; Farnsworth received royalties from RCA; MIT Technology Review, 'Who Really Invented Television?' (Sept 2000): the Sarnoff–Zworykin collaboration; the April 1930 Green Street lab visit; Westinghouse bankers' pressure to sell; LearnBright, 'Philo T. Farnsworth': Farnsworth won the case in 1935, ruling upheld on appeal; 'RCA licensed the patents in 1939 and paid Farnsworth a million dollars for them'; JDSupra, 'The Top Ten Patent Wars — Television (#10)': 'after taking a million-dollar license from Farnsworth after losing the interference proceedings'
Falsifiability: The Patent Office decision and the appeal loss are matters of legal record; a showing that the 1939 license was not taken — i.e., period RCA financial records with no Farnsworth royalty line — would overturn the agreement leg. The ~$1M figure, riding secondary sources, could be revised downward or upward without moving the legal grade.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1934-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0364,38.8951,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1934</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1937 New York City burlesque license-kill</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED on converging secondary documentation: the State Court of Appeals ruling that Moss needed a criminal conviction (the precondition), the April 1937 G-string raid and ensuing conviction (the trigger), the license revocation plus the citywide non-renewals (the weapon), the no-stripper reopening code and the wave of closures (the effect), and the post-election advertising bans on the words 'burlesque' and 'Minsky' (the afterword). Held below VERIFIED because the actual 1937 license orders and the conviction record are not yet in hand — stated honestly.
Layer: real · Place: New York City, USA (the burlesque houses; the April 1937 raid at the New Gotham Theater, Harlem)
Dates: 1935 → 1937-11 (confidence: medium)

In April 1937, a police raid on Abe Minsky's New Gotham Theater in Harlem — a stripper caught working without a G-string — gave New York City License Commissioner Paul Moss the criminal conviction he needed, and he then refused to renew the licenses of all of New York City's burlesque theaters, driving them to close.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1937 press coverage (e.g. the August 27, 1937 Evening Independent burlesque-column item); the full campaign sequence as documented in the secondary histories
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (secondary aggregator: the 1935 complaints, the Court of Appeals ruling, the April 1937 raid, the license revocation and non-renewals, the no-stripper code, the advertising bans); Wikipedia, 'American burlesque' (secondary aggregator: La Guardia's 1937 clampdown, burlesque 'effectively put out of business' in NYC by the early 1940s, lingering elsewhere); Sidney Pullen, 'Burlesque: The Other Side of Vaudeville,' American Vaudeville Museum / UA Collections (citing Robert C. Allen 1991: theaters refused renewal 'because of burlesque's supposed moral deviance'); Evening Independent (St. Petersburg), Aug 27, 1937, p. 20 — contemporary press on the post-raid scene ('burlesque followers,' the Oriental theater dark)
Falsifiability: The actual 1937 license docket showing renewals granted to strippers-included burlesque houses, or a court record overturning the conviction that grounded the refusals</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1935-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1935</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Berlin Olympic Games, 1936</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply documented event — official report, IOC archive, contemporary press, newsreel, and the physical stadium all converge; Owens's four golds are among the best-recorded results in sports history.
Layer: real · Place: Olympiastadion, Berlin, Germany
Dates: 1936 → 1936 (confidence: high)

The Games of the XI Olympiad were held in Berlin 1–16 August 1936, with 3,963 athletes from 49 nations; American sprinter Jesse Owens won four gold medals — 100 metres, 200 metres, long jump, and 4x100 metre relay — in Adolf Hitler's Berlin.

Earliest attestation: The Official Report of the Organising Committee for the XIth Olympiad, Berlin 1936 (1937); IOC records; contemporary newsreel footage
Sources: Organising Committee, Official Report of the Games of the XI Olympiad, Berlin 1936 (1937); International Olympic Committee — Berlin 1936 results archive; Britannica — Jesse Owens and the 1936 Berlin Olympics; Mark Dyreson, 'From Civil Rights to Scientific Racism: American Responses to the Legend of Jesse Owens and the Berlin Olympics' (Centre for Olympic Studies symposium, Oct 1994, Weber State)
Falsifiability: The Official Report, IOC archive, and newsreel record would all have to be fabrications; no serious historian disputes the games' occurrence.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.2394,52.5147,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dvorak-superiority claim</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The layout's existence is documented (1936 patent), but the 'scientifically proven faster' claim is attested-only and contested: its flagship evidence is an unpublished Navy study directed by the layout's own inventor; the 1956 GSA study found QWERTY typists as fast or faster; Liebowitz &amp; Margolis (1990) concluded there is 'no scientifically acceptable evidence that Dvorak offers any real advantage over QWERTY.' The lock-in parable built on the superiority claim collapses with it.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, DC (US Patent Office, 1936 patent; Navy and GSA studies)
Dates: 1936 → 1990 (confidence: high)

The Dvorak Simplified Keyboard is scientifically proven to be faster than QWERTY, and QWERTY's survival is therefore proof of an inefficient market lock-in.

Earliest attestation: Dvorak &amp; Dealey, US Patent 2,040,248 (1936); the superiority claim's evidentiary base is the unpublished 1940s US Navy typing study
Sources: Liebowitz, Stan J. &amp; Margolis, Stephen E. (1990). 'The Fable of the Keys.' Journal of Law and Economics, vol. XXXIII (April 1990) — Dvorak's own direction of the Navy study; GSA 1956 parity finding; ergonomic studies show 2.3%, 5%, 6.2% — 'no clear advantage'; US Patent 2,040,248 — Dvorak &amp; Dealey, 'Typewriter keyboard' (1936); Finkelstein, Seth. 'The Fable of the Fable' — rebuttal defending Dvorak, carried as the competing position
Falsifiability: A published, peer-reviewed, independently replicated typing trial showing Dvorak typists substantially and consistently faster than QWERTY typists</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Flint sit-down strike (1936–37) — the 44 days that unionized the auto industry</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED — the ninety-eighth family-adjacent event record: multiple independent contemporary lines converge (UAW records, GM records, Governor Frank Murphy's administration papers, national press): 30 Dec 1936 occupation, 44 days, the 'Battle of the Running Bulls' (January 1937), and the 11 February 1937 agreement with formal UAW recognition.
Layer: real · Place: Flint, Michigan — the Fisher Body plants
Dates: 1936-12-30 → 1937-02-11 (confidence: high)

The Flint sit-down strike — autoworkers occupying General Motors' Fisher Body plants in Flint, Michigan — ran from 30 December 1936 to 11 February 1937 (44 days), and ended with GM formally recognizing the United Automobile Workers as the collective bargaining representative, the victory that unionized the American auto industry.

Earliest attestation: The strike itself was contemporaneously documented — national press, the UAW's own records, Governor Frank Murphy's administration papers, and GM's corporate records all converge on the dates and the recognition outcome.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Flint sit-down strike': 'Date: December 30, 1936 – February 11, 1937; Location: Flint, Michigan; Result: Recognition of the UAW' — 'thousands of workers occupied the plants for 44 days, from December 30, 1936 to February 11, 1937'; Governor Frank Murphy 'refused to use force against the strikers, and sent in the Michigan National Guard to protect the workers from police and company guards, until an agreement was reached'; This Day In Automotive History, 'February 11, 1937: The Flint Sit-Down Strike Ends, UAW Recognized': the 44-day standoff ended 11 Feb 1937 when GM recognized the UAW — 'the first time a major U.S. automaker formally accepted an industrial union'; the 'Battle of the Running Bulls' police attempt to retake a plant, January 1937; Workers World (Martha Grevatt), 'The Occupy that won the union' (75th-anniversary retrospective, 2012): the sit-down began Dec. 30, 1936, ended in victory Feb. 11, 1937; workers won UAW recognition and the first industry contract; notes the misconception that the strike was spontaneous — it was the product of organized preparation
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous GM or UAW records showing a different start/end date, or showing that GM did not recognize the UAW in February 1937, would reverse the verdict.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.6875,43.0125,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Han van Meegeren's forged Vermeers</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The forger's own 1945 confession, his courtroom demonstration painting produced under observation, the Coremans commission's chemistry (Bakelite/Albertol resins — 20th-century materials — plus a resin bottle found in his studio), the 12 Nov 1947 forgery/fraud conviction, and the museum provenance records all converge on the same event.
Layer: real · Place: Amsterdam, Netherlands (arrest, demonstration painting, trial)
Dates: 1936 → 1947 (confidence: high)

Between 1936 and 1943 the Dutch painter Han van Meegeren (1889–1947) produced a series of forged 'Vermeer' paintings — including The Supper at Emmaus (1936–37) and Christ with the Woman Taken in Adultery (1941–42) — on genuine 17th-century canvases, using period pigments hardened with 20th-century phenol-formaldehyde resin, and sold them as rediscovered Vermeers.

Earliest attestation: The forger's own confession, May 1945; the courtroom demonstration painting Jesus among the Doctors, painted under observation July–Dec 1945
Sources: Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen — Boijmans Studies (new findings on van Meegeren's forgeries, reported 2025): the 1937 purchase, the Bredius authentication, the post-war unmasking; NPR — 'How Mediocre Dutch Artist Cast The Forger's Spell' (Edward Dolnick, 2008): the Emmaus sale, the Goering transaction (137 looted paintings traded), the unmasking via Nazi paperwork; Coremans commission (1945–47, led by Paul B. Coremans, Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium): phenol-formaldehyde resins Bakelite and Albertol identified in the paint; resin bottle found in van Meegeren's studio; essentialvermeer.com — list of known forgeries with sale prices (Emmaus 520,000–550,000 guldens; Adulteress 1,650,000 guldens); Amsterdam Regional Court record, Room 4, 29 Oct–12 Nov 1947: forgery and fraud conviction, one-year sentence; van Meegeren died 30 Dec 1947 (via Wikipedia, citing trial sources)
Falsifiability: Genuine 17th-century material signatures in the Emmaus paint — no Bakelite, no Albertol, no anachronistic pigments — would overturn the forgery finding</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.9041,52.3676,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Atanasoff–Berry Computer (1937–1942)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent lines converge: Atanasoff's own design manuscript and the 1939–40 grant trail, Mauchly's documented 1941 visit (both men's testimony), the 1997 working replica proving the engineering was real, and a federal court's factual findings. Held below VERIFIED because the priority narrative runs largely through the trial record and later biographies rather than contemporaneous engineering journals — the ABC's own press coverage at the time was effectively nil.
Layer: real · Place: Basement of the physics building, Iowa State College, Ames, Iowa
Dates: 1937 → 1942 (confidence: high)

The Atanasoff–Berry Computer (ABC), built by John Vincent Atanasoff and Clifford Berry in the basement of the physics building at Iowa State College (1937–1942), was a working electronic digital computer — binary arithmetic, vacuum-tube switching, regenerative capacitive memory kept separate from computation — and the documented prior art from which the ENIAC inventors derived the automatic electronic digital computer. John Mauchly visited Ames for five days in June 1941 as Atanasoff's houseguest, studied the machine in detail with the schematics, and helped Berry work on it.

Earliest attestation: Atanasoff's 35-page design manuscript (1939–40), reviewed by Mauchly in June 1941; the 1967–73 trial record formalizes the machine's priority role.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Atanasoff–Berry computer' — binary + vacuum tubes + separate memory; dismantled 1948; 1997 Delwyn Bluhm/John Gustafson working replica ($350,000); hhuc.hotbed.a2hosted.com, 'Name the First Electronic Digital Computer?' — 1939 prototype timeline ($650 grant, Berry hired spring 1939, working by end of 1939); Mauchly's May/June 1941 five-day visit, schematics review, denied copy request, machine 'mostly complete and functional'; thingshavehistory.com, 'The Atanasoff-Berry Computer, or the basement machine that unmade ENIAC' — June 1941 visit via Computer History Museum; binary arithmetic, electronic switching, memory separate from computation as the architecture; Electronic Design, 'John Atanasoff and Clifford Berry: Inventing the ABC' — June 26, 1947 ENIAC patent filing surprised Atanasoff; Honeywell sued May 26, 1967; Larson decision never appealed; some historians still question the ABC's standing
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous evidence that Mauchly's June 1941 visit did not occur, or that the machine's documented binary/electronic features postdate ENIAC — the trial record's 77 witnesses run the other way.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1937-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-93.6465,42.0267,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1937</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The nylon toothbrush (1938) — the end of animal bristles</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The 1938 introduction is multiply and independently attested across dental histories, broadcast archives, and the company's own record; the date does not wander.
Layer: real · Place: Wilmington, Delaware, USA (DuPont)
Dates: 1938 → 1938 (confidence: high)

In 1938, DuPont introduced the first toothbrush with synthetic nylon bristles (Dr. West's Miracle-Tuft Toothbrush), ending the centuries-long dependence on animal-hair bristles.

Earliest attestation: DuPont's 1938 product introduction; contemporary trade and advertising record.
Sources: Living on Earth, week of February 22, 2002 (with Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry): 'It was during this week, in 1938, that DuPont introduced the first nylon-bristled brush'; Dental-history convention: nylon invented mid-1930s; the first nylon toothbrush appeared on the market in 1938; softer nylon bristles by the 1950s (dental-history timelines); The post-nylon arc: the 1939 Swiss electric toothbrush; the 1960 US Broxodent (Squibb); the 1961 GE rechargeable cordless — the same documentary tradition, carried comparatively
Falsifiability: A documented synthetic-bristle toothbrush predating 1938 would move the milestone.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1938-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.6,39.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1938</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact (23 August 1939)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The public treaty text was published contemporaneously by both signatories and is multiply attested in both states' archives and the world press of 24 August 1939.
Layer: real · Place: Moscow, Kremlin
Dates: 1939-08-23 → 1939-08-23 (confidence: high)

The German-Soviet Nonaggression Pact — the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact — was signed in Moscow on 23 August 1939 by German Foreign Minister Joachim von Ribbentrop and Soviet Foreign Minister Vyacheslav Molotov, with Stalin present, committing both parties to ten years of nonaggression.

Earliest attestation: The treaty text itself, published 24 August 1939; contemporary press coverage worldwide
Sources: Treaty text, German-Soviet Nonaggression Pact, 23 August 1939 (seven public articles; 10-year term); Yale Avalon Project, Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941 document series; Contemporary world press coverage, 24 August 1939
Falsifiability: Proof the treaty text is a forgery — impossible against the converging originals in both states' archives and the contemporaneous global publication</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1939-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1939</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Secret Additional Protocol (23 August 1939)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Three independent lines converge: the captured German Foreign Office microfilms published in full in 1948; contemporary witness testimony (the protocol was referenced at Nuremberg during Ribbentrop's trial); and Moscow's own 1989 admission plus the 1992 declassification of the Soviet copy.
Layer: real · Place: Moscow, Kremlin (applies to Eastern Europe as the mapped region)
Dates: 1939-08-23 → 1939-08-23 (confidence: high)

On 23 August 1939, alongside the public Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, Ribbentrop and Molotov signed a Secret Additional Protocol dividing Eastern Europe into German and Soviet spheres of influence: the Baltic states (Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania) with Lithuania's northern boundary as the sphere line; Poland along the Narew-Vistula-San rivers; and Bessarabia noted as a Soviet interest — the document to be treated as 'strictly secret'.

Earliest attestation: The German Foreign Office originals, microfilmed (the von Loesch films) and captured by the Allies; published in full by the US State Department in 'Nazi-Soviet Relations, 1939-1941' (1948)
Sources: Secret Additional Protocol text, 23 August 1939 (four articles), from the captured German Foreign Office microfilms; US Department of State, 'Nazi-Soviet Relations, 1939-1941' (1948) — full verbatim publication of the protocol; Yale Avalon Project, Nazi-Soviet Relations 1939-1941: Secret Additional Protocol; 1989 Yakovlev commission findings; 24 December 1989 Congress of People's Deputies declaration confirming and denouncing the secret protocols; Soviet copy of the protocol, declassified 1992, published in a scientific journal early 1993
Falsifiability: Proof the microfilmed German originals are forgeries — foreclosed by the 1989 Soviet admission and the 1992 declassification of the Soviet copy</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1939-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1939</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Aeroelastic flutter: the mechanism that killed Galloping Gertie</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The engineering consensus across the 1941 board report, Billah &amp; Scanlan's analysis, and subsequent re-examinations; held at CORROBORATED (not VERIFIED) because it is an inferred mechanism from models and records, not a directly observed primary fact.
Layer: real · Place: Tacoma Narrows, Washington, USA
Dates: 1940-11-07 → 2002 (confidence: high)

The destructive final oscillation of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge on 7 November 1940 was aeroelastic flutter: the slender 8-foot plate-girder deck, displaced by the wind, shed motion-induced vortices whose aerodynamic forces fed energy back into the twist — a self-excited oscillation with effectively negative damping, in a fundamental antisymmetric torsion mode that had not manifested until ~45 minutes before the collapse. The wind (42 mph) was far in excess of the velocity required for incipient flutter.

Earliest attestation: Federal Works Agency Board of Engineers report (1941); Billah &amp; Scanlan 1991; Scanlan's 2002 ASCE re-examination
Sources: K. Y. Billah &amp; R. H. Scanlan, 'Resonance, Tacoma Narrows bridge failure, and undergraduate physics textbooks,' Am. J. Phys. 59, 118–124 (1991); R. H. Scanlan, 'Re-examination of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapse' (2002, ASCE); Federal Works Agency Board of Engineers, The Failure of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge (1941)
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed aerodynamic analysis showing the deck's flutter velocity above the recorded wind speed, or a forced-resonance mechanism matching the measured amplitude–wind-speed record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1940-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.5494,47.2833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1940</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Katyn Forest massacre, April-May 1940</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines converge: the perpetrators' own archived orders (1940), the executioner-state's internal count (1959), a twelve-doctor international commission (1943), the successor state's admission (1990) and document release (1992), exhumation archaeology (1994-95), and the 2010 parliamentary statement naming Stalin.
Layer: real · Place: Katyn forest district, near Smolensk, Russia
Dates: 1940-04 → 1940-05 (confidence: high)

In April-May 1940 the NKVD shot approximately 21,857 Polish prisoners - officers, police, and intelligentsia held in the Kozelsk, Starobelsk and Ostashkov camps and in prisons of western Ukraine and Belarus - pursuant to Lavrenty Beria's 5 March 1940 memorandum (No. 794/B) and the Politburo's same-day shooting order (protocol No. 13); burials at Katyn forest (4,421), Kharkov (3,820), Kalinin/Mednoye (6,311), and Ukraine/Belarus prisons (7,305).

Earliest attestation: The state's own 1940 paper chain: Beria's memorandum to Stalin, 5 March 1940 (No. 794/B, RGASPI f.17 op.166 d.621 l.130-133); the Politburo's shooting order, 5 March 1940 (excerpt from protocol No. 13); Aleksandr Shelepin's note to Khrushchev, 3 March 1959 (RGASPI f.17 op.166 d.621 l.138-139), reporting 21,857 shot and proposing the personal files be destroyed.
Sources: Beria's memorandum to Stalin proposing the shootings, 5 March 1940 (No. 794/B; RGASPI f.17 op.166 d.621 l.130-133), via Archives of Russia; Politburo shooting order, 5 March 1940 (excerpt from protocol No. 13 of the Central Committee meeting), via Archives of Russia; Shelepin's note to Khrushchev, 3 March 1959 (RGASPI f.17 op.166 d.621 l.138-139) - 21,857 shot; proposal to destroy personal files; International Medical Commission report, April-May 1943 (12 doctors, German-convened, including non-German neutrals): graves dated to spring 1940; Capt. Donald B. Stewart and Col. John H. Van Vliet Jr., 1943 Katyn visit (coded messages to U.S. superiors implicating the Soviets) and Van Vliet's 1945 report; released by the U.S. National Archives, September 2012; TASS statement, 13 April 1990 - USSR admitted NKVD responsibility ('profound regret'); 13 April declared Katyn Memorial Day; Yeltsin transfer of sealed 'Package No. 1' documents to Lech Walesa, 14 October 1992 (Beria note, Politburo excerpt, Shelepin note); Russian State Duma statement, 26 November 2010 - declared the massacre a Stalinist crime ordered by Stalin and other Soviet leaders; Russian exhumations at Katyn, Kharkov and Mednoye, 1994-95; Polish Institute of National Remembrance (IPN), decision to commence investigation, 30 November 2004
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous German order for the spring-1940 killings, or forensic proof that the Beria memorandum, the Politburo order, and the Shelepin note are all forgeries.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1940-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.68598,54.77032,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1940</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The paper clip as Norway's WWII resistance symbol</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: multiple independent secondary accounts converge on the core facts — Oslo University students, autumn 1940, after the Haakon VII button ban, the paper clip as binding-together symbolism, and a German crackdown once the meaning was understood (Gizmodo; War History Online; thezebra.org). Held below VERIFIED because the occupation-era primary documentation was not located in this pass and the exact ban/criminalization details rest on the accounts' claims; the widely cited link to Vaaler — the clips were worn partly because Norwegians believed a Norwegian invented them — is the belief's documented motive, carried in the record.
Layer: real · Place: Oslo, Norway (Oslo University; the reported origin point)
Dates: 1940 → 1945 (confidence: medium)

During the German occupation of Norway (1940–1945), Norwegians wore paper clips on their lapels as a quiet symbol of national unity and non-violent resistance — beginning with students at Oslo University in autumn 1940 after the occupiers banned buttons and badges bearing the exiled King Haakon VII's initials.

Earliest attestation: No single identified occupation-era primary source located in this pass; multiply reported in secondary accounts (Gizmodo, War History Online, Norwegian postwar telling)
Sources: Gizmodo, 'How the Humble Paperclip Became a Secret World War II Symbol': autumn 1940, Oslo University students; royal/state symbols banned; paperclip bracelets and jewellery; chosen partly because of the (incorrect) belief that Johan Vaaler invented it; War History Online, 'Paperclip, WWII Resistance Mark': Haakon VII buttons banned; students, workers, teachers adopted the clip; Germans later understood and wearing became a criminal violation; the idea that Vaaler invented the clip was prevalent and made it the perfect mark; The Zebra, 'Paper Clip History: From Office Tool to Symbol of Resistance' (2026): the paper clip as wartime resistance symbol
Falsifiability: Occupation-era Norwegian police or German decrees explicitly permitting or ignoring lapel clips where the symbol story requires a ban, or documented evidence that the 'resistance clip' telling originated only after 1945.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1940-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.7522,59.9139,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1940</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapses (7 November 1940)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by contemporary film, the federal commission report, newspapers, and the state DOT's own historical record.
Layer: real · Place: Tacoma Narrows, Washington, USA
Dates: 1940-11-07 → 1940-11-07 (confidence: high)

The first Tacoma Narrows suspension bridge ('Galloping Gertie'), opened 1 July 1940, collapsed into Puget Sound on the morning of 7 November 1940 after violent torsional oscillations in ~42 mph winds; the center span fell around 11:00–11:10 AM. No people died; the only casualty was Tubby, a cocker spaniel left in a car on the deck. The collapse was filmed (Barney Elliott, Harbine Monroe) and studied by a Federal Works Agency commission in 1941.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the Elliott/Monroe film (November 1940), newspaper coverage, and the Federal Works Agency Board of Engineers report (1941)
Sources: Federal Works Agency Board of Engineers, The Failure of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge (1941); WSDOT, Tacoma Narrows Bridge history (TNBHistory); Fox Weather / WSDOT historians: the Tubby account (Leonard Coatsworth, Farquharson rescue attempt)
Falsifiability: A contemporary record showing the bridge standing after November 1940, or proof the film is staged</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1940-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.5494,47.2833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1940</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1941 flight-strip program (CORROBORATED)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The program is documented by the FHWA's own historian and by the secondary record (strips near highways, not runways made of highways); held at CORROBORATED pending the Act's text as a primary source in the file.
Layer: real · Place: United States (flight strips near the wartime highway network)
Dates: 1941 → 1941 (confidence: medium)

The Defense Highway Act of 1941, Section 108, created a flight-strip program: the Army Air Force and the Public Roads Administration operated landing strips near (not on) highways for military use — the documented historical root from which the Interstate landing-strip myth grew.

Earliest attestation: Defense Highway Act of 1941, Section 108 — flight strips described as a program near highways (Weingroff, Public Roads, May/June 2000).
Sources: Richard F. Weingroff, 'Interstates as Runways: It 'Aint So' (Public Roads, May/June 2000) — Section 108 of the Defense Highway Act of 1941 describes a flight-strip program; the PRA and the Army Air Force operated flight strips near highways; the 1944 Federal-Aid Highway Act nearly included another flight-strip program but did not; HowStuffWorks, 'Were U.S. Interstates Really Designed as Runways?' — 'The Defense Highway Act provided flight strips, mostly for military use, that were near highways' [source: Weingroff]
Falsifiability: The text of the 1941 Act showing no Section 108, or PRA records showing no strips were built, would overturn the record; held at CORROBORATED on the secondary historical account rather than the Act's text itself.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1941-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1941</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>De Mestral invents the hook-and-loop fastener (1941–1954)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: the patent filings are public documents (Swiss filing 1951, grant 1954 per the company's own history; US patent 2,717,437 in the mid-1950s) and the development story is multiply and independently attested (Velcro Companies, Livescience, Smithsonian, AIER). Held below VERIFIED because the 1941 burr-walk itself survives only in de Mestral's later telling (walk vs hunting-trip variance) and the US grant year varies across secondary sources (1955 vs 1958).
Layer: real · Place: Switzerland (Swiss Alps walk; Swiss patent jurisdiction)
Dates: 1941 → 1954 (confidence: high)

Swiss engineer George de Mestral conceived the hook-and-loop fastener in 1941 after burdock burrs clung to his clothes and his dog's fur and he studied them under a microscope; after years of development he filed for Swiss patent protection in 1951 (first Swiss patent issued 16 March 1954), and named the product Velcro from the French 'velours' (velvet) + 'crochet' (hook).

Earliest attestation: De Mestral's patent filings, 1951 (Swiss); the 1941 observation survives only in his later accounts and the company's retelling.
Sources: Velcro Companies FAQ: de Mestral invented the technology; idea began 1941; Swiss filing 1951; first Swiss patent issued 16 March 1954.; LiveScience, 'Who Invented Velcro?': 1948 variant of the walk retelling; eight-plus years of development; patented 1955; name from velvet + crochet.; Smithsonian Magazine (2017): first patented 1958; patent expired 2 April 1978; cotton prototypes impractical, nylon the durable answer.; WIP News IP encyclopedia: US patent US2717437A, de Mestral, Swiss nationality, filing year 1951.
Falsifiability: Patent-office records showing no 1951 Swiss filing by de Mestral, or a dated prior-art hook-and-loop fastener patent predating his development.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1941-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>6.6,46.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1941</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Alexander son of Simon' ossuary (1941 Kidron find; the Simon-of-Cyrene identification pressed October 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The ossuary and its inscriptions are a documented 1941 find (Sukenik 1947); the names match Mark 15:21's 'Simon of Cyrene ... the father of Alexander and Rufus' on multiple points (Simon + Alexander + plausible Cyrene + pre-70 CE Jerusalem), and two named scholars (Ilan via Powers 2003; Johnston October 2026) have publicly endorsed the identification as likely — which CORROBORATES the artifact and its relevance but leaves the identification CLAIMED: Simon was the most common Jewish name of the period, Alexander common, the Cyrene reading disputed even in the quoting venue, and the tomb's eleven ossuaries include no Rufus.
Layer: real · Place: Kidron Valley tomb, Jerusalem (1941 find; approximate)
Dates: 1941 → 2026 (confidence: high)

A limestone ossuary discovered in a Jerusalem (Kidron Valley) tomb in 1941 carries the inscriptions 'Alexander' and 'Simon' — including 'Alexander, son of Simon' — with a lid inscription read by some as 'QRNYT' (Cyrene). Biblical Archaeology Review contributor Tom Powers (2003) and onomastics specialist Tal Ilan argued the names match Mark 15:21's Simon of Cyrene and his son Alexander; in October 2026 press coverage, New Testament scholar Jeremiah Johnston called the Simon-of-Cyrene-family identification 'very plausible, if not likely' — 'high probability' — while cautioning that Simon was the most common Jewish name of the period, Alexander common too, the Cyrene reading disputed, and no Rufus ossuary among the tomb's eleven boxes. The 1941 ossuary is a documented artifact; the Simon-of-Cyrene identification is a scholarly hypothesis, not a provenance.

Earliest attestation: Sukenik 1947 publication of the Kidron Valley tomb ossuaries; the 'Alexander son of Simon' inscription.
Sources: Sukenik, E. L. 1947 — publication of the 1941 Kidron Valley tomb ossuaries, the 'Alexander son of Simon' inscription; Powers, Tom — Biblical Archaeology Review 2003 analysis: 'Alexander' and 'Simon' inscriptions on front, back, and lid; inscription depth/style differences as possible two-burial sequence; Ilan, Tal (ancient Jewish names) — 'very likely' the Alexander of Mark 15:21, per Powers 2003 via the October 2026 press; Daily Mail, October 2026 (Jeremiah Johnston): the tomb's 11 ossuaries, pre-70 CE dating via Herodian oil lamp, the 'QRNYT' Cyrene reading proposed, Johnston's common-names caution and 'high probability' assessment; catholiconline.news, 5 October 2026 — 'Ancient Burial Box May Be Linked to the Man Who Helped Carry the Cross of Jesus' (aggregator recrawl of the Daily Mail piece; headline telling fenced as press, not evidence); Mark 15:21 — the textual anchor: Simon of Cyrene, 'the father of Alexander and Rufus' (naming sons as living witnesses)
Falsifiability: An epigraphic re-reading removing the 'QRNYT'/Cyrene connection, or identification of a different Alexander-son-of-Simon in a non-Cyrenian Jerusalem family, would collapse the identification while leaving the artifact's own grade intact.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1941-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2423,31.7666,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1941</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1941 Soviet exhumation of Timur's tomb at the Gur-e Amir (Samarkand)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The exhumation is multiply and contemporaneously documented (Soviet film/photography crews, Pravda's correspondent, the Central Asian intelligentsia present); the scientific findings (lameness, injuries, stature) are published osteology. The exact day of the coffin's opening (19, 20, or 21 June) varies by source, which is why this holds at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED, along with the open question of how much of the 'three old men' scene belongs to 1941 versus 2004 retellings.
Layer: real · Place: Gur-e Amir mausoleum, Samarkand, Uzbekistan
Dates: 1941-06-16 → 1942-11 (confidence: high)

In June 1941, a Stalin-ordered Soviet scientific expedition led by anthropologist Mikhail Gerasimov opened Timur's tomb at the Gur-e Amir mausoleum in Samarkand, examined his remains, and — after confirming key details of his appearance and injuries — reburied him with Islamic rites in November 1942.

Earliest attestation: The expedition was contemporaneously documented: Pravda correspondent Mikhail Sheverdin, still photographer I.P. Savalin, and cameraman Malik Qayumov's film brigade recorded the process. Gerasimov's osteological findings were published through Soviet academic channels (1950 USSR State Prize; his 1955 monograph).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Curse of Timur' — expedition composition (Gerasimov, Toshmuhammad Qori-Niyoziy, Sadriddin Ayni, Aleksandr Semyonov, Mikhail Masson, Lev Oshanin, Valentina Zezenkova, Boris Zasypkin; journalists and film brigade), excavation began 16 June 1941, coffin opened 19-21 June, reburied November 1942 with Islamic rites; Wikipedia, 'Mikhail Gerasimov (archaeologist)' — June 1941: Stalin sent Gerasimov to Uzbekistan to open the tombs of Timur and the Timurid dynasty; apocryphal curse story addressed; people close to Gerasimov call the curse story a fabrication; Wikipedia, 'Timur' — exhumed 19 June 1941, examined by Gerasimov, Oshanin, Zezenkova: tall (1.73 m / 5'8"), broad-chested, lame in the right leg (right thighbone knitted to kneecap, knee kept bent), withered right arm; facial reconstruction with Mongoloid features and Caucasoid admixture; Charles Shaw (2011), 'The Gur-i Amir Mausoleum and the Soviet Politics of Preservation', Future Anterior 8(1), 43-63 — the mausoleum's Soviet-period history and preservation politics; the source underpinning the no-curse-inscription finding (cited via Wikipedia); R. D. McChesney (2021), Four Central Asian Shrines: A Socio-Political History of Architecture, Brill — the expedition account and Gur-e Amir history (cited via Wikipedia)
Falsifiability: Gerasimov's expedition archives (photographs, film, osteological reports) are the positive evidence; evidence the expedition never occurred would require discrediting that entire contemporaneous documentary corpus.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1941-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>66.96,39.654,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1941</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Venona Project</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary lines converge: the NSA's own released summary history, the 1995/1996 declassification releases, the FBI's published history, and the 1999 Haynes &amp; Klehr archival study; the one-time-pad reuse blunder (~35,000 duplicate key pages) is cryptographically documented.
Layer: real · Place: Arlington Hall, Virginia (project headquarters)
Dates: 1943 → 1980 (confidence: high)

From 1 February 1943 to 1 October 1980, the US Army's Signal Intelligence Service (later the NSA) ran a counterintelligence program at Arlington Hall that decrypted and translated approximately 2,900–3,000 Soviet intelligence cables (NKVD/KGB/GRU, mostly 1942–1946), revealing extensive Soviet espionage in the United States during and after World War II. The decrypts were declassified and publicly released beginning 11 July 1995.

Earliest attestation: The project itself was secret until 1995; the earliest PUBLIC attestation of its existence by name is MI5 officer Peter Wright's memoir 'Spycatcher' (1987), followed by the official 11 July 1995 release ceremony hosted by DCI John M. Deutch at CIA headquarters.
Sources: NSA, 'Venona — Summary History' (18 May 1995, internal summary released with the 1995 materials): project ran 1 Feb 1943 – 1 Oct 1980; ~2,200 translations published; KGB NY 1944 traffic 49% readable; worked with GCHQ from 1948; CIA/DVIDS, 'Venona Officially Released to the Public (11 Jul 1995)': SSA began 1 Feb 1943 on Clarke's order; collection began 1939; Weisband tip 1948; ~2,900 messages shared by the 1996 conference; John Earl Haynes &amp; Harvey Klehr, 'Venona: Decoding Soviet Espionage in America' (Yale University Press, 1999), Chapter 1: Clarke's separate-peace motive; the 349 Americans with covert ties; the compartmentalization that kept McCarthy-era demagogues mixing 'accurate information about betrayal by men such as Harry White and Alger Hiss with falsehoods about Acheson and Marshall'; Sen. Daniel Patrick Moynihan, tribute to Meredith Gardner, Congressional Record vol. 145 (S8293): NSA 'spent four decades deciphering what Moscow intended to be an unbreakable Soviet cipher'; the decrypts 'finally revealed publicly in 1995'
Falsifiability: Authenticated Soviet archival records showing the decrypted plaintexts do not match the originals, or proof the 1995 releases were forged; neither has ever been produced — 'no one has ever challenged their authenticity' (Frederick M. Hess review, Free Library)</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1943-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.1,38.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1943</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The cryptonym caveat</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Corroborated by convergent independent lines (defectors, FBI files, confessions, post-1991 Russian archival releases), but held below VERIFIED because the identifications are inherently inferential: the same person sometimes had different cryptonyms, the same cryptonym was sometimes reused, and the flagship matches are disputed on the record.
Layer: real · Place: Arlington Hall, Virginia (the identifications' working room)
Dates: 1946 → 1980 (confidence: medium)

A decrypted Venona cable names a person. The NSA's cryptanalysts assigned real names to the Soviet cryptonyms, and the identifications are settled. (The methodological caveat every historian of Venona must carry: the transcripts identify about 349 Americans with a covert relationship to Soviet intelligence, but fewer than half have been matched to real-name identities; the cryptonym-to-person match is a method, not a conviction.)

Earliest attestation: The decrypted translations themselves (1946 onward), cross-filed against FBI investigative records and defector testimony; the NSA's published decryption-rate table (1942: 1.8%; 1943: 15.0%; 1944: 49.0%; 1945: 1.5%)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Venona project' (citing the NSA): 'To what extent the various individuals referred to in the messages were involved with Soviet intelligence is a topic of minor historical dispute... most of the individuals mentioned in the Venona decrypts were probably either clandestine assets and/or contacts'; decryption-rate table: 1942 1.8%, 1943 15.0%, 1944 49.0%, 1945 1.5%; 'Out of some hundreds of thousands of intercepted encrypted texts, it is claimed under 3,000 have been partially or wholly decrypted'; Haynes &amp; Klehr, 'Venona' (Yale, 1999), Ch. 1: the decrypted cables 'identify 349 citizens, immigrants, and permanent residents of the United States who had had a covert relationship with Soviet intelligence agencies'; 'less than half could be identified by their real names and nearly two hundred remained hidden behind cover names'; the same-cover-name cases: 'Muse' (unidentified OSS woman), 'Source No. 19' (claimed as Hopkins, Benes, or Duggan by different authors), 'Bibi', 'Donald', 'Quantum', 'Pers', 'Mole'
Falsifiability: Independent primary evidence (Soviet archives, trial records, confessions) contradicting a specific cryptonym match — which is exactly how the disputed matches are adjudicated in the literature</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1946-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.1,38.87,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1946</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The withheld 1948 Peace Prize (Gandhi)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The non-award and the stated reason are documented fact; the Gandhi reading is the near-universal standard interpretation, supported by the 1961 posthumous award showing the committee could have honored him.
Layer: real · Place: Oslo
Dates: 1948 → 1948 (confidence: high)

The Nobel Peace Prize was not awarded in 1948, the year Mohandas Gandhi was assassinated (30 January 1948); the Norwegian Nobel Committee stated that 'there was no suitable living candidate.' The non-award is understood as the withheld Gandhi prize — posthumous award was possible at the time (Dag Hammarskjold received the 1961 Peace Prize posthumously), and the formal ban on posthumous awards came only in 1974.

Earliest attestation: The Norwegian Nobel Committee's 1948 announcement; the 1961 posthumous Hammarskjold award; the 1974 statute change.
Sources: Norwegian Nobel Committee, 1948 — the non-award and the 'no suitable living candidate' statement; The 1961 posthumous Nobel Peace Prize to Dag Hammarskjold (the practice before the 1974 statute change)
Falsifiability: Committee minutes or correspondence from 1948 naming a different rationale for the non-award would refine the record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1948-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.7522,59.9139,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1948</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1949 Roland Doe case: the best-documented possession dossier</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The documentary event is multiply attested: a contemporary priest's diary, mid-1949 newspaper reports, a 1993 documentary book built from the diary and interviews, archdiocesan authorization, and named dissenting witnesses (Halloran). The grade covers the file — who wrote it, who corroborates, who dissents. The possession itself is ungraded, per the attestation rule.
Layer: real · Place: Cottage City, Maryland (family home); St. Louis, Missouri (St. Louis University Hospital; Alexian Brothers Hospital)
Dates: 1949 → 1949 (confidence: high)

From January to April 1949, a fourteen-year-old boy from Cottage City, Maryland (later moved to St. Louis), documented under the pseudonyms 'Roland Doe' and 'Robbie Mannheim', was the subject of a series of Catholic exorcisms authorized by Archbishop Joseph Ritter and performed by Jesuit priests including Fr. William S. Bowdern, with Fr. Raymond J. Bishop keeping a diary (reportedly ~26 pages). The family's Lutheran minister referred them to Catholic help; forty-eight witnesses are said to have observed episodes, nine of them Jesuits (per Thomas B. Allen). Mid-1949 newspapers printed anonymous reports of the case. William Peter Blatty used the events in The Exorcist (1971). In December 2021, The Skeptical Inquirer reported the boy's identity as Ronald Edwin Hunkeler (1935–2020). Fr. Walter H. Halloran, one of the last surviving eyewitnesses, refused to go on record affirming a possession.

Earliest attestation: Bishop's diary (contemporary, surfaced publicly in the 1980s); the mid-1949 anonymous newspaper reports; Thomas B. Allen's Possessed (1993), built from the diary and interviews
Sources: Fr. Raymond J. Bishop's diary (contemporary, ~26 pages; published via Thomas B. Allen, Possessed, 1993); Mid-1949 anonymous newspaper reports (the source thought to be the family's former pastor, Luther Miles Schulze); Wikipedia, 'Exorcism of Roland Doe' — summarizes the diary record, Ritter's authorization, Halloran's refusal to affirm, the 2021 Skeptical Inquirer identification; Skeptical Inquirer, December 2021 — reports the identity as Ronald Edwin Hunkeler (1935–2020); William Peter Blatty, The Exorcist (1971) — the cultural transmission of the case, not evidence for it
Falsifiability: Production of Bishop's diary showing the episodes were fabricated or never recorded; contemporary archdiocesan files showing no Ritter authorization; demonstration that the mid-1949 newspaper reports were hoaxes. This record claims the documentary trail, never the possession.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1949-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.95,38.94,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1949</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Fermi paradox (the argument, not the evidence)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The argument's existence, its 1950 origin anecdote, and its 1975/1980 formalizations are multiply documented. The paradox is graded as an argument — it proves nothing about whether anyone is out there.
Layer: real · Place: Los Alamos, New Mexico (origin of the anecdote)
Dates: 1950 → 1980 (confidence: high)

In 1950, over lunch at Los Alamos, Enrico Fermi asked 'Where is everybody?' — pointing at the tension between the high estimated number of habitable planets and the absence of observed extraterrestrials. The argument was formalized by Michael Hart ('An Explanation for the Absence of Extraterrestrials on Earth', 1975) and Frank Tipler (1980). It is a reasoning tool about scale versus silence, not evidence for or against extraterrestrial life.

Earliest attestation: Recollections of Fermi's 1950 lunch conversation (published in various memoirs); Hart, QJRAS 16 (1975)
Sources: Michael H. Hart, 'An Explanation for the Absence of Extraterrestrials on Earth', Quarterly Journal of the Royal Astronomical Society 16 (1975); Frank J. Tipler, 'Extraterrestrial Intelligent Beings Do Not Exist', QJRAS 21 (1980); Recollections of the 1950 Fermi lunch (Herb York; Emil Konopinski, 'Where is Everybody?', Physics Today, 2003)
Falsifiability: The argument cannot be falsified as an argument; its conclusion ('somebody should be here') would be settled by a confirmed technosignature, which does not exist</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1950-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-106.3031,35.88,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1950</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bette Nesmith Graham invents correction fluid (Mistake Out / Liquid Paper)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED — 246th family's foil record. Multiply attested by independent secondaries (NYT 2018 'Overlooked No More', USPTO 'Perseverance, thy name is Bette', MPL, Wikipedia, East Texas Historical Association); held below VERIFIED because the 1951/1956 invention-date variance is unresolved and no contemporary 1951 document was inspected this pass.
Layer: real · Place: Dallas, Texas
Dates: 1951 → 1958 (confidence: medium)

Bette Nesmith Graham, an executive secretary at the Texas Bank and Trust in Dallas, invented correction fluid in the early 1950s: a white tempera-paint mixture mixed in her kitchen blender, first called 'Mistake Out' and renamed 'Liquid Paper' in 1958.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary 1951 document inspected this pass; the origin rides her company's later telling plus independent biographies (NYT 2018, USPTO, National Women's History Museum) and the Smithsonian-cited 1958 patent/trademark applications.
Sources: NYT 'Overlooked No More: Bette Nesmith Graham, Who Invented Liquid Paper' (2018-07-11); USPTO, 'Perseverance, thy name is Bette'; Minneapolis Public Library, 'Bette Nesmith Graham, Inventor of Liquid Paper' — 1951 kitchen-blender origin, 'Mistake Out', 1967: 1M+ units/year, sold to Gillette 1979 ~$48M; East Texas Historical Association (Sosebee 2022): born 1924, executive secretary, son Michael Nesmith of the Monkees; Wikipedia 'Liquid Paper': Mistake Out Company formed 1956; renamed Liquid Paper Company 1958; fired after putting company name on bank letterhead; sold to Gillette 1979
Falsifiability: A dated pre-1951 commercial correction-fluid product with the same formulation purpose, or contemporary evidence that Graham's 1951 kitchen experiments postdate a documented competitor's invention.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1951-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-96.797,32.7767,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1951</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1952 Cuyahoga River fire (the big one)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1952 blaze is multiply attested as the large one — the $1.3M+ damage figure converges across accounts — and its photographs are the documented source of the Time 1969 illustration; held below VERIFIED on the damage figure's source variance ($1.3M vs $1.5M+) and thin primary-record citation in the secondary coverage.
Layer: real · Place: Cuyahoga River, Cleveland, Ohio
Dates: 1952 → 1952 (confidence: high)

The 1952 Cuyahoga River fire was a genuinely large blaze — the biggest of the river's documented fires — causing over $1.3 million (some accounts say $1.5 million+) in damage. It is the fire whose photographs the nation actually saw: Time's 1 August 1969 article about the 1969 fire illustrated it with this blaze's picture.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1952 Cleveland press and photographs — the famous Cuyahoga-fire photographs come from THIS fire, not 1969
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine: Time's 1 Aug 1969 article used 'an accompanying photo from an incident in 1952' — the 1952 fire's image as the myth's visual fuel; Discover Magazine: the 1952 fire 'caused more than $1.5 million in damage [Cleveland Plain-Dealer]'; Zweiglist retrospective ('When the Cuyahoga River Burned'): 'the massive 1952 Cuyahoga River blaze that caused upwards of $1.3 million in damages'; Water Tech Online (40th anniversary): the 1952 fire in the thirteen-fire sequence
Falsifiability: Cleveland archive records showing the 1952 fire caused only minor damage — against every account, which agrees the 1952 blaze was the century's big one.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1952-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.6944,41.4993,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1952</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Blake's betrayal of the Berlin Tunnel (1953)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The KGB's pre-knowledge is established by the CIA's own declassified history and post-Soviet releases, which describe the deliberate choice to protect the source; the exact month of Blake's first tip varies by source (late-1953 briefing vs January 1954 planning meeting), holding the grade below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: London (the SIS briefings and Blake's KGB handler meetings)
Dates: 1953 → 1956 (confidence: high)

George Blake, the MI6 officer assigned to the tunnel's planning side, informed his KGB handler of the tunnel plan in the autumn of 1953 — before construction began. Moscow faced a choice between exposing the tunnel (and protecting Soviet communications) and protecting Blake; it chose Blake. The KGB deliberately withheld knowledge of the tap even from the Soviet military users of the lines, let the tunnel operate for its full eleven months, and only staged its 'discovery' after Blake was transferred in 1955.

Earliest attestation: The betrayal itself was secret until Blake's 1961 arrest and confession; the pre-knowledge is established in the CIA's declassified official history (the 22 October 1953 briefing with Blake in the audience) and post-Soviet releases
Sources: CIA official-history excerpt (booklife.com): 'On 22 October 1953, US intelligence officers briefed a British Secret Intelligence Service (SIS) audience that included KGB mole, George Blake'; the KGB's 'first priority was to protect Blake'; early in 1956 the Soviets developed a plan for the tap's 'accidental' discovery; Encyclopedia of Espionage, 'Berlin Tunnel' (faqs.org): 'Blake, employed in a technical division, gave information about the tunnel to the KGB when the project was still in the planning stages'; the KGB 'did not inform anyone in Germany, including the East Germans or the Soviet users of the cable lines'; 'When Blake received a transfer in 1955, the Soviets were free to "discover" the tunnel'; Eyewitness to History, 'The Russians Discover a Spy Tunnel in Berlin, 1956': Blake 'reported the plan to his KGB masters even before ground was broken'; after the Soviet collapse it emerged that the KGB 'never revealed the existence of the tunnel to their military comrades for fear that closing down the operation might alert their American and British adversaries to the existence of a mole within their ranks'
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous KGB records showing no knowledge of the tunnel before 1956, or proof Blake's access to the planning was narrower than the planning-committee record shows</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1953-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.12,51.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1953</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bernice Eddy's ignored 1954 warning (August 1954)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 1954 warning is reported in multiple independent secondary lines (Offit 2005, the Washington Post's 2020 retrospective, Boulder Weekly) with consistent details (the monkey, the photographs, the two superiors); held below VERIFIED because the institutional-motive reading is retrospective and the primary correspondence is not quoted in the available sources.
Layer: real · Place: Bethesda, Maryland (National Institutes of Health)
Dates: 1954-08 → 1954-09 (confidence: medium)

In August 1954 — eight months before the Cutter Incident — NIH scientist Dr. Bernice Eddy, testing a submitted Cutter vaccine sample, found that it had given polio to a test monkey: the supposedly killed-virus vaccine contained live, infectious virus. She reported her findings, with photographs of the paralyzed monkeys, to her superiors — William Workman, head of the Laboratory of Biologics Control, and NIH director William Sebrell Jr. The warning was set aside; Eddy was removed from polio-vaccine safety work.

Earliest attestation: Eddy's 1954 laboratory findings and her report to Workman and Sebrell, as recounted in Offit's 'The Cutter Incident' (2005) and the Washington Post's April 14, 2020 retrospective.
Sources: Michael E. Ruane, Washington Post, April 14, 2020 — Eddy checking the Cutter sample August 30, 1954; the monkey given polio; 'There's going to be a disaster'; she was sidelined; Boulder Weekly, 'The Cutter incident: A cautionary tale for Operation Warp Speed' — Eddy reported paralyzed monkeys with pictures; Sebrell swept the warnings aside; The Maha Report, 'Public Health's Dirty Little Rule' — Eddy's report to Workman and Sebrell; her transfer for identifying the problem
Falsifiability: NIH archival correspondence showing no such monkey test or report in 1954, or records showing Eddy remained on polio-vaccine safety work without interruption</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1954-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0948,38.9847,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1954</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Operation Gold — the Berlin Tunnel</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The CIA's own declassified history, the US Army's military-intelligence chronicle, and multiple independent secondary accounts converge on the dig, the dates, and the operational scale; the exact reel and conversation totals ride secondary aggregators and the British half stays classified, holding the grade below VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Rudow (US sector) → Altglienicke (Soviet sector), Berlin — beneath the sector boundary
Dates: 1954 → 1956 (confidence: high)

In 1954–55 the CIA and MI6 (Operation Gold / Operation Stopwatch) dug a ~450-meter tunnel from Rudow in the American sector of Berlin, under the sector boundary, to Altglienicke in the Soviet sector, where a vertical shaft tapped three Soviet military trunk cables. The tap ran from May 1955 until the night of 21–22 April 1956, recording on the order of 50,000 reels of tape (~40,000 hours of telephone conversations), of which some 443,000 conversations were fully transcribed, producing about 1,750 intelligence reports; the backlog was processed into 1958.

Earliest attestation: The operation was secret until the 1956 Soviet publicity; the earliest documented internal attestation is the CIA's declassified official history (incl. the January 1954 approval and the 1968 after-action report)
Sources: US Army Intelligence Center of Excellence, 'This Week in Military Intelligence — Berlin Tunnel Ceases Operations' (DVIDS): ~50,000 six-hour reels recorded, ~30,000 transcribed; a mole had alerted the Soviets, who 'allowed the operations to continue to protect their source'; operations ceased 22 April 1956; Encyclopedia of Espionage, 'Berlin Tunnel' (faqs.org): tunnel construction ended March 1955, taps began in May; 443,000 transcribed conversations (368,000 Soviet, 75,000 East German), 1,750 intelligence reports; the KGB learned of the plan through Blake while it was still being planned; Eyewitness to History, 'The Russians Discover a Spy Tunnel in Berlin, 1956': Dulles approved the joint CIA/SIS plan January 1954; tunnel completed by May 1955, tapping cables with ~1,200 telephone lines; discovery 22 April 1956; Blake reported the plan 'even before ground was broken'
Falsifiability: Declassified construction or payment records showing no such dig was ever made, or proof the tapped Soviet cables never existed at that junction</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1954-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.5,52.42,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1954</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bandung Conference, April 1955</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and contemporaneously documented: the final communiqué itself, the Indonesian host records, the published opening speeches (Sukarno, Nehru, Nasser, Zhou Enlai), and contemporary world press all agree on the dates, the venue, the 29 delegations, and the Ten Principles adopted unanimously as item G.
Layer: real · Place: Gedung Merdeka (Merdeka Building), Bandung, Indonesia
Dates: 1955-04-18 → 1955-04-24 (confidence: high)

The Asian-African Conference met at Bandung, Indonesia, 18-24 April 1955: delegations from 29 Asian and African states, convened at the invitation of the five Colombo powers (Burma, Ceylon, India, Indonesia, Pakistan), condemned colonialism in its final communiqué, and adopted unanimously the ten-point 'Dasasila Bandung' (Ten Principles of Bandung) declaration on world peace and cooperation.

Earliest attestation: The Final Communiqué of the Asian-African Conference itself (April 1955); Indonesian host-government records; contemporary world press coverage.
Sources: Final Communiqué of the Asian-African Conference (Bandung, April 1955) — the Ten Principles, item G; Asian-African Conference Museum, Gedung Merdeka, Bandung — conference records and delegate lists; 'Bandung Conference', English Wikipedia — 29 states, 18-24 April 1955, Ten Principles (tertiary summary of the communique and scholarship); Vijay Prashad, 'The Darker Nations' (2007) — the 'Spirit of Bandung' and what the conference meant to the colonized world
Falsifiability: Indonesian state archives or contemporary delegate records contradicting the dates, the 29-delegation attendance, or the unanimous adoption of the Ten Principles; or proof the communiqué was drafted after the fact.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>107.6191,-6.9175,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cutter Incident (April–May 1955)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The incident's occurrence is multiply attested in independent contemporary government records (EIS determination, Surgeon General recall order, May 6 program suspension) plus contemporary press and the congressional record — independent primary and documentary lines agree.
Layer: real · Place: Berkeley, California (Cutter Laboratories)
Dates: 1955-04-12 → 1955-05-06 (confidence: high)

In April 1955, production lots of Cutter Laboratories' Salk polio vaccine contained live poliovirus despite passing the required safety tests. On April 27, 1955, the Epidemic Intelligence Service determined that two Cutter production pools — about 120,000 doses — contained live virus; the same day, U.S. Surgeon General Leonard Scheele ordered all Cutter vaccine recalled. On May 6, 1955, the national polio vaccination program was suspended pending investigation of every manufacturer.

Earliest attestation: Epidemic Intelligence Service determination, April 27, 1955; U.S. Surgeon General's recall order, April 27, 1955; contemporary press (The Saturday Evening Post, September 10, 1955).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Cutter Laboratories' — April 12 licensing, April 27 withdrawal, the Cutter incident section; Brill JOAH 2(1–2), 'To Boldly Remember Where We Have Already Been' — EIS April 27, 1955 determination (two production pools, 120,000 doses), Scheele recall the same day; SciELO (S0211-95362012000200003) — Scheele halted the American campaign May 6, 1955; American scientists met confidentially April 26; Steven M. Spencer, 'Where Are We Now on Polio?', The Saturday Evening Post, September 10, 1955 — contemporary press account
Falsifiability: NIH/EIS archival records showing no live virus in the April 1955 Cutter lots, or a contemporary document proving the recall was ordered for a different cause</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.273,37.8715,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cutter Incident's aftermath: blame, law, and the quiet Wyeth cases (1955–1960)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The congressional conclusion and resignations are consistently reported across independent secondary lines (Wikipedia's Cutter Laboratories article, Boulder Weekly, MedicalXpress/HealthDay); the Gottsdanker verdict is a matter of court record; held below VERIFIED because the Wyeth addendum rests on a single secondary line (the SciELO paper's account of the Nathanson report).
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (congressional hearing, June 1955)
Dates: 1955-06 → 1960 (confidence: high)

After the Cutter Incident, the June 1955 congressional inquiry concluded that Cutter's production methods were sound and that the failure lay primarily with the NIH's Laboratory of Biologics Control for inadequate scrutiny of the lots. Oveta Culp Hobby (HEW secretary), William Sebrell Jr. (NIH director), and William Workman (Lab of Biologics Control chief) lost their positions; a jury found Cutter not negligent but liable for breach of implied warranty (Gottsdanker v. Cutter, 1960); and the federal biologics oversight system was rebuilt (Division of Biologics Standards, 1955). Meanwhile Wyeth's vaccine also caused vaccine-associated polio (the Nathanson expert report described 37 cases) — known to a handful of officials, never released to the public — so the 'Cutter alone' verdict is incomplete.

Earliest attestation: Congressional hearing record, June 1955; Gottsdanker v. Cutter Laboratories jury verdict, 1960; Nathanson expert report on the Wyeth cases (1955, never released).
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Cutter Laboratories' — Tripp/Habel inspection found nothing wrong with Cutter's methods; June 1955 congressional conclusion blaming NIH Lab of Biologics Control; resignations (Hobby, Sebrell); Gottsdanker breach-of-warranty verdict; Boulder Weekly, 'The Cutter incident: A cautionary tale for Operation Warp Speed' — congressional inquiry conclusion; lawsuits; Cutter not negligent but liable for breach of implied warranty; SciELO (S0211-95362012000200003) — Wyeth's vaccine-associated cases (37, Nathanson expert report), known only to a handful, never released; Offit on Wyeth; MedicalXpress/HealthDay, 'A lasting legacy of science' — the incident prompted federal officials to step up oversight; Daniel Wilson (Muhlenberg) on the regulatory overhaul
Falsifiability: The congressional hearing record contradicting the reported conclusion, or archival proof that no Wyeth-associated cases existed in 1955</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cutter Incident's toll (April–December 1955)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The toll is consistently reported across independent secondary lines (Offit 2005 via the Washington Post's 2020 account; Wikipedia's Cutter Laboratories article; Boulder Weekly; the Brill JOAH article) — but held below VERIFIED because the recipient-paralytic count varies across sources (51 vs 56) and the figures are retrospective epidemiological estimates.
Layer: real · Place: California, Idaho, Arizona and other states receiving Cutter lots (Berkeley manufacture)
Dates: 1955-04 → 1955-12 (confidence: medium)

The Cutter lots' live virus infected the children they were meant to protect: about 40,000 developed abortive (non-paralytic) poliomyelitis; 51–56 developed paralytic poliomyelitis, of whom five died. Because polio is contagious, the recipients' infections seeded secondary spread: a further 113 people in their families and communities developed paralytic polio, and five of them died.

Earliest attestation: EIS case tracing, spring–summer 1955; consolidated in Paul A. Offit, 'The Cutter Incident' (2005).
Sources: Michael E. Ruane, Washington Post, April 14, 2020 — citing Paul A. Offit, 'The Cutter Incident' (2005): 120,000 children injected, ~40,000 abortive polio, 51 paralyzed, 5 died; Wikipedia, 'Cutter Laboratories' — 40,000 abortive, 56 paralytic, 5 deaths among recipients; 113 secondary paralytic, 5 secondary deaths; Boulder Weekly, 'The Cutter incident: A cautionary tale for Operation Warp Speed' — 120,000 live-virus doses; 40,000 abortive; 56 paralytic, 5 died; 113 secondary paralytic, 5 died; Brill JOAH 2(1–2) — by April 30, per Offit, Cutter's vaccine had 'paralyzed or killed twenty-five children' within 48 hours of the recall; Gallo's secondary-spread figures
Falsifiability: EIS archival case files showing materially different recipient-infection counts, or proof the secondary-spread attribution was epidemiological error (wild polio circulating independently)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.273,37.8715,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence (1956)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary sources converge: the 1955 proposal text itself, McCarthy's May 1956 Morison letter, and Ray Solomonoff's contemporary attendance notes (only McCarthy, Solomonoff, and Minsky stayed the full eight weeks; daily sessions ran 3–8 people).
Layer: real · Place: Dartmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire
Dates: 1955 → 1956 (confidence: high)

The Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence (June–August 1956) at Dartmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire, was the event that named artificial intelligence as a field — the proposal behind it introduced the term and set the research agenda.

Earliest attestation: 'A Proposal for the Dartmouth Summer Research Project on Artificial Intelligence' — J. McCarthy, M.L. Minsky, N. Rochester, C.E. Shannon (summer 1955); McCarthy's 26 May 1956 letter to Robert Morison (Rockefeller Foundation) listing 11 planned attendees
Sources: The Dartmouth proposal text (1955): 'The study is to proceed on the basis of the conjecture that every aspect of learning or any other feature of intelligence can in principle be so precisely described that a machine can be made to simulate it.'; Ray Solomonoff, 'Ray Solomonoff and the Dartmouth Summer Research Project in Artificial Intelligence, 1956' — attendee lists, daily attendance (only McCarthy, Solomonoff, Minsky full period; 3–8 per session); Dartmouth AI@50 (2006) conference archive: 'The term "Artificial Intelligence" was coined at the meeting making it a seminal event in the history of AI'
Falsifiability: A contemporary document showing the proposal was never submitted, or that the term 'artificial intelligence' predates the 1955 proposal in published use</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.2887,43.7044,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Interstate Highway System, 1956 (CORROBORATED)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The statute exists, the funding mechanism exists, the mileage is on the ground — but 'the system' is a living, grown artifact (41,000 authorized, ~48,000 today), so CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED, for the whole-claim level.
Layer: real · Place: United States (the Interstate Highway System)
Dates: 1956 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 (signed by President Dwight D. Eisenhower, 29 June 1956) authorized the National System of Interstate and Defense Highways: 41,000 miles of limited-access highways, 90 percent federally funded through the Highway Trust Fund (fuel taxes), built by the states to a single national design standard.

Earliest attestation: The Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 (Public Law 84-627); Eisenhower's signing statement, 29 June 1956.
Sources: Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 (Public Law 84-627), signed 29 June 1956 — 41,000 miles authorized, 90 percent federal / 10 percent state funding, Highway Trust Fund financed by fuel taxes; states build to a single national design standard; FHWA, 'Interstate Highway System — The Myths' — minimum overpass clearances were raised at the Defense Department's request so military equipment could pass (defense was a real, documented design input — for clearance, not for landing strips); Eisenhower's 1919 Transcontinental Motor Convoy — the then-lieutenant colonel rode as an observer from Washington, D.C. to San Francisco; 62 days through mud, failed bridges, and state-line road chaos — his lived education in the national road problem
Falsifiability: Congressional records showing no such Act, or an Act without the 90/10 Highway Trust Fund funding mechanism, would overturn the record; the statute and the trust fund are both on the books.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1956-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1956</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The eradication of smallpox (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary lines: the last natural case documented and investigated (Ali Maow Maalin, Merca, 26 October 1977), the Global Commission's 9 December 1979 certification after a two-year worldwide search, and WHA33.3's 8 May 1980 declaration; no natural case since.
Layer: real · Place: Merca, Somalia (last natural smallpox case, 26 October 1977)
Dates: 1958 → 1980 (confidence: high)

Smallpox was eradicated by human effort — the last natural case in 1977, global eradication certified by the World Health Assembly in May 1980. The only human disease ever eradicated.

Earliest attestation: Jenner, 'An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae', 1798 (the intervention); WHO Intensified Smallpox Eradication Programme records 1967-1980 and Fenner et al., 'Smallpox and its Eradication' (WHO, 1988) for the campaign
Sources: WHO Resolution WHA33.3, 8 May 1980: 'the world and its peoples have won freedom from smallpox'; WHO/Global Commission: last naturally occurring case — Ali Maow Maalin, hospital cook, Merca, Somalia, 26 October 1977 (variola minor); last variola major case — Rahima Banu, Bangladesh, October 1975; Global Commission certification 9 December 1979 after two years of intense worldwide verification; endorsed by the World Health Assembly 8 May 1980; CDC Sencer Museum / CDC: last case in the US 1949; routine US smallpox vaccination discontinued 1971; the campaign's surveillance-containment (ring) strategy via William Foege, programme led by Donald A. Henderson; Baxby (History Today): surveillance-containment replaced mass vaccination as the winning strategy — the Haygarth-derived isolation-plus-ring-vaccination approach; only official virus stocks remain (USA, Russia)
Falsifiability: A verified natural smallpox case after 1979 would falsify the certification; only laboratory stocks remain (USA, Russia) — no natural transmission since 26 October 1977.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1958-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.77,1.72,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1958</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baran's RAND memos on distributed survivable communications (1960-64)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: the memos are published primary documents; the Internet Society's own brief history assigns the nuclear-war consideration to 'the unrelated RAND study on secure voice' — the one genuine nuclear-attribution in the lineage, kept honestly so the grade kills the transplant (the ARPANET reading), never the donor.
Layer: real · Place: RAND Corporation, Santa Monica, California
Dates: 1960 → 1964 (confidence: high)

Paul Baran's RAND Corporation memoranda (1960-64, published as 'On Distributed Communications,' 1964) proposed a distributed, packet-switched communications system designed to remain operational under nuclear attack — in the Air Force secure-voice context. This is the genuine survivability work whose rationale the ARPANET nuclear myth later grafted onto the wrong network.

Earliest attestation: The RAND memoranda themselves (published 1964) — the primary documents; their content summarized in the Internet Society's 'A Brief History of the Internet' note.
Sources: Internet Society, 'A Brief History of the Internet' — the note: 'only the unrelated RAND study on secure voice considered nuclear war'; Paul Baran 'the first to propose a theoretical model for communication using packet switching, conducted the RAND study above' (carried via the ARPANET article's quotation and summary); fact-index.com, ARPANET summary — 'It was from the RAND study that the false rumor started claiming that the ARPANET was somehow related to building a network resistant to nuclear war... Although ARPANET did not exactly share the objective of Baran's project, he said his work contributed to the development of ARPANET'; Elmer Shapiro's minutes of the 9-10 October 1967 design meeting record a version of Baran's 'hot potato' routing as usable
Falsifiability: The published RAND memos are the evidence; a re-dating of the study out of the 1960s would revise the record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1960-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.4912,34.0059,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1960</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The SETI search record (1960–present): sixty years of listening, zero confirmed artificial signals</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The search programs are multiply documented by their own institutions' records, published papers, and observing logs; the null result (no confirmed artificial signal) is the programs' own reported outcome. 2026-10-17 (via #431): Mason et al. 2025 stellar-bycatch constraint appended (grade held — the Turin-shroud precedent).
Layer: real · Place: Green Bank, West Virginia (origin point; programs span many sites)
Dates: 1960 → 2026 (confidence: high)

A continuous, documented scientific search for extraterrestrial technosignatures has run since 1960 (Project Ozma, Frank Drake, Green Bank) through Ohio State's Big Ear all-sky surveys, Harvard's META/BETA, Project Phoenix (1995–2004), SERENDIP, SETI@home (1999–2020, ~5 million volunteers), and Breakthrough Listen (launched 2015, $100M). As of 2026, no confirmed artificial signal has been detected.

Earliest attestation: Frank Drake's Project Ozma observing logs, Green Bank, April–July 1960
Sources: SETI Institute — history of Project Ozma (Frank Drake, 1960); Breakthrough Listen (UC Berkeley / Breakthrough Initiatives) — program documentation, launched July 2015; SETI@home (UC Berkeley) — 1999–2020 participation and results archive; Frank Drake, 'The Drake Equation' (Green Bank meeting, 1961) — the probabilistic framing, documented via meeting records; Mason, Garrett, Siemion, 'Simulating the Stellar Bycatch: Constraining the Prevalence of Extraterrestrial Transmitters within Radio SETI Surveys' (submitted 25 Nov 2025, arXiv:2511.20231v1): Besançon Galactic Model applied to Breakthrough Listen's Enriquez/Price survey (6,182,364 stellar objects, 1,229 pointings, ≤25 kpc) — ≤0.000995% of stellar systems within 2.5 kpc contain high-duty-cycle transmitters (EIRP_min ≥ 5×10^16 W, near-zero drift)
Falsifiability: A single confirmed artificial technosignature would overturn the null result; it has not occurred in 60+ years of documented searching</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1960-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.8397,38.4331,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1960</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Stevenson reincarnation case literature</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The case literature is multiply attested: the books, the division, the case count, and the documented protocols are independently verifiable; the claim stops at documentation, never asserting what the cases mean.
Layer: real · Place: Charlottesville, Virginia (University of Virginia, Division of Perceptual Studies)
Dates: 1960 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The University of Virginia's Division of Perceptual Studies holds a documented body of ~2,500-3,000 cases of children reporting spontaneous memories of previous lives, assembled by psychiatrist Ian Stevenson (1920-2007) and continued by Jim Tucker: the case files exist, the 'solved' cases name verifiable details checked against the previous person's records, and the birthmark/birth-defect correspondence claims are published in the peer-reviewed and university-press literature.

Earliest attestation: Ian Stevenson, Twenty Cases Suggestive of Reincarnation (University Press of Virginia, 1966; 2nd ed. 1974)
Sources: Stevenson, I. — Twenty Cases Suggestive of Reincarnation (University Press of Virginia, 1966; 2nd ed. 1974); Stevenson, I. — Children Who Remember Previous Lives (McFarland, 1987); Stevenson, I. — Reincarnation and Biology: A Contribution to the Etiology of Birthmarks and Birth Defects (Praeger, 1997); Tucker, J.B. — Life Before Life (St. Martin's Press, 2005); Return to Life (St. Martin's Press, 2013); University of Virginia, Division of Perceptual Studies (founded 1967) — the institutional holder of the case archive; IFLScience, 19 Oct 2026 — re-report of Chadha &amp; Stevenson (1988): 326 'cases suggestive of reincarnation,' violent-death/intermission-period correlation; the piece's own framing ('not hard science,' data overreached) carried as the 2026 press state of the literature; Sadeghi, H., Pehlivanova, M. &amp; Cozzolino, P. 2026, Psychology of Consciousness: Theory, Research, and Practice (APA, online-first Aug 13, 2026); publicized via UVA Research News Oct 18, 2026 + ScholarPeer explainer Oct 20, 2026 — 1,678 DOPS archived cases analyzed; median death-to-birth gap 21 months after most-expected deaths vs 12 months after most-sudden (16-month overall median); modest effect (2-3% of variance); authors explicitly state the pattern cannot be read as evidence for or against reincarnation — a documented internal pattern of the archive, not a metaphysical finding
Falsifiability: Demonstrated fabrication across the flagship cases, or a controlled prospective study finding no above-chance correspondences, would falsify the documented-phenomenon claim; note this grades the FILES, not the metaphysics</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1960-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-78.508,38.0336,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1960</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>NASA adopts Velcro for Apollo — the myth's honest root</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED: multiple independent accounts converge — the Velcro company's own FAQ ('NASA later popularized its use... during the space race and Apollo era'), Smithsonian 2017 (aerospace industry first to recognize the usefulness; astronauts didn't want zippers and laces in spacesuits; items floating in zero-g), Livescience (Apollo astronauts used Velcro in the 1960s), Museum of Flight (NASA recognized its microgravity usefulness). Held below VERIFIED because the exact procurement documents were not inspected this pass.
Layer: real · Place: United States (NASA/Apollo program)
Dates: 1960 → 1978 (confidence: medium)

NASA adopted hook-and-loop fasteners for Apollo-era spaceflight in the 1960s to secure tools, food pouches, and equipment in weightlessness; the aerospace industry was Velcro's first serious market, and the visible space use popularized the fastener on Earth — this genuine association is the honest root of the 'NASA invented Velcro' myth.

Earliest attestation: No single dated NASA procurement document located in this pass; multiply reported in secondary accounts (Velcro Companies, Smithsonian 2017, Museum of Flight, Livescience).
Sources: Velcro Companies FAQ: 'NASA later popularized its use by relying on VELCRO Brand fasteners to secure items in weightlessness during the space race and Apollo era.'; Smithsonian Magazine (2017): the burgeoning aerospace industry was the first to recognize Velcro's usefulness — spacesuits, floating items in zero gravity; patent expired 2 April 1978.; LiveScience: 'In the 1960s, Apollo astronauts used Velcro to secure all manner of devices in space for easy retrieval.'; Museum of Flight blog: 'NASA recognized how useful this could be in microgravity to secure items.'
Falsifiability: Apollo-era NASA procurement or mission records showing no hook-and-loop use, or dated evidence that Velcro's consumer popularization predates the space program.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1960-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-95.09,29.56,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1960</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cuban Missile Crisis (14–28 October 1962)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Contemporaneously and multiply documented by both superpowers' archives: U-2 imagery, the ExComm tapes, the two Khrushchev letters, presidential addresses, Soviet records, and the participants' own later testimony — the crisis as an event is beyond dispute.
Layer: real · Place: Cuba (deployment sites); Washington D.C. (ExComm)
Dates: 1962-10-14 → 1962-10-28 (confidence: high)

The October 1962 Cuban Missile Crisis — the thirteen-day confrontation of 14–28 October 1962 over Soviet SS-4/SS-5 nuclear missiles in Cuba (discovered by U-2 photography on 14 October), met by Kennedy's naval 'quarantine' announced 22 October, Khrushchev's two letters of 26 and 27 October, and the announced settlement of 28 October — was the documented near-miss it is remembered as: on 'Black Saturday' 27 October a U-2 was shot down over Cuba (Maj. Rudolf Anderson killed) and a second U-2 strayed over Soviet Chukotka, prompting the secret back-channel settlement that ended the crisis the next morning.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: U-2 photography (14 Oct 1962), ExComm tapes, Khrushchev's letters, Kennedy's 22 &amp; 28 Oct addresses, the quarantine proclamation
Sources: U-2 reconnaissance photography, 14 Oct 1962 — the discovery; The Kennedy Tapes / ExComm meeting transcripts (May &amp; Zelikow, 1997); President Kennedy's quarantine address, 22 Oct 1962; crisis-resolution statement, 28 Oct 1962; Khrushchev's letters to Kennedy, 26 &amp; 27 Oct 1962; Scientific American, 'RFK's Secret Role in the Cuban Missile Crisis' — settlement timeline, RFK–Dobrynin meeting 27 Oct evening, Radio Moscow announcement 28 Oct 10 AM
Falsifiability: A contemporary record contradicting the missile deployment or the crisis timeline — none exists; the deployment is multiply confirmed by both sides' archives</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.5,21.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kennedy secret White House tapes (1962–1963)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by the physical tapes in the JFK Library's custody, the installer's own oral history, the 1973 public announcement, and the published forensic transcripts — a documentary corpus, not a memory.
Layer: real · Place: The White House, Washington D.C. (Oval Office, Cabinet Room)
Dates: 1962-07 → 1963-11 (confidence: high)

In the summer of 1962 President John F. Kennedy had a secret taping system installed in the White House — Tandberg reel-to-reel recorders (obtained from the U.S. Army Signal Corps by Secret Service Agent Robert Bouck) wired to concealed microphones in the Oval Office and the Cabinet Room — which recorded some 248 hours of meetings plus more than 17 hours of telephone conversations between July 1962 and November 1963 without the knowledge of the participants (probably except Robert Kennedy). The tapes preserve the real-time, unguarded deliberations of the ExComm during the October 1962 Cuban Missile Crisis, and were first deeded to the John F. Kennedy Presidential Library in 1975; crisis transcripts were published in May &amp; Zelikow, 'The Kennedy Tapes' (1997).

Earliest attestation: The tapes themselves (July 1962–Nov 1963); Secret Service Agent Robert Bouck's 1976 oral history on the installation; JFK Library holdings announcement, July 1973
Sources: Miller Center, Presidential Recordings Program — preface to 'John F. Kennedy: The Great Crises' (Zelikow &amp; May): Bouck's installation account, Tandberg machines from the Army Signal Corps, microphone placements; Ernest R. May &amp; Philip D. Zelikow, 'The Kennedy Tapes: Inside the White House During the Cuban Missile Crisis' (Harvard University Press, 1997) — published transcripts of the crisis tapes; ABC News via politicalwire (2012): 248 hours of meetings + 17+ hours of phone conversations; system installed starting July 1962; Washington Post, 18 July 1973 (Jules Witcover): JFK Library announcement of 68 dictaphone belts + 125 magnetic tapes; meetings recorded from July 1962; cubanmissilecrisis.org — audio archive: system installed summer 1962, known only to the President, his secretary, Robert Kennedy, and the technicians
Falsifiability: Proof that the tapes are fabrications or misattributed recordings — contradicted by the Library's chain of custody, Bouck's testimony, and corroborating participant recollections</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The secret Jupiter-missile trade (27 October 1962)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply and independently confirmed: the RFK–Dobrynin meeting, Dobrynin's cable, Khrushchev's correspondence, the actual Jupiter withdrawal from Turkey within months, and the 1989 public disclosures — both superpowers' archives converge on the secret clause.
Layer: real · Place: Washington D.C. (Justice Department — RFK–Dobrynin meeting); Izmir/Cigli, Turkey (Jupiter bases)
Dates: 1962-10-27 → 1963-04 (confidence: high)

The Cuban Missile Crisis was settled by a SECRET American concession: on the evening of 27 October 1962, at President Kennedy's instruction, Attorney General Robert Kennedy told Soviet Ambassador Anatoly Dobrynin that the United States would remove its Jupiter nuclear missiles from Turkey within a few months — on the condition that the concession remain secret and never be treated as a public quid pro quo. Khrushchev announced the Soviet withdrawal from Cuba the next morning (28 Oct); the Jupiters were removed from Turkey by April 1963 (Operation Pot Pie). The secrecy held for roughly 25 years — the assurance stayed unpublished until the 1987–1989 disclosures.

Earliest attestation: The RFK–Dobrynin meeting itself (27 Oct 1962); Dobrynin's cable to Moscow; corroborated by the ExComm transcripts, Sorensen's papers, and the 1987–1989 disclosures
Sources: Scientific American, 'RFK's Secret Role in the Cuban Missile Crisis' — RFK tasked to give Dobrynin the private assurance of Jupiter removal 'at a later date'; assurance kept secret until 1989; settlement timeline 27–28 Oct; American Diplomacy, 'Robert Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis — A Reinterpretation' — RFK arranged the Dobrynin meeting 27 Oct evening; informed the ambassador Jupiters would be withdrawn from Turkey as a secret component of the settlement; Dobrynin's cable to Moscow on the 27 Oct meeting (cited via secondary accounts and the 1989 disclosures); Jupiter withdrawal from Turkey, completed by April 1963 (Operation Pot Pie) — execution of the secret clause; The 1987–1989 declassification wave and Dobrynin's memoir — the secrecy broken after ~25 years
Falsifiability: A contemporary U.S. or Soviet record showing the Jupiter removal was unrelated to the crisis settlement, or proof the 27 Oct RFK–Dobrynin exchange never occurred — contradicted by both sides' records</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The mutual lifting of the 1054 excommunications, 7 December 1965</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Primary document survives (the declaration text, published by the Holy See) and the simultaneous Rome/Istanbul readings are contemporary-documented. Grade stops at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED pending nothing further — the document is public, but the criterion of 'multiple independent primary sources' is satisfied conservatively by the two readings; the event itself is uncontested in the scholarship.
Layer: real · Place: St. Peter's Basilica, Rome &amp; the Patriarchal Church, Istanbul (simultaneous)
Dates: 1965-12-07 → 1965-12-07 (confidence: high)

On 7 December 1965, Pope Paul VI and Ecumenical Patriarch Athenagoras I simultaneously lifted the excommunications of 1054 — read aloud at a public session of the Second Vatican Council in Rome and at a special ceremony at the Patriarchate in Istanbul — 'committing them to oblivion' in the words of the Joint Declaration.

Earliest attestation: The Joint Catholic-Orthodox Declaration of His Holiness Pope Paul VI and the Ecumenical Patriarch Athenagoras I, 7 December 1965 (text survives; published by the Holy See and republished widely)
Sources: Joint Catholic-Orthodox Declaration of His Holiness Pope Paul VI and the Ecumenical Patriarch Athenagoras I, 7 December 1965 (read simultaneously in Rome and Istanbul; text archived at christianunity.va); Public Orthodoxy, 'Leo XIV and Bartholomew: The Importance and Aftermath of a Meeting' (2025-12-09): the December 1965 ceremony as the origin of the entire modern joint-declaration series; National Catholic Reporter republication of the declaration text (2006-11-29); Seed record evt-east-west-schism-1054-001 (the 1965 lifting was scoped out there as 'a separate documented event' — now documented here); Holy See Press Office, Joint Declaration of Pope Leo XIV and Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew I, 29 Nov 2025 — re-quotes the 1965 declaration at length and commemorates its 60th anniversary (official English text); Order of St. Andrew / Archons of the Ecumenical Patriarchate: Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew's Doxology at St. George, Phanar, 7 Dec 2025 — marking the 60th anniversary of the lifting of the excommunications
Falsifiability: The record would move toward DEBUNKED if the declaration text proved fabricated or the simultaneous readings never occurred; toward a weaker grade if the text's key operative clause ('committed to oblivion') proved a later interpolation.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1965-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4539,41.9022,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1965</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Fisher Space Pen AG-7 (1965–1967)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent secondary attestation from three directions — Scientific American (2006), Smithsonian Magazine, and the patent record — agrees on independent private development, ~$1M of Fisher's own capital, two years of NASA testing, Apollo 7 first flight, and the ~400-unit / $2.95 1967 purchase; ~30 pens flew to the Moon (12 reaching the surface, per Space.com), and ISS crews still use them as of 2021.
Layer: real · Place: Boulder City, Nevada (Fisher Pen Company)
Dates: 1965 → 1968 (confidence: medium)

Paul C. Fisher independently developed the AG-7 pressurized-cartridge space pen (US Patent 3,285,228, filed 19 May 1965); after ~2 years of NASA testing it flew on Apollo 7 (Oct 1968), and NASA purchased ~400 pens at $2.95 each in 1967.

Earliest attestation: US Patent 3,285,228 (filed 19 May 1965) — the pressurized ball-point cartridge
Sources: US Patent 3,285,228 — pressurized ball-point pen cartridge, filed 19 May 1965; Scientific American, "Fact or Fiction?" (2006) — Fisher's ~$1M private investment, no NASA development money, pen could write −50°F to +400°F, underwater; Smithsonian Magazine — AG7 patented 1966, flew on Apollo 7 after two years of NASA testing; Wikipedia "Space Pen" — NASA purchased ~400 pens at $2.95 apiece (reported 1967); nearly 30 Fisher pens flew to the Moon, 12 reached the lunar surface; ISS use as of 2021
Falsifiability: NASA procurement records showing no AG-7 purchase in 1967–68, or a development contract funding Fisher's work (would also falsify the myth record's funding claim)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1965-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-114.83,35.98,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1965</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>NASA's $128.89 mechanical pencils (1965)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by NASA historians (via Scientific American 2006) with the exact order figures — 34 pencils, $4,382.50, $128.89 each — and the pencil's abandonment is multiply attested: graphite tips flaked and drifted near equipment, and pencils' flammability became unacceptable after the Apollo 1 fire. The irony is the foil for the myth record: the 'cheap pencil' cost ~44× the $2.95 pen that replaced it.
Layer: real · Place: Houston, Texas (NASA Manned Spacecraft Center / Johnson Space Center)
Dates: 1965 → 1967 (confidence: medium)

In 1965 NASA ordered 34 mechanical pencils from Houston's Tycam Engineering Manufacturing, Inc. for $4,382.50 — $128.89 per pencil — sparking a public outcry; pencils were then found hazardous in spacecraft (flaking graphite tips drifting in microgravity, flammable wood in the post-Apollo-1 cabin), and the agency moved away from them.

Earliest attestation: NASA procurement records (1965), via NASA historians quoted in Scientific American 2006
Sources: Scientific American, "Fact or Fiction?" (2006) — the 1965 Tycam order: 34 mechanical pencils, $4,382.50 total, $128.89 each; the public outcry; pencil hazards (flaking tips, flammability post-Apollo 1); ZME Science — "Myth busted": the 1965 mechanical-pencil order and the $128.89/unit public controversy; graphite shavings jeopardizing instruments; NASA's search for a cheaper, safer instrument; Indiana Public Media, "Pens in Space" (2019) — pencils broke, floating lead was a safety hazard; pencil lead and wood burn rapidly in pure oxygen environments; Apollo 1-era writing-instrument requirements
Falsifiability: NASA procurement records showing a different order quantity or price, or contemporary documentation of continued pencil use on crewed missions after 1968 without the hazard assessment</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1965-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-95.09,29.56,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1965</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Honeywell v. Sperry Rand: the courtroom that unmade ENIAC (19 October 1973)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary sources: the decision text itself (Finding 3.1.19 on Atanasoff's credibility; Order 26.1.1 declaring the patent invalid), the case metadata (citation, dates, judge), the Hagley Museum archive of the trial records, and contemporary secondary accounts all converge on the same facts.
Layer: real · Place: U.S. District Court for the District of Minnesota, Minneapolis
Dates: 1967 → 1973 (confidence: high)

On 19 October 1973, U.S. District Judge Earl R. Larson of the District of Minnesota decided Honeywell Inc. v. Sperry Rand Corp. (180 U.S.P.Q. 673; Case 4-67 Civ. 138), invalidating the 1964 ENIAC patent (U.S. Patent 3,120,606, filed 1947) on four grounds — including derivation: 'Eckert and Mauchly did not themselves first invent the automatic electronic digital computer, but instead derived that subject matter from one Dr. John Vincent Atanasoff.' The trial ran 1 June 1971 – 13 March 1972 with 77 witnesses and ~33,000 exhibits; the suits were filed 26 May 1967 (Honeywell's filing minutes earlier). The ruling placed the invention of the electronic digital computer in the public domain; it was never appealed. The news was largely buried: the Saturday Night Massacre broke the next day, 20 October 1973.

Earliest attestation: The decision text itself, 180 U.S.P.Q. 673 (D. Minn. 1973), signed 19 October 1973.
Sources: Honeywell, Inc. v. Sperry Rand Corp., 180 U.S.P.Q. 673 (D. Minn. 1973) — case metadata: Court, decided October 19, 1973, Judge Earl R. Larson; Decision text PDF (Atanasoff archive) — 3.1.19: the court finds Atanasoff's testimony credible on the knowledge Mauchly derived; 26.1.1: the ENIAC patent 'is hereby declared to be invalid and unenforceable'; dated October 19, 1973, Earl R. Larson; Hagley Museum and Library, Honeywell vs. Sperry Rand records 1935–1973 — decision handed down 19 Oct 1973 upholding Honeywell on every count; damages not awarded (no willful fraud); Gizmodo, 'Why Was the Computer Never Patented?' — IBM settled with Sperry Rand for $10 million; the four invalidation grounds; the Saturday Night Massacre buried the coverage
Falsifiability: A higher-court reversal or a competing ruling upholding the ENIAC patent — none exists; the decision was never appealed.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1967-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-93.2667,44.9783,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1967</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first undisputed North Pole arrivals over the surface — Plaisted 1968 (snowmobile), Herbert 1969 (on foot)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — VERIFIED on independently confirmed fixes: Plaisted's 1968 position was confirmed by the supporting airlift (the fuel drops and the radio fixes); Herbert's 1969 position came at the midpoint of a 476-day crossing of the entire Arctic Ocean by dog team, with continuous position records. These are the first surface arrivals with no serious scholarly dispute — the reason the Cook/Peary controversy (evt-cook-north-pole-claim-1908-001 DEBUNKED, evt-peary-north-pole-claim-1909-001 VERIFIED-expedition/CORROBORATED-point) can be closed honestly: the question 'who really was first, indisputably' has an answer, and it is 1968/1969.
Layer: real · Place: North Pole
Dates: 1968-04-01 → 1969-04-06 (confidence: high)

The first expedition to reach the North Pole over the surface with an undisputed, independently verifiable arrival was Ralph Plaisted's 1968 snowmobile expedition — Plaisted and three companions arrived at the Pole in April 1968. The first to reach it the traditional way, on foot with dog teams, was Sir Wally Herbert's 1968-69 British Trans-Arctic Expedition, which crossed the entire Arctic from Point Barrow, Alaska to Svalbard over 476 days and reached the Pole on 6 April 1969 — the 60th anniversary of Peary's claimed arrival.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary expedition records, 1968-1969; independently confirmed positions.
Sources: BBC Science Focus, 'Who really discovered the North Pole?': 'the first undisputed expedition to reach the North Pole over the surface did not achieve its goal until 1968, when the American Ralph Plaisted and three companions arrived on snowmobiles'; 'On 6 April the following year, the British explorer Wally — later Sir Walter — Herbert became the first to reach the North Pole the traditional way, on foot'; Wikipedia 'Peary's Expedition (1908-1909)': Herbert in 1968-1969 'crossed the entire Arctic from Point Barrow to Svalbard with four dog teams over 476 days, reaching the North Pole on April 6, 1969 — the 60th anniversary of Peary's claimed attainment'
Falsifiability: Independent positional evidence contradicting the 1968 and 1969 arrival fixes — none exists; both positions were independently confirmed.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1968-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,90.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1968</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The first AI winter (c. 1974–1980)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent secondary attestation of the funding phenomenon across multiple histories: the Lighthill report's devastating verdict and the UK funding halt, the Mansfield Amendment's 1969 mission-orientation requirement, DARPA's 1973–74 academic cutbacks, and Moravec's account (via Crevier) of the over-promising dynamic — held below VERIFIED because the periodization (1974–1980) is a conventional scholarly dating, not a measured fact.
Layer: real · Place: Washington, D.C. (DARPA) / London (Science Research Council) — the funding rooms
Dates: 1969 → 1980 (confidence: medium)

The first AI winter (c. 1974–1980) was a real funding-and-credibility collapse in artificial intelligence research, triggered by the Lighthill report (1973) and DARPA's subsequent cutbacks to academic AI research.

Earliest attestation: James Lighthill, 'Artificial Intelligence: A General Survey' (1973, UK Science Research Council); the Mansfield Amendment (1969) to the Defense Procurement Authorization Act; the 1984 AAAI debate where Schank and Minsky warned of the coming chain reaction (pessimism → press → funding cutback → end of serious research)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'AI winter' — term first appeared 1984 at the AAAI annual meeting (Schank &amp; Minsky debate); two major winters c. 1974–1980 and 1987–2000; Lighthill report 1973; Mansfield Amendment 1969; DARPA cutbacks 1973–74; Moravec (via Crevier) on the over-promising dynamic; TechTarget, 'What is AI Winter?' — the first AI winter 1974–1980 following the Lighthill report's verdict that AI 'had essentially failed to live up to the grandiose objectives it laid out'; UK ceased AI funding
Falsifiability: Funding ledgers showing no real decline in UK/US AI research funding 1973–1976, or a contemporary record showing the Lighthill report had no policy effect</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Apollo Moon landings, 1969-1972</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary lines converge: 382 kg of lunar samples analyzed by laboratories worldwide; laser retroreflectors still returning pulses from Earth (McDonald Observatory detected Apollo 15's within days of deployment, 31 July 1971); orbital photography of all six sites by non-US probes as well as LRO; contemporaneous tracking by non-US stations during the missions; and the hostile superpower's own monitoring with no fraud denunciation.
Layer: real · Place: Mare Tranquillitatis (Apollo 11 site; hardware and astronaut footpaths visible in LRO imagery)
Dates: 1969 → 1972 (confidence: high)

Between July 1969 and December 1972, six crewed Apollo missions (Apollo 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17) landed on the Moon, and twelve astronauts walked on its surface — beginning with Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin in Mare Tranquillitatis on 20-21 July 1969.

Earliest attestation: The missions themselves, July 1969: onboard telemetry, tracking-station logs, the Slow-Scan TV transmission, the returned hardware, and the Lunar Sample Laboratory chain of custody
Sources: NASA mission archives and the Lunar Sample Laboratory: 382 kg of rocks and regolith returned (Apollo 11, 12, 14-17), showing features unique to an airless, impact-dominated environment, analyzed by thousands of scientists worldwide; Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, LROC Narrow Angle Camera (2009-present), ASU LROC Science Operations Center: ~0.5 m/pixel imagery of all six Apollo landing sites — descent stages, rovers, ALSEP packages, astronaut footpaths and rover tracks; Japan's SELENE (Kaguya) and India's Chandrayaan-1: independent orbital imaging; ISRO (2009, Prakash Chauhan) identified surface disturbances and rover tracks at the Apollo 15 site and called it 'independent corroboration'; Laser ranging retroreflectors emplaced by Apollo 11, 14 and 15 — ongoing Earth-Moon laser ranging experiments in continuous use since 1969; Contemporaneous third-party tracking: Jodrell Bank (UK, Lovell Telescope) and Bochum Observatory (West Germany) independently tracked Apollo 11's radio signals; the Soviet deep-space network monitored the missions (Luna 15 was in lunar orbit in July 1969) — and the USSR never mounted a sustained fraud denunciation, the posture history records; Apollo 12 (Nov 1969): astronauts retrieved parts of the earlier unmanned Surveyor 3 lander and returned them to Earth — a testable physical interaction between two lunar missions
Falsifiability: Verified archival evidence that the telemetry originated from an Earth site rather than the Moon (e.g. an authenticated Soviet intercept log showing Earth-origin signals), or peer-reviewed geochemical proof that the 382 kg of returned samples are terrestrial manufactures — none has ever been produced; the USSR's own archives remain the cleanest open audit trail</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>23.4731,0.6741,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The ARPANET and the first message ('LO', 29 October 1969)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines converge: the contemporaneous IMP log at UCLA, the participants' own accounts (Kleinrock, Kline), the University of California's institutional account, and the encyclopedic record — on the date, the time, the two-letter first message, the crash, and the hour-later full login.
Layer: real · Place: UCLA, Boelter Hall room 3420, Los Angeles, California (transmission origin)
Dates: 1969-10-29 → 1969-10-29 (confidence: high)

The ARPANET — the first packet-switched computer network, funded by ARPA (later DARPA) — went live in 1969 with the stated purpose of letting researchers share scarce, expensive time-sharing computers. The first host-to-host transmission ran 29 October 1969 at 22:30 PST: UCLA graduate student Charley Kline, supervised by Leonard Kleinrock, typed 'login' on the UCLA SDS Sigma 7 host (Boelter Hall room 3420) toward Bill Duvall's SRI SDS 940 host — the SRI machine crashed after the letters 'l' and 'o', and the full login succeeded about an hour later. The first permanent ARPANET link followed 21 November 1969; the initial four-node network (UCLA, SRI, UCSB, Utah) was complete by 5 December 1969.

Earliest attestation: The UCLA IMP log (kept at UCLA, now in the Kleinrock Internet History Center) — the contemporaneous operational record of the transmission
Sources: ARPANET, Wikipedia — first successful host-to-host connection: SRI programmer Bill Duvall and UCLA student programmer Charley Kline, 10:30 pm PST 29 October 1969; Kline typed 'login', SDS 940 crashed after two characters; full login ~an hour later; 'lo' the first two characters; permanent link 21 Nov 1969; four nodes by 5 Dec 1969; University of California, 'Lo and behold: The internet' — Kleinrock-led basement lab at the engineering school; Kline got two letters of 'login' before the crash; 'Lo'; Gizmodo, 'The first internet message ever sent was LO' — the UCLA IMP Log excerpt: transmission from UCLA SDS Sigma 7 to SRI SDS 940, 22:30 hours 29 Oct 1969, 'l' and 'o' then the crash; full login ~an hour later
Falsifiability: A dated contradiction from a participant's contemporary record or the IMP log itself would revise this; none exists.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.44268,34.06883,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1969 Cuyahoga River fire</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent lines converge on the date, the ~24–30-minute duration, the ~$50,000 damage, the ignition context, and the fire sequence behind it; the event's smallness is itself the documented fact.
Layer: real · Place: Cuyahoga River, Cleveland, Ohio
Dates: 1969-06-22 → 1969-06-22 (confidence: high)

On the morning of Sunday, 22 June 1969, an oil slick on the Cuyahoga River at Cleveland caught fire — probably from sparks off a passing railcar — burning about 24–30 minutes, damaging railroad bridges spanning the river, costing roughly $50,000, and extinguished by land-based battalions and a city fireboat. The thirteenth and last of the river's documented fires.

Earliest attestation: Cleveland fire department record and the local press, June 1969 — the fire earned only small local coverage at the time
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine ('The Cuyahoga River Caught Fire at Least a Dozen Times, but No One Cared Until 1969'): 30 minutes, ~$50,000 damage, no photo taken, Time's 1952-photo substitution, Mayor Stokes's 23 June 1969 press conference; Outside Online ('51 Years Later, the Cuyahoga River Burns Again'): 1868 first fire, 11 more before 1969, 24-minute duration, Stokes's $100M water-initiative context; Discover Magazine ('40 Years After the Cuyahoga Burned'): half-hour fire, ~$50,000 damage, 1868 earliest burning, 1952 fire's $1.5M+ damage; Water Tech Online (40th anniversary, drawing on EPA): ~30 minutes, ~$50,000 damage; earlier fires 1868, 1883, 1887, 1912, 1922, 1936, 1941, 1948, 1952; Clean Water Act 1972 context
Falsifiability: The fire department's 1969 incident record or a contemporary photo showing a large, long-burning fire on 22 June 1969 — no such photo exists, and the record says 24–30 minutes.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.6944,41.4993,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Soviet Fisher pen purchase (1969)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The second kill of the myth record: the Soviets did not outwit the pen with a pencil — they bought the same pen. Soviet cosmonauts used pencils and grease pencils until adopting the Fisher AG-7 in 1969 (100 units for all future missions, per the Fisher account carried in secondary sources). Both space programs converged on the pressurized cartridge.
Layer: real · Place: Moscow, USSR (Soviet space program procurement)
Dates: 1969 → 1969 (confidence: medium)

In 1969 the Soviet space program purchased 100 Fisher Space Pens (with 1,000 ink cartridges) for use on all future missions — Soviet cosmonauts had used pencils and grease pencils on plastic slates, and, like NASA, adopted the Fisher pressurized pen.

Earliest attestation: Fisher Pen Company sales account, as reported in secondary space-history coverage
Sources: Wikipedia "Space Pen" — Soviet cosmonauts used pencils and grease pencils until adopting a space pen in 1969 with a purchase of 100 units for use on all future missions; ZME Science — "Myth busted": Soviet cosmonauts used pencils and grease pencils on plastic slates until also adopting a space pen in 1969; Space Daily (2026) — myth deconstruction: neither Soviet central planning nor NASA subcontracting solved weightless writing; both programs converged on Fisher's privately developed pen
Falsifiability: Soviet/Russian space-program records showing no Fisher pen procurement, or continued pencil-only use on Soviet missions after 1969</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The ozone hole and the Montreal Protocol (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: Molina &amp; Rowland's Nature paper (28 June 1974) showed chlorine from photochemical decomposition of CFCs could destroy stratospheric ozone; the US National Academy of Sciences concurred in 1976; CFC-based aerosols were banned in the US, Canada, Mexico, Norway, and Sweden in 1978; Joseph Farman, Brian Gardiner, and Jonathan Shanklin (British Antarctic Survey) published the Antarctic ozone hole in Nature in 1985 — depletion far worse than predicted, up to ~70% losses over Antarctica in spring; Susan Solomon's research explained the hole's chemistry; the Montreal Protocol on Substances that Deplete the Ozone Layer was adopted 16 September 1987, initially signed by 46 countries, now ratified by ~200 — the only universally ratified treaty in UN history; Crutzen, Molina, and Rowland shared the 1995 Nobel Prize in Chemistry; Molina lived to see measurable recovery beginning in 2006; the Antarctic hole is expected to disappear around mid-century given compliance. The science, the treaty, and the recovery are all settled.
Layer: real · Place: Antarctica (ozone hole); Montreal, Canada (treaty)
Dates: 1974-06-28 → 1987-09-16 (confidence: high)

Human-made CFCs depleted stratospheric ozone, creating the Antarctic ozone hole (discovered 1985); the 1987 Montreal Protocol banned them and the layer is recovering.

Earliest attestation: Molina &amp; Rowland, Nature vol. 249, p. 810, 28 June 1974
Sources: MIT News (1995 Nobel): Molina was lead author on the 1974 Nature paper on CFCs as a threat to the ozone layer; the prediction 'created an enormous attention'; the Academy found 'they were right in all essentials... they had even underestimated the risk'; the research laid the groundwork for Farman's 1985 discovery; the work led to the 1987 Montreal Protocol.; Linda Hall Library: Molina/Rowland Nature vol. 249, p. 810, 28 June 1974; the 1978 aerosol bans; the 1985 hole discovery; 'by 1990, most of the countries of the world had signed onto the Montreal Protocol of 1987'; the 1995 Nobel; Molina lived to see recovery begin in 2006.; Testbook (exam reference): Montreal Protocol adopted 16 September 1987; initially signed by 46 countries, now nearly 200 signatories; Rowland &amp; Molina's 1974 Nature publication; the 1978 CFC-aerosol bans; the 1995 Nobel shared with Paul Crutzen.
Falsifiability: Stratospheric CFC/ozone chemistry proven wrong</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1974-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,-90.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1974</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wow! signal (15 August 1977)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The detection itself is documented by the surviving printout, the observing logs, and Ehman's contemporary records; follow-up non-detections (~100 hours, Allen Telescope Array) are likewise documented. What the signal WAS is unattested — the cause is disputed, not the event.
Layer: real · Place: Ohio State Radio Observatory (Big Ear), Delaware, Ohio — demolished 1998
Dates: 1977-08-15 → 1977-08-15 (confidence: high)

On 15 August 1977, Ohio State University's Big Ear radio telescope recorded a 72-second narrowband signal near 1420 MHz (the hydrogen line), strong enough that astronomer Jerry Ehman circled it on the computer printout and wrote 'Wow!' in the margin. It has never been repeated.

Earliest attestation: Big Ear computer printout, 15 August 1977, annotated by Jerry Ehman days later; published in Ehman 1996 review
Sources: Jerry Ehman, 'The Big Ear Wow! Signal' (1996 review, North American Astrophysical Observatory); SETI Institute — The Wow! Signal (summary with original printout); Arecibo Wow! project reanalysis (Méndez et al., 2024 preprint, accepted by The Astrophysical Journal): revised frequency 1420.726 MHz, peak &gt;300 Jy; Robert H. Gray, 'The Elusive WOW: Searching for Extraterrestrial Intelligence' (2012) — follow-up attempts
Falsifiability: A second detection of an identical signal profile with an identified source (natural or artificial) would settle the cause question; the detection itself is not in doubt</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1977-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.0456,40.2492,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1977</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wow! signal (15 August 1977)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The detection itself is documented by the surviving printout, the observing logs, and Ehman's contemporary records; follow-up non-detections (~100 hours, Allen Telescope Array) are likewise documented. What the signal WAS is unattested — the cause is disputed, not the event. 2026-10-16 refinement (via #377 Dr. Emrys Morven — The Reanalysis Reader): the Arecibo Wow! project reanalysis (Méndez et al. 2024–2025) recalibrated the signal ~4x stronger from recovered calibration records — data archaeology, not new observation. The companion record evt-wow-signal-natural-explanation-001 grades the 'conclusively explained as natural' press telling DEBUNKED: the papers are preprints, the magnetar-HI mechanism is unconfirmed and never observed in operation, and the authors state the work does not rule out artificial sources. Detection CORROBORATED; explanation still unattested.
Layer: real · Place: Ohio State Radio Observatory (Big Ear), Delaware, Ohio — demolished 1998
Dates: 1977-08-15 → 1977-08-15 (confidence: high)

On 15 August 1977, Ohio State University's Big Ear radio telescope recorded a 72-second narrowband signal near 1420 MHz (the hydrogen line), strong enough that astronomer Jerry Ehman circled it on the computer printout and wrote 'Wow!' in the margin. It has never been repeated.

Earliest attestation: Big Ear computer printout, 15 August 1977, annotated by Jerry Ehman days later; published in Ehman 1996 review
Sources: Jerry Ehman, 'The Big Ear Wow! Signal' (1996 review, North American Astrophysical Observatory); SETI Institute — The Wow! Signal (summary with original printout); Arecibo Wow! project reanalysis (Méndez et al., 2024 preprint, accepted by The Astrophysical Journal): revised frequency 1420.726 MHz, peak &gt;300 Jy; Robert H. Gray, 'The Elusive WOW: Searching for Extraterrestrial Intelligence' (2012) — follow-up attempts; Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! I: The Wow! Signal as an Astrophysical Maser Flare' (2024 preprint, arXiv:2408.08513); Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! II: Revised Properties of the Wow! Signal from Archival Ohio SETI Data' (2025 preprint, arXiv:2508.10657); Leonard David, 'Perplexing Wow! Signal – New Clues Reported' (quoting Méndez: 'Our results don't solve the mystery of the Wow! Signal'); SpaceDaily, 'A 2025 reanalysis of the original Wow! signal...' (13 August 2026): 'This is one preprint, not settled consensus... It does not turn one observation into a repeat detection, and it does not identify what produced it.'
Falsifiability: A second detection of an identical signal profile with an identified source (natural or artificial) would settle the cause question; the detection itself is not in doubt</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1977-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.0456,40.2492,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1977</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wow! signal (15 August 1977) — 2026 evidence state</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The detection itself is documented by the surviving printout, the observing logs, and Ehman's contemporary records; follow-up non-detections (~100 hours, Allen Telescope Array) are likewise documented. 2026-10-17 refinement (via #431 Dr. Aislinn Farren — The Echo Reader): September 2026 data-page revision carried (positions, &gt;300 Jy, spectral-axis inversion correction, Wow2/Wow3 cold-HI identifications, interference-simulation nulls, comet-FAQ removal — chain strengthened, the -002 number set retired but its caution kept: the values are inferred, not measured twice). Detection CORROBORATED; explanation still unattested — the papers remain preprints (ApJ acceptance, unpublished as of ~Oct 2026), the hydrogen-cloud amplification mechanism unconfirmed, and the authors do not rule out artificial sources. Companion record evt-wow-signal-natural-explanation-001 (DEBUNKED) held: 'conclusively explained as natural' is still a press telling, not a finding. Chain strengthened 12→14 sources grade-neutral 2026-10-19 (via #646): the Ohio SETI archive history and an independent hypothesis ledger; Turin-shroud precedent.
Layer: real · Place: Ohio State Radio Observatory (Big Ear), Delaware, Ohio — demolished 1998
Dates: 1977-08-15 → 1977-08-15 (confidence: high)

On 15 August 1977, Ohio State University's Big Ear radio telescope recorded a 72-second narrowband signal near 1420 MHz (the hydrogen line), strong enough that astronomer Jerry Ehman circled it on the computer printout and wrote 'Wow!' in the margin. It has never been repeated.

Earliest attestation: Big Ear computer printout, 15 August 1977, annotated by Jerry Ehman days later; published in Ehman 1996 review
Sources: Jerry Ehman, 'The Big Ear Wow! Signal' (1996 review, North American Astrophysical Observatory); SETI Institute — The Wow! Signal (summary with original printout); Arecibo Wow! project reanalysis (Méndez et al., 2024 preprint, accepted by The Astrophysical Journal): revised frequency 1420.726 MHz, peak &gt;300 Jy; Robert H. Gray, 'The Elusive WOW: Searching for Extraterrestrial Intelligence' (2012) — follow-up attempts; Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! I: The Wow! Signal as an Astrophysical Maser Flare' (2024 preprint, arXiv:2408.08513); Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! II: Revised Properties of the Wow! Signal from Archival Ohio SETI Data' (2025 preprint, arXiv:2508.10657); Leonard David, 'Perplexing Wow! Signal – New Clues Reported' (quoting Méndez: 'Our results don't solve the mystery of the Wow! Signal'); SpaceDaily, 'A 2025 reanalysis of the original Wow! signal...' (13 August 2026): 'This is one preprint, not settled consensus... It does not turn one observation into a repeat detection, and it does not identify what produced it.'; Forfoxs evidence file, 'The Wow! Signal: What a 72-Second Radio Burst Actually Proved' (Sept 2026): Arecibo Wow! Project public data page update — RA 19h25m26s ± 5s / 19h28m18s ± 5s, dec −26°57′ ± 20′, peak flux &gt;300 Jy, spectral-axis inversion correction; Wow2/Wow3 cold-HI identifications; interference simulations; comet FAQ removal; 'Last reviewed September 2026'; Clownfish TV / Drezzed, 'Wow! signal may have been a natural cosmic flare, astronomers say' (~7 Oct 2026): Arecibo Wow! I accepted by The Astrophysical Journal, not yet published; no repeat in ~100 ATA hours; magnetar-HI superradiance hypothesis unconfirmed; authors do not 'conclusively rule out' artificial sources; CurioLink, 'The Wow! Signal: What a 2025 Reanalysis Found' (Oct 2026): Wow2/Wow3 in the same dataset confirmed as cold interstellar hydrogen cloud emissions; frequency recalculation gives radial velocity inconsistent with ordinary galactic rotation; Arecibo Wow! project page, Planetary Habitability Laboratory (UPR Arecibo), phl.upr.edu/wow (checked Oct 2026): lists 'Arecibo Wow! I: An Astrophysical Explanation for the Wow! Signal (Accepted in ApJ)' — publication status unchanged (accepted, unpublished), plus 'Arecibo Wow! II: Revised Properties' and the forthcoming 'Arecibo Wow! III (August 2027)' and the Wow@Home network of small telescopes. Grade-neutral: confirms the record's publication-status ledger, no new evidence on the cause.; Méndez, Dixon &amp; Childers, 'The Ohio SETI Program — The Last Decades' (2026): reconstructs Big Ear's later SETI history; the archive holds 40,000+ transient narrowband events available for modern investigation; signalsfromtheperiphery.com, 'The Wow! Signal' hypothesis ledger (checked Oct 2026): independent secondary venue grading the hypotheses LIVE (magnetar-triggered hydrogen-cloud brightening, intermittent artificial transmission, scintillation) vs DISFAVOURED (comet hypothesis, satellite/aircraft, ground RFI) — converges with the timeline's own split
Falsifiability: A second detection of an identical signal profile with an identified source (natural or artificial) would settle the cause question; the detection itself is not in doubt</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1977-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-83.0456,40.2492,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1977</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Double Eagle II — the first real Atlantic crossing by balloon (11–17 August 1978)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested by the FAI record, the Smithsonian's NASM gondola holding, and contemporary coverage: crew, dates, duration (137h 6m), route, and the 14-attempt history all agree. The crew declined the closed Le Bourget offer and landed in a barley field at Miserey, ~97 km northwest of Paris.
Layer: real · Place: Presque Isle, Maine → Miserey, France (Irish coast crossed evening of 16 August 1978)
Dates: 1978-08-11 → 1978-08-17 (confidence: high)

The first successful transatlantic balloon flight: Ben Abruzzo, Maxie Anderson, and Larry Newman flew the helium balloon Double Eagle II from Presque Isle, Maine (lift-off 8:42 pm, 11 August 1978) to Miserey, France (landing 17 August 1978) — 137 hours 6 minutes, ~5,001 km, the 14th known attempt, ending the 134-year wait that began with the Montgolfier flights.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press and FAI record; gondola preserved in the National Air and Space Museum (NASM A19790532000)
Sources: Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum, Gondola Double Eagle II (NASM A19790532000): lift-off 8:42 pm 11 August 1978 Presque Isle; safe landing 17 August 1978 Miserey; 137 hours 6 minutes; 3,100-mile flight; crew of Albuquerque, New Mexico; Wikipedia 'Double Eagle II': piloted by Ben Abruzzo, Maxie Anderson, Larry Newman; landed 17 August 1978 in Miserey near Paris; 14th known attempt; Irish coast crossed evening of 16 August; FAI press release (14 Aug 2018): 40 years since Abruzzo broke distance and duration records flying Presque Isle to Miserey; 137 hours 5 minutes 50 seconds; 5001.22 km
Falsifiability: A successful documented balloon crossing of the Atlantic before August 1978</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1978-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>1.5,48.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1978</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1979 Gillette sale of Liquid Paper</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED — held below VERIFIED on the single unresolved variance (one source's $48.5M vs the consensus $47.5M); the sale event, her 1980 death, and the half-to-son/half-to-philanthropy division are multiply attested (NYT 2018, USPTO, Forbes 2020, Wikipedia).
Layer: real · Place: Dallas, Texas
Dates: 1979 → 1980 (confidence: high)

In 1979 Bette Nesmith Graham sold the Liquid Paper Company to the Gillette Corporation for $47.5 million (plus a royalty on every bottle); she died in 1980, leaving half her fortune to her son Michael Nesmith (of the Monkees) and half to philanthropic foundations.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1979–80 business press (cited via Wikipedia); NYT 2018 obituary-history; USPTO inventor profile; Forbes (Tarr 2020).
Sources: NYT 'Overlooked No More' (2018): sold to Gillette 1979; died 1980; half to son Michael, half to foundations; Forbes, Tarr (2020-04-22): 'How This Former Secretary Built A Multimillion-Dollar Corporation'; Wikipedia 'Liquid Paper': sale 1979; $47.5M per most sources, one source $48.5M (variance noted); worleygig: sold 1979 for $47.5M plus a royalty on every bottle sold until 2000; died 1980; half to Michael, half to philanthropy
Falsifiability: Gillette's 1979 acquisition filings showing a materially different figure, or probate records contradicting the half-and-half estate division.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1979-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-96.797,32.7767,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1979</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Atari burial, September 1983: the inventory that went into the desert and the dig that found it</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Four independent lines converge: (1) September 1983 newspaper accounts reported 14 trucks dumping Atari products at the Alamogordo landfill; (2) the Atari manager who ran the disposal, James Heller, told the AP in 2014 he was tasked with disposing of 728,000 cartridges from the El Paso warehouse and chose Alamogordo, pouring concrete over the site; (3) the 26 April 2014 excavation (Fuel Industries, Microsoft Xbox Entertainment Studios, documentary 'Atari: Game Over') recovered ~1,300 cartridges of 60+ titles — E.T., Centipede, Asteroids, Missile Command — from the concrete-covered site; (4) the aftermath has a custody paper trail: 881 cartridges sold on eBay for $107,000+ with numbered city certificates, proceeds to Alamogordo's library/zoo/police and the Tularosa Basin Museum of History (New Mexico Magazine 2025; Reuters 2014; Polygon 2014). The burial happened; the 'millions of E.T.' scale of the telling is honestly reduced to Heller's ~728,000 of mixed games.
Layer: real · Place: Alamogordo landfill, Otero County, New Mexico
Dates: 1983-09-26 → 1983-09-26 (confidence: high)

In September 1983, Atari, Inc. buried a large quantity of unsold video-game cartridges, consoles, and other equipment in a landfill in Alamogordo, New Mexico.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary September 1983 newspaper reporting (14 trucks at the Alamogordo dump; company officials saying the waste was mostly broken and returned merchandise — consoles, boxes, cartridges). First-hand: James Heller, the Atari manager tasked with the disposal, to the AP in 2014 (728,000 cartridges from the El Paso warehouse; concrete poured over the site to stop scavenging). Eyewitness: Joe Lewandowski, Alamogordo waste-collection owner who witnessed the burial (New Mexico Magazine, Apr 2025).
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Atari video game burial': mass burial of unsold cartridges, consoles, computers in Alamogordo, Sept 26, 1983; 'frequently dismissed as an urban legend' before 2014; 'Though it was believed that millions of copies of E.T. were buried, Atari officials later verified the numbers to be around 700,000 cartridges of various games, including E.T.'; 2014 excavation by Fuel Industries/Microsoft, ~1,300 cartridges recovered.; New Mexico Magazine (Apr 23, 2025) — 'Gaming Legends: The Truth Behind Atari's Landfill Burial': James Heller's 728,000-cartridge El Paso warehouse disposal; Joe Lewandowski (waste-collection owner, burial witness): 'It was ranked as one of the biggest urban legends of all time... Even Atari said it didn't happen'; 2014 dig located 1,382 cartridges (E.T., Centipede, Pac-Man); auction raised $107,000 for the library, zoo, police, and Tularosa Basin Museum of History.; Reuters (Sep 2014): 1,300 unwanted vintage games found buried in the landfill; city council voted 7-0 to auction ~800 on eBay; 'The New York Times reported at the time that the game manufacturer buried the games in the New Mexico desert in the middle of the night'; unearthed games under Tularosa Basin Historical Society custodianship.; Parade (Oct 3, 2026): 'In September 1983, Atari dumped truckloads of game cartridges... For decades, people argued over whether the story was true. In 2014, a dig proved it was'; NPR: 'That September, according to newspaper accounts, 14 trucks backed up to the dump and dropped their loads'; company officials said the waste was mostly broken and returned merchandise; Heller's 728,000 figure (AP); 881 sold for more than $107,000; the dig turned up 60+ titles (Asteroids, Missile Command, Centipede).; Den of Geek — 'It came from the desert: the legend of Atari's E.T.': Warshaw to Digital Press — deal completed late July, Sept 1, 1982 deadline, ~five-and-a-half weeks of development; Warner spent ~$22m on the E.T. licence; Atari produced around five million cartridges (launched December 1982); only 1.5 million sold.
Falsifiability: The burial would fail as a claim if no contemporary 1983 reporting existed and the 2014 excavation had found nothing. The opposite obtained on both counts: it was reported in real time, and the dig recovered physical cartridges with a documented chain of custody (city custody, Tularosa Basin Historical Society, eBay certificates).</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1983-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-105.989361,32.739278,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1983</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The lead in the Franklin sailors' bones</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent lab programs across 12 years replicated the finding: Beattie's exhumation chemistry, Kowal's assays, Keenleyside's XRF — all reporting bone lead far above modern baselines. What this grade does NOT say: how the lead got there, whether it was expedition-caused, or whether it killed anyone. The measurement is solid; the causal story is a separate record.
Layer: real · Place: Beechey Island, Nunavut
Dates: 1984 → 1996 (confidence: high)

The crewmen of the 1845 Franklin expedition (HMS Erebus and HMS Terror) carried high body lead burdens, far above modern background levels.

Earliest attestation: Beattie 1984-86 exhumations on Beechey Island (Torrington, Hartnell, Braine); Kowal et al. 1989, 1991 bone-lead assays.
Sources: Beattie &amp; Geiger, Frozen in Time (1987) — 1984-86 exhumations: Torrington bone lead 69-183 ug/g; post-mortem analyses; Kowal et al., 1989/1991 — 27 bone samples from King William Island: 87-223 ppm bone lead vs 18-50 ppm in modern cadaver controls; Keenleyside et al., 1996 (XRF) — 52 bones from NgLj-2: blood-equivalent 600-1500 ug/dL, 3-10x modern levels; Swanston et al., PLOS ONE 2018 (doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0202983) — corroborates elevated lead as a finding while disputing its causal role; isotope and microstructural analysis
Falsifiability: A re-analysis showing Franklin crew bone lead within modern background ranges would overturn this record; three independent programs agree it is not.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1984-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-91.9,74.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1984</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>LTEE reaches 75,000 generations (2026 milestone)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A documented milestone in a continuously observed experiment with primary attestation (the lab's own trajectory updates); graded as the milestone, not as a new innovation — the Cit+ innovation itself is already carried on evt-common-descent-001.
Layer: real · Place: Michigan State University, East Lansing — Lenski lab
Dates: 1988 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Richard Lenski's Long-Term Evolution Experiment (LTEE) — twelve replicate E. coli populations founded 1988 — passed 75,000 generations in 2026; the frozen 'fossil record' (samples every 500 generations) continues to make evolution replayable: the Ara-3 citrate innovation (~gen. 31,500, Blount/Borland/Lenski 2008) remains the headline innovation, with Blount's follow-up work (2026) sequencing citrate-transporter variants to map the physiological and ecological consequences of the innovation and its counterfactuals.

Earliest attestation: Lenski et al., 1991, Am. Nat. (the LTEE design paper); Blount, Borland &amp; Lenski 2008, PNAS (Cit+ innovation)
Sources: Lenski lab 2026 milestone reporting (75,000 generations; fitness-trajectory updates planned with Wiser and Lake; Blount's citrate-transporter follow-up sequencing) — scienceaq.com 2026 summary; Blount, Borland &amp; Lenski 2008, PNAS: LTEE Ara-3 evolved aerobic citrate metabolism after ~31,500+ generations; replays from frozen samples showed contingency (re-evolution only from post-gen-20,000 clones)
