Richard Lenski's Long-Term Evolution Experiment (LTEE) — twelve replicate E. coli populations founded 1988 at UC Irvine, run at Michigan State University from 1991 — passed 80,000 generations in 2024, with the populations at all-time peak fitness and no plans to end the experiment; the LTEE now continues at the University of Texas at Austin under Dr. Jeffrey Barrick. The frozen 'fossil record' (samples every 500 generations, -80°C) continues to make evolution replayable: the Ara-3 citrate innovation (~gen. 31,500, Blount/Borland/Lenski 2008) remains the headline innovation, six of the 12 populations had become hypermutators by 50,000 generations, and adaptation has continued indefinitely without plateauing in a constant environment.

Earliest attestation: Lenski et al., 1991, Am. Nat. (the LTEE design paper); Blount, Borland &amp; Lenski 2008, PNAS (Cit+ innovation)
