<Placemark><name>The Quebec blackout, 13 March 1989 (VERIFIED)</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiply attested: through early March 1989, sunspot group AR 5395 fired dozens of major flares (including X15 on 6 March); on 10 March ~19 UT an X4.5 flare launched a fast halo CME at Earth; the cloud arrived ~54 hours later — at 01:27 UT on 13 March the storm commenced, Kp pinned at 9, Dst eventually bottoming near −589 nT, the deepest of the space age; at 2:44 a.m. Eastern, geomagnetically induced currents through Québec's long transmission lines tripped seven static VAR compensators in quick succession — 92 seconds later the entire Hydro-Québec grid had collapsed; Montréal woke without power in −15°C weather; the blackout lasted up to nine hours for 6 million people — the biggest outage ever caused by a geomagnetic storm; blood-red auroras were reported as far south as Texas, Florida, and Cuba, and some who had never seen an aurora feared nuclear war (Cold War); a transformer at a New Jersey nuclear plant was destroyed beyond repair; 200+ grid problems erupted across the US (New England Power Pool lost 1,410 MW); satellites glitched and TDRS-1 recorded hundreds of anomalies; Quebec's igneous Canadian Shield bedrock made it especially vulnerable — the induced currents found less resistance in transmission lines than in the earth. The event forced a paradigm shift in grid preparation — the May 2024 solar storm had little grid effect as a result.
Layer: real · Place: Quebec, Canada (grid collapse); global (storm effects)
Dates: 1989-03-13 → 1989-03-13 (confidence: high)

On 13 March 1989, a geomagnetic storm collapsed Quebec's entire power grid in 92 seconds, leaving 6 million people without power for up to 9 hours.

Earliest attestation: Hydro-Québec grid logs; contemporary press; Sten Odenwald's The 23rd Cycle
Sources: CME Tracker: AR 5395's flares, the 10 March X4.5 CME, the 13 March 01:27 UT commencement, Kp 9, Dst −589 nT; at 2:44 a.m. Eastern seven VAR compensators tripped and 92 seconds later the grid collapsed; −15°C in Montréal; auroras to Texas, Florida, Cuba; the New Jersey transformer destroyed.; Scientific American (Odenwald, The 23rd Cycle): the March 13 superstorm, auroras to Texas and Cuba, the nuclear-war fears, the 2:45 a.m. Hydro-Québec crash — 'the biggest outage ever caused by a geomagnetic storm' — six million people, up to nine hours.; Space Daily: at 2:44 a.m. Eastern a current surge through Quebec bedrock tripped a capacitor at James Bay and within 90 seconds the grid collapsed; six million in a sub-zero blackout; nine hours for most, days for some industrial sites.; Space.com: the Amarillo fire-truck aurora story; New England's 1,410 MW loss; 200+ US grid problems; the 'could it happen again' framing.
Falsifiability: Hydro-Québec records proven fabricated</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1989-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.208,46.8139,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1989</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The OOPArt autopsy method</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The method has repeatedly resolved famous anomalies against documented lab and provenance evidence; no 'impossible artifact' has survived the full autopsy.
Layer: real · Place: The lab bench — British Museum Scientific Research / Smithsonian NMNH (the method's home, not the objects')
Dates: 1992 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Out-of-place artifacts are adjudicated object-by-object: a lab test (tool marks under magnification, materials analysis, abrasive identification, dating where possible) plus a provenance chain (first appearance, dealer or finder, every transfer). The method separates genuine ancient objects (the Iron Pillar of Delhi, the Diquis spheres) from modern fabrications (the crystal skulls, the Coso artifact) and from genuine objects with contaminated find contexts (the Maine penny).

Earliest attestation: Jane MacLaren Walsh's Smithsonian crystal-skull investigation (1992–2008), culminating in Sax et al. 2008, Journal of Archaeological Science
Sources: Sax, Meeks, Collon &amp; Walsh 2008, J. Archaeol. Sci. 35:2751 — the British Museum and Smithsonian crystal skulls: rotary disc tool marks, carborundum abrasive; Walsh 2008 SEM examination of the Mitchell-Hedges skull (high-speed rotary tool marks; European facial features per forensic reconstruction); Stromberg &amp; Heinrich x-ray investigation of the Coso artifact with the Spark Plug Collectors of America — 1920s-era Champion spark plug identification; 12 April 2018 physical inspection confirming
Falsifiability: A crystal skull bearing only pre-19th-century tool marks, or any shelved object whose lab test AND provenance chain both support an anomalous age</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1992-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.13,51.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1992</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The scholarly consensus that the Tel Dan Stele attests the Davidic dynasty</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The consensus claim is multiply attested in the published scholarly record across hostile camps (maximalists and minimalists alike); the minority readings (Athas 2003 toponym, 'house of Dod' deity) are named and accounted for, never hidden.
Layer: real · Place: The published literature (Tel Dan stele at the Israel Museum, Jerusalem)
Dates: 1993 → 2026 (confidence: high)

By 2006-2007 the scholarly field had settled the question of whether an extra-biblical 'House of David' existed: the Tel Dan Stele's bytdwd is widely read as a 9th-century BCE Aramaic reference to the Judahite royal house, ending the argument that David was a purely literary invention; the live argument moved to the kingdom's scale, not the dynasty's existence.

Earliest attestation: The scholarly debate itself: Biran &amp; Naveh 1993/1995 (Israel Exploration Journal), summarized by Grabbe 2007
Sources: Biran, A. &amp; Naveh, J. 1993/1995 — the original publications; Grabbe, L.L. 2007 — 'now widely regarded (a) as genuine and (b) as referring to the Davidic dynasty'; Finkelstein, I. &amp; Silberman, N.A. 2001. The Bible Unearthed — 'not a literary invention of a much later period'; Finkelstein, I. 2006. The Quest for the Historical Israel — 'The mention of the House of David in the Tel Dan inscription... leaves no doubt that David and Solomon were historical figures' — the concession of the debate's most influential minimalist; 2026 review (garrettham.com, 'Did King David Exist? A Catholic Reader's View', updated 2026) — surveys Athas 2003 toponym minority, the dwd-as-deity proposal, and reports 'the dust has settled'; Rainey, A. 1994 — philological dismantling of the place-name alternative readings
Falsifiability: A major published survey of the field showing the 'House of David' consensus has collapsed or never existed, or the Tel Dan reading overturned by accepted epigraphic consensus</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1993-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.23,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1993</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Deep Blue defeats Kasparov, 1997</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary sources converge: the published game scores (Kasparov win G1, Deep Blue wins G2/G6, draws G3/G4/G5), the IBM team records, and contemporaneous worldwide reporting of Game 6's 19-move resignation.
Layer: real · Place: Equitable Center, New York City
Dates: 1997 → 1997 (confidence: high)

The 1997 Deep Blue–Kasparov rematch (3–11 May 1997, Equitable Center, New York City) ended 3½–2½ to IBM's Deep Blue — the first defeat of a reigning world chess champion by a computer under standard tournament time controls.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary match records, game scores, and worldwide press coverage, May 1997
Sources: Murray Campbell, A. Joseph Hoane Jr., Feng-hsiung Hsu, 'Deep Blue', Artificial Intelligence 134(1–2), 57–83 (2002) — the builders' own technical account: 32-node IBM RS/6000 SP, 256 dedicated VLSI chess processors, ~100–200 million positions/second; Contemporary match reportage: Kasparov won Game 1, Deep Blue Game 2 (the first computer win over a world champion under tournament controls), draws in Games 3–5, Deep Blue's Game 6 win; Kasparov's post-match 'human intervention' remarks reported in press; The Harvard Crimson, 12 May 1997 — contemporary commentary establishing the match's documented character ('didn't and couldn't learn a single thing throughout the match')
Falsifiability: Contemporary game scores or tournament records contradicting the 3½–2½ result, or evidence the match was not played under standard controls</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1997-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1997</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The pine cone as hygrometer (verified folk instrument)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The mechanism is published and replicated: each scale is a functional bilayer — a highly swellable lower sclereid layer and a far less swellable upper layer — that acts like a bimetallic strip responding to humidity instead of heat (Nature Scientific Reports). Under wet conditions the scales curve up and the cone closes, protecting the wind-dispersed seeds; under dry conditions they bend down and release them. The Freiburg livMatS team (2022, Advanced Science) analyzed the tissue structures in detail; the motion is passive, deterministic, and already used as a model for biomimetic moisture-reactive building systems (Stuttgart's HygroScope, Urbach Tower). The folk reading — open = dry/fair, closed = wet — tracks the physics.
Layer: real · Place: Freiburg, Germany (livMatS biomechanics research; folk use universal in temperate pine regions)
Dates: 1997 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Pine cones open their scales when good (dry) weather is coming and close them when wet weather approaches — the cone is a working hygrometer.

Earliest attestation: Antique folk use of pine cones (and seaweed) as humidity indicators; the mechanism literature from Dawson et al. 1997
Sources: Nature Scientific Reports, 'Hygroscopic motions of fossil conifer cones': 'The opening and closing of woody conifer cones... is governed by a hygroscopic swelling/shrinking mechanism... Under wet conditions... the pine scales are curved upwards, the cone is closed and the winged, airborne seeds are protected... Under dry conditions, however, the scales bend down and liberate the seeds.' The scale as 'a functional bilayer' reacting 'similarly to a bimetallic strip, but responds to humidity changes instead of heat.'; Phys.org / University of Freiburg livMatS (2022, Advanced Science): 'Pine cones open when dry and close when wet. In this way, pine seeds are released only under advantageous conditions, namely when it is dry and the seeds can be carried far by wind.' The actuation 'does not consume metabolic energy' — passive, deterministic; already a model for biomimetic building envelopes.; Museum of the Earth / Earth@Home (4-H curriculum): 'Pinecones are Mother Nature's hygrometers... pinecones only open their scales to disperse the seeds when the air is dry, and they close their scales when the air is moist to protect the seeds and keep them dry.'; Dairy Diary (UK), '7 Weather Folklore Sayings': 'Pine cones open up when good weather is coming. This is one of the few sayings that is based on scientific fact. In dry weather, pine cones dry out, which causes their scales to stand out with a more "open" appearance. In wet weather, the scales curl up and the cones return to a more closed shape.'
Falsifiability: A controlled humidity-chamber study showing pine-cone scales do not move deterministically with humidity — the opposite of every published result</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1997-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.85,47.99,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1997</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2001 NORC Media Recount of Florida 2000</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — The study's findings are multiply and independently reported by the sponsoring news organizations themselves (AP, NYT, WaPo, FactCheck.org) on the same NORC-coded dataset: 175,010 rejected ballots examined ballot-by-ballot over ten months — the most thorough re-examination of the election ever conducted.
Layer: real · Place: Florida, USA (statewide; 67 counties)
Dates: 2000 → 2001 (confidence: high)

The 2001 NORC media recount of Florida's 2000 presidential ballots showed George W. Bush would have kept Florida under every recount strategy Al Gore actually pursued — and that Al Gore could only have won under a full statewide recount of all uncounted ballots (undervotes plus overvotes) that was never requested and never happened.

Earliest attestation: The consortium's release, 11 Nov 2001 (AP, New York Times, Washington Post, et al.); the NORC-coded ballot database of 175,010 rejected ballots across all 67 Florida counties.
Sources: Wikipedia, '2000 United States presidential election recount in Florida' — NORC outcomes table: Gore's four-county strategy Bush by 225; FL Supreme Court order Bush by 430; FL Supreme Court order as implemented Bush by 493; full statewide review with uniform standard Gore by 60–171; the media consortium's conclusion quoted; FactCheck.org, 'The Florida Recount of 2000' — the NORC study: 175,010 ballots examined by trained investigators; AP: 'George W. Bush would have narrowly prevailed in the partial recounts sought by Al Gore, but Gore might have reversed the outcome — by the barest of margins — had he pursued and gained a complete statewide recount'; sponsors listed (AP, NYT, WSJ, CNN, St. Petersburg Times, Palm Beach Post, Washington Post, Tribune Co.); USA Today / earlier media consortium (May 2001) via the same article: 'George W. Bush would have won a hand recount of all disputed ballots... if the most widely accepted standard for judging votes had been applied'; a Gore win was impossible without a recount of overvotes, which he did not request; The NORC study's own caveat: 'But no study of this type can accurately recreate Election Day 2000 or predict what might have emerged from individual battles over more than 6 million votes in Florida's 67 counties.'
Falsifiability: A full ballot-level re-examination of the 175,010 rejected ballots producing materially different scenario margins, or a demonstrated coding-protocol flaw in NORC's review — neither has appeared in 25 years.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2000-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.7,27.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The category rehabilitated: dying-and-rising gods before Christianity</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The rehabilitation is demonstrated per column in this pass (evt-osiris-dying-rising-001, evt-dumuzi-dying-rising-001, evt-baal-return-001 CORROBORATED as belief-phenomena; evt-attis-dying-rising-001 CLAIMED as the weakest). The 1990s consensus overstated; Mettinger's case-by-case perusal stands. The borrowing thesis is fenced separately.
Layer: real · Place: Lund, Sweden (Mettinger's chair; historiographical anchor — the map pins the argument, not a place)
Dates: 2001 → 2001 (confidence: high)

The category 'dying-and-rising gods' names a real, pre-Christian phenomenon of attested death-and-return narratives in the ancient Near East — Osiris, Dumuzi/Tammuz, Baal, Melqart attested; Adonis and Eshmun probable — rehabilitated by Mettinger (2001) against the 1990s consensus ('they died but did not return'), which overstated its case. The category is a scholarly instrument graded on the columns, and the claim that early Christianity borrowed a ready-made 'resurrection template' from it remains unattested transmission — CLAIMED, not evidence.

Earliest attestation: Frazer, The Golden Bough (1890–1915) — the category's invention; J.Z. Smith, 'Dying and Rising Gods' (in Eliade's Encyclopedia of Religion, 1987) — the 'Drudgery Divine' dismissal; Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001) — the rehabilitation
Sources: Mettinger, Tryggve N.D., The Riddle of Resurrection: 'Dying and Rising Gods' in the Ancient Near East (Coniectanea Biblica OT Series; Eisenbrauns, 2001) — 'The category of dying and rising gods is not a phantom construct'; five defended cases; Frazer, J.G., The Golden Bough (1890–1915) — the category's Victorian invention; Smith, Jonathan Z., 'Dying and Rising Gods' (Encyclopedia of Religion, 1987) — the 'Drudgery Divine' dismissal and the 1990s consensus; This pass's five columns: evt-osiris-dying-rising-001, evt-dumuzi-dying-rising-001, evt-baal-return-001, evt-adonis-dying-rising-001, evt-attis-dying-rising-001; BMCR 2002.09.07 — review of Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection: 'Dying and Rising Gods' in the Ancient Near East (Almqvist &amp; Wiksell, 2001) — the reviewer's conclusion restates the held dispute: the category is 'a rather simplistic generalization for a very wide array of gods and a very murky range of rituals'; Osiris stands apart as a 'dead' god, not a dying-and-rising one; Greek Adonis lacks the resurrection stage; Dumuzi-Tammuz's 'rising' is embedded in ritual procession, not asserted as myth
Falsifiability: A demonstration that all five defended columns are post-Christian, or that Mettinger's textual readings are all wrong, would collapse the rehabilitation</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2001-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.19,55.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2001</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>ABC News 2004 poll: 64% say Moses parted the Red Sea 'literally true word-for-word'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief is attested by the pollster's own release via consistent secondary reporting; the record grades the attestation of the belief, never the fact.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2004-02-06 → 2004-02-10 (confidence: high)

In an ABC News poll of 1,011 American adults (Feb 6-10, 2004), 64% said the story of Moses parting the Red Sea was 'literally true, meaning it happened that way word-for-word'; about 30% said the Bible stories are meant as lessons, not to be taken literally.

Earliest attestation: ABC News poll, conducted Feb 6-10, 2004 (1,011 adults, telephone).
Sources: ABC News Primetime poll, Feb 6-10 2004, n=1,011 (the pollster's release; reported by Religion News Service via The Christian Century, 2004-03); Christian Post, 'Majority of Americans Believe in Literal Biblical Accounts,' 2004-02-17 (consistent secondary report: 64% Red Sea, 61% creation, 60% Noah); Washington Times report via forum reprint: denominational splits — evangelical Protestants 91% literal on the Red Sea, mainline 79%, Catholics 50%, 'no religion' nearly one-third
Falsifiability: Release of the ABC cross-tabs contradicting the 64% figure; or the pollster disowning the release.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2004-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2004</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ingredient delivery: returned asteroid/comet samples contain life's building blocks</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Three independent sample-return programs (Stardust, Hayabusa2, OSIRIS-REx) with peer-reviewed laboratory analyses converge: the building blocks were present in small bodies before Earth had life. Contamination is controlled by pristine curation, unlike meteorite finds.
Layer: real · Place: NASA Johnson Space Center, Houston (Bennu sample curation); JAXA Sagamihara (Ryugu)
Dates: 2006 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Pristine samples returned from carbonaceous asteroids and a comet contain the molecular building blocks of life — amino acids, nucleobases, sugars, phosphate — demonstrating that the ingredients were widespread in the early solar system before life existed on Earth. Delivery of ingredients is demonstrated; the mechanism of life's origin is not.

Earliest attestation: Stardust capsule return, January 2006; glycine in Wild 2 aerogel reported by Elsila et al. 2009
Sources: Elsila et al. 2009, Meteoritics &amp; Planetary Science — glycine in Stardust Wild 2 samples (first amino acid recovered beyond the Moon); Naraoka et al. 2023, Science — soluble organic molecules including amino acids in Hayabusa2 Ryugu samples; Glavin et al. 2025, Nature Astronomy — 14 of 20 protein amino acids, all five nucleobases, ammonia in OSIRIS-REx Bennu samples; amino acids racemic (~equal L/D); McCoy et al. 2025, Nature — brine evaporite minerals (sodium carbonates, phosphates) in Bennu samples; parent-body salty water; Furukawa et al. 2026, Nature Geoscience (13 Jul 2026) — ribose, glucose, lyxose, xylose, arabinose, galactose in pristine Bennu material; with nucleobases + phosphate, every chemical component needed to build RNA present in one asteroid's material; Connolly et al. 2026, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta (in press, DOI 10.1016/j.gca.2026.10.003) — Bennu vs Ryugu organics compared: divergent parent-body histories; Ryugu CI1-like with depleted soluble organics, Bennu heterogeneous with multiple alteration episodes; NASA OSIRIS-REx mission record: 121.6 g returned September 2023; pristine curation at Johnson Space Center; Drozdovskaya et al., COMPASS project, ALMA, 7 papers in A&amp;A October 2026 issue — methanol (the most abundant complex organic molecule in interstellar space) and methanol masers detected in planet-forming disks around &gt;half of 11 young low-mass protostars; deuterated methanol in IRAS 4A2 (Perseus, ~1,000 ly) — prebiotic inventory assembled DURING star/planet formation, not only delivered later: the inheritance-before-delivery line; strengthens the delivery competing hypothesis's premise (pantry was stocked early) without touching the mechanism gap; Connolly et al. 2026, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta (in press, 6 Oct 2026 listing, doi:10.1016/j.gca.2026.10.003) — 'Prebiotic organic compounds in asteroid Bennu and Ryugu reflect divergent parent body histories': pyrolysis, thermochemolysis, and one-pot derivatization GC-triple-quadrupole-MS of a Ryugu aggregate vs Bennu — Ryugu is CI1-like (increased alkylation of insoluble organics, depleted soluble organics; only tentative β-alanine and 2,4-diaminopyrimidine), Bennu heterogeneous with 14 proteinogenic α-amino acids and five canonical nucleobases by the same technique; the peer-reviewed publication of the divergent-parent-bodies line already on this record (Turin-shroud precedent: context strengthened, grade not). Ingredients delivered; the chemistry-to-cell sequence still unknown; Matsumoto et al. 2026, Nature Astronomy (27 Aug 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41550-026-02962-y) — 'Evolution of ammonia, ammonium and C≡N-bearing species in Ryugu samples': three nitrogen reservoirs mapped in two Ryugu grains (N K-edge XAS/X-ray/IR spectroscopy + electron microscopy) — ammonium in saponite clay (405.6 eV), C≡N-bearing species (399.8 eV), sodium nitrate crystals, all clustered with sodium-carbonate-rich material from the final-stage aqueous alteration (freezing/evaporation concentrating brines over millions of years); independent echo: OSIRIS-REx Bennu samples show similar ammonium-bearing clays; Nature Astronomy Oct 2026 - interstellar erythrulose in G+0.693-0.027 (evt-interstellar-erythrulose-2026-001, CORROBORATED): the delivery pillar's deep-time extension - sugar chemistry predates planetary systems; ingredients, not life
Falsifiability: Demonstrated terrestrial contamination across all three independent sample sets, or a flux model showing delivered organics were negligible against endogenous production</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2006-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-95.09,29.56,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2006</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew 2010: 72% identify Moses as the Exodus leader; the non-religious know the most</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The finding is attested by the pollster's own published topline and report with methodology disclosed (n=3,412, dual-frame, oversamples); the record grades the attestation of the survey result, never the event it asks about.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2010-05-19 → 2010-06-06 (confidence: high)

In Pew Research Center's U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey (fielded May 19-June 6, 2010, among 3,412 U.S. adults by landline and cell phone, in English and Spanish), 72% correctly identified Moses — not Job, Elijah, or Abraham — as the biblical figure who led the exodus from Egypt. On the survey's full 32 questions the national average was 16 correct; atheists and agnostics averaged 20.9, Jews 20.5, and Mormons 20.3 — the highest-scoring groups included people with no religious affiliation, while Protestants as a whole averaged 16 and Catholics 14.7.

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center, 'U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey,' Sept 28, 2010 (topline Q.47c: Job 2 / Elijah 3 / Moses 72 / Abraham 5 / Don't know 18)
Sources: Pew Research Center, 'New Pew Forum on Religion &amp; Public Life Survey Explores Religious Knowledge in the U.S.,' press release Sept 28, 2010 (the pollster's own release: 16/32 national average; atheists/agnostics 20.9; Jews 20.5; Mormons 20.3; field May 19-June 6, 2010, n=3,412); Pew Research Center, 'U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey' topline (assets.pewresearch.org — religious-knowledge-topline.pdf; Q.47c: Job 2, Elijah 3, Moses 72, Abraham 5, Don't know 18); Pew Research Center, 'U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey — Who Knows What About Religion,' Sept 28, 2010 report chapter (the Moses passage: 'more than seven-in-ten Americans (72%) know he was the biblical figure who led the exodus out of Egypt'; sub-group splits: Mormons 92%, Jews and atheists/agnostics ~92%, white evangelicals ~80%, white mainline ~68%, Catholics ~65%); Pew Research Center, 'What Americans Know About Religion,' July 2019 (follow-up wave: roughly eight-in-ten (79%) identify Moses vs Daniel/Elijah/Joseph; different instrument — carried as a related reading, not a re-measurement)
Falsifiability: Release of Pew's topline or dataset contradicting the 72% figure, or the pollster retracting the finding.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2010-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2010</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew religious-knowledge quiz: the 2010 telephone wave vs the 2019 online wave — the inversion survived the mode change</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The mode facts are attested by the pollster's own published methodology sections in both reports (primary documents); the inversion's survival is read off the published group-score tables, not inferred. Graded as poll-instrument attestation, never as a claim about religion itself.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national polls)
Dates: 2010-05-19 → 2019-07-23 (confidence: high)

Between Pew Research Center's two U.S. religious-knowledge surveys, the survey mode changed completely. The 2010 wave ('U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey,' report Sept 28, 2010) was interviewer-administered by telephone — dual-frame landline + cell random-digit dial, n=3,412 U.S. adults, fielded May 19–June 6, 2010, in English and Spanish. The 2019 wave ('What Americans Know About Religion,' report July 23, 2019) was self-administered online — fielded Feb 4–19, 2019, n=10,971, mostly on Pew's American Trends Panel (10,429 panelists, probability-recruited online) plus 542 Ipsos KnowledgePanel oversample interviews (Jewish, Mormon, Hispanic Protestant subgroups), margin of sampling error ±1.5 points. The headline finding — the religiously unaffiliated outscoring the affiliated — survived the crossing: 2010 (telephone): atheists/agnostics 20.9 of 32, general public 16, Protestants 16, Catholics 14.7. 2019 (online): Jews 18.7, atheists 17.9, agnostics 17.0, general public 14.2. The group-ranking pattern held after demographic controls in both waves. The inversion is mode-robust: it was not an artifact of interviewer administration, and it did not vanish under self-administration.

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center, 'U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey,' Sept 28, 2010 (methodology: dual-frame RDD telephone) and 'What Americans Know About Religion,' July 23, 2019 (methodology: survey conducted online Feb. 4–19, 2019, among 10,971 respondents, mostly American Trends Panel)
Sources: Pew Research Center, 'U.S. Religious Knowledge Survey,' Sept 28, 2010 — methodology: telephone interviews, dual-frame landline + cell RDD, n=3,412, May 19–June 6, 2010, English and Spanish; topline Q.47c Moses 72%; Pew Research Center, 'What Americans Know About Religion' full report, July 23, 2019 — 'a survey conducted online Feb. 4 to 19, 2019, among 10,971 respondents... mostly among members of Pew Research Center's American Trends Panel (a nationally representative panel of randomly selected U.S. adults recruited from landline and cellphone random-digit-dial surveys and an address-based survey), supplemented by interviews with members of the Ipsos KnowledgePanel'; margin ±1.5 pts; Pew Research Center, 'What Americans Know About the Holocaust' methodology markdown, Jan 22, 2020 — confirms the pair: '(The first was conducted in 2010.) This time... 10,429 were members of Pew's American Trends Panel (ATP), an online survey panel'
Falsifiability: A Pew-published mode experiment showing the 2019 group rankings are an artifact of the online mode, or retraction/correction of either report's methodology section.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2010-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2010</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wind setdown as a physical crossing mechanism (Drews &amp; Han 2010)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed hydrodynamic modeling confirms the mechanism is physically possible — which proves the mechanism, never the event; the timeline keeps those two claims on separate records.
Layer: real · Place: Lake of Tanis / Pelusiac branch reconstruction (eastern Nile Delta)
Dates: 2010-08-30 → 2010-08-30 (confidence: high)

Wind setdown — a sustained strong wind driving water back — can physically expose dry passage in shallow Delta waters: Drews &amp; Han (2010, PLoS ONE) modeled a 28 m/s (63 mph) easterly wind for 12 hours opening a 3-4 km long, 5 km wide mudflat for about 4 hours at the reconstructed Lake of Tanis / Pelusiac branch, with the water standing as barriers on both sides of the exposed flats.

Earliest attestation: Drews &amp; Han (2010), 'Dynamics of Wind Setdown at Suez and the Eastern Nile Delta,' PLoS ONE 5(8): e12481 — building on Nof &amp; Paldor's earlier wind-setdown modeling and Volzinger &amp; Androsov's 2004 reef study.
Sources: Drews C, Han W (2010), PLoS ONE 5(8): e12481 (peer-reviewed ocean-model study; satellite + bathymetric reconstruction); Volzinger &amp; Androsov (2004), Bulletin of the Russian Academy of Sciences (Suez-reef wind-setdown model; Drews &amp; Han later showed a non-uniform reef retains unwadeable channels); Nof &amp; Paldor (earlier wind-setdown work on the Gulf of Suez); Critical counter-reading: the tsunami-modeling paper (MDPI J. Marine Sci. Eng. 2015) notes the Tanis scenario depends on a hypothetical reconstructed topography and fits the Exodus itinerary poorly on Hoffmeier's own geography
Falsifiability: Proof that the reconstructed Tanis/Pelusiac bathymetry is wrong would kill the specific scenario (not wind setdown generally); wind setdown is ordinary fluid mechanics and survives regardless.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2010-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.3,31.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2010</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>El Azizia's 136°F record decertified (2012)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent accounts agree: Christopher Burt's investigation prompted a 13-climatologist WMO committee (including Burt and Randall Cerveny); after a year-long investigation the committee identified five major concerns — problematical instrumentation, a probable new and inexperienced observer, unrepresentative microclimate, poor correspondence to other locations, poor comparison to subsequent values — and the WMO World Archive of Weather and Climate Extremes rejected the 58°C reading, promoting Death Valley's 134°F to the official record.
Layer: real · Place: El Azizia, Libya
Dates: 2012-09-11 → 2012-09-11 (confidence: high)

The 136.4°F (58°C) temperature recorded at El Azizia, Libya, on 13 September 1922 — the long-standing world heat record — was formally decertified by the World Meteorological Organization in 2012 after a committee investigation found it invalid.

Earliest attestation: The WMO committee report and September 2012 announcement
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine: the WMO committee 'identified five major concerns with the 1922 El Azizia temperature extreme record, specifically (a) potentially problematical instrumentation, (b) a probable new and inexperienced observer at time of observation, (c) unrepresentative microclimate of the observation site, (d) poor correspondence of the extreme to other locations and (e) poor comparison to subsequent temperature values recorded at the site. Based on these concerns, the WMO World Archive of Weather and Climate Extremes has rejected this temperature extreme of 58°C as the highest temperature officially recorded on the planet.'; UPI via Space Daily (Dec 2012): 'The U.N. climate agency has changed the world's hottest day on record from Sept. 13, 1922, in Libya, to July 10, 1913, in California's Death Valley.' Burt: 'The more we looked at it, the more obvious it appeared to be an error.' The committee of 13 climatologists investigated for a year.
Falsifiability: WMO archive records showing the El Azizia reading still listed as the world record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2012-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.0167,32.5333,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2012</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Denisovan EPAS1 haplotype and Tibetan altitude adaptation</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed; haplotype frequency and Denisovan phylogeny replicated across studies
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 2014 → 2014 (confidence: high)

Huerta-Sanchez et al. 2014 (Nature, doi 10.1038/nature13408): Tibetans carry a Denisovan-derived EPAS1 haplotype at ~80-87% frequency, conferring efficient blood-oxygen response to hypoxia without the heart-straining side effects of other high-altitude adaptations — the clearest case of adaptive archaic introgression.

Earliest attestation: Huerta-Sanchez et al., Nature 2014
Sources: Huerta-Sanchez et al. 2014, Nature, doi:10.1038/nature13408
Falsifiability: Denser archaic sampling could reassign the haplotype's source lineage</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2014-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>88.0,32.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2014</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Erebus (2014) and Terror (2016) - the wrecks, found where the testimony said</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Two independently confirmed wreck identifications (diver visits, artefacts, official confirmation), at locations published by Parks Canada as Inuit-indicated; the institutional record explicitly credits the oral history.
Layer: real · Place: Queen Maud Gulf (Erebus) and Terror Bay, King William Island coast (Terror), Nunavut
Dates: 2014 → 2016 (confidence: high)

Both Franklin expedition wrecks were found in the 2010s at Inuit-indicated locations. HMS Erebus was located in September 2014 in the Queen Maud Gulf, northeast of O'Reilly Island (Wilfrid Laurier area) - 'near to where Inukpujijuk told Hall it had sunk' (Parks Canada), after six years of sonar searching guided by Inuit oral history and Louie Kamookak's consultations; the find followed a Government of Nunavut team's recovery of cached Erebus artefacts from an island. HMS Terror was located in September 2016 in Terror Bay, about 60 miles north of Erebus, by the Arctic Research Foundation's vessel Martin Bergmann - after Inuk crewmember Sammy Kogvik reported having seen a mast-like object in the Terror Bay ice six years earlier; Parks Canada confirmed the wreck resting upright in about 24 metres of water in remarkably preserved condition. In both cases the technology found the ship; the testimony found the place.

Earliest attestation: Parks Canada announcements (Erebus, September 2014; Terror confirmed September 2016) - the institutional record of both discoveries.
Sources: Parks Canada, Wrecks of HMS Erebus and HMS Terror National Historic Site - the official discovery record and the published Inuit-knowledge credit; September 2014: HMS Erebus located, Queen Maud Gulf, Wilfrid Laurier area - six-year sonar campaign; Nunavut team artefacts cached on island; 'near to where Inukpujijuk told Hall it had sunk'; September 2016: HMS Terror located, Terror Bay - Arctic Research Foundation R/V Martin Bergmann; Sammy Kogvik's mast-in-the-ice report; Parks Canada diver confirmation, ~24 m depth, preserved condition; Canadian Geographic (2016) - Kogvik's account and the Terror Bay discovery; Russell Potter's commentary
Falsifiability: Proof that either wreck identification is wrong (Parks Canada's diver confirmation + artefact recovery makes this near-impossible) or that no Inuit input informed the search boxes (contradicted by Parks Canada's published record) would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2014-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-97.9,68.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2014</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The measured destruction of Syria's archaeological heritage, 2015–2025</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed satellite study of 199 sites with a published damage taxonomy (traditional looting, mechanical looting, militarization, encampments) and a 2011–2016 vs 2015–2025 comparison; consistent with the VIII.f destruction argument: the measured pattern of what is missing, checked against what should have survived, is itself a record.
Layer: real · Place: Syria (199 surveyed sites incl. Palmyra, Mari, Apamea, Ain Dara)
Dates: 2015 → 2025 (confidence: high)

The destruction of archaeological heritage in Syria during and after the civil war is itself a measured, documented record: 59% of 199 surveyed sites suffered looting or destruction between 2015 and 2025 (vs 13% in 2011–2016), with mechanized bulldozer looting statistically associated with military activity — and the near-total leveling of Ain Dara by 2022. The pattern of what was destroyed, where, and by what means is evidence about the destroyers (actors, methods, motives), not merely loss.

Earliest attestation: Casana et al., Heritage journal, July 2026 (peer-reviewed); secondary reporting October 2026.
Sources: Casana et al., peer-reviewed study in Heritage, July 2026: satellite imagery (Google Earth + ESA Pleiades) analysis of 199 archaeological sites; 59% looted or destroyed 2015–2025 vs 13% in 2011–2016; four-category damage taxonomy (traditional looting, mechanical looting by bulldozer, militarization, construction/encampments); mechanized looting associated with military activity; lead author Jesse Casana (Dartmouth) quoted: 'the military is supposed to protect the people, not destroy archaeological sites, which is a war crime'; Ain Dara systematically bulldozed from 2019, 'almost completely' leveled by 2022, with the exception of a first-millennium-BCE stone temple; Center for Spatial Technologies + Crimean Institute for Strategic Studies, 16 October 2026: interactive satellite-mapped documentation of Russian fortifications (trenches, firing positions, shelters) cutting through archaeological sites along Crimea's western coast — burial mounds, fortress walls, buried layers recording ~3,000 years of continuous settlement (current parallel exhibit of destruction-as-record); Manhattan DA Antiquities Trafficking Unit, 23 Sept 2026: return of 36 antiquities (est. &gt;$5M) to Syria — incl. a monumental Palmyran relief looted under ISIS control and 14 mosaics from a Roman villa, trafficked into New York by Georges Lotfi; the recovery exhibit: the plundered object re-enters the record as evidence of the looting, not just of its origin; Arab World Institute (IMA) Paris, July 2026: return of 23 artifacts legally loaned for 2010/2011 exhibitions — Palmyrene funerary reliefs (2nd–3rd c. CE), Mari/Ishqi-Mari material, Qasr al-Hayr al-Gharbi wall-painting fragments, Roman bronzes, Byzantine and Islamic objects — aboard the French presidential plane during Emmanuel Macron's Damascus visit, handover 7 July 2026 at the People's Palace (Macron + President Ahmed al-Sharaa), now on display in Damascus Citadel's Throne Hall, the DGAM's first public presentation since return. Not looting recovery — the lawful-loan-return column of the recovery ledger, complementing the Manhattan DA 23-Sept-2026 trafficking-recovery exhibit. Reported via ancientist.com (9 Aug 2026) and wtoday.news (Oct 2026 re-report of the July event).; Raqqa civilian-safeguard return, Oct 2026 (Syria News Gazette, citing SANA): Khaled Abdul Halim, a Raqqa resident, handed a collection of archaeological artifacts he had safeguarded since 2014 to the Raqqa Museum / Antiquities Department (Jabbar Abdullah, Director) — eight archaeological pieces of various shapes plus two limestone crowns, one of which may be modern and will be verified. Abdul Halim acted through official channels under the Syrian Ministry of Culture's September 2026 community-safeguarding initiative encouraging citizens to hand over finds. The civilian-custody column of the recovery ledger: preservation without ownership. Single-venue so far (SANA via one aggregator) — fenced until corroborated.; Manhattan DA prosecution-seizure column, Sept 2026 (Apollo Magazine, citing the DA's office): on the same day as the 36-antiquity Syria repatriation (23 Sept 2026), the DA's Antiquities Trafficking Unit seized 13 objects from the Metropolitan Museum of Art, bringing the office's total seized from the Met since 2017 to 402. The seizure column runs beside the return column — sustained prosecutions plus museum seizures as the disruption mechanism for transnational trafficking. The 402 figure is single-venue (Apollo) until the DA's office confirms.; Foreign-mission resumption, Oct 2026 (Syria News Gazette): joint Syrian-Italian archaeological mission (University of Pavia + Syria's Directorate-General of Antiquities and Museums, with doctoral students from Italy and Türkiye) expanding under a three-year plan; national mission began 2015, resumed 2024 as joint mission — among the first foreign missions back in Syria post-war. The fieldwork-resumption column of the recovery ledger: excavation as repair.; Joint-mission first results, Oct 2026 (Syria News Gazette, 2026-10-19): the resumed Syrian-Italian mission (University of Pavia + DGAM, doctoral students from Italy and Türkiye) has produced its first reported results — three archaeological levels (Byzantine, Late Bronze, Middle Bronze Age), 4,000-year-old Middle Bronze Age remains unearthed, finds transferred to National Museum warehouses for documentation/registration/preservation, expanding under a three-year plan. Single-venue, fenced until corroborated (the Oct-19 mission-resumption announcement carried the same caveat; no second venue found in the Oct-22/23 sweep). The fieldwork-resumption column of the recovery ledger now has a finding, not just a flag.
Falsifiability: An independent ground-truth survey of a representative site sample that reverses the 59% finding, or shows the 'mechanical looting' category misattributed in the majority of cases</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2015-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>38.5,35.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2015</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>YouGov 2015: the 41% who believe in coexistence</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The figure comes from the pollster's own published release — it is a CORROBORATED record of what Americans SAID, i.e., of the belief. It attests the belief, never the fact: the timeline grades the attestation, not the metaphysics, and the belief it attests is itself DEBUNKED by the fossil record (see evt-dino-human-coexistence-001).
Layer: real · Place: United States (national sample)
Dates: 2015 → 2015 (confidence: high)

In a 2015 YouGov survey of American adults, 41% said humans and dinosaurs either 'definitely' (14%) or 'probably' (27%) lived on Earth at the same time; 43% said they 'definitely' (25%) or 'probably' (18%) did not; 16% were unsure. Among self-described 'born-again' Christians, 56% believed in coexistence; among non-born-again Americans, 51% rejected it.

Earliest attestation: YouGov writeup, June 2015 (today.yougov.com/society/articles/12615-jurassic-world)
Sources: YouGov, 'Over 40% of Americans believe humans and dinosaurs shared the planet' (June 2015), today.yougov.com/society/articles/12615-jurassic-world — the pollster's own release: 41% (14% definitely + 27% probably); 43% reject; 16% unsure; born-again 56% vs non-born-again 51% reject
Falsifiability: YouGov withdrawing or correcting its own published release; or a documented methodology disclosure that invalidates the numbers.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2015-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.58,39.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2015</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2018 Moscow–Constantinople rupture (the Tomos schism)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The communion-break and the Tomos are dated, documented acts; the recognition count and the unhealed state are carried by dated 2026 reporting; canonical status itself remains contested.
Layer: real · Place: Kyiv, Ukraine (Unification Council, St. Sophia)
Dates: 2018-10-15 → 2019-01-05 (confidence: high)

On 15 October 2018 the Russian Orthodox Church broke communion with the Ecumenical Patriarchate of Constantinople after Constantinople moved to grant autocephaly to a Ukrainian church; on 15 December 2018 the Kyiv Unification Council created the Orthodox Church of Ukraine (OCU) from the UOC-KP and UAOC, and on 5 January 2019 Patriarch Bartholomew issued the Tomos of Autocephaly to the OCU. As of October 2026 the rupture is unhealed: Moscow and Constantinople remain out of communion, the OCU is recognized by Constantinople and a small number of other churches, and the Ukrainian Orthodox Church under Moscow (UOC-MP) sits in a canonical limbo it did not choose.

Earliest attestation: ROC Holy Synod statement, 15 October 2018; the Tomos of Autocephaly document, 5 January 2019
Sources: Wikipedia, '2018 Moscow–Constantinople schism', retrieved 17 Oct 2026 — communion break, Unification Council, Tomos, the 2025-2026 knock-ons (Estonia, Abkhazia); helleniscope.com, 29 Sep 2026, 'Leaked Church Account Sheds New Light on the Political Pressure Behind Ukrainian Autocephaly' — Metropolitan Gregorios's account of the 15 Dec 2018 Unification Council; Filaret's March 2026 death noted (advocacy venue, fenced); nyxthimeron.com, Feb 2026, 'Bridging the Ecclesiastical Divide in Ukraine' — the UOC-MP 'canonical limbo' analysis (neither integrated nor independent in recognition); October 2026 status (19 Oct 2026 seventh pass): no movement toward reconciliation — the rupture is live and hardening. OCU head Epifaniy (Dumenko), 13–16 Oct 2026, ruled out dialogue on UOC terms (Ukrinform interview via spzh.eu): UOC's demands (OCU admit it is not autocephalous, no holy orders) framed as 'capitulation'; he expects 'victory on the religious front'. UOC-KP head Nikodym (Kobzar), 10 Oct 2026, accused authorities of building the OCU into a state church (Patriot interview via spzh.eu). Property dimension: Lubny District Prosecutor's Office Oct 2026 suit to transfer the 1914 Pisky Dormition Church (Poltava region, UOC community) to state ownership — Commercial Court proceedings opened Jan 2026, Ministry of Culture third party (Poltavshchyna via spzh.eu, 18 Oct 2026). VENUE FENCE: spzh.eu (Union of Orthodox Journalists) is UOC-aligned advocacy — events quoted, characterizations (e.g. 'seizure', 'state church') fenced. Grade unaffected (CORROBORATED held); this is context for the ongoing rupture, not evidence against the 2018–2019 documentary acts.; Oct 2026 state (restated 20 Oct 2026): recognition count unchanged — still 4 Local Churches recognizing the OCU (Dumenko 16 Oct 2026 interview, UOJ/spzh.eu — recognition prediction is a forecast, not an event); Patriarch Bartholomew (UOJ-Bulgaria, ~19 Oct 2026, during the Sofia/Rila visit) reiterated the Phanar's position urging Ukraine to unite around 'the canonical Primate, Metropolitan Epifaniy', and accused Patriarch Kirill of derailing the Council of Crete; UOC-MP judicial-termination proceedings in Ukraine continue (diplomacyireland.eu explainer, ~19 Oct 2026 — DESS mandate is compliance/termination of the legal entity, not forced transfer of parishes). All venue-not-evidence beyond the quoted positions; no grade move — the record covers the 2018 schism, and the 2026 split remains the live competing-hypotheses lane.; UOJ/spzh.eu, 19 Oct 2026, 'Bartholomew urges Ukraine to unite around Epifaniy': Bartholomew's three-day Bulgaria visit (Sofia + Rila Monastery) — concelebrated with Bulgarian Patriarch Daniel at Rila, commemorating OCU head Epiphanius despite Bulgaria's continued non-recognition of Ukrainian autocephaly; the piece restates the held recognition count (only four Local Churches: Constantinople, Alexandria, Greece, Cyprus); protesters' 'anaxios/anathema' shouts await independent confirmation (the venue says so itself). Helleniscope 19 Oct 2026 recap matches on the concelebration (confirmed by the Bulgarian Patriarchate and national broadcasters per the venue).; RISU — Metropolitan Epifaniy congratulates Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew on 35th anniversary of his enthronement (Facebook letter, published risu.ua)
Falsifiability: The record would fall if the 15 Oct 2018 communion-break or the 5 Jan 2019 Tomos were misdated; it would move toward VERIFIED on a settled, independently-counted recognition ledger</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2018-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.5234,50.4501,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2018</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew 2019: unbelievers again outscore believers on religious knowledge</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the pollster's own published report with methodology disclosed; the record grades the attestation of the survey result, never the content of any doctrine it asks about.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2019-02-04 → 2019-02-19 (confidence: high)

In Pew Research Center's 'What Americans Know About Religion' survey (report released July 23, 2019; nationally representative U.S. adults, 32 religious-knowledge questions), the highest-scoring groups were again the religiously unaffiliated critics of religion: Jews averaged 18.7 of 32 correct, self-described atheists 17.9, and agnostics 17.0 — above the general-public average of 14.2. Evangelical Protestants scored 15.5 (above average); mainline Protestants, Catholics, and Mormons scored 13.9-14.6, clustering near the public average. The group-score pattern held even after controlling for education and other demographics. Atheists were on a par with Catholics and Protestants on the Catholicism/Protestantism questions, more likely than any other group to answer the religion-in-public-life questions correctly, and averaged 8.6 of 14 on the Bible-and-Christianity items (vs 8.2 for agnostics and for Christians). The 2010 finding replicated: unbelievers outscore believers.

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center, 'What Americans Know About Religion,' July 23, 2019 (the pollster's own report)
Sources: Pew Research Center, 'What Americans Know About Religion,' July 23, 2019 — group scores: Jews 18.7/32, atheists 17.9, agnostics 17.0, general public 14.2; evangelicals 15.5; mainline/Catholics/Mormons 13.9-14.6; pattern holds after demographic controls; UPI/Aug 2019 write-up of the Pew 2019 report: 32-question quiz; atheists 18/32 vs agnostics 17 vs Christians 14.2; atheists 8.6/14 on Bible-and-Christianity vs Christians 8.2; atheists on a par with Catholics/Protestants on Catholicism/Protestantism items; atheists most likely to answer the religion-in-public-life questions correctly
Falsifiability: Release of Pew's report or dataset contradicting the group-score ordering, or the pollster retracting the finding.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2019-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2019</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>US creationism belief, Gallup June 2019</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published survey data from a professional pollster with stated method, sample, and error margin; independently summarized by NCSE. Held below VERIFIED: it measures self-reported belief, not fact.
Layer: real · Place: United States (Gallup national sample, all 50 states + DC)
Dates: 2019 → 2019 (confidence: high)

In June 2019, 40% of US adults told Gallup that 'God created human beings pretty much in their present form at one time within the last 10,000 years or so' (33% said humans evolved with God's guidance, 22% without God's involvement).

Earliest attestation: Gallup poll report '40% of Americans Believe in Creationism' (July 2019), summarized by the National Center for Science Education.
Sources: Gallup, '40% of Americans Believe in Creationism' (July 26, 2019) — 40% strict creationist / 33% God-guided evolution / 22% evolution without God; full report URL not recoverable from search output, cited via NCSE summary; National Center for Science Education, 'The latest Gallup poll on evolution' — confirms 1,015-adult sample, June 13–16, 2019 fieldwork, ±4% error, 38–47% historical trend band; Gallup, 'Majority Still Credits God for Humankind, but Not Creationism' (July 22, 2024) — the follow-on wave: 37% creationist / 34% God-guided / 24% secular; new record low and new record high. See evt-gallup-2024-human-origins-001
Falsifiability: A replicated Gallup run with the same question showing a materially different share would supersede this record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2019-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5795,39.8283,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2019</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ghost archaic introgression in West African populations (~600 ka divergence)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — Peer-reviewed statistical inference, but no physical specimen exists; 'ghost' claims stay CLAIMED until a fossil genome confirms
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 2020 → 2020 (confidence: medium)

Durvasula &amp; Sankararaman 2020 (Science Advances, doi 10.1126/sciadv.aax5097) found that West African populations (Yoruba, Mende, Esan, Gambian groups) derive 2-19% of their ancestry from an unknown archaic lineage that diverged before the modern-human/Neanderthal split (~600 ka). No fossils or DNA of this 'ghost' source exist — its signature is statistical.

Earliest attestation: Durvasula &amp; Sankararaman, Science Advances 2020
Sources: Durvasula &amp; Sankararaman 2020, Science Advances, doi:10.1126/sciadv.aax5097
Falsifiability: Ancient African DNA or better demographic models could dissolve or confirm the ghost signal</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2020-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.0,8.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2020</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>NBS 260108 — the 2026 FAST/K2-155 technosignature candidate</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The candidate's detection and its low-priority classification are the peer-reviewed paper's own documented outcomes; the 'not ET' framing is the authors' explicit statement. Graded as documented scientific outcome — what the paper proved vs. what the press told is kept separate, Turin-shroud precedent.
Layer: real · Place: FAST, Dawodang, Guizhou, China (the observing instrument's site; K2-155 lies ~238 light-years distant in Eridanus)
Dates: 2021 → 2026-10 (confidence: high)

A 2026 peer-reviewed pipeline re-analysis of 2021 Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical Telescope (FAST) observations of 33 exoplanet systems (Li, Z.-Q., et al., The Astronomical Journal 172, Article 180) found one narrow drifting radio signal toward K2-155 — NBS 260108, at 1148.4167 MHz with drift rate -0.038 Hz/s, seen in the on-target beam (Beam 1) and none of the other 18 beams. The authors classified it as a low-priority technosignature candidate, retained for internal follow-up only: the paper explicitly does not support an extraterrestrial explanation, and the suspected terrestrial or instrumental source has not been identified — the paper's own detail favors shared interference: the signal is strongly YY-polarized and absent in XX, and near-frequency YY-dominated features appeared toward K2-18, GJ 9066, and Ross 128 in the same 10 September 2021 session.

Earliest attestation: Li, Z.-Q., et al. (2026), 'A Wavelet-integrated Search Pipeline for Narrowband Technosignatures in FAST Observations of 33 Exoplanet Systems,' The Astronomical Journal, Volume 172, Article 180
Sources: Li, Z.-Q., et al. (2026), AJ 172, Article 180 — the peer-reviewed paper; detection and low-priority classification are the paper's own reported outcomes; October 2026 press recap — reports the paper's findings: narrow drifting signal (1148.4167 MHz, -0.038 Hz/s), Beam 1 only, low-priority candidate retained for follow-up (venue, not evidence — the paper's own wording is what the record holds); Press recap — 'An extraterrestrial explanation is not supported by the available evidence, while the suspected terrestrial or instrumental source has not been identified' (quoted wording used for the classification statement); Mason et al. (2025) stellar-bycatch constraint, cited comparatively — the null ledger against which new candidates are measured
Falsifiability: Voltage-level re-analysis of the preserved FAST data localizing the track to a known terrestrial source, or a repeat observation failing to reproduce the track under controlled conditions, would close the candidate as RFI/instrumental</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2021-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>106.8563,25.6529,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2021</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Only 1.5-7% of our DNA is uniquely Homo sapiens (SARGE, 2021)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed primary analysis; method and figure replicated in discussion; New Scientist video's core figure confirmed (with correction: UC Santa Cruz, not UCSF)
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 2021 → 2021 (confidence: high)

Schaefer et al. 2021 (UC Santa Cruz, Richard Green lab; doi 10.1126/sciadv.abc0776) built the Speedy Ancestral Recombination Graph Estimator (SARGE) from one Denisovan, two Neanderthal, and 279 modern human genomes, finding that only ~7% of the autosomal genome is uniquely human ('archaic deserts'), narrowing to ~1.5% for high-frequency human-specific derived alleles. The human-unique regions cluster around genes for prenatal brain development.

Earliest attestation: Schaefer et al., Science Advances 2021
Sources: Schaefer et al. 2021, Science Advances, doi:10.1126/sciadv.abc0776
Falsifiability: Re-analysis with larger archaic/modern panels could widen or narrow the human-unique fraction</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2021-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.06,36.99,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2021</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gallup 2022: biblical literalism at record low (20%)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The figures come from Gallup's own published release; the 20% literalism figure is the record low in the four-decade trend and is carried across independent secondary reporting. CORROBORATED as a belief-attribution record — it measures what Americans say about the Bible, not what the Bible says.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2022 → 2022 (confidence: high)

In Gallup's May 2022 Values and Beliefs poll (published June 2022), 20% of Americans said 'the Bible is the actual word of God and is to be taken literally, word for word' — the record low for biblical literalism in Gallup's four-decade trend (from 40% in 1980 to 20% in 2022), with 32% saying the Bible is inspired but not literal and 29% calling it ancient fables. The 2022 wave is the newest Gallup literalism measurement on the record.

Earliest attestation: Gallup, 'Belief in God in U.S. Dips to 81%, a New Low' / Values and Beliefs 2022 release.
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A different published Gallup release with different 2022 literalism figures would supersede.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2022-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5795,39.8283,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2022</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Retraction of the 2022 Giza SAR Doppler tomography paper (August 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports confirm the retraction and its stated grounds ('serious methodological flaws and statistical errors'); radar specialist Lawrence Conyers (Univ. of Denver, emeritus) is on record that satellite SAR cannot physically penetrate the pyramid, reflect off internal structures, and return to the sensor; Sarah Parcak (UAB) called the work willful misinterpretation of data. Graded CORROBORATED as the institutional event it is — not as a ruling on every claim the team ever made.
Layer: real · Place: Great Pyramid of Giza (the paper's subject; the retraction is an editorial event)
Dates: 2022-10 → 2026-08-10 (confidence: high)

The journal Remote Sensing (MDPI) formally retracted the 2022 paper by Filippo Biondi and Corrado Malanga, 'Synthetic Aperture Radar Doppler Tomography Reveals Details of Undiscovered High-Resolution Internal Structure of the Great Pyramid of Giza,' on 10 August 2026, confirming serious methodological flaws and statistical errors that undermined the paper's conclusions. The authors disagreed with the retraction.

Earliest attestation: Retraction notice: Remote Sensing 2026, 18(16), 2679 (published 10 August 2026)
Sources: Retraction notice: Biondi &amp; Malanga 2022 retracted — Remote Sensing 2026, 18(16), 2679 (published 10 August 2026): editors confirmed 'serious methodological flaws and statistical errors'; the authors disagreed with the retraction; Retraction Watch, 31 August 2026 — 'Retraction: corridors inside Great Pyramid of Giza' — reader-flagged concerns, editorial investigation; Conyers: satellite SAR cannot penetrate the pyramid and return (violates basic physics), declined to recommend publication without a mathematically qualified additional reviewer; Parcak: 'willful misinterpretation of data'; notes the March 2025 Khafre press-conference claims were never peer-reviewed; Ancient Origins, 6 September 2026 — coverage of the retraction and its grounds; Ahram Online, 8 September 2026 — Egyptian coverage of the retraction of the 2022 Biondi &amp; Malanga paper; Newsweek, 7 September 2026 — coverage of the retraction and its grounds; ancientist.com, September 2026 — scope analysis: the retraction formally covers only the 2022 Khufu paper; the later Khafre/Menkaure claims were never peer-reviewed and rest on the same disputed radar approach (corroborates this record's existing scope fence); Pomposi, Zenodo, April 2026 — independent rerun of the method (unreviewed preprint; fenced as grade-neutral pending peer review); Graham Hancock, grahamhancock.com — Oct 2026 postscript to the Giza-subsurface article: acknowledges the 10 Aug 2026 Remote Sensing retraction of Biondi &amp; Malanga 2022 ('serious methodological flaws and statistical errors'), carries the authors' dissent, and adds the author's own pressure-theory (retraction driven by skeptical detractors) — venue-echo of the recorded retraction facts, fenced as commentary; grade-neutral, appended under the Turin-shroud precedent; forfoxs.com, 5 Oct 2026 — 'The Great Pyramid's Hidden Spaces: What ScanPyramids Actually Found—and What It Didn't': separates the peer-reviewed muon evidence (Morishima et al. Nature 2017 Big Void; Procureur et al. Nat Comm 2023 North Face Corridor) from the retracted SAR-DT claim, and carries the 10 Aug 2026 Remote Sensing retraction notice facts — venue-echo of the recorded retraction, honest-limit witness; grade-neutral, appended under the Turin-shroud precedent; monkeyandelf.com, Oct 2026 — Giza subsurface retrospective (grade-neutral chain, ep978): surveys the SAR-vs-muon evidence lanes, repeats the 10-Aug-2026 Remote Sensing retraction facts; no new primary evidence graded.
Falsifiability: The retraction is a documented editorial act. It would be falsified by the journal withdrawing the retraction notice or by the paper being republished after corrected peer review.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2022-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.1342,29.9792,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2022</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>LifeWay State of Theology 2022: 53% say the Bible is 'not literally true' (recirculated Oct 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief is attested by the pollster's own 2022 release via consistent secondary reporting; the record grades the attestation of the belief, never the fact; the October-2026 recirculation is fenced as venue-echo of the 2022 wave.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2022-01-05 → 2022-01-23 (confidence: high)

In LifeWay Research's 2022 State of Theology survey (3,011 U.S. adults interviewed January 5-23, 2022; biennial study sponsored by Ligonier Ministries, measuring 'the current theological awareness of adult Americans'), about half of Americans said the Bible is 'completely accurate,' while 53% said it is 'not literally true' and 40% agreed that modern science disproves it. The 2022 wave was recirculated online in October 2026 (Evangelical Focus recrawl) as if current — the survey is 2022, not 2026.

Earliest attestation: LifeWay Research, State of Theology 2022, sponsored by Ligonier Ministries (3,011 interviews, Jan 5-23, 2022).
Sources: LifeWay Research, State of Theology 2022 (3,011 adult Americans, Jan 5-23, 2022; sponsored by Ligonier Ministries) — the pollster's release, via Evangelical Focus recrawl; Scott McConnell, executive director of Lifeway Research: 'views on the Bible probably best summarize how split Americans are when it comes to theology' — quoted in the release
Falsifiability: Release of the LifeWay cross-tabs contradicting the 53% figure; or LifeWay/Ligonier disowning the release.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2022-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2022</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew RLS 2023-24: 65% of U.S. adults believe in 'other spirits or unseen spiritual forces'</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by a published Pew Research Center report with methodology disclosed (RLS 2023-24, n=36,908 overall); the record grades the attestation of the belief figures, never the truth of any belief or entity.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2023-09-01 → 2024-03-31 (confidence: high)

Pew Research Center's Religious Landscape Study 'spiritual beliefs' short report (published 2023-12-07, from the RLS 2023-24 wave) asked U.S. adults directly about unseen spirits: 65% said they believe 'other spirits or unseen spiritual forces exist,' 14% said they do not, and about one-fifth were unsure. Among all U.S. adults, 39% said spirits can be both helpful and harmful (10% generally helpful, 4% generally harmful, ~10% neither). 81% took the position that 'there is something spiritual beyond the natural world, even if we cannot see it' vs 17% 'the natural world is all there is.' Subgroups: Protestants 72% and Catholics 68% believe in spirits/unseen forces; religious 'nones' about half (nothing-in-particular 62%, agnostics 43%, atheists 17%). The record grades the ATTESTATION of the belief figures, never the existence of any entity — the widest direct U.S. measure of spirit belief on the belief ledger.

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center, Religious Landscape Study, 'Americans' spiritual beliefs' short report, published 2023-12-07 (RLS 2023-24 wave, n=36,908 overall)
Sources: Pew Research Center, Religious Landscape Study, 'Americans' spiritual beliefs' short report, published 2023-12-07: 'Regardless of whether you believe in God or a higher power in the universe, do you believe that other spirits or unseen spiritual forces exist?' — 65% yes, 14% no, ~20% unsure; 39% of all U.S. adults say spirits can be both helpful and harmful; 81% 'there is something spiritual beyond the natural world.' Subgroups: Protestants 72%, Catholics 68%, Jewish Americans 33%; nones ~half (nothing-in-particular 62%, agnostics 43%, atheists 17%); Black 72% / Hispanic 70% / White 62% / Asian 58%.; Context: the RLS 2023-24 wave was fielded Sept 2023–March 2024 (n=36,908); the February-2025 RLS summary report's headline 83% 'God or a universal spirit' figure is carried separately on evt-pew-rls-2025-belief-001 — this record is the spirits-specific item.
Falsifiability: Release of the RLS 2023-24 microdata contradicting the reported figures, or Pew's retraction/correction of the short report.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2023-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2023</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew Religious Landscape 2025: belief broad, certainty thinning</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the pollster's own published report with methodology disclosed; the record grades the attestation of the belief figures, never the truth of any belief.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2023-01-01 → 2024-12-31 (confidence: high)

Pew Research Center's third Religious Landscape Study (fielded 2023-2024; report released Feb 26, 2025) found 83% of U.S. adults believe in God or a universal spirit — 9 percentage points less than in 2007 — while the share who hold that belief with absolute certainty fell further, to 54% (17 points less than 2007). 86% of adults believe people have a soul or spirit in addition to their physical body; 79% believe there is something spiritual beyond the natural world even if it cannot be seen; 70% believe in an afterlife (heaven, hell, or both). The Christian share of adults has been roughly stable since 2019 after years of decline; evangelical Protestants edged from about 26% of adults in 2007 to roughly 23% in 2023-24, mainline Protestants from 18% to 11%. 35% of U.S. adults no longer identify with the religion in which they were raised (about 90 million people who changed religious identities).

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center, 'Religious Landscape Study' report, FOR RELEASE FEBRUARY 26, 2025
Sources: Pew Research Center, Religious Landscape Study report, released Feb 26, 2025: 83% believe in God or a universal spirit (9 pts less than 2007); 54% absolutely certain (17 pts less than 2007); 86% soul/spirit; 79% spiritual beyond the natural world; 70% afterlife; evangelicals ~26% to ~23%, mainline 18% to 11%; Pew Research Center Religious Landscape Study collection, 2025: 35% of U.S. adults no longer identify with the religion they were raised in (~90 million switchers); Christian share relatively stable since 2019
Falsifiability: Release of Pew's report or dataset contradicting the headline figures, or the pollster retracting the finding.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2023-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2023</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>AARO historical record report, Vol. I (8 March 2024): the Pentagon finds no evidence of extraterrestrial technology</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The report exists as a published DoD document; its stated findings are quoted verbatim from the executive summary. It is graded as a finding of a review — the absence of evidence reported by the office, not proof of non-existence.
Layer: real · Place: The Pentagon, Arlington, Virginia (issuing office)
Dates: 2024-03-08 → 2024-03-08 (confidence: high)

The All-Domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO), reviewing U.S. government UAP investigations back to 1945 with full access to sensitive programs, reported: 'AARO found no evidence that any USG investigation, academic-sponsored research, or official review panel has confirmed that any sighting of a UAP represented extraterrestrial technology.' It judged the alleged reverse-engineering programs 'inaccurate,' attributing them largely to circular reporting. Many cases remain unsolved, which AARO attributes to insufficient data quality.

Earliest attestation: AARO 'Report on the Historical Record of U.S. Government Involvement with Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (UAP), Volume I' (8 March 2024)
Sources: AARO, Report on the Historical Record of U.S. Government Involvement with UAP, Vol. I (8 March 2024); NASA, UAP Independent Study Team final report (14 September 2023); Space.com, 'Pentagon UFO office finds no empirical evidence for alien technology' (8 March 2024)
Falsifiability: A verified, independently analyzed non-terrestrial artifact or confirmed technosignature would overturn the report's conclusion</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2024-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0563,38.8719,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2024</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>US creationism belief, Gallup May 2024</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published survey data from the pollster's own primary release, cross-checked against a secondary summary (friendlyatheist) — both agree on 37/34/24, the record low, and the record high. Held below VERIFIED: it measures self-reported belief, not fact.
Layer: real · Place: United States (Gallup national sample, all 50 states + DC)
Dates: 2024 → 2024 (confidence: high)

In May 2024, 37% of US adults told Gallup that 'God created human beings pretty much in their present form at one time within the last 10,000 years or so' (34% said humans evolved with God's guidance, 24% said humans evolved over millions of years with no role for God). The 37% is the lowest creationist share in Gallup's four-decade trend; the 24% is the highest secular-evolution share ever recorded.

Earliest attestation: Gallup, 'Majority Still Credits God for Humankind, but Not Creationism' (published 2024-07-22), poll fielded May 1–23, 2024.
Sources: Gallup, 'Majority Still Credits God for Humankind, but Not Creationism' (July 22, 2024) — 37% strict creationist (record low) / 34% God-guided evolution / 24% evolution without God (record high); fielded May 1–23, 2024; creationism and God-guided evolution peaked at 47% and 40% in 1999; Friendly Atheist summary of the same Gallup release — confirms 37% / 24% / 34% and the record-low/record-high framings, independently consistent with Gallup's own page
Falsifiability: A replicated Gallup run with the same question showing a materially different share would supersede this record; Gallup's own trend PDF would contradict the figures as stated.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2024-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5795,39.8283,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2024</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>American Christian belief, 2025 (Barna SALT Index, belief-ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Documented national survey (n=6,037) with published figures, consistent in direction with the independent ABS 2026 and Pew RLS waves; no survey asks the historicity question directly, and this record does not fill that gap.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national survey)
Dates: 2025 → 2025 (confidence: high)

Barna's 2025 State of Christianity in America (SALT Index, 6,037 U.S. adults, fielded November 2025) found: ~70% affirm Jesus died for our sins; 64% affirm the resurrection; 61% consider themselves followers of Jesus. Practice is narrower: ~60% pray or worship regularly, ~35% read the Bible weekly, ~31% have ever mentored/discipled another person.

Earliest attestation: Barna Group, State of Christianity in America 2025 (SALT Index)
Sources: Christian Post (~Aug 2026) reporting Barna 2025: 6,037 U.S. adults, Nov 2025 — 70% affirm Jesus died for our sins; 64% affirm the resurrection; 61% consider themselves followers of Jesus; ~60% pray/worship regularly; ~35% read Bible weekly; ~31% ever mentored/discipled; mentoring rate equal among non-followers.; American Bible Society, State of the Bible 2026 ch. 10 (n=2,649, Jan 8–27, 2026): 53% say Jesus's resurrection 'definitely' or 'probably' happened; 49% affirm the virgin birth — consistent direction, different wording/sponsor.; Pew Religious Landscape Study 2023–24 (Feb 2025): 83% believe in 'God or a universal spirit' — different question, belief side of the ledger.
Falsifiability: Barna revising the figures in a published erratum, or a later Barna wave showing the numbers materially moved under the same instrument</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baylor 2025: the secular rationalists who kept religion believe in magic at double the rate</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by a published peer-reviewed survey analysis with methodology disclosed (n=1,812, Baylor Religion Survey, Gallup fielded early 2025); the record grades the attestation of the belief figures, never the truth of any belief or entity.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2025-01-01 → 2025-12-31 (confidence: high)

A study published in the journal Social Forces (July 2026) analyzing the latest wave of the Baylor Religion Survey (a nationally representative survey of 1,812 American adults fielded by Gallup in early 2025) found that people who score high on 'secular rationalism' — the belief that science is the only way to understand the world and that morality should rest on reason — but who keep a tie to religion are about twice as likely to believe in 'magic' (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead): 40% vs 19% among Americans who believe in God, 28% vs 19% among service attenders. Among people who never attend services or do not believe in God, secular rationalism was no longer linked to belief in magic. Reported by the study's author via The Conversation (October 2026).

Earliest attestation: Social Forces, July 2026 (Baylor Religion Survey wave, Gallup fielded early 2025, n=1,812); author write-up via The Conversation, Oct 2026
Sources: Social Forces, July 2026: analysis of the Baylor Religion Survey (n=1,812 American adults, Gallup fielded early 2025): 'secular rationalists' who believe in God or attend services lean toward magic ~2x more (40% vs 19% among God-believers; 28% vs 19% among attenders); no link among the non-religious
Falsifiability: Release of the Baylor wave data or the Social Forces paper contradicting the reported figures, or the survey being retracted.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baylor 2026: secular rationalism plus church ties predicts more magic belief</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The finding comes from a peer-reviewed analysis (Social Forces, July 2026) of a nationally representative survey wave fielded by Gallup (early 2025, n=1,812) — it is a CORROBORATED record of what Americans said. It attests the belief, never the entities: the timeline grades the attestation, not the metaphysics. The figure is secondhand-reported (PopSci / phys.org, October 2026); the Social Forces paper is the primary venue, not independently read this run.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national sample)
Dates: 2025 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Analysis of the latest Baylor Religion Survey wave (nationally representative, n=1,812 American adults, fielded by Gallup in early 2025) found that Americans who score high on 'secular rationalism' — the belief that science is the only way to understand the world, that morality should rest on reason, and that spiritual matters should be interpreted personally rather than trusted to religious authorities — but who keep some tie to religion are MORE likely to believe in magic (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead — six non-theistic supernatural items) than secular rationalists without church ties. Among Americans who believe in God, high secular rationalists were about twice as likely to lean toward magic as those who reject secular rationalism (40% vs 19%); among church attenders, 28% vs 19%; among non-attenders and non-believers, secular rationalism no longer predicted magic belief. The belief is graded here as belief: it attests the belief-state, never the entities.

Earliest attestation: Social Forces, July 2026 (peer-reviewed); press coverage October 2026 (PopSci Oct 16, 2026; phys.org)
Sources: Baylor Religion Survey, latest wave — nationally representative, n=1,812 American adults, fielded by Gallup in early 2025. Survey design and fielding attested by the published analysis.; Social Forces, July 2026 — peer-reviewed analysis introducing 'secular rationalism' (science-only + morality-on-reason + self-interpretation) and finding it predicts magic-belief among the church-tied: 40% vs 19% (God-believers), 28% vs 19% (attenders), no link among the unchurched. Primary venue; not independently read this run.; PopSci, Oct 16, 2026 8:03 AM EDT — 'Americans who go to church and trust science are also more likely to believe in ghosts and curses' (originally The Conversation). Secondary venue reporting the July 2026 paper.; phys.org, Oct 2026 — same July 2026 Social Forces finding, re-reported.
Falsifiability: Baylor or Gallup publishing the wave's raw instrument showing the four secular-rationalism items or six magic items absent; or the Social Forces paper retracted.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.58,39.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Breakthrough Listen's technosignature search of interstellar object 3I/ATLAS (18 December 2025)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published preprint by the Breakthrough Listen collaboration reporting their own program's null outcome — graded as the search's own documented result, the V.28 attestation rule's SETI-side application: we grade the search's record, never the silence's meaning. The '74-million-signal June 2026 scan' telling is fenced out as unverified.
Layer: real · Place: Green Bank Observatory, West Virginia (observation) / 3I/ATLAS interstellar object (target)
Dates: 2025-12-18 → 2025-12-18 (confidence: high)

On 18 December 2025, the Breakthrough Listen program used the 100-m Robert C. Byrd Green Bank Telescope to conduct a technosignature search toward the interstellar object 3I/ATLAS (one day before its 19 December 2025 closest approach to Earth), scanning 1–12 GHz — the most sensitive radio search for a technosignature from an interstellar object to date. The team reported a nondetection of candidate signals down to the 100 mW level.

Earliest attestation: Jacobson-Bell, Croft, White, Siemion, Lebofsky, MacMahon, arXiv:2512.19763 (submitted 20 Dec 2025)
Sources: Jacobson-Bell et al., 'Breakthrough Listen Observations of 3I/ATLAS with the Green Bank Telescope at 1-12 GHz' (submitted 20 Dec 2025, arXiv:2512.19763): 'We report a nondetection of candidate signals down to the 100 mW level'
Falsifiability: A repeatable narrowband detection from 3I/ATLAS or a future interstellar object, verified on-off source, would overturn the null for that target</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.8398,38.4331,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Canadian paranormal belief, 2025 (belief-ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A representative national poll on belief, multiply reported; grades the belief-attestation (what people say they believe and report), never the entities — the attestation rule applied
Layer: real · Place: Canada (national survey)
Dates: 2025 → 2026 (confidence: high)

A nationally representative 2025 Canadian survey found that almost half of Canadians believe in at least one paranormal phenomenon, and about one-quarter report encounters with spirits — a new belief-ledger datum. The record grades the BELIEF-ATTESTATION (the poll), never the entities named in it.

Earliest attestation: Summer 2025 nationally representative Canadian survey of paranormal attitudes (probit panel)
Sources: 'New research reveals that almost half of Canadians believe in the paranormal — ghosts and all' (thefreelibrary.com): summer 2025 nationally representative survey (probit.ca methodology) — almost one in two Canadians believe in at least one paranormal phenomenon; one-quarter report encounters with spirits; first representative Canadian data on paranormal beliefs in 20 years. Questions covered ghostly hauntings, alien visitations, psychic abilities, telekinesis, astrology, unexplained phenomena, and cryptids (Cadborosaurus, Ogopogo); American Bible Society, State of the Bible 2026, Chapter 7: The Supernatural (released October 2026, n=2,649, AmeriSpeak panel): 55% of Americans believe in Satan, 53% in hell, 51% in demons — the US-side comparison ledger for the same belief universe
Falsifiability: An independent representative Canadian survey finding substantially different figures, or a published methodological audit showing the sample was unrepresentative, would force a re-grade</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-106.0,56.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Ancient southern-African genomes and the deep structured sapiens lineage (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published in Nature with full methods; 28 genomes, deep-coverage analysis, the isolation and split-timing results stated with their model dependencies; press coverage (phys.org, Technology Networks, Dec 2025) carries the authors' own framing.
Layer: real · Place: Southern Africa (the ancient genomes' origins)
Dates: 2025 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Jakobsson, Schlebusch et al. (Nature 650(8100):156–163, online 3 Dec 2025, in print 2026, doi 10.1038/s41586-025-09811-4) sequenced 28 ancient southern-African genomes (10.2 ka to 150 years old), finding ≥200 kyr of long-term population isolation, a deepest population split vs other groups at ~310–240 ka, and 490 Homo sapiens-specific variants — the genomic anchor for a pan-African, structured-population emergence of our species, contradicting the old East-Africa-only-then-southward-spread model.

Earliest attestation: Jakobsson, Schlebusch et al., Nature 650(8100):156–163 (the paper itself)
Sources: Jakobsson, Schlebusch et al. 2026, Nature 650(8100):156–163, doi 10.1038/s41586-025-09811-4 — 28 ancient southern-African genomes; ≥200 kyr isolation; ~310–240 ka deepest split; 490 sapiens-specific variants (PubMed 41339558); phys.org, Dec 2025 — 'Thousand-year genomes reveal deep roots of humans in southern Africa'; Technology Networks, Dec 2025 — 'Ancient DNA reveals deep roots of humans in southern Africa'
Falsifiability: Retraction, or ancient genomes showing a shallow southern-African coalescence (recent shared ancestry instead of ≥200 kyr isolation).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>20.0,-25.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The MUC19 'genetic sandwich': Denisovan DNA that arrived via Neanderthals (2025)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed primary analysis; the Neanderthal-flanked Denisovan segment is direct structural evidence
Layer: real · Place: 
Dates: 2025 → 2025 (confidence: high)

Villanea, Peede et al. 2025 (Science, doi 10.1126/science.adl0882): a 72-kb Denisovan-specific region embedded in a 742-kb Neanderthal-derived haplotype at MUC19, at high frequency in Indigenous American populations (1 in 3 Mexicans vs ~1% of Central Europeans) under positive selection — the first clear case of Denisovan DNA entering modern humans indirectly, through Neanderthals.

Earliest attestation: Villanea, Peede et al., Science 2025
Sources: Villanea, Peede et al. 2025, Science, doi:10.1126/science.adl0882
Falsifiability: Larger population panels could revise the frequency estimates</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-100.0,20.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Iznik commemoration of Nicaea's 1700th anniversary, 28 November 2025</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporaneous wire reporting with named participants, location, and the filioque omission (consistent with the standing papal practice of Benedict XVI, Francis, and Leo). Held at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED pending the published Holy See texts/photos of the service itself.
Layer: real · Place: Iznik (ancient Nicaea), Turkey — overlooking Lake Iznik
Dates: 2025-11-28 → 2025-11-28 (confidence: high)

On 28 November 2025, Pope Leo XIV and Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew led a joint ecumenical prayer service at Iznik, Turkey (ancient Nicaea) marking the 1700th anniversary of the First Council of Nicaea (325), jointly reciting the Nicene Creed — deliberately without the filioque clause — with the Greek Orthodox patriarchs of Antioch, Alexandria and Jerusalem (or their representatives) and Anglican and Protestant representatives present, on a platform overlooking the submerged Basilica of St. Neophytos in Lake Iznik.

Earliest attestation: Contemporaneous wire reporting (CNS, via Catholic Review), 28 November 2025
Sources: Contemporaneous reporting: Catholic Review (CNS), 'Though Nicaea is a ruin, its Creed stands and unites Christians, pope says' — Iznik service details: joint Creed without the filioque, participating patriarchs, the lake-side platform; Public Orthodoxy, 'Leo XIV and Bartholomew: The Importance and Aftermath of a Meeting' (2025-12-09) — places the visit in the post-1965 joint-declaration series (Paul VI, John Paul II, Benedict XVI, Francis); Assyrian International News Agency / WCC context (April 2025): the 1700th anniversary renewed the WCC's common-Easter-date push; Easter 2025 fell on 20 April in both calendars; The Wheel (24 January 2025): Orthodox clergy/theologians' statement on a common Easter date, issued at the Huffington Ecumenical Institute colloquium for the Nicaea anniversary
Falsifiability: The record would weaken if the contemporaneous reporting misidentified participants or the Creed's wording; it would strengthen (toward VERIFIED) on publication of the Holy See/Patriarchate service texts and photo record.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>29.7208,40.4297,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Iznik commemoration of Nicaea's 1700th anniversary, 28 November 2025</name><description>Grade: VERIFIED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary sources now document the event: the Holy See's official publication of the signed Joint Declaration text (press.vatican.va, 29 Nov 2025); contemporaneous CNS wire reporting with the Lola Gomez photo record of the 28 Nov Iznik service; the World Communion of Reformed Churches' first-hand witness account naming participants (Rev. Jerry Pillay, Rev. Philip Vinod Peacock); the archons.org institutional account corroborating the joint Creed-without-filioque detail and the Benedict VIII 1014 insertion; the Nov 23 2025 apostolic letter In Unitate Fidei. This satisfies the -001 record's own falsifiability condition (publication of Holy See/Patriarchate texts and the photo record).
Layer: real · Place: Iznik (ancient Nicaea), Turkey — overlooking Lake Iznik
Dates: 2025-11-28 → 2025-11-29 (confidence: high)

On 28 November 2025, Pope Leo XIV and Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew led a joint ecumenical prayer service at Iznik, Turkey (ancient Nicaea) marking the 1700th anniversary of the First Council of Nicaea (325), jointly reciting the Nicene Creed — deliberately without the filioque clause — with the Greek Orthodox patriarchs of Antioch, Alexandria and Jerusalem (or their representatives) and Anglican and Protestant representatives present, on a platform overlooking the submerged Basilica of St. Neophytos in Lake Iznik. On 29 November 2025 at the Phanar, Leo XIV and Bartholomew signed a Joint Declaration reaffirming the shared commitment to full communion and commemorating the 60th anniversary of the 1965 Paul VI–Athenagoras declaration.

Earliest attestation: Contemporaneous wire reporting (CNS, via Catholic Review), 28 November 2025; the signed Joint Declaration text, published by the Holy See, 29 November 2025.
Sources: Holy See Press Office, Joint Declaration of Pope Leo XIV and Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew I, signed at the Patriarchal Palace, Istanbul, 29 Nov 2025 (official English text); Pope Leo XIV, apostolic letter In Unitate Fidei (In the Unity of Faith) on the Creed and the 1700th anniversary of the Council of Nicaea, released 23 Nov 2025; Contemporaneous reporting: Catholic Review (CNS), 'Though Nicaea is a ruin, its Creed stands and unites Christians, pope says' — Iznik service details: joint Creed without the filioque, participating patriarchs, the lake-side platform; Lola Gomez/CNS photo record, 28 Nov 2025; World Communion of Reformed Churches first-hand account of the Iznik commemoration (Rev. Jerry Pillay, Rev. Philip Vinod Peacock present) — participant-witness corroboration; Order of St. Andrew / Archons of the Ecumenical Patriarchate: 'The Ecumenical Patriarch and the Pope in Nicaea: History in the Making' — the joint Creed without filioque; the 1014 Benedict VIII insertion; Focolare Movement: the Dec 12 2025 60th-anniversary-of-1965 seminar — messages from Patriarch Bartholomew and Pope Leo XIV (via Cardinal Parolin); Public Orthodoxy, 'Leo XIV and Bartholomew: The Importance and Aftermath of a Meeting' (2025-12-09) — places the visit in the post-1965 joint-declaration series (Paul VI, John Paul II, Benedict XVI, Francis); Assyrian International News Agency / WCC context (April 2025): the 1700th anniversary renewed the WCC's common-Easter-date push; Easter 2025 fell on 20 April in both calendars; The Wheel (24 January 2025): Orthodox clergy/theologians' statement on a common Easter date, issued at the Huffington Ecumenical Institute colloquium for the Nicaea anniversary; Seed record evt-nicaea-1700-commemoration-2025-001 (superseded; CORROBORATED — the pre-publication state)
Falsifiability: The record would weaken if the signed declaration text proved fabricated or the multiple contemporaneous accounts of the Creed's wording diverged on the filioque omission. The grade would have stayed at CORROBORATED without the published Holy See text and the photo/witness record.</description><styleUrl>#verified</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>29.7208,40.4297,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>VERIFIED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>1.0</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>PHerc.172 identified as Philodemus's On Vices (still-rolled scroll, 2025)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Institutional primary (Diamond Light Source announcement, Bodleian/Oxford, Vesuvius Challenge) plus independent replication (two parties read the same title page) plus independent papyrological review — the title identification is confirmed to the degree a still-rolled scroll permits; the content reading remains a work in progress.
Layer: real · Place: Bodleian Libraries, University of Oxford (current holding; excavated at Herculaneum, Villa of the Papyri, 40.8062, 14.3439)
Dates: 2025-02 → 2025-02 (confidence: high)

PHerc.172, a sealed Herculaneum scroll at Oxford's Bodleian Libraries (donated by Ferdinand IV of Naples in the early 19th century), was identified by virtual unwrapping as Philodemus's On Vices — an Epicurean ethical treatise whose full title reads On Vices and Their Opposite Virtues and In Whom They Are and About What. The end-title was deciphered from the inside of the still-rolled scroll: scanned July 2024 on Diamond Light Source's I12 beamline (unusually strong ink signal — denser contaminants, possibly lead), the title announced 5 February 2025 by the University of Oxford, independently achieved by Sean Johnson (Vesuvius Challenge) and by Marcel Roth &amp; Micha Nowak (University of Würzburg), independently reviewed by Federica Nicolardi's papyrology team, awarded the Vesuvius Challenge First Title Prize ($60,000).

Earliest attestation: University of Oxford / Bodleian Libraries + Vesuvius Challenge announcement, 5 February 2025
Sources: Diamond Light Source (2026): 'New secrets revealed from the Herculaneum scrolls' — I12 beamline scan, end-title deciphered mid-scroll, On Vices by the Epicurean Philodemus (greed, arrogance, flattery); PHerc.1667 summary: ~1.5 m / ~20 columns, 2nd c. BCE or late 3rd c. BCE, possibly Chrysippus-linked Stoic ethics; Fine Books Magazine: 'Title of Work Deciphered In Sealed Herculaneum Scroll Via Digital Unwrapping' — scan July 2024, first interior images February 2025, independent dual reading (Sean Johnson; Roth &amp; Nowak, Würzburg), review by Federica Nicolardi's team, $60,000 First Title Prize; spokenpast.com: scanned July 2024 on Diamond I12, virtually unwrapped interior announced 5 February 2025 by Oxford as a historic breakthrough — the first image of the inside of PHerc.172 since 79 CE
Falsifiability: A re-reading by the papyrology team contesting the end-title; demonstration that the columns were misassembled</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.2543,51.7548,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tomb of Thutmose II identified — first royal tomb since Tutankhamun (Feb 2025)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports agree: the 18 February 2025 announcement; Tomb C4 in Wadi C, ~2.4 km west of the Valley of the Kings; the joint Egyptian-British mission (SCA + New Kingdom Research Foundation, Piers Litherland); the alabaster vessel fragments inscribed with Thutmose II's name and Hatshepsut's; the initial misidentification as a royal consort's tomb; the flood damage shortly after burial with contents moved in antiquity; Hatshepsut overseeing the burial; the first funerary furniture of Thutmose II ever found; the first royal tomb since 1922. The king's mummy was already known from the 1881 Deir el-Bahri cache — the tomb, not the body, is the discovery.
Layer: real · Place: Wadi C, Theban Hills (~2.4 km west of the Valley of the Kings)
Dates: 2025-02-18 → 2025-02-18 (confidence: high)

On 18 February 2025, Egypt's Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announced that Tomb C4 in the Western Wadis near Luxor — excavated by a joint Egyptian-British mission (Supreme Council of Antiquities + New Kingdom Research Foundation, field director Piers Litherland) — is the tomb of Thutmose II (18th Dynasty, ~1479 BCE): the first royal Egyptian tomb discovered since Tutankhamun's in 1922.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, 18 February 2025
Sources: Egyptian State Information Service, 18 Feb 2025 (via Free Library): 'A joint Egyptian-British archaeological team has uncovered the long-lost tomb of King Thutmose II, the last missing royal tomb of the 18th Dynasty... the first royal tomb uncovered since Howard Carter revealed King Tutankhamun's tomb in 1922.' Alabaster jar fragments with the names of Thutmose II and Hatshepsut 'provided definitive evidence of the tomb's ownership.'; Space Daily: the blue-and-yellow starred ceiling as an 18th-Dynasty pharaonic marker; the duck-shaped alabaster cosmetic jars; Litherland's account of the decade-long Western Wadis programme (50+ tombs excavated) behind the identification.
Falsifiability: Reattribution of Tomb C4 to a non-royal owner by the excavating mission</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.6014,25.7402,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wellcome Global Monitor 2026: Americans' trust in science, via Gallup World Poll</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Wellcome's own published survey via the Gallup World Poll — the largest recurring trust-in-science survey, with published methodology (mixed in-person/telephone/online, population-weighted global estimates). It is a CORROBORATED record of what respondents SAID about trust — the belief ledger, never the evidence ledger. Figures secondhand-reported (STAT, Oct 10, 2026); Wellcome's release is the primary venue.
Layer: real · Place: Global (140+ nations); U.S. national subsample
Dates: 2025 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Wellcome Global Monitor 2026 — administered through the Gallup World Poll across 140+ nations, 140,000+ adults (2018, 2020, 2025 waves; Wellcome-funded) — found 52% of Americans report 'a lot' of trust in science vs a 26% global average, and 45% would accept recommended vaccines immediately vs 28% in Germany. Within the U.S., religious and non-religious Americans differed by 24 percentage points on whether science benefits most people (vs a 5-point average gap across other high-income countries); urban and rural Americans differed by 19 points (vs 3 elsewhere); and religious Americans' 'science benefits most people' rose 46% to 50% between 2018 and 2025. Attests trust-beliefs, never a scientific fact.

Earliest attestation: Wellcome Global Monitor 2026 release; press coverage October 2026 (STAT Oct 10, 2026; rocketnews re-report)
Sources: Wellcome Global Monitor 2026 — administered through the Gallup World Poll, 140+ nations, 140,000+ adults; population-weighted global estimates. Primary venue.; STAT News, Oct 10, 2026 — 'U.S. trust in science, vaccines higher than in other nations, survey shows' (52% 'a lot' trust vs 26% global; 45% immediate vaccine acceptance vs 28% Germany; the 24-point religious gap, the 19-point urban-rural gap, the 46-to-50% 2018-2025 rise among religious Americans). Secondary venue.; Matthew Motta (Boston University School of Public Health), via STAT — Americans are 'more extreme responders to public opinion surveys' (context: the internal-division readings may partly reflect response style, carried as the method caveat).
Falsifiability: Wellcome withdrawing or correcting its published release; or a documented fielding failure in the Gallup World Poll wave.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.58,39.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1831 mystery eruption identified: Zavaritskii caldera (Jan 2025)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports agree: the 7 January 2025 PNAS paper; the multiproxy ice-core geochemistry; S isotopes confirming a Northern Hemisphere stratospheric eruption and ruling out evaporite sulfur; cryptotephra (glass shards ~1/10th a human hair's width) matching Zavaritskii's youngest Plinian chemistry; radiocarbon confirming a &lt;300-year eruption; VEI 5–6; ~13 Tg sulfur; ~1°C NH cooling 1831–1833; the blue/green sun; the Guntur famine of 1832; the monsoon fluctuations. Fenced: the long-standing tropical attribution (Babuyan Claro, Philippines) — 'wrongly attributed to a tropical volcano for a long time period' (Brönnimann, via CNN); and the Ferdinandea phreatomagmatic hypothesis addressed and set aside in the paper. Zavaritskii's prior last-known eruption was 800 BCE; Simushir is uninhabited (former Soviet submarine base).
Layer: real · Place: Zavaritskii caldera, Simushir Island, Kurils
Dates: 2025-01-07 → 2025-01-07 (confidence: high)

On 7 January 2025, Hutchison et al. published in PNAS the identification of the Zavaritskii caldera (Simushir Island, Kuril archipelago) as the source of the mystery 1831 eruption — via sulfur-isotope and cryptotephra analysis of Greenland ice cores: microscopic glass shards matching Zavaritskii's youngest Plinian chemistry, ~13 Tg of stratospheric sulfur, ~1°C Northern Hemisphere cooling, VEI 5–6, radiative forcing comparable to Pinatubo 1991 — closing a ~200-year cold case.

Earliest attestation: PNAS, 7 January 2025 (Hutchison, Sugden, Burke, Abbott, Ponomareva et al.)
Sources: PNAS, 7 Jan 2025 (Hutchison et al.): 'These data provide compelling evidence that Zavaritskii was the source of the 1831 CE mystery eruption and solve a confounding case of multiple closely spaced observed and unobserved volcanic eruptions.' ~13 Tg sulfur; cryptotephra matching Zavaritskii; radiative forcing −2 ± 1 W m−2, comparable to Pinatubo 1991.; Smithsonian Magazine, Jan 2025: Zavaritskii 'an extremely remote volcano' on Simushir; Hutchison on the poor eruptive-history knowledge of remote volcanoes; the Babuyan Claro misattribution; Greenland's 6.5× sulfur fallout vs Antarctica placing it in the Northern Hemisphere.; Wikipedia, 'Zavaritski Caldera': sulfate peaks in polar ice cores; Japanese records of an abnormally colored sun; 0.5–1.0°C NH cooling; the Hutchison et al. PNAS citation (7 Jan 2025).
Falsifiability: A competing tephra match to another 1831-eruption candidate</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2025-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>151.9569,46.9264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2025</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>SETI Institute's June 2026 technosignature scan of interstellar object 3I/ATLAS</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The published AJ paper's own null result is multiply attested (the paper itself, the SETI Institute statement, independent secondary reports agreeing on the figures); graded as the study's reported finding — the null constrains transmitter power, it does not prove the object is natural in any absolute sense.
Layer: real · Place: Allen Telescope Array, Hat Creek Radio Observatory, Northern California
Dates: 2026-06-03 → 2026-06-03 (confidence: high)

In June 2026, a SETI Institute team led by Sofia Sheikh used the Allen Telescope Array to listen to the interstellar object 3I/ATLAS for more than seven hours across ~1-9 GHz, finding no narrowband technosignature: ~74 million candidate narrowband signals were filtered to ~200 and all were identified as Earth-based radio-frequency interference or satellites; the null sets an upper limit of ~10-110 watts on any radio transmitter on or near the object.

Earliest attestation: Sofia Z. Sheikh et al., 'A Search for Radio Technosignatures from Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS with the Allen Telescope Array', The Astronomical Journal (2026), DOI: 10.3847/1538-3881/ae6651
Sources: Sofia Z. Sheikh et al., 'A Search for Radio Technosignatures from Interstellar Object 3I/ATLAS with the Allen Telescope Array', The Astronomical Journal (2026), DOI: 10.3847/1538-3881/ae6651; SETI Institute press statement (via phys.org, June 3, 2026): seven-hour ATA scan, ~74M narrowband candidates reduced to ~200, all RFI/satellites, 10-110 W upper limits; 'the observations place upper limits on the power of any radio transmitter on or near 3I/ATLAS'; ts2.tech (Sept 15, 2026): Breakthrough Listen + ATA + MeerKAT campaigns all reported no artificial radio emission from 3I/ATLAS; Green Bank Telescope deep scan Dec 18 (date of the report ambiguous in the secondary — carried as reported); FAST periodic radio technosignature search toward 3I/ATLAS (accepted 2026 July 1; published 2026 August 24, DOI 10.3847/1538-3881/ae8755): no credible artificial periodic radio technosignature above 0.146 W detected; canonical polyadic decomposition of multibeam dynamic spectra; intelligentliving.co (2026-09-11, updated 2026-09-30): two independent radio surveys, both null — ATA ruled out transmitters stronger than ~10–110 W across 1–9 GHz; FAST constrained narrowband transmitters below 2.862 mW — the tightest limits ever placed on technosignatures from an interstellar object; Cochran, Zeimann, Jehin, Kawakita &amp; McKay 2026, 'IFU Observations of Comet 3I/ATLAS = C/2025 N1 (ATLAS) Using the Hobby-Eberly Telescope with the VIRUS Instrument', The Planetary Science Journal 7:226 (doi:10.3847/PSJ/aea086; arXiv:2609.01785, submitted 1 Sept 2026) — HET/VIRUS IFU spectra (9 Dec 2025): very strong FeI/NiI emission lines (metallic-form iron/nickel likely off the nucleus) and gas outflow at ~half solar-system-comet speed at the same heliocentric distance; the natural-comet characterization sharpened — the object the two 2025–26 technosignature nulls were placed against is now a better-measured natural body. Grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent).; Li, Jian-Kang, Tao, Zhen-Zhao &amp; Zhang, Tong-Jie, 'Narrowband Radio Technosignature Search toward 3I/ATLAS with FAST', The Astrophysical Journal (accepted 2026 June 19; published 2026 July 22, 2026; DOI 10.3847/1538-4357/ae8025; arXiv:2603.19023v3): FAST L-band 1.05-1.45 GHz narrowband drifting-signal search (bliss pipeline, SNR&gt;10) on four separate epochs Oct 2025-Jan 2026 (Mars closest, perihelion, Earth closest, post-Earth-closest) — no credible narrowband technosignature after visual inspection; null constrains transmitters above 2.862x10^-3 W (the tightest technosignature limits ever placed on an interstellar object, ~52,000x tighter than the ATA 10-110 W band); Bayesian inference framework for transmitter occurrence probability with physically motivated priors; a THIRD independent null survey — separate organization, different instrument — the independent-authentication discipline's own field demonstration; Chain-strengthening 2026-10-20 (V.30 sixth pass): JWST NIRSpec via NASA — CO2:H2O ~8:1, first methane detection on an interstellar object (ApJL 2026); Cordiner et al., Nature (June 2026) deuterium measurements — the composition case for the natural-comet reading deepened; 3I/ATLAS is outbound and fading, the single-pass sample exhausted (the measurements gathered are all anyone will ever get). The triple-null independent-authentication field test stands: ATA + FAST periodic + FAST narrowband, separate organizations, different instruments, all null; Avi Loeb, Medium essay (~Oct 16-21, 2026), 'Extending the Loeb Scale to the Search for Extraterrestrial Artificial Intelligence (SETAI)': assigns 3I/ATLAS (and 1I/'Oumuamua) C0 on the four-level SETAI Control Scale — 'no contingent behavior was observed' — the null stated inside the hypothesis's most prominent advocate's own framework; a framework proposal, not evidence; Frincke &amp; Seligman 2026 MNRAS mass-loss study (reported phys.org Oct-20-2026, archynetys Oct-22-2026): 3I/ATLAS lost ~18-58 billion kg (mostly water, CO2, CO) near perihelion; modeled nucleus erosion 1.7-5.2 m surface-averaged (up to ~26 m in dust-rich scenarios), nucleus ~1.3 km radius — fully natural cometary behavior; ESA Mars-orbiter imaging (Oct 2026) and Cochran et al. PSJ 7,226 chemistry (iron-nickel vapor, slow gas outflow) all natural-comet signatures. Grade-neutral: the material context in which the technosignature nulls (ATA June 2026, GBT Dec 2025) were recorded; no technosignature claim in any 2026 peer-reviewed finding.
Falsifiability: A repeatable narrowband detection from 3I/ATLAS surviving the same RFI-filtering pipeline would overturn the null; the object's Dec 19, 2025 closest approach (1.8 AU) is past and it leaves the solar system on a hyperbolic trajectory</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-121.4694,40.8174,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>AARO FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP (July 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Graded as the government report's own stated findings via two independent secondary reports that agree on the figures — the V.28 rule applied: we grade the office's publication, never its completeness. The report's own verdict is the relevant datum for the 'zero verified evidence' state: the Pentagon's 2026 report still finds no extraterrestrial technology. The 14-unexplained remainder is 'unresolved,' not 'unresolvable' — the office's own explanation is insufficient data, not inexplicability.
Layer: real · Place: The Pentagon, Arlington, Virginia (report issued)
Dates: 2026-07-01 → 2026-07-31 (confidence: medium)

In July 2026, the Pentagon's All-domain Anomaly Resolution Office released its Fiscal Year 2025 Consolidated Annual Report on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (reporting period 2 June 2024–30 May 2025): 319 reports received; 114 resolved as balloons, birds, satellites, aircraft, unmanned aerial systems, one rocket launch, and one manned jet pack; 191 had insufficient data for a definitive explanation; 14 remained unexplained; cumulative holdings stood at 1,870 reports as of 30 May 2025. The report reiterated that none of the resolved cases indicate advanced foreign adversarial capabilities or breakthrough technologies, and disclosed one maritime-domain case — a Navy asset off the Virginia coast reporting ~100 airborne UAP and two likely uncrewed surface systems — under active investigation.

Earliest attestation: AARO FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report on UAP (July 2026), mandated by the Intelligence Authorization Act FY2023 — via secondary reporting
Sources: Chris Rutkowski, 'New AARO Report Released July 20, 2026' (Substack, ~July 2026): 319 cases, 114 resolved, 191 insufficient data, 14 unexplained; 'None of the cases AARO resolved indicate advanced foreign adversarial capabilities or breakthrough technologies'; Disclosure Archives, 'AARO releases — every official UAP publication' (catalog, accessed Oct 2026): FY2025 Consolidated Annual Report ~21 July 2026; 319 reports; ~100-UAP Navy maritime encounter under active investigation; total holdings 1,870 as of 30 May 2025
Falsifiability: A subsequent AARO publication attributing any case to non-terrestrial technology would overturn the 'no evidence of ET technology' datum</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0563,38.8719,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>American biblical-miracle belief, 2026 (belief-ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A representative national poll on belief, published by its sponsor and multiply reported; grades the belief-attestation (what people say they believe about the biblical events), never the events' historicity — the attestation rule applied; the cross-decade Pew comparison carried with its wording caveat
Layer: real · Place: United States (national survey)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The American Bible Society's State of the Bible 2026, Chapter 7: The Supernatural (released October 8, 2026; n=2,649 US adults, AmeriSpeak, fielded January 8–27) found: 53% of Americans say the resurrection of Jesus 'probably' or 'definitely' happened; 49% say the same of the virgin birth — the first time a major national survey recorded belief in the virgin birth below 50% (Pew 2017: 66%; Pew 2014: 73%, with different wording noted); 44% of the parting of the Red Sea. Among practicing Christians: 99% resurrection, 97% virgin birth, 92% Red Sea. The record grades the BELIEF-ATTESTATION (the poll), never the events' historicity.

Earliest attestation: ABS State of the Bible 2026, Chapter 7: The Supernatural (released 2026-10-08; Leonardo Blair / Christian Post 2026-10-11; ABS press release 2026-10-08)
Sources: ABS State of the Bible 2026, Chapter 7: The Supernatural (released 2026-10-08; n=2,649, Jan 8–27): resurrection 53% probably/definitely, virgin birth 49%, Red Sea parting 44%; practicing Christians 99/97/92; companion to evt-abs-2026-supernatural-belief-001 (the entity figures 68/64/55/53/51); Leonardo Blair, Christian Post, 2026-10-11: 'American belief Jesus was born of a virgin falls below 50%' — first sub-50% reading in a major national survey; Pew comparison 2017 (66%) / 2014 (73%) with different-question-wording caveat carried; Beliefnet/Christ News Today, ~2026-10-14: 'Belief in Jesus' Virgin Birth Hits New Low' — independent press restatement of the ABS Ch. 7 figures (supernatural-belief 62%, God 68%, angels 64%, demons 51%, Satan 55%, resurrection 53%, virgin birth 49%, Red Sea parting 44%) — venue-echo of already-chained sources, grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent); Wheatley Institute (BYU), reported 2026-10-12 (n=2,005 US adults, Qualtrics panel, self-weighted; survey report, not journal peer-reviewed): 50% say they experience or witness 'what they consider to be a miracle' several times a year (10% &gt;weekly, 9% weekly, 11% monthly, 20% a few times a year); 68% have had a spiritual experience, 46% within the past month; 59% felt God telling them something in the past month. Belief-prevalence data only — grades the belief, never the entity. Appended grade-neutral 2026-10-19; Bible Gateway write-up of ABS SOTB 2026 ch.7 (published ~2026-10-16, same n=2,649 survey, no new data): generational cut — Gen Z believes less in spiritual entities than Boomers/Gen X, but Gen Z 18–21 believes MORE than 22–29 and Millennials; education gradient — more education, less belief across the board; the 'good beings &gt; bad beings' asymmetry (God 68%, heaven 62%, angels 64% vs Satan 55%, hell 53%, demons 51%) restates the chained entity figures. Venue echo with one new-to-record cut (generations/education) — belief attestation, never content evidence
Falsifiability: A published erratum from ABS, or an independent representative survey contradicting the figures, would force a re-grade</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>ABS State of the Bible 2026: 44% of Americans believe the Red Sea parting</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The belief is attested by the survey's own release (16th annual, nationally weighted sample) via consistent secondary reporting (Christian Post, Beliefnet, Charisma, Christianity.com, Oct 10-13 2026); the record grades the attestation of the belief, never the fact.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-01-08 → 2026-01-27 (confidence: high)

In the American Bible Society's 16th annual State of the Bible report (fielded Jan 8-27, 2026, n=2,649 American adults), 44% of Americans said the biblical parting of the Red Sea was an actual historical event ('definitely' or 'probably happened'); among practicing Christians, 92% affirmed it.

Earliest attestation: American Bible Society, State of the Bible 16th annual report, chapter 7, released ~Oct 9, 2026 (n=2,649, Jan 8-27, 2026).
Sources: Christian Post, 'American belief Jesus was born of a virgin falls below 50%: study,' 2026-10-11 (44% Red Sea parting general; 92% practicing Christians; n=2,649 Jan 8-27 2026); Beliefnet, 'Belief in Jesus' Virgin Birth Hits New Low,' 2026-10-13 (44% Red Sea; 49% virgin birth; 53% resurrection — corroborating outlet); Christianity.com, 'Americans Still Believe in God, Miracles and the Resurrection of Jesus, New Survey Finds,' 2026-10 (44% Red Sea parting as actual historical event — corroborating outlet); Beliefnet, 'Belief in Jesus' Virgin Birth Hits New Low,' 2026-10-13 (corroborates the ABS figures: 44% parting of the Red Sea as actual historical event, n=2,649 Jan 8-27 2026; 68% believe in God; 92% of practicing Christians believe the Red Sea was parted); The Baptist Paper, 'Fewer than half of Americans believe Jesus' virgin birth was a supernatural miracle? Study says yes,' 2026-10-12 (44% parting of the Red Sea 'definitely or probably happened' — same ABS chapter-7 figures; grade-neutral corroboration); christianity.com (Michael Foust), 'Americans Still Believe in God, Miracles and the Resurrection of Jesus, New Survey Finds,' 2026-10-09 (44% parting of the Red Sea 'actual historical event' — same ABS release, third independent outlet)
Falsifiability: Release of the ABS cross-tabs contradicting the 44% figure; or ABS disowning the reported numbers.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>ABS 2026: the supernatural stays popular; the wording moves the number</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by a published survey series with methodology disclosed (n=2,649, January 2026 field), carried via the October 2026 write-up; the record grades the attestation of the belief figures, never the truth of any belief.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-01-08 → 2026-01-27 (confidence: high)

The American Bible Society's State of the Bible 2026 (16th annual; fielded Jan 8-27, 2026, n=2,649) found 68% of Americans believe in God, 20% are not sure, and 13% do not — alongside broad supernatural belief: 62% believe in heaven, 64% in angels, 55% in satan, 53% in hell, 51% in demons. A slight majority believe the resurrection of Jesus was an actual event in history. The report juxtaposes its 68% God figure with Pew's 2025 Religious Landscape finding of 83% believing in 'God or a universal spirit' — a 15-point gap carried by question wording, not by the population.

Earliest attestation: American Bible Society, State of the Bible 2026 (16th annual report); reported in christianity.com write-up, Oct 2026
Sources: American Bible Society, State of the Bible 2026 (16th annual, n=2,649, fielded Jan 8-27, 2026): 68% believe in God / 20% not sure / 13% do not; 62% heaven, 64% angels, 55% satan, 53% hell, 51% demons; slight majority affirm the resurrection as an actual historical event; Pew 2025 RLS 83% 'God or a universal spirit' cited for contrast; ABS State of the Bible 2026, Chapter 7: The Supernatural (press release, October 2026 — same figures: 55% believe in Satan, 53% hell, 51% demons; younger Gen Z (21 and under) report higher belief in God than older Gen Z (22-29); chief innovation officer Dr. John Farquhar Plake's 'sense of the supernatural' framing carried as the organization's interpretation, not a finding) — grade-neutral source appended 2026-10-17 (the Turin-shroud precedent), grade held
Falsifiability: Release of the ABS 2026 chapter or topline contradicting the reported figures, or the ABS retracting them.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 molecular-clock revival of pre-Ediacaran animal origins (Durbin et al.)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed paper in Science Advances (Oct 2026) with primary taphonomic data (&gt;140 microfossil samples, SEM) for the Weng'an-inference demolition — CORROBORATED as a published argument. The 800–700 Ma date is carried as a clock-model inference (the timeline's own rule: models are models), fenced explicitly; the authors' own caveat ('does not prove animals existed 800 million years ago') is quoted in the record.
Layer: real · Place: Kheseen Biota, Mongolia; Weng'an Biota, China
Dates: 2026-10-02 → 2026-10-02 (confidence: high)

The October 2026 Science Advances paper by Orin Lole Durbin, Ross Anderson, Derek Briggs and colleagues ('Re-evaluating molecular clock maximum age calibrations revives pre-Ediacaran divergence estimates for animals', doi:10.1126/sciadv.aeg6289) makes two claims: (1) the taphonomic claim — the Kheseen Biota of Mongolia (latest Ediacaran/early Cambrian, ~40 Myr younger than Weng'an) is exceptionally preserved with &gt;140 sampled microfossils, including exquisite acritarchs and embryo-like forms, yet contains no identifiable animals even though animals are documented from other sites of the same age; therefore the absence of animals in the equally well-preserved 590-Ma Weng'an Biota does NOT prove animals had not yet evolved — preservation, not biology, decides; (2) the clock claim — rerunning molecular-clock analyses with the older Tonian-age deposits (Svanbergfjellet, Norway; Bitter Springs, Australia; Chuar Group, Arizona — 850–730 Ma) as maximum-age calibrations instead of Weng'an shifts crown-animal origin estimates back ~200 Myr, to 800–700 Ma, overlapping or predating the Cryogenian Snowball Earth glaciations. The authors explicitly stress the analysis does not prove animals existed at 800 Ma; it destroys the maximum-age argument that said they couldn't.

Earliest attestation: Durbin, Anderson, Briggs et al., Science Advances 2026.
Sources: Durbin, Anderson, Briggs et al. 2026, Science Advances (doi:10.1126/sciadv.aeg6289) — 'Re-evaluating molecular clock maximum age calibrations revives pre-Ediacaran divergence estimates for animals'; SWNS Discover report (Oct 2026, discover.swns.com/2026/10/animals-may-have-evolved-200-million-years-earlier-than-fossil-evidence-suggests/) — the Kheseen-vs-Weng'an taphonomic argument and the 800–700 Ma clock range, with the authors' own caveats quoted; phys.org report (Oct 2026, phys.org/news/2026-10-animals-evolved-vastly-earlier-fossil.html) — the Snowball Earth overlap implication; First-pass review 2026-10-18 (via #535 Dr. Stian Viken — The Explosion Reader): CORROBORATED assigned — the paper graded as a peer-reviewed argument; the date fenced as model inference per the LUCA-reconstruction precedent
Falsifiability: The taphonomic claim falls if Kheseen turns out to be preservationally inferior to Weng'an in a way the authors missed, or if animals are later found in Weng'an after all; the clock estimate is a model output — it is revised, not falsified, by better calibrations.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>106.9,47.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Assisi 2026: the 40th-anniversary interreligious peace meeting (announced)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Contemporaneous wire reporting of a public announcement by named organizers with named participants, dates, and venue; the meeting itself is pending at record time.
Layer: real · Place: Assisi, Italy (Lower Basilica of St. Francis)
Dates: 2026-10-25 → 2026-10-27 (confidence: high)

For 25-27 October 2026, the Community of Sant'Egidio announced an interreligious peace meeting at Assisi, titled 'Disarmed and Disarming Peace', where Pope Leo XIV and Orthodox Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew were each to offer a meditation — the theme drawn from Pope Leo XIV's 21 July 2026 book 'Disarmed and Disarming: Peace Is a Gift' in a shared prayer for peace at the Lower Basilica of St. Francis on 27 October 2026; 384 representatives of religions, cultures and institutions from 53 countries were expected, with 24 forums including ones on Christian unity and contemporary martyrs.

Earliest attestation: OSV News reporting (Catholic Review), published 9 October 2026; Vatican press conference 6 October 2026
Sources: Catholic Review / OSV News, 'Pope Leo to deliver peace message in Assisi 40 years after St. John Paul II's landmark gathering', 9 Oct 2026; InfoVaticana, 7 Oct 2026, 'Asís will revive 40 years later the interreligious meeting of 1986 with the presence of Leo XIV' — Oct 6 Impagliazzo press conference: 155 speakers, 24 forums, ~5,000 expected participants; Bartholomew I attending; OSV News via The Catholic Sun, 8 Oct 2026 — theme 'Disarmed and Disarming Peace' from Pope Leo XIV's 21 July 2026 book 'Disarmed and Disarming: Peace Is a Gift' (Vatican Publishing House); forums include Christian unity and contemporary martyrs; Sant'Egidio program wording (quoted 16 Oct 2026; partisan sedevacantist venue fenced as venue-not-evidence): Opening Assembly afternoon of Sun 25 Oct at UmbriaFiere, Bastia Umbra; Tue 27 Oct closing in the Lower Square of the Basilica of St. Francis with Leo XIV and Bartholomew I — appended 18 Oct 2026 as grade-neutral schedule context, Turin-shroud precedent; orthodoxtimes.com via RISU, 15 Oct 2026: Bartholomew I departs for Rome 23 Oct (Angelicum invitation; Vatican meeting with Leo expected), then joint travel to Assisi; Bartholomew's letter of fraternal support to Leo ahead of Leo's 13 Oct 2026 Gemelli Hospital surgery — goodwill context, no canonical substance; occurrence-pending watch item — appended 18 Oct 2026, grade-neutral; OSV News / Catholic Review, 8-9 Oct 2026 (re-crawled 20 Oct 2026): confirmation stands — Leo XIV returns to Assisi Oct 27 for the closing day; he and Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew will each offer a meditation in the Lower Basilica of St. Francis during the Christian prayer for peace; scale: 384 representatives of religions, cultures, institutions from 53 countries, 155 speakers, 24 thematic forums, ~5,000 expected attendees; Marco Impagliazzo (Oct 6 Vatican press conference): 'a new and creative perspective for interreligious dialogue was born' — the 40th annual 'Spirit of Assisi' meeting. Occurrence still pending as of 20 Oct 2026; no cancellation reported.; CathNews — Pope Leo XIV cancels Assisi visit on doctors’ advice (Holy See Press Office announcement 23 Oct 2026; post-Oct-13 lung surgery, lower right lobe removed for adenocarcinoma; doctors advised against helicopter travel; Pope to send a written message; meeting proceeds with 384 leaders from 53 countries); pollar.news — Pope Leo XIV will skip Assisi peace event on 27 October on medical advice after lung surgery
Falsifiability: The record would be downgraded if the meeting did not occur as announced or the named participants did not take part; toward VERIFIED on contemporaneous reporting that it happened</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.6186,43.0743,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Secular rationalism and magic belief, 2026 (belief-ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed finding from a representative national panel, multiply reported (10bmnews Oct 2026); grades the belief-attestation (the published association), never the magic — the attestation rule applied
Layer: real · Place: United States (national survey)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

A peer-reviewed 2026 study (Social Forces, July 2026) analyzing the Baylor Religion Survey (n=1,812 American adults, Gallup fielded early 2025) found that among Americans who believe in God, those who embrace 'secular rationalism' (science-as-only-way + reason-based morality + interpret-spirituality-oneself) are about twice as likely to believe in magic (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead — six no-god-required items) as those who reject it: 40% vs 19%; among service-attenders 28% vs 19%; among non-attenders/non-believers, the link disappears. The record grades the BELIEF-ATTESTATION (the published finding), never the magic.

Earliest attestation: Social Forces, July 2026 (Baylor Religion Survey wave, n=1,812, Gallup early 2025) — the 'secular rationalism' study
Sources: 'Here's who is most likely to believe in magic and supernatural powers' (10bmnews.com, Oct 2026): Social Forces July 2026 study of the Baylor Religion Survey (n=1,812, Gallup fielded early 2025) — secular rationalists who believe in God twice as likely to believe in magic (40% vs 19%); 28% vs 19% among attenders; effect vanishes among non-attenders/non-believers
Falsifiability: A published re-analysis of the same wave finding no effect, or a peer-reviewed failure to replicate in the next Baylor wave, would force a re-grade</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baylor/Social Forces July 2026: science-trusting believers twice as likely toward magic belief</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A peer-reviewed journal article (Social Forces, July 2026) analyzing a nationally representative survey (Baylor Religion Survey VI, n=1,812, Gallup fieldwork) with figures reported consistently across four outlets (The Conversation/PopSci, Religion News Service, phys.org, 10bmnews). Graded as belief-attestation — it documents reported belief, the mission's belief-ledger datum, fenced to the reporting layer.
Layer: real · Place: United States — a national survey, not a place
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The July 2026 Social Forces study of the Baylor Religion Survey (wave VI, n=1,812 American adults, fielded by Gallup in early 2025) found that Americans who trust science and reason MORE than their churches' teachings — but still believe in God or attend services — are MORE likely to believe in magic (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead, six items requiring no god or church) than those who prioritize religious teachings. Among Americans who believe in God, those scoring high in 'secular rationalism' were about twice as likely to lean toward magic as those who rejected it (40% vs 19%); among church attenders, 28% vs 19%. Among non-attenders and non-believers, secular rationalism showed no link to magic belief. The finding refines the mission's belief ledger: the gap is not a simple science-vs-religion split — the combination can widen it.

Earliest attestation: Social Forces, July 2026 (study); The Conversation authors' essay, Oct 16, 2026 (public report).
Sources: The Conversation essay by the study's authors (syndicated): analysis of Baylor Religion Survey wave VI (n=1,812 American adults, Gallup fielded early 2025); 'secular rationalism' measured from four survey questions; six-item magic battery (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead, etc., requiring no god or church); among God-believers: 40% vs 19% magic-leaning (high vs low secular rationalism); among attenders: 28% vs 19%; no link among non-attenders/non-believers. Study published Social Forces, July 2026.; Religion News Service, Oct 16, 2026: same figures (40% vs 19%; 28% vs 19%); n=1,812; Baylor Religion Survey; Social Forces July 2026.; American Bible Society, State of the Bible 2026 ch. 7 (released April 2026, n=2,649, fielded Jan 8-27 2026): the ambient belief level this finding refines — 68% God / 64% angels / 51% demons; 53% resurrection / 49% virgin birth / 44% Red Sea parting.
Falsifiability: Retraction or major correction of the Social Forces article, or a demonstrated sampling/analysis defect that voids the published figures.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baylor 2026: the churchgoing science-trusters believe more in ghosts</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed journal finding attested via a detailed secondary report (journal, date, survey name, sample size, fielding house, wave date, the four-question secular-rationalism measure, the six magic items, the two miracle items). The record grades the attestation of the survey finding, never the truth of ghosts, curses, miracles, or God.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national survey)
Dates: 2026-07-01 → 2026-10-16 (confidence: medium)

A peer-reviewed study published in the journal Social Forces (July 2026), analyzing the latest wave of the Baylor Religion Survey (n=1,812 American adults, fielded by Gallup in early 2025), found that respondents who combine 'secular rationalism' — trust in science and reason over their church's teachings, including the belief that people should interpret spiritual matters on their own rather than trust religious authorities — with belief in God or church attendance are MORE likely than traditionally church-prioritizing believers to believe in six 'magic' phenomena (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead, etc.). The comparison group believed in miracles (whether miracles still happen today, whether God is the source of all miracles); the paradox held for the church/science rationalists specifically on the six magic items.

Earliest attestation: Social Forces (July 2026), Baylor Religion Survey 2025 wave, n=1,812, Gallup fielded — reported via PopSci October 16, 2026
Sources: PopSci, 'Americans who go to church and trust science are also more likely to believe in ghosts and curses,' Oct 16, 2026 (originally The Conversation): Social Forces July 2026, Baylor Religion Survey 1,812 adults fielded by Gallup early 2025; 'secular rationalism' four-question measure; six magic items (ghosts, curses, talking to the dead); two miracle items; churchgoing science-trusters outscored church-teaching-prioritizers on the magic items
Falsifiability: The Social Forces article not containing the reported finding, a correction/retraction, or a failed replication on a newer Baylor wave.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Behbeit El-Hagar: the October 2026 Ramesses II statues</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The find is multiply reported from the ministry announcement via the Egyptian Gazette, HeritageDaily, and international press; the Ramesses II identification of the larger statue is the excavators' reading, the second statue's provisional — what is corroborated is the discovery, the identifications graded as stated.
Layer: real · Place: Behbeit El-Hagar, Gharbia Governorate, Nile Delta, Egypt
Dates: 2026-10-13 → 2026-10-14 (confidence: high)

On 13 October 2026 the Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announced that a farmer tilling land in front of his home at Behbeit El-Hagar (Gharbia Governorate, Nile Delta) uncovered a carved stone block and reported it; a Supreme Council of Antiquities excavation recovered the remains of two large royal statues, the larger identified as Ramesses II — a head over 5 ft tall wearing the double crown, from which the team estimated a full statue of ~27-30 ft including the base. A second, smaller (~8 ft) royal statue is provisionally attributed to the same ruler. Excavation continues.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, 13 Oct 2026 (reported via Egyptian Gazette).
Sources: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, announcement, 13 Oct 2026 — reported via the Egyptian Gazette: farmer's discovery at Behbeit El-Hagar, two large royal statues; New York Post, 'Massive 28-foot statue of biblical pharaoh who enslaved Israelites unearthed — by accident,' 2026-10-14 — 5-ft head with double crown, ~27-30 ft estimate, second ~8-ft statue; Heavenly Crafted case notes, 'Ramses II Statue Found: Was He the Pharaoh of Exodus?,' 2026-10-15 — sources carried (ministry Arabic announcement; Archaeology Magazine Oct 14; HeritageDaily Oct 13); the Exodus identification named as a separate evidentiary question; Ahram Online, 'Ramesses II statue fragments uncovered at Behbeit El-Hagar,' 2026-10-14 — SCA rescue excavation after farmer Khaled al-Shahat struck a carved block in his field; identification as Ramesses II via the Horus name 'Hor Ka Nakht Mery Maat' on the back pillar; pink-granite block with 'Nesu Beti'/'Sa Ra' titles; CBS News, 2026-10-15 — consistent details (1.6 m head, second ~2.4 m royal statue, pink-granite inscribed block); the 'biblical pharaoh' framing carried as media overlay, absent from the SCA statement
Falsifiability: Publication of the excavation's epigraphic analysis contradicting the Ramesses II attribution would revise the identification; the find itself is established.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.05,30.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Bianfu Cave: Denisovans in southwest China (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed Nature papers with full stratigraphic and dating documentation; the species identification rests on protein phylogeny (ancient DNA does not preserve in the warm climate) — which is why the grade stops at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED. The five-specimen sample and the 1,300-tool association are honestly bounded in the record.
Layer: real · Place: Bianfu Cave (Bat Cave), Heqing County, Yunnan, China
Dates: 2026-09-09 → 2026-09-09 (confidence: high)

Two Nature papers (9 September 2026; Fu Qiaomei team, IVPP, Chinese Academy of Sciences) report Denisovan fossils at Bianfu Cave (Bat Cave), Heqing County, Yunnan, China — 2,370 m altitude, southeastern edge of the Qinghai-Xizang Plateau: five specimens — two parietal skull fragments, one proximal radius (the first Denisovan forearm bone ever identified), two teeth — from more than 60,000 bone fragments, dated ~167,000-134,000 years ago (MIS 6), identified via morphological pre-screening plus ZooMS palaeoproteomics and peptide phylogeny; found alongside more than 1,300 stone and bone tools in a ~190,000-70,000-year cave sequence — one of the longest Denisovan cultural records known.

Earliest attestation: Two Nature papers, 9 September 2026 (doi:10.1038/s41586-026-10997-4 and the companion archaeology paper) — the primary publications
Sources: Two Nature papers, 9 September 2026 — the Bianfu Cave Denisovan fossils (doi:10.1038/s41586-026-10997-4): five specimens from &gt;60,000 bone fragments, ZooMS palaeoproteomic identification, ~167-134 ka, first Denisovan forearm bone; Smithsonian Magazine, 14 Sept 2026 (Margherita Bassi) — 'Scientists Find a Trove of Denisovan Fossils and Tools in Southwest China'; seven Denisovan fossils and &gt;1,300 stone/bone tools across two papers; the site's tens of thousands of years of occupation; National Geographic, 9 Sept 2026 (Tim Vernimmen) — the trove of bones, teeth, and &gt;1,300 stone tools; the first-known Denisovan radius; the Fu Qiaomei team's proteomic strategy for warm-climate sites; phys.org, 9 Sept 2026 — 'First Denisovan forearm bone discovered in southwest China'; fills the SW-China distribution gap; proteomic strategy for identifying hominin bones without ancient DNA; phys.org, 10 Sept 2026 (Kassi Klower) — the dating program: luminescence of buried sand grains + uranium decay in speleothems and tooth enamel; the 190-70 ka cave chronology; Bo Li's dating leadership
Falsifiability: Recovery of ancient DNA from the specimens contradicting the proteomic ID; a re-dating that moves the fossil-bearing layers outside MIS 6; a peptide-phylogeny re-analysis placing the specimens outside the Denisovan clade</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>100.18,26.56,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>BiSaME 2026 — the Sanaa collection's first systematic biography</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The funding, institutions, personnel, budget, and scope are published by the awarding bodies — a documentary fact of the 2026 evidence state.
Layer: real · Place: Hamburg / Berlin (research); Sanaa, Yemen (the collection)
Dates: 2026 → 2029 (confidence: high)

In 2026 the DFG funded BiSaME (University of Hamburg + Staatsbibliothek zu Berlin, led by Alba Fedeli and Thomas Eich): a first phase 2026–2029 (€934,301) using computer vision, AI, paleography, and philology to reconstruct the biography of the Great Mosque of Sanaa manuscript collection as an ensemble.

Earliest attestation: University of Hamburg press release PM36/2026 announcing the DFG award
Sources: University of Hamburg press release PM36/2026 — 'Oldest Quran manuscripts to be analyzed for the first time' (DFG-funded BiSaME, Fedeli &amp; Eich, €934,301, 2026–2029); DFG project record: 'From the Particular View to the Overall Picture' — computer vision, AI, paleography, philology on the Sanaa collection; idw-online (DFG wire release, 2026-09-29): the 12-year BiSaME project — 929 manuscripts, ~24,000 microfilm images + 5,000 color slides (shot 1990s, acquired 2019, now digitized) to be analyzed as an ensemble with AI computer vision + paleography + philology, University of Hamburg (Prof. Thomas Eich) + Staatsbibliothek zu Berlin (Dr. Alba Fedeli). First phase 2026–2029, EUR 934,301. Project announcement — no findings yet; watch item for 2027+.
Falsifiability: Retraction of the DFG award or of the press announcement — the claim is about the project's existence and scope, not its future findings</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>9.99,53.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'By the Rivers of Babylon' al-Yahudu exhibit (Bible Lands Museum, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The exhibition is a documented 2026 public event with independent pre-opening press; the tablets' documentary content is independently published (CUSAS 28, 2014; BaAr 6, 2022); the provenance caveat caps but does not undo the grade.
Layer: real · Place: Bible Lands Museum, Jerusalem (21 Stefan Wise Street, Museum Row)
Dates: 2026-10-18 → 2026-10-18 (confidence: high)

The Bible Lands Museum, Jerusalem, opened 'By the Rivers of Babylon' on 18 October 2026 — the first public display of ~100 al-Yahudu cuneiform tablets from the private David (and Cindy) Sofer collection, documenting Judean exiles' daily lives in 6th–5th c. BCE Babylonia (leases, taxes, debts, trade, marriages, inheritances); curator Dr. Filip Vukosavovic; two years in preparation; announced to run at least one year. Pre-opening coverage (Times of Israel / Evangelical Focus, 16–17 Oct 2026): the Samak-Yama family traced over four generations (father Samak-Yama → son → grandson → five children; inheritance to five great-grandchildren), Judean names including Natanyahu, a handful of Aramaic epigraphs, an animated model of a Mesopotamian village as centerpiece.

Earliest attestation: Times of Israel pre-opening coverage by Ilan Ben-Zion (published ~16 Oct 2026); Bible Lands Museum Jerusalem announcements
Sources: Ilan Ben-Zion, "'By the rivers of Babylon' exhibit breathes life into Judean exile", Times of Israel (pre-opening, ~16 Oct 2026): ~100 tablets, David Sofer's private collection, first public display, curator Dr. Filip Vukosavovic, two years of labor, opens Sunday 18 Oct 2026, runs at least one year; Evangelical Focus / Times of Israel pre-opening detail: the Samak-Yama four-generation family tree on the tablets; Judean theophoric names incl. Natanyahu; handful of Aramaic inscriptions; animated Mesopotamian-village centerpiece; PROVENANCE CAVEAT: unprovenanced — entered the antiquities market by unknown means (possibly looting); held in the private David and Cindy Sofer Collection; no excavated context. The exhibit publicizes the evidence; it does not add findspot data.; Post-opening coverage (Evangelical Focus, 22 Oct 2026 — venue-echo of the recorded pre-opening pieces): the exhibit opened to the public on Sunday 18 Oct 2026 as announced — the opening claim confirmed, not a new evidence item (Turin-shroud precedent).; Press-telling fence: curator press claims carried as interpretation, not evidence — "not slaves" (consistent with the scholarly state-dependent-tenant framing, Alstola), "1,500 deported to Lebanon/Syria in 587 BCE" (press figure, ungrounded), "80,000 Jews chose to stay" (press figure, ungrounded), "Nebuchadnezzar needed the Judeans to revive the struggling Babylonian economy" (curator gloss, unattested motive).
Falsifiability: Museum retraction of the opening, or demonstration that the displayed tablets are modern forgeries, would overturn the record</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2034,31.7726,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Fire over ice: the first planetesimals were chondrule-rich from the onset (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed in Nature Astronomy (DOI 10.1038/s41550-026-02976-6); two independent chemical tracers converge on the same matrix-poor composition; the authors state the reconstruction's indirectness openly. The composition finding is the claim; the aerodynamic-sorting mechanism is the team's proposed explanation.
Layer: real · Place: Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut (the study's home institution)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The first generation of solid bodies in the outer solar system was composed 83–92% of chondrules (heat-forged rock beads) rather than icy dust and fine matrix, within the solar system's first million years: Grewal, Zhang &amp; Drążkowska 2026 reconstructed the original composition of melted outer-solar-system iron-meteorite parent bodies from two independent chemical tracers — sulfur concentration (concentrated in matrix) and iron oxidation state (revealing water-ice and oxidized dust) — finding matrix levels of only 8–17%, lower than in any known chondrite, showing aerodynamic sorting operated from the onset of formation.

Earliest attestation: Grewal, Zhang &amp; Drążkowska, Nature Astronomy (18 September 2026)
Sources: Damanveer S. Grewal, Zhongtian Zhang &amp; Joanna Drążkowska, 'Planetesimal compositions governed by aerodynamic sorting from the onset of Solar System formation', Nature Astronomy (18 September 2026), DOI: 10.1038/s41550-026-02976-6 — first-generation outer-solar-system planetesimals 83–92% chondrules, matrix 8–17%, from sulfur + Fe-oxidation-state tracers in iron meteorites; Yale University news (18 Sep 2026) — 'From the start, the solar system chose fire over ice to build its first bodies' — the two-tracer method, the 8–17% matrix figure, the melting of the parent bodies via ²⁶Al, the missing-ancient-chondrules explanation; phys.org (18 Sep 2026, re-crawled Oct 2026) — 'From the start, the solar system chose fire over ice to build its first bodies' — Grewal's 'both tracers independently tell the same story' convergence statement; sorting pushed back from 2–4 Myr to the first million years; astrobiology.com (1 Oct 2026) — 'Our Solar System Chose Fire Over Ice To Build Its First Bodies' — the indirectness stated openly: no preserved first-Myr undifferentiated bodies survive; iron-meteorite tracers as the route in; Eighth-pass I.5 review 2026-10-20 (via #728 Dr. Sander Holm — The Condensation Reader): NEW CORROBORATED exhibit — the chondrule-vs-matrix sorting pushed to the first Myr; grade-neutral on the t=0 clock (no CAI age revision, the mechanism arm not the age arm); pairs naturally with the new specialist's condensation beat (the chemical fingerprint the sorting obeys); Levison, Deienno, Walsh, Johnson, Connolly, Wakita &amp; Grimm 2026, Science Advances 12(28), 8 Jul 2026 (open access) — "Chondrite Parent Bodies as Escaped Satellites of Proto-Planetary Embryos": a NEW competing chondrule-formation mechanism — embryo–embryo collisions in the 2–10 Myr terrestrial-planet-formation epoch generate melt sheets; impact ejecta bound to the surviving embryo settles into a circum-embryo disk, where droplets cool at 10–1,000 K/hour (the chondrule texture window) and accrete into asteroid-sized satellites later ejected onto heliocentric orbits; the same mechanism is presented as a parallel to Theia/Moon. Appended grade-neutral to the competing-hypotheses lane: it is a peer-reviewed MODEL of how chondrules form and concentrate, not an observation — it does not touch the Grewal et al. composition measurement or the 4567.3–4568.7 Myr t=0 window; it addresses the mechanism arm only. Turin-shroud precedent.
Falsifiability: A preserved undifferentiated first-Myr body (or a re-analysis showing the sulfur/Fe-oxidation tracers are model-dependent) with high matrix content would revise the finding; the authors' own convergence-of-two-tracers argument is the stated robustness</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.9223,41.3163,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>CORT intermission pattern, 2026 (UVA DOPS)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — One peer-reviewed 2026 analysis (DOI on the chain) plus the university's own announcements attesting the finding, with the authors' no-metaphysics fence quoted in the record itself; single-archive observational study, awaiting independent replication — CORROBORATED, not higher.
Layer: real · Place: Charlottesville, Virginia (University of Virginia, Division of Perceptual Studies)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In 1,678 vetted Cases of the Reincarnation Type (children whose statements were matched to a named deceased person, with corroborating interviews), the interval between the previous person's death and the child's birth — the 'intermission' — was systematically shorter when the death was unexpected: median 12 months after completely unexpected deaths versus 21 months after the most expected ones (overall median 16 months), a 40-45% drop across the unexpectedness range. The claim grades the REPORTED PATTERN in the case archive, not its cause; it asserts nothing about whether reincarnation occurs.

Earliest attestation: Sadeghi et al. 2026, University of Virginia Division of Perceptual Studies (DOPS) — 1,678 vetted CORT cases
Sources: Sadeghi, S. et al. 2026, DOI 10.1037/cns0000493 (APA PsycNet) — 1,678 vetted CORT cases; median intermission 16 months; 12 months after completely unexpected deaths vs 21 months after the most expected; association persists across bias controls and is stronger in families whose religions do not endorse reincarnation; authors fence from any metaphysical conclusion; UVA Research news, October 18, 2026 — 'Surprising Pattern Emerges in Reincarnation Reports' (Sadeghi; Cozzolino; Pehlivanova quotes; 'further study is warranted', 'not to say the researchers have concluded that reincarnation is real'); UVA Health / School of Medicine announcement, October 19, 2026 — 1,678 cases; unexpected deaths linked to shorter gaps; university's explicit no-conclusion fence
Falsifiability: An independent re-analysis of the DOPS archive finding no unexpectedness-intermission association after the same controls, or a prospective study showing the association vanishes under pre-registered case ascertainment, would falsify the pattern claim. The record makes no entity claim, so no entity finding can falsify it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-78.51,38.03,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DESI completes its five-year survey (April 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The survey's completion statistics are the collaboration's own instrument-verified output, multiply reported (DESI.lbl.gov, NOIRLab, Gizmodo 2026 Science Fair winner); graded below VERIFIED only in that the numbers are operational tallies awaiting final catalog publication.
Layer: real · Place: Mayall 4-m Telescope, Kitt Peak National Observatory, Arizona
Dates: 2026-04-15 → 2026-04-15 (confidence: high)

DESI concluded its original five-year survey on schedule in April 2026 — the largest three-dimensional map of the universe ever constructed: 47+ million galaxies and quasars, 20 million Milky Way stars, over 11 billion years of cosmic history (six times the data of all previous measurements combined), from the 4-m Mayall telescope (Kitt Peak) with 5,000 robotic fiber positioners.

Earliest attestation: DESI Collaboration / NSF NOIRLab milestone reporting (April 2026)
Sources: DESI Collaboration / NSF NOIRLab: five-year survey milestone reporting (April 2026) — 47+ million galaxies and quasars, 20 million stars, 11 billion years; six times all previous measurements; Gizmodo Science Fair 2026 write-up: DESI winner — largest high-resolution 3D map; Will Percival (Waterloo) on building history-of-the-universe slices; DESI YouTube milestone short (2026): five-year survey complete, dark-energy analysis of full dataset expected 2027, observations continuing through 2028
Falsifiability: Not falsifiable as a claim about the survey's own output; the cosmological results to come are testable against ΛCDM</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-111.5967,31.9583,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Durupınar Formation drilling (Noah's Ark search)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The 2026 drilling is a real, reported event — graded as an event, not as evidence for the Ark. The outcome is pending at record time, and the identification bar is stated in advance (Magness): an ancient vessel alone proves nothing without a contemporary inscription. The dig is CORROBORATED; the Ark association is unfalsifiable on current evidence and ungraded.
Layer: real · Place: Durupınar Formation, Doğubayazıt, Turkey
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In 2026, researchers began drilling at the Durupınar Formation — the boat-shaped geological feature about 20 miles south of Mount Ararat in eastern Turkey — collecting hundreds of core samples for laboratory analysis, in the ongoing search for traces of Noah's Ark.

Earliest attestation: HISTORY.com 'Was the Story of Noah's Ark Based on a Real Disaster?' (updated ~Oct 2026) — 'In 2026, researchers began drilling at the Durupınar Formation...'
Sources: HISTORY.com, 'Was the Story of Noah's Ark Based on a Real Disaster?' (updated ~Oct 2026) — the 2026 drilling report and Jodi Magness's association-bar quote; Magness on the pre-biblical flood stories: 'What we have in Genesis is just one version of this flood story that had been circulating widely for a long time' (same piece); Noah's Ark Scans official press release, 1 Oct 2026 (via GlobeNewswire) — 'It's Not Geological': structural layers, right-angle intersections, three levels beneath the surface; a preliminary report, unreviewed; abovethenormnews.com, 12 Oct 2026 — fieldwork wrap-up: 18 m borehole, 366 core/soil samples delivered to Ankara Yildirim Beyazit University (carbon-isotope, age determination), GPR to ~70 m, GOPHER robot to ~12 m, drill bit broken at a 4-5 m hard layer; lab results expected winter 2026-27; cupure.com, ~Sep 2026 — Sivas Cumhuriyet University data: ~40% more carbon inside the formation's outline (cause disputed), undated pottery fragments; researchers caution that none of this, on its own, confirms an archaeological structure; answeredfaith.com, Oct 2026 — Andrew Snelling (Answers in Genesis): location and geology rule the site out as Noah's Ark; Collins &amp; Fasold 1996 doubly-plunging-syncline reading unrefuted; Jones himself: 'too early to claim he has found evidence that the ark itself is buried there'; thecreationist.org, Oct 2026 — balanced ledger of the claims: radar/layering preliminary, angular features an unreviewed interpretation, pottery undated; 'promising claims, not yet tested'; cnirbc.com, Oct 18 2026 — the project's own Turkish research website calls the data 'suggestive, not conclusive' and states 'the research project is not claiming proof that the formation is Noah's Ark'; the broken drill bit is 'a question, not an answer'; novus2.com, Oct 8 2026 — 'When the drill does not find what the radar "saw," the problem lies with the interpretation of the radar': by the team's own report 'We were hitting mainly soil'; Israel Hayom: 'None of the findings have been published yet in peer-reviewed research, only in press statements'; Jerusalem Post: findings 'preliminary'; cupure.com, 19 Oct 2026 — the 2026 field season completed (June 28 – Sept 11 field phase, core drilling into late Sept); final tally 366 core and soil samples from 1–15 m depth, now at Ankara Yıldırım Beyazıt University for carbon-isotope/organic analysis; initial lab results expected winter, fuller scientific report planned spring 2027 — the work graded as work, the outcome still pending; njradaronline.com, ~late Sep 2026 — samples from the 2026 season 'not yet been identified as ancient wood'; lab analysis at Ankara Yildirim Beyazit University continues into 2027; 'no scientific basis for claiming that ancient ship timber has been recovered' — the investigators' own caution, appended grade-neutral per the Turin-shroud precedent (season close-out state, 2026-10-20 pass); NY Post, 'Noah's Ark researchers discover new evidence...' (3 Oct 2026) — Andrew Jones (Noah's Ark Scans): 12+ drill locations, 'did not find bedrock at all... hitting mainly soil'; 'rich black soil' for organic testing winter 2026-27 at Ankara (Sivas Cumhuriyet) University; the team's own report, not peer-reviewed — chained as the project's claim, not a finding; 'inching towards confirmation' is aspiration, not result.; Sarah Eaton, 'The Durupinar Site: What the 2026 Noah's Ark Investigation Is Actually Testing,' Living Passages (5 Aug 2026, mod. 23 Sep 2026): 'Nothing recovered at the site in 2026 amounts to a discovery of Noah's Ark, and the research team working there has not claimed one'; competing geological readings (Collins &amp; Fasold 1996 doubly-plunging syncline) carried as the standing counter-reading.; NewsNation, Unreported with Meagan Medick, 5 Oct 2026 — live from the site with Andrew Jones (Noah's Ark Scans) and Lauren Witzke: core samples sent for animal DNA testing; deep radar presented as 'man-made and non-geological'; described as the first permitted study under Turkish oversight (Prof. Dr. Cenker Atila, Sivas Cumhuriyet University); NewLife Magazine, ~6 Oct 2026 (citing Newsweek 28 Sep + Premier Christian News 29 Sep 2026) — Jones: no material identified as ancient wood or dated; organic layers a 'hypothesis under test'; drill bit broke at 4-5 m on an unidentified hard layer; initial lab results expected winter 2026-27, fuller report spring 2027; anatolianarchaeology.net, ~Sep 2026 — pottery fragments reported at the Durupinar Formation during the 2026 season; age not established; Ağrı governor's office via Turkish press, 2 Oct 2026 — Governor Bozkurt: 'no scientific certainty yet' from the 2026 drilling — the civil authority hosting the project fences the claims ahead of the Ankara lab analysis (expected to run into mid-2027); grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent.; Andrew Jones (Noah's Ark Scans) to DHA, 30 Sep 2026 — the project's own lead says the '100 percent confirmed' headlines are ahead of the lab work: samples in transit to Ankara Yıldırım Beyazıt University, nothing identified as ancient wood or dated; the team's own principal undercuts the telling, grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent.; cnirbc.com 'The Evidence Room — Investigation #003' (crawled Oct 2026) — reports the 2026 radar/deep-scan claims honestly: angular structures and a central corridor detected, but 'remains preliminary and has not yet been established through peer-reviewed archaeological publication; radar can reveal anomalies... it cannot by itself tell researchers, this is Noah\u2019s Ark.' Consistent with the CORROBORATED-as-event grade; fence-strengthening; grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent).; storiesbybabalo.blogspot.com, 8 Oct 2026 — 'Has Noah\u2019s Ark Been Found?' summary: straight lines/90-degree angles and 40%-higher carbon reported, but 'what they did NOT find: No ancient wood. No boat planks. Lab results are only expected in early 2027.' Honest venue, consistent with the held grades; grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent).; livingpassages.com 'Was Noah’s Ark Found at Durupinar? What the 2026 Scans Test' (crawled Oct 2026) — honest framing: the 2026 drilling/permits are the first real inside test of the seventy-year-old claim; scans are not an identification and permits are not a finding; 'worth testing' is not proven. Grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent; appended by the IV.26 seventh pass (ep920, #919).; Eric Vanden Eykel (Ferrum College), The Conversation, 7 Oct 2026 — the formation is a natural rock formation; 2026 social-media claims ('100% not geological') outran the researchers' official statements; official statements say lab analysis still underway — strengthens the competing-hypotheses lane and the telling's refutation; grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent.; abovethenormnews.com, 12 Oct 2026 update — Season 2 announced (adjacent archaeological site to be investigated), Bakhtar radar depth ~95 m (no clear bedrock even at that depth; strategy shifted to more shallower holes), 366 soil/rock samples confirmed; project statement, grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent.; Twelfth-pass review 2026-10-22 (VIII.b, ep939, #939): the Oct-2026 press cycle is venue-echoes of the already-chained field phase (NYPost Oct-3 Jones 'confirmation this winter' forecast; NewsNation Unreported Oct-5; imreal.life restatements; the cnirbc 'Evidence Room' confessional recap). cupure.com ~19-Oct-2026 balanced recap appended as the honest-limit witness: no worked timber, tools, or unambiguous vessel artifacts recovered so far; no laboratory results published as of 19 Oct 2026; Vanden Eykel (The Conversation, 6 Oct 2026) — natural formation, 2026 social-media claims outran the researchers' official statements; lab results expected early 2027. Grade-neutral, Turin-shroud precedent.; abovethenormnews.com, 23 Oct 2026 update — Prof. Dr. Cenker Atila formally signed off on Bakhtar's preliminary radar report, entering it into the official 2026 study record; samples now at several universities including Bar-Ilan; final report expected spring 2027; 2027 field season planned; novus2.com, 8 Oct 2026 ('Laying the Ark to Rest') — the skeptical ledger: no findings published in peer-reviewed research, only press statements (Israel Hayom); the drill that should have confirmed the radar did not ('hitting mainly soil'); the 4-5 m hard layer unidentified — advocacy venue, fenced as commentary not evidence; livingpassages.com 2026 recap (crawled Oct 2026) — the balanced honest-limit witness: 'No. Nothing recovered at the site in 2026 amounts to a discovery of Noah's Ark, and the research team working there has not claimed one... Scans are not an identification, and permits are not a finding.' Durupinar protected national park since 1987; 1988 10-m drilling settled nothing. Fenced venue-not-evidence, appended grade-neutral by the IV.26 ninth pass (ep975).; cupure.com, ~18 Oct 2026 ('Inside Turkey\u2019s 2026 Search for Noah\u2019s Ark at the Durupinar Site') — no worked timber, tools or other unambiguous artifacts tied to a vessel recovered so far; no laboratory results published as of 19 Oct 2026; Eric Vanden Eykel (The Conversation, 6 Oct 2026): the site is a natural formation and 2026 social-media claims outran the researchers' official statements — advocacy-adjacent venue, the negatives stated plainly, fenced grade-neutral; cnirbc.com Evidence Room Investigation #003 (page updated ~21 Oct 2026) — the formation's present width does not precisely equal 50 royal cubits, and researchers have not demonstrated a surviving height precisely corresponding to the biblical 30 cubits; radar 'cannot by itself tell researchers, This is Noah\u2019s Ark' — the dimension non-match carried as honest-limit context, grade-neutral
Falsifiability: The drilling is a documented 2026 event (reported in mainstream press). What it proves is fenced: no results are published, and even ancient wood at the site would not associate it with Noah — Jodi Magness (UNC Chapel Hill): there would be 'no way of associating it with Noah,' since we don't know if he existed or when he lived. Definitive proof would require a contemporary inscription — 'This is Noah's Ark.'</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.28,39.44,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pottery fragments reported at the Durupinar Formation (2026 season)</name><description>Grade: CLAIMED — The find is reported by a single outlet citing the season team; the sherds are undated and unidentified — attested as a report, not as evidence for the Ark.
Layer: real · Place: Durupinar Formation, Dogubayazit, Turkey
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

During the 2026 research season at the Durupinar Formation, pottery fragments were reported found at or near the site. Their age has not been established; suggestions that they could predate 'the flood' are interpretation, not dating.

Earliest attestation: anatolianarchaeology.net report, ~September 2026, citing the 2026 season team
Sources: anatolianarchaeology.net, ~Sep 2026 — 'Pottery Found at Turkiye's Mysterious Noah's Ark Formation': fragments reported; researchers suggested pre-flood-tradition date but age not established; fieldwork complete, samples awaiting lab analysis; newday.org.uk, 2026 — summary ledger of the 2026 season: pottery fragments and elevated carbon listed among finds; no exposed structure, no confirmed ancient wood, no tools/fittings, no verified archaeological context linking the site to Noah
Falsifiability: Published laboratory dating and contextual analysis of the sherds; if the lab reports never materialize or date them late, the pre-flood reading collapses</description><styleUrl>#claimed</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>44.28,39.44,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CLAIMED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.5</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>End-of-life dreams and visions (ELDVs), 2026</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — One peer-reviewed 2026 study (Death Studies, DOI on the chain) plus independent press coverage (Medium, medjouel) attesting the finding; the phenomenon is reported as reports, clinically fenced from delirium. Single study, awaiting independent replication — CORROBORATED, not higher (the bhitardari-chert precedent).
Layer: real · Place: Bologna, Italy (study setting; Rabitti et al.)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

End-of-life dreams and visions (ELDVs) — visions of deceased loved ones, threshold imagery, reunions — are a documented, frequently reported phenomenon in palliative care, described in a peer-reviewed 2026 survey of 239 palliative-care professionals (Rabitti et al., Death Studies 2026): frequent, often disclosed spontaneously, and clinically distinguished from delirium. The claim grades the REPORTED PHENOMENON, not its cause; it asserts nothing about what, if anything, the visions are encounters with.

Earliest attestation: Rabitti et al. 2026, Death Studies (239 palliative-care professionals surveyed)
Sources: Rabitti, E. et al. 2026, Death Studies, DOI 10.1080/07481187.2026.2646873 — 239 palliative-care professionals; ELDVs (deceased-loved-one reunions, threshold imagery) frequent, disclosed spontaneously, distinct from delirium; Medium (Oct 17, 2026) coverage of the Rabitti ELDV study; medjouel.com coverage of the Rabitti ELDV study (Oct 2026); Lerner, F., dos Santos, P.C.R. &amp; Tahar, G. 2026, Frontiers in Psychology 17:1760389, doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1760389 — "The perceptual geometry and chromaticity of near-death experience spaces" (received Dec 2025, revised Sep 2026, accepted Sep 23 2026, published Oct 15 2026): 50 participants, 112 reconstructed experiential parts; recurrent visual-field boundary configurations (conical forms, elliptic arcs, ellipsoidal spaces); maps what experiencers REPORT — the authors claim no finding about what happens after death; phenomenon-attestation context, entity unadjudicated — grade-neutral per the Turin-shroud precedent; unilad.com Oct 20, 2026 coverage of Dr. Christopher Kerr (hospice physician, ~1,500 patient interviews): ~90% of dying patients report vivid dreams/visions, overwhelmingly of deceased loved ones — venue echo of Kerr's long-standing ELDV work (Death is But A Dream), not a new finding; phenomenon-attestation, mechanism unadjudicated — grade-neutral
Falsifiability: A larger, multi-site prospective study finding no such visions reported in palliative settings, or demonstration that the 2026 survey's instrument confounded ELDVs with delirium, would falsify the phenomenon claim. The record makes no entity claim, so no entity finding can falsify it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.34,44.49,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Estonia 2026: the ECHR appeal against the church law</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Named claimants, named court, dated filing, reported by the national public broadcaster and a detailed legal analysis; the ECHR's admissibility decision and the ministry's response are not yet on record.
Layer: real · Place: Tallinn, Estonia (filing in Strasbourg, France)
Dates: 2026-10-08 → 2026-10-08 (confidence: high)

On 8 October 2026, the Estonian Christian Orthodox Church (EKOK, formerly the Estonian Orthodox Church of the Moscow Patriarchate) and the Pühtitsa Convent at Kuremäe lodged an application with the European Court of Human Rights challenging Estonia's amended Churches and Congregations Act, which requires them to sever ties with the Moscow Patriarchate by 28 December 2026.

Earliest attestation: ERR News reporting, October 2026; church announcement 8 Oct 2026
Sources: RISU (via ERR News), 'Estonian Christian Orthodox Church appeals to European Court, opposing break with Moscow', Oct 2026; silerenonpossum.com, legal analysis, 14 Oct 2026 — ECHR lodging; Churches and Congregations Act s.3(2^1); 8 June 2026 Supreme Court ruling (11-6); attorneys Artur Knjazev, Steven-Hristo Evestus; Bishop Daniel; Bytes Europe (via ERR), 'Estonian Orthodox Church and Pühtitsa Monastery appeal to ECHR over ties to Moscow', Oct 2026; Anti-confusion fence (18 Oct 2026 pass): the OrthoChristian July-2025 ECHR item (https://orthochristian.com/171325.html) concerns a SEPARATE earlier complaint against the May 2024 Riigikogu political statement, not the 8 Oct 2026 application against the amended Act — keep the two applications separate in future passes; OrthoChristian is pro-Moscow advocacy, its characterizations fenced; silerenonpossum.com, 'La disputa tra la Chiesa ortodossa estone e il governo è arrivata a Strasburgo', updated mid-Oct 2026 — STATUS UPDATE: the ECHR has accepted the 8 Oct 2026 complaints for consideration, finding they raise significant religious-freedom questions warranting urgent review; case classified as of potential serious public importance; Estonia asked to respond; settlement possibilities explored. SINGLE-SOURCE FENCE (2026-10-19 seventh pass): no ECHR press release or ERR confirmation of the acceptance/urgency classification was found this pass — treat as one venue's report, not as court-document fact. Grade unaffected (CORROBORATED held).; OrthoChristian, 9 Oct 2026, 'Estonian Church and Pükhtitsa Monastery take anti-Church law to European Court of Human Rights' (https://orthochristian.com/180724.html) — the lawyers' announcement framing of the 8 Oct 2026 lodging; President Alar Karis's two refusals to promulgate (Sep 2025) noted. ADVOCACY FENCE per 18 Oct 2026 pass: OrthoChristian is pro-Moscow advocacy — events cited, characterizations not.; OrthoChristian, 9 Oct 2026 (Tallinn): confirms the 8 Oct 2026 ECHR application — the Estonian Christian Orthodox Church + Pühtitsa Convent ask the Court to strike down the law forcing severance of Moscow-Patriarchate ties or dissolution; lawyer Artur Knjazev (via ERR): choice between 'another Church' or end of activities; Supreme Court upheld the law en banc 8 June 2026, reading it as barring administrative (not religious) ties — 6 of 17 judges dissented on legal-certainty grounds; Metropolitan Evgeny expelled from Estonia in 2024 (authorities acknowledged no threat) and remains Metropolitan of Tallinn; compliance deadline 28 Dec 2026 (new metropolitan + severed ties) or the Interior Minister seeks compulsory dissolution. The July-2025 OrthoChristian ECHR item remains a separate earlier complaint (anti-confusion fence, per the fifth/sixth pass) — not conflated.; ERR via linksgraph — Estonian Supreme Court declines to hear Church cassation appeal in Tallinn lease dispute (64 Pikk Street); lease-termination ruling entered into force 19 Oct 2026; silerenonpossum — Sweden/Russia church piece summarizing Estonia: law in force 27 June 2026, 28 Dec 2026 compliance deadline, ECHR cases filed, dissolution requires further legal proceedings
Falsifiability: The record would fall if the ECHR register shows no such application; it would rise on the court's admissibility ruling</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>24.7536,59.437,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Estonia 2026: the six-months ultimatum to the EOC-MP</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — National public-broadcaster reporting of a named ministry's dated administrative action; the church's response is not yet recorded.
Layer: real · Place: Tallinn, Estonia
Dates: 2026-07 → 2026-07 (confidence: high)

In July 2026, Estonia's Ministry of the Interior gave the Estonian Orthodox Church of the Moscow Patriarchate (EOC-MP) six months to sever its ties with the Russian Orthodox Church, as the Estonian state continued the 2025 Riigikogu process against church subordination to Moscow.

Earliest attestation: Eesti Rahvusringhääling (ERR), 7 July 2026
Sources: ERR (Estonian Public Broadcasting), Johannes Voltri, 7 July 2026, 'Riik andis õigeusu kirikule pool aastat'; Wikipedia, '2018 Moscow-Constantinople schism', § Reactions/2025-2026, retrieved 17 Oct 2026; RISU (via ERR News), 'Estonian Christian Orthodox Church appeals to European Court, opposing break with Moscow', Oct 2026 — 28 Dec 2026 deadline; ECHR application lodged; OrthoChristian.com, 'Estonian Christian Orthodox Church given six months to sever Moscow Patriarchate ties', 8 July 2026 — Ministry of the Interior letter; forced-dissolution warning; silerenonpossum.com legal analysis, 14 Oct 2026 — ECHR lodging announced 8 Oct; Churches and Congregations Act s.3(2^1); Kaitsepolitseiamet April report; 2025-26 priest entry bans
Falsifiability: The record would fall if the ERR report misstates the ministry's action; it would rise on the ministry's own published order</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>24.7536,59.437,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>FAST's September 2026 candidate NBS 260108 near K2-155 — the one that didn't survive</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Graded as the study's own reported findings via a fact-checked secondary that names the journal (The Astronomical Journal), the figures, and the failure mode; the candidate's non-survival is the finding — this is how the falsifiability bar of V.30 works in practice.
Layer: real · Place: FAST (Five-hundred-meter Aperture Spherical Telescope), Dawodang, Guizhou, China
Dates: 2026-09-26 → 2026-09-26 (confidence: high)

In September 2026, astronomers searching 33 planetary systems with China's FAST radio telescope reported a candidate technosignature — narrowband signal NBS 260108 at 1148.4167 MHz toward K2-155: narrow, drifting, appearing only in the on-target beam, the textbook checklist. It did not survive vetting: the signal appeared strongly in only one polarization channel, and similar signals at nearby frequencies appeared during observations of three other stars — clues pointing to terrestrial or instrumental interference whose exact source could not be identified. It remains unclassified, with an Earthly/instrumental origin considered more likely than an extraterrestrial one.

Earliest attestation: The Astronomical Journal study (September 2026) via ScienceAlert's September 26, 2026 report
Sources: ScienceAlert, 'Astronomers Searched 33 Planetary Systems For Alien Signals. One Caught Their Attention' (September 26, 2026, fact-checked) — NBS 260108, 1148.4167 MHz, single-polarization, three other stars' nearby-frequency analogues, unclassified, terrestrial/instrumental origin more likely
Falsifiability: Re-observation of NBS 260108 with the full FAST beam + independent confirmation at a second observatory would reopen the case; absent that, it joins the archive of interesting unclassified signals</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>106.8569,25.6529,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gallup June 2026: 39% say religion's influence is increasing (series high since 2006); 65% say more religion would benefit society (down from 75% in 2013)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the pollster's own published release (primary) with disclosed methodology, plus consistent secondary re-reports of both the 39% and 65% figures; graded as poll attestation, never as evidence about religion's actual societal role.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-05-01 → 2026-05-17 (confidence: high)

Gallup's June 2026 release 'Americans Still Say More Religiosity Would Benefit U.S.' (Values and Beliefs survey, fielded May 1–17, 2026, random sample of 1,001 U.S. adults) found: 39% of U.S. adults say religion is increasing its influence on American life — up from 34% in May 2025 and 20% in May 2024, and among the highest readings in two decades (only 41% in December 2025 and 40% in September 2006 were higher; the all-time series high is 71% in December 2001). The increase began after the 2024 Republican election victories. On the parallel question, 65% say a more religious America would be positive for society — down from 75% in 2013; 22% say it would be negative (up from 17% in 2013). The partisan split is asymmetric: Republicans rose from 11% (pre-2024-election) to 48% on the increasing-influence question; independents 21% to 39%; Democrats 32% to 35% (essentially flat). Perception and behavior diverge: personal religiosity measures sit near record lows (47% say religion 'very important,' Gallup 2025) while perceived influence climbs.

Earliest attestation: Gallup, 'Americans Still Say More Religiosity Would Benefit U.S.,' news.gallup.com poll/711455 (updated 2026-06-17; Values and Beliefs survey, May 1–17, 2026, n=1,001)
Sources: Gallup, 'Americans Still Say More Religiosity Would Benefit U.S.,' news.gallup.com/poll/711455 (updated 2026-06-17): 39% increasing influence (Dec 2025 41%, May 2025 34%, May 2024 20%); 65% more-religious-America positive (2013: 75%); partisan breakdowns R 11%→48%, I 21%→39%, D 32%→35%; field May 1–17, 2026, n=1,001; Gallup, 'More Americans See Religion Increasing Its Influence in U.S.,' news.gallup.com/poll/691424 (published 2025-06-11): the June-2025 wave, 34% increasing influence vs 20% a year earlier — the wave the Oct-2026 recrawl re-reported under a current date; Baptist Press, 'Americans less likely to see religion as positive influence' (~July 2026) and Christian Post, 'Most Americans still believe religion is good for society' (June 23, 2026): consistent secondary re-reports of the 65%/22% religiosity-benefit figures and the demographic breakdowns
Falsifiability: Gallup's own release contradicting the 39%/65% figures, or retraction.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gallup June 2026: 65% say more religious people would be positive — down from 75% in 2013, gender order reversed</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The attitude figures are the pollster's own release via consistent secondary reporting; the record grades the attestation of the belief, never the fact.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-06-16 → 2026-06-16 (confidence: high)

In a Gallup poll released June 2026, 65% of U.S. adults said that having more religious people in the country would have a positive effect on society — down ten points from 75% in 2013. The decline was driven by women (down 16 points, 77% to 61%) and adults under 54 (down 16 points among 18-34); men fell only 3 points, so men now outpace women (70% vs 61%) — the gender order reversed from 2013.

Earliest attestation: Gallup poll, reported 2026-06-16 (via Daily Caller)
Sources: Daily Caller, 'Women, Youth Led American Exodus From Religion Over Past 13 Years, Poll Shows,' 2026-06-16 (65% positive-effect, down from 75% in 2013; women 77%→61%, men 73%→70%; under-54 decline 16 points)
Falsifiability: Release of the Gallup cross-tabs contradicting the 65%/75% trend, or Gallup disowning the figures.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gallup 2026: the young-men reversal — attachment up, the aggregate flat</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the pollster's release as reported by multiple consistent secondary outlets; figures internally consistent across outlets (42% vs 28%, women 29-30%, Republican concentration). The record grades the attestation of the survey finding, never the truth of any belief.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-04-16 → 2026-04-16 (confidence: high)

Gallup's April 2026 release (data spanning 2024-2026, aggregated for stability) found that 42% of American men ages 18-29 say religion is 'very important' in their lives — up from 28% in 2022-2023 — marking the first time in 25 years (series since 2000) that young men surpass young women on this measure (young women ~29-30%, flat to record low). Among adults 30+, women remain more religious than men. The growth concentrates among young Republicans: weekly religious attendance has climbed among Republican young men since 2019 while declining among Democratic young men; ~60% of young Republican women attend at least monthly vs ~25% of young Democratic women. Gallup reports aggregate findings biennially to keep estimates stable.

Earliest attestation: Gallup release, April 16, 2026 (via SRN News, jubileecast, tippinsights, morningpress secondary reports)
Sources: Gallup, April 2026 release (via SRN News, Apr 16, 2026): 42% of men 18-29 say religion 'very important' (2024-2026 data) vs 28% in 2022-2023; first time young men exceed young women (29-30%) in 25 years; growth concentrated among young Republicans; jubileecast write-up, Apr 2026: same 42%/28% figures; 40% of young men attend services weekly-or-monthly (2026 tracking) — highest since 2012-2013; Barna corroborating Gen Z male commitment-to-Jesus +15 pts 2019-2025 (secondhand); The Banner/RNS, Mar 3, 2026: the aggregate counterpoint — 47% of Americans say religion 'very important' (flat since 2021); 57% rarely/never attend (vs 42% in 1992); Burge: 'no religious revival happening in America'; Barna Group 2025-2026 tracking (via JubileeCast 2026-04-30): Gen Z men commitment to Jesus +15 pts 2019–2025; Millennial men +19 pts; Gen Z churchgoers attend 1.9 weekends/month — highest since Barna tracking began; independent corroboration of the Gallup direction; Ryan Burge, Christian Post ~2026-08 ('Is there a revival in the United States? The real data says no'): ABS 2026 measured the 2025 Scripture-engagement bump evaporating — engagement back to 2024 levels; 'That is not a revival. That is a spike.'; aggregate 'religion very important' flat at 47% — carried as competing context, no grade change; EncuentraIglesias (2026-10, re-reporting Gallup): 40% monthly attendance among young men, highest since 2012–2013, +7 pts from 2022–23; 63% identify with some religion — consistent outlet, grade-neutral
Falsifiability: Gallup's own biennial aggregate contradicting the 42%/28% figures, or retraction of the release.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Gentoo penguin one-to-four species split (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed publication by a multi-institution team with genomic, morphological, and ecological lines; a single study, taxonomic splits provisional pending independent replication — so CORROBORATED, not VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Kerguelen Islands, subantarctic southern Indian Ocean (P. kerguelensis type range)
Dates: 2026-04 → 2026-04 (confidence: high)

In April 2026, an integrative study of 22 scientists (University of Chile + UC Berkeley, published in Communications Biology) split the gentoo penguin — treated as one species for over a century — into four: northern (Pygoscelis papua), southern (P. ellsworthi), eastern (P. taeniata), and the newly described southeastern gentoo (P. kerguelensis) of the Kerguelen and Heard Islands. Cryptic species: visually near-identical, separated by genomic, morphological, and ecological evidence; the first newly named penguin species in over a century.

Earliest attestation: Vianna et al. 2026, Communications Biology (peer-reviewed, April 2026)
Sources: Vianna et al. 2026, Communications Biology — integrative evidence reveals adaptive divergence and speciation in gentoo penguins (22-scientist team, genomic + morphological + ecological); Rauri Bowie (UC Berkeley Museum of Vertebrate Zoology) — curator statement on the century-long taxonomy debate and the integrative resolution; Smithsonian Magazine / SciTechDaily / ScienceBlog / BirdGuides / sci.news coverage of the Communications Biology study (venue-echoes of the recorded paper, grade-neutral)
Falsifiability: Genomic re-analysis showing gene flow across the four ranges at levels inconsistent with species rank, or a peer-reviewed rebuttal overturning the integrative diagnosis</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>69.22,-49.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The edge-on 'hamburger' disk: ALMA maps the vertical structure of a giant planet-forming system (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Independent instrument (ALMA) with stated resolution; the discovery team presented at AAS and the paper is accepted by ApJ; independent science press reports the team's findings with the stated caveats. The resolved structures are the claim; the planet-formation interpretations are fenced as the team's readings.
Layer: real · Place: ALMA observatory, Atacama, Chile (the instrument that made the observation)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

ALMA's 2026 observations of the nearly edge-on protoplanetary disk GoHam (Gómez's Hamburger) resolved its vertical layering — light ¹²CO gas floating highest above the midplane, a heavier CO isotope lower, sulfur-bearing gas closest to the plane, millimeter dust compact near the midplane while gas extends far above and below — mapped an asymmetric brighter dust region attributed to a vortex or other large-scale disturbance that may concentrate solid material for planet building, detected wispy CO consistent with a photoevaporative wind, and identified a dense clump ('GoHam b') consistent with material collapsing under its own gravity, a candidate earliest observable stage of a massive wide-orbit planet.

Earliest attestation: Law et al., American Astronomical Society meeting presentation + arXiv:2609.09290 (2026)
Sources: Charles J. Law et al., 'The ALMA View of the Edge-on Gómez's Hamburger System: A Highly-Dynamic, Asymmetric Protoplanetary Disk Reveals the Earliest Phases of Giant Planet Formation', arXiv:2609.09290 (2026), DOI: 10.48550/arxiv.2609.09290 — accepted for The Astrophysical Journal; presented at an AAS meeting; phys.org (19 Oct 2026) — 'Cosmic hamburger offers rare edge-on view of where giant planets may take shape' — the vertical layering (CO high, heavy-CO mid, sulfur gas near plane), the dust asymmetry/vortex reading, the photoevaporative-wind candidate, the GoHam-b collapsing clump; Everything Space (19 Oct 2026) — 'ALMA reveals rare edge-on view of giant planet formation in Gómez's Hamburger disk' — written from the paper; the edge-on geometry as the vertical-layering benchmark; Eighth-pass I.5 review 2026-10-20 (via #728 Dr. Sander Holm — The Condensation Reader): NEW CORROBORATED exhibit — the first edge-on disk benchmark for giant-planet formation: the vertical stratification is the measurement (stated ALMA resolution), the asymmetry and wind are candidate readings, GoHam b is a candidate earliest-stage planet, fenced as the team's interpretation; Law, Zhang et al., 'The ALMA View of the Edge-on Gómez's Hamburger System: A Highly-Dynamic, Asymmetric Protoplanetary Disk Reveals the Earliest Phases of Giant Planet Formation', The Astrophysical Journal, published 19 Oct 2026 (arXiv:2609.09290) — the published version of the accepted/preprint source already on this chain: the one-sided SO arc aligned with GoHam b clump (~280 AU, shock-heating from an early-stage giant planet or disk fragment), vertical stratification (¹²CO high, heavier-CO lower, sulfur-bearing near the plane), north–south dust asymmetry (particle traps/vortices), faint high-altitude emission as photoevaporative-wind candidate, central star ~2.2 M☉, revised distance 139 pc (Sco-Cen edge). Turin-shroud precedent — grade-neutral.; Eleventh-pass review 2026-10-22 (via #897 Dr. Nessa Delaney — The Volatile Reader): CORROBORATED held — chain strengthened 4→5 sources grade-neutral (Law et al. preprint source upgraded to the published ApJ version, Oct 19); no supersession, no grade move.
Falsifiability: Deeper ALMA imaging showing the dust asymmetry is a projection or processing artifact, or the GoHam-b clump dispersing rather than contracting, would revise the planet-formation readings; the vertical stratification of gas species stands on the ALMA band data</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-67.7532,-23.0193,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hartford EPIC 2026: median in-person worship attendance rises to 70 — first increase in 25 years</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The attendance figures are the research institute's own published findings via consistent secondary reporting; the record grades the attestation, never a verdict on whether a 'revival' is occurring.
Layer: real · Place: United States (congregational survey)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Hartford Institute for Religion Research's EPIC project (Exploring the Pandemic Impact on Congregations) reported that median in-person worship attendance at U.S. congregations had risen to 70 — up from 65 before the pandemic and 45 in 2021 — the first increase in 25 years of Hartford tracking. Regular volunteering rose to 40% of congregants, up from 15% in 2021.

Earliest attestation: Hartford Institute for Religion Research, EPIC project (reported ~August 2026, via Ryan Burge's Christian Post analysis)
Sources: Christian Post (Ryan Burge), 'Is there a revival in the United States? The real data says no,' ~2026-08 (median in-person attendance 70, up from 65 pre-pandemic and 45 in 2021; volunteering 40% vs 15% in 2021; researchers re-verified the data)
Falsifiability: Release of the EPIC figures contradicting the 70/45/65 sequence, or Hartford disowning the report.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>First Herculaneum scroll read end-to-end (PHerc.1667, June 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports agree: the full scroll virtually unwrapped and read beginning to end; 22 columns of Greek; ~1.4 m of papyrus; the Stoic treatise on human nature, impulse, and moral progress; the possible Chrysippus attribution (2nd–3rd c. BCE); the 2025 PHerc.139 precedent (first title from a still-rolled scroll); the ongoing leaded-ink hunt (up to 25× brighter on scans). The amateur 'AI deciphered Linear A / Indus / Rongorongo' claims of 2025–2026 are explicitly fenced as disputed — none peer-reviewed, no bilingual anchor — and are not evidence grades.
Layer: real · Place: Herculaneum (Villa of the Papyri)
Dates: 2026-06 → 2026-06 (confidence: high)

In June 2026, the Vesuvius Challenge team completed the first end-to-end reading of a still-rolled Herculaneum scroll — PHerc.1667: 22 columns of ancient Greek across ~1.4 meters of papyrus, virtually unwrapped by CT scanning and AI ink detection — revealing a Stoic philosophical treatise on human nature, impulse, and moral progress, possibly by Chrysippus.

Earliest attestation: Vesuvius Challenge announcement, June 2026
Sources: Gizmodo (2026 Science Fair): 'A volcano toasted this scroll 2,000 years ago — here's how scientists read it without ever opening it.' The first scroll read end-to-end: 22 columns of Greek across ~1.4 m; a Stoic treatise on human nature, impulse, and moral progress, possibly by Chrysippus.; Vesuvius Challenge (Brent Seales / EduceLab, University of Kentucky) with the papyrology team — announced June 2026; 2025 milestone PHerc.139 = Philodemus, On Gods, Book 8, the first title from a still-rolled scroll.; Vesuvius Challenge primary announcement (scrollprize.org/firstscroll, June 2026): the final preserved column names Aristocreon — nephew and disciple of Chrysippus — placing the text in a Stoic context and dating it to the 2nd century BC (handwriting grounds); possibly the oldest Herculaneum scroll; the library held Stoic as well as Epicurean works; readings are fragmentary with gaps where the surface is lost; the surviving remnant is ~8 cm of an original 19–24 cm roll height (outer layers destroyed by 19th-c., 1969, and 1980s unrolling attempts).; CNN (June 26, 2026): papyrologist Federica Nicolardi — the scroll was 'deemed completely unreadable when part of it was opened in the 1980s' and assigned a readability score of zero; 'with virtual unwrapping, we can follow sustained arguments across multiple columns. That's a transformational shift'; the handwriting suggests 2nd c. BC or late 3rd c. BC.; scienceblog.com (Aug 2026): eighteenth-century scholars 'destroyed hundreds of Herculaneum scrolls trying to prise them open' — the destruction side of the ledger (destruction cuts both ways: what the fire preserved, the unrollers destroyed); PHerc.139 yielded title + author line in the same work round — Philodemus, On Gods, Book 8 — the treatise ran to at least eight books.; todaytimeslive.com (Aug 2026): Vesuvius Challenge status update - 45 scrolls and fragments scanned so far; PHerc.1667 remains the first complete scroll virtually unrolled and read end-to-end; PHerc.139 end-title read as Philodemus, On Gods, Book VIII (grade-neutral status confirmation, no new title announcements since June 2026); arXiv (June 2026): Angelotti, Parsons, Nicolardi, Seales et al., 'Complete virtual unwrapping and reading of a rolled Herculaneum papyrus' — primary publication confirming the June 2026 end-to-end reading of PHerc.1667; authorship attribution remains 'possibly Chrysippus', outside the graded scope (grade-neutral chain strengthening, Turin-shroud precedent); Vesuvius Challenge First Letters Prize, 8 Oct 2026: Erwin Nieuwlaar, $50,000, recovered readable text from PHerc. 343 (carbonized, unopened since AD 79) — the program's first rewarded recovery from a scroll other than the PHerc. 1667/172 line; Prize structure refined, Oct 2026 (scrollprize.org Open Prizes page, crawled 2026-10-23; forfoxs 2026-10 piece ~2 days old): the 2027 Grand Prize is $800,000 to the first team/individual to fully digitally unroll and make readable one of 13 eligible CT scans (PHerc. 125/191/211/257/268/358/800/813/826/1203/1218/1447/1545), with $100k/$50k/$50k placements ($1,000,000 total pool), deadline June 25, 2027 — refining the earlier '$1M grand prize' shorthand (grand prize proper $800k; pool $1M). The forfoxs piece re-reports the Oct-8 PHerc. 343 $50,000 First Letters Prize (Erwin Nieuwlaar — ten readable letters in a new scroll, already chained) and carries the venue's own caution: ten letters in one roll is not a full-surface reading; detecting ink in one scroll does not guarantee a model works on another. No second full scroll as of 2026-10-23; the watch item stands.
Falsifiability: Retraction of the reading by the papyrology team or demonstration that the columns were misassembled</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.3439,40.8062,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The circular marble monument around the venerated chamber (Holy Sepulchre, announced 17 Oct 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The excavation team's own announcement, consistently reported: a documented monument around a venerated chamber, with dating explicitly left open.
Layer: real · Place: Church of the Holy Sepulchre, Jerusalem (traditional)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In the October 2026 reporting cycle on the Sapienza excavation beneath the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, archaeologists announced the remains of a circular marble monument ~20 ft across built around the venerated burial chamber — described as the earliest archaeologically documented monument constructed around the tomb.

Earliest attestation: 17 October 2026 press coverage of the Stasolla (Sapienza) excavation statements.
Sources: Press coverage 17 Oct 2026 (internewscast, truthsocial.org.uk aggregator): archaeologist Francesca Romana Stasolla on the circular marble monument — the rock around the chamber intentionally cut away, the monument's circular form matching early artistic representations of the shrine; Stasolla's own fence, carried in the same reporting: the surviving marble could date to a later construction phase; mortar analysis ongoing; the monument is described as honoring 'the chamber in which tradition recalled the deposition of Christ'
Falsifiability: Full excavation reporting could show the 'monument' to be a later pavement repair or a misread construction phase; a dated mortar analysis would narrow or falsify the veneration-era readings</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2297,31.7784,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Holy Sepulchre excavation — a quarry, a garden, and rock-cut tombs</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent 2026 reports of the same excavation project (Times of Israel, L'Osservatore Romano, Arkeonews, technical summaries) agree on the quarry→garden→tombs sequence, the Hadrianic structure, and Constantine's isolation of one chamber — and the excavators themselves state the findings do not identify any tomb as Jesus's.
Layer: real · Place: Church of the Holy Sepulchre, Jerusalem (traditional)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Archaeological excavation beneath the Church of the Holy Sepulchre (findings reported August 2026) shows the site was an ancient stone quarry on Gareb Hill, later reused for both cultivation (vine and olive signals) and rock-cut tombs — a funerary landscape consistent with the Gospel of John's description of a garden containing a new tomb near the crucifixion site. The excavation did NOT identify any particular tomb as that of Jesus.

Earliest attestation: Excavation findings reported August 2026 (Italian archaeological team led by Francesca Stasolla, reported via Times of Israel, L'Osservatore Romano, Arkeonews); study ongoing.
Sources: Excavation findings reported Aug 2026: the church stands over part of an extensive quarry on Gareb Hill (irregular rock surfaces, phased extraction); abandoned quarry sections reused for agriculture (vine wood found, olive pollen signals) and funerary use (rock-cut tombs). One chamber had been obstructed by a Roman structure of the Hadrianic 2nd century; Constantine-era works destroyed that structure and isolated one tomb chamber, giving it its own Christian shrine — consistent with a specific tomb already carrying a Christian memory. The excavators warn conclusions remain preliminary.; L'Osservatore Romano, July 2026: the excavated landscape's relationship to the ancient garden-and-tomb tradition near Calvary; frames the findings as archaeology of the setting, not identification of the tomb.; The excavator's own fence: the findings 'do not conclusively establish that the tomb belonged to Jesus' — the archaeology identifies a landscape, not a name (the Aedicule has been rebuilt many times; no name was recovered).; 17 Oct 2026 reporting of the Stasolla excavation's circular marble monument (~20 ft) around the venerated chamber — 'the earliest archaeologically documented monument built around the venerated tomb'; mortar analysis ongoing; the excavator's own fence (monument honors tradition, does not identify the occupant) carried — recorded separately as evt-holy-sepulchre-circular-monument-2026-001
Falsifiability: A dated inscription identifying a different tradition, or evidence that the isolated chamber was not a tomb at all, would change the landscape reading; nothing can confirm the name of the occupant — the grade fences that off</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2297,31.7784,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 15th Italian National Conference of the International Association of Exorcists (Sacrofano, Sept 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The conference event itself is attested by multiple independent Catholic news outlets (ZENIT, catholicnewsworld) with consistent dates, venue, and attendance; grades the institutional practice as a belief-attestation event, never the entity claim.
Layer: real · Place: Fraterna Domus, Sacrofano (Rome), Italy
Dates: 2026-09-21 → 2026-09-25 (confidence: high)

Around 120 Catholic exorcists and assisting priests gathered at Sacrofano (Rome), 21–25 September 2026, for the 15th Italian National Conference of the International Association of Exorcists — combining accounts of difficult pastoral cases with theological reflection on the Virgin Mary, sacred music, and formation; the meeting also marked the tenth anniversary of the death of Father Gabriele Amorth (d. 16 Sept 2016), the association's founder.

Earliest attestation: ZENIT News, Rome, 11 October 2026 (contemporary report)
Sources: ZENIT (11 Oct 2026): 'What is the role of the Virgin Mary and sacred music in exorcisms? A conference of exorcists in Italy provides the answer' — ~120 exorcists and priests, Fraterna Domus, Sacrofano, 21–25 Sept 2026; 10th anniversary of Fr. Gabriele Amorth's death; remarks of Fr. Francesco Bamonte (vice president) and Fr. Piermario Burgo; catholicnewsworld.com (Oct 2026): 'Rome's International Conference of Exorcists Highlights the Role of Sacred Music and the Virgin Mary in Exorcisms' — same event: Fraterna Domus, Sacrofano, 21–25 Sept 2026, ~120 attendees, alternating biennially with international gatherings; EWTN Vatican (Sept 2026): 'Father Gabriele Amorth remembered as exorcist, spiritual guide, and communicator' — 10th-anniversary conference, Basilica of St. Mary Queen of Apostles, 16 Sept 2026; Amorth founded the International Association of Exorcists in 1994, president until 2000; appointed exorcist of the Diocese of Rome 1985
Falsifiability: Denial by the International Association of Exorcists or evidence the event did not occur on those dates</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.45,42.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>IANDS 2026: nearly one in four American adults reports a near-death experience</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by a published survey with methodology disclosed (n=2,100, Centiment, March 2026); the record grades the attestation of the experience reports and their self-reported effects, never the metaphysical meaning of any experience.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-03-01 → 2026-03-31 (confidence: high)

A March 2026 survey by the International Association for Near-Death Studies (IANDS), conducted by Centiment among 2,100 American adults, found that 23% of American adults report having had a near-death experience, and 35% of those who have not had one say they know someone who has. Among those reporting an NDE, 51% said the experience gave them a deeper meaning and appreciation for life and 37.6% said they felt more connected to a 'spiritual realm.' Reported by The Debrief, 2026.

Earliest attestation: International Association for Near-Death Studies / Centiment, March 2026 (n=2,100); reported by The Debrief
Sources: IANDS / Centiment, March 2026 (n=2,100 American adults): 23% report having had an NDE; 35% of non-experiencers know someone who has; 51% of experiencers report deeper meaning/appreciation for life, 37.6% feel more connected to a 'spiritual realm'; Xue, X. et al. 2026, Frontiers in Psychology 17:1935246 (published Sept 21, 2026) — Chinese online sample, self-reported NDE/near-death-like experiences, mixed-methods; a second 2026 peer-reviewed NDE datum; no AWARE-II follow-up results located in the Oct 20-21, 2026 sweep; Lerner, F., dos Santos, P.C.R. &amp; Tahar, G. 2026, Frontiers in Psychology 17:1760389 (published 15 Oct 2026; doi:10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1760389) — 'The perceptual geometry and chromaticity of near-death experience spaces': graphic-reconstruction study of OBE/NDE visuo-spatial structure (recurrent visual-field boundary configurations, self-location/self-motion mapping); phenomenology of the experience, not evidence for entities — grade-neutral per the Turin-shroud precedent
Falsifiability: Release of the IANDS/Centiment data contradicting the reported figures, or a retraction of the survey.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Erythrulose in interstellar space: sugars before planets</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A peer-reviewed radio-astronomical detection with multiple unblended transitions; extends the ingredient ledger (sugars exist before planets) - not the sequence, not delivery to Earth, not life.
Layer: real · Place: G+0.693-0.027 molecular cloud, Galactic Centre (~8,200 ly)
Dates: 2026-10 → 2026-10 (confidence: high)

In October 2026, astronomers reported the detection of erythrulose - a four-carbon sugar - in the G+0.693-0.027 molecular cloud near the Galactic Centre, identified via the Yebes 40-metre and IRAM 30-metre radio telescopes (12 sets of spectral transitions, six unblended). Life's sugar chemistry predates planetary systems.

Earliest attestation: Nature Astronomy, October 2026 (reported via Medium/Syeda Khair-ul-Bariyah Oct 2026 summary; primary paper: erythrulose in G+0.693-0.027)
Sources: Nature Astronomy Oct 2026 - erythrulose detected in G+0.693-0.027 (Galactic Centre molecular cloud, ~8,200 ly): 12 sets of transitions, 17 individual transitions, 6 unblended sets; Yebes 40m + IRAM 30m; Medium @skbariyah Oct 2026 'Sugar in Space Does Not Mean Life Came from Space, But It Changes the Question' - honest popular telling: 'does not mean life came from space' but 'changes the question'; venue only
Falsifiability: Independent non-detection with deeper integration; or re-assignment of the transitions to another species</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.0,-29.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 digital face of Jebel Irhoud (composite reconstruction)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The reconstruction was published in a peer-reviewed journal; the morphometric measurements are verifiable from the fossil data; the composite construction and the authors' own 'approximation' framing are documented — the record is corroborated AS a documented reconstruction, with the portrait reading explicitly fenced.
Layer: real · Place: Jebel Irhoud, Morocco (fossils); reconstruction modeled from CT scans
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The digital facial approximation of the Jebel Irhoud hominins published in September 2026 (Heritage 9(9):369, Moraes et al.) is a composite: the braincase of Irhoud 1, jaw and tooth fragments from OTHER specimens in the same layer, reshaped onto a modern human anatomical template, with muscles, fat, skin, hair and beard estimated from living people. Endocranial volume measured from the composite is 1,364 mL (matching the earlier CT estimate of 1,375 mL). The authors call it a 'morphologically plausible approximation', not a portrait — no soft tissue of any kind survives from Middle Pleistocene hominins, so the face on the poster is not one person's face and could not be.

Earliest attestation: Moraes et al., Heritage 2026 — composite built from 3D models of Irhoud fossils (Flinders University, University of Pavia, Universiti Sains Malaysia)
Sources: Moraes et al. 2026, Heritage 9(9):369, doi:10.3390/heritage9090369 — composite digital facial approximation (grayscale anatomical + full-color); anthropology.net, 2026-09-24 — 'The 315,000-Year-Old Homo sapiens Face Is Not One Person' (composite critique; no Middle Pleistocene soft tissue preserved); phys.org 2026-09, ZME Science 2026-09-25, Earth.com 2026-10-06 — coverage of the reconstruction; Moraes interview on the familiar/ancient balance
Falsifiability: A single, complete Irhoud cranium with associated jaw would let the composite be tested against a real individual; the morphometric measurements stand or fall on the CT data, which are re-measurable.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-8.8725,31.855,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 facial approximation of the Jebel Irhoud composite skull</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Published, peer-reviewed, methods and composite provenance stated in the paper itself; the anthropology.net provenance audit (Sept 2026) independently documents the five-individual composite and the video-still photogrammetry.
Layer: real · Place: Jebel Irhoud, Morocco (the fossils' origin; the approximation was computed digitally)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In September 2026, Moraes, Varotto, Habicht, Galassi, Moura &amp; Abdullah published a digital facial approximation of a composite Jebel Irhoud skull (Heritage 9(9):369, doi 10.3390/heritage9090369, 13 Sept 2026) — built with open-source tools (Blender 2.91 + OrtogOnBlender), released in gray-anatomical and full-color versions, with a brain-volume estimate of ~1,230 cm³ (vs the prior CT endocranial estimate ~1,375 cm³). The 'face' is explicitly a composite assembled from five Irhoud individuals (Irhoud 1 braincase/upper face, Irhoud 11 mandible, Irhoud 10 upper teeth, Irhoud 21/22 dental fragments); the 3D mesh was derived from screenshots of the Max Planck Institute's 2017 press video (no scale markers); soft tissue was mapped from a modern CT donor and Karen T. Taylor forensic reference values; skin color, hair, and beard are, per the authors themselves, 'artistic conventions without grounding in the fossil'.

Earliest attestation: Moraes et al. 2026, Heritage 9(9):369 — the paper itself
Sources: Moraes, Varotto, Habicht, Galassi, Moura &amp; Abdullah 2026, Heritage 9(9):369, doi 10.3390/heritage9090369 — the facial approximation paper (brain volume ~1,230 cm³; composite methodology stated); anthropology.net, Sept 2026 — 'The 315,000-Year-Old Homo sapiens Face Is Not One Person': the five-individual composite, the 2017 press-video-screenshot photogrammetry, the modern-donor soft tissue; phys.org, Sept 2026 — 'Ancient human emerges pieced together from Moroccan fossils': the press coverage carrying the paper's own caveats
Falsifiability: Retraction of the Heritage paper, or demonstration that the 3D composite was not built from the Irhoud material.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-8.8725,31.855,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Jiangchuan biota: complex animals in the waning Ediacaran, ~539 Ma (Dunn et al.)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed paper in Science (April 2026) with primary fossil data (700+ specimens) — CORROBORATED. The complex-trait identifications rest on published morphological description; graded on the evidence, with the authors' own 'transitional fauna' framing quoted rather than amplified.
Layer: real · Place: Jiangchuan, Yunnan, China (near the Chengjiang World Heritage site)
Dates: 2026-04-02 → 2026-04-02 (confidence: high)

The April 2026 Science paper by Frankie Dunn (Oxford Museum of Natural History) and colleagues describes the Jiangchuan biota of Yunnan, China: 700+ fossils from ~539 million years ago — the waning Ediacaran, millions of years before the Cambrian boundary — including animals with mouths, anuses, the ability to hunt and burrow, and three-dimensional lives in the water column. Complex, recognizably animal traits, previously treated as Cambrian innovations, were already operational in the Ediacaran. The paper reframes the Cambrian explosion's 'integration cost': the transition was not from simple sponges to complex organisms, but from an already-complex transitional fauna.

Earliest attestation: Dunn et al., Science 2026.
Sources: Dunn et al. 2026, Science (April 2, 2026) — the Jiangchuan biota paper; phys.org/AP report by Seth Borenstein (April 4, 2026) — 'How we came to be: Scientists get first look at the evolution of early complex animals'; Emily Mitchell (Cambridge), quoted on the paper: 'absolutely fascinating… vital insights into life around the end of the Ediacaran Period'; First-pass review 2026-10-18 (via #535 Dr. Stian Viken — The Explosion Reader): CORROBORATED assigned — peer-reviewed primary data; the Ediacaran-complexity exhibit for the explosion-vs-unveiling dispute
Falsifiability: Re-study of the Jiangchuan material reassigning the complex morphologies to non-animal or simple-grade organisms; demonstration that the 'mouths and anuses' are preservational artifacts.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>102.9,24.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 Hattusa Kadesh-treaty fragment (Articles 12-20: the fugitives provisions)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED as a discovery-report: the identification announcement is multiply attested from the ministry's own release, and the fragment's provenance (Büyükkale, royal-citadel context, ~1 year of study) is stated. The grade does not extend to the not-yet-published transcription.
Layer: real · Place: Hattusa, Büyükkale area near Building E (Boğazköy, Çorum Province, Türkiye)
Dates: 2026-09-21 → 2026-09-21 (confidence: high)

On 21 September 2026 (International Day of Peace), Turkey's Ministry of Culture and Tourism announced the identification of a new cuneiform fragment of the Treaty of Kadesh, excavated at the Büyükkale (royal citadel) area of Hattusa near Building E, and authenticated after about a year of scientific and textual study. The Akkadian cuneiform fragment preserves portions of Articles 12-20 of the treaty — the provisions governing fugitives/refugees who crossed between Egypt and Hatti, including protections against destroying the refugees' homes — and joins the previously known Hattusa fragments (in Ankara, Berlin, Istanbul, and St. Petersburg) plus the Egyptian versions as a securely-located textual witness to the Hittite copy of the agreement. Excavation at Hattusa continues under the Istanbul branch of the German Archaeological Institute (Andreas Schachner); no full transcription of the new section was released with the announcement.

Earliest attestation: Turkey's Ministry of Culture and Tourism announcement (Anadolu Agency), 21 September 2026; reported by Smithsonian Magazine, Popular Science, arkeonews, science.report, particle.news.
Sources: Turkey Ministry of Culture and Tourism announcement, 21 Sept 2026 (via Anadolu Agency); Smithsonian Magazine, 2026-09-21: 'Researchers in Turkey Have Unearthed an Ancient Fragment Inscribed With the World's Oldest Peace Treaty'; Popular Science, 2026-09-21: 'Fragment of oldest known peace treaty discovered in Turkey'; arkeonews.net, 2026-09-21: 'New Fragment of One of the World's Oldest Peace Treaties Found in Türkiye Reveals Ancient Rules for Fugitives'; science.report, 2026-09-21: 'Hattusa Fragment Reconnects a Piece of the Kadesh Treaty' (caution noted: the fragment documents the treaty's wording and transmission, not enforcement — the attestation rule applied); The fragment joins the Hattusa fragment family previously recovered from 1906 onward (Ankara, Berlin, Istanbul, St. Petersburg holdings); Türkiye Ministry of Culture and Tourism release (via zmescience.com, Oct 2026) — the fragment's fugitives-provision translation: 'Their tongue and their eyes are not to be pulled out; their ears and their feet are not to be cut off; their houses with their wives and their children are not to be destroyed.' Ministry-release text, not the peer-reviewed transcription (still pending); the same outlet notes the fragment 'does not add anything new to the history of the treaty' — carried as press-level consistency, not evidence.; Wikipedia 'Egyptian–Hittite peace treaty' article (accessed 2026-10-18) — tertiary, grade-neutral: article updated c. Oct 2026 to carry the 21 Sept 2026 Anadolu-agency/Büyükkale find (Akkadian-script Hittite copy fragment, Articles 12–20 fugitives provisions); confirms transmission into the general reference literature, no new evidence, no grade effect (the ministry's own announcement remains the primary announcement record); ohmega.monster analysis (Sept 2026, re: the ministry announcement) — analytical caveat carried as grade-neutral source: distinguishes the treaty's extradition/forced-return-with-protections clauses from modern asylum framing; notes NO Akkadian transcription, specialist name, or script analysis has been published, so whether the fragment's wording duplicates known lines cannot yet be judged; Turin-shroud precedent — chain strengthened, grade held
Falsifiability: A published edition showing the fragment belongs to a different text, or a retraction by the excavation team, would falsify the identification; the identification is provisional on the not-yet-published transcription.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>34.6167,40.0147,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Hittite royal archive of 184 tablets at Kayalıpınar (Oct 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple reports agree: 131 tablets in the 2026 season + 53 from 2025 = 184 total; Tudhaliya II, ~1400 BCE; the epidemic texts, the infant princess's funerary ritual, the generals' letters begging for men/horses/chariots, the divination itineraries; described as the most extensive Hittite-language archive of the 21st century. Reported in Hittite and Hurrian. This is a find announcement, not a decipherment — the script (cuneiform) is long read; the news is the archive's scale and content.
Layer: real · Place: Kayalıpınar (Šamuḫa), Sivas Province, Türkiye
Dates: 2026-10 → 2026-10 (confidence: high)

In October 2026, Türkiye's Ministry of Culture and Tourism announced 131 new cuneiform tablets from the 2026 season at Kayalıpınar (ancient Šamuḫa) — joining 53 from 2025 for 184 total — dating to King Tudhaliya II (~1400 BCE): the most extensive Hittite-language archive found in the 21st century, recording a child-killing fever epidemic, a breastfeeding infant princess's death, desperate generals' letters, and bird-divination itineraries.

Earliest attestation: Turkish Ministry of Culture and Tourism announcement, October 2026
Sources: The Debrief, October 2026: 'Archaeologists uncover a Hittite royal archive of 184 cuneiform tablets' — 131 from the 2026 season joining 53 from 2025; Tudhaliya II (~1400 BCE); the fever epidemic, the infant princess, the generals' letters, the bird-divination itineraries; announced by Türkiye's Ministry of Culture and Tourism.
Falsifiability: Withdrawal of the tablet count or redating by the excavating team</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>36.0667,39.3167,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>2026 Nobel Prize in Chemistry — homochirality amplification (Kagan &amp; Soai)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The prize, its citation, and the cited experimental facts are documented by the awarding body and independent chemistry press; the origin-of-life application is fenced by the Academy's own caveats and the laureate's own words — amplification proven in the flask, not in the prebiotic Earth.
Layer: real · Place: Stockholm, Sweden (Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences)
Dates: 2026-10-07 → 2026-10-07 (confidence: high)

On 7 October 2026 the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry to Henri B. Kagan (Universite Paris-Saclay) and Kenso Soai (Tokyo University of Science) 'for the discovery of non-linear effects and autocatalysis in asymmetric organic synthesis' — experimental chemistry showing how a tiny imbalance between mirror-image molecules can be amplified into near-total one-handedness: Kagan's 1986 non-linear effects in asymmetric catalysis, and Soai's 1995 asymmetric autocatalysis (a 2% enantiomeric excess amplified to 87%, the product catalyzing its own formation) and 2003 spontaneous symmetry breaking from achiral ingredients (~99.99% of one hand; re-running the reaction, the other hand can win — chance picks the hand). The prize recognizes the amplification chemistry; it does not establish how life's homochirality actually originated.

Earliest attestation: Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences press announcement, 7 October 2026
Sources: C&amp;EN: '2026 Chemistry Nobel awarded for chiral chemistry advances' — Kagan and Soai, 'for the discovery of non-linear effects and autocatalysis in asymmetric organic synthesis'; Heiner Linke, chair of the Nobel Committee for Chemistry: 'Life as we know it is homochiral'; exactly how life came to favor one chiral form 'remains a mystery'; Chemistry World: 'Kagan and Soai honoured for asymmetric autocatalysis and origins of life' — Kagan's 1986 non-linear effects and Soai's 1995/2003 Soai reaction showed how tiny imbalances between molecular mirror images can be amplified into a single dominant form; 'the strongest experimental support' for models of how homochirality could emerge from near-racemic mixtures; Kantenna: the Academy's popular-science explainer notes the Soai reaction is artificial and differs from the chemistry of life, and that labs are now attempting the same with amino acids and sugars; Soai at his university press conference: the mystery 'has not been completely solved' — the prize-fence, carried verbatim from the laureate; Nordiskpost/Yle, 7 Oct 2026 — Nobel Committee member Peter Somfai: scientists may never know why life took a homochiral direction around four billion years ago; possible routes (minerals, radiation from space) open, the question remains open; Heiner Linke (committee chair): the prize solves how homochirality can emerge spontaneously (amplification), not what originally made terrestrial life choose its orientation — the committee fences its own prize off the origin question (grade-neutral: strengthens the telling-record fence, grade held).; Chemistry World, Mason Wakley feature (c. 15 Oct 2026) — Singleton: the Soai reaction demonstrated clearly and for the first time that the homochirality and self-replication bottlenecks can be overcome readily in a single ordinary reaction; but the Soai reaction itself is not what took place on prebiotic earth — the reaction is sensitive to water, life's medium; no cute magic-like explanation is needed or even helpful (grade-neutral: the mechanism-vs-realization distinction, grade held).
Falsifiability: A correction or withdrawal of the prize citation by the Academy; or independent failure to replicate the Soai reaction's amplification (no such failure is on record)</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.07,59.33,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>A lost planetary embryo: NWA 12774's 17.5-kbar crystals point to a destroyed Mars-sized world (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed in EPSL (DOI 10.1016/j.epsl.2026.120029); the ≥17.5-kbar crystallization pressure is the geobarometer's result, stated with the modeled size range (≥1,000 km, ~3,300 km). The lost-embryo identification is the authors' stated interpretation of a measured pressure.
Layer: real · Place: Northwest Africa, Sahara (the 2019 find site of NWA 12774)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The angrite meteorite NWA 12774 (found 2019 at a Sahara site, Northwest Africa; one of fewer than 70 angrites among 80,000 cataloged meteorites) crystallized its aluminum-rich clinopyroxene at pressures of at least 17.5 kilobars — over 15 times the pressure at the bottom of the Pacific — implying a parent body at least 1,000 km in radius, modeled at ~3,300 km (Mars-sized), that formed within ~4 million years of the solar system's birth and was destroyed by a catastrophic impact that launched the fragment to Earth: Bell, Waters et al. 2026, using a new computational geobarometer, interpret the crystals as the first high-pressure record from an embryonic planet never previously identified.

Earliest attestation: Bell et al., Earth and Planetary Science Letters (2026)
Sources: Aaron S. Bell et al., 'High-pressure clinopyroxene in Northwest Africa 12774 and new geobarometric evidence for a planetary embryo-sized angrite parent body', Earth and Planetary Science Letters (2026), DOI: 10.1016/j.epsl.2026.120029 — ≥17.5 kbar crystallization pressure, parent radius ≥1,000 km (modeled ~3,300 km), formed within ~4 Myr, destroyed by impact; New Mexico Tech news (5 Aug 2026) — 'New Study Suggests an Embryonic Planet Once Shared Earth's Solar System' — Laura Waters (co-author): 'we found evidence of a massive, long-lost planet formed early in the history of the solar system but destroyed through impact'; the angrite classification (&lt;70 of 80,000 meteorites), the aluminum-rich clinopyroxene requiring great pressure, the pebble-accretion implication; Eighth-pass I.5 review 2026-10-20 (via #728 Dr. Sander Holm — The Condensation Reader): NEW CORROBORATED exhibit — a lost differentiated world within the first 4 Myr; in-family for the solar-system-formation workshop (the I.5 clock family) and #313 Selma Vinter's meteorite archive; the parent's existence is inferred, not observed — the grade covers the barometric finding and its stated interpretation
Falsifiability: A re-analysis showing the clinopyroxene barometer miscalibrated, or a chemical match of NWA 12774 to a known body (Vesta, an established angrite parent), would retire the lost-embryo reading</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>9.5,28.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Patmos 2026: the global Bible-attitudes survey — 80% theism, 20% curious nones</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the commissioning body's release as reported in detail (sample frame, cluster methodology, country-level figures); the record grades the attestation of the survey's findings, never the truth of any belief.
Layer: real · Place: Global (150 countries)
Dates: 2026-10-01 → 2026-10-17 (confidence: medium)

The Patmos World Bible Attitudes Survey (commissioned by the British and Foreign Bible Society, fielded by Gallup: 91,000+ respondents in 150 countries and 89 languages, grouped into six cultural 'clusters'; reported October 2026) found that eight out of ten people globally believe in God or a 'higher power.' Among respondents worldwide who do not consider themselves religious, one in five (20%) said they were 'interested in learning more about the Bible.' Christians aged 18-24 report the highest confidence in telling the Bible's story, finding relevant passages, and talking about its message. In western Europe / North America / Australasia ('cluster 5'), 40% say religion is important in daily life (half the global average) yet 63% say they definitely or probably believe in a higher being; 53% identify as Christian while 44% are closed/sceptical/indifferent. In central/eastern Europe ('cluster 2'): 39% say religion is important daily vs 80% believing in God or a higher power; 77% identify as Christian but only 40% say faith makes a difference daily.

Earliest attestation: British and Foreign Bible Society / Gallup, Patmos World Bible Attitudes Survey, reported October 2026 (via Evangelical Focus)
Sources: BFBS/Gallup, Patmos World Bible Attitudes Survey, reported Oct 2026 (via Evangelical Focus): 91,000+ in 150 countries, 89 languages; 80% globally believe in God or a higher power; 20% of non-religious interested in learning more about the Bible; Christians 18-24 highest confidence; cluster-5: 40% religion important daily / 63% believe in a higher being; cluster-2: 39% / 80%
Falsifiability: The Patmos report PDF contradicting the reported figures, or BFBS/Gallup retracting them.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,20.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pew May 2026: 37% say religion is gaining influence in American life (highest since 2002)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Attested by the pollster's own published report (primary) plus consistent secondary re-reports; graded as poll attestation, never the content of the attitudes measured.
Layer: real · Place: United States (national poll)
Dates: 2026-04-06 → 2026-04-12 (confidence: high)

Pew Research Center's May 14, 2026 report 'How Americans Feel About Religion's Influence in Government and Public Life' (survey of U.S. adults conducted April 6-12, 2026, ~3,592 adults, American Trends Panel) found: 37% of U.S. adults say religion is gaining influence in American life — the highest share since the series began in 2002, up 19 points in two years — while 61% say it is losing influence. 55% express a positive view of religion's role in American life. 17% say the federal government should declare Christianity the nation's official religion (up from 13% in 2024). Familiarity with the term 'Christian nationalism' is growing; support for ideas associated with it is mostly unchanged. Sharp partisan divides: 75% of Republicans view religion's influence positively vs 38% of Democrats. Margin of error ±1.9 points. The 61%-losing-influence figure was already chained on evt-mission-argument-001 (VIII.e, 2026-10-19); this record carries the wave's other toplines for the VI.34 belief side.

Earliest attestation: Pew Research Center report, released May 14, 2026 (survey fielded April 6-12, 2026)
Sources: Pew Research Center, 'How Americans Feel About Religion's Influence in Government and Public Life', released May 14, 2026 (survey Apr 6-12, 2026, ~3,592 U.S. adults, American Trends Panel); Catholic World Report, May 14, 2026 re-report: 65% of Catholics positive view / 12% negative / 22% neutral; 37% gaining influence, highest since 2002; Christianity Daily, Oct 2026 re-report: 75% of Republicans view religion's influence positively vs 38% of Democrats; 17% official-religion figure, up from 13% in 2024
Falsifiability: Pew's own report contradicting the 37%/61%/55%/17% figures, or retraction.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.0,39.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>PHerc. 139 identified as Philodemus, 'On Gods' Book 8 (June 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports agree on the identification: the June 2026 Vesuvius Challenge announcement named PHerc. 139 as Philodemus's 'On Gods' Book 8 in the same preprint cycle that delivered the first complete virtual unwrapping of a rolled scroll (PHerc. 1667); prior scholarship knew only Books 1 and possibly 3 of the work. The authorship attribution rests on the title reading, fenced as the project's claim; the cautious coverage (O'Dwyer 2026: only ~300-400 complete words from 1667, the scroll's title itself unknown) keeps the overclaim fenced — the record grades the identification, not a content summary.
Layer: real · Place: Herculaneum, Villa of the Papyri (findspot); scroll housed at the Biblioteca Nazionale di Napoli
Dates: 2026-06-25 → 2026-06-29 (confidence: high)

In June 2026, the Vesuvius Challenge team identified the title and author of the still-rolled scroll PHerc. 139 as Philodemus, 'On Gods' (peri theon), Book 8 — extending the previously known run of that work (books 1 and possibly 3 were the only ones attested). The identification was reported in the same project preprint (Angelotti et al. 2026, 'Complete virtual unwrapping and reading of a rolled Herculaneum papyrus') that announced PHerc. 1667 as the first scroll ever virtually unwrapped and read end to end.

Earliest attestation: Vesuvius Challenge June 2026 announcement; Angelotti et al. 2026 preprint 'Complete virtual unwrapping and reading of a rolled Herculaneum papyrus'.
Sources: Angelotti et al. 2026, 'Complete virtual unwrapping and reading of a rolled Herculaneum papyrus' (project preprint — the PHerc. 139 title/author identification reported alongside the PHerc. 1667 end-to-end reading); Wikipedia, 'Herculaneum scrolls' — 'Besides PHerc. 1667, the team were able to read the title and author of PHerc. 139: Philodemus, On Gods, Book 8.' (citing the project preprint); Reuters, 'complete text of a carbonised Herculaneum scroll unlocked for the first time' — June 2026 announcement of the first scroll read end to end without physical unrolling; O'Dwyer 2026 blog-roundup (the fence: PHerc. 1667's full transcription = 300-400 complete words; the work's title is unknown; late-20th-c. invasive efforts had already reduced the scroll from ~14g to ~6g)
Falsifiability: A published transcription that contradicts the title/author reading would overturn the identification; the preprint's own methods are public (the project's $1M follow-on prize requires public method-sharing).</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.348,40.807,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>First readable text from PHerc. 343 (Vesuvius Challenge $50K First Letters Prize, Oct 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Institutional primary (the Vesuvius Challenge's own announcement) plus the project's strict technical-and-papyrological criteria publicly documented plus the papyrologists' reading superimposed on the released image — the prize award is attested fact; the letter sequence is the project's papyrological reading, not yet independently replicated, so CORROBORATED not higher
Layer: real · Place: Biblioteca Nazionale di Napoli (current holding; excavated at Herculaneum, Villa of the Papyri, 40.8062, 14.3439)
Dates: 2026-10-08 → 2026-10-08 (confidence: high)

PHerc. 343, a carbonized still-rolled Herculaneum scroll (Biblioteca Nazionale di Napoli, ~14 cm long), yielded its first-ever readable text since the 79 CE eruption: researcher Erwin Nieuwlaar identified ink on windings w047/w048 of a virtually unwrapped segment — four lines of text, the third line alone meeting the Vesuvius Challenge's strict criterion of 10 readable letters in 4 cm² — reading the sequence παντογινομ (papyrologists' reading: πᾶν τὸ γινόμε[νον, 'everything that happens/is generated'). Nieuwlaar traced the surface using the Challenge's open-source spiral-fitter on the 8.64 µm scan with his own scroll-axis estimate plus a new segment-snapping method. Awarded the Vesuvius Challenge $50,000 First Letters Prize (announced ~8 October 2026, project Substack). The scroll's authorship and work remain unidentified; only a fragment was read.

Earliest attestation: Vesuvius Challenge announcement, ~8 October 2026 (scrollprize.substack.com)
Sources: Vesuvius Challenge, '$50K First Letters Prize awarded for PHerc 343' (~8 Oct 2026, scrollprize.substack.com) — the project's own announcement: Erwin Nieuwlaar, 8.64 µm scan, windings w047/w048, 'πᾶν τὸ γινόμε[νον' reading, 10 letters in 4 cm² criterion; Discover Science, 'A Herculaneum scroll was rated unreadable after attempts to open it. AI recovered 20 columns without unrolling it' (~13 Oct 2026) — restates the PHerc. 343 prize within the Oct-2026 press cycle
Falsifiability: A re-reading by the papyrology team contesting the letter sequence; demonstration that the winding meshes were misassembled</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.25,40.8333,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Research Co./NCSE July 2026 human-origins belief poll (US + Canada)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The figures come from the pollster's own published release, carried by a named science-education institution; the US/Canada gap (47% vs 62% evolved; 39% vs 20% created) is the first cross-national belief ledger of 2026 and lands squarely in the VI.31/VI.32 belief record. CORROBORATED as a belief-attestation record (the attestation rule: we grade the poll's existence, not the metaphysics).
Layer: real · Place: United States (national sample); Canada (national sample)
Dates: 2026-07 → 2026-07 (confidence: high)

In a July 2026 Research Co. two-country poll (reported by the National Center for Science Education, 2 July 2026), asked which statement comes closest to their view on the origin and development of human beings, 47% of Americans chose 'Human beings definitely (27%) or probably (20%) evolved from less advanced life forms over millions of years,' while 39% chose 'God probably (10%) or definitely (29%) created human beings in their present form within the last 10,000 years'; 14% were not sure. In Canada the split was 62% evolved (35% definitely, 27% probably), 20% created (6% probably, 14% definitely), 17% unsure.

Earliest attestation: National Center for Science Education, 'A new poll of Canadians and Americans on evolution and creationism,' 2 July 2026 (ncse.ngo/new-poll-canadians-and-americans-evolution-and-creationism)
Sources: National Center for Science Education, 'A new poll of Canadians and Americans on evolution and creationism,' 2 July 2026 — US 47% evolved / 39% created-in-present-form / 14% unsure; Canada 62% / 20% / 17%; quoted via Research Co. press release; The July-2026 webpreneurships.com piece ('41% of Americans believe dinosaurs and humans coexisted') — a recycle of the YouGov 2015 14%/27% figures with a 2026 date stamp, NOT a new poll wave; fenced to the existing evt-yougov-2015-dino-coexistence-001 record
Falsifiability: Research Co. withdrawing or correcting its release; a methodology disclosure invalidating the numbers.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.58,39.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Research Co. July 2026 two-country evolution/creationism poll (39% American creationist)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A documented, named pollster's public release carried by a named science-education body (NCSE) — graded as belief-attestation (what respondents say they believe), never as evidence about human origins; the attestation rule. The 39% creationist figure is the newest American wave on the evolution question.
Layer: real · Place: United States (with Canada as comparison sample) — a poll, not a place
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Asked 'Which of these statements comes closest to your own point of view regarding the origin and development of human beings on earth?', 39% of Americans favored 'God created human beings in their present form within the last 10,000 years' (29% 'definitely', 10% 'probably'), 47% favored evolution from less advanced life forms over millions of years (27% 'definitely', 20% 'probably'), 14% were unsure; Canadians asked the same question: 62% favored evolution, 20% creationism, 17% unsure.

Earliest attestation: Research Co. press release, 2026-07-02, as reported by the National Center for Science Education (ncse.ngo)
Sources: NCSE, 'A new poll of Canadians and Americans on evolution and creationism' (reporting Research Co. 2026-07-02 press release) — American figures: 47% evolution (27 definitely/20 probably), 39% creationist (29 definitely/10 probably), 14% unsure; Canadian: 62% / 20% / 17%; Gallup, May 1-23 2024 (published July 22, 2024): 37% creationist / 34% God-guided / 24% secular — the comparison wave on the record (evt-gallup-2024-human-origins-001 CORROBORATED); the Research Co. 39% sits 2 pts above it, inside the 37-47% historical band; Badger Herald column citing Penn State study (2026 reporting): 13% of high-school teachers put forward creationism/ID as an alternative to evolution; 60% equivocate — belief-history commentary, not evidence, carried grade-neutral
Falsifiability: Retraction of the press release, or a demonstrated sampling defect that voids the published figures.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>RNA and amino acids help each other assemble (IISER Pune, 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A peer-reviewed prebiotic-chemistry experiment: mutual promotion of RNA-like and peptide assembly demonstrated in one pot - a partial step on the RNA-peptide interaction problem, not the origin of the code, not a cell.
Layer: real · Place: Pune, India (IISER Pune)
Dates: 2026-09 → 2026-09 (confidence: high)

In September 2026, researchers at the Indian Institute of Science Education and Research (IISER) Pune reported (Communications Chemistry) that nucleotide molecules and amino acids placed together under plausible prebiotic conditions promote each other's assembly - producing short RNA-like chains and peptides in a single reaction.

Earliest attestation: Communications Chemistry, 19 September 2026 (IISER Pune)
Sources: Communications Chemistry 19 Sept 2026 (IISER Pune) - nucleotide molecules + amino acids in a single reaction produce both short RNA-like chains and peptides under plausible prebiotic conditions; addresses the RNA-peptide interaction problem before enzymes/ribosomes; researchtoday.co.za Sept/Oct 2026 'RNA and amino acids helped each other form longer molecules in an origin-of-life experiment' - 'does not recreate the origin of life'; venue only
Falsifiability: Failure to reproduce the mutual assembly promotion; or demonstration that the conditions used are geochemically implausible for the early Earth</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>73.85,18.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The RNA-only repair enzyme (DasGupta 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A reported experimental result in a peer-reviewed journal — a demonstrated partial step for the RNA world; the enzyme is engineered, not prebiotic, and maintenance is not replication.
Layer: real · Place: Notre Dame, Indiana, USA (University of Notre Dame)
Dates: 2026-07 → 2026-07 (confidence: high)

In July 2026, Saurja DasGupta's group at the University of Notre Dame reported an engineered RNA enzyme (ribozyme) that recognizes broken RNA by its distinguishing terminal phosphate group and pastes the pieces back together — a genome-maintenance function demonstrated with RNA alone, no proteins required.

Earliest attestation: DasGupta et al., Nature Communications, July 2026 (University of Notre Dame)
Sources: phys.org: 'RNA-only repair enzyme reveals how primordial life could have protected genomes' — DasGupta (Notre Dame, Chemistry &amp; Biochemistry): an engineered ribozyme that 'selectively recognizes and repairs broken RNA', targeting the phosphate at the broken end (intact strands terminate in a hydroxyl); 'the molecular tools needed to preserve the RNA-based genetic code and pass it on to future generations could have been furnished by RNA alone — no proteins required'
Falsifiability: Failure to reproduce the phosphate-specific ligation in independent laboratories; or demonstration that the recognition chemistry cannot operate under any plausible prebiotic condition</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-86.24,41.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Romania 2026: the 7.6% who say humans lived with dinosaurs</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — CORROBORATED as attestation — it grades what the Romanian respondents SAID, on the peer-reviewed print itself, never the metaphysics; the belief the 7.6% hold is DEBUNKED by the fossil record (see evt-dino-human-coexistence-001). The first published belief record on this question newer than YouGov 2015; kept strictly separate from the US figure.
Layer: real · Place: Romania (national sample)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In a 2026-published peer-reviewed survey of post-pandemic Romania (Social Sciences 2026, 15, 551 — 'Correct, Incorrect, or Uncertain? Factual Science Knowledge, Conspiratorial Anti-Science Beliefs, and "Don't Know" Responses in Post-Pandemic Romania'; weighted clean analytic sample N=1007), on the core-fact item 'Humans lived with dinosaurs (False)', 68.1% answered correctly (False), 7.6% answered incorrectly, and 24.4% said they did not know.

Earliest attestation: Social Sciences 2026, 15, 551 (MDPI) — Table 1, weighted item-level response distributions in the clean analytic sample (N=1007); DOI 10.3390/socsci15080551.
Sources: Social Sciences 2026, 15, 551 — Table 1: core fact 'Humans lived with dinosaurs (False)' — 68.1% correct / 7.6% incorrect / 24.4% DK, weighted clean analytic sample N=1007, post-pandemic Romania; DOI 10.3390/socsci15080551
Falsifiability: The journal retracting the paper or the authors publishing a correction to Table 1; or a documented methodology disclosure invalidating the sample.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>24.97,45.94,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1988 Shroud fragments re-examined (2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The study examines the actual 1988 material (not a model, not a replica) and reports specific textile observations — flax fibers, z-twisted yarns, 3/1 herringbone twill, thread counts matching published descriptions — with no visible repair; the finding is documented independently in the radiocarbon-dating literature. It narrows, but cannot close, the representativeness question: two fragments are not the whole sample strip.
Layer: real · Place: University of Arizona, Tucson (where the surviving fragments were kept)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Two surviving fragments from the University of Arizona's share of the 1988 Shroud radiocarbon sample, re-examined in 2026, are textilely consistent with the main Shroud cloth and show no evidence of invisible medieval repair or contamination that could explain away the 1988 date.

Earliest attestation: Freer-Waters, R. &amp; Jull, A. J. T., 2026 laboratory report, re-examining the surviving Arizona fragments of the 1988 sample (reported via secondary science coverage and the Radiocarbon-dating-of-the-Shroud-of-Turin article).
Sources: Freer-Waters, R. &amp; Jull, A. J. T. (2026): direct examination of two surviving fragments from the University of Arizona's share of the original 1988 sample — consistent with the main Shroud (flax, z-twisted yarns, 3/1 herringbone twill, matching thread counts); 'no signs of repairs or contamination' in the examined material; the C14 dating was performed on pieces of the original fabric. Reported via the 2026 revision of the Radiocarbon-dating-of-the-Shroud-of-Turin article.; Secondary research note (Contact Project, 2026): the authors 'reported no visible signs of repair and no obvious surface contamination that, in their view, could explain away the medieval radiocarbon result' — carried with the critics' caveat that the data are modest and local while the controversy is global.
Falsifiability: Demonstration of a repaired weave in the actual 1988 sample strip, or new multi-site sampling returning 1st-century dates, would change the picture</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-110.9501,32.2319,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Swabian Jura notation — structured marks 40,000 years before writing (Feb 2026)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Multiple independent reports agree: the PNAS publication (Feb 2026); Bentz (Saarland) and Dutkiewicz (Museum für Vor- und Frühgeschichte); 3,000+ signs on ~260 objects; 43,000–34,000 years old (Aurignacian); 22 recurring sign types; structured, repeatable sequences with object-specific patterns (crosses on tools and animal figures, not human depictions); information density comparable to early proto-cuneiform; the interpretation as a conventional external memory system (calendric, ritual, or tallying). Fenced: the 'earliest writing' headlines overstate — the authors and reports are explicit that the marks fall short of representing spoken language and 'do not qualify as writing in the strict sense.' The grade covers the notational system, not writing.
Layer: real · Place: Swabian Jura caves (Vogelherd, Geißenklösterle, Hohle Fels, Hohlenstein-Stadel)
Dates: 2026-02-23 → 2026-02-26 (confidence: high)

In February 2026, researchers (Christian Bentz, Saarland University; Ewa Dutkiewicz, Museum für Vor- und Frühgeschichte Berlin) published in PNAS a computational analysis of 3,000+ engraved signs on ~260 Aurignacian objects from the Swabian Jura (43,000–34,000 years old) — 22 recurring sign types (lines, dots, notches, crosses) in structured sequences with information density statistically comparable to early proto-cuneiform: a conventional notational system tens of millennia before writing.

Earliest attestation: PNAS, February 2026 (Bentz &amp; Dutkiewicz)
Sources: Popular Science, 23 Feb 2026: 'New evidence published today in PNAS indicates humans experimented with symbolic writing as much as 40,000 years ago.' The Adorant (~38,000 yrs), the Vogelherd mammoth (~40,000 yrs); Dutkiewicz: the artifacts date 'to the time when Homo sapiens left Africa, settled in Europe, and encountered Neanderthal.'; ScienceDaily, 25 Feb 2026: 'sequences of signs were not random decorations. Instead, they display levels of complexity and information density similar to proto-cuneiform, the earliest known writing system, which appeared around 3,000 B.C.E.' The Lion Man's evenly spaced notches; the Adorant's rows of dots and notches.; Museum für Vor- und Frühgeschichte (Staatliche Museen zu Berlin), 26 Feb 2026: 'Researchers... have proven that humans were already using complex sign systems similar to early writing systems over 40,000 years ago.' Bentz: the work uncovers 'the unique statistical properties, or statistical signature, of these sign systems, which are an early predecessor of writing.'
Falsifiability: Demonstration that the sequences are random or purely decorative</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>9.7667,48.3833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tell Abu Seifi: the August 2026 Mesen lintel find</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The discovery is multiply reported from the ministry announcement; the Mesen identification is fenced as the excavators' hypothesis, explicitly provisional — what is corroborated is the find, not the identification.
Layer: real · Place: Tell Abu Seifi, northeastern Sinai, Egypt
Dates: 2026-08-15 → 2026-08-21 (confidence: high)

On 15 August 2026 the Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announced that excavations at Tell Abu Seifi (northeastern Sinai, ~145 km northeast of Cairo) uncovered a broken colossal sandstone lintel inscribed on three sides with the titles of Ramesses II, recording restoration work on a statue of Horus described as 'Lord of the City of Mesen' — the basis for the excavators' proposal that the site is the lost New Kingdom city of Mesen on the Horus Military Road. The identification is the excavators' stated hypothesis ('a strong archaeological and historical hypothesis'), explicitly not yet definitive; further excavation continues.

Earliest attestation: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities announcement, 15 Aug 2026.
Sources: Egyptian Ministry of Tourism and Antiquities, announcement on the Tell Abu Seifi discovery, 15 Aug 2026 (via Fox News/Arkeonews reporting of the ministry release); New York Post, 'Buried ancient city uncovered in Egypt may be linked to the Book of Exodus,' 2026-08-20 — lintel split in two, Ramesses II titles, 'Lord of the City of Mesen'; fortified temple citadel with mud-brick walls, gates, stone-paved roads; YourLifeChoices, 'Ancient Egyptian discovery may offer fresh evidence for the Bible's story of Moses and the Exodus,' 2026-08-21 — ministry's 'strong archaeological and historical hypothesis' phrasing; identification 'not yet treated as definitive'; Archaeology Magazine, 'New Discoveries Link Sinai Site with Lost Egyptian City,' 2026-08-19 — mudbrick enclosure wall with gateways, temple remnants; evidence 'suggests it may be buried at Tell Abu Saifi' (via Daily News Egypt report); Arkeonews, 'Ramesses II Lintel Discovered on Egypt's Ancient Road to Canaan May Point to Lost Mesen' — Aug 2026 season: massive mudbrick structure re-read as the temple precinct's outer enclosure (not the temple itself); two successive stone-paved roads (Ptolemaic, then Roman); 26th-Dynasty schist army-scribe statue holding a damaged naos; basalt/limestone falcon fragments; Gizmodo, 'Archaeologists Say 3,200-Year-Old Inscription May Point to Lost Ancient Egyptian City,' 2026-09-08 — ministry and Hesham Hussein stress findings 'aren't conclusive'; James Hoffmeier (unaffiliated, to Live Science): ceramic evidence needed to support the hypothesis; temple structure 'almost completely destroyed'; LiveQurious, 'Inscription May Reveal Egypt's Lost City of Mesn' — Sep/Oct 2026: the 'Horus, Lord of Mesen' title recurs on a Ptolemaic-period bronze seal and on coffins from the same site, connecting the cult title to this specific place across centuries (the identification's supporting lineage, still provisional per the ministry)
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed excavation report placing the lintel in primary stratigraphic context and confirming the Mesen reading would settle the identification; attribution of the lintel to a different find-spot would falsify it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.6,31.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 ERC 'Tracing Scribes and Scrolls' project — a materials database for the Dead Sea Scrolls</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — The grant award and the project's stated scope are documented facts of 2026; the findings it will produce are future and do not move any manuscript grade.
Layer: real · Place: University of Groningen (project lead); Israel Antiquities Authority, Jerusalem (collaborator)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

In 2026 the European Research Council awarded a five-year grant of 2.5 million euros to Mladen Popović (University of Groningen), with the Israel Antiquities Authority and European laboratories, for the project 'Tracing Scribes and Scrolls': the first large-scale 'chemical signature' database of the Dead Sea Scrolls — parchment composition, collagen, processing residues, inks, organic substances — combined with palaeography, codicology and artificial intelligence, to reconstruct where the manuscripts were produced (their provenance), not just where they were hidden.

Earliest attestation: ERC grant announcement and project launch materials, 2026.
Sources: ZENIT, 'AI and materials analysis offer new insights into the Qumran scrolls' (18 Aug 2026) — the ERC grant, €2.5M, Popović/Groningen, IAA collaboration, chemical-signature database
Falsifiability: If the ERC award record is inaccurate; the record makes no claim about findings, so no finding can falsify it.</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>6.57,53.22,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>American miracle-experience belief, 2026 (belief-ledger)</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — A representative national poll on reported experience, multiply reported (beliefnet Sept 2026); grades the belief-attestation (what people say they experience), never the events' supernatural character — the attestation rule applied
Layer: real · Place: United States (national survey)
Dates: 2026 → 2026 (confidence: high)

A 2026 nationally representative American survey (Wheatley Institute at BYU, Survey of Lived Religion, n=2,005 adults via Qualtrics) found about half of Americans say they experience or witness what they consider a miracle at least a few times a year — 10% more than once a week, 9% weekly, 11% once or twice a month, 20% a few times a year; 26% seldom, 24% never. 68% report a spiritual experience, 46% within the previous month. The record grades the BELIEF/EXPERIENCE-ATTESTATION (the poll), never the supernatural character of any reported event.

Earliest attestation: Wheatley Institute (Brigham Young University) Survey of Lived Religion, nationally representative sample of 2,005 American adults, self-weighted with quotas across Protestants/Catholics/general Christians/religious minorities/nones; reported September 2026
Sources: 'Half of Americans Say They Witness Miracles Several Times a Year, Study Finds' (beliefnet.com, Sept 2026): Wheatley Institute Survey of Lived Religion, 2,005 adults — ~50% experience/witness a miracle at least a few times a year (10% more than weekly, 9% weekly, 11% monthly, 20% few-times-a-year); 76% at least seldom; 24% never; 68% spiritual experience, 46% within last month; researchers: 'American religion is changing, not disappearing'
Falsifiability: An independent representative American survey finding substantially different figures, or a published methodological audit showing the sample was unrepresentative, would force a re-grade</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Xujiayao proteome 2026 — the juluensis holotype is molecularly Denisovan</name><description>Grade: CORROBORATED — Peer-reviewed Nature paper with explicit peptide-level evidence (Denisovan-specific variant, deamidation validation, &gt;99% posterior phylogeny) — molecular, not morphological. The two-specimen scope and the morphology-only extension are honestly bounded in the record, which is why the grade stops at CORROBORATED rather than VERIFIED.
Layer: real · Place: Xujiayao, Nihewan Basin, Hebei Province, China
Dates: 2026-10-21 → 2026-10-21 (confidence: high)

Palaeoproteomics (LC-MS/MS) on two Xujiayao Middle Pleistocene specimens (parietal PA1490/XJY-6; mandible ramus PA1497/XJY-14, ~199–138 ka, Nihewan Basin, northern China) recovered 18 endogenous ancient proteins; the Denisovan-specific COL1A2-996K variant was identified in high-quality, deamidation-validated peptides, and a Bayesian phylogeny (2,074 amino-acid positions) places Xujiayao monophyletic with Denisova3 at &gt;99% posterior support — the Xujiayao hominins are molecularly Denisovan.

Earliest attestation: Xing S., Wu Z., Shao Q., Ge J., Bennett E. A., Rao H., Lin S., Zhao Y., Liu F., Liu Y., Zhou K., Feng X., Jiangzuo Q., &amp; Fu Q. (Fu Qiaomei lab, IVPP Beijing), Nature 21 Oct 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-11132-z — the peer-reviewed primary publication
Sources: Xing S. et al. (Fu Qiaomei lab, IVPP Beijing), Nature 21 Oct 2026, DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-11132-z — 'The proteome of Middle Pleistocene Xujiayao and common Denisovan morphology': 18 endogenous proteins, the Denisovan-specific COL1A2-996K variant, Bayesian monophyly with Denisova3; Nature News, 21 Oct 2026 (d41586-026-03360-0) — 'This is what the mysterious Denisovans looked like'; Fabrice Demeter (Copenhagen): 'a major step forward'; Sally Wasef on the proteomic method; bioengineer.org, 22 Oct 2026 — press echo of the Xujiayao paper (venue, not evidence)
Falsifiability: Re-analysis showing the COL1A2-996K peptides are contamination or modern; a phylogeny placing the Xujiayao proteome outside the Denisovan clade; independent replication failing to recover the Denisovan-specific variants</description><styleUrl>#corroborated</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>114.37,40.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>CORROBORATED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>real</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.75</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
</Folder>
<Folder><name>FAKE — debunked, kept with refutation (321)</name>
<Placemark><name>The Apex chert 'microfossils' are pseudofossils — the 2026 resolution</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Converging independent lines falsify the biogenic interpretation: (1) Brasier et al. 2002, Nature — the fossil-bearing black cherts are hydrothermal feeder-dyke veins cutting across strata, not seafloor beds; shapes/sizes uncharacteristic of cyanobacteria; (2) Wacey/Brasier et al. 2015, PNAS — transmission-electron-microscopy nanoscale maps show the 'microfossils' are stacks of plate-like clay minerals with carbon absorbed on mineral edges (hydrothermal chemistry), lacking the coherent rounded carbon envelopes seen in all authentic microfossils examined with the same technique; (3) Marshall et al. 2026, Nature Geoscience — the microstructures' pale color is incompatible with organic carbon at this metamorphic grade, plus indigeneity analysis; Buick (UW, commentator): the results 'virtually seal the case... that these microstructures are not ancient microfossils of cyanobacteria.' Schopf's promised rebuttal was still unpublished as of October 2026; if it publishes with new evidence, the record reopens per the falsifiability line.
Layer: fake · Place: Apex chert, near Marble Bar, Pilbara, Western Australia
Dates: -3465000000 → -3465000000 (confidence: high)

The filamentous carbon-rich microstructures of the ~3.465 Ga Apex chert (Warrawoona Group, Pilbara, Western Australia), identified by Schopf (1993) as fossil cyanobacteria — until recently accepted as the oldest morphological evidence of life on Earth — are pseudofossils: reorganized carbonaceous matter in hydrothermal chert, not cells.

Earliest attestation: Schopf 1993, Science (the cyanobacterial-microfossil claim); closed by Marshall et al. 2026, Nature Geoscience
Sources: Schopf 1993, Science 260:640 — eleven species of filamentous prokaryote proposed as authentic Archean microfossils; taken as oldest morphological evidence of life and support for early oxygenic photosynthesis; Brasier et al. 2002, Nature 416:76 — re-mapping of the type material: 'microfossils' sit ~100 m down a hydrothermal dyke complex in metalliferous black/white BaSO4-rich vein cherts; size/shape distribution uncharacteristic of cyanobacteria; reinterpreted as pseudofossils from recrystallizing amorphous-to-spherulitic silica; Wacey, Kilburn, Saunders, Cliff &amp; Brasier 2015, PNAS 112(45):13990 — TEM ultrathin-slice nanoscale maps of size, shape, mineral chemistry and carbon distribution: the Apex structures are branched, tapered worm-like chains of clay-mineral plates with carbon absorbed on edges (chemistry compatible with high-temperature hydrothermal setting); false cellular compartments; no coherent rounded walls — a direct contrast with authentic microfossils examined identically. The late Prof. Brasier's final Apex paper.; Bower et al. (Carnegie Institution, 2016) — Eoleptonema apex re-studied: a cracked quartz grain filled with carbon-rich sheet material, carbon of indeterminate (biological or abiotic) origin but the structure itself not a fossil; 60% of the originally described alleged microfossils found in material younger than the host rock — the indigeneity problem; a new indigeneity test developed; Marshall et al. 2026, Nature Geoscience (online 20 Feb 2026) — 'New study finds apex fossils aren't life': the microstructures' pale color is incompatible with truly organic carbon in rocks of this metamorphic grade ('if they were truly organic, they shouldn't be pale in such metamorphosed rocks' — Buick, UW, commenting); the most important implication 'is that they virtually seal the case that has been building for many years that these microstructures are not ancient microfossils of cyanobacteria' (Buick); Schopf noted he and a colleague had a response in preparation — still unpublished as of Oct 2026. Mars-prospecting caution: Olcott Marshall (KU): if finding convincing evidence for life on early Earth is this hard when we know life exists here, interpreting Mars data demands extra caution.
Falsifiability: Publication of Schopf's prepared response with sub-micrometer evidence of biogenic indigeneity and coherent cell-wall carbon would reopen the record (grade moves up only on that evidence); the current DEBUNKED verdict stands on Brasier 2002 + Wacey 2015 + Marshall 2026.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>119.74,-21.17,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-346500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Paluxy 'man tracks'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Cleaned and mapped, the alleged 'human' prints are elongate metatarsal dinosaur tracks, erosional features, or carved blocks (Kuban's multi-year field study); even the ICR's John Morris conceded in 1986 that none of the Taylor-site trails 'can be today regarded as unquestionably human,' and Films for Christ withdrew the 'Footprints in Stone' film.
Layer: fake · Place: Paluxy River, Glen Rose, Texas (Dinosaur Valley State Park area)
Dates: -113000000 → -110000000 (confidence: medium)

Fossil human footprints ('giant man tracks') occur alongside dinosaur tracks in the Cretaceous limestone of the Paluxy River near Glen Rose, Texas, proving humans and dinosaurs walked the same riverbed at the same time.

Earliest attestation: Stanley Taylor's research and film at the Paluxy sites, late 1960s–early 1970s (Taylor, 1973), building on earlier creationist claims from the 1930s–1940s
Sources: Glen J. Kuban, Paluxy Dinosaur / 'Man Track' controversy (paleo.cc/paluxy) — the field-work summary: all alleged 'human' prints attributed; 'I have concluded that no genuine human tracks have been found in the Paluxy riverbed'; NCSE, 'A Summary of the Taylor Site Evidence' — Morris 1986 ICR Impact article: none of the four Taylor-site trails can 'be today regarded as unquestionably human'; Films for Christ withdrew Footprints in Stone (Taylor, 1985); Wilford, J. N. 1986, 'Fossils of Man Tracks Shown to Be Dinosaurian,' The New York Times, 17 June 1986; Hastings, R. J. 1988, 'The Rise and Fall of the Paluxy Mantracks,' Perspectives on Science and Christian Faith (American Scientific Affiliation); doesgodexist.today (John Clayton's old-earth creationist ministry), 'Acrocanthosaurus Tracks in Glen Rose, Texas' — site visits to Dinosaur Valley State Park and Jake McFall's ranch: 'The dinosaur tracks are very clear, but the human tracks are what today we would call "fake news."' — the young-earth telling rejected by an old-earth creationist venue, i.e. a hostile witness to the claim (page updated ~Sep 2026, drought-exposed trackways all Acrocanthosaurus, 113 Ma)
Falsifiability: A Paluxy print with verified human-specific anatomy — a true heel-to-toe human gait with human metrical proportions, documented in situ in the limestone bed — would reopen the case. The existing specimens have been cleaned, mapped, and attributed; none qualifies.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-97.8,32.23,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-113000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The September 2026 '40,000 BCE Great Pyramid' press telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim dies on the radiocarbon: mortar charcoal dates the construction to the 4th-millennium BCE horizon, and the ceramic-sequence, graffiti, and inscription lines converge independently on Khufu's reign. A single press-reported weathering comparison with an unverified equivalence assumption cannot stand against four independent dating lines. 2026 evidence state: no peer-reviewed rebuttal published as of October 2026 (only skeptical journalism — the preprint itself was never peer-reviewed, uploaded to ResearchGate January 2026); the grade rests on the radiocarbon and convergence lines, unchanged.
Layer: fake · Place: Giza Plateau, Egypt
Dates: -40000 → -40000 (confidence: low)

The Great Pyramid of Giza was built c. 40,000 BCE — millennia before the pharaohs — as reported in a September 2026 press telling (dailygalaxy, 2026-09-10) based on an engineer's weathering measurements of the pyramid's stone.

Earliest attestation: The September 2026 press report; no peer-reviewed publication of the weathering measurements is cited in the reporting.
Sources: dailygalaxy.com 2026-09-10: 'Far Older Than Khufu: Egypt's Great Pyramid May Have Been Built in the Stone Age, as Early as 40,000 BC' — the press telling, which also carries the mainstream rebuttal (Lehner on the 3,000-year material-culture sequence; Higham on radiocarbon calibration); Bonani, G. et al. 2001 — radiocarbon dating of pyramid mortar charcoal (the dating line the claim must defeat); ecoticias, February 2026 — methodological gaps in the REM preprint: Egypt's climate was not constant over tens of millennia, modern pollution/acid rain accelerates erosion, shifting sand may have shielded blocks, base-level tourist foot traffic confounds the wear signal (skeptical journalism, not a formal rebuttal)
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed, independently replicated weathering-dating method with published methodology, plus a published answer to the radiocarbon evidence, would reopen the question. The current record rests on one press report of one engineer's measurements with an unverified core assumption.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.1342,29.9792,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-40000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Atlantis (Plato's account)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No independent attestation of any detail; the internal 9600 BCE chronology is impossible; no site of any age matches the described city.
Layer: fake · Place: Claimed: beyond the Pillars of Hercules (Strait of Gibraltar) — no physical site
Dates: -9600 → -9600 (confidence: traditional)

That Atlantis — a vast maritime empire 'beyond the Pillars of Hercules' with a concentric-ringed capital city, founded by Poseidon's descendants and destroyed in a single day and night — was a real historical place, as Plato describes it in the Timaeus and Critias (c. 360 BCE).

Earliest attestation: Plato's Timaeus and Critias, c. 360 BCE — and no earlier source, and no independent ancient source at all. Plato is the only known ancient record of its existence.
Sources: Plato, Timaeus 23e–25d; Plato, Critias 108e–121c; Chapman University Survey of American Fears 2018: 56.9% agree/strongly agree that 'ancient, advanced civilizations, such as Atlantis, once existed' (belief data, not evidence)
Falsifiability: A dated, in-context site matching the described capital (concentric rings, central Poseidon temple, orichalcum-working, Plato's dimensions) at Plato's location and within an order of magnitude of Plato's era.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-6.0,36.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-9600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Chinchorro mummy has the oldest tattoos' myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED by radiocarbon re-analysis. The Chinchorro mummy Mo-1 T28 C22 — bearing a delicate dot-mustache tattoo on the upper lip — was reported as ~6000 BP, apparently older than Ötzi. The reexamination showed the date 3830 ± 100 radiocarbon years BP (from lung tissue) had been misread as 3830 ± 100 years BC, then transposed to 5780 BP to match Allison's 'ca. 6000 BP' — and later sources pushed it further to '6000 BC' (effectively 8000 BP), some 4,000 years earlier than the actual date. The corrected comparison 'clearly identifies Ötzi as the oldest tattooed human remains discovered to date, antedating the Chinchorro mummy Mo-1 T28 C22 by at least 500 years.' The Chinchorro dot-mustache remains a genuine early tattoo — ornamental, unlike Ötzi's therapeutic lines — but it is not the oldest.
Layer: fake · Place: Coastal Chile/Peru (Chinchorro culture region)
Dates: -6000 → -2000 (confidence: high)

The world's oldest tattoos belong to a Chinchorro mummy from South America, dated to around 6000 BCE — older than Ötzi the Iceman.

Earliest attestation: Popular and scholarly sources, pre-2015 (citing Allison 1996 and derivatives)
Sources: Journal of Archaeological Science: Reports (via HAL): the misreading of 3830 ± 100 BP as 3830 BC; the transposition to 5780 BP; the '6000 BC' inflation; Ötzi antedating Mo-1 T28 C22 'by at least 500 years.'; Hyperallergic: the Chinchorro dot-mustache contender; the radiocarbon comparison 'clearly identifies Ötzi as the oldest tattooed human remains discovered to date.'; RedOrbit: the reported 4000–6000 BC age range for the Chinchorro mummy; the 4,000-year dating error.
Falsifiability: Corrected radiocarbon dating of the Chinchorro specimen</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-70.3,-18.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-6000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Late Heavy Bombardment telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent 2026 peer-reviewed far-side studies (crater-chronology and argon-dating) find a smooth monotonic impact-flux decline with no 3.9 Ga spike; the original clustering was Imbrium-basin contamination of the near-side-only Apollo sample set.
Layer: fake · Place: Chang'e-6 landing site, Apollo crater, South Pole–Aitken Basin, lunar far side
Dates: -3900 → -3900 (confidence: low)

The inner solar system suffered a cataclysmic spike in impacts at ~3.9 billion years ago — the 'Late Heavy Bombardment' — a solar-system-wide storm of asteroids that scarred the Moon, Earth, and the terrestrial planets long after their formation.

Earliest attestation: Tera, Papanastassiou &amp; Wasserburg, Earth and Planetary Science Letters 1974 — the 3.9 Ga clustering of Apollo impact-melt ages that built the hypothesis
Sources: Tera, Papanastassiou &amp; Wasserburg, Earth and Planetary Science Letters 1974 — the original 3.9 Ga impact-melt age clustering from Apollo samples; Yue et al., Science Advances (2026), DOI 10.1126/sciadv.ady9265 — Chang'e-6 far-side samples: near- and far-side impact flux indistinguishable; smooth monotonic decline, no 3.9 Ga spike; 4.247 Ga SPA norites inconsistent with the LHB model and with sawtooth alternatives; Zhang et al., Science Advances (23 July 2026) — argon-isotope dating of 28 far-side impact melts from Chang'e-6: no clustering at 3.9 Ga; 'the lunar farside bombardment was not dominated by a cataclysmic spike' (Guangzhou Institute of Geochemistry); Institute of Geology and Geophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences research news (9 Feb 2026) — institutional summary of the Yue et al. result: revised chronology, smooth decline, 'providing no support for the Late Heavy Bombardment hypothesis'
Falsifiability: A statistically robust clustering of far-side impact-melt ages at ~3.9 Ga from an independent sample set, or a new far-side basin dated to ~3.9 Ga</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-153.99,-41.64,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The envelope was invented in 1840' — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent pre-1840 envelope lines kill the invention date: (1) clay bullae c. 3500-3100 BCE; (2) centuries of lettersheets and letterlocking — folded, secured letters sent without envelopes — per the Library of Congress; (3) a pre-1840 handmade-envelope trade, with George Wilson's 1840 tiling patent itself presuming envelope-making was already a going process to improve.
Layer: fake · Place: London — where the 1840s telling was born
Dates: -3500 → 1845 (confidence: high)

The telling that 'the envelope was invented in 1840' (by Edwin Hill and Warren de la Rue, or in the year of the Penny Post).

Earliest attestation: The 1840s British envelope-machine publicity (Hill/de la Rue patent 1845) as the moment the invention story crystallized around the machine.
Sources: Library of Congress, Inside Adams — 'Opening the Envelope' (letterlocking used alongside handmade envelopes; envelopes 'have been around for centuries'; Hill patent 1845); Baylor University, Texas Collection — 'envelope history' (pre-1840s world of cut-and-fold-your-own; George Wilson 1840 tiling patent; Hill &amp; de la Rue 1845 steam machine; Crystal Palace 1851); ThoughtCo — Clay Tokens (bullae 3500-3100 BCE) — the sealed-container line
Falsifiability: The debunk could only be revived if the clay bullae, the lettersheets, and the pre-1840 handmade-envelope trade all proved modern — an archaeological impossibility on three independent lines.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Narmer Palette as 'the first historical document in the world'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The superlative fails twice: (1) the Scorpion Macehead and Narmer Macehead were found in the same deposit and are equally old early-kingship depictions, so no 'first' is undisputed; (2) whether the palette is a historical document at all — rather than an ideological charter of kingship — is the live two-camp scholarly debate (evt-narmer-palette-001, CLAIMED). The grade kills the superlative only, never the palette, never Narmer, never the unification debate.
Layer: fake · Place: Hierakonpolis (Kom el-Ahmar) — Main Deposit
Dates: -3150 → -3000 (confidence: medium)

The Narmer Palette is the oldest Egyptian — or the first-ever — historical record (Brier &amp; Hobbs: 'The oldest Egyptian historical record'; the Egyptian Museum's own page: it 'has been considered the first historical document in the world').

Earliest attestation: The superlative is modern (Brier &amp; Hobbs; the Egyptian Museum's own artefact page); the palette itself is c. 3100 BCE
Sources: Brier &amp; Hobbs — the Narmer Palette as 'The oldest Egyptian historical record'; Egyptian Museum Cairo — Narmer Palette artefact page: it 'has been considered the first historical document in the world'; The Narmer Palette scholarly debate — two camps: those who read the palette as a record of an important event vs. those who argue it is an object designed to establish the mythology of united rule (Kohler 2002; O'Connor 2011; Baines; Wengrow; Millet; Davis; Schulman/Breasted); The Scorpion Macehead and Narmer Macehead, found with the palette in the Main Deposit at Hierakonpolis — equally old, equally early depictions of kingship, sharing the 'first' field
Falsifiability: A scholarly consensus that (a) the palette records a real event and (b) no equally old Egyptian object can claim the same — neither condition is met</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>32.7794,25.0972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'candles lit the premodern world' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Convergent kills: the rushlight record (centuries of near-free light for the poor, explicitly contrasted with costly candles), the price record (8s 6d per dozen in 1788; a dedicated 1709 tax), and the wax hierarchy (beeswax reserved for churches and festivals). The candle was a costly good, not the people's light.
Layer: fake · Place: British Isles (control sample)
Dates: -3000 → 1879 (confidence: medium)

Before electric light, candles were the main source of artificial light for ordinary people in the premodern world.

Earliest attestation: The modern popular telling has no single origin; it is carried in schoolbooks, period dramas, and museum placards.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Rushlight' — Cobbett: rushlights 'cost almost nothing to produce and was believed to give a better light than some poorly dipped candles'; Aubrey 1673 earliest printed description; Gilbert White 1789; rural use into the 19th c., WWII revival; Country Life, 'From fish oil to chandeliers' — rushlights the most common early domestic lighting; the 1709 tallow candle tax, from which rushlights were exempt; oldandinteresting.com, tallow candles — England 1788: a dozen candles (ten to the pound) at 8s 6d; tallow's odor, smoke, quick melting; braided wick as a 19th-c. discovery; Lakeville Journal / Colebrook Historical Society, colonial New England lighting — tallow dips; spermaceti candles (one worth three tallow) 'regarded as very expensive' and first used by the wealthy
Falsifiability: Household account books showing candles as the cheapest available light for the poor, or production records showing tallow candles cheaper than rushlights or oil.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-2.0,54.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Jesus copied the dying-and-rising gods' claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The category itself was dismantled by scholarship: the individual parallels collapse on details (gods who died stayed dead; those who 'returned' never died; the return mechanisms are vegetation cycles, not resurrection), leaving no genuine pre-Christian structural match for the Christian claim.
Layer: fake · Place: Eastern Mediterranean (scholarly debate, not a place)
Dates: -3000 → 200 (confidence: medium)

Popular internet-level versions of Frazer's 'dying-and-rising god' category claim Jesus was modeled on pagan saviors (Osiris, Tammuz, Attis, Adonis). Scholarship killed this parallel: Jonathan Z. Smith (1987 Encyclopedia of Religion) dismissed the category as 'largely a misnomer based on imaginative reconstructions and exceedingly late or highly ambiguous texts', arguing gods who 'died' did not return and those who returned never truly 'died'. Tryggve Mettinger's 2001 monograph found pre-Christian death-and-return for Baal, Melqart, and Dumuzi — but none match the structure of the Christian claim, and he warned against hypostasizing a single type.

Earliest attestation: Frazer, The Golden Bough (1890) assembled the category; J.Z. Smith, 'Dying and Rising Gods' in Eliade's Encyclopedia of Religion (1987); Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (2001)
Sources: Jonathan Z. Smith, 'Dying and Rising Gods', Encyclopedia of Religion (1987); Tryggve N.D. Mettinger, The Riddle of Resurrection (Stockholm, 2001); Mark S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford, 2001), 104–131; Marcel Detienne, The Gardens of Adonis (1977)
Falsifiability: A pre-Christian text describing a god dying as a human death and returning bodily to the world of the living in a structure matching the Christian resurrection claim</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.24,30.04,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-3000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The "House of Abraham" at Ur — Saddam-era tourist designation with no archaeological basis</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Woolley himself selected the house 'more or less at random' from Old Babylonian-period dwellings on the assumption Abraham was a wealthy merchant (diggings.co.uk); the houses date to the Ur III / Old Babylonian periods (c. 2112–1600 BCE) and are typical domestic structures of the quarter, not distinguishable as any individual's home (madainproject.com); the 1990s restoration and 'House of the Prophet Abraham' signage were ordered under Saddam Hussein's regime as national-religious heritage branding, following modern political agendas rather than archaeological accuracy; no excavation data links any Ur dwelling to Abraham.
Layer: fake · Place: 
Dates: -2112 → -1600 (confidence: None)

A specific excavated house at Tell el-Muqayyar (Ur) is presented to visitors as the house / birthplace of the biblical Abraham.

Earliest attestation: Woolley 1920s publicity; signage 1990s
Sources: madainproject.com 'House of Abraham (Ur)' — 'entirely speculative and has no basis in archaeology or historical evidence'; Saddam-era 1990s restoration and signage as political heritage campaign; diggings.co.uk 'Abraham in Ur' — Woolley picked one of the larger Old Babylonian houses 'more or less at random' and publicised it as the House of Abraham
Falsifiability: A contemporary inscription or stratified deposit linking a specific Ur dwelling to Abraham would reopen the claim; none exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>46.1031,30.9625,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2112</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The King List's one-at-a-time dynastic sequence is false</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Contemporary inscriptions and the ARCANE chronological tables contradict the list's sequential presentation: serialized cities were contemporary, Lagash is omitted while contemporary with listed kings, and only seven pre-Sargonic SKL kings have contemporary attestation. This grades the sequentiality claim only — the artifact's existence is CORROBORATED (evt-sumerian-king-list-002), the antediluvian reign lengths are DEBUNKED (evt-skl-antediluvian-reigns-001), the parallel dynasties are CORROBORATED as overlapping (evt-skl-parallel-dynasties-001 — this record is its mirror image: where that record corroborates the overlap, this one debunks the list's claim that no overlap existed), and the legitimation thesis remains CLAIMED (evt-skl-legitimation-charter-001).
Layer: fake · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (Kish, Uruk, Ur, Lagash, Adab, Mari, Akshak)
Dates: -2100 → -1800 (confidence: medium)

The Sumerian King List presents the dynasties of the Early Dynastic period as strictly sequential — kingship held by one city at a time, passed from city to city only when the previous city was 'smitten with weapons'. Contemporary evidence contradicts this: cities the list serializes (Kish, Uruk, Ur, Lagash) held power at the same moment; the list omits the Dynasty of Lagash entirely although its rulers are contemporary with listed kings (Ur-Nanshe(k) of Lagash was roughly contemporaneous with Mes-Anne-pada of Ur; the list's 45 pre-Sargonic rulers cannot have reigned sequentially in the period Lagash's 11 rulers cover); only seven pre-Sargonic SKL kings are attested in contemporary Early Dynastic inscriptions; the ESF-funded ARCANE project's final volume on third-millennium Mesopotamia lists no pre-Sargonic SKL ruler unless corroborated by contemporary inscriptions; recent handbooks warn the SKL should not be used as a historical source for the Early Dynastic period at all. The omission of important cities (Lagash, Larsa) is treated as deliberate.

Earliest attestation: The Sumerian King List copies themselves (the 'then X was defeated and the kingship was taken to Y' formula); contradicted by contemporary Early Dynastic inscriptions
Sources: Sumerian King List overview — ARCANE final volume excludes uncorroborated pre-Sargonic SKL rulers from chronological tables; only seven pre-Sargonic SKL kings attested in contemporary Early Dynastic inscriptions; recent handbooks warn against using the SKL as a historical source for the Early Dynastic period; omission of Lagash and Larsa judged deliberate; strict sequential ordering does no justice to overlapping reigns (Oct-2026 article state); Steinkeller 2003 (via Oct-2026 archaeological synthesis): the Ur III copy (dedicated to the living divinized Shulgi, ending with Ur-Namma's 18 years) carries no Flood-era opening — the antediluvian section is a later Old Babylonian editorial layer; 'Kish, Uruk, Ur and Lagash often held power at the same moment'; Enmebaragesi the earliest king with a contemporary inscription (debated); Thorkild Jacobsen, The Sumerian King List (AS 11, 1939): Mes-Anne-pada of Ur roughly contemporaneous with Ur-Nanshe(k) of Lagash — the list's 45 pre-Sargonic rulers cannot have followed one another sequentially in the period covered by Lagash's eleven; treatment of Kish III/Akshak/Kish IV shows the author working from city-specific date lists; Lagash priest-kings absent from the list (political play in the composition); contemporaneous dynasties placed one after another as if sequential; ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1 (Oxford) — the primary text of the sequential formula itself ('then the flood swept over'; 'then X was defeated and the kingship was taken to Y')
Falsifiability: A stratified, independently dated destruction/abandonment sequence matching the list's city order, or contemporary year-name/dated-inscription evidence that the serialized Early Dynastic dynasties were in fact strictly sequential</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.2308,32.1264,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-2100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The antediluvian reign lengths are schematic, not historical</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The numbers are arithmetically constructed from sexagesimal round units (8/10/12 x 3,600 etc.), no contemporary evidence names any of the eight as historical rulers, and the section's absence from major recensions proves it is a removable editorial layer.
Layer: fake · Place: Southern Mesopotamia (Eridu, Bad-tibira, Larag, Sippar, Shuruppak — the five antediluvian cities)
Dates: -1900 → -1800 (confidence: low)

The Sumerian King List's eight antediluvian kings did not literally reign 28,800 to 43,200 years each (241,200 years total): every reign is expressible in the Sumerian sexagesimal units sars (3,600), ners (600), and sosses (60), none of the eight kings is verified as historical by any contemporary inscription or excavation, and the antediluvian section is absent from some recensions including the Nippur majority copies.

Earliest attestation: Old Babylonian copies of the Sumerian King List (the section is absent from the Nippur majority and from the Ur III USKL)
Sources: Sumerian King List overview (antediluvian reigns measured in sars/ners/sosses; none of the eight verified historical; section absent from some copies); ETCSL transliteration t.2.1.1, lines 1-39 (the eight reigns: 28,800 / 36,000 / 43,200 / 28,800 / 36,000 / 28,800 / 21,000 / 18,600; total 241,200); Weld-Blundell Prism (the best-preserved carrier of the antediluvian section)
Falsifiability: A contemporary third-millennium inscription naming Alulim or any antediluvian king with a regnal span, or a recension-independent attestation of the 241,200-year total predating the Old Babylonian copies</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>45.99,30.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Hammurabi's Code was the first written law code' (the telling, killed)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three centuries before Hammurabi, Ur-Nammu's code stood in writing — and Lipit-Eshtar and Eshnunna came two centuries earlier too. The 'first' telling dies on the tablets themselves.
Layer: fake · Place: Babylon (stele findspot: Susa, Iran — carried as 12th-century BCE war booty)
Dates: -1754 → -1754 (confidence: high)

The popular telling that Hammurabi's Code (c. 1754 BCE) was the first written law code in human history.

Earliest attestation: The 'first law code' telling is a modern popularization (textbooks, museum copy); the stele itself makes no such claim
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Code of Ur-Nammu' (earliest extant legal text; three centuries older than Hammurabi; Urukagina's code survives by reference only); worldhistory.org, 'Code of Ur-Nammu' (c. 2100-2050 BCE; Laws of Eshnunna c. 1930 BCE; Lipit-Ishtar d. c. 1924 BCE — all before Hammurabi); Schøyen Collection and Istanbul Archaeological Museum tablets of the Code of Ur-Nammu (physical artifacts); Martha T. Roth, Law Collections from Mesopotamia and Asia Minor (SBL Writings from the Ancient World, 1997) — the standard English edition carrying Ur-Nammu, Lipit-Eshtar, Eshnunna, and Hammurabi side by side; sciencenewstoday.org, 'Hammurabi's Code: The First Written Laws' (c. 2026-10-01) — 'humanity's first great experiment with written law'; killed-telling instance recrawled, graded separately on the record; lawshun.com, 'Babylonian King And The First Codes Of Law' (recrawled 2026-10-21) — "The Code of Hammurabi is widely regarded as the world's first code of law"; killed-telling instance chained, graded separately on the record; travelmate.tech, Louvre audio guide 'Hammurabi Stele Richelieu Wing Hall 3' (recrawled 2026-10-24) — 'the so-called Code of Hammurabi — the earliest collection of written laws in the history of man'; killed-telling instance chained, graded separately on the record
Falsifiability: A demonstration that the Ur-Nammu, Lipit-Eshtar, Eshnunna, and Urukagina tablets are misdated or modern forgeries — no such challenge exists in the literature</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1754</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Exodus narrative is literally accurate in every detail as written</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The literalist reading fails at scale: the text's own arithmetic intends 603,550 as a literal headcount (2025 HIPHIL paper); a two-million-person migration through the Sinai would leave archaeological traces and leaves none (Finkelstein &amp; Silberman); the internal chronology is symbolic (2666 AM, 480 = 12x40); Canaan was Egyptian territory at the supposed date. This is the 267th DEBUNKED family.
Layer: fake · Place: Sinai / Nile Delta (route traditionally placed here; unattested)
Dates: -1446 → -1200 (confidence: traditional)

The Exodus narrative (Exodus 1-15) is literally accurate word-for-word as written: the ten plagues occurred as described, the census figure of 603,550 fighting men (Numbers 1:46) is a literal headcount, and a departure of roughly two million people from Egypt is historically accurate as stated.

Earliest attestation: The Exodus narratives (Exodus-Deuteronomy); the literalist reading as a doctrine is modern (19th-20th c. fundamentalism), not ancient
Sources: Numbers 1:46 (603,550 men); Exodus 12:37 (600,000); HIPHIL Novum 10 (2025), issue 1: eleph=1,000 algebra — summing the tribal counts in Numbers 1, the writer's own totals prove eleph was intended as 1,000, not 'clan'; Finkelstein &amp; Silberman, The Bible Unearthed (2001), pp. 61-63: even a considerably smaller group than 600,000 warriors could not pass through the Sinai/Negev without leaving traces; no such traces exist; Wikipedia, 'Sources and parallels of the Exodus': scholarly consensus — symbolic chronology (Tabernacle in Anno Mundi 2666, two-thirds of a 4,000-year era; 480 years = 12x40 symbolic generations), Canaan under Egyptian control ('escaping from Egypt to Egypt'), no Jericho destruction layer, 'the lack of evidence has led scholars to conclude that the Exodus story does not represent a specific historical moment'; Merneptah stele (c. 1207 BCE): 'Israel' in Canaan by then — terminus ante quem; says nothing about an exodus; Hoffmeier (maximalist): concedes no direct archaeological evidence for the Exodus as described (via 'The Historicity of the Exodus'); Finkelstein &amp; Silberman, The Bible Unearthed (2001): the exodus narrative may encode vague memories of the Hyksos expulsion (c. 1550 BCE), refashioned centuries later; ABC News poll, Feb 2004 (n=1,011): 64% said the Red Sea parting story is literally true word-for-word — the belief ledger, graded as attestation not evidence
Falsifiability: A stratified Late Bronze Age Sinai/Negev occupation record consistent with a migrating population of ~10^5-10^6 people, or a contemporary Egyptian record of the plagues and mass departure, would falsify this verdict.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>33.0,30.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1446</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Marco Polo brought noodles to Italy from China</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claimed event is contradicted three ways: pasta was made and exported in Italy a century before Polo's return (al-Idrisi 1154, Genoese inventories), Polo's own book contains no noodle-bringing passage and compares foreign food to familiar 'lasagne', and the story's earliest traceable form is a 1929 American pasta-trade article, not any medieval source.
Layer: fake · Place: Venice (the claimed homecoming, 1295)
Dates: -1295 → 1295 (confidence: traditional)

Marco Polo, returning to Venice in 1295 from his years in Asia, brought noodles home from China, thereby introducing pasta to Italy.

Earliest attestation: The Macaroni Journal, October 1929 — the U.S. pasta industry's trade journal: the earliest form of the story the food-history literature has produced (a piece about Polo arriving at a destination 'seeming more South Pacific than Chinese' and finding natives drying strands of dough; later retellings add a sailor named Spaghetti). No earlier attestation traced.
Sources: Macaroni Journal, October 1929 (U.S. pasta-industry trade journal) — earliest traceable form of the legend; Marco Polo, Description of the World (dictated c. 1298–1299): contains no passage about bringing noodles home; the Fansur chapter compares sago-flour dishes to 'lasagne'; John Dickie, Delizia! The Epic History of the Italians and Their Food (2007) — documents the myth as modern manufacture; Jen Lin-Liu, On the Noodle Road (2013); NPR interview 20 July 2013; al-Idrisi, Nuzhat al-mushtaq (Book of Roger), 1154: Trabia (Sicily) itriyya industry — pasta already in Italy 141 years before Polo's return; Genoese notarial records: 13th-century inventories listing macaroni (commonly cited 1279; some retellings give 1244 or 1284; Spaghetti Museum, Pontedassio)
Falsifiability: A pre-1929 text attributing noodle-bringing to Polo, or a passage in the Description of the World describing noodles carried home — either would revive the claim</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>12.3355,45.4408,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1295</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Phoenicians invented the alphabet' — the schoolbook claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Proto-Sinaitic inscriptions (Serabit el-Khadim, Wadi el-Hol) put alphabetic writing at c. 1800 BCE — roughly seven centuries before the Phoenician abjad. The Phoenicians adapted and standardized an existing alphabet; they did not invent it. Christopher Rollston (2022): 'There is a wide misconception in the general public that the Phoenicians invented the alphabet. They didn't. They adapted and standardized the alphabet several centuries after it was invented.'
Layer: fake · Place: The Levantine coast (Byblos, Tyre, Sidon — the Phoenician cities that did the standardizing, not the inventing)
Dates: -1100 → -1000 (confidence: traditional)

The Phoenicians invented the alphabet around 1050 BCE — the schoolbook version: the Phoenician abjad as the first true alphabet.

Earliest attestation: The claim as stated is a modern textbook formulation; the Proto-Sinaitic evidence that kills it dates to 1905-1916 (Petrie's finds, Gardiner's decipherment)
Sources: Rollston, C. (2022), 'Oldest Canaanite Sentence Found,' Bible History Daily / BAS — 'a wide misconception in the general public that the Phoenicians invented the alphabet. They didn't.'; Naveh, J. (1982), Early History of the Alphabet — the standard history of the transmission; Companion record evt-proto-sinaitic-alphabet-001 (CORROBORATED) — the ~1800 BCE evidence that does the killing
Falsifiability: The grade is closed unless the entire Proto-Sinaitic corpus — Petrie's 1905-1906 finds, Gardiner's 1916 decipherment, the Wadi el-Hol inscriptions published 1999/2005 — is shown to be fraudulent or misdated by ~700 years. Extraordinary evidence would be required.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.5,33.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Goatherd Kaldi Discovers Coffee (legend)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — First written appearance is 1671 CE — eight centuries after its claimed date; the personal name itself is a 20th-century invention; zero pre-15th-century evidence of coffee as a drink anywhere.
Layer: fake · Place: Kaffa region, Ethiopian highlands (legend's setting)
Dates: -850 → -800 (confidence: traditional)

A 9th-century Abyssinian goatherd named Kaldi discovered coffee when his goats became energetic and 'danced' after eating red berries from a certain shrub; he tasted the berries himself and brought them to a monastery.

Earliest attestation: Antoine Faustus Naironus, De Saluberrima potione Cahue (Rome, 1671) — a Maronite professor of Oriental languages; the first printed treatise devoted to coffee, describing a herder in the 'Kingdom of Ayaman, Arabia Felix'.
Sources: Antoine Faustus Naironus, De Saluberrima potione Cahue (Rome, 1671) — first written appearance of the legend; Ralph Hattox, Coffee and Coffeehouses: The Origins of a Social Beverage in the Medieval Near East (1985) — the legend is untraceable before the 17th century; Mark Pendergrast, Uncommon Grounds (1999) — traces the Kaldi story's paper trail from Naironus forward; William H. Ukers's coffee writings — the name 'Kaldi' itself is a 20th-century invention propagated by Ukers; No confirmed evidence, historical or archaeological, of coffee as a drink before the 15th century (see evt-coffee-sufi-yemen-001)
Falsifiability: A pre-1671 source (manuscript, inscription, or document) telling the goatherd story in any recognizable form would falsify the debunking; Ethiopia's genuine coffee-native flora and early chewing practices are not in dispute and do not require the legend</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>37.5,7.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-850</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Indian rope trick as an ancient feat — the legend that began in Chicago</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Lamont's 2004 audit found no accurate reference to the classic trick predating 1890; the legend's origin is a documented 1890 newspaper hoax by the Chicago Tribune's John E. Wilkie, which the paper later admitted was false; the London Magic Circle's 1930s reward for an outdoor performance of the classic trick went unclaimed; older rope-effect accounts describe different tricks.
Layer: fake · Place: India (where the legend locates the feat)
Dates: -0800 → 1890 (confidence: traditional)

The classic Indian rope trick — a fakir throws a rope into the air, it stays rigid without visible support, a boy climbs it and vanishes at the top, the magician climbs after him and throws down the boy's dismembered limbs, and the boy reappears unharmed — was actually performed as an ancient feat in India.

Earliest attestation: The classic version's modern legend begins with the 1890 Chicago Tribune hoax story; the earliest accurate attestation of the full classic sequence is the hoax itself, not an Indian eyewitness.
Sources: Peter Lamont — 'The Rise of the Indian Rope Trick: How a Spectacular Hoax Became History' (Little, Brown, 2004): no accurate references to the trick predating 1890; later stage performances were inspired by Wilkie's account; the older accounts (Shankara, Ibn Battuta, Jahangir) describe different tricks; Wikipedia — 'Indian rope trick': 'In the 1990s the trick was said by some historians to be a hoax perpetrated in 1890 by John Wilkie of the Chicago Tribune'; Robert Elliot of the London Magic Circle, offering a substantial reward in the 1930s, demanded that 'the rope must be thrown into the air and defy the force of gravity, while someone climbs it and disappears' — unclaimed; Peter Lamont — 'History of an illusion' (thefreelibrary.com, from History Today): the legend's rise began with a hoax newspaper story in Chicago; John E. Wilkie was city editor of the Chicago Tribune (later chief of the United States Secret Service); the paper later admitted the story was false
Falsifiability: A dated, authentic pre-1890 account matching the classic version (rope rigid, boy climbs and vanishes, limbs fall, boy reappears) — Lamont's audit found none; the ancient accounts describe different effects</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>79.0,22.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The winged angel: a popular image the text never describes</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The text is checkable: no mal'akh in the Hebrew Bible is ever given wings; the winged classes are named separately. The popular image is an art-historical accretion, not a textual claim.
Layer: fake · Place: Ancient Judah (textual origin); the image belongs to later Christian art
Dates: -800 → 400 (confidence: medium)

In the Hebrew Bible, the mal'akhim (angels, 'messengers') are never described as having wings. They appear as men: Abraham feeds three of them lunch in Genesis 18, the men of Sodom mistake two of them for human travelers in Genesis 19, and Hebrews 13:2 preserves the tradition that one might entertain angels unawares. Wings in the Hebrew Bible belong exclusively to two distinct classes — the cherubim (Exodus 25:20, Ezekiel 10) and the seraphim (Isaiah 6:2, 6, 7) — which the texts treat as separate from angels in general. The winged angel of Christmas trees, paintings, and popular imagination is a later artistic convention (early Christian art, 4th century CE onward), not a biblical description.

Earliest attestation: Genesis 18:1-8 and 19:1-3 (angels as men, eating food); Exodus 25:20 and Ezekiel 10 (wings on cherubim); Isaiah 6:2 (six wings on seraphim); New Interpreter's Dictionary of the Bible s.v. 'Angel': 'the winged seraphim and cherubim are classes of heavenly beings distinguishable from angels in general'
Sources: Hebrew Bible — Genesis 18:1-8; Genesis 19:1-3; Exodus 25:18-22; Isaiah 6:1-7; Ezekiel 1, 10; Hebrews 13:2; New Interpreter's Dictionary of the Bible, s.v. 'Angel' / 'Cherub, Cherubim' / 'Seraph, Seraphs' (quoted: mal'akhim appear as men; wings belong to cherubim and seraphim, 'distinguishable from angels in general'); Hamori, Esther J., God's Monsters (Broadleaf Books, 2023) — the biblical heavenly beings as the texts describe them vs. the popular image
Falsifiability: A single Hebrew Bible verse explicitly describing a mal'akh with wings would overturn this record</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'empty land' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Archaeology (Mizpah, Benjamin surveys, Ein Gedi, Mosa, Tell el-Ful) proves settlement continuity through the exilic period; no scholar defends literal emptiness — the live debate is the depopulation level (Faust ~15% vs Lipschits ~1/3 survival), not whether anyone remained.
Layer: fake · Place: Mizpah (Tell en-Nasbeh) — Babylonian administrative center after 586 BCE
Dates: -586 → -538 (confidence: traditional)

The popular reading that Nebuchadnezzar's 586/587 BCE conquest emptied the land of Judah entirely — 'the land enjoyed its sabbaths' (2 Chronicles 36:21) taken as literal demography: everybody deported, nobody left.

Earliest attestation: 2 Chronicles 36:20-21 — composed 4th c. BCE, two centuries after the events; a theological historiography, not a census.
Sources: Hans M. Barstad, The Myth of the Empty Land (Oslo: Scandinavian University Press, 1996) — continuity at Mizpah, Ein Gedi, Mosa, Tell el-Ful; Oded Lipschits, The Fall and Rise of Jerusalem: Judah under Babylonian Rule (Eisenbrauns, 2005) — ~one-third survival estimate; Mizpah as Babylonian administrative center; Avraham Faust, Judah in the Neo-Babylonian Period: The Archaeology of Desolation (SBL, 2012) — critical counter: ~15% survival, 'relative emptiness' but not literal; Benjamin consensus reexamined; Lipschits &amp; Blenkinsopp eds., Judah and the Judeans in the Neo-Babylonian Period (Eisenbrauns, 2003) — the debate volume; 2 Kings 25:12; Jeremiah 39:10; 52:16 — the Bible's own counter-testimony: 'the poorest of the land' left behind as vinedressers and farmers; Mizpah (Tell en-Nasbeh) excavations — continued occupation and administrative function through the exilic period
Falsifiability: A complete, survey-wide settlement hiatus across Benjamin and the Judean hills for 586–538 BCE would falsify; the surveys show the opposite</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.18,31.88,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-586</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cyrus Cylinder as 'first declaration of human rights'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The cylinder is genuine, but it is a traditional Babylonian royal foundation text, not a rights charter; its restoration passage names only Mesopotamian sanctuaries and never mentions Jews, Judah, or a general release of exiles.
Layer: fake · Place: Babylon (findspot of the cylinder)
Dates: -539 → -538 (confidence: high)

The Cyrus Cylinder is the world's first declaration of human rights, in which Cyrus the Great freed the Jewish exiles from Babylon and granted religious freedom to all peoples of his empire.

Earliest attestation: Cyrus Cylinder, BM 90920 (Rassam 1879 find), Akkadian cuneiform foundation-deposit text
Sources: Cyrus Cylinder, BM 90920 (British Museum); ll. 30-32 restoration passage — sanctuaries east of the Tigris (Agade, Eshnunna, Zamban, Me-Turnu, Der, Gutium region); names no Jews, no Judah; Amélie Kuhrt (1983), 'The Cyrus Cylinder and Achaemenid Imperial Policy': the piety passage as literary device contrasting Cyrus with the 'impious' Nabonidus; privileges to strategic cities following earlier Assyrian practice, not a general repatriation policy; Irving Finkel (2013), The Cyrus Cylinder (British Museum): the restoration lines concern Mesopotamian sanctuaries only and 'make no reference to the Judean refugees'; Lester L. Grabbe: Persian religious policy 'not that different from the basic practice of the Assyrians and Babylonians before them' — tolerating, not promoting, local cults; Shah of Iran's 1971 2,500-year celebrations: origin of the 'first declaration of Human Rights' framing; 2026 instance of the same telling family (DEBUNKED held, no supersession — the family kills the reading, not each retelling): usfandomstareveryday.com, c. Feb-Mar 2026 — "Scholars have translated its inscriptions, revealing Cyrus the Great's decree to restore the exiled Jewish people to their homeland" — the cylinder's ll. 30-32 name no Jews, no Judah; the restoration passage concerns Mesopotamian sanctuaries east of the Tigris (Ashur, Susa, Agade, Eshnunna, Zamban, Me-Turnu, Der, Gutium). The piece's "challenges Islamic teachings" framing is polemics, not evidence.; Nathan Steinmeyer (Sept 13, 2026), 'The Cyrus Cylinder,' Bible History Daily / Biblical Archaeology Society: restates the held scholarly position for the 2026 record — the cylinder is a foundation inscription (buried, 'meant to be read by the gods alone'), never speaks of Jerusalem or the Judeans (Babylonian cult centers only), and follows the earlier royal-inscription tradition (Kuhrt 1983; Esarhaddon precedent; 2009–2010 British Museum duplicate fragments from a tablet, likely a scribal-office copy per Finkel 2012). Grade-neutral restatement — DEBUNKED held, no supersession (Turin-shroud precedent).; YouTube documentary 'The Cyrus Cylinder Myth: Was It Really the First Human Rights Charter?' (~2026-10-12) — reads all 45 lines, dates the 'first human rights charter' label to the 1971 Pahlavi 2,500-year celebrations, cites Walker / Arnold &amp; Michalowski / Kuhrt (1983) / Finkel — a popular restatement of this record's held DEBUNKED grade, no new evidence (venue exhibit, grade-neutral, chained per the Turin-shroud precedent); UNESCO 43rd General Conference resolution (Samarkand, 2026-11-06; sponsors Iran, Tajikistan, Iraq) recognizing the Cylinder as 'the earliest written expression of freedom, justice, tolerance, and respect for cultural diversity' — an institutional/political act, not evidence; the scholarly autopsy (Kuhrt 1983; Finkel) stands — the official language outruns the grade (venue note, grade-neutral)
Falsifiability: A cylinder passage naming Judah or a general release of exiles would overturn the debunking; the extant text has none</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4275,32.5355,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-539</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Liubo was chess" — the identity claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Liubo is a documented distinct game (TLV board, racing/capture mechanics, divination use) with no chess pieces, chess moves, or chess board; even Needham framed it as a possible 'pre-game' inspiration, not as chess. The identity claim has no scholarly defense.
Layer: fake · Place: Han China (liubo's attested range)
Dates: -0500 → 0220 (confidence: medium)

Liubo, the Han-dynasty Chinese board game, was an early form of chess (i.e., the same game family, not merely a predecessor or inspiration).

Earliest attestation: The claim is modern; liubo itself is attested in Han tombs (e.g. Mawangdui) and texts. Needham (1962) — the chief champion of a Chinese route — treated the liubo-family board only as a possible divination 'pre-game' inspiration for chess, never as chess itself.
Sources: Joseph Needham, Science and Civilisation in China, Vol.4 Part 1 (CUP, 1962), pp. 314–334 — the liubo-family board as possible divination-board 'pre-game', not as chess; Excavated liubo sets (Han tombs, e.g. Mawangdui): TLV-pattern board, twelve-piece racing/capture mechanics, no chess armies; ChessBase, 'On the origins of chess (3/7): Chinese origin of chess' — the serious China-origin argument runs through Xiang Jing (569 CE) and Needham's divination hypothesis, not through liubo-identity
Falsifiability: A liubo text or excavated set showing the six-piece chess-family army structure, 8×8 field, or shatranj-like movement rules.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>108.94,34.34,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Sacrificed Son Unnamed</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Qur'an 37:102-107 names no son — the text says only 'O my son'. Early Islamic exegesis was genuinely divided: al-Tabari's Jami' al-Bayan records both views and inclines to Isaac on philological grounds; reports attributed to Ibn 'Abbas exist on both sides (al-Dhahabi attributes the Isaac-reports to Ka'b al-Ahbar); the Ishmael reading became the majority only later (Ibn Taymiyya, Ibn Kathir, 14th c.). The 'Qur'an names Ishmael' telling is a misreading compounded by a later consensus retrojected onto the text.
Layer: fake · Place: (varies by record — see sources)
Dates: -500 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

The Qur'an names the son Abraham was commanded to sacrifice as Ishmael

Earliest attestation: (see sources)
Sources: Qur'an 37:102-107 (Arabic text: no son named); Al-Tabari, Jami' al-Bayan (c. 883-923); Reem Firestone, 'The Other Ishmael in Islamic Scripture and Tradition' (almuslih.org, n. 19): 'the Qur'an does not specifically name the intended sacrifice'
Falsifiability: New primary documents could refine or overturn any of these records; grades move only on evidence.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The '300 vs Millions' reading of Thermopylae — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice from the ancient record itself: (1) the Persian 'millions' — Herodotus 7.186's 2,641,610 is rejected by modern scholarship as logistically impossible (modern consensus ~200–250,000 for the whole invasion force; Ctesias, the only other ancient witness to the battle's scale, says 80,000 fought at Thermopylae); (2) the '300 alone' — Herodotus's own contingent list (7.202) totals ~5,200–7,000 Greeks on the first two days, and on the last day 700 Thespians stayed and died with the 300 Spartans (7.222), plus 400 Thebans and likely helots. The odds were still enormous; the numbers are not.
Layer: fake · Place: Thermopylae (the Hot Gates), Phthiotis, Greece — the reading is about this pass
Dates: -480 → -480 (confidence: traditional)

At Thermopylae, 300 Spartans alone faced millions of Persian soldiers — the pop version of the battle (the school shorthand, Frank Miller's 1998 graphic novel, Zack Snyder's 2007 film): three hundred men against an army counted in the millions.

Earliest attestation: Herodotus, Histories VII.186 (the 2.6-million figure; c. 450s–420s BCE) — the ancient root of the modern myth; the pop form is 20th-century
Sources: Herodotus, Histories VII.186 — 1,700,000 infantry + 80,000 cavalry + 20,000 in chariots/camels + 517,610 in the fleet = 2,317,610 fighting men brought 'as far as Thermopylae'; 2,641,610 total with support/allies — the famous 2.6 million; Ctesias (via later fragments) — 800,000 as the total army Xerxes assembled; 80,000 Persians fought at Thermopylae — the only other ancient figure for the battle itself; Simonides (contemporary poet) — 4 million; the ancient inflation had multiple versions; Modern scholarly consensus (e.g., via the Battle of Thermopylae survey literature): the ancient millions rejected as unrealistic; modern estimates ~200–250,000 for the total invasion force, the Thermopylae force probably the majority (~200,000 by one estimate; range 120,000–300,000 across modern scholars); Herodotus VII.202–203 — his own Greek contingent list, totaling ~5,200 hoplites (plus Locrians and Malians, ~7,000 with all contingents) on the first two days; Herodotus VII.222 — the 700 Thespians 'very willingly stayed' to die with Leonidas; VII.233 — the 400 Thebans; helot attendants reported in the later tradition; Herodotus VII.206 — Sparta sent only 300, claiming it was their avantgarde (prophasis) — the '300' was a token force, not the army; Belief ecology: Frank Miller's '300' (1998 graphic novel) and Zack Snyder's '300' (2007 film) — the millions-vs-300 image's modern engine; no published Pew/Gallup/YouGov survey asks the question, so no belief number is attached
Falsifiability: A contemporary Persian administrative document (in the class of the Persepolis Fortification tablets) mustering millions for the 480 expedition, or archaeological evidence of a camp and logistics footprint sized for millions</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>22.5364,38.7961,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-480</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Saqqara Bird glider claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Rejected by mainstream science: Richard P. Hallion judged it 'far too heavy and unstable itself to fly'; Levitt &amp; Gross note replicas only glide when rebuilt in balsa wood WITH a horizontal stabilizer the original lacks; a 2023 CFD simulation (Institute of Aerospace Technology, Bremen, from a 3D scan) found low glide ratio, center of mass behind the neutral point (pitch-unstable), and asymmetric lift causing uncontrolled roll — 'not consistent with... ancient knowledge of aerodynamics.'
Layer: fake · Place: Saqqara necropolis, Egypt (now Egyptian Museum, Cairo)
Dates: -300 → -100 (confidence: medium)

The Saqqara Bird (sycamore-wood carving, ~200 BCE) is a model of a glider, proving ancient Egyptians understood aerodynamics.

Earliest attestation: Excavated 1898 at Saqqara; rediscovered in storage 1969 by Khalil Messiha, who proposed the glider reading
Sources: Saqqara Bird article: Messiha's hypothesis, Hallion's verdict, Levitt &amp; Gross on the balsa-plus-stabilizer problem, and the 2023 Bremen CFD study; Kevin Desmond (history of airplanes): no evidence found for the aerodynamics claim
Falsifiability: A flight-capable replica built from the original's actual materials (sycamore, not balsa) and geometry (no added horizontal stabilizer) would reopen the question. Every test to date fails without those two modifications.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>31.2165,29.8713,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Baghdad battery</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The artifacts exist (one Ctesiphon jar at the MET), but the battery function is unsupported: Prof. Elizabeth Stone (Stony Brook), returning from Iraq in 2012, stated she does 'not know a single archaeologist who believed that these were batteries'; no wires, terminals, or acid residues; the jars closely resemble known scroll-storage vessels from Seleucia.
Layer: fake · Place: Khujut Rabu'a, northeast of Baghdad, Iraq
Dates: -250 → 640 (confidence: low)

The 'Baghdad battery' vessels (Khujut Rabu'a, near Baghdad) were ancient electric batteries, proving knowledge of electrochemistry ~2,000 years before Volta.

Earliest attestation: Excavated 1936 at Khujut Rabu'a (dig led by Sherif Yousif and Jawad al-Saffar); published as a battery hypothesis by Wilhelm König (assistant/director, Baghdad Antiquities Administration)
Sources: Baghdad Battery article: physical description, dating dispute (Simpson), resemblance to Seleucia scroll vessels; Elizabeth Stone (2012): no archaeologist she knows believes they were batteries
Falsifiability: Residue analysis showing acidic electrolyte remains, wiring, or connected multi-cell assemblies would reopen the question. After decades of attention, none has been produced; no archaeologist defends the battery function.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>44.4,33.34,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-250</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Parchment was invented at Pergamum under a papyrus embargo</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) animal-skin writing surfaces predate Eumenes II by two millennia — Egyptian examples from the 24th c. BCE (NTU history-of-the-book), leather writing c. 2500 BCE Egypt, Herodotus (5th c. BCE) describing books written on skins; McClintock &amp; Strong (1870): Pliny, 'without good grounds, places the invention as late as 196 B.C.' (2) The embargo itself is single-source Pliny-via-Varro — no independent attestation of Ptolemy's export ban exists. The name 'parchment' (pergamena) is genuinely Pergamene; the invention is not.
Layer: fake · Place: Pergamum (Bergama, Turkey) — the claimed site of the invention
Dates: -197 → -158 (confidence: low)

Parchment — prepared animal skin used as a writing surface — was INVENTED at Pergamum in the 2nd c. BCE, when King Eumenes II's scribes, cut off from Egyptian papyrus by Ptolemy's export ban during the Alexandria-Pergamum library rivalry, were forced to invent the material (the 'pergamena' story, from Pliny NH 13.21 citing Varro).

Earliest attestation: Pliny the Elder, Natural History 13.21 (1st c. CE), citing Varro: 'when owing to the rivalry between King Ptolemy and King Eumenes about their libraries, Ptolemy suppressed the export of paper, parchment was invented at Pergamum.' No earlier source; no independent source.
Sources: Pliny the Elder, Natural History 13.21, citing Varro — the single source for the invention story ('parchment was invented at Pergamum' after Ptolemy's export suppression); the claim under test; NTU History of the Book blog — earliest documents on animal skin from the 24th c. BCE in ancient Egypt, two millennia before Eumenes II; McClintock &amp; Strong, Cyclopedia of Biblical, Theological and Ecclesiastical Literature (1870), 'Parchment' — 'Pliny, without good grounds, places the invention as late as 196 B.C.'; skins used 'at least as early as 500 years B.C.', Herodotus on skin books; Kenyon (1932), via the Lochac College of Scribes parchment paper — parchment documents (Avroman 88/22 BC; Dura-Europos 189-196 BC) already in circulation before the Pergamum refinement, and parchment 'in use before the time of the Pergamum library'; Reed (1975) — the Pergamene contribution was REFINEMENT, not invention: the simplified liquor bath and stretched-state drying that made the durable, smooth, uniformly opaque sheet (cited via the Lochac summary)
Falsifiability: A pre-Pergamene documentary record of skin writing destroys it — such records exist in abundance, which is why the grade is DEBUNKED and not merely CLAIMED.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>27.1836,39.1322,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-197</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The word-for-word Decalogue transmission telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The telling dies on the documents: Exodus 20 and Deuteronomy 5 give different Sabbath rationales and different wording for the same commandments (verifiable by reading any two printed Bibles — evt-decalogue-two-versions-001, CORROBORATED); the Nash Papyrus (2nd c. BCE) carries a harmonized Decalogue matching neither canonical version (evt-nash-papyrus-001, CORROBORATED); the Samaritan Pentateuch adds a tenth commandment (Mount Gerizim) absent from Jewish and Christian texts (evt-samaritan-decalogue-gerizim-001, CORROBORATED). Three independent witnesses, three different Decalogues. DEBUNKED as a transmission claim; the telling's persistence is graded as a belief phenomenon, not an evidence claim.
Layer: fake · Place: Jerusalem (textual tradition; witnesses span Egypt, Samaria, Qumran)
Dates: -0150 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Ten Commandments have been transmitted word-for-word identical from Moses to the present day, with no variation, no alternate versions, and no textual development. FALSE: the documentary record carries the Decalogue in multiple differing versions — Exodus 20 vs Deuteronomy 5 (different Sabbath rationales, different wording), the Nash Papyrus (2nd c. BCE, a harmonized pre-Masoretic text that matches neither), and the Samaritan tenth commandment (Mount Gerizim, absent from Jewish and Christian versions). The 'word-for-word' telling is the 293rd DEBUNKED family: the identical-transmission telling.

Earliest attestation: The variant Decalogue texts themselves (Exodus 20 vs Deuteronomy 5; Nash Papyrus 2nd c. BCE; Samaritan Pentateuch).
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A single uniform Decalogue text across all ancient witnesses (Masoretic, Nash, Samaritan, Septuagint, Qumran) would falsify; the witnesses disagree.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Aztecs built the pyramids of Teotihuacan</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) chronology — the civic core burned c. 550 CE, ~775 years before the 1325 founding of Tenochtitlan; (2) the Mexica's own attribution — they coined the Nahuatl name Teotihuacan ('place where gods were born') centuries after the fall and credited the gods, never themselves.
Layer: fake · Place: Teotihuacan, Valley of Mexico
Dates: -100 → 250 (confidence: medium)

The Mexica (Aztecs) built the pyramids of Teotihuacan — the Pyramid of the Sun, the Pyramid of the Moon, and the Temple of the Feathered Serpent.

Earliest attestation: Modern popular conflation — no ancient source supports it. The Mexica's own tradition (Florentine Codex Book X, Sahagun) attributes the ruins to the gods, never to themselves.
Sources: Wikipedia, Teotihuacan — 'The name Teotihuacan was given by the Nahuatl-speaking Aztecs centuries after the fall of the city around 550 CE' (glossed 'birthplace of the gods' / 'place where gods were born'; Thelma D. Sullivan: 'place of those who have the road of the gods'); Scientific American, 'Decoding Mexico's City of Gods' — 'Sometime in the 14th century, the first Mexica found their way into the valley'; they named the Avenue of the Dead and the two pyramids and called the city Teotihuacan, 'the place where gods are born'; Bernardino de Sahagun, Florentine Codex, Book X — the Mexica Fifth-Sun creation account set at Teotihuacan (the gods, not the Mexica, as builders); George L. Cowgill, Ancient Teotihuacan (Cambridge University Press, 2015) — the 550 CE burning and the multiethnic-builder debate
Falsifiability: A Mexica-period (14th–16th c.) construction inscription, or a sealed-context Mexica artifact layer beneath the Pyramid of the Sun's construction fill</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-99.8628,19.6925,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Library of Alexandria — the single-catastrophic-fire claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Every named destruction event fails as a single-fire explanation: Strabo's library outlives Caesar's fire, five contemporary accounts of 391 CE mention no library, the 642 story is a 13th-century fabrication, and the institution declined over centuries through defunding and unreplaced scrolls.
Layer: fake · Place: Alexandria, Egypt (Brucheion quarter, traditional)
Dates: -48 → 642 (confidence: low)

The Great Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single catastrophic fire (Julius Caesar's fire of 48 BCE being the most-cited candidate).

Earliest attestation: Seneca (1st c. CE, quoting Livy) on Caesar's harbor fire; no ancient source describes a single destruction of the Library itself.
Sources: Strabo, Geography 17.1.8 (c. 25 BCE) — describes the Mouseion and its library still functioning ~23 years after Caesar's fire; Caesar, Civil War 3.111 — describes burning ships in the harbor; says nothing about the Library; Five contemporary accounts of the Serapeum's destruction, 391 CE — none mention a library burning; Bar Hebraeus (13th c.) — the Caliph Omar story; fabricating ~600 years after the 642 conquest; Luciano Canfora, The Vanished Library (1987) — gradual-decline synthesis; Bernard Lewis — the Omar story as later fabrication
Falsifiability: A contemporary account describing the main Library burning in a single event, or an ancient source listing the destruction as one date — none exists in the record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>29.9187,31.2001,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-48</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that Jesus of Nazareth was born on December 25</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The date dies on the documents, not on skepticism: no Gospel gives a date, the December 25 date first appears c. 336 CE as a liturgical assignment, and the 'stolen from a pagan solstice festival' story it is usually paired with has thin evidence (Hijmans).
Layer: fake · Place: Bethlehem (traditional)
Dates: -0004 → 0004 (confidence: traditional)

Jesus of Nazareth was born on December 25 — that the calendar date of his birth is known and is the 25th of December.

Earliest attestation: No Gospel gives a month, day, or year. The December 25 date first appears in the Chronograph of 354 (Philocalian Calendar), whose Depositio Martirum ('VIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae') was compiled c. 336 CE — three centuries after the event.
Sources: The Gospels of Matthew and Luke: the nativity narratives name no month, day, or year; Chronograph of 354 (Philocalian Calendar), Depositio Martirum: 'VIII kal. Ian. natus Christus in Betleem Iudeae' — earliest undisputed attestation of December 25 as Christ's birth date; depositio compiled c. 336; Nothaft, C. P. E., 'The Origins of the Christmas Date: Some Recent Trends in Historical Research' — surveys the two main hypotheses (History of Religions vs calculation); Hijmans, S. E. (2003 article; 2009 Groningen dissertation, 583-95): the evidence for a December 25 festival of Sol Invictus is thin; none of Sol's recorded festivals fell on the winter solstice (Aug 8/9, Aug 28, Oct 19/22, Dec 11); Simmons, E., 'The Origins of Christmas and the Date of Christ's Birth,' JETS 58.2 (2015), 299-324; Forbes, B. D., Christmas: A Candid History (University of California Press, 2007)
Falsifiability: A contemporary or near-contemporary (1st-2nd c.) source dating the birth to December 25 would overturn the grade; the current earliest attestation of the date is c. 336 CE</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.2024,31.7054,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>-4</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The James ossuary inscription</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The limestone box is an authentic ancient ossuary, but the inscription — the claim that matters — is DEBUNKED per the strongest evidence: the 2003 IAA scientific committee verdict (modern forgery, patina artificially applied) and the Geological Survey of Israel's isotopic analysis showing the letters' patina could not have formed under natural Judean conditions in 2000 years. The forgery objections (Krumbein 2006; Harrell cleanser hypothesis) are named as minority positions, not consensus.
Layer: fake · Place: Reported as found near Silwan, Jerusalem (unverifiable — antiquities-market provenance)
Dates: 0 → 100 (confidence: medium)

The Aramaic inscription 'Ya'akov bar Yosef akhui di Yeshua' (James, son of Joseph, brother of Jesus) on the James ossuary is an authentic 1st-century inscription identifying the family of Jesus of Nazareth.

Earliest attestation: Inscription brought to scholarly attention by André Lemaire (2002); ossuary obtained by collector Oded Golan, who said he purchased it in the 1970s through the antiquities market — no controlled excavation provenance
Sources: Israel Antiquities Authority committee report, 18 June 2003: inscription declared a modern forgery; inscription carved through the natural rock varnish; Geological Survey of Israel (Dr. Amos Bein, 22 June 2003): oxygen-isotope analysis — letters' patina significantly different from authentic ossuary patinas; could not have formed under Judean conditions of the last 2000 years; ARCHAEOLOGY (Archaeological Institute of America), 'Ossuary Dethroned' (24 July 2003): IAA verdict and police confiscation context; Minority contest: Krumbein (2006, defense-called patina expert) — inscription ancient, patina removed by cleaning; Harrell — cleanser explains δ18O discrepancy. Named in competing hypotheses, not consensus; Oct-2026 press recycle (aggregator re-report ~Oct 7, 2026): 'the most important archaeological discovery ever linked to Jesus Christ's era' — no new evidence; the piece itself restates the 2003 IAA verdict, the 2012 acquittal, and the judge's caveat. Venue flagged advocacy-adjacent; grade-neutral (Turin-shroud precedent)
Falsifiability: A controlled-excavation parallel, or peer-reviewed material analysis showing the letter patina formed continuously with the surface patina over two millennia, would reopen the question. The minority patina analyses have not carried a peer-reviewed consensus.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>35.23,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>0</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Nero fiddled while Rome burned — the anachronistic legend</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills. (1) THE ANACHRONISM: the viol class of instruments, to which the fiddle belongs, was not developed until the 11th century (HISTORY.com, citing music historians); bowed instruments would not become popular for another 1,000 years (Live Science / Drake). If Nero played anything it would have been a cithara — a portable seven-string harp — not a fiddle. (2) TACITUS HEDGED: the source-critical fact — Tacitus reports the singing as a rumor (pervaserat rumor), in an indirect statement, 'unconfirmed by eyewitness accounts' (HISTORY.com). The most authoritative ancient source never states it as fact. (3) HE WASN'T THERE: Nero was at his villa at Antium, ~35 miles from Rome, when the fire broke out; he returned and organized relief (opened palaces and gardens, cut grain prices). The fiddle never existed, the singing was rumor, the player was 35 miles away.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome, Italy — the rumor's claimed scene
Dates: 64-07-18 → 1900 (confidence: medium)

Nero fiddled while Rome burned — the popular image of the emperor playing the violin during the Great Fire of Rome (July 64 CE).

Earliest attestation: Tacitus, Annals 15.39: the rumor (pervaserat rumor) that Nero mounted his private stage (domesticam scaenam) and sang of the destruction of Troy (cecinisse Troianum excidium) — reported as RUMOR, unconfirmed by eyewitnesses, in an indirect statement. Suetonius, Nero 38 and Dio 62.18 report the performance more flatly (singing the Sack of Ilium / Capture of Troy in stage costume). None of the three mentions a fiddle; none is presented by Tacitus as established fact.
Sources: Tacitus, Annals 15.39 (Latin text via Open Book Publishers commentary): 'pervaserat rumor ipso tempore flagrantis urbis inisse eum domesticam scaenam et cecinisse Troianum excidium' — the rumor of the private-stage performance of the Fall of Troy; reported as rumor only, in indirect statement; Suetonius, Nero 38 — the 'Sack of Ilium' sung in stage costume while watching from the Maecenas tower (singing, not fiddling); Dio Cassius 62.18 — Nero on the palace roof in lyre-player's costume singing the 'Capture of Troy' (the costume detail, not a fiddle); HISTORY.com, 'Did Nero Really Fiddle While Rome Burned?' — viol class not developed until the 11th century; cithara the plausible instrument; Tacitus's rumor 'unconfirmed by eyewitness accounts'; Nero at Antium, relief measures; Live Science / Prof. Anthony Drake — bowed instruments 'would not become popular for another 1,000 years'; cithara correction; Nero's relief efforts and exhaustion
Falsifiability: A contemporary (64 CE) source — a letter, graffito, or inscription — recording Nero playing a bowed string instrument during the fire would overturn the grade. The violin family's documented 16th-century Italian origin makes this effectively impossible.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0064-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>64</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Nero's sunglasses' — the emerald misreading (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED as eyewear: Pliny's sentence describes watching through (or in) an emerald with no optics, no frame, no correction — the 'first sunglasses' reading imports 2,000 years of invention into five Latin words. The honest readings are a concave emerald used as a mirror or a luxury viewing stone; neither is spectacles, and neither is sunglasses.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome (the arena Nero watched)
Dates: 77 → 77 (confidence: medium)

The Emperor Nero wore the first sunglasses — he watched the gladiatorial games through an emerald, which corrected or shaded his vision.

Earliest attestation: Pliny the Elder, Natural History 37.16.64 (c. 77 CE): Nero 'used to watch the gladiatorial shows in an emerald' (smaragdo spectabat) — a single sentence with no mention of correction, shading, framing, or any device held to the eyes
Sources: Pliny the Elder, Natural History 37.16.64 — 'Nero... used to watch the gladiatorial shows in an emerald' (smaragdo); the full extent of the ancient evidence for the claim; Eyeglass-retailer histories (e.g. VCC2020, Millennium Eye Center) repeating the modern telling: 'Emperor Nero couldn't see gladiator fights very well without his magnifying emerald' — the telling this record grades
Falsifiability: An artifact or text showing a framed, wearable, vision-correcting or light-shading device in 1st-century Rome</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0077-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4964,41.9028,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>77</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Both Genealogies Are Literal Compatible Pedigrees' Telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The two lists diverge completely after David — different names, different counts (Matthew's schematic 14-14-14), omitting names Matthew 1 itself skips (Ahaziah, Joash, Amaziah) — and the harmonizations require mechanisms the texts never state. Killed on the documents.
Layer: fake · Place: Jerusalem (compositional setting of the harmonization question)
Dates: 0080 → 0110 (confidence: traditional)

Matthew 1 and Luke 3 are both complete, literal, historically compatible family trees of Jesus.

Earliest attestation: The modern telling has roots in early harmonizing exegesis (Julius Africanus, Eusebius); its current popular form in confessional apologetics
Sources: Matthew 1:1-17 and Luke 3:23-38 compared name-for-name (the divergence is in the texts); The lists' own omissions: Matthew 1:8 skips three kings between Joram and Uzziah (Ahaziah, Joash, Amaziah) — telescoping visible in the text; Raymond E. Brown, The Birth of the Messiah (Doubleday, 1977/1993) — the genealogies as theological constructs; Cusack (2026), 'The Genealogies of Jesus: Matthew, Luke, Jewish Tradition, and the Apocryphal Gospels', IJRISS (genealogies as legal/tribal/theological legitimacy; divergence restated)
Falsifiability: Independent attestation of the intermediate names in either list from contemporary inscriptions, or a demonstration that the two lists can be reconciled without appealing to unnamed adoptions, levirate marriages, or textual error</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0080-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>80</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cai Lun invented paper in 105 CE (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the Fangmatan Tomb 5 map fragment — early Western Han, early 2nd c. BCE, the oldest extant sheet of paper, laid on a burial chest 200+ years before 105 CE; (2) Li Xiaocen's 2012 microscopic re-examination — a distinct paper-pouring method, not Cai Lun's dip-mould, ~250 years older than his process; (3) the 2006 Fangmatan specimen re-dating — paper bearing written Chinese characters from c. 8 BCE, paper already in military use more than a century before Cai Lun. The historiography's own verdict: Cai Lun systematized and improved the skill and fixed a recipe — he did not invent it.
Layer: fake · Place: Luoyang (Eastern Han capital, where the invention is said to have happened)
Dates: 105 → 105 (confidence: traditional)

Paper was invented by the Han court official Cai Lun in 105 CE.

Earliest attestation: The traditional reading of the Hou Hanshu 78/68:2513-14 passage (Fan Ye, 5th c.); carried into modern reference works — e.g. Encyclopaedia Britannica's 'Cai Lun (Chinese Inventor)' entry — and China's Four Great Inventions framing
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Cai Lun' — the Hou Hanshu 78/68:2513-14 passage: bamboo heavy, silk costly; Cai Lun devises paper from tree bark, hemp waste, old rags, fishing nets; presents it in the first year of Yuanxing (105); the emperor praises it; paper henceforth called 'Cai Hou paper'; Wikipedia, 'Fangmatan' — Tomb 5 excavated 1986, early Western Han (early 2nd c. BCE); paper map fragment 5.6 x 2.6 cm laid on the occupant's chest; drawn on the oldest extant piece of paper (citing Yi &amp; Liu 2010, p.64; Behr 2007, p.113); China Development Gateway (Xinhua), Nov 2016 — Li Xiaocen (Nanjing University of Information Science and Technology) on the 1986 Fangmatan scrap: uneven fiber distribution under the microscope, the paper-pouring process distinct from Cai Lun's method, ~250 years before Cai Lun; Wikipedia, 'History of paper' — 2006 Fangmatan specimens bearing written Chinese characters re-dated to c. 8 BCE (paper in military use more than 100 years before Cai Lun); 'Cai Lun's contribution was to improve this skill systematically and scientifically, fix a recipe for papermaking'; Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Cai Lun (Chinese Inventor)' — the reference-work version of the invention credit, carried into textbooks and the Four Great Inventions list (cited via simple.wikipedia's references)
Falsifiability: Proof that every pre-105 paper fragment is misidentified (not paper) or misdated (not pre-105) would restore the claim. A pre-Western-Han paper find would push paper earlier still — a refinement, not a rescue.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0105-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>112.45,34.66,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>105</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The alchemists' successful transmutation of base metals into gold</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the documented projection-frauds and illusions — hidden gold, copper-displacement 'transmutations', the Helvetius 1666 confidence trick; (2) the physics — Lavoisier's 1789 conservation of mass and Dalton's atomic theory make chemical transmutation impossible, since changing an element means changing the proton count, which is a nuclear not a chemical act; (3) the only genuine gold-making is nuclear (Rutherford 1919 -&gt; McMillan 1937 -&gt; Sherr/Bainbridge/Anderson 1941 -&gt; Seaborg 1980), and it is uneconomical by orders of magnitude. The claim fails on every leg.
Layer: fake · Place: Strasbourg (Sethon projection, July 1602, before Dr Jacob Zwinger); Cologne (August 1603)
Dates: 0300 → 1900 (confidence: low)

Medieval and early-modern alchemists succeeded in transmuting base metals into gold (chrysopoeia) — that they actually produced real gold from lead, iron, copper or mercury by alchemical or chemical means, before the twentieth century.

Earliest attestation: The early-modern 'projection' performances (e.g. Sethon's July 1602 Strasbourg projection before Dr Jacob Zwinger; the August 1603 Cologne projection) — the 'successes' are event-reports from credulous or interested witnesses, never controlled assays.
Sources: Principe, The Aspiring Adept (1998) — the Sethon/Sendivogius episode and the projection economy of early-modern courts; 1926 Science account of the classic 'copper water' case — an iron vessel immersed in copper-salt solution takes a real copper coating, reported for centuries as an iron-into-copper transmutation; displacement chemistry, not transmutation; Scientific American, 'Fact or Fiction?: Lead Can Be Turned into Gold' — the bismuth-to-gold experiment (1980, Bevalac, ~$5,000/hr beam time, ~a day of beam time; Seaborg: 'more than one quadrillion dollars per ounce' vs ~$560/oz gold in 1980) as the nuclear counterexample; Indiana Public Media, 'Modern Alchemy: Lab-Made Gold' — Sherr, Bainbridge &amp; Anderson 1941 (mercury + neutrons -&gt; radioactive gold, Phys. Rev. 60:147) as the first actual gold-making; Discover Magazine, 'Turning Lead Into Gold' — McMillan 1937 deuteron-beam artificial gold isotopes; Loveland on the decades of accelerator-made gold before Seaborg
Falsifiability: A surviving pre-1900 alchemical 'gold' sample from a documented projection, assayed as genuine gold and shown to have been produced in the projection itself. No such sample exists; the fraud literature documents the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.75,48.58,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The sack of Rome by Attila the Hun (the belief that Attila captured and sacked Rome)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Attila's 452 Italian campaign halted at the River Po; Leo I's embassy at the Mincio secured his withdrawal; the ancient sources never describe a sack of Rome by Attila, who died in 453 without entering the city. The sacks belong to Alaric (410) and Gaiseric (455).
Layer: fake · Place: Mincio river, near Mantua (meeting site); Rome (the city never taken)
Dates: 452 → 453 (confidence: high)

Attila the Hun sacked Rome — that is, his Huns captured and sacked the city of Rome itself.

Earliest attestation: The conflation lives in later popular retellings; the ancient sources (Prosper of Aquitaine, Priscus, Jordanes) describe the 452 Italian campaign, the Mincio meeting, and the withdrawal — never a sack of Rome by Attila.
Sources: Prosper of Aquitaine — short, reliable contemporary description of the 452 meeting at the Mincio near Mantua: Leo I (with Consul Avienus and Prefect Trigetius, at Valentinian III's wish) obtained Attila's promise to withdraw from Italy and negotiate peace; Priscus (fragments) — reports Attila's superstitious fear of Alaric's fate (Alaric died shortly after sacking Rome in 410) gave him pause at the Po; Jordanes, Getica — the 452 campaign narrative and the tradition of Leo's embassy; Wikipedia, 'Attila' (invasion of Italy and death section, citing the above) — Attila halted at the River Po; Leo's embassy at the Mincio; withdrawal; death in 453 of a nasal hemorrhage on his wedding night to Ildico; Encyclopaedia/Infoplease via FactMonster, 'Attila' — 'Attila turned back and invaded (452) N Italy but abandoned his plan to take Rome itself... withdrawal... appears to have been motivated by a shortage of provisions and the outbreak of pestilence'; Peter Heather, The Fall of the Roman Empire (2005) — famine, pestilence, and Marcian's Danube campaign as the withdrawal's real causes, carried as the modern interpretation; Christopher Kelly, The End of Empire: Attila the Hun and the Fall of Rome (2008) — the material-strategic reading of the 452 turn-back, carried as the modern interpretation; The sacks that did happen: Alaric's Visigoths, 410; Gaiseric's Vandals, 455 (Leo I negotiated sanctuary with Gaiseric but could not prevent the sack)
Falsifiability: A contemporary 5th-century account describing Attila's Huns inside Rome, or Roman administrative records of a 452 Attila sack (e.g., a breach in the Aurelian Walls, a ransom ledger), would revive the claim.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0452-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.79,45.15,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>452</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The droit du seigneur: the medieval lord's 'right of the first night'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the empty medieval record — across thousands of surviving charters, custumals, lawbooks, and court judgments, the legal right never appears (1911 Britannica: 'For the existence of the custom in a legalized form there is no trustworthy evidence'; Boureau: 'No law, no custom, no lord ever held such a right'); (2) the manufactured trail — the story's documentary birth is early-modern (Boece 1527, invented king; Acta Sanctorum 1643+; Voltaire 1756; Beaumarchais 1784), not medieval, exactly the 'old ugly times' pattern Barbero describes: 'not even a single medieval author' used the trope, while Renaissance writers invented it as something from the barbaric past.
Layer: fake · Place: Bologna, Italy (Gratian's seat — the seat of medieval canon law, where the right should appear and does not)
Dates: 500 → 1500 (confidence: low)

In medieval Europe, feudal lords held a legal right — known as the droit du seigneur or jus primae noctis — entitling them to spend the wedding night with their vassals' brides.

Earliest attestation: No medieval attestation of any kind: no statute, charter, lawbook, manorial roll, or court judgment anywhere in medieval Europe records such a right. The first historian to record it is Hector Boece in 1527 (Scotland), who attributes it to a legendary king, Ewen (Evenus III), and says it was abolished by Malcolm III under St Margaret's influence — Boece's King Ewen was probably invented (Alain Boureau). The phrase in its modern sense belongs to the 18th century: Voltaire's attacks on feudal abuses and Beaumarchais's The Marriage of Figaro (1784), whose Count's abolished right drives the plot.
Sources: Alain Boureau, The Lord's First Night: The Myth of the Droit de Cuissage (University of Chicago Press, 1998) — the full autopsy: no law, no custom, no lord ever held such a right; Boece's King Ewen probably invented; the Jangnoma-shaped legend industry of the 19th century; 1911 Encyclopedia Britannica, 'Jus Primae Noctis' — 'For the existence of the custom in a legalized form there is no trustworthy evidence. That some such abuse of power may have been occasionally exercised by brutal nobles in the lawless days of the early middle ages is only too likely, but the jus, it seems, is a myth, invented no earlier than the 16th or 17th century.' Also records the Council of Carthage 398 continence custom and Louis Veuillot's 1854 treatment; Wikipedia, 'Droit du seigneur' / 'Primae noctis' — Hector Boece 1527 (first historian to record; Scotland; Malcolm III abolition story; merchet instituted in its place); Melissa/Francesco Campitelli 1624 ordinance episode ('historicity and legal basis remain unverified'); Acta Sanctorum mentions from 1643; play connection to Beaumarchais; historymedieval.com, 'Primae Noctis Myth' (updated Jan 2026) — 'While there is little historical evidence to confirm this practice ever took place'; carries Boureau's verdict and Vasily Polenov's 1874 Le droit du Seigneur as belief-art; Alessandro Barbero (via UCCR) — 'no testimonies to its diffusion in medieval Europe,' no secular or ecclesiastical directives; 'not even a single medieval author' used the trope while modern scriptwriters do; the legend begins in the 16th century 'as something that happened during the old ugly times'
Falsifiability: Produce one medieval source — a statute, charter, custumal, or court judgment dated before 1500 — that institutes, records, or enforces a lord's right to the wedding night as a legal right. One parchment moves the grade; there are none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.3426,44.4949,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The medieval non-bather: "medieval people never bathed"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiple independent primary/contemporary sources — monastic rules, guild statutes, royal ordinances, chronicles, and surviving bathhouses across four regions — document public and private bathing as ordinary medieval practice; the universal 'never' is a modern construction.
Layer: fake · Place: Medieval Europe (exemplified by Paris)
Dates: 500 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

Medieval Europeans (c. 500-1500 CE) rarely or never bathed — regular bathing was essentially unknown in medieval Europe, and public bathhouses were a Roman-era relic that did not exist in medieval cities.

Earliest attestation: The universal-non-bathing reading is an early-modern/modern projection (Victorian 'Dark Ages' shorthand); medieval sources describe bathing as ordinary practice — see evt-medieval-bathhouses-001.
Sources: Rule of St. Benedict, Ch. 36 (c. 530) — 'Let the use of the bath be offered to the sick as often as it is useful'; Livre des métiers (Étienne Boileau, Paris, 1268) — guild statutes of the étuviers, professional bathhouse-keepers; Henry II's 39 Ordinances for the stewholders of Southwark (1161) — Bishop of Winchester's licensed bathhouses; Primary Chronicle / Tale of Bygone Years (c. 1113) — the Apostle Andrew passage describing Slavic wooden bathhouses as routine practice; Ellen Wurtzel, 'Passionate Encounters, Public Healing: Medieval Urban Bathhouses in Northern France' (2023, Oberlin Digital Commons) — 13th-16th c. urban bathhouse resurgence from medical treatises, trial records, archives; Katherine Ashenburg, The Dirt on Clean: An Unsanitized History (2007) — documents the 1348 Paris-medical-faculty plague verdict on baths; Georges Vigarello, Concepts of Cleanliness (1988, orig. Le propre et le sale, 1985) — the hygiene low point by modern standards was the 17th-18th centuries, not the Middle Ages
Falsifiability: Credible evidence that bathing infrastructure and practice were absent throughout medieval Europe — e.g., medieval cities with no bath references at all in their records</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The spice-covered rot: "medieval cooks used spices to hide rotten meat"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiple independent lines — food historians' audits of the recipe corpus, the economics of who could afford spices, and the documented preservative economy of salt/vinegar/smoke — all show spices as elite display cooking on fresh meat, not a spoilage disguise.
Layer: fake · Place: Medieval Europe (the schoolbook tradition's setting)
Dates: 500 → 1500 (confidence: medium)

In medieval Europe, cooks used expensive imported spices to disguise or mask the taste of rotten or spoiled meat.

Earliest attestation: The universal rot-masking reading is a modern popular-history construction; medieval recipe books describe spices used on fresh meat at feasts — see evt-spices-status-medieval-001.
Sources: Paul Freedman, Out of the East: Spices and the Medieval Imagination (Yale University Press, 2008) — the rot-masking reading as urban legend: those rich enough to afford spices could afford fresh meat; spices not as good at preservation as salt and smoke; spices as symbols of beauty, affluence, taste, and grace; Jack Turner, Spice: The History of a Temptation (Knopf, 2004) — 'puts to sleep the nostrum about rotting food'; spices as food of the wealthy, medicine, and status display from Augustus to the Renaissance; Forme of Cury (c. 1390, British Library Add MS 5016) — royal English cookbook: heavy spicing on court dishes, meats described as fresh; Le Ménagier de Paris (1393) — household manual: spices added late to sauces (the instruction contradicts the simmer-in-to-hide model); Constance Hieatt &amp; Sharon Butler, Pleyn Delit: Medieval Cookery for Modern Cooks — much medieval cooking, rich and poor alike, bland by modern standards; highly spiced dishes a minority even at feasts
Falsifiability: A medieval cookbook or household manual instructing cooks to add imported spices specifically to meat described as spoiled or putrid — distinct from saucing, hanging, or humoral correction</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0500-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>500</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pakal's sarcophagus lid ('Palenque astronaut')</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Every element the spaceship reading calls machinery maps to known Maya cosmology: the 'exhaust flames' are the open jaws of the jaguar/Xibalba below, the 'capsule' is Pakal reclining in death, the 'antenna' is the cosmic tree (Wacah Chan) rising behind him to the celestial bird (Itzam-Ye). The surrounding inscriptions read as ancestral record, per Palenque dynastic epigraphy.
Layer: fake · Place: Temple of the Inscriptions, Palenque, Chiapas, Mexico
Dates: 683 → 692 (confidence: high)

The sarcophagus lid of K'inich Janaab' Pakal the Great (603–683 CE) depicts him piloting a spacecraft (the 'Palenque astronaut').

Earliest attestation: The lid itself, discovered in situ by Alberto Ruz Lhuillier in 1952 in the Temple of the Inscriptions, Palenque
Sources: Temple of the Inscriptions — sarcophagus lid description: Pakal between the heavens and the underworld, cosmic tree, serpent bar, ancestors in six-generation line; ThoughtCo: the lid depicts Pakal 'being reborn into eternal life... along with the cosmic tree' emerging from the earth monster with the quadripartite sun monster
Falsifiability: The claim would need independent textual evidence (a Maya glyphic reading mentioning flight machinery) or an artifact inconsistent with the cosmological reading. The glyphic record says otherwise: the lid's edge inscriptions list prior rulers — a dynastic death list, not a flight manual.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0683-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-91.9903,17.4847,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>683</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'blind Homer wrote the epics' schoolbook picture</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim dies on the record. (1) No contemporary evidence exists for Homer's blindness or for single written authorship — every biographical tradition is centuries late, and the Homeric Lives contradict each other on his birthplace, dates, and manner of death. (2) The blindness has a traceable genealogy: the blind bard of Chios in the Hymn to Apollo, Demodocus the blind bard of Odyssey 8 read as self-portrait, and the folk-etymology ho me horon ('he who does not see') — a legend manufactured from a character and a pun, never from evidence; the scholarly readings of the name (homeros = 'hostage/surety'; Nagy's 'he who fits the Song together') point to a generic function, not a man with no eyesight. (3) Parry's formulaic analysis (1928) plus the 1933–35 Yugoslav fieldwork demonstrated the poems' composition mechanism is oral-traditional, not written composition by one hand — the 'wrote it' half of the picture is as dead as the 'blind' half. Fences hold: the blindness as cultural image (the busts, the statues), the unitarian monumental-composer thesis as a live scholarly position, and the epics' own greatness — untouched.
Layer: fake · Place: Chios / Ionia — the traditional homeland of the blind bard (the seven-cities tradition)
Dates: 700 BCE → present (confidence: medium)

The historical Homer was a blind poet who personally composed the Iliad and the Odyssey as written works — the lone blind genius of the schoolroom busts.

Earliest attestation: The blindness: the Homeric Hymn to Apollo, lines 172–173 (the blind bard of Chios), cited by Thucydides (History of the Peloponnesian War 3.104) as Homer's own self-description; the Homeric Lives (Pseudo-Herodotus, Pseudo-Plutarch) — Hellenistic/Roman and mutually contradictory. The written-authorship: the default assumption until Milman Parry's 1928 Harvard theses began the oral-formulaic demolition.
Sources: Wikipedia-adjacent Homer overview (poetrynook.com/poet/homer): the blind-bard tradition — Demodocus in Odyssey 8 read as self-referential; homeros = 'hostage/surety'; the Cymean-dialect meaning 'blind'; the Delian Hymn to Apollo cited by Thucydides 3.104; Ephorus; the false etymology ho me horon ('he who does not see'); The Handy Mythology Answer Book (papertrell.com): Demodocus of Phaeacia as the self-portrait; 'Homer' mythologized as 'a blind bard who more than makes up for physical sight with powers of insight'; the epics as 'probably the works of more than one person, composed in various stages between 900 and 700 B.C.E.'; the Homeric question; Wikipedia 'Homeric Question' (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homeric_Question): the name-as-generic-function readings (Nagy, Durante); the Homeridae hypothesis; the competing 'blind' derivations; the Wolf 1795 analyst/unitarian split and Parry–Lord as the bridge to the traditional composer
Falsifiability: A contemporary or near-contemporary record of the poet's life — e.g., an 8th- or 7th-century BCE inscription, administrative text, or dedicatory artifact naming Homer as a blind individual and author of the poems — or manuscript evidence of single written composition (authorial drafts, a dated autograph tradition).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>26.1,38.4,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Tariq ibn Ziyad burned his ships at Gibraltar (711) — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice from the record itself: (1) no early attestation — the earliest substantial Arabic account (Ibn Abd al-Hakam, Futuh Misr, 9th c.) describes Julian's vessels 'crossing and recrossing' — a working ferry, not a pyre — and says nothing of destruction; the full speech does not exist in print before Ibn Khallikan's 13th-century Wafayat al-A'yan and al-Maqqari's 17th-century Nafh al-tib; (2) Wikipedia's own khutba article labels the speech 'of dubious authenticity', and Tahera Qutbuddin's analysis places it in the standard Arabic battle-oration genre of 'the troops' desperation as a rhetorical strategy' — a literary topos, not a dispatch.
Layer: fake · Place: Gibraltar (Jabal Tariq) — the claimed landing site of the burned fleet
Dates: 711 → 711 (confidence: traditional)

Tariq ibn Ziyad, after landing in Iberia in 711, burned his ships (or had them scuttled) to deny his men any retreat, then delivered the famous khutba: 'the sea is behind you, the enemy before you.'

Earliest attestation: No 8th- or 9th-century source reports a burning; the full khutba is first written down in Ibn Khallikan's Wafayat al-A'yan (completed 1274) and al-Maqqari's Nafh al-tib (early 17th c.).
Sources: Ibn Abd al-Hakam, Futuh Misr wa'l-Maghrib (9th c.) — the earliest substantial Arabic account: reports ~7,000 men and vessels 'crossing and recrossing'; no fleet destruction, no speech; Ibn Habib (9th-c., Andalusian) — records a Tariq exhortation (faith, enemy's vulnerability) without the full dramatic rhetoric and without burned ships; Ibn Khallikan, Wafayat al-A'yan (completed 1274) — the full khutba's first known written home, ~563 years after 711; Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Maqqari, Nafh al-tib (early 17th c.) — the full speech again, drawing on medieval traditions; absent from the earliest conquest chronicles; Tahera Qutbuddin — analysis of the khutba as Arabic battle oration: the 'desperation of the troops' as a rhetorical strategy, in the company of Asim ibn Amr at al-Qadisiyyah and Abu Sufyan at Yarmuk; the speech enumerates worldly rewards, unlike Ali's orations; Wikipedia, 'Khutba of Tariq Ibn Ziyad' — 'A khutba of dubious authenticity' — transmission: Ibn Khallikan's Wafayat al-A'yan and al-Maqqari's Nafh at-Tib; Wikipedia, 'Muslim conquest of the Iberian Peninsula' — the khutba is a 'khutba of dubious authenticity' delivered upon 'the legendary burning of the ships'; Ibn Abd al-Hakam's vessels 'crossing and recrossing'
Falsifiability: A contemporary witness — the Latin Chronicle of 754, an 8th-century papyrus or inscription, or an archaeological horizon at Gibraltar consistent with a burned 711 fleet — would reopen the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0711-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.35,36.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>711</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Donation of Constantine — a forgery</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Zero attestation before the 8th century; the Latin carries 8th-century vocabulary impossible in a 4th-century imperial chancery; the document dates itself with two consuls who never served together; Valla's 1440 proof, Cusa's 1433 prior doubt, and Pecock's 1450s independent confirmation converge, and modern scholarship is unanimous.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome (composition: Roman scriptorium, mid-to-late 8th century)
Dates: 750 → 850 (confidence: high)

The Constitutum Constantini is an authentic imperial decree issued by Constantine the Great in the 4th century (dated in the document to March 30, consulships naming Constantine's fourth term and Gallicanus), granting Pope Sylvester I and his successors: spiritual supremacy over the four great sees and all churches, the imperial insignia (diadem, tiara, pallium, scepter), the Lateran palace, the city of Rome, and all the provinces, places and cities of Italy and the western regions.

Earliest attestation: No 4th-, 5th-, 6th- or 7th-century witness of any kind exists. First surviving witness: the 9th-century Pseudo-Isidorian (False) Decretals collection; no pope used it in the 9th or 10th centuries; first papal official citation by Leo IX, c. 1050 (controversy with the Byzantines, per the 1911 Britannica).
Sources: Catholic Encyclopedia (1913), 'Donation of Constantine': 'without doubt a forgery, fabricated somewhere between the years 750 and 850'; Valla (1440) proved it 'with certainty'; Nicholas of Cusa (De concordantia catholica III.ii, 1433) called it dictamen apocryphum; Reginald Pecock independently reached the same conclusion (1450-57); Baronius's Annales (ad an. 324) admitted the forgery; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Donation of Constantine': forged at Rome between the middle and end of the 8th century; included in the 9th-century False Decretals; no papal use in the 9th-10th centuries; first official use by Leo IX c. 1050; Wikipedia, 'Donation of Constantine': consul-dating error (Constantine's fourth term 315 paired with Gallicanus 317 — never served together); narrative built on the 5th-century Acts of Sylvester; content details (Lateran palace, Rome, Italy, the western provinces, landed estates across the empire)
Falsifiability: A 4th-century manuscript witness or any contemporary 4th-century reference to the document's existence would revive it; none exists, and the philology of the text's own vocabulary is independently fatal.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0750-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4777,41.8972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Lilith was Adam's first wife' (the Genesis claim)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The name occurs once in the entire Hebrew Bible (Isaiah 34:14); Genesis never names her. The first-wife story is first told in the medieval Alphabet of Ben Sira — a satirical work, ~2,000 years after Isaiah — which assembled older pieces (the demon name, birth-protection amulets, the two-creation-accounts puzzle) into a narrative that had not previously existed (Payton).
Layer: fake · Place: Medieval Jewish literary culture (textual origin)
Dates: 800 → 1000 (confidence: high)

'Lilith was Adam's first wife' is a biblical statement (Genesis).

Earliest attestation: Alphabet of Ben Sira (8th–10th c. CE, anonymous, satirical): God forms Lilith from earth as he formed Adam; they quarrel over precedence; she speaks the divine name and departs
Sources: Alphabet of Ben Sira (8th–10th c. CE) — the first 'Adam's first wife' narrative (Snvi, Snsvi, Smnglof; the quarrel; the Ineffable Name; the departure); Payton, Jeremy (2026): the legend assembles older pieces ~2,000 years after Isaiah; the book is satirical and unrelated to the biblical Sirach; Jewish Encyclopedia, s.v. Lilith — the Ben Sira account as Adam's concubine and mother of demons; the Gen. R. xx / b. Eruvin 18b Adam's-130-years tradition; Genesis 1–2; Isaiah 34:14 — the texts the claim misreads (the name's sole biblical occurrence; Genesis's silence)
Falsifiability: A pre-medieval (pre-8th c. CE) text naming Lilith as Adam's wife would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0800-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Classic Maya 'collapse' — no vanishing, a transformation</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 'vanishing' is a 19th-century explorer's framing: what ended was the institution of divine kingship in the southern lowlands — monument carving stops, palaces burn or are abandoned — while Maya-speaking communities persist continuously to the present (~7 million speakers today); the transformation was multi-causal and regionally staggered, not one mysterious night.
Layer: fake · Place: Southern Maya lowlands (Petén, Guatemala; adjacent Chiapas, Belize)
Dates: 800 → 1000 (confidence: medium)

The Classic Maya civilization mysteriously vanished — its people disappeared in a single, unexplained collapse.

Earliest attestation: Archaeological: the cessation of dated monument erection in the southern lowlands; colonial-period Relaciones; modern demographic record
Sources: Demarest, Arthur — Ancient Maya: The Rise and Fall of a Rainforest Civilization (Cambridge, 2004): the multi-causal, regionally staggered model of the Terminal Classic transformation; Archaeological record: last dated southern-lowland monuments c. 900–909 CE (Tonina 909 CE); Postclassic centers (Chichen Itza, Mayapan) continue — no single collapse horizon; Living record: Maya languages spoken today (Yucatec, K'iche', Q'eqchi', Tzotzil, Tzeltal, etc.) — ~7 million speakers; continuity is the default state of the evidence
Falsifiability: Evidence of a total, synchronous depopulation of the Maya world in a short span — with no surviving Maya-descended communities — would support the vanishing; the opposite is what exists</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0800-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-90.0,17.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The hardline claim that the Scandinavians played NO role in the formation of the Rus'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the 944/945 treaty's 76 plenipotentiaries carry overwhelmingly Old Norse names (Igor=Ingvar, Olga=Helga, Akun=Hákon, Uleb=Óleifr) — impossible if no Scandinavian elite existed; (2) the Frankish court at Ingelheim in 839 identified the Rhos' envoys as Sueones (Swedes); (3) Constantine VII records Norse names for the Dnieper rapids alongside Slavic ones.
Layer: fake · Place: Kiev (the polity the claim is about)
Dates: 0839 → 0945 (confidence: high)

The Scandinavians (Varangians/Norse) played NO role in the formation of the Rus' polity — the early Rus' were entirely East Slavic in origin and identity, and any Norse presence was non-existent or historiographically irrelevant.

Earliest attestation: Soviet official historiography, 1930s–1980s — anti-Normanism as doctrine, adopted partly in response to Nazi propaganda casting the Kievan Rus' as a Germanic-founded state (Waldman &amp; Mason 2005: the Soviet wishful-thinking theories 'are more likely the product of wishful thinking than of fact').
Sources: Annales Bertiniani, s.a. 839 — the Rhos' envoys identified as Sueones at Ingelheim; Rus'-Byzantine Treaty 944/945, preserved in the Primary Chronicle — 76 names, overwhelming majority Old Norse (Melnikova 2003; Feldbrugge 2008); Constantine VII, De Administrando Imperio ch. 9 — Norse (Rhos') names for the Dnieper rapids; C. Waldman &amp; C. Mason (2005) — Soviet anti-Normanist theories as 'the product of wishful thinking'
Falsifiability: Would require explaining away three independent attestations simultaneously: the Frankish identification of the Rhos as Swedes (839), the overwhelmingly Norse 944/945 treaty name-list, and the Norse Dnieper-rapids names in Constantine VII — no credible re-reading does this.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0839-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.5234,50.4501,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>839</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>St Swithin's Day and the forty days (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Met Office states that 'since the start of records in 1861, there has never been a record of 40 dry or 40 wet days in a row following St Swithin's Day.' The legend was put to the test on 55 occasions when it rained on St Swithin's Day, and forty days of rain followed on none of them; the closest approach in the modern record was 34 days at Cassley, Highland. Companion saints'-day prophecies fail the same test.
Layer: fake · Place: Winchester, England (St Swithin's Old Minster — the legend's origin)
Dates: 0971-07-15 → 2026 (confidence: low)

If it rains on St Swithin's Day (15 July), it will rain for the next forty days; if it is fair, forty days of fair weather follow.

Earliest attestation: The 15 July 971 reburial legend (St Swithin, Bishop of Winchester 852–862, moved from the churchyard into the Old Minster; the forty-day storm as the saint's anger); first written weather-prophecy trace in a 13th/14th-century Emmanuel College manuscript
Sources: Met Office (via multiple outlets): 'since the start of records in 1861, there has never been a record of 40 dry or 40 wet days in a row following St Swithin's Day'; 'Numerous studies have been carried out on past weather observations and none of them have proved the legend true.'; St George's News (2004): the legend 'put to the test on 55 occasions when it has been wet on St Swithun's Day, and 40 days of rain did not follow any of them'; 'there are several saints' days that are supposed to be followed by periods of wet or fine weather... And it doesn't happen on any of these, either.'; Channel 4 News: the 15 July 971 reburial, the forty-day storm as the bishop's displeasure; 'the Met Office has put the legend to the test on 55 occasions when it has been wet on St Swithin's Day, and 40 days of rain did not follow' (citing Jane Struthers, Red Sky at Night: The Book of Lost Country Wisdom); BBC (via Mumsnet summary, 2025): 'The closest we have got in the modern record was in Cassley, Highland, which once experienced rain on St Swithin's Day and on the following 34 days.'; The rhyme: 'St Swithin's day if thou dost rain / For 40 days it will remain / St Swithin's day if thou be fair / For 40 days 'twill rain no more.'
Falsifiability: The Met Office station record showing forty consecutive wet or dry days following a corresponding St Swithin's Day — the exact test the Met Office ran, which found zero occurrences since 1861</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>0971-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.308,51.0632,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>971</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bayeux Tapestry is not a tapestry</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The fiber testifies against the label: the Bayeux images are wool yarn sewn onto linen cloth — embroidery, not tapestry. LiveScience's Norton summary: 'The Bayeux Tapestry is not technically a tapestry, as its design is embroidered onto the linen rather than woven.' Smarthistory: 'the images are not woven into the cloth; instead, the imagery and inscriptions are embroidered using wool yarn sewed onto linen cloth.' History.com: 'actually an embroidery... as opposed to a tapestry, which is woven on a loom with images created during the weaving process.' Columbia: 'embroidered in worsteds of eight colors in couching and stem stitch.' There is no loom-woven imagery in the object. The name survives by habit, not by technique.
Layer: fake · Place: Musée de la Tapisserie de Bayeux, Normandy, France (the examined object)
Dates: 1066 → 1082 (confidence: high)

The Bayeux Tapestry — the famous ~70-meter 11th-century depiction of the 1066 Norman Conquest — is a tapestry (a textile whose images are woven into the cloth on a loom).

Earliest attestation: The object's own fiber: wool yarn stitched (stem stitch, laid-and-couched work) onto a plain-weave linen ground — every technical examination finds embroidery, never loom-woven imagery
Sources: LiveScience / Norton (JBA) — 'The Bayeux Tapestry is not technically a tapestry, as its design is embroidered onto the linen rather than woven'; Smarthistory — 'Although it is called the Bayeux Tapestry... the images are not woven into the cloth; instead, the imagery and inscriptions are embroidered using wool yarn sewed onto linen cloth'; History.com — 'Despite its title, the Bayeux Tapestry is actually an embroidery, with pictures and text inscriptions stitched in wool onto a linen canvas as opposed to a tapestry, which is woven on a loom'; Columbia Encyclopedia (FactMonster) — 'This so-called tapestry is in fact an embroidery'; 'embroidered in worsteds of eight colors in couching and stem stitch'; Smithsonian Magazine (AP/Bayeux Museum, 2021) — 'Though the work is widely known as a tapestry, it technically counts as a work of embroidery'
Falsifiability: A technical textile examination showing the Bayeux images as weft-faced woven tapestry (a single cloth in which colored wefts build the design) — every examination since the 18th century finds stitched wool on a linen ground instead</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1066-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.7009,49.2749,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1066</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The University of Bologna's 1088 founding date</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 1088 date is a commemorative back-computation from the 1888 centenary, not a documented founding: no surviving record fixes a founding to 1088, and the first documentary milestones belong to the 12th century.
Layer: fake · Place: Bologna, Italy
Dates: 1088 → 1888 (confidence: traditional)

The University of Bologna was founded in the year 1088, as a dated and documented founding event.

Earliest attestation: The 1088 date traces to the 1888 eighth-centenary committee (Giosuè Carducci + rector Giovanni Capellini). The university's own official history states the year 1888 was 'conventional and was selected' by that committee.
Sources: University of Bologna official history (Eighth Centenary account: the year 1888 was conventional, selected by the Carducci-Capellini committee); Paul Grendler: 'it is not likely that enough instruction and organization existed to merit the term university before the 1150s, and it might not have happened before the 1180s'; Authentica Habita (Frederick I Barbarossa, Diet of Roncaglia, November 1158) — the earliest imperial charter protecting scholars at Bologna
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous 11th-century document fixing a founding act or corporate existence at Bologna to 1088</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1088-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.3426,44.4949,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1088</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Two Deaths That Weren't: 'al-Ghazali/Mongols Ended Islamic Science'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim requires an ending; the record shows an afterlife. Al-Ghazali explicitly exempted the demonstrative sciences (his own second introduction to the Tahafut); post-Ghazali philosophy-science continued (al-Razi, Suhrawardi, Tusi, Mulla Sadra); the Mongols themselves funded the Maragha observatory in 1259; and Islamic astronomical devices sit inside Copernicus's 1543 masterwork. The 1111 and 1258 'deaths' are periodization, not events.
Layer: fake · Place: Maragha, East Azerbaijan, Iran (site of the observatory founded 1259, one year after the sack of Baghdad — the fact that falsifies the claim)
Dates: 1095 → 1577 (confidence: high)

The popular two-cause story: Islamic scientific research effectively ENDED with (a) al-Ghazali's critique of philosophy (Tahafut al-Falasifa, 1095; d. 1111) and/or (b) the Mongol sack of Baghdad (1258) — so that nothing significant in science happened in the Islamic world after those events.

Earliest attestation: The claim as popularized: 19th-century European historiography (Renan's L'Islamisme et la science, 1883) — the al-Ghazali-as-assassin story; the 1258 cutoff as 'the end' is a later textbook convenience.
Sources: al-Ghazali — Tahafut al-Falasifa, second introduction (eclipses rest on 'geometrical and arithmetical demonstrations'; disputing them is 'a disservice to religion') — Marmura trans. (BYU Press, 1997), pp. 5-7; excerpt at vaticanobservatory.org; Griffel, F. — Al-Ghazali's Philosophical Theology (2009): Tahafut targeted metaphysics (eternity of the world, divine knowledge, resurrection), not the empirical sciences; Saliba, G. — Islamic Science and the Making of the European Renaissance (MIT Press, 2007): ch. 7, 'Age of Decline: The Fecundity of Astronomical Thought' — most creative Arabic astronomy AFTER the supposed endings; Swerdlow endorsement on the endorsement page; Ragep, F.J. (2014, 2016, 2017): on the Maragha school and transmission to Copernicus; Maragheh observatory: founded 1259 under Hulagu Khan, waqf-funded, director al-Tusi, active ~58 years — Wikipedia 'Maragheh observatory' (crawled 2026-10-13) with refs [2][4][6][7]; Huff, T.E. (2008, Middle East Quarterly review of Saliba): the serious counter-position — no major innovations in Muslim astronomy after Shatir (14th c.), in motion after Ibn Bajja (12th c.), in optics after the early 14th c.; the real divergence 16th-17th c. — carried as the honest dissent, not the claim
Falsifiability: Already falsified. It would require that no significant scientific work postdates 1111/1258; a single institution (Maragha, 1259, funded by the Mongols themselves) and a single transmitted device (the Tusi couple inside Copernicus's De revolutionibus) suffice to falsify the absolute claim.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1095-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>46.24,37.39,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1095</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Dutch invented the windmill' myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED: practical windmills predate the Dutch by three centuries. The earliest recorded working windmill design is the Persian panemone — vertical lightweight wooden sails on a central vertical shaft — invented around 700–900 AD in the Sistan region (modern Iran/Afghanistan), first built to pump water and later modified to grind grain. Muslim geographers describe 9th-century vertical-axle windmills in Sistan; the design spread across the Islamic world to India and China. The legendary Abu Lu'lu'a account (pre-644) may be a 10th-century amendment, but the 9th-century evidence is clear. The vertical-axis European windmill appeared in northwestern Europe only in the 12th century — a separate reinvention, not a Dutch invention. The Dutch achievement was real but different: they perfected the horizontal-axis smock mill and deployed it at unmatched scale for grain, drainage, and industry. They industrialized the windmill; they did not invent it.
Layer: fake · Place: Netherlands (the claimed origin)
Dates: 1100 → 1300 (confidence: medium)

The Dutch invented the windmill.

Earliest attestation: Popular telling, early modern onward
Sources: Wikipedia 'Windmill': the Persian panemone 700–900 AD; the 9th-century Sistan vertical-axle mills described by Muslim geographers; the 12th-century northwestern European vertical windmill.; Wikipedia 'Panemone windmill': the earliest recorded windmill design, Persian, 700–900 AD; the Abu Lu'lu'a legend and its possible 10th-century amendment; the spread across the Islamic world.; Green Prophet (Nashtifan): ~30 windmills, 50–60 feet high, vertical axis between clay-and-straw walls, 1,000+ years old, still grinding grain; the design predating the Dutch by centuries.
Falsifiability: Documented earlier windmills</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.9041,52.3676,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'cathedral glass flows' myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED: the physics kills it twice. Zanotto (1998, Am. J. Phys. 66:392-395) calculated the room-temperature relaxation time of window glass at ~10^32 years — far longer than the age of the universe; Zanotto &amp; Gupta (1999) revised the estimate downward and still found flow impossible on human timescales; a recent Mauro-team recalculation for medieval Westminster Abbey glass found ~1 nanometer of flow per billion years. The thick bottoms are manufacturing, not gravity: crown-glass disks were uneven (thicker toward the bullseye/edge), and glaziers deliberately set the heavy side down for stability and to keep water out of the lead cames.
Layer: fake · Place: European cathedrals (Westminster Abbey, Sainte-Chapelle studied)
Dates: 1100 → 1600 (confidence: medium)

Medieval cathedral window glass has flowed downward over the centuries — old panes are thicker at the bottom because glass is a slow-moving liquid.

Earliest attestation: Popular telling, 19th–20th century (textbooks, cathedral tour guides)
Sources: Edgar D. Zanotto, 'Do cathedral glasses flow?', American Journal of Physics 66(5):392-395 (1998) — relaxation time ~10^32 years at room temperature; E. D. Zanotto &amp; P. K. Gupta, 'Do cathedral glasses flow? — Additional remarks', Am. J. Phys. 67:260-262 (1999) — revised estimate, still confirms no flow on human timescales; American Ceramic Society news report on the Mauro team's Westminster Abbey calculation: medieval glass flows ~1 nm per billion years — 'the long-lasting myth about the flow of glasses at room temperature is still just that: a myth'; Cambridge DoITPoMS, 'Do cathedral glasses flow?' — the manufacturing explanation: cylinder/crown process, non-uniform thickness, glaziers fitting thick sides down; Wikipedia 'Crown glass (window)': the crown process — blown globe spun into a disk, thickest toward the bullseye/edge, cut panes mounted in lead lattice; Scientific American, 'Fact or Fiction?: Glass Is a (Supercooled) Liquid' — Ediger (UW–Madison): glass is an amorphous solid; the belief's carriers named as tour guides, internet rumors, and high-school chemistry teachers
Falsifiability: A measured thickness change in an undisturbed pane over decades, or a flow-rate calculation for medieval compositions matching the observed change</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1100-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1272,51.4995,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1100</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>No blood-of-Christ relic has verified 1st-century provenance</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Documentary gap of 1100+ years with a known alternative origin (1204 Constantinople loot); the vessel itself is Byzantine secular work
Layer: fake · Place: 
Dates: 1150 → 1256 (confidence: high)

No blood-of-Christ relic anywhere has verified 1st-century provenance. The Bruges Holy Blood has a 1150 tradition (Thierry of Alsace) but no documentary record before 1256; most historians link it to the 1204 sack of Constantinople, and the crystal tube is a Byzantine perfume bottle. The Mantua relic's chain is likewise medieval. The claim of a 1st-century blood relic is therefore DEBUNKED as a provenance claim — what exists are medieval devotional objects.

Earliest attestation: Bruges documentary record, 1256
Sources: Bruges Holy Blood documentary record 1256
Falsifiability: A documented 1st-century chain of custody would overturn; none exists</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1150-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.22,51.21,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1150</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'libraries burned for months' claim (Nalanda)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Seventy-fifth DEBUNKED family. The only named near-contemporary source, the Tabaqat-i Nasiri, carries no burn duration in the translated passage the retellings quote; the month-figures appear only in modern retellings and cannot agree among themselves (three, six, nine, 'several' months — a diagnostic signature of unattested numbers). The 'nine million manuscripts' is a pop inflation with no traceable contemporary provenance. The 13th-c. Tibetan witnesses (Chag Lotsawa 1235, Dharmasvamin 1234–36) describe a damaged-but-functioning Nalanda, not a site extinguished in one fire.
Layer: fake · Place: Nalanda Mahavihara, Bihar, India
Dates: 1193 → 1193 (confidence: traditional)

After Bakhtiyar Khilji's 1193 sack of Nalanda, the university's manuscript libraries (Dharmaganja) burned continuously for months — told as three months, six months, nine months, or 'several' months depending on the telling, often paired with 'nine million manuscripts'.

Earliest attestation: No pre-modern attestation traced. The widely-quoted Raverty excerpt of the Tabaqat-i Nasiri's Bihar campaign passage describes the assault, the killing of the shaven-headed inhabitants, and the book collections — with no library-fire duration and no month-count (this run could not verify the full Persian text; what the retellings quote carries no duration). The month figures (3/6/9/'several') appear only in modern retellings — Indian exam-prep banks (testbook), blogs, popular histories — variously misattributed to the Tabaqat or vaguely to 'Tibetan sources', none producing the text.
Sources: Minhaj-i Siraj, Tabaqat-i Nasiri (c. 1260), Raverty translation — the Bihar campaign passage: assault, the shaven-headed inhabitants, the books; the quoted passage carries NO fire duration (the absence is the evidence); Testbook exam-prep banks ('Nalanda Vihar was destroyed by: Bakhtiyar Khalji — books burned for 3 months'; 'In which year was Nalanda University finally destroyed? — burnt for months') — the pop telling, captured as belief ecology; lankaweb.com 2019 retelling ('burning continued for over six months'); myindiamyglory.com ('nine months' form) — the unstable month-figures documented across tellings; Rahula Shribhadra / Dharmasvamin material (Wikipedia 'Rahula Shribhadra', citing Tibetan sources) — Nalanda 1234–36: damaged but functioning, &lt;100 monks; the positive record against the one-fire extinction frame
Falsifiability: A pre-1500 source — Persian, Tibetan, or Chinese — stating a Nalanda library-fire duration in months</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1193-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>85.4439,25.1368,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1193</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that Magna Carta created trial by jury</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills from the record itself: (1) the clause's own words — 'judicium parium' is baronial feudal justice, fellow vassals judging vassals, not a sworn jury of fact-finders (Klerman); (2) the chronology — juries of presentment existed by Henry II's Assize of Clarendon (1166), 49 years before Runnymede (companion record evt-henry-ii-assize-1166-001); (3) the reading's provenance — the jury gloss is Coke's 17th-century anachronism, corrected by the 700th-anniversary scholarship.
Layer: fake · Place: Runnymede, Surrey, England (the myth's alleged birthplace)
Dates: 1215 → present (confidence: high)

Magna Carta (1215) created, invented, or first granted the right to trial by jury — that before Runnymede there was no jury, and the charter gave England (and by inheritance, America) the jury as a new institution.

Earliest attestation: The myth has no medieval attestation — no 13th- or 14th-century source reads clause 39 as creating the jury. It enters as a 17th-century common-law fiction (Coke), is repeated by Lilburne (1640s), Blackstone, and into textbooks and civics teaching.
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Trial by Jury — Magna Carta: Muse and Mentor': 'The origins of the jury trial precede the creation of Magna Carta' and 'Magna Carta did not institute the jury system in the modern sense'; Daniel Klerman (USC Gould): 'this is not trial by jury' — jury trials were unknown in criminal cases in 1215; criminal cases were resolved by ordeal or battle; The Economist (via endofyourarm.com summary): Magna Carta 'did not... spawn democracy... or trial by jury (which was already in use)'; 700th-anniversary scholarship summary: Coke's interpretation of chapter 29 was anachronistic — a 17th-century reading of a 13th-century text
Falsifiability: A 13th-century reading of clause 39 as creating or mandating the jury; or evidence that no jury of any form existed in England before 15 June 1215</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1215-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.5336,51.4433,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1215</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Turin Shroud linen</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent AMS labs converged on AD 1260-1390 for the LINEN (Damon et al., Nature 1989, 95% confidence; labs still stand by it; Lirey's 1354 documentary appearance corroborates). A 1st-century burial cloth cannot be woven of 14th-century flax. The 2026 peer-reviewed metagenomic study (Barcaccia et al., Sci Rep) found the cloth's DNA to be a contamination mosaic dominated by its own 1978 sampler — it says nothing about age, and changes nothing about the linen date.
Layer: fake · Place: Turin Cathedral, Italy (cloth kept; sample taken 1988)
Dates: 1260 → 1390 (confidence: high)

The linen of the Turin Shroud is the burial cloth of Jesus of Nazareth, woven in the 1st century CE.

Earliest attestation: The cloth's first certain historical record is the second half of the 14th century (Lirey, France, 1354) — consistent with the radiocarbon result
Sources: Damon, P.E. et al., 'Radiocarbon Dating of the Shroud of Turin', Nature (1989): 1260-1390 CE at 95% confidence; 'conclusive evidence that the linen of the Shroud of Turin is mediaeval'; Raw-data release (British Museum, 2017, after legal request); Casabianca et al. 2019 statistical re-analysis — heterogeneity in the data, procedure should be reconsidered (caveat carried, does not overturn the medieval result); Statistical heterogeneity in the three labs' data: Riani, Atkinson &amp; Corbellini (2013); First certain historical record: Lirey, France, mid-14th century — corroborates the radiocarbon window from the documentary side; Barcaccia et al., 'DNA Traces on the Shroud of Turin: Metagenomics of the 1978 Official Sample Collection', Scientific Reports (2026), doi:10.1038/s41598-026-60684-7 — first PCR-free metagenomic sequencing of the 1978 official samples: the strongest human DNA in three clean profiles matched the 1978 sampler himself (Pierluigi Baima Bollone, co-author, d. Nov 2025), plus a contamination mosaic of human, animal (cat, dog, cattle, pig), plant (incl. maize, banana, peanut — post-Columbian species, a contamination fingerprint), fungal, and archaeal DNA. The authors state explicitly the study does not determine age or authenticity; Scientific American (2026) reported experts' verdict that it 'doesn't show the relic's origins'. Added 2026-10-16 (III.21 second pass).; Freer-Waters &amp; Jull (2026) — direct examination of the surviving Arizona fragments of the 1988 sample: textilely consistent with the main Shroud (flax, z-twisted yarns, 3/1 herringbone twill, matching thread counts); no signs of repair, no obvious contamination. The study did NOT perform new carbon dating — it tested the integrity of the surviving material from the old sample, and closes the repair-patch objection for the examined fragments (not for the whole strip). Record: evt-shroud-arizona-fragments-2026-001. Added 2026-10-16 (III.21 third pass).
Falsifiability: Re-sampling the main body of the cloth from multiple locations, with independent blind analysis converging on a 1st-century date, would reopen the claim. The custodians have not authorized further sampling.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1260-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.68,45.07,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1260</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Jehovah' as the original pronunciation of the divine name</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiple independent encyclopedic authorities (Britannica, Jewish Encyclopedia 1901, Encyclopedia Judaica, Harper's Bible Dictionary) agree 'Jehovah' is an artificial hybrid of YHWH's consonants with the vowels of 'Adonai,' never used by the Jews.
Layer: fake · Place: Jerusalem — locus of the divine-name tradition
Dates: 1278 → 1520 (confidence: medium)

'Jehovah' is the original pronunciation of the Hebrew divine name — the way ancient Israelites spoke YHWH.

Earliest attestation: The misreading itself is a late invention: Jewish Encyclopedia (1901): 'comparatively recent invention'; the pre-Masoretic and ancient sources (Theodoret, Jerome, Origen, Philo) report the Tetragrammaton was written but not pronounced.
Sources: Encyclopaedia Britannica ('Yahweh' article): 'The Masoretes ... replaced the vowels of the name YHWH with the vowel signs of Adonai or Elohim. Thus the artificial name Jehovah came into being.'; The Jewish Encyclopedia (1901), 'Jehovah': 'A mispronunciation (introduced by Christian theologians, but almost entirely disregarded by the Jews) of the Hebrew Yhwh ... grammatically impossible.'; Harper's Bible Dictionary (1985), 'tetragrammaton': 'The hybrid word Jehovah is a combination of the vowels of Adonai with the consonants of the tetragrammaton.'; Webster's New Collegiate Dictionary (1973): 'Jehovah — False reading of the Hebrew YAHWEH.'
Falsifiability: A pre-Masoretic Hebrew inscription or transcription actually voweled as 'Jehovah' would reopen it; none exists in the consulted record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1278-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1278</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'died at thirty' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The number ~30 is a REAL statistic misread as a death age. It is life expectancy AT BIRTH — a population mean dominated by extreme infant/child mortality (in the Roman Empire up to a third of infants died before one; in Sweden 1750-1780 40% of children died before 15, per parish records). The conditional figure tells the truth: in medieval England, landowning boys with life expectancy at birth of 31.3 years had life expectancy at age 25 of +25.7 years — they lived to ~51 on average, and many into their 70s and 80s. The myth takes a mean and mistakes it for a biography.
Layer: fake · Place: England (the IPM data's coverage)
Dates: 1300 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The typical (average) person in medieval Europe died at about age 30 — most medieval people were dead by their thirties.

Earliest attestation: The statistic's source data: the Inquisitions Post Mortem (English landholders, c. 1300-1500), analyzed in Josiah C. Russell, British Medieval Population (1958).
Sources: Josiah C. Russell, British Medieval Population (1958): Inquisitions Post Mortem data — life expectancy at birth for landowning males ~31.3 years, but life expectancy at age 25 of ~25.7 additional years (average age ~50.7 for those reaching 25); Max Roser, Esteban Ortiz-Ospina, Hannah Ritchie, 'Life Expectancy' (Our World in Data, first published 2013, revised 2019): premodern life expectancy ~30 in all regions; the increase since is driven largely by falling child mortality (CC-BY); Max Roser, 'Mortality in the past: every second child died' (Our World in Data, 2023): historical child-mortality evidence — ~one-quarter of infants died in their first year, ~half of all children before the end of puberty across many premodern societies; E.A. Wrigley &amp; R.S. Schofield, The Population History of England, 1541-1871 (1981): life-table method; even in the early-modern era, survivors of childhood had decades of life remaining — the demographic mechanism predates medicine
Falsifiability: A medieval cohort life table in which the modal age at death for those surviving childhood was ~30 — i.e., half the population dying in its thirties despite surviving infancy.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1300-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.5,52.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1300</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Kebra Nagast as ancient eyewitness account (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The ancient-date reading dies on the book's own testimony: its colophon anchors the compilation to the 14th century (Conti Rossini/Littmann/Cerulli: 1314–1321/22; Marcus: six Tigrayan scribes, early 1300s), composed to legitimize Yekuno Amlak's 1270 restoration. Munro-Hay: no Ethiopian monarch claimed Solomonic descent before the 13th century. A text cannot be an eyewitness to events 2,300 years before it was written.
Layer: fake · Place: Axum (Aksum), Tigray Region, Ethiopia — composition circle
Dates: 1314 → 1322 (confidence: high)

That the Kebra Nagast is an ancient text — an eyewitness-era account reaching back to the 10th century BCE, contemporary with Solomon and the Queen of Sheba — whose antiquity vouches for the Ark's migration to Ethiopia as recorded history.

Earliest attestation: The manuscript tradition itself — in its existing form at least seven hundred years old, i.e., 14th c. — attests the claim's AGE as a tradition, not the claim's truth as history
Sources: Colophon testimony (Arabic Year of Mercy 409 / 1225 CE translation chain) — the book dates itself to the 13th–14th c.; Harold Marcus, A History of Ethiopia, p. 17 — six Tigrayan scribes, early 14th c., blending oral traditions, biblical texts, apocrypha, Jewish and Islamic commentaries; Stuart Munro-Hay — no pre-13th-century Solomonic-descent claims in the monarchic record
Falsifiability: This claim was falsified by the manuscript and colophon evidence itself: the book's own colophon places the compilation in the 13th–14th c., and no pre-14th-century witness to the received narrative exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1314-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>38.7229,14.1379,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1314</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Gutenberg invented printing"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The dated Chinese woodblock print of 868 CE and the Korean movable-metal-type book of 1377 each predate Mainz by centuries; the claim 'first in the world' is disproven by the objects themselves.
Layer: fake · Place: Mainz (Holy Roman Empire)
Dates: 1440 → 1455 (confidence: medium)

Johannes Gutenberg of Mainz invented printing — the first person in the world to develop a method of printing text.

Earliest attestation: The Mainz workshop's printed works and the court record of the 1455 Fust v. Gutenberg suit (the invention's transmission-era documentation)
Sources: Gutenberg Bible (42-line Bible), Mainz, 1454–55 — ~180 copies printed, ~49 extant; the masterpiece of the Mainz workshop; The Diamond Sutra (868 CE) and Bi Sheng's movable type (1041–48 CE) — the prior art, carried in evt-diamond-sutra-001 and evt-bi-sheng-movable-type-001; Fiveable AP European History study guide, unit 1.4 — lists 'Gutenberg invented printing itself' as a common misconception: 'Printing existed earlier, including in China. Gutenberg's contribution in Europe was a fast, practical movable-type press in the 1450s.'
Falsifiability: Authentication of a printed book earlier than the Diamond Sutra would not save the claim; only evidence that the Chinese dates are all wrong could revive it</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1440-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.28,50.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1440</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Flavio Gioja 'invented the compass at Amalfi in 1302' — the inventor who was a misplaced comma (legend killed at the Latin)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The attribution chain is fully documented and self-destroying: (1) Flavius Blondus (~1450) wrote only that use of the magnet was known at Amalfi, origin unknown; (2) Baptista Pio (1511) repeated it 'as related by Flavius' ('a Flavio dicitur' — 'as Flavius says'); (3) Lilio Gyraldus (1540) misread the phrase as 'by a certain Flavius'; (4) the 17th century solidified the misreading into an inventor named 'Flavio Gioja' and an invention date of 1302 (Amir Aczel, The Riddle of the Compass, 2001: the whole riddle turns on 'the correct interpretation of a single Latin phrase'; multiple sources note he 'may have never existed'). Independent kill: two surviving European compass descriptions predate 1302 anyway — Neckam ~1190 (VERIFIED, evt-compass-europe-neckam-1190-001) and Petrus Peregrinus's Epistola de magnete (1269) — so even a real Gioja could not be the inventor. Fenced: Amalfi's genuine early *use* of the floating magnet (1911 Britannica) survives the grade; it is the invention and the person that die.
Layer: fake · Place: Amalfi, Italy — the attributed invention place (the statue stands there)
Dates: 1450-01-01 → 1600-12-31 (confidence: high)

That Flavio Gioja (Gioia) of Amalfi invented the magnetic compass in 1302 — the schoolbook 'inventor' of the mariner's compass. The claim is an attribution legend built on a 16th-century Latin misreading; the person most likely never existed.

Earliest attestation: Flavius Blondus, Italia illustrata (~1450) — which says only that the magnet's use was known at Amalfi, origin unknown.
Sources: Wikipedia 'Flavio Gioja': 'Ioannes Gira Amalphensis' (c. 1300-?); 'It is also likely that Flavio Gioja did not exist at all'; the name 'Flavio' is a translation error (probably Giovanni); the misplaced-comma story (Pio 1511: 'In Amalfi... the use of the magnet was invented, according to Flavio' misread as 'invented by Flavio'); 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Compass': the Gioja legend genealogy — Flavius Blondus (1450) wrote only that use of the magnet was known at Amalfi, origin unknown; Baptista Pio (1511) repeated it 'as related by Flavius'; Lilio Gyraldus (1540) misunderstood 'a Flavio dicitur' as 'by a certain Flavius'; the legend took shape in the 17th century as invention in 1302 by 'Flavio Gioja of Amalfi'; Italian sailors used the floating magnet 'probably introduced... by traders belonging to the port of Amalfi'; Amir Aczel, The Riddle of the Compass (2001) (review via Science News/thefreelibrary.com): the Flavio Gioia of 1302 'may have never existed,' with the riddle turning on 'the correct interpretation of a single Latin phrase' in a 16th-c commentary on Lucretius; Naples Life, Death &amp; Miracle — Amalfi page: 'Flavio Gioia, in spite of the nice statue in Amalfi (photo), most likely never existed'; confusion from Flavio Biondo (1392-1463) reporting the compass as an Amalfitan invention, passed on as 'a Flavio dicitur'; Scipione Mazzella (~1600) located him in Gioia, Puglia
Falsifiability: A contemporary (c. 1300) document naming a Flavio Gioja of Amalfi as compass inventor would revive the claim; five centuries of scholarship have produced none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1450-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>14.6,40.63,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1450</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'millions burned as witches' — the nine-million figure</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) SCALE — the scholarly estimates (Levack: ~90,000 prosecutions, ~45,000 executions 1450-1750; Oxford Handbook: ~100,000 tried, ~60,000 executed) converge on tens of thousands, two orders of magnitude below "millions." (2) ERA — the hunts peak 1560-1630, early modern and largely secular/Protestant courts (Germany, Switzerland, Scotland, England), not the Middle Ages. (3) INSTITUTION — the medieval Inquisition targeted heretics, not witches: Canon Episcopi (c. 906, carried in Gratian's Decretum) declared belief in night-flight witchcraft a delusion; inquisitorial courts were often the skeptics (Spain's 1614 reversal ended witch-burnings).
Layer: fake · Place: Europe (witch-hunt era 1450-1750); myth's birthplace: 19th-century Germany and the United States
Dates: 1450 → 1750 (confidence: high)

Millions of people were executed as witches during the Middle Ages, primarily by the medieval Inquisition — the popular "millions burned as witches" figure (often nine million).

Earliest attestation: Matilda Joslyn Gage, Woman, Church and State (1893) — the earliest documented mass-circulation of the "nine million" figure (repeated from German authors per the scholarly reception history); the millions figure has no earlier counting basis.
Sources: Brian P. Levack, The Witch-Hunt in Early Modern Europe (2006): ~90,000 prosecutions, ~45,000 executions across Europe 1450-1750; Oxford Handbook of Witchcraft in Early Modern Europe and Colonial America (Levack ed.): ~100,000 tried, ~60,000 executed; Wikipedia, 'Feminist interpretations of witch trials in the early modern period': Gage (1893) repeated the erroneous nine-million figure from German authors
Falsifiability: Contemporary court records showing executions in the hundreds-of-thousands per decade, sustained across the hunt era, would force a revision. The actual registers — where they survive — run to the low tens of thousands total.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1450-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1450</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Marble Emperor legend (Constantine XI)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The emperor's death in the final assault is the near-universal tradition across independent Greek and Ottoman sources; no contemporary source in any tradition attests the angel, the marble, or the cave. The legend is centuries-deep folk belief, not evidence.
Layer: fake · Place: Constantinople — the Golden Gate (Yedikule) of the legend
Dates: 1453-05-29 → 1453-05-29 (confidence: high)

Constantine XI Palaiologos did not die when Constantinople fell on 29 May 1453; instead an angel rescued him, turned him into marble, and hid him in a cave beneath the Golden Gate of Constantinople, where he sleeps awaiting God's call to awaken and reconquer the city for Christendom.

Earliest attestation: The death: near-universal tradition across Greek (Doukas, Kritovoulos, Sphrantzes) and Ottoman (Tursun Beg) sources, 1453-1470s. The marble legend: Greek demotic folk-song tradition of the Halosis (fall), documented by scholars from the 19th century onward — centuries after the event, never contemporary.
Sources: Michael Kritovoulos, History of Mehmed the Conqueror — the last charge tradition; Doukas, Historia Turco-Byzantina — the death tradition; Tursun Beg, Târîh-i Ebü'l-Feth — Ottoman account of the fall; Greek demotic folk-song tradition of the Halosis (the fall) — the Marble Emperor (Marmaromenos Vasilias) legend's attested home; documented in 19th-c. collections onward
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1453-1460s) attestation of the angel, the marble transformation, or the Golden Gate cave in any of the four source traditions — none exists in Venetian, Genoese, Byzantine, or Ottoman accounts</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1453-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1453</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Aixa's "weep like a woman" rebuke to Boabdil (1492)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No contemporary (15th-century) attestation of the quote or the scene exists; the near-contemporary eyewitness record of the surrender describes a different ceremony entirely. The earliest traceable forms sit in fictionalizing romance literature a century-plus later, and the canonical wording is Irving's 1829 English — a literary scene, not a documented utterance.
Layer: fake · Place: El Suspiro del Moro, near Padul, Granada (traditional site of the alleged scene)
Dates: 1492 → 1492 (confidence: traditional)

As Boabdil departed into exile in 1492, pausing at the hill called El Suspiro del Moro to look back at Granada, his mother Aixa (Aisha al-Hurra) rebuked him with words to the effect of "Weep like a woman for what you could not defend as a man" (Spanish: "Llora como mujer lo que no supiste defender como hombre").

Earliest attestation: No 15th-century attestation known. Earliest traceable forms sit in the early-modern morisco romance tradition (Perez de Hita, 1595/1619, writing through a fictitious Arabic narrator); the canonical Anglophone wording is Washington Irving's Chronicle of the Conquest of Granada (1829).
Sources: Eyewitness letter to the bishop of Leon, 8 January 1492 (six days after the surrender) — describes the surrender ceremony at the Genil in detail; records no hill-top farewell and no maternal rebuke; Christopher Columbus, journal prologue 1492 ("on the 2nd January I saw the royal banners ... planted ... on the towers of the Alhambra") — contemporary record of the surrender, no quote; Gines Perez de Hita, Historia de los bandos de los Zegries y los Abencerrajes (1595), 2nd part Guerras civiles de Granada (1619) — the morisco romance tradition that fictionalized the fall of Granada in chivalric idiom; Washington Irving, A Chronicle of the Conquest of Granada (1829) — canonical Anglophone form: "You do well ... to weep like a woman for what you failed to defend like a man"; Irving also fixed the hill's name as "El ultimo suspiro del Moro"; Edward Bulwer Lytton, Leila, or The Siege of Granada (1838) — repeats the rebuke as "the reproach which history has preserved"; the 1900 Nuttall Encyclopedia entry for Boabdil repeats it as fact
Falsifiability: A pre-1600 source attesting the quote or the hill-top farewell scene would overturn this grade</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.63,36.99,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that Columbus discovered the Americas in 1492</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) The Norse reached North America ~470 years earlier — VERIFIED at L'Anse aux Meadows (1021 CE, metal-cut timber). (2) The Americas were inhabited for millennia before any European arrived, so 'discovery' of the land is a framing, not a fact. What Columbus did — initiate sustained, consequential European contact, the Columbian Exchange — is historically pivotal but is not discovery.
Layer: fake · Place: San Salvador Island, Bahamas (traditional Guanahani landfall, 12 Oct 1492)
Dates: 1492 → 1492 (confidence: high)

Christopher Columbus discovered the Americas in 1492 — i.e., he was the first European to reach them.

Earliest attestation: The 'discovery' framing crystallized in the 19th century (Washington Irving's 1828 biography; U.S. quadricentennial 1892); Columbus's own journal (via Las Casas's abstract) never claims a first landing.
Sources: L'Anse aux Meadows archaeology: Norse settlement c. 1000 CE, felled timber dated 1021 CE (Kuitems et al. 2021, Nature); Vinland sagas: Eiríks saga rauða, Grænlendinga saga; Columbus's journal (Bartolomé de las Casas abstract); the four voyages 1492-1504; death at Valladolid 1506 believing he had reached Asia; Archaeological consensus: human presence in the Americas for 15,000+ years before 1492
Falsifiability: The Norse leg is in principle revisable (if L'Anse aux Meadows were ever re-attributed, which no evidence supports); the Indigenous-presence leg is not — inhabited land cannot be 'discovered' by a later arrival under any evidence.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.52391,24.05139,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1492 'transfusion' of Pope Innocent VIII</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Sole-source hostile gossip from an explicitly unreliable chronicler; the 'transfusion into the pope' leg is a later misreading of a blood-letting account; and a transfusion was conceptually impossible in 1492 — circulation was unknown until Harvey 1628.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome, Vatican
Dates: 1492 → 1492 (confidence: low)

Pope Innocent VIII (Giovanni Battista Cybo/Cibo, d. 25 July 1492) received the first recorded blood transfusion in history — the blood of three young boys transfused into the dying pontiff on the suggestion of a Jewish physician.

Earliest attestation: Stefano Infessura, Diarium urbis Romae (contemporary Roman diary, partly Latin, partly Romanesco; Infessura d. c. 1500)
Sources: Stefano Infessura, Diarium urbis Romae — the boys' blood-letting account (three ten-year-old boys, a ducat each, boys die, physician flees, pope not healed); Wikipedia, 'Innocent VIII' — 'A possibly apocryphal story stated that Innocent's physician drew the blood from three young boys to transfuse it into the Pope but the treatment failed and all four involved died.'; Scientific American guest blog, 'Blood Lust: The Early History of Transfusion' — 'skeptical historians rightly doubt that there is much truth behind this politicized papal story'; New Catholic Dictionary (via DBpedia 2014) on Infessura — 'he inserted every fragment of the most preposterous and malevolent gossip current in Roman society, and is therefore not considered a reliable chronicler'; Infessura belonged to the antipapal faction of the Roman Academy; Phil Learoyd, 'The Early History of Blood Transfusion' (historyofbloodtransfusion.co.uk, 2023) — argues the 'transfusion' reading stems from a later misinterpretation (Villari) of Infessura's blood-letting account; notes circulation was unknown; William Harvey, De Motu Cordis (1628) — blood circulation unknown until 136 years after 1492; no concept of circulation and no method of intravenous access existed in 1492, making a transfusion anachronistic by definition
Falsifiability: A second, independent contemporary source confirming a deliberate vein-to-vein (or vessel-to-mouth) blood transfer in Rome in 1492 — no such source exists, and the anachronism makes the search nearly closed</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4534,41.9029,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'pristine wilderness' myth of the Americas in 1492</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The scholarly record contradicts the claim on every leg: populations were large, forests were managed (fire, terra preta, milpa), earthworks and cities (Cahokia, Upano) are on the ground, and Denevan's closing argument — that human presence was LESS visible in 1750 than in 1492 — shows the 'wilderness' the romantics admired was the aftermath, not the original.
Layer: fake · Place: The Americas (the claim covers the hemisphere)
Dates: 1492 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The belief that in 1492 the Americas were a sparsely populated wilderness, 'a world of barely perceptible human disturbance' — an almost untouched, Edenic land untrammeled by man.

Earliest attestation: The claim as a scholarly refutation: William M. Denevan, 'The Pristine Myth: The Landscape of the Americas in 1492', Annals of the Association of American Geographers 82(3), September 1992, pp. 369-385
Sources: Denevan 1992 abstract: 'There is substantial evidence, however, that the Native American landscape of the early sixteenth century was a humanized landscape almost everywhere. Populations were large. Forest composition had been modified, grasslands had been created, wildlife disrupted, and erosion was severe in places. Earthworks, roads, fields, and settlements were ubiquitous. With Indian depopulation in the wake of Old World disease, the environment recovered in many areas.'; Denevan's Amazonia field record: prehistoric raised fields, causeways, and canals reported in Scientific American (1967) and Science (1970); terra preta (anthropogenic dark earth) across Amazonia; Upano Valley laser mapping (Science, January 2024): a ~6,000-year-old urban complex under the Ecuadorian Amazon — the largest and oldest yet found; Charles C. Mann, 1491: New Revelations of the Americas Before Columbus — the popular synthesis: 'Columbus set foot in a hemisphere thoroughly dominated by humankind'
Falsifiability: A continent-wide survey of pre-1492 landscapes showing no pre-contact modification — no earthworks, no anthropogenic soils, no managed forests; every line of evidence has gone the opposite way</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1492-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-60.0,0.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1492</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The "toothbrush was invented in China in 1498" telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No contemporary source attests the year 1498; the date is a modern reference-book convention, and the literature cannot agree whether the brush is 7th-, 13th-, or 15th-century. Two independent kills: documentary absence + the wandering date.
Layer: fake · Place: China (Hongzhi reign, 1488–1505, conventionally)
Dates: 1498 → 1498 (confidence: low)

The toothbrush was invented in China in 1498, during the reign of the Hongzhi Emperor.

Earliest attestation: Modern reference-work convention; no contemporary Ming source for the year has been located (realrareantiques.com: 'We have not found a contemporary source for it, and it should be treated with caution').
Sources: realrareantiques.com, 'Hongzhi Emperor (r. 1487–1505)' — 'A widely repeated modern claim credits the Hongzhi reign with the invention of the bristle toothbrush, in 1498. We have not found a contemporary source for it, and it should be treated with caution.'; Dr. Scott Swank, National Museum of Dentistry, via HowStuffWorks: 'The first device that we would consider the precursor to the modern toothbrush is recorded in China circa 1500' — 'circa', not 1498; The wandering date in the literature: Tang-dynasty 7th c., 600–900, 13th-c. first written mention, c. 1500, 15th–16th c. — a precise year that moves by eight centuries across sources has no document behind it
Falsifiability: A contemporary Ming document — court record, workshop order, or traveler's diary — explicitly dating a bristle toothbrush to 1498 would restore the year (the brush itself needs no defense).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1498-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>105.0,35.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1498</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Here be dragons' — the convention that never existed</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The phrase survives on exactly ONE accepted historical object — the Hunt–Lenox Globe (c. 1508–1510), 'HC SVNT DRACONES' below the equator on the eastern Asian coast. No medieval mappamundi, portolan chart, or earlier globe carries it; the classical cartographers' actual formula was 'hic sunt leones' (here are lions). The 'medieval practice' is a modern invention wearing period costume.
Layer: fake · Place: New York Public Library, Rare Book Division, New York City (the phrase's surviving habitat)
Dates: 1508 → 1510 (confidence: high)

'Here be dragons' (hic sunt dracones) was a standard warning that medieval and Renaissance cartographers inscribed on unexplored or dangerous regions of their maps.

Earliest attestation: Hunt–Lenox Globe, c. 1508–1510 — the only accepted surviving historical object bearing the phrase; no medieval mappamundi or earlier chart carries it
Sources: NYPL — The Hunt-Lenox Globe (institution page: 'one of only two medieval or Renaissance-era maps or globes known to bear the famous motto Hic Sunt Dracones'); Wikipedia — 'Here be dragons' ('the only known historical use of this phrase... is on the Hunt-Lenox Globe'; 'the phrase itself is largely an anachronism'; the classical medieval phrase was 'hic sunt leones'); Geography Realm — 'The Map Myth of Here Be Dragons' (survey of known instances: the Lenox Globe as the only documented example; the Ostrich Egg Globe as the contested second)
Falsifiability: A pre-1508 map, chart, or mappamundi bearing 'hic sunt dracones' (or a vernacular equivalent) in an unexplored region, verified by independent scholarly transcription — the scholarly survey to date has found none</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1508-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9822,40.7532,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1508</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The handful-of-conquistadors myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The conquering force was overwhelmingly indigenous: the final assault on Tenochtitlan was carried out with roughly 200,000 native allies against ~900 Spaniards — Cortés's own figure — and Restall's archival audit shows Africans equaled or exceeded the Spanish conquistadors in number; native sources saw a civil war in which the Spaniards played an important but secondary role.
Layer: fake · Place: Tenochtitlan (present-day Mexico City)
Dates: 1519 → 1521 (confidence: high)

Hernán Cortés conquered the Aztec Empire with about five hundred Spaniards — a handful of Europeans toppling a great empire by their own arms.

Earliest attestation: The conquest narratives themselves — Cortés's Cartas de relación (1519–1526), Bernal Díaz del Castillo's Historia verdadera (written c. 1568, published 1632), and the later popular histories that foregrounded Spanish arms.
Sources: Matthew Restall, Seven Myths of the Spanish Conquest (Oxford University Press, 2003), ch. 3: 'The Myth of the White Conquistador' — the actual military operations were undertaken by indigenous allies outnumbering the Spanish by many hundreds to one; WorldCat/OCLC 51022823 + Stanford SearchWorks record for Seven Myths of the Spanish Conquest (Restall, 2003, OUP) — bibliographic verification; Hernán Cortés, letter to Charles V (the third carta de relación, 1522) — his own report that the final assault was carried out with 'we Spaniards only some nine hundred' against allied forces 'numbering more than 150,000'; Wikipedia, 'Siege of Tenochtitlan' — force figures for 1521: 900–1,300 Spanish infantry plus ~200,000 native allies, with cited references; Wikipedia, 'Seven Myths of the Spanish Conquest' — summary of Restall's chapter: the final assault was carried out with 200,000 native allies, and Africans equaled or exceeded the Spaniards in number
Falsifiability: Contemporary muster rolls or logistics records showing the 1521 assault was conducted primarily by Spanish troops would refute the indigenous-coalition account and revive the myth.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1519-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-99.131,19.435,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1519</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Catherine de' Medici introduced the fork to France in 1533' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 220th DEBUNKED family. Three kills: (1) Francis I, Catherine's father-in-law, dined at elite Italian tables and encountered — even imported — Italian culinary practice long before 1533; (2) the fork was already circulating in Italian elite circles (56 silver forks in the Medici kitchen by the 1400s) — the 'introduction' credits Catherine with a transmission that predates her; (3) the popular timeline itself grades her French campaign as a failed promotion — the myth refutes itself. No period source names her as the fork's introducer.
Layer: fake · Place: The French court, Paris (the alleged introduction)
Dates: 1533 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Catherine de' Medici introduced the table fork to France when she traveled there in 1533 to marry the future Henri II, acting as the fork's emissary to the French court.

Earliest attestation: Modern popular-history tellings; no period (16th-century) source names Catherine as the fork's introducer
Sources: History Expose / Factinate: 'Foodie Fashions: Debunked!' — the idea that Catherine introduced the fork from Italy to France 'is a routinely discredited myth'; historians note her father-in-law Francis I dined at elite Italian tables and encountered their culinary practices long before her arrival; National Geographic History Jan/Feb 2022 fork timeline: 'Traveling to France to marry Henry II in 1533, Catherine de Médicis became the emissary of the fork in the royal court. She ran into immediate resistance... tries (and fails) to promote it' — the popular version frames her campaign as a failed promotion, not a successful introduction; PopSci: 'The thousand-year story of how the fork crossed Europe' — forks common in Italy by the 14th c.; Florence's Medici kitchen held 56 silver forks by the 1400s; the fork's European spread ran through Italy's wealthy merchant classes in the 16th century
Falsifiability: A 1533-era French record — court inventory, letter, or account — naming Catherine as the fork's introducer to France; none has been produced</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1533-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1533</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Goree Door-of-No-Return throughput: 15-20 million through one house</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Arithmetic: the entire transatlantic trade embarked ~12.5 million Africans from all of Africa (evt-middle-passage-scale-001). A claim that 15-20 million passed through one building on one island exceeds the total of the whole trade by 20-60%. Documentary: Curtin estimated ~50,000 spent time on the island, ~26,000 exported; Araujo (2020) ~33,000; the island was never a key departure point (the Senegal and Gambia river mouths were), and the House of Slaves was built 1775-1778 by a wealthy merchant as one of the island's finest homes — not as a slave warehouse. The 'Door of No Return' cannot have been the mass loading point: rocks prevented safe docking; the beach was the loading place.
Layer: fake · Place: Goree Island, Senegal — House of Slaves
Dates: 1536-01-01 → 1848-04-27 (confidence: medium)

Fifteen to twenty million enslaved Africans passed through the House of Slaves on Goree Island, Senegal, through its 'Door of No Return,' on the way to the Americas — making the house the trade's principal departure point.

Earliest attestation: Portuguese record of slave trading at Goree, 1536; the '15-20 million' figure comes from the House of Slaves' own memorial literature under curator Joseph Ndiaye (1962-2009), not from any documentary source.
Sources: Chronicle of Higher Education, 'Debating the History of Slavery on Senegal's Goree Island' — the museum's literature puts 15-20 million through the House of Slaves; Curtin on the 50,000 vs 20 million; HNN (History News Network), 'The Myth of Goree Island' — Curtin's assessment: island was never a key departure point; ~50,000, not 20 million; House built 1775-1778 by a wealthy merchant; door's rocks, beach as loading point; RIA Scolony blog, 'Goree Island, House of Slaves and the Door of No Return' — Ndiaye's 10-15M figures vs Araujo's documentation; Curtin ~26,000; 40-45,000 (1677-1818); Ana Lucia Araujo (2020) — ~33,000 trafficked through Goree (cited via historytools.org summary); Voyages: The Trans-Atlantic Slave Trade Database — 12.5 million embarked across the entire trade (the denominator that kills the 15-20M claim)
Falsifiability: A documentary corpus (voyage records, company ledgers) showing Goree embarkations in the millions — against the database's ~35,000 voyages, which place the whole trade at 12.5 million.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1536-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-17.3978,14.6671,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1536</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Ottoman 'continuous decline' thesis</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The thesis confuses a literary genre with an economic diagnosis: Ottomanists showed the nasihatname complaints were a trope present even in Suleiman's 'golden age' (Howard, Abou-El-Haj), while the archives document adaptation — 'transformation, not decline' (Faroqhi, İnalcık). A 350-year unbroken decline that produces a 623-year state refutes itself; the thesis is 'discarded' (Tezcan 2010).
Layer: fake · Place: Istanbul (historiographical debate centered on the Porte's archive and European Orientalist historiography)
Dates: 1550 → 2010 (confidence: high)

The Ottoman Empire entered a continuous, unbroken decline starting in the late 16th century (after Suleiman the Magnificent's death in 1566), decaying steadily in military, fiscal, and administrative power for ~350 years until 1922 — the traditional 'decline thesis.'

Earliest attestation: The thesis's raw material: Ottoman nasihatname ('advice for kings') literature, 16th-17th c. — the complaints themselves are real documents; the reading of them as objective evidence of decline is the 20th-c. error.
Sources: Jane Hathaway, The Arab Lands under Ottoman Rule, 1516-1800 (Pearson, 2008), pp. 7-8 — 'deconstruction of the so-called Ottoman decline thesis'; Baki Tezcan, The Second Ottoman Empire: Political and Social Transformation in the Early Modern World (Cambridge UP, 2010), p. 9 — the conventional narrative 'has been discarded'; Halil İnalcık, 'Military and Fiscal Transformation in the Ottoman Empire, 1600-1700' — the period titled as transformation, not decline; Douglas Howard and Rifa'at Ali Abou-El-Haj — the nasihatname critique: 'complaint about the times' as literary trope, writers not uninfluenced by their own biases; present even during Suleiman's golden age; Rifa'at Ali Abou-El-Haj, Formation of the Modern State: The Ottoman Empire, Sixteenth to Eighteenth Centuries (SUNY, 1991); Wikipedia, 'Ottoman decline thesis' — summary of the deconstruction literature
Falsifiability: Already falsified by the archival record: 17th-century military-fiscal adaptation, monetization, decentralization policies, and three centuries of state continuity after the supposed terminal date.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1550-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.9784,41.0082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1550</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Byzantine Empire' — the name is 1557, not 330</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The name is a documented 1557 coinage applied 104 years after the empire's end; the empire's own endonym is multiply attested as Roman (see evt-rhomaioi-001). No pre-1557 source uses 'Byzantine' as a self-name.
Layer: fake · Place: Augsburg (publication place of Wolf's Corpus, Germany)
Dates: 1557 → 1557 (confidence: high)

The empire we call 'Byzantine' called itself 'Byzantine' — that 'the Byzantine Empire' was its own name and it was a civilization separate from Rome.

Earliest attestation: Hieronymus Wolf (1516–1580), Corpus Historiae Byzantinae (1557) — the first systematic application of 'Byzantine' to the medieval Greek state; popularized in the 18th–19th c. (Montesquieu's Considerations, 1734; 19th-c. scholarship)
Sources: Hieronymus Wolf, Corpus Historiae Byzantinae (1557); Kaldellis, Anthony (2019), Romanland: Ethnicity and Empire in Byzantium, Harvard UP; Browning, Robert (1992), The Byzantine Empire; Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Byzantine Empire' — the inhabitants 'referred to themselves as Romans'
Falsifiability: A pre-1557 source — imperial document, inscription, coin, or the empire's own literature — using 'Byzantine'/'Byzantines' as the state's or people's name</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1557-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.582,48.1351,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1557</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Leaning Tower drop — the experiment Galileo never ran</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No account by Galileo himself exists in his writings, letters, or notes; the story first appears in a hagiographic biography written 12 years after his death; most historians treat the event as a thought experiment at best, not a physical test.
Layer: fake · Place: Leaning Tower of Pisa, Pisa, Italy
Dates: 1589 → 1592 (confidence: low)

Galileo Galilei, while professor of mathematics at the University of Pisa (c. 1589-1592), publicly dropped balls of different weights from the Leaning Tower of Pisa to demonstrate that they fall at the same rate, disproving Aristotle in front of his colleagues and students.

Earliest attestation: Vincenzo Viviani's Racconto istorico (biography of Galileo), written 1654 - twelve years after Galileo's 1642 death - and published posthumously in 1717.
Sources: Vincenzo Viviani, Racconto istorico della vita del Sig.r Galileo Galilei (1654; publ. 1717) - sole origin of the tower story; Lane Cooper (1935) - showed Viviani's story unsupported by any evidence in Galileo's own papers; Palmieri 1989, 'Galileo, Viviani and the Tower of Pisa', Studies in History and Philosophy of Science, Vol. 20, No. 4, pp. 435-451 - Viviani's biography unreliable as modern history of science; Museo Galileo (Florence) - the tower story treated as dubious; Galileo's own writings contain no account of it; Galileo's Two New Sciences (1638) - presents inclined-plane experiments (Third Day, 'a full hundred times'), never a Pisa drop
Falsifiability: A contemporary document - a letter, university record, or notebook entry from 1589-1592 - attesting the Pisa demonstration would overturn this record; Viviani's posthumous 1654 account alone cannot.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1589-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.3964,43.7229,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1589</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'wampum was Indian money' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Indigenous-side evidence is convergent and direct: 17th-c. Dutch observation, Haudenosaunee educators' own statements, and Ceci's archaeological finding that treaty/condolence belts were curated, not traded — against zero pre-contact monetary evidence.
Layer: fake · Place: Eastern Woodlands / coastal New England and New Netherland
Dates: 1600 → 1700 (confidence: high)

The popular telling that wampum — the white and purple shell beads of the Eastern Woodlands — was 'Indian money', a currency of Indigenous societies, is false. In Munsee, Haudenosaunee, and neighboring societies the beads were not used as money: they served spiritual and ceremonial purposes, signified authority, sealed diplomacy, and were woven into belts whose designs acted as mnemonic devices for history, law, and tradition. The currency reading is the colonists' projection, not the makers' practice.

Earliest attestation: Dutch traders' 1620s observations of wampum's place in Munsee culture (noted as ceremonial/diplomatic, not monetary); modern statement: Philipse Manor Hall / Smithsonian National Numismatic Collection exhibit text
Sources: Philipse Manor Hall — 'Wampum and Colonial Currency' (citing the Smithsonian National Numismatic Collection): 'Centuries of misinformation spread the idea, still repeated today, that wampum acted as currency in the Indigenous cultures of the Eastern Woodlands... they were not used as money in Munsee society or in those of neighboring Indigenous cultures'; Rogue Art Historian — 'Law You Can Hold: Wampum, Sovereignty, and the Refusal to Disappear' — Haudenosaunee Guide for Educators: Europeans' legal-tender use was appropriation; 'Haudenosaunee themselves did not use wampum as money'; Ceci's archaeological study: condolence strings and treaty belts curated, not freely traded; Cronon, William (via veridelisi synthesis) — wampum as 'medium of gift giving' among Indigenous societies rather than medium of commerce; JSTOR Daily — 'Wampum was Massachusetts' First Legal Currency' — Bradley: wampum's success as cross-cultural communication ('show respect, negotiate and seal agreements'); beads cut from seashells, value by belt length and quality
Falsifiability: A pre-contact Indigenous price schedule, account, or legal code denominating obligations in wampum — none has been found; the colonial legal-tender phase (separate record) is the documented money use</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1600-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0,42.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1600</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Don Quixote was the first novel ever written</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The dates kill the superlative, not the book. Genji's c. 1008–1021 attestation is near-contemporary and independent (two diarists); Quixote's is 1605. Six centuries of fiction separate the two.
Layer: fake · Place: Madrid, Spain (1605 first-part printing, Juan de la Cuesta)
Dates: 1605 → 1615 (confidence: high)

Don Quixote by Miguel de Cervantes — first part published 1605, second part 1615 — was the first novel ever written.

Earliest attestation: The 1605 Madrid edition itself; the 1614 Tarragona edition of Avellaneda's Segundo tomo (the unauthorized sequel that forced Cervantes's Part II); Cervantes's contemporary dedications, prologues, and the 2015 Cervantes-remains findings carry the biography, not the publication.
Sources: El ingenioso hidalgo don Quixote de la Mancha, Part I (Madrid: Francisco de Robles / Juan de la Cuesta, 1605); Part II (1615) — the dated artifacts; Avellaneda's Segundo tomo del ingenioso hidalgo don Quixote de la Mancha (Tarragona, 1614) — unauthorized sequel; independent external witness that Part I was in circulation by 1614, and that Cervantes answered it inside his own Part II; Intellicurean, 'the novel' (2026): 'the book that literary history calls the first modern novel' — the qualifier as the field states it; 2002 Norwegian Book Clubs poll of 100 authors from 54 countries: Don Quixote named the best literary work ever written — the reputation is real; the 'first novel' superlative is the inflation
Falsifiability: Already falsified: The Tale of Genji (evt-tale-of-genji-001) is securely attested at c. 1008–1021 — six centuries before Quixote's first part. Only a redefinition of 'novel' that excludes Genji could resurrect the bare claim, and the popular statement carries no such qualifier.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1605-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.7,40.42,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1605</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: 'Galileo invented the telescope'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Direct chronological contradiction by contemporaneous documents: the invention is on record in the States-General Acts ten months before Galileo's first instrument. Galileo's own account agrees — he never claimed the invention. The textbook sentence has no source; the Dutch priority has a government paper trail.
Layer: fake · Place: Middelburg, Netherlands (where the device was made); Venice, Italy (where Galileo learned of it, Nov 1608)
Dates: 1608 → 1610 (confidence: high)

The textbook sentence 'Galileo invented the telescope' is false. Galileo Galilei never claimed to have invented the telescope — he learned of the Dutch device from his friend Paolo Sarpi in Venice (who received the Hague newsletter in November 1608), built his own improved instruments from August 1609 (up to ~20-30x), and was the FIRST to turn a telescope to systematic astronomical observation: the four moons of Jupiter (January 1610), the mountains of the Moon, the phases of Venus — published in Sidereus Nuncius (March 1610). The device demonstrably predates his first build by documented months; the States-General Acts show it already being copied in Middelburg before he had heard of it. Twenty-eighth DEBUNKED family.

Earliest attestation: The same documentary trail as evt-telescope-invention-001 (States-General Acts, Oct 1608) precedes Galileo's instrument by ~10 months; Galileo's own correspondence documents learning of the device and then building one. His achievement is attested in Sidereus Nuncius (March 1610) — the claim under grading is the priority sentence, not his observations.
Sources: Pelling, 'Who invented the telescope?': Sarpi received the Hague newsletter in Venice in November 1608 and discussed it with Galileo — the documented learning chain; Britannica (print article, 22 March 2024): Jacques Bovedere of Paris reported the invention to Galileo, 'who promptly built his own telescope' — the secondary tradition's own phrasing concedes the build came second; Galileo's Sidereus Nuncius (March 1610) + the January 1610 Jupiter observations — primary attestation of what he ACTUALLY did first, kept fenced in the grade scope
Falsifiability: A document showing Galileo built a working telescope before October 1608 — none exists; his own letters document the learning sequence (Sarpi → build → improve → point skyward).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1608-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.6136,51.4988,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1608</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'seven candles of the Temple' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The documents kill it twice: Exodus 27:20 prescribes pure beaten olive oil ('to cause the lamp to burn always'), and Exodus 25:31-37 describes seven lamps on a gold lampstand — lamps, not candles. 'Candlestick' is the 1611 KJV translation of 'menorah' (lampstand), and it smuggled the modern candle image in; wax candles as ritual light belong to medieval Europe, a millennium-plus after the Temple fell.
Layer: fake · Place: Jerusalem (the Temple); the retelling lives in modern illustration
Dates: 1611 → 2026 (confidence: low)

The Temple menorah is pictured in modern retellings as 'seven candles' — the Temple lit by seven wax candles.

Earliest attestation: The modern 'seven candles' visualization (children's books, films, greeting-card art); no ancient source describes wax candles in the Temple
Sources: Exodus 27:20; Leviticus 24:2 — the oil prescription (against the wax reading); Exodus 25:31-37 — seven lamps on the lampstand (against the candle reading); The KJV's 'candlestick' rendering of 'menorah' (lampstand) — the translation layer that manufactured the telling; the Hebrew means lampstand
Falsifiability: A Second Temple-era text or artifact showing wax candles as the menorah's fuel would falsify the debunk</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1611-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1611</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Genesis 5 fixes Creation to 4004 BCE' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — A literal chronology requires one unique number-set; the manuscript traditions give three (1656, 1307, 2242 for Adam-to-Flood, and 365/1015/1245 post-Flood-to-Abraham). The telling collapses on the documents themselves.
Layer: fake · Place: Textual tradition (Jerusalem/MT; Samaria; Alexandria/LXX)
Dates: 1650 → 2026 (confidence: low)

Read literally, the Genesis 5 genealogy yields one consistent chronology that fixes Creation to 4004 BCE — Archbishop Ussher's famous date — as the text's own calendar.

Earliest attestation: Ussher's Annals of the World (1650) computed Creation to the evening of Saturday 22 / Sunday 23 October 4004 BCE (Julian). The telling persists in young-earth chronologies.
Sources: Ellicott's Commentary, Genesis 5 — Hebrew letter-numerals corrupt easily ('as the words thus formed are unmeaning, they easily become corrupted'); St. Jerome: the best Samaritan MSS of his day agreed with the Hebrew; Barnes Notes, Genesis 5 — the Samaritan numbers for Jared, Methuselah, and Lamech are 'so shortened as to terminate all in the year of the flood. This alteration betrays design.'; Lange's Commentary, Genesis 5 — Creation-to-Flood: 1656 (Hebrew) / 1307 (Samaritan) / 2242 (LXX); post-Flood-to-Abraham: 365 / 1015 / 1245; evt-genesis5-genealogy-divergence-001 (this timeline) — the three-tradition number divergence, VERIFIED
Falsifiability: If all three manuscript traditions agreed on the begetting ages, the chronology claim would be documentarily coherent (it would still need external corroboration to move above CLAIMED)</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1650-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.2137,31.7683,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1650</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Walking the plank as a Golden Age pirate punishment</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No Golden-Age evidence exists: the admiralty courts tried hundreds of pirates (1715–1726) and recorded every real punishment — marooning, flogging, throwing overboard — and never the plank; the trope is literary (1837–1904).
Layer: fake · Place: Caribbean Sea (the Golden Age's theater)
Dates: 1650 → 1726 (confidence: traditional)

Pirates during the Golden Age of Piracy (c. 1650–1726) forced prisoners to walk a plank extending from the ship's side into the sea as a characteristic method of execution.

Earliest attestation: The practice as a phrase: Francis Grose, Dictionary of the Vulgar Tongue (1788), defines it as a MUTINY practice (mutineers blindfolding and walking officers off a plank) — not piracy. The literary plank enters via Charles Ellms, The Pirate's Own Book (1837, 'death plank' illustration), Robert Louis Stevenson, Treasure Island (1883 — Billy Bones's 'dreadful stories' of 'walking the plank'), Howard Pyle's 1887 Harper's Weekly painting, and J.M. Barrie's Peter Pan (1904 play / 1911 novel).
Sources: HISTORY.com, 'Did pirates really make people walk the plank?' — 'there is no proof that swashbucklers ever made their enemies and victims walk the plank'; Defoe 1724 first English writer (ship's ladder, ancient Mediterranean); Ellms 1837; Stevenson; Pyle 1887; National Geographic (history magazine): 'Little to no historical evidence exists to support that practice, which was largely pulled from fiction, including Treasure Island'; real punishments: keelhauling, thrown overboard, marooned; Houston CultureMap / HMNS 'Real Pirates' exhibition (2011): 18th-century pirates 'made very little use of this glamorized method of punishment'; 'shoved or threw an offending sailor overboard. No plank required'; J.M. Barrie's Peter Pan named as the mythology's source; Wikipedia 'Walking the plank': Grose 1788 definition (mutiny); George Wood 1769 Newgate confession (mutineers; Botting: 'alleged confession... may or may not be true, and in any case had nothing to do with pirates'); 1788 Commons slave-trade testimony (plank = throw themselves overboard); Times, 23 July 1829 — Vhan Fredericka, crew walked plank with shot tied to feet; Times, 14 Feb 1829 — packet Redpole, crew made to walk the plank by pirate schooner President; 1822 Blessing/Emanuel deposition (William Smith); Derdrake 'may have been a fictional character'; pyracy.com (pirate-history research forum, 'Captain Twill'): 'two genuine examples of people being made to walk the plank (Cordingly says one)' — both post-Golden-Age: Vhan Fredericka 1829 (Times) and Blessing/Emanuel 1822 (deposition)
Falsifiability: A contemporary Golden-Age source — an admiralty trial record, ship's log, or first-hand memoir from c. 1650–1726 — describing pirates making captives walk a plank.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1650-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-70.0,18.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1650</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Zhou-dynasty (3,000-year) Chinese magnetic compass — the 1658 Jesuit conflation of the mechanical south-pointing chariot with the magnetic needle</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Meynard &amp; Zhang's peer-reviewed autopsy (Journal of Jesuit Studies 13, 2026) shows the whole chain: 17th-century Jesuit missionaries — chiefly Martino Martini in Sinicae historiae decas prima (1658) — confused the mechanical south-pointing chariot (zhinanche, a geared vehicle attributed to Ma Jun, 220-280 CE) with the much later magnetic needle (zhinanzhen); Martini projected Cui Bao's Gujin zhu (fl. 290-306 CE) chariot story back to the Zhou dynasty and concluded, 'Without any doubt, the magnetic needle was invented by the Chinese at that time.' The authors state plainly: 'there was no technological connection in ancient China between the south-pointing chariot and magnetism.' The Zhou-dynasty magnetic compass is therefore a 1658 invention, not a 3,000-year-old one. Fenced: the Han lodestone spoon and the Song nautical compass survive the grade (CORROBORATED, evt-compass-chinese-invention-001) — the antiquity inflation dies, the real priority does not.
Layer: fake · Place: Zhou China (claimed antiquity); the conflation was born in Jesuit Rome/Munich (Martini's publication world)
Dates: 1658-01-01 → 2026-03-01 (confidence: high)

That China had the magnetic compass as early as the Zhou dynasty (3,000+ years ago) — the reading that credits the Yellow Emperor / Zhou Gong with the compass, treating the mechanical 'south-pointing chariot' (zhinanche) as a magnetic instrument. The claim is a 17th-century conflation, not ancient history.

Earliest attestation: Martino Martini, Sinicae historiae decas prima (1658) — the first Western text crediting China with the compass, via the conflation.
Sources: Meynard, Thierry &amp; Zhang, Yijing, 'The Chinese Compass: How the Jesuits Created and Spread the Myth,' Journal of Jesuit Studies 13 (2026), 96-116: Jesuits (esp. Martino Martini, Sinicae historiae decas prima, 1658) confused the mechanical south-pointing chariot (zhinanche, attributed to Ma Jun, 220-280 CE) with the later magnetic needle (zhinanzhen); Martini projected Cui Bao's Gujin zhu (fl. 290-306 CE) chariot story to the Zhou; 'Though the magnetic lodestone spoon was used since the Han dynasty, it was not used for navigation... In other words, there was no technological connection in ancient China between the south-pointing chariot and magnetism'
Falsifiability: A Zhou-era Chinese text describing a magnetized direction-finding instrument would revive the claim; the surviving Zhou texts describe the chariot as mechanical (gears), and none describes magnetism in navigation.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1658-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>113.62,34.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1658</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Fire 'burned out' the plague — the schoolbook one-liner</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills. (1) THE TIMELINE: 'It is now thought that the plague had largely subsided before the fire took place' (Wikipedia, Great Plague of London) — plague deaths were already declining through the summer of 1666; the fire arrived at the epidemic's tail, not its peak. (2) THE DEATHS AFTER: 'people also continued to die of the plague after the fire' (HISTORY.com / Heyl) — the Bills of Mortality do not show the clean stop the myth requires. (3) THE SILENCE: no contemporary made the connection — not the diarists, not the Gazette, not even the royalist spin doctors (Heyl). A cause that nobody at the time noticed is a later invention. (4) THE GEOGRAPHY: the fire burned only ~436 acres of the City; 'most of the later cases of plague were found in the suburbs' (Wikipedia) — the fire could not have sterilized a city it never reached. Historians 'don't really know why the Great Plague ended' (HISTORY.com) — an honest gap, not a fire's trophy.
Layer: fake · Place: City of London (the fire burned ~436 acres — the suburbs, where later plague cases were found, were untouched)
Dates: 1666-09-02 → 1666-09-06 (confidence: high)

The Great Fire of London (2–6 September 1666) burned out the Great Plague of London — that is, the fire ended the epidemic, and without the fire the plague would have continued.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary connected the two: historian Christian Heyl (quoted in HISTORY.com) — 'If you look at the discourse of the time, there was never any connection between the end of plague and Great Fire' — not even in royalist propaganda spinning the fire as a political success for Charles II. The belief is a later schoolbook invention; Simple English Wikipedia's Great Plague article (2026) is the belief's current carrier.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Great Plague of London' — 'Plague cases continued to occur sporadically at a modest rate until mid-1666. That September, the Great Fire of London destroyed much of the City of London, and some people believed that the fire put an end to the epidemic. It is now thought that the plague had largely subsided before the fire took place'; Bills of Mortality: 68,596 plague deaths in London in 1665; HISTORY.com, 'When London Faced a Pandemic — And a Devastating Fire' (quoting historian Christian Heyl and Museum of London curator Meriel Jeater) — 'Plague deaths in London were already declining by the time the fire started, and people also continued to die of the plague after the fire'; 'If you look at the discourse of the time, there was never any connection between the end of plague and Great Fire'; the fire burned 436 acres — the City — not the suburbs where later plague cases were found; no concurrent hygienic improvements with the brick rebuild; Simple English Wikipedia, 'Great Plague of London' (belief ecology — the belief's current carrier): 'The Great Fire of London, which happened on 2-6 September 1666, may have helped end the outbreak by killing many of the rats and fleas who were spreading the plague'
Falsifiability: A 1666–1667 document — a bill of mortality, a diarist, a pamphlet — recording zero plague deaths before 2 September followed by zero after, would weaken the grade. Instead the bills record the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1666-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0859,51.5123,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1666</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth: Leeuwenhoek invented the microscope</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim dies twice. (1) The compound microscope predates Leeuwenhoek by decades: documented exemplars include Galileo's occhiolino (1609–10, shown to Cesi 1624), Cornelis Drebbel's two-convex compound microscope presented in London (1619), and the Janssen c. 1590 attribution recorded by Willem Boreel's 1655 investigation and Pierre Borel's 1656 De vero telescopii inventore (the attribution itself disputed — held at CLAIMED in the companion record — but the instrument's existence by 1619 is not). (2) Leeuwenhoek did not build compound microscopes at all: his ~500 instruments were single-lens 'bead' microscopes — one tiny lens set in brass — and he built hundreds of them across his working life. Fences hold: the animalcules themselves (the discovery is real; companion record evt-leeuwenhoek-animalcules-001 CORROBORATED), the superb quality of his single lenses (~270× on his best), and Hooke's compound-microscope Micrographia (1665) as a separate, parallel tradition.
Layer: fake · Place: Delft, Dutch Republic (Leeuwenhoek's workshop; the instruments)
Dates: 1674 → 1723 (confidence: high)

Antonie van Leeuwenhoek invented the microscope — i.e., that he was the instrument's inventor, and that the microscope dates to his 1670s work.

Earliest attestation: The telling being graded is the modern schoolbook/institutional attribution ('inventor of the microscope') — the documentary record it contradicts is 60+ years older.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Timeline of microscope technology' — the 1590 Janssen attribution (disputed), Galileo occhiolino 1609–10, Drebbel's London compound microscope 1619, Faber's 1625 coinage 'microscope', Hooke's Micrographia 1665, Leeuwenhoek's 1674 simple-microscope work; Wikipedia, 'Zacharias Janssen' — the compound-microscope attribution: Willem Boreel's 1655 investigation, Johannes Zachariassen's sworn testimony (invented 1590), Pierre Borel's 1656 De vero telescopii inventore; the attribution's problems (Zacharias aged 2–5 at the alleged date; grandfather Hans Martens the possible maker); IMSS (Florence) / E. Freddi &amp; P. Galluzzi, 'History of the microscope' (estoria04.pdf) — Francesco Fontana's 1646 claim of a 1618 compound microscope; Viviani's 1654 testimony crediting Galileo; the Middelburg spectacle-makers' c. 1590 instruments as curiosities for princes
Falsifiability: A Leeuwenhoek-built or Leeuwenhoek-commissioned compound microscope (two-lens tube) dated before any documented compound instrument, or a primary source showing he claimed the compound microscope's invention</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1674-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.3571,52.0116,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1674</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Salvino D'Armati invented eyeglasses' claim (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED: del Migliore never produced the burial register and it has never been found; Isidoro del Lungo (1920) showed the epitaph's grammar ('la peccata' for 'le peccata') and vocabulary ('inventor', unknown in 14th-century Florentine vernacular) betray a later hand, and that the only 14th-century Salvino degli Armati on record (d. 1340) was a humble artisan with no eyeglass connection; critics (Ronchi, Rosen, Albertotti) and the Dizionario enciclopedico italiano (1955) concur — the 'inventor' is a 17th-century fabrication built on a 16th/17th-century misreading, and the Armati genealogy around 1286 cannot accommodate him.
Layer: fake · Place: Florence, Italy (Church of Santa Maria Maggiore — the claimed, obliterated tomb)
Dates: 1684 → 1855 (confidence: high)

Salvino D'Armati (or degli Armati) of Florence invented eyeglasses in the 13th century — the named inventor on the Florence plaque and in the schoolbook histories.

Earliest attestation: Ferdinando Leopoldo del Migliore, Firenze città noblissima illustrata (1684) — claims a Santa Maria Maggiore burial register recording 'Salvino d'Armato degl'Armati di Fir., Inventor degl'occhiali... Anno D. MCCCXVII'
Sources: Wikipedia 'Salvino D'Armati' — del Migliore's 1684 claim, the unproduced burial register, del Lungo's 1920 three-point demolition (grammar, vocabulary, the 1340 artisan), Manni's 1738 treatise, Villari's 1855 plaque (removed), and the 20th-century exposures (Dizionario enciclopedico italiano 1955; Ronchi; Rosen; Albertotti); Museum of Ophthalmology history of spectacles (mrcophth.com) — Manni's 1738 Degli occhiali da naso inventati da Salvino Armati attacked as unsubstantiated; the Armati genealogy around 1286 cannot reconcile a 'Salvino Armati'; the claimed epitaph ('Here rests Salvino d'Armato of the Armati of Florence, the inventor of spectacles... A.D. 1317') quoted from the restored tomb
Falsifiability: The lost burial register surfacing with 14th-century handwriting, or an Armati family record naming a Salvino who dealt in eyeglasses</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1684-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>11.2558,43.7696,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1684</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>TELLING (DEBUNKED): 'rule of thumb' = wife-beating law</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Henry Ansgar Kelly's 1994 Journal of Legal Education paper (44(3):341-365) surveyed the legal record and found no such law; the phrase never occurs in legal history; the first domestic-violence context is a 1782 satirical cartoon (The Rambler, 'Mr. Justice Thumb in the Act of Flagellation') about a Judge Buller rumor, not a rule.
Layer: fake · Place: London (legal and fencing print culture)
Dates: 1692 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

TELLING (DEBUNKED): the phrase 'rule of thumb' originates from an English common-law rule permitting a husband to beat his wife with a stick no thicker than his thumb.

Earliest attestation: Sir William Hope, The Compleat Fencing-Master (2d ed., 1692): 'What he doth, he doth by rule of thumb, and not by art' (OED 2d ed.)
Sources: Kelly, Henry Ansgar (1994). 'Rule of Thumb and the Folklaw of the Husband's Stick', Journal of Legal Education 44(3):341-365; Men and Masculinities 5(1), July 2002 — Kelly's demolition summarized: earliest known reference 1692 Hope; first recorded domestic-violence appearance the 1 Feb 1782 Rambler cartoon satirizing the Buller rumor; Cecil Adams, The Straight Dope — 'A couple of 19th-century U.S. trial opinions referred to an 'ancient law'... but rejected said law'; 'While this alleged rule involved a thumb, it wasn't the origin of 'rule of thumb''; Patricia T. O'Conner — 'one of the most persistent myths of political correctness'
Falsifiability: A statute or judgment explicitly codifying a thumb-width standard for domestic discipline would resurrect the telling</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1692-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1692</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dom Pérignon invented champagne (4 August 1693)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the empty contemporary record — Pérignon's documented work ran the opposite direction: as cellar master he researched how to AVOID making sparkling wine, because secondary fermentation in the bottle caused explosions, the infamous le vin du diable ('the devil's wine'); (2) the manufactured trail — the invention legend was built in the 1820s by Dom Grossard, and the famous stars quotation only surfaces in an 1880s champagne advertisement (National Geographic; thisdayinwinehistory). Meanwhile the deliberate sugar-addition method that became the basis of Champagne production was documented in London in December 1662 by Christopher Merret — six years before Pérignon even arrived at Hautvillers (National Geographic, Smithsonian, Tim Atkin MW).
Layer: fake · Place: Abbey of Saint-Pierre d'Hautvillers, Champagne, France (the legend's theater)
Dates: 1693 → 1693 (confidence: low)

The Benedictine monk Dom Pérignon invented champagne (sparkling wine) on 4 August 1693 at the Abbey of Saint-Pierre d'Hautvillers, exclaiming 'Come quickly, I am drinking the stars!'

Earliest attestation: No contemporary attestation of any kind: no document from Pérignon's lifetime credits him with inventing sparkling wine, and the 'Come quickly, I am drinking the stars!' exclamation is first traced to an advertisement of the 1880s — nearly 200 years later. The legend itself originates in the 1820s, when Dom Grossard, one of Pérignon's successors at the Abbey of Saint-Pierre d'Hautvillers, falsely attributed the invention to the 17th-century cellar master (National Geographic, citing the champagne historiography).
Sources: National Geographic, 'Who put the bubbles in Champagne?' — the full autopsy: Pérignon did not invent champagne; the legend originated in the 1820s with Dom Grossard's false attribution; Merret's December 1662 Royal Society paper ('Our wine-coopers of recent times use vast quantities of Sugar Molasses to all sorts of wines to make them brisk and sparkling') is the first documented use of deliberately adding sugars to wine in a sealed bottle; Pérignon devoted his time to avoiding sparkling wine; English thick glass bottles withstood the pressure; Smithsonian Magazine, Sarah Zielinski (2009), 'Inventing Your Way to a Better Champagne Experience' — 'It was an English scientist and physician, Christopher Merrett, who discovered the process of double fermentation; he presented his paper on the subject to the Royal Society in 1662. (The Benedictine monk Dom Pérignon often erroneously gets credited with Merrett's discovery.)'; Tim Atkin MW, 'A beginner's guide to Champagne' (Observer Food Monthly) — 'Dom Pérignon didn't invent bubbles; we did. Christopher Merret presented a paper to the Royal Society about sparkling wine in December 1662, six years before Dom Perignon arrived in Champagne.'; thisdayinwinehistory.com, 'The Discovery of Sparkling Wine' — the stars quotation has no evidence Pérignon ever said it; earliest reference in an 1880s advertisement, ~200 years after the fact; 'Dom Pérignon initially attempted to remove the bubbles to prevent them from splitting underneath the stresses of the secondary fermentation'; Laithwaites (Richard Bull), 'Beginner's Guide to English Sparkling Wine' (updated 22 May 2026) — 'Royal Society archives show that in December 1662, an Englishman named Christopher Merret delivered a paper detailing how to add sugars and molasses to finished wines to make them 'brisk and sparkling.' This documented the traditional method a full 30 years before Champagne officially embraced it!'; Tom Stevenson, A World Encyclopaedia of Champagne and Sparkling Wine (1998), via The Free Library / Eileen Murphy — Stevenson published the Merret document: 'The documentary evidence describing the addition of sugar to a finished wine to induce a second fermentation for the specific purpose of making it sparkle does exist... it is in London. There is absolutely no evidence that Dom Perignon made a single bottle of sparkling wine. The French didn't like sparkling wine.'
Falsifiability: Produce one document from before 1750 — a letter, account book, abbey record, or Pérignon's own writing — crediting Dom Pérignon with the invention of sparkling wine. One parchment moves the grade; there are none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1693-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>3.9453,49.0778,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1693</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Cornish wreckers' false lights (the systematic-luring legend)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Pearce's systematic 2010 study of 155 recorded Mounts Bay wrecks (1738-1860) found only 11 reports of direct attack (7%) while 77% of cases involved lifesaving activity; wrecking decomposed into three activities with direct attack-and-plunder the rarest; the 'deliberately luring ships onto the rocks' image is explicitly judged a myth.
Layer: fake · Place: Cornwall, England (Mounts Bay sample)
Dates: 1700 → 1860 (confidence: medium)

Cornish wreckers in the 18th and 19th centuries routinely and systematically lured ships onto the rocks with deliberately displayed false lights (the 'lights tied to donkeys' tails' image) as an organized criminal industry — wrecking as deliberate murder-by-deception.

Earliest attestation: The folk-devil image was 'firmly entrenched by the mid-eighteenth century' (Deacon's review of Pearce); the systematic scholarly refutation is Pearce 2010.
Sources: Cathryn J. Pearce, 'Cornish Wrecking 1700-1860: Reality and Popular Myth' (Boydell Press, 2010) — first comprehensive systematic inquiry; shows the image of Cornish wreckers as villains deliberately luring ships onto the rocks is a myth (PhD, Greenwich Maritime Institute); Bernard Deacon's review of Pearce — the 'folk devil' firmly entrenched by the mid-18th c.; Pearce breaks wrecking into three activities; of 155 Mounts Bay wrecks 1738-1860 only 11 attack reports (7%) vs 77% lifesaving; sources: manor wreck presentments, EIC letter books, wreck case papers, salvage accounts, assize records, Trinity House correspondence; William Blackstone, Commentaries on the Laws of England, vol. I (3rd ed., 1768), p. 294 — on 26 Geo. II c. 19: plundering vessels in distress or wrecked, preventing escape, wounding with intent to destroy, or 'putting out false lights in order to bring any vessel into danger, are all declared to be capital felonies' (evidence of the FEAR, not the prevalence; the statute was repealed 1854); 26 Geo. II c. 19 — Stealing Shipwrecked Goods Act 1753 (royal assent 15 May 1753; repealed by Merchant Shipping Repeal Act 1854)
Falsifiability: Contemporary court or Admiralty records showing false-light luring as a prosecuted, repeated, organized practice across multiple Cornish communities would revive the claim; Pearce's 155-wreck Mounts Bay sample (1738-1860) found the opposite pattern.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.5,50.1,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The black-clad ninja assassin</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No period document records the black uniform; the image's documentary trail runs through the theater (kuroko/bunraku stagehands) and 19th-c. woodblock art, while the historical record shows shinobi working in civilian disguise.
Layer: fake · Place: Edo/Tokyo — the theater district where the image was born
Dates: 1700 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

Historical Japanese ninja (shinobi) wore distinctive all-black outfits (the classic black shinobi shōzoku) and fought primarily as stealth assassins relying on thrown shuriken.

Earliest attestation: Kabuki/kuroko stage convention (18th c.); Hokusai's early-19th-c. black-clad ninja imagery
Sources: Kuroko (Wikipedia) — black costume as the theatrical convention for invisibility; historian Stephen Turnbull's suggestion that the black-ninja stereotype derives from kabuki/kuroko; Matt Alt, via Kotaku (2013) — Hokusai credited with the first image of the ninja in the classic black suit, early 19th c.; the word 'ninja' itself does not appear until the 1800s; prior vocabulary: shinobi, 'a dude from Iga'; Jennifer R. Povey, 'No, Ninjas Do Not Wear Black' (Medium, 2022) — the kuroko prop-handler explanation; black is poor night camouflage; real operatives dressed as civilians; Ninja (geishaworld wiki, compiled from published sources) — 'there is no written evidence for such a costume'; the black-clothing notion is 'likely rooted in artistic convention'; operative clothing was civilian dress, with concealable light armor (kusari karuta, tenugui) between layers
Falsifiability: A period (pre-1868) Japanese document, woodblock print, or excavated garment reliably depicting an all-black shinobi uniform in operational use</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1700-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>139.6503,35.6762,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1700</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1712/1713 verdict against Leibniz: a verdict that invalidates itself</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The verdict's own process disqualifies it: Newton, as Royal Society president, appointed the committee, controlled the documentary selection, and wrote the report himself (anonymously) — the plaintiff was the judge, the jury, and the author. The committee never solicited Leibniz's version; he saw the verdict only in autumn 1714. Modern scholarship (Hall 1980) holds both men invented the calculus independently.
Layer: fake · Place: London, Royal Society
Dates: 1712 → 1713 (confidence: high)

The Royal Society's 1712/1713 report, the Commercium Epistolicum (published 8 January 1713), fairly adjudicated the calculus priority dispute between Newton and Leibniz and stands as valid evidence that Leibniz plagiarized Newton.

Earliest attestation: Commercium Epistolicum D. Johannis Collinsii et aliorum de analysi promota (London, 1712/1713)
Sources: Commercium Epistolicum (London, 1712/1713) — the report itself; Newton's drafts of the report, in his own hand (Cambridge/Portsmouth papers); Leibniz–Newton calculus controversy — summary of the committee's composition, Newton's authorship, and Leibniz's autumn-1714 sighting (Wikipedia, aggregating Hall, Westfall); Hall, A.R., Philosophers at War (1980): the consensus statement of independent invention
Falsifiability: Leibniz's own papers from 1675 proving dependence on Newton's manuscripts — none exists; or evidence the committee solicited Leibniz's testimony — the record shows it did not</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1712-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1712</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'prompt £20,000 prize' version of the Harrison story</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The ledger kills the prompt-full-award reading: the Board demanded replication (two copies of H4) before the final £10,000, paid Harrison in stages, and Parliament's 1773 Act was a benevolence award 'in spite of the Board, instead of from it' — the £20,000 was never formally awarded to anyone. Scope fenced: the grade kills the prompt-award version only — never the Jamaica trial's success, never the man's achievement, never the real £23,065.
Layer: fake · Place: Greenwich / London (Board of Longitude, Royal Observatory, Parliament)
Dates: 1714 → 1773 (confidence: high)

John Harrison's marine chronometer H4 won the longitude problem, and the Board of Longitude promptly awarded him the full £20,000 Longitude Prize under the 1714 Longitude Act.

Earliest attestation: The Longitude rewards ledger (Derek Howse appendix; Wikipedia 'Longitude rewards'): staged payments £250 (1737) → £2,000 (1741–55) → £2,865 (1760–65) → £7,500 (1765, for H4's performance) → £8,750 by Act of Parliament (1773); total ~£23,065; the £20,000 never formally awarded to anyone.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Longitude rewards' — Howse appendix payment chain, £23,065 total over ~36 years; full £20,000 never awarded to anyone; Dava Sobel, Longitude (1995), via the Board's 24 April 1773 minutes as reported: 'this amount nearly totaled the remainder of the longitude prize due him, but it was not the coveted prize. Rather, the sum was a bounty awarded by the benevolence of Parliament — in spite of the Board of Longitude, instead of from it'; Spacedaily/explorersweb long-form accounts: the Kew 1772 trial, George III's intervention, the 1773 Act of Parliament
Falsifiability: A Board-of-Longitude minute or Treasury warrant recording a formal £20,000 award to Harrison under the Act's terms would overturn this; the ledger's thirty-six-year disbursement trail is what currently stands.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1714-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0005,51.4769,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1714</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Lightships were safe — they never dragged, never sank'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed by the documented casualty record: South Goodwin LV-90 (1954, 7 crew), wartime sinkings (TH's own 1945 fleet count acknowledges them), the Olympic ramming (1934), the 1944 hurricane loss, the U-boat sinking (1918), and anchor-dragging incidents — lightship duty was officially 'the most dangerous duty' in lighthouse organizations. Scope: kills the 'safe/never in danger' reading only; the lightship institution itself is CORROBORATED in evt-nore-lightship-1732-001.
Layer: fake · Place: South Goodwin Sands, off Deal, Kent (LV-90 loss, Nov 1954)
Dates: 1732 → 1954 (confidence: high)

Lightships were a safe, uneventful duty: anchored vessels that sat quietly on station and were never in serious danger — the romantic assumption that the boats just sat there.

Earliest attestation: The romantic assumption lives in museum-plaque language and maritime nostalgia writing; the documentary killers are the casualty records cited below
Sources: List of lightvessels of Great Britain — 'South Goodwin ... 27 Nov 1954 ... Sank in a storm in 1954 with the loss of all lives except one'; JaySea Archaeology / goodwinsands.org.uk — LV-90 (South Goodwin, commissioned 1937, LV number 90), lost night of 26-27 Nov 1954: hurricane-force winds, separated from her anchor chain, unpowered, seven crew lost; the sole survivor was Ronald Murton, a bird-migration observer, not crew; Lighthouse Digest (via goodwinsands.org.uk) — 'Being a crewman stationed on a lightship was considered the most dangerous duty by nearly every lighthouse organization in the world ... regardless of the weather, a lightship was not allowed to leave its station'; Dominic Winter auction footnote (Trinity House WWII bell) — 'At the end of the Second World War (i.e. 1945), TH had, in theory, 43 lightships but some had been sunk and/or damaged during the War'; Lightvessel LV-95 (Inner Dowsing) — 'on the Varne station, she unknowingly dragged anchor in a gale' on 1 December 1966, found 2 nautical miles off-station; Swansea &amp; Port Talbot docks history — English &amp; Welsh Grounds lightvessel sprang a leak in a severe gale, 24 August 1953; Barry lifeboat stood by; US lightship losses (Lighthouse Digest via goodwinsands.org.uk) — Nantucket LV-117 rammed by RMS Olympic 1934; Vineyard Lightship sunk by a September 1944 hurricane with her entire crew; Diamond Shoals lightship sunk by a German U-boat in 1918
Falsifiability: Would require the 1954 wreck record, the wartime sinkings, and the documented American losses all to be false — multiple independent casualty records would have to be fabricated</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1732-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>1.58,51.23,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1732</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth: Celsius invented the Celsius scale as we use it (0 freezing, 100 boiling)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Celsius's own 1742 paper documents the reverse of the modern scale: 0 represented the boiling point of water, 100 the freezing point. The modern orientation is documented elsewhere, later: Jean-Pierre Christin published the inverted scale on 19 May 1743 (the 'Thermometer of Lyon,' built by Pierre Casati); Linnaeus used the reversed version from 1744 (thermometer by Daniel Ekström), with the first known forward-scale temperature readings in his 16 Dec 1745 Hortus Upsaliensis letter to Samuel Nauclér; Mårten Strömer (Celsius's own student) also built forward-oriented instruments in Uppsala. The name survived; the orientation did not. The schoolbook telling is false as a claim about Celsius's invention. Fences hold: Celsius's real 1742 achievements — the pressure-independence of ice's melting point, the pressure-calibration of boiling at mean sea level (the seed of the standard atmosphere) — survive in the companion record, untouched.
Layer: fake · Place: Uppsala, Sweden (Celsius's observatory; the 1742 paper)
Dates: 1742 → 1745 (confidence: high)

Anders Celsius invented the Celsius temperature scale with 0° representing the freezing point of water and 100° representing the boiling point of water — the scale as it hangs on every classroom wall.

Earliest attestation: Celsius's 1742 paper 'Observations of two persistent degrees on a thermometer' — the telling being graded here is the modern schoolbook attribution (0 = freezing, 100 = boiling, invented by Celsius)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Degree Celsius' — History section: Celsius's 1742 reversed original (0 boiling, 100 freezing); Christin's 19 May 1743 inversion and the Thermometer of Lyon (Pierre Casati); Linnaeus's 1744 reversed-scale thermometer by Daniel Ekström; the 16 Dec 1745 Hortus Upsaliensis letter to Samuel Nauclér; Mårten Strömer; Wikipedia 'Celsius' (Simple English) — 'In 1742, Anders Celsius made a "reversed" version of the modern Celsius temperature scale' (archival classroom mirror, rachel.education.gov.ck); University of North Carolina Asheville ATMS 320, 'History of the Thermometer' lecture slides (Godfrey) — the 1742 / 1744 / 1743 chronology in a meteorology curriculum; Testbook exam Q&amp;A bank — the schoolbook telling being graded: 'Celsius invented the Celsius (Centigrade) temperature scale... 0 represents the freezing point of water, and 100 represents the boiling point'
Falsifiability: A Celsius-published or Celsius-built forward-oriented thermometer (0 freezing, 100 boiling) dated before 19 May 1743</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1742-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>17.6389,59.8586,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1742</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The first stapler was made for King Louis XV' — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the man who spread the story retracted it after his own research — collector Curtis A. Scaglione traces the tale to a 1997 Swingline company letter, finds 'no evidence to support or validate this stapler ever existed at all,' and shows the embellishments (gold staples, 'kept it to himself') were added later by other writers; (2) material culture: dozens of Louis XV documents examined show single sheets folded with red wax seals bearing the royal insignia — no multi-page documents, no puncture holes, no fasteners ever observed.
Layer: fake · Place: Versailles, France — where the telling places the stapler
Dates: 1750 → 1997 (confidence: high)

The telling that 'the first stapler was made in the 18th century for King Louis XV of France, with handmade staples each inscribed with the insignia of the royal court.'

Earliest attestation: The story's traceable source: a 1997 Swingline Company letter to collector Curtis A. Scaglione ('The earliest stapling machine we know of was one built during the 18th Century for King Louis XV of France. The individual staples were handmade and inscribed with the insignia of the Royal Court.')
Sources: Curtis A. Scaglione, 'Fastener History' (Fact, Fiction, and Folklore) — officemuseum.online: origin traced to 1997 Swingline letter; retraction: 'no evidence to support or validate this stapler ever existed at all'; embellishments identified; documents examined — single sheets, folded, red wax seals, no punctures; Discover Magazine, 'They Invented it' (Fenella Saunders) — repeats the telling ('One of the first known staplers belonged to King Louis XV of France. Each staple was decorated with the royal insignia') — belief ecology, not evidence; University at Buffalo, 'Keeping it all together: The history of the stapler' — 'Legend has it that the first known stapler device was made in the 18th century as a regal gift for Louis XV' — frames it as legend
Falsifiability: A surviving 18th-century fastening device with documented French royal provenance, or a contemporary (pre-1800) description of such a device, would reopen the claim.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1750-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1750</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Beccaria's book did not end judicial torture — the single-cause myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three kills: (1) CHRONOLOGY — Prussia abolished judicial torture in 1754, a decade before Beccaria's 1764 book; (2) MECHANISM — abolition was a decades-long, multi-state administrative reform driven by the reformed law of proof (Langbein), not a book's magic; (3) the book's real influence (Tuscany 1786, the French debate, Catherine's Nakaz) survives and is fenced — the grade kills only the single-cause origin story.
Layer: fake · Place: Europe (the abolition century: Prussia 1754 → Milan 1764 → France 1780/1788)
Dates: 1754 → 1789 (confidence: high)

Cesare Beccaria's 1764 book 'On Crimes and Punishments' (Dei delitti e delle pene) ended judicial torture in Europe — the single-book origin story of the abolition.

Earliest attestation: The abolition-first timeline: Frederick II's Prussian cabinet-order abolition of judicial torture, 1754 — ten years before Beccaria's 1764 publication.
Sources: Frederick II of Prussia, cabinet-order abolition of judicial torture, 1754 (following the 1740 restriction order) — standard scholarly dating; Langbein, Torture and the Law of Proof; Cesare Beccaria, Dei delitti e delle pene (1764), Milan — the book's publication and its documented reception (22 translations by 1800, Tuscany's 1786 code, the French reform debate); Louis XVI's abolitions: question préparatoire (1780), question préalable (1788) — both postdate the book; the ordonnance criminelle abrogated 9 October 1789; John H. Langbein, Torture and the Law of Proof: Europe and England in the Ancien Régime (Univ. of Chicago Press, 1977; 2nd ed. 2006) — abolition tracked the reformed law of proof, not persuasion
Falsifiability: Evidence that no European state abolished judicial torture before 1764, and that Beccaria's book directly caused the Prussian, Swedish, Tuscan, Austrian, and French abolitions, would force revision; the Prussian 1754 abolition alone defeats the single-cause claim.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1754-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1754</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth of the first English dictionary: not Johnson, but Cawdrey (1604)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed one way, conclusively: Robert Cawdrey's A Table Alphabeticall, conteyning and teaching the true writing, and understanding of hard usually English words — a 120-page monolingual English dictionary of 2,543 headwords — was published in London in 1604, 151 years before Johnson's Dictionary. The OED's own historical introduction treats it as the acorn to its oak; the Bodleian Library holds the sole surviving first-edition copy and republished it in 2007 with an introduction by OED chief editor John Simpson. Between Cawdrey and Johnson lies a century and a half of continuous English dictionary-making.
Layer: fake · Place: London, England (Johnson's Dictionary published 1755; Cawdrey's Table Alphabeticall published 1604, while Cawdrey lived in Coventry)
Dates: 1755 → 1755 (confidence: high)

Samuel Johnson wrote the first English dictionary — that no English dictionary existed before Johnson's A Dictionary of the English Language, and that the English dictionary tradition begins with Johnson's 1755 work.

Earliest attestation: The belief itself is the textbook and popular shorthand that makes Johnson's 1755 work the origin story; readings.com.au's 2015 Cawdrey edition notes directly: 'Contrary to popular belief, the author of the first English dictionary is not Samuel Johnson, but Robert Cawdrey.'
Sources: Wikipedia — 'A Table Alphabeticall': 'A Table Alphabeticall is the first monolingual dictionary in the English language by Robert Cawdrey and first published in London in 1604.' 120 pages, 2,543 words; sole surviving copy at the Bodleian Library, Oxford.; readings.com.au — 'The First English Dictionary 1604: Robert Cawdrey's A Table Alphabeticall' (Bodleian Library Publishing, 2015): 'Contrary to popular belief, the author of the first English dictionary is not Samuel Johnson, but Robert Cawdrey, who published his Table Alphabeticall in 1604 — nearly 150 years before Johnson's dictionary.'; Wikipedia — 'Early English dictionaries': Cawdrey's 1604 work 'is generally regarded as the first genuine dictionary in English.'
Falsifiability: If no English wordbook before 1755 could be produced, the myth would stand. Instead the shelves are full: Cawdrey 1604, Henry Cockeram's English Dictionarie 1623, Thomas Blount's Glossographia 1656, Edward Phillips's New World of English Words 1658, John Kersey's New English Dictionary 1702, Bailey 1721.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1755-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1755</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"E pur si muove" — the whisper that wasn't</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Zero contemporary attestation across the full trial dossier and Viviani's biography; first appearance 115 years post-mortem in a source that is wrong on its other trial facts (six-year imprisonment, torture — both false); the supporting '1643 painting' is a 19th-century-style copy whose experts place it post-1837.
Layer: fake · Place: Santa Maria sopra Minerva, Rome (site of the 22 June 1633 abjuration — where the whisper allegedly happened)
Dates: 1757 → 1757 (confidence: high)

After abjuring heliocentrism before the Roman Inquisition on 22 June 1633, Galileo Galilei muttered "E pur si muove" ("And yet it moves") — the defiant whisper under the recantation. No contemporary source contains it. The earliest documented print attribution is 115 years later: Giuseppe Baretti's The Italian Library (London, 1757), which presents a garbled version ("stamping with his foot, in a contemplative mood") alongside claims the trial documents themselves refute (six years' imprisonment, torture). Vincenzo Viviani, Galileo's own disciple and first biographer (1655-56), never mentions it; the Inquisition trial record never mentions it. A 1911 '1643 painting' showing the words was exposed as a near-copy of an 1837 painting. Scholars treat the whisper as an 18th-century invention.

Earliest attestation: Giuseppe Baretti, The Italian Library (London, 1757): 'The moment he was set at liberty, he looked up to the sky and down to the ground, and, stamping with his foot, in a contemplative mood, said, Eppur si muove; that is, still it moves, meaning the earth.' — per Antonio Favaro's 1911 determination after four decades of research (reported in Scientific American).
Sources: Antonio Favaro's 1911 determination of Baretti 1757 as earliest print mention (after four decades of research culminating in the National Edition) — reported in Scientific American; Wikipedia, 'And yet it moves' — first reported in English print in 1757; the van Belle painting episode (dated 1643/45, near-identical to Van Maldeghem 1837, experts doubt pre-19th c., whereabouts unknown); The full trial dossier (Favaro's National Edition, 1890-1909) and Vincenzo Viviani's 1655-56 biography: silence on the utterance in both
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1633-or-earlier document (letter, deposition, biography) recording the utterance would revive it; none exists despite the fully preserved trial dossier. The silence of Viviani's 1655-56 biography, written by the man who lived with Galileo, is decisive.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1757-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4777,41.8972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1757</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Melvil Dewey invented the index card' — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the man's own preface names a predecessor — Natale Battezzati's card system, 'the most fruitful source of ideas'; (2) Linnaeus's standardized paper slips of the mid-1760s (~7.5x13 cm, unbound, shufflable — Charmantier &amp; Muller-Wille 2012), a century before Dewey; (3) the 1791 Bureau de Bibliographie playing-card catalogs — over a million confiscated playing cards filled and sent to Paris within three years (Library of Congress blog).
Layer: fake · Place: Amherst, Massachusetts — where the telling places the invention (Dewey's 1876 pamphlet)
Dates: 1760 → 1876 (confidence: high)

The telling that 'Melvil Dewey invented the index card in 1876' — that the index card began with Dewey and the American library movement.

Earliest attestation: The telling's popular form: library-science lore and exam-bank shorthand crediting Dewey's 1876 pamphlet year. Dewey's own 1876 preface to 'A Classification and Subject Index for Cataloguing and Arranging the Books and Pamphlets of a Library' cites the card system of Italian publisher Natale Battezzati as 'the most fruitful source of ideas' — the claimed inventor names a predecessor.
Sources: Melvil Dewey, 'A Classification and Subject Index for Cataloguing and Arranging the Books and Pamphlets of a Library' (1876) — preface cites Natale Battezzati's card system as 'the most fruitful source of ideas'; Project Gutenberg ebook 12513 (public domain); Isabelle Charmantier &amp; Staffan Muller-Wille, 'Carl Linnaeus's Botanical Paper Slips (1767-1773)' — mid-1760s standardized slips ~7.5x13 cm, uniform format, never bound in a fixed order; the authors' own caveat: 'the invention was entirely inadvertent'; Library of Congress blog, 'Inquiring Minds: The Unheralded Story of the Card Catalog' (2017) — 1791 instructions to catalog confiscated aristocratic/clerical libraries on blank-backed playing cards; 'within three years, over a million cards were sent to overwhelmed offices in Paris'; liswiki.org, 'History of the card catalog' — Bureau de Bibliographie practice (Jackson 1974; Hopkins 1992): title transcribed, filing word underlined, collation, cards strung through the lower-left corner; Sharp 1935 on the 1876 'modern form' via the Library Bureau
Falsifiability: A demonstrated 19th-century documentary tradition attributing the index card's invention to Dewey in his own lifetime would reopen it; instead his own preface disclaims it.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1760-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.52,42.377,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1760</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Prussian schools were designed to produce obedient soldiers</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The design claim dies on the documents: the 1763 decree's stated aims were Christian formation, literacy, and productive subjects (ages 5-13/14, tax-funded, Hecker's program) — no military-obedience design; the soldier narrative postdates the design by 43 years and rides modern polemic, not historians of education.
Layer: fake · Place: Berlin, Prussia
Dates: 1763 → 1808 (confidence: medium)

The Prussian school system was designed — from Frederick the Great's 1763 Generallandschulreglement onward — to produce obedient soldiers: a factory-model education 'deliberately designed to produce mediocre intellects... and to ensure docile and incomplete citizens' (Gatto), the template for modern schooling's bells, rows, and 45-minute periods.

Earliest attestation: The soldier-factory form is modern polemic (Gatto's Underground History; YouTube pipeline 2026); the 1763 decree is the documented primary instrument
Sources: Wikipedia — Prussian education system (Generallandschulreglement 1763, Hecker, ages 5-13/14 tax-funded compulsory; the critics' obedience claim carried as contested); Academy of Ideas — the Gatto form of the claim ('deliberately designed to produce mediocre intellects... docile and incomplete citizens,' Weapons of Mass Instruction); ushistoryscene — Mann's 1844 tour and the Prussian 'common schools' model (the genuine Prussian influence on American schooling: the model, not the motive)
Falsifiability: The 1763 decree text showing military-obedience training as a stated design aim, or an 18th-century Prussian source stating the schools were built for the army</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1763-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1763</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"James Watt invented the steam engine"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) chronology — Newcomen's commercially successful atmospheric engine of 1712 predates Watt's patent by 57 years and Watt's own birth; (2) the patent's own title — 'A New Invented Method of Lessening the Consumption of Steam and Fuel in Fire Engines,' an improvement on existing fire engines, not an invention of the engine; (3) Watt's entry point — he was asked to repair a model Newcomen engine at Glasgow University, meaning the engine he 'invented' was already on his workbench.
Layer: fake · Place: Britain (the schoolbook tradition's setting)
Dates: 1769 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

James Watt (1736–1819) invented the steam engine.

Earliest attestation: The unqualified 'inventor of the steam engine' title is a popular-history and exam-bank construction; no technical history of the engine credits Watt with the invention
Sources: ASME Engineering History Landmark: Newcomen atmospheric engine — first success 1712 near Dudley Castle; over 2,000 Newcomen-type engines installed before the Watt era; UK Letters Patent No. 913, 5 January 1769 — James Watt's own title: 'A New Invented Method of Lessening the Consumption of Steam and Fuel in Fire Engines' (the title confesses the improvement reading); Age of Revolution / Science Museum tradition: James Watt, instrument maker at Glasgow University, asked to repair a model Newcomen engine — the separate condenser born from diagnosing the existing engine's inefficiency; Medium/science-history literature: 'Obviously no, he did not' — the explicit correction that 'inventor of the steam engine' is a title histories sometimes bestow upon Watt without warrant; Belief ecology (the myth in the wild): answers.com's muddled Q&amp;A ('He invented the steam engine'; 'invented by James Watt in 1712') — the confusion itself is the evidence of the myth's circulation
Falsifiability: Proof that no working steam engine existed before Watt's 1769 patent — impossible against Newcomen's 1712 installation and the 2,000+ atmospheric engines that followed</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1769-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.89,52.48,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1769</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'eraser was invented in 1770' telling — DEBUNKED by bread</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiple independent kills: (1) bread-crumb erasers in documented use before and long after rubber — including a first-person Meiji-era account of bread erasers as school issue; (2) wax tablets for lead/charcoal; (3) sandstone/pumice for ink on parchment/papyrus. 1770 marks the rubber eraser's arrival, not erasing's.
Layer: fake · Place: London (telling's home market)
Dates: 1770 → 1912 (confidence: high)

The telling that the eraser was invented in 1770 — that Nairne's rubber cube was the beginning of erasing — is false. Erasing predates rubber by centuries. Before rubber erasers, tablets of wax were used to erase lead or charcoal marks from paper; bits of rough stone such as sandstone or pumice removed small errors from parchment or papyrus written in ink; and crustless bread was the standard pencil-mark eraser — a Meiji-period (1868–1912) Tokyo student recalled: 'Bread erasers were used in place of rubber erasers, and so they would give them to us with no restriction on amount.' The bread-crumb eraser is itself the probable source of Nairne's 'accidental pickup' tradition. What 1770 invented was the rubber eraser — not the eraser.

Earliest attestation: The telling: modern popular histories compressing 'first rubber eraser' to 'first eraser'; the refutation: documented bread/wax/pumice practice plus Meiji-era first-person account
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Eraser' — History section: wax tablets, sandstone/pumice, crustless bread; Meiji-period Tokyo student testimony on bread erasers; ThoughtCo, 'The History of Pencils, Markers, Pens, and Erasers': 'Before rubber, breadcrumbs had been used to erase pencil marks'
Falsifiability: Evidence that no erasing practice existed before 1770 — the bread, wax, and pumice attestations already rule this out.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1770-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.0872,51.5133,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1770</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Jenner-was-first myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) Jesty's 1774 family inoculation, examined and cross-examined in London in 1805 with the son re-challenged against smallpox on the spot; (2) Plett's 1791 Holstein vaccinations, reported to Kiel and published via von Heinze; (3) the wider pre-Jenner list in Plett, Plett &amp; Jenner 2006 (Bose 1769, Fewster, Sevel, Jensen, Rendall). The 'first to use cowpox' reading is dead; what survives is Jenner's real achievement — systematic documentation, experimental challenge, publication, and global dissemination.
Layer: fake · Place: Berkeley, Gloucestershire, England (Jenner's 1796 experiment)
Dates: 1774 → 1798 (confidence: high)

Edward Jenner was NOT the first person to use cowpox to protect against smallpox. The documented predecessors include Benjamin Jesty (1774, Dorset — 22 years before Jenner), Peter Plett (1791, Holstein — 5 years before), and others named in the literature (Bose 1769, Fewster's 1760s observations, Sevel, Jensen, Rendall).

Earliest attestation: Jesty: 1805 Original Vaccine Pock Institute examination (of the 1774 event); Plett: 1802 von Heinze interview (of the 1791 event); Jenner: the 1798 Inquiry (of the 1796 experiment)
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Benjamin Jesty' — the 1774 inoculation, 22 years before Jenner; Wikipedia, 'Peter Plett' — the 1791 vaccinations, 5 years before Jenner; Plett, P.C., Plett, P., &amp; Jenner, E. (2006). 'Peter Plett and other discoverers of cowpox vaccination before Edward Jenner.' Sudhoffs Archiv 90(2): 219–232 — 'at least six people in England, Denmark and Germany (Bose in 1769, Sevel, Jensen and Jesty in 1774, Rendall, Plett in 1791)'; Country Life, 'Curious questions: Who administered the first vaccination?' — the popular form of the question
Falsifiability: Proof that Jesty's and Plett's documented inoculations never happened (e.g., fraud proven in the 1805 examination records), which would restore Jenner's priority</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1774-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-2.46,51.69,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1774</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tarot as the Egyptian 'Book of Thoth'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim has a dated birth certificate — de Gébelin's 1781 essay, which offered no evidence — and a documented true origin: the tarot is a 15th-century Milanese card game (evt-tarot-milan-game-001).
Layer: fake · Place: Paris (publication site)
Dates: 1781 → 1781 (confidence: traditional)

The tarot deck is an ancient Egyptian artifact — the 'Book of Thoth,' the esoteric wisdom of the Egyptian temples preserved in card form and carried to Europe by the Romani people.

Earliest attestation: Antoine Court de Gébelin, 'Le Monde primitif' vol. VIII (1781) — essay on the tarot; Comte de Mellet's companion essay in the same volume supplied the Hebrew-letter link
Sources: Antoine Court de Gébelin, Le Monde primitif vol. VIII (1781) — the tarot essay; Donald Tyson's English translation notes; Michael Dummett, The Game of Tarot (1980) — the documented Italian origin; Kit Stone, 'Tarot in Late Enlightenment France' (Journal Article, Oct 2024) — context of the 1781 invention; Morgan Library &amp; Museum, Visconti-Sforza Tarot Cards (MS M.630.1-35) — the physical refutation
Falsifiability: A pre-1781 document connecting tarot to Egypt, or any Egyptian artifact of the 22-trump structure — none has ever been produced</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1781-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1781</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bastille's 'hundreds of political prisoners' (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The fortress's own inventory shows seven prisoners, none a political leader: four forgers, one mentally ill Irishman, one would-be regicide of 1759, and one aristocrat held by his family's lettre de cachet. The Marquis de Sade — the one famous political-adjacent prisoner — had been transferred out ten days earlier. A decision to replace the fortress with an open public space had already been made before the storming.
Layer: fake · Place: Place de la Bastille, Paris
Dates: 1789-07-14 → 1789-07-14 (confidence: high)

The popular claim that the Bastille held hundreds of political prisoners, liberated by the storming of 14 July 1789.

Earliest attestation: The prisoner's inventory of 14 July 1789, recovered from the fortress's own records; the myth in Latude's Mémoires (1787) and Linguet's Mémoires de la Bastille (1783)
Sources: Bastille fortress's own records: seven prisoners on 14 July 1789 — four forgers held under Grand Châtelet warrants; James F.X. Whyte, an Irish-born 'lunatic' suspected of spying, held at his family's request; Auguste-Claude Tavernier, who had attempted to assassinate Louis XV thirty years earlier; and the Comte de Solages, a 'deviant' aristocrat suspected of murder, held by his father via lettre de cachet (via Wikipedia 'Storming of the Bastille', citing Godechot); 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Bastille': 'At the time of its capture only seven prisoners were found in it.' Also notes the horror tales found currency in the revolutionary excitement but 'historical evidence... tends to strip of their more dreadful features, and even in many cases to refute altogether.' (public domain); Bastille archives discovered 1840 by François Ravaisson in the Arsenal vaults (via 1911 Britannica) — the registers that let later scholarship audit the legend; Lüsebrink &amp; Reichardt, The Bastille (via the Colorado dissertation): pre-revolutionary cultural memory of the Bastille was built by Linguet's and Latude's memoirs into 'the sinister tool of a despotic, almost feudal regime' — the great irony being that this memory had already diverged from reality before 1789; The Collector (2026, citing Funck-Brentano's Third-Republic archival work): archivist Frantz Funck-Brentano debunked the 'black legend' of the Bastille from its own documents; secrecy around the prison bred suspicion that memoirs monetized; Wikipedia 'Jean Henri Latude': the 1787 Mémoires (ghost-written, which Latude himself denied authoring accurately) enjoyed 'considerable popularity at the time of the French Revolution'; Latude was pensioned by the Revolution
Falsifiability: An authenticated 1789 Bastille register showing dozens or hundreds of political prisoners, or contemporary evidence that the seven-prisoner inventory is itself a fabrication</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3692,48.8533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The namesake who never invented it — Dr. Guillotin and the machine that stole his name</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills on the inventor attribution. (1) THE PAPERWORK NAMES OTHER MEN: the design is credited to Antoine Louis, the Paris Academy of Surgery's secretary, and the first machine was built by Tobias Schmidt — so firmly that the machine's first nickname was the 'Louisette'/'Louison.' (2) GUILLOTIN'S DOCUMENTED ROLE IS ADVOCACY: his December 1789 appearance before the National Assembly argued for a decapitating machine as the standard, equal method of execution; on 3 June 1791 the Assembly decreed it the sole legal means of criminal execution. Advocate, not maker. (3) THE NAMESAKE REJECTED THE NAME: Guillotin opposed capital punishment, was embarrassed by the association for the rest of his life, and his family petitioned the government to rename the machine — refused, they changed their own family name instead. A man does not flee a machine he proudly invented.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris (where Guillotin made his December 1789 Assembly motion, Louis designed the machine, and Schmidt built it; first use at the Place de Grève, 25 April 1792)
Dates: 1789-12-01 → 1792-04-25 (confidence: high)

Joseph-Ignace Guillotin invented the guillotine — the machine as his personal invention.

Earliest attestation: The popular form travels in the name itself — every encyclopedia entry is filed under 'guillotine.' The contemporary counter-attestation is the machine's first nickname: the 'Louisette'/'Louison,' after Antoine Louis, and Guillotin's own documented role as proposer (his December 1789 Assembly motion), not designer or builder.
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine, Kat Eschner (28 Mar 2017), 'The Guillotine's Namesake Was Against Capital Punishment' — Guillotin 'did not in fact invent the machine his name is associated with — that was Dr. Antoine Louis, writes History.com'; machine first nicknamed 'Louison' or 'Louisette'; Guillotin popularized it; December 1789 Assembly appearance ('Now with my machine I take off your head in the twinkling of an eye'); 3 June 1791 decree (sole means of legal criminal execution, per Cavendish); namesake opposed capital punishment; Wikipedia, 'Joseph-Ignace Guillotin' — design by Antoine Louis, build by Tobias Schmidt; family's petition to rename refused, changed own family name; Donegan (1990, JRSM 83(10):637–639) as scholarly backing; ThoughtCo, 'A History of the Guillotine in Europe' — Schmidt as builder; confirmation of the invention-attribution structure
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1790–1792 document crediting Guillotin with the design or construction — the design memoranda name Antoine Louis (secretary of the Paris Academy of Surgery) and the build is credited to Tobias Schmidt, the German harpsichord maker in Paris.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: Marie Antoinette said 'Let them eat cake'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Direct chronological contradiction (the anecdote is on record in Rousseau's 1765 Confessions manuscripts when Antoinette was nine, in Vienna, and had never been to France) plus a complete absence of contemporary attribution — no 18th-century source of any kind names her — plus a documented first-attribution half a century after her death. The remark's paper trail starts and ends with Rousseau's unnamed 'great princess.'
Layer: fake · Place: Versailles, France (where the claim sets the remark)
Dates: 1789 → 1789 (confidence: traditional)

Marie Antoinette, queen of France, when told that the French peasants had no bread, replied "Let them eat cake" (French: "Qu'ils mangent de la brioche") — the remark that became the universal shorthand for aristocratic indifference on the eve of the Revolution.

Earliest attestation: None. No contemporary source — letter, diary, newspaper, pamphlet, or trial record — attributes the remark to Marie Antoinette. The first printed attribution dates to 1843, fifty years after her 1793 execution (Britannica).
Sources: Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Confessions, Book 6 (first six books written c. 1765, published 1782): the 'great princess' anecdote — 'Qu'ils mangent de la brioche'; Encyclopaedia Britannica: the phrase first connected to Marie Antoinette in print in 1843, fifty years after her 1793 execution; Antonia Fraser, Marie Antoinette: The Journey (2001): no reference to the remark in the queen's trial records, correspondence, or the 1780s pamphlet attacks on her; Marie Antoinette to Maria Theresa, May 1775 (after the Flour War bread riots): concern for the suffering of the people during the high price of bread — the documented attitude, opposed to the remark's indifference
Falsifiability: A contemporary document — written before 1789 and naming Marie Antoinette as the speaker — recording the remark. None exists; the 1780s libelle literature, her correspondence, and her 1793 trial records (surveyed by Antonia Fraser) all omit it.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1789-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1789</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Napoleon invented the semaphore' — the authorship myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills on the authorship claim. (1) CHRONOLOGY: the invention is dated and paper-trailed 1790–1794 — the 2 March 1791 demonstration, the 24 March 1792 Assembly address, the 1792/93 ingénieur-télégraphiste appointment — all under the revolutionary government; Napoleon seized power in 1799. An 1804 emperor cannot have invented a 1791 machine. (2) THE NAME: France calls it the Télégraphe Chappe, and the words 'semaphore' (1801) and 'telegraph' were coined by and for Chappe — an inventor's name is stamped on his machine in the Army's own vocabulary. (3) EVERY SOURCE CREDITS HIM ONLY WITH USE: the standard accounts — including the Napoleonic-era histories — record Napoleon 'making good use of the Chappe telegraph' (Italy campaign, imperial coordination); no source of any era credits him with the invention.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris (the popular telling's home — where the Assembly adopted Chappe's machine in 1792, seven years before the 18 Brumaire coup)
Dates: 1791-03-02 → 1804-12-02 (confidence: high)

Napoleon Bonaparte invented the semaphore (optical) telegraph — the 'Napoleonic semaphore' label as an authorship claim.

Earliest attestation: The popular form travels in the label itself: Wikipedia notes semaphore telegraphs are 'also called "Chappe telegraphs" or "Napoleonic semaphore"'; the army's own paper — the word 'telegraph' coined for Chappe's machine, the French name Télégraphe Chappe — is the contemporary counter-attestation
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Optical telegraph': semaphore telegraphs 'also called "Chappe telegraphs" or "Napoleonic semaphore"'; 'the word semaphore was coined in 1801 by the French inventor of the semaphore line itself, Claude Chappe'; the system 'was used by Napoleon to coordinate his empire and army' (use, not invention); Wikipedia, 'Claude Chappe': 1763–1805, the 1794 Paris–Lille messages, 'the system was widely copied by other European states, and was used by Napoleon to coordinate his empire and army'; 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Chappe, Claude' (public domain): inventor of the optical telegraph; adopted by the Legislative Assembly 1792; appointed ingénieur-télégraphe 1793; Sam Hallas, 'Short History of Telegraphy — Part 1': 'Just such a system was invented in the 1790s by Frenchman Claude Chappe... Napoleon made good use of the Chappe telegraph in his invasion of Italy' — the use/invention distinction stated in one breath
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1790–1794 document in which Napoleon (then an artillery officer in the south) originated, designed, or commissioned the optical system — the Assembly papers name only the Chappe brothers</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The legend that Salieri poisoned Mozart</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) no contemporary primary-source origin — the rumor's first durable form is a work of fiction (Pushkin 1830); (2) the witnessed symptoms (fever, rash, edema) match an infectious outbreak and do not match poison signatures, corroborated by the 2009 Vienna death-register epidemiology; (3) scholarly consensus (Grove, Britannica) states there is no foundation for the claim, and Salieri denied it during his competent years.
Layer: fake · Place: Vienna, Austria — the rumor's city and the play's stage
Dates: 1791 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Antonio Salieri poisoned Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart out of professional jealousy, as depicted in Peter Shaffer's 1979 play Amadeus and Milos Forman's 1984 film.

Earliest attestation: Post-1791 Viennese rumor, unattributed and undocumented at the time; the first durable attestation is Alexander Pushkin's 1830 play "Mozart and Salieri," which dramatized the hearsay as a tragedy of envy — literature, not history
Sources: Hektoen International ('Mozart and Salieri: From Pushkin to Shaffer'): Pushkin's 1830 play as the myth's literary birth; the Don Giovanni-whistle anecdote is suspect (Salieri was in Paris during the 1787 Prague premiere); Mozart Portal ('Did Salieri Poison Mozart? The Myth and the Evidence'): five-point debunk — symptom mismatch, no autopsy, Salieri's denials, contemporary epidemic accounts, Grove/Britannica consensus; Norton/Grove Concise Encyclopedia of Music: "He [Mozart] was not poisoned"; Britannica: "no foundation for the belief that Salieri tried to poison Mozart"; Wikipedia 'Antonio Salieri': the rumor has been disproved — Mozart's symptoms did not indicate poisoning; the two were at least mutually respectful peers; Medical symptom audit (MedicalBag): arsenic (throat burning, GI pain, delirium) and mercury (salivation, tremors, memory loss) signatures were not observed by witnesses
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1791) primary document establishing poisoning — an attending physician's report, a police or court inquiry, or a confession in Salieri's own hand from his competent years. No such document exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1791-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3738,48.2082,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1791</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The death the machine never dealt him — Guillotin, 1814, of a carbuncle</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) THE DEATH RECORD: Guillotin died at home in Paris in 1814, aged 75, of natural causes (a carbuncle), and is buried in Père-Lachaise Cemetery — documented across Wikipedia (citing Donegan's 1990 JRSM biography) and the Smithsonian (Eschner 2017, citing Cavendish's History Today piece, which notes his funeral 'occurred on this day in 1814' while his family was lobbying to rename the machine). A man cannot be guillotined in 1793 and die at home in 1814. (2) THE CONFUSION'S FINGERPRINT: the error has a documented source — a different person, J.M.V. Guillotin, a doctor of Lyon, really was executed by the guillotine, and the shared surname seeded the legend. With the inventor attribution already dead (evt-guillotin-invented-guillotine-001), the 'inventor destroyed by his invention' moral has nothing left to stand on: he didn't invent it, and it didn't kill him.
Layer: fake · Place: Père-Lachaise Cemetery, Paris — Guillotin's burial; the myth dies at the graveside
Dates: 1793-01-01 → 1814-03-26 (confidence: high)

Joseph-Ignace Guillotin was executed by the guillotine — killed by the machine that bears his name.

Earliest attestation: The popular form travels as a joke and a moral — 'the inventor died by his own machine' — repeated without a source. The documentary record gives the opposite: Guillotin's funeral occurred in 1814 (Cavendish, History Today, via Smithsonian), and Wikipedia's article records his death at home in Paris in 1814, aged 75, of a carbuncle, burial in Père-Lachaise.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Joseph-Ignace Guillotin' — died at home in Paris 1814, aged 75, natural causes (carbuncle); buried Père-Lachaise; family petitioned to rename (refused), changed own name; J.M.V. Guillotin of Lyon executed by the guillotine — the coincidence behind the error; Smithsonian Magazine, Kat Eschner (28 Mar 2017) — funeral in 1814; family lobbying to rename; 'contrary to popular myth, he died of natural causes, not by beheading'; ThoughtCo, 'A History of the Guillotine in Europe' — 'contrary to legend, Doctor Joseph Ignace Guillotin was not executed by his own machine; he lived until 1814, and died of biological causes'
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1793–1794 execution record naming Joseph-Ignace Guillotin among the condemned — no such record exists; the executed Guillotin is a different man (J.M.V. Guillotin of Lyon).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1793-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3933,48.8614,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1793</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Captain Bligh the sadistic tyrant' reading (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The punishment ledger (below the Navy average, not above it), the Admiralty's own 1790 honourable acquittal, and the documented two-stage manufacture of the image (1794 family advocacy, then 1935 film inventions) kill the sadistic-tyrant reading; what survives is a foul-tempered, verbally abusive captain — a different, lesser charge.
Layer: fake · Place: Near Tofua, Tonga — where the mutiny's story was born
Dates: 1794 → 2026 (confidence: high)

William Bligh, captain of HMS Bounty, was a sadistic tyrant whose cruelty drove his crew to mutiny on 28 April 1789.

Earliest attestation: Edward Christian's Appendix to the 1794 'Minutes of the Proceedings on the Court-Martial' — the Christian family's advocacy publication, interviewing mutineer-sympathetic witnesses five years after the event.
Sources: Bligh's Bounty log (Mitchell Library, State Library of NSW) — punishment entries for the 1787-1789 voyage; Caroline Alexander, The Bounty (2003): 229 lashes administered over 17 months aboard the Bounty, against 278 lashes in three and a half weeks aboard HMS Brunswick by one of the captains who sat on the mutineers' court-martial; Greg Dening, Mr Bligh's Bad Language (1992): Bligh's flogging rate of less than one in ten seamen was exceptionally low for the period; his violence was more verbal than physical (Alan Frost); Court-martial of William Bligh for the loss of HMS Bounty, October 1790 — honourable acquittal, the Admiralty's own verdict on his conduct; 1935 MGM Mutiny on the Bounty (Frank Lloyd, Charles Laughton as Bligh): stages a keelhauling and the flogging of a dead man — neither happened; keelhauling was not a Royal Navy punishment
Falsifiability: A Bounty punishment ledger showing above-average flogging, or a contemporary (pre-1794) account describing systematic cruelty, would revive the reading; none has surfaced in two centuries of archive work.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1794-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-175.07,-19.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1794</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Phrenology (skull-bump faculty mapping)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The diagnostic system is disproven: skull bumps do not reflect cortical development (the skull's outer table follows its own growth), and every faculty-to-region mapping was refuted — first by Flourens's 1824 lesion experiments showing functional loss from cortical ablation, then by Broca (1861) locating speech in a region Gall had assigned to something else entirely.
Layer: fake · Place: Vienna, Austria (Gall's lecture halls)
Dates: 1796 → 1830 (confidence: high)

The shape of the skull reveals the strengths of the mind: 27 (later 35) mental faculties can be read from the bumps on a person's cranium, allowing diagnosis of character and ability by palpation.

Earliest attestation: Franz Joseph Gall's Vienna lecture series (1796); publications with Johann Spurzheim, 1810-1819.
Sources: F.J. Gall &amp; J. Spurzheim, Anatomie et physiologie du système nerveux en général, et du cerveau en particulier (1810-1819); Pierre Flourens, Recherches expérimentales sur les propriétés et les fonctions du système nerveux (1824) — lesion experiments; Paul Broca (1861) on the Leborgne ('Tan') case — speech located in the inferior frontal gyrus, not where Gall's faculty maps put it
Falsifiability: Cranial topography predicting cognitive faculties under controlled blinded testing — attempted throughout the 19th century and never demonstrated above chance.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1796-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.373,48.208,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1796</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Napoleon's soldiers shot off the Sphinx's nose (1798)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice by dated evidence: (1) Frederic Louis Norden's 1737 drawings, engraved and published in 1755, show the Sphinx already without its nose — 61 years before the French campaign; (2) Napoleon's troops were never in position anyway — the battle was fought at Imbaba, hours' march from the plateau (Bassam el-Shamaa, 2009). The myth's mechanism is the 20th-century pub-story + cinema pipeline, most recently Ridley Scott's Napoleon (2023), whose own consultant Michael Broers confirmed 'nothing like that happened' and the director kept the scene anyway as 'a fast way of saying he took Egypt'.
Layer: fake · Place: Great Sphinx of Giza, Giza Plateau, Egypt
Dates: 1798 → 1798 (confidence: low)

Napoleon Bonaparte's soldiers shot the nose off the Great Sphinx of Giza with cannon fire during the French invasion of Egypt in 1798.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary attestation: the story appears only in much later retellings. Scholarly tracing (artnet, citing the myth's historiography) dates the Napoleonic version's appearance to around the dawn of the 20th century — a full century after the campaign.
Sources: historytools.org, 'The Enigma of the Great Sphinx's Missing Nose' — Norden's 1737 drawing 'clearly depicts the Sphinx without a nose, confirming that this feature was already missing by the early 18th century'; artnet / Verity Babbs (Nov 2023), 'How Ridley Scott's Napoleon Repeats One of Art History's Biggest Conspiracies' — drawings by Norden in 1737 (displayed 1744) show the nose already lost; Broers/Schott exchange; myth traced to the dawn of the 20th century; Smithsonian Journeys, Sarah Erdman (Sept 2022), 'What happened to the Sphinx's nose?' — Norden's 1737 sketches depict no nose; al-Maqrizi's 14th-century account; allbuzznews.com, Megan Levine (2026-10-09), '20 Pyramid Myths Hollywood Helped Spread' — Norden drew the Sphinx noseless in 1737, 32 years before Napoleon was born
Falsifiability: Produce one document, letter, order, or image dated 1798-1801 attesting French artillery damage to the Sphinx's face. One contemporaneous parchment moves the grade; there are none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1798-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.1376,29.9753,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1798</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Mount Sapo naming legend (soap's false etymology)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) no such place exists in any classical, late-antique, or medieval source — the location and the 'ancient Roman legend' are both unattested; the story only appears in articles about the history of soap. (2) Roman animal sacrifice would not yield soap-making quantities of fat: the edible meat and fat went to humans; the gods got bones and inedible entrails. (3) Latin sapo is documented as a loan from Celtic/Germanic (Pliny's own account ties it to the Gauls), not from a Roman toponym.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome (the legend's claimed setting — no such place)
Dates: 1800 → present (confidence: high)

Soap takes its name from Mount Sapo (or Sappo), a supposed Roman hill where animals were sacrificed to the gods; rain washed the sacrifices' fat and ashes down the slope into the Tiber, where laundresses discovered the mixture cleaned their clothes.

Earliest attestation: 19th-century soap-trade/popular histories (no classical source)
Sources: soaphistory.net — 'First Soap': 'there is no such place and no evidence for the apocryphal story'; Stuff You Missed in History Class — 'A History of Soap': Mount Sappo 'is not a real place'; the legend 'only seems to show up in articles about the history of soap'; no Roman document references it; sacrificial animals would not have yielded the fat needed; heavy dilution by rain and river; Soap history survey (Wikipedia 'History of soap', citing primary literature): 'The location of Mount Sapo is unknown, as is the source of the "ancient Roman legend"... Roman animal sacrifices usually burned only the bones and inedible entrails... would not have included enough fat to make much soap. The legend about Mount Sapo is probably apocryphal.'
Falsifiability: A classical, late-antique, or medieval Latin text naming a Mons Sapo or a sacrifice-to-soap origin</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1800-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.5,41.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Richelieu invented the table knife' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the documentary absence — no 17th-century source records the incident; the anecdote's own phrasing in reference works is 'attributed by tradition', the tell-tale grammar of an unattested retelling; (2) the chronology — the rounded-tip table knife is a later, gradual French development (the fork's spread removed the need for a pointed knife; court etiquette standardized the blunt form under Louis XIV, with 1669/1699 edict variants in the telling), which puts the real transition decades after Richelieu's death in 1642. The grade kills the invention claim only — never the blunt tips, never the tooth-picking habit, never the man.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris (the telling's claimed setting)
Dates: 1800 → present (confidence: high)

Cardinal Richelieu (1585-1642) invented the blunt-tipped table knife around 1637 — the schoolbook telling that he ordered all table knives' points ground off at his own table after seeing a dinner guest pick his teeth with a pointed knife.

Earliest attestation: No 17th-century source; the anecdote is an 18th/19th-century retelling, 'attributed by tradition'
Sources: The tradition's standard phrasing ('attributed by tradition to Cardinal Richelieu around 1637, reputedly to cure dinner guests of the habit of...') — the 'by tradition' qualifier is itself the evidence of no attestation; The Retrospectors, 'Cardinal Richelieu and the Table Knife' (2024): dates the alleged incident to 13 May 1637; traces the telling, the tooth-picking context, and the 1699 royal-edict popularization; Rhodri Marsden, 'interesting objects: The table knife' (The Independent, 2015); Henry Petroski, The Evolution of Useful Things (1992): the gradualist account — forks, pins, clips and zippers evolve by accumulation, not by single-inventor moments
Falsifiability: A contemporary 17th-century source (household order, memoir, letter) documenting Richelieu's 1637 knife-grounding order</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1800-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1800</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'valid firman' defense of the Elgin removal</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) Content: the surviving text authorizes drawing, casting, measuring, excavation of gravel, and taking 'a few pieces of stone' with 'no harm' to figures and edifices — not removing reliefs from the building (St. Clair's own 1967 content analysis: the Sultan did not allow the removal and export of statues and reliefs; Elgin's associates conceded before the 1816 committee the act was 'probably illegal'). (2) Provenance: no Ottoman original or archival entry has ever been found for a document of this kind (Korka 2019; Aygen and Sakin). The popular 'valid permit' reading is therefore disproven on its own cited document.
Layer: fake · Place: The Parthenon, Athenian Acropolis
Dates: 1801 → 1801 (confidence: high)

Lord Elgin held a valid Ottoman firman (royal decree) that legally authorized the removal of the Parthenon sculptures from the Parthenon itself — that the surviving document authorizes taking statues and reliefs off the building, not merely drawing, casting, measuring, and excavating around it.

Earliest attestation: The 1801 Italian translation in Lord Elgin's papers (translated by Pisani, the British Embassy dragoman); Philip Hunt's later English rendering; laid before the 1816 Select Committee.
Sources: William St. Clair, 'Lord Elgin and the Marbles' (1967) — content analysis of the Italian translation; William St. Clair, 'Imperial Appropriations of the Parthenon', in J.H. Merryman (ed.), 'Imperialism, Art and Restitution' (2006); David Rudenstine, 'A Tale of Three Documents: Lord Elgin and the Missing Historic 1801 Ottoman Document', Cardozo Law Review 22:5-6 (2001); Eleni Korka et al., 'The Legality of the Removal of the Parthenon Marbles', Cardozo AELJ (2023); The 1801 Italian translation of the document (Elgin's papers; British Museum archives)
Falsifiability: Discovery of the Ottoman original in the imperial archives expressly authorizing the removal of the Parthenon reliefs and statues — the archives record firmans of this period meticulously, and none has been found.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1801-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>23.7267,37.9715,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1801</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'short Napoleon' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The '5 feet 2 inches' figure is real — but it is 5 pieds 2 pouces in French units (pied du roi 32.48 cm), converting to ~168-169 cm English, average for French men of the era. The shortness is a conversion error weaponized by British propaganda, not a measurement.
Layer: fake · Place: London (caricature press) / Paris (measurement origin)
Dates: 1803 → 2023 (confidence: high)

Napoleon Bonaparte was unusually short — about 5 feet 2 inches (English measure).

Earliest attestation: British wartime caricature (Gillray et al., c. 1803) depicting Napoleon as tiny; later English-language biographies carrying '5 feet 2 inches' without unit conversion.
Sources: Autopsy report, Saint Helena, 6 May 1821 — 5 pieds 2 pouces (French); National Geographic, 'Was Napoleon even short?' — pied vs English foot analysis, Gillray's size manipulation per art historian Constance McPhee, Imperial Guard height-requirement contrast effect; YouGov Eurotrack, 'How does Europe remember Napoleon?' (fieldwork 7-22 Sept 2023; n=2144 GB / 2134 Germany / 1000 France / 1010 Denmark / 1015 Sweden / 1056 Spain / 1008 Italy) — 'physically short' selected among top-3 descriptors: Germany 52%, GB 37%, Italy 35%, France 29%, Sweden 27%, Denmark 26%, Spain 25%; British Museum print collection — Gillray-era satirical prints (e.g. 1803 tiny-Napoleon-in-cage satire)
Falsifiability: Already falsified: the French-unit reading converts to ~168-169 cm English. Would require proof the pied du roi equaled the English foot.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1803-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.13,51.51,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1803</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Luddites were anti-technology'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The anti-technology reading dies on the Luddites' own documents: they targeted specific machines wielded by specific employers to cut wages and evade standard labor practices (Thomis 1970), were themselves skilled machine operators, demanded negotiation in their letters, and used machine-breaking — a century-old protest tactic predating the movement — selectively, not indiscriminately.
Layer: fake · Place: Arnold, Nottinghamshire, England
Dates: 1811 → 1817 (confidence: high)

The Luddites were opposed to technology itself — blind enemies of progress who smashed machines because they feared or hated machinery, the modern dictionary-shorthand meaning of 'luddite'.

Earliest attestation: The re-reading is modern (the word's generalized pejorative use); the original meaning is recoverable from the movement's own 1811–1813 writings
Sources: Kevin Binfield (ed.), 'Writings of the Luddites' (Johns Hopkins UP, 2004) — Luddite letters demand fair prices and negotiation alongside threats; The National Archives — 'The proclamation of Ned Ludd' (HO 42/118, 23 December 1811) — the threat is aimed at informers and enemies of 'the Cause', not at machinery in the abstract; Malcolm I. Thomis, 'The Luddites' (1970): machine attacks 'did not imply any necessary hostility to machinery as such'; Eric Hobsbawm, 'Labouring Men: Studies in the History of Labour' — machine-breaking as 'collective bargaining by riot'; Wikipedia — Luddites ('Luddites were not opposed to the use of machines per se (many were skilled operators in the textile industry); they attacked manufacturers who were trying to circumvent standard labour practices'); HISTORY — 'Who Were the Luddites?' (HISTORY: 'Luddite is now a blanket term used to describe people who dislike new technology, but its origins date back to an early 19th-century labor movement that railed against the ways mechanized manufacturers and their unskilled laborers undermined the skilled craftsmen of the day')
Falsifiability: A Luddite letter or proclamation opposing a machine on the grounds that machinery itself is evil or progress must stop, rather than on wages, deskilling, or broken labor standards</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1811-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1271,52.9793,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1811</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The French burned Moscow'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice: (1) the Russian-initiation record — Rostopchin's letter to Bagration asking him to 'turn the city to ashes', the removed fire equipment, the released convicts, his 1823 Paris denial pamphlet followed by his admission; (2) French interest — Napoleon wanted Moscow intact as winter quarters and as a bargaining chip, and executed alleged arsonists on the spot.
Layer: fake · Place: Moscow
Dates: 1812 → 1826 (confidence: high)

The French army set the 1812 Moscow fire as policy — that is, Napoleon or his commanders ordered the burning of the city.

Earliest attestation: Napoleon's accusations against Rostopchin, September-October 1812 (including the European brochure campaign describing the governor-general's 'deceit and cruelty'); French officers' reports of Russian agents with incendiaries
Sources: Mikaberidze, Alexander. The Burning of Moscow: Napoleon's Trial by Fire 1812 (Pen &amp; Sword, 2014) — majority-historian consensus: Russian initiation; Rostopchin, Fyodor — Wikipedia biography: 1823 Paris pamphlet denying the arson, 'subsequently admitted to his role in ordering the city's destruction'; Rostopchin's letter to Bagration (1812): asking him to 'turn the city to ashes so that the enemy army does not get bread shops and food warehouses'
Falsifiability: Already falsified: a French order to burn Moscow would have to surface in the Grande Armée's own orders, bulletins, or the commanders' memoirs — the one army with a motive to keep the city intact. Rostopchin's documented order-chain stands as the positive case.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'General Winter defeated Napoleon'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed four ways: (1) the timeline — the army lost most of its strength June-October to hunger, disease, desertion, and battles, before any serious cold (Zamoyski 2004); (2) the propaganda origin — the 29th Bulletin blamed the cold to protect the Emperor's reputation, and Caulaincourt's memoirs carried it; (3) the weather — October 1812 was mild, first snows fell after the army left Moscow on 19 October, and -30°C arrived only in late November at the Berezina; (4) the 2025 Vilnius teeth study found paratyphoid and relapsing fever, not cold, in the dead of the retreat.
Layer: fake · Place: Western Russia (Niemen to Moscow to Berezina)
Dates: 1812-12 → 2025 (confidence: high)

The Grande Armée was destroyed in 1812 primarily by the Russian winter — that is, the extreme cold, rather than hunger, disease, desertion, and the Russian army, was the main cause of the army's destruction.

Earliest attestation: Napoleon's 29th Bulletin, 3 December 1812 — attributes the disaster to the cold; Caulaincourt's memoirs (published 1935) carry the winter narrative
Sources: Zamoyski, Adam. Moscow 1812: Napoleon's Fatal March (HarperCollins, 2004); Napoleon's 29th Bulletin, 3 December 1812 — the original cold-weather alibi; Barbieri et al., 'Paratyphoid fever and relapsing fever in 1812 Napoleon's devastated army', Current Biology (2025), 10.1016/j.cub.2025.09.047 — aDNA from 13 French soldiers' teeth at Vilnius: Salmonella enterica enterica (paratyphoid) + Borrelia recurrentis (relapsing fever); no typhus or trench fever; Minard, Charles Joseph. 'Carte figurative des pertes successives en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne de Russie 1812-1813' (1869) — the famous casualty visualization
Falsifiability: Would require evidence that the army was substantially intact through October and that cold-caused deaths (not hunger, disease, desertion, battle) dominated the losses. The campaign returns, Zamoyski's narrative, and the Vilnius teeth show the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.0,55.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The War of 1812 was fought to stop impressment'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed three ways: (1) Madison's 1 June 1812 war message lists British trade restrictions, ship seizures, and the western frontier alongside impressment — never impressment alone; (2) the Orders in Council were suspended 16 June 1812, two days before the 18 June declaration — the war's principal economic cause had been removed before the first shot; (3) the Treaty of Ghent (24 December 1814) never mentions impressment — the alleged cause does not appear in the peace that ended the war.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington, D.C. (war message, declaration) / Ghent (treaty)
Dates: 1812-06 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The War of 1812 was fought primarily to stop the British practice of impressment — that is, impressment was the war's principal cause.

Earliest attestation: The documentary record the shorthand simplifies: Madison's war message to Congress, 1 June 1812 (trade restrictions, impressment, seizures, frontier); the schoolbook single-cause version is 19th–20th century
Sources: Madison, James. Message to Congress, 1 June 1812 — the war message names British trade restrictions, impressment, seizures, and the western frontier; Orders in Council (1807), Wikipedia — Repeal section: suspended 16 June 1812 (Castlereagh announcement in Parliament, two days before the US declaration), provisionally repealed 23 June 1812; news reached Madison 12 August 1812 (citing Hickey 1989, The War of 1812; Hitsman 1999; Mahon 1991); peacehistory-usfp.org, 'The War of 1812' — April–June 1812 British conciliation record: Madison himself later believed the declaration 'would have been stayed' had he known of the British measures; Hickey on the Madison administration brushing aside accommodations; Treaty of Ghent — eleven articles; no mention of impressment, maritime rights, or trade (americanhistorycentral; US Naval History &amp; Heritage Command, 'Stalemate: Treaty of Ghent Ends War of 1812 in a Draw'; alexluyckx blog citing the Avalon Project's treaty text)
Falsifiability: Would require evidence that impressment was the sole listed cause in Madison's 1 June 1812 message, that the Orders in Council were still in force on 18 June 1812, or that the Treaty of Ghent addressed impressment. The records show the opposite on all three.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1812-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0364,38.8951,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1812</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Star-Spangled Banner has always been the national anthem'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed three ways: (1) the song was written in September 1814 — 38 years after 1776, for a specific War of 1812 battle, not the founding; (2) Congress made it the official national anthem only on 3 March 1931 (46 Stat. 1508, codified at 36 U.S.C. § 301, signed by President Herbert Hoover) — 117 years after the writing, 155 years after the Declaration; (3) before 1931, 'Hail, Columbia' and 'My Country, 'Tis of Thee' served as the de facto anthems at official functions.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington, D.C. (Congress, 3 March 1931)
Dates: 1814 → 1931 (confidence: high)

The Star-Spangled Banner has always been the national anthem of the United States — since the founding era, effectively from 1776 onward.

Earliest attestation: The 'always the anthem' reading is a schoolbook/textbook assumption; the documented adoption trail runs 1889 (Navy) → 1916 (Wilson, armed forces) → 1931 (Congress, the nation)
Sources: LibreTexts (citing the documentary record) — recognized for official use by the Navy 1889 and by President Wilson 1916; made national anthem by congressional resolution 3 March 1931 (46 Stat. 1508, codified at 36 U.S.C. § 301), signed by Hoover; 'Hail, Columbia' served at official functions for most of the 19th century; 'My Country, 'Tis of Thee' also served as a de facto anthem; HISTORY.com — adopted as the national anthem on March 3, 1931; Wilson 1916 official-events designation as the stepping stone; The Morgan Library &amp; Museum, 2014 — 'Not until 1931 was The Star-Spangled Banner declared the nation's official anthem by an act of Congress'
Falsifiability: Would require a congressional or executive act making the Banner the national anthem before 1931, or evidence that 'Hail, Columbia' never served officially; the 46 Stat. 1508 record stands against it.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1814-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1814</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cambronne's 'La Garde meurt, elle ne se rend pas!'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the directional silence of the battlefield — no British or French witness of 18 June 1815 records the sentence, and the print trail starts in Paris with a journalist who was not there; (2) Cambronne's own lifelong denials; (3) the British counter-tradition that Halkett's men captured him before he could reply at all.
Layer: fake · Place: Waterloo battlefield, Walloon Brabant, Belgium
Dates: 1815-06-18 → 1815-06-18 (confidence: traditional)

When the British called on the last square of the Imperial Old Guard to surrender at Waterloo, General Pierre Cambronne answered with the noble sentence 'La Garde meurt, elle ne se rend pas!' ('The Guard dies, it does not surrender!').

Earliest attestation: No battlefield witness ever attested it; the print trail begins with journalist Michel-Nicolas Balisson de Rougemont's version in the Journal général de France (the Wikipedia 'Arnouph Deshayes de Cambronne' article explicitly traces the phrase's popularization to Rougemont, later quoted by Victor Hugo in Les Misérables, 1862)
Sources: Michel-Nicolas Balisson de Rougemont, Journal général de France (the sentence's Paris print debut — not a battlefield account); Victor Hugo, Les Misérables (1862) — popularized the legend 47 years after the event; Cambronne's own denials (he stated he could not have said such a thing and remained alive); Letters to The Times, June 1932 — Colonel Hugh Halkett's claim that he captured Cambronne before any reply was made; Peter Hofschroer — Cambronne surrendered to the Osnabrück Landwehr Battalion (possibly first to a man named Führing), wounded and thrown from his horse; Andrew Roberts, Waterloo: June 18, 1815 — 'A myth was soon created that he had in fact replied: La Garde meurt, elle ne se rend pas'
Falsifiability: A contemporary battlefield witness's letter, diary, or dispatch quoting the sentence would revive it; no such source is known after 211 years of searching.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1815-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.4125,50.6794,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1815</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: 'no one proposed the stepped lens before Fresnel'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills, both published and dated: Buffon proposed the stepped lens in 1748 (Memoires de l'Academie royale des sciences — concentric annular prisms as steps in a single piece of glass), and Condorcet proposed assembling the annular sections separately on a frame in 1790. Both were intended for burning glasses, not lighthouses, which fences the kill: it destroys the 'unanticipated idea' version only, never Fresnel's independent invention or his genuine breakthrough — making the idea feasible and building the first working lighthouse optic (Cordouan, 25 July 1823).
Layer: fake · Place: Paris, France (the telling's home)
Dates: 1822 → 1822 (confidence: medium)

The stepped/annular lighthouse lens — the Fresnel lens — was entirely Augustin-Jean Fresnel's own unanticipated idea: no one had proposed a stepped lens before him. (The ex nihilo lone-genius version of the invention.)

Earliest attestation: The schoolbook and museum-plaque shorthand 'Fresnel invented the Fresnel lens' read as a lone-genius claim (no single primary originator; the fiction lives in the telling, not in a document)
Sources: Fresnel lens history — 'In 1748, Georges-Louis Leclerc, Comte de Buffon was the first to replace a convex lens with a series of concentric annular prisms, ground as steps in a single piece of glass, to reduce weight and absorption'; Fresnel lens history — 'In 1790, the Marquis de Condorcet suggested that it would be easier to make the annular sections separately and assemble them on a frame' (intended, like Buffon's, for burning glasses, not lighthouses); Arago obituary of Fresnel (translated, arXiv) — 'Buffon had imagined dividing the convex surface of a lens into several annular zones... Fresnel, who had the same idea, found — which no one before him had found — the means of making it feasible, in having the rings of the same stepped lens executed separately'; Brewster 1811/1820 proposals and advocacy — a third independent pre-Fresnel thread (carried as context that the stepped-lens idea was 'in the air')
Falsifiability: No falsifier can rescue the ex nihilo version: the 1748 and 1790 proposals are published and dated. What the record WOULD change is if evidence emerged that Fresnel derived his design from Buffon/Condorcet rather than reinventing it — that would change the story's shape, not the grade</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1822-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1822</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The telling that died: 'the mechanical pencil is modern' (234th family)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills from the documentary record: the patent file and its manufactured survivors, and the 19th-century patent industry. The fences hold: the kill is on the invention claim, never on the 20th century's genuine refinements (thin leads, the .046-inch Eversharp standard, spring mechanisms).
Layer: fake · Place: London, England
Dates: 1822 → 2026 (confidence: high)

DEBUNKED: the mechanical pencil is a 20th-century invention — killed twice: (1) the 1822 British patent (Mordan &amp; Hawkins, #4742) is a documented 203-year-old propelling-pencil patent with manufactured, hallmarked survivors; (2) the 19th century ran a whole propelling-pencil industry — 160+ British patents 1822–1874, the 1877 spring-loaded mechanism, and the 1791 Pandora example — before the 20th-century brands existed.

Earliest attestation: The telling is the unexamined assumption in popular histories that begin the mechanical pencil's story with 20th-century brands; no published survey measures the belief — stated plainly.
Sources: Yore Write!, 'S. Mordan &amp; Co.': first patent (#4742) in England, 1822, jointly with John Isaac Hawkins — the kill document.; Wikipedia, 'Mechanical pencil': 'Between 1822 and 1874, more than 160 patents were registered pertaining to a variety of improvements to mechanical pencils. The first spring-loaded mechanical pencil was patented in 1877.' — the kill industry.; Wikipedia, 'Sampson Mordan': manufactured silver mechanical pencils from 1823–1837 (SMGR) and as S. Mordan &amp; Co. until the factory's 1941 bombing — the survivors.
Falsifiability: A showing that the 1822 grant was not a propelling mechanism after all, plus the disappearance of the 1822–1874 patent record, would be required — the bar is deliberately high.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1822-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1822</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Victorian secret language of the fan' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 221st DEBUNKED family. Two kills: (1) the codebook was manufactured as advertising — Country Life: Jean-Pierre Duvelleroy (Paris fan house 1827, London shop at 121 New Bond Street, supplier to Queen Victoria) published 'Le Langage de l'Eventail' as an advertising ploy, detailing 23 gestures, many loosely based on flamenco dancing; The National independently reports the system as born with Duvelleroy, with the specific gesture meanings his. (2) No evidence of wide adoption — Country Life: 'little evidence that complex signing systems involving the use of fans were widely adopted', citing the practical difficulties (time-consuming, required undivided attention and shared mastery, far from private). A manufacturer's pamphlet is not a social practice.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris and London (the alleged practice)
Dates: 1827 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Victorian (and Regency) women communicated through a codified, widely-used secret 'language of the fan' — a fixed system of fan positions and gestures carrying specific messages ('fan in the left hand in front of the face = I desire your acquaintance', 'touching the handle to the lips = kiss me', etc.).

Earliest attestation: No period evidence of a widely-used fan-gesture code; the pamphlet system is Duvelleroy's own publication
Sources: Country Life, 'Curious Questions: Did English ladies really have a secret language of fans?': Duvelleroy's pamphlet 'as an advertising ploy' detailing 23 movements; 'little evidence that complex signing systems involving the use of fans were widely adopted'; contrasted with the genuinely widespread language of flowers; The National, 'Tracing the evolution of hand-held fans': 'in 1827, Jean-Pierre Duvelleroy launched his fan house in Paris... The notion of a "fan language" was born. According to Duvelleroy, if a woman fans slowly, it signifies that she is married, and if her fanning is fast, it means she is engaged. If she carries it in her right hand in front of her face, it means "follow me", and if she twirls it in her left hand, it means "we're being watched"'; The Free Library ('Antiques &amp; Collecting'): lists the standard popular version of the code (left-hand-before-face = desire for acquaintance, right cheek = yes, left cheek = no, handle to lips = kiss me) — the belief's canonical form, carried as belief ecology, not evidence
Falsifiability: A diary, letter, novel, or conduct book from 1780–1900 describing an actual encounter conducted via the codified fan system (as opposed to describing the pamphlet itself)</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1827-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1827</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The match is a Swedish invention' — DEBUNKED (225th family)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two kills, both documentary: (1) chronology — Walker's friction match (recorded sale 7 April 1827) predates the Swedish safety-match patent (1844) by seventeen years, and Chancel's chemical matches (1805) precede both; (2) category error — Sweden invented the safety match (Pasch 1844 + Lundström manufacture), not the match; 'Swedish match' names a monopoly and a brand. Grade kills the blanket claim only: the Walker, Chancel, Pasch, Lundström, and white-phosphorus records all stand untouched.
Layer: fake · Place: Stockholm, Sweden (the myth's home — Pasch's city; the fiction belongs where the truth was partial)
Dates: 1827 → 1844 (confidence: high)

The claim that the match is a Swedish invention is DEBUNKED as a blanket statement. The friction match was English: John Walker's Stockton-on-Tees matches were on sale from 7 April 1827, seventeen years before Gustaf Erik Pasch's 1844 Swedish safety-match patent — and Chancel's Paris chemical matches (1805) preceded both. What Sweden genuinely contributed was the SAFETY match: Pasch's 1844 separated design (red phosphorus on the striking surface, not in the head) plus the Lundström brothers' commercial manufacture from Jönköping (1845–47 development, 1855 Paris award). 'Swedish match' is a brand and an industrial monopoly (Jönköpings &amp; Vulcans Tändsticksfabriks AB 1903 → Swedish Match), not an invention credit for the match itself.

Earliest attestation: Walker's sales ledger 7 April 1827 (primary) vs Pasch's Swedish patent 1844 (patent record)
Sources: Tees Valley Museums — Walker first sale 7 April 1827; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_Erik_Pasch — Pasch granted safety-match patent 1844; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safety_matches — Lundström manufacture; Jönköpings &amp; Vulcans Tändsticksfabriks AB 1903; Swedish Match lineage
Falsifiability: A dated Swedish friction-match record predating Walker's April 1827 sale</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1827-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.0686,59.3293,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1827</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that Columbus sailed to prove the Earth was round</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Educated Europeans had known the Earth was spherical since antiquity and taught it through the Middle Ages; the Salamanca commission's actual objection — that Columbus grossly underestimated the westward distance to Asia — was correct on the evidence. The flat-earth council is Irving's 1828 invention, not a 1492 event.
Layer: fake · Place: Salamanca, Spain (the 1486-87 commission)
Dates: 1828 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Christopher Columbus sailed west to prove the Earth was round, against scholars and churchmen who believed it was flat.

Earliest attestation: Washington Irving (1828); the myth's manufacture traced by Jeffrey Burton Russell, 'Inventing the Flat Earth' (1991).
Sources: Eratosthenes of Cyrene, c. 240 BCE: measured Earth's circumference within a few percent of the true value; Medieval curriculum: Bede, Aquinas, Macrobius — spherical Earth taught as standard; the university scholars at Salamanca (1486-87) rejected Columbus over DISTANCE, not shape; Jeffrey Burton Russell (1991), 'Inventing the Flat Earth: Columbus and Modern Historians' — the myth's 19th-century manufacture; Columbus's Toscanelli/Marinus-based estimate (~3,000 nautical miles to Asia vs. actual ~10,600): the experts were right about the distance; he was lucky the Americas were in the way
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1486-87 record showing the Salamanca commission actually disputed Earth's sphericity (rather than the distance estimate) would revive the Irving version; the surviving record shows the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1828-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-5.66385,40.96426,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1828</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Elias Howe invented the sewing machine' — the sole-invention telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent chronological kills: Thomas Saint's 1790 patent predates Howe by 56 years; Thimonnier's 1830 functional machine predates him by 16 years; Hunt's 1834 working lockstitch predates him by 12 years.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (the popular telling)
Dates: 1830 → 1851 (confidence: high)

Elias Howe alone invented the sewing machine — the 1846 lockstitch patent (U.S. 4,750) was the invention of the sewing machine itself, with no prior inventors of working sewing machines.

Earliest attestation: The sole-invention telling is a popular-summary reading of Howe's 1846 patent (Smithsonian NMAH caption notes 'contrary to popular belief, he was not the first to conceive of the idea of a sewing machine')
Sources: Alamy/Smithsonian NMAH caption — 'Contrary to popular belief, he was not the first to conceive of the idea of a sewing machine' (Kill 1 framing); thoughtco.com — History of Sewing Machines: Saint's 1790 patent (Kill 1: chronological — 56 years before Howe); Smithsonian Magazine — Thimonnier's 1830 functional chain-stitch machine and tailor riot (Kill 2: chronological — 16 years before Howe); garmenco.org — Walter Hunt's 1834 unpatented lockstitch machine (Kill 3: chronological — 12 years before Howe; Hunt also invented the safety pin)
Falsifiability: Proof that no working sewing machine predated Howe's 1846 filing — defeated by the three kills.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1830-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.06,42.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1830</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Morse invented the telegraph' — the sole-inventor myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills on the SOLE-inventor reading. (1) HENRY WAS FIRST: Joseph Henry demonstrated the 'magnetic telegraph' at Albany Academy in 1831 — ringing a bell through a mile of wire — and Morse learned electromagnetism from Henry (HISTORY.com); Union College's assessment: Henry was 'the first person to send a message over an electric wire (nearly 20 years before Morse)'. (2) COOKE &amp; WHEATSTONE WERE FIRST COMMERCIAL AND FIRST PATENTED: English patent 10 June 1837; working trial Euston–Camden 25 July 1837; the world's first commercial telegraph, Paddington–West Drayton 1839 — all before Morse's US patents (1840) and his 1844 line; Morse's 1838 English patent application was opposed by Cooke &amp; Wheatstone as covering already-existing ideas. (3) THE CODE WASN'T EVEN MORSE'S: Alfred Vail's own 1845 'Early History' documents that the telegraphic code was 'originated and applied to the telegraph in the latter part of January, 1838, by Alfred Vail' — and Vail designed the practical hand key; the partnership agreement gave Vail half the foreign-patent proceeds. (4) THE FIELD WAS CROWDED BEFORE MORSE: Ronalds's 1816 static telegraph (the first published work on electric telegraphy, Admiralty-rejected as 'wholly unnecessary'), Schilling 1832, the operative Gauss &amp; Weber telegraph 1833 (Göttingen), Davy's 1837 Regent's Park demonstration and 1838 patent — independent workers, independent evidence.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington, D.C. (the Capitol rotunda, where the 1844 line terminated and the stamp's version of the story begins)
Dates: 1831-01-01 → 1844-05-24 (confidence: high)

The electric telegraph was invented single-handedly by Samuel F. B. Morse — one man, one invention, c. 1832–1844.

Earliest attestation: The sole-inventor form: the 19th-century American popular telling and its schoolbook afterlife; the strongest period defense is the Scientific American / Paris Moniteur formulation — principles not his, practical application his
Sources: HISTORY.com, 'Morse Code &amp; Telegraph: Invention &amp; Samuel Morse': 'the credit for inventing the telegraph generally falls to two sets of researchers: William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone in England, and Samuel Morse, Leonard Gale and Alfred Vail in the United States'; 'Morse... learned more about the topic from American physicist Joseph Henry'; Library of Congress, Samuel F. B. Morse Papers: 'Henry's experiments, Gale's assistance, and, soon after, hiring the young technician Alfred Vail were keys to Morse's success'; Morse met Wheatstone in England and judged his system 'ingenious' but his own 'simpler, more efficient, and easier to use'; Union College exhibit on Joseph Henry: 'the first person to send a message over an electric wire (nearly 20 years before Samuel F. B. Morse)'; Henry mentored Morse on the telegraph; Wikipedia, 'Telegraphy' / 'Electric telegraph': Cooke &amp; Wheatstone patent 10 June 1837; first commercial telegraph Paddington–West Drayton (13 mi); Gauss &amp; Weber 1833 first operative system; Ronalds 1816 rejected by the Admiralty; Alfred Vail, 'Early History of the Electro-magnetic Telegraph' (1845): 'the telegraphic code was originated and applied to the telegraph in the latter part of January, 1838, by Alfred Vail'; King's College London, Wheatstone exhibition: June 1837 patent; July 1837 Euston–Camden trial; 9 July 1839 Paddington–West Drayton permanent line; 1840 'first successful commercial application of the electric telegraph in the world'; Morse's 1838 English patent application opposed by Cooke &amp; Wheatstone; ThoughtCo, 'Samuel Morse and the Invention of the Telegraph': the popular American form — 'The credit for this invention belongs to Samuel Finley Breese Morse' (the telling under grade)
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1830s evidence that Morse originated the electromagnetic principle itself — e.g., a Morse notebook deriving the electromagnet independently of Henry's published 1828–31 work, or a working Morse system predating Henry's 1831 mile-of-wire demonstration</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1831-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.03687,38.89707,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1831</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Liberty Bell's crack is NOT from its first ringing</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice by the documents. First: the 1752 first-strike crack was melted away — the bell was recast twice by Pass &amp; Stow in 1753, so no crack from the first ringing can survive in the present metal. Second: the visible crack is a 19th-century injury — it 'acquired its distinctive large crack sometime in the first half of the 19th century' (1835 Marshall tolling is the widespread tradition), was widened and stop-drilled in 1846 after ringing for Washington's birthday, and the bell has not rung since. The schoolbook telling collapses three separate cracks (1752, the 19th-century injury, the 1846 widening) into one.
Layer: fake · Place: Liberty Bell Center, Independence National Historical Park, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1835 → 1846 (confidence: low)

The famous crack visible on the Liberty Bell today dates to the bell's first ringing — i.e., the bell has carried the same crack since it was first struck in Philadelphia in 1752.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary documentation fixes the moment; the 1835 Marshall story is later tradition; the 1846 Washington's-birthday widening and stop-drilling repair are the documented end of its ringing life
Sources: Wikipedia 'Liberty Bell' — first cracked when rung after arrival, twice recast by Pass &amp; Stow; 'acquired its distinctive large crack sometime in the first half of the 19th century'; 1835 Marshall story; 1846 repair; not rung since; National Park Service — 'The Liberty Bell Story': 'By 1846 a thin crack began to affect the sound of the bell. The bell was repaired in 1846 and rang for a George Washington birthday celebration, but the bell cracked again and has not been rung since. No one knows why the bell cracked either time.'; OnThisDay.com Liberty Bell photo archive (INHP Library and Archives): 'The bell cracked on its first test ring so it was then melted down and recast by local metalworkers John Pass and John Stow'; 'The bell's famous crack formed in the 1830s. Efforts were made to repair the bell in 1846 by widening the crack but these failed.'
Falsifiability: Metallurgical continuity between the 1752 cracked metal and the present bell — impossible: the 1752 bell was melted down and recast twice by Pass &amp; Stow (1753); the metal carrying the first crack no longer exists</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1501,39.9489,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Liebig invented the mirror in 1835' — the quiz telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Chronology kill: glass mirrors predate Liebig by at least five centuries; his documented contribution is the silvering process.
Layer: fake · Place: Giessen, Germany (Liebig, 1835)
Dates: 1835 → 1835 (confidence: high)

The telling that 'Justus von Liebig invented the mirror in 1835' — repeated in quiz formats and trivia — is false: glass mirrors existed centuries before Liebig (14th-century Italy; the Venetian tin-mercury mirrors of the 16th century). Liebig's 1835 contribution was the chemical silvering PROCESS, not the mirror.

Earliest attestation: The telling: modern trivia/quiz formats (e.g. SSC CHSL 2024 exam question, 'created the first mirror'). The refutation: 14th-century Italian glass mirrors (The Handy Physics Answer Book) and 16th-century Venetian tin-mercury mirrors.
Sources: The Handy Physics Answer Book — 'The earliest glass mirrors, backed with shiny metal, appeared in Italy during the fourteenth century'; Testbook SSC CHSL 2024 question — the myth's own telling: 'created the first mirror' attributed to Liebig; evt-liebig-silvering-1835-001 — the actual 1835 contribution, for contrast
Falsifiability: No honest falsifier — the chronology is settled; only a redefinition of 'invented' ('invented the silvering process') would salvage a true reading, which is the myth's own escape hatch.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.6727,50.5839,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The legend that nobody questioned the Moon Hoax</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: the Herald named it a hoax on the series' last day; Poe, a practicing hoax-writer, saw through it instantly; and the circulation-doubling leg is flagged as 'certainly exaggerated in popular accounts' by the documentary record.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City — the city that supposedly was fooled whole
Dates: 1835 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The 1835 Moon Hoax fooled New York wholesale: nobody questioned the articles, the public believed them entirely, and the hoax permanently doubled the Sun's circulation, establishing it as the world's most-read paper.

Earliest attestation: The popular retelling itself: the 'all New York believed it' and 'circulation doubled permanently' forms — attestation of the BELIEF (the legend), not of the fact
Sources: Interesting Engineering ('The Great Moon Hoax of 1835: The Birth of Fake News?'): James Gordon Bennett's Herald, back in print 31 August 1835 after the office fire, published 'The Astronomical Hoax Explained' — accused the Sun of fabricating the story, identified Locke as its true author, and pointed out that the Edinburgh Journal of Science was no longer being published; iHeart / Stuff You Missed in History Class (Great Moon Hoax, Part 2): by the fifth installment 'most of the other papers had started denouncing the entirety of this series as a hoax'; Locke's August 31 nebulous denial letter to the Herald; a Philadelphia paper reprinted the letter beside a real-estate auction advertisement for Herschel-discovered lunar property — the joke was already being run with within the series' own week; Wikipedia 'Great Moon Hoax': 'the degree to which the hoax increased the paper's circulation has certainly been exaggerated in popular accounts of the event'; Untapped Cities ('The Great Moon Hoax: NYC Penny Papers and Man-Bats in Space'): Edgar Allan Poe saw the series as a ripoff of his own June 1835 story and recognized the game instantly; 'by the end of the summer, however, newspapers that had initially republished the story were now calling it a hoax'
Falsifiability: Already falsified: the Aug 31, 1835 debunk and the circulation-legend audit both predate the legend.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1835-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1835</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Horace Mann invented the public school</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The invention claim dies on the documents: public, town-supported schooling predated Mann by two centuries (Boston Latin 1635, Dedham's taxpayer-funded school 1644, the 1647 Old Deluder Satan Act), and compulsory-schooling statutes predated him by the same margin.
Layer: fake · Place: Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1837 → 1852 (confidence: high)

Horace Mann (1796-1859) invented the American public school — that is, before Mann's reforms as Secretary of the Massachusetts Board of Education (1837-1848), there were no public schools in America, and the public school is his invention.

Earliest attestation: The 'Father of American Education' honorific in modern reference works; the textbook one-liner is 20th-century
Sources: Wikipedia — Horace Mann (Secretary of the Massachusetts Board of Education 1837-1848; 'The Father of American Education'); Fiveable APUSH exam guide — Mann as 'the reason he gets called the Father of the American Public School System' (belief ecology); Quizlet/U.S. education history flashcard sets — Boston Latin School founded 1635; Dedham 1 Jan 1644 authorized the first taxpayer-funded public school; EdWeek — 'The Foundation of Universal Education' (Carl F. Kaestle: nowhere was schooling entirely tax-supported and compulsory in the colonial era; 1852 Massachusetts compulsory-attendance law)
Falsifiability: A document showing Massachusetts had no tax-supported or town-maintained schools before 1837</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1837-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1837</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Paperweights are an ancient art' — the myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three kills: (1) no glass paperweight predates the 1840s — the earliest datable examples are French (Baccarat, Saint-Louis, Clichy) and Venetian, all mid-1840s; (2) the telling conflates the millefiori TECHNIQUE (centuries old — Murano 16th c., Roman murrine before) with the OBJECT (the glass dome weight), which is modern; (3) the 'Venetian antique' framing is 19th-century dealer/collector romance, contradicted by the documented 1840s invention boom.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris, France — the trade center of the 1840s paperweight boom
Dates: 1840 → 1845 (confidence: high)

Glass paperweights — the glass dome weight as a desk object — are an ancient art, with the object itself dating back to antiquity (ancient Egypt, Rome, or the old world).

Earliest attestation: The telling circulates in trivia-site and antique-dealer copy in the 20th/21st century; the earliest datable glass paperweights are mid-1840s French/Venetian (see companion records).
Sources: Antique Trader, 'The Zen of Glass Paperweights' — glass paperweights originated in France around 1845 (Baccarat, Saint Louis, Clichy), developed as affordable objets d'art/gift items, exhibited at the 1851 Great Exhibition; L. H. Selman Fine Glass Paperweights, 'Why Collect' — by 1845 France was the undeniable paperweight center of the world; after about 1860 the art all but disappeared for eighty years; Christopher Proudlove (Free Online Library) — Pietro Bigaglia of an old Venetian glass family 'generally credited with the manufacture of the first glass paperweight in 1845'; Venetian millefiori cane work since the 15th century; Carters price guide (Baccarat paperweights) — the competing 'Venice-first' tradition: paperweights 'evolved in Venice in the early nineteenth century, and spread to France via Bohemia about 1845' — both tellings agree on the 1840s, never antiquity
Falsifiability: A datable glass paperweight object (distinct from a mere glass lump or millefiori bead) with a secure pre-1800 provenance — none has ever been produced; the object's earliest documented examples are all 1840s.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Pyramids are Egypt's monopoly"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The numbers are decisive and have been stable in the archaeological literature for a century: ~255 catalogued Nubian pyramids against 118–138 Egyptian. The Kushite building program also outlasted Egypt's by ~2,000 years. The monopoly claim is false on count and on tradition.
Layer: fake · Place: The claim is a global belief about pyramid distribution; pin carried at Meroë, the counter-evidence's representative site
Dates: 1840 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Pyramids are an Egyptian monopoly: Egypt has the most pyramids in the world, and the pyramid-building tradition belongs to Egyptian civilization alone.

Earliest attestation: The 19th-century European archaeological and popular frame (Lepsius, Baedeker, the museum gallery); the modern textbook/tourism sentence 'Egypt, land of the pyramids'
Sources: The archaeological corpus itself: ~255 Nubian pyramids (Reisner 1908–1932 corpus; UNESCO 2011 Meroe listing) vs. 118–138 Egyptian pyramids; The Vintage News 2016 summary of the standard figures: 255 Sudan / 118–138 Egypt — reflecting the established literature, not a reclassification; National Geographic on the Nubian pyramid tradition: c. 900 BCE–400 CE, versus Egyptian royal pyramid construction ending c. 1500 BCE
Falsifiability: A full inventory showing Egypt's pyramid count exceeding Sudan's — impossible against the catalogued ~255 standing Nubian structures vs. 118–138 Egyptian</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1840-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>33.75,16.94,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1840</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Thomas Cook invented tourism' (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Organized leisure travel predates Cook by centuries: the Grand Tour (c. 1660–1840s), the English spa towns, and commercial guidebooks — Mariana Starke's Travels on the Continent (1820, John Murray) and Murray's Hand-Books (1836) — all predate the 1841 excursion, and ERIH places Cook as 'one of several' mid-century entrepreneurs who popularised railway travel. Cook's genuine innovation was the chartered group excursion at a fixed per-head price, the escorted tour, and the package-holiday business — not tourism itself.
Layer: fake · Place: Leicester, England (the 'birthplace of tourism' claim's origin point)
Dates: 1841-07-05 → 1841-07-05 (confidence: high)

Thomas Cook invented tourism — the 5 July 1841 Leicester–Loughborough excursion created the travel and leisure industry.

Earliest attestation: The retrospective 'birthplace of tourism' framing (e.g., Story of Leicester's 'birthplace of popular tourism' accolade); the underlying excursion is attested in 1841
Sources: ERIH biography of Thomas Cook: 'Thomas Cook was one of several entrepreneurs who in the mid-nineteenth century popularised railway travel throughout Europe through publications and through the organisation of excursions and holidays' — the 'one of several' directly contradicts the lone-inventor framing; Wikipedia 'Thomas Cook': the pre-existing travel world Cook entered — his later commercial milestones show the actual shape of his innovation (1845 first profit-making excursion, 1846 Scotland, 1851 Great Exhibition 165,000, Cook's Excursionist monthly magazine, the continental coupon system from the 1855 Paris Exhibition); The pre-existing travel infrastructure, via the team's own tourism beat (Dr. Verity Hollis, #174): the Grand Tour (c. 1660–1840s), the English spa towns, and published guidebooks — Mariana Starke's Travels on the Continent (1820, John Murray) and Murray's Hand-Books (1836) — all predate the 1841 excursion; the excursion was a new business model applied to an old human habit
Falsifiability: Evidence that no organized leisure travel, guidebooks, group excursions, or travel businesses existed anywhere before July 1841</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.1333,52.6333,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Slocum invented the stapler in 1841' — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The patent's own drawing and description show a machine that stuck rows of pins into folded paper to package them for sale — 'not a paper fastener at all.' Slocum's life's work was pin manufacturing (solid-head pins, 'Poughkeepsie Pins'); the stapler credit is a category error that the primary document refutes.
Layer: fake · Place: Poughkeepsie, New York — Slocum's pin works
Dates: 1841-09-30 → 1841-09-30 (confidence: high)

Samuel Slocum of Poughkeepsie, New York, received U.S. Patent 2,275 on September 30, 1841, for a 'machine for sticking pins in paper' — and this machine is often misidentified as the first stapler.

Earliest attestation: U.S. Patent 2,275 (30 Sept 1841), 'machine for sticking pins in paper' — examined by collector-historian Curtis A. Scaglione from the patent drawing and description.
Sources: Curtis A. Scaglione, 'Fastener History' — officemuseum.online: patent drawing/description examined — 'not a paper fastener at all, but a machine that stuck a number of pins to paper for the purpose of packaging them in quantity'; pin-sticking machine in use by 1841 (Chauncey Depew, One Hundred Years of American Commerce, via Stanford Library); Wikipedia, 'Samuel Slocum' — the 30 Sept 1841 patent (#2275) 'is often believed to be the first stapler. In fact, this patent... is for a device used for packaging pins'
Falsifiability: The patent document itself is the killer — it describes sticking pins into paper for packaging; a different specification would change the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.921,41.7005,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The tulip crash that bankrupted a nation — the ruin reading of 1637</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: Goldgar's archival audit finds hundreds of traders and almost no bankruptcies; the futures contracts moved no money, so there were no losses to transmit; and the Dutch economy shows no 1637 downturn — the Republic stayed the world's richest economy.
Layer: fake · Place: The Dutch Republic (claim) vs the archive (Amsterdam/Haarlem)
Dates: 1841 → 1637 (confidence: high)

The 1637 collapse of tulip prices devastated the economy of the Dutch Republic — ruining fortunes across the nation and inflicting a severe, years-long shock on Dutch commerce.

Earliest attestation: Charles Mackay, Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds (1841) — attestation of the BELIEF (all levels of society, merchants 'reduced almost to beggary', commerce suffering 'a severe shock'), not of the fact
Sources: Anne Goldgar, Tulipmania (2007): archival audit — trade limited to a few hundred merchants/craftsmen; 'fewer than half a dozen' experienced financial troubles in the period, and even of these it is not clear tulips were to blame; 'no real effect on the economy whatsoever' (HISTORY.com interview); The futures-contract structure itself (Goldgar 2007; Wikipedia Tulip mania, citing the scholarship): money had not changed hands between buyers and sellers, so profits were never realized — the collapse could not transmit losses to the wider economy; The Dutch economy's continued primacy: the Republic remained one of the world's leading economic and financial powers with the highest per-capita income in the world from about 1600 to about 1720 — no 1637 downturn in the record
Falsifiability: Notarial records showing mass bankruptcies clearly attributable to tulips in 1637–1638, or fiscal records showing a Dutch economic downturn beginning in 1637 — the archive has been searched for a century and produced neither</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1841-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.9,52.37,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1841</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Poe's 'Balloon-Hoax' — the Atlantic crossing that never happened (13 April 1844)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The hoax is proven by its own paper trail: the Sun printed the 'extra' on 13 April 1844 and its own retraction on 15 April — 'we are inclined to believe that the intelligence is erroneous.' The first real Atlantic balloon crossing came 134 years later (Double Eagle II, August 1978). DEBUNKED as NEWS — the hoax EVENT (that Poe and the Sun printed it) is VERIFIED on the same documents.
Layer: fake · Place: New York Sun building (publication); purported landing at Sullivan's Island, South Carolina
Dates: 1844-04-13 → 1844-04-15 (confidence: high)

Edgar Allan Poe's 'Balloon-Hoax' — printed as a news extra in the New York Sun on 13 April 1844 with the headline 'THE ATLANTIC CROSSED IN THREE DAYS!' — reported that Monck Mason's steering balloon 'Victoria' had crossed the Atlantic in 75 hours and landed on Sullivan's Island, South Carolina. No such flight occurred: the Victoria never existed, the passengers were invented (the real novelist William Harrison Ainsworth was listed among them), and the Sun printed a retraction on 15 April 1844.

Earliest attestation: New York Sun, 13 April 1844 — 'ASTOUNDING NEWS! BY EXPRESS VIA NORFOLK: THE ATLANTIC CROSSED IN THREE DAYS!'
Sources: Wikipedia 'The Balloon-Hoax': publication history — 13 April 1844 extra with headline text; retraction printed 15 April 1844 ('we are inclined to believe that the intelligence is erroneous'); Thomas Monck Mason's 1836 book as source material; real William Harrison Ainsworth listed as passenger; Museum of Hoaxes, 'The Great Balloon Hoax (1844)': Sun extra 13 April 1844; quickly revealed as Poe-authored hoax; Victoria illustration redrawn from the 1843 'Remarks on the Ellipsoidal Balloon' frontispiece; Poe on the Sun building steps that day telling crowds it was a hoax; Smithsonian Magazine, 'A Brief History of Trans-Atlantic Balloon Crossings' (11 Aug 2017): Poe 'authored a hoax about a successful crossing in 1844 — and actually fooled The New York Sun'; 14 failed trans-Atlantic missions before the 1978 success
Falsifiability: A landing record, customs entry, or corroborating eyewitness from Sullivan's Island, April 1844, for the Victoria's passengers</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1844-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1844</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: the famine was caused solely by the potato blight</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed on the documents, three ways: (1) Ireland kept producing and exporting other food throughout the famine — 'large quantities of food were exported from Ireland during the famine, and the refusal of London to bar such exports, as had been done on previous occasions, was an immediate and continuing source of controversy'; (2) the relief doctrine was ideological, not logistical — Trevelyan in 'The Irish Crisis' (1848) called the calamity 'the judgement of God' sent to teach the Irish a lesson and warned it 'must not be too much mitigated,' locating 'the real evil' not in hunger but in 'the moral evil of the selfish, perverse and turbulent character of the people'; (3) the statute book made starvation administrative — the 1847 Gregory quarter-acre clause barred relief to anyone holding more than a quarter acre. The blight destroyed the crop; the policies decided who ate. Scope fenced: never the blight's reality (evt-potato-blight-herb1-001 VERIFIED), never the death toll (evt-irish-famine-001 VERIFIED), never the honest bound on the exports (Ó Gráda's one-seventh, carried in evt-famine-food-exports-1845-001).
Layer: fake · Place: Ireland — the telling is about the whole island; the evidence is the British policy record at Westminster and Dublin Castle
Dates: 1845 → 1852 (confidence: high)

The Irish Potato Famine (1845–1852) was caused solely by the potato blight — that is, the million deaths and mass emigration were a pure natural disaster with no significant human or political causes.

Earliest attestation: The blight-only telling is the textbook shorthand of the later 19th century; Mitchel's 1861 counter ('The Almighty, indeed, sent the potato blight, but the English created the famine') is the earliest dated rebuttal in the record consulted.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Great Famine (Ireland)' (blight as proximate cause; absentee landlordism and single-crop dependence; Whig laissez-faire; exports continued and London refused to bar them; quarter-acre clause); Adams auction catalogue — Trevelyan's 'The Irish Crisis' (1848): 'the judgement of God sent the calamity to teach the Irish a lesson, that calamity must not be too much mitigated'; 'the real evil ... the moral evil of the selfish, perverse and turbulent character of the people'; History Education Research Journal (UCL Press) — 'The Great Irish Famine in Irish and UK history textbooks, 2010–2020' (Trevelyan's providentialism; Ó Gráda 1999:124 — exported grain ≈ one-seventh of potatoes lost; imports exceeded exports after 1846; the genocide thesis and the scholarly corrective, Nally 2008); irisharoundtheworld.com — 'Irish Potato Famine: Ireland's Great Hunger, 1845 to 1852' (the Great Hunger naming argument; most famine historians reject the genocide label while condemning the response; Mitchel 1861; Coogan 2012)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that Ireland produced no exportable food 1845–1852, that London barred food exports, and that relief policy was maximal and non-ideological, would overturn this. The export manifests, Trevelyan's own writings, and the statute book all point the other way.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-9.05,53.27,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Stephen Perry invented the rubber band alone' — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — One kill, and it is the patent document itself: British Patent 13880/1845 was granted jointly to Stephen Perry of St John's Wood AND Thomas Barnabas Daft of Birmingham. Every 'Perry alone' telling drops the co-patentee.
Layer: fake · Place: London, England — where the telling places the solo invention (Perry's city)
Dates: 1845 → 1845 (confidence: high)

The telling that 'Stephen Perry invented the rubber band alone in 1845' — that the rubber band began with one man, Perry, and the 1845 patent is his alone.

Earliest attestation: The telling's popular form: quiz and trivia sites crediting 'Stephen Perry' alone for the 1845 patent (e.g. the QuizzClub trivia card: 'The inventor Stephen Perry acquired the patent for what item in 1845?'; the WCSA 'On This Day' column naming Perry alone in its headline telling). The patent specification names Perry AND Thomas Barnabas Daft.
Sources: British Patent 13880/1845 — joint grant to Stephen Perry and Thomas Barnabas Daft, sealed 17 March 1845 (the killing document; cited via the rubber-band article's patent reference, todayinsci archive); QuizzClub trivia, 'The inventor Stephen Perry acquired the patent for what item in 1845?' — the popular solo telling in its quiz-card form: Perry alone, 1845, no Daft; WCSA 'On This Day — March 17, 2021' — headline telling credits 'Stephen Perry (a British inventor and businessman)' alone for the first rubber band; the body names Messrs Perry and Co. but never Daft
Falsifiability: A re-examination of the 13880/1845 specification showing Daft's name absent, or documentary proof Daft was struck from the grant — the joint grant is on the record as sealed.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1845-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1845</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: 'Ether Day was the first ever surgical anesthesia'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent prior uses precede it on the documentary record: Crawford Long's ether surgeries in Jefferson, Georgia (from 30 March 1842, documented by certificates and the patient's account, published 1849) and Horace Wells's nitrous-oxide dental use (December 1844, demonstrated publicly in Boston January 1845) — plus the recreational 'ether frolics' both traditions drew on. The event stays CORROBORATED; the superlative dies.
Layer: fake · Place: Boston (the monument and the dome)
Dates: 1846-10-16 → 1846-10-16 (confidence: high)

The 16 October 1846 Ether Dome demonstration was the first ever use of inhaled anesthesia in surgery — the moment pain was abolished.

Earliest attestation: The 'first ever' reading is carried in granite on the Ether Monument (Boston Public Garden, 1868): 'First proved at Mass. General Hospital in Boston October AD MDCCCXLVI' — a claim Morton himself pushed and Mark Twain called a lie that would outlast the monument a million years.
Sources: The Conversation/MedicalXpress (republished): the monument's inscription quoted verbatim ('First proved at Mass. General Hospital in Boston October AD MDCCCXLVI'); the claim called 'untrue' — Long's 1842 use had six witnesses in his public office; Holmes's 'ether or either'; Twain's 'the lie it tells will outlast it a million years.'; Long's 1849 Southern Medical and Surgical Journal account (via the 1849 original at archive.org/NIH): the 30 March 1842 operation on James Venable, the 6 June 1842 second operation, the certificates of patient and witnesses — the documentary existence of pre-1846 surgical ether.; Harvard Countway ('Monument to E(i)ther'): 'Crawford W. Long may well have employed an anesthetic before either Morton or Wells but neglected to develop or promote the idea' — the priority verdict from the medical-history establishment.
Falsifiability: Proof that no earlier surgical use of an inhaled anesthetic occurred anywhere — impossible against Long's 1842 records and Wells's 1844–45 records.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0686,42.3632,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'First Thanksgiving' National-Origin Myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Scope fenced: the 1621 feast itself is CORROBORATED (evt-1621-feast-001) and the holiday is a real American tradition — what dies is the retrojection. The feast was never called a 'thanksgiving' by its participants; Separatists held no annual holidays (even Christmas was rejected); the 1623 rain thanksgiving is the first day identified as 'thanksgiving' in Plymouth primary sources; and the national holiday's documented origin is Hale's 1846-1863 campaign plus Lincoln's October 3, 1863 proclamation, not a continuous line from 1621.
Layer: fake · Place: Plymouth, Massachusetts (site of the retrojected claim)
Dates: 1846 → 1941 (confidence: high)

That the 1621 Plymouth harvest feast was 'the first Thanksgiving' and the founding of an annual national tradition leading directly to the American Thanksgiving holiday.

Earliest attestation: The 1621 feast was re-baptized as 'the first Thanksgiving' in the Victorian era: Sarah Josepha Hale's campaign in Godey's Lady's Book (1846-1863), Abraham Lincoln's Proclamation of October 3, 1863, and the mass-circulation imagery of the 1880s-1910s (Jane G. Austin's fiction, Jennie Augusta Brownscombe's 1914 painting, Jean Leon Gerome Ferris's c. 1912-15 painting).
Sources: Sarah Josepha Hale's letter to Abraham Lincoln, September 28, 1863 (Library of Congress, Manuscript Division); Abraham Lincoln's Thanksgiving Proclamation, October 3, 1863 (last Thursday of November); Act of Congress, December 26, 1941 (Public Law 77-379) — Thanksgiving fixed as the fourth Thursday of November; Edward Winslow's letter, Mourt's Relation (1622) — what it does NOT say: no 'thanksgiving' label, no annual recurrence, no turkey centerpiece; Library of Congress blog, 'The Woman Who Helped Put Thanksgiving on the Calendar' (2024) — documents Hale's campaign and Lincoln's proclamation; Caleb Johnson's Mayflower History research: no buckles anywhere; hat buckles not fashionable until late 17th century; black clothing formal wear, everyday clothing colorful
Falsifiability: A pre-1863 American source calling the 1621 feast 'the first Thanksgiving' as the start of an annual national tradition — none exists; the two 17th-century sources call it a feast, not a thanksgiving</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-70.6673,41.9584,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>TELLING (DEBUNKED): 'crap' = Thomas Crapper's name</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Wikipedia's 'List of common false etymologies': the word comes from Medieval Latin crappa ('chaff') via Middle English crappe, not from the plumber's surname; the OED's 1846 waste-sense citation antedates Crapper's fame by decades; Crapper (1836-1910) did not invent the flush toilet either — the 1969 Reyburn satire is the myth's vector.
Layer: fake · Place: London (Victorian plumbing and English print culture)
Dates: 1846 → 2026 (confidence: high)

TELLING (DEBUNKED): the word 'crap' (excrement) derives from the surname of Thomas Crapper (1836-1910), the Victorian plumber — and Crapper invented the flush toilet, so his name became the word.

Earliest attestation: OED: first recorded 'crap' in the bodily-waste sense, 1846
Sources: Wikipedia, 'List of common false etymologies of English words' — 'The word 'crap' did not originate as a back-formation of British plumber Thomas Crapper's surname ... ultimately comes from Medieval Latin crappa, meaning 'chaff''; TheCollector (Oct 10, 2026), Greg Beyer — 'The word 'crap' likely comes from the Middle English 'crappe,' which refers to chaff or grain husks ... The first recorded use of crap referring to bodily waste, according to the OED, is in 1846, just 10 years after Thomas Crapper was born'; datesandtimes.org (Thomas Crapper bio) — the 1969 Wallace Reyburn satirical biography as the myth's origin; Crapper did not invent the flush toilet
Falsifiability: A dated waste-sense use of 'crap' arising after Crapper's fame (post-1880s), with no earlier attestations</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1846-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1846</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Hunt invented the safety pin" (1849) — the invention telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Prior art kills the invention claim: the Mycenaean violin-bow fibula (14th c. BCE, Late Mycenaean III) is a single wire with a unilateral spring, sharp point at one end, hooked catch at the other — described in the archaeological literature as "remarkably like a modern safety pin." Three millennia of fibula types carry the same principle. Hunt's contribution is the 1849 patent on the modern commercial form, graded separately (evt-hunt-safety-pin-patent-1849-001 VERIFIED).
Layer: fake · Place: Greek Peloponnese (Mycenaean violin-bow fibula find region, 14th c. BCE)
Dates: 1849 → 1849 (confidence: high)

Walter Hunt invented the safety pin in 1849.

Earliest attestation: Modern invention histories attributing the invention to Hunt (the schoolbook version)
Sources: Wikipedia "Fibula (brooch)" — Bronze Age fibulae: violin bow fibulae (Drahtbügel) of the 14th century BC (Late Mycenaean III), introduced by the Mycenaeans on the Greek Peloponnese; "looks remarkably like a modern safety pin"; Smarthistory — "Fibulae": bow fibulae consist of a body, a pin, and a catch — "like safety pins"; Metropolitan Museum of Art Journal v.35 (2000) — Dandridge: "in its most basic form the fibula is a safety pin made from a single wire with a sharp point at one end and a hooked catch, designed to capture the point, at the other"; Hull Museums Collections — Roman brooches: "This would be how the Romans fastened the brooch to their clothing, in much the same way we would use a safety-pin today"
Falsifiability: Demonstration that no pre-1849 pin combined a single wire, a spring coil, a sharp point, and a shielding catch — refuted by the fibula record</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1849-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>21.68,37.25,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1849</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Lightning never strikes the same place twice' (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — NOAA and NASA document repeated strikes on the same structures: the Empire State Building is hit about 23–25 times per year (NOAA; NASA's LIS climatology), was struck eight times in 24 minutes during one storm, and the Eiffel Tower about ten times a year — lightning seeks the tallest conductive path, so a spot struck once is proven to be an excellent target, not a spent one.
Layer: fake · Place: Empire State Building, New York (the canonical counter-example)
Dates: 1850 → 2026 (confidence: low)

Lightning never strikes the same place twice — once a particular spot has been hit by lightning, it will not be hit again.

Earliest attestation: The folk saying in 19th-century English usage as a metaphor; the physical refutation from 20th-century strike records
Sources: NOAA, '5 striking facts versus myths about lightning': 'Myth: Lightning never strikes in one place twice. Fact: Actually, lightning can, and often does, strike the same place repeatedly — especially if it's a tall and isolated object. For example, the Empire State Building is hit about 25 times per year.'; NASA Scientific Visualization Studio, 'Where Does Lightning Strike?' (2017): 'However, this common phrase is a myth. Lightning often strikes the same place repeatedly—especially tall, pointy, and isolated objects. According NOAA, the Empire State Building is hit approximately 23 times a year.' Based on 16+ years of Lightning Imaging Sensor data (1998–2015).; weather.com, 'Lightning Never Strikes Twice – Or Does It?': the Empire State Building 'is hit on average 25 times per year, according to the National Weather Service' and 'was struck eight times in 24 minutes during one storm'; LPS France, 'Does Lightning Strike the Same Place Twice?': the expression's origin as an old metaphor for misfortune; modern detection networks show repeated impacts are frequent; the Eiffel Tower struck about ten times per year
Falsifiability: A long-term strike record showing that tall structures are statistically no more likely to be re-struck than any random point — contradicted by every existing dataset</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1850-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9857,40.7484,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1850</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Mere Conduit' Myth of Medieval Islamic Medicine — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — A universal claim ('nothing original') dies on a single counterexample; the timeline holds four: evt-ibn-al-nafis-001 (pulmonary circulation, 1242), evt-al-zahrawi-001 (~200 illustrated surgical instruments, c. 1000), evt-bimaristan-001 (the waqf-endowed hospital institution), evt-al-jazari-001 (crankshaft/camshaft, 1206). Note_on_grade_scope: the grade kills the 'nothing original' reading only — the translation movement was real and foundational, Galen and Hippocrates were the base texts, and the conduit story survives as PART of the truth, not the whole of it.
Layer: fake · Place: Damascus (nominal pin for the historiographical claim; the evidence lives in Cairo, Cordoba, and Diyarbakir)
Dates: 1850 → 2026 (confidence: low)

'Medieval Islamic medicine produced nothing original — it was merely a conduit for Greek texts, a translation project between Galen and the European Renaissance, contributing no new knowledge of its own.'

Earliest attestation: The claim lives in the modern secondary literature and popular 'dark ages' narratives, not in any ancient source
Sources: evt-ibn-al-nafis-001: pulmonary circulation, 1242 — an anatomical claim Galen got wrong, corrected 300 years before Servetus; evt-al-zahrawi-001: ~200 illustrated surgical instruments, c. 1000 — the first illustrated surgical manual; evt-bimaristan-001: waqf-endowed hospitals, 872-1284 — no ancient parallel at this institutional scale; evt-al-jazari-001: crankshaft and camshaft, 1206 — rotary-to-reciprocating conversion, reconstructible from the diagrams
Falsifiability: It is already falsified: any ONE demonstrated original contribution collapses the 'nothing original' universal — this record carries four (pulmonary circulation, illustrated surgical instruments, the institutional hospital, the crankshaft/camshaft automata)</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1850-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>36.2765,33.5138,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1850</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Charles Morton invented the music hall' — the invention credit, corrected</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Theatres Trust — the institutional authority on British theatres — states it outright: Canterbury 'was not, as is often said, 'the first music hall' and Morton was not 'the inventor' of music-hall entertainment.' The song-and-supper rooms predate 1852; the 1852 building was 'a fairly typical pub concert room.'
Layer: fake · Place: Canterbury Hall, Upper Marsh, Lambeth, London, England (the telling's location)
Dates: 1852-05-17 → 1852-05-17 (confidence: medium)

Charles Morton invented the music hall — he was the inventor of music-hall entertainment as a form.

Earliest attestation: The invention credit is a later popular flattening; the Theatres Trust entry documents the correction with the full misdating history ('starting with a Select Committee report in 1866 and continuing to the latest edition of Grove's Dictionary of Music &amp; Musicians')
Sources: Theatres Trust theatrical database — Canterbury Hall: 'It was not, as is often said, 'the first music hall' and Morton was not 'the inventor' of music hall entertainment, but the second Canterbury was, beyond argument, the first of the giant grand music halls'; misdating history traced to the 1866 Select Committee report through Grove's Dictionary; BBC 'What a Performance!' — music hall episode: taproom concerts as background diversion in early-19th-century pubs; mid-1830s 'song and supper rooms' as established institutions before Morton
Falsifiability: Pre-1852 song-and-supper rooms are the falsifier that already exists: the invention credit requires nothing to have existed before the Canterbury; Evans's, the 'Free and Easys,' and the whole taproom-concert tradition did.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1852-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1136,51.4972,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1852</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth: Alexander Wood invented the hypodermic syringe (sole inventor, 1853)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The sole-inventor reading dies on three independent facts: (1) Charles-Gabriel Pravaz of Lyon built a working hypodermic syringe — graduated cylinder, screw piston, silver — in the same year, 1853, with no channel of transmission between the two men; (2) Francis Rynd's hollow-needle injection at Dublin's Meath Hospital came nine years earlier (3 June 1844, reported in the Dublin Medical Press, 12 March 1845 — 'the first known hypodermic injection'); (3) the deeper prior art — Anel's 1714 syringe, de Graaf's 17th-century metal syringe, Heron's ~180 BC piston-and-barrel syringe, Wren's 1656 quill-and-bladder injections. Wood's real, surviving contribution (the all-glass syringe, the 1855 neuralgia paper, subcutaneous morphine practice) is carried in the companion record — the lone-genius story is not.
Layer: fake · Place: Edinburgh, Scotland (Wood's practice, 19 Royal Circus)
Dates: 1853 → 1853 (confidence: high)

Alexander Wood was the sole inventor of the hypodermic syringe in 1853 — the lone genius of the glass tube.

Earliest attestation: Wikipedia, 'Alexander Wood (physician)': 'In 1853, he invented the first hypodermic needle that used a true syringe and hollow needle' — the telling being graded here
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Francis Rynd' — Dublin Medical Press 12 March 1845 report of the 3 June 1844 injection; 'the first known hypodermic injection'; MDDI Online, 'The Hypodermic Syringe: Greatest Medical Device of All Time?' — 'The first devices recognizable as hypodermic syringes were independently invented virtually simultaneously in 1853 by Scottish physician Alexander Wood and French surgeon Charles Gabriel Pravaz'; ThoughtCo, 'The History of Hypodermic Needles and Syringes' — Pravaz's screw mechanism and graduated syringe; Wood's all-glass syringe and 1855 neuralgia paper; The Free Library (anesthesia history), 'The early development of intravenous apparatus' — Pravaz 'widely but erroneously credited with the invention of the syringe'; Rynd's work; Wood's 1855 publication; Fordyce Barker 1856 US introduction; Wikipedia, 'Alexander Wood (physician)' — the sole-inventor telling being graded; Wood vs Hunter 'subcutaneous' vs 'hypodermic' dispute; Brunton 2000 priority paper
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that Pravaz's 1853 work was derivative of Wood's (a documented channel of transmission), or that Rynd's 1844 report was fabricated</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1853-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.1883,55.9533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1853</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Göbel's 1854 lamp — the priority claim the courtroom killed</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed by the courtroom it was tested in: in Edison Elec. Light Co. v. Beacon Vacuum Pump &amp; Elec. Co. (D. Mass., 1893), Judge Colt ruled it 'extremely improbable that Henry Goebel constructed a practical incandescent lamp in 1854,' noting such claims rest on recollection 'to be received with great caution.' Add the documentary gap — no contemporary 1854 record of any kind — and the anachronistic bamboo-filament detail, and the claim dies twice. The grade kills the 1854 priority telling, never the court's careful words and never the real priority trail (de Moleyns 1841, Starr, Swan).
Layer: fake · Place: New York, New York — the claimed site of the 1854 lamp (no contemporary site documentation)
Dates: 1854 → 1893 (confidence: medium)

Heinrich Göbel built a working incandescent electric lamp with a carbonized bamboo filament in New York in 1854 — beating Swan and Edison by decades.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary 1854 documentation; the claim rests on decades-later recollection and commemorative promotion. Earliest hard documentary engagement is the 1893 federal court record.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Göbel; en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Göbel
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1854 document (lab notebook, dated receipt, witnessed account) showing a working Göbel incandescent lamp — no such document has surfaced.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1854-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1854</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The anti-Stratfordian claim — 'Shakespeare didn't write Shakespeare'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills: (1) no contemporary ever expressed doubt and no contemporary document links any candidate to the authorship; (2) each leading candidate fails on the record — de Vere died in 1604 before The Tempest and other late plays, Marlowe died in 1593, and Meres (1598) lists de Vere and Shakespeare as separate writers; (3) at least 80 rival candidates cancel each other — every camp's proof is another camp's problem; (4) the whole thesis rests on argumentum ex silentio — absence of school records read as evidence of absence, a method scholars reject for every other period writer.
Layer: fake · Place: Stratford-upon-Avon, England (the claim is about Stratford; the evidence is London's archives)
Dates: 1856 → 2026 (confidence: high)

William Shakespeare of Stratford-upon-Avon did not write the plays and poems attributed to him; a hidden aristocrat or courtier (Francis Bacon, Christopher Marlowe, Edward de Vere, Mary Sidney, Queen Elizabeth I, or another candidate) wrote them and used his name as a front.

Earliest attestation: Delia Bacon, The Philosophy of the Plays of Shakespeare Unfolded (1856) — the first published alternative-authorship proposal
Sources: Delia Bacon, The Philosophy of the Plays of Shakespeare Unfolded (1856) — origin of the modern question; Wikipedia, 'Shakespeare authorship question' — documentary record, candidate problems, the 80-candidate count; New York Times survey of 265 American Shakespeare professors, 22 April 2007 — 6% 'yes' good reason to question, 11% 'possibly'; 61% 'a theory without convincing evidence', 32% 'a waste of time and classroom distraction'; Shakespeare Authorship Coalition, 'Declaration of Reasonable Doubt' — 3,348 signatories by 23 April 2016 (named fairly as the agnostic position); James Shapiro, Contested Will (2010) — the question as 19th-century invention
Falsifiability: Already falsifiable and falsified: a single contemporary link between any candidate and the plays would reopen the question — 170 years of searching has produced none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1856-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.71,52.19,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1856</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The crystal skulls (ancient-artifact claim)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Electron microscopy reads 19th–20th-century manufacture on every tested skull: rotary disc tools, carborundum (synthesized 1890s), and no provenance before Boban's 1880s shop.
Layer: fake · Place: Eugène Boban's Paris curio shop (first appearance); British Museum / Smithsonian (the lab verdicts)
Dates: 1860 → 1950 (confidence: high)

Life-size quartz crystal skulls are genuine pre-Columbian (Aztec or Maya) artifacts, thousands of years old.

Earliest attestation: The British Museum skull first appears 1881 in Eugène Boban's Paris shop catalogue — no crystal skull is referenced anywhere before the 1860s
Sources: Sax, Meeks, Collon &amp; Walsh, 'The origin of two purportedly pre-Columbian Mexican crystal skulls', J. Archaeol. Sci. 35 (May 2008): British Museum skull worked with a rotary disc tool and harsh abrasive (corundum/diamond); Smithsonian specimen with silicon carbide (carborundum, synthesized 1890s) — 'suggest[ed] ... made in the 1950s or later'; Jane MacLaren Walsh, Smithsonian — 2008 SEM examination of the Mitchell-Hedges skull: high-speed rotary tool marks; facial reconstruction (Gloria Nusse) showing European features; British Museum catalogue — the skull is 'probably European, 19th century AD' and 'not an authentic pre-Columbian artefact'; Sotheby's London 1943 — F.A. Mitchell-Hedges purchased the 'Skull of Doom' for £400 (not excavated at Lubaantun)
Falsifiability: A crystal skull excavated in a documented, undisturbed pre-Columbian context — or any large skull bearing only pre-industrial tool marks</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.35,48.86,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Edison made the first sound recording' — the misattributed superlative</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills. (1) The artifact: a dated 9 April 1860 phonautogram of a human voice singing 'Au Clair de la Lune' survives in the French Academy of Sciences / Paris patent-office papers — seventeen years before Edison's machine existed. (2) The patent: Scott's phonoautograph patent of 25 March 1857 (#17,897/31,470) predates the tin-foil phonograph's December 1877 build entirely. (3) The playback: in March 2008, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory's Carl Haber converted Scott's soot tracings into audible sound with a virtual stylus, independently confirming the sheet captured a human voice — a fact Scott himself never lived to hear. Edison's title as 'father of recorded sound' rests on the one thing he was first at: recording AND reproducing. The recording-only first is French, 1860, and unplayed for 148 years.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris, France (the 1860 phonautogram's archive)
Dates: 1860 → 2008 (confidence: high)

Thomas Edison made the first sound recording — the claim that the human voice was first captured in 1877 on Edison's tin-foil phonograph, and that Edison stands as the 'father of recorded sound' by virtue of the first recording.

Earliest attestation: The 9 April 1860 'Au Clair de la Lune' phonautogram, in Scott's own papers (dated by his hand)
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Phonautograph': 'Prior to this point, the earliest known record of a human voice was thought to be an 1877 phonograph recording by Thomas Edison' — the sentence the 2008 recovery rewrote; LiveScience — 'Edison Voice Recording Is Old, but Not Oldest': December 2007 discovery of the phonautograms in the Paris patent office by an American audio historian; the 1860 'Au Clair de la Lune' recording 'predat[es] Edison's recording by a full two decades' (New Atlas summary of the finding); New Atlas (28 March 2008) — 'Edison's first sound recording predated by 20 years': Scott's 1860 phonoautogram playback; Scott's 1878 self-published memoir railing against Edison for 'appropriating' his methods — 'the goal, Scott argued, was not sound reproduction, but "writing speech, which is what the word phonograph means"'; Symmetry Magazine (Dec 2009): 'Scott never intended to play back his recording... Edison would not invent the phonograph until 17 years later'
Falsifiability: A pre-1860 recognizable human-voice recording attributed to Edison (or anyone) would not save the claim — the claim is specifically that EDISON was first; only proof that the 1860 sheet is fake or misdated would reopen it</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mouhot 'discovered' Angkor Wat — the discovery reading</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) Angkor was never lost: the entire series of Angkor sites was always known to the Khmers, who continuously lived, worshipped, and farmed in and around the temple complexes — and Angkor Wat specifically stayed an active religious center into the present. (2) Westerners predated Mouhot by three centuries: the Portuguese trader Diogo do Couto wrote accounts c. 1550, the monk Antonio da Magdalena wrote of his visit in 1586, and Mouhot's own contemporary Father Bouillevaux published his account in 1858 after visiting at least five years before Mouhot's 1860 visit. The grade kills the 'discovery' reading, never the man's genuine achievement: Mouhot popularized Angkor in the West.
Layer: fake · Place: Angkor Wat — the temple whose 'discovery' is the claim
Dates: 1860 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Henri Mouhot, the French naturalist, 'discovered' Angkor Wat in 1860 — he was the first person to find the lost temple in the jungle.

Earliest attestation: Mouhot's posthumously published 'Travels in Siam, Cambodia and Laos' (1863–64); the 'discovery' reading is the Western reception tradition — modern scholarship explicitly flags it as 'mistakenly credited'. Mouhot himself acknowledged predecessors in his own journals.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Mouhot; rooneyarchive.net — 'In the footsteps of Henry Mouhot'; ecommons.cornell.edu — doctoral chapter on Mouhot (Bouillevaux citations); wondersofcambodia.com — 'Henri Mouhot and the Rediscovery of Angkor: Explorer, Myth and Legacy'
Falsifiability: Evidence that no Khmer knew Angkor's location and no Westerner visited before 1860 — impossible against the documented record: Khmer continuous knowledge, do Couto c. 1550, da Magdalena 1586, Bouillevaux's 1858 published account.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>103.867,13.4125,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The long-lived Pony Express (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills from the attested record: (1) DURATION — the service ran about eighteen months (3 April 1860 to 26 October 1861), not a long era; (2) the LONE RIDER — the system was a relay: stations every 10-15 miles for fresh horses, fresh riders every 75-100 miles, ~190 stations total; (3) the SUCCESS — it went bankrupt within eighteen months despite a heavy subsidy, never won the government postal contract it was built to chase, and was killed within days of the transcontinental telegraph's completion on 24 October 1861.
Layer: fake · Place: St. Joseph, Missouri (headquarters; the myth's eastern terminus)
Dates: 1860-04-03 → 1861-10-26 (confidence: high)

The popular image of the Pony Express as a long-running American mail institution — years or decades of lone heroic riders carrying the West's mail as a great national success — rather than what it was: an eighteen-month relay service that went bankrupt.

Earliest attestation: The myth's components postdate the service: romanticization documented in the decades after 1861 (Wild West show from 1883, dime-novel pipeline)
Sources: Pony Express service record: 18 months of operation, relay-station mechanics, bankruptcy, telegraph terminus — see evt-pony-express-1860-001; Buffalo Bill — Wikipedia (Cody's Pony Express claim; historians' difficulty documenting it; the parent-firm errand theory); Paul Fees (ret.) / True West Magazine, 'Did Buffalo Bill Cody Ever Ride for the Pony Express?' (the Gray debunk challenged: 'generally assumed, not without controversy'); Columbia Electronic Encyclopedia, 'Buffalo Bill' ('he claimed, apparently falsely, to have ridden for the pony express in 1860')
Falsifiability: Documentary evidence that the service ran for years beyond October 1861, or that the parent company profited from it — either would overturn this grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-94.85607,39.7666,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The storm glass predicts the weather (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Charles Tomlinson tested FitzRoy's claims over several months in 1862 and concluded 'all of the phenomena were caused by changes in temperature and nothing else' — 'the storm glass acts as a rude kind of thermometer' — and recommended the Meteorological Office abandon it, which it did. In 2008, Allan Mills (Leicester) and Kazushige Nagashima (Meiji University) independently confirmed the crystals respond to temperature and are pure camphor. Modern experiments consistently find the glasses 'aren't very accurate' as forecasters.
Layer: fake · Place: London (Meteorological Office / Tomlinson's tests)
Dates: 1860 → 2008 (confidence: medium)

The storm glass (the camphor-liquid 'FitzRoy storm barometer') predicts the weather — its crystallization patterns foretell storms, frost, and fair weather.

Earliest attestation: FitzRoy's documented weather-reading chart for the storm glass (clear liquid = clear weather, cloudy = cloudy, dots = humid/foggy, stars = thunderstorms, etc.), promoted in the 1860s
Sources: New Scientist, 'Storm warning: Can crystal-gazing predict the weather?': Tomlinson 'decided to test FitzRoy's claims scientifically. Over several months in 1862, Tomlinson recorded the response of a storm glass to the weather... he reasoned that all of the phenomena were caused by changes in temperature and nothing else. "It may be fairly concluded that the storm glass acts as a rude kind of thermometer," Tomlinson wrote... left him recommending that the instrument be abandoned by the Meteorological Office. Tomlinson carried the day.' Also: 2008, Allan Mills (Leicester) and Kazushige Nagashima (Meiji University) 'confirmed that the crystals in the glass respond to temperature' and 'proved the crystals were pure camphor.'; Leeds City Museums, 'For All Seasons' exhibition (Admiral FitzRoy barometer): the storm glass 'holds a mixture of different chemicals in water which were thought to change in appearance in anticipation of different weather patterns. However, most modern experiments have found that the glasses aren't very accurate.'; FitzRoy's storm-glass reading chart (via the Constantinople storm-glass manual, reproducing FitzRoy's documented patterns): clear = clear weather, cloudy = cloudy, dots = humid/foggy, stars = thunderstorms, flakes = overcast/snow, bottom crystals = frost, top threads = wind — the specific predictive claims Tomlinson tested
Falsifiability: A controlled trial showing storm-glass crystallization predicting weather changes independently of temperature — the exact trial Tomlinson ran in 1862, which found the opposite</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1860-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1860</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'states' rights, not slavery' cause claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The seceding states' own Declarations of Immediate Causes — adopted as formal state acts — name slavery and hostility to slavery as their reasons; South Carolina's (Dec 24, 1860) lists northern states' refusal to return fugitive slaves, attacks on slavery as sinful, abolition societies, and a president 'hostile to slavery'; Mississippi's (Jan 1861) opens 'Our position is thoroughly identified with the institution of slavery'; the Confederacy's own vice-president, Alexander Stephens, said slavery was 'the immediate cause of the late rupture and present revolution' (Savannah, March 21, 1861). The 1861 evidence contradicts the postwar slogan.
Layer: fake · Place: Charleston, South Carolina (the Secession Convention, December 1860)
Dates: 1861 → 1865 (confidence: high)

The American Civil War (1861-1865) was fought mainly over 'states' rights' and not mainly over slavery.

Earliest attestation: The postwar Lost Cause literature of the 1870s (e.g. the Southern Historical Society Papers, founded 1869) re-framed the cause as states' rights; the 1861 secession documents themselves say slavery.
Sources: South Carolina, Declaration of the Immediate Causes Which Induce and Justify the Secession of South Carolina from the Federal Union (adopted Dec 24, 1860); Mississippi, A Declaration of the Immediate Causes Which Induce and Justify the Secession of the State of Mississippi from the Federal Union (Jan 1861); Alexander H. Stephens, 'Cornerstone Speech,' Savannah, March 21, 1861 (reported in the Savannah Republican; text public domain 1861); Pew Research Center, 'Civil War at 150: Still Relevant, Still Divisive' (April 8, 2011; national survey March 30-April 3, 2011, n=1,507) — 48% said the war was mainly about states' rights, 38% mainly about slavery, 9% volunteered both equally; Charles B. Dew, Apostles of Disunion (UVA Press, 2001) — the secession commissioners' speeches; Dew documents his own change of mind from the states'-rights version he grew up on
Falsifiability: The secession declarations naming economic or constitutional causes unrelated to slavery as their stated reasons — the actual documents do the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1861-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.9311,32.7765,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1861</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The staged Gettysburg sharpshooter</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Forensic photo-historical demonstration (Frassanito, Gettysburg: A Journey in Time, 1975) that the corpse in 'Home of a Rebel Sharpshooter' is the same man photographed ~40 yards away in 'A Sharpshooter's Last Sleep'; the body was moved to the stone-wall enclosure, a prop rifle added. Minority dissents (Pougher 1988, Groves 1998) are on the record but have not displaced the mainstream reading. The 'where he fell' caption is false.
Layer: fake · Place: Devil's Den, Gettysburg battlefield, Pennsylvania
Dates: 1863 → 1975 (confidence: high)

Alexander Gardner's 'Home of a Rebel Sharpshooter' (Gettysburg, July 1863) is an unmanipulated documentary record: the dead Confederate soldier lies exactly where he fell.

Earliest attestation: The plate itself (July 1863) with Gardner's 1865 caption describing a Confederate sharpshooter who died a slow death thinking of family; Gardner also claimed the body and gun were still there when he returned in November 1863 — itself impossible, since no body lay unburied for months and no rifle would have escaped relic hunters.
Sources: William A. Frassanito, Gettysburg: A Journey in Time (1975) — six-plate analysis, blanket-drag route from the south edge of Devil's Den to the stone-wall enclosure; Frederic Ray, Civil War Times (1961) — first published suggestion that the same corpse appears in two locations; NARA / DOCSTEACH record for 'Home of a Rebel Sharpshooter': Gardner and O'Sullivan moved the corpse more than 40 yards to stage the exposure; Alexander Gardner, Photographic Sketch Book of the War (1865), vol. 1, plate 41 — the original caption; Richard D. Pougher (1988) — dissent via uniform/hair/facial analysis; James C. Groves (1998) — photographic analysis contradicting Frassanito
Falsifiability: Already falsified: the corpse is identifiable as the same man in six plates (facial features, clothing, the blanket-drag route); proving the caption true would require the body to have fallen in the stone enclosure and the rifle to be a sharpshooter's arm.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1863-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.24,39.81,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1863</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Bixby letter's premise: five sons lost</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Muster and service records show only two of the five sons were confirmed killed in battle; one was honorably discharged and the others deserted or died as prisoners — the Adjutant General's October 12, 1864 report, which the letter honestly quotes, was wrong.
Layer: fake · Place: Boston, Massachusetts
Dates: 1864 → 1864 (confidence: high)

Lydia Bixby, the Boston widow addressed in the November 21, 1864 condolence letter, had lost five sons fighting for the Union — the letter's own premise ('a statement of the Adjutant General of Massachusetts that you are the mother of five sons who have died gloriously on the field of battle').

Earliest attestation: William Schouler's report to the War Department, October 12, 1864 (forwarding Bixby's own claim of five sons dead).
Sources: William Schouler's report to the War Department, Oct 12, 1864; Michael Burlingame, research summarized in Smithsonian Magazine (2017): two sons died in battle, one honorably discharged, one deserted for certain, the fifth may have deserted; original letter destroyed by Bixby or discarded by the Transcript; Lapham's Quarterly: two sons killed in action, a third either deserted or died as a prisoner of war, a fourth honorably discharged, the fifth deserted; Baltimore Post-Examiner (2018): two killed, two deserted (one possibly to the Confederate Army), one honorably discharged
Falsifiability: Muster rolls showing all five Bixby sons killed in action</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1864-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1864</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Burlesque was always about stripping'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED on two independent lines: (1) the 1868-1890s record shows a literary-parody form — Thompson's Ixion was a parodied Greek myth with puns, travesty roles, and topical farce, attested by the contemporary New York Times report and Gänzl's 2002 scholarly biography; (2) the strip turn is documented and dated: the 1893 Midway's hootchy-kootchy, the Minsky brothers' 1917 Mae Dix 'accident' (Morton Minsky's memoir), and the scholarly consensus that striptease became the dominant ingredient only by the mid-1920s.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City, USA (the burlesque houses)
Dates: 1868-09-28 → 1925 (confidence: high)

American burlesque was, from its beginning, essentially the striptease — the 'always about stripping' telling.

Earliest attestation: The telling itself is a modern shorthand (textbook one-liners, trivia columns, the 1968 film 'The Night They Raided Minsky's'); no 19th-century source attests it.
Sources: Kurt Gänzl, 'Lydia Thompson: A Biography' (Routledge, 2002); Robert C. Allen, 'Horrible Prettiness: Burlesque and American Culture' (UNC Press, 1991) — the scholarly anatomy of the form; Morton Minsky &amp; Milt Machlin, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (Knopf, 1986) — the Mae Dix 1917 anecdote (single interested source, graded accordingly); Contemporary New York Times report on the British Blondes' sold-out 1868 opening (via the Opera Research Center reprint)
Falsifiability: A 19th-century burlesque house's books showing striptease as the billed center of the show before 1893, or a contemporary review describing systematic clothing-removal as the act in the 1868-1890 era.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The giant that was carved in Chicago — the Cardiff Giant hoax, 1868–1869</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed by the hoaxer's own confession plus the documented manufacturing chain: Hull noticed blue-streaked gypsum resembling veins in an Iowa quarry, had a twelve-foot slab cut and shipped to Chicago, had stone-cutters carve a giant 'died in great pain' (right hand clutching stomach), aged it chemically, buried it at his cousin's farm, and had well-diggers 'find' it on cue. When Barnum built a rival replica and Hannum sued, Hull confessed; the judge ruled both exhibits were fakes. Confession + paper trail + court ruling: the petrified-man reading is dead.
Layer: fake · Place: Cardiff, New York — the Newell farm where the gypsum figure was buried and 'discovered' in October 1869
Dates: 1868-01-01 → 1870-12-31 (confidence: high)

The Cardiff Giant — unearthed near Cardiff, New York, in October 1869 — was a genuine petrified prehistoric man (or an ancient statue of a giant).

Earliest attestation: The counter-attestation is the hoaxer's own: George Hull confessed to carving the 'giant' from a twelve-foot slab of Iowa gypsum, having it shaped by Chicago stone-cutters (Edward Burghardt and assistants, sworn to secrecy, needlepoint-mallet skin pores, chemically aged), shipping it to his cousin William Newell's farm near Cardiff, New York, burying it, and arranging its 'discovery' six months later. Hull's confession — made when banker David Hannum sued P. T. Barnum — is the hoax's death certificate.
Sources: BookBrowse, 'Why do we say There's a sucker born every minute?' — Hull's 1868 carving (Iowa gypsum, Chicago stone-cutters, needlepoint pores, chemical aging), the burial at William Newell's farm, the October 1869 'discovery,' the 25–50 cent admissions, the expert split; Medium / Skeptikai, 'The REAL Story Behind the Quote' — Hannum's purchase and Syracuse exhibit; Barnum's $50,000–$60,000 refused offers; Barnum's replica claimed as the true Giant; Hull's confession at trial; the judge's ruling that both were hoaxes
Falsifiability: Geological evidence that the Cardiff figure is fossilized tissue or a genuinely ancient artifact — against Hull's confession, the documented quarry-to-burial paper trail, and the court record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-76.08,42.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Jonas Hanway invented the umbrella' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the umbrella predates Hanway by millennia — royal parasols in ancient Assyria and Egypt (iconographic record, conventionally ~4,000 years), waterproof Chinese oil-paper umbrellas (conventionally 11th c. BCE), the Roman umbraculum; (2) even in England, umbrellas preceded him — the Female Tatler of 12 December 1709 advertises a coffee-house umbrella borrowed by a Customs House gentleman, and General Wolfe writing from Paris in 1752 wonders the practice does not obtain in England. Hanway's documented achievement was popularization, never invention.
Layer: fake · Place: London (the telling's claimed setting)
Dates: 1868 → present (confidence: high)

Jonas Hanway (1712-1786), the London merchant and philanthropist, invented the umbrella — the schoolbook-and-quiz telling that credits him with the umbrella's invention.

Earliest attestation: Popular biography/quiz shorthand, 19th-20th century (no 18th-century source)
Sources: 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Umbrella' (public domain): credits Hanway with having been 'the first Englishman who habitually carried an umbrella' — popularization, not invention; William Sangster, Umbrellas and Their History (1868; Project Gutenberg edition): Hanway's ~30 years of carrying (hence c. 1750), the derision, the hackney coachmen; Wolfe's 1752 Paris letter; The King's Parasol (aina.org, 2021): Egypt/Assyria origins, Chinese 11th-c.-BCE waterproof umbrellas, Hanway as popularizer ('men even called their umbrellas a Hanway'); acrosswalls.org Hanway notes: Female Tatler 12 Dec 1709 coffee-house umbrella announcement; John Macdonald's 1770 memoirs ('Frenchman, why don't you get a coach?')
Falsifiability: A credible 18th-century source attributing the umbrella's invention to Hanway, or proof that the ancient parasol records (Assyrian reliefs, Egyptian iconography, Chinese oil-paper umbrellas) are forgeries</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1868-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1868</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The line the showman never said — Barnum and 'a sucker born every minute'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills on the Barnum authorship. (1) THE BIOGRAPHER'S EMPTY SEARCH: Arthur H. Saxon — Barnum's own biographer, with every motive to confirm the line — tried to track down when Barnum said it and could not verify it; no contemporary account exists. (2) THE CHRONOLOGY: a variant of the saying was published in the European Magazine in January 1806, four years before Barnum was born. (3) THE COMPETING ATTRIBUTIONS: the Oxford Dictionary of American Quotations credits con man Joseph 'Paper Collar Joe' Bessimer; the common story credits banker David Hannum, stung by Barnum's rival Cardiff Giant. (4) THE INTERESTED WITNESS: the attribution to Barnum comes from circus competitor Adam Forepaugh's 1880s newspaper interview — a rival's discredit, not testimony. The line's own logic dies too: Barnum's business depended on patrons, not contempt for them.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City (Barnum's American Museum and the 1869–1870 Cardiff Giant affair); Syracuse, New York (Hannum's exhibit)
Dates: 1869-01-01 → 1891-04-07 (confidence: high)

P. T. Barnum said 'There's a sucker born every minute.' — the most famous sentence in show business, as his own philosophy.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary account exists of Barnum uttering the line — Barnum's biographer Arthur H. Saxon tried to track down an occasion and could not verify it: 'There's no contemporary account of it, or even any suggestion that the word "sucker" was used in the derogatory sense in his day. Barnum was just not the type to disparage his patrons.' The line first travels in late, second-hand attributions.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'There's a sucker born every minute' — 'a quotation often associated with American showman P. T. Barnum (1810–1891), although there is no evidence that he actually said it'; Saxon's failed verification ('no contemporary account of it'); Bessimer per Oxford Dictionary of American Quotations; Hannum version via the Cardiff Giant affair; Forepaugh's 1880s attribution as likely discredit; the January 1806 European Magazine variant ('there vash von fool born every minute'); The Independent (Lafayette), 'Too Good To Be True' — Hannum's Syracuse exhibit, Barnum's refused $50,000–$60,000 offers, the replica, the 1870 judicial ruling that both were hoaxes; BookBrowse, 'Why do we say There's a sucker born every minute?' — Hull's gypsum carving (1868), the buried 'discovery' (1869), the pore-chiseling and chemical aging, the 25-cent admissions, the expert split
Falsifiability: A contemporary 1810–1891 document — letter, newspaper, memoir — quoting Barnum saying the line. Biographer Saxon's exhaustive search found none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1869-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1869</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth: Wood's wife was the first morphine addict, killed by his syringe</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) Richard Davenport-Hines, The Pursuit of Oblivion (2003, p. 68), calls it a myth — 'she outlived him, and survived until 1895'; (2) the Dean Cemetery gravestone records Rebecca Massey Wood's death on 6 February 1895 — eleven years after Wood's own death on 26 February 1884. The 1869 overdose death is impossible on the stone's own dates. What survives the grade: the genuine 19th-century phenomenon of iatrogenic morphine dependence that Wood's 1855 paper helped make possible — and the real tragedy that the legend smuggles, minus the wrong woman.
Layer: fake · Place: Dean Cemetery, Edinburgh (Wood family grave)
Dates: 1869 → 1895 (confidence: high)

Rebecca Massey Wood — wife of Alexander Wood — was the first known intravenous morphine addict, and she died of a morphine overdose delivered by her husband's invention.

Earliest attestation: Wikipedia, 'Alexander Wood (physician)': 'It is, however, a false rumour that Rebecca Massey was the first known intravenous morphine addict and died of an overdose delivered by her husband's invention'
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Alexander Wood (physician)' — 'It is, however, a false rumour that Rebecca Massey was the first known intravenous morphine addict and died of an overdose delivered by her husband's invention; Richard Davenport-Hines says, "It is a myth: she outlived him, and survived until 1895."' — gravestone date 6 February 1895; Richard Davenport-Hines (2003). The Pursuit of Oblivion: A Global History of Narcotics. W.W. Norton, p. 68; C-SPAN3 Lectures in History, 'Drug Addiction in 19th-Century America' — the genuine 1840–1870 rise in opiate importation and the beginnings of hypodermic morphine use (the fenced real phenomenon)
Falsifiability: A contemporary death certificate or burial record showing Rebecca Massey Wood died in 1869 of a morphine overdose</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1869-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.1883,55.9533,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1869</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The lawless Wild West (constant gunfights)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The towns' own recorded body counts are two orders of magnitude below the myth — Dodge City averaged fewer than two violent deaths per year across its entire 1876-1886 cattle-trading period, 45 homicides total across five towns in fifteen years — and the myth's manufacture is itself documented: a paid Police Gazette sensationalist, magazine mythmaking from 1902, and Hollywood.
Layer: fake · Place: Dodge City, Kansas (control sample; Abilene, Ellsworth, Caldwell, Wichita as the comparison set)
Dates: 1870 → 1890 (confidence: high)

The frontier towns of the American West, c. 1870-1890, were lawless places where gunfights in the streets were a daily or near-daily occurrence — the Hollywood version: every disagreement settled at high noon.

Earliest attestation: Harry Gryden's sensationalist dispatches from Dodge City to the National Police Gazette, c. 1878-1883 (myth construction); the underlying reality is attested in the towns' own records and contemporary newspapers (see evt-cattle-towns-bodycount-001)
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine, 'How Dodge City Became a Symbol of Frontier Lawlessness' (2018); Robert R. Dykstra, The Cattle Towns (Knopf, 1968; repr. Univ. of Nebraska Press, 1983): 45 homicides in 15 years across five Kansas cattle towns; Marshall Trimble (Arizona's official historian), 'Violence in Old West Towns,' True West magazine: Dodge City 5 killings in its most homicidal year; Deadwood 4; Tombstone 5; Tombstone's gun ordinance; Terry L. Anderson and Peter J. Hill, The Not So Wild, Wild West (Stanford UP, 2004): property-rights and private-order institutions in the West
Falsifiability: A documented town where coroners' ledgers, newspapers, and court records show dozens of gunfight deaths per year across multiple years — the paper trail Dykstra found to be absent.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1870-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-100.017,37.7528,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1870</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The foreign builders of Great Zimbabwe — the colonial myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent refutations (Randall-MacIver 1905 artifact analysis; Caton-Thompson 1929–31 stratigraphy) plus the Rhodesian establishment's documented 1970s censorship regime — museum staff instructed to withhold the African-origin evidence, Garlake and Summers driven out — because the archaeology would not cooperate; no credible archaeological support for the foreign reading has ever existed.
Layer: fake · Place: Great Zimbabwe, Masvingo, Zimbabwe
Dates: 1871 → 1980 (confidence: medium)

Great Zimbabwe was built by non-African foreigners: Phoenicians, Arabs, the Queen of Sheba's people from the biblical Ophir, Egyptians, Vikings, Atlanteans — 'more civilized races' than the Africans who lived around it.

Earliest attestation: Carl Mauch 1871 (the 'Ophir' visit); formalized by James Theodore Bent, The Ruined Cities of Mashonaland (1892), and Hall &amp; Neal, The Ancient Ruins of Rhodesia (1902)
Sources: Bent 1891 excavation report (British Association, Rhodes-funded): the 'bastard race' reading — white northern fathers, destroyed stratigraphy around the conical tower, discarded Arab/Persian trade beads; Hall &amp; Neal 1902: Phoenician/Arabian authorship as the official colonial version; Hall's 1–3 m of discarded 'primitive' deposits (he was dismissed for the damage); David Randall-MacIver, Medieval Rhodesia (1905): the first refutation — 'unquestionably African in every detail' — rejected by the colonial establishment; Gertrude Caton-Thompson, The Zimbabwe Culture: Ruins and Reactions (1931): stratigraphic excavation proving medieval Shona-culture construction; The 1970s Rhodesian censorship regime (Paul Sinclair testimony, 'None But Ourselves'; SA History Online): the Museums and Monuments director told archaeologists to withhold the African-origin evidence from the press; guidebooks, displays, and textbooks censored; Peter Garlake and Roger Summers departed — the myth's official version needed the state's help; Wikipedia 'Archaeology and racism' / Great Zimbabwe historiography summaries: the post-1980 archaeological consensus (Shona origin unchallenged)
Falsifiability: Pre-11th-century sealed contexts with non-African construction technology or inscriptions; none have been found in 130+ years of excavation</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1871-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.9331,-20.2731,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1871</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The O'Leary cow that started the Great Chicago Fire (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 219th DEBUNKED family. Three kills: (1) the story's own author, reporter Michael Ahern of The Chicago Republican, admitted more than 20 years later that he had concocted the cow tale because it made for a better story (reported Chicago Tribune, 1996); (2) the 1871 Board of Police and Fire Commissioners' investigation could not determine the cause — and the O'Learys testified under oath they were asleep when the fire broke out; (3) the Chicago City Council passed a resolution in October 1997 officially exonerating Catherine O'Leary and her cow. Historians (Ann Durkin Keating, North Central College) note the telling caught on through anti-Irish, anti-immigrant sentiment — a convenient scapegoat: Irish, Catholic, immigrant, a woman.
Layer: fake · Place: The O'Leary barn, DeKoven Street, Chicago (the fire's origin point, per the 1871 Board)
Dates: 1871 → 1997 (confidence: high)

Mrs. Catherine O'Leary's cow kicked over a lantern while being milked in the O'Leary barn on the night of 8 October 1871, starting the Great Chicago Fire.

Earliest attestation: Chicago newspaper coverage, October 1871 — first reported by Michael Ahern of The Chicago Republican
Sources: Chicago Tribune, 9 Nov 1996 (ct-xpm-1997-10-07-9710080245 trail): Michael Ahern, reporter for The Chicago Republican, admitted more than 20 years after the fire that he concocted the cow tale because it made for a better story; Ann Durkin Keating on the anti-Irish/anti-immigrant scapegoating; Chicago City Council resolution, October 1997 — officially exonerated Mrs. O'Leary and her cow (Chicago Tribune ct-xpm-1997-10-07-9710080245); 1871 Board of Police and Fire Commissioners investigation report — cause could not be determined; the O'Learys testified under oath they were asleep; Richard Bales, 'The Great Chicago Fire and the Myth of Mrs. O'Leary's Cow' — the Sullivan theory and the myth's historiography; herhalfofhistory.com, 8 Oct 2026: the commissioner's report blamed poor building codes and insufficient fire-department funds; Catherine O'Leary's testimony ('I always milked my cows by daylight and never had a lamp of any kind or a candle about the barn'); she died 1895 of pneumonia, ~68; Parade, Oct 2026: the 1997 exoneration; Catherine O'Leary died 1895 without living to see her name cleared
Falsifiability: The claim is killed by its own author's confession plus the official record; the fire's true cause remains undetermined — the grade kills the cow, not the uncertainty</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1871-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6469,41.8688,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1871</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The gladiatorial 'thumbs down' death signal</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No Roman text specifies the direction of 'pollice verso' (Juvenal, Prudentius, Martial are all directionally silent), and the only surviving physical depiction of a gladiatorial thumb gesture shows the MERCY signal (pressed thumb, Cavillargues medallion). The modern thumbs-down=death mapping traces to Gérôme's 1872 painting, was contested in print within seven years (1879 pamphlet), and was re-imported by Gladiator (2000) — a 19th-century invention, not a Roman record.
Layer: fake · Place: The Colosseum (Flavian Amphitheatre), Rome
Dates: 1872 → 2000 (confidence: high)

In the Roman arena, the crowd signaled death for a defeated gladiator with a thumbs-down gesture (thumbs-up meaning spare him).

Earliest attestation: The popular up/down mapping originates with Jean-Léon Gérôme's 1872 painting 'Pollice Verso'; the phrase 'pollice verso' itself is Roman (Juvenal Satires 3.36, Prudentius Contra Symmachum 2.1096).
Sources: Juvenal, Satires 3.36-38 ('verso pollice') — the thumb is 'turned', direction unspecified; Prudentius, Contra Symmachum 2.1096 ('converso pollice') — direction unspecified; Cavillargues medallion (Nîmes) — 'STANTES MISSI', shows the mercy gesture: closed fist with thumb pressed down; the only surviving physical evidence of a gladiatorial thumb gesture; Anthony Corbeill, Nature Embodied: Gesture in Ancient Rome (Princeton UP, 2004) — the death signal was most likely a thumb turned UP or outward (a sword thrust); mercy a thumb pressed down on a closed fist; Gérôme, 'Pollice Verso' (1872; Phoenix Art Museum) — sold to Alexander Turney Stewart for 80,000 francs, exhibited in New York; the source of the modern up=life/down=death mapping; 1879 pamphlet 'Pollice Verso': To the Lovers of Truth in Classic Art (26 pages), incl. Gérôme's 8 Dec 1878 letter — the painting's accuracy was publicly contested immediately; Gladiator (2000, dir. Ridley Scott) — re-manufactured the gesture, with Commodus's thumbs-up sparing and thumbs-down killing, the Gérôme mapping again; Wikipedia, 'Pollice verso' — summary of the textual silence and the painting's cultural transmission
Falsifiability: A Roman-period depiction of the death gesture showing a downward thumb, or a text explicitly specifying a downward motion for the kill signal</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1872-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.4922,41.8902,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1872</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Troy II as Homer's Troy — Schliemann's 'burnt city'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The stratigraphy is decisive: Troy II's burnt destruction dates c. 2300 BCE — over a thousand years before the Trojan War era — while the candidate Homeric layers (VI–VIIa, c. 1700–1180 BCE, VIIa showing violent destruction c. 1180 BCE) sit several layers above it. Schliemann's fixation on the bottom of the mound led him to dig through and destroy much of the real candidate layer on his way down.
Layer: fake · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: 1872 → 1890 (confidence: high)

Troy II — Schliemann's 'burnt city,' the layer from which 'Priam's Treasure' came — is the Troy of Homer's Iliad, the city besieged in the Trojan War.

Earliest attestation: Schliemann's reports from 1872 onward — his own identification, never shared by the stratigraphy.
Sources: Archaeology.org, Interactive Map of Troy (Troy II, ca. 2550–2300 BCE) — 'destroyed by a fire around 2300 B.C.'; treasures 'more than 1,000 years before the supposed events of the Trojan War'; Smithsonian, 'In Search of Troy' — the city of Homer's epic 'is thought to correspond to Troy VI-VIIa'; National Geographic — 'Homer's Troy, circa 1200 B.C., probably corresponds to layer VIIa, nowhere near the bottom'; Schliemann never recorded strata; Popular Archaeology interview — scholars split Troy VIh vs VIIa as 'Homer's' city (both c. 13th century BCE); Schliemann 're-found and mostly destroyed... Homer's imaginary Troy, not the Trojan War'; Smithsonian — Schliemann's methods 'savage and brutal': a 45-foot trench plowed through layers 'without proper record keeping'; Turkish archaeologists are still repairing the damage
Falsifiability: A Late Bronze Age date for the Troy II destruction horizon — radiocarbon and the ceramic sequence both forbid it</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1872-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1872</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Priam's Treasure' — Schliemann's 1873 gold hoard</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) STRATIGRAPHY — the treasure's layer (Troy II) dates c. 2400 BCE, roughly a thousand years before the Trojan War era, so it cannot be Priam's; (2) FRAUD — Traill (Anatolian Studies 2000) showed the 'single find' was an artificial composite assembled from several sources, with falsified findspot and date, and the Sophia-present/Sophia's-shawl stories were Schliemann's own inventions.
Layer: fake · Place: Hisarlik (Troy), Tevfikiye, Çanakkale Province, Turkey
Dates: 1873 → 1873 (confidence: high)

The gold hoard Heinrich Schliemann reported discovering on 31 May 1873 at Troy is the personal treasure of Homer's King Priam — one single find, in the ruins of Homeric Troy, photographed on Sophia Schliemann and smuggled out in her shawl.

Earliest attestation: Schliemann's own reports (from 1874) — self-attested. No independent contemporary witness to the single-find story exists.
Sources: Traill, David (2000), '"Priam's Treasure": clearly a composite', Anatolian Studies 50:17–35 — the hoard is an artificial collection from several sources, not a single find; findspot and discovery date falsified; some photographed pieces appear in 1872 photos of earlier finds; Easton, D.F. (1998), 'Heinrich Schliemann: Hero or Fraud?', The Classical World 91(5):335–343; Archaeology.org, Interactive Map of Troy (Troy II, ca. 2550–2300 BCE) — the Troy II treasures 'date to around 2400 B.C., more than 1,000 years before the supposed events of the Trojan War' and 'cannot possibly be associated with any of Homer's heroes'; Smithsonian, 'In Search of Troy' — the Troy II layer dated 2500–2300 BCE, 'about 1,000 years earlier than the agreed upon time of Homeric Troy'; the treasure is 4,000+ years old goldwork; Wikipedia, 'Priam's Treasure' — the artefacts are 'likely to be a composite of multiple finds across the site rather than one hoard'; Schliemann falsified the finding circumstances; the Sophia's-shawl smuggling story was planted by Schliemann himself (citing Allen 1999); Smithsonian/National Geographic — Schliemann smuggled the treasure out of Ottoman territory; the Ottoman government sued; he paid a 10,000 gold-franc fine and kept digging
Falsifiability: An independent contemporary document confirming the single 31 May 1873 find in a Late Bronze Age context — none exists; Traill's 1872 photographs of the same pieces would need explaining</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1873-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>26.2392,39.9575,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1873</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alexander Graham Bell alone invented the telephone (the sole-inventor myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills on the 'alone': (1) Johann Philipp Reis publicly demonstrated his 'Telephon' transmitting speech in 1861 — fifteen years before Bell's patent (LoC: 'transmitted speech (albeit badly)... first time the term was used'); (2) Antonio Meucci demonstrated his teletrofono in New York in 1860 and filed a US patent caveat Dec 28, 1871, lapsed 1874 for the $10 renewal — recognized by H. Res. 269 (2002); (3) Elisha Gray's lawyer filed a telephone caveat on the SAME day as Bell's application, Feb 14, 1876, both built around a liquid transmitter. The telephone had multiple fathers; the patent had one winner.
Layer: fake · Place: Boston, Massachusetts (the myth's home city)
Dates: 1876-03-07 → 1876-03-07 (confidence: high)

That Alexander Graham Bell alone invented the telephone — the single-inventor reading of the telephone's origin carried by textbooks and popular history.

Earliest attestation: The sole-inventor reading consolidated in the Bell Company's own histories and the schoolbook tradition after the patent wars; the competing inventors were in the record from the start (Reis 1861, Meucci 1871 caveat, Gray 1876 caveat).
Sources: Library of Congress, 'Who is credited with inventing the telephone?' — Bell credited 'since his patent and demonstrations... were successful' AND 'his invention did not come to be in isolation. There are many others who are often put forward as co-inventors, such as Antonio Meucci, Johann Reis and Elisha Gray.'; Wikipedia, 'Elisha Gray and Alexander Bell telephone controversy' — the Feb 14, 1876 same-day filings; Bell's application 5th entry, Gray's caveat 39th; both documents incorporating a liquid transmitter.; U.S. House of Representatives, H. Res. 269 (107th Congress), passed June 11, 2002 — 'the life and achievements of Antonio Meucci should be recognized, and his work in the invention of the telephone should be acknowledged'; preamble: 'if Meucci had been able to pay the $10 fee to maintain the caveat after 1874, no patent could have been issued to Bell.'; HISTORY.com, 'First speech transmitted by telephone' (March 10, 1876) — Bell's 1875 harmonic-telegraph work as the origin of the telephone ideas; the June 1875 tests.
Falsifiability: Period evidence that Reis never demonstrated a working voice-transmission device before 1876, that Meucci's teletrofono never existed, AND that Gray's caveat was not filed Feb 14, 1876 — all three kills would have to fall — would reopen this record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.0589,42.3601,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Mask of Agamemnon' (Mycenae, 1876)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The mask is a genuine 16th-century BCE gold artifact — but it dates 300 to 400 years before the Trojan War era, so it cannot be Agamemnon's. The telegram story's own provenance is disputed (reported as 'supposed to have told'; one account calls it a press invention), and Schliemann himself later conceded the attribution ('let's call him Schulze').
Layer: fake · Place: Mycenae, Argolis, Greece (Grave Circle A)
Dates: 1876 → 1876 (confidence: high)

The gold funeral mask found at Mycenae in 1876 is the death mask of Homer's King Agamemnon — the face Schliemann claimed in his telegram to have gazed upon.

Earliest attestation: Schliemann's identification at discovery, 1876. The telegram story itself ('I have gazed upon the face of Agamemnon') is attributed — reported as 'is supposed to have told' and described elsewhere as a press invention.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Mask of Agamemnon' — 'modern archaeological research suggests that the mask dates to around the 16th century BC, pre-dating the period of the mythical Trojan War by 300–400 years'; found 1876 in Grave V, Grave Circle A; 'He is supposed to have told the king in a telegraph, "I have gazed upon the face of Agamemnon"'; Schliemann's late-life concession: 'So this is not Agamemnon... All right, let's call him Schulze'; Brewminate, 'The Legendary (or Not) Ancient Funerary Mask of Agamemnon' — 'the mask dates to about 1600 BC, predating the period of the legendary Trojan War by about 400 years'; Perceptivetravel / Tony Perrottet — the telegram story: 'Unfortunately, the story was invented by the press'
Falsifiability: A secure c. 1200 BCE date for the mask, or a contemporary inscription naming Agamemnon — neither exists</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>25.6645,37.7308,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'An electrical toy' — the Western Union dismissal quote (misquotation)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two kills from the archival record itself: (1) NO PERIOD ATTESTATION — the scholarly account of the 1876 decision (Carlson, citing the Hubbard Papers and Watson) contains no such wording; the quote has no primary-source lineage anywhere in the telephone literature — it exists only as unsourced pop-history embroidery, typically traceable to the Wikipedia-era Bell article ('the president of Western Union balked, countering that the telephone was nothing but a toy'); (2) the documented reasoning contradicts the caricature — Orton's actual documented logic (telephone's 20-mile range, one wire per conversation, mismatch with WU's short-business-message model) was specific and businesslike, not a sneer. The companion 'two years later he said $25 million would be a bargain' line rides the same unsourced rail and is fenced with it.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City (the attributed scene)
Dates: 1876-10-01 → 1876-12-31 (confidence: high)

That Western Union president William Orton, on being offered Bell's telephone patent in 1876, dismissed it with the words 'an electrical toy' (in some tellings 'nothing but a toy') — the famous attributed quote of the Western Union telephone story.

Earliest attestation: The wording circulates in modern pop retellings, commonly sourced (explicitly or implicitly) to the Wikipedia article on Alexander Graham Bell and its derivatives — not to any 1876 document, letter, memo, or contemporary report
Sources: W. Bernard Carlson, 'Entrepreneurship in the Early Development of the Telephone,' Business and Economic History v23 n2, pp. 161-192 — the scholarly reconstruction of the 1876 decision; contains the documented business logic and NO 'toy' wording; Pop retelling exemplars carrying the unattributed quote (the belief ecology): rismedia 'You've Come a Long Way Baby' (explicitly sourcing Wikipedia's Bell article: 'the president of Western Union balked, countering that the telephone was nothing but a toy. Two years later, he told colleagues that if he could get the patent for $25 million he'd consider it a bargain')
Falsifiability: A period document — an 1876 letter, memo, or contemporary report — carrying the 'electrical toy' / 'nothing but a toy' wording from Orton or a Western Union decision-maker would overturn this grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1876-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1876</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The name "Silk Road" (and the single-road idea)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The name is a documented 1877 German coinage; no pre-1877 source uses any general system name for the routes, and the routes themselves were a braided network, not one road.
Layer: fake · Place: Berlin (publication place of Richthofen's China vol. 1, 1877)
Dates: 1877 → 1877 (confidence: high)

The "Silk Road" is a single ancient trade road whose name dates to antiquity — the ancients knew it as one road under that name.

Earliest attestation: Ferdinand von Richthofen, "China: Ergebnisse eigener Reisen und darauf gegründeter Studien", vol. 1 (Berlin: Dietrich Reimer, 1877), and his 1877 journal article on the Tarim-basin trade routes.
Sources: Richthofen, China vol. 1 (1877) — four uses of Seidenstrasse/Seidenstrassen; plus the 1877 article (English versions 1878 in Geographical Magazine and Popular Science Monthly); D. C. Waugh, "Richthofen's 'Silk Roads': Toward the Archaeology of a Concept" (2007) — the coinage, the limited use, the later appropriation; Marinus of Tyre via Maes Titianus (1st c. CE), preserved in Ptolemy — the ancient route description Richthofen mapped (untitled); no ancient name for the system as a whole; Valerie Hansen, The Silk Road: A New History (2012) — many routes, not one road
Falsifiability: A pre-1877 text using 'Silk Road'/Seidenstrasse (or a calque) as a general system name would falsify the coinage; an ancient administrative or legal text naming the whole network as one entity would falsify the no-ancient-name leg</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1877-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.4,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1877</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The QWERTY slowdown myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) no contemporary source attributes slowdown intent to Sholes — Yasuoka &amp; Yasuoka (2011), working from his patents and typewritten letters, conclude he 'never intended to slow down the operator'; (2) the internal logic fails — the most common English digraphs T+H and E+R sit adjacent in QWERTY, and a 1949 statistical analysis found QWERTY has more close letter-pairs than a random layout; (3) the arrangement demonstrably emerged from operator feedback — the first professional users were telegraph operators who found the alphabetical layout unworkable, and the keys moved in response to how the machines were actually used.
Layer: fake · Place: Milwaukee, Wisconsin (geography of the claim's subject — the 1870s design work)
Dates: 1878 → 2011 (confidence: medium)

The QWERTY keyboard layout was deliberately designed to slow typists down, so that the typewriter's typebars would not jam.

Earliest attestation: 20th-century popular histories of the typewriter (no Sholes-era source states or implies slowdown as a design goal)
Sources: Yasuoka, Koichi &amp; Yasuoka, Motoko (2011). 'On the Prehistory of QWERTY.' ZINBUN 42:161-174 — 'We don't agree it. Christopher Latham Sholes... did not arrange the typebars in such way. And he never intended to slow down the operator.'; New Scientist, 'The truth about the QWERTY keyboard' — T+H and E+R adjacency; 1949 statistical analysis (QWERTY has more close pairs than random); 'TYPE WRITER' top-row salesman story has no historical evidence; Smithsonian Institution (via secondary reporting) — the mechanics-drove-design account questioned; QWERTY emerged from how the first typewriters were used
Falsifiability: A Sholes-era document — letter, patent text, or workshop record — stating that slowing the operator was a design goal</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1878-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.9065,43.0389,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1878</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison 'invented' the light bulb — the solo-inventor reading</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The first-inventor reading is killed by the patent record it claims to rest on: Frederick de Moleyns was granted the first incandescent-lamp patent in 1841 (forty years before Edison's); John W. Starr got a US carbon-filament patent in 1845; and Joseph Swan demonstrated working carbon-filament lamps in the 1860s and was granted a UK patent in 1878, demonstrating publicly in February 1879 — months before Edison's Oct 1879 test. Edison sued Swan for infringement and ended by merging with him (Edison &amp; Swan United, 1883) — you do not merge with a man whose patent you can beat. The grade kills the solo-inventor reading; the Menlo Park system (evt-edison-menlo-park-system-001) and Swan's lamp (evt-swan-incandescent-lamp-001) survive it untouched.
Layer: fake · Place: Menlo Park, New Jersey — the laboratory where the legend was written, not where the lamp began
Dates: 1879 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Thomas Edison invented the (incandescent) light bulb — he was the first person to create a working electric incandescent lamp.

Earliest attestation: Popular encyclopedia and textbook treatments crowning Edison sole inventor, consolidated after the 1883 Edison-Swan merger and his 20th-century publicist era; the 'sole inventor' form is a later simplification, not a period claim.
Sources: legaleraonline.com — 'Whose Electric Bulb Is It Anyway?' (patent-by-patent history); famousinventors.info — Joseph Swan biography; Smithsonian Institution (demo date); Science Museum Group (Edison-Swan United); scribd.com document 631252062 — History of the Incandescent Light Bulb (timeline); scribd.com — History of the Incandescent Light Bulb; I.E.E.E.-reprinted 1979 Technology &amp; Culture centennial reassessment (via iarchive)
Falsifiability: A documented pre-1879 working incandescent lamp by another inventor; satisfied by de Moleyns's 1841 patent, Starr's 1845 patent, and Swan's 1860 lamps and 1878 patent.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1879-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.2341,40.7839,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1879</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison 'invented' the light bulb — the solo-inventor reading (evidence chain corrected)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The first-inventor reading is killed by the paper trail: Frederick de Moleyns was granted the first incandescent-lamp patent in 1841 (evt-de-moleyns-1841-patent-001); John W. Starr documented carbon-filament lamps in the 1840s; Joseph Swan demonstrated working carbon-filament lamps publicly on 18 December 1878, 17 January 1879, and 3 February 1879 — months before Edison's October 1879 test. Mindful of Swan's dated evidence of prior research and publication, the British Edison Company dropped its infringement suit and negotiated a merger instead (Edison &amp; Swan United, 1883, reported in the Manchester Guardian 3 Oct 1883). The grade kills the solo-inventor reading; the Menlo Park system (evt-edison-menlo-park-system-001) and Swan's lamp (evt-swan-incandescent-lamp-002) survive it untouched. Supersedes evt-edison-invented-lightbulb-001, whose Swan evidence carried the imprecise '1878 patent' shorthand (corrected: British Patent No. 4933 granted 27 Nov 1880).
Layer: fake · Place: Menlo Park, New Jersey — the laboratory where the legend was written, not where the lamp began
Dates: 1879 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Thomas Edison invented the (incandescent) light bulb — he was the first person to create a working electric incandescent lamp.

Earliest attestation: Popular encyclopedia and textbook treatments crowning Edison sole inventor, consolidated after the 1883 Edison-Swan merger and his 20th-century publicist era; the 'sole inventor' form is a later simplification, not a period claim.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Swan; gatesheadhistory.com/joseph-swan.html; centaur.reading.ac.uk/79610/1/Fundamental patents.pdf (Arapostathis &amp; Gooday, Patently Contestable, MIT Press 2013); centaur.reading.ac.uk/79610/1/Fundamental patents.pdf; Science 1979 vol 204 no 4388 (centennial reassessment, via iarchive.299792458.su); legaleraonline.com — 'Whose Electric Bulb Is It Anyway?'; patents.google.com/patent/US223898A/en; ipwatchdog.com (Edison patent history); d1dagyb1ctngx1.cloudfront.net/news/uk/1350225/inventions-ignorant-creators-henry-ford-thomas-edison
Falsifiability: A documented pre-1879 working incandescent lamp by another inventor; satisfied by de Moleyns's 1841 patent (evt-de-moleyns-1841-patent-001), Starr's 1845 work, and Swan's 1878–1879 dated public demonstrations (evt-swan-incandescent-lamp-002).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1879-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.2341,40.7839,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1879</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The "America built the Panama Canal alone" myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the purchase — the U.S. bought the defunct French company's works and assets outright ($40M to the French company, $10M to Panama), inheriting the excavated cut, the Panama Railroad, and the plant; (2) the hands — the American-era digging army was overwhelmingly West Indian (Barbados, Jamaica, Martinique, Guadeloupe); the silver roll bore the work and the dying (5,609 dead, only 350 white); (3) the design — the lock canal the Americans built was the conversion the French had already adopted in October 1887.
Layer: fake · Place: Isthmus of Panama (Colon → Panama City)
Dates: 1882 → 1914 (confidence: high)

The United States built the Panama Canal from scratch and on its own — the canal was an entirely American achievement, with no foreign effort before it and American hands doing the digging.

Earliest attestation: The American-era popular narrative, 1904–1914 onward (textbook shorthand, Roosevelt-era triumphalism); the corrective documentary record is continuous from the French period.
Sources: MIT Project Evaluation paper (US $10M to Panama + $40M to the French company; US construction cost $352M; 5,609 dead 1904–1914); Smithsonian 1898 exhibition: U.S.-directed construction; crews from the West Indies began 1904, carving 100M m³ of rock and soil; History of the Panama Canal survey (1888 French workforce ~20,000, nine-tenths Afro-Caribbean; the sea-level-to-locks conversion October 1887); Canal Zone labor-history survey: gold roll exclusively white, silver roll mainly Black Caribbean migrants; official toll 5,609, only 350 white
Falsifiability: Records showing the French excavation was trivial or unusable (it was used — the Culebra cut and Gatun/Limón infrastructure carried forward), or U.S. payrolls showing an American-majority digging force (they show the opposite)</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1882-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.68,9.15,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1882</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The 1884 conference created the world's time zones'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed three ways: (1) the published resolutions draw no time zones at all — Resolution II adopts a meridian, Resolution IV recommends a universal day 'for purposes for which it may be found convenient,' and every resolution is framed as a proposal to governments, with no binding force; (2) the American zones predated the conference by a year — US railroads adopted standard time at noon on 18 November 1883, eleven months before the delegates met; (3) national adoption took decades afterward: France kept Paris Mean Time until 1891 and only aligned with Greenwich (minus 9 minutes 21 seconds, without naming it) in 1911 — 27 years later. No one drew the zones in Washington; everybody drew them at home, slowly.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington, D.C. (the conference that did not draw them)
Dates: 1884-10 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The 1884 International Meridian Conference created the world's time zones in a single meeting — that 41 delegates in Washington drew the zone system the world now uses.

Earliest attestation: The shorthand is the popular schoolbook version; the conference's own resolutions (Final Act, October 1884) are the contemporary refutation — no time zones appear in them
Sources: Published proceedings, Final Act (Gutenberg eBook 17759): Resolutions I–VII — no time-zone scheme, no binding act; Resolution IV's own wording limits the universal day to purposes 'for which it may be found convenient'; Library of Congress, In Custodia Legis: 'Whose Time is it Anyway? A Brief History of Standardized Time Zones in the United States' — General Time Convention, Grand Pacific Hotel Chicago, 11 October 1883; railroad clocks set to the new standard at noon on 18 November 1883; 'The Day of Two Noons'; five zones (Intercolonial, Eastern, Central, Mountain, Pacific); Wikipedia, 'Railway time': standard railway time introduced at noon 18 November 1883; incorporated into US federal law only in 1918 (Standard Time Act, 19 March 1918). France: Paris Mean Time 1891; 1911 adopted Paris Mean Time delayed 9 min 21 s (equal to GMT without mentioning Greenwich). Germany: uniform time reckoning law 1 April 1893.
Falsifiability: Would require the conference's resolutions to include a time-zone scheme with country-by-country adoption, or a binding act of law — the published proceedings show neither.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1884-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1884</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Waterman invented the fountain pen' — the myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two kills: (1) Petrache Poenaru registered a French patent for a self-inking portable pen on 25 May 1827 — 57 years before Waterman's; the first patent for a pen holding its own ink supply is Poenaru's, not Waterman's. (2) Waterman's own 1884 patent was for the 'three fissure feed' — a capillary-action feed that solved leaking — fitted onto pens of Frank Holland's abandoned 1883 company that Waterman took over; his patent is an improvement filing, not an invention filing.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City, USA — where Waterman's pen company was founded
Dates: 1884 → 1884 (confidence: high)

Lewis Waterman invented the fountain pen in 1884.

Earliest attestation: The telling circulates in modern exam banks and trivia (e.g. testbook.com Kolkata Police Constable 2012 exam: 'Fountain Pen was invented by — Lewis E. Waterman'); pen-trade sites (ksgills.com 'Lewis Waterman – Inventor of Fountain Pen')
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Lewis Waterman' — 'It was for this "three fissure feed" which his first pen-related patent was granted in 1884'; Holland 1883 takeover context; Antique Trader, 'Putting pen to paper with distinction' — 'The first patent for a pen holding its own ink supply was issued in 1827 to Romanian Petrache Poenaru'; Waterman granted a patent 12 February 1884 for his 'Fountain Pen'; describes his capillary air-channel feed; Wikipedia, 'Petrache Poenaru' — French Ministry of the Interior registration 25 May 1827, code 3208, 'plume portable sans fin, qui s'alimente elle-meme avec de l'ancre' (citing 'Brevets d'Invention Nr. 3208' PDF, 25 May 1827); testbook.com exam bank (Kolkata Police Constable 2012): 'Fountain Pen was invented by — Lewis E. Waterman' — the belief ecology: the telling as a test answer; ksgills.com, 'Lewis Waterman – Inventor of Fountain Pen' — the pen-trade telling in its bald form
Falsifiability: A pre-1884 patent or grant for a self-inking fountain pen stronger than Poenaru's 1827 brevet would deepen the kill, not weaken it; the grade only falls if the 1827 Poenaru patent is proven a fabrication and no other pre-1884 self-inking pen can be documented</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1884-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1884</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Soennecken invented the hole punch' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Benjamin Smith's 1885 conductor's punch — a single-hole punch with a confetti collector, documented in USPTO-linked reporting — predates Soennecken's 14 November 1886 Papierlocher filing. The first-inventor claim fails on the calendar.
Layer: fake · Place: Bonn, Germany (Soennecken's city; the telling attaches to the German patent tradition)
Dates: 1885 → 2026 (confidence: low)

Friedrich Soennecken invented the hole punch in 1886 — he was the first inventor of the device.

Earliest attestation: The telling is modern trivia transmission; the counter-evidence is contemporary: Smith's 1885 conductor's punch (documented in USPTO-linked technology reporting) and Soennecken's 1886 German patent filing.
Sources: Inverse, 'Hole Punch History: The Brilliantly Simple Mechanism' (14 Nov 2017) — 'the conductor's punch invented by Benjamin Smith in 1885... Smith might actually have beat Soennecken to the punch'; Inverse, 'Hole Punch History: Why Google Marked the 131st Anniversary With This Doodle' — Smith's 1885 Massachusetts conductor's punch for train conductors, with the confetti collector
Falsifiability: A dated document showing Soennecken's punch in use or filed before Smith's 1885 device, or proof the conductor's punch never existed</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1885-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>7.0982,50.7374,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1885</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Henry Ford invented the automobile' — the misattributed superlative</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills. (1) The patent: Benz's DRP 37435, filed 29 January 1886 for a purpose-built gasoline automobile, predates Ford's first car by a decade and is inscribed in UNESCO's Memory of the World register as the automobile's 'birth certificate'. (2) The road test: Bertha Benz's August 1888 Mannheim–Pforzheim drive (~104 km, the Wiesloch pharmacy as the first filling station) proved the automobile a decade before Ford's Quadricycle. (3) The inventor's own testimony: Ford stated 'I invented nothing new' — the credit belongs to his system of mass production, not to the machine. The 1911 Ford v. Selden decision (2d Cir.) further documents a working American automobile industry before and independent of Ford's company. The Model T's real achievement — chassis assembly time cut from 12h08m to 93 minutes, price from $825 (1908) to $260 (1924) — is untouched by the debunk; it was mass production, never invention.
Layer: fake · Place: Berlin, Germany (filing location of DRP 37435)
Dates: 1886 → 1913 (confidence: high)

Henry Ford invented the automobile — the claim that the automobile originates with Ford's work (the Ford Quadricycle of 1896, the Ford Motor Company of 1903, or the Model T of 1908) and that Ford is the automobile's inventor.

Earliest attestation: Deutsches Reichspatent DRP 37435, filed by Carl Benz at the Imperial Patent Office, Berlin, 29 January 1886
Sources: Mercedes-Benz Public Archive / UNESCO Memory of the World — Benz &amp; Cie. Patentschrift DRP 37435, 'vehicle with gas engine operation', filed 29 January 1886; inscribed in the Memory of the World register from 2011; patent described as the 'birth certificate of the automobile'; Germany Travel / Wikipedia 'Bertha Benz Memorial Route' — 5 August 1888 (early August 1888) drive Mannheim to Pforzheim, ~104 km, with sons Eugen (15) and Richard (14); ligroin bought at the Stadt-Apotheke Wiesloch (the world's first filling station); chain repaired by a smith in Bruchsal; brake linings replaced; fuel line cleared with a hatpin; telegram from Pforzheim on arrival; Detroit Historical Society — Model T introduced 1 October 1908; Highland Park plant opened 1 January 1910; moving assembly line 1913 (chassis assembly 12h08m to 93 minutes); price $850 in 1909 to $260 in 1924; 'any color as long as it's black' true only after 1913; Ford's own statement (via Entrepreneur, attributed): 'I invented nothing new. I simply assembled into a car the discoveries of other men behind whom were centuries of work' — Ford's self-attributed account rejects the invention credit; 2d Cir. 196 F. 615 (Ford v. Electric Vehicle Co. / Selden patent), decision 9 January 1911 — Selden patent held valid only for Brayton-type engines; documents a working American automobile industry independent of Ford (BPM Legal summary of patent 549,160, filed 8 May 1879, issued 5 November 1895)
Falsifiability: Proof that Benz's patent is forged or misdated AND that no working gasoline automobile predates Ford's 1896 Quadricycle — including Daimler's 1886 motor carriage and de Rivaz's 1807 engine — would reopen the claim</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1886-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.405,52.52,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1886</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The obituary that supposedly created the Nobel Prize</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the known Figaro record's actual text is milder and lacks the 'merchant of death' passage — the version that supposedly horrified him is unattested; (2) seven and a half years separate the notice from the will, and no statement by Nobel ever links them; (3) the documented influence is Bertha von Suttner's pacifism, and the story's paper trail runs to Halasz 1959, not to 1888.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris
Dates: 1888-04-15 → 1895-11-27 (confidence: low)

Alfred Nobel established the Nobel Prizes because he read the mistaken 1888 obituary and was horrified at the 'merchant of death' reputation it promised him — the obituary shock caused the 1895 will.

Earliest attestation: No primary source from Nobel links the obituary to the will; the story's popular form traces to Nicholas Halasz, 'Nobel: a Biography' (1959), filling a silence — Nobel left no explanation for the bequest.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Alfred Nobel' — 'historians have been unable to verify it, and some dismiss the story as a myth'; Weird Science History, 'Did a Premature Obituary Inspire the Nobel Prize?' (2026-07-28) — 'no one has ever produced the newspaper'; the Juliette Adam-Lambert letter; Britannica and Nobel Foundation outreach printed the story; Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Alfred Nobel' — 'there is abundant evidence that his friendship with the prominent Austrian pacifist Bertha von Suttner inspired him to establish the prize for peace' (the documented alternative inspiration)
Falsifiability: A diary entry, letter, or contemporary witness quoting Nobel tying the will to the obituary would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1888-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1888</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Ferris invented the Ferris wheel' myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED: rotating passenger wheels predate Ferris by centuries. 'Pleasure wheels' — chairs suspended from large wooden rings turned by strong men — are documented in 17th-century Bulgaria: Peter Mundy's Travels (1620, Philippopolis) describes children riding 'like a Craine wheele,' always sitting upright; Pietro Della Valle rode one at a Constantinople Ramadan festival in 1615. Similar wheels appeared in 17th-century England and across India, Romania, and Siberia. Antonio Manguino built a wooden pleasure wheel in Walton Spring, Georgia, in 1848. William Somers built a 50-foot wooden wheel in Atlantic City in 1891, received the first US 'Roundabout' patent in 1893, and sued Ferris for infringement — the case was dismissed because Ferris's steel, steam-powered, 2,160-passenger machine differed categorically from Somers's wooden wheel. Ferris invented the monumental Ferris wheel, not the concept. The name is his; the idea is ancient.
Layer: fake · Place: Chicago, Illinois, USA (World's Columbian Exposition)
Dates: 1893-06-21 → 1893-06-21 (confidence: high)

George Washington Gale Ferris Jr. invented the Ferris wheel — the rotating passenger wheel was his original invention for the 1893 World's Columbian Exposition.

Earliest attestation: Popular telling, late 19th century onward
Sources: Wikipedia 'Ferris wheel' (Early history): Mundy 1620, Della Valle 1615, the 17th-century English wheels, Manguino 1848, Somers 1891 and the dismissed 1893 infringement suit.; Timeless Patents (History of the Ferris Wheel): the 17th–18th century Bulgarian/Russian timber wheels ('Russian swings'); the early-19th-century European fair wheels; the categorical engineering gap between the predecessors and Ferris's steel machine.; The Free Library (Ferris Wheels: An Illustrated History — Norman Anderson, 1992): pleasure wheels 'reaching back some two thousand years'; the 1893 wheel as 'the star of the Columbian Exposition.'
Falsifiability: Documented earlier passenger wheels</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.6298,41.8781,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Edwin Seibels invented the vertical filing cabinet in 1893"</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The solo-inventor telling dies four ways: Seibels's own prototype dates to 1898, not 1893; the Library Bureau's Chicago office built a prototype for Dr. Rosenau in 1892 — six years earlier; the patent office denied Seibels (his application described 'a system that was an idea, not a device'); and the scholarly verdict is at least two inventors, likely more unrecorded.
Layer: fake · Place: Chicago, Illinois (the 1892 Library Bureau prototype — the evidence that predates the claim)
Dates: 1893 → 1893 (confidence: traditional)

Edwin Seibels, an insurance salesman, invented the vertical filing cabinet in 1893 — first and alone.

Earliest attestation: 20th/21st-century popular histories, textbooks, and homework sites; Seibels's own 1898-prototype story (a later date than the telling claims).
Sources: Craig Robertson, The Filing Cabinet: A Vertical History of Information (University of Minnesota Press, 2021) — emergence in 1891; at least two inventors; Rosenau 1892 prototype; Seibels 1898 prototype; patent denied as 'an idea, not a device'; News@Northeastern interview with Craig Robertson (Oct 2021) — Dewey among contributors; emergence 1891; Wikipedia, 'Library Bureau' — 'In the 1890s, the Library Bureau introduced vertical filing'; Catherine Insler, 'The Day We Turned Paper Sideways' (Medium, Jan 2026) — belief ecology: 'In 1893, a young insurance man named Edwin Seibels was drowning in paper... created the world's first Vertical File'; University lecture notes, PSYC305A (2008) — belief ecology: 'The vertical filing system was invented in 1898 by Edwin G. Seibels (1866-1954). One of his original filing cabinets is in the Smithsonian museum.' (the Smithsonian conflation noted in the record)
Falsifiability: A dated 1893 Seibels prototype or filing predating the 1892 Rosenau/Library Bureau prototype would reopen the priority question.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-87.63,41.878,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'America never adopted the metric system': the myth that mistakes the road signs for the statute books</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed one way, conclusively, on the law: the Mendenhall Order of 1893 made the international meter and kilogram the fundamental standards of the United States — NIST's own words: 'Our customary measurements — the foot, pound, quart, etc. — have been defined in relation to the meter and the kilogram ever since.' The United States signed the Treaty of the Metre (Metric Convention) in 1875, received national prototype meters Nos. 27 and 21 in 1890, and in 1959 agreed with five other nations to define the inch as exactly 2.54 centimeters — a definition written in metric. 'The U.S. never adopted metric' confuses the commercial system on the road signs with the legal system in the statute books; the road signs are honestly fenced here, and the statute books say otherwise.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (a legal-fact claim, not a place-bound event)
Dates: 1893 → present (confidence: high)

The United States has NEVER adopted the metric system — that metric units have never been the legally fundamental measurement standards of the country.

Earliest attestation: The shorthand version of the belief is standard textbook and journalistic framing; its strongest documentary form is the 'three countries' claim (U.S., Liberia, Myanmar) repeated in metric-advocacy journalism (e.g., The Collegian, 2026). The legal refutation is the Mendenhall Order text itself, 1893.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Mendenhall Order': 1 yard = 3600/3937 meter; 1 pound = 0.4535924277 kilogram; the order 'amounted to a formal announcement of a change that had already occurred in practice.'; NIST Letter Circular 1136 (1997 revision): 'In 1893, metric standards... were adopted as the fundamental standards for length and mass in the United States. Our customary measurements... have been defined in relation to the meter and the kilogram ever since.'; The Collegian (2026): 'There are three countries in the world that do not formally use the metric system: Liberia, Myanmar and the United States' — and concedes the counter-argument: 'One could try to make the argument that the United States has technically implemented the metric system already — we've defined our units of measurement in terms of the meter since the Mendenhall Order of 1893. But that's not quite the same as common everyday usage.' Also carries: the 1975 Metric Conversion Act (voluntary), the 1988 Omnibus Act (metric 'the Preferred system'), the 1971 'A Metric America' report's ten-year plan.; Popular Science (Jason Schneider, June 2016): the history of the failed conversions — Gallup polling showed 45 percent of Americans opposed the metric switch in the late-1960s debate; the 1968 three-year study recommended a ten-year plan that Congress declined to make mandatory.
Falsifiability: The myth would stand if U.S. customary units were still legally defined by non-metric artifacts (the bronze yard, the mint pound). They are not: every customary unit is legally a defined fraction or multiple of an SI unit (the international yard and pound agreement, 1959, set 1 inch = 2.54 cm exactly; NIST states customary units have been defined in relation to the meter and kilogram since 1893).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1893-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0369,38.9072,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1893</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Dreyfus guilty verdict: the bordereau was Esterhazy's, the evidence was forged</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the handwriting attribution was formally transferred to Esterhazy — Picquart's 1896 identification (petit bleu telegram, handwriting comparison) became one of the Court of Cassation's two stated 'new facts' in 1906; (2) the army's own counterintelligence officer, Colonel Hubert-Joseph Henry, manufactured forged evidence to support the conviction, confessed when exposed (30 August 1898), and killed himself the next day — the case's own builder admitted it was built on forgery; (3) the French state's highest court annulled the conviction on 12 July 1906. The 1899 Rennes retrial's guilty-with-extenuating-circumstances verdict is carried in competing hypotheses as the counterweight; it was itself the product of military pressure, and it was the verdict the 1906 court erased.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris, France
Dates: 1894-09-01 → 1906-07-12 (confidence: high)

Captain Alfred Dreyfus was guilty of the treason for which he was convicted on 22 December 1894 — that he personally authored the bordereau (the torn-up memorandum found in the German military attache's wastebasket) offering French military secrets to Germany.

Earliest attestation: The 1894 court-martial record (the guilty verdict itself, 22 December 1894); refuted by the Court of Cassation, 12 July 1906.
Sources: Court of Cassation, 12 July 1906 decision: annulment grounded on (1) the bordereau's attribution to Esterhazy and (2) the secret communication of the 'canaille de D...' document; history.com 'What Was the Dreyfus Affair': Henry 'admitted that he had forged much of the evidence against Dreyfus and then Henry committed suicide'; 1906 exoneration; Wikipedia 'Resolution of the Dreyfus affair': the two 'new facts' (Esterhazy attribution; secret communication); Rennes guilty verdict with extenuating circumstances, 9 September 1899; Wikipedia 'Dreyfus affair': Esterhazy court-martial acquitted unanimously January 1898 after minutes of deliberation; Zola's J'Accuse in response
Falsifiability: An authenticated Dreyfus-authored copy of the bordereau, or forensic proof that the Esterhazy attribution is itself mistaken.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1894-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1894</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Edison 'invented the movies' — the solo-inventor reading</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The invention has too many fathers for one grave. W.K.L. Dickson, not Edison, did most of the 1889–1892 Kinetoscope development (Eastman Museum, 2021); the Kinetoscope was a single-viewer peep-show, and Edison initially disdained projection as financially nonviable — the shared-experience cinema came from the Lumières' 28 December 1895 paid screening (VERIFIED, evt-lumiere-first-screening-001); and motion photography predates Edison's lab entirely (Le Prince 1888, Marey 1882, Muybridge 1878). The grade kills the sole-inventor reading only — never the genuine Edison/Dickson system, never the commercial parlor achievement.
Layer: fake · Place: West Orange, New Jersey — the laboratory where the legend was written
Dates: 1894 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Thomas Edison invented motion pictures — he was the sole inventor of the movies.

Earliest attestation: American popular and educational treatments crediting Edison as inventor, consolidated during his publicist-era dominance; the singular-inventor form is a later simplification, not a period claim.
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A single documented origin point with Edison as sole inventor — instead the record shows Louis Le Prince's Roundhay Garden Scene (1888), Marey's chronophotography (1882), Anschütz's Schnellseher (1891), and the Lumière public projection (1895), any one of which breaks the monopoly.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1894-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.2341,40.7839,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1894</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Einstein failed math' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED because the original documents show the opposite. Einstein's 1896 Matura certificate records 6s — the HIGHEST mark — in Algebra, Geometry, Descriptive Geometry, and Physics (5s in German, Italian, Chemistry, Natural History; a 3 in French). By age 15 he had mastered differential and integral calculus. The 'failure' is a two-part transmission accident: (1) the Aargau Cantonal School changed its grading scale mid-career — under the old system 1 was the highest and 6 the lowest; under the new, 6 was the highest — so biographers reading his grades as '1s turning into 6s' got the meaning exactly backwards; (2) he did fail the October 1895 ETH entrance exam — but in languages, botany, and zoology, not mathematics. Einstein himself refuted the story in letters and interviews: he never failed mathematics. The grade kills the myth, never the man's real struggles — French was his worst subject (3), and he was a rebellious, often frustrated student.
Layer: fake · Place: Aarau, Switzerland (Aargau Cantonal School); Zurich (ETH)
Dates: 1895 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Albert Einstein failed mathematics in school — he was a poor student of math as a child.

Earliest attestation: The misreading: Einstein's Aarau report cards showing grades of '1' (best under the old scale) and later '6' (best under the new scale) — read backwards by biographers who mixed the two scales. Popularized via Ripley's Believe It or Not (1935) and the comfort-myth circuit.
Sources: ETH-Bibliothek (Zurich) via ETH history holdings, as cited in 2026 analysis of the 1896 Matura certificate: final grades — Algebra 6, Geometry 6, Descriptive Geometry 6, Physics 6 (6 = highest under the new scale); 5s in German, Italian, Chemistry, Natural History; 4s in Geography, Art and Technical Drawing; 3 in French; factually.co fact-check (2026), 'What were Albert Einstein's actual grades in mathematics?': Aarau report cards show 6s in Algebra, Geometry, Descriptive Geometry, Physics; calculus mastery before age 15; the failed October 1895 entrance exam concerned botany, zoology, and languages, not mathematics; Historical analysis of the Aargau Cantonal School grading change: under the earlier system 1 was the highest grade and 6 the lowest; under the new system 6 was highest and 1 lowest — Einstein's algebra/geometry grades went from 1 to 6 because the scale flipped, not because his performance collapsed; ww2.jacksonms.gov article, 'Did Albert Einstein Fail Math': Einstein's documented excellence in mathematics and physics; Einstein refuted the myth in later letters and interviews, stating he never failed mathematics
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous school report in which Albert Einstein received a failing grade in a mathematics subject.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>8.05,47.39,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Cinema is an invention without a future' — the Louis Lumière attribution</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent scholarly kills on the Louis-attribution. (1) The 'jeune homme' absurdity: James Leo Cahill's October 109 note — Louis Lumière (born 1864) was YOUNGER than Georges Méliès (born 1861); 'jeune homme' from Louis to Méliès makes no sense. (2) The absent Louis: Cahill again — Louis was not at the screening Méliès purportedly attended, so a direct address is unlikely 'even if the transaction occurred through the postal system.' (3) The father's claim: Tom Gunning, quoting Bernard Chardère, attributes the sentiment to Antoine Lumière — the attribution to Louis is, in Cahill's word, a 'misattribution.' The grade kills the Louis-to-Méliès utterance as documented fact, never the commercial pessimism itself (the Lumières' 1905 return to photography corroborates the mood) and never the Antoine hypothesis, which stays CLAIMED.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris — the legend's claimed scene
Dates: 1895 → 1965 (confidence: medium)

Louis Lumière told Georges Méliès that the cinema was 'an invention without a future' (the famous 1895–96 warning, usually in the form: 'Jeune homme, remerciez-moi. Mon invention n'est pas à vendre... elle n'a aucun avenir commercial').

Earliest attestation: Maurice Bessy &amp; Lo Duca, Georges Méliès: Mage (1945) — fifty years after the event; C.W. Ceram, Archaeology of the Cinema (1965) — the full 'Jeune homme' form, seventy years late.
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1895–96) source recording Louis Lumière saying it — none exists in the film-studies literature despite the quote's canonization.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3303,48.8719,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The train panic — audiences fled the Lumière screening in terror</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) The wrong program: the train film was not in the 28 December 1895 program (the original program lists ten other films; it was first projected in January 1896, per the IAFOR paper and the film's own trivia record — sources disagree on whether it was filmed in summer 1895 (IMDb trivia) or 1896 (the IAFOR paper), but all agree it was absent from the December program; the third version dates to summer 1897). The panic story's claimed setting never happened. (2) The empty contemporary record: no major newspaper covered the December 1895 screening at all, and no 1895–96 account reports flight — the flight reports begin with Schönwald in 1916, twenty years late, and the mainstream historiography (the Loire–Péardinger chapter) notes film historians repeat the claim 'without examination,' providing 'neither evidence nor even references to contemporary sources.' The grade kills the panic and its placement, never the genuine astonishment — de Parville's captivated neighbor, Gorky's 1896 'plunge into the darkness' prose, and Gunning's roller-coaster thrill all survive untouched.
Layer: fake · Place: Salon Indien du Grand Café, Paris — the legend's claimed scene
Dates: 1895 → 1916 (confidence: high)

When the Lumières projected 'L'Arrivée d'un train en gare de La Ciotat,' the audience panicked — leapt up, ducked, fled the room in terror at the train rushing toward them (the founding legend of cinema's power, usually dated to the 28 December 1895 premiere).

Earliest attestation: Post-war film histories (Sadoul 1959 et al.) — all decades after the event. No newspaper or eyewitness account from 1895–96 reports any flight.
Sources: 
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1895–96) report of audience flight at a train-film projection — none exists despite the IAFOR paper's and Workers' Liberty's surveys of the record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1895-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3303,48.8719,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1895</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Spanish mine that never was — 'Remember the Maine, to hell with Spain'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills: the 1898 board itself could not name any responsible person; no evidence of Spanish involvement exists after 128 years; Spain had no motive and its own commission read the explosion as internal; the 1976 Rickover engineering investigation — the US Navy's own — concluded an internal coal-bunker fire was the most probable cause.
Layer: fake · Place: Havana Harbor, Cuba
Dates: 1898-02-15 → 1898-02-15 (confidence: high)

Spain — a Spanish-placed mine, a Spanish-ordered sabotage — destroyed the battleship USS Maine in Havana Harbor on 15 February 1898, and the 'Remember the Maine — to hell with Spain!' war cry named the true culprit.

Earliest attestation: The 1898 American press cycle ('Spanish Treachery!' headlines; Hearst's New York Journal offering a reward for the 'Perpetrator of the Maine Outrage') and the wartime slogan itself — attestation of the belief, not of the fact.
Sources: Louis Fisher, 'Destruction of the Maine (1898)' (Law Library of Congress): the 1898 board 'was unable to obtain evidence fixing the responsibility for the destruction of the MAINE upon any person or persons'; Spain's commission, denied access to the wreck, concluded an internal explosion; the board ignored known spontaneous-combustion incidents on other US ships; Adm. Hyman G. Rickover, 'How the Battleship Maine Was Destroyed' (1976, reissued 1995): Navy engineering investigation concluding an internal coal-bunker fire — not a mine — was the most probable cause of the magazine explosion; NHHC (DVIDS, 125th anniversary, 2023): 'While the cause of the great tragedy remains unsettled, contemporary American popular opinion blamed Spain, and the Spanish-American War followed' — the Navy's own institution distinguishes the event from the attribution; Smithsonian Magazine (Tom Miller, Feb 1998): the Rickover team's finding that the demise was self-inflicted, likely a coal bunker fire; the external-blast position surviving only as 'there are those who still maintain it'
Falsifiability: This claim has already met its falsifiers: (1) no evidence of Spanish involvement has surfaced in 128 years — no Spanish mine, no Spanish order, nothing in Spanish archives; (2) Spain had every reason to avoid the war it was about to lose, and its own commission — though denied access to the wreck — concluded an internal explosion; (3) the 1976 Navy engineering investigation found an internal coal-bunker fire the most probable cause; (4) even the 1898 board that the war cry leaned on could not fix responsibility on any person.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1898-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-82.35,23.14,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1898</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The invention of bushido</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The coherent 'ancient code' has no premodern documentary existence: the term is scarcely used before the modern period (Friday 2007), and the canonical text was written in English in 1900 to make the samurai legible to Western readers; the modern invention is documented in Benesch 2014.
Layer: fake · Place: Tokyo — the samurai tradition the doctrine claims to describe
Dates: 1899 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Bushido was the ancient, timeless code of honor followed by the samurai class throughout Japan's feudal history.

Earliest attestation: Nitobe Inazō, Bushido: The Soul of Japan — written in English for Western readers, published 1899/1900
Sources: Oleg Benesch, Inventing the Way of the Samurai: Nationalism, Internationalism, and Bushido in Modern Japan (Oxford University Press, 2014, Past &amp; Present series) — the modern bushido tradition as a Meiji-and-later invention; Nitobe's 1900 English book as the tradition's founding act; Karl Friday (2007), cited via IJAPS 2011 — 'Japanese scarcely used the term Bushidō until the modern period, and there was little written discussion in premodern Japanese times as to what constituted the way of the warrior'; Jayson Chun, 'Bushido: The Last Samurai' (IJAPS Vol. 7 No. 3, 2011) — Nitobe a pacifist Quaker; the warrior code propagated by the Meiji government through education and mass media as a national myth to teach commoners 'the supposed militaristic values of the samurai'; Nitobe Inazō, Bushido: The Soul of Japan (1900) — the founding text itself, written in English for the West; seven virtues organized to parallel European chivalry, Stoicism, and Christian ethics (belief-ecology: Theodore Roosevelt bought dozens of copies)
Falsifiability: A pre-1868 Japanese text naming and expounding 'bushido' as a coherent samurai-wide code</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1899-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>139.6503,35.6762,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1899</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The "Conrad Hubert invented the flashlight" telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) U.S. Patent 617,592 for the tubular hand-held flashlight was issued 10 January 1899 to David Misell, filed 12 March 1898 and assigned to Hubert's company; (2) Hubert's own documented role is buyer/marketer — he purchased Misell's patents, workshop, and the unfinished tubular design in 1897-98 and commercialized the device.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City
Dates: 1899 → 1899 (confidence: low)

Conrad Hubert invented the flashlight.

Earliest attestation: Modern reference-work convention crediting Hubert; contradicted by the primary patent record.
Sources: U.S. Patent 617,592 ('electric device'), issued to David Misell, 10 January 1899, filed 12 March 1898; assigned to the American Electrical Novelty and Manufacturing Company; Wikipedia, 'Eveready Battery Company': 'On January 10, 1899, the company obtained U.S. Patent No. 617,592 (filed March 12, 1898) from David Misell, an inventor... Misell, the inventor of the tubular hand-held "electric device" (flashlight), assigned his invention over to Hubert's company.'; ThoughtCo, 'The Invention of the Flashlight': 'While Conrad Hubert might have known the flashlight was a bright idea, it was not his. British inventor David Misell... patented the original flashlight and sold those patent rights to the Eveready Battery Company.'; Hubert met Misell in 1897 and bought his patents, workshop, and the unfinished tubular design.; Discover Magazine, 'They Invented it': Misell 'assigned his patents to his employer, Conrad Hubert'; with Misell's help Hubert developed the first tubular flashlight.
Falsifiability: A Hubert-signed patent, dated workshop drawing, or contemporary document showing the tubular design in Hubert's hand before Misell's 1898 application would restore the claim.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1899-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1899</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Johan Vaaler invented the paper clip' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 222nd DEBUNKED family. Two kills. (1) The Gem design predates and outlives Vaaler: the loop-within-a-loop paper clip (torsion principle) was in widespread use by 1900, never patented as a clip, and was the product of the Gem Manufacturing Co. in England (Design News; Scientific American). William Middlebrook's 1899 machine patent (US 636,272, Waterbury CT) illustrates a perfectly proportioned Gem — the invention predates Vaaler's filing in commerce, whatever the patent office shows. (2) Vaaler's design was NOT the Gem: it lacked the critical second loop — the papers were inserted by lifting the outer wire so the rest of the clip stood out at roughly ninety degrees, held by wire flexibility alone, poorly. It was never manufactured or sold, and his patents expired (Gizmodo; War History Online). Vaaler did not patent in his native Norway at all. The 1989 seven-meter Sandvika monument and the commemorative stamp honor Vaaler but both depict the Gem design, not his — the memorials themselves document the confusion.
Layer: fake · Place: Sandvika, Norway (the 1989 monument site; Vaaler was an Oslo clerk)
Dates: 1899 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Johan Vaaler, a Norwegian inventor, invented the paper clip — filing his patent in 1899 and receiving German (DRP 121,067) and US patents in 1901 — and the modern paper clip is his invention.

Earliest attestation: Vaaler's patent documents (German DRP 121,067; US patent), 1901
Sources: Gizmodo, 'How the Humble Paperclip Became a Secret World War II Symbol': patents granted Germany + US 1901, none in Norway; Vaaler's design lacked the second loop, stood out at ~90°, held poorly, never manufactured, never sold; encyclopedias falsely credit him; the 23-foot 1989 statue and stamp depict the Gem, not his design; War History Online, 'Paperclip, WWII Resistance Mark': Vaaler's patents 1901 (Germany, US), not Norway; single-loop design lacked the second vital loop; never manufactured, patents expired; encyclopedias credit him falsely; monument + stamp depict the Gem; Design News, 'Lessons From Paper Clips': the classic loop-within-a-loop clip known as the Gem after the late-19th-century stationery firm Gem Ltd.; because no patent was taken out, other manufacturers copied and sold it; Scientific American, 'The Paper Clip' (via Early Office Museum): Gem-type paper clip in use by ~1892, never patented; Henry Petroski on the right wire + wire-bending machinery as preconditions; Answers.com Q&amp;A page (accessed Oct 2026): 'The paperclip was invented in 1899 by Johan Vaaler. He received an American patent in 1901' — the misconception's canonical school-answer form, carried as belief ecology, not evidence
Falsifiability: Evidence that Vaaler's design was manufactured and sold before the Gem's documented trade use, or a patent showing Vaaler invented the loop-within-a-loop design before the Gem appeared in commerce.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1899-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>10.525,59.895,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1899</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Cows lying down means rain (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Met Office: 'there is no scientific evidence for it at all.' BBC Science Focus: 'there is no scientific evidence for this piece of weather folklore' — cows spend up to half their time lying down (rest, cud-chewing), so there is a 50:50 chance of seeing them down at any moment, rain or shine; herd mimicry makes whole-herd lying look meaningful. Live Science and Discover concur: no scientific evidence. The 2013 Arizona/Northern Missouri study found only a temperature link (cows stand in heat to cool, lie down in cold) — cold often precedes rain, but that is not rain-sensing and not the claim as stated.
Layer: fake · Place: England (UK Met Office survey — over 60% of the British public believe it)
Dates: 1900 → 2026 (confidence: low)

Cows lying down in a field is a sure sign that rain is on the way — cattle can sense approaching rain and lie down in anticipation.

Earliest attestation: The folk saying in long-standing British and American rural usage
Sources: BBC Science Focus Magazine, 'Is it true that cows lie down when it's about to rain?': 'According to a recent survey by the UK Met Office, over 60 per cent of the British public believe that cows lying down is a sure sign of rain.' And: 'In truth, however, there is no scientific evidence for this piece of weather folklore... cows spend up to half of their time lying down, either to rest or to chew their cud, so there's a 50:50 chance that they'll be lying down at any given moment, come rain or shine.' Also the 2013 US study (Arizona/Northern Missouri): cows stand in hot weather to cool off, lie down when colder — cold air often precedes rain.; Live Science, 'Do Cows Really Lie Down When It's About to Rain?': 'Not likely – cows lie down for many reasons, and there's no scientific evidence that rain is one of them. As the Farmer's Almanac says, "Cows lying down in a field more often means they're chewing their cud, rather than preparing for raindrops."'; Dairy Diary (UK), '7 Weather Folklore Sayings': 'Cows lie down when it is going to rain. Many believe that the sight of cows lying down means there is rain on the way but, according to the Met Office, there is no scientific evidence for it at all.'; Discover Magazine, 'Old Wives' Tales to Predict Weather': 'Cows lie down when rain is on the way. This claim lacks any scientific evidence, however.'; Fox Weather, 'Bovine barometers' (2021): Justin Rhinehart, UTenn beef cattle specialist — 'There may be some truth to it... I don't think we understand it enough to base a lot of planning around it... it may be something like, if you see all of your cows laying down in a field you might not want to mow hay and risk getting rained on.' Don't rule out pressure response, 'but it's also possible they are simply taking a nap.'
Falsifiability: A controlled study showing cattle lying-down behavior predicting rainfall independently of temperature, rest cycles, and herd mimicry</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.17,52.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>MYTH: John Cotton Dana invented the library's card-and-pocket checkout system</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Nina E. Browne's card-and-pocket charging system dates to 1895 — five years before the Newark system's ~1900 debut — and Freedman's 1981 survey explicitly describes Newark as 'incorporating features of earlier systems'; the single-inventor telling cannot survive both facts.
Layer: fake · Place: Newark Public Library, Newark, New Jersey (the telling's home; the refutation's geography is Boston 1895)
Dates: 1900 → 2026 (confidence: traditional)

The popular telling that John Cotton Dana invented the library book-card checkout — that the card-and-pocket system (book pocket, book card, date slip, borrower's card) began with Dana's Newark system about 1900, with no predecessor.

Earliest attestation: The myth's vector: 'invented about 1900' tellings of the Newark system (e.g. Dean 2015 DHQ timeline), quiz-bank formulations crediting Dana, and the Newark system's own institutional memory.
Sources: Dean, Gabrielle (2015). DHQ timeline 'invented about 1900' formulation — the source of the single-inventor shorthand.; Freedman, Maurice J. (1981). 'Circulation Systems Past and Present' — Newark 'incorporating features of earlier systems' (the kill).; Gorman, Michael (2011). Broken Pieces, Ch. 3 fn 4 — Browne 1895 priority (the kill).; Browne Issue System article — 'replaced in America by the Newark and Detroit methods and others based on them' (the sequence).
Falsifiability: This record is already falsified-in-reverse: it would be un-DEBUNKED only if Browne's 1895 date collapses or Newark is shown to predate it — neither currently holds</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0776,40.7282,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The giant destrier — the draft-horse-sized medieval war horse</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The zooarchaeological sample (1,965 remains) shows medieval horses were small by modern standards: 16 hands rare even at the royal studs' zenith; none approached modern draft or police-horse sizes.
Layer: fake · Place: England (171 excavated sites, AD 300–1650 — the study sample that killed the image)
Dates: 1900 → 2022 (confidence: medium)

The medieval knight's war horse — the destrier — was a huge animal, the size of a modern draft horse (17–18 hands high at the withers), a mount built to carry an armored man like a small tank.

Earliest attestation: The modern draft-horse image: 20th-century film (shire horses cast as warhorses) and museum displays; not a medieval source
Sources: Ameen, C. et al., 'In search of the 'great horse': A zooarchaeological assessment of horses from England (AD 300–1650)', International Journal of Osteoarchaeology (2021), DOI 10.1002/oa.3038; Press coverage of the study, 10 Jan 2022: tallest Norman horse ~15 hands (Trowbridge Castle); 16 hands first appears in the 13th c. and was 'very rare' even at the 13th–14th c. royal stud zenith; horses of 16 and even 15 hands 'very rare'; none of the ~2,000 studied could serve as a modern police horse (UK minimum 16.2 hands); Outram (Univ. of Exeter): 'High medieval destriers may have been relatively large for the time period, but were clearly still much smaller than we might expect for equivalent functions today'; Benkert (Univ. of Exeter): 'Neither size, nor limb bone robusticity alone, are enough to confidently identify warhorses in the archaeological record' — the study's own honesty fence
Falsifiability: A securely-dated medieval European horse skeleton at 17 hands or above (1.73 m+) from a documented war-horse context</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.5,52.6,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>TELLING (DEBUNKED): 'posh' = Port Out, Starboard Home</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The OED, quoted verbatim by Ben Yagoda: 'However, no evidence has been found for the existence of such tickets.' Acronym etymologies for words predating the acronym era are, in Kory Stamper's phrase, by and large total horseshit — the genre is the tell.
Layer: fake · Place: London (P&amp;O shipping and English print culture)
Dates: 1900 → 2026 (confidence: high)

TELLING (DEBUNKED): 'posh' is an acronym of 'Port Out, Starboard Home' — the Peninsular &amp; Oriental Steam Navigation Company stamped wealthy Britain-India passengers' tickets P.O.S.H. for the cooler (more expensive) cabins.

Earliest attestation: The acronym story circulates in the 20th century; the word 'posh' predates it by a century
Sources: OED, 'posh' entry — 'no evidence has been found for the existence of such tickets' (quoted in Ben Yagoda, Gobsmacked! and benyagoda.substack.com); origin officially 'unknown', leading guess Romani money-slang; Kory Stamper (former Merriam-Webster lexicographer), via Yagoda — 'Acronymic etymologies are, by and large, total horseshit'; Cambridge UP excerpt (OUP researchers) — 'Posh ... has been mistakenly claimed to be a blend of 'Port Out, Starboard Home' ... The Oxford English Dictionary considers this etymology to be without foundation'
Falsifiability: A P&amp;O ticket, ledger, or letter stamped P.O.S.H. in the stated sense</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1900-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1900</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>"Elementary, my dear Watson" — the line Doyle never wrote</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed on two lines: (1) the negative — across every story Doyle wrote, the phrase never appears; Quote Investigator, Phrase Finder, and Sherlockian scholarship (Redmond's Quotations from Baker Street, 2009) agree; (2) the positive manufacture trail — the line is traceable through dated parodies and newspapers BEFORE the famous 1915 and 1929 landmarks, showing it condensed out of the press culture, not out of Doyle's pen.
Layer: fake · Place: London (221B Baker Street — the fictional setting) / the canon as published
Dates: 1901 → 1929 (confidence: high)

Sherlock Holmes said "Elementary, my dear Watson" in Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's original published stories (the canon of 56 short stories and four novels, 1887–1927).

Earliest attestation: Quote Investigator traces the components: 'Elementary' is said by Holmes in 'The Adventure of the Crooked Man' (1893) — Watson cries 'Excellent!' and Holmes answers 'Elementary,' said he — and 'my dear Watson' / 'Exactly, my dear Watson' appear elsewhere; the two fragments NEVER appear together in the canon. Exact-phrase print history: a 1901 Northampton Mercury parody ('Elementary, my dear Potson'), The Globe (London) 27 February 1908 (earliest exact print match), The Times Dispatch 24 August 1909 ('as Sherlock Holmes was wont to say' — already circulating as established folklore), Wodehouse's Psmith, Journalist (1915, book; serial in The Captain Oct 1909–Mar 1910: 'Elementary, my dear Watson, elementary,' murmured Psmith), and the 1929 film The Return of Sherlock Holmes (Clive Brook, first Holmes talkie) whose final lines put the phrase in Holmes's own spoken voice.
Sources: Quote Investigator, 'Quote Origin: Elementary, My Dear Watson' (2016-07-14) — full print-history trace: 1913 Everybody's Magazine, 1915 Wodehouse, 1922 NY Tribune, 1929 Clive Brook film; The Phrase Finder, 'Elementary my dear Watson' — 'one of the best known phrases that Sherlock Holmes never said'; nearest-miss citations (The Crooked Man, The Cardboard Box); artwaste.land, 'The Phrase Holmes Never Said' — six-dated rise: canon (never), Gillette 1899 (unverified), Northampton Mercury 1901 parody, The Globe 1908, Wodehouse 1915, 1929 talkie; I Hear of Sherlock Everywhere, '“Elementary, my dear Watson” — The Birth of a Quotation' (2016-08) — Wodehouse serial Feb 1910, Times Dispatch 24 Aug 1909 pre-Wodehouse print occurrence
Falsifiability: A citation of the exact phrase in any of the 56 stories or 4 novels as published by Doyle — the full canon is machine-searchable.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1901-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.12,51.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1901</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Brontosaurus never existed' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) Riggs's 1903 paper says nothing about nonexistence — it moves excelsus into Apatosaurus on priority, and everyone including Riggs agreed the species was real; (2) Tschopp, Mateus &amp; Benson 2015 (PeerJ 3:e857, ~300 pages, 477 characters, 81 specimens) reinstated Brontosaurus as a valid genus, adopted by the Paleobiology Database and modern mounts.
Layer: fake · Place: Como Bluff, Wyoming — the animal's type locality; the myth lived in textbooks and on a postage stamp
Dates: 1903 → 2015 (confidence: high)

The popular claim 'Brontosaurus never existed — it was a scientific error from the start.' The animal was always real: Riggs 1903 performed a taxonomic sinking (synonymy under the priority rule), not a fraud exposure, and the type skeleton sat in museums throughout; the 2015 peer-reviewed reinstatement of the genus makes the 'never existed' version false twice over.

Earliest attestation: Riggs 1903 synonymy, as misread in popular science writing; the schoolbook shorthand 'Brontosaurus didn't exist' hardened through the 20th century
Sources: Riggs 1903: the synonymy sentence itself — 'the two genera may be regarded as synonymous. As the term Apatosaurus has priority, Brontosaurus will be regarded as a synonym.' — a filing decision, not a fraud exposure; Tschopp, E., Mateus, O. &amp; Benson, R.B.J. (2015), PeerJ 3:e857, doi:10.7717/peerj.857 — the reinstatement; 'the famous genus Brontosaurus is considered valid by our quantitative approach'; Stephen Jay Gould, 'Bully for Brontosaurus' (1991, W.W. Norton): the stamp-era counterpoint — 'Brontosaurus and Apatosaurus are simply different names for the same dinosaur'; the issue is nomenclature, not existence; USPS 1989 four-dinosaur stamp issue (Tyrannosaurus, Stegosaurus, Pteranodon, Brontosaurus): public outrage, accusations of 'fostering scientific illiteracy,' demands for recall — the belief ecology that froze the myth into postage
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed demonstration that the 2015 Tschopp–Mateus–Benson analysis is methodologically unsound AND that the YPM 1980 holotype is non-diagnostic or fabricated — neither has been produced</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1903-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-107.55,41.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1903</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The myth: Edison electrocuted Topsy to win the War of Currents</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) agency — the execution was ordered by Topsy's owners Thompson &amp; Dundy (their hanging plan was blocked by the SPCA; electrocution was the fallback; cyanide-laced carrots and strangulation were also attempted), not by Edison; (2) presence — Edison was never at Luna Park; the film was shot by his company's camera operator; (3) chronology — the War of Currents had ended by ~1892 (Edison General Electric + Thomson-Houston merger; Edison no longer attached to GE), so a 1903 execution could not have been a War-of-Currents weapon.
Layer: fake · Place: Luna Park, Coney Island, Brooklyn, New York
Dates: 1903-01-04 → 1903-01-04 (confidence: high)

Thomas Edison personally ordered the 1903 electrocution of the elephant Topsy as an anti-AC demonstration during the War of Currents against George Westinghouse.

Earliest attestation: The myth is modern: its traceable vector is an unsourced 2008 Wired piece by Tony Long, per the electrotechnical-history record (brian.carnell.com); it has since circulated in pop culture, film, and some journalism (e.g., the Gizmodo 1903-framing piece) as an 'Edison did it' story.
Sources: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrocuting_an_Elephant — 'War of the currents misconception' section: execution was Thompson &amp; Dundy's, Edison never visited Luna Park, 10 years after the war ended, Edison no longer attached to GE (1892 merger); onthisday.com/photos/topsy-the-elephant-is-electrocuted — 'urban legend that Thomas Edison had ordered the elephant electrocuted ... This is false — the war between AC and DC took place ten years before the death of Topsy and Edison was never at Luna Park'; brian.carnell.com electrotechnical archive — 'Edison was not responsible ... nor did he participate in the euthanization'; the 2008 Wired article by Tony Long 'made this baseless claim without citing any evidence or sources'; the AC/DC dispute 'had largely ended with the creation of General Electric in 1892'
Falsifiability: A document showing Edison ordered, directed, or was present at the execution — or evidence the War of Currents was still commercially live in 1903 — would overturn the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1903-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9814,40.5755,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1903</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Earthquake Destroyed the City' Telling (1906)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiple independent lines agree the fires did at least 80% (up to ~95%) of the total destruction: the Lawson report, Hansen &amp; Condon's 1989 re-examination, the UC Berkeley Seismology Lab, and Scientific American's centenary history. The shaking triggered the disaster; it did not do most of the destroying.
Layer: fake · Place: San Francisco, California
Dates: 1906 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The telling that the 1906 San Francisco earthquake's ground shaking destroyed the city — i.e., that the shaking, not the subsequent fires, did the vast majority of the destruction.

Earliest attestation: Named as 'the San Francisco earthquake' from 1906; the fire's dominant role documented by the Lawson report (1908) and consolidated by Gladys Hansen &amp; Emmet Condon, Denial of Disaster (1989)
Sources: Wikipedia: 'at least 80%, and at most 95%, of the total destruction was the result of the subsequent fires' (citing Lawson-derived scholarship); Scientific American (history-of-geology): the three-day firestorm 'will be responsible for 90 percent of the 28,000 destroyed buildings'; Foxweather / UC Berkeley Seismology Lab: 'most of the damage to the city was caused by another factor: fire'; Philip Fradkin review (California History): Hansen &amp; Condon, Denial of Disaster (1989) — establishment minimized the death toll (official 498 vs ~3,000+ from morgue/coroner records) to protect rebuilding
Falsifiability: A building-by-building damage audit showing shaking destroyed ≥50% of the built city — no such audit exists; all converge on the fires</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1906-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.4194,37.7749,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1906</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Marie Lloyd 'led' the 1907 music-hall strike</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The strike was organized by the VAF — founded February 1906, launched by the 20 January 1907 Surrey Theatre mass meeting of the federation, the Amalgamated Musicians Union and the National Association of Theatrical Employees, settled through the union's arbitration. Lloyd's documented role is star backer — picket-line performances, funding, the Scala donation — prominent, but not organizational leadership. The grade kills the leadership authorship only.
Layer: fake · Place: London
Dates: 1907-01-20 → 1907-02-28 (confidence: medium)

That Marie Lloyd — the 'Queen of the Music Hall' — led the 1907 music-hall strike, as the popular shorthand runs.

Earliest attestation: Lloyd's biographical tradition; the leadership shorthand travels through popular summaries
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Music Hall Strike of 1907' (secondary; Lloyd funded, performed free on picket lines, donated her Scala fee; the 'poorer members of the profession' quote); Wikipedia — 'Marie Lloyd' (secondary; 'supported the strike, acted as a picket for the strikers and gave generously to the strike fund'); Wikipedia — 'Variety Artistes' Federation' (secondary; the union's founding 18 Feb 1906 from GOWR/Music Hall Artists Railway Association/Terriers discussions; launched by the union, not by Lloyd); Feldner, K. — 'Music Hall and the Age of Resistance' (McMaster M.A. thesis, 2019; secondary academic — the 'one of the leaders' framing; carried honestly in competing hypotheses)
Falsifiability: VAF founding or organizing records naming Lloyd as an organizer or committee member would overturn the verdict; her attendance records (pickets, Scala benefit) confirm the fenced star-backer role, not leadership</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'only carrier' legend of Typhoid Mary</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) by Mallon's death in 1938, New York officials had identified more than 400 healthy typhoid carriers — and none had been forced into confinement (National Geographic; Wikipedia citing the carrier literature); (2) named contemporaries falsify the singularity directly — Frederick Moersch, a German-immigrant confectioner sent to North Brother Island in 1915, released in 1944 with a Brooklyn hospital job and state-paid rent; Alphonse Cotils, a Belgian baker banned from his bakery, given a suspended sentence in 1924 after a judge held he could not be imprisoned 'on account of his health'; Tony Labella, responsible for 87 cases and 2 deaths (later 35 more cases and 3 deaths after evading restrictions) — officials helped him find work outside the food industry; (3) the biology — up to 6% of people who have had typhoid become chronic carriers long after recovery (Stanford microbiologist Denise Monack, via Smithsonian), so carrier status was never unusual. What was unique about Mallon was the punishment, not the pathology: she was the only carrier confined indefinitely. The singularity claim is DEBUNKED; the confinement record stands as graded in evt-typhoid-mallon-confinement-001.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City (identification, confinement on North Brother Island)
Dates: 1907 → 1938 (confidence: high)

Mary Mallon was the only healthy typhoid carrier — the one dangerous exception among well people.

Earliest attestation: The 'one dangerous woman' framing: George Soper's identification work and his 1913 speech ('Since Typhoid Mary was discovered, the whole problem of carriers... has assumed an immense importance'); the press's 'Typhoid Mary' branding from 1907 onward
Sources: National Geographic, 'Typhoid Mary's tragic tale exposed the health impacts of super-spreaders' — 'at the time of Mallon's death, more than 400 healthy carriers of typhoid who had been identified by New York officials, and none had been forced into confinement'; 'at least 51 people caught typhoid through Mallon, and three died'; Wikipedia, 'Mary Mallon' — Frederick Moersch (North Brother 1915, released 1944, Brooklyn hospital job, state-paid rent); Alphonse Cotils (Belgian baker, 1924 suspended sentence, judge: cannot be imprisoned 'on account of his health'); Tony Labella (87 cases/2 deaths, then 35 cases/3 deaths after evading; helped into non-food work); 'over four hundred recorded cases of asymptomatic typhoid carriers' by her death, 'none of them were detained or quarantined for as long as she was'; Smithsonian Magazine, 'The Frightening Legacy of Typhoid Mary' — Leavitt's Typhoid Mary: Captive to the Public's Health; 'thousands across the country and hundreds in New York'; up to 6% of typhoid cases become long-term carriers (Monack, Stanford); Columbia's James Colgrove: 'the million-dollar question that nobody can answer' — why Mallon alone was imprisoned for life; Judith Walzer Leavitt, Typhoid Mary: Captive to the Public's Health (1996) — via secondary citation: Mallon's working-class immigrant status shaped public and scientific perceptions; health officials asked the wrong questions (how to stop her shedding bacilli rather than how to keep her from cooking)
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous New York City Health Department records showing zero other identified asymptomatic carriers between 1907 and 1938 would restore the claim. A finding that the '400 carriers' figure is itself fabricated would weaken the kill — the named cases (Moersch, Cotils, Labella) are the load-bearing evidence.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1907-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1907</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Frederick Cook's 1908 North Pole claim — the pole that wasn't reached (claimed 21 April 1908, disproven 21 December 1909)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED as a reaching-of-the-Pole claim, by three independent findings: (1) the University of Copenhagen commission, 21 December 1909, examined Cook's submitted evidence and ruled his records did not contain proof he reached the Pole; (2) Cook never produced detailed original navigational records — he said they were in three boxes left with hunter Harry Whitney at Annoatok in April 1909, and those boxes were never recovered (Peary refused them aboard the Roosevelt); (3) modern analysis concludes Cook's 1908 journey did not venture far onto the Arctic Ocean before turning around — a formidable sledge journey, but not a polar one. Bryce (1996, 'Cook and Peary: The Polar Controversy, Resolved') concluded neither man reached the Pole. The claim dies; the expedition's genuine distance traveled is not denied.
Layer: fake · Place: North Pole (claimed arrival point)
Dates: 1908-04-21 → 1909-12-21 (confidence: high)

Frederick A. Cook claimed that he reached the North Pole on 21 April 1908, traveling by dogsled across the polar ice cap, and announced the achievement in September 1909 — days before Robert Peary announced his own 6 April 1909 claim. No verifiable proof of Cook's arrival at the Pole has ever been produced.

Earliest attestation: Cook's announcement, September 1909, after two years out of contact.
Sources: Wikipedia 'Frederick Cook': announced September 1909 after two-year absence; claimed 21 April 1908; never produced detailed original navigational records ('three boxes' left with Harry Whitney at Annoatok, April 1909 — Whitney tried to bring them back aboard the Roosevelt, Peary refused, boxes never found); University of Copenhagen commission, 21 December 1909, ruled his records did not contain proof; National Geographic Society held Peary's papers and refused researchers access for decades; Explorersweb, 'Fakin' It: Exploration Hoaxes, Part I': Cook's claim largely discredited — 'he made a great journey but did not venture far out on the Arctic Ocean before turning around'; Wikipedia 'Peary's Expedition (1908-1909)', summarizing Bryce (1996) 'Cook and Peary: The Polar Controversy, Resolved': Bryce's conclusion — neither Cook nor Peary reached the Pole; History News Network, 'When Did Americans First Become Shocked by Lying': Cook claimed the 1908 arrival 'but he actually never got that far'; modern scholarship scrutinizing suppressed papers shows 'neither reached it, and both knew it'
Falsifiability: Verifiable original navigational records from Cook's 1908 sledge journey, independently corroborated — the very thing the 1909 commission asked for and never received.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1908-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>0.0,90.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1908</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1910 Halley cyanogen panic (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The panic is VERIFIED; the poisoning claim is DEBUNKED. Spectroscopic analysis (notably Yerkes Observatory) did find cyanogen in the tail — that part was real. Camille Flammarion warned it would 'impregnate the atmosphere and possibly snuff out all life on the planet,' and the New York Times ran the story. The public response is documented: sealed homes, gas masks, 'anti-comet pills' and 'anti-comet umbrellas' sold by street vendors, church prayer vigils. But other astronomers immediately dismissed the danger as nonsense — the gas was far too diffuse to have any effect — and on 18–19 May 1910 the Earth spent six hours passing through the tail with no ill effects whatsoever: no one sickened, no one died, the atmosphere unmeasurably changed. The anti-comet pills were later found to be merely quinine and sugar. The hoax layer (the 'Sacred Followers' virgin-sacrifice story) was also debunked. Mark Twain — born 30 November 1835, two weeks after the comet's perihelion, died 21 April 1910, the day after its next perihelion — really did bookend his life with the comet, as he predicted in his 1909 autobiography.
Layer: fake · Place: New York, USA (panic epicenter); global (tail passage)
Dates: 1910-05-18 → 1910-05-19 (confidence: high)

When Earth passed through Halley's tail on 18–19 May 1910, the cyanogen gas in the tail would poison the atmosphere and kill all life.

Earliest attestation: Camille Flammarion's warning, amplified by the New York Times, early 1910
Sources: History.com: the NYT's Flammarion story — cyanogen 'might impregnate the atmosphere and snuff out all life on the planet'; other scientists dismissed it as nonsense; the sealed homes, gas masks, churches, and 'anti-comet pills.'; Cook Islands education wiki: the 1910 approach (naked-eye ~10 April, perihelion 20 April, 0.15 AU); Flammarion's claim; the panicked buying; 'in reality, as other astronomers were quick to point out, the gas is so diffuse that the world suffered no ill effects.' The Sacred Followers hoax. Twain's 1835/1910 bookend.; FunFactz: Earth passed through the tail 18–19 May 1910; 'the cyanogen in the comet's tail had no measurable effect on Earth's atmosphere or life.'
Falsifiability: Contemporary atmospheric measurements showing harm</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1910-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1910</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'lost city discovered by Bingham, 1911' — the Machu Picchu discovery myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The site was inhabited and farmed when Bingham arrived; a previous visitor had signed a wall in 1902 (and Bingham's own journal credits Lizárraga as discoverer); it appeared on 19th-century maps; and Bingham himself believed he had found a different city (Vilcabamba). What Bingham genuinely did — the first archaeological survey and worldwide publicity — is the surviving truth, carried in the competing hypotheses, not the grade.
Layer: fake · Place: Machu Picchu ridge, Urubamba Province, Peru
Dates: 1911-07-24 → 1911-07-24 (confidence: high)

Machu Picchu was a lost city, unknown to the world until Yale explorer Hiram Bingham III discovered it on 24 July 1911.

Earliest attestation: Bingham's own expedition accounts and the 1912-1913 National Geographic publicity; the 'lost city' framing is his and the press's, not the locals'.
Sources: Bingham's field journal for 24 July 1911 — 'Agustin Lizarraga is the discoverer of Machu Picchu.'; The 'Lizarraga 1902' charcoal inscription on the site (Temple of the Three Windows) — pre-dates Bingham by nine years; Expedition record: farmer-innkeeper Melchor Arteaga led Bingham and Sgt. Carrasco up the ridge (paid one sol); farmers Richarte and Álvarez were living in huts and farming cleared terraces at the top; Álvarez's 11-year-old son Pablito walked Bingham to the main ruins; 1874 Göhring expedition map and 19th-century references (Berns 1867; von Hassel) — the site appears on maps decades before 1911; Bingham's own later statements that he believed Machu Picchu was Vilcabamba, the last Inca refuge — he was hunting a different city and remained convinced to the end of his life; Gonzales &amp; Bauer 2021 (Ñawpa Pacha) — no Inca reference to a city called 'Machu Picchu' predates 1912; Bingham adopted the name from his guide's mountain; locals knew the site by another name
Falsifiability: Contemporary evidence that no one lived at or knew of the site in 1911 — contradicted by the journal, the inscription, the farmers, and the maps</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1911-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.545,-13.163,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1911</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The fictional Flannan logbooks and the supernatural explanations</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Journalist Mike Dash's investigation for the Fortean Times revealed the dramatic logbooks to be fictional, later additions to the story; the NLB's actual records show written log entries dated 13 December and slate particulars for 14–15 December with no such content, and no storms were reported in the area on 12–14 December. Gibson's poem scene is contradicted by Moore's first-hand account. No evidence supports the serpent, phantom-ship, spies, or arranged-escape claims.
Layer: fake · Place: Eilean Mòr, Flannan Isles, Outer Hebrides, Scotland
Dates: 1912 → 1900 (confidence: high)

The famous 'dramatic log entries' of the Flannan Isles keepers (12 Dec: severe winds and McArthur crying; 13 Dec: storm still raging, all three praying; 15 Dec: 'Storm ended, sea calm. God is over all') are authentic contemporary records, and the keepers were taken by supernatural means (a sea serpent, the Phantom of the Seven Hunters, arranged escape, or foreign spies).

Earliest attestation: Wilfrid Wilson Gibson's ballad 'Flannan Isle' (1912) — the overturned chair and uneaten meal; the dramatic log entries circulated later still
Sources: Mike Dash, Fortean Times investigation: the dramatic logbook entries are fictional, later additions (secondary investigation cited via periodical reporting); Northern Lighthouse Board station page (nlb.org.uk): last written entries 13 Dec, slate 14–15 Dec, everything in order, lamp ready — no dramatic entries; Joseph Moore's relief-keeper account: 'The kitchen utensils were all very clean' — contradicts Gibson's overturned-chair/uneaten-meal scene; Wikipedia, 'Flannan Isles Lighthouse', 'Speculation and conjecture' + 'Later theories' sections (secondary compilation: Gibson 1912 poem; no reported storms 12–14 Dec; serpent/spies/phantom-ship/escape claims)
Falsifiability: Authenticated contemporary logbook pages bearing the dramatic entries, or any pre-1912 source attesting the sea-serpent/phantom-ship/espionage claims.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1912-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-7.58806,58.28806,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1912</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Legend of the Organized Christmas Truce Football Match</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The organized-fixture reading dies on the evidence, not on skepticism. (1) THE HISTORIANS: Prof. Mark Connelly (Kent) — 'there is no absolute hard, verifiable evidence of a match'; IWM's Dan Richards — the one-big-organized-game idea 'is simply inaccurate, and many historians get quite prickly at its erroneous prominence.' (2) THE PHOTOGRAPH: the image most commonly used to illustrate the Christmas Truce football match was taken in Salonika, Greece, in 1915 — a year later, 1,600 miles from Flanders — and has nothing to do with the truce. (3) THE PADDING: elements of the legend were invented after the fact — poet Robert Graves reconstructed the encounter in a 1962 story, adding the sardonic fictional flourish of the 'Reverend Jolly' referee and the offside winning goal, which entered the legend as fact. (4) THE COUNTER-WITNESS: Ernie Williams, there at Messines, told the IWM it 'wasn't a team game in any sense of the word... There was no score, no tally at all. It was simply a melee.'
Layer: fake · Place: Western Front, Flanders — the legendary fixture is placed variously and vaguely across the truce sectors
Dates: 1914-12-24 → 1914-12-25 (confidence: high)

During the Christmas truce of 1914, British and German soldiers played an organized, regulation football fixture — eleven players a side, ninety minutes, officials, a definitive final score — the great Germany-3-2-England 'match' of popular legend.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary attestation of a regulation fixture exists. The legend's paper trail runs through later retellings: the 2014 centenary popularization (Sainsbury's Christmas ad, the Premier League Christmas Truce Tournament, the UEFA-backed 2014 Ploegsteert memorial) — commemoration, not documentation.
Sources: Prof. Mark Connelly (University of Kent, Centre for War, Propaganda and Society): 'there is no absolute hard, verifiable evidence of a match' — CNN; Dan Richards (IWM, head of documents and sound): the one-big-organized-game idea 'is simply inaccurate'; the truces were localized 'mini truces'; The famous truce-football photograph: Salonika, Greece, 1915 — misattributed to Flanders 1914 (photo-historical correction, repeated across truce scholarship); Robert Graves's 1962 fictional reconstruction — the 'Reverend Jolly' referee and offside-winner flourishes that entered the legend (Smithsonian); Ernie Williams (IWM interview, Messines): 'no score, no tally at all. It was simply a melee' — direct counter-testimony to the scored-fixture legend
Falsifiability: Already falsified: contemporary evidence of a regulation 11-a-side fixture with a kept score would overturn this — none has ever surfaced in 110 years of searching</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.93,50.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Taxis of the Marne decided the battle and saved Paris (the 'miracle' reading)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) scale — ~5,000 men in a battle of ~2 million; (2) most taxied troops were held in reserve; (3) the battle's decision is attributed by the campaign record to von Kluck's flank exposure, Joffre's counter-stroke, and the BEF wedge, not the taxi convoy.
Layer: fake · Place: Nanteuil-le-Haudouin — where the taxis arrived; the battle's decision happened elsewhere
Dates: 1914 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Paris taxis of September 1914 were decisive in the Allied victory at the First Battle of the Marne — the reinforcements they carried saved Paris and decided the battle.

Earliest attestation: French wartime press and the 'union sacrée' propaganda, autumn 1914; the reading persists in popular histories
Sources: Express (2014): 'In a battle involving two million men, 5,000 mattered little, especially as most of the taxied troops were held in reserve. The taxi-borne reinforcements were a flea bite, although the episode was artfully used by the French government to promote the idea that a "union sacrée" of soldiers and civilians had joined together'; Standard campaign histories (Keegan 1998; Doughty 2005): the Marne's decisive factors were von Kluck's exposed flank, Joffre's mass of manoeuvre, the BEF wedge, and Moltke's detached command — not the taxi-borne reinforcement
Falsifiability: Divisional after-action records showing the taxi-borne brigades as the decisive force at the decisive point of the battle, or a battle-level analysis attributing the German withdrawal primarily to their arrival</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.81303,49.14033,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Shackleton's 'Men wanted for hazardous journey' advertisement — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed four times from the record itself: (1) no original has ever been found — citizen historians have searched since the early 2000s, the Antarctic Circle's $100 reward (plus Headland's case of Madeira, 2016) is unclaimed; Bob Headland (Scott Polar Research Institute): 'fairly convinced this haystack doesn't have a needle in it'; (2) the trail begins in print, not paper — Elmore 1944 and Watkins 1959, the latter dating the ad to 1900, years before the expedition; (3) the alternative date fails too — it did not run in The Times on 29 December 1913 (the paper's issues have been read and programmatically searched); (4) it made no sense for Shackleton to advertise — free press coverage gave him abundant applicants, and Frank Worsley (crew member) wrote his memoirs with no mention of an ad, recalling instead that he simply walked into the expedition's offices.
Layer: fake · Place: London, England — the claimed placement venue (the ad is about this city's newspapers)
Dates: 1914 → 1914 (confidence: traditional)

Ernest Shackleton placed a famous newspaper recruitment advertisement for his Antarctic expedition reading: 'Men wanted for hazardous journey. Low wages, bitter cold, long hours of complete darkness. Safe return doubtful. Honour and recognition in event of success.' (Often said to have drawn ~5,000 applications.)

Earliest attestation: Carl Hopkins Elmore, Quit You Like Men (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1944), p. 53 — a candidate earliest printed form; Julian Lewis Watkins, The 100 Greatest Advertisements 1852–1958 (1959), the vector that spread it to the advertising canon (claiming London papers, 1900 — years before the expedition it supposedly recruited)
Sources: South Georgia Heritage Trust, 'Men Wanted' (sght.org/timeline/men-wanted) — 'there are no facts to support this'; one of the first books to feature the ad was Watkins 1959, claiming London newspapers 1900, 'several years before Shackleton's hazardous journey'; other biographies claim The Times 29 December 1913 — 'it did not run on that day'; reward unclaimed; Frank Worsley made no mention of the ad; 'almost certainly a myth'; Smithsonian Magazine, 'Ernest Shackleton's Famous Job Ad ... Is Probably a Myth' — citizen historians searching since the early 2000s came up empty; Antarctic Circle $100 reward; Bob Headland (then-curator, Scott Polar Research Institute, Cambridge) 2004: 'This is a needle-in-a-haystack situation... I'm fairly convinced this haystack doesn't have a needle in it'; 2016: reward sweetened with a case of Madeira; still unclaimed; Discerning History, 'Shackleton's Ad – Men Wanted for Hazardous Journey' — the months covering Shackleton's preparations read and the paper programmatically searched, both empty; descendants' memories 'likely a recollection based on reading the ad rather than something they were actually told'; Worsley wrote his memoirs and did not record seeing an ad; Julian Lewis Watkins, The 100 Greatest Advertisements 1852–1958 (Dover, 1949/1959) p. 1 — the advertising-canon vector; dates the ad to 1900, impossible for the 1914 expedition; Campaign magazine, 1999 — ranked the ad 86th in its 100 best ads of the century — belief ecology: the ad industry's own canonization of a copy that likely never ran; Frank Worsley, Endurance: An Epic of Polar Adventure (1931) — the crew member's own memoir: no ad mentioned; he applied by walking into the offices — the negative-evidence anchor from inside the expedition
Falsifiability: A single surviving newspaper clipping with masthead, date, and page — the Antarctic Circle's $100 reward (later plus a case of Madeira, 2016) has gone unclaimed for 20+ years</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1914-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1278,51.5074,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1914</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that Sykes-Picot drew the modern Middle East's borders</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 1916 lines were never implemented as drawn — Russia dropped out, France ceded Palestine and Mosul, the international Palestine zone never happened — and today's borders were drawn by the 1920-1926 settlement; the 1916 map bears no resemblance to the Middle East today.
Layer: fake · Place: The Ottoman Arab provinces (the Levant and Mesopotamia as partitioned in the 1916 map)
Dates: 1916-05 → 1916-05 (confidence: high)

The borders of today's Middle Eastern states were drawn by the 1916 Sykes-Picot Agreement — the lines on the modern map are essentially the lines Sykes and Picot drew on their 1916 map.

Earliest attestation: The agreement itself: exchange of letters between Sir Edward Grey and Paul Cambon, 15-16 May 1916; publicly exposed by the Bolsheviks, Moscow, 23 November 1917 (reprinted in the Manchester Guardian, 26 November 1917).
Sources: The Cairo Review, 'A Century After Sykes-Picot' (centenary essay): 'many still believe the agreement set the borders of the modern Middle East. In fact, the map as drawn by Sykes and Picot bears no resemblance to the Middle East today.'; MERIP, Middle East Brief 103: 'the borders of today's Middle East were not actually drawn by colonial authorities via the Sykes-Picot Agreement... the lineup of states in the region has evolved, with some borders changed and others finally demarcated. Locals played important roles throughout.'; Nick Danforth, 'Forget Sykes-Picot. It's the Treaty of Sèvres That Explains the Modern Middle East,' Foreign Policy (cited in MERIP Brief 103); Sara Pursley, ''Lines Drawn on an Empty Map': Iraq's Borders and the Legend of the Artificial State,' Jadaliyya, 2 June 2015 (cited in MERIP Brief 103); James Barr, A Line in the Sand: The Anglo-French Struggle for the Middle East, 1914-1948 (W.W. Norton, 2012) — the counter-position: the pact survived to form the basis of the mandate division; Smithsonian Magazine, 'The Origins of the World War I Agreement That Carved Up the Middle East' (agreement terms: Grey-Cambon letter, 16 May 1916; zones A/B, blue/red/brown)
Falsifiability: A demonstrable one-to-one correspondence between the 1916 map's boundaries and today's state borders would restore the claim; the existing comparisons (e.g., the Cairo Review's centenary overlay) show none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1916-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.0,34.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1916</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Mata Hari the 'master spy' who killed 50,000 men</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 50,000 figure has no evidentiary lineage — no name, no unit, no operation, no transmission was ever attached to it; it was pure prosecution rhetoric (Chatin), and no intelligence accounting exists. The master-spy reading is independently dead: the evidence against her was circumstantial and partly forged, and historians concur she transmitted no military information of consequence.
Layer: fake · Place: Paris, France (the court-martial)
Dates: 1917-07-24 → 1917-07-24 (confidence: high)

Mata Hari was a master German spy (agent H21) whose treason caused the deaths of at least 50,000 French soldiers — the charge on which she was convicted at her 24 July 1917 trial.

Earliest attestation: The 24 July 1917 military trial: the prosecution's charge sheet and Captain Chatin's argument; reported in Morain (1930) and the contemporary press.
Sources: Wikipedia 'Mata Hari': tried 24 July 1917 'accused of spying for Germany and consequently causing the deaths of at least 50,000 soldiers'; no definite evidence produced by French or British intelligence; prosecutor's evidence tampering (Ladoux); her own prosecutor André Mornet later admitted doubts; Captain Jean Chatin, trial jurist: 'collective assassination' of 'more than 50,000 men' (Morain 1930: 210), quoted in the City Research Online study of the Mata Hari trial; German documents unsealed in the 1970s: she entered German service autumn 1915 (codename H21, Major Roepell, III B chief Walter Nicolai, handler Captain Hoffmann); some historians argue she 'may have merely accepted money from the Germans without actually carrying out any spy duties'; warhistoryonline.com: trial accused her of passing information 'that had caused the deaths of 50,000 French soldiers' while 'neither French nor British Intelligence could provide concrete evidence that Hari was spying for Germany'
Falsifiability: A documented intelligence accounting tying her to specific casualties — a named unit, operation, or transmission with a casualty consequence.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.3522,48.8566,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The telegram alone brought America into the war' — the sole-cause myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills on the SOLE-cause reading. (1) CHRONOLOGY: the US had already severed diplomatic relations with Germany on 3 February 1917 over the resumed unrestricted submarine warfare — before the telegram was published (1 March). (2) THE TELEGRAM FAILED ITS OWN TEST: Wilson released it hoping to pressure Congress into arming merchant ships; antiwar senators filibustered the measure anyway (CFR) — the telegram moved the public but not the votes it was deployed for. (3) THE BRITISH TREATED IT AS A TIMED WEAPON, not a self-evident trigger: Hall held the 17 January decrypt five weeks (Foreign Office 5 February, Page 23 February) until the moment it would land hardest. (4) THE HISTORIANS' VERDICT: HISTORY.com's 'accelerated... coupled with the submarine attacks' — the shared-cause consensus; Wilson's 2 April war address made submarine warfare the case, and the March sinkings of US ships (Algonquin, Vigilancia, City of Memphis) were the immediate pressure.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington, D.C. (Congress, declaration of war)
Dates: 1917-04-06 → 1917-04-06 (confidence: high)

The Zimmermann telegram, by itself, brought the United States into World War I — i.e., without the telegram the US would not have declared war in April 1917.

Earliest attestation: The sole-cause reading's popular form: Barbara Tuchman, The Zimmermann Telegram (1958), and the schoolbook/YouTube-shorts telling downstream of it
Sources: CFR, 'Remembering the Zimmermann Telegram': relations severed 3 Feb over the U-boat war; the telegram 'failed to advance Wilson's immediate goal' of arming merchant ships; HISTORY.com, 'The Secret History of the Zimmermann Telegram': 'the march to war was no doubt accelerated' — 'coupled with the submarine attacks, it finally turned the U.S. government'; Tuchman, B., The Zimmermann Telegram (1958): the 'last straw' reading that built the popular sole-cause telling; Friends of the Intelligence Museum, 'The Zimmermann Telegram' (2017): Hall's five-week hold; the Mexico cover story; Zimmermann's admissions; the Carranza lie
Falsifiability: Contemporary 1917 evidence that Wilson and Congress treated the telegram, not the U-boat war, as the casus belli — e.g., a war address citing the telegram as the cause (the 2 April address centered submarine warfare)</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1917-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.03687,38.89707,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1917</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'female Cher Ami' myth (DEBUNKED by 2021 DNA)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED by DNA. The original U.S. Army Signal Corps report described Cher Ami as a black check hen, and the 'female hero pigeon' telling persisted for a century — amplified by Rose Wilder Lane's 1919 Ladies' Home Journal piece describing a male French pigeon (the stories contradicted each other from the start) and by the American Legion Weekly's 1919 description of 'her' exhaustion. The Smithsonian had long classified the bird as male. In 2021, DNA testing by the Smithsonian and the National Zoo confirmed Cher Ami was a cock bird, settling the century-long controversy. The bird's heroism is unaffected — the grade is on the sex claim only.
Layer: fake · Place: Smithsonian National Museum of American History, Washington D.C. (where the bird is displayed)
Dates: 1918-10-04 → 2021-01-01 (confidence: high)

Cher Ami, the famous WWI homing pigeon, was female (a hen).

Earliest attestation: U.S. Army Signal Corps report, 1918 (described the bird as a 'black check hen')
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine (NMAH, July 2021): the Signal Corps' original 'black check hen' report; the media blurring of the bird's sex; the August 1919 Ladies' Home Journal (male French pigeon) vs American Legion Weekly ('her' exhaustion) contradiction; the 2021 DNA confirmation of a cock bird.; FunFactz: 'Wartime Signal Corps records described Cher Ami as a hen, but the Smithsonian had always classified him as male. In 2021, DNA testing conducted by the Smithsonian and its National Zoo confirmed Cher Ami was a cock bird, resolving a century-long controversy.'
Falsifiability: DNA evidence of the bird's sex</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1918-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0199,38.8882,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1918</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Smithsonian's claim that Langley's 1903 Aerodrome was the first flight-capable man-carrying aircraft</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 'evidence' was a 1914 flight of a substantially modified machine (new floats, different engine, rerigged bracing, altered wings/controls) flown by a litigant with motive; the Smithsonian itself published the retraction in 1942. A modified replica is not the 1903 aircraft.
Layer: fake · Place: Smithsonian Institution, Washington, D.C. (site of the 1918-1942 display and the 1942 retraction)
Dates: 1918 → 1942 (confidence: high)

That the Smithsonian Institution was correct (1918-1942) to display Langley's Great Aerodrome as the first man-carrying aeroplane capable of free flight — i.e., that Langley's 1903 machine, as Langley built and launched it, had been demonstrated capable of sustained powered flight before the Wrights' December 1903 flights.

Earliest attestation: Albert F. Zahm's 1913 Smithsonian report and the Institution's 1918 display label; Orville Wright's documented refutation (his papers catalog the Curtiss modifications); Charles G. Abbott's October 26, 1942 Smithsonian statement
Sources: Air &amp; Space Forces Magazine, 'The Aerodrome Fraud' — Hallion's account: Walcott lent Curtiss the Aerodrome; Curtiss 'essentially used this as the base for a whole new machine, adding many components, including pontoons for takeoff... rerigged the bracing and ultimately put in a new engine'; the result 'bore only a vague visual similarity to the 1903 machine'; the Smithsonian displayed it under Zahm's claim that Langley 'developed and built the first man-carrying aeroplane capable of free flight'; Orville Wright's papers documenting Curtiss's modifications in exhaustive detail; his 1928 decision to send the original 1903 Flyer to the London Science Museum: 'I have sent our original 1903 machine to the British National Museum because of the hostile and unfair attitude shown towards us by the officials of the Smithsonian Institution'; Scientific American, July 1925 'Our Point of View': the editors on Orville's decision — the Smithsonian had Langley 'retrofitted to prove it could have flown — if only it hadn't failed — and used that to award him the credit'; the display 'misleading'; Langley 'definitely did not beat the Wright brothers'; Air &amp; Space Forces Magazine, 'Orville Wright Plays Hardball' — the 20-year London exile; Abbott's 1942 report 'acknowledging the injury done the Wrights and repudiating any claims to the Great Aerodrome's airworthiness'; the Flyer came home in 1948 — nine months after Orville's death in January 1948; Fred C. Kelly, 'The Wright Brothers: A Biography Authorized by Orville Wright' (1943) — carries the full text of Abbott's October 26, 1942 apology/statement, giving the retraction its widest distribution
Falsifiability: A pre-December-17-1903 test showing the unmodified 1903 Aerodrome in sustained powered free flight — none exists; Langley's two 1903 attempts (October 7, December 8) both ended in the Potomac</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1918-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.03,38.89,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1918</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'war guilt clause' reading of Article 231 (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the text itself — the word 'guilt' never appears, and the article names 'Germany and her allies' (plural) as a legal hook for reparations, with Article 232 capping payment to civilian damage; (2) the identical clause in the Austrian and Hungarian treaties drew no war-guilt reading at all — the 'war guilt clause' label is a Weimar-era propaganda coinage, documented by Marks (1978).
Layer: fake · Place: Hall of Mirrors, Palace of Versailles
Dates: 1919-06-28 → 1923-12-31 (confidence: high)

Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles assigned sole moral blame ('war guilt') to Germany alone for World War I.

Earliest attestation: Treaty of Versailles, Article 231 (28 June 1919) — foil record evt-versailles-article-231-001; the 'war guilt' reading from Weimar-era German publications, early 1920s
Sources: Treaty of Versailles, Article 231 (signed text): 'The Allied and Associated Governments affirm and Germany accepts the responsibility of Germany and her allies for causing all the loss and damage to which the Allied and Associated Governments and their nationals have been subjected as a consequence of the war imposed upon them by the aggression of Germany and her allies.' — the word 'guilt' does not appear; Treaty of Versailles, Article 232: payment limited to civilian damage and Germany's capacity to pay; Sally Marks, 'The Myths of Reparations', Central European History Vol. 11, No. 3 (Sept 1978): Article 231 was designed to lay a legal basis for reparations; the same clause mutatis mutandis appeared in the treaties with Austria and Hungary, neither of which read it as a war-guilt declaration; ISS Forum Article Review No. 429 on Marks: the 'war guilt clause' myth 'propagated in the early 1920s by Weimar officials and opinion makers... continues to find its way into histories of the peace settlement'; René Albrecht-Carrié (May 1940): Article 231 'gave rise to an unfortunate controversy, unfortunate because it served to raise a false issue'; Wolfgang Mommsen: Austria and Hungary 'understandably paid no attention to this aspect of the draft treaty'
Falsifiability: A signed 1919 instrument in which Article 231 contains the word 'guilt' or assigns blame to Germany alone, or a contemporary (1919) Allied or German legal document reading it that way.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.1204,48.8049,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'war guilt clause' reading of Versailles Article 231</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The clause contains no word 'guilt', names 'Germany and her allies' rather than Germany alone, sits as the legal preamble of the reparations section above the limiting Article 232, was drafted by the moderate Americans (Dulles/Davis) precisely to constrain — not maximize — liability, and the historical consensus (Marks 1978; the commission's own record) is that guilt was never attached to the article; the 'confession' reading is the Weimar propaganda industry's product, not the text's.
Layer: fake · Place: Quai d'Orsay, Paris, France (Paris Peace Conference drafting)
Dates: 1919-06-28 → 1919-06-28 (confidence: high)

Article 231 of the Treaty of Versailles — commonly called the 'war guilt clause' — formally required Germany to confess sole moral guilt for starting World War I.

Earliest attestation: The German delegation's May–June 1919 protest notes and the German 'war guilt' press campaign of 1919 onward (the reading is born in protest, not in the clause).
Sources: Treaty of Versailles, Article 231 text (contains no 'guilt'; 'Germany and her allies'); Sally Marks, 'The Myths of Reparations,' Central European History vol. 11 no. 3 (1978): 'Article 231, which was designed to lay a legal basis for reparations, in fact makes no mention of war guilt.'; John Foster Dulles and Norman Davis authorship of the clause as a liability-limiting legal instrument (Dulles later regretted the wording as aggravating German opinion); Treaty of Saint-Germain and Treaty of Trianon: identical responsibility clauses accepted by Austria and Hungary without a 'war guilt' controversy — the control sample
Falsifiability: A drafting-history document from the Reparation Commission showing Dulles, Davis, or their principals intended a moral confession of sole war guilt rather than a legal basis for reparations — the American authors' memoranda show the opposite intent, so the grade stands unless such a document surfaces</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1919-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>2.32,48.86,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1919</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'all women got the vote in 1920' reading</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The universality reading dies on four documented fronts: the amendment's own text (a negative prohibition, not an affirmative grant — the 15th-Amendment pattern); Native American women were not even citizens until the 1924 Indian Citizenship Act, and states barred them for decades more; Chinese Americans could not naturalize until the Magnuson Act of 1943; Black women in the South faced poll taxes, literacy tests, white primaries, and violence until the Voting Rights Act of 1965.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (national scope)
Dates: 1920 → 1920 (confidence: high)

The 19th Amendment gave all American women the right to vote in 1920 — the universal version of the suffrage victory story.

Earliest attestation: The universal shorthand is a popular/educational framing, not a documentary source — it is what the record grades
Sources: National Geographic, '100 years of suffrage' (2020): 'Native Americans — men and women — weren't even citizens until 1924; Chinese Americans had to wait until 1943. The real watershed moment for many minority women would be Congress's passage of the Voting Rights Act of 1965.'; Martha S. Jones (quoted in National Geographic): the same Jim Crow machinery keeping Black men from the polls would keep Black women from them; 'There's good evidence that the promoters of the 19th Amendment were counting on that assumption.'; Harvard Radcliffe Institute, 'The Long 19th Amendment' (unit 5): after 1920, Black women in the South blocked by white primaries, 'literacy tests,' and violence; Native women ineligible until the 1924 Indian Citizenship Act; poll taxes in thirteen states; unnaturalized immigrant women barred entirely; Democracy Docket, 'The Complicated Legacy of the 19th Amendment': the amendment 'did nothing to protect women of color from disenfranchising laws that were legal at the time'; Native Americans not citizens until 1924; state-level bans persisted (Arizona/New Mexico until 1948 court rulings, Utah until 1957); The 19th Amendment text itself (National Archives): a negative prohibition — 'shall not be denied or abridged... on account of sex' — creating no affirmative grant and removing no other barrier
Falsifiability: Proof that Native American, Chinese American, and Southern Black women voted freely and unobstructed in the 1920 elections would collapse the record. The opposite is what the sources show.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.58,39.83,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Coso artifact (500,000-year-old spark plug claim)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — X-ray investigation plus the 2018 physical inspection identify the device as a 1920s-era Champion spark plug; the 'geode' is a rust concretion that forms in decades; the 500,000-year figure was never published by anyone.
Layer: fake · Place: Olancha, California (reported find site)
Dates: 1920 → 1961 (confidence: high)

The Coso artifact — a device found inside a 500,000-year-old geode at Olancha, California, in 1961 — is an ancient or advanced-technology object (Atlantis, ancient astronauts, time travelers).

Earliest attestation: February 1961 collection by Mike Mikesell, Virginia Hahn and Wallace Lane at Olancha, California; the '500,000-year' figure comes from an unnamed, unpublished geologist's letter
Sources: Stromberg &amp; Heinrich x-ray investigation with the Spark Plug Collectors of America (Chad Windham, Bill Bond, Mike Healy, Jeff Bartheld): the object is a 1920s-era Champion spark plug, the type used in Ford Model T and Model A engines; the 'brass hat' explains the rib; 12 April 2018 physical inspection (Stromberg + University of Washington geologist): confirmed the spark-plug identification; no fossil shells found — undercutting the shell-dating claim the fame rested on; The stone matrix is an iron-oxide concretion from the rusting plug — such concretions form over decades or years, not millennia (Cronyn); Pacific Science Center, Seattle — the artifact displayed in the 'What Is Reality?' exhibition (as of 2019)
Falsifiability: A documented in-situ excavation with geological dating of the matrix — or material evidence inconsistent with a 1920s Champion spark plug</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.0,36.28,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Great Wall visible from the Moon / from space with the naked eye</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Direct eyewitness testimony from Moon-walkers and orbiters falsifies the naked-eye claim, and optical physics makes it impossible: the human eye resolves ~1 arcminute; a 6-m-wide wall at lunar distance subtends ~1/10,000 of an arcminute — 'seeing a human hair from a kilometer away'.
Layer: fake · Place: The claim is about the Wall seen from afar; pin carried as Badaling representative section
Dates: 1920 → 2007 (confidence: high)

The Great Wall of China is visible with the naked eye from the Moon — and/or is the only man-made structure visible from space with the naked eye.

Earliest attestation: Ripley's Believe It or Not cartoon, 1932: 'the mightiest work of man, the only one that would be visible to the human eye from the moon'; an earlier form appears in William Henry Hudson's 'Dead Man's Plack and an Old Thorn' (1920)
Sources: Ripley's Believe It or Not cartoon, 1932 (origin of the popular Moon-visibility form); Neil Armstrong: 'definitely not visible from the Moon'; Michael Collins, Liftoff (1988); Jim Lovell ('the claim is absurd'); Jim Irwin ('out of the question') — primary astronaut testimony; Jay Apt, National Geographic, Nov 1996: 'made largely of materials that have the same color as the surrounding soil ... almost invisible from only 180 miles up'; Yang Liwei (Shenzhou 5, 2003): 'I didn't see the Great Wall' — CCTV interview; China's Ministry of Education subsequently revised textbooks; Dai Changda, Jiang Xiaoguang, Xi Xiaohuan (CAS), Science and Technology Review, 2007: a 10-m-wide object is naked-eye visible from at most 36 km in ideal weather — below the 100 km space boundary; Scientific American (Phil Plait): from ISS altitude the wall subtends ~0.1 arcminute (eye limit ~1 arcminute); from the Moon, 1/10,000 arcminute
Falsifiability: A controlled naked-eye sighting from lunar distance with confirmed identification (extraordinary, given the optics); from low orbit, a confirmed identification under documented conditions would only soften the low-orbit leg</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>116.57,40.36,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'KDKA made the first radio broadcast' (2 Nov 1920) — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The 'first broadcast' is a textbook shorthand killed by the pre-1920 record: Frank Conrad's own 8XK was making semi-regular entertainment broadcasts from October 1919; 8MK Detroit was on the air August 1920; XWA Montreal late 1919; Radio Argentina August 1920; Charles Herrold's San Jose station was airing regular programs for a general audience by 1912 (on the air 1909); Lee de Forest broadcast music from the Eiffel Tower in 1908. Even the 'first commercial license' reading fails: the first U.S. commercial license went to WBZ (Sept 1921), not KDKA. KDKA's genuine achievement — the first license issued for the specific purpose of broadcasting, and the election-night program that riveted national attention — is fenced out of the kill.
Layer: fake · Place: East Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania (Westinghouse plant shack)
Dates: 1920-11-02 → 1920-11-03 (confidence: high)

Pittsburgh's KDKA made the first radio broadcast in history on 2 November 1920, when it transmitted the Harding-Cox presidential election returns.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary 1920 sources: the Pittsburgh Post's own coverage of the election-night program; IEEE milestone text quotes the broadcast's own words ('Will anyone hearing this broadcast please communicate with us...'); the station's first official listing in the July 1, 1916 Radio Stations of the United States as amateur experimental 8XK (the antecedent)
Sources: IEEE Engineering and Technology History Wiki, 'Milestones: Westinghouse Radio Station KDKA, 1920' — 2 Nov 1920 election-night broadcast, 18 hours, the antecedent 8XK (1916); the milestone text documents the event it celebrates; Wikipedia, 'KQV' — 8MK Detroit on air Aug 1920; KDKA's 1946 'world's first regularly scheduled broadcast' claim and the pre-dating challengers (KQV, WWJ, KCBS); Conrad's 8XK semi-regular broadcasts from 17 Oct 1919; gammaelectronics.xyz, 'The Development of Radio Broadcasting' — KQW San Jose from 1909, regular programs by 1912; de Forest's 1908 Eiffel Tower music broadcast; the first commercial license not issued until mid-September 1921; answers.com, 'Who became the first professional radio station in the world?' — XWA Montreal 1919, 8MK Detroit Aug 1920, 1XE Medford 1920, Herrold's San Jose 1912 regular programs; KDKA 'insisted' as first though it really wasn't; WBZ received the first commercial license
Falsifiability: Proof that no earlier station transmitted regular programs to a general audience — contradicted by the pre-1920 stations below.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-79.76,40.43,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Vinland Map as a pre-Columbian artifact</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The ink contains titanium-dioxide anatase, a pigment formulation unavailable before the 1920s; the verso inscription was altered to fake a 15th-century binding context. Two independent analyses (1974, 2021) converge; Yale, the map's owner since the 1960s, closed the case itself.
Layer: fake · Place: Beinecke Rare Book &amp; Manuscript Library, Yale University, New Haven, Connecticut (current holding)
Dates: 1920 → 1965 (confidence: high)

The Vinland Map is a genuine 15th-century map depicting Norse exploration of North America (Vinland) before Columbus.

Earliest attestation: Surfaced 1957; published as pre-Columbian 1965 ('The Vinland Map and the Tartar Relation', Yale UP); challenged 1974 (McCrone), closed 2021 (Yale).
Sources: Raymond Clemens et al., Yale Beinecke analysis (2021): titanium compounds throughout the ink, 'not used in ink until the 1920s'; altered Latin inscription on the verso; Walter McCrone (1974): anatase (titanium dioxide) in the ink — the first scientific challenge; Yale News / NPR reporting, Sept 2021: Clemens — 'The Vinland Map is a fake. There is no reasonable doubt here.'
Falsifiability: A pre-1920s source for the titanium-bearing ink formulation actually used on the map, or demonstration that the anatase is contamination rather than pigment, would reopen the case.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1920-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.92721,41.31163,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1920</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Nobel for relativity' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED because the claim is contradicted by the awarding body's own published document. The official motivation names the law of the photoelectric effect — special relativity, general relativity, curved spacetime, and E = mc² appear nowhere in it. The presentation speech explicitly said the relativity theory was unproven as of 1921/1922 and would remain so 'for a long time.' The belief is a category error: fame for X plus a prize does not make the prize for X. The grade kills the belief, never Einstein's physics — relativity stands on the 1919 eclipse plates and everything since.
Layer: fake · Place: Stockholm (the award); myth carried globally
Dates: 1921 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Albert Einstein won the Nobel Prize in Physics for his theory of relativity.

Earliest attestation: The popular reading: Einstein-the-relativity-man + a Nobel Prize = the prize was for relativity. The official motivation was published 9 November 1922 naming the photoelectric effect.
Sources: Nobel Foundation, 'The Nobel Prize in Physics 1921: Albert Einstein' — motivation quotes the photoelectric effect; no mention of relativity (primary); Svante Arrhenius, presentation speech for the 1921 Physics prize (Nobel ceremony 1922) — relativity addressed only to note it remained unproven; ScienceDaily (29 April 2005), 'Photoemission 100 years after Einstein': 'In 1921 Einstein won the Nobel Prize not for his work on relativity, but for solving a puzzle that had baffled scientists since 1887 — the photoelectric effect'; spacedaily.com (July 2026): the Nobel Committee avoided honouring the still-controversial theory directly, awarding instead for the explanation of how light knocks electrons out of metal; the 1921 prize was received in 1922
Falsifiability: The Nobel Foundation's published 1921 Physics citation mentioning relativity as the grounds of the award.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1921-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.07,59.33,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1921</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Marie Lloyd died on stage' — the compressed ending, corrected</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The contemporary record is explicit: taken ill on stage at the Alhambra Theatre on 7 October 1922, found in her dressing room with stomach cramps, returned home that evening, where she died of heart and kidney failure. The collapse on stage was real (4 Oct, Edmonton; 7 Oct, Alhambra); the dying on stage is a compressed retelling.
Layer: fake · Place: London, England (the telling's location; the real death was at home, not on stage)
Dates: 1922-10-07 → 1922-10-07 (confidence: medium)

Marie Lloyd, the 'Queen of the Music Hall,' died on stage in 1922 — she collapsed and died before an audience, the great performer dying in harness on the boards.

Earliest attestation: The 'died on stage' compression is a popular retelling of the well-documented 4–7 October 1922 final week; no contemporary source places her death on the boards
Sources: Wikipedia / Marie Lloyd (citing The Times death announcement, Gänzl biography): taken ill on stage at the Alhambra 7 Oct; died at home that evening of heart and kidney failure; Leeds Heritage Theatres — 'Marie Lloyd in Leeds': 4 Oct 1922 collapse at Empire Edmonton; three days later at the Alhambra, too ill to proceed; died at home of heart and kidney failure; talesfromthebraziersgrotto (Gillies biography): collapsed at the Empire (Edmonton, London) at the beginning of October 1922; died 7 October; notes venue variance across secondary accounts — 'even the venue... changes from source to source'
Falsifiability: The falsifier already exists: contemporary reports place her death at home, of heart and kidney failure, that evening — not on stage.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1922-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.1,51.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1922</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'overnight' Vitaphone death (1928–1929) — a compression debunked</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The overnight telling is killed twice on the documents: Warner Bros. did not stop recording to disc until mid-1931 (recording and editing optically from then on, dubbing to disc only for Vitaphone projection), and studios kept pressing dubbed soundtrack discs for disc-only theaters on an ever-dwindling scale into the mid-1930s. The disc died as a recording medium in 1931 and as an exhibition medium slowly through the mid-1930s — never overnight, never in 1929.
Layer: fake · Place: Hollywood / New York — the studio-and-laboratory corridor
Dates: 1926 → 1935 (confidence: high)

The common shorthand that Hollywood abandoned sound-on-disc 'overnight' as soon as sound-on-film proved itself (1928–1929) — the instant format-war ending.

Earliest attestation: The shorthand lives in pop-film-history compression; the documented timeline it contradicts comes from the 1926 licensing record, the 1931 Warner switch, and the mid-1930s dubbing practice.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'Vitaphone' ('By the middle of 1931, Warner Bros.-First National had thrown in the towel... only then dubbing the completed soundtrack to discs for use with the Vitaphone projection system... This practice continued, although on an ever-dwindling scale, into the mid-1930s.'); Warner Bros. Entertainment Wiki (fandom) — 'Vitaphone' (same mid-1931 switch and mid-1930s dwindling practice; theater owners' disc-only equipment as the continuing driver)
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that Warner Bros. ceased all disc recording and all disc exhibition by the end of 1929 would overturn this; none exists — the mid-1931 switch and the mid-1930s dubbing practice are the documentary record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1926-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.32,34.17,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1926</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Jazz Singer was the first sound film'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed three ways by the surviving record: (1) Don Juan premiered 14 months earlier (6 August 1926) as the first feature-length film with a synchronized Vitaphone musical score and sound effects - it was staged and shot as a silent film, but its soundtrack was synchronized sound throughout; (2) Lee de Forest's Phonofilms were commercial sound-on-film shorts from 1923, with 20 Phonofilm outfits on theatrical roadshows by 1924 (Case/Sponable system); (3) the Dickson Experimental Sound Film (1894/95) is the first known film with live-recorded sound, in the National Film Registry since 2003. The grade kills ONLY the flat 'first' - never The Jazz Singer's genuine first (first feature with synchronized dialogue sequences - VERIFIED in evt-jazz-singer-1927-001), never its industry impact, never the talkie era's conventional start.
Layer: fake · Place: Warner's Theater, Times Square, New York City (the premiere that owns the myth)
Dates: 1927-10-06 → 1927-10-06 (confidence: high)

The Jazz Singer (1927) was the first motion picture with synchronized sound - that no film before October 1927 carried synchronized sound of any kind.

Earliest attestation: The 'first sound film' telling is retrospective popular history; the 1927 premiere's own publicity never claimed primacy in sound - it claimed a dialogue breakthrough.
Sources: Wikipedia - 'Don Juan (1926 film)' (6 August 1926 premiere; first feature-length film with synchronized Vitaphone score and sound effects, no spoken dialogue); MoMA - 'Audience breathless as Vitaphone and talkies make debuts' (archival feature; the 6 August 1926 Warner's Theatre premiere: first synchronized score in history, Hays shorts, New York Philharmonic); Gizmodo - 'The First Feature-Length Vitaphone-Synchronized Film Delighted Audiences 84 Years Ago' (sound-on-disc explained; Vitaphone the last and most successful sound-on-disc process); cinemontage.org - 'When Movies Learned to Talk' (Phonofilm: Case/Sponable Aeo Light; 20 outfits on roadshows by 1924; Lauste 1910 sound-on-film); Wikipedia - 'The Dickson Experimental Sound Film' (1894/95; first known film with live-recorded sound; Kinetophone; LOC National Film Registry 2003; never exhibited in original format)
Falsifiability: A complete catalog proving no synchronized-sound film or phonograph-record film existed before October 1927 - which the surviving Phonofilms, Don Juan discs, and the Dickson cylinder all contradict.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1927-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1927</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'talkies killed vaudeville overnight' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed three ways. (1) THE CLOCK: The Jazz Singer premiered October 1927; vaudeville's flagship house, the Palace, played straight vaudeville until July 1932 and mixed bills until 12 November 1932 — five years of receipts the 'overnight' telling cannot account for, then flipped BACK to vaudeville-before-film in January 1933. (2) THE CORPORATE RECORD: on 23 October 1928 RCA merged Keith-Albee-Orpheum (700+ theatres, 15,000 acts booked) with Film Booking Offices to form RKO — vaudeville's owners chose film for Photophone sound-on-film market-making; the industry moved on corporate strategy, not a technological thunderbolt. (3) THE MULTIPLE CAUSES: Britannica gives three causes (Great Depression, radio's growth, motion pictures); the American Vaudeville Museum traces the fade from WWI through phonographs and silent film, free network radio and cheap talkies only after the 1929 collapse — and small-time vaudeville lingered into the early 1950s. Scope fenced via note_on_grade_scope: kills the overnight monocause ONLY — never The Jazz Singer's genuine impact, never RKO's film pivot, never the real decline, never the Palace's eventual conversion. The 188th DEBUNKED family on this timeline.
Layer: fake · Place: Palace Theatre, New York City (venue of the five-year evidence gap)
Dates: 1927-10-06 → 1932-11-12 (confidence: n/a)

The arrival of the talkies (synchronized-sound film, from The Jazz Singer's October 1927 premiere) killed American vaudeville overnight — that live vaudeville effectively ended in 1927–1928.

Earliest attestation: No documented single origin found for the 'overnight' telling; it circulates as popular shorthand (schoolbook framing, podcast/retrospective narration). The scholarly record has always told it slower: Britannica's three-cause account; the American Vaudeville Museum's 'began fading during WWI... by 1932 big-time dead except a handful... small time lingered into the early 1950s'.
Sources: Britannica — Vaudeville: 'Vaudeville declined due to the Great Depression, the growth of radio, and the advent of motion pictures' (three-cause account); Wikipedia — Palace Theatre (New York City): 1932 transition detail; Wikipedia — RKO Pictures / Keith-Albee-Orpheum: RCA announced FBO+KAO merger 23 October 1928; Sarnoff engineered it for RCA Photophone market-making; KAO's 700+ theatres and 15,000 booked acts; American Vaudeville Museum / Frank Cullen (sites.arizona.edu) — the slow decline: crested 1910s, fading WWI through phonographs/silent film 1920s, free network radio + cheap talkies after the 1929 collapse; small-time vaudeville lingered into the early 1950s; Wikipedia — Vaudeville: 'There was no abrupt end to vaudeville... the form was clearly sagging by the late 1920s'; Palace's exclusive-cinema shift (Nov 1932) as the 'death knell'
Falsifiability: Documented evidence that no substantial live vaudeville performance occurred in the United States after 1928 — one ledger line from the Palace Theatre alone would falsify this; the record has years of them.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1927-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9847,40.7614,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1927</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'No postage necessary' means free — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The permit holder's payment obligation is stated explicitly and unconditionally in the USPS Domestic Mail Manual — postage plus per-piece fee on every returned piece, blanks included — with Royal Mail, Dutch, and New Zealand analogues independently confirming the licensee/recipient pays; the 243rd DEBUNKED family.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (USPS system; Royal Mail, Dutch PostNL, NZ Post analogues cited)
Dates: 1928 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The legend 'NO POSTAGE NECESSARY' on business reply mail means the mail travels free — that nobody pays for the postage. This is false. In the United States, the USPS Domestic Mail Manual (DMM 505) requires the BRM permit holder to guarantee payment of the applicable First-Class or Priority Mail postage, plus a per-piece fee, on ALL returned BRM — including incomplete, blank, or empty cards and envelopes — and to pay an annual permit fee. Under Royal Mail's Business Reply and Freepost services the licensee is billed per returned item (Freepost Standard/Plus, Business Reply Standard/Plus, Freepost Name licences). The Dutch antwoordnummer system bills the recipient the costs plus a fee; New Zealand freepost bills only when the permit number and recipient address match. In every system, 'no postage necessary' is a sender-facing instruction about affixing stamps, not a statement about who pays.

Earliest attestation: USPS Domestic Mail Manual 505, Return Services (current); the 'NO POSTAGE NECESSARY' legend itself on BRM pieces
Sources: USPS Domestic Mail Manual 505, Return Services (DMM 505.1) — 'The permit holder guarantees payment of the applicable First-Class Mail or Priority Mail postage, plus a per piece fee, on all returned BRM' including 'any incomplete, blank, or empty BRM cards and envelopes'; annual permit fee; 'pay postage and a per piece fee only for the pieces returned'; engineersealstamps.com, 'No Postage Necessary Meaning: Business Reply Mail' — 'The permit holder pays everything: the returned piece's postage plus a per piece fee' per DMM 505.1.1 (annual permit fee, per piece fees, account maintenance fee); postgrid.co.uk, 'Freepost and Business Reply Envelopes' — Royal Mail Business Reply: 'you need to pay the postage cost to Royal Mail while receiving these responses'; 'Freepost means free for the person who posts the envelope, not for you'; four services: Business Reply Standard/Plus, Freepost Standard/Plus, Freepost Name; Wikipedia, 'Freepost' — Netherlands: recipient pays costs plus a fee when no stamp used; New Zealand: permit number and recipient address must match for the recipient to be billed; UK: Freepost Name licence lets the public send free 'using any envelope,' the licence holder billed
Falsifiability: A postal regulation under which returned business reply mail costs the permit holder nothing — which would require rewriting the DMM's payment-guarantee clause itself</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1928-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1928</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Nobile abandoned his men' charge, 1928 (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The charge dies on the documents: Lundborg was ORDERED to take Nobile first; the Fokker had exactly one spare seat ('the plane would never take off with the weight of even one more person'); Nobile was the worst-injured mobile case (broken leg, broken arm, cracked rib, head wound) — triage, not desertion; Lundborg crashed on his return flight, so the promised follow-up failed mechanically, not by Nobile's choice; and the fascist-era blame was formally withdrawn — the 1945 review cleared him of all charges, restored his rank, and promoted him with back pay to 1928.
Layer: fake · Place: The Red Tent crash site, Arctic pack ice northeast of Svalbard (where the rescue priority was decided)
Dates: 1928-06-23 → 1945 (confidence: high)

Umberto Nobile cowardly abandoned his men in June 1928 by having himself rescued first from the Italia crash site — deserting them on the ice.

Earliest attestation: Contemporary press and poetry, July 1928 (e.g., the NYT-reported Soviet poem of 16 July 1928); the Italian Commission of Inquiry report, Rivista Marittima, January 1930; Nobile's own 1930 defense (With the Italia to the North Pole); the 1945 rehabilitation
Sources: Wikipedia 'Italia (airship)': 'Swedish pilot Einar Lundborg is ordered to rescue Nobile first, but crashes his plane when he returns for more survivors and is trapped on the ice with the others' (23 June chronology); Nobile's injuries (broken leg, right arm, cracked rib, head wound); 'the Italian fascist government blamed Nobile for the crash and was the victim of a smear campaign in the press'; naplesldm.com, 'Nobile' page: Lundborg 'said truthfully that the plane would never take off with the weight of even one more person'; Nobile's 1930 book: 'he had been ordered off the ice'; the NYT-reported Soviet poem (16 July 1928) calling him 'The Fascist general who took the Cross to the pole but deserted his men'; the 1945 board of inquiry that officially 'rehabilitated' him and reinstated his rank; Wikipedia 'Umberto Nobile': 'In 1945, the Italian air force cleared Nobile of all charges related to the Italia crash, and not only reinstated him at his former rank as major general, but promoted him to lieutenant general and awarded him back pay dating to 1928'; Peroni, Irene, 'Into thin air' (thefreelibrary.com): 'A new inquiry in 1945 had cleared him of the accusations that had cost him his job and his title of general was restored'; the Amundsen-Nobile feud and the rumor-driven hostility toward Nobile
Falsifiability: A contemporary order or account showing Nobile requested to leave first for his own safety against the orders of his superiors, or evidence that the Fokker could have carried additional men and he refused them</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1928-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>28.233,81.233,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1928</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Wall Street suicide wave of 1929 (myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Five independent kills from the contemporary record: Galbraith's suicide statistics, the medical examiner's refutation, the falsified London window reports, the misdated Churchill body, and the documented humor-to-myth pipeline.
Layer: fake · Place: Wall Street financial district, Manhattan, New York City (geography of the claim)
Dates: 1929-10-24 → 1929-11-30 (confidence: high)

The popular image of a mass epidemic of ruined Wall Street brokers and speculators throwing themselves from office windows in the days and weeks after the October 1929 crash.

Earliest attestation: London newspaper reports of window-jumpers; Will Rogers's newspaper quip (25 Oct 1929); Churchill's Daily Telegraph account (9 Dec 1929)
Sources: John Kenneth Galbraith, The Great Crash 1929 — on the suicide statistics: Oct/Nov 1929 suicides were among the lowest of any month that year, higher in the summer before the crash; on the London reports: no substance; 'In memory, some of these tragedies may have been moved back a year or two to the time of the stock market crash'; HISTORY.com, '1929 Stock Market Crash: Did Panicked Investors Really Jump From Windows?' (2019, updated 2025) — Norris's refutation, the Churchill/Matthies incident, the Borowski/Cutler/Stewart/Schwitzgebel/Motiska/Betts/Lytle cases, Galbraith's 17.0 → 21.3 per 100k figures; Wikipedia: The Great Crash, 1929 — 'London newspapers reported that ruined speculators were throwing themselves from windows but Galbraith asserts there was no substance to these claims of widespread suicides'; Charles Norris, NYC chief medical examiner, mid-November 1929 public refutation: 44 suicides in the prior four weeks in Manhattan vs 53 over the same period in 1928
Falsifiability: Manhattan suicide data for October–November 1929 showing a spike above comparable prior periods (the Norris comparison reversed) would overturn this grade</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1929-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.0113,40.7075,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1929</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Salt March ended the salt tax' — the seventeen-year gap</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The tax outlived the march by seventeen years. The abolition paper trail runs through 1946–1947, not 1930: Gandhi had to formally request abolition again on 6 April 1946; Rowlands issued an abolition order that Viceroy Wavell vetoed; the Interim Government (Nehru; Finance Minister Liaquat Ali Khan) finally abolished the tax in March 1947, with the monopoly ceasing October 1946 and the duty from January 1947.
Layer: fake · Place: Dandi, Gujarat, India (where the reading locates the victory)
Dates: 1930-03-12 → 1930-04-06 (confidence: high)

Mahatma Gandhi's 1930 Salt March ended the British salt tax in India — the tax was abolished as a result of the march.

Earliest attestation: The popular reading lives in schoolbook shorthand, the 1982 film 'Gandhi''s victory framing, and Indian exam-prep culture — where 'Government monopoly in salt ended immediately after the Salt Satyagraha' is a standard FALSE distractor (GKToday). No primary source dates abolition to 1930.
Sources: Wikipedia — 'History of the salt tax in British India': 'The Dandi March was only partially successful... the salt tax continued'; Gandhi's 6 April 1946 formal request to Sir Archibald Rowlands; Rowlands's abolition order vetoed by Viceroy Lord Wavell; tax continued in force until March 1947, abolished by the Interim Government of India (Nehru; Finance Minister Liaquat Ali Khan); Collected Works of Mahatma Gandhi — 'Gandhiji's Correspondence with the Government, 1944-47' (freedomopedia PDF): 'Salt monopoly shall cease as from the first day of October 1946, and the duty on salt as from the first day of January 1947'; GKToday UPSC Prelims bank — 'Government monopoly in salt ended immediately after the Salt Satyagraha' carried as a FALSE statement: 'the government monopoly did not end immediately after the Salt Satyagraha in 1930, and salt taxes were abolished only in 1946 when Jawaharlal Nehru became head of Interim Government'
Falsifiability: A 1930–1931 abolition order for the Indian salt tax in the statute books — which does not exist; the paper trail runs the other way</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1930-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>72.6242,21.3436,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1930</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The workers entombed in Hoover Dam's concrete</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed four ways: the dam was built as interlocking blocks poured in shallow lifts, not a monolithic pour; a body inside a block would create a structural weak spot (decay leaves a void); the documented 1933 W.A. Jameson form-collapse victim's body was recovered after sixteen hours, not left; and mid-1980s interviews with surviving workers found no witness to any purposeful burial. The genuine entombment belongs to Montana's Fort Peck Dam (1938 slide: eight died, six bodies still entombed) - the dams got mixed up in the retelling.
Layer: fake · Place: Hoover Dam, Black Canyon, Arizona-Nevada border
Dates: 1931 → 1936 (confidence: high)

Construction workers who fell into wet concrete during Hoover Dam's construction (1931-1936) were left there, entombed in the dam's structure.

Earliest attestation: Undocumented oral/worker lore; the stories surface in mid-1980s interviews with surviving construction workers (Todd King, University of Nevada) - none of the interviewees witnessed a burial.
Sources: Gizmodo - 'Who is buried in the Hoover Dam?' (the 1933 Jameson accident, Todd King interviews, Fort Peck correction, structural argument); Cracked - '6 Historical Myths You've Lapped Up As Fact' (Fort Peck mix-up, 1986 worker rumors); Construction Knowledge - 'The Men Who Built Hoover Dam' (96 deaths, death-benefit incentive, 8-inch lifts, no bodies); Boulder City 2020 Community Guide - Hoover Dam (officially 96 died; none buried in the dam); National Historic Landmark summary - Hoover Dam (gradual pour techniques made burial impossible)
Falsifiability: Ground-penetrating radar or coring revealing human remains inside the dam's concrete, or a contemporary (1931-1936) site record documenting a body left in a pour.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1931-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-114.737,36.016,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1931</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: 'Alan Turing single-handedly broke the Enigma'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Direct chronological contradiction: the documented 1932 break and 1939 transfer precede Turing's Enigma work by years, and Britain's own records confirm it received the solution rather than inventing it. The 'single-handedly' reading has no source; the Polish-first record has three governments' paper trails.
Layer: fake · Place: Bletchley Park, UK (where the myth is set); Warsaw/Pyry (where the break happened)
Dates: 1932 → 1945 (confidence: high)

The popular version — most concentrated in the 2014 Oscar-nominated film 'The Imitation Game' — that Alan Turing single-handedly broke the Enigma code is false. The military Enigma was first broken by Marian Rejewski and the Polish Cipher Bureau in December 1932 — seven years before Bletchley Park's wartime program began — and Poland handed Britain the complete solution (documentation, machines, replicas) at Pyry on 25 July 1939, when Britain had made zero pre-war progress. Turing's genuine, major contributions — the Bombe (built with Gordon Welchman's diagonal board), Banburismus, and the breaking of naval Enigma (Shark) — belong to the wartime industrial phase of a break that already existed. Colossus (the Lorenz-cipher machine) belongs to Bill Tutte, Max Newman, and Tommy Flowers, not Turing. Twenty-ninth DEBUNKED family.

Earliest attestation: The same documentary trail as evt-polish-enigma-break-001 (Pyry attendee lists, GCCS records, the 2002 Bletchley plaque) precedes every Turing Enigma claim by seven years; Turing's own wartime papers document the Bombe and Banburismus as later-stage work.
Sources: The Pyry attendee record and British GCCS manufacturing of perforated sheets from Polish documentation (mid-Dec 1939) — Britain receiving the solution, per the same sources as evt-polish-enigma-break-001; Bletchley Park 2002 plaque: 'in first breaking the Enigma code' — the institution's own commemoration of the Polish priority
Falsifiability: Documents showing Turing reconstructed the military Enigma independently before or without the 1939 Polish transfer — none exist; the British records show the transfer was the starting point.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1932-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.7414,51.9975,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1932</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'YKK invented the zipper' — the manufacturer, not the inventor (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The founding date (1934) is documented by the company and independent sources; the 1917 Sundback patent and 1923 Goodrich naming are public records. A 1934 manufacturer cannot invent a 1917 device.
Layer: fake · Place: Tokyo, Japan (YKK headquarters; founded in Nihonbashi, Tokyo)
Dates: 1934 → 1934 (confidence: high)

The Japanese company YKK — the letters stamped on most of the world's zippers — invented the zipper. The documents say otherwise: YKK (Yoshida Kogyo K.K.) was founded January 1, 1934 by Tadao Yoshida in Tokyo as a zipper MANUFACTURER, seventeen years after Sundback's 1917 separable-fastener patent and eleven years after Goodrich coined the name. YKK overtook the inventor's own company (Talon, the former Hookless Fastener Co.) for the American market in the 1970s-80s; it conquered zipper MANUFACTURE, not zipper INVENTION.

Earliest attestation: The chronological impossibility is itself the attestation: Sundback 1917 / Goodrich 1923 predate Yoshida 1934 by 17 and 11 years
Sources: YKK Corporation corporate history — founded January 1, 1934, Tadao Yoshida, Tokyo; Smithsonian Magazine — 'One Japanese Company Makes Half of the World's Zippers' (2015): Sundback's 1917 patent precedes Yoshida by 17 years; Pennsylvania Center for the Book / Penn State — 'Zip It Up: Talon Zippers' (2009): YKK overtook Talon for the American market in the 1980s
Falsifiability: A Yoshida zipper patent or product predating 1917 — none exists; the company's own history begins in 1934</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1934-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>139.6917,35.6895,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1934</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Invented Once, in the Fertile Crescent' Claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The single-origin model fails on its own requirement: other centers hold earlier or overlapping dates with unrelated domesticated species (New Guinea taro/banana/yam ~10 ka; Chinese rice; Mesoamerican maize) — a single invention cannot produce independent, contemporaneous, species-distinct agricultures.
Layer: fake · Place: The Fertile Crescent (the claimed single hearth)
Dates: 1936 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Agriculture was invented exactly once in human history — in the Fertile Crescent — and then spread outward from that single origin to every other farming society in the world.

Earliest attestation: V. Gordon Childe, Man Makes Himself (1936) — the Neolithic Revolution model; the postwar diffusionist tradition that made the Fertile Crescent the single hearth.
Sources: V. Gordon Childe, Man Makes Himself (1936) — the Neolithic Revolution; single-hearth model; J. G. Hawkes, The Diversity of Crop Plants (1983) — 'Agriculture began not once but several times, more or less simultaneously'; Denham et al., Origins of agriculture at Kuk Swamp in the highlands of New Guinea, Science (2003) — taro/banana cultivation 10,220-9,910 cal BP, 'agriculture at Kuk as early as anywhere in the world' (Spriggs); UNESCO World Heritage listing, Kuk Early Agricultural Site (2008) — criterion iv: 'one of the few places in the world where archaeological evidence suggests independent agricultural development'; Centers-of-origin synthesis (Vavilov center data, 2003 map): Fertile Crescent ~11,000 BP; Yangtze/Yellow River basins ~9,000 BP; New Guinea Highlands 9,000-6,000 BP; Central Mexico 5,000-4,000 BP; northern South America 5,000-4,000 BP; sub-Saharan Africa 5,000-4,000 BP; eastern North America 4,000-3,000 BP — overlapping dates, unrelated species
Falsifiability: Demonstration that all crop traditions trace to a single botanical lineage and chronology would reopen the claim; the domesticated species are distinct (wheat, rice, maize, taro, banana, potato, sorghum) at overlapping dates — ruled out.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>39.0,34.0,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Hitler–Owens 'Snub' at Berlin 1936</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) Hitler congratulated German winners only on day one, the IOC told him to congratulate all or none, and he then congratulated nobody and left the stadium before Owens's first race — there was no personal encounter to refuse; (2) the one documented exchange was a wave returned — sports reporter Paul Gallico saw it in Berlin, and Owens himself confirmed it in the Pittsburgh Press two weeks later; (3) Owens's own lifelong verdict, stated plainly: the man who did not snub him was Hitler — 'Hitler didn't snub me, it was Roosevelt who snubbed me. The president didn't even send me a telegram.'
Layer: fake · Place: Olympiastadion, Berlin, Germany
Dates: 1936 → 1936 (confidence: high)

Adolf Hitler personally snubbed Jesse Owens at the 1936 Berlin Olympics — deliberately refusing to meet, greet, or congratulate him because he was Black.

Earliest attestation: Rumors in Berlin during the games, per Mark Dyreson (1994); the cinematic version (Owens crossing the line cut against Hitler leaving) is decades later
Sources: Britannica — 'Was Jesse Owens Snubbed by Adolf Hitler at the Berlin Olympics?'; Paul Gallico, sports reporting from Berlin, 1936 (contemporary account of Owens below the honor box; Hitler's seated return salute); Pittsburgh Press, 24 August 1936 — Owens: 'before he left I was on my way to a broadcast and passed near his box. He waved at me and I waved back'; Professor Buzzkill — 'Hitler, Jesse Owens, and the 1936 Berlin Olympics' (IOC president de Baillet-Latour's all-or-none instruction; Hitler's absence after day one); ThoughtCo — 'Did Hitler Snub Jesse Owens at the Berlin Olympics?' (Hitler left before Owens competed; German crowds' ovations); Mark Dyreson, 'From Civil Rights to Scientific Racism: American Responses to the Legend of Jesse Owens and the Berlin Olympics' (LA84 digital library, 1994)
Falsifiability: Contemporary film or photography of Hitler refusing an offered greeting from Owens, or any statement by Owens himself that he felt personally snubbed in Berlin — the subject's own repeated, public, lifelong denials are the record's strongest evidence.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.2394,52.5147,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The '10% of the brain' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Every line of modern neuroscience contradicts it: fMRI/PET show activity across the whole brain over a day; the brain consumes ~20% of resting energy on ~2% of body mass; damage to essentially any region produces impairment; there is no unused 90% of tissue to unlock.
Layer: fake · Place: Cambridge, Massachusetts (Harvard — James's lecture halls; Thomas's attribution point)
Dates: 1936 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Human beings use only 10 percent of their brains; 90 percent of the brain's tissue is unused and available to be unlocked.

Earliest attestation: Lowell Thomas's 1936 foreword to Dale Carnegie's 'How to Win Friends and Influence People': 'Professor William James of Harvard used to say that the average man develops only ten percent of his latent mental ability.'
Sources: Lowell Thomas foreword to Dale Carnegie, How to Win Friends and Influence People (1936); William James, 'The Energies of Men' (1906 lecture; Science, 1907): 'we are only half awake... making use of only a small part of our possible mental and physical resources' — no percentage, and about energy/potential, not tissue; 1929 World Almanac self-help advertisement: 'Scientists and psychologists tell us we use only about TEN PERCENT of our brain power' (pre-1936 circulation of the figure); Barry Beyerstein, 'Whence Cometh the Myth that We Only Use Ten Percent of our Brains?' in Mind Myths (1999) — the archival hunt; Einstein Archives could locate no Einstein statement of the claim (apocryphal attribution); BPS Research Digest, 'Great myths of the brain: We only use 10 per cent' — survey data and myth history
Falsifiability: A documented region of the human brain, present in healthy adults, whose removal or destruction produces no functional, cognitive, or metabolic change whatsoever — repeatedly observed across subjects.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1936-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-71.116,42.377,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1936</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'La Guardia's 1937 ban killed burlesque'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED on three independent lines: (1) it was never a ban — it was a license campaign: the State Court of Appeals had ruled Moss could not revoke without a criminal conviction, and the weapon was the refusal to renew licenses, not a prohibition statute; (2) it did not kill burlesque — it killed burlesque in New York City: the Minskys opened productions in Miami, Chicago, and New Orleans, road shows toured the country, Harold Minsky's Follies ran six years at the Dunes in Las Vegas from 1957, the form lingered into the 1970s, and neo-burlesque revived it in the late 1990s; (3) the New York houses were offered a choice — reopen under the no-stripper code — and it was the economics of the compliant shows, not the law, that closed them ('business under the new code was so bad that many closed their doors for good'). Scope fenced: kills the flat 'ban = national death' reading, never the April 1937 raid, never the license refusals, never the no-stripper code, never New York burlesque's genuine collapse, never the advertising restrictions on the words 'burlesque' and 'Minsky'.
Layer: fake · Place: New York City, USA (the telling's home address; the form survived elsewhere)
Dates: 1937 → 1999 (confidence: medium)

La Guardia's 1937 action against burlesque was a ban that killed burlesque — the form died in 1937.

Earliest attestation: The telling itself is a modern shorthand (pop-history one-liners, the 'La Guardia banned burlesque' formulation); no contemporary source describes a 1937 ban statute.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (secondary aggregator: the no-ban, license-refusal mechanism; the Minskys 'finished in New York' — in New York); UNLV Special Collections / ONE article, 'Minsky's Burlesque' (institutional: family opened productions in Miami, Chicago, and New Orleans; road shows; Harold Minsky's six-year Follies run at the Dunes, Las Vegas, beginning 1957); Wikipedia, 'American burlesque' (secondary aggregator: 'Burlesque lingered on elsewhere in the U.S.'; the form's 'final shabby demise' only in the 1970s)
Falsifiability: A 1937 New York City statute criminalizing burlesque performance per se (rather than the documented license-refusal campaign), or evidence that no burlesque-form performance continued anywhere in the United States after 1937.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1937-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-74.006,40.7128,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1937</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Supper at Emmaus as a genuine Vermeer</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the painter's own confession (May 1945) plus his courtroom re-demonstration under observation; (2) the Coremans commission's chemistry — phenol-formaldehyde resins Bakelite and Albertol, 20th-century inventions, in paint supposedly three centuries old, with a resin bottle found in his studio; (3) the museum record itself: Boijmans withdrew the Vermeer attribution after the confession.
Layer: fake · Place: Rotterdam, Netherlands (Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen)
Dates: 1937 → 1945 (confidence: high)

The Supper at Emmaus — bought by Museum Boijmans (Rotterdam) in 1937 for 520,000 guilders after Abraham Bredius authenticated it in The Burlington Magazine as 'the masterpiece of Johannes Vermeer of Delft' — is a genuine painting by Johannes Vermeer (1632–1675).

Earliest attestation: Abraham Bredius's authentication in The Burlington Magazine, 1937
Sources: Coremans commission (1945–47, Paul B. Coremans): Bakelite + Albertol resins identified in the paint; resin bottle found in van Meegeren's studio (via Wikipedia, citing commission sources); Van Meegeren's confession (May 1945) + the courtroom demonstration painting Jesus among the Doctors, painted under observation July–Dec 1945; Amsterdam Regional Court record, 29 Oct–12 Nov 1947: forgery and fraud conviction; collaboration charges dropped because the 'Vermeer' sold to Goering was not Dutch cultural property; Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen: the painting displayed as the collection's highlight 1937–1945, the Vermeer attribution withdrawn after the confession (Boijmans Studies, reported 2025); NPR (Dolnick, 2008): the unmasking came from Goering's meticulous paperwork tracing the Adulteress back to van Meegeren — 'Most forgers fool one person too few... that never happened with van Meegeren'
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous 17th-century provenance for the canvas, or paint analysis showing no 20th-century materials, would reopen the attribution</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1937-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>4.4777,51.9244,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1937</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Roman-chariot-rut origin of standard gauge — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED as a transmission claim — the 81st DEBUNKED family. Three independent kills: (1) no documented chain: nothing in Stephenson's workshop practice, letters, or the 1846 Royal Commission record (48 witnesses, 35 for Stephenson's gauge) invokes Roman ruts; (2) the actual workshop history is recoverable and Roman-free: 4 ft 8 in Killingworth/Stockton &amp; Darlington edge-rail practice, the half inch added to reduce binding on curves (the summarize.tech railway-gauge video summarizes this; the 'arbitrary five feet' version is quadibloc); (3) tramway gauges in Stephenson's own district varied widely — 4 ft, 4 ft 4 in, 4 ft 7 1/2 in, 5 ft — which is exactly what you expect from local workshop practice and exactly not what you expect from a preserved imperial standard. Snopes categorizes the legend 'false.' The grade kills the LINEAGE, not the gauge: 1,435 mm is real, Stephenson's choice is real, and the Evans/Pompeii measurements (Roman vehicles ~4 ft 9 in) stand as honestly-documented coincidence, carried in competing hypotheses.
Layer: fake · Place: 
Dates: 1937 → 1937 (confidence: high)

The world's standard railroad gauge of 1,435 mm (4 ft 8 1/2 in) descends from the ruts left by Roman chariots and war wagons — the story that George Stephenson's gauge, and the whole modern railway system, preserves the Roman wheel standard through two millennia of road vehicles that kept running in the same ruts.

Earliest attestation: The legend 'has circulated since at least 1937' (Standard-gauge railway, Wikipedia, citing earlier folklore histories); a model-railroading writer traces the Roman-rut supposition to references in 1914 and 1958 (quadibloc). The specific form — Roman ruts → Stephenson → standard gauge — is modern folklore with no ancient attestation.
Sources: Standard-gauge railway, Wikipedia — Origins section: legend 'circulated since at least 1937'; Snopes categorized 'false'; tramway gauge table (4 ft to 5 ft variants); Trains magazine reader answer, reprinted in power-eng 'Dimensions in Time – Revisited' — Stephenson's 4 ft 8 in choice, the Gauge Wars, the Royal Commission (48 witnesses, 35 in favor), the 1846 Act; Trains.com, 'Track gauge: Different degrees of separation' — Walton W. Evans's Pompeii rut measurements (~4 ft 9 in center-to-center), Charles E. Lee on horse-sized optima; quadibloc.com, 'Gauge is Not Scale — Does Ancient Rome Come into the Picture?' — the 1914/1958 folklore references; the 'arbitrary five feet' workshop reconstruction
Falsifiability: A contemporary document (pre-1846) in which Stephenson, his associates, or the gauge commission cite Roman wheel ruts as the basis for the gauge would reverse the verdict. Two centuries of railway archives have produced none.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1937-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.6178,54.9783,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1937</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'RCA invented television' (the Sarnoff claim)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The U.S. Patent Office decided Interference No. 64,027 against RCA in February 1935 — Farnsworth, not Zworykin, had priority on the electrical image — RCA's 1936 appeal lost, and RCA took a Farnsworth license in 1939 (about $1 million in royalties). A company that licenses the invention from the priority winner did not invent it; the World's Fair story was the marketing of a licensee.
Layer: fake · Place: Flushing Meadows, Queens, New York (1939 World's Fair RCA pavilion)
Dates: 1939-04-30 → 1939-04-30 (confidence: high)

Electronic television was invented by RCA — David Sarnoff's Radio Corporation of America — and specifically by RCA's engineer Vladimir Zworykin, the 'father of television'; the independent inventor Philo Farnsworth contributed nothing original to electronic TV.

Earliest attestation: RCA publicity around the 1939 New York World's Fair television launch; Sarnoff-era corporate claims that RCA 'invented television'
Sources: MIT Technology Review, 'Who Really Invented Television?' (Sept 2000): Zworykin's April 1930 visit to Farnsworth's Green Street lab ('Dr. Zworykin was there for three days, and he saw everything' — Pem Farnsworth); Farnsworth built an image dissector before his eyes; Sarnoff–Zworykin collaboration; Wikipedia, 'Philo Farnsworth': patent interference brought by RCA in 1934, decided February 1935 (Tolman's 1922 sketch; spring-1922 blackboard drawing testimony); RCA appealed in 1936 and lost; Farnsworth received royalties from RCA; Zworykin's 1930 visit to Farnsworth's lab before leaving Westinghouse, Westinghouse team made copies of the Image Dissector; JDSupra, 'The Top Ten Patent Wars — Television (#10)': 'RCA used its marketing might, after taking a million-dollar license from Farnsworth after losing the interference proceedings, and claimed to the world that it had invented television through a much-hyped launch at the 1939 New York World's Fair'
Falsifiability: A pre-1934 RCA laboratory record showing a working Zworykin electronic television camera transmitting an electronic image independently of anything seen at Farnsworth's Green Street lab — or a showing that Interference No. 64,027 was decided on grounds other than Farnsworth's priority — would reopen the grade.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1939-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.8458,40.7467,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1939</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The fifty-year denial of the secret protocol (1939-1989)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice: (1) the captured German Foreign Office microfilms of the signed originals, published verbatim by the US State Department in 1948, predate any possible Cold War fabrication and were examined by the FRG in 1989; (2) Moscow itself abandoned the denial in 1989 — the Yakovlev commission's findings and the 24 December 1989 Congress declaration confirming and denouncing the secret protocols, followed by the 1992 declassification of the Soviet copy.
Layer: fake · Place: Moscow (the denial's issuing authority)
Dates: 1939 → 1989 (confidence: high)

The secret protocol of 23 August 1939 never existed — it was a Western forgery, and the Soviet Union's official position denying its existence (maintained from 1939 to 1989) was correct.

Earliest attestation: Soviet objections at the Nuremberg Tribunal (1945-46) blocking presentation of the protocol; Stalin's 'Falsifiers of History' (1948) in response to the State Department publication; the position persisted in all Soviet histories, memoirs, and textbooks until the USSR's dissolution
Sources: US Department of State, 'Nazi-Soviet Relations, 1939-1941' (1948) — the 1948 publication that made the denial's factual position untenable; Stalin (ed.), 'Falsifiers of History' (1948) — the Soviet counter-narrative; Yakovlev commission findings, Pravda 19 August 1989; Congress of People's Deputies declaration, 24 December 1989; FRG declaration of 1 September 1989 (secret protocols invalid from the moment of signing), following examination of the microfilmed German originals
Falsifiability: The denial would stand if the protocol text were a forgery — it is not; the denial is dead</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1939-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>37.62,55.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1939</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Tacoma Narrows 'resonance' explanation (the textbook myth)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the physics — the wind was a steady ~42 mph, not a periodic forcing at the natural frequency; the destructive motion was torsional flutter, a self-excited oscillation with negative aerodynamic damping (Billah &amp; Scanlan 1991); (2) the amplitude record — Feldman 2003 showed 'no resonance behavior in the amplitude as a function of wind velocity' for the torsional mode, and the 1941 federal board had already judged ordinary vortex resonance 'very improbable.'
Layer: fake · Place: Tacoma Narrows, Washington, USA
Dates: 1940-11-07 → 1991 (confidence: high)

The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed on 7 November 1940 because the wind's frequency matched the bridge's natural resonant frequency — i.e., the cause was externally forced mechanical resonance, the textbook 'swing-pushed-at-the-right-moment' story.

Earliest attestation: Miller's collapse-film loop title screen ('resonance vibration of bridge in torsional mode', c. 1940); Halliday &amp; Resnick physics textbooks, early 1960s
Sources: K. Y. Billah &amp; R. H. Scanlan, 'Resonance, Tacoma Narrows bridge failure, and undergraduate physics textbooks,' Am. J. Phys. 59, 118–124 (1991); B. J. Feldman, 'What to say about the Tacoma Narrows Bridge to your introductory physics class,' Phys. Teach. 41, 92 (2003); Harvard legacy physics page: the AAPT DVD user guide ('was not a case of resonance'), the Miller film-loop title screen, and the Halliday/Resnick textbook telling; Federal Works Agency Board of Engineers, The Failure of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge (1941) — three candidate causes; ordinary vortex resonance judged very improbable
Falsifiability: A measurement showing the wind's periodic forcing matched the bridge's natural torsional frequency at the moment of failure — the 1941 board and all subsequent analyses find the opposite</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1940-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.5494,47.2833,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1940</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The curse of Tamerlane: opening the tomb in June 1941 unleashed the invasion of the USSR</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) The inscribed warning does not exist: the coffin's interior carries a simple Islamic prayer, the jade slab a genealogy — Semenov's translation found no curse; the 'warning' came from a 19th-century legend book, and even the 1941 team's own men attributed the curse idea to a grave-robber deterrent. (2) The causal mechanism is impossible: Operation Barbarossa was planned since July 1940 and formally ordered by Directive No. 21 on 18 December 1940 — six months BEFORE the tomb was opened. The June 1941 timing is post hoc ergo propter hoc, the purest specimen on the timeline.
Layer: fake · Place: Gur-e Amir mausoleum, Samarkand, Uzbekistan (the legend's scene)
Dates: 1941 → 2004 (confidence: medium)

Opening Timur's tomb in June 1941 released a supernatural curse — announced by the inscribed warning 'whomsoever opens my tomb shall unleash an invader more terrible than I' — that caused Nazi Germany's invasion of the Soviet Union (Operation Barbarossa) on 22 June 1941; the curse was lifted when Timur was reburied in November 1942, before Stalingrad.

Earliest attestation: No ancient or Timurid-era curse text exists. The modern legend's first detailed Soviet publication was a 1990 Zvezda Vostoka article; it was popularized by Aleksandr Fetisov's 2004 documentary (cameraman Malik Qayumov's interview). The 1941 'warning' the legend leans on came from a 19th-century Jangnoma book of local legends, not an ancient manuscript.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Curse of Timur' — the inscription dispute: 'In reality, there is no curse carved on the inside of the coffin or the jade slab tomb, but rather a simple Islamic prayer in the coffin and the jade slab just had a list of ancestors of Timur' (citing McChesney 2021 pp. 122-123 and Shaw 2011 p. 54-55); first detailed Soviet publication Zvezda Vostoka 1990; Fetisov 2004 documentary; Stalin ordered reburial November 1942; five weeks (not days) before Stalingrad; Wikipedia, 'Mikhail Gerasimov (archaeologist)' — the curse story is 'apocryphal'; 'people close to Gerasimov claim that this story is a fabrication'; Vocal.media, 'The Curse of Tamerlane's Tomb' — jade coffin translation: 'genealogical data and Quranic prayers — no mention of curses' (via Gerasimov's linguistic assistant Aleksandr Semenov, by 2003); excavations began June 16, coffin opened ~June 19-20; three old men's warning documented; Yale Avalon Project, Nuremberg Judgement on the Aggressive War Against the USSR (citing captured OKW file 446-PS) — Directive No. 21 issued 18 December 1940, 'issued more than six months in advance of the attack'; planning since July 1940 under 'Operation Otto'; preparations complete by 15 May 1941. The invasion was committed before the tomb was touched.; Wikipedia, 'Operation Barbarossa' — final plans received 5 December 1940; Fuehrer Directive 21, 18 December 1940; tentatively set for May 1941. Cited as the planning chronology that kills the causal reading.; The three-old-men scene (multiple accounts): men handed Sadriddin Ayni a manuscript warning of a curse — but it was the Jangnoma, a 19th-century book of local legends, not an ancient script; Ayni 'shooed them away with a stick' and attributed the curse idea to a post-Timur legend invented to deter grave robbers; Qayumov's 2004 retelling (Persian-script warning he could not read; Ayni's son Kamal confirmed the men, disputed details)
Falsifiability: The claim is already falsified twice over; reviving it would require either (a) the production of the inscribed warning in the tomb or coffin (it has been translated: it isn't there), or (b) evidence that Barbarossa's planning postdated June 1941 (it predates it by eleven months).</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1941-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>66.96,39.654,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1941</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Soviet attribution of Katyn to Nazi Germany (1944-1990)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the perpetrators' own paper chain - Beria's 5 March 1940 proposal, the Politburo's same-day shooting order, Shelepin's 1959 count of 21,857; (2) the 1943 International Medical Commission's spring-1940 dating, before the Germans reached Smolensk (July 1941), confirmed by the 1945 Van Vliet report and the 1994-95 exhumations; (3) the successor state confessed - the 1990 TASS admission, the 1992 Package No. 1 release to Walesa, and the 2010 Duma statement naming Stalin.
Layer: fake · Place: Katyn forest district, near Smolensk, Russia
Dates: 1943 → 1990 (confidence: high)

The Katyn Forest massacre of Polish officers in 1940 was committed by Nazi Germany in the fall of 1941 - the Soviet state's official position from 1943 to 1990, established by the Burdenko Commission (January 1944) and carried into the Nuremberg indictment.

Earliest attestation: The Soviet Special State Commission for Establishing and Investigating the Circumstances of the Shooting of Polish Officer Prisoners by the Fascist German Invaders in the Katyn Forest (the Burdenko Commission), January 1944 - headed by Lt.-Gen. of Medical Service Nikolai Burdenko, with writer Alexei Tolstoy and Metropolitan Nikolay (Yarushevich); the Soviet prosecution included Katyn in the Nuremberg indictment (1946), but the tribunal did not include it in the judgment.
Sources: Burdenko Commission report, January 1944 (Soviet Special State Commission; headed by Nikolai Burdenko); Nuremberg Tribunal: Soviet indictment included Katyn; the tribunal's judgment did not - the charge was dropped from the verdict; International Medical Commission report, April-May 1943: 12 doctors dated the graves to spring 1940; Van Vliet 1945 report (concluding Soviet responsibility) and the September 2012 NARA release of the Stewart/Van Vliet 1943 coded messages; TASS statement, 13 April 1990 - NKVD responsibility admitted ('profound regret'); Yeltsin transfer of 'Package No. 1' to Walesa, 14 October 1992; Russian State Duma statement, 26 November 2010 - 'a Stalinist crime' ordered by Stalin and other Soviet leaders; Timothy Snyder, 'Russia's Reckoning with Katyn', New York Review of Books, December 2010; IPN, 'The Katyn lie: its rise and duration' - the lie as the founding lie of the Polish People's Republic; individual epigones (e.g. Yuri Mukhin) maintain the German version to this day; Russian denialist position: 'a number of Russian politicians and publicists continue to deny all Soviet guilt, call the released documents fakes'
Falsifiability: A German 1941 order for the killings, or forensic proof that the Beria memorandum, the Politburo order, and the Shelepin note are all forgeries.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1943-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.68598,54.77032,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1943</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>DEBUNKED: 'ENIAC was the first electronic computer'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — A federal court, after 135 days of testimony and 77 witnesses, found as fact that the ENIAC inventors derived the subject matter of the electronic digital computer from Atanasoff's earlier machine — which the ENIAC camp's own John Mauchly had studied on site in 1941.
Layer: fake · Place: Moore School of Electrical Engineering, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia (where the claim was built); U.S. District Court, Minneapolis (where it was unmade)
Dates: 1945 → 1946 (confidence: high)

The claim that ENIAC (Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer) was the first electronic computer is false. The invention of the automatic electronic digital computer was legally assigned by a federal court to John Vincent Atanasoff, whose Atanasoff–Berry Computer predates ENIAC by years; John Mauchly examined Atanasoff's machine in detail during a five-day visit to Ames, Iowa, in June 1941, three years before ENIAC's construction began. Seventy-third DEBUNKED family.

Earliest attestation: The 'ENIAC = first computer' claim enters the press with the 1946 unveiling; the counter-evidence enters the legal record with Honeywell v. Sperry Rand, filed 26 May 1967, decided 19 Oct 1973.
Sources: Honeywell, Inc. v. Sperry Rand Corp., 180 U.S.P.Q. 673 (D. Minn. 1973) — Wikipedia summary of the ruling: ENIAC patent invalidated; inventors derived the subject matter from the ABC; Atanasoff should have legal recognition as the inventor of the first electronic digital computer; Gizmodo, 'Why Was the Computer Never Patented?' — the longest federal trial to date: 77 witnesses, ~33,000 exhibits, four grounds for invalidation including derivation; Chronicle of Higher Education, 'An Ancient Feud: Who Invented the Computer?' — Mauchly's June 1941 five-day Ames visit; Atanasoff's charge of borrowing; ENIAC operational 1946; Associated Press obituary (Roanoke Times, 17 June 1995) — 'waited more than 30 years to receive credit'; National Medal of Technology 1990; prototype completed 1939
Falsifiability: Contemporaneous documentation that Eckert and Mauchly developed the electronic digital computer independently of Atanasoff's machine and the 1941 visit — the 1973 court's factual findings run the other way.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1945-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.1896,39.9522,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1945</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>TELLING (DEBUNKED): the whole nine yards = WWII ammo belt</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The documented prints (1946-51 Lexington KY, 1956/57 KHHG) predate the theory's reach, and the 1912 'whole six yards' variant shows the number was never fixed to any nine-yard object — a belt of exactly nine yards cannot explain 'six yards.'
Layer: fake · Place: Kentucky print culture (where the phrase is documented)
Dates: 1946 → 2026 (confidence: high)

TELLING (DEBUNKED): 'The whole nine yards' comes from the length of a World War II fighter plane's machine-gun ammunition belt — nine yards of belt, hence 'give 'em the whole nine yards.'

Earliest attestation: The WWII-belt story circulates in print from the late 20th century; the phrase is documented in print from 1946-51
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Nine yards' — the antedating chain vs. the ammunition-belt theory; Fred R. Shapiro, Yale Alumni Magazine — 'every theory must be assessed in light of the dated documentation — or lack thereof'
Falsifiability: A WWII-era document connecting the phrase to ammunition belts, antedating the 1946-51 Kentucky prints</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1946-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-84.5037,38.0406,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1946</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'peaceful handover' myth of decolonization</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills from the colonizers' own paper trails. (1) Kenya: the 1952-1960 Emergency's detention/torture system, exposed by the Hanslope Park 'migrated archives' (~8,800 files from 37 colonies) and settled by the British government itself — £19.9M to 5,228 Kenyans in 2013 (Elkins 'Imperial Reckoning', 2005; Anderson 'Histories of the Hanged', 2005). (2) Malaya: the 1948-1960 counterinsurgency — the Briggs Plan's forced resettlement of ~half a million people into ~600 'New Villages' and the Batang Kali killings of 11-12 Dec 1948 (24 unarmed civilians, Scots Guards; covered up for decades). (3) Algeria: the 1 Nov 1954 - 19 Mar 1962 war that France legally called 'operations for the maintenance of order' for 37 years, renamed the 'guerre d'Algérie' only by Law 99-882 of 18 Oct 1999; 350,000-400,000 Algerian dead by census comparison (Stora 2005; Algerian official figure 1.5M), systematic torture documented (Vidal-Naquet; Branche 2001). (4) India 1947: ~15 million displaced and 0.5-2 million killed at the empire's exit (Satia/Stanford; Dalrymple). The blanket 'peaceful handover' reading is disproven by the colonizers' own archives, courts, and legislatures.
Layer: fake · Place: Cape Town, South Africa (the 'Wind of Change' speech, 3 Feb 1960 — the myth's emblematic telling; the claim itself spans the decolonizing world, 1947-1990)
Dates: 1947 → 1990 (confidence: medium)

The European empires ended peacefully — independence was granted by the colonizers rather than taken by the colonized, without war or systematic violence on the colonizers' part.

Earliest attestation: The postwar 'orderly withdrawal' narrative — emblematically Harold Macmillan's 'Wind of Change' speech to the South African parliament, Cape Town, 3 February 1960, framing the end of empire as an accommodating inevitability rather than a contested fight.
Sources: Caroline Elkins, 'Imperial Reckoning' (2005) — the Kenya detention-camp system (Pulitzer Prize, history); David Anderson, 'Histories of the Hanged' (2005) — the Kenya Emergency's executions and the colonial state's paper trail; Brill, 'Architecture of Denial' — the Hanslope Park migrated archives (~8,800 files from 37 former colonies) and the Mau Mau court case; 2013 UK Mau Mau settlement — £19.9M to 5,228 Kenyans (Foreign Secretary William Hague's statement); 'Malayan Emergency', English Wikipedia — Briggs Plan resettlement (~500k-1M into ~600 New Villages), Batang Kali 11-12 Dec 1948 (24 unarmed civilians, Scots Guards, cover-up); French Law 99-882 of 18 Oct 1999 (Journal Officiel) — substituting 'guerre d'Algérie' for 'opérations effectuées en Afrique du Nord'; Stora/Eldridge via Perlego, 'The Algerian War, The Algerian Revolution' — census-comparison death toll 350,000-400,000 (Algerian official figure 1.5M), 'war without a name' until 1999; Priya Satia (Stanford historian) on the Partition of India — ~15M displaced, 0.5-2M killed; UN Digital Library — UNGA Resolution 1514 (XV), 14 Dec 1960: 89-0-9 vote record (the anti-colonial powers' own paper trail, adopted against the abstentions of the colonial powers)
Falsifiability: Archival proof that the cited counterinsurgency campaigns, death tolls, and cover-ups never happened — e.g. the 2013 Mau Mau settlement and the Hanslope Park files being forgeries, or the French 1999 law having been unnecessary because a war had already been declared.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1947-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.4241,-33.9249,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1947</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Dogon Sirius knowledge (Sirius Mystery)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Dutch anthropologist Walter van Beek's 1991 fieldwork found no Dogon outside Griaule's informant circle had ever heard of sigu tolo or po tolo, and none knew Sirius as a double star; astronomy is marginal in Dogon ritual life. Earlier critics (Goody 1967; Douglas 1968; Clifford 1983; Ridpath 1978) had already flagged the single-informant methodology. The extraterrestrial leap was Temple's, not Griaule's — and it rests on data that cannot be reproduced.
Layer: fake · Place: Bandiagara plateau, Mali (Dogon country)
Dates: 1950 → 1976 (confidence: medium)

The Dogon people of Mali possessed inexplicable knowledge of Sirius B (invisible companion, ~50-year orbit, extreme density) — evidence of extraterrestrial contact (Robert Temple, The Sirius Mystery, 1976).

Earliest attestation: Marcel Griaule &amp; Germaine Dieterlen's 1950 publication on Dogon cosmology, based largely on conversations with one informant (Ogotemmêli)
Sources: The Sirius Mystery article: Temple's claim, van Beek's findings, Brosch's cultural-transfer explanation, Ridpath's 1978 critique; badarchaeology.com: van Beek's 1991 fieldwork, single-informant problem, Sigu tolo/to polo identifications
Falsifiability: Dated Dogon artifacts or records predating Western contact that unambiguously describe Sirius B's orbit and density would reopen the question. None exists; the claimed '400-year-old artifacts' have never been produced for examination.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1950-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.61,14.35,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1950</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Ipuwer-as-Exodus-plagues telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Egyptological consensus (Lichtheim, Enmarch, Parkinson, Quirke, Willems, Gardiner) places the composition centuries before any conventional Exodus date and reads it as literary lament, not reportage; 'river is blood' is a generic national-distress topos; the copy speaks of Asiatics ARRIVING in Egypt, not leaving
Layer: fake · Place: Egypt (the text's setting; the claim concerns the plagues' geography)
Dates: 1952 → 2026 (confidence: traditional)

The Admonitions of Ipuwer (Papyrus Leiden I 344) are an Egyptian eyewitness record of the ten plagues of Exodus: 'the river is blood' describes the first plague, and the text's chaos imagery corroborates the biblical narrative. (Claim form; adjudicated.)

Earliest attestation: Immanuel Velikovsky, Ages in Chaos (1952), reading Ipuwer as a plague record
Sources: Velikovsky, I. (1952). Ages in Chaos — the origin of the plague-reading; Habermehl, A. (2018). 'The Ipuwer Papyrus and the Exodus', Proceedings of the Eighth International Conference on Creationism; Enmarch, R. (2011). A World Upturned — Enmarch on 'river is blood' as red-sediment flood imagery or poetic turmoil; electsavedbygrace.substack.com (Oct 2026) — the telling's 2026 confessional recurrence, citing Admonitions ch. 5-7 as plague evidence; brianprogrammer.substack.com 'Prove It — or Disprove It: Installment Ten' (Oct 2026) — even-handed apologetic review that DROPS Ipuwer as evidence: Maarten Raven 'I see no connection'; Lichtheim 'historically impossible'; composition no earlier than late 12th Dynasty per Willems; Enmarch's red-sediment reading; Wilkinson on royal-propaganda function; aaronjudkins.substack.com 'The Archaeology of Egypt, Part 5' (2026-10-19/20) — evangelical series, same conclusion: Middle Kingdom composition 'fits no conventional Exodus date in the Late Bronze Age, so the poem predates the event it is said to record'; 'none of them name Israel, and none is a smoking gun'
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed Egyptological case placing the Admonitions' composition at a conventional Exodus date (c. 1446/1260/1208 BCE) AND identifying plague-specific (not generic-lament) parallels would reopen the claim</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1952-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>32.55,29.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1952</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Wheeler's 'Massacre' at Mohenjo-daro</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The skeletons span multiple stratigraphic levels (some post-abandonment); forensic analysis shows no fatal trauma; no invader weapons or material culture were found; no informed scholar now defends the invasion reading.
Layer: fake · Place: Mohenjo-daro, Sindh, Pakistan
Dates: 1953 → 1964 (confidence: high)

Sir Mortimer Wheeler's 1953 interpretation that 37 skeletons found at Mohenjo-daro were the victims of an invading army that massacred the city's inhabitants in a single violent event — 'Indra stands accused' of destroying the Harappan civilization.

Earliest attestation: Wheeler, M. — The Indus Civilization (1953)
Sources: Wheeler, M. — The Indus Civilization (1953); Wheeler's later qualification: 'This is a possibility, but it can't be proven, and it may not be correct'; Dales, G.F. (1964) — 'mythical massacre' refutation: skeletons from multiple stratigraphic levels, some in post-abandonment deposits; Kennedy, K. (1982/1984) — forensic reanalysis: skull 'damage' is post-mortem erosion, no fatal wound evidence; no proof the people were killed on the spot; Possehl, G. — Indus Age (1999): definitive obituary of the invasion hypothesis in the mainstream literature; Shinde et al. (2019, Cell) — Rakhigarhi ancient DNA: Harappan population was an indigenous South Asian group with deep continuity, no steppe-ancestry intrusion
Falsifiability: Already falsified. To revive it would require a secure single destruction layer containing contemporary massacre victims plus identifiable invader material culture — the exact evidence the excavations failed to produce</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1953-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>68.14,27.33,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1953</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The flawless-1955-launch myth (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the EIS's April 27, 1955 determination that two Cutter production pools (~120,000 doses) contained live poliovirus; (2) the casualty record — ~40,000 abortive cases, 51–56 paralytic cases with 5 deaths among recipients, plus 113 secondary paralytic cases and 5 deaths; (3) the Surgeon General's April 27 recall and the May 6, 1955 national program suspension. A 'flawless triumph' that was recalled within fifteen days is dead as a reading.
Layer: fake · Place: Berkeley, California (Cutter Laboratories)
Dates: 1955-04-12 → 1955-12 (confidence: high)

The Salk polio vaccine's rollout after its April 12, 1955 licensing was a smooth, flawless triumph — no vaccine-caused polio outbreak occurred during the launch of America's first polio vaccine.

Earliest attestation: EIS determination and Surgeon General recall, April 27, 1955 — the contemporary documents that killed the flawless-launch reading in real time.
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Cutter Laboratories' — the Cutter incident section: lots withdrawn April 27 despite passing safety tests; Brill JOAH 2(1–2) — EIS April 27, 1955 determination; Scheele recall; program suspension May 6; Michael E. Ruane, Washington Post, April 14, 2020 (via Offit 2005) — 'one of the worst biological disasters in American history: a man-made polio epidemic'
Falsifiability: Proof that no Cutter lot in April 1955 contained live virus (e.g., the EIS determination retracted), which would restore the flawless-launch reading</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1955-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-122.273,37.8715,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1955</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'AI was invented at Dartmouth 1956' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) working AI predated the workshop — McCulloch &amp; Pitts's 1943 neuron model, Turing's 1950 'Computing Machinery and Intelligence', Samuel's checkers program (begun at IBM 1952; Samuel attended Dartmouth), and decisively Newell &amp; Simon's Logic Theorist, developed at RAND in 1955–56 and demonstrated before/independently of the workshop — the conferees arrived with it; (2) the workshop's own thin output — of ~20 participants only three (McCarthy, Solomonoff, Minsky) stayed the full eight weeks, daily sessions ran 3–8 people, and the standard historical verdict records no breakthrough from the sessions themselves: the product was the network and the name.
Layer: fake · Place: Dartmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire (the workshop's site)
Dates: 1956 → 1956 (confidence: high)

Artificial intelligence was invented at the 1956 Dartmouth Summer Research Project — the field's working invention happened there, and the first AI programs and research originated at the workshop.

Earliest attestation: The popular shorthand 'birthplace of AI' for Dartmouth (textbook and press usage); the corrective documentary record is contemporary — the pre-1956 papers and programs below.
Sources: Newell &amp; Simon's Logic Theorist (1955–56, RAND) — 'Even before Dartmouth convened, Allen Newell, Herbert Simon, and Cliff Shaw had created the Logic Theorist (LT) at RAND — the first program deliberately engineered for automated reasoning' (AI: A Modern Approach history lineage); McCulloch &amp; Pitts 1943 ('A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity') and Turing 1950 ('Computing Machinery and Intelligence') — the pre-Dartmouth foundations; Arthur Samuel's checkers program begun at IBM 1952 (Samuel an attendee); Ray Solomonoff's contemporary Dartmouth notes — attendance reality (only three full-period stayers; 3–8 per daily session); the workshop's thin output is the standard historical verdict
Falsifiability: A contemporary record showing the first working AI reasoning program dated after August 1956, or evidence that the Logic Theorist's 1955–56 development is misdated</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1956-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-72.2887,43.7044,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1956</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The staged 'discovery' of the Berlin Tunnel (April 1956) — the accident that was scheduled</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the KGB knew the tunnel plan from late 1953 via Blake and made a documented decision to protect the source, withholding the knowledge even from the Soviet military users of the lines — there was nothing left to 'discover' by accident; (2) the 'accidental' discovery was itself a planned operation — declassified records describe the early-1956 Soviet plan for the tap's 'accidental' discovery, executed by a special signal corps team on the night of 21–22 April 1956 as a staged publicity coup (timed around Khrushchev's UK visit, with the British role deliberately downplayed).
Layer: fake · Place: Altglienicke, East Berlin (the tap chamber, south of the sector boundary)
Dates: 1956 → 1956 (confidence: high)

The claim that the Berlin Tunnel's unearthing on the night of 21–22 April 1956 was a genuine accident — a maintenance crew stumbling onto the American tap by luck (in the popular version, after heavy rains flooded the tunnel and exposed the wires).

Earliest attestation: Contemporary: the Soviet press conference of April 1956 presented the discovery as an accident. The debunk rests on declassified post-Cold-War records (CIA official history; post-Soviet releases) — not available to the contemporary press.
Sources: CIA official-history excerpt (booklife.com): 'Early in 1956, the Soviets developed a plan whereby the tap would be "accidentally" discovered'; 'On the night of 21-22 April 1956, a special signal corps team... penetrated the tunnel in the full glare of a well-organized publicity coup'; Encyclopedia of Espionage, 'Berlin Tunnel' (faqs.org): the KGB 'did not inform anyone in Germany, including the East Germans or the Soviet users of the cable lines'; when Blake was transferred in 1955 the Soviets were free to 'discover' the tunnel; 'Soviet troops began to dig on the night of April 21, 1956... Given ample warning, the Americans retreated behind the steel door'; Steve Vogel, 'Betrayal in Berlin' (via History News Network): Blake tipped the KGB; the service 'took no action against the tunnel' and 'didn't even warn Red Army commanders, for fear of compromising Blake'; the Soviets announced the discovery in April 1956
Falsifiability: A contemporaneous Soviet record showing genuine surprise (e.g. an internal damage-assessment that treats the tap as news) — none has ever surfaced; instead the record shows pre-knowledge and a plan</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1956-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>13.52,52.42,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1956</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'NASA invented Velcro' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 245th DEBUNKED family. Two kills. (1) The defendant's own witness: the Velcro company's own FAQ states 'NASA did not invent the hook and loop fastener. George de Mestral invented the technology, and NASA later popularized its use.' (2) Chronology: de Mestral filed for Swiss patent protection in 1951 and the first Swiss patent issued 16 March 1954 — NASA came into existence in 1958. An invention cannot predate the founding of its supposed inventor by seven years. NASA's genuine Apollo-era adoption (graded separately) is the myth's honest root.
Layer: fake · Place: Switzerland (de Mestral's filing jurisdiction; NASA founded in the United States)
Dates: 1958 → 2026 (confidence: medium)

NASA (the US National Aeronautics and Space Administration) invented Velcro, the hook-and-loop fastener.

Earliest attestation: The telling is folk/trivia transmission, undated; the debunking literature documents the belief's prevalence (NASA Spinoff reports, Museum of Flight myth-busting, phys.org 2012 'contrary to popular belief' framing).
Sources: Velcro Companies FAQ: 'Did NASA invent hook and loop fasteners?' — 'NASA did not invent the hook and loop fastener. George de Mestral invented the technology, and NASA later popularized its use by relying on VELCRO Brand fasteners to secure items in weightlessness during the space race and Apollo era.'; also: idea began 1941, Swiss filing 1951, first Swiss patent issued 16 March 1954.; Museum of Flight blog, 'Debunking NASA Space Spin-off Myths': Velcro invented in the 1940s by a Swiss engineer who observed burrs in his dog's fur; NASA recognized its usefulness in microgravity.; LiveScience, 'Who Invented Velcro?': 'Many sources attribute the creation of Velcro to NASA, though this claim proves quite false.' Apollo astronauts' 1960s use documented.; phys.org (NASA Spinoff coverage, 2012): 'Contrary to popular belief, Tang, Velcro and Teflon (along with the zero-gravity "space" pen) aren't derived from NASA technology.' — the belief's prevalence, carried as belief ecology.; AIER, 'NASA Did Not Invent Velcro: Public and Private Innovation': de Mestral, 1941 Alps walk with dog, decade of private development, no state direction or funding.
Falsifiability: A NASA (or predecessor NACA) document predating 1951 describing the hook-and-loop fastener, or evidence that de Mestral's 1951 Swiss filing was copied from US government work.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1958-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>6.6,46.5,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1958</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The severed hands: Shah Jahan mutilated the Taj Mahal's artisans to keep it unrepeatable</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills. (1) The empty primary record: four hundred years of Mughal court paperwork, Persian histories, and European travelers' accounts contain zero mentions of any artisan mutilation, and the ASI — the monument's guardian — states there is no evidence and calls it a folk tale. (2) The positive counter-evidence: the same chief architect, Ustad Ahmad Lahauri, went on to design Delhi's Red Fort with his own two hands afterward; Shah Jahan founded the Taj Ganj settlement to house the workers' families, whose descendants still live there practicing the marble craft today (Times of India / Manimugdha Sharma 2017). The trail leads back to 1960s guide folklore, not 1648.
Layer: fake · Place: Taj Mahal, Agra, Uttar Pradesh, India (the legend's scene)
Dates: 1960 → 2021 (confidence: traditional)

After the Taj Mahal was completed, the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan ordered the hands of the artisans and workers who built it to be cut off (or otherwise mutilated — killed, blinded, imprisoned) so that they could never build another monument like it.

Earliest attestation: No contemporary source of any kind: no Mughal court record, no Persian history, no European traveler's account from the construction period mentions it. The Archaeological Survey of India — the monument's official guardian — states there is no historical evidence for it and classifies it as a folk tale. Trained historians call it an urban myth; S. Irfan Habib traces it to 1960s word-of-mouth, and the 1971 Ranchi University Journal of Historical Research treats it as an urban legend.
Sources: Newschecker fact-check — 'Did Shah Jahan Cut Off The Hands Of Those Who Built Taj Mahal? No, It's Nothing But A Myth': quotes Shashank Shekhar Sinha, 'Delhi, Agra Fatehpur Sikri' (Pan Macmillan, 2021), trained historian (formerly University of Delhi): 'This story again has no historical basis and such tales are a part of folklore surrounding monuments in many cultures, including England, Ireland, Russia and parts of Asia.'; radiofree.asia fact-check — 'Fact-check: Did Shah Jahan chop off the hands of Taj Mahal workers?': historian S. Irfan Habib — 'neither any evidence to back this story nor any credible historian has ever made this claim'; urban myth traced to the 1960s, heard through word of mouth; Journal of Historical Research (Ranchi University, 1971) addresses it as an urban legend; Times of India 2017 debunk (Manimugdha Sharma): Taj Ganj settlement still exists, descendants still practice the crafts; The Guardian made the same claim six years earlier; Hindustan Times / Dawn on Rajiv Joseph's play.; DFRAC fact-check (December 2021) — refuting the News18/Amish Devgan broadcast; cites Aaj Tak / Live Hindustan reports calling the claim false and misleading.; missouri.framer.media — 'Myths of the Taj Mahal and the True Story of Shah Jahan's Artisans': the ASI has 'repeatedly stated that there is no historical evidence whatsoever' and classifies the story as a folk tale / urban legend; notes the logical flaw (designs live in plans and minds, not hands) and the multicultural design team (India, Persia, Central Asia, Ottoman Empire).; themonktravel.com — cites Diana and Michael Preston, 'Taj Mahal: Passion and Genius at the Heart of the Mughal Empire' (scaffold-removed-overnight anecdote); Fergus Nicholl, 'Shah Jahan: The Rise and Fall of the Mughal Emperor' (workers' origins: Hindus from Kannauj, flower carvers from Pokhara, Ram Lal of Kashmir on the garden): 'there is no evidence of this incident, nor has any historian mentioned it.'
Falsifiability: The claim is already falsified by the empty primary record plus the positive counter-evidence below; reviving it would require (a) a 17th-century Mughal or European account describing the mutilation, or (b) skeletal evidence from Mumtazabad/Taj Ganj consistent with mass hand amputation of the workforce.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1960-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>78.042,27.175,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1960</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Lions led by donkeys' as a real German generals' remark</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the cited source does not exist — the exchange appears nowhere in Falkenhayn's memoirs, which London and Stuttgart librarians searched in vain; (2) the author confessed, reportedly telling a friend 'well I invented it' when challenged on the provenance.
Layer: fake · Place: London — where 'The Donkeys' was published and where the attribution was first printed
Dates: 1961 → 1961 (confidence: high)

The phrase 'lions led by donkeys' was a genuine remark made by German generals in World War I — Erich Ludendorff saying 'The English soldiers fight like lions,' and Max Hoffmann replying 'True. But don't we know that they are lions led by donkeys,' in a conversation published in the memoirs of German chief of staff Erich von Falkenhayn.

Earliest attestation: Alan Clark, 'The Donkeys' (1961), epigraph — attributed to a Ludendorff-Hoffmann exchange 'published in the memoirs of General Erich von Falkenhayn.' No such passage has ever been found in Falkenhayn's memoirs, which remain untraced as containing it.
Sources: Alan Clark, 'The Donkeys' (1961), epigraph attribution; Daily Telegraph correspondence, July 1963 — librarians in London and Stuttgart failed to trace the quotation; Clark's letter unanswered; Euan Graham recollection (reported 2007): Clark, challenged on the provenance in the mid-1960s, 'looked sheepish and said "well I invented it"'; Richard Holmes on 'The Donkeys': the title's premise has 'no evidence whatever for this: none. Not a jot or scintilla'; Dan Snow, BBC 2014 ('10 big myths about World War One debunked'): 'the incident was made up by historian Alan Clark'; phrases.org.uk — phrase in wide use since mid-19th century; Clark's Hoffmann attribution incorrect
Falsifiability: A contemporary (pre-1961) printed attestation of the Ludendorff-Hoffmann exchange, or the passage located in a genuine edition of Falkenhayn's memoirs, would overturn this — sixty-plus years of searching has produced neither.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1961-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.13,51.51,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1961</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'resolved by resolve alone' reading of the Cuban Missile Crisis</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills: (1) the secret RFK–Dobrynin pledge documented in both sides' records; (2) the public no-invasion pledge in Kennedy's 27 Oct letter — a concession on the face of the settlement; (3) the 'Trollope Ploy' structure itself — publicly ignoring Khrushchev's 27 Oct letter while privately accepting its substance; (4) the executed quid pro quo — the Jupiters left Turkey by April 1963.
Layer: fake · Place: Washington D.C. (the settlement); the myth lived in textbooks and film
Dates: 1962-10-28 → 1989 (confidence: high)

The popular reading that the Cuban Missile Crisis was resolved PURELY by the American naval quarantine and U.S. resolve — with no American concessions — is false. The settlement of 28 October 1962 contained two American concessions: the public no-invasion pledge against Cuba (in Kennedy's 27 Oct letter) and the secret pledge to remove the Jupiter missiles from Turkey within months (delivered by Robert Kennedy to Dobrynin on the evening of 27 Oct, kept secret ~25 years). The resolve-only reading survived in textbooks and film precisely because the trade was secret; the declassifications of 1987–1989 killed it.

Earliest attestation: The myth is the absence: the public settlement record of 28 Oct 1962 contains no Jupiter clause; the correction's earliest attestation is the 1987–1989 declassification wave
Sources: evt-jupiter-trade-secret-1962-001 — the secret assurance, both sides' records; Kennedy's letter to Khrushchev, 27 Oct 1962 — the public no-invasion pledge; Scientific American, 'RFK's Secret Role in the Cuban Missile Crisis' — the secret kept until 1989; the settlement timeline; American Diplomacy, 'Robert Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis — A Reinterpretation' — the secret component of the settlement
Falsifiability: A showing that no secret assurance was given on 27 Oct 1962 — contradicted by the RFK–Dobrynin meeting records, Dobrynin's cable, and the Jupiter withdrawal executed by April 1963</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-77.0365,38.8977,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that the stirrup created feudalism</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills from the scholarly record: the chronology fails (true shock combat postdates the stirrup's arrival by centuries), Bachrach/Bullough demolished White's evidentiary chain in 1970, and the profession never answered them — White himself never replied.
Layer: fake · Place: Oxford, England (publication of the thesis, 1962)
Dates: 1962 → 1962 (confidence: high)

Lynn White Jr.'s 1962 thesis (Medieval Technology and Social Change): the stirrup's 8th-century arrival in Europe enabled true mounted shock combat, and Charles Martel — seeing this — built a cavalry army funded by confiscating Church lands, thereby creating feudalism.

Earliest attestation: Lynn White Jr., Medieval Technology and Social Change (Oxford, 1962), chapter 'Stirrup, Mounted Shock Combat, Feudalism, and Chivalry'
Sources: Lynn White Jr., Medieval Technology and Social Change (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1962) — chapter 1; contents page preserved at archive.org; Peter Sawyer, review 1963 — the 'technical determinism' critique (first critical response); D.A. Bullough (1970) and Bernard S. Bachrach (1970) — primary-source dismantling of White's evidentiary points, point by point; Kelly DeVries's survey: Bachrach's critique so meticulous that 'the debate basically went quiet'; White never answered, and no historian has resurrected the causal argument (as of 1992); Rebus Community — History of Applied Science &amp; Technology, ch. 5 (The Tools of War): 'the dates do not work out as White expected'; couched-lance shock combat is a later development; cavalry emphasis rises with Charlemagne's policies, not Martel's stirrups
Falsifiability: Contemporary 8th-century evidence that Martel's cavalry policy was stirrup-driven and that the fief system originated from that specific funding mechanism</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.25,51.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that the horse collar caused the medieval agricultural revolution</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills from the scholarly record: (1) the collar was Chinese centuries before White's European dating (Han-era depictions, documented in Needham's Science and Civilisation in China) — the device was inherited, not a medieval breakthrough; (2) Sawyer &amp; Hilton (1963, 'Technical determinism: the stirrup and the plough', Past &amp; Present 24) named the move itself — technology cannot be read as a single sufficient cause; (3) Langdon (1986, Horses, Oxen and Technological Innovation) showed English adoption was gradual and governed by relative prices and fodder costs, not by the device's arrival. The profession kept the collar and discarded the causal chain.
Layer: fake · Place: Oxford, England (publication of the thesis, 1962)
Dates: 1962 → 1962 (confidence: high)

Lynn White Jr.'s 1962 thesis (Medieval Technology and Social Change), chapter 'The Discovery of Horse-Power': the padded horse collar — which White dated to roughly 10th-century Europe — together with the nailed horseshoe, unlocked the horse as a draught animal, displaced the ox, and thereby CAUSED the medieval agricultural revolution: oat cultivation, the three-field rotation, the resulting food surplus, population growth, and the northward shift of Europe's civilizational center.

Earliest attestation: Lynn White Jr., Medieval Technology and Social Change (Oxford, 1962), chapters 'The Agricultural Revolution of the Early Middle Ages' and 'The Discovery of Horse-Power'
Sources: Lynn White Jr., Medieval Technology and Social Change (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1962) — chapters 'The Agricultural Revolution of the Early Middle Ages' and 'The Discovery of Horse-Power'; contents page preserved at archive.org; P.H. Sawyer &amp; R.H. Hilton, 'Technical determinism: the stirrup and the plough', Past &amp; Present 24 (1963) — the original 'technical determinism' label applied to both of White's causal claims; John Langdon, Horses, Oxen and Technological Innovation (1986) — English adoption gradual, driven by relative prices of horses vs oxen and fodder costs, not by the collar's arrival; Joseph Needham et al., Science and Civilisation in China — the padded horse collar attested in Han-era China, centuries before White's European dating; Thomas E. Lambert, 'The Horse Versus the Ox during Medieval Times' (2023, Univ. of Louisville) — horse/ox economics of medieval draught: the horse ~50% faster but costlier to feed; the mix was economic; W. Graessle, 'Lynn White Jr.'s Medieval Heavy Plow' (Footnotes: A Journal of History, 2018) — historiographical review of White's agricultural claims and their weakening under technical historians' scrutiny
Falsifiability: A sudden, collar-caused ox-to-horse substitution across northern Europe with no intervening price or land-regime change; or securely dated evidence that the padded collar was unknown outside Europe before 900 CE</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1962-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-1.25,51.75,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1962</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Lead poisoning caused the fall of Rome'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed twice: (1) the toxicology kill — Delile et al. 2014 (PNAS) measured the pipe lead in Rome's water (~100x natural levels) and concluded the contamination was 'unlikely to have been truly harmful' because a calcium-carbonate crust insulated the pipes and the water kept flowing; the study's own author: 'Lead is no longer seen as the prime culprit of Rome's demise'; (2) the philology kill — Scarborough 1984 showed the Romans knew lead was dangerous (Vitruvius, De architectura 8.6.10-11 warns water through lead pipes 'must be injurious'), drank wine watered rather than routinely sweetened, and cooked in copper, not lead-lined pots; Simpson &amp; Garvie-Lok 2026 note the archaeology has not even found the lead cooking vessels the sapa story requires.
Layer: fake · Place: Rome, Italy (myth's supposed locus)
Dates: 1965 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Lead poisoning caused the decline and fall of the Roman Empire — lead exposure from the Romans' lead water pipes (fistulae) and lead-sweetened wine (sapa/defrutum) was a primary cause of the Empire's collapse.

Earliest attestation: S. Colum Gilfillan, 'Lead Poisoning and the Fall of Rome,' Journal of Occupational Medicine 7(1), 1965 — restated earlier arguments by Karl Hofmann and Rudolf Kobert.
Sources: Delile, Hugo et al., 'Lead in ancient Rome's city waters,' PNAS 111(18):6594-6599, 2014 — sediment-core Pb isotopes from the Tiber bedload and Portus harbor; conclusion: tap-water lead clearly measurable but 'unlikely to have been truly harmful'; Scarborough, John, 'The Myth of Lead Poisoning Among the Romans: An Essay Review,' Transactions &amp; Studies of the College of Physicians of Philadelphia, 1984 — classicist's refutation of Nriagu: Romans knew the dangers, misread citations, watered wine, copper cookware; Gilfillan, S. Colum, 'Lead Poisoning and the Fall of Rome,' Journal of Occupational Medicine 7(1), 1965 — the myth's origin paper: aristocratic infertility/miscarriage/child mortality from lead vessels and adulterated wine, decline of innovation; Vitruvius, De architectura 8.6.10-11 — the Roman text that warns against lead: water conducted through earthen pipes is more wholesome than through lead; lead workers' pallor noted as evidence the metal 'destroys the vigor of the blood'; Simpson, R.M.L. &amp; Garvie-Lok, S.J., 'Lead exposure in the Roman Empire: a review of the written, material, and bioarchaeological evidence' (2026, Cambridge) — the 2026 review: the relative scarcity of lead cooking vessels is inconsistent with the texts-based sapa-poisoning theories; McConnell, Joseph et al. (Desert Research Institute), reported NYT 2025-01-06, 'Lead Poisoning May Have Made Ancient Romans a Bit Less Intelligent' — Arctic ice-core lead from Roman-era mining/smelting; ambient pollution, not the water-pipe fall story
Falsifiability: Skeletal lead burdens from across Roman social strata showing lead-induced infertility and mortality at population scale, or evidence that Roman tap water routinely exceeded levels known to cause mass poisoning — the actual 2014 study found the opposite ('unlikely to have been truly harmful').</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1965-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>12.5,41.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1965</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Walt Disney was cryogenically frozen' (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Multiply attested against: cremated two days after death, urn with ashes interred at the family mausoleum in Forest Lawn Memorial Park, Glendale; daughter Diane Disney Miller (1972 biography and 2012 Daily Mail): 'absolutely no truth' to the rumor, doubted he had heard of cryonics; Bob Nelson (California Cryogenics Society president, 1972 LA Times): 'They had him cremated. I personally have seen his ashes. They're in Forest Lawn. Two weeks later we froze the first man' — the first human cryopreservation (James Bedford) was January 1967, the year AFTER Disney died, making the legend chronologically impossible as well as factually false; Snopes point-by-point debunking; the Mosley (1986) and Eliot (1993) biographies that fed the legend were criticized for factual errors and unverifiable sources. The legend's persistence is itself documented (Gizmodo, AllEars, PBS, Joblo).
Layer: fake · Place: Forest Lawn Memorial Park, Glendale, California
Dates: 1966-12-15 → 1966-12-17 (confidence: high)

Walt Disney was cryogenically frozen after his death in December 1966 (in some versions, only his head, stored under Disneyland's Pirates of the Caribbean or Cinderella Castle).

Earliest attestation: Tabloid rumor, early 1967; Bob Nelson's 1972 Los Angeles Times remarks
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Cryonic Walt Disney urban legend': 'In reality, Walt Disney was cremated two days after his death and an urn containing his ashes was interred at the family mausoleum in Forest Lawn Memorial Park in Glendale, California.' Diane Disney Miller: 'absolutely no truth' to the rumor.; Gizmodo: Bob Nelson (1972 LA Times) — 'They had him cremated. I personally have seen his ashes. They're in Forest Lawn. Two weeks later we froze the first man. If Disney had been the first it would have made headlines around the world.'; AllEars.net: 'the first human being wasn't cryopreserved until 1967, the year after Disney died. Walt's remains were actually cremated and interred at Forest Lawn Memorial Park Cemetery in Glendale, California.'
Falsifiability: Cremation records or Forest Lawn interment records proven falsified</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1966-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-118.2436,34.1237,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1966</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Stevenson proved reincarnation' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The telling misrepresents its own source: Stevenson published 'suggestive evidence,' never proof, and the methodological objections stand unfalsified in print — 'proves' asserts what the evidence cannot bear. The cases are CORROBORATED; the proof-claim is DEBUNKED.
Layer: fake · Place: Charlottesville, Virginia (the telling cites UVA's Division of Perceptual Studies)
Dates: 1966 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Popular tellings present Ian Stevenson's case research as having PROVED reincarnation — the claim that the documented cases constitute proof that consciousness survives death and is reborn.

Earliest attestation: Popular reception of Twenty Cases Suggestive of Reincarnation (1966) — the 'suggestive of' in the title was dropped by the telling
Sources: Stevenson's own framing: the book titles themselves — 'Suggestive of Reincarnation,' 'Children Who Remember Previous Lives' — and his published position that the evidence is suggestive, not conclusive; Edwards, P. — Reincarnation: A Critical Examination (Prometheus Books, 1996): the mainstream critical literature's methodological objections (cryptomnesia, expectancy, cultural transmission); The telling: popularizations that drop the 'suggestive' — podcast/YouTube/advocacy claims of proof (e.g. reincarnationresearch.com: 'provide proof of reincarnation'); IFLScience, 19 Oct 2026 — the 2026 press state: the piece's own framing ('not hard science,' researchers 'drew much broader conclusions than their data could necessarily support') refuses the proof reading even while covering the cases
Falsifiability: Already falsified as a proof-claim: proof requires ruling out the conventional alternatives (cryptomnesia, expectancy, cultural transmission, fraud), which no study has done; the principal investigator's own 'suggestive' framing is the telling's refutation from inside its own source. The cases stand; the proof-claim dies.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1966-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-78.508,38.0336,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1966</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Nazca Runways' Claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim collapses on the archaeology: centimeters-deep soft-soil grooves cannot land aircraft; the 'runways' are continuous single-line figures tied to Nazca culture; no aviation artifacts, infrastructure, or indigenous flight tradition exists.
Layer: fake · Place: Nazca pampa, Ica region, Peru
Dates: 1968 → 1968 (confidence: high)

The claim (popularized by Erich von Däniken's 'Chariots of the Gods?', 1968) that the long straight lines and trapezoid shapes on the Nazca pampa were built as runways or landing strips for aircraft — ancient or extraterrestrial.

Earliest attestation: Erich von Däniken, Erinnerungen an die Zukunft / Chariots of the Gods? (1968), arguing the pampa was an ancient airfield.
Sources: Erich von Däniken, Chariots of the Gods? (1968) — the airfield/runway claim; Construction archaeology: the figures are shallow grooves a few centimeters deep made by clearing dark desert-varnish stones (iron/manganese oxides) over pale gypsum-caliche subsoil — structurally incapable of supporting heavy craft; Maria Reiche — debunked the von Däniken reading by linking geoglyph designs to Nazca pottery and culture (indigenous authorship); Field archaeology: lines form continuous single-line labyrinths and figurative geoglyphs (spider, monkey, hummingbird), not runway geometry; many figures are visible from the surrounding foothills — no flight required; Wanderlust / Oxford Business Group / general research consensus: the works are 'dismissed by most researchers as little more than science fiction'; zero aviation-era artifacts or infrastructure at the site
Falsifiability: Discovery of aircraft-grade landing infrastructure, aviation-era artifacts, or a documented indigenous flight tradition at the site would reopen the claim; a century of excavation has produced none</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1968-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.13,-14.739,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1968</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Nazca Runways' Claim</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claim collapses on the archaeology: centimeters-deep soft-soil grooves cannot land aircraft; the 'runways' are continuous single-line figures tied to the Nazca culture by pottery motifs and art-historical dating; no aviation artifacts, infrastructure, or indigenous flight tradition exists. The 2024 Sakai PNAS survey strengthened the refutation: with 733 figurative geoglyphs the pattern is testable — the smaller relief-type figures (81.6% human motifs) sit an average of 43 metres from ancient winding footpaths, at exactly walker-seeing scale ('billboards' for people on foot), and the giant line-type figures average 34 metres from the great line/trapezoid networks, suited to community ritual. The residual form of the claim — 'they must have been made for sky-viewers' — is answered by walked, hillside-visible design, not altitude.
Layer: fake · Place: Nazca pampa, Ica region, Peru
Dates: 1968 → 1968 (confidence: high)

The claim (popularized by Erich von Däniken's 'Chariots of the Gods?', 1968) that the long straight lines and trapezoid shapes on the Nazca pampa were built as runways or landing strips for aircraft — ancient or extraterrestrial.

Earliest attestation: Erich von Däniken, Erinnerungen an die Zukunft / Chariots of the Gods? (1968), arguing the pampa was an ancient airfield.
Sources: Erich von Däniken, Chariots of the Gods? (1968) — the airfield/runway claim; Construction archaeology: the figures are shallow grooves a few centimeters deep made by clearing dark desert-varnish stones (iron/manganese oxides) over pale gypsum-caliche subsoil — structurally incapable of supporting heavy craft; Maria Reiche — debunked the von Däniken reading by linking geoglyph designs to Nazca pottery and culture (indigenous authorship); Field archaeology: lines form continuous single-line labyrinths and figurative geoglyphs (spider, monkey, hummingbird), not runway geometry; many figures are visible from the surrounding foothills — no flight required; Wanderlust / Oxford Business Group / general research consensus: the works are 'dismissed by most researchers as little more than science fiction'; zero aviation-era artifacts or infrastructure at the site; Sakai, M. et al. (2024) 'AI-accelerated Nazca survey nearly doubles the number of known figurative geoglyphs and sheds light on their purpose', PNAS 121(40), e2407652121 — 303 new figurative geoglyphs confirmed in six months (Yamagata University + IBM Research); relief-type geoglyphs as trail-side 'billboards' at walker scale, line-type for community ritual. Directly answers the 'made for sky-viewers' residual of the runway claim.
Falsifiability: Discovery of aircraft-grade landing infrastructure, aviation-era artifacts, or a documented indigenous flight tradition at the site would reopen the claim; a century of excavation has produced none</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1968-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-75.13,-14.739,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1968</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The legend of the catastrophic 1969 Cuyahoga fire</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Three independent kills: (1) the documented brevity — ~24–30 minutes, ~$50,000 damage, per the fire department record and every secondary account; (2) the thirteen-fire century — 1868, 1883, 1887, 1912, 1922, 1936, 1941, 1948, 1952, then 1969 — the 1969 blaze was among the SMALLEST; (3) the photo the nation 'saw' was from the 1952 fire — the famous image shows a different, larger, seventeen-years-earlier blaze.
Layer: fake · Place: Cleveland, Ohio — the myth's home, not the fire's scale
Dates: 1969 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The June 1969 Cuyahoga River fire was a massive, catastrophic, singular blaze — a towering inferno that burned for hours or days, caused enormous damage, was the river's first fire, and was photographed in dramatic full color — the apocalyptic spark that woke the nation.

Earliest attestation: Time magazine, 1 August 1969 — the national article illustrated the 1969 fire with a photograph of the much larger 1952 blaze; National Geographic's December 1970 cover story ran the river with the fire's date wrong
Sources: Smithsonian Magazine: the 30-minute duration, the $50,000 damage, no photo of the 1969 fire, Time's 1 Aug 1969 photo substitution (1952 blaze), Stradling quote — 'The fire took on mythic status, and errors of fact became unimportant to the story's obvious meaning'; Outside Online: 24-minute extinguishment, 'no photos are thought to have been taken of it', the 1868 + 11-more sequence, Stokes's 23 June 1969 press conference and the Time article pipeline; Space Daily review: 'lasted somewhere between twenty and thirty minutes, damaged a couple of railroad bridges, cost around fifty thousand dollars' — and the key sentence: nothing about the fire itself was extraordinary; what changed was who noticed; Water Tech Online (EPA-derived 40th anniversary): the full earlier-fire list (1868, 1883, 1887, 1912, 1922, 1936, 1941, 1948, 1952) — the 1969 fire as the last of thirteen, not the first
Falsifiability: A contemporary (1969) photograph of a large, long-burning Cuyahoga blaze on 22 June 1969, or fire-department records showing damage in the millions — the Smithsonian reports no photo of the 1969 fire exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1969-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-81.6944,41.4993,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1969</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Pazuzu the possessor is the film's invention</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The tradition's Pazuzu is documented as an apotropaic figure invoked against Lamashtu; the possessing-demon reading originates with Blatty/Friedkin 1971–1973 and has no cuneiform warrant. 265th DEBUNKED family.
Layer: fake · Place: Assyria (the tradition's geography; the inversion happened in Hollywood, 1971–1973)
Dates: 1971 → 1977 (confidence: high)

William Peter Blatty's novel The Exorcist (1971) and William Friedkin's film (1973) made Pazuzu a possessing demon — the entity inside Regan MacNeil, named outright in Exorcist II: The Heretic (1977). In the cuneiform tradition Pazuzu was the wind-demon king invoked APOTROPAICALLY against Lamashtu (evt-pazuzu-001, CORROBORATED; Heeßel 2002): the film inverted the tradition's hired protection into its possessor. The possession reading is modern fiction, not ancient religion.

Earliest attestation: Blatty 1971 (novel); Friedkin 1973 (film); the Iraq-prologue Pazuzu statue
Sources: Blatty, The Exorcist (1971) — Iraq prologue, the Pazuzu statue; Regan's possession; Friedkin, The Exorcist (1973); Exorcist II: The Heretic (1977) — Pazuzu named as the possessing demon; Heeßel 2002 (Pazuzu's apotropaic function against Lamashtu — the tradition the film inverts); evt-pazuzu-001 (the tradition's record, CORROBORATED)
Falsifiability: A cuneiform text depicting Pazuzu possessing a human would overturn the inversion claim; none is known — his corpus is amulets, plaques, and anti-Lamashtu incantations.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1971-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>43.0,36.2,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1971</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Leonardo bicycle sketch (Codex Atlanticus)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) zero provenance — the sketch has no documented existence before its 1974 appearance during the Codex Atlanticus restoration (Lessing 1997); (2) material evidence — two inks on the page date from post-1880 and post-1920 (Lathière 1998).
Layer: fake · Place: Biblioteca Ambrosiana, Milan (the Codex Atlanticus)
Dates: 1974 → 1974 (confidence: high)

Leonardo da Vinci designed a bicycle (a two-wheeled, chain-driven machine) circa 1500, as shown in a sketch in the Codex Atlanticus.

Earliest attestation: The sketch has no documented existence before 1974 — it 'surfaced' during the restoration of the Codex Atlanticus and the 1974 facsimile publication, never transmitted through the historical record.
Sources: Hans-Erhard Lessing, paper to the International Cycling History Conference, Glasgow, August 1997 (reported in New Scientist, October 1997); Serge Lathière, 1998: ink analysis of the page — two inks dated post-1880 and post-1920; BikeBiz, 'Colnago duped by hoax': the sketch 'discovered' 1974 during Codex restoration; scholars have known it a forgery since the mid-1990s; Reuters coverage of the 2004 Rome exhibition 'Leonardo Da Vinci: A Curious Genius' (curator Carlo Barbieri: 'designed [diving suits, a bicycle and a car] 500 years before they could even be built') — belief ecology
Falsifiability: Pre-1974 documentation of the sketch in any catalogue or study of the Codex, or materials analysis showing period-appropriate ink and paper.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1974-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>9.19,45.4642,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1974</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Wite-Out came first' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — DEBUNKED — 246th DEBUNKED family. The chronology kill: Liquid Paper's origin (Mistake Out, 1951/1956, Dallas) predates Wite-Out's market entry in every source inspected this pass — Wikipedia's Liquid Paper history lists Wite-Out among the later 'competitors' Liquid Paper's market 'spawned', and the texashappens history states Liquid Paper 'inspired similar products, such as Wite-Out'. The myth's honest root: Wite-Out became America's dominant brand and 'white-out' became the generic word.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (category origin: Dallas, Texas; Wite-Out's own origin not dated this pass)
Dates: 1975 → 2026 (confidence: low)

Wite-Out was the first correction fluid — the brand that invented (or originated) the correction-fluid product category.

Earliest attestation: The telling is folk/trivia transmission, undated; the 'who invented white out' confusion is documented by the historycooperative.org piece 'Correcting History: Uncovering Who Invented White Out' (Jan 2024).
Sources: Wikipedia 'Liquid Paper': 'a multimillion-dollar market that had spawned several competitors, like Wite-Out' (by 1975: 25M bottles/year); texashappens, 'The Texas Secretary Who Invented Correction Fluid': 'Liquid Paper became so popular that it inspired similar products, such as Wite-Out'; BIC product listing: Wite-Out as 'America's No. 1 correction brand' (NPD Group, 12ME June 2022) — the dominance behind the myth's honest root; historycooperative.org, 'Correcting History: Uncovering Who Invented White Out' (Jan 18, 2024) — documents the 'who invented white-out' confusion
Falsifiability: A dated pre-1951 Wite-Out product or company record, or evidence that Graham's 'Mistake Out' postdates Wite-Out's first sale.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1975-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-96.797,32.7767,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1975</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'The Moon landings were faked'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Falsified five independent ways: (1) MATERIAL — 382 kg of lunar rocks analyzed worldwide, composition unique to an airless impact environment, cross-checked against Soviet Luna samples; (2) OPTICAL — LRO's ~0.5 m/pixel images of all six sites plus independent imaging by Japanese, Indian and Chinese orbiters; (3) PHYSICAL — retroreflectors at the Apollo 11/14/15 sites still returning Earth laser pulses; (4) CONTEMPORANEOUS — Jodrell Bank, Bochum, and Soviet stations tracked the missions live; (5) ADVERSARIAL — the USSR had the motive, the means, and its own lunar probes in flight, yet never denounced the landings as faked.
Layer: fake · Place: The claim is about fabrication on Earth, not a place; pin carried at the stated landing site (Mare Tranquillitatis) for reference
Dates: 1976 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The Apollo program's crewed Moon landings (1969-1972) never happened — NASA staged them on Earth, in a studio or a remote desert, to fool the public.

Earliest attestation: Bill Kaysing, 'We Never Went to the Moon: America's Thirty Billion Dollar Swindle' (1976); the mass-market form is the Fox TV special 'Conspiracy Theory: Did We Land on the Moon?' (2001)
Sources: Bill Kaysing, 'We Never Went to the Moon: America's Thirty Billion Dollar Swindle' (1976) — origin of the popular hoax claim; Kaysing worked for Rocketdyne 1956-1963, not as an engineer; The five independent evidence lines for the landings, carried in full in evt-apollo-11-landing-001 (samples; LRO/SELENE/Chandrayaan-1 imagery; retroreflector laser ranging; third-party tracking; Soviet non-denunciation); MythBusters, 'NASA Moon Landing' (Season 6, 2008): physical tests of the flag-in-vacuum, footprint-in-regolith, and single-light-source shadow claims — the 'tells' reproduced and explained with lunar physics; Gallup 1999 / YouGov belief context (see research log): the claim lives as a minority belief (6% per Gallup 1999; 18% per YouGov US 2023) — the belief is real and graded as belief data, never as evidence
Falsifiability: The honest asymmetry, stated on the record: no single experiment can disprove a conspiracy that treats every counter-evidence as planted — the hoax claim is DEBUNKED because the positive evidence for the landings is convergent, independent, and includes hostile-power monitoring, not because one test kills faking as a concept</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1976-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>23.4731,0.6741,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1976</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The '24-chromosome blood of Christ' claim (Ron Wyatt) fails basic biology</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No publication, no sample, no lab; the chromosome claim contradicts human biology
Layer: fake · Place: 
Dates: 1982 → 1982 (confidence: high)

Ron Wyatt's claim (from 1982) of Christ's blood found under the crucifixion site — '24 chromosomes' (23 from Mary + 1 Y from the Father), allegedly rehydrated 'living' after 2,000 years — has no peer-reviewed publication, no documented lab, no verifiable sample. It fails basic biology: viable human blood has 46 chromosomes, and 2,000-year-old 'living' blood cells are impossible. Graded DEBUNKED.

Earliest attestation: Wyatt's claim, 1982 (no publication)
Sources: No peer-reviewed source exists (that is the finding)
Falsifiability: A verifiable sample in a real lab would reopen; none has ever been produced</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1982-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.21,31.77,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1982</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'It never happened': the urban-legend dismissal of the Atari burial, killed by the 2014 dig</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed two ways. (1) The wrong chronology: the burial was not a later invention — September 1983 newspaper accounts reported 14 trucks dumping Atari products at the Alamogordo dump, with company officials saying the waste was mostly broken and returned merchandise. The denial tradition pretends the story was born after the fact; it was reported in the moment. (2) The dig: on 26 April 2014, an excavation by Fuel Industries and Microsoft's Xbox Entertainment Studios (documented as 'Atari: Game Over') pulled ~1,300 cartridges of 60+ titles out of the concrete-covered site — E.T. among them, some with boxes and manuals. 881 were later sold on eBay with numbered city certificates of authenticity for $107,000+, proceeds to Alamogordo's library, zoo, police, and the Tularosa Basin Museum of History; one E.T. cartridge from the dig went to the Smithsonian's National Museum of American History (reported via 2014 press). Andrew Reinhard's subsequent archaeological analysis (Journal of Contemporary Archaeology) treats the site as the first formal archaeological excavation of a video-game assemblage. You cannot un-find 1,300 cartridges.
Layer: fake · Place: Alamogordo landfill, Otero County, New Mexico
Dates: 1983 → 2014 (confidence: high)

Atari never buried any unsold E.T. cartridges in the New Mexico desert — the whole story is just an urban legend that never happened.

Earliest attestation: The 'never happened' reading is the gaming-press and fan-community dismissal tradition of the 1990s–2000s: ranked among the biggest urban legends of all time; 'Even Atari said it didn't happen' (Joe Lewandowski, burial witness, New Mexico Magazine Apr 2025). The event it denies was itself reported in September 1983 newspapers.
Sources: New Mexico Magazine (Apr 23, 2025): Joe Lewandowski, the waste-collection owner who witnessed the burial: 'It was ranked as one of the biggest urban legends of all time... Even Atari said it didn't happen'; the 2014 team located 1,382 cartridges including E.T., Centipede, Pac-Man under ~30 feet of trash and concrete.; Wikipedia — 'Atari video game burial': 'Since the burial was first reported, there had been doubts as to its veracity and scope, and it was frequently dismissed as an urban legend'; 2014 excavation 'revealed discarded games and hardware.'; Polygon (Nov 13, 2014): first excavated cartridges sold on eBay; crushed-but-authentic E.T. copy bid to $1,537; numbered certificates of authenticity from the city of Alamogordo 'proving the legend to be true'; ~1,300 cartridges pulled from the landfill; documentary 'Atari: Game Over' debuted Nov 20 on Xbox.; Times Now World: excavation 'finally resolved' the legend in 2014; ~1,300 cartridges unearthed; Andrew Reinhard (Journal of Contemporary Archaeology) — 'the first formal archaeological dig focused on a collection of video games'; E.T. developed in ~5.5 weeks; sold only slightly over one million units of a four-million goal; Smithsonian's National Museum of American History cites the failure as 'a contributing factor' to the 1982–1985 industry crash.
Falsifiability: The denial would stand if the burial had been first reported decades later with no contemporary record, or if the 2014 excavation had dug the marked site and found nothing. Instead: September 1983 newspapers reported 14 trucks dumping Atari product at Alamogordo; the 26 April 2014 excavation recovered ~1,300 physical cartridges (60+ titles, E.T. among them) from the concrete-covered site with a city-certified custody chain. A denial that physical evidence this complete exists cannot stand.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1983-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-105.989361,32.739278,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1983</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Orion correlation theory (Giza 'mirrors Orion's Belt')</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The claimed correlation fails on its own terms in the peer-reviewed archaeoastronomy literature; no specialist venue defends it
Layer: fake · Place: Giza plateau
Dates: 1983 → 2026 (confidence: low)

The 'Orion correlation theory' (Robert Bauval, 1983/1994, popularized in The Orion Mystery) holds that the layout of the Giza pyramids mirrors the stars of Orion's Belt and that the 'airshafts' point at specific stars — evidence of astronomically precise, supposedly impossible knowledge. The theory was never published in a peer-reviewed archaeology or astronomy venue; Egyptologist-archaeoastronomers (notably Juan Belmonte's survey work) showed the fit fails: the belt stars' brightness order does not match the pyramids' size order, the southern 'shaft' angles do not hold across the measured stellar positions at 2500 BCE, and the theory treats the pyramids as an isolated cipher rather than a 4th-Dynasty royal necropolis with a documented construction context.

Earliest attestation: Bauval's 1983 discussion in the Journal of the British Interplanetary Society (not a peer-reviewed archaeology venue)
Sources: Bauval 1994 — The Orion Mystery (the telling's own venue, not peer-reviewed); Belmonte — archaeoastronomy of ancient Egypt (the peer-reviewed survey corpus: brightness-order and shaft-angle mismatches); Bauval &amp; Gilbert popular-press chain — carried as the telling, not as evidence
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed measurement showing the pyramid layout statistically matches the 2500 BCE belt configuration in position, orientation, and relative magnitude</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1983-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>31.1342,29.9792,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1983</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The claim that lead-poisoned tinned food destroyed the Franklin expedition</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills on the claim as stated. (1) THE BASELINE WAS WRONG: the original inference compared Victorian sailors to modern cadavers (18-50 ppm); the 2018 synchrotron study with properly contemporary controls — Royal Navy dead of the same era from the Antigua cemetery — found the Franklin sailors' lead distributions were not unusual for the time: no marked elevation near time of death, no 'pivotal role.' (2) THE SOURCE WAS CHALLENGED: Farrer's 1993 challenge to the solder-isotope linkage, and Battersby's documented alternative — the ships' unique water systems as the far likelier source. The 'tins killed them' narrative survives in textbooks and tours; the lab record killed it.
Layer: fake · Place: Beechey Island, Nunavut
Dates: 1987 → 1987 (confidence: high)

Lead poisoning — from the lead solder sealing the expedition's tinned-food provisions — was the principal cause of the death and disintegration of the 1845 Franklin expedition.

Earliest attestation: Beattie &amp; Geiger, Frozen in Time (1987) — the popular form: lead from the cans 'the ultimate cause of the Franklin expedition's demise.'
Sources: Beattie &amp; Geiger, Frozen in Time (1987) — the popular claim: solder-matched lead in the bodies proves tinned food caused the deaths; Canadian Geographic, 'Celebrating 30 years of Franklin fascination' — the correction-in-progress: 'we've learned a good deal more, and recent research on the lead issue has rendered a more complex verdict'; baselines higher than realized; scurvy, tuberculosis, exhaustion as aggravating factors; Swanston et al., PLOS ONE 2018 (doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0202983) — synchrotron confocal X-ray fluorescence of bone microstructure with contemporary Royal Navy controls (Antigua cemetery): 'the skeletal microstructural [lead] distribution data do not support the conclusion that [lead] played a pivotal role in the loss of Franklin and his crew'; Battersby (Hakluyt Society) — the source-kill: 'massive and sustained intake of lead' from the ships' unique water systems, 'a much more likely source ... than the tinned food'; tin-source challenged
Falsifiability: A study with properly matched contemporary controls showing Franklin crew lead markedly above that baseline, or isotope evidence re-linking the burden specifically to the expedition's tins, would overturn this record.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1987-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-91.9,74.7,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1987</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Pray Codex 'proves' the Shroud of Turin existed in 1192</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — A resemblance, however striking, is not an identification: the skeptical conventional-iconography reading is unfalsified, and 'proves' asserts what the evidence cannot bear. The illumination is CORROBORATED; the identification stays CLAIMED; the proof-claim is DEBUNKED.
Layer: fake · Place: Budapest (National Széchényi Library)
Dates: 1993 → 2026 (confidence: high)

Apologetic tellings present the Pray Codex's shroud illumination as a 'fingerprint' or 'DNA match' that PROVES the Shroud of Turin existed in Constantinople before 1192 — i.e., the illumination is treated as a positive identification of the cloth as the Shroud of Turin.

Earliest attestation: Lejeune's 1993 CIELT Rome presentation and its popularizations (the 'had before his eyes' reading hardened into 'proves')
Sources: Lejeune, J. (1993) — the first-hand correspondences, as popularized ('had before his eyes'); Apologetic popularizations: the 'fingerprint/DNA match' tellings (blogspot FAQ, catholic.com 'Trial of the Shroud' — the illumination presented as proof of pre-1192 existence); evt-pray-codex-shroud-illumination-001 (the CORROBORATED illumination the telling overclaims from)
Falsifiability: Already falsified as a proof-claim: proof requires ruling out the conventional-iconography alternative, which no study has done. The telling dies; the identification question stays open.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1993-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>19.0402,47.4979,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1993</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Deep Blue thought like a human' myth</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Disproven by the builders' own published technical account (Campbell, Hoane &amp; Hsu 2002): Deep Blue was a 32-node RS/6000 SP with 256 dedicated VLSI chess processors searching ~100–200 million positions per second with hand-crafted evaluation, a grandmaster-built opening library (GM Joel Benjamin), and endgame tablebases — no learning capability in the match build. The 'thinking' was a brute-force search tree with human-written chess knowledge; the machine could not learn a single thing during the match (contemporary Crimson commentary, 12 May 1997). The victory is real; the reading of it as machine 'thought' is not.
Layer: fake · Place: Equitable Center, New York City (the match's site)
Dates: 1997 → 1997 (confidence: high)

Deep Blue defeated Garry Kasparov in 1997 by out-thinking him — playing and thinking like a human chess player, demonstrating machine intelligence in the human sense.

Earliest attestation: 1997 worldwide press and IBM marketing framing of the match as an 'intelligent machine' victory; Kasparov's own post-match claim that the play felt human
Sources: Campbell, Hoane &amp; Hsu, 'Deep Blue', Artificial Intelligence 134(1–2), 57–83 (2002) — 200M positions/sec, alpha-beta search, hand-tuned evaluation, grandmaster opening book; no in-match learning; The Harvard Crimson, 12 May 1997 — 'Deep Blue didn't and couldn't learn a single thing throughout the match, as it relied on its sheer processing power to calculate its next move. Relentless computation is not artificial intelligence.' (contemporary counterweight to the hype framing); Kasparov's post-match remarks (1997) — the 'human-aid' suspicion reported in press; allegation attested, evidence never produced (kept as competing hypothesis, not as the debunk's target)
Falsifiability: IBM's match-build logs showing self-modifying strategy or in-match learning — the builders' published account says the opposite</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1997-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-73.9855,40.758,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1997</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 1998 Lancet MMR-autism fraud (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills: (1) Deer's 2004 Sunday Times investigation exposed undisclosed payment from the anti-MMR lawsuit's lawyers plus Wakefield's patent on a rival single-measles vaccine; (2) 10 of 12 co-authors retracted the interpretation (March 2004); (3) the GMC (January 2010) found dishonest research and unnecessary invasive procedures on developmentally delayed children without ethical approval — struck off May 2010; (4) The Lancet fully retracted (February 2010, 'utterly false'); the BMJ called it 'an elaborate fraud' (January 2011). The claim itself fails on epidemiology: Madsen 2002 (537,303 children) and Hviid 2019 (657,461; adjusted HR 0.93) find no association.
Layer: fake · Place: Royal Free Hospital, Hampstead, London (Wakefield's 1998 research base)
Dates: 1998 → 2010 (confidence: high)

MMR vaccination causes autism (the claim built on Wakefield et al.'s 1998 Lancet paper).

Earliest attestation: Wakefield et al., 'Ileal-lymphoid-nodular hyperplasia, non-specific colitis, and pervasive developmental disorder in children', The Lancet 351(9103):637-641, 28 February 1998 (fully retracted 2010).
Sources: Wakefield et al. 1998, The Lancet 351(9103):637-641 — the paper; fully retracted February 2010, editor-in-chief Richard Horton: 'utterly false', journal 'deceived'; Deer, B. — Sunday Times investigation 2004: undisclosed payment from the Legal Aid Board lawyers pursuing the anti-MMR lawsuit; Wakefield held a patent on a rival single-measles vaccine; 10 of 12 co-authors retracted the interpretation (March 2004); General Medical Council verdict 28 January 2010: dishonest research; acted against patients' best interests; developmentally delayed children subjected to unnecessary invasive procedures (colonoscopy, lumbar puncture) without ethical approval; struck off the Medical Register 24 May 2010; BMJ, January 2011: Godlee editorial — 'an elaborate fraud'; Deer's BMJ series showing the medical records were falsified (children did not have the inflammatory bowel disease the paper reported); Madsen et al. 2002, NEJM — 537,303 Danish children: no association between MMR and autism; Hviid et al. 2019, Annals of Internal Medicine — 'Measles, Mumps, Rubella Vaccination and Autism: A Nationwide Cohort Study': 657,461 Danish children (born 1999-2010), fully adjusted hazard ratio 0.93 — no increased risk, including in susceptible subgroups (siblings with autism); follow-up to the 2002 study; Cochrane systematic review 2020 — no evidence of increased autism risk from MMR/MMRV
Falsifiability: A replicated, independently reproduced study with raw-data access showing elevated autism risk after MMR; instead, two decades of population studies (Denmark 537k + 657k, UK, US, Taylor 2014 meta-analysis, Cochrane 2020) show no association.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>1998-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-0.17,51.55,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>1998</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Interstate 'one mile in five' landing-strip rule (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The Federal Highway Administration's own 'Interstate Highway System — The Myths' page kills the reading outright — the claim has no basis in law, regulation, design manual, or fact — and the documented root (the 1941 Act's Section 108 flight strips, placed near highways) explains the confusion without saving the claim.
Layer: fake · Place: United States (the Interstate Highway System)
Dates: 2000 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The United States Interstate Highway System was designed so that one mile in every five is straight, for use as emergency landing strips for airplanes in wartime.

Earliest attestation: Richard F. Weingroff's May/June 2000 Public Roads article 'Interstates as Runways: It 'Aint So' — responding to a claim already widespread on the Internet and in reference sources.
Sources: FHWA, 'Interstate Highway System — The Myths' — 'One in five miles of the Interstate System is straight so airplanes can land in emergencies': 'This myth is widespread on the Internet and in reference sources, but has no basis in law, regulation, design manual—or fact. Airplanes occasionally land on Interstates when no alternative is available in an emergency, not because the Interstates are designed for that purpose.'; Richard F. Weingroff, 'Interstates as Runways: It 'Aint So' (Public Roads, May/June 2000) — the FHWA information liaison specialist traces the myth's root to the Defense Highway Act of 1941, Section 108's flight-strip program (PRA + Army Air Force strips near highways); notes the 1944 Act came close to including another flight-strip program but did not, and the 1956 Act contains no such language; HowStuffWorks, 'Were U.S. Interstates Really Designed as Runways?' — the Defense Highway Act provided flight strips mostly for military use that were near highways; all three acts (1941, 1944, 1956) are real, none contains the one-in-five language; Lead Stories fact check, 2021-06 — the recirculating Facebook 'Fun fact of the day' post ('The Eisenhower interstate system requires that one mile in every five must be straight...') rated not true
Falsifiability: Produce the law, regulation, or design manual containing the one-mile-in-five requirement — the FHWA's challenge; none has ever been produced because no such text exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2000-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-98.5,39.8,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2000</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Like painting the Forth Bridge' — the never-ending job (DEBUNKED)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two independent kills: (1) the event — the 2002–2011 restoration ended the continuous cycle: old paint blasted to bare metal, triple layer of specialist glass-flake epoxy (offshore-oil-industry chemistry, W J Leigh), designed to last 25 years; on 9 December 2011 the bridge was scaffolding-free for the first time in a decade and 'no additional full-scale repainting will be needed for at least 20 years'; (2) the owner's own words — Network Rail's Iain Coucher: the restoration would 'preserve the steel-work for decades to come'; the Sun's Solin Hardie: 'For the first time there will be no painters required on the bridge. Job done!'
Layer: fake · Place: Firth of Forth, Scotland
Dates: 2002 → 2011 (confidence: high)

Painting the Forth Bridge is a never-ending task — a job that must begin again the moment it is finished, because by the time one end is painted the other end already needs a fresh coat.

Earliest attestation: The idiom 'like painting the Forth Bridge' — established by common usage; the ANI report (6 Sept 2011): the phrase 'came into being because of the task of painting the bridge in Scotland, which would start again as soon as the workmen reached the end.'
Sources: newcivilengineer.com (9 Dec 2011): painting complete after 10-year, £130m refurbishment; scaffolding removed; no full paint job for at least 20 years; old paint removed by abrasive blasting; triple layer of glass-flake epoxy designed for 25 years, expected to last much longer; newcivilengineer.com (19 Feb 2008): 'The announcement explodes the popular myth that the painting of the 120-year old structure is a job that never ends'; Balfour Beatty £74m contract; glass-flake epoxy designed for at least 20 years, anticipated up to 30; Asian News International via thefreelibrary.com (6 Sept 2011): ~10 years, ~200 workers, 240,000 litres of glass-flake epoxy; 'For the first time there will be no painters required on the bridge. Job done!'; the idiom's origin stated: the job 'would start again as soon as the workmen reached the end'; edinburghlive.co.uk: repeated repainting continued until 2002, when glass-based epoxy was introduced; workers completed the last repainting in 2011; now ~once every 25 years; next due ~2027; edinburghlive.co.uk (21 Jul 2020): the 25-year paint job 'appears to be peeling off just nine years later' — the lifespan claim publicly questioned; carried as the unresolved fence on the 25-year promise, not on the myth
Falsifiability: Find a continuous repainting cycle on the bridge after December 2011 — the owner-operator's record says there is none: 'no additional full-scale repainting will be needed for at least 20 years.'</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2002-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-3.3886,56.0004,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2002</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The '1421' thesis: Zheng He's fleet discovered America</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The thesis is rejected across the professional literature: Robert Finlay, 'How Not to (Re)Write World History' (Journal of World History, June 2004) demolished the method; Timothy Brook (2019) advises serious readers to avoid Menzies entirely; the '1418 map' star evidence is an 18th-century copy of a European map (Geoff Wade, Asia Research Institute; Sally Church, Cambridge, via LiveScience); no Ming document supports voyages beyond the Indian Ocean; claimed artifacts lack archaeological context.
Layer: fake · Place: Beijing (publication/imperial-center anchor of the claim)
Dates: 2002 → 2002 (confidence: high)

Gavin Menzies' 2002 thesis (1421: The Year China Discovered the World): that during or after Zheng He's sixth voyage, detached Chinese fleets circumnavigated the globe, reached the Americas, Australia, and Antarctica, mapped them, and returned — so China 'discovered' America ~70 years before Columbus and the world ~100 years before Magellan.

Earliest attestation: Gavin Menzies, 1421: The Year China Discovered the World (London: Bantam, 2002)
Sources: Robert Finlay, 'How Not to (Re)Write World History: Gavin Menzies and the Chinese Discovery of America', Journal of World History, June 2004; Geoff Wade, '1421 And All That' (H-Asia exchange, June 2006) — 'not one single piece of recognised evidence'; Timothy Brook (2019) — 'Serious readers are advised to avoid anything written by Gavin Menzies'; Sally K. Church (Cambridge) and Geoff Wade on the '1418 map': an 18th-century copy of a European map (LiveScience reporting)
Falsifiability: A Ming-dynasty document, inscription, shipwreck, or excavated artifact context placing a Zheng He fleet in the Atlantic or Pacific (or the Americas) would reopen it; Menzies' 'evidence' is repeatedly shown to be misdated, misread, or contextless — and the bar he would need (fleet-scale traces) is a standard his own case set, not an invention of his critics</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2002-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>116.4,39.9,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2002</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Sarajevo Sandwich Myth (the lunch-hour coincidence)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Four independent kills: (1) the trial record — neither Princip nor any witness ever mentions eating; (2) the route — Schiller's stood on the original planned motorcade route, so Princip was stationed where he should have been, not where luck put him; (3) the clock — ~10:55 a.m., early for lunch; (4) the provenance — no telling predates the 2003 documentary, and the sandwich is not a 1914 Bosnian menu item (Serbian readers: it would have been burek or pljeskavica).
Layer: fake · Place: Sarajevo — Moritz Schiller's delicatessen, Franz Josef Street
Dates: 2003 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The telling that Gavrilo Princip only happened to be standing outside Moritz Schiller's delicatessen because he had stepped in to buy a sandwich — and that the Archduke's car stopped right in front of him by outrageous coincidence, so that 'no sandwich, no shooting; no shooting, no war.'

Earliest attestation: Lion TV 'Days That Shook the World' (2003), at 5:15: 'Gavrilo Princip has just eaten a sandwich, and is now standing outside Schiller's delicatessen ... Completely by chance, fate has brought the assassin and his target within 10 feet of each other.'
Sources: Mike Dash, 'The Origin of the Tale that Gavrilo Princip Was Eating a Sandwich When He Assassinated Franz Ferdinand' (Smithsonian, 15 Sept 2011) — trial-transcript research by Gaius Trifkovic; documents that no telling predates 2003 and no witness ever mentioned eating; Princip's trial testimony (via Trifkovic/Dash): 'merely said he was present in the vicinity of the Latin bridge when the car came along'; witnesses Mihajlo Pusara (talking to Princip moments before), Smail Spahovic, and Milan Drnic (6 paces away, saw the Browning) — none mentions eating; Joachim Remak (1959): Princip waited OUTSIDE Schiller's, where he spoke to a friend — no lunching; Roberta Strauss Feuerlicht: Schiller's stood on the ORIGINAL planned motorcade route; David James Smith (2008): the murder took place around 10:55 a.m., early for lunch; Christopher Clark (NPR/WCMU, 2014): 'it's a myth that Princip had just stepped out of a general store after buying a sandwich' — the miscommunication took the royal couple 'right in front of Gavrilo Princip, one of the assassins who was stationed on the original path'; Richard Bond (writer/director, 'Days That Shook the World'), email to Dash: research was 'incredibly meticulous' but he could no longer remember how he sourced the sandwich detail — 'It's possible that sandwich was a colloquial translation that appeared in these sources'
Falsifiability: A pre-2003 source attesting the sandwich, or trial testimony / a witness statement describing Princip eating anything that morning — the trial record is silent, and every telling found dates after 2003</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2003-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>18.41,43.85,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2003</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Semmelweis 'persecuted martyr' narrative (popular version)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The martyrdom narrative is falsified point by point by the contemporary record: the discovery was circulated contemporaneously (Hebra 1847-48, Routh 1848); the Vienna clinic kept the chlorine washings while he was there; Semmelweis himself published almost nothing for fourteen years and his 1861 book's open letters called critics murderers; modern historians (Carter &amp; Carter 2005; Loudon) attribute the slow acceptance to a missing germ-theory mechanism, weak publication, polemics, and politics — not a conspiracy of ridicule.
Layer: fake · Place: Vienna (the historiographic claim's origin point)
Dates: 2003 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The popular 'persecuted martyr' narrative — that Ignaz Semmelweis was mocked and ridiculed by a closed medical establishment that refused to look at his evidence, and that he was driven into an asylum and to his death by their persecution — is an exaggeration of the real record, largely assembled by later popularizers (notably Nuland's The Doctor's Plague, 2003).

Earliest attestation: Popular retellings; Nuland, S. B. — The Doctor's Plague (2003) as the modern martyr-narrative's chief popularizer.
Sources: Carter, K. C. &amp; Carter, B. R. — Childbed Fever: A Scientific Biography of Ignaz Semmelweis (2005); Nuland, S. B. — The Doctor's Plague (2003) — the popular martyr narrative's chief modern popularizer; NY Review of Books, 'The Fool of Pest' (2004) — review of Nuland; notes Semmelweis left publication to colleagues, Hebra published Dec 1847/Apr 1848, Routh reported 1848; Springer Medizin, 'Ignac Semmelweis — Father of Hand Hygiene' — mortality drop to ~2.4% after one year of chlorine handwashing; evt-semmelweis-handwashing-1847-001 (VERIFIED) and evt-semmelweis-rejection-001 (CORROBORATED) — the two records this myth distorts
Falsifiability: Contemporary documentary proof of an organized campaign of mockery — ridicule as policy — or that the discovery was systematically suppressed rather than widely circulated</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2003-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>16.3531,48.2177,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2003</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Ark-Encounter/Creation-Museum-exhibit-as-evidence telling (dino-human coexistence)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The telling dies on provenance: the exhibits are the claimant's own staging, not an independent witness. The Paluxy 'man tracks' — the one exhibit-adjacent claim that once had a physical anchor — were autopsied in the field (Kuban) and partially retracted by their own side (Morris 1986, ICR); the remaining displays rest on nothing the museums dug up. DEBUNKED as evidence; the attractions themselves are graded as real public events (the 1.5M-visitor figure is the ministry's own claim, fenced). The Catholic Church's 2014 position (Francis to the Pontifical Academy: evolution does not conflict with Creation) is carried as competing context — the telling is a young-earth sectarian reading, not the Christian consensus.
Layer: fake · Place: Ark Encounter, Grant County, Kentucky, USA
Dates: 2007 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The dioramas, animatronics, and placards at Answers in Genesis's Creation Museum (Petersburg, KY) and Ark Encounter (Grant County, KY) — the 'dragons were dinosaurs' exhibit, dinosaur animatronics in cages, dinosaurs standing with Adam and Eve in the Garden of Eden, dinosaurs aboard the Ark — constitute historical evidence that humans and non-avian dinosaurs lived at the same time. FALSE: a diorama is a claim in three dimensions, and the claim it makes is the young-earth telling — Day-6 creation, juveniles on the Ark, post-Flood climate extinction — which the evidence record (evt-dino-human-coexistence-001, DEBUNKED; evt-paluxy-mantracks-001, DEBUNKED) already kills. The exhibits are the 292nd DEBUNKED family: the museum-exhibit-as-evidence telling.

Earliest attestation: The Creation Museum and Ark Encounter coexistence exhibits themselves (2007–present); documented in the July 2026 Cincinnati Enquirer 10-year anniversary coverage (cincinnati.com, 10 July 2026)
Sources: Cincinnati Enquirer, 'Ark Encounter celebrates 10 years marked by a flood of tourists, controversy,' 10 July 2026 — dinosaur animatronics in cages 'because the ministry believes dinosaurs (including carnivorous dinosaurs) were alive at the same time as humans'; Bill Nye: 'a monument dedicated to anti-science'; cajunlimeys.com, 'Summer '26 Trek: Day 21: The Creation Museum,' 1 Aug 2026 — the museum's 'dragons' exhibit ('a movie attempted to explain the link between mythical dragons and dinosaurs'); 'a key point of the Young Earth Creation argument is that dinosaurs were created on the sixth day and coexisted with man'; 'The presence of dinosaurs with man was a prevalent theme'; worldeventsandthebible.com, Ark Encounter visit (~Sept 2026) — 'a picture of Adam and Eve standing in the water with Dinosaurs off in the distance'; 'There is no Biblical or historical documentation to support such a belief'; citizendailypost.com FAQ, October 2026 — 'the Creation Museum and Ark Encounter both show dinosaurs and humans living alongside each other'; 'This is contrary to the scientific consensus that non-avian dinosaurs went extinct about 66 million years ago, long before humans appeared'
Falsifiability: A single fossil of a human in undisturbed Mesozoic strata (the record's standing falsifiability line) — the exhibits cannot supply it, because they are not excavations.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2007-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-84.5866,38.6866,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2007</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Venona 'vindicates' McCarthy</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Killed by the scholars who built the case: Klehr himself documents that most of McCarthy's targets are not in the Venona content and the accusations remain largely unsupported by evidence, and Haynes &amp; Klehr state flatly that Venona gives 'no basis' for McCarthy's signature conspiracy charges (Acheson, Marshall).
Layer: fake · Place: Austin, Texas (Texas State Board of Education, 2009)
Dates: 2009 → 2009 (confidence: high)

The Venona decrypts prove Senator Joseph McCarthy's accusations were right — that the evidence shows he was justified in his zeal in exposing those he believed to be Soviet spies or communist sympathizers (the form in which the Texas State Board of Education proposed revising high school history curricula in 2009).

Earliest attestation: Texas State Board of Education curriculum revision proposal, 2009, as reported in the press; the vindication argument's circulation in the 1990s–2000s after the 1995 release
Sources: Wikipedia, 'Venona project' (citing TFN Insider, 29 Oct 2009, 'Rehabilitating Joseph McCarthy?'): 2009 Texas textbook policy suggested 'Venona evidence shows Senator Joseph McCarthy to have been justified in his zeal in exposing those whom he believed to be Soviet spies or communist sympathizers'; historian Harvey Klehr rebuts — 'most people and organizations identified by McCarthy as possible Soviet assets or sympathizers, such as those brought forward in the Army-McCarthy hearings or rival politicians in the Democratic Party, were not mentioned in the Venona content and that his accusations remain largely unsupported by evidence'; Haynes &amp; Klehr, 'Venona: Decoding Soviet Espionage in America' (Yale, 1999), Ch. 1: 'There is no basis in Venona for implicating Acheson or Marshall in a Communist conspiracy, but because the deciphered Venona messages were classified and unknown to the public, demagogues such as McCarthy had the opportunity to mix together accurate information about betrayal by men such as Harry White and Alger Hiss with falsehoods about Acheson and Marshall that served partisan political goals'
Falsifiability: A systematic mapping showing that a substantial share of McCarthy's named targets appear in the Venona decrypts as Soviet assets; Klehr's audit finds the opposite</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2009-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-97.74,30.27,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2009</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2012 Mayan 'end of the world' — debunked</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The only ancient reference (Tortuguero Monument 6) is eroded and describes an investiture ceremony of the deity Bolon Yokte', not an apocalypse; the Long Count is an odometer that rolls over; other inscriptions project dates to ~4772 CE; contemporary Maya leaders and Mexico's INAH publicly rejected the apocalyptic reading as a Western distortion.
Layer: fake · Place: Tortuguero, Tabasco, Mexico (site of Monument 6 — heavily quarried/damaged before its significance was recognized)
Dates: 2012 → 2012 (confidence: high)

The Mayan calendar predicted the end of the world on 21 December 2012.

Earliest attestation: Modern phenomenon: the apocalyptic reading is a 20th–21st-century Western construction (Jenkins 1987; the film '2012' 2009); the only Classic-period reference is Tortuguero Monument 6, 7th century CE
Sources: Tortuguero Monument 6 (Tabasco; ruler Bahlam Ajaw 'Jaguar Lord', early 7th c. CE) — the only Classic-period reference to 13.0.0.0.0; stone cracked and eroded, end of passage near-illegible; Gronemeyer, Sven &amp; MacLeod, Barbara — 'What Could Happen in 2012: A Re-Analysis of the 13-Bak'tun Prophecy on Tortuguero Monument 6' (Wayeb Notes 34, 2010): the text describes the 'display'/'investiture' of Bolon Yokte' — a ceremony, not an apocalypse; Archaeology Magazine, Nov/Dec 2012 — 'The Maya Sense of Time': of ~5,000 known Maya inscriptions, only two (Tortuguero, Comalcalco) reference the 2012 date; the Palenque Temple of the Inscriptions projects dates to ~4772 CE; Mexico's INAH, Nov 2011 (AP): 'western messianic thought has twisted the cosmovision of ancient civilizations like the Maya' — the institute convened 60 Maya experts at Palenque to dispel the doomsday reading; Oxlaljuj Ajpop / Felipe Gomez (AFP, Oct 2012): Guatemala's Maya alliance publicly protested the twisting of their traditions for doomsday tourism; Reuters/Ipsos Global Public Affairs poll, May 2012 (n=16,262, 21 countries): 10% believed the Mayan calendar could signify the end of the world in 2012; ~15% believed the world would end in their lifetime — carried as belief data, not evidence
Falsifiability: A Maya inscription explicitly describing destruction, cataclysm, or a final ending at 13.0.0.0.0 would reopen it; no such text exists among ~5,000 known inscriptions</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2012-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-92.55,17.88,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2012</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>'Found by technology alone' - the myth that erased the map-keepers</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Two kills from the institutional record itself: (1) Parks Canada's published account says the team 'never gave up on the belief that Inuit oral histories were accurate,' consulted Kamookak and Gjoa Haven/Cambridge Bay residents throughout, and located Erebus 'near to where Inukpujijuk told Hall it had sunk'; (2) Terror was found in Terror Bay on the direct tip of Inuk crewmember Sammy Kogvik's mast-in-the-ice sighting, detouring the Martin Bergmann - and the Bay matches the 1869 In-nook-poo-zhe-jook account to Hall. And the 'vanished without a trace' leg was already dead in 1859: the Victory Point note (evt-victory-point-note-001) is the expedition's own written record of its fate.
Layer: fake · Place: Queen Maud Gulf / Terror Bay, Nunavut
Dates: 2014 → 2016 (confidence: high)

The wrecks of HMS Erebus (found 2014) and HMS Terror (found 2016) were located purely by modern search technology - sonar, satellites, and systematic grid searching - with no meaningful help from Indigenous Inuit knowledge, and the Franklin expedition otherwise vanished without leaving any record of its fate.

Earliest attestation: Post-discovery popular coverage minimizing the Inuit role - the belief this record grades.
Sources: Parks Canada, Wrecks of HMS Erebus and HMS Terror - Inuit traditional knowledge page: the published institutional credit ('never gave up on the belief that Inuit oral histories were accurate'; Kamookak consultations; Erebus 'near to where Inukpujijuk told Hall it had sunk'); Canadian Geographic / The Guardian (2016) - Sammy Kogvik's mast-in-the-ice report and the Martin Bergmann's detour to Terror Bay; Parks Canada's confirmation; Indian Country Today (2014) - 'The Inuit Were Right': Louie Kamookak's quote on oral history; the Ugjulik/Utjulik search area matching Inuit accounts; Archaeology Magazine (2017) - Terror 'around 60 miles north of its sister ship'; found by ARF vessel detouring on an Inuk crewmember's report; The Victory Point note (evt-victory-point-note-001) - the 1848 written record of the expedition's fate, found 1859: the 'without a trace' leg was dead before the ships were ever found
Falsifiability: Parks Canada retracting its published Inuit-knowledge credit, or evidence the search boxes came from technology alone, would overturn this record; the institutional record says the opposite.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2014-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-97.9,68.3,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2014</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 'Ein Gedi scroll proves the Hebrew Bible goes back 2000 years' 2026 telling — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Both load-bearing claims are independently disproven: the Dead Sea Scrolls contain older Masoretic-type Leviticus witnesses, and the Ein Gedi scroll is dated 3rd–4th c. CE (~1500–1700 years old), not 2000.
Layer: fake · Place: En-Gedi synagogue, Dead Sea shore (scroll's findspot)
Dates: 2016 → 2026 (confidence: high)

The telling, recirculated September–October 2026 (a rehash of the 2016 virtual-unwrapping story), that the Ein Gedi Leviticus scroll constitutes 'the first physical evidence that the version of the Hebrew Bible used today goes back 2000 years.' The telling fails on both its load-bearing words. FIRST: older Masoretic-type Leviticus witnesses already exist among the Dead Sea Scrolls (2nd century BCE). 2000 YEARS: the Ein Gedi scroll itself dates to the 3rd–4th century CE — roughly 1,500–1,700 years old (Seales et al., Science Advances, September 2016; the scroll was excavated in 1970 from the synagogue ark at En-Gedi, carbonized in a fire). What the scroll genuinely proves — its consonantal text is identical to the medieval Masoretic text, bridging the Qumran-to-medieval gap — is real and important; the 'first' and '2000 years' claims are not.

Earliest attestation: Seales et al., Science Advances, 21 Sept 2016; the 2026 rehash via quantumstudybible.com, 30 Sept 2026.
Sources: Seales, Bailey, Segal, Tov, Shor et al., Science Advances (Sept 2016) — virtual unwrapping of the Ein Gedi scroll; 35 lines of Leviticus 1–2; consonantal text identical to the Masoretic text; 3rd–4th c. CE dating; phys.org, 'Digitally unwrapped scroll reveals earliest Old Testament scripture' (Sept 2016) — 'the earliest Pentateuchal book ever found in a synagogue' (not the earliest anywhere); earlier Leviticus evidence in the Dead Sea Scrolls, per Segal; Catholic News Agency (Sept 2016) — 'at least 1,500 years old'; 1970 En-Gedi synagogue excavation; previously undecipherable; quantumstudybible.com, 'Leviticus 000 Prologue' (5 Oct 2026) — the rehash: 'the first physical evidence that the version of the Hebrew Bible used today goes back 2000 years'
Falsifiability: A redating of the Ein Gedi scroll to the 1st century CE would repair the '2000 years' claim; proof that no older Masoretic-type Leviticus witness exists would repair the 'first' claim. Neither exists.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2016-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.39,31.46,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2016</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Petra qibla theory (Gibson 2016–2017) — DEBUNKED</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The orientations Gibson read as 'facing Petra' were his own readings measured against modern directions — a methodologically invalid comparison. The measured early-qibla data (Wasit 706 toward winter sunset; Egypt's winter-sunrise qiblat al-sahaba; Damascus's cardinal alignment inherited from the Roman temple; the Maghrib's SSE, parallel to the Kaaba's NW wall) is explicable by 7th-century folk astronomy, and the first generations had no means to aim at Petra to degree accuracy.
Layer: fake · Place: Petra, Jordan (the claimed qibla target)
Dates: 2016 → 2017 (confidence: high)

Dan Gibson's claim (the 2016 film 'The Sacred City'; 'Qur'anic Geography' and 'Early Islamic Qiblas', 2017): the first mosques were built facing Petra, Jordan, not Mecca — the original qibla was Petra.

Earliest attestation: Dan Gibson, 'The Sacred City' film (2016); 'Qur'anic Geography' and 'Early Islamic Qiblas' (2017)
Sources: Dan Gibson, 'Early Islamic Qiblas' (2017) — the claim as published; David A. King, 'The Petra fallacy: Early mosques do face the Sacred Kaaba in Mecca but Dan Gibson doesn't know how' (The Muslim World, 1 Dec 2018) — the methodological demolition; David A. King, review of Gibson's 'Earliest Qiblas' (2019) — the follow-up refutation
Falsifiability: Already falsified: King's re-measurement of the same mosques. The theory would revive only with a documented 7th-century surveying method capable of degree-accurate great-circle targeting, which no one has produced.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2016-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>35.4444,30.3285,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2016</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The Nazca tridactyl "alien mummies" (2023–2026 forensic verdict)</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — Convergent independent lines close the question: (1) DNA from the specimens, tested by the Paleo DNA laboratory at Lakehead University, is 100% human (male), not 'unknown'; (2) Peruvian forensic archaeologists (Ministry of Culture) found the specimens to be composite constructions — three-fingered extremities assembled from human, llama, dog, and bird bones held together with modern synthetic glues, with animal-hide or gelatin paste coating; (3) carbon-14 dates (245–410 AD range reported) date the source bones, not the assemblies; (4) the Peruvian World Congress on Mummy Studies classified the case a hoax made from real human remains and demanded an inquiry into possible archaeological crimes; (5) Giorgio Tsoukalos — the public face of ancient-astronaut television — publicly dismissed the specimens as handcrafted dolls ('This is people gluing parts together'). The fringe venues (vibewire '30% unknown genome', project-unity CT-scan claims) offer no peer-reviewed evidence and are fenced as uncitable advocacy.
Layer: fake · Place: Nazca region, Peru (reported find site; specimens seized in Lima, October 2024)
Dates: 2023 → 2026 (confidence: high)

