<Placemark><name>'The Wow! signal has been conclusively explained as natural'</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The published record disproves 'conclusively': the reanalysis papers are still preprints (labelled for ApJ submission as of August 2026), the magnetar hypothesis is explicitly unconfirmed and never observed in operation, and the authors themselves state the work does not rule out artificial sources — the telling kills itself on its own sources. 2026-10-17 exhibit (via #442 Dr. Naina Bhatt — The Perfume Reader, master-list VII.7 second pass): the gsnsp.com 'Solved It' telling appended — asserts conclusive natural explanation while its own cited sources remain preprints with the authors' non-rule-out caveat intact; grade held, no supersession. Chain strengthened 6→7 sources 2026-10-19 (via #646): the telling persists in the October-2026 press (the undated 'mystery solved at last' rehash) — documented as telling, not finding.
Layer: fake · Place: Arecibo Observatory, Puerto Rico (project site; Big Ear at 40.2492, -83.0456, demolished 1998)
Dates: 2024-08 → 2026-08 (confidence: high)

The 'Wow!' signal of 1977 has been conclusively explained as a natural phenomenon.

Earliest attestation: Popular press coverage of Méndez et al. 2024 preprint (arXiv:2408.08513), August 2024
Sources: Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! I: The Wow! Signal as an Astrophysical Maser Flare' (2024 preprint, arXiv:2408.08513); Méndez et al., 'Arecibo Wow! II: Revised Properties of the Wow! Signal from Archival Ohio SETI Data' (2025 preprint, arXiv:2508.10657); SpaceDaily, 'A 2025 reanalysis of the original Wow! signal...' (13 August 2026): 'This is one preprint, not settled consensus... It does not turn one observation into a repeat detection, and it does not identify what produced it.'; Leonard David, 'Perplexing Wow! Signal – New Clues Reported' (quoting Méndez: 'Our results don't solve the mystery of the Wow! Signal'); Clownfish TV / National Geographic reporting: the team 'doesn't claim it's closed the case'; the paper says the work doesn't 'conclusively rule out' artificial or extraterrestrial sources; gsnsp.com, 'Wow! Signal Explained: How Hydrogen Masers Solved It' (~Aug–Oct 2026): headline asserts the Arecibo work 'finally unraveled the cosmic puzzle' and that the signal 'was not an alien civilization shouting into the dark' — while its own cited papers are preprints (ApJ accepted, unpublished), the maser mechanism is unconfirmed (never observed in operation), and the authors state the work does not rule out artificial sources. The telling asserts what the papers deny.; newspointapp.com, "Cosmic 'wow' alien signal mystery solved at last, claim scientists" (undated aggregator piece, crawled Oct 2026): sensationalized rehash of the Aug-2025 preprint — the 'conclusively explained' telling persisting in the October-2026 press cycle; appended as the telling's persistence exhibit, not as evidence; paranormalscience.co.uk, 'The 50-Year-Old Phantom Radio Signal That Has Utterly Stumped Scientists. Until Now?' (crawled Oct 2026, ~11 days old): 'they've finally cracked the case' framing over the same unreviewed arXiv preprints — a second October-2026 instance of the telling persisting despite the papers' own fences; appended as the telling's persistence exhibit, not as evidence; curiolink.net, 'The Wow! Signal: What a 2025 Reanalysis Found' (published 2026-10-01): a careful October-2026 recap — 'found the signal's properties consistent with a rare natural astrophysical event, but its exact source remains unconfirmed' — explicitly ruling out terrestrial interference/satellites/solar/glitches while fencing the natural alternative as consistency, not closure; appended as the careful-press counterpart to the 'cracked the case' telling exhibits; skylens.yantraai.app, 'Wow Signal: The 72-Second Transmission From Deep Space That Scientists Still Can't Explain — 49 Years On' (2026-09-22): the honest-answer telling — 'Not definitively. No. That is the honest answer, and scientists deserve credit for saying so' — '0 Confirmed explanations, 49 years on'; explicitly warns against the inverse distortion: treating the 2024–2026 hydrogen-cloud work as a formal announcement that Wow! has finally been solved ('neither the source nor the amplification process has been independently confirmed'); appended as a third October-window careful-press counterpart exhibit to the 'solved at last' telling exhibits; transitsearch.org, "The 'Wow! Signal': Solved, and It's Not Aliens" (crawled 2026-10-19, ~1 day old): headline declares the signal 'Solved' while the body hedges — 'may finally have a natural explanation', 'suggests', 'could have' — and its cited evidence is the same unreviewed Méndez preprints (Arecibo Wow! I accepted by ApJ, unpublished; maser mechanism unconfirmed, authors' non-rule-out caveat intact); conclusion re-asserts 'now explained as a natural astrophysical phenomenon'. The telling's headline-outruns-its-body pattern documented as the newest October-2026 persistence exhibit, not as evidence
Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed, independently replicated confirmation of the magnetar-HI mechanism reproducing the signal's exact profile — or any consensus natural explanation accepted by the radio-astronomy community — would overturn this verdict; press headlines are not the unit of proof</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2024-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>-66.7528,18.3442,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2024</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 'Amarna 'Apiru are the Hebrews' telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — The telling grades DEBUNKED as a telling: the equation is asserted by re-translation, not evidence — the term is attested from Mari to Egypt across ~7 centuries (predating Abraham), and mainstream scholarship reads it as a social category, not an ethnic name. The 'deliberate disguise' framing fails the provenance rule: no evidence of concealment, only disagreement with the consensus.
