Falsifiability: A peer-reviewed philological publication accepting the 'Apiru = 'Ivrim equation as a settled translation, or a text explicitly equating the two populations, would reopen the record; re-rendered confessional quotations do not.</description><styleUrl>#debunked</styleUrl><TimeStamp><when>2026-01-01</when></TimeStamp><Point><coordinates>30.9,27.64,0</coordinates></Point><ExtendedData><Data name="grade"><value>DEBUNKED</value></Data><Data name="layer"><value>fake</value></Data><Data name="year"><value>2026</value></Data><Data name="weight"><value>0.6</value></Data></ExtendedData></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>The 2026 'Ark of the Covenant found at Shiloh' press telling</name><description>Grade: DEBUNKED — No credible party claims the Ark was found — ABR itself explicitly disclaims it; the 2026 finds are walls, gates, and jars, not the chest; and the biblical narrative itself has the Ark leaving Shiloh (1 Samuel 4–6), so the telling is contradicted by the excavation team, the skeptics, and the text alike.
